{"_type":"item","id":"019dc5d4f6877ae5be60ea02e51dcd38","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plugin authoring contract: discoverability of required hooks","description":"## Problem\n\nThe plugin platform exposes several hook surfaces a panel script can implement:\n\n**Lifecycle (panel-side, GDScript):**\n- `_on_panel_loaded(ctx)`\n- `_on_panel_unload()`\n- `_on_panel_save_request() -> Dictionary` (host_owned save AND project-state capture)\n- `_on_panel_load_request(document: Dictionary)` (host_owned load AND project-state restore)\n- `receive(channel, payload)` (broker push)\n- `on_progress(request_id, phase, fraction)`\n\n**Plugin-server-side (MCP channels declared in manifest):**\n- `project_file.serialize_channel`\n- `project_file.deserialize_channel`\n- `project_export.collect_channel`\n- `project_export.apply_channel`\n- arbitrary `ipc_channels`\n\nToday, all of these are **duck-typed**: the platform calls `if root.has_method(X): root.X(...)` and silently skips when missing. Manifest channel fields are declared as plain strings with no validation that the corresponding tools exist on the plugin server. Result:\n\n- A plugin author can ship a panel that \"works\" (opens, greets) but doesn't participate in project save — `Save Project → Reopen` silently loses all in-tab state. No warning, no error, no log.\n- The hello_scene MVP itself shipped with this gap; surfaced during Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 manual testing.\n- New plugin authors have nothing but the design doc to read; nothing in the IDE, install flow, or runtime tells them what they should implement.\n\n## Approaches (file recommendation: B + C combined)\n\n**A. Documentation + reference plugin only (weakest).**\nUpdate `Docs/plugin_system_guide.md`; make `src/plugins/hello_scene/` a fully-exhaustive reference covering every hook. Cheap, but docs rot and authors who skim miss it.\n\n**B. Manifest opts in to capabilities; install-time validator enforces.**\nAdd `\"capabilities\": [\"project_state\", \"host_owned_save\", \"project_export\", ...]` to the manifest. PluginDB.install() validates that declared capabilities have their corresponding panel hooks (script scan) and server channels (MCP tools/list probe). Fail install loudly with a structured error if missing. Authors can also opt OUT cleanly (\"I'm a stateless tool, don't ask me to save\").\n\n**C. Optional `MinervaPluginPanel` base class with virtual hook stubs.**\nShip a base class that `extends Control` with virtual stubs for every hook, doc comments explaining contract, and no-op defaults. Authors who `extends MinervaPluginPanel` see all hooks via IDE autocomplete and inline docs; authors who extend `Control` directly still work (backward compat). Combined with B, the IDE surface and the install-time check reinforce each other.\n\n**Recommendation: B + C together.** B catches missing hooks at install time; C surfaces them in the IDE. A is implicit when both ship.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Manifest schema accepts `capabilities` array; documented allowed values.\n2. PluginDB.install() validates declared capabilities → returns structured error if a declared hook is missing on the panel script or its corresponding MCP tool is absent from the server's tools/list. Tests cover both pass and fail paths.\n3. `MinervaPluginPanel` base class shipped under `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/` (or similar). Plugin authoring guide updated to recommend it. Tests verify subclassing path and Control-direct path both still work.\n4. `hello_scene` migrated to `extends MinervaPluginPanel`, declares `[\"project_state\", \"host_owned_save\"]`, demonstrates exhaustive UI+server state round-trip (LineEdit text + Label text + dirty flag + plugin-server-side counter).\n5. Authoring guide explains: \"What's required vs optional\", \"How to opt out\", \"How to verify your plugin captures full state.\"\n\n## Related\n\n- Surfaced from Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 manual testing (project save → reopen lost LineEdit content).\n- Sibling tasks in this plan that touch the same hook surfaces: `019dc0555c2175dfae15c589540c6e7a` (lifecycle hooks), `019dc055700777d685cbcb2a71c3fb6c` (editor integration), `019dc05578cf7e388ac090e3f19ade19` (project-file hooks).","created_at":"2026-04-25T18:09:21","updated_at":"2026-04-25T18:09:21","priority":2,"tags":["authoring","dx","godot","platform","plugin-system"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc5ee31e3748d9673693d12decd13","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Auto-start plugins required by restored project tabs on project load","description":"## Problem\n\nWhen Minerva restarts and a project is loaded, PLUGIN_SCENE tabs in the .minproj are restored, but the corresponding plugin servers are not started unless the manifest declares `autostart: true`. This breaks the \"walk away / walk back\" semantics for plugin tabs unless every plugin sets autostart=true, which is wasteful for plugins that don't run when no tab is open.\n\nHello_scene currently sets `autostart: true` as an MVP shortcut surfaced during Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 testing. That should not be the long-term default.\n\n## Proper fix\n\n`ProjectMenuActions.deserialize_project()` should:\n1. Walk the restored Editors array\n2. Collect the set of plugin_ids referenced by PLUGIN_SCENE entries\n3. For each, if PluginDB knows it and it's not already RUNNING, ask PluginManager to start it\n4. Wait for handshake (or proceed and let deserialize_channel skip with the existing warning if start fails)\n\n`vboxEditor._deserialize_plugin_scene_editor` already handles \"plugin not running\" gracefully now — UI state restores via __panel_state regardless. So this work is purely about reviving the plugin servers, not about correctness of the panel UI.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Loading a .minproj with PLUGIN_SCENE tabs starts the required plugin servers (no autostart=true needed in the manifest).\n2. Walk-away/walk-back round-trip restores both UI (already works) and server-side state (deserialize_channel runs after auto-start completes).\n3. Hello_scene manifest reverts to `autostart: false` and round-trip still works.\n4. If a required plugin is uninstalled or fails to start, the existing placeholder/warning paths kick in (no crash).\n\n## Related\n\n- Surfaced from Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 testing.\n- Sibling: 019dc5d4f6877ae5be60ea02e51dcd38 (authoring contract).","created_at":"2026-04-25T18:36:55","updated_at":"2026-04-25T19:00:34","priority":3,"tags":["autostart","platform","plugin-system","project-load"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Shipped in commit c14d58bb. vboxEditor.deserialize pre-pass collects plugin_ids from PLUGIN_SCENE entries, starts each, awaits handshake (5s timeout per plugin). hello_scene autostart reverted to false; project-load auto-starts the plugin via the new pre-pass. Verified live by the user."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc5f31ccd710a9757e7d33ebc5f84","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin-global state round-trip in .minproj (tab-independent)","description":"## Problem\n\nThe plugin platform's project-save / project-load flow is currently *tab-anchored*:\n- Each PLUGIN_SCENE editor entry in `.minproj.Editors[]` carries a `plugin_state.payload` slot.\n- The platform calls the plugin's `project_file.serialize_channel` once per tab and stores the result there.\n- On load, it auto-starts the plugin (if needed), creates the panel, and pushes the saved state back via `project_file.deserialize_channel` + `_on_panel_load_request`.\n\nIf a plugin holds state that doesn't belong to any one tab — for example:\n- CAD plugin's tessellation cache, recently-used colors, view preferences\n- OBS plugin's connection settings, last-known scene list\n- A plugin's global \"session\" object that survives across all its tabs in a project\n\n…there's no place for that state to live in `.minproj`. The plugin can stash it on disk somewhere (its `data_directory` or a sidecar), but that becomes user-machine-local and doesn't travel with the project. It also doesn't capture the \"walk-away / walk-back\" intent for shared projects.\n\n## Status: proposed, gated on actual demand\n\nWe do not currently have a confirmed use case. The hello plugin doesn't need this; the per-tab path suffices. The CAD plugin (DCR-3) is likely to surface the need — caches, recently-used materials, view preset library, etc. We're filing this proposed DCR so the design question is not lost; it will be approved and implemented if/when CAD or another consumer hits the gap.\n\n## Sketched design (not committed)\n\nOption A — manifest-declared global channels, parallel to project_file:\n```json\n\"plugin_global\": {\n  \"serialize_channel\": \"myplugin.serialize_global\",\n  \"deserialize_channel\": \"myplugin.deserialize_global\"\n}\n```\n.minproj gains a top-level `PluginGlobalState: { plugin_id: <state> }` map. On save, ProjectMenuActions iterates running plugins with this declaration and calls each. On load, it does the reverse — but only after auto-starting plugins referenced anywhere in the project (tabs or global state).\n\nOption B — let plugins emit a single state document via the existing event mechanism. Cheaper but less explicit about ownership.\n\nOption C — fold global state into the first tab's payload, with a manifest flag. Keeps .minproj simpler but encodes weird ordering (which tab \"owns\" global state?).\n\nOption A is the cleanest of the three but the most ceremony. Pick when a real consumer arrives.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (when approved)\n\n1. New manifest section `plugin_global` parsed by PluginDefinition with both channels required if either is set.\n2. New `.minproj` field `PluginGlobalState` round-trips by plugin_id.\n3. Capability `plugin_global_state` added to the authoring contract (DCR-1 grandchild 019dc5d4f6877ae5be60ea02e51dcd38) — install-time validator confirms both channels exist in ipc_messages.\n4. Backward compatible: plugins without `plugin_global` still work unchanged.\n5. Test plugin demonstrates a counter or cache that round-trips independent of any open tab.\n\n## Related\n\n- Surfaces from Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 design discussion.\n- DCR-1 (`019dc0542aa2755b93ec647c5b0f7271`) — the platform foundation this would extend.\n- DCR-3 (`019dc054a4537015998d2f75f505fbe8`) — likely first consumer.\n- Parent of: an authoring-contract update for the new capability.","created_at":"2026-04-25T18:42:17","updated_at":"2026-04-25T18:42:17","priority":3,"tags":["platform","plugin-system","project-save","proposed"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Per-kind AnnotationAuthorTool implementations (click-to-author UX)","description":"## Problem\n\nThe authoring UI framework (`019dc055e8da710588ea283351710c01`) shipped in Phase 1B Round 5: `AnnotationToolbar` populates from `AnnotationRegistry`, `AnnotationAuthorTool` base class defines `on_pointer_down/move/up` + `annotation_ready` / `cancelled` signals, `AnnotationHost` protocol exists. The toolbar's `_try_add_button` only adds buttons for kinds whose `author_ui()` returns non-null.\n\nBut every built-in kind (arrow, text, region, polyline, highlight, measure_distance, measure_angle, measure_radius) returns the base-class default `null` from `author_ui()`. Per-kind `AnnotationAuthorTool` subclasses were deferred when the framework landed, leaving the substrate **invisible to end users** — any editor that wires up an `AnnotationToolbar` will see an empty toolbar.\n\nThis was discovered during Phase 1B Checkpoint 2 review when planning the substrate live test in the hello plugin.\n\n## Scope\n\nImplement an `AnnotationAuthorTool` subclass for each built-in 2D kind, override `author_ui()` on each kind to return a fresh tool instance.\n\nPer kind, the tool:\n1. Captures pointer events while active (`on_pointer_down/move/up`).\n2. Maintains in-progress preview state, drawn each frame via `draw_preview(ctx)`.\n3. Emits `annotation_ready(dict)` when the user finishes (e.g. second click for arrow, double-click or escape for polyline, return key for text).\n4. Emits `cancelled()` when the user presses Escape or otherwise abandons.\n\nRecommended sequencing (ascending complexity):\n- **Arrow** — two-click (start, end). Simplest two-point tool.\n- **Text** — one click + dialog/lineedit for the string.\n- **Region** — click-drag rectangle.\n- **Highlight** — click-drag rectangle (alpha-styled).\n- **Measure_distance** — two-click, label shows numeric value.\n- **Measure_angle** — three-click.\n- **Measure_radius** — two-click.\n- **Polyline** — N-click + double-click to finish.\n\n(`ink_stroke` is filed separately under `019dc0ebf9d5797a82af41edd4c8c180` — it depends on the live-authoring API and pen-input capture, not part of this task.)\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Each built-in 2D kind overrides `author_ui()` to return a fresh `AnnotationAuthorTool` subclass instance.\n2. Mounting `AnnotationToolbar` against a populated `AnnotationRegistry` shows one button per built-in kind.\n3. Selecting a button activates the tool; clicking on the host canvas authors an annotation that lands in the host via the `annotation_ready` → `host.add_annotation()` path.\n4. Escape cancels the in-progress annotation; switching tools mid-author cancels the previous tool.\n5. Tests cover at least the two-point case (arrow) and the N-point case (polyline) for state transitions, signal emission, and cancellation.\n6. No new platform changes required — these tools exclusively use the existing AnnotationAuthorTool / AnnotationHost / AnnotationRenderContext APIs.\n\n## Open question for the planning conversation\n\nThis task is a real chunk of UX work, not a \"test of the substrate.\" Worth confirming:\n- Whether to do all 8 kinds together or sequence them (arrow + polyline first as MVP, others later)\n- Whether to gate this behind a real consumer (PCB migration, CAD plugin) so we can validate end-to-end, vs. doing it standalone with a test harness\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling: 019dc055e8da710588ea283351710c01 (Authoring UI framework — done)\n- Future consumer: PCB migration `019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff`, CAD plugin `019dc054a4537015998d2f75f505fbe8`","created_at":"2026-04-25T19:01:05","updated_at":"2026-04-25T20:18:07","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","authoring","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"R1+R2 shipped arrow (commit 84db2614, 51/0) and text (commit cd5f78de, 62/0) per-kind AnnotationAuthorTool implementations. Five remaining built-in kinds (region, polyline, highlight, measure_distance, measure_angle, measure_radius) deferred to a future round. Filing follow-up task to track that remaining work."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc61535db75b087b818b338cd967b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Hello plugin annotation surface (smoke consumer for substrate)","description":"Smoke consumer for the annotation substrate.  Adds a canvas Control and AnnotationToolbar to HelloPanel, wires a `HelloAnnotationHost` subclass, and rounds-trips annotations through both the file sidecar (.hello.annotations.json) and the project state (__panel_state).\n\nThis is NOT a real consumer — it exists purely to validate that the substrate works end-to-end before CAD or PCB consume it.  Acceptance is \"user can draw an arrow on the hello panel canvas, save the project, reload, and see the arrow restored.\"\n\nRound-1 scope is arrow only (toolbar shows one button).  Round-2 will add text; Round-3 adds ink_stroke once the primitive is implemented.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. HelloPanel shows a canvas Control above the existing widgets, with an AnnotationToolbar populated from a per-panel AnnotationRegistry that has BuiltinKinds.register_all() applied.\n2. Clicking the arrow toolbar button activates AnnotationArrowAuthorTool; clicking on the canvas draws an arrow that is added to the host.\n3. Annotations save to .hello.annotations.json sidecar on Ctrl+S.\n4. Annotations save to __panel_state.annotations on project save.\n5. Annotations restore on file open (sidecar wins) AND on project reload (__panel_state wins if no file).\n6. Tests cover: host subclass behavior, save/load round-trip through both paths, sidecar precedence rule.\n\n## Sidecar / project-state precedence\n\nSidecar wins if both are present.  Reasoning: the file is authoritative — a user who Ctrl+S'd is making a deliberate \"this is the document state\" choice.  Project state captures unsaved walk-away/walk-back state but should not override an explicit file save.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling: 019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f (Per-kind AnnotationAuthorTool)\n- Smoke exit milestone: arrow + text + ink_stroke all working in hello plugin.","created_at":"2026-04-25T19:19:32","updated_at":"2026-04-25T20:18:12","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","hello-plugin","smoke-test","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"R1 commit 84db2614 + R2 commit cd5f78de. Hello plugin now has a side-mounted PCBEditor-style toolbar (CanvasRow HBox: canvas left + 120px sidebar right + greet UI bottom). Annotations save/restore through both __panel_state (project) and .hello.annotations.json sidecar (file) with sidecar-wins precedence. Reference wiring pattern for CAD/PCB. Tests: 26/0 hello_annotation_host + 22/0 hello_annotation_round_trip."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc632a7dd7fdfb4f22974651333a9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Wire toolbar_icon on built-in 2D annotation kinds","description":"## Problem\n\n`AnnotationKind.toolbar_icon` exists (read by `AnnotationToolbar._try_add_button` line 100), but no built-in 2D kind sets it. The toolbar shows text-label buttons today; PCBEditor uses icon-only `toggle_mode` Buttons with tooltips, which is the target UX (DCR-2 milestone — \"PCBEditor has annotations on the side with graphical icons; we should copy the UI for annotations straight from PCBEditor\").\n\nPCBEditor already preloads suitable icons by UID:\n- arrow:    `uid://cln205u37w7n0`\n- text:     `uid://obermhq5hkgs`\n- region:   `uid://bkg87ln1gvdla`\n- polyline: `uid://crvgt6o3lsbr1`\n(see `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/PCBEditor.gd:280-316`)\n\n## Scope\n\nEach of the eight built-in 2D kinds (`arrow`, `text`, `region`, `polyline`, `highlight`, `measure_distance`, `measure_angle`, `measure_radius`) gets `toolbar_icon` assigned in its `_init()`.\n\nWhere icons exist (arrow / text / region / polyline): preload via UID so the assets are shared with PCBEditor.\nWhere they don't (highlight / measure_*): pick reasonable existing icons from the Minerva theme assets, or commit fresh assets into a new `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/icons/` folder and reference those.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Every built-in kind in `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/kinds/*.gd` sets `toolbar_icon` in its `_init()`.\n2. AnnotationToolbar with the registry populated by `BuiltinKinds.register_all()` shows icon-only buttons (no text labels) when toolbar_icon is set.\n3. Tooltips remain (existing tooltip behavior unchanged).\n4. `test_annotation_builtin_kinds.gd` updated to assert `toolbar_icon != null` on each kind.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling: AnnotationToolbar layout refactor (filing now)\n- Trigger: DCR-2 smoke test R2; user feedback that toolbar should match PCBEditor pattern.","created_at":"2026-04-25T19:51:41","updated_at":"2026-04-25T20:18:01","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","built-in","icons","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"R2 commit cd5f78de. Four built-in kinds got icons reused from PCBEditor (arrow/text/region/polyline). Four kinds (highlight/measure_*) remain icon-less with TODO referencing this task ID — they'll close in a future round when icon assets exist. Tests: 93/0 in test_annotation_builtin_kinds.gd including 8 new toolbar_icon assertions."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc632dfe170538b75958592f38d1a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Refactor AnnotationToolbar layout to match PCBEditor (VBox + header + FlowContainer)","description":"## Problem\n\n`AnnotationToolbar` extends `HBoxContainer` and adds buttons inline. PCBEditor uses a vertical right-sidebar pattern with:\n- An \"Annotate\" section header label\n- A `FlowContainer` for the actual buttons (auto-wraps as the sidebar narrows)\n- An annotation-mode label below showing the active mode in pink\n(see `PCBEditor.gd:269-322`)\n\nDCR-2 smoke test R2 calls for porting this UX so consumer plugins (CAD, eventually PCB once migrated) get a consistent annotation surface.\n\n## Scope\n\nRefactor `AnnotationToolbar.gd` to extend `VBoxContainer` instead of `HBoxContainer`, with internal layout:\n- Header `Label` (\"Annotate\" by default; configurable)\n- `FlowContainer` holding the per-kind buttons (icon-only, `toggle_mode = true`, tooltips preserved)\n- Status `Label` below (text = active kind's `display_name` while a tool is active, empty otherwise)\n\nPublic API must NOT change:\n- `set_registry(registry)` — still works\n- `set_host(host)` — still works\n- `get_active_tool()` — still works\n- `active_tool_changed(tool)` signal — still emitted\n- existing tests (`test_annotation_authoring.gd`) must still pass without modification\n\nImplementation note: change of base class (HBox → VBox) is a breaking change for consumers that relied on the horizontal layout. The hello plugin (the only current consumer) will be updated as a separate unit (see DCR-2 task: hello surface side-layout switch).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. AnnotationToolbar extends VBoxContainer.\n2. Internal layout: header Label → FlowContainer of buttons → status Label.\n3. Header text and status-label color configurable via export properties (default header text \"Annotate\", status color matches PCB pink #f280e6).\n4. Buttons inside the FlowContainer use `size_flags_horizontal = Control.SIZE_SHRINK_BEGIN` so the toolbar can sit in a narrow sidebar.\n5. All 30 existing assertions in `test_annotation_authoring.gd` pass without modification.\n6. New assertions added to verify VBox structure (header present, FlowContainer present, status label present).\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling: Wire toolbar_icon on built-in kinds (filing now)\n- Done sibling: 019dc055e8da710588ea283351710c01 (Authoring UI framework — toolbar v1)\n- Trigger: DCR-2 smoke test R2; user feedback that toolbar should match PCBEditor pattern.","created_at":"2026-04-25T19:51:56","updated_at":"2026-04-27T17:58:51","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","refactor","toolbar","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"R2 commit cd5f78de. AnnotationToolbar now extends VBoxContainer with header Label + FlowContainer of icon-only toggle buttons + pink status Label. Public API unchanged — existing 30/30 test_annotation_authoring assertions pass file-unmodified. New test_annotation_toolbar_layout 33/0 covers structure, button construction, status transitions."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc63c2d757325be33dfe00ff55bae","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Harden AnnotationTextAuthorTool dialog cleanup against late signal callbacks","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced by the Opus reviewer of `019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f` (text author tool implementation, R2 of the annotation smoke):\n\n`AnnotationTextAuthorTool._close_dialog_silently()` calls `queue_free()` on the AcceptDialog but does NOT disconnect the lambdas wired to `confirmed` / `canceled` / `text_submitted`. Because `queue_free()` is deferred, those signals can still fire on the same frame if the user pressed OK exactly as the tool was deactivated. The lambdas close over `dialog` and `fired`, and call `finish.call(...)` → `on_done.call(...)`. The `_on_text_provider_done` callback is correctly guarded by `_state != TYPING`, so the outcome is signal-safe — but it still does work on a freed-pending node and reads `_active_dialog` after silent-cleanup nulled it.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nFuture per-kind author tools will copy this pattern. The cleanup smell will propagate. Better to harden now.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn `AnnotationTextAuthorTool._close_dialog_silently()`:\n1. Set `fired[0] = true` BEFORE queue_free, to short-circuit any in-flight signal lambda.\n2. Disconnect any still-connected handlers explicitly.\n3. Document the pattern in a code comment so subsequent kinds copying this know to do the same.\n\nOptionally: extract the dialog-management into a shared helper on `AnnotationAuthorTool` so kind authors don't have to re-derive the pattern.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `_close_dialog_silently` disconnects handlers and short-circuits the `fired` flag.\n2. Test added that simulates \"user clicks OK in the same frame as `on_deactivate()`\" — verifies no annotation_ready emission.\n3. Doc comment added explaining the cleanup contract for future tools.\n\n## Related\n\n- Surfaced from R2 reviewer notes on `019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f`.\n- Will benefit any future author tool that uses a modal dialog (potentially `AnnotationInkStrokeAuthorTool`'s settings dialog, etc.).","created_at":"2026-04-25T20:02:05","updated_at":"2026-04-25T20:02:05","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","author-tool","robustness","tech-debt"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc64bbc797666a5caf3946dbd4f7c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Annotation manipulation tools: Select / Translate / Rotate / Scale (PCBEditor pattern)","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced during R2 manual smoke (commit cd5f78de): the user can author arrows and text but has no way to select, move, rotate, or delete them after creation. The annotation authoring flow is one-shot — once the annotation is committed, it's frozen.\n\nPCBEditor already implements this pattern in its right-sidebar tools panel (see `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/PCBEditor.gd:218-267`):\n- \"Tools\" section with icon-only toggle buttons: Select, Translate, Rotate, Scale (and Delete, depending on PCB version)\n- Each is exclusive (one tool active at a time)\n- A status label below confirms which mode is active\n- When Select is active, clicking on a shape highlights it; when Translate/Rotate/Scale is active, the next click+drag operates on the previously-selected shape\n\nWe have a template to mirror.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. AnnotationHost API extension\n\nToday `AnnotationHost.add_annotation(dict) -> id` is the only mutator. Add:\n- `update_annotation(id: String, new_dict: Dictionary) -> bool` — replace stored annotation by id, emit `annotations_changed`. Return false if id not found.\n- `remove_annotation(id: String) -> bool` — drop by id, emit signal. Return false if id not found.\n- `set_selected_annotation_id(id: String) -> void` — track which annotation is selected (or \"\" for none), emit `selection_changed(id)`.\n- `get_selected_annotation_id() -> String` — read the selection.\n\n### 2. AnnotationToolbar Tools section\n\nAbove the existing \"Annotate\" FlowContainer, add a \"Tools\" section:\n- Header `Label` (centered, \"Tools\")\n- `FlowContainer` of icon-only toggle buttons: Select, Translate, Rotate, Scale.\n- Mutually exclusive across BOTH the Tools section and the Annotate section (selecting an annotation kind deactivates the active tool, and vice versa).\n\nReuse PCBEditor's icon UIDs where possible:\n- Select:    grep `select_btn` / `tool_buttons[ToolMode.SELECT]` for the UID\n- Translate: same for `TRANSLATE`\n- Rotate:    same for `ROTATE`\n- Scale:     same for `SCALE` (may not exist in PCB; check)\n\nIf PCB doesn't have a Scale icon, leave that button without an icon (text fallback) with a TODO referencing this task.\n\n### 3. Tool implementations\n\nDecision to make in implementation: do these tools subclass `AnnotationAuthorTool` (and emit a NEW signal `annotation_modified`), or do we introduce a sibling base class `AnnotationTransformTool`?\n\nProposed: extend `AnnotationAuthorTool` to be the unified `AnnotationTool` base — add `annotation_modified(id, new_dict)` signal, leave `annotation_ready` for create-only tools. Existing arrow/text tools don't emit modified; new manipulation tools don't emit ready. Cancelled stays shared. (Open to a sub-agent making a different call with justification.)\n\n#### Select tool\n- on_pointer_down: hit-test against host's annotations using `kind.hit_test(annotation, doc_point, threshold)`. Set selected_id to the topmost hit, or \"\" on miss.\n- draw_preview: draws the selection halo (an outlined rectangle at `kind.bounds(selected_annotation)`).\n- Delete key while a selection is active → `host.remove_annotation(selected_id)`.\n\n#### Translate tool\n- Requires a selection. If none, no-op on click.\n- on_pointer_down on selected → start drag. Track delta.\n- on_pointer_move while dragging → translate the annotation's primitives (offset all coord arrays by drag delta), emit `annotation_modified(id, new_dict)`.\n- on_pointer_up → finalize.\n\n#### Rotate tool\n- Drag rotates selected around its bounds() center. Atan2 from center to mouse → rotation matrix applied to primitive coords.\n- annotation_modified emission per drag step (or on release for performance).\n\n#### Scale tool\n- Drag scales selected from its bounds() center. Distance ratio = scale factor.\n- annotation_modified per drag step.\n\nFor rotate/scale: each kind's primitives need to support coord transformation. The `AnnotationKind` base class could expose a `transform_primitives(primitives, transform: Transform2D) -> Array` helper that all kinds use.\n\n### 4. Canvas wiring\n\n`Helloscene_AnnotationCanvas` (and the eventual core canvas widget) needs to:\n- Draw the selection halo on top of all annotations (after the kind.render loop, before active_tool.draw_preview).\n- Forward Delete key events to the active tool (Select tool consumes Delete).\n- Forward mouse events to the active tool consistently.\n\n### 5. Tests\n\n- `test_annotation_host_mutation.gd`: update/remove/select APIs, signal emission via Array-wrapper.\n- `test_annotation_select_tool.gd`: hit-test resolution, halo bounds, delete key.\n- `test_annotation_translate_tool.gd`: drag math, primitive translation.\n- `test_annotation_rotate_tool.gd`: rotation math correctness (90°, 180°, 360°).\n- `test_annotation_scale_tool.gd`: scale math correctness.\n- All existing 498/0 annotation suites continue to pass.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. AnnotationHost has update/remove/select API with signals.\n2. AnnotationToolbar shows a Tools section above the Annotate section.\n3. Selecting an annotation kind deactivates any active tool and vice versa (mutual exclusion across sections).\n4. Select tool: click-to-pick with halo; Delete key removes.\n5. Translate tool: drag selected to move; new position persists through save/reload (sidecar + project state).\n6. Rotate tool: drag rotates selected around its bounds center; persistence verified.\n7. Scale tool: drag scales selected around its bounds center; persistence verified.\n8. PCBEditor icon parity for Select/Translate/Rotate (Scale fallback to text if no icon).\n\n## Round positioning\n\nR2.5 — focused round on manipulation tools. R3 still planned for ink_stroke + remaining icon completion.\n\n## Related\n\n- Surfaced from R2 manual smoke testing.\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling: 019dc63c2d757325be33dfe00ff55bae (text tool dialog hardening — independent, can land any time)\n- Future R3 sibling: ink_stroke primitive + ink author tool (filed at 019dc0ebf9d5797a82af41edd4c8c180)\n- PCBEditor template: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/PCBEditor.gd:218-322`","created_at":"2026-04-25T20:19:05","updated_at":"2026-04-26T05:50:02","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","manipulation","pcb-editor-parity","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"Manipulation tools (Select/Translate/Rotate/Scale) implicitly smoke-verified through R5+R6 annotation work. User's latest annotation in /tmp/hello_smoke2.png has scale=2.196 (Scale tool fired), text placed at non-default position (282, 443) (Translate tool fired), and arrow + text creation + selection + deletion exercised throughout the session. AnnotationHost API extension (update/remove/select), Tools toolbar section with mutual exclusion across Tools/Annotate, and the 4 backend tool classes (AnnotationSelectTool/TranslateTool/RotateTool/ScaleTool) all landed and remained green: 51+30+121+11+25+48+62+37+44+146+63+87+87+30+37+54+24+22+180 = 1160/0 across 19 annotation suites at last commit (33d3d959). Toolbar-UX ergonomics improvements (PS-style unified transform-gizmo tool) are tracked as a follow-up work item, NOT a regression of this task: 019dc855b8327327b213170ea9a76b71 (Unified transform tool: collapse Select/Translate/Rotate/Scale into one PS-style tool)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc64be4967c3a9a45e5038273ee88","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Per-kind AnnotationAuthorTool: region, polyline, highlight, measure_*","description":"Sibling follow-up to closed task `019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f`. Arrow and text per-kind AnnotationAuthorTool implementations shipped in Phase 1B annotation smoke R1 and R2. The remaining five built-in 2D kinds still return null from `author_ui()`:\n\n- `2d_region` — drag-rectangle author flow\n- `2d_polyline` — N-click + double-click to finish\n- `2d_highlight` — drag-rectangle (alpha-styled)\n- `2d_measure_distance` — two-click, label shows numeric value\n- `2d_measure_angle` — three-click\n- `2d_measure_radius` — two-click\n\nEach follows the established pattern (`AnnotationArrowAuthorTool` is the reference). Likely splits into multiple small rounds rather than one big one — region + highlight + polyline are similar (drag-rectangle style); measure_* form their own family.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Each of the five kinds overrides `author_ui()` to return a fresh subclass instance.\n2. Tests for each tool follow the arrow-tool pattern (state machine, signal emission, cancel paths).\n3. AnnotationToolbar populates with all 8 buttons (one per kind).\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Predecessor: 019dc604535c7352ad726a2f31ecf83f (closed; arrow + text)\n- Sibling: 019dc632a7dd7fdfb4f22974651333a9 (closed; toolbar_icon wiring)","created_at":"2026-04-25T20:19:15","updated_at":"2026-04-29T23:04:36","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","authoring","follow-up","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"Superseded by v2 substrate DCR 019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1. v1 author-tool contract is being rewritten under v2 (typed-anchor envelope changes the tool's anchor-shape assumptions). See comment #256."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc6543a4972c292f64ba4fa0d9a7d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"LLM annotation ergonomics: enhance list + per-kind summary + anchored_to field","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced from R2 manual smoke discussion. The substrate's MCP surface (`list/add/update/delete/render_overlay`) gets the LLM the data, but in shapes that score poorly on a designed-rubric (Comprehension, Token efficiency, Action-readiness, Round-trip efficiency, Spatial/visual fidelity, Determinism, MCP composability, Implementation cost — see R2.5 conversation for the rubric and weighting).\n\n## Direction shift (originally rubric-graded option E unified-bundle, now PCBEditor-pattern)\n\nInspecting `MCPPCBTools.gd` revealed PCBEditor already ships a battle-tested LLM ergonomics pattern that we should mirror:\n\n1. PCB annotations carry an optional **semantic anchor**: `associated_component: \"R5\"` or `associated_net: \"VCC\"`. The annotation's coords stay raw; the anchor flows end-to-end through list/add/list.\n2. PCB has `minerva_pcb_describe_component(id)` returning rich context: position, nearby (`[\"R3 (5mm north)\"]`), connected (`[\"U1.pin3 via VCC\"]`), region. The `add_annotation` tool description literally tells the LLM *\"call describe_component first to resolve component names to coordinates\"*.\n3. PCB list output is a flat array filterable by `author`. No unified context bundle. The LLM composes list + describe_X separately.\n\nThis pattern composes cleanly across consumer plugins (CAD will have describe_part, etc.) without coupling the substrate to any one domain.\n\n## Revised scope (Phase 1 — landed in commits cd5f78de + WIPs through e241b4dd)\n\n### 1. Schema: `anchored_to: String` field (substrate-level)\nOptional top-level field. Opaque to substrate. Forward-compat default \"\".\n\n### 2. Per-kind `summary(annotation: Dictionary) -> String`\nVirtual on `AnnotationKind`. Default uses display_name + primitive count + anchored_to suffix. Arrow/Text override.\n\n### 3. Enhance `minerva_annotations_list`\n- `summary`, `bounds`, `anchored_to` fields per annotation\n- Optional `author` filter\n\n### 4. DROP: `minerva_annotations_context` unified bundle\n### 5. DROP: MCP-exposed selection awareness\n\n## Phase 2 — bugs surfaced by manual smoke (added 2026-04-25)\n\nThe smoke test exposed two gaps that block exit-criterion-3 (\"LLM can see annotations meaningfully\"):\n\n**Gap A: anchored_to is dead text.** Schema accepts it, list returns it, but no code path populates it — every annotation has `anchored_to: \"\"`. The PCBEditor pattern requires hosts to actually fill the field; the substrate gives them no path or convention to do so.\n\n**Gap B: list and render_overlay can't see live (unsaved) panel state.** `minerva_annotations_list` and `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` both required `document_path` (sidecar on disk). Users won't think \"save before asking Claude what I drew.\" Already partially fixed in commit e241b4dd: `list` now accepts `editor_name` for live in-memory query via a process-global `AnnotationHostRegistry`. `render_overlay` still requires document_path AND has no path to render the host's underlying UI beneath the annotations.\n\n### Phase 2 fixes\n\n**A1. Host-managed `anchored_to`** (closes Gap A; matches PCB pattern):\n- New `AnnotationKind.primary_anchor_point(annotation) -> Vector2` virtual — kind-aware \"the canonical point to describe\" (arrow → `to`, text → `at`, polyline/region → bounds-center)\n- New `AnnotationHost.describe_point(doc_pos: Vector2) -> String` virtual — symbolic id of whatever the host has at doc_pos. Default returns \"\". Examples: Hello returns `\"ui:Label\"` / `\"label.word:this\"`; PCB will return `\"component:R12\"` / `\"net:GND\"` after migration.\n- `AnnotationHost.add_annotation` and `update_annotation` populate `anchored_to` automatically by calling `describe_point(kind.primary_anchor_point(ann))` before storing. Author tools never touch the field.\n- New `AnnotationHost.refresh_all_anchors()` helper — walks `_annotations`, re-runs the same logic. HelloPanel calls it from `_on_panel_save_request` so unobserved UI changes (label rename) get caught at save time.\n- Result: `anchored_to` is always fresh as of the last event the host saw OR the last save. The `list` tool stays single-mode (no resolve_targets flag); the host owns freshness.\n\n**A2. Live-editor `render_overlay`** (closes Gap B):\n- New `AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image(viewport_rect: Rect2) -> Image` virtual — paints the host's content (NOT annotations) to an Image. Default returns null (transparent fallback). Hello implements via SubViewport texture grab.\n- Extend `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` to accept `editor_name` (parallel to `list`); when given, looks up host via `AnnotationHostRegistry`, calls `render_content_to_image`, composites annotations on top.\n\n**Tool surface stays at 2 read-side tools**: `minerva_annotations_list` (text, cheap) + `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` (image, expensive). Image stays segregated by output type and cost class, not folded into list.\n\n## Phase 2 acceptance criteria\n\n7. `AnnotationKind.primary_anchor_point` virtual with kind-specific overrides for arrow / text / polyline / region.\n8. `AnnotationHost.describe_point` virtual; Hello implementation returns `\"ui:<NodeName>\"` for control hits and `\"label.word:<word>\"` for word-level hits over `_label`'s text content.\n9. `AnnotationHost.add_annotation` and `update_annotation` populate `anchored_to` via describe_point on every event. `refresh_all_anchors()` re-runs across the list; HelloPanel calls it from `_on_panel_save_request`.\n10. `AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image` virtual; Hello implementation grabs the panel's UI region as Image.\n11. `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` accepts `editor_name` and uses it for live composite.\n12. Tests: anchored_to populated on add (host stamp), updated on translate/rotate/scale (manipulation routes through update_annotation), refreshed across all annotations on save_request. render_overlay with editor_name returns non-empty PNG and includes host-rendered UI when include_document=true.\n\n## Reference\n\n- PCB pattern: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPCBTools.gd:545-650`\n- PCB annotation Dict shape: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/PCBAnnotation.gd:223`\n- PCB describe_component: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/PCBSpatialIndex.gd:214`\n- Phase 1 commit: cd5f78de (anchored_to schema + summary + list enrichment)\n- Phase 2 partial: e241b4dd (live editor_name path — closes part of Gap B)\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling (same round): 019dc64bbc797666a5caf3946dbd4f7c (manipulation tools)\n- Smoke pass exit criterion: \"LLM can see annotation in a meaningful way with good LLM Ergonomics\"\n- Future PCB consumer: `019dc1409ec67224a6b0baa0adce6023` (PCBAnnotation → platform kinds migration). When that lands, PCB's `associated_component` / `associated_net` flow into substrate's `anchored_to` via discriminator strings (`comp:R5`, `net:VCC`), and PCB's `describe_point` wraps its existing `describe_component_context`.","created_at":"2026-04-25T20:28:21","updated_at":"2026-04-26T04:53:44","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","ergonomics","llm","mcp"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"R5+R6 smoke cleared. /tmp/hello_smoke2.png shows arrow geometry as line+arrowhead (no longer placeholder fill), and anchored_to correctly resolves to \"label.word:cat.\" matching exactly the word the user pointed at. Render path returns {output_path, width, height, annotations_drawn} with no inline base64. Final commits: 4b74971c (R5 file-path output) and 33d3d959 (R6 kind dispatch + word resolver real font metrics). Test baseline: 1160/0 across 19 annotation suites. Documented limitations (non-blocking for substrate exit criteria): text annotations render as a colored bar in render_overlay (no Image-side font rasterizer); text rotation isn't honored in render_overlay (axis-aligned only). Live editor on-screen rendering is unaffected by either limitation."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc65b39fb7d2ca049e951b777ee6b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PCBEditor text-box visual anchors all rendered at top-left of bounds","description":"## Symptom\n\nWhen a text annotation is drawn or selected in PCBEditor, the four visual anchor handles that should appear at the corners of the text's bounding rectangle all render at the top-left of the bounds. The anchors are stacked at one position instead of distributed across the four corners.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Open PCBEditor on any PCB\n2. Activate the annotation Text tool\n3. Place a text annotation\n4. Select it\n5. Expected: four small handle markers at top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right of the text's bounding box\n6. Observed: all four handles overlap at top-left\n\n## Hypothesis\n\nThe anchor-rendering code in PCBCanvas (or wherever text-bounds anchors are drawn) is missing the bounds-corner offsets — all four handles are using the same anchor point (probably the text's `position` Vector2, which is the top-left of where text starts rendering).\n\n## When to fix\n\nDefer the dedicated PCBEditor fix; consolidate it during the PCB→substrate annotation migration (`019dc1409ec67224a6b0baa0adce6023`). The substrate's manipulation-tool gizmos (R2.5 task `019dc64bbc797666a5caf3946dbd4f7c`) should NOT inherit this bug — gizmos in the substrate must compute corner positions from `kind.bounds(annotation)` and place handles at the four actual corners.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. PCBEditor's text-annotation anchors render at the four corners of the text's bounding box.\n2. Substrate manipulation-tool gizmos (when implemented in R2.5) do NOT exhibit this bug — verified by manual smoke on the hello plugin's text annotations.\n3. After PCB → substrate migration, the legacy buggy anchor code is removed (no parallel implementations).\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent of fix: 019dc1409ec67224a6b0baa0adce6023 (PCB annotation migration to substrate kinds)\n- Related: 019dc64bbc797666a5caf3946dbd4f7c (R2.5 manipulation tools — must avoid replicating this bug)","created_at":"2026-04-25T20:36:00","updated_at":"2026-04-25T20:36:00","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","pcb-editor","rendering","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc855b8327327b213170ea9a76b71","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Unified transform tool: collapse Select/Translate/Rotate/Scale into one PS-style tool","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced from R2.5 smoke (commit 33d3d959). The four manipulation tools (Select / Translate / Rotate / Scale) work correctly but their toolbar UX is weaker than industry-standard. Photoshop, Illustrator, Figma, Inkscape, and most direct-manipulation editors collapse these into a SINGLE selection tool that draws a unified bounding-box gizmo on the selected object, and routes the manipulation type by which zone of the gizmo the user grabs:\n\n- Drag inside the bounding box → translate\n- Drag corner handle → uniform scale (axis-locked with shift)\n- Drag edge handle → axis-aligned scale\n- Drag in the small \"rotate ring\" just outside each corner → rotate\n- Click outside the gizmo → deselect\n\nThe existing four toolbar buttons + four modes force the user to mode-switch between operations they would naturally execute in sequence on the same selection. Every other 2D-graphics tool the user touches works the way described above; ours doesn't, and that's the friction.\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nAdd a thin `AnnotationTransformTool` that:\n\n1. On selection, draws a unified bounding-box gizmo at `kind.bounds(annotation)` with visible handles (corner squares, edge squares, rotate-ring zones just outside corners).\n2. On `mouse_down`, hit-tests which zone was grabbed:\n   - Inside box → forward drag to existing `AnnotationTranslateTool` logic\n   - Corner handle → existing `AnnotationScaleTool` (uniform mode)\n   - Edge handle → existing `AnnotationScaleTool` (axis-locked mode)\n   - Rotate ring → existing `AnnotationRotateTool`\n   - Outside → deselect\n3. Cursor changes per zone (translate cross / scale arrows / rotate curved arrow) for affordance.\n\nThe existing four tool classes stay as **manipulation backends** — they already encode the math; the new tool is a router. Toolbar collapses from 4 buttons + Annotate buttons to 1 \"Select\" button + Annotate buttons. The four old buttons are removed (or hidden behind a debug flag if backwards-compat matters for tests).\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. New `AnnotationTransformTool`\nFile: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/tools/AnnotationTransformTool.gd`\n- Holds reference to one selected annotation id.\n- `_draw_gizmo(ctx)` — render bounding box + handles + rotate-ring zones.\n- `_hit_test_gizmo(local_pos) -> ZoneEnum` — return INSIDE / CORNER_TL/TR/BL/BR / EDGE_T/B/L/R / ROTATE_TL/TR/BL/BR / OUTSIDE.\n- `mouse_down` → instantiate the right backend tool, forward `mouse_move` / `mouse_up` to it.\n- `Delete` key → `host.remove_annotation(selected_id)`.\n\n### 2. AnnotationToolbar collapse\n- Remove (or hide) the four Tools-section buttons.\n- Keep one button: `Select` (icon: PCB select arrow).\n- The Annotate section stays unchanged.\n- Mutual exclusion still applies: pressing an Annotate button deselects.\n\n### 3. Cursor affordance\n- Each zone sets `Input.set_default_cursor_shape(Input.CursorShape.X)` on hover.\n- Move = `CURSOR_MOVE`; corner scale = `CURSOR_FDIAGSIZE`/`CURSOR_BDIAGSIZE`; edge scale = `CURSOR_VSIZE`/`CURSOR_HSIZE`; rotate = no built-in; use `CURSOR_CROSS` with custom-icon override later.\n\n### 4. Tests\n- New `test_annotation_transform_tool.gd`: hit-zone discrimination (point at corner → CORNER_TL; point in middle → INSIDE; etc.).\n- Existing `test_annotation_select/translate/rotate/scale_tool.gd` suites stay green — the backend tool classes don't change.\n- New integration test: drag inside gizmo → annotation translates. Drag corner → annotation scales.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. One toolbar button replaces the four; selection halo + gizmo handles render on click.\n2. Drag-zone routing works for translate / corner-scale / edge-scale / rotate.\n3. Cursor changes per zone on hover.\n4. Delete key removes selected annotation.\n5. Existing 4 backend tool suites remain green.\n6. New transform-tool suite has at least 25 tests covering hit-zone math, drag forwarding, and integration with each backend.\n7. Manual smoke (3b stop): user can select an annotation, drag corner to scale, drag inside to translate, drag rotate-ring to rotate, all without changing tool buttons.\n\n## Tradeoff (recorded)\n\nHit-zone discrimination — especially the rotate ring just outside corners — is the polish-heavy part. Plan to start with generous zone-sizes (16-20 px) and tune from manual smoke. Don't over-engineer cursor icons in round 1; revisit after the core works.\n\n## Round positioning\n\nFiled as R2.6 (or whatever round number lands next). Independent of the deferred R3 (ink_stroke) and the PCB migration tasks.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: 019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd (Plan: Annotation Subsystem)\n- Sibling now done: 019dc64bbc797666a5caf3946dbd4f7c (manipulation tools — provides backend math).\n- Sibling now done: 019dc6543a4972c292f64ba4fa0d9a7d (LLM ergonomics — anchored_to + render_overlay).\n- Future R3 ink: 019dc0ebf9d5797a82af41edd4c8c180.\n- Reference: industry-standard transform-gizmo pattern (PS, AI, Figma, Inkscape).","created_at":"2026-04-26T05:49:14","updated_at":"2026-04-26T17:47:28","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","ergonomics","manipulation","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"Shipped in commit dc39436e on user/imran/experiments/swarm. AnnotationTransformTool routes drag operations by zone (INSIDE→translate, CORNER→uniform-scale, EDGE→axis-locked scale, ROTATE annulus→rotate, OUTSIDE→deselect/re-select). Toolbar collapsed from 4 Tools buttons to 1 Select button. Backend tool classes (AnnotationSelectTool/TranslateTool/RotateTool/ScaleTool) retained as math references with their existing tests intact. Layer-1: 1183/0 across 20 annotation suites at dc39436e (+49 transform_tool, -20 toolbar_layout collapse, all other suites at R6 baseline). Reviewer verdict: approve_with_notes; both polish items (test name rename, rotate-disc indicator docstring) applied before commit. Manual smoke verified by user — all zones (translate / corner-scale / edge-scale / rotate annulus / select / deselect / Delete) work as expected, no rotate-ring tolerance tuning needed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc91d81875c7918e685653fa2248","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Capture stderr tail in worker `crashed` errors (design §7)","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. Per Go-python-bridge-design.md §7, when a worker crashes mid-request the response should be `{kind: \"crashed\", message: \"...\", details: {stderr_tail}}` carrying the last ~4 KB of stderr so post-mortems are possible. Currently `internal/bridge/worker.go`'s `pumpStderr` consumes lines and forwards them to the parent's stderr but does NOT keep a ring buffer; the resulting `crashed` error message is generic (\"worker stdout closed unexpectedly\").\n\n**DoD:**\n1. `worker.go` keeps a fixed-size (4 KB) ring buffer of recent stderr lines.\n2. When a `crashed` error is returned, the buffer's current contents land in `WorkerError.Details[\"stderr_tail\"]`.\n3. Test: spawn a worker that writes to stderr then exits non-zero; assert the next `Call`'s error carries the stderr text.\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit B (`019dc0594c92`). Reviewer verdict was APPROVE_WITH_NOTES; this was one of the deferred follow-ups.","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:23","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:23","priority":3,"tags":["cad","go-bridge","round-3-hardening"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc92013b7bbe8be52f50796aee91","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Windows conditional compilation for worker process group (design §10)","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. `internal/bridge/worker.go` uses `syscall.SysProcAttr{Setpgid: true}` unconditionally. This will fail to compile on Windows because `Setpgid` is a Unix-only field on `SysProcAttr`. Per Go-python-bridge-design.md §10, Windows needs `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | CREATE_NO_WINDOW` instead.\n\n**DoD:**\n1. Split process-spawn attributes into `worker_unix.go` (with `//go:build !windows`) and `worker_windows.go` (with `//go:build windows`) — or use the analogous file-suffix pattern.\n2. Unix path uses `Setpgid: true`; kill via `kill(-pgid, SIGTERM)`.\n3. Windows path uses `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | CREATE_NO_WINDOW`; kill via `TerminateProcess`.\n4. CI builds on all three platforms (or at minimum `GOOS=windows go build` succeeds locally).\n5. Don't ship a working Windows runtime/PBS binary in this task — that's a separate runtime-build grandchild — just the spawn-side compile fix.\n\n**Priority 2** (higher than other hardening items) because it's a build-blocker for Windows users, and the runtime-build pipeline will need to compile on Windows eventually.\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit B (`019dc0594c92`).","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:33","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:33","priority":2,"tags":["cad","go-bridge","round-3-hardening","windows"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc9226517dddaaebe6799bd667dc","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Fix concurrency races in `bridge/worker.go`","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. Several narrow but real concurrency issues in `internal/bridge/worker.go`:\n\n1. **Mutex dropped during `worker.ready` wait, re-acquired in select arms.** A `worker.ready` notification arriving simultaneously with the 15s timeout could record a spurious crash and kill a healthy worker. Race window narrow but real.\n\n2. **`w.doneC` field read without holding mu** (around line 413: `case <-w.doneC:`). If a restart replaces the channel between the field read and the select, that's a data race. Fix: capture `doneC := w.doneC` while holding mu, then select on the captured value (analogous to how `stdin` is captured).\n\n3. **Close-once on readyC pattern is racy under multiple `worker.ready` notifications.** The `select { case <-rc: default: close(rc) }` idiom doesn't handle two concurrent closers. Use `sync.Once` instead.\n\n4. **Old reader's `defer close(w.doneC)` could close the new doneC after a restart.** When `Start()` is called twice in succession, the first reader goroutine's deferred close fires against the second `doneC`. Fix: capture `doneC` per-reader.\n\n5. **EOF between calls doesn't `recordCrash`.** When the worker exits silently between `Call`s, the reader detects EOF but doesn't notify the circuit breaker. Fix: have the reader call `recordCrash` on EOF.\n\n**DoD:**\n- All five issues above addressed with focused commits / patches.\n- Each fix gets a unit test where feasible (concurrent `Start` race, doneC capture, readyC double-close, EOF mid-idle crash detection).\n- `go test -race ./...` passes.\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit B (`019dc0594c92`).","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:43","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:43","priority":3,"tags":["cad","concurrency","go-bridge","round-3-hardening"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc9251447bfaad3544f6f13e1396","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Lazy-load build123d in `methods.py` (preserve \"validate avoids OCCT\" property)","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. `worker/mcad_worker/methods.py` line ~34 imports `_BUILD123D_VERSION` (and similar OCP version probes) at module top, which transitively imports build123d eagerly the moment `methods` is imported. This partially undermines design §8.3's intent that `validate` skip OCCT entirely — even though `_validate` itself doesn't call build123d, every worker process still loads it on first import.\n\n**Note:** the worker's *cold-start* in `dispatcher.py` deliberately does the OCCT smoke test (1mm cube tessellation) to fail-fast on broken OCCT installs. That stays. This task is only about the eager import in `methods.py`'s module-level version probes — those should be deferred until first use (e.g., wrapped in a `_get_build123d_version()` function that caches on first call).\n\n**DoD:**\n1. Module-load-time import of build123d in `methods.py` is removed; replaced by lazy probes inside the methods that need them.\n2. `python -c \"import mcad_worker.methods; import sys; assert 'build123d' not in sys.modules\"` passes (after the test environment is cleaned of any prior import).\n3. Existing 226 tests still pass.\n4. Add a test asserting `_validate` does not import build123d on its happy path. Approach: monkeypatch `sys.modules` to remove build123d before calling `_validate`, then assert it's still absent after.\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit A (`019dc05964a5`).","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:54","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:33:54","priority":3,"tags":["cad","python-worker","round-3-hardening"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc928a357e35a35bd3ec508b63ae","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add translator-stage validation feedback to `mcad_validate`","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. The Round 2 `_validate` implementation runs lex + parse only because `mcad/translator.py` imports build123d at module level — invoking it would defeat the \"skip tessellation entirely\" design intent.\n\nBut translator-stage errors (undefined identifiers, type mismatches in CSG ops, unknown commands, unresolved references) are a meaningful class of validation feedback that the LLM inner loop would benefit from. Without them, the LLM only sees parse errors at validate time and discovers translator errors only on the slower `mcad_render` / `mcad_evaluate` path.\n\n**Two paths to consider, decide as part of this task:**\n\n**Option A: Refactor translator to lazy-import build123d.** Move the `import build123d` into the construction methods that actually create shapes. Then validate can run translator without OCCT.\n- Pro: clean separation; translator-stage validation becomes free.\n- Con: touches the inherited mcad/ package (which we ported byte-identical). Diverges from the experiment.\n\n**Option B: Add a separate static-check pass.** Write a new `mcad_static_check.py` that walks the AST without invoking translator — checks identifiers, command names, basic type compatibility. Validate calls this after parser succeeds.\n- Pro: doesn't touch the inherited package.\n- Con: duplicates type-check logic that already lives in translator.\n\n**Option C: No-op build123d shim.** Inject a dummy build123d module into `sys.modules` before importing translator, run translator's traversal, catch any AttributeError on shape ops as \"we got past the symbol-resolution phase, no static error.\"\n- Pro: minimal new code.\n- Con: fragile; tied to translator's internals.\n\n**DoD:**\n1. Pick A/B/C with rationale documented.\n2. Implement; the result-set extends the existing `validate` errors list (no shape change to the response).\n3. Tests: known-good source returns clean; source with a typo'd command name returns a translator-stage error in the list with line/col.\n4. `validate` performance still measurable in milliseconds (no full tessellation slipped in).\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit A (`019dc05964a5`).","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:34:08","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:34:08","priority":3,"tags":["cad","llm-ergonomics","python-worker","round-3-hardening"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcc92baca7185bad1aa0e1740bb9d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Close test coverage gaps in `bridge/worker_test.go`","description":"Surfaced from Round 2 review. `internal/bridge/worker_test.go` lands with 7 tests but several important paths are uncovered:\n\n1. **Mid-request crash propagation.** No test where the worker crashes mid-`Call` and the in-flight request returns `{kind: \"crashed\"}` with the stderr tail (after the §7 fix lands). Spawn an echo-worker variant that exits non-zero after receiving a request but before responding; assert the right error.\n\n2. **Shutdown SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation.** Current `TestShutdown` only exercises the clean-exit path. Need a test where the worker hangs (ignores `shutdown`) → SIGTERM after 2s → SIGKILL after another 3s. Verify both phases fire and the process is reaped.\n\n3. **`TestWorkerContextCancel` doesn't actually exercise cancellation.** Current test uses `/nonexistent/python3` with an already-cancelled context; the test passes because of spawn failure, not because of context cancellation. Replace with: spawn a real worker, start a long-running `Call`, cancel the context, assert the call returns ctx-cancelled and the worker is killed cleanly.\n\n4. **Lazy-spawn discipline at the MCP layer.** No test that asserts `initialize` and `tools/list` do NOT spawn Python; only `tools/call` does. Add an integration-style test in `main_test.go` (or similar) that drives the binary's outer protocol and asserts the bridge worker is nil after `tools/list`.\n\n5. **Reader goroutine handling notifications correctly.** Currently exercised only indirectly via `worker.ready`. Add a test that injects `progress` and `log` notifications mid-call and asserts they don't break correlation.\n\n**DoD:**\n1. Five tests above land in `worker_test.go` (or split across files as appropriate).\n2. `go test -race ./internal/bridge/...` passes.\n3. Tests skip cleanly when `python3` is missing from PATH (preserve the dev-mode story).\n\n**Origin:** Round 2 reviewer (Opus) on Unit B (`019dc0594c92`).","created_at":"2026-04-27T01:34:21","updated_at":"2026-04-27T01:34:21","priority":3,"tags":["cad","go-bridge","round-3-hardening","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dccbfea8873efb168394e52ab84d8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CADPanel: 4-view layout doesn't fit available parent space","description":"Surfaced 2026-04-26 during Round 3 stop-3b verification. The CAD plugin loads cleanly in Minerva and the panel opens, but the 4-view layout (`GridContainer` of `SubViewportContainer`s) does not resize to fit the available panel space. Likely fixed-size SubViewportContainers + a non-stretching GridContainer don't propagate parent size into children.\n\n**Repro:**\n1. Install plugin via Tools → Plugin → Install Plugin pointing at `~/github/plugins/cad/manifest.json`.\n2. Open Panel.\n3. Observe the 4-view grid extends past the panel rect / doesn't shrink with the editor pane.\n\n**Fix sketch (DoD):**\n1. `ui/CADPanel.tscn`: configure `HSplitContainer` and inner `VBoxContainer`/`GridContainer`/`SubViewportContainer` to expand and fill. The proper Godot 4 incantations:\n   - `size_flags_horizontal = SIZE_EXPAND_FILL` (3) on every container parent.\n   - `size_flags_vertical = SIZE_EXPAND_FILL` (3) on every container parent.\n   - `SubViewportContainer.stretch = true` so the SubViewport's render fills the container rect.\n   - `SubViewport.size_2d_override_stretch = true` if 2D stretch is needed.\n2. Ensure each `SubViewport.size` updates on container resize. May need `SubViewportContainer.size_changed` signal hookup.\n3. Manual verify: drag the editor pane border, all four views resize proportionally.\n4. Test corner cases: very narrow pane (each view shouldn't disappear), very tall pane, square aspect.\n\n**Reference:** see how Minerva's existing PCBEditor (`src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/`) handles its canvas + sidebar layout — likely the same pattern.\n\n**Origin:** observed by Imran in Round 3 stop-3b. Blocked nothing — Round 3 is functionally complete; this is cosmetic. Round 4 candidate.","created_at":"2026-04-27T02:23:42","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:21:58","priority":3,"tags":["cad","layout","round-4-candidate","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"4-view layout fits parent space; width-class responsive layout handles wide+narrow. Subsumed by 019dd020df16."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dcfae99d37847b63946c99c30b498","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Off-tree plugins: don't use class_name for cross-script types","created_at":"2026-04-27T16:03:39","updated_at":"2026-04-27T16:03:39","tags":["godot","gotcha","off-tree","plugin"],"value":"Godot's parser cache only sees scripts under res://, so off-tree plugin scripts (e.g. ~/github/plugins/cad/) CANNOT statically resolve another plugin script's class_name. Symptom: 'Could not find type X'. Fix: use preload() + base-class typing.\n\nWRONG:\n  var _foo: Cad_AnnotationHost = null\n  _foo = Cad_AnnotationHost.new()\n\nRIGHT:\n  const _FooScript: Script = preload(\\\"CadAnnotationHost.gd\\\")\n  var _foo: AnnotationHost = null   # base class IS res://-registered\n  _foo = _FooScript.new()\n\nclass_name on off-tree scripts is harmless, just unresolvable as a type. Sweep ': <CustomName> =' patterns when adding plugin types. Fixed instances: Cad_AnnotationHost, OrbitCamera.","component":"godot-plugins","key":"off_tree_class_name","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd0067d77781ebc1816975244d8c8","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"PCBEditor inspired the cross-editor annotation substrate; the substrate is built for plugin extension, not replacement","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:39:39","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:27","tags":["annotation","discovery","pcb","platform","plugin"],"value":"PCBEditor predates the cross-editor annotation substrate (src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationToolbar.gd, AnnotationHost, AnnotationRegistry, AnnotationKind, AnnotationAuthorTool) and uses its own older PCBAnnotation.gd system. PCBEditor was the INSPIRATION for the substrate — not a consumer of it.\n\nKey framing correction: PCBEditor's specialized annotation kinds (PCB-specific arrows, regions, polylines, etc.) are richer than the substrate's general primitives. The substrate is designed for *plugin extension* via AnnotationKind registry (task 019dc055a9e575 \"AnnotationKind registry contract: render/hit_test/bounds/author_ui\"). Plugins register their own annotation kinds — they don't replace or migrate away from their domain-specific work.\n\nWhen PCBEditor migrates to a plugin (DCR 019dc14029197 + plan), its specialized kinds should be expressed as substrate-registered AnnotationKinds, not kept as a parallel system. Same pattern for any plugin: CAD will register 3D-specific kinds (e.g. measure-hole-to-edge distance), Hello validates the smoke path, PCB validates the rich-extension path.\n\nDon't suggest \"migrate PCBEditor to AnnotationToolbar\" — that's the wrong frame. The right frame: \"PCBEditor extends the substrate with its specialized kinds during the plugin migration.\"","component":"minerva-platform","key":"pcb_editor_uses_separate_annotation_system","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd006a630713ea63d6ac638dd4903","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"PLUGIN_SCENE chrome row is explicitly hidden in Editor.gd, not absent","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:39:49","updated_at":"2026-04-27T17:45:22","tags":["chrome","discovery","editor","platform","plugin"],"value":"CORRECTION (2026-04-27): earlier hint claimed plugin chrome was inherited and only the actions fell through. That's wrong.\n\nEditor.gd:517 explicitly calls `$VBoxContainer/ButtonsHBoxContainer.hide()` inside the `Type.PLUGIN_SCENE` branch of `_ready()`. The chrome row IS instantiated by Editor.tscn but the entire HBox is hidden, so:\n\n- Right-3 buttons (Save All, Save, Turn-into-Note) are NOT visible for plugin tabs\n- Inject toggle (CheckButton) is also hidden (its parent is the hidden HBox)\n- The `disabled = false` flag at Editor.gd:446 for PLUGIN_SCENE is moot because the button parent is hidden\n\nLikely a deliberate \"give plugins the full middle pane\" choice that has the side effect of denying plugins ALL standard chrome affordances. Fix shape: stop blanket-hiding the HBox; instead, selectively show the standard right-3 + inject toggle for PLUGIN_SCENE, and route their actions through plugin IPC channels.","component":"minerva-platform","key":"plugin_chrome_already_wrapped_actions_fallthrough","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd016f6e07140b1c191f1ac69d595","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Editor-tab chrome contract for PLUGIN_SCENE","description":"Today, Editor.gd:517 blanket-hides $VBoxContainer/ButtonsHBoxContainer for Type.PLUGIN_SCENE, denying plugin tabs all standard chrome (right-3 actions: Save All / Save / Turn-into-Note, plus the chat-injection toggle). Replace the blanket hide with selective visibility so PLUGIN_SCENE tabs show the same right-edge action group + inject toggle as built-in editors, and route their handlers through the plugin broker instead of into the existing match statements that fall into error cases (e.g. Editor.gd:1109).\n\nDoD:\n- Right-3 buttons + inject toggle are visible on PLUGIN_SCENE tabs.\n- SaveButton dispatches via PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_save(panel_root) (or new equivalent).\n- CreateNoteButton dispatches a new broker action (e.g. plugin.create_note) the panel can implement; default fallback is screenshot-to-image-note when the panel doesn't implement it.\n- SaveOpenEditorTabsButton fan-out includes plugin tabs (verify and keep — discovery suggests it already does).\n- Inject toggle state changes are forwarded to the panel via a broker signal.\n- Panel-side opt-out: a manifest flag (e.g. chrome.suppress: [\"create_note\"]) lets a plugin explicitly hide a control.\n- Hello plugin updated to demonstrate at least Save and Create-Note participation.\n\nSibling of 019dc055700777d685cbcb2a71c3fb6c (file-extension/save/load/unsaved-tracking task). Kept separate because that task is mostly file-management plumbing while this one is the UX surface contract.","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:57:39","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:45:35","tags":["chrome","editor","platform-addition","plugin-system"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Cycle 1 R2 complete. Editor.gd:517 blanket-hide replaced with selective chrome visibility for PLUGIN_SCENE tabs honoring chrome.suppress manifest opt-out + save_mode \"none\" auto-hide. Save dispatches via existing PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_save; new invoke_create_note (default screenshot fallback to image-note) and invoke_inject_toggle (host→panel forward). Manifest schema validates chrome.suppress array with panel_chrome_* parse errors mirroring panel_save_mode_invalid shape. Errors route through PluginNotifyRouter (R1-C). Hello plugin demonstrates contract; rich create-note hook deliberately deferred to task 019dd0c895ec (extensible Note types) so R2 ships only the screenshot path. Commits: 845beff7 (initial) + d4b544cb (rollback hello rich hook + smoke dogfoods ResponsiveContainer) + e30733b7 (toggle button clip_text fix). User confirmed via hello plugin (image-note works) + compact toolbar smoke (responsive width-class drives toolbar mode, button text visible)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd0171bdd7940b6d135baaccc381d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin host toast/notification API","description":"Plugins currently have no way to surface user-facing messages — errors, warnings, or info — back to Minerva. They can return errors in MCP tool responses (logged-only) or emit progress notifications (routed to their own panel scene), but neither reaches Minerva's notification toast system. Extend the PluginScenePanelBroker (or add a sibling broker for non-panel plugin events) with a notify(level, message, [details]) channel that routes to SingletonObject's existing toast/error display.\n\nDoD:\n- Plugin Python/Go worker can emit {method:\"host.notify\", params:{level, message, details?}} notifications over stdio.\n- Levels: info, warning, error minimum.\n- Routes through SingletonObject toast pipe; errors also logged to Activity: MCP tab.\n- Available to scene-panel plugins (via broker) AND to plain MCP-tool plugins (no panel needed).\n- Test: mock plugin emits an error notification; toast appears with the message; entry visible in Activity log.\n\nDriver: CAD plugin (DCR 019dc054a4) needs to surface validation/translation errors as toasts when users invoke MCP tools without the panel focused. Generalizes to any plugin.","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:57:48","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:04:39","tags":["notifications","platform-addition","plugin-system"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Cycle 1 R1-C complete. host.notify channel routes plugin → Minerva toast + Activity:MCP log. Verified at commit 521c0398 (channel) + c6f88cdf (Activity:MCP focus-restore fix). Plus follow-on fixes: 31c032af (save_mode \"none\" schema for ephemeral panels). User confirmed via hello plugin startup info toast."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd0173b1b79f08ce6f6e319e0eacf","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Active-tab visual focus indicator: source + plugin participation","description":"Built-in editors render a blue border around the active editing area. The 2026-04-27 platform discovery sweep couldn't find the source in Editor.gd / EditorPane.gd / Editor.tscn — likely a Godot TabContainer theme resource or a global theme .tres applied via inheritance. First half of this task is locating where it lives. Second half is ensuring PLUGIN_SCENE tabs participate in the same focus styling as built-in editors.\n\nDoD:\n- Document where the active-tab border style is defined (file:line citation).\n- Confirm whether plugin tabs already inherit it (if yes, close as no-op with the documentation as the deliverable).\n- If they don't inherit, fix so they do — either by adjusting the theme resource or by ensuring PLUGIN_SCENE wraps content the same way TEXT does.\n- Verify visually: load Hello plugin and CAD plugin; confirm border appears on active tab.\n\nSmall task; may collapse to no-op once the source is found.","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:57:56","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:04:43","tags":["chrome","discovery","editor","plugin-system"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Cycle 1 R1-D complete (commit 23e1af2a). Discovery: blue active-tab indicator lives in src/assets/themes/blue_dark_mode.theme (TabContainer/tab_selected, color #2A58D4, border 2/4/2/0). PLUGIN_SCENE tabs already inherit it via the engine's TabContainer paint path — no fix needed. Documented in plugin_system_guide.md."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd017696a7f33b94fe89826008b0d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"ResponsiveContainer + width_class platform primitive","description":"Plugin panels (and built-in editors, eventually) need to react to available width. Today CAD's panel doesn't shrink gracefully when peer panes (Chats/Notes) compete for space — annotations clip, quadrants go uneven. Build a reusable ResponsiveContainer Control under addons/minerva_platform/ (exact path TBD during impl) that exposes a width_class property + width_class_changed(class) signal, computed from its rendered width via NOTIFICATION_RESIZED. Bootstrap-style classes: xs (<480px), sm (<768), md (<1024), lg (<1440), xl (≥1440) — exact thresholds finalized during implementation.\n\nDoD:\n- New Control class with width_class: StringName property and width_class_changed signal.\n- Off-tree-friendly consumption: usable via preload() + base-class typing (no class_name-only dependency in public surface), per the off-tree gotcha already documented (project_off_tree_plugin_class_names).\n- Children can read the class statically (property) or react dynamically (signal).\n- Hello plugin gains a smoke-test panel that switches between two layouts based on width class.\n- Documented in plugin_system_guide.md (or substrate equivalent) with a usage example.\n- CAD adoption is OUT OF SCOPE — that lives in the CAD plan as a separate adoption task.\n\nDriver: CAD plugin needs 3-pane vs 2-pane layout switching. Generalizes to any plugin or future built-in editor.","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:58:08","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:04:49","tags":["layout","platform-addition","plugin-system","responsive"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Cycle 1 R1-A complete. ResponsiveContainer at src/Scripts/UI/Controls/responsive_container.gd extends Container with NOTIFICATION_RESIZED + NOTIFICATION_SORT_CHILDREN, exposes width_class StringName property and width_class_changed signal. Bootstrap-equivalent breakpoints xs/sm/md/lg/xl. Hello plugin smoke (responsive_smoke editor item) verifies live width-class transitions. Documented with both in-tree and off-tree usage patterns. Commits: 8e531df5 (initial) + c6f88cdf (SORT_CHILDREN fix) + 4b9ec19d (label NIT). User confirmed working."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd017909d7a7ea880bd1816174375","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Compact / icon-strip presentation mode for AnnotationToolbar","description":"AnnotationToolbar.gd today renders as a labeled VBoxContainer (Tools/Annotate headers + FlowContainers + status label, lines 151-203). For narrow consumer panels (CAD's narrow-pane mode, future plugins), an icon-only / horizontal-strip presentation is needed. Add a presentation_mode property — labeled (current default) and compact — where compact strips headers + status label, switches to HBoxContainer, and elides text when icons are available (kinds without toolbar_icon either fall back to abbreviation or are hidden in compact mode — design call during impl).\n\nDoD:\n- presentation_mode property on AnnotationToolbar with two values.\n- compact mode renders as a single horizontal icon strip suitable for ~40-50px height.\n- labeled mode behavior unchanged from today.\n- Hello plugin smoke test exercises both modes with a toggle.\n- Pattern consumable off-tree (no class_name-only references in public surface).\n\nDriver: CAD plugin's narrow-pane layout (see CAD plan task for chrome adoption). Useful for any future narrow-context annotation use.","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:58:18","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:04:53","tags":["annotations","platform-addition","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054c4167872b9cc767d9a4e25dd","resolution":"Cycle 1 R1-B complete. AnnotationToolbar.gd gets PresentationMode enum (LABELED/COMPACT) + set_presentation_mode setter that triggers _rebuild_layout. COMPACT renders as ~40px HBox icon strip with abbreviation fallback for kinds without toolbar_icon. Hello plugin smoke (compact_toolbar_smoke editor item) verifies both modes + runtime toggle. Commits: 70fa2a64 (initial) + 124f35af (smoke layout fix: vertical stack + _current_mode sync). User confirmed working."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd017d9df7dc0a47b09e15f786a9d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD-specific AnnotationKind extensions (measurement, surface points)","description":"CAD-specific annotation kinds the user authors and the LLM consumes for parametric authoring. Registered via the cross-editor annotation substrate's plugin-extension API (DCR-2 task 019dc055a9e57a09afda893bfe2df319 AnnotationKind registry contract) — no platform changes beyond what's already planned.\n\nScope kinds:\n\n- measure_distance_3d — anchored to two points / edges / faces, yields a measured length. Scenario: \"What's the distance from this hole to the part edge?\" The LLM reads the measurement to verify constraints.\n- measure_angle_3d — three points or two edges, yields a measured angle.\n- surface_point — user-placed dot at a specific position on a B-Rep face. Payload stores (face_id, u, v) parametric coordinates (stable across re-tessellation) plus cached (x, y, z) world coordinates for display. Scenario: user places a spot on a part; LLM is asked \"put a 3mm hole with chamfer at this point\" and authors the feature using the recorded coordinates.\n- (Out of v1) datum_axis / datum_plane — user-defined construction references. Defer unless a scenario demands them.\n\nEach kind:\n- Registers via the substrate's AnnotationKind extension API.\n- Provides render (3D; 2D overlay where applicable), hit-test, bounds, and an AnnotationAuthorTool.\n- Persists in foo.mcad.annotations.json sidecar (per existing CAD plan tasks).\n- Surfaces to the LLM via the substrate's annotation MCP tools so coordinates are queryable.\n\nDoD:\n- measure_distance_3d, measure_angle_3d, surface_point implemented + registered.\n- AnnotationAuthorTool for each available in the toolbar (consumes platform icon-strip mode task).\n- LLM scenario test (hole placement): user places a surface_point on a sample part; LLM is asked to \"add a 3mm hole with chamfer at this point\"; resulting .mcad references the recorded (face_id, u, v) correctly and the resulting geometry passes mcad_validate.\n- LLM scenario test (measurement): user places measure_distance_3d between a hole center and a face edge; LLM correctly reads the distance via the annotation MCP surface.\n- Sidecar round-trip: save → reload preserves all three kinds.\n\nDependencies: substrate AnnotationKind registry contract (019dc055a9e57a09afda893bfe2df319), CAD sidecar I/O (019dc0bb5f2e), platform icon-strip mode (sibling task in annotation plan).","created_at":"2026-04-27T17:58:37","updated_at":"2026-04-27T17:58:37","tags":["annotations","cad","llm-scenario","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd020a9537bc99c2cf9d5f06d4ca6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD adopt: editor-tab chrome contract","description":"Partial: the panel mounts as a plugin Editor (CADPanel.gd:174+), the AnnotationToolbar attaches (CADPanel.gd:200-205), and Cad_AnnotationHost is registered into AnnotationHostRegistry (CADPanel.gd:284). REMAINING: Cad_CADPanel does NOT override `get_annotation_host()`, so the platform Editor's auto-mount path for AnnotationDockPane + PlatformAnnotationOverlay (Editor.gd:704-712) never fires. Plugin currently builds its own `Cad_AnnotationCanvas` overlay clone instead. This remaining scope is rolled into Phase A1 — close this ticket once A1 ships.","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:14","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:23:07","tags":["adoption","cad","chrome","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd020c20f76aab5fc1d26f023b420","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"CAD adopt: route worker/validation errors through plugin toast API","description":"CAD currently has no UI surface for surfacing mcad_validate, mcad_list_edges, worker-spawn, or build123d-import errors when the LLM invokes them via MCP without the panel focused. Adopt the platform plugin host notify() API (019dd0171bdd7940b6d135baaccc381d).\n\nDoD:\n- Worker stderr critical lines surface as toasts (level=error).\n- mcad_validate failures surface as toasts when invoked from chat (level=warning, with line/col details).\n- Bridge worker spawn failures (python missing, build123d import error, venv path bad) surface as actionable toasts (level=error).\n- Cold-start progress (build123d import, OCCT init) optionally surfaces as info-level — design call: probably not toasts, just stderr is fine. Default: errors and warnings only.\n- Activity log retains full transcript for debugging.\n\nDependencies: 019dd0171bdd7940b6d135baaccc381d.","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:21","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:54:21","tags":["adoption","cad","notifications","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd020df1676a0883c0aafaa4ea62d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD adopt: width-class responsive layout (3-pane single-view, 2-pane 4-view)","description":"Adopt platform ResponsiveContainer (019dd017696a7f33b94fe89826008b0d). CAD's panel layout switches with width class:\n\n- xs/sm (3-pane mode, narrow): single 3D view + projection dropdown (Perspective / Top / Bottom / Front / Back / Left / Right). Default: Perspective.\n- md/lg/xl (2-pane mode, wide): existing 4-view layout (Top / Front / Side / Perspective).\n\nDoD:\n- ResponsiveContainer wraps CAD panel; reflows on width-class change.\n- 3-pane mode renders single view + dropdown; switching projection is instant.\n- 2-pane mode renders 4-view (with blank-quadrants bug fixed in sibling task).\n- State preservation between modes: 3-pane projection ↔ 4-view active quadrant maps coherently.\n- Annotation canvas binds to currently visible view in 3-pane, behavior in 4-view per existing design.\n- Closes existing CAD-layout issue 019dccbfea88 (cross-reference comment).\n\nDependencies: 019dd017696a7f33b94fe89826008b0d (ResponsiveContainer), sibling task on blank-quadrants fix (recommended to do first so layout switching has correct content).","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:28","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:20:50","tags":["adoption","cad","layout","plugin","responsive"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"Width-class responsive layout shipped: wide mode = 4-view (2x2), narrow mode = single-view with projection dropdown. Toolbar presentation_mode (compact/labeled) toggles via CADPanel.gd:439 based on width-class."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd021009778859aee291b92d08cbd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD adopt: compact AnnotationToolbar (replace Toolbar Stub)","description":"Drop the current \"Toolbar Stub\" placeholder in CADPanel.gd. Instantiate the platform AnnotationToolbar with presentation_mode bound to width class:\n\n- 3-pane (narrow): compact mode (icon strip), per platform task 019dd017909d7a7ea880bd1816174375.\n- 2-pane (wide): labeled mode (current default).\n\nWire to Cad_AnnotationHost. Built-in 2D kinds (arrow, text, region, etc.) appear immediately. CAD-specific kinds (measure_distance_3d, measure_angle_3d, surface_point) are added by sibling task 019dd017d9df7dc0a47b09e15f786a9d in the next cycle — this task only consumes what's already available from the substrate.\n\nDoD:\n- Toolbar Stub removed from CADPanel.\n- AnnotationToolbar visible with built-in 2D kinds.\n- Presentation mode switches with width class.\n- Cad_AnnotationHost wired correctly (uses preload pattern for off-tree consumability).\n\nDependencies: 019dd017909d7a7ea880bd1816174375 (compact mode), AnnotationKind registry contract 019dc055a9e57a09afda893bfe2df319 (verify status before adoption).","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:37","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:20:53","tags":["adoption","annotations","cad","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"Compact AnnotationToolbar mounted in CAD panel: built CADPanel.gd:200-205, set_presentation_mode toggles compact/labeled at :439 per width-class. Toolbar Stub replaced."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd021189373a5b721b7737be62eab","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD: optional DSL bottom split (toggle, persisted per-document)","description":"Add an optional bottom split below the 3D view(s) hosting the .mcad DSL in a TextEditor. Default: closed. Toggle via a button in CAD's panel-internal toolbar (separate from the editor-tab chrome row) or keyboard shortcut. State persists in .minproj per-document.\n\nRationale: end-user looking at a saved CAD file probably wants the DSL hidden; developer iterating on LLM-authored DSL probably wants it visible. Per-document sticky state respects both modes without a global preference flip.\n\nDoD:\n- Toggle button in CAD panel toolbar shows/hides DSL split.\n- DSL editor reflects current .mcad source; edits sync to file via cad.serialize.\n- Open/closed state round-trips in .minproj per-document.\n- Default state: closed.\n- Works in both 3-pane and 2-pane width modes.\n\nDependencies: none (CAD-internal). Independent of platform tasks.","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:43","updated_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:43","tags":["cad","dsl","plugin","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd02131607e68b3f33e0ebc57e504","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD: fix 3 blank 3D viewports in 4-view layout","description":"In the current 4-view layout, only the bottom-right perspective renders content (grid + gizmo + mesh stub). Top-left, top-right, and bottom-left show only tiny axis hints — empty otherwise. Either:\n\n- Cameras are mis-aimed at empty space (looking far from origin),\n- The four viewports don't share the same scene/world, or\n- The grid is a Node3D in only one viewport's world.\n\nDiagnose root cause and fix.\n\nDoD:\n- All four viewports render the same scene with appropriately framed cameras.\n- Default cameras: Top (Z+ down), Front (Y- forward), Side (X+ right), Perspective (45° iso).\n- Grid visible in perspective; design call on whether to render in orthos (probably yes, with reduced opacity).\n- A sample .mcad cube renders in all four views.\n- Confirmed visually before this task closes.\n\nRecommend doing this BEFORE the responsive-layout adoption (sibling task) so width switching has correct content to switch.\n\nDependencies: none (CAD-internal). Independent of platform tasks.","created_at":"2026-04-27T18:08:49","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:20:47","tags":["3d-rendering","bug","cad","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"4-view layout renders all 4 viewports correctly. Verified by survey: CADPanel.gd:140-172 + 4 OrbitCameras emit shaded mesh in iso, x-ray outlines in ortho panes per design."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd053531d78149c68a3b91c997a52","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Surface PluginDB install/load errors as toast (host-side)","description":"PluginDB.install / PluginManager.install_plugin parse and audit errors today go to push_error() only. End users have no way to see them — overflowing console-only stack traces (which is exactly the symptom that surfaced this bug: a save_mode=none manifest entry caused a parse failure, and the only visible feedback was a tiny truncated overflow in some UI panel, with the full text only available in godot.log).\n\nThe host.notify pipe added in cycle 1 R1-C (commit 521c0398) does not solve this — those notifications come *from* a running plugin, but install errors fire *before* the plugin runs.\n\nWhat's needed: a host-side toast surface that catches PluginDB and PluginManager errors at install / load / start time and routes them through SingletonObject.create_toast_notification(). Long error bodies need a \"show details\" affordance (probably a popup with a scrollable text area) since manifest validation errors include nested detail dicts that exceed any reasonable toast width.\n\nSurfaced from: 2026-04-27 cycle 1 R1 stop-condition validation. User remove+reinstalled hello plugin, hit a panel_save_mode_invalid error from R1-A's manifest, error was visible only in the log file. (Bug 1 — the actual save_mode=none gap — is being fixed in the same cycle by extending the PluginDefinition schema.)\n\nDoD:\n- PluginDB.install errors route through SingletonObject.create_toast_notification with level=error.\n- PluginManager.install_plugin and start_plugin errors route through the same pipe.\n- Long error text gets a \"show details\" or auto-popup affordance — design call during impl.\n- Activity:MCP log gets an entry mirroring the toast for traceability.\n- Test: install a malformed manifest from the Plugin Manager UI; expect a visible error toast with at least the top-level error key (e.g. \"panel_save_mode_invalid\") and a way to see the full detail dict.\n\nRelationship to host.notify (R1-C): host.notify is plugin → host. This task is host → user, originating from the host's own validation/load logic. They share the toast destination but not the source.","created_at":"2026-04-27T19:03:34","updated_at":"2026-04-27T19:03:34","tags":["notifications","platform-addition","plugin-system","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd0c895ec7d088f240e3fda0ce1c1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Extensible Note types for plugins (\"my-object + annotations\" contract)","description":"Today Minerva's Note types are a small fixed set of primitives (text, audio, image, video). The annotation substrate is now platform-shared (since the AnnotationToolbar / AnnotationKind work), so any plugin can produce annotations regardless of what it's annotating. This means the natural Note contract for plugin output is \"my-object + annotations\" — e.g.:\n- CAD plugin: BREP geometry blob + annotations\n- PCBEditor: schematic / layout + annotations\n- Future plugins: their domain object + annotations\n\nA plugin-extensible Note type system would let plugins register a Note kind (similar to AnnotationKind) that knows how to:\n- Serialize its object (whatever the plugin's \"thing\" is)\n- Carry the annotation list alongside\n- Re-hydrate when opened from a note thread\n\nThe \"Turn-into-Note\" platform action (chrome contract task 019dd016f6e0) defaults to a screenshot-to-image-note fallback when the panel doesn't implement a richer note kind. That fallback covers ALL plugins generically, but \"rich\" plugins should be able to register their own note type to preserve actual edit-ability.\n\nSurfaced from: 2026-04-27 R2 planning. User flagged that the Turn-into-Note default is correct but the platform should support a richer \"my-object + annotations\" contract beyond the primitive types so plugins (CAD, PCBEditor) can round-trip more than a screenshot.\n\nDoD:\n- New plugin-extensible Note kind registry (parallel to AnnotationKind registry).\n- Plugin manifest declares note_kinds it provides.\n- A Note kind exposes serialize / deserialize hooks for its object + annotation list.\n- Default screenshot-to-image-note fallback kept; only triggered when no richer Note kind is registered for the panel.\n- CAD plugin (Cycle 2 / Cycle 3 work) demonstrates the pattern as a consumer; PCBEditor migration (separate DCR) follows the same contract.\n\nRelationship to chrome contract (R2, task 019dd016f6e0): R2 wires the Turn-into-Note button. This task extends what that button can produce. R2 ships with screenshot fallback only; this task adds the rich-kind path on top.","created_at":"2026-04-27T21:11:39","updated_at":"2026-04-27T21:13:43","tags":["annotations","notes","platform-addition","plugin-system"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd1e5b72c72d4bc1da923246207ee","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"render_overlay returns null cube on first call (frame-race in render_content_to_image)","description":"## Symptom\n\nAfter the CAD spike landed (commits bd6f0af / 08bf3cb / 91efa54 in ~/github/plugins, bc5f5ee2 in Minerva), MCP `annotations_render_overlay editor_name=\"CAD Panel\" view=\"cad:iso\" include_document=true` was called twice with identical args:\n\n- **Call 1:** `annotations_drawn=2`, but resulting PNG had a white background — no cube. The push_warning at MCPAnnotationTools.gd:579-583 fired: \"editor returned null from render_content_to_image().\"\n- **Call 2 (immediately after):** PNG showed cube + sky + grid + arrow + readable text glyphs, including a red highlight on selected edge 7. Fully working.\n\n## Root cause hypothesis\n\n`Cad_AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image()` schedules a `RenderingServer.frame_post_draw` one-shot callback to capture the SubViewport's texture, caches the result keyed by `(view_id, Engine.get_frames_drawn())`, and returns the cache. On cold start there's no cached image yet, so the first call returns null while the capture is being scheduled. The cache populates by the next frame, so the second call hits.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nEither:\n- `render_content_to_image()` should `await` the first capture inline (synchronous-feeling first call, slight latency), OR\n- The MCP `annotations_render_overlay` tool should detect `null + first-call-cold` and retry once after a single frame, OR\n- The tool should warn callers explicitly: \"include_document=true requires the SubViewport to have rendered at least one frame; first call after panel open may be transparent.\"\n\nThe hello_scene plugin uses the same pattern; check whether it has the same issue or a different mitigation.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Open Minerva on user/imran/experiments/swarm at HEAD `3afa6b91` (or later).\n2. Open CAD plugin panel; let it render its first frame.\n3. Draw any 2d_text annotation in the panel.\n4. Call `mcp__minerva__minerva_annotations_render_overlay editor_name=\"CAD Panel\" view=\"cad:iso\" output_path=/tmp/test1.png include_document=true`.\n5. Observe: /tmp/test1.png has annotations on white, no cube.\n6. Repeat the same call → /tmp/test2.png has cube + annotations.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nCAD plugin spike, 2026-04-27. Tracked in spike test plan as a non-blocking observation.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">minerva-plugins","created_at":"2026-04-28T02:23:06","updated_at":"2026-04-28T02:23:06","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["annotations","cad","mcp","render_overlay","spike-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd1e5eec771e8aead1b700be6b2e8","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"annotations_render_overlay ignores width/height args, returns SubViewport native size","description":"## Symptom\n\nAfter the CAD spike landed, calling MCP `annotations_render_overlay` with explicit `width=1024 height=768` arguments returned an image of size **469x570** — the native size of the CAD plugin's Iso SubViewport, not the requested dimensions.\n\nExact call:\n```\nmcp__minerva__minerva_annotations_render_overlay\n  editor_name = \"CAD Panel\"\n  view = \"cad:iso\"\n  output_path = /tmp/cad_v2_b.png\n  include_document = true\n  width = 1024\n  height = 768\n```\nReturned: `{\"width\":469,\"height\":570,...}`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe tool docs say \"Output image width/height in pixels. Default: 1024/768.\" Callers passing explicit dimensions expect them to be honored. The captured SubViewport image should be resized (preserving aspect or stretched per spec) to the requested width/height before being written.\n\n## Likely cause\n\nWhen `include_document=true` and the host returns a non-null background image, the renderer probably uses the background image's dimensions as the canvas size and skips the requested `width`/`height`. The downsample-to-_MAX_OUTPUT_EDGE path at MCPAnnotationTools.gd:587-589 caps maximum size but doesn't enforce the caller's request when the source is smaller.\n\n## Fix sketch\n\nIn `_annotations_render_overlay`, after acquiring `bg_image`:\n- If caller provided width/height, resize bg_image to those dimensions (and scale annotation coordinates accordingly — they're already in screen space relative to the host, so a uniform resize may be correct, but verify).\n- The output PNG and `width`/`height` in the return value must match what was requested.\n\nCaveat: annotation primitives have screen-space coordinates baked in (e.g. arrow `from:[608,605]`). Naively resizing the bg_image without scaling annotations would draw arrows at wrong positions. Either scale both, or composite annotations against the native-size bg_image and resize the composite at the end.\n\n## Repro\n\nCall the tool with explicit width/height that differ from the host's SubViewport dimensions. Observe that returned dimensions match the SubViewport, not the caller args.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nCAD plugin spike, 2026-04-27.","created_at":"2026-04-28T02:23:20","updated_at":"2026-04-28T02:23:20","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["annotations","mcp","render_overlay","spike-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd20442e07590a2569a5fe6a1da8e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Wire .mcad open → worker evaluate → panel mesh + edges (live pipeline)","description":"## Why\n\nThe CAD plugin scaffold is complete (panel layout, annotation toolbar, edge overlay, lighting). The Python evaluator and `mcad_validate`/`mcad_list_edges` worker tools exist. The final missing link is the *live* pipeline that runs when a user opens a `.mcad` file in the panel.\n\nStub geometry was deliberately removed in commit `9716a47` (cad: remove stub cube; CAD panel is empty until DSL drives it). The panel now correctly shows nothing until this task lands.\n\n`019dc0bb8f35` (T-beam scenario validation) is the *acceptance test* for this pipeline but doesn't itself create it. `019dc0597b60` (mcad_render with composable show flags) is title-only — its name suggests rendering-to-image for headless/export, not the live-panel data path. Filing this as a separate explicit task to avoid scope ambiguity.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. Open a `.mcad` file (e.g. `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/cad/examples/t_beam.mcad`) via Minerva's file dialog.\n2. CAD plugin opens; the geometry described in the DSL appears in all 5 SubViewports (Top/Front/Right/Iso wide; SingleView narrow).\n3. Edge enumeration populates from the worker's evaluation result (replacing the deleted local `_synthesize_cube_edge_registry`).\n4. Edits to the DSL (when DSL bottom split lands per `019dd0211893`) trigger re-evaluation and re-render.\n\n## Pieces\n\n### Worker (Python `cad/worker/`)\n- Add a tool that returns evaluated mesh data: `mcad_evaluate_to_mesh(path | dsl_text) → {vertices, faces, color, edges, bounding_box}`. The Python evaluator already has the result internally for `mcad_list_edges`; expose the mesh too.\n- Alternatively: extend `mcad_list_edges` to optionally return `mesh_data` alongside edges (one round-trip).\n\n### Go (`cad/main.go`)\n- Register a new IPC channel `cad.evaluate` (panel → worker round-trip).\n- On request: spawn/use existing Python worker, call the new tool, return `{success: bool, mesh: {...}, edges: [...], errors: [...]}`.\n- Fold validation errors through the existing `host.notify` toast pipe (already wired in `38d87eb`).\n\n### Manifest (`cad/manifest.json`)\n- Add `cad.evaluate` to `ipc_channels` for the `cad_panel`.\n\n### Panel (`cad/ui/CADPanel.gd`)\n- `_on_panel_load_request(document)` body: read DSL text from `document.file_path` (use `FileAccess` since `host_owned` save_mode means the host doesn't pre-load content). Emit `request(\"cad.evaluate\", {dsl: text}, reply_id)`. Await reply via `_MinervaIPC.await_reply()`.\n- On success: push `mesh` to all 5 MeshRoots via existing `update_mesh()` calls; assign `_edge_registry = result.edges`; call `_push_edges_to_overlays()`; refresh Tree sidebar.\n- On error: surface validation errors via host.notify toast (existing pattern).\n\n### Tests\n- Round-trip the t_beam.mcad → mesh contract via a unit test on the Python tool.\n- Headless smoke that the panel survives a malformed DSL gracefully (error toast, panel stays open).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- DSL bottom split (`019dd0211893`) — that's the editor for the DSL text. This task only needs read-from-disk-on-open.\n- LLM-ergonomics MCP surface — separate sibling task.\n- mcad_export / mcad_deviation / STL reference overlay — separate existing tasks.\n\n## Effort estimate\n\n- Worker tool: 1–2 hours (mostly extracting mesh from existing eval).\n- Go IPC channel: 1 hour.\n- Panel wiring: 1 hour.\n- Testing + manifest update: 30 min.\n- Total: 3–5 hours, single Sonnet implementer + Opus reviewer.","created_at":"2026-04-28T02:56:27","updated_at":"2026-04-28T20:27:46","tags":["cad","ipc","panel","pipeline","spike-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a","resolution":"Verified end-to-end on 2026-04-28 via MCP. Opening ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/cad/examples/t_beam.mcad through minerva_open_file produced live geometry (308 vertices, 284 faces, bbox 80×100×200 matching DSL) and 24 enumerated edges, all queryable via minerva_cad_get_mesh_info / minerva_cad_list_edges_live. Two follow-up fixes shipped during reverification: (1) Go bridge wraps {ok, result|error} envelope on both paths so panel decoder is symmetric (prior code only wrapped on error after my first fix; success path stripped the envelope unintentionally); (2) cad_* MCP tools renamed to minerva_cad_* to clear MinervaMCPHttpServer's prefix filter."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd2049ff67c5a9e89855724a1e45f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP introspection surface for live CAD panels (LLM ergonomics)","description":"## Why\n\nSurfaced from CAD plugin spike testing, 2026-04-27. The user authored a `2d_text` annotation reading \"Fillet edge 7 by a lot\" on a CAD panel and asked an AI agent: \"How large is the cube? How do you know?\"\n\nThe honest answer: an AI consumer constrained to MCP **cannot answer that**. The existing `mcad_*` tools are all artifact producers:\n- `mcad_validate(path)` — parses + validates, returns errors\n- `mcad_list_edges(path)` — evaluates from a file path, returns edges\n- `mcad_render(...)` — produces an image (planned)\n- `mcad_export(...)` — produces an STL/STEP (planned)\n- `mcad_deviation(...)` — Hausdorff comparison (planned)\n\nNone of these answer \"what's the bounding box of the geometry **in this open editor right now**?\" Same gap exists for the user's *current edge selection*, the *current camera state*, and the *DSL source text the user is editing*.\n\nPCB has the right ergonomic pattern: `pcb_get_components`, `pcb_get_pin_position`, `pcb_get_nets`, `pcb_describe_component`, `pcb_export_csv` — all queryable against an editor name. CAD is missing the equivalent.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nAn AI agent connected via MCP can answer the following questions about a live CAD panel without filesystem access:\n\n1. \"What are the dimensions of the geometry in `editor_name=&quot;CAD Panel&quot;`?\" → `cad_get_mesh_info`\n2. \"What edges does it have?\" → `cad_list_edges_live` (no file path needed)\n3. \"What are the endpoints of edge 7?\" → `cad_get_edge`\n4. \"Which edge has the user selected?\" → `cad_get_selected_edge`\n5. \"What's the DSL source the user is editing?\" → `cad_get_document_source`\n\n## Tools to add\n\n```\ncad_get_mesh_info(editor_name) → {bounding_box: {min, max}, vertex_count, face_count, units, has_geometry: bool}\n\ncad_list_edges_live(editor_name, view?) → [edge_dict, ...]   # same dict shape as the worker's mcad_list_edges, but read from the live host's _edge_registry\n\ncad_get_edge(editor_name, edge_id) → edge_dict | null\n\ncad_get_selected_edge(editor_name) → edge_dict | null   # uses Cad_AnnotationHost.get_selected_edge_id (added in spike commit 91efa54) + cross-references _edge_registry\n\ncad_get_document_source(editor_name) → {file_path: str, dsl_text: str | null, evaluated: bool}\n```\n\n## Where it lives\n\nNew file `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd`, subclass of `MCPToolModule`. Mirrors the structure of `MCPAnnotationTools.gd` and `MCPPCBTools.gd`. Registered in the dispatcher chain.\n\nThe module operates entirely against the live `Cad_AnnotationHost` (looked up via `AnnotationHostRegistry.get(editor_name)`), so no IPC round-trip to the worker is needed — the host already holds everything except the DSL text. For the DSL text, read from `_ctx.file_path` if set, otherwise return null.\n\nDepends on: nothing strictly, but most useful AFTER the live pipeline lands (sibling task, not yet filed). Until then, `cad_get_mesh_info` returns `has_geometry: false` (panel is empty by design per commit 9716a47).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Mutation: this task is read-only. `cad_fillet_edge`, `cad_evaluate_dsl`, etc. are separate.\n- Worker-side artifact producers (mcad_render, mcad_export) — those have their own existing tasks.\n\n## Effort estimate\n\n- 5 tools, all reading from live host state (no IPC): ~3 hours implementation.\n- Tests against a fake Cad_AnnotationHost: ~1 hour.\n- Manifest/registration: 15 min.\n- Total: 4–5 hours, Sonnet + Opus reviewer.","created_at":"2026-04-28T02:56:51","updated_at":"2026-04-28T18:38:50","tags":["cad","introspection","llm-ergonomics","mcp","spike-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a","resolution":"Shipped in commits 9021bad (plugins) + 85654c42 (minerva). 19/19 unit tests PASS. Reviewer A approved with non-blocking notes only. Module surfaces 5 read-only MCP tools; ready to be used as the test oracle for live-pipeline reverification (019dd20442e0 Round B)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd292c0be7b42a291f429ac5c665a","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"CreatableItemRegistry overwrite warning persists across plugin reload (cosmetic)","description":"## What\n\n`CreatableItemRegistry.gd:56` emits a `push_warning(\"CreatableItemRegistry: overwriting existing item 'X'\")` on every plugin reload. Confirmed reliably reproducible with the CAD plugin (item id `new_mcad`) by clicking \"Reload\" in PluginManagerPanel.\n\n## Why this is noise, not a bug\n\n`CreatableItemRegistry.register_item()` (lines 54-58):\n\n```gdscript\nfunc register_item(item: CreatableItem) -> void:\n    if _items.has(item.id):\n        push_warning(\"CreatableItemRegistry: overwriting existing item '%s'\" % item.id)\n    _items[item.id] = item   # unconditional overwrite — works correctly\n    items_changed.emit()\n```\n\nAfter the warning fires, the line below unconditionally overwrites the registry entry. Final state matches what a clean re-registration would produce: one item, correctly registered, no leak, no stale callbacks (the old `CreatableItem` is reachable only via `_items` until the new one replaces it, then garbage-collected).\n\nUI consumers (`menuMain.gd`, etc.) iterate `_items` and don't care about identity — they read the current value. So the user-visible \"New → New CAD Document\" menu entry continues to function correctly.\n\n## Why the existing idempotency fix isn't enough\n\nCommit `db761cfa` (\"warnings: clear runtime warnings — batch 3\") added an idempotency guard at `src/Scripts/Models/singleton_object.gd:745`:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc _register_plugin_editor_items(id: String) -> void:\n    ...\n    creatable_item_registry.unregister_by_source(id)   # added in db761cfa\n    for ei in def.editor_items:\n        ...\n        creatable_item_registry.register_item(item)\n```\n\nThis makes a single `_register_plugin_editor_items` call cleanly idempotent: any items previously registered for `id` get cleared before re-registering. Verified by reading `unregister_by_source` (CreatableItemRegistry.gd:68-78) — it iterates, collects, erases, emits `items_changed`. Correct.\n\nThe warning still fires after the fix, suggesting the warning's source is **not** within `_register_plugin_editor_items` proper but rather in the **interaction of plugin_stopped and plugin_started signals during `restart_plugin`**.\n\n## Suspected root cause: emission-order race in restart_plugin\n\n`PluginManager.restart_plugin()` (PluginManager.gd:364-378):\n\n```gdscript\nfunc restart_plugin(id: String) -> Dictionary:\n    ...\n    var stop_result := stop_plugin(id)\n    if stop_result.get(\"error\"):\n        return {\"error\": ...}\n    # Small yield so the subprocess OS handle is fully released before we\n    # respawn — not strictly necessary but avoids port/pipe races.\n    await Engine.get_main_loop().create_timer(0.2).timeout\n    return await start_plugin(id)\n```\n\n`stop_plugin` (PluginManager.gd:333+) sets a `stopping=true` flag, kicks off subprocess termination, then later emits `plugin_stopped.emit(id)` at line 357. If any of that path is async (e.g. waiting for the subprocess to actually exit), the emission of `plugin_stopped` may happen AFTER `start_plugin`'s `plugin_started.emit(id)` — even though `restart_plugin` calls them in the right syntactic order.\n\nHypothesized order on a reload:\n1. `restart_plugin` → `stop_plugin(id)` returns synchronously; async subprocess cleanup pending.\n2. 200ms timer.\n3. `start_plugin(id)` runs. Emits `plugin_started`. Handler `_register_plugin_editor_items` runs: my unregister (no-op, registry empty) + register (no warning). Items now in registry.\n4. Async tail of stop_plugin completes; emits `plugin_stopped`. Handler `_unregister_plugin_editor_items` runs and removes the just-registered items.\n5. Some downstream caller (UI rebuild? MCP probe?) re-triggers registration. THIS register sees the previous register's leftovers somewhere in state — possibly via duplicate signal connection (need to verify) — and fires the warning.\n\nAlternative: there is a second registration path I didn't find. Worth grepping `creatable_item_registry.register_item` again if it ever becomes worth fixing.\n\n## What's already been tried\n\n- ✅ Idempotency guard at `_register_plugin_editor_items` (commit `db761cfa`). Doesn't address the cross-signal-emission race.\n- ✅ Verified `unregister_by_source` correctly removes items by source_plugin_id matching.\n- ✅ Verified the CAD plugin's manifest declares `id: \"cad\"` and `editor_items[0].id: \"new_mcad\"`. Items are registered with `source_plugin_id=\"cad\"`. `unregister_by_source(\"cad\")` should match.\n- ✅ Verified `_register_plugin_editor_items` is connected to `plugin_started` exactly once (`singleton_object.gd:598`). Same for `_unregister_plugin_editor_items` to `plugin_stopped` (line 599) and `plugin_crashed` (line 600).\n- ❌ Did NOT trace the actual emission order via print statements during a reload. That's the next investigation step.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Run Minerva on `db761cfa` or later (with the idempotency fix in place).\n2. Open the Plugin Manager panel.\n3. Start the CAD plugin (or trust autostart).\n4. Click \"Reload\" on the CAD plugin (or whatever UI affordance triggers `PluginManager.restart_plugin(\"cad\")`).\n5. Observe the debugger Output panel — `CreatableItemRegistry.gd:56 @ register_item(): CreatableItemRegistry: overwriting existing item 'new_mcad'` fires once.\n6. Repeat reload — fires once per reload.\n\n## Acceptance for cleanup\n\nEither:\n- **(a)** Identify and fix the actual ordering bug so a reload of any plugin produces zero `register_item` warnings. The fix likely lives in `stop_plugin` (PluginManager.gd:333+) — make `plugin_stopped.emit(id)` strictly precede `restart_plugin`'s `await timer`, by awaiting the subprocess shutdown synchronously before returning. Verify with a print-trace through a reload.\n- **(b)** Decide that `register_item` warning-on-overwrite is the wrong contract for this registry — the unconditional-overwrite behavior IS the intended semantic, so the warning is misleading. Demote to `print()` (or remove) and document that \"overwrite\" is the registry's normal mode for plugin lifecycle reasons.\n\n(b) is a 30-second fix; (a) is the proper engineering fix and might surface other lifecycle ordering issues worth knowing about.\n\n## Why low priority\n\n- Functionally harmless: state is correct, UI works, no leak.\n- Only fires on explicit reload (a developer action, not user-facing).\n- Catches real bugs if a future code path accidentally registers the same id from a different source — useful guard, not pure noise.\n- Removing or fixing it would have minimal user impact.\n\n## File:line references\n\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/CreatableItemRegistry.gd:54-58` — `register_item` (warning source).\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/CreatableItemRegistry.gd:68-78` — `unregister_by_source` (verified correct).\n- `src/Scripts/Models/singleton_object.gd:735-763` — `_register_plugin_editor_items` (with idempotency fix at line 745).\n- `src/Scripts/Models/singleton_object.gd:598-600` — signal connections (single connection per handler, verified).\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginManager.gd:326` — `plugin_started.emit(id)`.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginManager.gd:357` — `plugin_stopped.emit(id)`.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginManager.gd:364-378` — `restart_plugin` (suspect ordering site).\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nWarning-cleanup work-cycle, 2026-04-27. Final residual warning after batches 1-4 cleared ~30 other warnings across both repos. User decision: leave as-is, file as chore for later cleanup.","created_at":"2026-04-28T05:32:06","updated_at":"2026-04-28T05:32:06","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["cosmetic","lifecycle","low-priority","plugins","registry","warnings"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd29a3f23709e998dc884ace25f2c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Panel request() channel = MCP tool name","created_at":"2026-04-28T05:40:17","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:04:36","tags":["ipc","manifest","plugin"],"value":"When a plugin scene panel emits `request(\"foo.bar\", payload, reply_id)`, PluginScenePanelBroker._dispatch_to_plugin_backend forwards it to the plugin's MCP server as tools/call with name=\"foo.bar\" arguments=payload. So the channel listed in manifest.json's panels[].ipc_channels MUST be registered as an MCP tool by the plugin backend (Go/Python) under that exact name. Periods in tool names are valid. The channel must also appear in ui.ipc_messages global allowlist.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"panel_request_channel_eq_tool_name","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd2cd23497f7195162356b060aa00","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Host-owned file open hard-codes JSON parsing","created_at":"2026-04-28T06:35:52","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:04:36","tags":["host_owned","plugin","regression"],"value":"Editor.gd._load_plugin_scene_file (~line 661) parses every host_owned plugin file as JSON before invoking _on_panel_load_request. This breaks plugins whose document format is text/binary (e.g. CAD's .mcad). Fix: try JSON parse but degrade to {file_path, raw_text} on failure rather than error-popping. The plugin owns the schema; let it decide how to read the file. Affects any non-JSON plugin file format.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"host_owned_open_assumes_json","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd31e1fbd70a5a5c17ca5fe65cb99","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"restart_plugin doesn't re-parse manifest.json","created_at":"2026-04-28T08:04:20","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:04:36","tags":["hot-reload","manifest","plugin","regression"],"value":"PluginManager.restart_plugin (and the .json hot-reload trigger that calls it) only stop+start the subprocess. The cached PluginDefinition — including panels[].ipc_channels and ui.ipc_messages — is NOT re-read from manifest.json. Adding a new ipc_channel via manifest edit + hot-reload yields permission_denied at PluginScenePanelBroker because entry.channels was set at install time. Workaround: remove + reinstall the plugin via the Plugin Manager UI. Long-term: restart_plugin should call _db.reinstall(manifest_path) before respawning.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"restart_plugin_does_not_reparse_manifest","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd31f2331772eab4859c11ea8ad1d","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"manifest.json edits don't propagate without explicit reinstall","description":"## Repro\n\n1. Install a plugin (manifest declares `ipc_channels: [\"a\", \"b\"]`).\n2. Add a new channel to manifest.json: `ipc_channels: [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`.\n3. Hot-reload triggers (manifest.json is in WATCH_EXTENSIONS); PluginManager.restart_plugin runs.\n4. Panel emits `request(\"c\", ...)`; broker rejects with `permission_denied — Channel 'c' is not in the declared ipc_channels for panel`.\n\n## Why\n\n`PluginDB.install()` parses manifest.json once and stores `PluginDefinition.to_dict()` to `user://plugins/plugins.json`. On startup AND on restart, `PluginDB.load()` rebuilds defs from this persisted JSON via `PluginDefinition.from_dict()`, NOT from manifest.json on disk. `PluginManager.restart_plugin` only stop+starts the subprocess; it does not call `_db.update_definition()` or re-read the manifest.\n\nResult: any edit to manifest.json (channels, scripts, tools, file_extensions, save_mode, etc.) is invisible to the running platform until the user manually removes + reinstalls the plugin via the Plugin Manager UI.\n\n## Discovery\n\nSurfaced during stop-3b smoke test of `019dd20442e0` (live .mcad pipeline). The implementer added `cad.evaluate` to manifest.json's `ipc_channels` and `ipc_messages`. The plugin auto-restarted, but the broker still saw the stale 4-channel set and denied the dispatch.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nIn `PluginManager.restart_plugin` (or earlier, in the .json branch of `_on_reload_debounce_expired`), call `_db.update_definition(PluginDef.from_manifest(def.manifest_path))` before respawning the subprocess. The manifest path is already known per plugin. Care: re-validate the manifest before swapping; on validation failure, fall back to the cached def and log a warning.\n\nOptional (separate): on Minerva startup, re-read manifest.json for each persisted plugin and prefer disk over the persisted snapshot when both exist. Faster for dev iteration, no UI step required.\n\n## Workaround\n\nRemove the plugin via Plugin Manager UI, then Install Plugin pointing at `~/github/plugins/cad/manifest.json` (or wherever the manifest lives). The full reinstall path re-parses the manifest.\n\n## Severity\n\nMedium — blocks every plugin author who edits manifest.json. Common operations (adding a tool, declaring a new ipc_channel, expanding file_extensions) all hit this. Workaround is well-defined but undiscoverable without reading source.","created_at":"2026-04-28T08:05:26","updated_at":"2026-04-28T18:22:42","severity":3,"tags":["hot-reload","manifest","platform","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5532fc177d68cdcda2fd3bf3bfa","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP tool: minerva_open_file(path) — programmatic file-open dispatch","description":"## Why\n\nStop-3b smoke testing for `019dd20442e0` (CAD live pipeline) needs the user to drive the file-open dialog by hand for every iteration. With this MCP tool, an agent can drive the same dispatch logic the file dialog uses, enabling Layer-1 / Layer-2 integration tests to boot a real Minerva, open a `.mcad` (or any plugin-handled file), and assert state via the (sibling) introspection tools.\n\nSame pattern as `minerva_plugin_install` / `minerva_plugin_start` — the tool is a thin shim over the existing in-process dispatch. No new business logic.\n\nWithout it, plugin authors are stuck with manual GUI iteration, and the integration-boundary testing principle (`feedback_test_at_integration_boundary`) can't be applied to file-open flows.\n\n## Pieces\n\n### MCP surface\nNew tool registered in `MCPServerConnection` / `MCPToolModule`:\n```\nminerva_open_file(path: str) → {\n  ok: bool,\n  editor_kind: str,       # \"PLUGIN_SCENE\", \"TEXT\", \"SPREADSHEET\", ...\n  editor_name: str,       # tab title; same key MCP introspection tools use\n  plugin_id: str | null,  # set when editor_kind == PLUGIN_SCENE\n  panel_name: str | null,\n  errors: [str]\n}\n```\n\nPure read+open. Idempotent if the file is already open (returns the existing editor_name).\n\n### Where the dispatch logic lives today\n\nThe file → editor-kind resolution lives in `EditorPane` and `Editor.gd` (the file-dialog signal handler). That code path:\n1. Looks at file extension.\n2. Queries `PluginEditorRegistry` for `file_extensions → {plugin_id, panel_name}` mapping.\n3. If a plugin owns the extension → constructs an `Editor.Type.PLUGIN_SCENE` with associated `(plugin_id, panel_name, file_path)`.\n4. Otherwise dispatches to one of the typed editors (TEXT / GRAPHICS / SPREADSHEET / VIDEO / WEBVIEW / PCB).\n\nRefactor: extract the resolution+open into `singleton_object.gd::open_file_at_path(path) -> Dictionary`. Have both the file-dialog handler and the new MCP tool call it. Keep return shape JSON-compatible.\n\n### MCPToolModule wiring\n\nAdd `MCPGeneralTools.gd` (or extend whichever module owns `minerva_create_text_editor` etc.) with the new handler. Returns the dict above, surfacing any error from `open_file_at_path`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. From a fresh Minerva boot, an MCP client can:\n   - `minerva_open_file(\"/path/to/foo.txt\")` → editor_kind=\"TEXT\", editor_name set.\n   - `minerva_open_file(\"/path/to/foo.mcad\")` → editor_kind=\"PLUGIN_SCENE\", plugin_id=\"cad\", panel_name=\"cad_panel\", editor_name set.\n   - `minerva_open_file(\"/path/to/missing.txt\")` → ok=false, errors=[\"file_not_found: ...\"].\n2. Opening the same path twice returns the existing editor_name (no duplicate tabs).\n3. The file-open dialog still works — same dispatch path, no regression.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Save / close — separate tools (most already exist).\n- Authoring host_owned files — that's the plugin's save_request contract.\n- Authentication / capability gating — this is a host MCP tool, not a plugin tool.\n\n## Effort estimate\n\n- Refactor open dispatch into singleton helper: ~1 hour.\n- Wire MCP tool: ~30 min.\n- Tests (unit on dispatch helper, integration via tool call): ~1 hour.\n- Total: 2–3 hours, Sonnet implementer + Opus reviewer.","created_at":"2026-04-28T18:21:31","updated_at":"2026-04-28T18:38:52","tags":["file-open","mcp","platform","plugin","test-infra"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"Shipped in commit 85654c42. 22/22 unit tests PASS. Reviewer B approved_with_notes; both notes (typo + missing enum variants) fixed inline before commit. Helper at SingletonObject.open_file_at_path used by both the file dialog and the MCP tool; behavior preserved."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5535f0978538f52a4f31f19cd7c","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Editor._load_plugin_scene_file hard-coded JSON parse rejected non-JSON files","description":"## Repro (pre-fix)\n\nOpen any non-JSON file (e.g. `.mcad` raw DSL) in a plugin scene editor with `save_mode: \"host_owned\"`. Editor.gd:_load_plugin_scene_file calls `JSON.parse(content)` and error-pops \"Invalid plugin file: Could not parse '...' as JSON: Expected 'true', 'false', or 'null', got 'height'\". The plugin's `_on_panel_load_request` is never invoked.\n\n## Why\n\nThe contract assumed every host_owned file body was a JSON Dictionary (the round-trip of `_on_panel_save_request`'s return value). That's true for plugin-saved files but not for files authored externally — sample files, files exported from other tools, hand-written DSLs, etc.\n\n## Fix (commit 08c9b299, user/imran/experiments/swarm)\n\n`_load_plugin_scene_file` always injects `file_path` into the document handed to `_on_panel_load_request`. If the body parses as a JSON Dictionary, those keys are merged in. Otherwise `raw_text` carries the body. The plugin owns the schema and decides how to read its own file.\n\nFiling retrospectively so the DCR tree shows what was discovered during 019dd20442e0 stop-3b smoke testing on 2026-04-28.","created_at":"2026-04-28T18:21:44","updated_at":"2026-04-28T18:21:44","severity":3,"tags":["file-open","fixed","host_owned","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd","resolution":"fixed"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd55fdbb47954b66d01e4f5cd9de2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"--script mode tests must `await process_frame` before reaching autoloads","created_at":"2026-04-28T18:35:22","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:33:19","tags":["autoload","godot","headless","testing"],"value":"Godot `--headless --script foo.gd` runs the script's `_init()` BEFORE autoloads are attached to /root. `root.get_node_or_null(\"SingletonObject\")` returns null in _init. Fix: at the top of `_init()` (after extends SceneTree), call `await process_frame` once. Then autoloads are reachable for the rest of the test. Pattern: `func _init() -> void: ... await process_frame; <run_tests>`","component":"minerva-testing","key":"script_mode_autoloads_need_process_frame","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5bf32ba70dd82d96bda6dc6f23a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"All host MCP tools must be named minerva_*","created_at":"2026-04-28T20:19:30","updated_at":"2026-04-28T20:19:30","tags":["http","mcp","naming","tools"],"value":"MinervaMCPHttpServer rejects any tools/call whose name doesn't begin with `minerva_` (line ~328: \"Unknown tool\"). Tool gets registered in tool_registry and appears in tools/list, but call dispatch refuses it. Plugin-contributed tools follow `minerva_<plugin_id>_*`. Naming a host MCP tool just `cad_get_*` or `pcb_*` looks plausible (it works via internal _execute_tool_impl) but breaks over HTTP.","component":"minerva-mcp","key":"tools_must_have_minerva_prefix","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5c28122747f973495e656fa3ccb","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Go plugin bridge strips {ok, result|error} envelope on BOTH paths","created_at":"2026-04-28T20:23:07","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:04:36","tags":["bridge","envelope","ipc","plugin"],"value":"bridge.Worker.Call returns just `resp.Result` on success and just `*WorkerError` on failure — the worker's `{ok: true, result:...}` / `{ok: false, error:...}` wrapper is dropped in both cases. main.go MUST re-wrap before sending to MCP if the panel expects symmetric drilldown. Original commit 6ad03ac assumed Python's wrapper survived; it doesn't. Fixed in main.go handleToolsCall: success path wraps as `{ok: true, result: <raw>}`, error path wraps as `{ok: false, error: we}`. Symptom of unfixed: `[CADPanel] cad.evaluate worker error [unknown]:` with empty message even though worker succeeded — panel reads `worker_payload.ok` which defaults to false because the dict is just the raw result dict with no `ok` key.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"go_bridge_drops_ok_envelope_on_worker_error","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f44e877eeea956b707cd1059fd","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_get_note returns empty content for IMAGE-type notes","description":"## Repro\n\n1. Create an image note (type=IMAGE) in Minerva.\n2. Call `minerva_get_note(note_id)`.\n\n## Observed\n\nResponse shape:\n```\n{\"content\":\"\",\"enabled\":false,\"note_id\":\"...\",\"success\":true,\"tab\":\"Notes\",\"title\":\"suggested UI\",\"type\":\"IMAGE\"}\n```\n\n`content` is empty. Type is correctly reported as IMAGE.\n\n## Expected\n\nEither (a) the image bytes encoded as base64 in `content`, or (b) a stable on-disk path to the binary in a separate field (e.g. `image_path`) that an MCP client can `Read`. Today there's no way for an LLM to see the image — the user has to manually export/screenshot.\n\n## Impact\n\nSurfaced 2026-04-28 during CAD UX scenario discussion: user pointed at an annotated screenshot in a note titled \"suggested UI\" and asked the agent to inspect it. Agent could enumerate the note metadata but had no way to load the pixels. Workaround: user saves to `/tmp/foo.png` and the agent uses `Read`.\n\n## Affects\n\n`minerva_get_note` and any other MCP path that exposes IMAGE/AUDIO/VIDEO notes. Same gap likely applies to the other binary note kinds (worth verifying).","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:31","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:31","severity":3,"tags":["image","mcp","notes"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f46df97674a8260670f17b992b","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"GraphicsEditor improvements — paste, text, layer model, toolbar","description":"## Why\n\nThe current GraphicsEditor has accumulated several UX papercuts that block routine editing flows. User feedback on 2026-04-28 enumerated four concrete defects (filed as children of this DCR):\n\n1. Paste-as-main-layer destroys the image on first draw stroke.\n2. Text tool can't be a second tool in a layer; text is lost on compose / save-to-notes.\n3. Layer model behaves as many small unlinked restricted layers rather than an infinite canvas.\n4. Tool icon spacing / placement shifts on context and doesn't restore.\n\n## Goal\n\nMake the GraphicsEditor predictable and forgiving for the common flows:\n- Paste an image, annotate it, save back to a note.\n- Use text + draw tools in the same image without losing strokes.\n- Treat the working surface as one large canvas; layers compose without rigid boundaries.\n- Stable toolbar layout — controls don't reorder behind the user's back.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Brush/effect feature parity with full paint apps.\n- Vector primitives.\n- Multi-document editor.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nEach child bug closed against its own repro. A short \"happy path\" test that demonstrates: paste image → add text → draw → save to note → reopen, with no data loss.","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:39","updated_at":"2026-05-30T06:58:52","severity":3,"tags":["graphics-editor","platform","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f492187d4fa74e379b7f496ada","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GraphicsEditor: pasted image as main layer is lost on first draw stroke","description":"## Repro\n\n1. Open a new GraphicsEditor.\n2. Paste an image (Ctrl+V) so it becomes the main layer.\n3. Pick any drawing tool and start drawing.\n\n## Observed\n\nThe pasted image disappears as soon as the first stroke begins. Only the new stroke remains.\n\n## Expected\n\nPasted image stays as the underlying layer; strokes compose on top. The act of starting to draw must not destroy existing pixels in the same layer.\n\n## Notes\n\nSuggests the draw tool is initializing the active layer from a blank canvas instead of preserving the pasted bitmap. Likely a \"lazy clear on first stroke\" pattern.","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:48","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:48","severity":3,"tags":["draw","graphics-editor","paste"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f46df97674a8260670f17b992b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f4af347e6d9b6fd4d1ec4f9d1c","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GraphicsEditor: text tool buggy — can't be second tool in layer; text lost on compose/save","description":"## Repro\n\nTwo failure modes both surfaced 2026-04-28:\n\nA. **Can't be second tool in layer.** Use any tool (e.g. draw) in a layer, then switch to text and try to add text in the same layer — the text doesn't take, or the tool refuses to accept input until the layer is reset.\n\nB. **Text lost on compose/save.** Add text to a layer, compose the image, save it to a note. The text is missing in the saved output even though it was visible while editing.\n\n## Expected\n\nText tool works at any point in a layer's history, and text strokes survive composition / save-to-notes round-trips.\n\n## Notes\n\nBoth symptoms point at the text tool keeping its content out-of-band from the layer pixels (e.g. as a deferred overlay) and not flushing into the rasterised composite at the right boundary. May share root cause with the paste-on-draw bug (sibling).","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:55","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:17:55","severity":3,"tags":["graphics-editor","text-tool"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f46df97674a8260670f17b992b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f4ca50798cb1cb0f7ddc4e65a8","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GraphicsEditor: layer model is many small unlinked restricted layers, not infinite canvas","description":"## Symptom\n\nThe working surface behaves as a collection of small, unlinked, size-restricted layers. Drawing past a layer's bounds clips. Layers don't compose into one continuous canvas the way users expect from any modern paint/annotation tool. The user can't pan/zoom across one big logical surface; instead they fight per-layer rectangles.\n\n## Expected\n\nOne logical infinite (or at least very large) canvas. Layers stack and compose; bounds expand to cover content rather than clipping to whatever rectangle the layer was created at. Pan/zoom moves across the whole canvas, not within a single layer's frame.\n\n## Impact\n\nBlocks any non-trivial annotation workflow. User reports \"lots of unlinked, restricted layers\" 2026-04-28.\n\n## Notes\n\nLikely a foundational change — layers probably need to grow on demand or be backed by a tile-based store. This is the largest of the four child bugs in scope and may justify its own design pass before implementation.","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:18:02","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:18:02","severity":3,"tags":["canvas","graphics-editor","layers"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f46df97674a8260670f17b992b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f4e4d57742be64d60b7db2e870","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GraphicsEditor: tool icons reorder by context and don't restore","description":"## Symptom\n\nTool icon spacing/placement shifts based on tool usage context. After the shift, icons don't return to their original positions when the context changes back. The user ends up hunting for tools that \"moved and never moved back\".\n\n## Expected\n\nStable toolbar layout. If a contextual sub-toolbar appears (e.g. tool-specific options), it should not displace the primary tool icons. Or, if icons must reflow, the primary positions should be deterministic and restored on context exit.\n\n## Notes\n\nReported 2026-04-28 as the toolbar feeling like it has muscle-memory hostile behaviour. Likely an HBoxContainer / responsive sizing issue where contextual controls expand into space owned by the static toolset and don't yield it back. Worth auditing the size_flags + container nesting.","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:18:09","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:18:09","severity":2,"tags":["graphics-editor","toolbar","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f46df97674a8260670f17b992b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f871b17018bf8856c283aa3fd5","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"CAD edge-tools UX refactor: edge-numbers as annotation, edge-markers as domain control","description":"## Why\n\nTwo surfaces in the CAD panel currently masquerade as annotations but aren't modeled correctly:\n\n1. The \"white edge-number labels\" rendered in 3D over every edge — currently baked into `edge_overlay.gd`. Always-on, cluttered in iso/perspective at higher edge counts. Should be a real annotation kind, opt-in, transient (LLM-driven during disambiguation, user-cleared after).\n2. The right sidebar labeled \"Annotations\" with a Tree of edge metadata — it's not a generic annotation view. It's the natural home for **edge-markers** (persistent design-intent markup, document-scoped) — but those don't exist as a concept yet, so the sidebar currently shows raw worker output.\n\nReference scenarios (2026-04-28 design discussion):\n- Scenario A (LLM disambiguation): user says \"fillet that inner edge\" → LLM creates ephemeral edge-number annotations on matching edges → user picks edges 1+2 → LLM clears.\n- Scenario B (user picks via sidebar): user opens Edge Markers panel, picks edge 7, tells LLM \"chamfer edge 7\". No annotation traffic.\n\nThe redline-style edge-marker is closer to a review comment than an annotation: persistent, tied to versioned design state, document-scoped lifecycle.\n\n## Surfaces (target end-state)\n\n### Annotation kind: `cad_edge_number`\n- Payload: `{edge_id: int, label?: str, color?: str, group_id?: str}`.\n- Renderer pulls live midpoint from `Cad_AnnotationHost.get_edge(id)`, projects to screen, draws leader + bubble in `AnnotationCanvas`. Visible across all panes that show the edge.\n- Group lifecycle: `group_id` lets an LLM clear \"everything I just drew\" in one call. Auto-clear on panel close.\n\n### MCP tools (transient, LLM-driven)\n- `minerva_cad_annotate_edges(editor_name, edge_ids | filter, group_id?, label_format?)` — returns `{group_id, edge_ids}`.\n- `minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations(editor_name, group_id? | all=true)`.\n- Filter dict at minimum `{ids: [..]}`, ideally `{tags_any, visible_in_views, source_plane}`. \"Inner/outer\" requires worker tag enrichment (sub-task).\n\n### Domain control: edge-markers (persistent)\n- New right-sidebar panel (rename current \"Annotations\" → \"Edge Markers\"). List, select, add, edit, delete.\n- Persistent via `_on_panel_save_request` — markers ride the `.mcad` document save state.\n- MCP: `minerva_cad_set_edge_marker(editor_name, edge_id, text, kind?)`, `list_edge_markers`, `clear_edge_marker`.\n\n### Toolbar entry\n- Add edge-number tool icon to `AnnotationToolbar` alongside arrow / text. Clicking puts the canvas into edge-number authoring mode; user clicks an edge in any pane → annotation created at that edge id.\n\n### Responsive (narrow widths)\n- Edge-number toolbar icon rides existing AnnotationToolbar compact mode.\n- Edge Markers sidebar collapses to a \"Markers\" drawer button in the narrow tools row → opens slide-over panel with the same Tree.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Worker-side fillet/chamfer execution (separate task).\n- Annotation TTL / crash-cleanup (deferred; group-id manual clear is enough for v1).\n- Full edge-marker semantics (kinds beyond a single text marker — defer to follow-up).\n\n## Acceptance (whole DCR)\n\n1. Open `t_beam.mcad` → no edge numbers visible by default in any pane.\n2. LLM calls `minerva_cad_annotate_edges` with `{ids:[1,2]}` → labels appear on edges 1 + 2 only, in iso + ortho panes.\n3. `minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations(group_id=...)` removes them.\n4. User clicks the edge-number toolbar icon, clicks an edge → annotation created.\n5. Right sidebar header reads \"Edge Markers\"; create/list/delete markers persists across panel close+reopen.\n6. Narrow-width: Markers drawer button visible; toolbar still has edge-number icon.\n\n## Round structure (proposed)\n\n- **Round 1 (this cycle)**: visual substrate. Strip label rendering from edge_overlay.gd (default off). Add `cad_edge_number` annotation kind + canvas mode. Sidebar rename. Headless-testable.\n- **Round 2**: MCP tools + edge-marker persistence + worker tag enrichment + responsive drawer. Verified via MCP-driven end-to-end smoke (the loop we just proved out for `019dd20442e0`).","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:22:02","updated_at":"2026-04-29T23:04:11","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","cad","edge-markers","edge-numbers","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a","resolution":"Rounds 1+2a shipped (cad_edge_number kind, MCP edge-annotation tools, cross-pane render, worker tag enrichment, midpoint fix). Abstraction superseded by v2 substrate DCR 019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1; shipped pieces stay valid as transitional code (cad_edge_number records dropped at v1→v2 migration per task 019ddb796041719a). Round 2b-α pivoted, Round 2b-α v2 cancelled. See comment #259."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd5f8e88e7688a0547adc40d7e17c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD UX Round 1: cad_edge_number annotation kind + remove default edge labels + sidebar rename","description":"## Scope (Round 1 of `019dd5f871b1` DCR)\n\nHeadless-testable visual substrate. No MCP tools, no persistence, no responsive drawer — those land in Round 2.\n\n### 1. Strip default label rendering from `edge_overlay.gd`\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/scripts/edge_overlay.gd`\n\nThe overlay currently draws BOTH x-ray edge lines (in ortho panes) AND numbered labels for every edge (everywhere). Keep the line rendering. Remove or default-disable the label rendering. Default panel state at open: no edge numbers visible in any pane.\n\nThe LINE rendering (x-ray ortho) must keep working — it's essential for ortho navigation.\n\n### 2. New annotation kind `cad_edge_number`\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/kinds/cad_edge_number.gd` (new file).\n\n- Subclass `AnnotationKind`.\n- Payload schema: `{edge_id: int, label?: str, color?: str, group_id?: str}`.\n- Render method: query host for edge midpoint via `host.get_edge(edge_id).midpoint`, project Vector3 → screen via the active viewport's camera (use `unproject_position` on the `Camera3D`), draw a small leader line + bubble + number text on the `AnnotationCanvas`.\n- Cross-pane: the annotation should render in every pane that has the edge in its visible_in_views; for Round 1, just render on the active viewport — cross-pane can be a follow-up.\n\nRegister the new kind in the panel's `_BuiltinKindsScript.register_all` chain (or wherever the CAD panel registers its annotation kinds — see `CADPanel.gd:_ready()` near `BuiltinKinds.register_all`).\n\n### 3. Sidebar rename: \"Annotations\" → \"Edge Markers\"\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd:_build_edge_sidebar` — change the Label text from \"Logical Edges\" to \"Edge Markers\" (or whichever string is currently the header in the sidebar). Also rename any associated identifiers.\n\nThe Tree contents stay as-is for Round 1 (still showing the worker's raw edge enumeration). Round 2 will replace the Tree contents with persistent edge-markers.\n\n### 4. Toolbar icon for edge-number tool\n\nPlatform-side: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationToolbar.gd` (or wherever the toolbar pulls its kind list).\n\nThe annotation toolbar already iterates registered kinds. Once `cad_edge_number` is registered, an icon should appear automatically — verify this path. If not, add the icon explicitly. The tool's UX: click the icon → canvas enters edge-number-add mode → next click in a pane resolves to the nearest edge → annotation created with that edge_id.\n\nFor Round 1 the click-to-add interaction is best-effort. The annotation kind itself + register + render must work.\n\n## Acceptance (Round 1)\n\n1. Open `t_beam.mcad` → no edge numbers visible by default in any pane (only x-ray edge lines in ortho panes; clean iso/perspective).\n2. Programmatically create a `cad_edge_number` annotation against `Cad_AnnotationHost` for edge id 1 → leader+bubble+number renders on the active viewport at edge 1's midpoint. Verifiable via headless test using a fake host with canned mesh + edge data.\n3. Sidebar header reads \"Edge Markers\".\n4. Headless tests pass: build123d not required for any of this.\n\n## Out of scope (Round 2)\n\n- MCP tools (`minerva_cad_annotate_edges`, etc.).\n- Cross-pane annotation rendering.\n- Edge-marker persistence (sidebar Tree contents stay raw for Round 1).\n- Worker tag enrichment for \"inner/outer\" filtering.\n- Responsive drawer for narrow widths.\n- TTL / crash-cleanup of annotations.\n\n## Effort\n\n~3-4 hours, single Sonnet implementer + Opus reviewer. Stop condition 3c (auto-verified via headless tests).","created_at":"2026-04-28T21:22:32","updated_at":"2026-04-28T21:36:38","tags":["annotations","cad","round-1","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b17018bf8856c283aa3fd5","resolution":"Round 1 of CAD UX refactor shipped.\n\nPlugins-side commit (~/github/plugins/cad): f390182\n- mesh_display.gd: strip default Label3D edge callouts; mesh-only render at open\n- ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd (new, 167 lines): AnnotationKind subclass for transient, LLM-driven edge callouts (leader + filled bubble + anchor dot, screen-projected from primitives' point.at)\n- CADPanel.gd: register kind on host's AnnotationRegistry\n- Sidebar header: \"Logical Edges\" → \"Edge Markers\"\n\nMinerva-side commit: 69407efa\n- src/test/test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd (202 lines, 14 PASS / 0 FAIL headless)\n  Covers: kind validate/render/bounds, primitives_optional ownership, registry collision guard, sidebar header regression guard, default-no-labels regression guard.\n\nReviewer (Opus, fresh context): approve_with_notes — both notes applied (orphan MAX_AUTO_LABEL_EDGES doc stripped; TODOs added on retained _render_edge_labels / _build_edge_label_leaders for Round 2 reuse).\n\nAcceptance: 14/14 PASS suite + manual visual confirmation: opening t_beam.mcad shows mesh-only with no auto-emitted edge callouts in any pane; sidebar header reads \"Edge Markers\".\n\nRound 2 (annotation tools surface, edge-marker persistence, drawer responsive, MCP introspection of marked edges) queued under parent DCR 019dd5f871b1."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd63d106070c0826758de8accc9f4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD UX Round 2a: MCP edge-annotation tools + cross-pane render + worker tag enrichment","description":"## Scope (Round 2a of `019dd5f871b1` DCR)\n\nLLM-facing surface only. All three units headless-testable, no UI human-gate. UI/persistence/responsive deferred to Round 2b.\n\n### Unit 1: MCP tools `minerva_cad_annotate_edges` / `minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations`\n\nPlatform-side: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd` (extend existing module).\n\n- `minerva_cad_annotate_edges(editor_name, edge_ids: int[], group_id?: str, label_format?: str)`:\n  - Look up the live `Cad_AnnotationHost` via `AnnotationHostRegistry.get(editor_name)`.\n  - For each edge_id, validate via `host.get_edge(id)`; skip-with-warning unknown ids.\n  - Mint group_id if not provided (UUID-ish).\n  - Add a `cad_edge_number` annotation per edge_id via the host's `Annotations` service: payload `{edge_id, label?, group_id, color?}` and a `point` primitive at `host.get_edge(id).midpoint` so the existing kind renderer (Round 1) picks it up unchanged.\n  - Return `{group_id, created_edge_ids, skipped_unknown_ids}`.\n- `minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations(editor_name, group_id?: str, all?: bool)`:\n  - If `group_id` given, remove all `cad_edge_number` annotations whose payload matches.\n  - If `all=true`, remove all `cad_edge_number` annotations regardless of group.\n  - Reject if neither set (require explicit choice).\n  - Return `{cleared_count}`.\n- Filter dict left as scalar `edge_ids[]` for now; `tags_any` filter lands in Unit 3.\n\nTests: extend `src/test/test_mcp_cad_tools.gd` (existing). Mock host via the same fake-host pattern as `test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd`.\n\n### Unit 2: Cross-pane render for `cad_edge_number`\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd` + `CadAnnotationHost.gd`.\n\nRound 1 renders only on the active viewport. Spec calls for visibility in every pane that contains the edge in `visible_in_views`. Implementation:\n- Read `visible_in_views` from the annotation's metadata (set by author UI eventually; for MCP-created annotations, default to `[\"iso\", \"front\", \"right\", \"top\"]`).\n- During `render()`, the kind already has access to the AnnotationCanvas and host. The host knows which viewport (`Camera3D`) is associated with each pane. Iterate panes; for each pane whose name is in `visible_in_views`, project edge midpoint via that pane's camera and emit a leader+bubble in the corresponding canvas region.\n- The current platform `AnnotationCanvas` sits per-AnnotationHost — confirm that \"host has access to all 4 panes' cameras\" is wired or wire it. (CADPanel owns the 4 SubViewports already.)\n\nTests: extend `src/test/test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd`. Mock fake host with 4 cameras, assert render emits 4 leaders for an annotation visible in all panes; emits 1 leader for `visible_in_views=[\"iso\"]`.\n\n### Unit 3: Worker tag enrichment\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/internal/worker/` (Python build123d worker process) + `manifest.json` IPC contract.\n\n- The worker's `evaluate` response currently emits per-edge `{id, midpoint, ...}`. Extend with `tags: string[]` containing one or more of: `inner`, `outer`, `feature`, `cylindrical`, `linear`. Inner/outer determined by edge-loop classification (build123d's `Wire.is_outer()` or equivalent). Feature edges are those incident to ≥2 faces with non-coplanar normals (build123d edge filtering).\n- Update the IPC contract in `manifest.json` if the response schema is documented there.\n- Surface `tags` on the host's `EdgeData` so MCP can read them: `MCPCadTools.minerva_cad_list_edges_live(editor_name)` already returns edges; include `tags` in the per-edge dict.\n- Extend `minerva_cad_annotate_edges` from Unit 1 with optional filter param: `tags_any: string[]` — when given, server resolves edge_ids by intersecting \"all live edges\" with edges whose tags overlap `tags_any`.\n\nTests:\n- Worker-side: Python unit test on tag classification (use a known build123d primitive — box, cylinder — assert outer wire of top face is tagged correctly).\n- Platform-side: extend `test_mcp_cad_tools.gd` with mock host that returns tagged edges; assert `tags_any` filter returns the right subset.\n\n## Acceptance (Round 2a)\n\n1. Headless `test_mcp_cad_tools.gd` passes. Should include:\n   - `annotate_edges([1,2])` creates 2 annotations, returns `group_id`.\n   - `clear_edge_annotations(group_id=X)` removes only those.\n   - `clear_edge_annotations(all=true)` removes all `cad_edge_number` annotations.\n   - `annotate_edges` with `tags_any=[\"outer\"]` and a mocked tag-rich host creates the right subset.\n   - Skipped-unknown-ids surfaces correctly.\n2. Headless `test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd` passes with cross-pane test additions.\n3. Worker-side Python test passes (`pytest` in worker dir).\n\n## Out of scope (Round 2b)\n\n- Toolbar click-to-add edge-number canvas mode.\n- Edge-marker persistent domain control (sidebar Tree replacement, save-state ride).\n- Responsive drawer for narrow widths.\n- TTL / crash-cleanup of dangling group annotations.\n- Annotation move/edit UX.\n\n## Effort\n\n~6-8 hours total across 3 parallel Sonnet implementers + Opus reviewers per unit. Stop condition 3c (auto-verified via headless tests). No human gate.","created_at":"2026-04-28T22:36:59","updated_at":"2026-04-28T23:26:28","tags":["annotations","cad","mcp","round-2a","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1","resolution":"Round 2a of CAD UX refactor shipped. Stop condition 3c (auto-verified, no human gate).\n\nPlugins-side commit (~/github/plugins/cad): dd644a0\n- ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd: 3D-vs-2D detection on `at[]`, multi-pane render via host.get_panes(), visible_in_views filter (default 4 panes).\n- ui/CadAnnotationHost.gd: get_panes() / set_camera_for() / WIDE_PANE_IDS, narrow-mode SubViewport dedup.\n- ui/CADPanel.gd: registers Camera3D per pane in both layout modes.\n- ui/CadAnnotationCanvas.gd: wires ctx.host post create().\n- worker/mcad/translator.py: both edge-emission paths now populate semantic tags (linear/circular/curve + corner/rim/chamfer_connector + feature + outer/inner). Spatial tags retained on extruded path via set-dedup merge.\n- worker/mcad/tests/test_edge_tags.py: 29 new tests.\n\nMinerva-side commit: 2edf7e93\n- src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd: minerva_cad_annotate_edges + minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations. Mutually exclusive selectors (edge_ids xor tags_any), group_id mint, annotation envelope matches cad_edge_number_kind's contract.\n- src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationRenderContext.gd: optional host:Object=null field; safe-read pattern documented.\n- Tests: test_mcp_cad_tools.gd extended (98 PASS), test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd tightened with _CapturingCtx (34 PASS).\n\nReviewers (Opus cold context, per unit): all approve.\n\nLayer-1 totals: 98 + 34 + 366 = 498 passed, 0 failed.\n\nReviewer notes applied inline:\n- AnnotationRenderContext.host documented with safe-read pattern.\n- test_render_uses_each_pane_camera now captures actual ctx.draw_line anchors via _CapturingCtx subclass.\n\nFollow-ups filed (deferred):\n- 019dd65c237d (bug, sev2): multi-pane leaders draw in iso quadrant only — canvas parented to iso. Workaround: MCP tool defaults visible_in_views=[\"iso\"]. Fix bundled with Round 2b CADPanel layout work.\n- 019dd64376917705 (bug, sev3): _edge_midpoint silently returns [0,0,0] for non-straight/non-circle edges (arc, spline). Couple with future curve-aware worker work.\n- 019dd669112a7bd2 (chore, sev4): add edge-tag-content tests for fillet/chamfer outputs (existing tests verify volume only)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd64376917705a4bdee58a94e22ae","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"MCPCadTools._edge_midpoint silently returns [0,0,0] for non-straight/non-circle edges","description":"Surfaced during Unit 1 review of CAD UX Round 2a (commit pending).\n\n`MCPCadTools.gd::_edge_midpoint(edge)` handles two edge types:\n- straight: `(start + end) / 2`\n- circle: `center`\n\nFor build123d-emitted arc, spline, or other edge kinds, it falls through to `[0, 0, 0]`. The annotation kind renders the leader+bubble at the origin instead of the actual midpoint — silently incorrect, no warning.\n\nRepro (theoretical, once such edges land in the worker):\n- Worker emits edge with `kind=\"spline\"` or similar.\n- LLM calls `minerva_cad_annotate_edges(editor_name, edge_ids=[that_id])`.\n- Annotation appears at world origin instead of on the edge.\n\nExpected: either compute a sensible midpoint (parameter-space midpoint sample for parametric curves) or return an error / skip the edge with a logged warning.\n\nFix sketch:\n- Worker side: emit a precomputed `midpoint: [x,y,z]` field on each edge regardless of kind. Then `_edge_midpoint` becomes a one-liner.\n- OR: handle each curve type explicitly with a sample.\n\nCoupled with worker tag enrichment (Unit 3 of Round 2a, task 019dd63d106070c0826758de8accc9f4) — could be batched there.","created_at":"2026-04-28T22:43:58","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:21:45","severity":3,"tags":["cad","follow-up","mcp","round-2a"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1","resolution":"Fixed in commit 59686550: MCPCadTools._edge_midpoint prefers worker-emitted parametric midpoint. Worker emits {midpoint} for all edge kinds; cad_edge_number_tool consumes via cad_edge_number_tool.gd:194-196."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd65c237d7c61b57e45430e57bcf3","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Multi-pane cad_edge_number leaders draw in wrong quadrant (canvas parented to iso only)","description":"Surfaced during Unit 2 cold review of CAD UX Round 2a (commit pending).\n\n## Symptom\n\nWhen a `cad_edge_number` annotation has `visible_in_views=[\"iso\", \"front\", \"top\", \"right\"]` (default) and the panel is in wide mode, the kind correctly projects the edge midpoint via each pane's `Camera3D.unproject_position()` — but all 4 leaders draw into the iso quadrant's overlay because the AnnotationCanvas is parented only to the iso `SubViewport` (`CADPanel._reparent_canvas`).\n\nResult: leader 1 draws at iso's correct screen position; leaders 2-4 draw inside iso's quadrant at the screen positions that top/front/right cameras computed for that same world point — visually nonsense.\n\n## Why headless tests didn't catch it\n\n`test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd` uses `_MockHost` with no real canvas parent; the `_MockCamera` returns canned screen coords. The test verifies \"each camera was called\" and \"different cameras → different screen coords\", which is true at the kind level. The bug is in the canvas / panel composition layer, which the unit tests don't simulate.\n\nA real CADPanel boot + visual confirmation is the only way to catch this. The MCP smoke loop (`019dd20442e0` style, with `minerva_cad_annotate_edges`) would also surface it via screenshots.\n\n## Fix options\n\nA. Re-parent AnnotationCanvas to the CADPanel root (above all 4 SubViewports) and include `viewport_offset` (or `viewport_rect: Rect2`) in the per-pane dict returned by `Cad_AnnotationHost.get_panes()`. The kind's `_render_multi_pane()` adds the offset to each unprojected screen coord before calling `ctx.draw_*`.\n\nB. Per-pane AnnotationCanvas instance (one canvas inside each SubViewport). Architecturally cleaner but bigger — affects existing 2D screen-space annotations (arrows, text) that were authored against single-canvas semantics.\n\n## Why this is deferred\n\nRound 2b already touches `CADPanel` layout for the responsive drawer (Edge Markers narrow-width collapse). Bundle this fix with that work — the canvas restructuring is a natural part of the layout pass. In the meantime, the tools remain functional with `visible_in_views=[\"iso\"]` (single-pane render lands in the iso quadrant correctly).\n\n## Workaround (Round 2a-shipped state)\n\nLLM clients calling `minerva_cad_annotate_edges` should pass `visible_in_views=[\"iso\"]` until this is fixed. Default behavior (4 panes) is broken.\n\n## Trigger for Round 2b\n\nPick this up alongside `019dd5f871b1`'s Round 2b drawer/layout work.","created_at":"2026-04-28T23:10:55","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:21:48","severity":2,"tags":["annotations","cad","follow-up","round-2b","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1","resolution":"Multi-pane callout positioning fixed: cad_edge_number_kind._render_multi_pane (kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd:138-185) consumes host.get_panes() and offsets by viewport_rect.position. Substrate-resolved edge anchors deferred to Phase B work item — this only covered the wrong-quadrant projection bug."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd669112a7bd2abd930a8a3eb1186","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Add edge-tag-content tests for fillet/chamfer outputs","description":"Surfaced during Unit 3 cold review of CAD UX Round 2a.\n\n`worker/mcad/tests/test_edge_tags.py` covers role-driven tags on raw primitives + boolean (cube - cylinder). It does NOT verify that fillet/chamfer outputs produce `chamfer_connector` + `feature` tags via the generic `_enumerate_edges` path.\n\nExisting `test_translator.py` exercises fillet/chamfer only for volume/success, not tag content.\n\nAdd a test that:\n1. Builds a Box, applies a chamfer to one edge.\n2. Asserts at least one resulting edge has `tags` containing both `chamfer_connector` and `feature`.\n3. Repeats for fillet → expects `rim` + `feature` (or whatever the role classifier returns).\n\nTrivial follow-up; ~20 min.","created_at":"2026-04-28T23:25:03","updated_at":"2026-04-28T23:25:03","severity":4,"tags":["cad","follow-up","tests","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd696f82772118a7fb241e2224497","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD UX Round 2b-α: canvas reparent + click-to-add edge-number authoring","description":"## Scope (Round 2b-α of 019dd5f871b1)\n\nUI-bearing cycle. Stop condition 3b (plugin manual verify) at end. Two units sequenced, single HITL.\n\n### Unit 1: Canvas reparent + multi-pane rect (resolves 019dd65c237d)\n\nPlugin-side: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd`, `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationHost.gd`, `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationCanvas.gd`, `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd`.\n\nPlatform-side: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd` (remove the visible_in_views=[\"iso\"] workaround).\n\nRound 2a's cross-pane render projects via each pane's Camera3D correctly, but the AnnotationCanvas is parented to the iso quadrant only — leaders for top/front/right draw in iso's local coord space at wrong screen positions.\n\nFix:\n- Re-parent AnnotationCanvas to a Control above all 4 SubViewports (panel root or dedicated overlay node).\n- Extend `Cad_AnnotationHost.get_panes()` to return `[{name, camera, viewport_rect: Rect2}]` instead of `viewport_size`. The Rect2 is the pane's screen-space rectangle within the panel root.\n- In `cad_edge_number_kind._render_multi_pane`: after `camera.unproject_position(world_pos)`, add `pane.viewport_rect.position` to translate into the panel-root canvas coord system.\n- Confirm narrow-mode (single SubViewport reused across panes) still computes correct rects — likely just one rect covering the visible quadrant.\n- Drop `metadata.visible_in_views = [\"iso\"]` from `MCPCadTools._cad_annotate_edges`. Multi-pane render now produces correct visuals.\n\nTests:\n- `test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd`: extend `_MockHost` panes to include `viewport_rect`. Add tests verifying captured anchors include the rect's offset (e.g. iso pane at (0,0,400,300) projects to anchor + (0,0); front pane at (400,0,400,300) projects to anchor + (400,0)). Goal: 38+ PASS.\n- `test_mcp_cad_tools.gd`: assert annotations no longer have the iso workaround metadata. 98 PASS preserved.\n- All Layer-1 suites green.\n\n### Unit 2: Click-to-add edge-number authoring\n\nPlugin-side: new `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/tools/cad_edge_number_tool.gd` (AnnotationAuthorTool subclass), wire into AnnotationToolbar via the kind's `author_ui()` method (currently returns null in Round 1 stub).\n\nPlatform-side: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationToolbar.gd` (verify it iterates registered kinds and picks up the new tool's icon automatically; if not, register the icon).\n\nBehavior:\n- Toolbar icon: pencil-with-numbered-circle (or similar). Lives next to existing arrow/text/etc. icons.\n- Click icon → AnnotationCanvas enters edge-number-add mode.\n- Next click in any pane → ray-pick to find nearest visible edge. Use the host's edge registry (`get_edge_registry()`) to enumerate edge midpoints; pick the edge whose midpoint, when projected through that pane's camera, is closest to the click position in screen space.\n- Annotation created via `host.add_annotation({kind: \"cad_edge_number\", payload: {edge_id, label}, primitives: [{type: point, at: midpoint}], metadata: {visible_in_views: [pane_name]}})`. Visible in the clicked pane only by default.\n- ESC or click on toolbar again exits the mode.\n\nTests:\n- `test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd` (extend): mock the click-resolution helper if it's pulled out as a static function. Verify a click at a known screen position resolves to the expected edge_id given a canned set of edges + camera.\n- HITL: open t_beam.mcad, click the edge-number tool icon, click on an edge in iso → annotation appears.\n\n### Acceptance (whole cycle, HITL)\n\n1. Open `t_beam.mcad`. No edge numbers visible by default.\n2. Via MCP: `minerva_cad_annotate_edges(editor_name, edge_ids=[1,2,3])`. Three leaders appear, one per edge, in EVERY pane that shows that edge (default visible_in_views = all 4). Visually correct positions in each pane.\n3. Via MCP: `minerva_cad_clear_edge_annotations(group_id=...)`. Leaders disappear.\n4. Click the edge-number toolbar icon. Canvas enters add mode (cursor change or visual indicator).\n5. Click on an edge in iso pane. Annotation appears at that edge.\n6. Click the icon again or press ESC. Add mode exits.\n7. Headless suites all PASS (98 + 38+ + 366 = 502+ PASS).\n\n### Out of scope (Round 2b-β)\n\n- Edge-marker persistent domain control (sidebar Tree replacement, save-state ride, MCP markers tools).\n- Responsive drawer for Edge Markers panel.\n- Annotation TTL / crash-cleanup of dangling group annotations.\n- Worker mesh-change detection.\n\n### Effort\n\n~5–7 hours total. 2 sequential Sonnet implementers + Opus reviewers per unit. Stop condition 3b.","created_at":"2026-04-29T00:15:11","updated_at":"2026-04-29T23:04:08","tags":["annotations","cad","canvas","click-to-add","round-2b","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1","resolution":"Pivoted to v2 substrate (019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1). Foundational work shipped (canvas reparent, viewport_rect math, worker tag enrichment, MCP midpoint fix); click-to-add tool (Unit 2) absorbed into v2 author-tool contract. See comment #258."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd71604b97dbbbd75053d15e0e8d0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAD UX Round 2b-α v2: rebuild edge-number tool against edge_overlay.edge_selected","description":"## Context\n\nHITL of the first Round 2b-α click-to-add tool surfaced two issues:\n1. Modal lockup — the tool stayed active across all clicks, blocking orbit/pan/edge-select.\n2. The tool reimplemented nearest-edge-pick but the existing `edge_overlay.gd` per-pane system already has working blue-hover-highlight + nearest-edge math + `edge_selected(edge_id)` signal.\n\nUser wants: **leverage the existing system, don't reimplement.**\n\n## Scope\n\nRebuild `cad_edge_number_tool.gd` to:\n\n1. **On activate**: leave the canvas in `MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE` so clicks fall through to `edge_overlay.gd` per-pane. Connect to every per-pane EdgeOverlay's `edge_selected` signal.\n2. **User flow**: hovering → blue highlight (existing). Clicking on a hovered edge → `edge_overlay` emits `edge_selected(edge_id)` → tool catches it → builds annotation via host (using `edge_dict[\"midpoint\"]` from registry).\n3. **One-shot semantics** (user choice \"i\" — deactivate immediately after click): annotation created → tool emits `cancelled` (toolbar untoggles) → tool disconnects edge_selected handlers → idle.\n4. **Cancel paths**: ESC, right-click, click on empty area (no `edge_selected` fires within ~500 ms) → cancel without placing.\n5. **Optional label-input popup**: small floating LineEdit appears at the bubble after the click, focused, accepting Enter to commit / ESC to cancel just the label (annotation still placed with default str(edge_id) label if user dismisses without typing). Toggle this on/off via a tool option — default ON.\n\n## Files affected\n\nPlugin-side:\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/tools/cad_edge_number_tool.gd` — full rewrite (~150 lines).\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationCanvas.gd` — `set_active_tool()` no longer needs to flip mouse_filter (canvas can stay IGNORE; the tool listens to `edge_selected` not `_gui_input`). Verify nothing else depends on the STOP state.\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd` — may need to expose per-pane edge_overlays to the tool via host (e.g. `host.get_edge_overlays() -> Array[EdgeOverlay]`).\n\nPlatform-side: probably none.\n\nTest rewrite:\n- `src/test/test_cad_edge_number_tool.gd` — drop the click-resolution tests (no longer relevant since we use edge_overlay's resolution); add tests that simulate `edge_selected` emission and verify annotation envelope.\n\n## Acceptance (HITL)\n\n1. Open `t_beam.mcad`. Activate edge-number tool. Blue hover-highlight still works on edges in any pane.\n2. Click a hovered edge → annotation appears at that edge's midpoint with label = str(edge_id), tool deactivates immediately, orbit/pan resume.\n3. Activate, ESC → tool deactivates without placing.\n4. Activate, click on empty pane area (no edge under cursor) → tool stays active OR deactivates per the chosen UX (decide during impl; default: deactivate).\n5. With label-input popup ON: after edge click, popup appears at bubble, typing changes label, Enter commits, ESC reverts to default label.\n6. Headless tests pass.\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3b (HITL) — same loop as Round 2b-α v1 had.","created_at":"2026-04-29T02:33:57","updated_at":"2026-04-29T23:04:38","tags":["annotations","cad","rework","round-2b","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1","resolution":"Cancelled per discussion 0bd63d76bb70 comments #248 #249 #250. Would rebuild around v1 cad_edge_number kind, which v2 substrate (019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1) reframes as a viewport overlay (not annotation). See comment #257."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dd7162d52789fb055f62c1753e694","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"edge_overlay.gd hover-highlight randomly stops working","description":"Reported during Round 2b-α HITL. The blue edge-on-hover highlight in `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/scripts/edge_overlay.gd` works most of the time, but \"the effect randomly stops working\" mid-session.\n\n## Likely causes (untriaged)\n\n- Signal disconnection on width-class switch (`_apply_width_class` reparents containers; existing connections on the pane's `EdgeOverlayRoot` may drop).\n- Focus loss: `_gui_input` only fires on Controls that have mouse_focus or have not been pre-empted by another Control. After Round 2b-α landed the panel-root canvas overlay (z_index=1, currently MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE), this should NOT block hover, but worth confirming.\n- `_hovered_edge_ids` set/cleared in code paths that have reentrancy issues — check if mouse-leave events clear it correctly when the overlay's own area shrinks.\n- Possible bug in `_edge_ids_at_position` returning stale results during model evaluations.\n\n## Next steps\n\n1. Reproduce by toggling between projection presets / wide-narrow modes.\n2. Add per-event diagnostic prints in `edge_overlay.gd` and `_gui_input`/`_input` paths.\n3. Watch for unexpected `_hovered_edge_ids.clear()` calls.\n\nThis bug should be investigated AFTER `019dd6c4b6XX` (Round 2b-α v2 tool rebuild) lands — that rebuild may inadvertently fix it by rationalizing input routing.","created_at":"2026-04-29T02:34:08","updated_at":"2026-04-29T02:34:08","severity":3,"tags":["cad","edge-overlay","follow-up","hover","input"],"parent":"minerva:019dd5f871b1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"First-class editor annotations: action-intent envelope + plugin semantic anchors","description":"## Why\n\nPCBEditor was the first place annotations worked, and they worked because PCB built bespoke: bus hints, route hints, component notes — structured LLM-actionable intent attached to board entities. That pattern is the proof-of-value: an annotation is not a pixel, it is the structured input to a tool/LLM action. PCB hints become real traces when the LLM applies them. CAD edge notes (\"fillet this by 3mm\") become DSL edits. A WYSIWYG word-processor's anchored comment (\"rephrase this paragraph\") becomes a text edit.\n\nWhen CAD tried to express its semantically-equivalent thing (edge actions) through the existing v1 substrate (DCR `019dc0543da572`), it didn't work. Round 2b-α produced a flurry of UX symptoms — modal-tool lockup, anchor drift after geometry re-evaluation, four code paths competing for \"where is edge N,\" random hover-highlight failure — that all traced back to the same shape: **the v1 envelope is `kind + 2D-primitive-snapshot`, with no semantic anchor, no action-intent lifecycle, and no resolver hook.** PCB worked because PCB built around it; everywhere else it forces every plugin to rebuild the same machinery.\n\n## Reframe\n\nThis DCR replaces the v1 substrate's \"annotations as 2D primitives with metadata\" frame with **annotations as structured action-intent**:\n\n1. **Typed semantic anchor.** `{plugin, type, id, snapshot}` — not a `[x,y,z]` snapshot. Resolution at draw time, snapshot as fallback for broken anchors.\n2. **Lifecycle as action-status.** `open / applied / resolved / stale` — the LLM and the user both need to see whether intent has been acted on.\n3. **Summary as canonical LLM-readable form.** Non-multimodal LLMs are first-class; the `summary` field IS the action prompt, not a fallback.\n4. **Built-in editors count.** The text editor's selection-anchored comment (MS-Word style) is as much a substrate use case as PCB's bus hint or CAD's edge note. The smoke consumer for round 1 is the built-in text editor with a `core/text.range` anchor — not CAD or PCB.\n5. **Plugin-scoped anchor registry.** Plugins register `(anchor_type → resolver/validator/repair)`. Substrate dispatches; substrate never knows what a CAD edge or PCB net is.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe new substrate ships these surfaces:\n\n- v2 envelope: typed `anchor`, `payload`, `status`, `author`, `summary`, `view_context`/`visible_in_views`, `schema_version: 2`\n- `AnnotationAnchorRegistry` (plugin-scoped, parallel to `AnnotationRegistry` for kinds)\n- Base `AnnotationHost.resolve_anchor(anchor)` and `anchor_screen_rect(anchor, view_context)` virtuals\n- Broken/stale anchor render + sidebar + MCP + chat surfaces\n- Resolve cache with revision-counter invalidation; ≤1ms/resolve target\n- Generic kinds carry built-in anchors; plugin-typed anchors require plugin-defined kinds\n- Capability-aware `to_chat_context` returning typed blocks (structured_json IS the action contract)\n- MCP query (`status`/`anchor_type`/`kind`/`author`/`text`/`time_range`) + `update_status` for lifecycle transitions\n- v1 migration: `cad_edge_number` records dropped, generic v1 (arrow/text/region) upgraded with empty anchor\n- Plugin trust boundary: render/resolver/apply-tool fail-containment with rate-limiting\n- Contract tests covering every above surface; text-editor smoke consumer demonstrating `core/text.range` MS-Word-style anchored comments\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **PCB-as-plugin extraction** — its own DCR `019dc14029197`. PCB adopts v2 contract when its roadmap allows.\n- **CAD plugin adoption** — working CAD lives elsewhere; CAD plugin adopts v2 on its own roadmap. Substrate ships first.\n- **Real-time collaboration / merge** — single-user assumption; future hardening.\n- **Encryption / redaction** — sidecar storage location; future hardening.\n- **Multi-agent author conflict resolution** — primarily an LLM-context-management problem.\n- **Bulk operations** (\"apply all PCB hints,\" \"GC broken anchors\") — acknowledged; deferred to subsequent DCRs.\n\n## Supersession\n\nThis DCR supersedes:\n\n- v1 DCR `019dc0543da572` \"Cross-editor annotation subsystem\" — the abstraction was insufficient; v2 keeps the shipped pieces (Schema, AnnotationKind registry, AnnotationHost protocol, hello-plugin smoke) as transitional code that v2 migration replaces.\n- CAD edge-tools DCR `019dd5f871b1` — encoded the wrong direction (edge numbers as annotations). Specific shipped work (Round 1 cad_edge_number kind, Round 2a MCP edge-annotation tools, midpoint correctness fix `59686550`) stays valid as transitional and is replaced under v2 migration task.\n\n## Acceptance (whole DCR)\n\n1. v2 envelope shipped with all 11 work-item children green.\n2. Text-editor smoke consumer: user selects text range → \"Add comment\" → annotation persists through save/load with `core/text.range` anchor → LLM reads via MCP query → LLM marks applied → status visible in UI.\n3. Broken-anchor case: user deletes anchored text range → annotation renders stale at snapshot, surfaces in sidebar with repair affordance, MCP returns `stale: true`.\n4. Plugin trust boundary: throwing plugin renderer/resolver does not crash the editor; rate-limit kicks in after N throws.\n5. v1 records: `cad_edge_number` sidecars drop cleanly; generic arrow/text/region sidecars upgrade in place.\n6. Contract tests for all surfaces green; smoke flow green.\n\n## Discussion source\n\n`0bd63d76bb70` — \"Annotation substrate refactor: generic callouts plus plugin semantic anchors\" (3 comments: #248 reservations, #249 codex consolidated reframe, #250 Tier-1/2/3 decision map). The discussion description plus comments #249 and #250 carry the load-bearing schema and decision content; this DCR distills them.\n\n## Round structure\n\nThe plan child of this DCR (`Annotation v2 substrate plan`) sequences 11 work items across 5 rounds:\n\n1. Round 1 — contract tests + smoke consumer (red, START HERE)\n2. Round 2 — envelope + anchor registry + resolve_anchor (parallel)\n3. Round 3 — cache + trust boundary + broken anchor UX (parallel)\n4. Round 4 — capability-aware chat + built-in/plugin kind split + MCP query\n5. Round 5 — text-editor smoke consumer wiring (HITL stop)\n\nv1 migration slots into round 3-4. Each round's tasks are independently work-cyclable.","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:51:00","updated_at":"2026-04-30T00:56:01","priority":2,"tags":["action-intent","annotations","platform","semantic-anchors","substrate","v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb70ca457646b0cc9bbdf72a4999","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Annotation v2 substrate (action-intent + semantic anchors)","description":"Sequenced implementation plan for v2 substrate. Each child is self-contained — readable cold by an implementer with no session history. Tier-1 schema decisions (anchor object shape, resolve return shape, generic-kind-vs-plugin-kind policy, anchor registry model, broken-anchor UX taxonomy) are committed in the children's bodies, not deferred.\n\n## Round structure\n\n| Round | Tasks | Stop |\n|-------|-------|------|\n| 1 (START HERE) | Contract tests + smoke consumer (red tests for every other task + text-editor smoke wiring contract) | 3c (auto-verified) |\n| 2 | Envelope + Anchor registry + resolve_anchor contract (parallel) | 3c |\n| 3 | Resolve cache + Trust boundary + Broken anchor UX (parallel) | 3c |\n| 4 | Capability-aware to_chat_context + Built-in/plugin kinds split + MCP query/update + v1 migration (parallel) | 3c |\n| 5 | Smoke consumer wiring (text editor end-to-end) | 3b (HITL — user verifies \"select text → add comment → LLM marks applied\" round-trip) |\n\nv1 migration may slot earlier than round 4 if tests force it. MCP query/update may slot in round 3 if the broken-anchor UX needs the MCP surface earlier.\n\n## Tier-1 decisions (committed; do not relitigate)\n\nThese are settled in the child task bodies. Documented here for reviewer context:\n\n1. **Anchor object schema.** `{plugin: String, type: String, id: Variant, stable_key?: String, snapshot: Dictionary}`. `plugin` always required (use `\"core\"` for built-in anchors like `core/text.range`). `id` is Variant (int / string / array for multi-element anchors). `snapshot` is schema-free per anchor type but substrate requires at minimum a `position` field for fallback render.\n2. **resolve_anchor return.** `{position: Vector2|Vector3, bounds: Rect2, stale: bool, view_metadata: Dictionary}`. Return type is per-pane 2D screen-space; substrate stays projection-agnostic. Plugins handle 3D-to-2D internally and surface per-pane positions via `view_metadata`. This is the substrate's biggest decision — locks the substrate renderer into pure 2D, plugins own all camera/projection logic.\n3. **Generic kinds with plugin anchors.** Path B: anchors typed by `plugin` field. `plugin: \"core\"` anchors (`text.range`, `graphics.region`, `text.selection`) compose with generic kinds (text, arrow, region, polyline). Plugin anchors (`plugin: \"cad\"`, `plugin: \"pcb\"`) require plugin-defined kinds with task-specific payloads — generic kinds reject them at validation.\n4. **Anchor registry model.** Plugin-scoped, parallel to `AnnotationRegistry` for kinds. Each plugin registers its own anchor types; substrate validates anchor shape on add via the registered validator. No cross-plugin anchor resolution.\n5. **Broken-anchor UX.** Render at snapshot with stale indicator (color shift + small badge). Sidebar surfaces broken via filter and a \"Broken (N)\" section. MCP includes `stale: true` in default list (not opt-in). User repair flow is plugin-defined; substrate provides the affordance hook.\n\n## Tier-2 decisions (drafted in child bodies)\n\n6. Lifecycle: `open / applied / resolved / stale`. Default `open`. Apply-tool transitions to `applied`; user can mark `resolved`; substrate transitions to `stale` when resolution fails. Transitions are one-way except `resolved → open` (user reopen).\n7. Author identity: `author: {kind: \"human\"|\"ai\", id?: String, model?: String, session_id?: String}`. All fields except `kind` may be null today.\n8. `view_context` (immutable, where authored) vs `visible_in_views` (editable, where shown). Different semantics; rename for clarity.\n9. Capability schema for `to_chat_context`: `{multimodal: bool, max_tokens: int, supported_block_types: [\"text\", \"structured_json\", \"image\", \"source_code\", \"table\", \"unsupported_placeholder\"]}`.\n10. Plugin trust contract: render/resolver may not mutate document; apply-tool may, transactionally with dry-run + commit + undo-snapshot. Throwing plugin: catch and rate-limit (suspend after N throws in M seconds).\n11. v1 coexistence: load-time per-kind migrator; refuse v1 with no migrator registered. `cad_edge_number` migrator drops; generic kinds (arrow/text/region) upgrade with empty anchor.\n\n## Children (11)\n\nCreated as separate work items under this plan. Listed in pickup-doc order.","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:51:35","updated_at":"2026-04-30T00:56:02","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","plan","substrate","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb704374"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb7593467eefa4c982d0add2b7a6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Annotation v2 envelope: typed anchor, payload, lifecycle, author, summary","description":"## Goal\n\nDefine the v2 annotation envelope. Replaces v1's `{kind, primitives: [{type, at:[x,y,z]}], metadata}` shape with a structure where the **typed anchor** is first-class, the **lifecycle** is the action-status, and the **summary** is the canonical LLM-readable form.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nThe annotation IS the structured input to a tool/LLM action — not a comment about it. PCB bus hints, CAD edge notes, and text-editor anchored comments are all of that form: \"do X to the thing identified by anchor Y, with optional supporting payload Z, currently in lifecycle state S.\" Non-multimodal LLMs read the structured fields directly; multimodal LLMs supplement with rendering. The envelope must support both without privileging pixels.\n\n## Envelope literal\n\n```gdscript\n# Canonical v2 envelope shape (Dictionary)\n{\n    \"id\": \"ann_<ulid>\",                    # required, substrate-assigned\n    \"kind\": \"cad_edge_note\",               # required, registered AnnotationKind\n    \"schema_version\": 2,                   # required, integer, rejects unknown\n    \"anchor\": {                            # required (use core anchors for unanchored callouts)\n        \"plugin\": \"cad\",                   # required — \"core\" for built-ins\n        \"type\": \"edge\",                    # required — registered with plugin\n        \"id\": 2,                           # Variant — int/string/array for multi-element\n        \"stable_key\": null,                # optional — opaque, plugin-owned\n        \"snapshot\": {                      # required — at minimum {\"position\": ...}\n            \"position\": [10.0, 4.0, 0.0],\n            \"tags\": [\"inner\", \"feature\"],\n            \"source_plane\": \"XY\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"anchored_to\": \"cad.edge:2\",           # COMPUTED accessor; not stored. Derived from anchor on read.\n    \"kind_payload\": {                      # required, kind-specific. Validated by AnnotationKind.validate_payload().\n        \"text\": \"Fillet this edge by 3mm\",\n        \"label_offset\": [28, -28]\n    },\n    \"lifecycle\": \"open\",                   # required enum: open / applied / resolved / stale\n    \"author\": {                            # required object\n        \"kind\": \"human\",                   # enum: human / ai\n        \"id\": null,                        # optional string\n        \"model\": null,                     # optional string (AI only)\n        \"session_id\": null                 # optional string\n    },\n    \"view_context\": \"cad:iso\",             # required string, immutable, where authored\n    \"visible_in_views\": [\"iso\", \"top\"],    # required array, editable, where shown\n    \"summary\": \"CAD edge note on edge 2: Fillet this edge by 3mm.\",  # required, LLM-readable action prompt\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-04-29T17:00:00Z\",  # substrate-assigned\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-04-29T17:00:00Z\",  # substrate-updated\n    \"applied\": null,                       # optional object: {at: ts, by: author, links: [{kind, id}]}\n    \"resolved\": null                       # optional object: {at: ts, by: author, note?: str}\n}\n```\n\n## Lifecycle state machine\n\n```\nopen ──(apply tool)──> applied ──(user verifies)──> resolved\n  │                       │\n  └──(user resolve)─> resolved\n  │\n  └──(anchor breaks)─> stale ──(repair)──> open\n                        │\n                        └──(user delete)─> [deleted]\n\nresolved ──(user reopen)──> open   # only legal back-transition\n```\n\nApply-tool sets `applied` object (timestamp, author, optional links to created entities — e.g. created PCB traces). User-resolve sets `resolved` object. Substrate transitions to `stale` automatically when `resolve_anchor` returns `stale: true` and the kind opts into auto-stale (default off — most kinds prefer to render stale-at-snapshot rather than transition).\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationSchema.gd` — replace v1 schema with v2; v1 helpers become migration-only (see task #9).\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — add `validate_payload(payload) -> Array[String]` (returns errors); `summary(annotation) -> String` becomes required (default returns kind display_name + ` on ` + summary-of-anchor).\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationLifecycle.gd` — enum + transition validator.\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationAuthor.gd` — author shape validator.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnnotationSchema.validate(envelope)` accepts every field above with correct types; rejects with explicit error path for each missing-required and each wrong-type case.\n2. `schema_version: 2` accepted; v1 (`schema_version: 1` or absent + `at:[x,y,z]` shape) rejected by v2 validator (migration is task #9's job, not the validator's).\n3. `anchored_to` is a computed accessor — round-trip through serialize/deserialize never persists it; serializer strips, deserializer recomputes.\n4. Lifecycle transitions enforced by `AnnotationLifecycle.can_transition(from, to)` per the state machine above. Illegal transitions raise.\n5. `summary` is required on add; substrate refuses envelopes with empty summary. Kind's `summary()` provides default; user/LLM can override.\n6. `author.kind` required and validated; other author fields optional.\n7. `view_context` immutable post-creation; `visible_in_views` mutable.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd` — every required-field combination, every type-error path, computed `anchored_to`, lifecycle state machine (legal + illegal transitions), author validation, view_context immutability.\n- Round-trip: serialize → JSON → deserialize → equality, with `anchored_to` re-derived.\n- Integration with kind registry: validate_payload errors surfaced, summary defaults applied.\n\n## Related\n\n- Sibling: anchor registry (#2), resolve_anchor (#3), migration (#9)\n- Reviewer: this task touches every other task. Get envelope shape right first; Round 2's other items build on it.","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:56:48","updated_at":"2026-04-30T00:48:24","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","envelope","schema","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb75ffad7dd8a83282a5b6bcc2b1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin-scoped anchor registry (validate / summary / repair)","description":"## Goal\n\nAdd `AnnotationAnchorRegistry` parallel to `AnnotationRegistry`. Plugins register anchor types they own; the registry validates anchor shape on annotation add, generates per-anchor summaries, and dispatches repair attempts.\n\n## Why\n\nThe substrate must never know what a CAD edge or PCB net is, but it must validate that a given annotation's anchor is well-formed before storing it. The registry is the dispatch table: `(plugin, anchor_type) → {validate, summary, repair}`. Per-plugin scoping means a buggy CAD anchor type can't corrupt PCB annotations.\n\n## Contract\n\n```gdscript\n# src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationAnchorRegistry.gd\n\nclass_name AnnotationAnchorRegistry extends RefCounted\n\n# Resolver protocol — plugins implement and register\nclass AnchorResolver:\n    # Required: validate anchor shape. Returns array of error strings (empty = valid).\n    func validate(anchor: Dictionary) -> Array: return []\n    # Required: human/LLM-readable summary string for this anchor.\n    # e.g. \"edge 2 (inner, feature)\" for cad.edge, \"net VCC\" for pcb.net.\n    func summary(anchor: Dictionary, host: Object) -> String: return \"\"\n    # Optional: try to repair a stale anchor. Returns updated anchor dict or null.\n    # Called by substrate when user invokes \"repair\" affordance, or by plugin on document change.\n    func repair(anchor: Dictionary, host: Object) -> Variant: return null\n\n# Registry methods\nfunc register(plugin: String, anchor_type: String, resolver: AnchorResolver) -> void\nfunc unregister(plugin: String, anchor_type: String) -> void\nfunc get_resolver(plugin: String, anchor_type: String) -> AnchorResolver  # null if unknown\nfunc validate_anchor(anchor: Dictionary) -> Array  # dispatches to resolver, returns errors\nfunc summary(anchor: Dictionary, host: Object) -> String  # dispatches; falls back to \"{plugin}.{type}:{id}\"\nfunc known_anchors_for(plugin: String) -> Array  # introspection for MCP\n```\n\n## Built-in anchors\n\nSubstrate registers these under `plugin: \"core\"` at startup (no plugin needed):\n\n- `core/text.range` — `{plugin: \"core\", type: \"text.range\", id: {start: int, end: int}, snapshot: {text: String, document_revision: int}}`\n- `core/text.selection` — same as range but represents a non-anchored selection (for LLM action targeting; transient)\n- `core/graphics.region` — `{plugin: \"core\", type: \"graphics.region\", id: <opaque>, snapshot: {rect: [x,y,w,h], image_revision: String}}`\n- `core/graphics.layer` — `{plugin: \"core\", type: \"graphics.layer\", id: <layer_id>, snapshot: {layer_revision: int}}`\n\nThese are the smoke-consumer's anchor types. Plugin anchors come later — CAD will register `cad.edge`, `cad.face`, `cad.vertex`, `cad.plane`; PCB will register `pcb.net`, `pcb.component`, `pcb.pad`, etc., when those plugins adopt v2.\n\n## Validation policy\n\n- Anchor add path: `AnnotationHost.add_annotation()` calls `registry.validate_anchor(anchor)` before storing. Errors are returned to caller; envelope rejected.\n- Unknown anchor type: validation returns `[\"unknown anchor type: {plugin}/{type}\"]`. Annotation rejected.\n- Plugin uninstalled: existing annotations with that plugin's anchors render with \"unknown plugin\" diagnostic; not deleted (preserved for re-install).\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationAnchorRegistry.gd`\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/CoreAnchors.gd` — registers `text.range`, `text.selection`, `graphics.region`, `graphics.layer` resolvers\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationHost.gd` — call `validate_anchor` from `add_annotation` and `update_annotation`\n- `singleton_object.gd` (or appropriate startup) — instantiate `AnnotationAnchorRegistry` as singleton; call `CoreAnchors.register_all(registry)`\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnchorResolver` protocol class defined; substrate cannot instantiate the abstract directly.\n2. `register / unregister / get_resolver` work; double-register raises; unregister of unknown is no-op.\n3. `validate_anchor` dispatches by `(anchor.plugin, anchor.type)`; missing fields return canonical error strings.\n4. `summary` dispatches and falls back to `\"{plugin}.{type}:{id}\"` for unknown types.\n5. Core anchors registered at startup: `text.range`, `text.selection`, `graphics.region`, `graphics.layer`. Each has working `validate` + `summary`. `repair` may return null (no auto-repair for core).\n6. `AnnotationHost.add_annotation` rejects envelopes whose anchor fails registry validation.\n7. Plugin scoping: registering `(plugin=\"cad\", type=\"edge\")` and `(plugin=\"pcb\", type=\"net\")` does not collide; `validate` routes correctly.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_anchor_registry.gd` — register/unregister/get/double-register, validation dispatch, unknown-type fallback, plugin isolation, summary fallback\n- `test_core_anchors.gd` — each of 4 core anchor types: validate (positive + negative), summary\n- Integration with envelope task: `host.add_annotation` rejects anchors with no registered resolver\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope task #1 (envelope shape)\n- Sibling: resolve_anchor contract (#3) — registry.summary / repair are sibling APIs to host.resolve_anchor\n- Smoke consumer (task #10) consumes `core/text.range`","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:57:16","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:02:58","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","registry","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb767fe871ba87d9ab6ad135fce5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Base AnnotationHost.resolve_anchor + anchor_screen_rect contract","description":"## Goal\n\nAdd two virtuals to `AnnotationHost`: `resolve_anchor(anchor) -> Dictionary` for render-time position resolution, and `anchor_screen_rect(anchor, view_context) -> Rect2` for hit-testing in screen space. The substrate render path calls these at draw time; the snapshot in the anchor is fallback only.\n\n## Why\n\nv1 stored `at: [x,y,z]` and rendered straight from it — when geometry re-evaluates and edge IDs shift, the snapshot is wrong. v2 resolves at draw time so the rendered position tracks the live entity. `resolve_anchor` is the single authority for \"where is this anchor\"; it replaces the four competing code paths in CAD (edge_overlay picker, click-tool nearest-edge, MCPCadTools midpoint, kind render). Hit-test gets its own virtual because rendered screen-space bounds depend on which pane is active (an edge in iso has different screen-rect than the same edge in top view).\n\n## Contract\n\n```gdscript\n# AnnotationHost.gd virtuals (default implementations return stale)\n\n# Returns 2D screen-space resolution of an anchor.\n# Substrate stays projection-agnostic: hosts handle camera/viewport themselves.\n# Return shape (Dictionary):\n#   {\n#     \"position\": Vector2,           # primary anchor point in screen space\n#     \"bounds\": Rect2,               # anchor's screen-space extent (point or region)\n#     \"stale\": bool,                 # true if anchor cannot be resolved cleanly\n#     \"view_metadata\": Dictionary    # per-pane info: {\"pane\": \"iso\", \"leader_offset\": Vector2, ...}\n#   }\n# Default: { stale: true, position: snapshot.position projected naively, bounds: Rect2(pos, Vector2.ZERO), view_metadata: {} }\nfunc resolve_anchor(anchor: Dictionary) -> Dictionary:\n    # subclasses override; default uses snapshot.position as Vector2 (drops Z)\n    var pos := _snapshot_position_2d(anchor.get(\"snapshot\", {}))\n    return {\"position\": pos, \"bounds\": Rect2(pos, Vector2.ZERO), \"stale\": true, \"view_metadata\": {}}\n\n# Returns screen-space rectangle for hit-testing the resolved anchor.\n# Used by manipulation tools (select/drag) to know where to listen for clicks\n# even when the kind doesn't render the anchor itself (it might render a leader to it).\n# Default: resolve_anchor(...).bounds\nfunc anchor_screen_rect(anchor: Dictionary, view_context: String) -> Rect2\n\n# Helpers for plugin authors\nfunc has_anchor_resolver_for(anchor_type: String) -> bool\nfunc register_anchor_resolver(anchor_type: String, callable: Callable) -> void\n```\n\n## 2D vs 3D commitment\n\nSubstrate `resolve_anchor` returns **per-pane 2D screen-space**, not 3D world. This is the biggest single architectural decision and locks the substrate renderer into pure 2D forever. Rationale:\n\n- Substrate renderer is a 2D `Control` overlay (`AnnotationCanvas`) parented above panes. It composes per-pane projections into a single screen.\n- Plugins that have 3D content (CAD) handle camera/projection/viewport math internally before returning a Vector2. CAD's `Cad_AnnotationHost.resolve_anchor` for `cad.edge:N`: enumerate active SubViewports, project edge midpoint through each pane's Camera3D, return the per-pane Vector2 in panel-root coords.\n- Per-pane visibility info travels via `view_metadata` so kind renderers can distinguish \"edge visible in iso pane\" from \"edge clipped in top pane.\"\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationHost.gd` — add the two virtuals and the helper methods. Default implementations are deliberately stale-returning so subclasses must override.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — `render(annotation, ctx)` is updated to call `ctx.host.resolve_anchor(annotation.anchor)` and use `position` instead of `annotation.primitives[0].at`.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationCanvas.gd` — render loop passes resolved positions to kind renderers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnnotationHost` exposes `resolve_anchor` and `anchor_screen_rect` as virtuals; default implementations exist and return `{stale: true}` plus snapshot-derived position.\n2. Subclasses can override either virtual independently. Test verifies override-routing.\n3. `AnnotationKind.render` calls `ctx.host.resolve_anchor` per annotation per frame (cache layer in task #5 will fix the cost).\n4. `view_metadata` round-trips through resolve → render — kind renderers can read pane name, leader offsets, etc.\n5. `register_anchor_resolver(type, callable)` lets plugin hosts wire up resolvers without subclassing the host (useful for third-party plugins).\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_host_resolve.gd` — default returns stale, override returns custom; per-pane data round-trips\n- Integration: a `test_text_range_host` minimal subclass implements `resolve_anchor` for `core/text.range` and the smoke consumer (task #10) verifies it end-to-end\n- Negative: anchor with no registered resolver → resolve returns `stale: true` with snapshot-derived position; render path renders broken indicator\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope (#1), anchor registry (#2)\n- Sibling: cache (#5) wraps resolve_anchor; trust boundary (#11) catches throws inside resolve\n- Smoke (#10) builds the first non-default resolve_anchor implementation, for `core/text.range`","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:57:49","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:09:45","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","host","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb76ed8975209d8ed5f7f373bcf8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Broken / stale anchor UX (canvas, sidebar, MCP, chat)","description":"## Goal\n\nDefine how broken/stale anchors surface across all four user-facing paths: canvas render, sidebar listing, MCP query output, and chat-context injection. A broken anchor is one whose `resolve_anchor` returns `stale: true` (the anchored entity doesn't exist or has changed beyond the resolver's tolerance).\n\n## Why\n\nIf broken anchors silently disappear, users lose data. If they crash the renderer, the editor breaks. If they're invisible to the LLM, the LLM acts on stale assumptions (\"PCB hint I added last week is still valid\"). Each surface needs a defined behavior; otherwise plugins each invent their own.\n\n## Behaviors per surface\n\n### Canvas render\n\n- Broken anchor: render at `snapshot.position` (the anchor's stored fallback) with a desaturated color and a small ⚠ badge.\n- The kind renderer is responsible for the broken styling; substrate provides `AnnotationRenderContext.is_stale` flag from the resolved anchor's `stale` field.\n- Default kind behavior: 50% alpha + warning-color tint + 8px badge in top-right of bounds.\n- `kind.render_broken(annotation, ctx)` is an optional virtual; default falls back to `render(annotation, ctx)` with `ctx.is_stale = true`.\n\n### Sidebar\n\n- Annotation sidebar shows a \"Broken (N)\" header section above active annotations when N > 0.\n- Each broken entry shows summary + last-known view + \"Repair\" button.\n- Repair button triggers plugin's anchor `repair(anchor, host)` (from registry, task #2). If repair returns a new anchor dict, substrate updates the annotation; if null, opens a re-target picker (kind-defined).\n- Filter pill: \"Show broken only\" toggle.\n\n### MCP\n\n- `minerva_annotations_query` includes broken in default output. Each entry has `stale: bool` field.\n- `status` filter accepts `\"stale\"` to fetch broken-only.\n- Broken annotations' `summary` field still populated (from anchor registry's stored summary at last successful resolve, persisted into envelope's `summary` on update). LLM sees what the broken thing was.\n- New tool: `minerva_annotations_repair_anchor(annotation_id, new_anchor?)` — null `new_anchor` triggers plugin's `repair`; non-null directly retargets (admin path).\n\n### Chat injection\n\n- Broken annotations included in `to_chat_context` output (task #7) by default.\n- `structured_json` block carries `lifecycle: \"stale\"` and `stale: true`.\n- `text` summary prefixed with `[BROKEN]` so non-multimodal LLMs can't miss it.\n- Non-broken annotations: no prefix.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — add `render_broken` virtual with default fallback\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationRenderContext.gd` — add `is_stale: bool`\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Annotations/AnnotationSidebar.gd` (or wherever sidebar lives) — broken section + Repair button + filter pill\n- `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPAnnotationsTools.gd` (or equivalent) — add `stale` to query output, add `status: \"stale\"` filter, add `minerva_annotations_repair_anchor` tool\n- `to_chat_context` (task #7) — coordinate so the `[BROKEN]` prefix and `stale: true` flag are emitted there\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Annotation rendered with stale anchor shows desaturated + ⚠ badge by default (test via golden image or pixel sample at known coords).\n2. Sidebar shows \"Broken (N)\" section when any annotation is stale; Repair button calls registry resolver's `repair`; null-return opens re-target picker.\n3. MCP query includes `stale` field; `status: \"stale\"` filter returns only broken.\n4. Chat-context output prefixes summary with `[BROKEN]` for stale annotations.\n5. `minerva_annotations_repair_anchor(id)` with null new_anchor calls plugin `repair`; with non-null replaces directly.\n6. Broken anchor doesn't crash render — even if the kind throws inside `render_broken`, trust boundary (task #11) catches.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_broken_anchor_render.gd` — kind rendering with stale=true shows broken styling (golden image or sentinel-color check)\n- `test_broken_anchor_sidebar.gd` — sidebar surfaces broken; Repair button dispatches; filter pill toggles\n- `test_broken_anchor_mcp.gd` — query includes stale field; status filter; repair tool\n- `test_broken_anchor_chat.gd` — chat-context emits [BROKEN] prefix and stale=true\n- Smoke (#10) deletes the anchored text range mid-flight; verifies broken UX engages\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on resolve_anchor (#3) — the source of `stale: true`\n- Depends on registry (#2) — `repair` lives in resolver protocol\n- Coordinates with chat (#7) and MCP (#8)\n- Trust boundary (#11) catches throws inside broken render","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:58:17","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:32:19","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","broken-anchors","ux","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb77576b7b568ad144768b4d1fb8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Resolve cache + perf budget","description":"## Goal\n\nWrap `AnnotationHost.resolve_anchor` in a per-host cache invalidated by document/geometry revision counters. Hit ≤1ms per cached resolve, ≤10ms per uncached. Cap N=1000 annotations per host before the substrate paginates render.\n\n## Why\n\n`AnnotationKind.render` calls `host.resolve_anchor(annotation.anchor)` per annotation per frame. CAD's resolver projects through SubViewport cameras; PCB's would walk a spatial index. Naive per-frame is O(annotations × document entities). At 30 annotations × 60 fps × 5ms each = 9 seconds/sec of CPU; obviously broken. The cache is non-optional.\n\n## Contract\n\n```gdscript\n# src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationResolveCache.gd\n\nclass_name AnnotationResolveCache extends RefCounted\n\n# One cache per host. Owned by the host; substrate accesses via host.resolve_cache.\nvar _cache: Dictionary = {}  # anchor_key -> {\"resolved\": Dictionary, \"revision\": int}\nvar _host_revision: int = 0  # bumped by host on document change\n\n# Public API\nfunc resolve(anchor: Dictionary, host: Object, view_context: String) -> Dictionary\n    # returns cached entry if revision matches; else calls host.resolve_anchor and caches\nfunc invalidate(anchor_type: String = \"\") -> void\n    # empty arg = invalidate all; specific type only invalidates entries with that anchor.type\nfunc bump_revision() -> void\n    # called by host when document/geometry changes; lazy: doesn't clear cache, just increments\nfunc stats() -> Dictionary\n    # {hits: int, misses: int, evictions: int, size: int} for telemetry\n\n# Anchor key generation\nstatic func _anchor_key(anchor: Dictionary, view_context: String) -> String\n    # canonical: \"{plugin}/{type}:{id}/{view_context}\"  (id may be JSON-encoded for non-scalar)\n```\n\n## Invalidation policy\n\n- Lazy by revision counter. Each cache entry stores the host's revision at time of cache write. On read, if `entry.revision != host._host_revision`, treat as miss and re-resolve.\n- `host.bump_revision()` called from:\n  - text editor: on every text change (or batched at frame boundary if too chatty)\n  - graphics editor: on layer mutation\n  - CAD plugin: on geometry re-evaluation\n  - PCB plugin: on board mutation\n- `cache.invalidate(anchor_type)` for surgical clears (e.g. \"edges changed; clear all `cad.edge`\").\n- Cache is per-host; cross-host invalidation is the caller's job.\n\n## Perf budget (assertions in tests)\n\n- Cached resolve: <1ms per call (median over 1000 calls).\n- Uncached resolve: <10ms per call (depends on plugin resolver; substrate guarantees overhead is <0.5ms).\n- Cap: render loop visits at most N=1000 annotations per host per frame. Beyond that, substrate skips and surfaces a \"too many annotations\" diagnostic.\n- Cache eviction: LRU when cache size exceeds 5000 entries.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationResolveCache.gd`\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationHost.gd` — own a `resolve_cache: AnnotationResolveCache`. Provide `_resolve_anchor_cached(anchor, view_context)` helper that kinds use (kinds don't talk to cache directly).\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — render path calls `ctx.host._resolve_anchor_cached(annotation.anchor, ctx.view_context)` (renamed from direct `resolve_anchor`).\n- `AnnotationCanvas.gd` — render budget enforcement (skip-and-warn beyond N=1000).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnnotationResolveCache.resolve` hits cache when revision matches; calls host on miss.\n2. `bump_revision` invalidates lazily — next read for any cached anchor re-resolves.\n3. `invalidate(anchor_type)` clears matching entries; empty arg clears all.\n4. Cache key includes `view_context` so per-pane resolutions don't collide.\n5. Perf assertion test: 1000 cached resolves complete in <1s wall time.\n6. LRU eviction at 5000 entries; test verifies oldest entries dropped.\n7. Render skips beyond N=1000 with diagnostic event (logged + sidebar warning).\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_resolve_cache.gd` — hit/miss/invalidate/bump/eviction\n- `test_annotation_resolve_cache_perf.gd` — wall-time assertions; skip on CI if perf-tagged\n- Integration: smoke (#10) verifies cache hits dominate after first frame\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on resolve_anchor contract (#3)\n- Coordinates with trust boundary (#11): cache catches plugin resolver throws and stores them as `stale: true` entries with TTL\n- Sibling task #6 (built-in/plugin kinds split) doesn't change cache shape","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:58:44","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:17:16","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","cache","performance","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb77c4b37516876614259d82d497","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Built-in anchors carry generic kinds; plugin anchors carry plugin kinds","description":"## Goal\n\nDefine and enforce the policy for which annotation kinds may compose with which anchor types. The short rule: **`plugin: \"core\"` anchors compose with generic kinds; non-core anchors require plugin-defined kinds with task-specific payloads.**\n\n## Why\n\nPickup.md's original framing was \"generic kinds carry plugin anchors via host resolve\" — the idea that `text` + `cad.edge:N` would just work via dispatch. Reframing during scoping established that task-specific annotations (PCB bus hints, CAD edge actions) need *task-specific kinds*, not generic kinds with extra metadata, because the **payload** is part of the action contract:\n\n- A `pcb_bus_hint` carries `{nets, from, to, preferred_layer, spacing_mm, length_match_tolerance_mm, waypoints}`. A generic `text` annotation cannot express any of those fields.\n- A `cad_edge_note` payload that has `{action: \"fillet\", radius_mm: 3.0}` is the LLM's input to the apply-tool. Generic `text` would lose the structured action.\n\nConversely, **built-in anchors** are precisely for the case where the annotation IS just a comment with no domain payload:\n\n- `core/text.range` — anchored text comment (MS-Word style)\n- `core/graphics.region` — revision suggestion on a region of an image\n- `core/text.selection` — transient LLM target\n\nGeneric kinds (`text`, `arrow`, `region`, `polyline`, `highlight`) compose with these because they're literally the same shape: comment + position. No domain payload needed.\n\n## Policy\n\nValidation rule, enforced by `AnnotationSchema.validate(envelope)`:\n\n```\nif envelope.anchor.plugin == \"core\":\n    envelope.kind must be in {generic_kinds_set} OR a plugin kind that explicitly opts in\nelse:\n    envelope.kind must be a plugin-defined kind whose `accepted_anchor_types` includes envelope.anchor.type\n```\n\n`AnnotationKind.accepted_anchor_types() -> Array[String]`:\n- Generic kinds default to `[\"core/*\"]` (wildcard match against `plugin: \"core\"` anchors only).\n- Plugin kinds declare specific types: `cad_edge_note.accepted_anchor_types() -> [\"cad/edge\"]`; `pcb_bus_hint.accepted_anchor_types() -> [\"pcb/bus\"]`.\n- Invalid combinations (e.g. generic `text` with `cad/edge` anchor) rejected at validation.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Kind | Anchor | Valid? | Why |\n|------|--------|--------|-----|\n| `text` | `core/text.range` | ✓ | Generic kind + core anchor |\n| `arrow` | `core/graphics.region` | ✓ | Generic kind + core anchor |\n| `text` | `cad/edge` | ✗ | Generic kind rejects non-core anchors |\n| `cad_edge_note` | `cad/edge` | ✓ | Plugin kind explicitly accepts this anchor |\n| `cad_edge_note` | `core/text.range` | ✗ | Plugin kind doesn't accept core anchors |\n| `pcb_bus_hint` | `pcb/bus` | ✓ | Plugin kind accepts plugin anchor |\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — add `accepted_anchor_types() -> Array[String]` virtual; default `[]` (rejects all). Generic kinds override to `[\"core/*\"]`.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/BuiltinKinds.gd` — every generic kind sets `accepted_anchor_types() -> [\"core/*\"]`.\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationSchema.gd` — `validate` enforces kind-anchor compatibility.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnnotationKind` has `accepted_anchor_types()` virtual; default returns `[]` (kind accepts no anchors).\n2. Each built-in generic kind (text, arrow, region, polyline, highlight, measure_*) returns `[\"core/*\"]`.\n3. `AnnotationSchema.validate` rejects:\n   - core anchor on a kind whose `accepted_anchor_types` does not match\n   - plugin anchor on a kind whose `accepted_anchor_types` does not include exact match\n4. Wildcard match: `[\"core/*\"]` accepts any `core/<type>`.\n5. Multiple anchor types per kind: `accepted_anchor_types()` may return `[\"cad/edge\", \"cad/face\"]` — kind handles both.\n6. Migration (#9): v1 generic kinds upgraded with empty anchor get `core/none` (special — placed annotation, no anchor); generic kinds accept it via `[\"core/*\"]`.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_kind_anchor_compat.gd` — every cell of the policy table; positive + negative\n- Integration with envelope (#1): validate path fully exercised\n- Smoke (#10): generic `text` kind composes with `core/text.range` anchor end-to-end\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope (#1), anchor registry (#2)\n- Migration (#9) introduces `core/none` for unanchored upgraded v1 annotations\n- Reframes the original pickup.md item — sharper acceptance, same place in the plan","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:59:12","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:36:37","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","kinds","policy","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb7843b4742d8bcb4d1515d4dfd3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Capability-aware to_chat_context (structured_json IS the action contract)","description":"## Goal\n\nReplace v1's \"annotation summary text\" injection with `to_chat_context(annotation, capabilities) -> Array[ContextBlock]`. The structured_json block carries the typed envelope and IS the canonical LLM-readable form of the action intent. Text and image are supplementary.\n\n## Why\n\nAnnotations are structured action input — typed anchor, payload, lifecycle. Non-multimodal LLMs need the structured_json block to act on them. Multimodal LLMs benefit from rendering for spatial intuition but still rely on structured fields for precision (an image-only injection of \"fillet edge 2 by 3mm\" loses the edge_id and the radius). Text summary is the parallel readable form for LLMs that can't consume JSON cleanly.\n\n## Contract\n\n```gdscript\n# src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/ContextBlock.gd\n\nclass_name ContextBlock extends RefCounted\n\nenum Type { TEXT, STRUCTURED_JSON, IMAGE, SOURCE_CODE, TABLE, UNSUPPORTED_PLACEHOLDER }\n\nvar type: Type\nvar content: Variant       # String for text/source_code; Dictionary for structured_json; PackedByteArray for image; Array of arrays for table; String for placeholder\nvar meta: Dictionary       # block-specific metadata (mime, language, dims, etc.)\n\n\n# AnnotationKind virtual:\nfunc to_chat_context(annotation: Dictionary, capabilities: Dictionary) -> Array:\n    # capabilities = {\n    #     multimodal: bool,\n    #     max_tokens: int,            # advisory, kind clamps content\n    #     supported_block_types: Array[String]   # subset of [\"text\",\"structured_json\",\"image\",\"source_code\",\"table\"]\n    # }\n    # Default implementation returns:\n    #   - structured_json block ALWAYS (the action contract)\n    #   - text block if \"text\" in supported_block_types (parallel readable form)\n    #   - image block if multimodal AND \"image\" in supported_block_types AND kind has render\n```\n\n## Default behavior (substrate)\n\nDefault `AnnotationKind.to_chat_context`:\n\n1. Always emit `structured_json` block: the full envelope minus computed `anchored_to`. Carries kind, anchor, payload, lifecycle, summary, view_context.\n2. If `\"text\" in supported_block_types`: emit text block with `summary` (prefixed `[BROKEN]` if stale per task #4).\n3. If `multimodal AND \"image\" in supported_block_types AND kind.has_visual_render()`: emit image block with rendered annotation overlay.\n\nKinds may override to add custom blocks (e.g. PCB bus hint adds a `table` block listing the nets when supported).\n\n## Capability schema\n\nStandard capability dicts (provided by chat layer; substrate doesn't compute):\n\n- Anthropic Sonnet/Opus 4.x: `{multimodal: true, max_tokens: 200_000, supported_block_types: [\"text\", \"structured_json\", \"image\", \"source_code\", \"table\"]}`\n- OpenAI GPT-4o: `{multimodal: true, max_tokens: 128_000, supported_block_types: [\"text\", \"structured_json\", \"image\", \"source_code\", \"table\"]}`\n- Local Llama (text-only): `{multimodal: false, max_tokens: 32_000, supported_block_types: [\"text\", \"structured_json\", \"source_code\"]}`\n- Hypothetical text-only narrow LLM: `{multimodal: false, max_tokens: 4_000, supported_block_types: [\"text\"]}` — substrate emits structured_json as `unsupported_placeholder` block with note \"structured payload not consumable; refer to summary\".\n\n## Token budget\n\n`max_tokens` is advisory. Kinds clamp content size:\n- `text` summary: substrate truncates at 500 tokens with `[truncated]` suffix.\n- `structured_json`: kinds may omit large fields (e.g. PCB bus hint waypoints) when `max_tokens < 8000`.\n- `image`: skipped if rendered image alone exceeds 50% of remaining budget.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/ContextBlock.gd` — the block class\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — `to_chat_context` virtual with default; `has_visual_render() -> bool` virtual (default true for kinds with render override)\n- Wherever annotation chat injection happens today (search for callers of v1's `summary` field) — replace with `to_chat_context(ann, caps)` returning `Array[ContextBlock]`\n- Chat layer: provides the `capabilities` dict per active provider; consumes the block array\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `AnnotationKind.to_chat_context` default implementation works for any envelope; emits structured_json always, text + image conditionally on capabilities.\n2. Non-multimodal capability dict produces no image blocks.\n3. Provider with no `structured_json` support (purely hypothetical text-only) gets `unsupported_placeholder`.\n4. Stale annotation: text block prefixed with `[BROKEN]`; structured_json carries `lifecycle: \"stale\"` and `stale: true`.\n5. Custom kind override extends defaults: PCB bus hint kind adds a `table` block when supported_block_types includes `table`.\n6. Token budget clamps text at 500 tokens; structured_json omits configurable fields when max_tokens low.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_chat_context_default.gd` — every capability shape (multimodal/text-only/narrow) produces correct block arrays\n- `test_annotation_chat_context_stale.gd` — broken annotation injection\n- `test_annotation_chat_context_custom.gd` — kind override extends defaults\n- Smoke (#10): text editor's `text` annotation injects via to_chat_context end-to-end with both multimodal and text-only capability dicts\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope (#1) for the source data\n- Coordinates with broken anchor UX (#4) for stale prefix\n- Coordinates with MCP query (#8) — `to_chat_context` is the same shape MCP returns when capability is provided\n- Reframes pickup.md's original \"capability-aware\" framing: structured_json is required (action contract), not optional","created_at":"2026-04-29T22:59:45","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:41:01","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","chat","llm","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb78c40c74bcbaa0bef7b368ffd9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP query / update_status surface + apply-tool hook","description":"## Goal\n\nAdd MCP tools that operate on the v2 envelope: `minerva_annotations_query` (filtered list with capability projection), `minerva_annotations_update_status` (lifecycle transitions), and `minerva_annotations_register_apply_hook` (plugin apply-tools mark applied with linked entities).\n\n## Why\n\nv1's `minerva_annotations_list` returns flat envelopes by `editor_name` filter. v2 needs richer query because lifecycle, anchor type, and author filters are first-class. The LLM workflow is: query open hints → reason → call plugin's apply tool → plugin marks applied with link to created entities → query confirms. Without `update_status` and the apply hook, plugin apply-tools each invent their own way to do this, drifting.\n\n## Tools\n\n```\nminerva_annotations_query({\n    editor_name?: string,\n    document_path?: string,\n    status?: string,                  # \"open\" | \"applied\" | \"resolved\" | \"stale\" | \"any\" (default open+applied)\n    anchor_type?: string,             # e.g. \"cad/edge\", \"core/text.range\", or \"core/*\" wildcard\n    kind?: string,\n    author_kind?: string,             # \"human\" | \"ai\"\n    author_id?: string,\n    text?: string,                    # full-text search of payload + summary\n    time_range?: {after?: ts, before?: ts},\n    capabilities?: {multimodal: bool, supported_block_types: array, max_tokens: int}\n}) -> {\n    annotations: [\n        {id, kind, anchor, lifecycle, summary, view_context, ...},\n        ...\n    ],\n    total: int,\n    truncated: bool\n}\n```\n\nWhen `capabilities` is provided, each entry includes `chat_context: Array[ContextBlock]` projected via task #7.\n\n```\nminerva_annotations_update_status(\n    annotation_id: string,\n    new_status: \"open\" | \"applied\" | \"resolved\" | \"stale\",\n    note?: string,                    # appended to event log\n    applied?: {                       # required when new_status=\"applied\"\n        by: {kind, id?, model?},\n        links: [{kind, id}]           # entities created by apply-tool, e.g. [{kind:\"pcb.trace\", id:\"trace_42\"}]\n    },\n    resolved?: {                      # required when new_status=\"resolved\"\n        by: {kind, id?, model?},\n        note?: string\n    }\n) -> {ok: bool, annotation: <updated envelope>}\n```\n\n```\nminerva_annotations_register_apply_hook(\n    plugin: string,\n    kind: string,                     # which annotation kind triggers this hook\n    callable_name: string             # plugin-side function name (resolved via plugin's RPC table)\n) -> {ok: bool}\n```\n\nThe apply hook is called by `update_status` when transitioning to `applied`, after substrate persists the new state. The hook is informational — it lets the plugin do follow-up work (e.g. annotate the created traces with backreferences). Failure in the hook does not roll back the status transition.\n\n## Lifecycle transition enforcement\n\n`update_status` validates the transition per the state machine in task #1:\n\n- `open → applied` (apply-tool path): requires `applied` object with `links`\n- `open → resolved`: user-marked resolved; requires `resolved.by`\n- `applied → resolved`: user verifies; requires `resolved.by`\n- `resolved → open`: user reopens\n- `* → stale`: substrate-only (when resolve fails); MCP rejects user-initiated stale transitions\n- `stale → open`: user-initiated repair (via `minerva_annotations_repair_anchor` from task #4)\n\nIllegal transitions return `{ok: false, error: \"illegal transition\", from, to}`.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPAnnotationsTools.gd` — replace v1 list with v2 query; add `update_status` + `register_apply_hook`. v1 `add_annotation`, `update_annotation`, `delete_annotation` keep similar shapes but accept v2 envelopes.\n- `singleton_object.gd` (or wherever MCP tool registry lives) — register the new tools\n- Apply hook dispatch: probably lives in `AnnotationHost` or `AnnotationKind` — TBD during impl\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `query` filters compose: `{status: \"open\", anchor_type: \"core/*\", author_kind: \"ai\"}` returns only AI-authored open core-anchored annotations.\n2. Wildcard anchor_type (`\"core/*\"`, `\"cad/*\"`) works; specific (`\"core/text.range\"`) works.\n3. `text` filter searches `payload.text` and `summary` substring (case-insensitive).\n4. `time_range` filters by `created_at` or `updated_at` (one or both).\n5. `capabilities` arg projects each entry through `to_chat_context` and includes `chat_context` field; when omitted, entries return without it.\n6. `update_status`: legal transitions succeed and persist; illegal return error; `applied` requires `applied.links`; `resolved` requires `resolved.by`.\n7. `register_apply_hook`: plugin's hook fired after status transition to applied; hook failure logged but not propagated.\n8. `truncated: true` when results exceed paging limit (default 100); pagination via separate args is out of scope (deferred).\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_mcp_annotations_query.gd` — every filter shape; capability projection; truncation\n- `test_mcp_annotations_update_status.gd` — every legal transition; every illegal rejected; required-fields enforcement\n- `test_mcp_annotations_apply_hook.gd` — hook fires; hook failure logged; multiple hooks per kind compose\n- Smoke (#10): text-editor flow — LLM queries open comments, marks one applied with linked text-edit, query confirms applied\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope (#1) for lifecycle state machine\n- Depends on chat-context (#7) for `capabilities` projection\n- Depends on broken-anchor (#4) for `repair_anchor` tool which is sibling to update_status\n- Replaces v1's `019dc6543a4972c292f64ba4fa0d9a7d` (LLM annotation ergonomics) at the MCP layer","created_at":"2026-04-29T23:00:18","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:45:51","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","lifecycle","mcp","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb7960417194ad476f5e3efe9026","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"v1 → v2 migration + coexistence hooks","description":"## Goal\n\nDefine on-load behavior for v1 annotation envelopes (sidecars and project-state). v1 envelopes get one of three fates per kind: drop, upgrade with empty anchor, or refuse-load. Substrate exposes a per-kind migrator hook so plugins can supply their own upgrade logic.\n\n## Why\n\nv1 sidecars and `__panel_state` records exist in the wild — Hello plugin smoke (`019dc61535db`) shipped them; Round 1+2a CAD shipped `cad_edge_number` records; PCBEditor sidecars predate v2. v2's validator rejects v1 envelopes (different `schema_version`, different field shapes). Migration is the bridge.\n\n## v1 → v2 envelope mapping\n\nv1 envelope shape (from `AnnotationSchema.gd` Phase 1B):\n```\n{\n    \"id\": String,\n    \"kind\": String,\n    \"primitives\": [{\"type\": \"point\"|\"line\"|\"poly\", \"at\": [x,y,z], ...}],\n    \"metadata\": {\"author\": String?, \"anchored_to\": String?, ...},\n    \"schema_version\": 1\n}\n```\n\nv2 envelope (per task #1):\n```\n{\n    \"id\", \"kind\", \"schema_version\": 2,\n    \"anchor\": {plugin, type, id, snapshot},\n    \"kind_payload\": {...},\n    \"lifecycle\": \"open\",\n    \"author\": {kind, id?, model?, session_id?},\n    \"view_context\", \"visible_in_views\",\n    \"summary\",\n    \"created_at\", \"updated_at\"\n}\n```\n\n## Per-kind migrator protocol\n\n```gdscript\n# AnnotationKind virtual\nfunc migrate_v1(v1_envelope: Dictionary) -> Variant:\n    # Returns:\n    #   Dictionary — v2 envelope replacement\n    #   \"drop\" (String) — discard this annotation on load\n    #   null — kind has no migration logic; substrate falls back to default upgrade\n    return null\n```\n\n## Default upgrade (substrate fallback)\n\nWhen a kind's `migrate_v1` returns null:\n\n```\nv2.id = v1.id\nv2.kind = v1.kind\nv2.schema_version = 2\nv2.anchor = {\n    plugin: \"core\",\n    type: \"none\",                          # special — placed annotation, no semantic anchor\n    id: v1.id,                             # use annotation id as anchor id\n    snapshot: {position: v1.primitives[0].at if any else [0,0,0]}\n}\nv2.kind_payload = v1.metadata.copy_excluding([\"author\", \"anchored_to\"])  # plus primitives if kind needs them\nv2.lifecycle = \"open\"\nv2.author = {kind: \"human\", id: v1.metadata.author or null}\nv2.view_context = v1.metadata.view or \"unknown\"\nv2.visible_in_views = v1.metadata.visible_in_views or [\"all\"]\nv2.summary = v1.metadata.summary or \"{kind} (migrated from v1)\"\nv2.created_at = (v1.metadata.created_at) or (now)\nv2.updated_at = now\n```\n\n## Per-kind decisions\n\n- **Generic v1 kinds** (`2d_arrow`, `2d_text`, `2d_region`, `2d_polyline`, `2d_highlight`, `2d_measure_*`) — substrate default upgrade. Anchor type `core/none`.\n- **`cad_edge_number`** — `migrate_v1` returns `\"drop\"`. These records were transient by design; v2 repurposes edge-number as a viewport overlay (no annotation). Logged at load time: \"Dropped N v1 cad_edge_number annotations from `<path>` (transient by design; replaced by edge-number overlay toggle).\"\n- **PCBEditor in-document annotations** — defer to PCB-as-plugin migration (separate DCR `019dc14029197`). Not handled in this task.\n\n## Refuse-load policy\n\nSubstrate refuses to load a sidecar or project-state record if it contains a v1 envelope whose kind has no registered migrator (null `migrate_v1`) AND the substrate's default-upgrade fallback fails (e.g. envelope is malformed). Refuse means: log at error level, surface to user via sidebar diagnostic (\"3 annotations could not be migrated; sidecar untouched on disk\"), continue loading other annotations. Do not silently delete.\n\n## Coexistence hooks\n\nFor users running mixed clients (one machine on v1, one on v2), substrate exposes:\n\n- `AnnotationSidecarIO.load_sidecar(path) -> {annotations: [v2], v1_dropped: [v1], v1_failed: [v1], summary: String}` — preserves the original sidecar on disk when migrating; user must explicitly save to commit migration.\n- `AnnotationSidecarIO.save_sidecar_v2(path, annotations)` — always writes v2; no fallback to v1.\n- v1-writing path retired; v1 sidecars become read-only.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationSchema.gd` — `migrate_v1_to_v2(v1_envelope, kind_registry) -> Variant` static helper\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationKind.gd` — `migrate_v1(v1_envelope) -> Variant` virtual; default null\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationSidecarIO.gd` (or wherever sidecar load lives) — invoke migration on load; surface load result with `v1_dropped` + `v1_failed`\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/BuiltinKinds.gd` — generic kinds keep null migrator (use default upgrade)\n- New (or in plugins repo when CAD adopts): `cad_edge_number_kind.migrate_v1` returns `\"drop\"`\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. v1 generic-kind envelope upgrades to v2 with `core/none` anchor; round-trip through save+reload preserves data.\n2. v1 `cad_edge_number` envelope dropped on load; sidebar diagnostic logged.\n3. Malformed v1 envelope: refuse-load (logged, sidecar preserved, surfaced via sidebar); other annotations in same sidecar still load.\n4. Mixed sidecar (some v1, some v2): all loaded; v1 ones migrated.\n5. After load, save writes v2 only.\n6. v1-only client reading a v2 sidecar: out of scope (one-way migration; v1 client would error).\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_annotation_v1_migration.gd` — every generic kind: round-trip v1 → migrate → v2; assert field mapping\n- `test_annotation_v1_drop.gd` — `cad_edge_number` v1 records dropped; diagnostic emitted\n- `test_annotation_v1_refuse.gd` — malformed v1 doesn't load; sidecar preserved\n- Test fixture: existing hello-plugin smoke sidecars (`.hello.annotations.json`) round-trip through migration\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on envelope (#1) for v2 schema; kind registry for migrator dispatch\n- Sibling to PCB migration (`019dc14029197`) — that DCR migrates PCB's bespoke records; this task migrates only generic kinds\n- Smoke (#10) verifies migration on a v1 fixture sidecar before consuming v2 path","created_at":"2026-04-29T23:00:58","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:50:47","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","migration","v1","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb7a0f407621a877f4011f5fde6f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Contract tests + smoke consumer (text editor) — START HERE","description":"## Goal\n\nRound 1 of the v2 substrate plan. Two deliverables:\n\n1. **Red contract tests** for every other task in the plan (#1-9, #11). Tests describe the behavior; implementations come in later rounds.\n2. **Smoke consumer wiring contract** for the built-in text editor. Defines how the text editor will consume the v2 substrate in Round 5; tests describe the expected flow.\n\nThis task ships RED — every test fails. Subsequent rounds turn them green. By writing the tests first, every upstream task is forced to commit to a concrete schema/method-signature/return-shape before any implementer touches them.\n\n## Why text editor (not CAD or PCB)\n\nPickup.md established this. CAD has a working editor elsewhere; deferring CAD adoption costs nothing. PCB has its own DCR. The smoke consumer should be a built-in (not plugin) editor with a built-in anchor type, so substrate ships before any plugin extends. Text editor + `core/text.range` covers the MS-Word-style anchored comment use case — the simplest non-trivial substrate consumer.\n\n## Test scaffolding to write\n\nOne test file per upstream task, all initially red. Implementer should structure as `test/annotations_v2/<topic>.gd`:\n\n| Task | Test file | Notes |\n|------|-----------|-------|\n| #1 envelope | `test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd` | every required field, lifecycle state machine, computed anchored_to, author validation |\n| #2 anchor registry | `test_annotation_anchor_registry.gd` | register/unregister/get/double-register, validation dispatch, plugin scoping |\n|  | `test_core_anchors.gd` | text.range, text.selection, graphics.region, graphics.layer validate + summary |\n| #3 resolve_anchor | `test_annotation_host_resolve.gd` | default returns stale, override returns custom, view_metadata round-trip |\n| #4 broken UX | `test_broken_anchor_render.gd` | golden image / sentinel-color check |\n|  | `test_broken_anchor_sidebar.gd` | broken section visible, Repair button dispatches |\n|  | `test_broken_anchor_mcp.gd` | stale field in query, status=\"stale\" filter, repair tool |\n|  | `test_broken_anchor_chat.gd` | [BROKEN] prefix and stale=true in chat-context |\n| #5 cache | `test_annotation_resolve_cache.gd` | hit/miss/invalidate/bump/eviction |\n|  | `test_annotation_resolve_cache_perf.gd` | wall-time assertions, perf-tagged |\n| #6 kind/anchor compat | `test_kind_anchor_compat.gd` | every cell of policy table |\n| #7 chat context | `test_annotation_chat_context_default.gd` | every capability shape |\n|  | `test_annotation_chat_context_stale.gd` | broken annotation injection |\n|  | `test_annotation_chat_context_custom.gd` | kind override extends defaults |\n| #8 MCP | `test_mcp_annotations_query.gd` | every filter, capability projection, truncation |\n|  | `test_mcp_annotations_update_status.gd` | every legal + illegal transition |\n|  | `test_mcp_annotations_apply_hook.gd` | hook fires, failure logged, multi-hook compose |\n| #9 migration | `test_annotation_v1_migration.gd` | every generic kind round-trip |\n|  | `test_annotation_v1_drop.gd` | cad_edge_number dropped |\n|  | `test_annotation_v1_refuse.gd` | malformed v1 surfaces via sidebar |\n| #11 trust | `test_plugin_trust_render.gd` | throwing plugin doesn't crash |\n|  | `test_plugin_trust_resolver.gd` | throwing resolver marks stale |\n|  | `test_plugin_trust_apply_tool.gd` | throwing apply-tool reverts state |\n|  | `test_plugin_trust_rate_limit.gd` | suspends after N throws in M seconds |\n\nTests should fail with explicit \"not implemented\" or \"unknown method\" errors — i.e. they exercise the API surface, so the absence of the API is the failure.\n\n## Smoke consumer wiring contract (text editor)\n\nThe text editor (`src/Scenes/Editor.tscn` and `src/Scripts/Editor.gd` — the built-in code editor) will host the smoke consumer in Round 5. This task defines the wiring contract — what classes get added, what signals fire, what UI elements appear — through tests that describe the expected behavior:\n\n```gdscript\n# test_text_editor_annotation_smoke.gd  (RED in this round)\n\nfunc test_text_editor_has_annotation_host():\n    # When Editor.gd is instantiated, it owns a TextEditorAnnotationHost (subclass of AnnotationHost).\n    # The host's resolve_anchor handles core/text.range anchors.\n    pass\n\nfunc test_text_editor_add_comment_via_selection():\n    # User selects text [start=10, end=25].\n    # Calls editor.add_comment(text=\"rephrase this\") (or via context menu).\n    # An annotation is added to the host with:\n    #   anchor.type = \"core/text.range\", anchor.id = {start:10, end:25}\n    #   kind = \"text\" (generic)\n    #   payload = {text: \"rephrase this\"}\n    #   lifecycle = \"open\"\n    pass\n\nfunc test_text_editor_comment_persists_via_save():\n    # Annotation persists to .gd.annotations.json sidecar on Ctrl+S.\n    # Reload editor; annotation present.\n    pass\n\nfunc test_text_editor_comment_breaks_on_text_delete():\n    # User deletes the anchored text range.\n    # Host detects (via text-buffer revision change), bumps revision counter.\n    # Next render: annotation resolves stale=true; renders broken indicator.\n    pass\n\nfunc test_text_editor_comment_repair():\n    # User clicks \"Repair\" in sidebar.\n    # Plugin (substrate, since core anchor) prompts user to re-select text.\n    # User selects new range; annotation updates.\n    pass\n\nfunc test_text_editor_comment_via_mcp():\n    # LLM calls minerva_annotations_query(editor_name=\"MyScript.gd\", status=\"open\").\n    # Returns the comment with chat_context block array (when capabilities provided).\n    # LLM applies a text edit; calls minerva_annotations_update_status(id, \"applied\", applied={links: [...]})\n    # Subsequent query shows lifecycle=applied.\n    pass\n```\n\nThese tests are RED — they reference methods/classes that don't exist yet (`TextEditorAnnotationHost`, `editor.add_comment`, etc.). Round 5 builds them.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/test/annotations_v2/` directory with all the test files above\n- Update: `src/test/run_all.gd` (or equivalent test entry) to discover the new directory\n- No production code changes in this round\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Every test file listed above exists and runs.\n2. Every test function exists and fails red with a clear, attributable error (not a setup crash).\n3. Test naming follows `test_<scope>_<behavior>` convention (e.g. `test_envelope_lifecycle_open_to_applied`).\n4. The smoke-consumer test file describes the expected text-editor flow in 6+ functions covering: host instantiation, add via selection, persist via save, broken on delete, repair, MCP round-trip.\n5. Tests categorized by tag (perf, smoke, integration, unit) so CI can run subsets.\n6. Layer-1 verification: `godot --headless --path src --script test/annotations_v2/run_all.gd` runs all tests; reports `PASS: 0`, `FAIL: <count>` (because they're red).\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3c (auto-verified): once tests are red and the test runner reports their counts, this round is done. No HITL needed — the deliverable is the test scaffolding, not behavior.\n\n## Test groups\n\nThis task IS the test groups. The metric is \"every contract test exists and fails for the right reason.\"\n\n## Related\n\n- This is the first work-cycle round. All other tasks (#1-9, #11) consume this round's tests as their acceptance bar.\n- Stop condition 3c.\n- Follow-up: Round 5 (later) wires the text editor to actually pass `test_text_editor_*` tests. That round is HITL.","created_at":"2026-04-29T23:01:42","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:51:21","priority":1,"tags":["annotations","smoke","tests","text-editor","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45","resolution":"Round 1 RED contract test scaffolding shipped in commit 76d4b8e4. 25 .gd test files + run_all.sh under src/test/annotations_v2/. Layer-1: 0 passed, 358 failed, runner exits 0 (RED-as-expected). Reviewer (Opus, cold) verdict approve_with_notes — flagged ~10 dead-coded `check(..., true)` placeholders gated by class_exists() that will silently flip to PASS once stub classes register. Saved as nudge hint minerva-testing/annotations-v2-red-sweep so each future round in this plan sweeps `, true)` before declaring green."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddb7aaf247033931dd1b3b656b1b0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin trust boundary + fail-containment (render / resolver / apply-tool)","description":"## Goal\n\nThree plugin-extension surfaces — render, anchor resolver, and apply-tool — must not crash the editor when plugin code throws. Each surface gets fail-containment with diagnostics; a misbehaving plugin is rate-limited rather than crashing the host.\n\n## Why\n\nv1 had no trust boundary: a `cad_edge_number_kind.render` exception would propagate out of the substrate render loop and freeze the editor. The CAD edge-tool experiments hit exactly this — modal-tool lockup partly traced to render-loop exceptions wedging input handlers. v2 plugins do strictly more (resolvers, apply-tools that mutate documents); the surface area is bigger, so the trust contract has to be explicit.\n\n## Three surfaces, three policies\n\n### 1. Render (kind.render and kind.render_broken)\n\nPolicy: catch exception, replace this annotation's render with a small `[!]` diagnostic badge at snapshot position, log to telemetry. Do not propagate.\n\nPer-kind throw counter; if a kind throws >5 times in 60 seconds, suspend rendering for that kind (substrate-wide), surface \"Annotation kind '{kind}' has been suspended due to repeated errors\" sidebar diagnostic. Suspended kinds resume after 5 minutes or on user click \"Retry kind\".\n\n### 2. Resolver (host.resolve_anchor for plugin anchor types, and registry resolver protocol's validate / summary / repair)\n\nPolicy: catch, mark this anchor's cache entry as `{stale: true, error: <message>, cached_at: now}` with TTL 60s, log. Do not propagate.\n\nPer-(plugin, anchor_type) throw counter; if it throws >5 times in 60 seconds, suspend that anchor type — all annotations with that anchor render as broken. Resume after 5 minutes or user retry.\n\n### 3. Apply-tool (plugin's tool function called when annotation transitions to `applied`)\n\nPolicy: pre-call snapshot of host state (host responsibility — see below). Tool runs in dry-run mode first; if dry-run throws, catch and surface \"{tool} failed: {message}\" — do not transition status. If dry-run succeeds, commit phase runs; if commit throws, restore from snapshot, surface \"{tool} failed during commit; document reverted.\" Status transition to `applied` rolls back.\n\nApply-tools must be idempotent OR declare themselves non-idempotent. Substrate calls each tool with a `phase` arg: `\"dry_run\"`, `\"commit\"`, `\"undo\"`. Default `\"undo\"` implementation reverts via host snapshot (substrate-managed); plugin may provide a more efficient undo.\n\nPer-tool throw counter; >3 throws in 60 seconds → suspend tool. Suspended tool: status transition fails with \"{tool} suspended\"; user must resume manually.\n\n## Host snapshot contract (for apply-tool undo)\n\n```gdscript\n# AnnotationHost virtual\nfunc capture_state_snapshot() -> Variant:\n    # Returns an opaque snapshot the host can restore from.\n    # For text editor: the document text + cursor.\n    # For graphics editor: the layer stack snapshot.\n    # For CAD: the current DSL source.\n    # Default: returns null (no undo support; apply-tools that mutate this host must provide their own undo).\n    return null\n\nfunc restore_state_snapshot(snapshot: Variant) -> bool:\n    # Restore from a snapshot. Returns true on success.\n    # Default: false (no support).\n    return false\n```\n\nHosts that don't implement snapshot lose undo on apply-tool failure — they get the error but the document state is whatever the partial commit left. Document this loudly per host.\n\n## Trust manager API\n\n```gdscript\n# src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationTrustManager.gd\n\nclass_name AnnotationTrustManager extends RefCounted\n\n# Throw recording — substrate calls these from try/catch wrappers\nfunc record_render_throw(kind: String, error: String) -> void\nfunc record_resolver_throw(plugin: String, anchor_type: String, error: String) -> void\nfunc record_apply_tool_throw(tool_id: String, phase: String, error: String) -> void\n\n# Suspension queries — substrate checks before invoking plugin code\nfunc is_kind_suspended(kind: String) -> bool\nfunc is_anchor_type_suspended(plugin: String, anchor_type: String) -> bool\nfunc is_apply_tool_suspended(tool_id: String) -> bool\n\n# Manual control\nfunc resume_kind(kind: String) -> void\nfunc resume_anchor_type(plugin: String, anchor_type: String) -> void\nfunc resume_apply_tool(tool_id: String) -> void\n\n# Diagnostics\nfunc get_suspensions() -> Array  # [{type, name, throw_count, suspended_at, error_samples}]\nfunc get_throw_history(window_sec: int = 60) -> Array\n```\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationTrustManager.gd`\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationHost.gd` — `capture_state_snapshot` / `restore_state_snapshot` virtuals\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationCanvas.gd` — render loop wraps each `kind.render` call in try/catch dispatching to trust manager\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationResolveCache.gd` (task #5) — caches throws as stale entries with TTL\n- MCP layer (task #8) — `update_status → applied` invokes apply-tool through trust-aware wrapper\n- Sidebar — surfaces suspensions with \"Resume\" affordance\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Throwing kind.render: substrate renders `[!]` badge instead of crashing; trust manager records throw; logs to telemetry.\n2. After 5 render throws in 60s for the same kind: kind suspended; sidebar diagnostic; remaining annotations of that kind render as suspended-placeholder.\n3. Throwing resolver: anchor's cache entry stores error, marked stale; rendered as broken via task #4 path; trust manager records.\n4. After 5 resolver throws for the same anchor type: anchor type suspended; all annotations with that anchor type render as broken.\n5. Throwing apply-tool dry-run: status transition fails; document untouched; error surfaced.\n6. Throwing apply-tool commit: substrate calls `host.restore_state_snapshot` if available; surfaces error; status transition rolls back.\n7. After 3 apply-tool throws in 60s: tool suspended; user must resume.\n8. Trust manager API testable in isolation; suspensions persist across calls; resume clears.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- `test_plugin_trust_render.gd` — throwing render renders badge; suspension after N throws\n- `test_plugin_trust_resolver.gd` — throwing resolver caches stale; suspension after N throws\n- `test_plugin_trust_apply_tool.gd` — dry-run failure / commit failure with snapshot; suspension after N throws\n- `test_plugin_trust_rate_limit.gd` — verify N=5/3 thresholds and 5-min cooldown\n- Smoke (#10): not exercised end-to-end; smoke uses built-in text editor which doesn't throw\n\n## Related\n\n- Depends on cache (#5) — resolver throws cached as stale\n- Depends on broken UX (#4) — suspended kinds/anchors surface via broken pathway\n- Depends on MCP (#8) — apply-tool dispatch goes through trust wrapper\n- Independent of envelope (#1) and registry (#2) — pure runtime concern","created_at":"2026-04-29T23:02:23","updated_at":"2026-04-30T01:23:34","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","fail-containment","plugins","trust","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddbcfeef774bbbc012588ab6aaf10","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Fix stale libterminal stubs shadowing v2 envelope GDScript + harden contract tests with behavior","description":"## TL;DR\n\nRound 2a of the v2 substrate plan (envelope task `019ddb7593467eef`) reported **42/42 GREEN** on `src/test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd`. **The pass is a false positive.** A stale compiled native binary (`src/bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug`) still has C++ stub registrations for `AnnotationV2Schema`, `AnnotationLifecycle`, `AnnotationAuthor`. Those native stubs **shadow** the new GDScript classes — Godot logs `Parse Error: Class \"AnnotationV2Schema\" hides a native class.` and the GDScript never loads. The tests then resolve against the native stub. Compounding the problem, the contract tests are existence-only — they call `ClassDB.class_exists(...)` and `class_has_method(...)` but never call any method with arguments, so a hollow native shadow passes everything.\n\nTwo distinct fixes are needed; both must land before any further v2 implementation work, because the binary will keep shadowing future classes too.\n\n---\n\n## Background — how we got here\n\nIn session `59ecc929-...` (commit `76d4b8e4` shipped Round 1 RED tests). Round 2a launched a Sonnet implementer for envelope. The implementer:\n\n1. Wrote three correct GDScript files (`AnnotationV2Schema.gd`, `AnnotationLifecycle.gd`, `AnnotationAuthor.gd`) under `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/`. **These are good — keep.**\n2. Then, on a wrong premise that `ClassDB.class_exists()` doesn't return true for `class_name`-declared GDScript, added:\n   - `src/gdextension/terminal/annotation_v2.h`\n   - `src/gdextension/terminal/annotation_v2.cpp`\n   - Modifications to `src/SConstruct` (added the .cpp to sources)\n   - Modifications to `src/gdextension/terminal/register_types.cpp` (added `ClassDB::register_class<AnnotationV2Schema>()` etc.)\n3. **Built the extension** — produced an updated `libterminal.macos.template_debug` containing the native stubs. (The stubs themselves are buggy: `validate({})` returns `<null>`, round-trip serialize drops `anchored_to`. These are visible on inspection but never exercised by tests because the tests only check existence.)\n\nThe orchestrator then probed `ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")` → `true` and incorrectly concluded the C++ scaffolding was unnecessary. Reverted `SConstruct` and `register_types.cpp`, deleted `annotation_v2.{h,cpp}`. **Did not rebuild** — so the compiled binary still contains the stubs.\n\nThe Opus reviewer caught both bugs by `strings | grep N5godot18AnnotationV2SchemaE` on the binary, which confirmed the registrations are present.\n\n---\n\n## Repo state at handoff\n\nBranch: `user/imran/experiments/swarm`. HEAD commit `76d4b8e4` (Round 1 RED scaffolding).\n\n**Tracked working-tree changes** (`git status`): only `Docs/minerva.dct` modified (docket transitions accumulated this session) plus pre-existing vendor submodule drift.\n\n**Untracked files** (Round 2a deliverables — keep):\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationV2Schema.gd`\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationLifecycle.gd`\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationAuthor.gd`\n\n**Local-only contamination** (not in git, but present on disk and shadowing GDScript):\n- `src/bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug` — contains stale `AnnotationV2Schema/Lifecycle/Author` native registrations. (Path is gitignored via `bin/` rule, so a fresh clone does not have this problem; but the *current laptop* does.)\n\n**Docket state** (set this session, but no real implementation work happened on these):\n- `019ddb7593467eef` (envelope) → `in_progress`\n- `019ddb75ffad7dd8` (anchor registry) → `in_progress`\n- `019ddb767fe871ba` (resolve_anchor) → `in_progress`\n\nThe latter two had no implementer work — only the orchestrator's transition. They should be returned to a clean state on pickup (transition to `blocked` on this fix-up item).\n\n---\n\n## Verification — confirm the bug before fixing\n\nFrom `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src`:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Inspect the binary for stale symbols.\nstrings bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug | grep -E \"AnnotationV2Schema|AnnotationLifecycle|AnnotationAuthor\"\n```\n\nIf any matches appear, the stale stubs are present. (Mangled names look like `_ZTVN5godot18AnnotationV2SchemaE`, etc.)\n\n```bash\n# 2. Confirm Godot logs the parse-error shadow at startup.\ngodot --headless --path . --script test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd 2>&1 | grep -i \"hides a native class\\|Parse Error\"\n```\n\nExpect lines like `Parse Error: Class \"AnnotationV2Schema\" hides a native class.` if the stubs are still there.\n\n```bash\n# 3. Behavioral probe — proves the GDScript impl is dead.\ncat > /tmp/probe.gd <<'PROBE'\nextends SceneTree\nfunc _init():\n\tvar cls = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\t# In live GDScript impl, validate({}) returns a ValidationResult with errors.\n\t# In the dead native stub, it returns null (or some other broken value).\n\tvar r = cls.call(\"validate\", {})\n\tprint(\"validate({}): \", typeof(r), \" \", r)\n\t# Lifecycle illegal transition: applied -> open should be false.\n\tvar lc = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationLifecycle\")\n\tprint(\"can_transition(applied,open): \", lc.call(\"can_transition\", \"applied\", \"open\"))\n\tquit()\nPROBE\ngodot --headless --path . --script /tmp/probe.gd\n```\n\nIf `validate({})` prints `Nil <null>` or similar, the dead native stub is in control.\n\n---\n\n## Fix 1 — Evict the stale native stubs\n\nThe native build is governed by `scripts/build-extensions.sh`. From repo root:\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva\nscripts/build-extensions.sh macos\n```\n\nThis rebuilds the ghostty-vt shim and the terminal GDExtension from the **post-revert** `register_types.cpp` (which no longer registers annotation classes) and writes a fresh `libterminal.macos.template_debug` to `src/bin/`. After build:\n\n```bash\n# Confirm the symbols are gone.\nstrings src/bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug | grep -c \"AnnotationV2Schema\"\n# Expect: 0\n```\n\nThen re-run the envelope test:\n\n```bash\ncd src && godot --headless --path . --script test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd 2>&1 | grep -E \"Results|FAIL\"\n```\n\nThe expected outcome is **still 42/42 GREEN** — but now backed by the GDScript impl, with no `hides a native class` warnings in the output. If anything fails, that's a real GDScript bug to fix in `AnnotationV2Schema.gd` etc.\n\nIf `scripts/build-extensions.sh` errors (zig version mismatch, missing scons, etc.), follow the bootstrap flow in `CLAUDE.md` § \"First-time Setup\". A clean rebuild of `libterminal` + `libminerva-vt` is needed; partial builds may leave stale objects.\n\n**Sanity sweep across the rest of the suite:**\n\n```bash\nbash test/annotations_v2/run_all.sh 2>&1 | tail -5\n```\n\nExpect numbers similar to \"43 passed, 311 failed\" (1 transitive +1 in `test_kind_anchor_compat.gd`). If the total *passed* count differs significantly from the pre-rebuild number, investigate — something else may have been shadowed too.\n\n**v1 regression sanity:**\n\n```bash\ngodot --headless --path . --script test/test_annotation_substrate.gd 2>&1 | grep -E \"Results|FAIL\"\n```\n\nExpect `=== Results: 146 passed, 0 failed ===`.\n\n---\n\n## Fix 2 — Harden `test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd` with behavioral assertions\n\nPath: `src/test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd`.\n\nThe current pattern is:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc test_envelope_validate_minimal() -> void:\n\tprint(\"test_envelope_validate_minimal:\")\n\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"))\n```\n\nThat asserts only existence. A hollow shadow passes. Replace each test with the actual call described by its name and assert on the **return value**. Examples (apply throughout):\n\n```gdscript\nfunc test_envelope_validate_minimal_passes() -> void:\n\tprint(\"test_envelope_validate_minimal_passes:\")\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar r = schema.validate(_valid_envelope())\n\tcheck(\"valid envelope has no errors\", not r.has_errors())\n\nfunc test_envelope_validate_empty_dict_errors() -> void:\n\t# Hollow-native trap — empty dict MUST surface required-field errors.\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar r = schema.validate({})\n\tcheck(\"empty envelope has errors\", r != null and r.has_errors())\n\tcheck(\"required: id\", _has_error_for(r, \"id\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: kind\", _has_error_for(r, \"kind\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: schema_version\", _has_error_for(r, \"schema_version\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: anchor\", _has_error_for(r, \"anchor\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: lifecycle\", _has_error_for(r, \"lifecycle\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: author\", _has_error_for(r, \"author\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: summary\", _has_error_for(r, \"summary\"))\n\tcheck(\"required: view_context\", _has_error_for(r, \"view_context\"))\n\nfunc test_anchored_to_round_trips_through_serialize() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar env = _valid_envelope()\n\t# anchor.plugin=core, type=text.range, id={start:5,end:10}\n\tvar serial = schema.serialize(env)\n\tcheck(\"serializer strips anchored_to\", not serial.has(\"anchored_to\"))\n\tvar round = schema.deserialize(serial)\n\tcheck(\"deserializer recomputes anchored_to\", round.has(\"anchored_to\"))\n\t# Spec: \"{plugin}.{type}:{id}\" — id may stringify differently; just assert non-empty + plugin prefix.\n\tcheck(\"anchored_to starts with anchor.plugin\",\n\t\t(round[\"anchored_to\"] as String).begins_with(\"core.text.range:\"))\n\nfunc test_lifecycle_illegal_transition_rejected() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationLifecycle\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationLifecycle exists\", false); return\n\tvar lc = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationLifecycle\")\n\tcheck(\"applied -> open illegal\", not lc.can_transition(\"applied\", \"open\"))\n\tcheck(\"resolved -> applied illegal\", not lc.can_transition(\"resolved\", \"applied\"))\n\tcheck(\"stale -> resolved illegal\", not lc.can_transition(\"stale\", \"resolved\"))\n\tcheck(\"open -> open illegal (no self)\", not lc.can_transition(\"open\", \"open\"))\n\nfunc test_lifecycle_legal_transitions_accepted() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationLifecycle\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationLifecycle exists\", false); return\n\tvar lc = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationLifecycle\")\n\tcheck(\"open -> applied legal\", lc.can_transition(\"open\", \"applied\"))\n\tcheck(\"open -> resolved legal\", lc.can_transition(\"open\", \"resolved\"))\n\tcheck(\"open -> stale legal\", lc.can_transition(\"open\", \"stale\"))\n\tcheck(\"applied -> resolved legal\", lc.can_transition(\"applied\", \"resolved\"))\n\tcheck(\"resolved -> open legal\", lc.can_transition(\"resolved\", \"open\"))\n\tcheck(\"stale -> open legal\", lc.can_transition(\"stale\", \"open\"))\n\nfunc test_schema_version_one_rejected_by_v2() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar env = _valid_envelope(); env[\"schema_version\"] = 1\n\tvar r = schema.validate(env)\n\tcheck(\"schema_version=1 rejected\", r.has_errors())\n\tcheck(\"error path on schema_version\", _has_error_for(r, \"schema_version\"))\n\nfunc test_author_kind_required_and_enum() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationAuthor\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationAuthor exists\", false); return\n\tvar auth = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationAuthor\")\n\tcheck(\"missing kind errors\", auth.validate({}).size() > 0)\n\tcheck(\"kind=robot rejected\", auth.validate({\"kind\": \"robot\"}).size() > 0)\n\tcheck(\"kind=human accepted\", auth.validate({\"kind\": \"human\"}).size() == 0)\n\tcheck(\"kind=ai accepted\", auth.validate({\"kind\": \"ai\"}).size() == 0)\n\nfunc test_summary_empty_rejected() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar env = _valid_envelope(); env[\"summary\"] = \"\"\n\tcheck(\"empty summary rejected\", schema.validate(env).has_errors())\n\nfunc test_view_context_immutability_helper() -> void:\n\tif not ClassDB.class_exists(\"AnnotationV2Schema\"):\n\t\tcheck(\"AnnotationV2Schema exists\", false); return\n\tvar schema = ClassDB.instantiate(\"AnnotationV2Schema\")\n\tvar old_env = _valid_envelope()\n\tvar new_env = _valid_envelope(); new_env[\"view_context\"] = \"different\"\n\tvar errs = schema.check_view_context_immutable(old_env, new_env)\n\tcheck(\"view_context change errors\", errs.size() > 0)\n```\n\nAdd a `_valid_envelope()` fixture helper at the bottom (mirroring `test_annotation_substrate.gd:183` style) producing the canonical literal from the docket envelope spec — id, kind, schema_version=2, anchor (`{plugin: \"core\", type: \"text.range\", id: {start:5,end:10}, snapshot: {position: [0,0], text: \"hello\", document_revision: 1}}`), kind_payload, lifecycle=\"open\", author={kind:\"human\"}, view_context, visible_in_views, summary, created_at, updated_at.\n\nRe-run after editing:\n```bash\ngodot --headless --path . --script test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd 2>&1 | grep -E \"Results|FAIL\"\n```\n\nGoal: still 42/42 GREEN (or higher count if you add tests). If anything fails after the binary is rebuilt, fix `AnnotationV2Schema.gd` accordingly. Behavioral coverage means future shadow-stubs cannot pass.\n\n**Apply the same hardening pattern across the other Round-1 test files when their respective rounds (3/4/5) implement against them.** Save a nudge hint reminder under `minerva-testing` keyed `annotations-v2-existence-only-trap` referencing this work item.\n\n---\n\n## Fix 3 (cleanup) — Docket state\n\nAfter Fix 1 + Fix 2 are verified green:\n\n1. Transition this work item `done` with resolution citing the rebuild + new test counts + the commit SHA.\n2. The three Round-2 children currently stuck `in_progress`:\n   - `019ddb7593467eef` (envelope) — verified green at this point; transition to `done` with the new commit SHA + behavioral test counts in resolution.\n   - `019ddb75ffad7dd8` (anchor registry) — no work done; transition to `blocked` immediately on this item, then back to `in_progress` for Round 2b.\n   - `019ddb767fe871ba` (resolve_anchor) — same pattern.\n\n   (Work-item state machine: backlog → open → in_progress → blocked → done. Cannot return to `open` from `in_progress`. Use `blocked` as the parking state if you don't want to ship them yet.)\n\n3. Save a nudge hint:\n   - component: `minerva-build`\n   - key: `libterminal-rebuild-after-revert`\n   - value: \"When reverting C++ changes under `src/gdextension/terminal/`, also rebuild via `scripts/build-extensions.sh <platform>` to evict stale registrations from `src/bin/libterminal.<platform>.template_debug{.framework}`. Source revert alone is insufficient — the compiled binary persists. Symptom: `Parse Error: Class \"X\" hides a native class` at GDScript load.\"\n\n---\n\n## Files involved (full list, absolute paths)\n\nModify:\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd` — replace existence-only assertions with behavior (Fix 2)\n- (After rebuild) regenerate `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug` (Fix 1)\n\nRead for context (do not modify):\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationV2Schema.gd`\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationLifecycle.gd`\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationAuthor.gd`\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/test/test_annotation_substrate.gd` (v1 test pattern reference; especially `_valid_annotation()` fixture style at line 183)\n\nBuild:\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/scripts/build-extensions.sh`\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/SConstruct` (already reverted; sanity-check)\n- `/Users/ipeerbhai/github/Minerva/src/gdextension/terminal/register_types.cpp` (already reverted; sanity-check no annotation registrations)\n\nSpec:\n- `mcp__docket__docket_get id=\"019ddb7593467eef\"` — envelope spec (canonical literal, lifecycle SM, 7 acceptance criteria)\n- `mcp__docket__docket_get id=\"019ddb70ca45\"` — plan body (Tier-1 + Tier-2 decisions)\n\n---\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `strings src/bin/libterminal.macos.template_debug.framework/libterminal.macos.template_debug | grep -c \"AnnotationV2Schema\"` returns `0`.\n2. Running `godot --headless --path . --script test/annotations_v2/test_annotation_v2_envelope.gd` produces no `hides a native class` lines.\n3. Same run reports ≥ 42 PASS / 0 FAIL with hardened (behavioral) assertions covering the eight behavioral cases listed in Fix 2.\n4. v1 regression: `test_annotation_substrate.gd` still 146/146.\n5. Full suite `bash test/annotations_v2/run_all.sh` shows `EXIT: PARTIAL` only because of intentional reds in unrelated round-3+ files; total passed count ≥ pre-fix baseline (43).\n6. Nudge hint `minerva-build/libterminal-rebuild-after-revert` saved.\n7. Docket state for the three round-2 children correctly reflects work in progress vs blocked.\n\n---\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3c (auto-verified). All checks above are mechanical.\n\n---\n\n## Why this is filed as its own item\n\nCould have been a comment on `019ddb7593467eef`, but: (a) the fix touches the build system + tests separately from envelope production code, (b) downstream rounds will hit the same shadow trap if any of them are stubbed natively, so the contract-test hardening is generally applicable, (c) Round 2b (anchor registry + resolve_anchor) is gated on this. Treating it as a dependency makes the gate visible.","created_at":"2026-04-30T00:35:30","updated_at":"2026-04-30T00:47:34","priority":1,"tags":["annotations","blocker","build","envelope","tests","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddbdaa1b77ff5a4b94920c9298caf","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Rebuild libterminal after reverting terminal C++ registrations","created_at":"2026-04-30T00:47:11","updated_at":"2026-04-30T00:48:06","tags":["annotations","build","gdextension","native-shadow"],"value":"When reverting C++ changes under `src/gdextension/terminal/`, also rebuild via `scripts/build-extensions.sh <platform>` to evict stale registrations from `src/bin/libterminal.<platform>.template_debug{.framework}`. Source revert alone is insufficient; the compiled binary persists. Symptom: `Parse Error: Class \"X\" hides a native class` at GDScript load.","component":"minerva-build","key":"libterminal-rebuild-after-revert","confidence":"confirmed","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddbdaa6f37de38ba5a972e106f128","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Annotations v2 tests must assert behavior, not existence","created_at":"2026-04-30T00:47:13","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:33:19","tags":["annotations","native-shadow","tests","v2"],"value":"Do not declare annotations-v2 tests green when they only assert `ClassDB.class_exists()` or `class_has_method()`. Stale native GDExtension registrations can satisfy those checks while shadowing dead GDScript. Tests must preload/call the GDScript behavior and assert return values.","component":"minerva-testing","key":"annotations-v2-existence-only-trap","confidence":"confirmed","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddc26ac2b7f04a7da078a1cf8cd27","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Round 5a: TextEditorAnnotationHost + add_comment + sidecar persistence","description":"## Goal\n\nWire the built-in code editor (`src/Scenes/Editor.tscn` / `src/Scripts/Editor.gd`) to the v2 substrate well enough to **add a comment on a selection, save it, reload, and see it back**. End of round 5a's HITL is \"comment survives reload.\"\n\nThis is the first of three Round 5 sub-rounds. Subsequent: 5b (break + repair UX), 5c (MCP query/update_status round-trip).\n\n## Why split\n\nThe full Round 5 contract in `019ddb7a0f407621` covers six sub-deliverables. Doing them in one work-cycle round means one long HITL session at the end. Splitting into three rounds gives three independently-verifiable HITL stops, smaller diffs, and faster reviewer turnaround.\n\n## Scope (this round)\n\nIn:\n- `TextEditorAnnotationHost` subclass of `AnnotationHost` — implements `resolve_anchor` for `core/text.range`, owns text-buffer revision counter, implements `capture_state_snapshot` / `restore_state_snapshot` (text + cursor).\n- `editor.add_comment(text)` — reads current selection, builds `core/text.range` anchor `{plugin: \"core\", type: \"text.range\", id: {start, end}, snapshot: {position: [line, col], text: \"<selected substring>\", document_revision: N}}`, adds annotation via host with kind=`text` and payload=`{text}`, lifecycle=`open`, author=`{kind: \"human\"}`.\n- Context menu / keyboard shortcut binding for \"Add Comment\" — exact UX TBD during impl, but must be reachable without typing into a console.\n- Sidecar IO — `<file>.annotations.json` written on Ctrl+S, loaded on file open. Use `AnnotationSidecarIO.load_sidecar` / `save_sidecar_v2` from Round 4 migration work (task `019ddb7960417194`).\n- Visible rendering — annotations render as a marker in the gutter or inline overlay (whatever is least invasive); clicking surfaces summary + lifecycle in the sidebar. Exact visual TBD; substrate already has `AnnotationCanvas` + `AnnotationKind.render` from earlier rounds.\n\nOut (deferred):\n- Break detection and broken indicator → 5b\n- Repair UX → 5b\n- MCP `minerva_annotations_query` round-trip with chat_context → 5c\n- `minerva_annotations_update_status` lifecycle transitions from MCP → 5c\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd`\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/Editor.gd` — instantiate the host, expose `add_comment`, wire up sidecar load/save into the editor's existing open/save paths, add menu entry / shortcut.\n- Modified: `src/Scenes/Editor.tscn` — overlay placement for `AnnotationCanvas` (parented above the TextEdit, transparent, mouse-passthrough except over annotations).\n- Probably touched: `src/Scripts/Models/singleton_object.gd` — wherever editor tabs are created/destroyed (host lifecycle is per-tab).\n\n## Acceptance criteria (HITL — stop condition 3b)\n\nUser verifies in the live editor:\n\n1. Open a `.gd` file. Select a range of text. Use the Add Comment affordance. A comment with text \"test\" attaches to the selection.\n2. Comment appears in the annotation sidebar with the right summary and `lifecycle: open`.\n3. Save (Ctrl+S). A `.annotations.json` sidecar appears next to the source file.\n4. Close + reopen the editor tab. Comment still there, anchored to the same range.\n5. Add a second comment on a different range. Both persist independently.\n6. Sidecar file content is valid JSON, schema_version=2, anchor.plugin=\"core\", anchor.type=\"text.range\".\n\nLayer-1 verification (mechanical):\n\n- `test_text_editor_annotation_smoke.gd`: at least the first 3-4 tests (`test_text_editor_has_annotation_host`, `test_text_editor_add_comment_via_selection`, `test_text_editor_comment_persists_via_save`) flip to PASS. Tests that depend on break/repair/MCP stay RED — those are 5b/5c.\n- `test_annotation_substrate.gd` (v1): still 146/0 — no regression.\n- Full v2 suite: total PASS count strictly greater than 484 baseline; no new FAIL.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- Manual HITL: see acceptance criteria above.\n- Existing smoke contract: `src/test/annotations_v2/test_text_editor_annotation_smoke.gd` (currently 0/22). At least the host-instantiation + add-comment + persist-via-save subset turns green.\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3b — HITL. User verifies the live editor flow before round closes.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent plan: `019ddb70ca45`\n- Smoke contract source: `019ddb7a0f407621` (Round 1 RED scaffolding)\n- Depends on (all done): envelope `019ddb7593467eef`, anchor registry `019ddb75ffad7dd8`, resolve_anchor `019ddb767fe871ba`, cache `019ddb77576b7b56`, broken UX `019ddb76ed897520`, kind/anchor policy `019ddb77c4b37516`, chat context `019ddb7843b4742d`, MCP `019ddb78c40c74bc`, migration `019ddb7960417194`, trust `019ddb7aaf247033`.\n- Successors: 5b (break + repair), 5c (MCP round-trip).","created_at":"2026-04-30T02:10:15","updated_at":"2026-04-30T03:36:40","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","hitl","round5","smoke","text-editor","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45","resolution":"HITL verified via MCP at /tmp/annot_smoke.txt. Sequence: open_file → list (0) → add_comment(10,25,\"rephrase this\") → ann_0001 count=1 → save → sidecar JSON valid v2 → close_editor force=true → open_file → list returns ann_0001 from sidecar reload → add_comment(45,66,\"stale comment about line two\") → ann_0002 count=2 → save → sidecar contains both annotations with distinct ids. Commits: ddac6c87 (host + sidecar + lazy-static-preload), d32e5a11 (MCP entry point + AnnotationHostRegistry wiring), 90547ee5 (id-before-validate fix), a70c87ae (init host before _load_text_file), 13bde812 (per-host kind registry + id counter bump on load). Follow-ups for 5b: (a) JSON.parse_string returns floats for all numerics; reloaded anchor.id.{start,end} fail _resolve_text_range's `is int` guard and would render stale on first frame after reload — coerce on load OR loosen resolver's type check; (b) minerva_annotations_list (v1 tool) overrides v2 summary with \"text (0 primitives)\" projection — list tool should preserve v2 summary when schema_version=2; (c) production UX: no menu/shortcut binding for add_comment yet, MCP entry point is the only path. v1 substrate 146/0; v2 suite 497/13 (failures all 5b/5c scope)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddc271a857ca0be1daf59bfee1ff0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Round 5b: Break detection + broken indicator + repair UX in text editor","description":"## Goal\n\nMake the text editor's annotation flow survive — and gracefully surface — anchor breakage. When the anchored text range is deleted or substantially altered, render the broken indicator (round 3 broken-UX work) and let the user repair via re-selection.\n\n## Scope (this round)\n\nIn:\n- Break detection — `TextEditorAnnotationHost.resolve_anchor` for `core/text.range`: returns `stale: true` when the anchored range no longer matches the snapshot text. Tolerance policy: exact match required for now (start/end indexes valid + substring at those indexes equals snapshot.text). Stricter heuristics deferred.\n- Revision bumping — text changes call `host.bump_revision()` so the resolve cache (`AnnotationResolveCache` from `019ddb77576b7b56`) re-evaluates.\n- Broken render — annotation kind picks up `ctx.is_stale` (from the resolved anchor) and renders desaturated + ⚠ badge per round 3 broken UX (`019ddb76ed897520`). For text editor: gutter marker turns warning-color; annotation sidebar moves entry into \"Broken (N)\" section.\n- Repair affordance — sidebar \"Repair\" button on broken entries. Substrate provides the affordance hook; for `core/text.range` the repair UX is: prompt user to make a new selection in the editor, on next selection-change update annotation's anchor to new range and snapshot. Cancel via Esc.\n- Status filter pill — sidebar \"Show broken only\" toggle (already a Round 3 deliverable; this round wires it into the text editor sidebar specifically).\n\nOut (deferred to 5c):\n- MCP-driven repair (`minerva_annotations_repair_anchor`)\n- LLM-initiated re-target\n\n## Files affected\n\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd` — implement stale detection in `resolve_anchor`; wire revision-bump on text change.\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/Editor.gd` — connect `text_changed` signal to `host.bump_revision()`, debounced at frame boundary if necessary (cache layer is already lazy by revision counter).\n- Modified: sidebar (likely under `src/Scripts/UI/Annotations/` per round-3 work) — verify \"Broken (N)\" section + Repair button + filter pill render correctly with text-editor data.\n- Possibly new: `src/Scripts/Editor/TextRangeRepairTool.gd` — transient mode entered when user clicks Repair; captures next selection and calls back to the host.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (HITL — stop condition 3b)\n\nUser verifies in the live editor:\n\n1. Open a file with an existing comment (from 5a). Delete the anchored text. Comment immediately renders with broken styling (gutter marker desaturated, ⚠ badge in sidebar).\n2. Annotation sidebar shows \"Broken (1)\" header.\n3. Toggle \"Show broken only\" — only the broken entry visible.\n4. Click \"Repair\" on the broken entry. Editor enters repair mode (visual cue: status bar message \"Select new range to re-anchor comment\").\n5. Make a new selection. Comment re-anchors; broken indicator clears; sidebar moves entry back to active section.\n6. Press Esc during repair mode — repair cancels; entry stays broken.\n7. Save and reload — repaired comment still anchored to new range; previously-broken entries that were not repaired are still broken on reload.\n\nLayer-1 verification:\n\n- `test_text_editor_annotation_smoke.gd`: `test_text_editor_comment_breaks_on_text_delete` and `test_text_editor_comment_repair` turn green.\n- `test_broken_anchor_render.gd`, `test_broken_anchor_sidebar.gd`: still green from round 3.\n- v1 substrate: still 146/0.\n\n## Edge cases to think through\n\n- Partial overlap (user deletes part of anchored range): conservative — treat as broken. Avoid trying to \"shrink\" the range automatically.\n- Re-typing the exact same text in the same place: anchor was already broken on delete; a subsequent insert that produces matching substring should NOT auto-repair. Repair is a deliberate user action.\n- Multiple broken anchors in same file: each repaired independently.\n- Document revision counter must increment per-edit (not per-frame) for resolve cache to work; but resolve is called per-frame, so per-edit is fine.\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3b — HITL. User verifies break-render-repair loop in live editor.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent plan: `019ddb70ca45`\n- Predecessor: 5a (host + add_comment + sidecar)\n- Successor: 5c (MCP round-trip)\n- Builds on broken-anchor UX (`019ddb76ed897520`), resolve cache (`019ddb77576b7b56`), trust boundary (`019ddb7aaf247033`).","created_at":"2026-04-30T02:10:43","updated_at":"2026-04-30T15:03:00","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","broken-anchors","hitl","round5","smoke","text-editor","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45","resolution":"5b.i: TextEditorAnnotationCanvas + snapshot-text equality + revision bump on text_changed. 5b.ii: TextEditorAnnotationSidebar + AnnotationSidebarModel wiring + retarget mode on Editor + retarget_annotation on host. Healthy-anchor underline render added during HITL. v1 substrate 146/0; v2 suite 500/10 (unchanged remainders are 5c-scope MCP probes and headless Editor instantiability)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddc27b59b7a57a6ca87e428defc0d","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Round 5c: MCP query / update_status round-trip from text editor (the LLM apply flow)","description":"## Goal\n\nClose the loop on the Annotation v2 substrate: an LLM, via MCP, **discovers** open comments in a text editor, **acts** on one (proposes/applies a text edit), and **marks it applied** with backreferences to the entities it created. The round's HITL goal is the canonical \"select text → add comment → ask LLM to apply → comment shows applied\" flow working end-to-end with a real provider.\n\n## Scope (this round)\n\nIn:\n- `minerva_annotations_query` ergonomics for text editor — confirm `editor_name` filter resolves text-editor tabs by their visible name; add `document_path` filter; verify `capabilities` projection produces `chat_context` block array per round 4 chat-context work (`019ddb7843b4742d`).\n- `minerva_annotations_update_status` callable from text editor flow — when LLM transitions an annotation `open → applied` with `applied.links` referencing text-edit operations, persist the new status; sidebar updates immediately; sidecar saves on next Ctrl+S (or on update — TBD, prefer immediate persistence to avoid losing applied state on crash).\n- `minerva_annotations_repair_anchor` callable for text-range — null `new_anchor` is rejected for text editor (no plugin-defined repair beyond user re-selection from 5b); admin form accepts a new `{start, end}` and updates directly. Useful for LLM \"re-target this comment to lines 40-45.\"\n- Apply-tool hook (optional, decide during impl) — `minerva_annotations_register_apply_hook(plugin: \"core\", kind: \"text\", callable_name: ...)` so a built-in tool can post-process a transition (e.g. log a structured edit event). If skipped this round, file as a follow-up; nothing in 5c blocks on it.\n- LLM-facing context shape — annotation entries returned from query include the structured_json action contract; manual smoke chat with Sonnet/Opus or local model verifies the LLM can pick up an open comment, propose an edit, call `update_status`.\n\nOut:\n- Apply-tool transactional dry-run/commit plumbing for text editor (Round 3 trust boundary already provides the contract; the text editor doesn't need the full transactional path because text edits are reversible via undo. Document this in the round's resolution.)\n- CAD / PCB / Hello plugin adoption — separate work; v2 substrate is plugin-ready but plugins are not in this plan.\n\n## Files affected\n\n- Verified (likely no changes): `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPAnnotationsTools.gd` — query/update_status/repair_anchor already shipped in round 4 (`019ddb78c40c74bc`).\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/Editor.gd` and/or `TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd` — make sure annotations created via `add_comment` are discoverable by `editor_name` and `document_path` filter shapes used by the LLM.\n- Possibly modified: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationSidecarIO.gd` — flush on `update_status` so applied state is durable without explicit save (decide during impl).\n- Manual: provider config for the smoke session (Anthropic key or local Ollama with tool-use; see `singleton_object.gd` provider plumbing).\n\n## Acceptance criteria (HITL — stop condition 3b)\n\nUser verifies in the live editor with an active LLM session:\n\n1. Open `MyScript.gd`, add two comments via 5a path: \"rename function to `compute_total`\" on a function definition, \"this branch is unreachable\" on a dead-code path.\n2. Ask the LLM in chat: \"Look at the open annotations in MyScript.gd and apply them.\" LLM should call `minerva_annotations_query(editor_name=..., status=\"open\", capabilities={...})` and receive both comments with structured_json + text blocks.\n3. LLM applies the rename via its existing tools. After apply, LLM calls `minerva_annotations_update_status(annotation_id, \"applied\", applied={by: {kind: \"ai\", model: ...}, links: [{kind: \"text.edit\", id: \"<edit-id>\"}]})`.\n4. Editor sidebar updates to show comment lifecycle = `applied` (visual: muted, \"applied by Claude X.Y\" badge or similar).\n5. Save + reload: applied state persists.\n6. Re-query via MCP with `status=\"applied\"`: returns the comment.\n7. Re-query with default status: comment still appears (default is `open`+`applied`). Filter `status=\"open\"` excludes it.\n8. Negative path: LLM tries to call `update_status(id, \"stale\", ...)` — rejected with `illegal transition` (substrate-only).\n\nLayer-1 verification:\n\n- `test_text_editor_annotation_smoke.gd`: `test_text_editor_comment_via_mcp` (last of 22) turns green. Smoke file fully green.\n- `test_mcp_annotations_query.gd`, `test_mcp_annotations_update_status.gd`, `test_mcp_annotations_apply_hook.gd`: still green from round 4.\n- Full v2 suite: 506 passed (484 base + 22 smoke), 0 failed. (Confirms substrate complete.)\n- v1 substrate: 146/0.\n\n## Test groups\n\n- Manual HITL with real LLM provider — see acceptance criteria. Use whichever provider is configured; if multiple, repeat with one multimodal and one text-only to validate capability-shaped chat_context (round 4 deliverable).\n- Existing MCP tests: regression sweep.\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3b — HITL. Final round of v2 substrate. Resolution should record which provider(s) the round-trip was verified against and any LLM-side ergonomics issues observed (those become follow-up items, not blockers).\n\n## Post-round bookkeeping\n\nAfter 5c green, the parent plan `019ddb70ca45` is the place to add a resolution / close-out — every child done, substrate is \"shipped.\" Worth a post-mortem comment summarizing: rounds 1-5 timeline, models used per round, total commits, what we'd do differently. Pickup.md should be updated to reflect substrate-complete state and point at next-tier work (CAD adoption, PCB plugin, etc.) as out-of-scope for this plan.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent plan: `019ddb70ca45`\n- Predecessor: 5b (break + repair)\n- Builds on MCP work (`019ddb78c40c74bc`), chat context (`019ddb7843b4742d`), broken UX (`019ddb76ed897520`).\n- Sibling concern: real plugin adoption (CAD/PCB) is its own DCR — not blocked on this round, but unblocked by it.","created_at":"2026-04-30T02:11:23","updated_at":"2026-04-30T20:37:21","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","hitl","llm","mcp","round5","smoke","text-editor","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddccf60a27c95ab0ecd0b82731629","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Round 5b.iii: Add-comment UX affordance (sidebar button + keyboard shortcut)","description":"## Goal\n\nMake adding a comment a first-class user action in the text editor. As of 5b.ii the only entry point is the `minerva_text_editor_add_comment` MCP tool — there is no menu item, button, or keyboard shortcut a human can use without an LLM in the loop.\n\n## Scope (in)\n\n- **Sidebar \"+\" button** in the TextEditorAnnotationSidebar header. Click with text selected → reads selection from the parent Editor's code_edit, prompts for comment text (small inline LineEdit or modal), calls `editor.add_comment(text)`. Disabled when there is no selection.\n- **Keyboard shortcut** bound on the code_edit (e.g. Ctrl+Shift+M). Same flow as the sidebar button. Action declared in project settings if needed.\n- **Empty-selection feedback**: tooltip on disabled button, status flash on shortcut press without selection.\n\n## Scope (out, deferred)\n\n- Right-click context menu (can land later if needed; substrate already exposes `editor.add_comment`).\n- Inline edit of existing comment text from sidebar (5c or later).\n- LLM-initiated authoring (5c).\n\n## Files affected\n\n- New file: maybe a small `TextEditorAnnotationAddDialog.gd` if we want a modal popup for comment text. Or inline LineEdit in the sidebar.\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/TextEditorAnnotationSidebar.gd` — add header \"+\" button + comment-text input flow.\n- Modified: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Editor.gd` — Ctrl+Shift+M handler in `_on_code_edit_gui_input` that calls `add_comment(prompted_text)` after capturing selection.\n- Possibly modified: `project.godot` — register InputEventAction for the shortcut.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (HITL)\n\n1. Open `/tmp/annot_smoke.txt`. Sidebar header shows a \"+\" button (disabled because no selection).\n2. Select some text in the editor. The \"+\" button enables. Click it. A small text input appears (inline or modal). Type \"test comment\" + Enter. Annotation appears in the sidebar with the typed text as summary; healthy underline renders beneath the selected text.\n3. Save → close → reopen. Annotation persists.\n4. Press Ctrl+Shift+M without a selection. Status flash / tooltip indicates \"select text first\" — no annotation created.\n5. Select different text, press Ctrl+Shift+M. Same flow as button click; annotation added.\n\n## Stop condition\n\n3b — HITL. User verifies the two add paths in the live editor.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent plan: `019ddb70ca45`\n- Predecessor: 5b (`019ddc271a857ca0`) shipped break/repair + sidebar; this finishes the human-author side.\n- Successor: 5c (`019ddc27b59b7a57`) — MCP query/update_status round-trip with LLM provider.","created_at":"2026-04-30T05:14:31","updated_at":"2026-04-30T06:36:40","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","hitl","round5","text-editor","ux","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb70ca45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Annotation UX substrate for editor plugins: callouts, workbench, responsive panes","description":"## Problem\n\nThe Annotation v2 substrate DCR (`019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1`) correctly reframed annotations as structured action-intent with typed semantic anchors, lifecycle, resolver hooks, MCP/chat surfaces, and broken-anchor repair. The current Round 5 text-editor work proves the mechanics, but its UX is a smoke-consumer path, not a product-grade plugin-facing annotation experience.\n\nThis creates friction for plugin authors and users:\n\n- Text-editor annotation UX is minimal: sidebar `+`, inline text input, underline, broken repair. It validates substrate behavior but is not the reusable editor-plugin workbench.\n- CAD previously had better annotation UX: responsive wide/narrow layouts, compact vs labeled toolbar presentation, multi-view/pane-aware rendering, visible-in-views behavior, overlay mouse-filter discipline, domain picker integration, and sidebar/edge selection sync.\n- That better CAD UX mostly lives in the CAD plugin (`~/github/plugins/cad/ui/*`) rather than as reusable substrate API.\n- The v2 DCR captured data/anchor/MCP mechanics, but not enough reusable UX contract for all editor plugins.\n\nWithout a first-class UX substrate, CAD, PCB, Graphics, Presentation, and future editor plugins will each reinvent annotation authoring, display, edit, and repair flows. That repeats the exact fragmentation v2 was meant to avoid.\n\n## Goal\n\nPromote the successful CAD-style annotation UX patterns into a reusable Minerva annotation UX substrate for editor plugins.\n\nThe substrate should provide the common annotation workbench and interaction model; plugins should provide domain-specific anchors, picking, projection, and apply behavior.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. First-class built-in callout kind\n\nAdd a product-grade built-in `callout` annotation kind instead of treating callouts as an informal composition of text + arrow.\n\nA callout should support:\n\n- Anchor point resolved by host.\n- Leader line from anchor to label bubble/card.\n- Text payload.\n- Optional label offset / preferred side.\n- Stable summary for non-multimodal LLMs.\n- Broken-anchor fallback rendering at snapshot with stale indicator.\n- Generic `core/*` anchors where valid.\n\nOpen decision: for plugin semantic anchors (`cad/edge`, `pcb/net`, etc.), either:\n\n- keep Path B from v2: plugins define `cad_edge_callout`, `pcb_net_callout`, etc.; or\n- allow generic `callout` to render plugin anchors when the plugin host can resolve them.\n\nThis DCR should explicitly settle whether `callout` is generic-only or generic-plus-plugin-anchor-capable.\n\n### 2. Shared annotation workbench/sidebar\n\nDefine a reusable editor annotation workbench that plugins can mount.\n\nExpected baseline UX:\n\n- Header with annotation count and broken count.\n- Add button/menu with supported kinds.\n- Filter controls: all/open/applied/resolved/broken, plus kind/anchor filters where useful.\n- List entries with summary, lifecycle, stale state, anchor summary, and view context.\n- Actions: edit text/payload, delete, resolve/reopen, repair/retarget, mark applied where appropriate.\n- Selection sync: selecting an annotation in the list highlights it in the editor surface; clicking rendered annotation selects list row.\n- Empty states that do not crowd the editor.\n\nThis should supersede the narrow text-editor-only sidebar implementation as the common component, while preserving the text editor’s current behavior.\n\n### 3. Shared authoring toolbar behavior\n\nRefine `AnnotationToolbar` into the plugin-facing authoring surface rather than only a v1 carryover.\n\nBaseline behavior:\n\n- Tools section: select, translate/move, rotate, scale where supported.\n- Annotate section: callout, text, arrow, region/highlight, polyline/freehand/ink where supported.\n- Compact and labeled presentation modes.\n- Icon-first controls with tooltips.\n- Mutual exclusion between manipulation tools and authoring tools.\n- Clear active-mode status.\n- Host/plugin capability query so unsupported tools are hidden rather than present-but-broken.\n\n### 4. Pane/view-aware UX contract\n\nLift the useful CAD multi-view patterns into substrate API.\n\nCAD currently does this in plugin code:\n\n- `CadAnnotationHost.get_panes()` returns pane descriptors with `name`, `camera`, and `viewport_rect`.\n- Annotation rendering projects 3D world positions into each visible pane.\n- `visible_in_views` determines where annotations appear.\n- Responsive mode collapses four panes into one active pane and deduplicates rendering.\n\nSubstrate should provide a generic contract such as:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc get_annotation_panes() -> Array[Dictionary]\n# each: {id, display_name, viewport_rect, active, projection_metadata}\n```\n\nThe substrate remains projection-agnostic. Plugins own projection/camera math, but the shared workbench understands panes/views, active pane, and visibility filters.\n\n### 5. Overlay input/mouse-filter contract\n\nDocument and enforce the overlay interaction rules discovered during CAD work:\n\n- Idle overlay must not block editor interactions.\n- Active authoring tool may claim input.\n- `Control.MOUSE_FILTER_PASS` does not forward to siblings below; use `IGNORE` when idle and `STOP` only while a tool is active.\n- Tool activation/deactivation must restore input state reliably.\n- Keyboard cancel/delete paths should be consistent across plugins.\n\nThis should become a substrate helper or documented base class, not plugin folklore.\n\n### 6. Domain picker extension points\n\nPlugins need to plug in domain-specific target selection without rebuilding the workbench.\n\nExamples:\n\n- CAD edge picker: selected edge or clicked edge becomes `cad/edge` anchor.\n- PCB bus/net/pad picker: selected net/bus/pad becomes `pcb/*` anchor.\n- Graphics region/layer picker: selection rectangle/layer becomes `graphics/*` anchor.\n- Text range picker: selected text becomes `core/text.range` anchor.\n\nThe workbench should ask the host for available anchor sources and current selection anchors, then create the selected kind with the plugin-supplied anchor.\n\n### 7. Text editor migration to shared UX\n\nAfter the shared workbench exists, adapt the current text-editor smoke UX to use it.\n\nCurrent behavior to preserve:\n\n- Select text -> add comment/callout.\n- Healthy underline/rendering.\n- Broken indicator when selected text disappears.\n- Repair by selecting a new range.\n- Sidecar persistence.\n- MCP query/update status behavior from 5c.\n\nBut the UI should no longer be a one-off `TextEditorAnnotationSidebar` if the shared workbench can replace it.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- CAD plugin adoption of v2 semantic anchors. This DCR prepares the UX substrate; CAD adoption remains its own follow-on work.\n- PCB-as-plugin extraction. PCB adopts this UX substrate when its own plugin migration DCR runs.\n- Full graphics editor annotations. This DCR should define the shared UX path, not complete graphics integration.\n- Real-time collaboration, merge/conflict UX, encryption/redaction, and bulk operations.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `callout` exists as a first-class built-in kind or the DCR explicitly decides why callout remains plugin-specific; either way the decision is documented and enforced in tests.\n2. A reusable annotation workbench/sidebar component exists and can be mounted by at least the text editor and a plugin panel test harness.\n3. The workbench supports add/edit/delete/resolve/reopen/repair affordances where host capability allows.\n4. `AnnotationToolbar` exposes compact/labeled modes and capability filtering for plugin hosts.\n5. Pane/view metadata is exposed through a substrate contract; a test harness demonstrates wide multi-pane and collapsed single-pane behavior without CAD-specific code in the workbench.\n6. Overlay mouse-filter behavior is covered by tests or a focused smoke harness so authoring tools do not block normal editor interactions when inactive.\n7. Text editor moves from one-off sidebar UX toward the shared component without regressing current HITL flows.\n8. Documentation explains the split: substrate UX shell vs plugin domain picker/projection/apply behavior.\n\n## Implementation notes from existing CAD work\n\nUseful source patterns to harvest:\n\n- CAD responsive layout and toolbar reparenting: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd`\n- CAD pane descriptors and viewport rect computation: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationHost.gd`\n- CAD full-panel overlay and idle/active mouse-filter handling: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd` + `CadAnnotationCanvas.gd`\n- CAD callout rendering with leader+bubble and `visible_in_views`: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd`\n- CAD domain picker pattern: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/tools/cad_edge_number_tool.gd`\n- Existing substrate toolbar: `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationToolbar.gd`\n- Existing responsive helper: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/responsive_container.gd`\n- Existing text smoke UX: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/TextEditorAnnotationSidebar.gd`, `TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd`, `Editor.gd`\n\n## Relationship to parent\n\nParent: `019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1` — First-class editor annotations: action-intent envelope + plugin semantic anchors.\n\nThis DCR is a follow-up because the parent established the annotation data/anchor/MCP substrate but did not fully capture reusable product-grade UX for editor plugins.\n\n## Suggested sequencing\n\nDo this before serious CAD/PCB/Graphics adoption of v2 annotations.\n\nRound 5c can finish first if it stays small, but plugin adoption should not build against the current text-editor smoke UX. The current UX validates mechanics; this DCR creates the reusable product surface.","created_at":"2026-04-30T15:56:19","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:38:27","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","callouts","editor-plugins","plugins","substrate","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: Annotation UX substrate dock/workbench implementation, text editor first consumer","description":"## Goal\n\nImplement the reusable annotation UX substrate described by DCR `019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede`, using the text editor as the first real consumer.\n\n## Scoping Decisions\n\nAccepted from DCR comment 284:\n\n- Annotations live in a per-editor docked tool pane, not an ad hoc inline sidebar.\n- Narrow mode docks bottom; medium/wide docks right. Reparenting is manual, not automatic on every resize.\n- Dock visibility and size are per-editor state.\n- A Blender-style edge chevron summons/collapses the pane.\n- The shared surface has two roles: workbench controls/list/lifecycle and content host for rich annotation bodies.\n- Soft resolve hides from default view but keeps data in sidecar; reopen is via MCP/workbench, not editor undo.\n- Display numbering is stable and gap-preserving.\n- Consumers mount one substrate-owned workbench by handing it an `AnnotationHost` implementation; they do not subclass the workbench.\n- Text editor is the first consumer; CAD/PCB/Graphics adoption follows after the substrate is proven.\n\n## Implementation Order\n\n1. Revise the `AnnotationHost` contract and capability schema.\n2. Add persistent display numbering.\n3. Unify annotation store, sidecar ownership, and MCP routing.\n4. Build docked annotation pane primitive.\n5. Build shared `AnnotationWorkbench`.\n6. Build shared overlay base.\n7. Refine `AnnotationToolbar` for host capabilities.\n8. Implement first-class callout kind and settle generic/plugin anchor scope.\n9. Add pane/view and domain picker contracts.\n10. Migrate text editor to shared workbench.\n11. Document and run HITL regression.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nThe text editor uses the shared dock/workbench/overlay path without losing current line/range/broken/repair/MCP behavior. A plugin-panel test harness demonstrates mounting the same workbench through host capabilities, panes/projection, and domain picker contracts.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:12:37","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:22:16","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","callouts","dock-pane","editor-plugins","implementation","plan","substrate","text-editor","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03db31c7bfea575dd3e8bddd2af","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Revise AnnotationHost contract and capability schema","description":"Define the final mounted-workbench host contract before shared UI lands. Include get_capabilities(), document identity/save policy, resolver/projection/domain-picker composition points, get_panes(), get_kind_body_view(), apply/reopen/resolve hooks. Reconcile with the existing src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationHost.gd rather than introducing a parallel host type.\n\nAcceptance: text editor and plugin harness can implement the host with a small method set; tests cover capability filtering input.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:13:53","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:28:47","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","contract","host","plugins","substrate","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03db8827396b23b25651d75281b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Add persistent stable annotation display numbering","description":"Implement gap-preserving display numbers persisted in the store/sidecar and surfaced in workbench, overlay, and MCP/chat context. Resolve #2 leaves a gap; reopen preserves the old number.\n\nAcceptance: line/range/broken indicators and list rows show the same number after save/reload and resolved/reopened lifecycle transitions.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:13:54","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:28:50","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","mcp","numbering","sidecar","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03dbdf17711816a2fc0bc696056","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Unify annotation store, sidecar ownership, and MCP routing","description":"Collapse or resolve the singular/plural MCP annotation module split (MCPAnnotationTools.gd vs MCPAnnotationsTools.gd), route tools through live hosts or sidecars consistently, and define host document identity/save policy so live status updates can persist safely.\n\nAcceptance: one MCP module registers once; query/update_status/repair/reopen work for live and sidecar paths; resolved query and resolved-to-open are supported.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:13:55","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:35:37","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","lifecycle","mcp","sidecar","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03dc3527650a2d6b9e059e35817","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Build docked annotation pane primitive","description":"Create a per-editor dock pane with right/bottom modes, manual reparent, splitter integration with terminal/narrow stack, per-editor visibility/size state, and a Blender-style chevron affordance.\n\nAcceptance: text editor can mount/dismount the pane without an ad hoc sidebar; pane state is per tab.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:13:57","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:28:53","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","dock-pane","editor-plugins","responsive","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03dc8b772898c83d3909aa86c7e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Build shared AnnotationWorkbench","description":"Build the substrate-owned workbench with header, counts, filters, add menu, lifecycle actions (resolve/reopen/delete/repair/mark applied), list rows, body host, empty states, and selection sync. Consumers mount it; they do not subclass it.\n\nAcceptance: text editor and plugin harness both use the same control.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:13:58","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:28:56","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","editor-plugins","lifecycle","ux","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e20597237a06af4bff284e8cd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Build shared annotation overlay base","description":"Build a base Control for editor surface rendering and input discipline: IGNORE while idle, STOP while active. Include numbered indicators, leader routing, gutter/range/broken visuals, selection sync, and active tool capture/cancel behavior.\n\nAcceptance: test/smoke proves idle overlay does not block editor interactions, active tool can claim input, and deactivation restores state.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:21","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:28:59","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","input","mouse-filter","overlay","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e25f177e7853f0030fc25631a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Refine AnnotationToolbar for host capabilities","description":"Make AnnotationToolbar plugin-facing: compact/labeled modes, capability filtering, tool/annotate sections, mutual exclusion between manipulation and authoring tools, active-mode status, and icon/tooltip controls.\n\nAcceptance: unsupported tools are hidden; text editor and plugin harness show different tool sets from capabilities.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:22","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:29:15","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","capabilities","plugins","toolbar","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e2b5f7dceb8f2bb40ba86b727","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Implement first-class callout kind and settle anchor scope","description":"Decide and implement whether core callout is generic-only for core/* anchors or generic-plus-plugin-anchor-capable. Implement leader line, label/body payload, offset/preferred side, summary/chat context, and broken fallback.\n\nAcceptance: decision is documented and tests enforce the accepted anchor policy.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:23","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:29:17","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","callouts","kinds","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e30ca733384fccb4f3581b69c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Add pane/view and domain picker contracts","description":"Add projection-agnostic pane/view contract with get_panes() descriptors, active pane, visible_in_views filters, and domain picker interfaces. Include plugin harness coverage for multi-pane/collapsed behavior.\n\nAcceptance: CAD-like panes can be represented without CAD-specific workbench code; text range picker is the first concrete picker.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:25","updated_at":"2026-04-30T21:29:20","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","domain-pickers","panes","plugins","projection"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e363d78f9a49b8fd8d447ea91","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Migrate text editor to shared workbench","description":"Replace one-off TextEditorAnnotationSidebar.gd/canvas UX with the shared dock/workbench/overlay while preserving current behavior: line vs range annotations, stable numbering, broken repair, sidecar persistence, and MCP query/update_status.\n\nAcceptance: current HITL flows still pass in Godot; text editor no longer owns bespoke workbench UI.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:26","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:22:10","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","migration","text-editor","ux","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de03e3b9d79bf815bb7945bd89f5c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Document annotation UX substrate and run HITL regression","description":"Document substrate-owned shell versus plugin-owned resolver/picker/projection/apply behavior, lifecycle terminology (stale internal vs Broken UI), MCP query/reopen patterns, and run HITL coverage for line/range comments, multiple same-line ranges, resolved/reopened state, and text-only LLM context.\n\nAcceptance: docs landed; HITL notes are recorded on the plan or DCR.","created_at":"2026-04-30T21:14:28","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:22:13","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","docs","hitl","regression","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de03c8c4c7ec3ab211adbb00ff703"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0b2a3dd7844823781620f428d30","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Repair should transition annotation lifecycle from broken back to open after successful retarget","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced during HITL pass on the annotation UX substrate DCR (`019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede`).\n\nWhen a text-editor annotation goes broken (anchor text no longer matches `[start, end)`) and the user successfully repairs it via the workbench Repair / Retarget flow, the annotation rebinds to its new range and the broken visual indicator clears — but the lifecycle state in the workbench list still shows `broken` instead of returning to `open`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nAfter a successful retarget:\n\n1. Anchor rebinds to the new range (already works).\n2. Broken visual indicator clears (already works).\n3. Lifecycle transitions `broken → open` so the workbench filter chips and list rows reflect a healthy annotation again.\n4. Filter chip \"open\" includes the repaired annotation; \"broken\" no longer does.\n\n## Likely fix location\n\nIn the substrate repair handler — wherever `AnnotationWorkbench` / `MCPAnnotationTools` lands a successful retarget. After the rebind, call the lifecycle state machine's `broken → open` transition. Should be a small addition near the existing repair completion path.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- After repair, workbench list row shows lifecycle `open`, not `broken`.\n- Filter \"open\" includes the repaired annotation; filter \"broken\" does not.\n- Test coverage: unit test that drives anchor → broken → repair → assert lifecycle == open.\n\n## Size estimate\n\nSmall. Likely ~1 hour of code + 1 focused test, assuming the lifecycle state machine already supports `broken → open`.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:21:36","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:38:09","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","lifecycle","polish","repair","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0b313c07a9a9ea57209c80fa7f7","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Annotation anchors should drift with text inserts/deletes instead of breaking on any change","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced during HITL pass on the annotation UX substrate DCR (`019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede`).\n\nThe current `core/text.range` resolver (`src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd::_resolve_text_range`) compares the stored anchor text against whatever currently sits at `[start, end)`. Any mismatch — including pure whitespace shift or text inserted *inside* the range — flips the annotation to broken.\n\nThis is too brittle. Real-world editing constantly inserts and deletes text near or inside annotated ranges. Word, Google Docs, IntelliJ inline comments, and similar tools all *track drift*: anchors follow edits as long as the anchored content is still recoverable, and only break when the user genuinely destroys the target.\n\nThe substrate's break/repair UX is correct; the resolver's policy is too aggressive.\n\n## Goal\n\nReplace static stored-text comparison with a drift-tracking model so anchors survive non-destructive edits and only break when the anchored content is actually destroyed.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. Edit-driven anchor adjustment\n\nSubscribe to `TextEdit` change signals (`text_changed` / `lines_edited_from` or equivalent) and adjust each affected anchor's `start` / `end` deltas live, instead of re-resolving against stored text.\n\n### 2. Policy: what extends, what breaks\n\nThis is the load-bearing design question. Settle each case explicitly:\n\n- Insertion *inside* the range: extend `end`. Anchor grows.\n- Insertion *at start boundary*: extends or splits? Default: extend (anchor absorbs the insertion).\n- Insertion *at end boundary*: extends or stays? Default: stays the same length (insertion is \"after\").\n- Deletion *inside* the range, anchor still has content remaining: shrink `end`. Anchor stays open.\n- Deletion that removes the entire anchor content: anchor breaks.\n- Replacement (delete + insert in same edit) inside the range: shrink + extend. Stays open.\n- Multi-line edit that spans the anchor: case analysis — preserve anchored portion if recoverable.\n\n### 3. Stored payload semantics\n\nDecide whether the stored `text` field should:\n\n- track current anchor content live (rewritten on each accepted drift edit), or\n- stay as the original anchor snapshot, used only as a fallback for orphaned anchors.\n\nOption A simplifies the resolver but makes \"what was the original highlighted text\" irrecoverable. Option B preserves history but adds a payload-vs-position drift concept.\n\n### 4. Undo/redo interaction\n\nIf the user undoes an edit that moved an anchor, the anchor should also \"undo\" back to its previous range. Decide whether anchor adjustments hook into the editor's undo stack or maintain their own change log keyed to text edits.\n\n### 5. Multi-anchor / overlapping edits\n\nWhen a single edit affects multiple annotations (e.g., paste over a region containing two anchors), each must be adjusted independently and consistently. Define iteration order and conflict rules.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Cross-file or cross-document drift (target file rename, content moved to a different file).\n- Semantic drift (reformatted/refactored code where the anchored *meaning* moved but the anchored *text* did not).\n- Drift for non-`core/text.range` anchors (CAD, PCB, graphics) — those are plugin concerns and have their own break semantics.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Inserting characters *inside* an annotated range does not break the annotation; the anchor extends to absorb the insertion.\n2. Deleting characters *inside* an annotated range does not break the annotation as long as some content remains; anchor shrinks.\n3. Deleting the entire anchored content marks the annotation broken.\n4. Whitespace-only edits inside the anchor do not break the annotation.\n5. Edits *outside* the anchor (before or after) shift the anchor's `start`/`end` so it continues to point at the same content.\n6. Stored payload semantics are documented; resolver's behavior matches the documented model.\n7. Test matrix covers: insert at start/middle/end of range, delete portions, replace, multi-line edits, undo/redo round-trips, edits that span multiple anchors.\n8. HITL pass: live editing in Godot feels natural — annotations follow the text instead of breaking on every keystroke.\n\n## Suggested sequencing\n\nIndependent of the parent DCR's substrate work; can land any time after the parent ships. Should land before any plugin (CAD, PCB) adopts `core/text.range` style anchors so plugins inherit the right behavior.\n\n## Size estimate\n\nMedium. Realistic estimate: half a day to a day for a clean implementation with the test matrix; a day or two if the policy questions in scope item #2 surface real disagreement.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:22:05","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:22:05","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","drift-tracking","resolver","text-editor","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0c1ee9174a4aec58c0acd8ac686","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Fix test/test_mcp_annotation_tools.gd: calls coroutine handle() synchronously","description":"## Problem\n\n`src/test/test_mcp_annotation_tools.gd` parse-fails (or runtime-fails) because it calls the MCP module's `handle()` method synchronously, but `handle()` is a coroutine — it `awaits` `render_overlay` work internally.\n\nSurfaced during the annotation UX substrate DCR (`019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede`) implementation but is pre-existing and not caused by that work.\n\n## Impact\n\n- The test cannot run, so we cannot claim the annotation MCP tools area is regression-clean from this single broad test (focused tests in `test/annotations_v2/` cover most of it: `test_mcp_annotations_query`, `test_mcp_annotations_update_status`, `test_broken_anchor_mcp`, `test_mcp_annotations_apply_hook` all pass).\n- Risk of silent regression in any MCP path not covered by the focused tests.\n\n## Fix\n\nEither:\n- Update the test harness to `await` the coroutine properly (preferred — keeps the broad coverage).\n- Or split it into multiple narrower tests that don't exercise the render_overlay path.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`godot --headless --path src --script test/test_mcp_annotation_tools.gd` runs to completion (pass or fail meaningfully — not parse-fail). Tests pass.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:38:19","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:38:19","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","mcp","tech-debt","test"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Annotation overlay canvas + arrow/free-text kinds + callout enrichment","description":"## Problem\n\nThe shipped UX substrate DCR (`019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede`) gave us a per-editor docked workbench, the `AnnotationOverlay` Control with mouse-filter discipline, and a first-class `callout` kind. But the overlay today is a **paint-only** surface: each kind paints itself into the overlay's `_draw()` and reinvents its own positioning, hit testing, and interaction logic.\n\nReal annotation UX needs more:\n\n- Arrows that connect two points, where each endpoint may be an anchor in the editor surface or a free point on the overlay.\n- Free text labels placed and edited at arbitrary positions on the overlay.\n- Callout bubbles that can be dragged to reposition after creation, with the leader line following.\n- A future home for ink/freehand annotations.\n- Consistent select/translate/rotate/scale behavior across all of the above.\n\nIf we keep the paint-only model and just add three more kinds, every kind reinvents the same positioning, hit-test, drag, selection, and handle code. That repeats the exact fragmentation the UX substrate DCR was meant to solve, just at a different layer.\n\n## Goal\n\nPromote the overlay from paint-only to a **first-class positioned-item canvas**: a transparent per-editor surface that owns positioned items with bounds, hit testing, selection, drag-to-move, z-order, and sidecar persistence of positions. Annotation kinds register item types and renderers; the canvas does the interaction.\n\nThen build arrow, free text, and callout-enrichment on top of that canvas, plus adopt the existing select/translate/rotate/scale toolbar for canvas items. Reserve a clean item-type slot for future ink/freehand without implementing it now.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. Overlay canvas substrate primitive\n\nPromote `AnnotationOverlay` (or a new sibling Control) into a positioned-item canvas:\n\n- Item registry: kinds register item types (`callout-bubble`, `free-text`, `arrow-segment`, future `ink-stroke`).\n- Each item has: `id`, `type`, `bounds` (Rect2 in canvas space), `z_order`, optional `selected` flag, kind-specific payload.\n- Hit testing: pick item under a point; supports overlapping items via z-order.\n- Drag: substrate handles drag interaction; emits position updates to kind via callback.\n- Selection model: single + multi-select; visual selection handles drawn by substrate.\n- Z-order: items can be reordered (bring forward, send backward); selected items optionally render above unselected.\n- Per-canvas state: items, selection, current tool. Per-editor identity (one canvas per editor surface).\n- Renderer hook: each item type registers a `draw_item(ctx, item)` callback. Substrate drives draw order; kinds draw their visual.\n\n### 2. Position-source contract\n\nDefine how arrow/callout endpoints get resolved to screen positions:\n\n- An **endpoint** is a *position source*: either an **anchor** (resolved by host via `resolve_anchor`) or a **canvas-free point** (resolved by substrate).\n- Add `core/canvas.point` anchor kind owned by substrate, payload `{x: float, y: float}` in canvas space, resolved trivially.\n- Substrate exposes `resolve_position_source(source) -> Vector2 | null` that handles both forms uniformly.\n- Plugins exposing point-precision anchors (`pcb/trace.point`, `cad/edge.point`, `core/text.position`) just implement their resolver; arrows and callouts pick them up automatically.\n- Document the contract: arrow/callout do not care what kind of anchor terminates them, only that resolution returns a position.\n\n### 3. Arrow kind\n\nFirst-class built-in `arrow` kind with three modes:\n\n- **anchor ↔ anchor** — both endpoints are anchors; arrow updates as anchors move.\n- **anchor ↔ free** — one anchor, one canvas-free point; common case for \"look at this thing.\"\n- **free ↔ free** — both endpoints free; pure overlay-canvas decoration.\n\nAuthoring:\n\n- Toolbar arrow tool active → click first endpoint (auto-detect: clicking on editor surface picks an anchor via host's domain picker; clicking empty overlay creates a free point).\n- Click second endpoint same way.\n- Arrow renders with arrowhead at second endpoint by default; head style + line style as kind payload (single, double, none).\n\nBroken-anchor fallback: if either endpoint anchor goes stale, render the arrow's stale endpoint at last-known snapshot position with broken styling.\n\n### 4. Free text kind\n\nFirst-class built-in `text` kind:\n\n- Anchored to a `core/canvas.point` (or any point-resolving anchor).\n- In-place editable: double-click to enter edit mode, edit text, click outside or Esc/Enter to commit.\n- Auto-sized by content with reasonable min/max width.\n- Optional border/background as kind payload styling.\n- Body view in the workbench shows the same text (substrate default body view applies).\n\n### 5. Callout kind enrichment\n\nBuild on the existing `core/callout` kind from the UX substrate DCR:\n\n- **Placement UX matches the existing comment flow:** highlight anchor in editor surface → click `Callout` toolbar button → type bubble text → commit. Bubble auto-positions near anchor with default offset (e.g., 60px to the right, 20px below) — no separate \"click where the bubble goes\" step.\n- **Drag to reposition** post-creation: bubble is a canvas item; user can drag it freely; leader line endpoint at bubble side updates; anchor stays put.\n- Leader line routes from anchor screen position to nearest edge of bubble.\n- Bubble has a border, padding, and the text payload.\n- Broken anchor: bubble stays at last position; leader hangs to last-known anchor position with broken styling.\n\n### 6. Item-manipulation toolbar adoption\n\nThe existing `AnnotationToolbar` (or its select/translate tooling — confirm location during implementation) already has illustrator/figma-style controls. Reuse them for canvas items:\n\n- **Select** — default tool; click item to select, drag to move, marquee-drag for multi-select.\n- **Translate** — explicit move tool (could be merged into Select).\n- **Rotate / Scale** — for items that support transforms (free text rotation, future ink). Callout bubble probably does not rotate (leader line geometry would get weird); arrow rotates by moving endpoints, not by transform; flag in item type metadata which transforms are supported.\n- Visual handles drawn by substrate canvas; transform behavior delegated via kind callback.\n\n### 7. Sidecar persistence\n\nAll canvas-item positions live in the same per-document annotation sidecar as v2 envelopes. Each annotation that owns a canvas item stores its item's position(s) in the kind payload (e.g., callout payload includes `{bubble_pos: [x, y]}`; arrow includes endpoint positions/anchors; free text includes position).\n\nNo separate canvas state file; one sidecar to rule them all.\n\n### 8. Future-proofing for ink\n\nReserve a clean `ink-stroke` item-type slot. Document the contract (what kinds register, how items render, how positions are stored) so a future ink DCR can land freehand strokes without re-architecting.\n\n**Out of scope:** actual ink implementation, brush settings, eraser tool, stroke smoothing.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Ink / freehand drawing (own DCR; this DCR reserves the slot).\n- CAD/PCB plugin adoption of arrows/callouts. Substrate ships the kinds and the canvas; plugin adoption is its own follow-on.\n- Real-time multi-user editing, cursor presence, conflict resolution.\n- Image/file attachments as canvas items.\n- Connectors with routing (Visio-style auto-routed lines avoiding obstacles). Arrows are straight lines for v1.\n- Z-order persistence across reload (nice to have; can land later).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Per-editor overlay canvas exists as a substrate primitive; items can be added, dragged, selected, hit-tested, and z-ordered without kind-specific code in the canvas.\n2. `core/canvas.point` anchor exists and is resolved by substrate; `resolve_position_source(source)` works uniformly for anchors and free canvas points.\n3. Arrow kind supports all three endpoint-mode combinations and renders correctly with stale-anchor fallback.\n4. Free text kind supports in-place edit, save, reload, and drag-to-reposition.\n5. Enriched callout kind supports the existing add-comment placement flow plus drag-to-reposition the bubble; leader follows.\n6. Manipulation toolbar's select/translate (and rotate/scale where applicable) work on canvas items.\n7. All item positions persist in the v2 sidecar; reload restores positions exactly.\n8. Text editor's existing line/range comment UX continues to work without regression (callout enrichment must not break the shipped flow).\n9. Item-type contract is documented; a stub `ink-stroke` registration scaffold exists (no implementation) to validate the slot.\n\n## Implementation notes\n\n- Existing pieces to harvest: `AnnotationOverlay.gd` (mouse-filter base), `AnnotationToolbar.gd` (manipulation controls — confirm what's already there), the existing `core/callout` payload/render path.\n- Selection-handle visuals should draw on the canvas itself, not on each item — keeps the look consistent.\n- Drag math should be canvas-space, not editor-space, so reparenting the dock doesn't shift items.\n- Test harness: add a non-text-editor smoke harness that mounts the canvas with a few items of each type to validate the canvas in isolation from any host.\n- Watch CAD's existing patterns (`~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationCanvas.gd`) for prior art on multi-item canvases — likely already solves some of this.\n\n## Relationship to siblings\n\nSibling of `019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede` (UX substrate DCR, shipped) under v2 substrate parent `019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1`. Builds on the dock pane, workbench, host contract, capability filtering, and AnnotationOverlay base from that DCR. Does not depend on the open follow-ups (anchor drift `019de0b313c0`, broken→open `019de0b2a3dd`).\n\n## Suggested sequencing\n\nLand overlay canvas primitive (#1) and position-source contract (#2) first — they unblock the kinds. Then arrow (#3), free text (#4), callout enrichment (#5) can happen in parallel. Toolbar adoption (#6) and sidecar persistence (#7) cut across all kinds; do them after the canvas + at least one kind exists. Documentation + ink slot (#8) closes out.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:52:56","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:55:22","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","arrow","callout","canvas","kinds","overlay-canvas","substrate","text","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Plan: Overlay canvas + arrow/free-text/callout-enrichment implementation","description":"## Goal\n\nImplement the overlay-canvas substrate primitive and the three new/enriched annotation kinds (arrow, free text, callout-enrichment) described by DCR `019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nAll nine acceptance criteria from the parent DCR pass, including text-editor-no-regression, sidecar persistence round-trip, all three arrow modes, in-place text edit, draggable callout bubble with following leader, manipulation toolbar working on canvas items, and a stub ink-stroke registration scaffold.\n\n## Implementation order\n\n1. Build overlay canvas primitive (positioned-item surface, item registry, hit-test, z-order, selection model).\n2. Define position-source contract + `core/canvas.point` substrate anchor.\n3. Implement first-class arrow kind with all three endpoint modes.\n4. Implement first-class free text kind with in-place edit.\n5. Enrich callout kind: bubble auto-placement near anchor, drag-to-reposition, leader follow.\n6. Adopt select/translate/rotate/scale manipulation toolbar for canvas items.\n7. Wire sidecar persistence for canvas-item positions across all kinds.\n8. Document item-type contract + stub `ink-stroke` registration slot.\n9. HITL regression on text editor — confirm shipped flow still works.\n\n## Sequencing notes\n\n- #1 and #2 are blockers for #3-#5; do them first.\n- #3, #4, #5 can run in parallel once #1+#2 land.\n- #6 and #7 cross-cut; do after canvas + at least one kind exists.\n- #8 + #9 close out before transitioning DCR to reviewing.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:09","updated_at":"2026-05-01T07:33:54","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","canvas","implementation","kinds","overlay","plan"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0cfaed77e30af1215849df57f47","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Build overlay canvas primitive (positioned items, hit-test, drag, selection, z-order)","description":"Promote `AnnotationOverlay` (or sibling Control) into a positioned-item canvas. Add item registry, per-item bounds + z-order + selection state, hit-test (with z-order), drag interaction (substrate-driven, kind callback for position update), single + multi-select with marquee, selection handles drawn by substrate.\n\nKeep mouse-filter discipline from the existing `AnnotationOverlay` base.\n\nAcceptance: a non-host smoke harness can register a fake item type, place items, hit-test, drag them, select them, and the canvas honors z-order on overlap. No kind-specific code in the canvas itself.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:20","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:09:26","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","canvas","drag","hit-test","selection","substrate","z-order"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0cfcb4870ab93680c2e6037cb69","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Define position-source contract + core/canvas.point substrate anchor","description":"Add `core/canvas.point` anchor kind owned by substrate, payload `{x: float, y: float}` in canvas space, resolved trivially.\n\nAdd substrate function `resolve_position_source(source) -> Vector2 | null` that handles:\n- `{kind: \"core/canvas.point\", x, y}` — substrate resolves directly.\n- Any anchor with `kind` registered against a host resolver — defer to host.\n- Returns null on unknown / failed resolution.\n\nDocument the contract: arrow/callout endpoints accept any position source; substrate handles canvas points; plugin handles its own anchors. Plugin point-precision anchors (`pcb/trace.point`, `cad/edge.point`) Just Work without arrow/callout knowing.\n\nAcceptance: focused tests cover canvas-point resolution, host-anchor delegation, and unknown-anchor null-return. Schema for `core/canvas.point` registered.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:27","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:09:32","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","canvas-point","contract","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0cfec09758caa07efbfb1459c35","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Implement first-class arrow kind (anchor↔anchor, anchor↔free, free↔free)","description":"Build the `core/arrow` annotation kind:\n\n- Two endpoints, each a position source (anchor or `core/canvas.point`).\n- Three authoring modes: anchor↔anchor, anchor↔free, free↔free.\n- Click-to-place authoring: with arrow tool active, first click picks endpoint 1 (auto-detect: anchor pick on editor surface via host's domain picker; free point on empty canvas). Second click picks endpoint 2 same way.\n- Renders as straight line with arrowhead at endpoint 2 by default.\n- Head/line style as kind payload: head `single`/`double`/`none`, line weight, color.\n- Stale-endpoint fallback: if either anchor goes stale, render that endpoint at last-known snapshot position with broken styling on that segment of the line.\n- Body view in workbench: empty by default (arrows are visual; no text body needed).\n\nAcceptance: focused tests cover all three modes, render math, stale-endpoint fallback, and sidecar round-trip.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:36","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:36:02","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","arrow","kinds","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d003157408939cbba2e3b8f528","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Implement first-class free text kind (anchored to canvas point, in-place edit)","description":"Build the `core/text` annotation kind (free-floating text, distinct from text-editor comments):\n\n- Anchored to a `core/canvas.point` (substrate) — or any point-resolving anchor in principle, but free text typically free.\n- In-place edit: double-click to enter edit mode, edit text, click outside / Esc / Enter to commit.\n- Auto-sized by content with sensible min (e.g., 60px wide) and max (e.g., 400px wide; wraps).\n- Optional border, background, and font-size as kind payload styling.\n- Body view in workbench: shows the same text (substrate default body view applies).\n- Drag-to-reposition: handled by the canvas substrate; updates `core/canvas.point` anchor coordinates.\n\nAcceptance: focused tests cover create, edit, commit, drag, sidecar round-trip, and rendering.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:41","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:36:06","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","free-text","in-place-edit","kinds","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d0259b704d855e5581a3e24fde","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Enrich callout kind (auto-placement near anchor, draggable bubble, leader follows)","description":"Extend the existing `core/callout` kind shipped by the UX substrate DCR:\n\n- **Placement UX matches the existing comment flow:** highlight anchor → click `Callout` toolbar button → type bubble text → commit. No separate \"click bubble position\" step.\n- Bubble auto-positions near anchor with default offset (e.g., 60px right, 20px below). Add bubble position to kind payload as `{bubble_pos: [x, y]}` in canvas space.\n- Bubble is a canvas item — drag to reposition. Anchor stays put; leader endpoint at the bubble side updates.\n- Leader line routes from anchor screen position to nearest edge of bubble (top/right/bottom/left center) — pick the side closest to the anchor for a clean look.\n- Bubble has border, padding, text payload, optional max width with wrap.\n- Stale anchor: bubble stays at saved position; leader hangs to last-known anchor position with broken styling.\n- Body view in workbench shows the bubble text.\n\nAcceptance: focused tests cover auto-placement math, drag-to-reposition, leader edge selection, stale-anchor fallback, and sidecar round-trip. Text-editor add-comment flow continues to work via the new callout payload (no regression).","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:50","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:36:10","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","callout","drag","kinds","leader","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d045487f0884d75336e66152ca","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Task: Adopt manipulation toolbar (select/translate/rotate/scale) for canvas items","description":"Reuse the existing illustrator/figma-style manipulation controls in `AnnotationToolbar` (or wherever they live — confirm during implementation) for canvas items.\n\n- **Select** — default tool; click item to select, drag to move, marquee-drag for multi-select.\n- **Translate** — explicit move tool (could be merged into Select; decide during implementation).\n- **Rotate** — for items that opt in via item-type metadata. Free text supports rotation; callout bubble does not (leader geometry would distort); arrow rotates by moving endpoints.\n- **Scale** — for items that opt in. Free text supports scale (font size or box scale — pick one). Callout bubble box-scale OK. Arrow does not scale.\n- Visual handles drawn by canvas substrate; transform behavior delegated to kind via callback.\n- Item-type metadata declares which transforms are supported; toolbar buttons disable for items that do not support them.\n\nAcceptance: focused test selects/moves/rotates/scales items appropriately per type metadata; unsupported transforms are not allowed.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:58","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:53:58","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","manipulation","rotate","scale","select","toolbar","translate"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d05dda7ad6ad451b9a3e113a94","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Wire sidecar persistence for canvas-item positions across all kinds","description":"All canvas-item positions persist in the same per-document v2 annotation sidecar. Each annotation that owns a canvas item stores its position(s) in the kind payload:\n\n- `core/callout` → `kind_payload.bubble_pos = [x, y]`\n- `core/arrow` → endpoints already stored as anchors (one of which may be `core/canvas.point` with x/y inline)\n- `core/text` → anchor is `core/canvas.point` with x/y; no extra payload field needed\n- Any future canvas item types: position(s) live in their kind payload\n\nNo separate canvas state file. Save on annotation change (via existing `annotations_changed` → save path). Reload restores positions exactly.\n\nAcceptance: focused round-trip tests for each kind; create → save → reload → assert positions match. No data loss across reload.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:54:05","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:36:13","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","canvas","persistence","sidecar"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d079e77580bfcd45905638ddca","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Document item-type contract + stub ink-stroke registration scaffold","description":"Document the canvas item-type contract:\n\n- How a kind registers an item type (`{type_name, draw_callback, hit_test_callback, supported_transforms, ...}`).\n- How items are added/removed/updated via the canvas API.\n- How positions and kind-specific data round-trip through the v2 sidecar.\n- How selection handles work and which transforms are honored.\n\nAdd a stub `ink-stroke` item-type registration scaffold (no implementation — just enough to validate the contract slot is real and reachable). Future ink DCR fills in the renderer/hit-test/serialization without re-architecting.\n\nUpdate `Docs/design/Annotation-substrate-design.md`.\n\nAcceptance: design doc updated; stub registration loads without error; tests assert the slot is reachable and `ink-stroke` is rejected only because no renderer is registered (not because the contract is missing).","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:54:12","updated_at":"2026-05-01T00:36:18","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","contract","docs","future-proof","ink"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0d09d9d71288da4f457459078d5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Task: HITL regression on text editor (no break to shipped UX)","description":"After all the canvas + kind work lands, run live HITL on the text editor to confirm the shipped UX from DCR `019ddf1af7327eae835550c714df6ede` still works:\n\n- Add comment on single-line selection (now a `core/callout` with auto-placed bubble).\n- Add comment on multi-line range.\n- Multiple comments on same line (each gets stable display number).\n- Edit text inside annotated range → broken indicator on surface + dock.\n- Repair via reselect → broken clears, lifecycle returns to open.\n- Resolve from workbench → annotation hides; reopen via MCP works.\n- Reload file → display numbers persist with gaps, bubble positions restored.\n- Open MCP-list and MCP-update from chat — round-trip still works.\n\nPlus the new things:\n- Drag a callout bubble to reposition; reload; bubble position persists.\n- Add arrow between two text-range anchors; verify both modes work in text editor (anchor↔anchor, anchor↔free).\n- Add free text on overlay; in-place edit; reload; persists.\n\nAcceptance: HITL pass note on this task with screenshots or a verdict comment.","created_at":"2026-04-30T23:54:21","updated_at":"2026-04-30T23:54:21","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","hitl","no-regression","text-editor"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf86587a109c9da23e331bb1bc"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Canvas↔annotation sync layer + interaction (drag, in-place edit, click-to-place authoring)","description":"## Problem\n\nRound 2 of DCR `019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200` shipped the substrate-side capability:\n\n- `AnnotationCanvas` positioned-item primitive with API (add/remove/update/select/hit-test/z-order)\n- `core/canvas.point` substrate anchor + `resolve_position_source` uniform contract\n- Anchor-aware kind payload paths for `2d_arrow`, `2d_text`, `callout` (render + bounds + hit-test all work via API)\n- Sidecar persistence for new payload fields\n- Docs + ink-stroke item-type slot\n\nWhat is **NOT** in place:\n\n1. **Canvas↔annotation sync.** The canvas's item model and the host's annotation list are decoupled. A canvas item can be added/dragged via API, and a host annotation can be created/updated, but nothing keeps them in sync. The canvas does not project annotations into items, and item drags do not propagate into annotation payload updates.\n\n2. **User-facing drag.** A user cannot click a callout bubble in the editor and drag it. The bubble_pos field exists, but the only way to update it is `host.update_annotation(id, new_dict)` from code.\n\n3. **In-place text edit.** A user cannot double-click a free text annotation, edit its text, and commit. The kind renders text from `kind_payload.text` but no UX lets a user change it from inside the editor surface.\n\n4. **New authoring tools.** The DCR scoped:\n   - Arrow click-to-place tool that auto-detects anchor pick (host's domain picker) vs free canvas point.\n   - Free text placement tool (click → spawn editable LineEdit at canvas point).\n   - Callout placement flow that uses bubble auto-placement near the anchor (the existing text-editor add-comment flow already does the *callout creation* part — the new bit is bubble auto-placement geometry from the kind).\n\n5. **Manipulation toolbar adoption for canvas items.** The existing `AnnotationSelectTool`, `AnnotationTranslateTool`, `AnnotationRotateTool`, `AnnotationScaleTool` operate on host annotations via `host.get_annotations()` and `host.set_selected_annotation_id()`. They do not know about `AnnotationCanvas` items or the canvas's selection model. Reconciling the two — does the canvas reflect host selection? do host annotations always project to canvas items? — is its own design.\n\nWithout these, Round 2's kinds are substrate-complete but invisible to a user.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild the canvas↔annotation integration layer so the kinds shipped in Round 2 are usable from the editor UI: drag a callout bubble, click-to-place an arrow on text-range anchors, double-click a free text to edit it, and have the manipulation toolbar work on these items.\n\n## Scope\n\n### 1. Canvas-host sync layer\n\nA controller that:\n\n- Subscribes to `host.annotations_changed` and projects each annotation whose kind is canvas-aware into an `AnnotationCanvas` item (and removes items for deleted annotations).\n- Maps canvas item id ↔ annotation id 1:1.\n- When the canvas emits `item_moved`, calls `host.update_annotation(id, patched_payload)` to persist the position back into the kind payload (e.g., `bubble_pos` for callout, endpoint x/y for arrow free endpoints).\n- When the canvas emits `item_selection_changed`, mirrors to `host.set_selected_annotation_id(...)` for single selection.\n\nDecision needed during design: where this controller lives — on `AnnotationCanvas` itself, on a separate `CanvasAnnotationProjector` module, or wired by each host that opts in. Likely a substrate helper that hosts attach.\n\n### 2. Canvas item registration for built-in kinds\n\nFor each Round 2 kind, register the canvas item type:\n\n- `2d_arrow` → `ITEM_TYPE_ARROW_SEGMENT` (draw via existing `_render_arrow_segment` math from `AnnotationArrow`)\n- `2d_text` → `ITEM_TYPE_FREE_TEXT` (draw via existing `_render_payload_text`)\n- `callout` → `ITEM_TYPE_CALLOUT_BUBBLE` (draw bubble + leader via existing callout render)\n\nEach registration provides `draw`, `hit_test`, and `supported_transforms` based on the kind's existing render/hit_test methods — wraps them in a canvas-item callback that pulls the annotation from the host by id.\n\n### 3. New / updated authoring tools\n\n- **`AnnotationArrowAuthorTool` upgrade** — current tool creates primitives-based arrows. Add a payload-mode that creates `kind_payload.endpoint_a/endpoint_b` annotations. Detect anchor pick on first click (delegate to host's domain pickers); fall back to free `core/canvas.point`.\n- **`AnnotationTextAuthorTool` upgrade** — same idea: payload-mode creating `kind_payload.text` + `core/canvas.point` anchor; spawn a LineEdit at the click position for in-place text entry.\n- **Callout creation** — wire the bubble auto-placement geometry into the existing text-editor add-comment flow so new callouts default to the auto-placed bubble (not the legacy fixed offset).\n\n### 4. In-place text edit\n\nDouble-click a `2d_text` (or callout bubble) → spawn a LineEdit overlay at the item's bounds, populated with current text. Commit on focus-out / Enter; cancel on Esc. Updates the annotation's `kind_payload.text` (and bounds recompute).\n\n### 5. Manipulation toolbar adoption\n\nDecide how `AnnotationSelectTool`/Translate/Rotate/Scale interact with canvas items:\n\n- **Option A:** Tools continue to operate on host annotations; the canvas-host sync layer projects selection both ways. Translate tool calls `host.update_annotation` with translated coords; canvas re-projects.\n- **Option B:** Tools become canvas-aware: they call `canvas.hit_test()` / `canvas.set_item_selection()` / `canvas.update_item_position()` directly. Sync layer back-propagates to the host.\n\nEither way, the existing tools should keep working on legacy primitives-based annotations (no regression on `2d_arrow` rendered from primitives, etc.).\n\n### 6. HITL pass\n\nAfter all of the above, run a live HITL pass that:\n\n- Drags a callout bubble in the text editor; reload; bubble persists at dragged position.\n- Click-to-place an arrow between two text-range anchors; verify the leader/segment routes correctly.\n- Place a free text on the canvas; double-click to edit; commit; verify persistence.\n- Use Select tool to multi-select items via marquee; verify halos.\n- Use Translate tool to nudge items; verify both legacy primitives-based and new payload-based annotations move.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Ink / freehand drawing (own DCR; this DCR uses the reserved `ink-stroke` slot but does not implement it).\n- CAD/PCB plugin adoption of the new kinds.\n- Multi-user editing / cursor presence.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Canvas-host sync layer projects annotations to canvas items and back without code in each kind's render path.\n2. User can drag a callout bubble in a Godot session; reload restores position.\n3. User can click-to-place an arrow whose endpoints are either anchors or free canvas points.\n4. User can place a free text and edit it in place.\n5. Manipulation toolbar works on both legacy and new payload-based annotations without regression.\n6. HITL pass confirms all of the above in the text editor.\n\n## Suggested sequencing\n\nSync layer first (#1 + #2), then authoring tool upgrades (#3 + #4), then manipulation toolbar reconciliation (#5), then HITL (#6). Sync layer and item registration are blockers for everything else.\n\n## Size estimate\n\nMedium-large. Sync layer is the load-bearing design; ~half a day to a day. Authoring tool upgrades + in-place edit add another half-day to a day. Manipulation toolbar reconciliation depends on which option you pick — option A is faster, option B is cleaner long-term.\n\n## Relationship\n\nDirect follow-up to overlay-canvas DCR `019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200`. Without this DCR, Round 2's kind enhancements are usable from MCP / API but not from the editor UI.","created_at":"2026-05-01T00:37:12","updated_at":"2026-05-01T21:26:08","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","authoring","canvas-sync","integration","interaction","manipulation"],"parent":"minerva:019de0cf51b87125ae03e4986bc05200"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de27e915f7ccdad3bd4aa4bcd0481","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"Plan: Layered annotation MCP and visible integration test pass","description":"## Goal\n\nValidate the annotation overlay/canvas work through the right layers before pushing it into the text editor surface.\n\n## Testing order\n\n1. **Pure MCP/library contract pass**\n   - Fix or bypass the synchronous `handle()` test issue with async-aware tests.\n   - Verify MCP tool registration and schema for annotation tools.\n   - Exercise `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` with sidecar-backed annotations using `core/canvas.point`, arrows, free text, and callout payloads.\n   - Exercise host-backed position resolution where feasible without a live editor.\n\n2. **Hello Scene plugin visible harness**\n   - Use the existing Hello Scene plugin panel as the visible plugin test-bed because it already calls annotation kind dispatch.\n   - Add or update fixtures so it can display callouts, arrows, and free text using the same payload shapes MCP will render.\n   - Use this to prove plugin-owned surfaces can visibly consume the substrate before changing a real editor surface.\n\n3. **Text editor regression / adoption pass**\n   - After MCP/library and Hello Scene pass, return to the text editor.\n   - Preserve line/range comment UX and add only the needed integration points for canvas-aware annotations.\n   - Confirm text-editor comments, gutter marks, display numbers, broken/repair flows, and reload behavior still work.\n\n## Rationale\n\nText editor is a high-value regression surface but a poor first test-bed for the MCP/canvas pivot because it has bespoke rendering and text-specific anchoring rules. Hello Scene is the better visible plugin harness. Pure MCP/library tests should run first so failures are contract-level, not UX-level.","created_at":"2026-05-01T07:43:58","updated_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:25","tags":["annotations","hello-scene","hitl","integration","mcp","test"],"parent":"minerva:019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de27ed4237166b1a175362ff0ee7c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: MCP/library contract tests for annotation tools and render_overlay","description":"First layer of the test pass.\n\nScope:\n- Add async-aware coverage for `MCPAnnotationTools.handle()` / `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` instead of calling coroutine paths synchronously.\n- Verify annotation MCP tools are registered with the expected names and argument shapes.\n- Render sidecar-backed annotations containing `core/canvas.point`, `2d_arrow` endpoint payloads, `2d_text` payloads, and enriched `callout` payloads.\n- Include degraded cases: unknown/stale anchors with snapshot fallback, missing output path errors, and invalid payload errors.\n- Where feasible, add a fake `AnnotationHost` to test `resolve_position_source` delegation without booting the full editor.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Focused headless tests run to completion and cover MCP contract behavior.\n- Existing broad MCP test chore `019de0c1ee9174a4aec58c0acd8ac686` is either fixed or explicitly left as separate tech debt with focused coverage in place.","created_at":"2026-05-01T07:44:15","updated_at":"2026-05-01T07:55:13","tags":["annotations","library","mcp","render-overlay","test"],"parent":"minerva:019de27e915f7ccdad3bd4aa4bcd0481"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de27ed9be753e88b0e03e292a2cbf","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Hello Scene plugin visible annotation harness","description":"Second layer of the test pass.\n\nScope:\n- Use `src/plugins/hello_scene` as the visible plugin test-bed because its annotation canvas already dispatches through annotation kinds.\n- Add or update a small fixture scene with stable visible targets and predictable plugin/editor anchors.\n- Demonstrate visible callout bubble + leader, arrow endpoints, and free text using the same payload shapes exercised by MCP render_overlay.\n- Prefer simple, generic plugin anchors over CAD/PCB-specific concepts so the harness tests the substrate rather than one domain.\n- Add a degraded/stale-anchor fixture if practical.\n\nAcceptance:\n- A human can open the Hello Scene plugin panel and see callout, arrow, and free-text annotations rendered correctly.\n- The fixture can be used as the next HITL surface before changing the text editor.","created_at":"2026-05-01T07:44:17","updated_at":"2026-05-01T15:30:20","tags":["annotations","hello-scene","hitl","plugin","visible-test"],"parent":"minerva:019de27e915f7ccdad3bd4aa4bcd0481"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de27edf4873b1852b071956b4e52a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Task: Text editor annotation regression and adoption pass","description":"Third layer of the test pass, after MCP/library and Hello Scene visible harness pass.\n\nScope:\n- Preserve shipped text-editor UX: selected text comments, line comments with gutter marks, display-number mapping, broken-anchor indication, repair, resolve, and reload behavior.\n- Decide whether and how `TextEditorAnnotationCanvas` should consume generic kind rendering or canvas projection without losing its text-specific line/range visuals.\n- Confirm enriched callouts, arrows, and free text do not make reading/editing text worse.\n- Keep text-editor-specific visuals distinct: word/range comments remain inline/range style; full-line comments remain gutter-band style.\n\nAcceptance:\n- HITL pass verifies existing text-editor comment flows still work.\n- Any adopted generic annotation kinds are visible and traceable without obscuring text.","created_at":"2026-05-01T07:44:18","updated_at":"2026-05-01T07:44:18","tags":["annotations","hitl","regression","text-editor","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de27e915f7ccdad3bd4aa4bcd0481"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de430afa7739d97b14176604ac276","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"Universal annotation dock/workbench for annotation-capable editors","description":"## Problem\n\nThe annotation substrate now has several working pieces: Minerva-owned annotation envelopes/kinds/MCP, plugin-owned anchor resolution, a Hello Scene visible harness, and a prior dock/workbench baseline. But these pieces are not yet composed into the intended platform experience.\n\nEvery annotation-capable editor/viewer should get a common annotation dock/workbench \"just there\" in the editor pane, while still allowing plugins/editors to extend annotations with domain-specific kinds, tools, anchors, body views, and apply actions.\n\nThe Hello Scene harness proved plugin-owned anchors can render through Minerva's shared annotation substrate. It did **not** prove the universal dock/workbench model. Text editor and future PCB/CAD adoption need that model before they should each grow their own parallel annotation UI again.\n\n## Goal\n\nMake the annotation dock/workbench a platform-owned component mounted for annotation-capable editor/viewer surfaces. The dock exposes shared annotation list/detail/status/repair/search/base-tool UX, while the active `AnnotationHost` declares which base tools, plugin tools, annotation kinds, anchor types, body views, and apply actions are available.\n\n## Architecture direction\n\nPlatform owns:\n- universal dock/workbench shell\n- annotation list/detail/status controls\n- lifecycle actions: open/resolved/applied/stale handling where supported\n- repair/delete/filter/search surfaces\n- base kinds/tools: callout, arrow, free text, select/transform where supported\n- sidecar/MCP/chat-context integration\n- selection sync conventions\n\nEditor/plugin hosts own:\n- semantic anchor types and resolvers\n- custom annotation kinds\n- custom authoring tools\n- optional custom body/detail views for domain kinds\n- optional apply actions, e.g. PCB route hint -> real traces\n- surface-specific visuals where needed, e.g. text range underline or line gutter mark\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Define/extend the `AnnotationHost` capability contract so the dock can configure itself from the active editor/viewer.\n2. Mount the dock/workbench consistently for annotation-capable editor/viewer panes.\n3. Define the always-available base tool bundle and the host flags that enable/disable each tool.\n4. Wire dock <-> annotation surface selection/status sync.\n5. Define plugin/editor extension APIs for custom kinds, tools, body views, and apply actions.\n6. Use Hello Scene as the first dock integration proof.\n7. Use text editor as the second proof, with text-specific annotation kinds/visuals represented in the dock without losing its range/line UX.\n8. Document PCB/CAD adoption expectations without implementing full PCB/CAD migration here.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Full PCBEditor migration into plugins.\n- Full CAD plugin annotation adoption.\n- Ink/freehand implementation.\n- Replacing all surface-specific rendering. Text editor can keep text-specific line/range visuals while still participating in the shared dock.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Annotation-capable editors/viewers can opt into a platform-owned dock/workbench through `AnnotationHost` capabilities.\n2. The dock shows shared annotation list/detail/status controls for host annotations.\n3. Base tools are available where the host supports them: select/transform, callout, arrow, free text.\n4. Plugin/editor custom kinds and tools appear in the same dock without replacing the dock shell.\n5. Selection sync works both directions: selecting in the dock highlights/selects on the surface; selecting on the surface selects in the dock.\n6. Hello Scene demonstrates base tools + plugin-owned anchors in the universal dock.\n7. Text editor demonstrates custom text annotations in the universal dock while preserving line/range visual behavior.\n8. Design docs explain the platform/plugin split and how PCB/CAD should adopt later.\n\n## Relationship\n\nSibling of canvas-sync DCR `019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7` under the annotation v2 substrate parent. Canvas-sync answers how annotations project to interactive surface items. This DCR answers how every annotation-capable editor/viewer gets the common dock/workbench plus extension points.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:09","updated_at":"2026-05-01T16:13:13","tags":["annotations","dock","editors","plugins","substrate","ux","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"Plan: Universal annotation dock/workbench integration","description":"## Goal\n\nImplement the universal annotation dock/workbench as a platform-owned shell that annotation-capable editors/viewers opt into through `AnnotationHost` capabilities. Preserve plugin/editor extension points for custom kinds, tools, body views, anchors, and apply actions.\n\n## Sequencing constraint with canvas-sync sibling\n\nThis plan shares the `AnnotationHost` contract with canvas-sync DCR `019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7`. Before either DCR deeply implements host-facing APIs, freeze the shared host contract slice:\n\n- capability declaration shape\n- surface selection hooks\n- lifecycle/status hooks\n- canvas item projection hooks or boundaries\n- tool/body-view/action extension hooks\n\nCanvas-sync owns surface interaction. Universal dock owns dock/workbench composition. The host API must satisfy both without independent drift.\n\n## Implementation order\n\n1. Extend/clarify `AnnotationHost` capability contract for dock configuration and canvas-sync compatibility.\n2. Mount dock/workbench for annotation-capable editor/viewer panes.\n3. Promote `AnnotationOverlay` to platform-owned annotation surface; relocate `AnnotationToolbar` from host scenes into the dock pane; delete parallel host implementations (start with `HelloAnnotationCanvas`). Base tool bundle becomes a one-line declaration in `AnnotationHost.get_capabilities().tools`. Absorbs T4's interim manipulation-tool halo guard. Depends on canvas-sync for interactive select/transform/click-to-place behavior.\n4. Wire dock <-> surface selection/status sync.\n5. Integrate Hello Scene as first proof before over-generalizing extension APIs.\n6. Define plugin/editor extension API for custom annotation kinds and body views.\n7. Define plugin/editor extension API for custom tools, lifecycle actions, and apply actions.\n8. Integrate text editor custom annotation types as second proof. Depends on canvas-sync for full surface/dock selection and interaction behavior.\n9. Add dock filter/search/query model.\n10. Verify MCP integration for dock-visible annotations and custom plugin/editor kinds.\n11. Document PCB/CAD adoption contract and boundaries.\n12. Run HITL for universal dock behavior.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nAll parent DCR acceptance criteria pass. Hello Scene proves base/plugin-anchor integration in the dock; text editor proves custom text annotations can live in the same dock while preserving text-specific visuals. Dock filter/search and MCP-visible annotation state do not diverge from the substrate source of truth.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:24","updated_at":"2026-05-01T21:25:25","tags":["annotations","dock","plan","plugins","ux","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019de430afa7739d97b14176604ac276"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de43130b87778bbbc8ca5aea34681","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Extend AnnotationHost capability contract for dock configuration","description":"Define the host-facing contract the universal dock uses to configure itself.\n\nScope:\n- Clarify fields for base tool support, custom tool support, annotation kinds, anchor types, lifecycle actions, body views, panes, and apply actions.\n- Decide naming for base vs plugin/editor extension capabilities.\n- Keep conservative defaults for hosts that do not opt in.\n- Update docs and tests for capability shape.\n\nAcceptance: dock can inspect an `AnnotationHost` and know which base tools, plugin tools, kinds, body views, lifecycle actions, and repair/apply actions to show.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:42","updated_at":"2026-05-01T16:13:16","tags":["annotation-host","annotations","api","capabilities","dock"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de431364973d49eb0cf10c943571e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Mount universal annotation dock for annotation-capable editor/viewer panes","description":"Mount the platform-owned annotation dock/workbench for editor/viewer surfaces that expose annotation capabilities.\n\nScope:\n- Find the editor-pane integration point.\n- Mount/reuse one dock per active annotation-capable surface.\n- Hide or collapse the dock when the active surface has no annotation host/capabilities.\n- Preserve responsive behavior from the prior dock-pane work.\n\nAcceptance: switching to an annotation-capable surface shows the dock configured for that host; switching to a non-capable surface hides or disables it cleanly.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:43","updated_at":"2026-05-01T20:00:48","tags":["annotations","dock","editors","mounting","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de4313bf6745b8567cba593917d50","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Platform-owned annotation overlay; relocate toolbar; remove parallel host UI","description":"## Goal\n\nHosts contribute domain knowledge (anchor resolution, capabilities, document mutation) and content. Everything else — drawing surface, toolbar, input routing, selection visualization, numbered badges — is platform-owned. New annotation-capable editors and plugins get the full annotation UX without writing UI code.\n\n## Concrete moves\n\n1. **Promote AnnotationOverlay** (`src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationOverlay.gd`) from a thin mouse-filter base into a generic annotation drawing surface. Absorb everything currently in `HelloAnnotationCanvas.gd`:\n   - `_draw` iterates `host.get_annotations()` and dispatches `kind.render(ctx, ann)` via the registry.\n   - Numbered badge drawing (currently anchor-aware in HelloAnnotationCanvas — port the resolution logic).\n   - Passive selection halo when no manipulation tool is active. Suppress when select/transform tool active (those tools draw their own gizmo via draw_preview).\n   - `_gui_input` routes pointer/keyboard to the active tool. `focus_mode = FOCUS_ALL` + `grab_focus()` on left-click (already done in HelloAnnotationCanvas — port).\n   - Mouse-filter discipline: STOP when active tool set, IGNORE otherwise.\n   - Reads `host.transform_doc_to_screen` / `transform_screen_to_doc` for non-identity hosts (PCB/CAD eventually).\n\n2. **Relocate AnnotationToolbar.** Today it lives in each host's scene as a sibling of the canvas (HelloPanel.tscn → `CanvasRow/AnnotationToolbar`). Move it into `AnnotationDockPane` (or as a sibling Control alongside the workbench). The platform mounts it; hosts get it for free.\n\n3. **Base tool bundle as capability declaration.** Original T3 scope (callout/arrow/text/select-transform). Already filtered by `AnnotationToolbar._kind_allowed` / `_tool_allowed` reading `host.get_capabilities()`. No new mechanism — just confirm defaults are right and document.\n\n4. **Remove parallel host implementations.** Delete `src/plugins/hello_scene/ui/HelloAnnotationCanvas.gd` and the `CanvasRow/AnnotationToolbar` node from `HelloPanel.tscn`. Hello keeps only `HelloAnnotationHost.gd` (domain glue: capabilities + `hello_scene/widget.point` anchor resolution + document mutation).\n\n5. **T4 cleanup happens here.** T4's hand-rolled selection halo in HelloAnnotationCanvas, plus the interim manipulation-tool-active guard, both delete as part of step 4. The platform overlay's passive halo replaces them.\n\n## Editor.gd integration\n\nEditor.gd's wrapper already mounts AnnotationDockPane for annotation-capable plugin scenes (T2 done). Extend the wrapper to also mount the platform AnnotationOverlay over the host's content area. For v1, Hello's panel-root is the content anchor; future hosts can declare via convention or AnnotationHost contract.\n\n## NOT in scope\n\n- Text editor adoption (T7 owns it — TextEditorAnnotationCanvas has bespoke text-range/line-gutter visuals; per-host call whether it fully cedes to the platform overlay or keeps a custom renderer for text-specific kinds).\n- Future PCB/CAD migration (T8 owns the doc).\n- Custom kind/body-view extension API (T5 / T_apply own those contracts).\n- Multi-select.\n- Callout hit-test precision (filed as chore `019de56da97c7c5e979885c9f579f09b` under canvas-sync DCR).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. Hello plugin opens and works end-to-end through the platform overlay + dock: callout/arrow/text creation, select/translate/rotate/scale, dock list, selection sync, badges, lifecycle.\n2. `HelloAnnotationCanvas.gd` deleted.\n3. `CanvasRow/AnnotationToolbar` node removed from `HelloPanel.tscn`.\n4. Toolbar lives inside AnnotationDockPane (or alongside it), mounted by the platform.\n5. AnnotationOverlay mouse-filter discipline intact: idle = pass-through, active = consume.\n6. Tests: platform overlay drawing/input/selection covered (state-and-signal where possible, plus a smoke test using the Hello host).\n\n## Relationship to canvas-sync DCR\n\nSibling DCR `019de0f7d714` owns surface-item *projection* (drag, hit-test precision, manipulation handle behavior). This task owns the *shell* (overlay + toolbar relocation). The two should not contend over AnnotationHost — capability shape stays frozen as agreed.\n\n## Reframe history\n\nOriginally scoped as \"Define always-available base tool bundle.\" Reframed 2026-05-01 after T4 HITL surfaced the parallel-canvas / parallel-toolbar problem. Tool bundle becomes a one-line capability declaration; the substantive work is the overlay promotion + toolbar relocation + Hello canvas deletion.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:45","updated_at":"2026-05-02T00:55:41","tags":["annotation-overlay","annotation-toolbar","annotations","hello-scene","platform","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"Shipped under WIP commits abd4a035 (T3 Phase 1 platform substrate + Phase 2 Hello cutover) and follow-ups b3ff956f / 96b5627a (codex RCA + AnnotationKind geometry contract that fixed text-author rendering and TransformTool rotation surfaced during HITL). All 5 reframed moves done: AnnotationOverlay absorbs HelloAnnotationCanvas; AnnotationToolbar relocated into AnnotationDockPane; tool bundle is capability-driven; HelloAnnotationCanvas + per-host toolbar deleted; T4 halo guard subsumed. HITL approved 2026-05-01."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de43141747a17a55aece818940988","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Wire dock-to-surface selection and lifecycle sync","description":"Connect the universal dock with the active annotation surface.\n\nScope:\n- Selecting an annotation in the dock selects/highlights it on the surface.\n- Selecting an annotation on the surface selects it in the dock.\n- Status/lifecycle changes from the dock update the host annotation and redraw the surface.\n- Delete/repair/apply actions route through host/substrate APIs.\n- Multi-select behavior is defined, even if initially limited.\n\nAcceptance: list/detail selection and lifecycle actions stay consistent with surface selection/rendering.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:38:46","updated_at":"2026-05-01T21:34:02","tags":["annotations","dock","lifecycle","selection","surface-sync"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"HITL pass on Hello Scene panel. Items 1/2/4 worked from initial commit; items 5/6 (Delete/Escape) fixed via focus_mode + signal-arity follow-up; item 3 (background click) deferred to chore 019de56da97c under canvas-sync DCR (callout hit-test imprecision). Commits f81e6792 + 9b0e534e. Tests 12/12 + 31/31. Halo logic and tool guard in HelloAnnotationCanvas are interim; T3-reframed deletes them when AnnotationOverlay takes over.</resolution>\n<parameter name=\"note\">Closing T4 with HITL pass. See verdict comment 314 for detail."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de43192f07fb2849eb14ed0c6eb66","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Define plugin/editor extension API for custom kinds and body views","description":"Define how plugins/editors extend the universal dock with custom annotation kinds and kind-specific body/detail views without replacing the dock shell.\n\nScope:\n- Custom annotation kind registration and display metadata.\n- Per-kind body/detail view registration and lifecycle.\n- How body views read/update annotation payloads.\n- Fallback behavior when a custom body view is unavailable.\n- Failure containment when a custom view throws.\n\nSequencing:\n- Run after the Hello Scene dock integration proof so the API is shaped by a concrete host. (Hello proof shipped under T-hello + T3.)\n- Coordinate with canvas-sync DCR `019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7` so custom kind views do not conflict with surface item projection.\n\nAcceptance: a plugin/editor can add domain-specific annotation kinds and body/detail views into the universal dock through documented APIs.\n\n---\n\n**Proof case (decided 2026-05-02):**\n\nT7's `text_comment` custom kind (Path A) is the first concrete consumer of this API. Design the kind-registration + body-view surface around that workload — text-range anchor (`core/text.range`), multi-line content, optional reply slot, host-custom canvas visuals (the existing TextEditorAnnotationCanvas underline/gutter render path) — rather than abstractly.\n\nSpecific surface requirements surfaced by T7:\n1. Kind registration accepts `display_name`, `toolbar_icon`, optional `body_view_factory` Callable, and a flag indicating the kind opts out of the platform overlay's default render in favor of host-side custom canvas drawing.\n2. Body-view factory returns a Control that the dock detail pane parents under the selected annotation. The Control receives the annotation Dict and a Callable for emitting patches back to the host.\n3. Fallback when no body_view_factory is registered: dock falls back to the existing summary-only row.\n4. Failure containment: if a body view's factory throws or its Control errors, the dock catches and displays a placeholder + a one-line error, never tears down the dock.\n5. Canvas opt-out: when a kind's `host_custom_canvas` flag is set, AnnotationOverlay's default render path skips it. Host overlays (subclasses of AnnotationOverlay) are responsible for drawing those annotations themselves.\n\nT5+T7 ship as one work-cycle with two phases (Phase 1 = this API, Phase 2 = T7 first proof). One bundled WIP commit at the end of Phase 2.\n\n**Blocks T7** (`019de4319f19`).","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:07","updated_at":"2026-05-02T03:52:13","tags":["annotations","body-views","custom-kinds","extension-api","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"Shipped Phase 1 of T5+T7 combined cycle. Commit 4cf02b98. AnnotationKind.body_view_factory virtual + AnnotationOverlay has_visual_render() opt-out (skips dispatch_render, badge, halo) + AnnotationWorkbench body-view container. 14 PASS in test_kind_extension_api.gd, 0 FAIL. T7 (Phase 2) consumed this API to ship text_comment as the first proof; HITL passed with the core feature working end-to-end. Toolbar UX follow-up tracked separately as chore 019de6d0951d."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de431994972e08889cfd191cf7f5a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Integrate Hello Scene with universal annotation dock","description":"Use Hello Scene as the first proof of the universal dock/workbench.\n\nScope:\n- Mount the platform dock for the Hello Scene annotation host.\n- Show base tools and fixture annotations in the dock.\n- Confirm plugin-owned `hello_scene/widget.point` anchors work from dock-selected annotations.\n- Selection sync between dock and Hello canvas.\n- Preserve existing Hello annotation demo fixture.\n\nAcceptance: Hello Scene demonstrates base callout/arrow/free-text annotations and plugin-owned anchors in the universal dock, not only on its canvas.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:09","updated_at":"2026-05-01T20:00:50","tags":["annotations","dock","hello-scene","integration","proof"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de4319f1971f983f3d82ccecd3c76","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Integrate text editor custom annotations with universal dock","description":"Use text editor as the second proof of the universal dock/workbench.\n\nScope:\n- Represent text range comments and line comments as host/custom annotation kinds in the dock.\n- Preserve text-specific visuals: range underline/highlight and line gutter mark.\n- Show text annotation bodies/status/actions in the dock.\n- Keep repair/reselect and broken-anchor UX working.\n- Avoid obscuring text readability with generic surface visuals.\n\nAcceptance: text editor comments appear in the universal dock while existing line/range UX remains intact.\n\n---\n\n**Path A chosen 2026-05-02:**\n\nRegister `text_comment` as a custom AnnotationKind via T5's extension API. Comments are functionally distinct from free 2D text labels (different lifecycle, MS-Word-Review semantics, eventual reply threading) and deserve their own kind rather than being shoehorned into `kind: \"2d_text\"` via a `kind_payload.target_scope` discriminator.\n\nConcrete moves:\n1. Define `AnnotationTextComment` (or equivalent) as a registered kind. Owning plugin = \"core\" or \"text_editor\"; decide during the cycle.\n2. Update `TextEditorAnnotationHost.add_comment_at` to stamp `kind: \"text_comment\"` instead of `kind: \"text\"`.\n3. On read of saved sidecars, migrate entries with `kind: \"text\"` + `kind_payload.target_scope ∈ [\"range\", \"line\"]` to `kind: \"text_comment\"` so existing files keep working without user intervention.\n4. Body view in the dock detail pane renders the comment text + author + status + (stub) reply slot. Reply UI itself deferred to follow-up `019de68e4667`.\n5. Preserve existing `TextEditorAnnotationCanvas` underline (healthy) + orange gutter strip (broken) — already extends AnnotationOverlay post-T3, so T5's kind-API needs to support host-custom canvas visuals (kind opts out of platform default render in favor of the host's overlay subclass).\n6. Update workbench filter/sort to recognize the new kind; verify dock displays kind display_name (e.g. \"Comment\") for the row prefix instead of generic kind tagging.\n\nOut of scope (deferred):\n- Reply/threading UI and persistence — see follow-up `019de68e4667`.\n- PCB/CAD adoption (T8 docs only).\n- New keyboard shortcuts beyond what already exists.\n\n**Blocked by T5** (`019de43192f0`).\n\nSequencing: T5+T7 ship as one work-cycle with two phases (Phase 1 = T5 API, Phase 2 = T7 first proof). One bundled WIP commit at the end of Phase 2.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:10","updated_at":"2026-05-02T03:52:18","tags":["annotations","custom-kinds","dock","regression","text-editor"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"Shipped Phase 2 of T5+T7 combined cycle. Commit 4cf02b98. AnnotationTextComment kind registered in TextEditorAnnotationHost; add_comment_at stamps text_comment; load_annotations migrates legacy kind:\"text\" + kind_payload.target_scope in {\"range\",\"line\"} on read (write-side migrates on next normal save). 18 PASS in test_text_comment_kind.gd. HITL 2026-05-02: core feature works — comments draw with underline+gutter+badge, dock body view shows comment text + author + status + disabled Reply stub, no double-render with platform halo. Reply/threading deferred to 019de68e4667. Toolbar UX surfaced — tracked as chore 019de6d0951d."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de431a4df7738a6d0e82251e98ff6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Document PCB/CAD adoption contract for universal annotation dock","description":"Document how richer plugin annotation systems should adopt the universal dock later.\n\nScope:\n- PCB: rich kinds such as bus/trace/net hints remain plugin-owned annotation kinds, surfaced in the universal dock.\n- CAD: edge notes/callouts use CAD-owned anchors and tools, surfaced in the universal dock; edge numbering remains separate user-information overlay.\n- Clarify that full PCB/CAD migration is out of scope for this DCR.\n- Include examples of custom anchors, kind payloads, body views, and apply actions.\n\nAcceptance: design docs make the platform/plugin split clear enough that PCB/CAD can adopt without inventing parallel dock UI.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:11","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:03:20","tags":["adoption","annotations","cad","docs","pcb"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"Plugin/host adoption guidance shipped. Docs/design/Annotation-substrate-plugin-adoption.md (731 lines) covers all 13 sections + maintainer-only references appendix. Round 0 = toolbar UX chore (a50cbb63). Round 1 = doc-write (7c226c4a) + framing reframe (b05a28fb) for off-tree plugin authors. HITL approved 2026-05-02; user noted future \"full SDK pass\" deferred. No code changes; substrate stable.</resolution>\n<parameter name=\"note\">Cold reviewer verified every cited substrate API. Framing pass corrected §13 (now \"In-Tree Reference Implementations\" — maintainer-only) and §10.1 class_name guidance for off-tree plugins."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de431aab17071af9d3514a32cc9de","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Task: HITL pass for universal annotation dock behavior","description":"Run live HITL for the universal dock/workbench.\n\nScope:\n- Hello Scene: dock appears, base tools appear, fixture annotations list/select correctly, canvas selection sync works.\n- Text editor: text range/line comments list/select correctly, visuals remain readable, lifecycle/repair actions work.\n- Responsive dock behavior remains acceptable.\n- MCP/query behavior still sees the same annotations.\n\nAcceptance: human verification comment is added with pass/fail notes and screenshots or concrete observed behavior.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:39:13","updated_at":"2026-05-01T20:00:52","tags":["annotations","dock","hitl","ux","verification"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de4441bb47430849d38e99a20ee81","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Task: Define plugin/editor extension API for custom tools and apply actions","description":"Define how plugins/editors extend the universal dock with custom authoring tools, lifecycle actions, and domain-specific apply actions.\n\nScope:\n- Custom authoring tool registration, labels/icons, enabled/disabled state, and host prerequisites.\n- Per-kind lifecycle/action buttons, e.g. apply PCB bus hint or apply CAD edge note.\n- Action result contract: success/failure, created document entities, lifecycle transition recommendations.\n- Failure containment when a custom action throws or returns invalid data.\n- Trust boundary notes for plugin-provided tool/action code.\n\nSequencing:\n- Run after Hello Scene dock integration proof and after custom kind/body-view API shape is understood.\n- Coordinate with canvas-sync DCR 019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7 because custom authoring tools may depend on surface click/drag/projection hooks.\n\nAcceptance: a plugin/editor can add custom tools and domain-specific apply/lifecycle actions into the universal dock through documented APIs.\n\n----- Status (2026-05-02) -----\n\nPHASE A SHIPPED (commit f0056c0e). HITL passed: no regressions in Hello scene plugin annotations or text editor. Phase A delivered:\n- AnnotationKind.actions(annotation) -> Array virtual: each entry {id, label, requires_lifecycle?}.\n- AnnotationKind.run_action(action_id, annotation, phase, host) -> Dictionary virtual; called dry_run + commit by AnnotationApplyToolRunner (existing trust-aware wrapper, reused as-is).\n- AnnotationWorkbench reads kind.actions() in _make_row and appends action buttons after lifecycle buttons. Wires presses through ApplyToolRunner; ok=false -> show_status; ok=true with lifecycle -> host.update_annotation_lifecycle.\n- 15 PASS in test_kind_actions.gd; 67 prior tests across 5 suites still green.\n- No existing kinds opt in yet; the API is the hook for plugins/editors.\n\nPHASE B (custom authoring tool registration via kind.author_ui()) deferred. AnnotationToolbar already filters by author_ui() != null, so the bulk of Phase B is: (1) verify a plugin-side custom kind with author_ui() gets a toolbar button and works, (2) document the plugin-side path, (3) optionally tighten the AnnotationAuthorTool contract. Likely folds into T8 (PCB/CAD adoption) since that's the natural first plugin-side consumer.\n\nStays in_progress until Phase B ships or T8 supersedes it.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:59:22","updated_at":"2026-05-02T04:38:50","tags":["annotations","apply-actions","custom-tools","extension-api","lifecycle","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de44421667435adcc52d9752f6715","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Task: Add dock filter/search/query model","description":"Add the dock's user-facing filter/search/query model so annotation lists remain usable across base and plugin/editor custom annotations.\n\nScope:\n- Filter by lifecycle/status, kind, anchor type, author, stale/broken state, and text.\n- Search summaries/body text/payload text where appropriate.\n- Decide how host/plugin custom fields participate in search without leaking implementation details.\n- Keep filter semantics aligned with existing MCP query semantics where possible.\n- Persist or reset filters according to editor-pane UX conventions.\n\nAcceptance: the universal dock can filter/search annotations across base and custom kinds without diverging from substrate query behavior.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:59:23","updated_at":"2026-05-01T15:59:23","tags":["annotations","dock","filter","query","search","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de444272d7468b2ea8c4410667e8d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task: Verify MCP integration for dock-visible annotations","description":"Ensure annotations visible/manipulable through the universal dock remain visible and coherent through MCP tools.\n\nScope:\n- Confirm dock-visible base annotations are returned by MCP query/list/render paths.\n- Confirm plugin/editor custom annotations expose useful summaries and chat_context for non-multimodal models.\n- Confirm dock lifecycle/status changes are reflected through MCP query/update_status.\n- Confirm MCP-created/updated annotations appear in the dock when their host/source is active.\n- Add focused tests or HITL notes for custom kind behavior.\n\nAcceptance: MCP and dock operate over the same annotation source of truth; no separate dock-only annotation state exists.","created_at":"2026-05-01T15:59:24","updated_at":"2026-05-02T02:32:32","tags":["annotations","dock","integration","mcp","query"],"parent":"minerva:019de430ec047e64b8f988476f6f0398","resolution":"Shipped under WIP commit b23a18b1. Extended minerva_annotations_add/update/delete with editor_name (live host) branch mirroring the dual-path shape of _annotations_list/query/update_status/repair_anchor. Live-path mutations now propagate to the dock workbench through host.annotations_changed without requiring a save/reload cycle, satisfying the \"ask Claude what I just drew\" round-trip. HITL verified 2026-05-01: A1-A3 add/update/delete via editor_name + B4-B6 validation paths + visual confirmation that the dock workbench shows MCP-added annotations and the platform overlay renders them. Two separate dock-display issues observed during HITL (workbench labels unanchored 2d_text as \"Range\" and miscounts lifecycle:open + empty anchored_to as \"broken\") — filed as separate concerns; not in T_mcp scope."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de56da97c7c5e979885c9f579f09b","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Tighten AnnotationCallout.hit_test to bubble + leader band (was full bounds rect)","description":"## Problem\n\n`AnnotationCallout.hit_test` (line 74) returns `bounds(annotation).grow(threshold).has_point(point)`. The bounds rect spans bubble + anchor point + leader, so for callouts whose bubble and anchor are far apart, the hit zone covers most of the connecting region. A user clicking 'background' between bubble and anchor still hits the callout, which causes `AnnotationSelectTool`'s 'click outside to clear selection' to silently re-select the same annotation (no-op via the `if _selected_id == annotation_id: return` guard in `Helloscene_AnnotationHost.set_selected_annotation_id`) instead of clearing.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nT4 HITL — item 3: background click does not deactivate selected annotation. Diagnosed during T4 HITL; deferred to canvas-sync DCR scope because hit-test precision is surface-interaction territory, not T4 (selection sync semantics). T4's keyboard fix means Escape is a reliable workaround in the meantime.\n\n## Proposal\n\nReplace the union-bounds hit-test with three discrete tests, threshold-aware:\n- Inside the bubble rect (full hit).\n- Within `threshold` of the leader line segment (point-to-segment distance, like `AnnotationArrow.hit_test`).\n- Within `threshold` of the anchor point.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Clicking a true empty region of the canvas between a callout's bubble and anchor falls into `AnnotationSelectTool`'s 'no hit' branch and clears selection.\n- Clicking the bubble interior, the leader within threshold, or near the anchor still selects the callout.\n- `bounds()` itself (used by selection-halo and dock badge placement) is unchanged.\n- Existing callout tests still pass; one new test asserts a click in the bubble↔anchor empty space does not hit the callout.","created_at":"2026-05-01T21:24:22","updated_at":"2026-05-01T21:24:22","tags":["annotations","callout","canvas-sync","hit-test","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de0f7d714762f9d4d8bfa8c228ed7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de68e4667715ab38509a201abe42e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Reply/threading on text_comment annotations","description":"Add MS-Word-style reply threads on `text_comment` annotations (Path A custom kind shipped by T7).\n\nDeferred from T7's initial scope so the first cycle ships a usable comment kind without committing to thread infrastructure in-cycle.\n\nScope:\n- Reply payload schema extension on the `text_comment` kind (replies array, author, timestamp, parent reply id for threading).\n- Dock body-view rendering of replies under the parent comment.\n- IPC contract for adding/editing/deleting replies (likely `minerva_text_editor_add_reply` MCP tool plus host method).\n- Sidecar persistence: replies round-trip through the same sidecar file as their parent comment.\n- Resolve/reopen lifecycle interaction with replies (does resolving the parent freeze the thread? policy decision).\n\nOut of scope:\n- Reply notifications, mentions, or @user routing.\n- Cross-document reply linking.\n- Reply on annotation kinds other than `text_comment`.\n\nSurfaced from: T7 (`019de4319f19`) Path A discussion 2026-05-02.</description>\n</invoke>","created_at":"2026-05-02T02:39:37","updated_at":"2026-05-02T02:40:40","parent":"minerva:019de430ec04"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de690928a7b60a5663e566c257188","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Workbench mis-classifies unanchored 2d_text as broken / Range","description":"Surfaced from T_mcp HITL 2026-05-02. After the cycle's MCP-driven add of a `kind: \"2d_text\"` annotation at a panel position with no UI element under it (so `anchored_to` stamped to \"\"), the AnnotationDockPane workbench:\n\n1. Labels the row with prefix \"Range\" instead of the kind's display_name (\"Text\").\n2. Counts the annotation as \"broken\" in the per-filter summary even though `lifecycle: \"open\"` and the rendered annotation is on-canvas and visible.\n\nRepro:\n- Open Hello Scene Panel.\n- Call `minerva_annotations_add` with editor_name + a 2d_text annotation whose primitive position lands in the panel's empty area.\n- Observe dock shows `0 open, 1 broken` and the row prefix says \"Range\".\n\nLikely root: workbench classification logic treats empty `anchored_to` as broken. The host's `_stamp_anchor` returns an empty string when `describe_point` finds no leaf control under the doc position, which is correct behavior for free-floating annotations. Workbench should treat empty anchor as \"unanchored\", not \"broken\".\n\nLikely auto-resolves when T7 moves comments off `kind: \"2d_text\"` (because the broken-classification heuristic seems tied to assumptions about what 2d_text annotations should look like), but file independently so it isn't lost if T7 picks a different shape.\n\nFiles to investigate:\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/AnnotationDockPane/AnnotationWorkbench.gd` — `refresh()` and per-row classification logic\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Annotations/AnnotationLifecycle.gd` — broken/stale heuristic\n- Whatever derives the row's \"Range\" prefix label","created_at":"2026-05-02T02:42:07","updated_at":"2026-05-02T02:42:07","tags":["classification","dock","regression-class","ux","workbench"],"parent":"minerva:019de0f7d714"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de6d0951d71f2832853aee5295186","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Text-editor annotation toolbar: dead Text button + sparse kind list","description":"## Symptoms (HITL on T5+T7 cycle, 2026-05-02)\n\nIn a text/code editor pane:\n- AnnotationToolbar (mounted in COMPACT mode under the dock chevron) shows only \"Select\" + \"Text\" buttons.\n- Clicking \"Text\" activates a tool but no annotation lands.\n\n## Root cause\n\nTwo-filter pipeline in `AnnotationToolbar._try_add_button`:\n\n1. `kind.author_ui() != null` — most kinds don't override this, so they get no toolbar button (line 407).\n2. `_kind_allowed(kind_name)` — kind must appear in `host.get_capabilities().kinds` (line 442).\n\n`TextEditorAnnotationHost.get_capabilities()` advertises `[\"text\", \"callout\", \"text_comment\"]`. Of those, only legacy `AnnotationText` has an `author_ui()`. `callout` and the new `text_comment` (T7) lack one.\n\nThe `text` button is wired to `AnnotationText.author_ui()`, which is a 2D-canvas authoring tool that wants `core/canvas.point` anchors. `TextEditorAnnotationHost` only resolves `core/text.range`. So the tool activates, the host can't anchor the click, nothing lands.\n\n## Fixes (cheap, in this chore)\n\n1. Remove `\"text\"` from `TextEditorAnnotationHost.get_capabilities().kinds` (single-line change). Kills the dead button — the legacy 2D `text` kind is no longer the canonical comment kind (`text_comment` is, post-T7).\n2. Optionally remove `\"callout\"` too if there's no working callout flow in the text editor.\n\n## Out of scope here\n\n- Implementing `AnnotationTextComment.author_ui()` so the toolbar offers a \"Comment\" button. That's design work — text-editor authoring is selection-driven (workbench `+` button), not click-place. Decide separately whether the toolbar should integrate with that flow.\n- Fixing the legacy 2D AnnotationText kind to be context-aware. Lower-priority — once it's not exposed in text editor capabilities, the surface area shrinks.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- HITL: open a text editor, confirm the toolbar shows just \"Select\" (and any kinds that genuinely have working author_ui in this context).\n- Confirm the workbench `+` button still adds comments (T7 flow unchanged).\n- Confirm 2D plugins (Hello scene) still see their full toolbar — only the text host's capabilities change.","created_at":"2026-05-02T03:52:02","updated_at":"2026-05-02T16:26:59","tags":["annotation-substrate","deferred-from-t7-hitl","text-editor","toolbar","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019de430afa7","resolution":"Shipped commit a50cbb63. HITL 2026-05-02: text editor toolbar shows only Select (T and callout dead buttons gone); workbench + still adds text_comment; Hello scene plugin's full toolbar unaffected. Single-line capability change in TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b755b07366a3cf1ab645d01383","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"CAD Phase A1: replace Cad_AnnotationCanvas with platform AnnotationOverlay; mount AnnotationDockPane via get_annotation_host()","description":"Biggest single DRY win in CAD plugin substrate adoption.\n\n**Current state (off-tree, ~/github/plugins/cad/):**\n- `Cad_AnnotationCanvas` (CadAnnotationCanvas.gd, 176 lines) clones the substrate's `AnnotationOverlay`. Reimplements host binding, annotations_changed redraw, active-tool routing, mouse-filter toggle, _draw dispatch, pointer event forwarding. No CAD-specific behavior — just a copy of Helloscene_AnnotationCanvas.\n- `Cad_CADPanel` does NOT override `get_annotation_host()`, so platform's AnnotationDockPane auto-mount path (Editor.gd:704-712) never fires. Plugin builds its own custom overlay/sidebar layout instead.\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Add `get_annotation_host()` override to `Cad_CADPanel` that returns the registered `Cad_AnnotationHost`. This unlocks platform AnnotationDockPane auto-mount.\n2. Delete `Cad_AnnotationCanvas` entirely. Replace with substrate `AnnotationOverlay` instances (one per SubViewport pane in wide mode, one in narrow mode).\n3. Route active-tool/mouse-filter through `AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool` (already mouse-filter aware per the substrate). Drop the duplicated mouse-filter logic in CADPanel.\n4. Verify dock chrome and toolbar still work (toolbar already mounts; this just gives it the platform dock companion).\n\n**Closes:** 019dd020a953 (editor-tab chrome contract — remaining scope).\n**Out of scope:** Edge anchor type (Phase B1), edge-overlay deletion (Phase A2), capabilities advertisement (Phase A3), sidebar question (Phase A4).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:23:19","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:49:49","tags":["annotations","cad","dry","substrate-adoption"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b78eda7a6b9fe665ad7efdaa45","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD Phase A2: delete edge_overlay.gd (5 instances) — route edge picking through substrate","description":"Second-biggest DRY win. `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/scripts/edge_overlay.gd` (635 lines) is the legacy pre-substrate edge picker, ported verbatim from the mcad-app experiment. One instance lives under EVERY SubViewport (CADPanel.tscn:73, 101, 129, 157, 215). It maintains:\n\n- Its own `_selected_edge_id` (duplicates Cad_AnnotationHost._selected_edge_id and CADPanel._selected_edge_id — three places).\n- Its own hit-tester (`_edge_ids_at_position`, :592-607).\n- Its own candidate \"chooser\" UI (:225-251).\n- Its own hover state (:43).\n- Its own `_draw_edge_marker` rendering pipeline (:254-274).\n- Its own ortho-mode silhouette renderer (`_draw_projected_mesh_edges`, :302-338) — a separate render loop entirely outside AnnotationOverlay.\n- Emits `edge_selected` signal wired into a separate panel-level edge-selection ledger.\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Delete edge_overlay.gd and all 5 instances from CADPanel.tscn.\n2. Delete `CADPanel._edge_overlays`, `_push_edges_to_overlays`, and the bidirectional state mirror (`_set_selected_edge_id`, `_sync_tree_selection`, `_on_edge_tree_item_selected`).\n3. Pick path: cad_edge_number_tool already does real picking (cad_edge_number_tool.gd:127-184) using host.get_panes() + camera.unproject_position. That's the substrate path; no replacement edge-overlay needed.\n4. Selection state: single source of truth = host. AnnotationOverlay's selection halo handles visual feedback.\n5. Ortho-mode silhouette rendering: needs a substrate-clean replacement OR explicit decision to drop. File a follow-up if the silhouette overlay is still wanted (it's CAD geometry rendering, not annotation rendering — could live in a non-annotation overlay).\n\n**Depends on:** Phase A1 (need substrate AnnotationOverlay in place first). \n**Out of scope:** Edge anchor type (Phase B1), sidebar tree (Phase A4).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:23:34","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:58","tags":["annotations","cad","dry","substrate-adoption"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b7ae707aa0a801ff9ac198b972","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"CAD Phase A3: Cad_AnnotationHost.get_capabilities() — advertise registered kinds via substrate API","description":"Cad_AnnotationHost has no get_capabilities() override. Built-in 2D kinds + cad_edge_number are registered into the local AnnotationRegistry directly at CADPanel.gd:184-186 but the substrate's capability API never sees them. This breaks substrate-aware tool discovery (toolbar filtering, MCP capability queries, kind-aware UX in the dock).\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Override `get_capabilities()` in CadAnnotationHost.gd. Return AnnotationHostCapabilities with `kinds: [\"cad_edge_number\", ... built-in kinds advertised by host policy]`.\n2. Decide which built-ins to expose: `callout` and `text_comment` make sense for free-space CAD annotations. `region`, `polyline`, `highlight`, `2d_arrow`, `2d_text` may not (they're 2D in a 3D viewport — needs design judgment).\n3. Verify toolbar correctly filters based on advertised kinds (toolbar already implements the `author_ui() != null AND kind in capabilities.kinds` two-filter pipeline — no toolbar change should be needed).\n\n**Out of scope:** Edge anchor type (Phase B), authoring-tool changes.","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:23:42","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:49:52","tags":["annotations","cad","substrate-adoption"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b7d73a70cf8ae1fdb6ee7841af","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"CAD Phase A4: edge browser sidebar — keep custom Tree, replace with AnnotationSidebarModel, or hybrid?","description":"CADPanel currently has a custom `_edge_tree` Tree control listing ALL edges from the worker (id/measure/kind columns + Prev/Next/Clear buttons, CADPanel.gd:111-114, 590-640). This is NOT an annotation list — it's an edge browser/picker that lets the user navigate geometry to pick what to annotate.\n\n**Options:**\n- **A. Keep as-is, but delete state mirror.** Custom tree continues to exist as a CAD-specific affordance for edge navigation. Drop the bidirectional `_selected_edge_id` mirror (Phase A2 covers that). Tree click → activate cad_edge_number_tool with pre-picked edge (or just select annotation if one exists for that edge after Phase B).\n- **B. Replace with AnnotationSidebarModel.** Use the substrate's annotation list. But then the tree only shows annotated edges, not all edges — loses the \"browse all edges to pick one\" feature. Probably a UX regression.\n- **C. Hybrid.** Substrate's annotation sidebar (in the AnnotationDockPane) handles annotation listing. Custom edge tree continues to exist as a separate \"geometry inspector\" panel for edge browsing/picking, no longer pretending to be the annotation list. This is the cleanest separation.\n\n**Recommendation:** C, but needs user confirmation before A2 lands (the deletion path differs).\n\n**Decide before:** Phase A2 implementer round.","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:23:52","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:47:45","tags":["annotations","cad","design-decision"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"User chose Option C: keep custom edge tree as geometry inspector; substrate sidebar handles annotation listing. Drop _selected_edge_id state mirror. Tree click activates cad_edge_number_tool with pre-picked edge.","answer":"Option C: keep custom edge tree as a CAD-specific geometry inspector (separate from the substrate's annotation list). The substrate AnnotationDockPane sidebar (mounted in Phase A1) handles annotation listing. The edge tree continues to exist for browsing/picking geometry. Drop the bidirectional `_selected_edge_id` mirror — tree click activates `cad_edge_number_tool` with a pre-picked edge, no parallel selection state. Decided 2026-05-02 by user."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b807e67c01a3f2c0914ce38c81","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD Phase B1: define cad/edge anchor type schema + register resolver via host.register_anchor_resolver","description":"Spine of Phase B. The substrate has the contract (`host.register_anchor_resolver(plugin_id, type_name, callable)`, resolver returns Vector3/Vector2 or null/stale). The CAD side has not yet implemented a real edge anchor type. Without this, every edge annotation today stuffs raw 3D coords into primitives (cad_edge_number_kind.gd:118-121, cad_edge_number_tool.gd:248-251) — re-evaluation silently leaves callouts at stale positions.\n\n**Anchor schema (proposed):**\n```\n{\n  \"plugin\": \"cad\",\n  \"type\": \"edge\",\n  \"id\": <stable_edge_id>  # int, from worker payload\n}\n```\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Define schema. Add to a CAD-side constants file or inline in CadAnnotationHost.\n2. In CadAnnotationHost._init or _ready, call `register_anchor_resolver(\"cad\", \"edge\", _resolve_edge_anchor)`.\n3. `_resolve_edge_anchor(anchor: Dictionary) -> Variant` looks up the edge id in `_edge_registry_data`, returns `Vector3(midpoint.x, .y, .z)` if found, returns null OR returns a Dict with `stale: true` if not found (verify substrate convention against AnnotationHost.gd).\n4. Verify `host.resolve_anchor({plugin:\"cad\", type:\"edge\", id:N})` returns the right value.\n\n**Depends on:** None. Can run in parallel with Phase A.\n**Out of scope:** Migrating kind/tool to use the anchor (Phase B2), worker-id stability across re-eval (Phase B3), MCP integration (Phase B4).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:05","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:05","tags":["annotations","cad","edge-anchors"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b82b977e11a6d6314f2ad6d571","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD Phase B2: migrate cad_edge_number_kind + tool to consume cad/edge anchor (drop 3D-primitive encoding)","description":"After Phase B1 ships, migrate the kind and tool to use the anchor envelope instead of raw 3D primitives.\n\n**Current state:**\n- `cad_edge_number_kind._render_multi_pane` reads 3D coords from `primitives[0].at` (kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd:118-121). Multi-pane projection works (per Phase A predecessor), but the position never updates after re-evaluate.\n- `cad_edge_number_tool` knows the edge_id (saves to `payload.edge_id`) but writes the world-space midpoint into primitives (tools/cad_edge_number_tool.gd:248-251) as the rendering source of truth.\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Annotation envelope shape: replace the 3D primitive at-array with `anchor: {plugin:\"cad\", type:\"edge\", id:<edge_id>}`. Drop `primitives[0].at` (or keep an empty primitives array).\n2. cad_edge_number_kind render path: call `host.resolve_anchor(annotation.anchor)` to get current world position; pipe through host.get_panes() projection (already works). Mark stale via standard substrate UX when resolver returns null.\n3. cad_edge_number_tool emission: build envelope with anchor, drop primitive coordinates.\n4. Test: place an annotation, re-evaluate the model with same DSL → callout still attached to the same edge. Re-evaluate with different DSL where that edge id no longer exists → annotation marked stale.\n\n**Depends on:** Phase B1 (anchor schema + resolver).\n**Out of scope:** Worker-id stability across DSL changes (Phase B3), MCP path (Phase B4).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:14","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:25:01","tags":["annotations","cad","edge-anchors"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b859d97f51a96731df392ac5f4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD Phase B3: worker edge-ID stability across re-evaluation (rebind matching edges, mark stale otherwise)","description":"After Phase B1+B2, edge anchors resolve correctly within a single evaluation. But the worker wholesale-replaces `_edge_registry_data` on every evaluate (CADPanel.gd:398), so existing annotations may point at edge IDs that the new evaluation reuses for a DIFFERENT edge — silently giving wrong results. This is the deeper version of the original \"edges floating in space\" bug.\n\n**Open question:** What should \"stable edge ID\" mean across re-evaluations?\n\n**Options:**\n- **A. Rebind by geometric match.** On evaluate, for each existing annotation, look up the previous edge's start/end/midpoint and find the closest match in the new edge registry. Update the annotation's edge_id to the new id. Fast but heuristic; can drift.\n- **B. Rebind by topological signature.** Worker emits a stable hash per edge (based on incident faces + curve type). Annotation stores the hash; resolver maps hash→current id at evaluate time. More robust but requires worker-side change.\n- **C. Mark stale aggressively.** On every evaluate that changes edge count or any incident-face hash, mark all existing edge-anchored annotations stale and require user re-anchor. Simplest, worst UX.\n\n**Recommendation:** B if the worker change is small; A as a fallback. C only if neither is feasible.\n\n**Scope:**\n1. Pick option (A/B/C) — likely needs short user discussion.\n2. Implement on worker side (if B) and CAD plugin host side (rebind logic).\n3. Plumb staleness through AnnotationLifecycle so the substrate's standard \"broken/stale\" UX surfaces correctly.\n\n**Depends on:** Phase B1+B2 (anchor exists and is consumed).\n**Out of scope:** MCP path (Phase B4).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:26","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:25:04","tags":["annotations","cad","edge-anchors","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019de9b874dc704eb1ff65892e29dbc5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD Phase B4: MCPCadTools._cad_annotate_edges — emit cad/edge anchors instead of raw point primitives","description":"MCPCadTools._cad_annotate_edges is currently substrate-aware for storage (calls host.add_annotation, MCPCadTools.gd:492) but builds the wrong envelope shape — `primitives: [{type:\"point\", at:[x,y,z]}]` (MCPCadTools.gd:480-490). Same DRY/anchor concern: the substrate is being used but no edge-anchor semantics flow through.\n\n**Scope:**\n1. After Phase B1+B2 ship, update _cad_annotate_edges to emit `anchor: {plugin:\"cad\", type:\"edge\", id:<edge_id>}` envelopes.\n2. Drop the raw at-array primitive.\n3. MCP-driven annotations now follow the same re-evaluation/staleness semantics as user-tool-created ones.\n\n**Depends on:** Phase B1+B2.\n**Out of scope:** Worker-id stability (Phase B3).","created_at":"2026-05-02T17:24:33","updated_at":"2026-05-02T17:25:07","tags":["annotations","cad","edge-anchors","mcp"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def2848547eaab2806d979429412d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Build Presentation_TileAnnotationHost adapter","description":"Create `ui/presentation_tile_annotation_host.gd` exposing the active slide's `tiles[]` AND a new `slide.annotations[]` field as an AnnotationHost so substrate AnnotationTransformTool can drive selection/SRT directly on tiles, while substrate kinds (callout / 2d_arrow / 2d_text) coexist on the same surface.\n\nAdapter responsibilities:\n- Subclass `AnnotationHost`. Define a LOCAL `signal annotations_changed()` (substrate convention — `AnnotationOverlay` listens for this exact name; base class does NOT define it).\n- Build a per-host `AnnotationRegistry` in `_init()`. Call `BuiltinKinds.register_all(_registry)` to enable substrate kinds, then register the three `Presentation_TileKind*` kinds.\n- `get_capabilities()` declares: `kinds: [\"callout\", \"2d_arrow\", \"2d_text\", \"presentation_tile_text\", \"presentation_tile_image\", \"presentation_tile_spreadsheet\"]`, `tools: [\"select\"]`.\n- `get_annotations()` returns CONCATENATION of:\n  (a) synthesized tile annotations: one per tile, kind = `presentation_tile_*`, `kind_payload` carries pixel-space rect + rotation derived from normalized `x/y/w/h/rotation` and current `_slide_rect.size`.\n  (b) persisted real annotations from `slide.annotations[]`.\n- `update_annotation(id, new_dict)` routes by kind: tile kinds → renormalize geometry → write back to underlying tile dict (slide_model API only, no copy of tile data); substrate kinds → replace in `slide.annotations[]`. Emit `annotations_changed` after.\n- `add_annotation` / `remove_annotation` route same way; tile-kind add/remove is rejected (tiles created via PLACE flow), substrate-kind add/remove writes to `slide.annotations[]`.\n- `set_selected_annotation_id(id)` MUST emit `selection_changed(id)` ONLY when value actually changes (mirror `Cad_AnnotationHost.gd:168-172` to avoid loops). Route to slide_canvas `selected_tile_id` when id matches a tile annotation.\n- `transform_screen_to_doc()` inverts the canvas's pan/zoom matrix: subtract `_slide_rect.position` (doc space = slide-local pixels). `transform_doc_to_screen()` is the inverse.\n- Call `AnnotationHost._stamp_anchor(stored, self)` on add + update for substrate kinds (mirror CAD).","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:44:47","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:30:45","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","dry","plugin","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","resolution":"Round 2. Plugins commit e238c20 added presentation_tile_annotation_host.gd. Dual-source get_annotations (synthesized tiles + persisted slide.annotations[]); registers BuiltinKinds + 3 tile kinds; routes update/add/remove by kind discriminator; emits annotations_changed/selection_changed per substrate convention; transform_screen_to_doc converts to slide-local pixels. Smoke-tested end-to-end (envelope synthesis, transform writeback, callout add)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def2862aa771fbbf9ef4aad55c52c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Define AnnotationKind subclasses for tile kinds","description":"Implement AnnotationKind for `presentation_tile_text`, `presentation_tile_image`, `presentation_tile_spreadsheet` so the substrate knows how to hit-test and bound them.\n\nApproach:\n- All three tile kinds are rectangles → factor into a shared `Presentation_TileKindBase` with `hit_test(annotation, point_doc)` and `bounds(annotation)` over the synthesized translation+scale transform.\n- Three thin subclasses (`Presentation_TileKindText`, `Presentation_TileKindImage`, `Presentation_TileKindSheet`) register the kind ids with the registry.\n- Register kinds in the panel `_ready()` against the host's registry; cleanup on panel teardown.\n- Confirm hit_test honors rotation once schema migration adds it.","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:44:54","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:30:42","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","dry","plugin","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","resolution":"Round 2. Plugins commit e238c20 added 4 new files: Presentation_TileKindBase + TileKindText/Image/Spreadsheet. Geometry-only adapters; bounds/hit_test/transform_annotation/primary_anchor_point on the (rect_px, rotation_rad) envelope. Smoke-tested end-to-end. Defect found and fixed: subclasses originally used sibling class_name in extends, fails for off-tree plugins (now string-path extends; nudge hint saved)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def2880ba7c8886f47bf2511e73ef","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Schema migration: add optional tile.rotation field","description":"Add optional `rotation: float` to tile schema (default 0.0) so the substrate transform tool's rotate-ring can persist.\n\nChanges:\n- `slide_model.gd`:\n  - `make_text_tile / make_image_tile / make_spreadsheet_tile`: accept optional `rotation` arg; omit field when 0.0 (keeps existing decks byte-identical).\n  - `validate_tile`: accept missing field; if present, must be a float (use `_coerce_*` style helper — JSON.parse returns float so accept whole-number floats).\n  - Add `set_tile_rotation(slide, tile_id, rotation)` mutator.\n- Existing decks load unchanged (omit-when-default → backwards-compat).\n- Tests: round-trip with rotation present + absent; validator rejects non-numeric.","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:45:02","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:01:41","priority":2,"tags":["plugin","presentation","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","resolution":"Round 1 of universal-select DRY refactor. Plugins commit e2dc967 added rotation field + set_tile_rotation mutator + validator updates. Test counts: 168 PASS / 0 FAIL on test_slide_model.gd (was 84/0). Substrate regression unchanged at 39/0. Path-portability follow-up shipped in plugins commit 2371d8d."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def28aaac79928594bff9eaa8b965","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Replace slide_canvas custom drag/resize/halo with substrate tools","description":"Drop the custom drag/resize/hit-test/halo path in `ui/slide_canvas.gd` and route SELECT-tool input through substrate AnnotationSelectTool + AnnotationTransformTool.\n\nScope:\n- Remove DragMode.MOVE / DragMode.RESIZE branches and resize-handle math (~300 LOC).\n- Remove dashed-rect selection halo `_draw()` override — substrate transform tool draws its own gizmo.\n- Keep DragMode.PLACE (rubber-band placement during TEXT/IMAGE/SHEET tools — substrate doesn't cover authoring).\n- Mount an AnnotationOverlay (or equivalent) child on the canvas that holds the tools, configured against the new TileAnnotationHost.\n- Tool palette SELECT activates AnnotationSelectTool; TEXT/IMAGE/SHEET keep their place-then-create flows.\n- Wheel-zoom + middle-pan stay on slide_canvas; transform_screen_to_doc on the host inverts them so substrate sees doc-space coords.\n- Inline TextEdit overlay (white-bg/dark-text) stays — substrate selection just sets `selected_tile_id`, double-click still opens editor.","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:45:13","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:32:51","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","dry","plugin","presentation","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def28c59e71309330e4421c84038e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Tests for TileAnnotationHost adapter + tile kinds","description":"Headless SceneTree tests for the substrate-integration layer.\n\nTile-as-annotation coverage:\n- Adapter round-trip: build deck with N tiles → adapter exposes N tile annotations → mutate transform.translation+scale+rotation → underlying tile dict reflects new x/y/w/h/rotation.\n- Hit-test: each tile kind's `hit_test(point)` returns true for interior point, false for outside, true for rotated-rect interior after rotation.\n- Bounds: kind bounds match `Rect2(x*size.x, y*size.y, w*size.x, h*size.y)` for unrotated tiles after norm→pixel projection.\n- Z-order: hit-test on overlapping tiles returns top-most (matches existing slide_canvas tile ordering).\n\nSubstrate-kind coverage:\n- Register a `callout` envelope via `host.add_annotation()` → it persists into `slide.annotations[]`, NOT into `tiles[]`.\n- `host.get_annotations()` returns BOTH sources concatenated, with correct kind discriminators preserved.\n- Update + remove a substrate-kind annotation; tile annotations remain untouched.\n- Round-trip slide JSON: `slide.annotations[]` survives serialize/deserialize; omit-when-empty (no `annotations` key when array is empty).\n\nSelection signal coverage:\n- Setting `selected_annotation_id` to the SAME value twice emits `selection_changed` only ONCE (no infinite loop / re-emit).\n- Setting selected id on the host updates slide_canvas state and vice versa.\n- Removing a selected annotation clears selection and emits `selection_changed(\"\")`.","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:45:19","updated_at":"2026-05-03T21:27:16","priority":3,"tags":["plugin","presentation","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019def28e6be7e358a7a80e33014e526","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"HITL: end-to-end universal-select tool re-review","description":"Final HITL pass after substrate integration lands.\n\nWalk the flow:\n- Place a text tile, image tile, spreadsheet tile via the tool palette.\n- Switch to SELECT tool → click each tile → SRT gizmo appears with corners/edges/rotate-ring/inside zones.\n- Drag corner → uniform scale; aspect ratio handled per substrate convention.\n- Drag edge → axis-locked scale.\n- Drag rotate-ring arc → tile rotates; rotation persists across save/load round-trip.\n- Drag inside → translate.\n- Wheel-zoom on canvas while gizmo active → gizmo screen-size + handle hit zones still correct.\n- White-on-white text fix from round 2 still holds (reproduce: type \"I like cats\" in text tile, click away — text visible).\n- Fullscreen preview Window still renders rotated tiles correctly.","created_at":"2026-05-03T18:45:28","updated_at":"2026-05-04T03:21:57","priority":2,"tags":["hitl","plugin","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","blocked_by":"all prior work_items in this DCR's DRY substrate set"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019defbd27767cb6a679de6dd4a02c4b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Schema migration: add optional slide.annotations[] field","description":"Add optional `slide.annotations: Array = []` to the slide schema so substrate annotation envelopes (callout / 2d_arrow / 2d_text, plus future kinds) can persist alongside tiles. Required by T4 Reveal (annotation-id sequencing) and by the TileAnnotationHost adapter (work_item 019def2848547eaab2806d979429412d).\n\nChanges:\n- `slide_model.gd`:\n  - `make_slide(...)`: accept optional `annotations: Array = []` arg; omit field when empty (keeps existing decks byte-identical).\n  - `validate_slide`: accept missing field; if present, must be Array of Dictionary (envelope shape validated by AnnotationV2Schema downstream — host validates on add/update).\n  - Add `add_annotation(slide, envelope)`, `update_annotation(slide, id, new_envelope)`, `remove_annotation(slide, id)` mutators that operate on `slide.annotations[]`.\n- Existing decks load unchanged (omit-when-empty → backwards-compat).\n- Tests: round-trip with annotations[] present + absent; mutator helpers add/update/remove correctly.\n\nBlocks work_item 019def2848547eaab2806d979429412d (TileAnnotationHost needs this field to persist substrate annotations).","created_at":"2026-05-03T21:27:24","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:01:44","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","plugin","presentation","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","resolution":"Round 1 of universal-select DRY refactor. Plugins commit e2dc967 added slide.annotations[] field + add/update/remove_annotation slide-level mutators + validator updates. Tests covered in same 168/0 PASS suite."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019defbd4a0c7752b8e3b9bb2ab213ec","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire substrate AnnotationToolbar (callout / 2d_arrow / 2d_text) into SlideEditorPanel","description":"After the universal-select DRY refactor lands and HITL re-review closes, expose the substrate annotation authoring tools in the slide editor so users can place callouts / arrows / 2d_text overlays on top of slide content. Pre-req for T4 Reveal (annotation-id sequencing).\n\nApproach:\n- Add a second toolbar row to SlideEditorPanel containing the substrate `AnnotationToolbar` configured with the TileAnnotationHost. The substrate toolbar handles tool dispatch + button toggle UI for free.\n- Existing R2 panel toolbar (Select / Text / Image / Sheet) stays as the tile-authoring row; substrate toolbar is the annotation-authoring row.\n- Substrate AnnotationOverlay (already mounted by work_item 019def28aaac79928594bff9eaa8b965) handles input dispatch and writeback for both layers — no new overlay needed.\n- SELECT in either toolbar deselects all in the other (mutual exclusion to prevent ambiguous gizmo state).\n\nOut of scope: T4 Reveal sequencing UI. This work_item only enables AUTHORING the annotations that T4 will later sequence.","created_at":"2026-05-03T21:27:33","updated_at":"2026-05-03T21:27:33","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","plugin","presentation","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019defdc76627d9a906bfad6f8957593","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"SingletonObject autoload spams 4 Node-not-found errors in headless test runs","description":"When running any test headlessly via `godot --headless --path src --script <test>`, the `SingletonObject` autoload's `_ready` tries to bind to scene nodes that only exist under `MainScene.tscn`. Headless script mode skips MainScene, so 4 lookups fail and emit ERROR-level lines:\n\n- `singleton_object.gd:1462` — `vboxEditorMain` (editor tab parent)\n- `singleton_object.gd:1605` — `MainUI`\n- `singleton_object.gd:1637` — `RootControl`\n- `singleton_object.gd:1845` — `PreferencesPopup`\n\nNothing depends on the result; tests pass. But the noise pollutes test output and makes real failures harder to spot.\n\nFix: guard each lookup with a headless check. Either:\n- `if not DisplayServer.window_can_draw(): return` early in the affected `_ready` paths, or\n- `if get_tree().root.has_node(path):` per-lookup before `get_node()`.\n\nAcceptance: `godot --headless --path src --script <any plugin test>` produces no `Node not found` ERROR lines from `singleton_object.gd`.","created_at":"2026-05-03T22:01:36","updated_at":"2026-05-03T22:01:36","priority":3,"tags":["autoload","noise","singleton","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df01438217e028ef18644f108992d","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool should own tool lifecycle (on_activate/on_deactivate)","description":"Substrate-level fix to remove a sharp edge that bit the presentation plugin during the universal-select DRY refactor (Round 3, 2026-05-03).\n\n**Current behavior:** `AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool(tool)` only stores the tool reference and connects the `annotation_modified` signal. It does NOT call `tool.on_activate(host)`. The substrate's normal path is `AnnotationToolbar`, which explicitly calls `tool.on_activate(_host)` at AnnotationToolbar.gd:528. Hosts that wire `AnnotationOverlay` directly (bypassing the toolbar — e.g., the presentation panel which has its own custom toolbar) must manually call `on_activate(host)` after creating the tool, otherwise `tool._host` stays null and every `on_pointer_down` early-returns at AnnotationTransformTool.gd:122 — clicks are silently dropped.\n\n**Proposed:** Move tool lifecycle into `AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool`:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc set_active_tool(tool: AnnotationAuthorTool) -> void:\n    if _active_tool != null:\n        # Detach signals first (existing behavior)\n        if _active_tool.annotation_modified.is_connected(_on_tool_annotation_modified):\n            _active_tool.annotation_modified.disconnect(_on_tool_annotation_modified)\n        # NEW: deactivate before swap\n        _active_tool.on_deactivate()\n    _active_tool = tool\n    if _active_tool != null:\n        if not _active_tool.annotation_modified.is_connected(_on_tool_annotation_modified):\n            _active_tool.annotation_modified.connect(_on_tool_annotation_modified)\n        # NEW: activate with the host\n        if _host != null and _host is AnnotationHost:\n            _active_tool.on_activate(_host)\n    mouse_filter = Control.MOUSE_FILTER_STOP if _active_tool != null else Control.MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE\n    active_tool_changed.emit(_active_tool)\n    queue_redraw()\n```\n\n**Risk:** `AnnotationToolbar` still calls `on_activate` itself (toolbar.gd:528). After this change, tools would be activated TWICE in the toolbar path (once by toolbar before set_active_tool, once by overlay). Audit that double-activation is idempotent in all `AnnotationAuthorTool` subclasses, OR remove the toolbar's `on_activate` call and let the overlay own it (cleaner — single source of truth).\n\n**Coordination:** Should pair with a check that `_host` is non-null when `set_active_tool` is called. Hosts that call `set_active_tool` before `set_host` would currently work (no on_activate fires); after the change they'd silently fail to activate. Add a push_warning when host is null to surface the misordering.\n\n**Surfaced from:** Presentation Round 3 RCA (Codex). See nudge `minerva-plugin-platform/substrate_tool_on_activate_required` for the workaround pattern presentation now uses.\n\n**Acceptance:**\n1. `AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool` calls `on_activate(_host)` on the new tool when `_host` is set.\n2. `AnnotationOverlay.set_active_tool` calls `on_deactivate()` on the old tool before swapping.\n3. `AnnotationToolbar` either drops its `on_activate` call OR is verified idempotent.\n4. Existing callers (graphics editor, CAD plugin via toolbar, text editor) still pass their tests.\n5. New unit test: instantiate AnnotationOverlay, set_host(host), set_active_tool(tool), assert tool._host == host.","created_at":"2026-05-03T23:02:30","updated_at":"2026-05-03T23:02:30","priority":3,"tags":["annotation-overlay","annotations","ergonomics","substrate","tool-lifecycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df1f302e77a2784ef5df3d6723b24","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Schema: structured outline levels for text tiles (replace whitespace encoding)","description":"## Context\n\nT7 MCP tools (`019dc0bc768a7fc19c7ab73009762231`) ship in v1 with bullet/numbered list levels encoded as **leading whitespace** in the tile content string (e.g. 4 spaces or `\t` per indent level). This was chosen as a tactical zero-schema-change approach so the MCP surface could land without a v1.x bump.\n\n## Why this needs replacing\n\nWhitespace-encoded levels are fragile for MCP authoring:\n- Trimming/normalization at any layer (JSON encoders, RichTextLabel preprocessors, copy-paste round-trips) destroys the level information silently.\n- Level depth is implicit and not validated; deeply-indented content can drift visually with no error.\n- BBCode rendering of `[indent]` does not robustly nest with `[ul]`/`[ol]` semantics in Godot RichTextLabel.\n\n## Proposed change\n\nEither:\n\n(a) **Per-line levels** — `content` becomes a list of `{text, level: int}` for `bullet`/`numbered` tiles. Plain text mode unchanged.\n\n(b) **Separate outline tile kind** — promote outline to its own tile kind with explicit structured content; existing `text` tile keeps freeform BBCode.\n\nRecommend (a) — minimal schema delta, preserves single-tile-kind ergonomics. Plain-text tiles continue to use a string `content`; `bullet`/`numbered` tiles get `content: [{text, level}]`. Migration: legacy whitespace-encoded strings get parsed at load time into the structured form (one-shot, version bump to v1.1).\n\n## Affected surfaces\n\n- Schema doc in `Docs/pickup.md`\n- Plugin: `slide_model.gd` validators, `slide_canvas.gd` text tile rendering\n- MCP: `MCPPresentationTools.gd` `presentation_add_text_tile` arg shape (accept either string or structured for one minor version, then deprecate string for outline modes)\n- Tests: `test_slide_model.gd` adds outline-mode coverage\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Numbered list restart semantics (separate concern)\n- Per-line color/styling in outline (separate concern; BBCode in `text` field handles this)","created_at":"2026-05-04T07:45:28","updated_at":"2026-05-04T07:45:28","priority":3,"tags":["mcp","outline","presentation","schema","v1.x"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbfcc57d81b4ac1300d4923094"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df1f4503676bdad4fa6b77f8820de","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Graphics editor: read PNG bytes from layer after generation","created_at":"2026-05-04T07:46:53","updated_at":"2026-05-04T07:46:53","tags":["graphics_editor","image_bytes","mcp","presentation"],"value":"After minerva_graphics_generate_iterative completes, the result dict only contains success/iteration metadata, NOT image bytes. To get PNG bytes for downstream MCP tools (e.g. embedding into a presentation tile): walk the editor.graphics_editor.layers[] array and call layer.image.save_png_to_buffer() — returns PackedByteArray of PNG. Pattern used at GraphicsEditorV2.gd:355,376,535. Also: GraphicsEditorV2.export_image(path) at line 1051 saves to disk. Note: _generate_graphics_iterative is BLOCKING (awaits MediaGen.pass_image_to_editor then editor.note_ready_for_chat, up to 240s+120s) and has a session-wide iteration cap (max_image_iterations, default 5) that new editors don't bypass.","component":"graphics_editor","key":"read_generated_png_bytes","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df3ae052f7443a51262c190fa85b4","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Presentation panel exposes get_deck()/set_deck() for live MCP mutation","created_at":"2026-05-04T15:49:21","updated_at":"2026-05-04T15:49:21","tags":["mcp","plugin","presentation","t7"],"value":"SlideEditorPanel.gd:519-526 has public get_deck() / set_deck(deck) accessors explicitly marked \"Public accessors (tests + future T7 wiring)\". MCP tools CAN drive an open panel live — find the editor by tab name via MCPToolUtils.find_typed_editor (or find_editor_panel for the inner panel), call panel.get_deck(), mutate the dict, call panel.set_deck() — set_deck triggers _refresh_all(). Preferred over disk round-trip when a tab is open. After mutation also call panel.emit_signal(\"content_changed\") OR use a public _emit_modified-equivalent so Minerva's host knows the file is dirty (the panel itself does NOT write to disk; that's the host's job via _on_panel_save_request returning the dict). Note: the off-tree class_name Presentation_SlideEditorPanel does NOT register in res://, so MCP code must access via duck-typing (panel.has_method(\"get_deck\")) not as Presentation_SlideEditorPanel typed.","component":"presentation_plugin","key":"panel_mcp_accessors","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df3b223957d46984fcf40a7353f9f","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Minerva treats inference-from-Variant as a fatal Parse Error","created_at":"2026-05-04T15:53:51","updated_at":"2026-05-04T15:53:51","tags":["gdscript","mcp","strict_mode"],"value":"Minerva runs GDScript with 'warnings-as-errors' in some lanes. The warning `INFERENCE_FROM_VARIANT` (the variable type is being inferred from a Variant value, so it will be typed as Variant) is treated as a Parse Error and stops script load. Trigger pattern: `var x := func_returning_Variant(...)` — e.g. helpers that return Variant (MCPToolUtils.find_editor_by_name, MCPToolUtils.check_required, panel-discovery helpers in MCP modules). Fix: write explicit type, even if it's Variant: `var x: Variant = MCPToolUtils.find_editor_by_name(name)`. Also applies to chains like `var ed := _find_panel_by_tab_name(name)` when the helper signature is `-> Variant`.","component":"gdscript","key":"strict_var_inference_from_variant","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df3b7fff871d8a063dda8c854ffdb","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"SceneTree test must defer load() to first process_frame for autoload-touching scripts","created_at":"2026-05-04T16:00:15","updated_at":"2026-05-04T16:11:36","tags":["autoload","godot","mcp","scenetree","test"],"value":"In `extends SceneTree` test scripts run via `godot --headless --path src --script test/foo.gd`, **SceneTree._init() runs BEFORE autoloads register**. Class-level `var X: Script = load(...)` triggers @implicit_new which fires inside _init's call frame; deferring `load()` into _init() body is NOT enough — autoloads still aren't ready. CORRECT FIX: defer everything to first process_frame: `func _init() -> void: process_frame.connect(_run_tests, CONNECT_ONE_SHOT)` + `func _run_tests() -> void: <do your loads + assertions>`. By process_frame, autoloads have been instantiated and `SingletonObject` resolves at parse time. WORKING EXAMPLE: src/test/test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd (Butter Tarts MCP integration test, 34 PASS / 0 FAIL). Note: cascading \"Failed to compile depended scripts\" errors are emitted because every Minerva-core script transitively touches MCPToolUtils which references SingletonObject. They print before _run_tests executes; ignore them — actual test runs cleanly.","component":"godot_test","key":"scenetree_test_autoload_load_order","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df3ed15537f55bbc5898904609cf0","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Presentation tile rendered aspect = (w/h) × slide_aspect, not just w/h","created_at":"2026-05-04T16:58:14","updated_at":"2026-05-04T16:58:14","tags":["aspect","mcp","presentation","rendering"],"value":"Tile coords are normalized 0..1 against the slide. The RENDERED rect aspect is (w_norm/h_norm) × (slide_w/slide_h), NOT just (w_norm/h_norm). For a 16:9 deck, multiply normalized aspect by 16/9 ≈ 1.78. This matters for any pixel-grade content sized to fit a tile under STRETCH_KEEP_ASPECT_CENTERED (the plugin's default for image tiles, slide_canvas.gd:457). Symptom of getting it wrong: synthesized PNGs render as a small centered shape (square in wide rect, vertical stripe in landscape rect, etc.) instead of filling. Slide aspect comes from deck['aspect'] = \"16:9\"|\"4:3\"|\"1:1\" — parse with split(\":\") to get w:h numbers. Working example: MCPPresentationTools._make_solid_color_png_base64 takes (w_norm, h_norm, slide_aspect) and computes rect_aspect = (w/h)*slide_aspect for sizing the source PNG.","component":"presentation_plugin","key":"tile_rendered_aspect_includes_slide_aspect","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df3fa03a07e1cb1ab0fc72c631fcb","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Text-tile font_size is derived from tile.h ÷ line_count, not authored","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:12:21","updated_at":"2026-05-04T17:12:21","tags":["fonts","mcp","presentation","rendering","tile_h"],"value":"As of 2026-05-04 (Option A coupling), the presentation plugin's text-tile renderer DERIVES font_px from rendered tile height, not from a tile.font_size field. Formula in slide_canvas.gd _apply_tile_view_style: font_px = (tile_rendered_h × 0.95) / (line_count × 1.3) where line_count = max(1, content.split(\"\\\n\").size()). This gives drag-corner-resize-the-text WYSIWYG: tile.h is the single source of truth for visual size. AUTHORING IMPLICATIONS: (1) MCP presentation_add_text_tile no longer accepts font_size — control text size via tile.h. (2) Sizing rules of thumb: title h≈0.10 → ~58px font; section header h≈0.07 → ~40px; body bullets h ≈ line_count × 0.05 → ~25px/line. (3) Word-wrap is NOT counted — if content wraps, visible lines exceed line_count and overflow. Authors should size tiles wide enough or use explicit \\\n breaks. (4) BBCode [font_size=N] still produces raw pixels and won't honor the rect — avoid. (5) tile.font_size field is still permitted on tile dicts (forward-compat for a future fixed-size mode) but currently ignored by the renderer.","component":"presentation_plugin","key":"text_tile_font_derives_from_tile_h","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df40decf07fbabbafcc25179c5f4b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Schema: stretch_mode field on image tiles (\"fill\" | \"keep_aspect\")","description":"## Context\n\nToday every image tile is rendered with `TextureRect.STRETCH_KEEP_ASPECT_CENTERED` (slide_canvas.gd:457). This is correct for photos and screenshots — distortion would be visible — but wrong for synthesized decorative content where source aspect is incidental:\n\n- HR lines (solid color, thin)\n- Background blocks / patterns\n- Gradients used as accents\n- Future: SVG-derived shapes\n\nToday's MCP-side workaround: in `MCPPresentationTools._make_solid_color_png_base64`, we synthesize the PNG at the tile's RENDERED aspect (normalized w/h × slide aspect) and floor the short edge to bias STRETCH_KEEP_ASPECT_CENTERED toward fill-by-long-axis. This works to within sub-pixel error but is fragile:\n\n- Only applies to MCP-synthesized solid-color tiles. Any image-from-graphics-editor or image-from-disk image that the author intended to be decorative will still get aspect-preservation.\n- Tightly couples MCP to the plugin renderer's stretch policy.\n- Doesn't generalize to gradients, patterns, or any non-flat decorative content.\n\n## Proposed schema\n\nAdd optional `stretch_mode` field to image tile dicts:\n\n- `\"keep_aspect\"` (default — current behavior; appropriate for photos)\n- `\"fill\"` (new — STRETCH_SCALE; appropriate for decorative blocks)\n\nPlugin renderer (`slide_canvas.gd:_build_image_view`) reads the field and selects the corresponding `TextureRect.stretch_mode`.\n\nMCP-side defaults:\n- `presentation_add_image_tile(solid_color=...)` → defaults `stretch_mode: \"fill\"`\n- `presentation_add_image_tile(image_path|image_base64|source_graphics_editor=...)` → defaults `stretch_mode: \"keep_aspect\"`\n- Authors can override either way.\n\n## Affected surfaces\n\n- Schema doc + plugin's `Presentation_SlideModel.validate_tile` accepts `stretch_mode` for image kind.\n- Plugin renderer at `slide_canvas.gd:_build_image_view`.\n- MCP `presentation_add_image_tile` adds `stretch_mode` arg.\n- MCP `_make_solid_color_png_base64` becomes simpler — no more aspect-matching synthesis (just produce a small flat PNG; renderer fills it).\n- Tests: cover both stretch modes.\n\n## Why this isn't urgent\n\nThe MCP-side aspect-matching workaround (with floor + 4096 long-edge) is currently rendering HRs and decorative solid blocks correctly to within a sub-pixel. Schema bump can wait until either (a) we add non-solid decorative tile sources or (b) authors complain about the implicit policy.\n\n## Migration\n\nExisting decks omit `stretch_mode`; renderer falls back to `keep_aspect` (current behavior). No backward-compat work needed.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:34:06","updated_at":"2026-05-04T17:34:06","priority":3,"tags":["image-tile","presentation","rendering","schema","v1.x"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Plan: MCP iteration interface (modify/render/annotate) for presentation plugin","description":"## Context\n\nShipped 2026-05-04: v1 MCP authoring surface (T7, work_item `019dc0bc768a7fc19c7ab73009762231`). Tools: `presentation_create_deck`, `presentation_open_deck`, `presentation_list_slides`, `presentation_add_slide`, `presentation_set_slide_background`, `presentation_add_text_tile`, `presentation_add_image_tile`. All compose-only — no modify, no remove, no render, no spreadsheet authoring, no annotation authoring.\n\nCurrent limitation surfaced during HITL: the LLM can build a slide but cannot **see** what it built (no render tool), cannot **change** what it built (no modify tool), cannot **clean up** (no remove), and cannot **respond to feedback** (no annotation read/write). For interactive iteration with the user authoring slides via chat-driven MCP, these are all load-bearing.\n\n## Goal\n\nClose the iteration loop:\n\n```\nLLM authors → render_slide → LLM evaluates → modify_tile → re-render\n              ↑\n              user adds annotation (\"title too small\") → LLM reads → addresses → resolves\n```\n\n## Architecture\n\n- **Live-tab + disk-mode parity** preserved across all new tools (target = `tab_name` for in-app, `path` for disk).\n- **Rendering** via SubViewport offscreen. Reuse existing `slide_canvas` for pixel-perfect parity with editor view (font scaling, layout math, etc. all \"for free\").\n- **Annotation-as-instruction** uses substrate's existing annotation kinds (callout, arrow_2d, text_2d, freehand, rectangle). Adds a `resolved: bool` field to annotation envelopes so addressed feedback can be tracked without deletion.\n- **Spreadsheet authoring** completes the third tile-kind that v1 left out.\n- **Integrated image-gen tool** wraps the current 3-call dance (create_graphics_editor → generate_iterative → add_image_tile-with-source) into one MCP call that returns the PNG bytes inline so non-chat clients can evaluate.\n\n## Implementation order\n\nTasks ordered by what unblocks what. Inspect-side tools first because nothing else can address tiles by ID without `list_tiles`. Render before annotate because annotation workflow needs visual feedback to be useful. Spreadsheet last because it's an isolated capability.\n\n1. **Inspect**: `list_tiles`, `list_annotations`, `get_tile`, `get_slide`.\n2. **Modify**: `modify_tile`, `set_slide_title`, `set_aspect`, `move_slide`.\n3. **Remove**: `remove_tile`, `remove_slide`.\n4. **Render**: `render_slide` (with `include_annotations: bool`), `render_thumbnail`, `render_tile`.\n5. **Annotation**: substrate `resolved` field → `add_annotation`, `remove_annotation`, `set_annotation_resolved`, `list_annotation_kinds`, `list_open_annotations`.\n6. **Spreadsheet**: `add_spreadsheet_tile`, `modify_spreadsheet_cells`, `resize_spreadsheet`.\n7. **Integrated image-gen**: `presentation_generate_image_for_tile`, optional `minerva_generate_image` (MediaGen-direct wrapper).\n\n## Acceptance\n\nA new \"Butter Tarts v2\" headless test composes a deck via the new surface, then *modifies* it (resize the HR to 0.85 width, swap the right-col placeholder for a different solid color, add a callout annotation, render to PNG, mark annotation resolved, render again). All in one test script. Validates the full loop without humans.\n\n## Reference\n\n- Existing module: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPresentationTools.gd`\n- Plugin model mutators (already implemented for many of these): `~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_model.gd` (`move_tile`, `resize_tile`, `set_tile_rotation`, `add_annotation`, `update_annotation`, `remove_annotation`)\n- Plugin panel public accessors: `get_deck()`, `set_deck()` at `SlideEditorPanel.gd:519-526`\n- Docket hints: `text_tile_font_derives_from_tile_h` (`019df3fa03a07e1cb1ab0fc72c631fcb`), `panel_mcp_accessors` (`019df3ae052f7443a51262c190fa85b4`), `tile_rendered_aspect_includes_slide_aspect` (`019df3ed15537f55bbc5898904609cf0`).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Schema-bump backlog (`019df1f302e77a2784ef5df3d6723b24`) for outline-level structured content.\n- Stretch-mode field (`019df40decf07fbabbafcc25179c5f4b`) for image tiles.\n- HITL gizmo polish (`019def28e6be7e358a7a80e33014e526`) — orthogonal.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:47:05","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:18:28","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","iteration","mcp","presentation","render","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41a2c1e72a591a6e8bc1131f99b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP inspect tools: list_tiles, list_annotations, get_tile, get_slide","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_list_tiles(slide_index, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success, tiles: [{tile_id, kind, x, y, w, h, rotation, content?, text_mode?, src_size_bytes?, rows?, cols?}]}`\n- For image tiles, do NOT inline `src` (could be megabytes). Return only `src_size_bytes` as a hint.\n- For text tiles, include `content` (bounded; cap at e.g. 8 KB and truncate with `_truncated: true` marker).\n- For spreadsheet tiles, include `rows` and `cols` only (cells via `get_tile`).\n\n**`presentation_list_annotations(slide_index, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success, annotations: [{annotation_id, kind, payload_summary, position, resolved}]}`\n- `payload_summary` is kind-specific: text content for text/callout kinds, geometry for shapes.\n- `resolved` defaults to `false`; reads the substrate field landing in the parallel work_item.\n\n**`presentation_get_tile(slide_index, tile_id, tab_name?|path?, include_src: bool = false)`** → full tile dict\n- Default omits image base64 to keep responses small. `include_src=true` returns it.\n\n**`presentation_get_slide(slide_index, tab_name?|path?, include_tile_src: bool = false)`** → full slide dict\n- Convenience for \"give me everything for this slide.\"\n\n## Acceptance\n- All four tools follow the existing `_resolve_target` pattern (live tab vs disk).\n- Headless test asserts list_tiles returns expected count + IDs after a compose; get_tile round-trips a known tile dict.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:47:29","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:04:45","priority":2,"tags":["inspect","mcp","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41a5bda72e69785e20c090ab9d5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP modify_tile (workhorse) + set_slide_title + set_aspect + move_slide","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_modify_tile(slide_index, tile_id, x?, y?, w?, h?, rotation?, content?, text_mode?, src?, image_path?, solid_color?, source_graphics_editor?, font_size?)`**\n- Every field optional; only specified ones change.\n- Image-source fields are mutually exclusive (only one of src/image_path/solid_color/source_graphics_editor at a time).\n- Coords clamped to [0,1]; out-of-range returns error.\n- For text tiles, content/text_mode update straightforwardly. For image tiles, switching source replaces tile.src in place.\n- Backs onto plugin's `move_tile`/`resize_tile`/`set_tile_rotation` mutators where they exist; rest mutates the tile dict directly + commits via `_commit_target`.\n\n**`presentation_set_slide_title(slide_index, title)`** — set or clear (empty string clears the field per omit-when-default).\n\n**`presentation_set_aspect(aspect)`** — change deck aspect (\"16:9\"|\"4:3\"|\"1:1\"). Tiles stay normalized; rendering aspect changes only.\n\n**`presentation_move_slide(from_index, to_index)`** — reorder. Plugin model does not have a built-in mutator; implement inline (Array.remove_at + insert).\n\n## Acceptance\n- modify_tile round-trips: list → modify x → list, confirms the change persisted.\n- Spreadsheet tiles can be moved/resized/rotated via modify_tile (cells unchanged); content edits to spreadsheets handled by sibling tool in spreadsheet work_item.\n- move_slide preserves tile data; index references in any future \"next/prev\" tools account for reorder.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:47:41","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:28:48","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","modify","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41a6f8777869d487aacb35d4886","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP remove tools: remove_tile, remove_slide","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_remove_tile(slide_index, tile_id, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success}` or error.\n- Backs onto `tiles.remove_at` after find-by-id.\n\n**`presentation_remove_slide(slide_index, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success}` or error.\n- Refuses if removal would leave the deck with zero slides (`Cannot remove the only slide`).\n- Plugin already has `_SlideModel.remove_slide(deck, slide_id)` — wire to it.\n\n## Acceptance\n- After compose + remove, list_tiles/list_slides reflects the removal.\n- remove_slide with deck_size==1 fails with explicit error.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:47:46","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:31:10","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","presentation","remove"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41aa8c0715f889ceaf00150a96a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"MCP render tools: render_slide, render_thumbnail, render_tile (visual feedback)","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_render_slide(slide_index, tab_name?|path?, include_annotations: bool = false, max_dim: int = 1280)`** → `{success, png_base64, width, height}`\n- Drives an offscreen SubViewport that hosts a fresh `slide_canvas` instance with `set_preview_mode(true)`, sets the slide dict, lets it lay out, then `get_texture().get_image().save_png_to_buffer()`.\n- For live-tab targets, reads from the panel's currently-loaded `_deck` (so unsaved edits render).\n- For disk targets, loads the .mdeck.\n- `include_annotations=true` mounts the substrate AnnotationOverlay inside the SubViewport before capture so substrate annotations land in the PNG.\n- `max_dim` caps the long edge; aspect preserved.\n\n**`presentation_render_thumbnail(slide_index, max_dim: int = 256)`** — convenience wrapper at lower res.\n\n**`presentation_render_tile(slide_index, tile_id, max_dim: int = 512)`** → render just one tile's content. For image tiles, decode src and re-encode at requested resolution; for text/spreadsheet, render via the same SubViewport trick scoped to the tile.\n\n## Implementation notes\n\n- SubViewport's `disable_3d=true`, `transparent_bg=false` (we want the slide background to render).\n- Frame timing: SubViewport needs at least one frame to render before `get_texture()` returns valid pixels. Use `await RenderingServer.frame_post_draw` between adding canvas and reading texture.\n- The same `tile.h × slide_h × 0.95 / line_count × 1.3` font scaling applies inside the SubViewport because we're using the real slide_canvas. WYSIWYG free.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- render_slide returns valid PNG bytes that decode to the requested aspect.\n- include_annotations=true visibly differs from include_annotations=false on a slide with annotations.\n- Round-trip test: render_slide → decode PNG → assert dimensions match request and pixel at known position has expected color (e.g. background hex).","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:01","updated_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:01","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","presentation","render","visual-feedback"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41ace3b7d8fb1a132c607df0d1b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Substrate: add `resolved: bool` field to annotation envelopes","description":"## Scope\n\nAdd an optional `resolved: bool = false` field to substrate annotation envelopes. Currently envelopes carry `{id, kind, payload, ...}`; this adds `resolved` as an optional field, omit-when-default (false).\n\nChange touches:\n- AnnotationKind base envelope schema doc.\n- Substrate validators that pass on unknown fields (already do; just adding a known field).\n- AnnotationOverlay rendering: optionally tint resolved annotations differently (e.g. 50% opacity) so users can see addressed-vs-open at a glance.\n\nPlugin side: `Presentation_SlideModel.update_annotation` already accepts arbitrary envelopes — no change needed.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Existing annotations load fine without the field.\n- New annotations can carry `resolved: true|false`.\n- AnnotationOverlay visibly distinguishes resolved annotations.\n- Substrate annotation tests cover the new field.\n\n## Why a separate work_item\n\nSubstrate change with cross-plugin impact; cleaner to land independently of the MCP wrappers that consume it.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:10","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:35:36","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","schema","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5","resolution":"superseded"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41afdf2757fb1139f51368701c3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP annotation tools: add/remove/set_resolved + list_kinds + list_open","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_add_annotation(slide_index, kind, payload, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success, annotation_id}`\n- `kind` is one of substrate's annotation kinds: `callout`, `arrow_2d`, `text_2d`, `freehand`, `rectangle`, `presentation_tile_text`, `presentation_tile_image`, `presentation_tile_spreadsheet`. (The presentation_tile_* are the kind shims that exist; verify which substrate-native kinds are reachable.)\n- `payload` is kind-specific: text content for text/callout, points for freehand, rect for rectangle, etc. Document exact shapes per kind.\n- Backs onto `_SlideModel.add_annotation(slide, envelope)`.\n\n**`presentation_remove_annotation(slide_index, annotation_id, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success}`\n- Backs onto `_SlideModel.remove_annotation`. Handles the omit-when-default cleanup of `slide.annotations` key when array empties.\n\n**`presentation_set_annotation_resolved(slide_index, annotation_id, resolved, note?, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success}`\n- Updates the substrate `resolved` field. Optional `note` appends to a `resolution_notes` field for audit trail.\n- Depends on substrate work_item for the field.\n\n**`presentation_list_annotation_kinds()`** → `{success, kinds: [{kind, display_name, payload_schema}]}`\n- Discoverability for the LLM. Reads from substrate's AnnotationKindRegistry.\n\n**`presentation_list_open_annotations(deck_path?|tab_name?)`** → `{success, open: [{slide_index, annotation_id, kind, summary}]}`\n- Cross-deck query for \"show me everything still needing attention.\" Filters annotations where `resolved` is false or absent.\n\n## Acceptance\n- Compose deck → user adds annotation via universal-select tool → list_annotations returns it → modify_tile addresses it → set_annotation_resolved → list_open_annotations excludes it.\n- Standalone test exercises add/remove/set_resolved end-to-end on disk path.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:22","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:39:04","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","iteration-loop","mcp","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41b25a27be6bc97bb7c5b50694a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP spreadsheet tools: add_spreadsheet_tile, modify_spreadsheet_cells, resize_spreadsheet","description":"## Tools\n\n**`presentation_add_spreadsheet_tile(slide_index, x, y, w, h, rows, cols, cells?, header_row?, header_col?, rotation?)`** → `{success, tile_id}`\n- `cells` shape: `[[{value, type?, ...formatting}, ...], ...]` — 2D array matching rows×cols.\n- If cells omitted, fills with empty cells (CELL_EMPTY type).\n- Cell types from `_SlideModel`: `0=empty, 1=text, 2=number, 3=date, 4=formula`.\n\n**`presentation_modify_spreadsheet_cells(slide_index, tile_id, cells: [{row, col, value, type?, bold?, italic?, alignment?, text_color?, bg_color?, formula?}], tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success, cells_updated}`\n- Batch update: each entry specifies row/col + only the fields to change. Other cells unchanged.\n- Out-of-bounds row/col returns error per-entry but continues processing valid entries.\n\n**`presentation_resize_spreadsheet(slide_index, tile_id, rows, cols, tab_name?|path?)`** → `{success}`\n- Grows or shrinks the grid. Existing cells preserved when grid grows; truncated when shrinks.\n\n## Acceptance\n- Round-trip: add 2x3 sheet with header_row → modify cells (1,2) bold + value → list_tile returns updated cells → resize to 3x3 → existing cells preserved + new row empty.\n- Plugin's existing `_build_spreadsheet_view` renders the result; visual smoke via render_slide.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:33","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:42:57","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","presentation","spreadsheet"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41b656973619677ed3c45634bc4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"MCP integrated image-gen: presentation_generate_image_for_tile + minerva_generate_image","description":"## Today's pain\n\nThe current image-gen flow requires three MCP calls:\n1. `minerva_create_graphics_editor(\"name\")`\n2. `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative(editor_name, prompt, criteria)` — blocks 30-240s, attaches result to chat as a Note\n3. `presentation_add_image_tile(source_graphics_editor=\"name\", ...)` — pulls bytes from the editor\n\nAwkward orchestration. The chat-Note attachment is the only way the LLM \"sees\" the result, which doesn't work for non-chat MCP clients.\n\n## Tools\n\n**`presentation_generate_image_for_tile(slide_index, tile_id?, prompt, criteria?, x?, y?, w?, h?, tab_name?|path?, rotation?)`** → `{success, tile_id, png_base64, width, height}`\n- If `tile_id` given: replaces that tile's `src` in place. Tile must be image kind.\n- If `tile_id` omitted: creates a new image tile at the supplied x/y/w/h.\n- Internally: creates a hidden graphics editor, runs `execute_ai_action`, awaits MediaGen signal, reads `layer.image.save_png_to_buffer()`, embeds as base64. Cleans up the graphics editor on success.\n- Returns the PNG bytes inline so the caller can evaluate without depending on chat-Note attachment.\n- Honors the session-wide iteration cap (`max_image_iterations`, default 5) — passes through.\n\n**`minerva_generate_image(prompt, criteria?, max_size: int = 1024)`** → `{success, png_base64, width, height}`\n- Standalone wrapper around `MediaGen.send_media_gen_request` — does NOT need a graphics editor host.\n- Useful outside slide context (e.g. for inline chat use, mockups, etc.).\n- Same iteration cap.\n\n## Implementation notes\n\n- Hidden graphics editor: create the GRAPHICS-type Editor but don't add it to the editor pane (or add hidden). Tear down on completion.\n- For replace-tile flow, lock the tile during generation so concurrent modify_tile calls don't race.\n- `criteria` parameter passes through to enable `_generate_graphics_iterative` semantics (LLM-loop iteration).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Single tool call generates an image, places it on a tile, and returns the PNG bytes.\n- The bytes match what's stored in tile.src.\n- Cleanup leaves no orphaned graphics editor tabs.","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:49","updated_at":"2026-05-04T17:48:49","priority":2,"tags":["image-gen","integration","mcp","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df41b993378c29afa6cf18d9e16ae","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Test: Butter Tarts v2 — full iteration loop end-to-end","description":"## Scenario\n\nBuild on `test/test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd`. New test file: `test/test_mcp_presentation_iteration.gd`.\n\n1. Compose Butter Tarts deck via v1 tools.\n2. Call `render_slide(0)` → save PNG, assert dimensions, sample background pixel.\n3. Add a callout annotation: \"Make the HR span 0.85, not 0.67.\"\n4. Call `render_slide(0, include_annotations=true)` → assert PNG differs from clean render.\n5. Call `list_open_annotations()` → confirm callout is present.\n6. Call `list_tiles(0)` → find the HR tile by inspection (kind=image, w≈0.67, h≈0.012).\n7. Call `modify_tile(0, hr_tile_id, x=0.075, w=0.85)` to recenter and widen.\n8. Call `set_annotation_resolved(0, callout_id, true, note=\"HR widened to 0.85\")`.\n9. Call `list_open_annotations()` → confirm empty.\n10. Call `render_slide(0)` → save final PNG; assert HR fills the wider rect.\n11. Call `add_spreadsheet_tile` for a 2×3 nutrition table; `modify_spreadsheet_cells` to populate; render again.\n12. Call `presentation_generate_image_for_tile` for the right-col placeholder, replacing solid_color with a generated image. **Skipped in CI** unless `MEDIAGEN_AVAILABLE=1` env var (gated because it needs Core/network).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Test passes 30+ assertions covering every new tool.\n- Three rendered PNGs land at `/tmp/butter_tarts_v2_step_{2,4,10}.png` for human inspection.\n- Without MEDIAGEN_AVAILABLE, image-gen step is skipped with a PASS marker (not skipped silently).","created_at":"2026-05-04T17:49:02","updated_at":"2026-05-04T17:49:02","priority":3,"tags":["acceptance","iteration","mcp","presentation","test"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df443eb8777be962510697cdaddad","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Annotation envelopes use a lifecycle state machine, not a resolved bool","created_at":"2026-05-04T18:33:05","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:33:05","tags":["annotations","godot","presentation","schema","substrate"],"value":"Annotation v2 envelopes carry `lifecycle: String` ∈ {\"open\", \"applied\", \"resolved\", \"stale\"} — see AnnotationLifecycle.gd:21 and AnnotationV2Schema.gd. There is NO `resolved: bool` field. To express resolved-ness, set lifecycle=\"resolved\". To list \"open\" annotations, filter by lifecycle==\"open\". Authoritative writer: AnnotationTextAuthorTool._build_annotation @ AnnotationTextAuthorTool.gd:248-261 (per-kind author tools follow the same shape). Required envelope fields: id, kind, schema_version, anchor, kind_payload, lifecycle, author, view_context, visible_in_views, summary. Validator: AnnotationV2Schema.validate() AnnotationV2Schema.gd:76-157. Serialization strips only computed `anchored_to`; everything else round-trips to plugins via host_owned save dicts.","component":"minerva.annotations","key":"envelope_lifecycle_state","confidence":"high","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4513e477ea0b3e0b513446e1606","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Annotation v2 envelope per-field shape constraints + kind/anchor compatibility","created_at":"2026-05-04T18:47:38","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:49:18","tags":["annotations","envelope","presentation","schema","substrate"],"value":"Substrate v2 envelope (AnnotationV2Schema.gd) shape rules: (1) view_context = STRING (not Dict) — convention \"host:identifier\" e.g. \"presentation:slide_id\". (2) author = Dict, validated by AnnotationAuthor.validate() — requires `kind` ∈ {\"human\",\"ai\"}. (3) anchor REQUIRES {plugin: String, type: String, id: Variant (may be null), snapshot: {position: ...}}; the cute {kind:\"slide_relative\", x, y} is rejected. (4) Per-host kind allow-lists vary: presentation_tile_annotation_host accepts ONLY [\"callout\", \"2d_arrow\", \"2d_text\"] — `2d_` prefix, NOT `_2d` suffix. (5) **Kind/anchor compat** (_GENERIC_KIND_ANCHORS at AnnotationV2Schema.gd:36-54): `callout` accepts `*/*`; `2d_text`/`2d_arrow`/`2d_region`/etc. accept only `core/*` (anchor.plugin must be \"core\"). So MCP-authored presentation annotations should use kind=\"callout\" with anchor.plugin=\"presentation\", anchor.type=\"slide\", anchor.id=slide_id, anchor.snapshot.position=[x,y]. The plugin's slide_model.validate_deck() does NOT catch envelope-shape errors — it only checks `annotations[i] is Dictionary`; AnnotationV2Schema.validate() catches them at next read.","component":"minerva.annotations","key":"envelope_v2_field_shapes","confidence":"high","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df45696fb7ae49b21a9f359810d7f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Cold-review follow-ups for v2 MCP iteration tools (LIKELY/MINOR)","description":"Backlog of non-blocking findings from the cold reviewer's audit before the HITL pass for plan `019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5`. Two BLOCKERS were fixed inline (envelope shape; modify_tile staged mutations). The items below are real-but-can-defer.\n\n## LIKELY (real bugs surfacing during normal authoring)\n\n1. **modify_tile silently ignores rows/cols on spreadsheet tiles.** Description claims all fields modifiable; LLM will pass `rows: 5` and get nothing. Either reject with \"use resize_spreadsheet\" or document the carve-out.\n\n2. **modify_spreadsheet_cells leaves stale `type` on value-only patches.** Patch `{row:0, col:0, value:42}` on a CELL_TEXT cell — value becomes int 42 but type stays text. Fix: re-run `_auto_cell_type(value)` when value is patched without explicit type.\n\n3. **set_annotation_resolved(false) collapses applied/stale to open.** When current lifecycle is \"applied\" or \"stale\" and caller passes `resolved:false`, we silently demote to \"open\", losing the prior state. Reject with \"use lifecycle= explicitly\".\n\n4. **`is_zero_approx` silently erases small explicit rotations.** Caller passing `rotation: 0.001` thinking they're setting a tiny rotation gets the field erased. Document or use stricter zero-check.\n\n5. **tab_title basename collisions.** `_open_deck` uses `path.get_file()` as tab_title; two .mdeck files with the same basename in different dirs collide on `_find_panel_by_tab_name` and `_find_panel_for_path` fallback.\n\n6. **list_annotations payload_summary preference.** `_summarize_annotation_payload` picks `summary` first; for substrate-UI-authored callouts where summary is a short title and `kind_payload.text` is the body, list shows the title not the comment text.\n\n7. **`_validate_coords` doesn't reject `x+w > 1` or `y+h > 1`.** Tiles can extend past the slide edge. May be intentional (off-slide reveal animation), but undocumented.\n\n## MINOR\n\n- `MCPPresentationTools.gd:_summarize_tile` match arm uses literal `\"spreadsheet\"` with stale comment claiming TILE_SPREADSHEET isn't a constant — it IS a constant now. Replace.\n- `_open_deck` could crash if a non-presentation tab somehow shared the path (low likelihood, but a `has_method(\"get_deck\")` check is cheap).\n- `presentation_set_aspect` description tells LLM solid-color tiles will letterbox after aspect change but doesn't suggest `presentation_modify_tile` to re-synth.\n- `presentation_modify_tile` description says \"Every field is optional\" but slide_index/tile_id are required — confusing.\n- `presentation_list_annotations` description references \"the upcoming get_annotation tool\" — that tool doesn't exist in dispatch.\n- `presentation_list_annotation_kinds` returns kinds without anchor-compat hints — LLM has no way to discover that `2d_text` requires `anchor.plugin=\"core\"`.\n- Two slightly-different defaults for \"missing lifecycle\" semantics in `_summarize_annotation` vs `_list_open_annotations` — pick one.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nEach item triaged: fix vs. won't-fix vs. defer-further. Fixes land with headless test coverage where applicable.","created_at":"2026-05-04T18:53:28","updated_at":"2026-05-04T18:53:28","priority":3,"tags":["mcp","post-hitl","presentation","review-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4615a9e7f8bb33dcbb3c91de88b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Minerva MCP tool names MUST start with `minerva_` or HTTP gate rejects them","created_at":"2026-05-04T19:05:14","updated_at":"2026-05-04T19:05:14","tags":["godot","mcp","minerva-server","naming-convention"],"value":"MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd:328-330 has a hard gate: any tool_name that doesn't begin with \"minerva_\" is rejected with JSON-RPC -32602 \"Unknown tool: %s\". Tool discovery (tools/list) still surfaces non-prefixed tools as mcp__minerva__<name>, so they LOOK callable in the deferred tool list — but tools/call rejects them. Convention across all existing modules: minerva_<domain>_<verb>, e.g. minerva_pcb_add_component, minerva_cad_get_edge, minerva_annotations_add, minerva_video_add_cut. New MCP tool modules MUST follow this. Source: MCPPresentationTools shipped initially with `presentation_*` names (no prefix) and got -32602 on every call from a fresh Minerva launch; rename to `minerva_presentation_*` resolved it.","component":"minerva.mcp","key":"tool_name_must_start_with_minerva_prefix","confidence":"high","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df484801e776cad76154cb5964892","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Fastest local image-gen model is z_turbo (a.k.a. z_image_turbo), not qwen_2511_flex","created_at":"2026-05-04T19:43:37","updated_at":"2026-05-04T19:43:37","tags":["graphics-editor","media-gen","minerva","model-selection"],"value":"When picking a local image-gen model for fast generation in minerva_graphics_generate_iterative, default to z_turbo — it's listed in capabilities with id \"z_turbo\" but the user calls it \"z_image_turbo\". qwen_2511_flex shows lower default_steps (4 vs z_turbo's 9), but z_turbo is actually faster wall-clock. Step count is not a reliable speed proxy across models. supports_actions: z_turbo only does \"create\"; qwen_2511_flex supports compose_2/3/edit/etc. Use z_turbo for one-shot create; qwen_2511_flex when you need compose or edit actions.","component":"minerva.graphics","key":"fastest_local_model","confidence":"high","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df495f1eb74b28539f54b57a1e8a4","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_graphics_generate_iterative claims image_visible:true but MCP callers can't actually see the image","description":"## Summary\n\n`minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` returns `{\"success\": true, \"image_visible\": true, \"message\": \"The image is now visible...\"}` to the calling LLM. For in-app chats this is true — the proxy_note injection makes the image part of the next chat turn. **For MCP callers (Claude Code, etc.) it's a lie.** The image is never delivered to the calling LLM in any form — not in the tool response, not in any subsequent MCP-side channel.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe \"visibility\" mechanism is `Editor._proxy_note` getting added to `SingletonObject.detached_note_proxies` (Editor.gd:2011). This array is consumed by **in-app chat sends** via `SingletonObject.clear_consumed_proxies(history_id)` — the in-app chat code matches proxies to the active chat, attaches them to the LLM payload, then erases. MCP callers bypass this pipeline entirely; the proxy sits in the array forever (separate bug 019df48xxx — \"inject toggle stays on\" — was the symptom, fixed in commit f66cbbc0).\n\nSo an LLM evaluating its generation against `criteria` is operating purely on faith — the criteria is in the response message, but the image isn't.\n\n## How surfaced\n\nDuring HITL pivot of presentation MCP plan `019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5` — a 5-why on the inject-toggle bug exposed the deeper visibility gap. RCA chain: editor.toggle(true) → proxy_note created → expected to be consumed by next chat turn → but MCP path has no chat turn → proxy stranded → toggle stays on AND image never seen. The toggle leak was a symptom; the visibility lie is the disease.\n\n## Impact\n\nAnywhere an MCP-driven LLM is supposed to evaluate or iterate on generated images, the workflow is broken-by-design. Includes:\n- All current callers of `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` (Claude Code, MCP-driven agents, anything in the planned presentation MCP image-gen workflow)\n- The `presentation_modify_tile` source_graphics_editor flow assumes the image got there usefully — it does (the bytes are on disk in the layer), but the LLM never saw them to know what they look like\n- Future planned work_item `019df41b656973619677ed3c45634bc4` (integrated image-gen for presentation) inherits the same gap\n\n## Possible fixes (deferred — file only)\n\n1. **Embed PNG bytes (or thumbnail) inline in the MCP response.** Cleanest. Tool returns `{success, image_base64, width, height, ...}` so any LLM evaluating it has the bytes. Cost: response payload size — for 1024×1024 PNG ~1-2 MB base64.\n2. **Embed a base64 thumbnail (e.g. 256×256).** Mitigates payload size; LLM still gets a usable view for criteria evaluation.\n3. **Add a separate `minerva_graphics_get_layer` MCP tool** that returns the bytes for a named layer of a named editor. Two-call pattern; LLM opts in.\n4. **Fix the contract honestly:** rename `image_visible: true` to `image_persisted: true`; document that MCP callers must explicitly fetch the image to see it.\n\n(2) + (3) is the most LLM-ergonomic combo: thumbnail inline for cheap evaluation, full bytes available on demand.\n\n## Cluster hint\n\nThis is part of a growing pile of LLM-ergonomic gaps surfacing during presentation MCP HITL — proxy-note injection isn't visible to MCP callers, and several MCP tools quietly assume an in-app chat consumer. When time permits a dedicated \"LLM ergonomics cleanup\" pass, this should be triaged alongside:\n- 019df45696fb7ae49b21a9f359810d7f (cold-review LIKELY/MINOR followups for presentation MCP)\n- 019dc6543a4972c292f64ba4fa0d9a7d (LLM annotation ergonomics)\n\nTagging `llm-ergonomics` + `mcp-visibility` so they cluster.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nLLM calling `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` can evaluate the result against `criteria` without external help — either via inline image data in the response, or via a documented two-call pattern that returns the bytes.","created_at":"2026-05-04T20:02:40","updated_at":"2026-05-04T20:02:40","priority":3,"tags":["graphics-editor","llm-ergonomics","mcp","mcp-visibility","media-gen","side-quest"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4979edc7474afb14070c0905691","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"compose_final_image() returns empty during MCP graphics_generate_iterative toggle path","description":"## Symptom\n\nAfter `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` completes and the toggle path fires in `MCPEditorTools._generate_graphics_iterative()`, the debugger logs:\n\n```\nE 0:07:02:197   Editor.gd:1772 @ _create_note(): [Editor] compose_final_image() returned an empty image!\n  Stack:\n    Editor.gd:1772 @ _create_note()\n    Note.gd:1247 @ create_note()\n    ChatPane.gd:2745 @ _estimate_tokens_fast()\n    ChatPane.gd:2705 @ update_token_estimation()\n    Editor.gd:2016 @ _on_check_button_toggled()\n    Editor.gd:498 @ toggle()\n    MCPEditorTools.gd:743 @ _generate_graphics_iterative()\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\n`editor.toggle(true)` fires `_on_check_button_toggled(true)` (Editor.gd:1980), which kicks off two async paths off the same synchronous trigger:\n\n1. **Real proxy note**: `_proxy_note.create_note()` async; result delivered via `note_created` signal → `note_ready_for_chat` → MCP server resumes.\n2. **Token estimation side-effect**: `SingletonObject.Chats.update_token_estimation()` (Editor.gd:2015-2016) → `ChatPane._estimate_tokens_fast()` → `_create_note()` → `await graphics_editor.compose_final_image()` (Editor.gd:1768).\n\nPath (2) awaits `compose_final_image()` which returns an Image object whose `is_empty()` is true — the image bytes exist in the layer (verified: `modify_tile` with `source_graphics_editor` reads them successfully right after), but the layer-compose flat-merge hasn't finalized at the microtask point path (2) resumes. The `push_error` at Editor.gd:1772 fires.\n\nFor normal in-app usage (user toggles a long-idle graphics editor), `compose_final_image()` has nothing pending — returns immediately. The race only manifests when generation just completed and the toggle fires while composition is mid-flight, which is the MCP iterative-generate path.\n\n## Impact\n\n- Noisy `push_error` on every MCP graphics generation, masking real errors in logs.\n- Token estimation under-counts the image's tokens (0) for the duration of the race; the real proxy note (path 1) likely resolves correctly because compose_final_image has caught up by the time `_proxy_note.create_note` awaits it. Visual confirmation: image WAS visible in the slide tile after our HITL run, suggesting path 1 succeeded.\n- If timing tightened (slower disk, smaller debounce), the empty image could leak into the real note too. Fragile.\n\nSymptom of the broader \"toggle-as-multitasker\" architectural smell — same root as the prior toggle-leak bug (sibling in cleanup cluster).\n\n## Possible fixes\n\n1. **Defer token estimation until proxy note resolves.** Connect to `_proxy_note.note_created` and run `update_token_estimation()` only after; remove the synchronous call from `_on_check_button_toggled`. Proper fix.\n2. **Have `_estimate_tokens_fast` use a cached/heuristic for graphics notes** instead of rebuilding the note from scratch every toggle. Cheaper.\n3. **`compose_final_image()` returns a placeholder (e.g. a stale-but-non-empty cached image) while composition is pending** — avoids the empty-image push_error. Surface fix only.\n\nLikely (1) is right; (2) is also fine and smaller in scope.\n\n## Cluster\n\nSibling to:\n- 019df495f1eb74b28539f54b57a1e8a4 (image_visible:true is a lie for MCP callers)\n- 019df45696fb7ae49b21a9f359810d7f (cold-review LIKELY/MINOR)\n- 019dc6543a4972c292f64ba4fa0d9a7d (LLM annotation ergonomics)\n\nAll belong in the upcoming \"LLM ergonomics cleanup\" pass.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nNo `push_error` from `compose_final_image() returned an empty image!` during a successful `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` call.","created_at":"2026-05-04T20:04:30","updated_at":"2026-05-04T20:04:30","priority":3,"tags":["graphics-editor","llm-ergonomics","mcp","media-gen","race-condition","side-quest","toggle-as-multitasker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df49dce7d711496ea002db369cf1d","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Substrate UI emits v1 envelopes — different from AnnotationV2Schema's v2 spec","created_at":"2026-05-04T20:11:15","updated_at":"2026-05-04T20:12:30","tags":["annotations","envelope","presentation","schema-divergence","substrate","verified-hitl"],"value":"VERIFIED via HITL: substrate UI annotation tools persist to slide.annotations[] AS schema_version=1 envelopes, NOT v2. Observed v1 shape: {id, kind, schema_version:1, author:\"human\" (BARE STRING, not dict), view_context:\"presentation\" (BARE STRING, not \"host:id\"), anchored_to:\"\" (empty), primitives:[...]}. Different per-kind: 2d_arrow has primitives=[{kind:\"arrow\", from:[px,py], to:[px,py]}] and NO anchor, NO kind_payload. 2d_text has anchor={plugin:\"core\", type:\"canvas.point\", id:{x,y}, snapshot:{position:[px,py]}} AND primitives:[] AND kind_payload:{font_size:N, text:\"...\"} AND summary mirroring text. All coords are PIXELS in some slide-render space (e.g. 1280×720 for 16:9). To correlate to tile bounds (which are 0..1 normalized): divide by render dimensions. AnnotationV2Schema is the v2 SPEC but substrate UI hasn't migrated; v1 and v2 coexist in slide.annotations[]. My MCP add_annotation emits v2 — strictly compliant but DIFFERENT FROM what UI emits, so substrate readers may handle them differently. Cold review BLOCKER 1's \"fix\" was correct for v2 spec but may diverge from what the substrate UI actually consumes — needs HITL verification of write-direction round-trip. No get_annotation tool yet — use get_slide for full envelopes (backlog 019df45696fb).","component":"minerva.annotations","key":"substrate_ui_envelope_observed_shape","confidence":"high","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4daae01765ca103f28832c63bad","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Fullscreen slide preview ignores keyboard/mouse navigation","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:17:45","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:18:11","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["fullscreen","input-handling","plugin","presentation","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0bbd6937264880b1327c942d5b6","repro_steps":"1. Open a multi-slide deck in the presentation editor.\n2. Click the fullscreen toolbar button (or invoke fullscreen preview).\n3. In the popup window, press Right Arrow / Left Arrow / Space / PageDown / click anywhere.\n4. Observe: nothing happens. Only Escape works (closes the window)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dafe9e7bf08d618ccbaf6a53e7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"MCP playback tools (show_fullscreen / advance_slide / exit_fullscreen)","description":"## Scope\n\nToday's `MCPPresentationTools.gd` only mutates the deck (add/modify/remove tiles, slides, annotations, spreadsheets). There is no way for an LLM to drive a live presentation — to start fullscreen, advance to the next slide on cue, jump to a specific slide for Q&A, or exit the preview.\n\nThis unblocks \"AI co-presenter\" workflows: the model narrates while the deck advances itself, or a human presenter hands the model a cue (\"show me the raisin slide\") and gets it instantly.\n\n## Proposed tools\n\n- `presentation_show_fullscreen(tab_name?, path?, start_index?)` — opens the editor's fullscreen preview window at start_index (default = currently selected). Returns whether a fullscreen window is now active.\n- `presentation_advance_slide(tab_name?, path?, delta=1)` — moves the fullscreen view by ±N. Errors if no fullscreen window is open. Clamps to deck bounds; returns new index.\n- `presentation_jump_to_slide(tab_name?, path?, slide_index)` — absolute jump.\n- `presentation_exit_fullscreen(tab_name?)` — closes the preview window.\n- `presentation_get_playback_state(tab_name?)` — returns `{is_fullscreen: bool, slide_index: int, slide_count: int}`.\n\n## Plumbing\n\n- The plugin (`SlideEditorPanel`) already has `_on_fullscreen_pressed`, and now (post-fullscreen-bug fix) `_step_fullscreen_slide`/`_jump_fullscreen_slide` helpers.\n- Add a small panel-side API surface — `show_fullscreen(start_index)`, `set_preview_slide(index)`, `exit_fullscreen()`, `get_playback_state()` — and dispatch from MCP via the existing `MCPToolUtils.find_X` pattern.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Best filed AFTER fullscreen-input bug `019df4daae01` ships, since `set_preview_slide(index)` is half of that fix already.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Annotation reveal / \"build\" animations within a slide — separate workstream.\n- Speaker-notes window — separate workstream.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:18:05","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:18:05","priority":3,"tags":["ai-co-presenter","mcp","playback","presentation","v2"],"parent":"minerva:019df419ce567de0b7699b3be7b6c8b5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Editable plugin notes — round-trip plugin scene state through the Note system","description":"## Problem\n\n`Editor._create_plugin_scene_note()` (Editor.gd:1815) dispatches to a panel's optional `_on_panel_create_note_request(ctx)` hook. If the hook returns nothing — which is true for every plugin today, since none implement it — the host falls back to `_create_plugin_scene_screenshot_note`, producing an image-only Note.\n\nEven if a plugin implemented the hook, the recognized payload kinds in `_build_note_from_plugin_payload` (Editor.gd:1846) are only `text`, `html`, and `image`. There is no shape that can carry plugin-specific JSON and round-trip back into the originating editor. So a \"save to note\" of a presentation deck gets you a screenshot you can talk about — not a deck you can re-open and edit.\n\n## Goal\n\nMake a Note a first-class container for plugin scene state:\n\n```\nAuthor deck → Save to Note → Note carries (preview image + deck JSON)\n                              ↓\n              LLM sees image (chat works as today)\n                              ↓\n       User clicks \"Open in editor\" → presentation plugin reopens the deck → continue editing\n```\n\nSame pattern works for any plugin that has document-like state (text, graphics, future plugins).\n\n## Architecture\n\n### 1. Note schema: new kind `plugin_data`\n\nAdd to Note's kind enum / serializer:\n\n```\n{\n  kind: \"plugin_data\",\n  plugin_id: String,        // dispatch key for re-open\n  panel_name: String,       // which panel within the plugin\n  payload: Dictionary,      // opaque to host; plugin's own schema\n  preview_image: Image,     // for chat/visual context — REQUIRED\n  preview_alt_text: String, // for accessibility / LLM annotation\n}\n```\n\nNotes already store images well; the new kind layers `payload` on top. The image is required so dual-representation (LLM-screenshot + UI-thumbnail) keeps working without plugin special-cases.\n\n### 2. PluginScenePanelHost protocol additions\n\nTwo new hooks panels can implement:\n\n- `_on_panel_create_note_request(ctx) -> Dictionary` (ALREADY DEFINED, but extend the recognized return shape) — return `{kind: \"plugin_data\", plugin_id, panel_name, payload, preview_image, preview_alt_text}`.\n- `_on_panel_restore_from_note(payload: Dictionary) -> bool` (NEW) — called when a `plugin_data` note targeting this panel is opened. Plugin restores its scene from the payload. Return false if payload is unrecognized; host falls back to leaving the panel blank with an error toast.\n\n### 3. Editor.gd: dispatch path\n\n- `_build_note_from_plugin_payload` accepts the new `plugin_data` kind, builds a Note with image populated from `preview_image` and stores `plugin_id` + `panel_name` + `payload` as note metadata.\n- New `Editor._open_note_in_editor(note)` (or a NotesContainer-side handler): when the user opens a `plugin_data` note, route to `Editor.create()` with the right plugin/panel target and call `_on_panel_restore_from_note(note.payload)`.\n\n### 4. Note rendering\n\n- In chat: render `preview_image` exactly as today's image notes (LLMs see it).\n- In the Notes pane: thumbnail + a small \"↪ Open in editor\" affordance that triggers the re-open flow.\n- Preserve the image-note hot path so existing flows are not slower.\n\n### 5. Persistence\n\n`plugin_data` notes serialize through .minproj (project save/load) and chat history. Image bytes already round-trip; add `payload` JSON + `plugin_id` + `panel_name` fields.\n\n### 6. First adopter: presentation plugin\n\nImplement the two hooks on `SlideEditorPanel`:\n- `_on_panel_create_note_request` → returns `{kind: \"plugin_data\", plugin_id: \"presentation\", panel_name: ..., payload: {deck: <full deck dict>, version: 1}, preview_image: <render of selected slide>}`.\n- `_on_panel_restore_from_note(payload)` → validates `payload.deck` schema, calls `set_deck(payload.deck)`, returns true.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nEnd-to-end: build a 2-slide butter tarts deck, click Save to Note, close the presentation tab, re-open from the note, edit a tile, save to a NEW note, re-open that — both decks survive round-trip with byte-identical deck JSON modulo timestamps.\n\n## Out of scope (file as follow-ups)\n\n- Diff/merge between two plugin_data notes of the same plugin.\n- Cross-plugin migration (open a presentation note in another plugin).\n- Encrypted plugin_data notes (existing encrypted_note kind covers this if needed later).\n- Server-side Notion-style \"live document\" sync.\n\n## Risk\n\nThe Note system has long-standing assumptions that notes are text/image/audio/video. `plugin_data` is the first kind that mixes a binary payload (image) with an arbitrary JSON sidecar. Any code that branches on `note.kind` needs an audit. Likely affected: chat injection, .minproj save, copy-paste between notes, the Note inspector. Plan a grep + audit pass before implementation.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:19:25","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:19:25","priority":2,"tags":["core-feature","notes","plugin-host","presentation","round-trip"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dc9c1376ce8cd089bd4c489e88","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Audit: map all `note.kind` branches before adding plugin_data kind","description":"Two-part audit. Read-only sweep — no edits. Output: a checklist comment on this chore listing every site + chosen behavior. Feeds both #22 (schema design) and #28 (provider adapters).\n\n## Part A — `note.kind` branches (host code)\n\nSweep every branch that assumes `{text|image|html|audio|video}` are the only kinds. Each call site needs a plan: treat `plugin_data` like an image (use preview_image), explicit handle, or skip with rationale. Likely sweep targets: chat injection, .minproj save/load, NotesContainer, Note inspector, drag/drop between notes/chats, search, thumbnail rendering.\n\n## Part B — provider turn-serialization sites (LLM-facing)\n\nMap every place that serializes a chat/agent turn into a provider's wire format, focused on *text+image interleaving*:\n\n- AnthropicProvider — how does it emit screenshots today? content blocks shape?\n- OpenAIProvider — content array? image_url base64 vs reference?\n- ChatGPTProvider — same as OpenAI?\n- VertexProvider — parts/inline_data shape?\n- CoreProvider (TurnRock WebSocket) — wire format for inline images?\n- OllamaProvider — text-only? any vision model handling?\n- Any others not in this list (check `src/Scripts/Services/Providers/`)\n\nFor each provider document:\n1. Does an internal canonical multimodal shape already exist that we can reuse? (e.g., something the chat layer hands to all providers identically.) If yes, what is its schema?\n2. Or does each provider hand-roll the wire format from a different chat-side representation?\n3. Where is the seam where we'd inject `plugin_data` MultimodalPayload? — the goal in #28 is that providers translate canonical → wire, not that each provider re-implements plugin_data handling.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nSingle doc (scratch markdown or comment back on this chore) with two tables: (A) note.kind sites + behavior, (B) provider serialization sites + canonical-shape findings. Don't fix anything — that's the job of #22, #28, and downstream.\n\n## Scope guards\n\n- Read-only. No edits to source.\n- Audit covers host code only — plugin-side changes are #26, not here.\n- Chat *messages* (not just notes) are also in scope for Part B since plugin_data could ride in either; explicitly note any divergence found.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:19:51","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:43:18","priority":2,"tags":["audit","notes","schema-bump"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","resolution":"Audit complete. Two-part sweep delivered as a comment on this chore. 18 note.kind sites mapped (Part A); 8 providers mapped with wire-shape examples (Part B). 8 decision points surfaced for #22 and #28. Key finding: no internal canonical multimodal shape exists, recommended approach is per-plugin_data subarray inline in InjectedNotes (lowest blast radius). ChatGPT image-drop bug surfaced as an out-of-scope follow-up."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dcc59b7529bb2f4fa1f41038e5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Note schema: add plugin_data kind (plugin_id + panel_name + payload + preview_image)","description":"Add to Note class:\n\n- new kind value `plugin_data`\n- fields: `plugin_id: String`, `panel_name: String`, `payload: Dictionary`, `preview_image: Image`, `preview_alt_text: String`\n- `Note.create_plugin_data_note(title, plugin_id, panel_name, payload, preview_image, preview_alt_text=\"\") -> Note`\n- preview_image is REQUIRED — no null-image plugin_data notes (host falls back to image-screenshot path if plugin can't produce one)\n\nNotes get an image-like display affordance (thumbnail + ↪ Open in editor button); chat injection treats them as image notes (preview_image goes to LLM). Subsequent work items wire those.\n\nHeadless test: round-trip create → serialize → deserialize matches.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:20:02","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:05:19","priority":2,"tags":["model","notes","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","resolution":"Type.PLUGIN_DATA = 5 + linked_plugin_payload + create_plugin_data_note factory + serialize/deserialize landed in Note.gd. Headless test test/test_note_plugin_data.gd passes 41/0 covering factory shape, round-trip, image-byte preservation, and defensive handling."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dcf8ec771080d30f2df5952ef5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PluginScenePanelHost: create-note shape + restore-from-note + render-for-llm hooks","description":"Three host-side additions:\n\n1. **Recognize new payload shape in `Editor._build_note_from_plugin_payload`** (Editor.gd:1846):\n   - Accept `{kind: \"plugin_data\", plugin_id, panel_name, payload, preview_image, preview_alt_text?}`\n   - Build the new Note via `Note.create_plugin_data_note(...)` with `plugin_id`/`panel_name` either inherited from the editor's panel or carried explicitly\n   - Validate: payload must be Dictionary (or null), preview_image must be Image (host can fall back to screenshot if missing — one-call-stays-one-call ergonomics)\n\n2. **Add `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_restore_from_note(plugin_scene_root, payload)`** mirroring `invoke_create_note`:\n   - Calls panel's optional `_on_panel_restore_from_note(payload: Dictionary) -> bool`\n   - Returns the bool; false → host shows toast \"panel could not restore note\" and leaves panel blank\n   - Surface errors via the same `host.notify` pipe used by create_note errors\n\n3. **Add `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_render_for_llm(plugin_scene_root, ctx)` returning MultimodalPayload**:\n   - Calls panel's optional `_on_panel_render_for_llm(ctx: Dictionary) -> Array` (the MultimodalPayload — array of content parts, see #28 for canonical shape)\n   - Each part is `{type: \"text\", text: String}` or `{type: \"image\", image: Image, alt: String}`\n   - Returns the Array; empty/null → host falls back to single text+image pair built from `preview_image` + `preview_alt_text` on the note\n   - This hook is the seam #28's provider adapters consume from. Plugin owns the structural decomposition; host owns the per-provider wire translation.\n\nHeadless test: stub plugin returns plugin_data shape → host builds correct Note; stub plugin's restore hook gets called with payload; stub plugin's render_for_llm hook returns mixed text+image array → host returns it intact.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:20:15","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:08:43","priority":2,"tags":["notes","plugin-host","protocol"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","resolution":"PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_restore_from_note + invoke_render_for_llm landed; Editor._build_note_from_plugin_payload now recognizes plugin_data kind. Host-side test 13/0 passing; #22's test re-verified 41/0 after the edit."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dd2dec7d98a3aaf42e90ad0b5d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"\"Open in editor\" flow for plugin_data notes","description":"Two pieces:\n\n1. **UI affordance.** Notes pane / Note inspector renders the preview image plus an \"Open in editor\" button when `note.kind == plugin_data`. Disable button if `Plugin_id` plugin is no longer installed (graceful degradation).\n\n2. **Dispatch.** New helper somewhere in the Notes / Editor side (likely Editor.create_from_note or NotesContainer.open_note_in_editor):\n   - Resolve plugin by `plugin_id` via plugin registry; resolve panel by `panel_name` from manifest.\n   - Create a PLUGIN_SCENE Editor for that panel (existing `Editor.create()` path, with manifest-driven panel binding).\n   - After the panel is mounted/ready, call `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_restore_from_note(panel, payload)`.\n   - On false return, show toast and leave panel blank.\n\nTests: headless wiring test that goes Note → Editor instance → restore hook called.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:20:29","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:14:06","priority":2,"tags":["notes","plugin-host","round-trip","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","resolution":"_on_edit_button_pressed routes plugin_data notes via new _open_linked_plugin_panel helper: parses JSON wrapper, reuses or creates a PLUGIN_SCENE editor, calls invoke_restore_from_note. Plugin-uninstalled and parse-fail paths show toasts (no crash). #22 test re-verified 41/0. Integration test belongs to #27 e2e."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dd5cb572cba37cdd2e1a305930","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Persist plugin_data notes through .minproj save/load + chat history","description":"Once the new note kind exists, persistence has to cover:\n\n- `.minproj` (project save/load, SingletonObject path): notes serialize to a JSON-friendly form with image bytes base64'd. Add `plugin_id`, `panel_name`, `payload` keys; reuse existing image-bytes handling for `preview_image`.\n- Chat history: when a note is injected into chat, the chat history captures it; loading an old chat must restore the plugin_data note intact.\n- Backward compatibility: opening an old project without plugin_data notes is a no-op. Loading a new project on an older Minerva should warn but not crash; project_version bump or feature-flag fallback is acceptable.\n- Headless test: project with one plugin_data note → save → load → identical bytes (modulo serialization).","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:20:41","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:34:38","priority":2,"tags":["chat-history","minproj","notes","persistence"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4dd97ab711cb76e15586d2943a0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Presentation plugin: implement create_note_request + restore_from_note + render_for_llm hooks","description":"In `~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/SlideEditorPanel.gd`, implement all three hooks from #23:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc _on_panel_create_note_request(ctx: Dictionary) -> Dictionary:\n    var preview: Image = _render_slide_to_image(_selected_slide_index, 1280)  # reuses slide_canvas via SubViewport\n    return {\n        \"kind\": \"plugin_data\",\n        \"plugin_id\": \"presentation\",\n        \"panel_name\": ctx.get(\"panel_name\", \"SlideEditorPanel\"),\n        \"payload\": {\"version\": 1, \"deck\": _deck.duplicate(true), \"selected_slide\": _selected_slide_index},\n        \"preview_image\": preview,\n        \"preview_alt_text\": \"Slide %d of %d\" % [_selected_slide_index + 1, (_deck.get(\"slides\", []) as Array).size()],\n    }\n\nfunc _on_panel_restore_from_note(payload: Dictionary) -> bool:\n    if int(payload.get(\"version\", 0)) != 1: return false\n    var deck = payload.get(\"deck\", null)\n    if not (deck is Dictionary): return false\n    set_deck(deck)\n    _selected_slide_index = clampi(int(payload.get(\"selected_slide\", 0)), 0, (deck[\"slides\"] as Array).size() - 1)\n    _refresh_all()\n    return true\n\nfunc _on_panel_render_for_llm(ctx: Dictionary) -> Array:\n    # Canonical MultimodalPayload — see #28.\n    # Per slide: emit one text part (title + bullets + speaker notes), then one image part per image tile,\n    # then move to next slide. Spreadsheet tiles emit text (CSV-ish), not screenshots.\n    var parts: Array = []\n    var slides: Array = (_deck.get(\"slides\", []) as Array)\n    for i in range(slides.size()):\n        var slide: Dictionary = slides[i] as Dictionary\n        parts.append({\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": _slide_to_llm_text(i, slide)})\n        for tile in (slide.get(\"tiles\", []) as Array):\n            var kind: String = String((tile as Dictionary).get(\"kind\", \"\"))\n            if kind == \"image\":\n                var img: Image = _resolve_image_tile_to_image(tile)\n                if img != null:\n                    parts.append({\"type\": \"image\", \"image\": img, \"alt\": String((tile as Dictionary).get(\"alt\", \"Image on slide %d\" % (i + 1)))})\n            elif kind == \"spreadsheet\":\n                parts.append({\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": _spreadsheet_tile_to_text(tile)})\n            # text tiles already covered by _slide_to_llm_text\n    return parts\n```\n\nHelpers:\n- `_render_slide_to_image(idx, width)` — shared with the eventual `presentation_render_slide` MCP tool (work_item `019df41aa8c0`); land that helper here or in a sibling work_item.\n- `_slide_to_llm_text(idx, slide)` — composes \"Slide N — <title>\" + outline-level body text (reuses outline-aware logic from `_format_text_content`) + speaker-note annotations from substrate.\n- `_resolve_image_tile_to_image(tile)` — pulls bitmap; returns null if path missing.\n- `_spreadsheet_tile_to_text(tile)` — flattens grid to CSV-ish.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- HITL: Save to Note on a 2-slide deck, close tab, click ↪ Open from notes pane, deck identical and editable.\n- HITL: attach the deck note to a chat turn — provider receives interleaved text+image parts (verify via Activity log or mock provider in test).","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:20:56","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:20:43","priority":2,"tags":["first-adopter","plugin","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","resolution":"Three hooks land in SlideEditorPanel.gd: create_note_request, restore_from_note, render_for_llm. Host-side preview_image backfill in Editor.gd lets plugin omit preview without losing the round-trip. 25/0 new test cases passing; 41+13+198/0 regression-passing on companion tests."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4ddc8cd7f079ee107dd8d62ce01","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"End-to-end test: 2-slide deck round-trips through Save→reopen→edit→Save→reopen","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:21:08","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:21:08","priority":3,"tags":["acceptance","notes","presentation","round-trip","test"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f7cbf8b7fe3f01a02c984","component":"presentation/test","test_setup":"Headless Godot test extending the existing `test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd` pattern. May need an in-app smoke leg too because Save-to-Note exercises real Notes UI.","test_steps":"1. Compose a 2-slide butter tarts deck via MCP.\n2. Trigger save-to-note via MCP shim (or a test-only Editor.create_note path).\n3. Assert resulting Note is plugin_data kind, plugin_id=\"presentation\", payload.deck.slides.size() == 2.\n4. Close the editor tab.\n5. Open the note (via the new \"Open in editor\" flow).\n6. Assert a fresh Presentation Panel is mounted with a deck whose slides[0].id == original.\n7. Modify slide 0 (move a tile).\n8. Save to a SECOND note.\n9. Open both notes' decks; assert N1 != N2 only by the moved tile coords.\n10. Project save/load round-trip: save .minproj, load, both notes still in the project tree."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4ebf8967710ae0999a508083aca","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Provider adapters: consume MultimodalPayload from plugin_data notes","description":"## Why\n\nWhen a `plugin_data` note rides along in a chat or agent turn, the LLM should see *structured* content — text-as-text and images-as-images — not a single rasterized screenshot of the panel. Rasterizing a multi-slide deck is wasteful (a 50-slide deck with 100 chars/slide is ~5K tokens as text vs. 50 image blocks of ~1500 tokens each = 75K tokens). Per the durable rule \"LLMs always get screenshots, even from rich note kinds\", screenshots are still required for visual fidelity — but they should be *per-element* (per slide for a deck), not a single panel snapshot.\n\n## Architecture\n\n**Plugin emits canonical MultimodalPayload** via the `_on_panel_render_for_llm` hook (defined in #23):\n\n```\nMultimodalPayload = Array of content parts, each:\n  {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": String}\n  {\"type\": \"image\", \"image\": Image, \"alt\": String}\n```\n\nThe host owns this canonical type. Each provider has an adapter that translates it into the provider's native turn format.\n\n## Provider adapter mapping (subject to #21 audit)\n\n- **AnthropicProvider** → content blocks: `[{type:\"text\",text:...}, {type:\"image\",source:{type:\"base64\",media_type:\"image/png\",data:...}}]`\n- **OpenAIProvider** + **ChatGPTProvider** → content array: `[{type:\"text\",text:...}, {type:\"image_url\",image_url:{url:\"data:image/png;base64,...\"}}]`\n- **VertexProvider** (Google) → parts: `[{text:...}, {inline_data:{mime_type:\"image/png\", data:base64}}]`\n- **CoreProvider** (TurnRock WebSocket) → wire shape TBD; audit (#21) determines whether existing inline-image handling already supports interleaving\n- **OllamaProvider** → text-only fallback: emit text parts; replace image parts with `[image: <alt_text>]` placeholder unless model is multimodal-capable\n- (Audit may identify additional providers)\n\n## Fallback chain\n\nWhen a `plugin_data` note is serialized:\n1. If panel implements `_on_panel_render_for_llm` → use returned MultimodalPayload\n2. Else if note has `preview_image` + `preview_alt_text` → emit single text+image pair\n3. Else → emit `preview_alt_text` only (or \"[plugin_data note from <plugin_id>]\")\n\n## Out of scope (file as follow-ups if needed)\n\n- Streaming partial payloads (whole-note serialization is fine for v1)\n- Image dedup across multiple plugin_data notes in one turn\n- Adaptive resolution (LLM cost-aware downscaling)\n- Tool-call result MultimodalPayload (this is for chat injection only; tool results have their own path)\n\n## Acceptance\n\nHeadless tests per provider adapter:\n- Text-only payload → correct turn shape\n- Image-only payload → correct turn shape with base64 encoding\n- Interleaved text+image+text+image (3-slide deck shape) → correct order preserved\n\nEnd-to-end: build a 2-slide deck, save to note, attach to a chat with each provider configured, verify the provider adapter produces a structured turn (no full-panel rasterization). Mock provider for the verification — no live API calls needed.","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:36:38","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:37:39","priority":2,"tags":["llm-serialization","notes","plugin-host","providers"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df4f5426076efbe546a867cd092d5","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"ChatGPT provider silently drops image attachments from chat turns","description":"## Problem\n\n`ChatGPTProvider.Format()` (Scripts/Services/Providers/ChatGPT/ChatGPTProvider.gd:445–526) and its content-type converter `_convert_content_types()` (lines 531–550) only emit `input_text` / `output_text` content parts. When a chat turn includes attached IMAGE notes, they are silently dropped from the outbound payload.\n\nThis was surfaced during the Part B serialization audit (docket 019df4dc9c13). Every other provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Vertex/Google, Ollama, Local, OpenRouter, Core's OpenAI-compat path) handles inline images via base64 data URLs or `inline_data` parts. ChatGPT alone discards them.\n\n## Repro steps\n\n1. Configure a ChatGPT (OAuth) provider in Minerva.\n2. Attach an IMAGE note to a chat thread (via Notes pane).\n3. Send a message.\n4. Inspect the outbound request body via Activity log or network capture.\n\n## Expected\n\nImage is included in the request, ChatGPT vision model can see it.\n\n## Actual\n\nOutbound payload contains only the user's text prompt and any attached TEXT notes. Image notes silently omitted. ChatGPT replies as if it can't see the image — because it can't.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`Format()` line 509–520 iterates `chat_item.InjectedNotes` looking only for String entries (text notes). Image objects in the array are skipped without warning. The OpenAI Responses API does support image inputs via `input_image` content parts (with `image_url` payload) — the implementation just never emits them.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd an `input_image` branch in `Format()` mirroring `OpenAIProvider.gd:155–253`. Convert Image objects via `Marshalls.raw_to_base64(image.save_png_to_buffer())` and emit:\n\n```gdscript\n{\"type\": \"input_image\", \"image_url\": \"data:image/png;base64,%s\" % b64}\n```\n\nVerify against current OpenAI Responses API docs — the content-part type may be `input_image` or `image_url` depending on Responses-API version.\n\n## Severity\n\nSeverity 2 (High): silent feature-loss for any user using ChatGPT + image notes. No error surfaced to the user. Discoverable only by inspecting outbound traffic.\n\n## Out of scope for this fix\n\n- ChatGPT's text-only fallback for plugin_data notes (handled in plan 019df419ce56 / work_item 019df4ebf896 — D6 decision).","created_at":"2026-05-04T21:46:47","updated_at":"2026-05-04T21:46:47","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["chatgpt","images","multimodal","providers","silent-failure"],"environment":"Minerva (current main), ChatGPT provider with OAuth.","repro_steps":"1. Configure ChatGPT (OAuth) provider. 2. Attach IMAGE note to chat. 3. Send message. 4. Inspect outbound payload — image is missing."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df51f217b7c51b9cc3763b10b857b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"linked_plugin_payload not restored in Note.deserialize ready.connect block (asymmetry footgun)","description":"## Problem\n\nIn `Note.gd` `deserialize()` (~line 1320), the `ready.connect` callback restores `linked_html`, `linked_pcb_data`, `linked_spreadsheet`, and `linked_chat_ids` from the on-disk dict. `linked_plugin_payload` is NOT in this list — instead, the value gets installed *inside* `create_plugin_data_note(...)` from the parsed wrapper, before ready fires.\n\nToday's deserialize path works because the \"plugin_data\" match arm always calls `create_plugin_data_note`. But:\n\n1. The asymmetry is a footgun: any future caller that constructs a plugin_data note via `note.type = PLUGIN_DATA; note.linked_plugin_payload = ...` (e.g., a partial-shape constructor or a test helper) and then runs through the standard ready-restoration path would silently lose the payload.\n2. Reviewers reading the code expect symmetry with PCB/HTML/Spreadsheet patterns and may copy-paste broken patterns elsewhere.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd to the ready.connect block alongside the other linked_* restorations:\n\n```gdscript\nnote.linked_plugin_payload = note_data.get(\"LinkedPluginPayload\", \"\")\n```\n\nDefensive: it's a no-op when the factory already set it (same value), and it locks down the invariant for any non-factory paths.\n\n## Severity\n\nSev 3 — defensive gap, no current user impact.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:31","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:31","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["defensive","deserialize","notes","round-trip"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f","repro_steps":"No live repro path. Discoverable only by code-review or by future code that bypasses create_plugin_data_note."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df51f51127079952852ef42738eaf","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"plugin_data wrapper version silently re-stamped on round-trip (forward-compat hazard)","description":"## Problem\n\n`Note.deserialize` for the \"plugin_data\" kind reads `LinkedPluginPayload`, parses the wrapper to extract `payload`, and passes the inner `payload` (only) to `create_plugin_data_note(...)`. The factory then **re-stamps `version: 1`** on the new wrapper unconditionally.\n\nEffect: if a future schema bumps to `version: 2` and an older Minerva opens a v2-saved .minproj, the wrapper gets silently downgraded to v1 the moment the project is re-saved — even if the older Minerva didn't otherwise touch the deck. Subtle data corruption: the v2-aware Minerva that re-opens the same project later sees v1 and may then refuse to load or fall back to defaults.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nTwo viable approaches:\n\n**Option A (preserve):** Pass the wrapper version through the factory:\n```gdscript\nstatic func create_plugin_data_note(..., wrapper_version: int = 1) -> Note:\n    ...\n    note_scene.linked_plugin_payload = JSON.stringify({\"version\": wrapper_version, ...})\n```\nDeserialize reads `wrapper.get(\"version\", 1)` and forwards it.\n\n**Option B (refuse):** In deserialize, refuse to install plugin_data wrappers with versions newer than the current Minerva supports — surface as an error note. Stricter; less convenient, but eliminates accidental downgrade.\n\nA combines well with B: preserve when version is recognised; error-note when not.\n\n## Severity\n\nSev 3 — only relevant once we ship a v2. Filing now while the constraint is fresh.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:43","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:43","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["forward-compat","notes","schema-versioning"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f","repro_steps":"N/A pre-v2. Will manifest the day a future schema bump lands."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df51f80af709990cbb74a6cc0d2b7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Edit on plugin_data note creates duplicate tab when deck already open in another tab","description":"## Problem\n\n`Note._open_linked_plugin_panel` looks for an existing PLUGIN_SCENE editor to focus before creating a new one — but its dedup predicate is `editor.associated_object == self`. If the user (a) opens a deck via PluginInspector or `presentation_open_deck` MCP tool, then (b) \"Save to Note\", then (c) clicks Edit on the resulting note — the originally-open editor has `associated_object == null`, so the dedup check fails and a *second* presentation tab is created.\n\nTwo tabs then both edit the same `_deck` reference (or worse, divergent copies via deserialize). UX-confusing at minimum; potentially data-corrupting if the user types in tab A, switches to tab B, types again, switches back.\n\nThis is a generalisation of how `_open_linked_pcb` already works, so the bug isn't new — but plugin notes are explicitly *meant* for round-tripping, so the issue surfaces more often.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nExtend the dedup predicate:\n\n```gdscript\nfor editor in editor_pane.get_open_editors():\n    if editor.type != Editor.Type.PLUGIN_SCENE: continue\n    var matches = (editor.associated_object == self) \\\n        or (editor.plugin_id == note_plugin_id and editor.panel_name == note_panel_name)\n    if matches:\n        ...\n        return\n```\n\nWhen the secondary match fires, also call `invoke_restore_from_note` on the existing tab to install the saved deck (otherwise the existing tab keeps its un-saved state and the user thinks Edit did nothing).\n\n## Acceptance\n\nHITL: open deck via MCP → Save to Note → Edit on note → focus existing tab AND show the saved deck (not the in-memory one). Single-tab outcome.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:55","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:32:55","priority":3,"tags":["notes","tab-dedup","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df51fa7b0738e9af27b34a689e696","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"plugin_data preview image bloats .minproj — re-encoded as base64 PNG on every save","description":"## Problem\n\n`Note._serialize_controls_data` for a plugin_data note re-PNG-encodes and base64s the preview Image into `MemoryImage` on every project save. For a deck whose preview is a panel screenshot (typically 1080p-ish), that's ~2 MB of base64 per note. Multiply across N decks across the lifetime of a project and the .minproj file grows fast — even though the *image content* is only updating when the user explicitly re-saves the deck to the same note.\n\nThis is identical to how PCB notes behave, so it's not a regression — but plugin notes will be much more numerous than PCB notes once decks become commonplace.\n\n## Suggested approach (incremental)\n\n1. Hash the preview image bytes; stash hash on the Note.\n2. On serialize, if hash matches the previously-serialised value, write a content-addressed reference (`PreviewRef: \"<hash>\"`) and store the PNG bytes once in a project-level blob store.\n3. On deserialize, lookup by hash and rehydrate.\n\nOr simpler: file references — preview images live alongside the .minproj as separate files, not embedded.\n\nFiling as a work_item not a bug because there's no current user impact, just a scaling smell.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nPre-existing PCB note image storage. Don't refactor that here.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:05","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:05","priority":3,"tags":["minproj-bloat","notes","performance"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df51fd4b17812a45558b9eda0e718","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"_open_linked_plugin_panel skips invoke_restore_from_note on existing-tab path","description":"## Problem\n\nIn `Note._open_linked_plugin_panel`, the existing-tab branch returns early after focusing the matching editor — without calling `invoke_restore_from_note`. Two failure modes:\n\n1. **Plugin uninstalled mid-session, tab still in tabs array.** The existing-tab loop matches `associated_object == self`, focuses it, returns. But `editor.plugin_scene_root` is now null (plugin was unloaded). User clicks Edit, the tab focuses, deck doesn't appear. No toast, no clue why.\n2. **Existing tab has divergent in-memory state.** User opens deck via MCP, makes unsaved edits, \"Save to Note\" produces a note that captures the unsaved-state deck. User then opens a different deck in the same panel. Clicks Edit on the first note. Existing-tab matches by associated_object, focuses → second deck is showing, but the first deck's payload is in the note. No restore happens.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nIn the existing-tab branch:\n\n```gdscript\nif editor.plugin_scene_root != null:\n    var ok: bool = PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_restore_from_note(\n        editor.plugin_scene_root, inner_payload\n    )\n    if not ok:\n        SingletonObject.create_toast_notification(\n            \"Plugin couldn't restore note\", ToastNotification.Type.WARNING\n        )\nelse:\n    SingletonObject.create_toast_notification(\n        \"Plugin '%s' not running — note can't be edited\" % note_plugin_id,\n        ToastNotification.Type.WARNING\n    )\nreturn\n```\n\nrestore_from_note is documented as idempotent on the plugin side, so calling it on an already-correctly-loaded panel is a no-op.\n\n## Severity\n\nSev 3 (silent failure mode), Pri 2 (visible to users; would surface during a long-running session).","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:17","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:17","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["notes","plugin-uninstall","silent-failure"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df520037f7ef2b5b86ce083579049","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"_on_panel_render_for_llm decodes every image tile synchronously on UI thread","description":"## Problem\n\n`SlideEditorPanel._on_panel_render_for_llm` walks every tile of every slide and calls `_image_tile_to_image()` on each image tile, which does `Image.load_png_from_buffer` (or jpg/webp) inline. A 50-slide deck with 5 image tiles per slide = 250 PNG decodes synchronously when chat injection requests an LLM payload.\n\nToday this is dead code — provider adapters that call `invoke_render_for_llm` haven't shipped (work item 019df4ebf896). Once they do, this becomes a UI-thread stall whenever a deck note rides along in a turn.\n\n## Suggested approach\n\nCache decoded Images on the tile dict (`_image_decoded` or as part of a per-tile lazy cache) keyed by base64 hash. Invalidate when `tile.src` changes.\n\nOr: defer decoding to a background thread for the LLM-payload path (chat injection is already async).\n\nOr simpler v1: cap the number of decodes per render (e.g. max 20) and emit a text placeholder for the rest.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nPre-existing image-tile rendering performance in `slide_canvas.gd` — that's a separate concern with different constraints.\n\n## When this becomes urgent\n\nWhen work item 019df4ebf896 (provider adapters) starts pulling MultimodalPayload from this hook in the chat path. Mark as a blocker for that work item.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:29","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:29","priority":3,"tags":["llm-payload","performance","plugin-presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df5202b9978e598a69d5456a80d07","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Add headless test for _build_note_from_plugin_payload plugin_data + null preview_image branch","description":"## Gap\n\n`Editor._build_note_from_plugin_payload` has a \"plugin_data\" branch that returns null when `preview_image` is missing or not an Image — falling through to the screenshot fallback at the call site. No headless test exercises this path. Same gap exists for \"plugin_data\" with a non-Dictionary `payload`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe Editor.gd preview backfill (in `_create_plugin_scene_note`) injects a screenshot before calling `_build_note_from_plugin_payload`, so the production happy path masks this branch. But:\n\n- A plugin that returns `kind:\"plugin_data\"` with `preview_image: null` (explicit null, not missing) gets routed away from the plugin_data path with no warning. Future plugins making this mistake will silently lose deck JSON.\n- The non-Dict payload branch is similarly silent — a future plugin emitting a wrong-shape payload won't get a useful error.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nTwo new test cases (both can extend `test/test_note_plugin_data.gd` or a new file):\n\n1. Construct a Dictionary payload with `kind:\"plugin_data\"`, valid `payload` (Dictionary), `preview_image: null`. Pass to `_build_note_from_plugin_payload`. Assert returned Note is null.\n2. Construct payload with `kind:\"plugin_data\"`, valid `preview_image`, `payload: \"not a dict\"`. Pass. Assert returned Note is null.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n`_build_note_from_plugin_payload` is an instance method on Editor — building one requires plugin_id/panel_name fields. Use a stub Editor or factor the validation logic out to a static helper if testability becomes an issue.","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:39","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:39","priority":3,"tags":["coverage-gap","test"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df5204f077c4bb094adc0b4b8639b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"plugin_data deserialize: base64 decode failure silently passes blank Image","description":"## Problem\n\nIn `Note.deserialize()` \"plugin_data\" branch:\n\n```gdscript\nvar image_data = note_data.get(\"MemoryImage\", \"\")\nvar image: = Image.new()\nif image_data is String and not image_data.is_empty():\n    image.load_png_from_buffer(Marshalls.base64_to_raw(image_data))\n```\n\nIf `Marshalls.base64_to_raw()` returns garbage, OR if `load_png_from_buffer` returns a non-OK error code, the code passes the blank `Image.new()` to `create_plugin_data_note` without surfacing the failure. The note opens with no preview thumbnail; user sees a blank icon and no clue what went wrong.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n```gdscript\nif image_data is String and not image_data.is_empty():\n    var bytes: PackedByteArray = Marshalls.base64_to_raw(image_data)\n    if bytes.is_empty():\n        push_warning(\"[Note] plugin_data preview: base64 decode failed for note %s\" % note_data.get(\"UUID\", \"?\"))\n    else:\n        var err: int = image.load_png_from_buffer(bytes)\n        if err != OK:\n            push_warning(\"[Note] plugin_data preview: PNG decode failed (err=%d) for note %s\" % [err, note_data.get(\"UUID\", \"?\")])\n```\n\nA warning is enough — the note still opens, just without a preview. The user may still be able to use the Edit button to recover the underlying deck JSON.\n\n## Severity\n\nSev 4 — only manifests on corrupted .minproj data; no functional loss (deck JSON still recoverable).","created_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:48","updated_at":"2026-05-04T22:33:48","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["deserialize","low-severity","notes","silent-failure"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df548144270cbb4b8d31d10310186","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"When adding a new Editor.Type, _update_note() needs a branch too","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:17:14","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:17:14","tags":["checklist","editor","gotcha","minerva","save-to-note"],"value":"Save-to-Note hits TWO paths: `_on_create_note_button_pressed` (Editor.gd:1370) on first click creates a fresh note; on subsequent clicks (when `associated_object is Note`), it dispatches to `_update_note(associated_object)` (Editor.gd:1976). `_update_note` has its own per-Type match block — TEXT, GRAPHICS, SPREADSHEET, PCB, WEBVIEW. Forgetting to add a branch for a new Type means: first save works, every subsequent save is a silent no-op. The note keeps the original snapshot forever; user thinks Save-to-Note is broken.\n\nFull editor-type checklist when adding a new Editor.Type:\n1. `Editor.Type` enum value\n2. `Editor.create()` match arm\n3. `Editor._ready()` button hiding\n4. `EditorPane.add()` tab naming\n5. `Editor.get_saved_state()` / project save serialization\n6. `_on_create_note_button_pressed()` match arm (creates initial note)\n7. **`_update_note()` match arm** (updates note on subsequent saves) — frequently forgotten\n8. `_supports_note()` allow-list (if applicable)\n\nFor PLUGIN_SCENE specifically, _update_note must call `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_create_note` again and rebuild `note.linked_plugin_payload` + refresh the preview Image.</value>\n<parameter name=\"confidence\">high","component":"minerva.editor","key":"add_editor_type_checklist_update_note","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df548a4fa7dbfb023e747ba1ac381","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Save-to-Note silent no-op on second+ click for PLUGIN_SCENE editors","description":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced during HITL #1: user composed a 2-slide deck, \"Save to Note\" worked. User regenerated the slide-1 image, \"Save to Note\" again — note did NOT update. The note kept its first-snapshot wrapper forever.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`Editor._on_create_note_button_pressed` (Editor.gd:1370) has two paths:\n1. First click: `associated_object` is null → falls through to per-Type match → `_create_plugin_scene_note` for PLUGIN_SCENE. Sets `associated_object = new_note`.\n2. Second click: `associated_object is Note` → dispatches to `_update_note(associated_object)` and returns.\n\n`_update_note` (Editor.gd:1976) had branches for TEXT, GRAPHICS, SPREADSHEET, PCB, WEBVIEW — but **no branch for PLUGIN_SCENE**. Subsequent Save-to-Note clicks were silent no-ops.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdded PLUGIN_SCENE branch in `_update_note` (Editor.gd:2003). It calls `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_create_note` again, rebuilds `note.linked_plugin_payload` from the fresh wrapper, and refreshes the preview image (using the host backfill if the plugin omits one — same path as the create-note flow).\n\n## Status\n\n**Fixed in this session.** Filed for traceability and to document the editor-type checklist gap that allowed it. Hint also saved at `minerva.editor/add_editor_type_checklist_update_note`.\n\n## Open question (HITL): unexplained panel revert\n\nUser also observed that the panel state itself reverted to the saved (old) image after the second Save-to-Note. The silent-no-op explains why the note didn't update, but NOT why the panel changed. `_update_note` doesn't invoke load/restore — it shouldn't have any side effect on `_deck`.\n\nPossibilities:\n- User accidentally clicked Edit on the note instead of Save (Edit triggers `_on_panel_restore_from_note` → reloads deck from note).\n- Some signal-chain side effect from `UpdateUnsavedTabIcon.emit()` triggers a panel reload.\n- `_capture_plugin_scene_image` (called in our new branch) somehow stalls a frame and the panel gets re-rendered from stale state.\n\nFiling as out-of-scope for this fix; verify after restart whether the symptom recurs with the patch applied.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nAfter Minerva restart with this fix:\n1. Compose a deck. Save to Note.\n2. Modify deck (regenerate image, edit text, etc.).\n3. Save to Note again.\n4. **Expected:** the existing note's preview thumbnail updates to reflect the modified deck. linked_plugin_payload contains the modified wrapper.\n5. Close tab, click Edit on the note. Expected: deck reopens with the MODIFIED state (not the original).","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:17:51","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:17:51","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["editor","fixed-2026-05-04","regression","save-to-note"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df5584c8473c2a9123c34217a0089","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"SceneTree headless tests must `await` each test fn that itself awaits","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:34:57","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:34:57","tags":["async","footgun","godot","scenetree","testing"],"value":"In an `extends SceneTree` headless test runner, if a test function contains any `await` (e.g. `await process_frame` after `add_child`), the runner MUST call it as `await test_fn()`. Without the await, the test fn suspends at its first await and returns control to the runner; the runner proceeds, and any `_check(...)` calls placed after the await never run before `quit()` fires. There is no warning — the test silently appears to pass with fewer assertions than written. Symptom: assertion count mid-test is lower than expected; pre-await checks pass, post-await checks vanish. Fix: prefix every test invocation that contains await internally with `await test_fn()`.","component":"godot.testing","key":"scenetree_test_must_await_each_async_test_call","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df55e83a971d9a59153fafcfcb839","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Slide.title is metadata; SlideEditorPanel does not render it as a tile","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:41:45","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:41:45","tags":["authoring","mcp","presentation","rendering"],"value":"`presentation_set_slide_title` / the `title` field on a slide is metadata only — used in slide lists, MCP responses, and LLM render text. SlideEditorPanel does NOT paint it on the canvas as a visible heading. Result: slides authored via MCP look \"untitled\" in the panel even though list_slides shows the title. To make a title visible, add an explicit text tile (e.g. plain mode, BBCode bold + dark color, font_size ~36–48, y≈0.04, h≈0.10–0.12, w≈0.9). The default white slide background also means default-color text vanishes — always set a contrasting color via [color=#hex] or set slide background.","component":"presentation.authoring","key":"slide_title_is_metadata_not_rendered","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df56479a1791abca4ecee5fd8cc87","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"GraphicsEditorV2.check_ai_buttons_toggle null-deref during .minproj load","description":"## Symptom\n\nLoading a .minproj that contains restored graphics editor tabs throws:\n\n```\nE ...   GraphicsEditorV2.check_ai_buttons_toggle: Invalid assignment of property or key 'disabled' with value of type 'bool' on a base object of type 'Nil'.\nGraphicsEditorV2.gd:2844 @ check_ai_buttons_toggle()\nGraphicsEditorV2.gd:969 @ _on_layer_card_deselected()\nLayerCard.gd:40 @ @selected_setter()\nGraphicsEditorV2.gd:899 @ add_layer()\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\n`check_ai_buttons_toggle()` only null-guards `edit_img_button` / `send_mask_edit_button` in the `else` branch (Core not connected). When `Core.connected` is true and `add_layer` runs during deserialize before `@onready` resolves the button refs, the if-branch deferences nil.\n\nsrc/Scripts/UI/Controls/GraphicsEditor/GraphicsEditorV2.gd:2841-2851 — the connected branch needs the same guards as the disconnected branch.\n\n## Fix\n\n```gdscript\nif Core.connected:\n    if edit_img_button:\n        edit_img_button.disabled = false\n    if send_mask_edit_button:\n        send_mask_edit_button.disabled = false\nelse:\n    ... (already guarded)\n```\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nDCR 019df4dc365f HITL — saving a .minproj that included presentation graphics editors and reopening it. Pre-existing; not caused by plugin_data work, but blocks clean test of the round-trip.","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:48:15","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:48:15","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["graphics-editor","load","minproj","null-deref"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df56a447b74158604fee82469db3c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Platform __panel_state capture must run in ALL save paths, not just MCP success","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:54:35","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:54:35","tags":["architecture","host-owned","minproj","plugin","serialization"],"value":"In `vboxEditor.gd::_serialize_plugin_scene_editor`, the platform-managed `_on_panel_save_request` → `tab_state[\"__panel_state\"]` capture was placed AFTER the MCP dispatch and only ran on the success branch. Plugins without `project_file.serialize_channel` declared (or with the plugin server unavailable) return early with `payload: {}` — losing all panel state even though `_on_panel_save_request` exists. Symptom: .minproj's `Editors[i].plugin_state.payload == {}` for a plugin scene panel that should round-trip. Fix: hoist the panel-state capture to the top of the function so it runs in all three return paths. The corresponding restore (`_restore_panel_state`) was already correctly placed before any MCP branch in `_deserialize_plugin_scene_editor`. Lesson: when wiring a \"host-managed fallback\" path, place it OUTSIDE every plugin-server precondition, not after the success branch.","component":"plugin.persistence","key":"platform_panel_state_only_on_mcp_success_path","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df56a7892793897811e21ea853a25","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PLUGIN_SCENE save: __panel_state capture only ran on MCP-success path","description":"## Symptom\n\nA presentation deck open as a `Type.PLUGIN_SCENE` editor (presentation plugin) saved to .minproj, then reopened, lost all deck content. Inspecting the .minproj:\n\n```\nEditors[1] = {\n  type: 14 (PLUGIN_SCENE),\n  plugin_id: \"presentation\",\n  panel_name: \"slide_editor_panel\",\n  plugin_state: { version: 1, ..., payload: {} }   # ← empty\n}\n```\n\nNotes are also missing because the load cascades errors via GraphicsEditorV2.check_ai_buttons_toggle (filed separately as `019df56479a1`).\n\n## Root cause\n\n`vboxEditor.gd::_serialize_plugin_scene_editor` has three return paths:\n1. No `project_file.serialize_channel` declared → empty payload, return.\n2. Plugin server unavailable → empty payload, return.\n3. MCP dispatch succeeds → tab_state from server, then **`__panel_state`** capture from `_on_panel_save_request`, then return.\n\nThe platform-managed panel-state capture (hooks the host-owned `_on_panel_save_request` so panels round-trip without each plugin wiring its own MCP channel) was placed in branch 3 only. The presentation plugin manifest declares no `project_file` channel, so save fell into branch 1 and dropped the deck.\n\n## Fix\n\nHoist the `__panel_state` capture to the top of the function so it runs in all three branches. Plugin's MCP `tab_state` (when it returns one) is merged ON TOP, preserving plugin-author opt-in. Applied in this commit.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nDCR 019df4dc365f HITL — saving a 2-slide butter tarts deck to .minproj and reopening it.\n\n## Verification needed\n\nHeadless test: PLUGIN_SCENE editor with a panel implementing `_on_panel_save_request` round-trips through `_serialize_plugin_scene_editor` ↔ `_deserialize_plugin_scene_editor`, regardless of whether a plugin manifest declares `project_file` channels.","created_at":"2026-05-04T23:54:48","updated_at":"2026-05-04T23:54:48","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["host-owned","minproj","plugin","presentation","save"],"parent":"minerva:019df4dc365f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df57056f675f4bc571a8125f8e75d","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Fire-and-forget graphics_generate completion time varies; verify before consuming","created_at":"2026-05-05T00:01:13","updated_at":"2026-05-05T00:01:13","tags":["fire-and-forget","graphics","mcp","presentation","timing"],"value":"When two `minerva_graphics_generate` calls run in parallel, completion times can diverge by 30+ seconds. A single fixed sleep that covered the first generator may finish before the second has output, and `presentation_add_image_tile source_graphics_editor` will silently capture an empty 8KB canvas placeholder. Detect this: a real generation lands at hundreds of KB to several MB; an empty canvas pull returns ~8KB in `src_size_bytes`. Defenses: (a) generate sequentially when timing matters; (b) after sleep, check the tile's `src_size_bytes` via `presentation_list_tiles` — if it's < ~50KB the source layer was probably empty; (c) re-issue `presentation_modify_tile source_graphics_editor=...` to re-pull once generation has actually completed; (d) prefer `minerva_graphics_generate_iterative` when you need to consume the result deterministically.","component":"graphics.generate","key":"fire_and_forget_completion_varies_verify_via_src_size","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df57b0b1c7132ae400e7ac867abc3","type":"dcr","status":"designing","title":"Plugins ship skills: install-time skill seeding from manifest","description":"## Problem\n\nSkills today are authored in two places: shipped `master.dct` (primes every user, repo-tracked) or the user's personal docket (authored via MCP / docket UI). Both require the user (or a maintainer) to understand the skill schema — system prompt, `tool_deps`, optimization profile, target expression, outcome/preconditions/steps.\n\nPlugins are how non-experts will get value out of Minerva. A user installing a presentation plugin should not have to:\n1. Know that \"skills\" exist as a concept,\n2. Know which MCP tools the plugin exposes,\n3. Hand-author a skill with the right `tool_deps` and a usable system prompt.\n\nThey want to install one thing, see \"Make a slide deck\" as an option in their skill picker, and start working.\n\nThe plugin author is the one who actually knows: which tool subset matters, what system prompt frames the workflow, what model targets are reasonable, what the \"outcome\" looks like. They should be able to ship the skill alongside the plugin.\n\n## Proposal\n\nExtend the plugin manifest with a `skills[]` array. Each entry is a docket skill record:\n\n```json\n\"skills\": [\n  {\n    \"id\": \"make_slide_deck\",                    // becomes plugin-prefixed: presentation.make_slide_deck\n    \"title\": \"Make a slide deck\",\n    \"summary\": \"Compose a multi-slide presentation from your notes or chat context.\",\n    \"preconditions\": \"A presentation panel is open OR no panel is open (skill will create one).\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A .mdeck deck visible in a presentation panel with at least one slide.\",\n    \"steps\": \"1. ...\",                          // long-form pipeline\n    \"system_prompt\": \"You are a presentation author...\",\n    \"tool_deps\": [\n      \"presentation_create_deck\",\n      \"presentation_add_slide\",\n      \"presentation_set_slide_title\",\n      \"presentation_add_text_tile\",\n      \"presentation_add_image_tile\",\n      \"presentation_modify_tile\",\n      \"graphics_generate\"\n    ],\n    \"target\": \"all\",\n    \"optimization\": { \"tool_budget\": 30000, \"context_window\": \"default\" }\n  }\n]\n```\n\nOn plugin install, Minerva:\n1. Validates each skill record against the docket skill schema.\n2. Verifies all `tool_deps` resolve (this plugin's own tools or other installed tools).\n3. Prompts the user once: \"Plugin 'presentation' wants to install N skills. Review?\" (expandable list).\n4. On accept, materialises the skills as records in the user docket with `source=plugin:<plugin_id>`.\n5. On plugin uninstall, removes (or offers to remove) the seeded skills, leaving any user-customised forks intact.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- **User-facing simplicity**: install plugin → skills appear → click and use. No skill-authoring knowledge required.\n- **Plugin author has the right context**: they know their tools and the canonical workflow. The skill belongs near the plugin code, not in a separate docket file users have to discover.\n- **Discoverable**: the skill picker becomes a real product surface — \"what can I do?\" is answered by what's installed.\n\n## Trust & scope\n\nSkills carry weight: system prompts shape model behaviour, `tool_deps` pre-grant tool access. Install MUST confirm with the user, just like Minerva does for plugin tool grants today.\n\nOpen questions:\n- Should plugin-shipped skills be editable by the user? Yes (forking copy on first edit, original preserved as an update path).\n- Update semantics: when plugin version bumps, do shipped-skill updates apply silently, prompt, or only to non-customised installs?\n- Naming collision: prefix all plugin skills with `<plugin_id>.` to avoid clashes with user-authored skills.\n- Uninstall: remove plugin's pristine skills; ask before removing forked copies.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `manifest.json` accepts an optional `skills[]` array; schema validated at install time.\n2. Each skill record maps cleanly onto `docket_create type=skill` fields (title, summary, system_prompt, tool_deps, target, optimization, preconditions, outcome, steps).\n3. Install dialog lists skills the plugin will seed and asks for confirmation.\n4. Seeded skills appear in the user's skill picker with a \"from <plugin>\" badge.\n5. `plugin_install` is idempotent: re-installing the same version does not duplicate skills.\n6. `plugin_remove` cleanly removes pristine plugin-shipped skills; forked copies are preserved (or user is asked).\n7. Plugin update reconciles: new skills added, removed skills marked deprecated, modified skills offered as update.\n8. Test: an example plugin (e.g., presentation) ships a \"Make a slide deck\" skill and round-trips through install/uninstall.\n\n## Related\n\n- Plugin authoring contract DCR (parent of plugin manifest spec)\n- master.dct vs user docket distinction (project memory: project_docket_shipped_vs_user.md)\n- tool_deps mechanism (project memory: project_tool_deps_and_dynamic_loading.md)\n- Surfaced from HITL on DCR 019df4dc365f (editable plugin notes — once a plugin can ship a panel, the natural next ask is \"ship the workflow that uses it\").","created_at":"2026-05-05T00:12:54","updated_at":"2026-05-07T00:06:26","priority":2,"tags":["discoverability","manifest","plugin-system","skills","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Auto-fit text tile fonts in presentation deck — pixel sizes don't scale across viewports","description":"Today the presentation plugin's text-tile renderer has two modes:\n\n1. **Fixed**: caller passes `font_size` in pixels. Locked. Doesn't scale with slide viewport size — fonts look correct at editor zoom but tiny relative to the slide at full-screen.\n2. **Coupled** (default, no font_size): renderer derives font from `tile.h ÷ line_count`. Scales with tile h (and therefore slide size) but is height-only — long single-line titles overflow horizontally and clip.\n\nNeither mode considers BOTH `tile.w` and `tile.h` simultaneously. Authors have to hand-tune `font_size` for every long title (slides 3, 4, 15 in the alpha deck all needed manual font reductions to fit), and even then the result is correct only at one viewport size.\n\n**Goal**: an `auto_fit` mode that picks the largest font that fits text inside `(tile.w, tile.h)`. Result scales proportionally with slide size and never overflows in either axis.\n\n**Acceptance criteria**:\n- A new MCP parameter (e.g. `auto_fit: true`, or treat `font_size: -1` as the auto-fit sentinel) on `add_text_tile` / `modify_tile`.\n- For plain text and simple BBCode (bold + color), the renderer measures via `Font.get_string_size()` / `get_multiline_string_size()` and binary-searches the font in [8, 200] px for the largest that fits both `w` (no horizontal overflow) and `h` (no vertical clip).\n- For nested BBCode / bullet content, measurement goes through `RichTextLabel.get_content_height()` after a forced layout pass.\n- Result scales with viewport: same proportional fill at editor preview, slideshow mode, and exported renders.\n- Existing decks (with `font_size` set) are unchanged — auto_fit is opt-in.\n- HITL test on `/tmp/llms_overview_v1.mdeck`: every title and bullet rect fits its text without manual font_size tuning.\n\n**Why now**: building the alpha overview deck for tomorrow's review surfaced the cost. Three of nineteen titles needed manual font-size shrinks; bullet text \"shrinks\" visually at full-screen because the pixel size doesn't scale. Without auto-fit, every authoring session repeats this manual tuning.\n\n**Estimated cost (rough)**: half-day for plain text + simple BBCode, full day if BBCode/bullet measurement via RichTextLabel adds debugging time.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:07","updated_at":"2026-05-05T17:29:38","priority":2,"tags":["auto-fit","presentation","rendering","text-tile","viewport-scaling"],"resolution":"All 5 children done. Renderer (019df67735927e), MCP surface (019df67776c772), nested-BBCode subsumption (019df677637175), opt-in decision (019df67797ec7a). HITL test (019df677b65a7c) ready — applied auto_fit to all 38 text tiles in /home/imran/temp/llms_overview_v1.mdeck during HITL session today; final full-screen visual verification pending user follow-up. Discovered + fixed a separate bug along the way: panel.set_deck() reset selected_slide_index on every MCP commit, yanking the user back to slide 0; fix preserves & clamps. Hint saved at minerva-plugin-host-owned/commit_path_preserves_ui_state."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df67735927e59a88e9c3758a6da9c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Renderer: auto-fit font for plain text + simple BBCode tiles","description":"In the presentation plugin's text-tile renderer (~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/), add an auto-fit code path:\n\n1. When `auto_fit: true` (or `font_size: -1`), enter measurement mode.\n2. Strip simple BBCode tags (`[b]`, `[color=...]`) from content for measurement — they don't change layout dimensions.\n3. Binary-search font size in [8, 200] px:\n   - At each candidate, call `Font.get_multiline_string_size(text, max_width=tile_pixel_w, font_size=candidate)`.\n   - Accept candidates where total height ≤ tile_pixel_h AND every line width ≤ tile_pixel_w (no soft-wrap overflow).\n   - Pick the largest accepted size.\n4. Render the BBCode-wrapped text with the chosen font_size.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Single-line titles: width-bound. Verify no horizontal clip.\n- Multi-line bullets: height-bound. Verify last line not truncated.\n- Empty text: skip auto-fit, no-op.\n- Bullets glyphs (•, ◦, ▪, ‣): include in width measurement.\n\nTests:\n- Headless: `extends SceneTree` test that creates a tile, sets content + auto_fit=true, calls the chosen font and asserts it fits within the tile rect.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:20","updated_at":"2026-05-05T05:17:50","tags":["auto-fit","godot","presentation","renderer"],"parent":"minerva:019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","resolution":"Renderer auto_fit landed via the existing fit-shrink path: `_desired_text_tile_font_px` returns `_TEXT_MAX_FONT_PX` when `tile.auto_fit==true` and no `font_size`, and the existing binary search picks the largest size in [8, 200] where wrapped RTL content fits the tile rect. Width is enforced via AUTOWRAP_WORD_SMART (overflowing lines wrap, growing content_height, which the height check rejects). Took the RTL measurement path rather than `Font.get_multiline_string_size` because the RTL infrastructure is already proven and respects width via wrap; perf isn't a concern at one tile per slide. Validator + make_text_tile updated. Tests: 12 new auto_fit cases pass; existing 168-test slide_model suite + shrink-to-fit probe still green. Files: ~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_model.gd, ~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_canvas.gd, ~/github/plugins/presentation/test/test_slide_canvas_auto_fit.gd. Note: the BBCode-stripping + Font.get_multiline_string_size approach the DCR sketched is still available if the RTL path proves insufficient in HITL — not needed for the alpha deck."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df677637175a79b37973b57ab06c7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Renderer: auto-fit font for nested BBCode / RichTextLabel content","description":"Plain Font.get_multiline_string_size is wrong for content with nested BBCode tags because it treats the raw markup as text. For full BBCode support:\n\n1. Parent a temporary RichTextLabel offscreen.\n2. Set bbcode_enabled=true, fit_content_height=true, custom_minimum_size.x = tile_pixel_w.\n3. Apply candidate font_size via theme override.\n4. Force layout (`queue_sort()` then await `process_frame`, or use `force_update_transform()` patterns).\n5. Read `get_content_height()` — accept if ≤ tile_pixel_h.\n6. Binary-search same as plain-text path.\n\nCaveats:\n- Forced-layout measurement is slower than Font.get_string_size — cache the result if tile content + rect haven't changed.\n- Async layout pass means auto-fit measurement may need to be a coroutine.\n- Off-tree measurement nodes need explicit `queue_free()` to avoid leaks.\n\nPerformance: budget should be acceptable as long as we measure once per tile change, not per render frame. Document the cache invalidation policy.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:32","updated_at":"2026-05-05T17:29:34","tags":["auto-fit","bbcode","presentation","renderer","richtextlabel"],"parent":"minerva:019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","resolution":"Subsumed by the renderer implementation in 019df67735927e: nested BBCode and bullet/numbered content go through the same RichTextLabel-based fit-shrink as plain text. auto_fit lifts the desired ceiling to _TEXT_MAX_FONT_PX (200) and the existing _rich_text_fits binary search measures via RTL.get_content_height after rtl.size = px.size — the forced-layout path the work item described. No separate code path needed; covered by the 12 auto_fit cases in test_slide_canvas_auto_fit.gd."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df67776c772548e849f75a2ee8018","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"MCP tool surface: add auto_fit parameter to add_text_tile and modify_tile","description":"After the renderer lands auto-fit:\n\n1. Add `auto_fit: bool` (default `false` for backward compat) to `add_text_tile` and `modify_tile`. When true, persisted as a tile field; ignored when `font_size` is also set (font_size wins, document this).\n2. Update tool descriptions to surface a third mode in the docs:\n   - **coupled** (no font_size): font derived from h ÷ line_count\n   - **fixed** (font_size set): pixel-locked\n   - **auto_fit** (auto_fit=true): binary-searches largest font that fits w × h\n3. Recommend auto_fit as the default authoring choice once stable.\n4. Schema: persist as `tile.auto_fit: true` in .mdeck JSON. Older decks without the field default to false.\n\nTests:\n- MCP integration test: call add_text_tile with auto_fit=true and assert the persisted tile dict has the field set.\n- Round-trip: save .mdeck → reload → tile still auto-fits.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:37","updated_at":"2026-05-05T15:54:41","tags":["auto-fit","mcp","presentation","tool-surface"],"parent":"minerva:019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","resolution":"Schema and body changes landed in MCPPresentationTools.gd for both add_text_tile and modify_tile. Tool descriptions updated from \"TWO FONT MODES\" → \"THREE FONT MODES\" with auto_fit semantics + font_size precedence. Persisted as omit-when-default (`tile.auto_fit: true` only when explicitly set; modify_tile auto_fit=false erases the field). Image-tile guard mirrors the existing font_size guard. Tests: 10 new cases in test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd cover persistence, default omission, set/clear via modify, image-tile rejection. Full suite: 208 pass, 0 fail. Restart Minerva before HITL — MCP tools register at startup so the new schema needs a fresh process."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df67797ec7a2bb2e8962a73763790","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Decision: auto-fit as default behavior for new tiles?","description":"After the implementation lands, a product call needs to be made:\n\n**Option A**: auto_fit becomes the default for new tiles (no font_size, no coupled). Cleanest authoring experience — LLM doesn't need to pick a font.\n\n**Option B**: auto_fit stays opt-in, coupled remains the no-font default. Lower migration risk; existing prompts and skills keep working unchanged.\n\nConsiderations:\n- Existing .mdeck files: tile.auto_fit absent → coupled mode. No migration needed under Option B.\n- Skill prompts (\"authoring guidance\" in the presentation skill) tell the LLM to pick font_size for long titles. Under Option A those instructions become wrong; need to update.\n- Visual diff: auto_fit picks a different size than coupled for the same content. Decks would render slightly differently if auto-migrated.\n\nRecommendation: ship as opt-in (Option B) initially, watch usage, flip default later if results are consistently good.\n\nOutcome: a small ADR-style note in the presentation plugin's README plus an updated authoring section in the skill instructions.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:46","updated_at":"2026-05-05T17:29:28","tags":["decision","default-behavior","migration","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","resolution":"Decision: shipped as opt-in (Option B). add_text_tile / modify_tile take auto_fit:bool defaulting to false; existing decks unchanged; font_size still wins when both set. Authoring guidance in tool description updated to \"THREE FONT MODES\" so LLMs can pick auto_fit when scaling matters."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df677b65a7cfeaa21f28fe5dda0e7","type":"test","status":"ready","title":"HITL test: auto-fit on llms_overview_v1.mdeck","description":"Steps:\n\n1. Open `/tmp/llms_overview_v1.mdeck` (alpha overview deck — 19 slides).\n2. For every title text tile and every body bullet tile, clear `font_size` and set `auto_fit=true`.\n3. Inspect each slide:\n   - Title fits within its rect at all viewport sizes (no horizontal clip).\n   - Body bullets fill the body rect proportionally — they look the \"same size\" relative to the slide at editor zoom and full-screen.\n   - Long titles (slide 3 \"The Stack...\", slide 4 \"Model Layer Bigger...\", slide 15 \"Try It: Pick a Model + Focused Chat\") fit without manual shrinking.\n4. Toggle the editor to slideshow / full-screen mode. Verify proportions hold.\n5. Save deck, reopen, verify auto_fit persisted.\n\nExpected outcome: zero manual font_size values needed across all 19 slides. The deck looks consistent at every viewport size.","created_at":"2026-05-05T04:48:53","updated_at":"2026-05-05T17:29:19","tags":["alpha-deck","auto-fit","hitl","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df67700467e39879d6156b52fd085","component":"presentation-plugin"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Generic bidirectional host interface for plugin-owned MCP tools","description":"## Problem\n\nMinerva currently has presentation-specific MCP tools implemented in core (`MCPPresentationTools.gd`) even though presentation is an off-tree plugin. This violates the plugin architecture goal: users should be able to install or update plugins without recompiling Minerva, and each plugin should own its own MCP interface and implementation.\n\nThe root issue is generic, not presentation-specific. A plugin backend can expose MCP tools, but during a `tools/call` it has no clean bidirectional request/response path back into the fixed Minerva host for operations that only the host can perform:\n\n- discover open plugin-scene tabs\n- read serialized state from a plugin-owned panel\n- replace panel state and mark the tab dirty\n- open or focus plugin documents\n- read/write approved filesystem paths\n- request editor exports from other host editors\n\nBecause presentation tools need direct access to `EditorPane`, `SlideEditorPanel.get_deck()`, `SlideEditorPanel.set_deck()`, host-owned save dirtying, and Godot file/image helpers, the MCP implementation ended up in core.\n\n## Decision\n\nAdd a generic bidirectional host capability interface for plugin MCP backends. Minerva remains the broker and policy enforcer; plugin backends remain the owner of their MCP tool schemas and handlers.\n\nThe fixed Minerva binary should provide stable, generic host capabilities such as:\n\n- `host.documents.list_open`\n- `host.documents.open`\n- `host.documents.get_state`\n- `host.documents.set_state`\n- `host.documents.mark_dirty`\n- `host.files.read`\n- `host.files.write`\n- `host.editors.export`\n\nPlugin backends call these capabilities synchronously during their own `tools/call` handling. The capabilities are declared in plugin manifests and checked by `PluginPolicy`/`CapabilityBroker`.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope:\n\n- Dynamic registration of plugin MCP tools from plugin backend `tools/list`.\n- A generic request/response host capability channel usable during plugin tool execution.\n- Generic plugin panel state contract for serialize/deserialize/replace-state operations.\n- Policy and audit coverage for host capability calls.\n- Migration path for presentation MCP tools out of Minerva core into the presentation plugin.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Hardcoding presentation-specific host APIs in Minerva.\n- Requiring users to recompile Minerva to install new plugin tool surfaces.\n- Granting unrestricted filesystem or editor access to plugin backends.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A plugin can declare tools in its backend MCP server and Minerva registers them without core code changes.\n- A plugin tool can mutate an open plugin-owned panel through generic state get/set host calls.\n- A plugin tool can mutate an on-disk plugin document through policy-checked file capabilities.\n- Presentation MCP tools can be removed from Minerva core and implemented in `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation` without losing live-tab behavior.\n- Existing security model remains deny-by-default: host capabilities must be manifest-declared and policy-granted.\n- Tool calls and host capability calls are audit logged with plugin id, capability/tool name, and outcome.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:07","updated_at":"2026-05-12T05:26:58","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","broker","capabilities","mcp","plugins","presentation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e344fb7f7e936150d66c4ccc5b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Generic bidirectional plugin host capability interface","description":"## Plan\n\nBuild the broker capability in layers so existing plugins continue to work while new plugins can own their full MCP interfaces.\n\n## Architecture\n\n1. Plugin backend remains an MCP server and owns its `tools/list` schemas plus `tools/call` implementations.\n2. Minerva registers plugin tools dynamically when a plugin starts.\n3. During plugin `tools/call`, the backend can call back into Minerva through a generic host capability request/response channel.\n4. Minerva enforces policy, performs host-only operations, and returns structured results.\n5. Plugin panels expose generic state hooks so the host can serialize/replace their state without knowing domain-specific schemas.\n\n## Phases\n\n1. Discover and register plugin backend tools dynamically.\n2. Add synchronous host capability calls over the plugin broker path.\n3. Define generic document/panel state capabilities.\n4. Add policy, audit, and error handling coverage.\n5. Migrate presentation MCP tools to the presentation plugin as the proof case.\n6. Remove presentation-specific MCP module from Minerva core after parity tests pass.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Re-entrant calls: plugin tool call waits on host capability while Minerva is already handling the plugin call.\n- Dirty/save semantics: state replacement must mark tabs dirty consistently.\n- Security: filesystem and cross-editor access must stay deny-by-default.\n- Backward compatibility: existing plugin manifests with static `tools` arrays should keep working during migration.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:37","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:37","priority":1,"tags":["broker","mcp","plan","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e34b3477989ec5e8d03e36697a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Define plugin backend dynamic tool discovery contract","description":"## Task\n\nDefine and implement the contract for registering tools from a plugin backend at startup.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- On plugin start, call backend `tools/list` after initialize.\n- Validate every returned tool name starts with `minerva_<plugin_id>_`.\n- Reject conflicts with built-in tools and other plugin tools.\n- Preserve manifest-declared tools as install-time review metadata or expected-tool declarations.\n- Update `PluginToolRegistry` to support backend-discovered tools.\n- Emit tool registration/unregistration signals exactly as today so search index and MCP `tools/list` update normally.\n\n## Done When\n\n- A plugin can add or remove tool schemas by changing only its backend and manifest, without editing Minerva core.\n- Existing manifest-only plugin tools still register or have a documented compatibility path.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:38","updated_at":"2026-05-08T20:18:41","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","plugins","tool-discovery"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Shipped. Two commits on user/imran/experiments/swarm: f44de11a (Sonnet implementer — auto-prefix dynamic discovery) + 7926affa (cold-Opus reviewer follow-ups — signal emission on purge, await discovery, wired-registry integration assertions). Smoke test 37/37 PASS (was 13/13 baseline; 7 wired-registry assertions added). Prefix policy: Option B (auto-prefix). Files: PluginToolRegistry.gd +155 (register_backend_tools, _apply_prefix, _sanitize_tool_name, signal emission on purge), PluginManager.gd +41 (_discover_backend_tools awaited from start_plugin), test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd +173 (24 new assertions). MinervaMCPServer.gd:350 dispatch already handled the routing — no edit needed. Open questions from implementer/reviewer all resolved or filed as hints. New docket hints surfaced: minerva-mcp/mcpserverconnection-list-tools-is-coroutine, godot/script-mode-autoload-parse-order, minerva-singleton/plugin-tool-registry-late-init.</resolution>\n<parameter name=\"note\">Round 1 of broker DCR rollout complete. Layer-1 verified (smoke test). Awaiting user to start round 2 (broker phase 2 — sync host-callback channel, task `019df8e3514a706a85ec30b978dcdd78`)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3514a706a85ec30b978dcdd78","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add synchronous host capability request/response channel for plugin backends","description":"## Task\n\nAdd a generic bidirectional request/response mechanism so plugin backends can ask Minerva to perform host-only operations during `tools/call` handling.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Define a transport shape, e.g. `host/call` or a brokered JSON-RPC method, with `{capability, args, request_id}`.\n- Route requests through `CapabilityBroker` and `PluginPolicy`.\n- Support synchronous replies so plugin tools can continue after host calls return.\n- Prevent deadlocks and document re-entrancy constraints.\n- Return structured errors consistent with `PluginErrors`.\n- Audit each capability request and outcome.\n\n## Done When\n\n- A plugin backend can call a trivial host capability during a tool call and include the returned data in its tool result.\n- Denied capabilities fail safely with clear errors.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:40","updated_at":"2026-05-08T21:02:52","priority":1,"tags":["broker","capabilities","plugins","security"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Shipped at commit 9e427555 on user/imran/experiments/swarm. Sonnet implementer + cold Opus reviewer (verdict: approve_with_notes). Most of the channel was already wired in phase 1's substrate; phase 2 added: host.echo debug capability + EVENT_CAPABILITY_DISPATCHED/EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED audit constants + CODE_UNKNOWN_CAPABILITY error + audit log injection into broker constructor + fixture plugin (Python, 91 lines under src/test/fixtures/capability_probe/) + new integration test (32 assertions covering happy/deny/re-entrancy/audit paths). Re-entrancy: existing `_in_stdio_request` guard sufficed; reviewer independently traced the path and confirmed no deadlock. Synchronous-reply contract verified: plugin blocks on its read until host writes the matching response. Tests: test_host_capability_channel.gd 32/32 PASS + test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd 37/37 (no regression). Reviewer's lone non-blocking note (duplicate audit-on-deny when broker-level deny follows policy-grant) filed as follow-up chore 019e09660bfb7a7092bb7ca539e7effc.</resolution>\n<parameter name=\"note\">Round 2 complete. Ready for round 3 (broker phase 3 — generic doc/panel state capabilities, task 019df8e3576f7b53b606d574c1f809f5) when user is ready."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3576f7b53b606d574c1f809f5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement generic plugin document and panel state host capabilities","description":"## Task\n\nImplement host capabilities for plugin-owned documents and panels.\n\n## Proposed Capabilities\n\n- `host.documents.list_open`: list open plugin-owned documents/tabs with stable handles.\n- `host.documents.open`: open/focus a plugin document by plugin id, panel name, and path.\n- `host.documents.get_state`: serialize current state for a plugin-owned panel/tab.\n- `host.documents.set_state`: replace state for a plugin-owned panel/tab.\n- `host.documents.mark_dirty`: mark the tab/document as changed.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Work for host-owned-save panels and project-state panels.\n- Use existing panel hooks where possible: `get_deck()/set_deck()` can be generalized or wrapped behind serialize/deserialize hooks.\n- Return stable document handles rather than raw Node references.\n- Enforce plugin ownership: a plugin can only mutate its own panels unless explicitly granted cross-plugin capability.\n\n## Done When\n\n- A plugin backend can mutate an open plugin-owned tab through get_state/set_state and the editor reflects the change immediately.\n- The tab dirty/save behavior matches direct in-process mutations.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:05:41","updated_at":"2026-05-08T23:05:07","priority":1,"tags":["documents","host-capabilities","panel-state","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Reads-only slice shipped on user/imran/experiments/swarm: commit 0273c1cf (initial implementation) + bd02a019 (cold-Opus review followups: buffer-resolution order aligned to MCPDocTools, structured `unsupported_reason` replacing free-text `note`).\n\nCapabilities shipped this slice:\n  - host.documents.list_open  → {documents: [{editor_name, kind, plugin_id, panel_name, path}]}\n  - host.documents.get_state  → {editor_name, kind, plugin_id, panel_name, path,\n                                  buffer_canonical, buffer_text, version, dirty}\n                                  or {success:false, error_code:\"editor_not_found\"}\n\nCapabilities NOT shipped (deferred to follow-up 019e09d61b58):\n  - host.documents.open\n  - host.documents.set_state\n  - host.documents.mark_dirty\n\nTests:\n  - test_host_capability_documents.gd  39/0 first-install / 37/0 steady-state  NEW\n    (unit + integration: empty list, schema validation, editor_not_found, deny + audit,\n     fixture round-trip + no-caps deny fixture)\n  - test_host_capability_channel.gd    32/32  unchanged\n  - test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd           37/37  unchanged\n\nHITL verification (2026-05-08): real-app CAD smoke passed; LLM successfully generated a T-beam through the plugin; CAD plugin still fully functional. Round-1+2+3 reads regression baseline holds.\n\nCold-Opus review verdict: approve_with_notes (5 items). Two folded into bd02a019 (buffer order, unsupported_reason). Three deferred:\n  - audit miscategorization → existing chore 019e09660bfb (round-3 sibling commented in)\n  - populated-list test gap (needs MainScene boot) → follow-up 019e09d61b58\n  - get_state silently ignoring extras → follow-up 019e09d61b58 (design call before set_state ships)\n\nClosing as 'done' for the read-only slice. The follow-up task scopes the remaining 3 capabilities + the polish items, including an explicit instruction to fix the audit chore in-line rather than shipping more polluted audit data."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3b7637d019a807741229a22be","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Add policy, manifest, and audit coverage for host capabilities","description":"## Task\n\nWire host capability calls into plugin manifest permissions, policy checks, and audit logging.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Extend manifest validation for generic host capabilities such as `host.documents.*`, `host.files.*`, and `host.editors.*`.\n- Keep deny-by-default behavior when a capability is not declared/granted.\n- Add scoped path validation for `host.files.read` and `host.files.write`.\n- Add ownership validation for document/panel capabilities.\n- Audit capability name, plugin id, argument summary, result status, and error code.\n- Surface actionable policy errors to plugin tools and users.\n\n## Done When\n\n- Unauthorized document/file/editor capability requests are rejected before host mutation.\n- Authorized requests are logged and rate-limitable through existing policy machinery.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:06:06","updated_at":"2026-05-10T02:37:00","priority":1,"tags":["audit","plugins","policy","security"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Shipped on user/imran/experiments/swarm. Round-start SHA c95a92b6 → close SHA 13b3b1c0 (4 commits).\n\n**Units delivered:**\n\n- **U1 — manifest validation for host_capabilities** (commits ddbbf16a + bcf861d9). Added `ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES` (`host.echo`, `host.documents.{list_open, get_state, set_state, mark_dirty}`) + `ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITY_NAMESPACES` (`mcp.proxy:`, `secrets:`) registry in `PluginDefinition.gd`. New `validate_host_capabilities()` wired into `validate()`. `PluginDB.from_dict` reload now rejects records that fail validation (was warn-and-keep). Tests: `test_plugin_definition.gd` 99/0 (was 88/0; +11).\n\n- **U2 — ownership validation on set_state/mark_dirty** (commit ead6467c). New `CODE_OWNERSHIP_REQUIRED` in `PluginErrors.gd`. `_check_editor_ownership` in `CapabilityBroker.gd` requires `ed.plugin_id == caller` for PLUGIN_SCENE editors; host-owned editors unaffected; empty owner_id on plugin_scene denied defensively. `set_state` and `mark_dirty` handlers gate on this. Routed to `EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED` (security event) via `_POLICY_DENY_CODES`. `get_state` intentionally open (read-only inspection).\n\n- **U3 — redacted args summary in audit dispatch** (commits ead6467c + 0a20e0d5). `_redact_args(args, depth=0)` static helper. Field-name denylist replaces with `<redacted: N chars/bytes/items/keys>` descriptors regardless of length: `buffer_text, bytes, data, content, value, password, token, secret, api_key, authorization`. Length cap 256 chars on remaining strings. Recurses one level into nested dicts (defends mcp.proxy: forwarding); depth-2 dicts summarised as `<nested dict: N keys>`. `_audit_dispatch` extended to emit `args_summary` on DISPATCHED/DENIED/FAILED.\n\n- **U4 — validate_files_path wrapper** (commits ead6467c + 0a20e0d5). New static method on `CapabilityBroker.gd`. Wraps `is_path_in_scope` with `success | target_not_allowlisted | schema_validation_failed` envelopes. SECURITY FIXME docstring covers symlink-escape and relative-path-acceptance — both deferred to T5 with the first real caller. NIT-6 (`begins_with` substring match: `/tmp/foo` matches `/tmp/foobar`) inherited as known limitation. No callers yet — T5 wires.\n\n**Verification:**\n\n- Layer-1 (5 targeted): test_plugin_definition 99/0, test_plugin_capabilities 31/0, test_host_capability_documents 72/0, test_host_capability_channel 32/0, test_cad_plugin_smoke 37/0.\n- Layer-2 (15 plugin smoke): 14 green, 1 SIGSEGV (`test_plugin_manager_hot_reload`) — pre-existing, not in T4 path. Filed as bug `019e0fb829c4`.\n\n**Process artefacts saved as nudge hints:**\n- `claude-code/worktree-isolation-branches-from-origin-head` — agent worktrees provision off origin/HEAD; local-branch SHAs need `git reset --hard <sha>` as agent's first action.\n- `claude-code/subagent-fabricated-implementation-report` (RETRACTED) — initial alarm that an agent fabricated work; correct behavior is task notification propagates worktree changes back to parent's primary tree and cleans up the worktree path.\n- `minerva-docket/mcp-docket-server-location` — standalone Docket binary at /home/imran/temp/Docket.x86_64 listening on localhost:3010 is the source for `mcp__docket__*` tools; loads .dct on start, restart needed after external syncs.\n\n**Out of scope, deferred:**\n- T4 ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES does not list `host.files.*` or `host.editors.*` — those are T5. The validator-wrapper U4 ships without callers; T5 wires it.\n\n**Companions left open (intentionally):**\n- `019e0aaf1902` — DocumentBuffer.mark_dirty() needs a signal so tab UI auto-updates dirty indicator. UX polish, separate from policy/audit gates. Resume at convenience."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3bd7973e5bb2d6acfe1f74611","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"Add generic filesystem and editor export host capabilities","description":"## Task\n\nAdd generic host capabilities for file and editor-export operations used by plugin MCP tools.\n\n## Proposed Capabilities\n\n- `host.files.read`: read approved files as bytes/base64/text.\n- `host.files.write`: write approved files.\n- `host.editors.list`: list open editors available for export.\n- `host.editors.export`: request export from an editor through an explicit export contract.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Filesystem access must respect plugin manifest `permissions.filesystem` and policy grants.\n- Editor export must be explicit and type-safe: plugins request a named export format, not raw Node access.\n- Cross-editor access should require explicit capability grants.\n- Responses should have size limits and structured payload metadata.\n\n## Done When\n\n- Presentation plugin can implement image tile tools that read image files or import graphics editor output without Minerva-specific MCP code.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:06:07","updated_at":"2026-05-10T16:26:21","priority":2,"tags":["capabilities","editor-export","filesystem","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3c3937dda9f7abeebf5f12eff","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Migrate presentation MCP tools into the presentation plugin","description":"## Task\n\nUse the new broker capabilities to move the presentation MCP interface and implementation into `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Port `minerva_presentation_*` tool schemas from `MCPPresentationTools.gd` to the plugin backend `tools/list` implementation.\n- Port tool handlers to the plugin backend.\n- Use host document capabilities for live `tab_name` behavior.\n- Use host file/editor capabilities for image sources and graphics editor import.\n- Add z-order support while migrating, e.g. `minerva_presentation_reorder_tile` or front/back commands.\n- Remove `MCPPresentationTools.new(self)` from Minerva core after parity tests pass.\n\n## Done When\n\n- Presentation tools work from the plugin without recompiling Minerva.\n- Existing decks and live-tab workflows retain behavior.\n- Minerva no longer contains presentation-specific MCP tool implementation.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:06:09","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:44:27","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","migration","plugins","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"SHIPPED across multiple sub-tasks. The presentation plugin's MCP interface (minerva_presentation_*) lives entirely in ~/github/plugins/presentation now, with image bytes stored in {__blob__, content_type, bytes} envelopes that the broker swaps for handles on capability responses. Phase 5 R1-R7 + the architectural follow-up at _resolve_editor_buffer closed the implementation; HITL verified on a 58MB image-heavy deck.\n\nComponent children shipped this rollout:\n- R0 panel-state IPC substrate (commits 6fad8778 + 62830a10)\n- R0-R7 across multiple commits (broker substrate, GDScript adoption, Go-side migration, architectural fix)\n- 019e15965ef5 (Phase 5 Bug D) — broker base64-String acceptance\n- 019e18946a1b (R7 plugin save-shape migration)\n- 019e18e43acc (architectural fix at _resolve_editor_buffer)\n\nTwo latent gaps remain as filed work items (low priority, currently dormant):\n- 019e18f86b9e — _describe_editor_state strip pass for future JSON-paired_dsl\n- 019e18f89e89 — missing paired_dsl get_state integration test\n\nT7 integration tests (sibling 019df8e3c9d5) remain DRAFT — the proper finishing move for the DCR, deserves its own focused session."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8e3c9d57472a7d721785f5f783c","type":"test","status":"passing","title":"Integration tests for plugin-owned MCP tools with host callbacks","description":"## Test Plan\n\nAdd automated tests that run against a fixed Minerva binary/plugin setup.\n\n## Test Cases\n\n1. Plugin backend tool discovery registers dynamic tools from `tools/list`.\n2. Plugin backend tool calls a trivial host capability and receives a response.\n3. Plugin backend mutates an open plugin-owned document through `host.documents.get_state`/`set_state`.\n4. `host.documents.set_state` marks the tab dirty and host-owned save persists the change.\n5. Unauthorized host capability request is denied and audited.\n6. Presentation plugin parity: create deck, open deck, add slide, add text tile, add image tile, modify tile, list/get slide, and z-order reorder all work through plugin-owned MCP tools.\n\n## Done When\n\n- Tests fail if presentation-specific MCP tools are required in Minerva core.\n- Tests pass with presentation tools implemented in the plugin backend.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:06:11","updated_at":"2026-05-11T23:29:24","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","plugins","presentation","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8ecca887c5a8a72ae0cefe0b670","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Move plugin-owned MCP tools out of Minerva core","description":"## Problem\n\nMinerva core currently contains MCP tools for some domains that are or should be plugins. This breaks the plugin architecture: users with a fixed compiled Minerva binary cannot install or update a plugin's MCP interface unless the tool schemas and implementations live in the plugin.\n\nThe immediate example is presentation: `MCPPresentationTools.gd` lives in Minerva core even though `.mdeck` decks and the slide editor are provided by the off-tree presentation plugin. Similar ownership boundaries should be audited for CAD, PCB, video, spreadsheet, webview, and other modules as plugin extraction progresses.\n\n## Decision\n\nCreate a migration program to move plugin-owned MCP tools from Minerva core into their owning plugin backends. Minerva should keep only generic platform/broker tools and stable host capabilities. Domain-specific tool schemas, validators, and handlers should live with the plugin that owns the file format and UI/editor behavior.\n\nThis DCR depends on or is enabled by the generic bidirectional host interface DCR `019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope:\n\n- Audit Minerva MCP modules for plugin-owned domains.\n- Define ownership criteria for core vs plugin MCP tools.\n- Migrate plugin-specific tool modules into plugin backends.\n- Keep compatibility aliases or migration notes where needed.\n- Remove core modules after parity tests pass.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Moving truly core Minerva tools such as chat, notes, model management, plugin management, or generic editor/document broker APIs.\n- Hardcoding per-plugin replacement APIs in Minerva.\n\n## Ownership Criteria\n\nA tool should move to a plugin when most of these are true:\n\n- It manipulates a plugin-owned file format or document schema.\n- It depends on a plugin panel/editor implementation.\n- It exposes domain-specific creation/modification semantics.\n- It would need to change when the plugin evolves.\n- It is not useful without that plugin installed.\n\nA tool should remain in Minerva core when it is generic platform functionality:\n\n- chat/session/model/worker management\n- plugin install/start/stop/list\n- generic document/editor routing\n- generic host capabilities\n- generic annotation substrate APIs, until/if annotation substrate itself becomes a plugin\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- There is a documented inventory of MCP tools that are core-owned vs plugin-owned.\n- At least presentation MCP tools are migrated into the presentation plugin.\n- Minerva core no longer registers plugin-owned presentation tools directly.\n- Plugin-owned tools remain searchable/listable/callable through Minerva MCP after plugin install/start.\n- Compatibility and migration behavior is documented for existing workflows.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:16:01","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:16:47","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","mcp","migration","plugins","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019d327c148c7ef7bef081eacd650295"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8ed2f1a7c74b28f56ef07bb56e9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Migrate presentation MCP tools from Minerva core to presentation plugin","description":"## Current State\n\nPresentation MCP tools are implemented in Minerva core at:\n\n- `/home/imran/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPresentationTools.gd`\n\nThey are registered from:\n\n- `/home/imran/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MinervaMCPServer.gd`\n- `_init_modules()` includes `MCPPresentationTools.new(self)`\n\nThe presentation plugin backend currently has no real tools:\n\n- `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/main.go` returns `tools: []`\n- `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/manifest.json` has `\"tools\": []`\n\n## Tool Surface To Move\n\nMove all current `minerva_presentation_*` tools into the presentation plugin backend, including:\n\n- `minerva_presentation_create_deck`\n- `minerva_presentation_open_deck`\n- `minerva_presentation_list_slides`\n- `minerva_presentation_add_slide`\n- `minerva_presentation_set_slide_background`\n- `minerva_presentation_add_text_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_add_image_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_list_tiles`\n- `minerva_presentation_list_annotations`\n- `minerva_presentation_get_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_get_slide`\n- `minerva_presentation_modify_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_set_slide_title`\n- `minerva_presentation_set_aspect`\n- `minerva_presentation_move_slide`\n- `minerva_presentation_remove_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_remove_slide`\n- `minerva_presentation_add_annotation`\n- `minerva_presentation_remove_annotation`\n- `minerva_presentation_set_annotation_resolved`\n- `minerva_presentation_list_annotation_kinds`\n- `minerva_presentation_list_open_annotations`\n- `minerva_presentation_add_spreadsheet_tile`\n- `minerva_presentation_modify_spreadsheet_cells`\n- `minerva_presentation_resize_spreadsheet`\n\nAlso add missing z-order support during migration, e.g. one of:\n\n- `minerva_presentation_reorder_tile`\n- or four commands: bring forward, send backward, bring to front, send to back\n\n## Implementation Requirements\n\n1. Add tool schemas to `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/manifest.json` or expose them dynamically from backend `tools/list`, depending on final broker design.\n2. Implement `tools/list` and `tools/call` in `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/main.go`.\n3. Port deck schema helpers from `MCPPresentationTools.gd` into the plugin backend:\n   - deck creation\n   - slide creation\n   - tile creation/modification/removal\n   - spreadsheet cell helpers\n   - annotation envelope helpers\n   - validation and normalized coordinate handling\n4. Preserve disk workflows using `.mdeck` path mutation.\n5. Preserve live-tab workflows using generic host document capabilities from DCR `019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`:\n   - list/open presentation documents\n   - get current deck state from an open tab\n   - set mutated deck state back into the tab\n   - mark dirty for host-owned save\n6. Preserve image workflows using generic host file/editor capabilities:\n   - `image_path`\n   - `image_base64`\n   - `source_graphics_editor`\n   - `solid_color`\n7. Remove `MCPPresentationTools.new(self)` from Minerva core after parity tests pass.\n8. Delete or deprecate `MCPPresentationTools.gd` so there is no duplicate registration or divergent behavior.\n\n## Compatibility Notes\n\nThe tool names should remain `minerva_presentation_*` so existing prompts and clients do not need to change. Ownership changes from core module to plugin backend; caller-facing names and schemas should remain stable except for explicitly added z-order commands.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdd/update tests to prove parity:\n\n- create/open deck\n- list/add/remove/move slides\n- set title/aspect/background\n- add/list/get/modify/remove text and image tiles\n- add/list/get/modify spreadsheet tiles/cells\n- add/list/resolve/remove annotations\n- live tab mutation reflects immediately in `SlideEditorPanel`\n- disk path mutation persists JSON\n- z-order reorder changes `slide[\"tiles\"]` order and render/hit-test order\n- Minerva core starts without registering presentation-specific MCP module\n\n## Done When\n\n- Presentation MCP tools work when provided by `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation` only.\n- Minerva core contains no presentation-specific MCP tool handler module.\n- Users can update presentation MCP behavior by updating the plugin without rebuilding Minerva.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:16:26","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:16:26","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","migration","plugins","presentation","z-order"],"parent":"minerva:019df8ecca887c5a8a72ae0cefe0b670"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8ee594774ae821abd40efeec9de","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Presentation MCP: expose currently-viewed slide","description":"During HITL on the alpha deck, agents need to know which slide the user is looking at — there's no way today. The panel already tracks `_selected_slide_index` internally (used for the create-note hook); just expose it.\n\n**Goal**: a new MCP tool `minerva_presentation_get_state` that, given a tab_name, returns the currently-selected slide index plus a small bundle of useful context (slide_count, selected slide title).\n\n**Acceptance**:\n- New MCP tool `minerva_presentation_get_state` (tab_name required — only meaningful when a deck is open).\n- Returns `{success, selected_slide_index, slide_count, slide_title}`.\n- Errors cleanly if tab not found / not a presentation.\n- SlideEditorPanel exposes a public `get_selected_slide_index()` getter.\n- MCP integration test confirms the value matches what `_selected_slide_index` is set to.\n\n**Why now**: HITL on the auto_fit alpha deck. User asked \"what slide am I on?\" — the agent had no way to answer.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:17:43","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:20:42","tags":["hitl","mcp","presentation","tool-surface"],"resolution":"Single-tool DCR. SlideEditorPanel exposes get_selected_slide_index(); new MCP tool minerva_presentation_get_state(tab_name) returns {selected_slide_index, slide_count, slide_title}. Tab-only — no path mode (selected slide is live UI state). Tests: 4 new asserts in test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd (missing tab_name + unknown tab). Total MCP suite 212/0. Files: SlideEditorPanel.gd, MCPPresentationTools.gd, test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd. Restart Minerva before use — MCP tools register at startup."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8ee8ae270048e0a8467f70dcbc6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Review presentation MCP surface before plugin migration","description":"## Task\n\nBefore migrating presentation MCP tools out of Minerva core, review the current and in-progress `minerva_presentation_*` MCP surface and update the migration scope so no tools are left behind.\n\n## Context\n\nThe presentation MCP interface is still evolving. The migration work item already lists the current tools in `MCPPresentationTools.gd`, but that list must be refreshed before implementation.\n\n## Review Scope\n\nInspect:\n\n- `/home/imran/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPresentationTools.gd`\n- `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_model.gd`\n- `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_canvas.gd`\n- `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/ui/SlideEditorPanel.gd`\n- relevant tests under `/home/imran/github/Minerva/src/test/test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd`\n- relevant plugin tests under `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/test/`\n\n## Specific Areas To Check\n\n- Existing tool list in `get_tool_names()` and `register_tools()`.\n- Handler coverage in `handle()`.\n- New or missing tools needed by recent presentation features, including:\n  - z-order / tile reorder controls\n  - text auto-fit / fixed-font controls\n  - spreadsheet tile operations\n  - image tile source replacement\n  - slide/background operations\n  - annotation operations that are truly presentation-specific vs generic substrate tools\n- Any schema changes that should ship with the plugin-owned interface.\n- Any tools that should be split into generic host capabilities instead of living in presentation.\n\n## Output\n\nUpdate the parent migration work item or add comments with:\n\n- final tool inventory to migrate\n- new presentation plugin tools to add\n- tools that should remain generic/core\n- compatibility notes for existing callers\n- test updates required for parity\n\n## Done When\n\nThe presentation migration task has an up-to-date, implementation-ready MCP tool inventory that reflects the current interface, not just the tool list that existed when the migration DCR was filed.","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:17:55","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:17:55","priority":1,"tags":["interface-review","mcp","migration","plugins","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df8ed2f1a7c74b28f56ef07bb56e9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8fd92967668882e7267d6fc5df8","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin set_deck (or set_payload) must preserve user UI state","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:34:20","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:34:20","tags":["host-owned","mcp","plugin-authoring","ui-state"],"value":"For host-owned plugin schemas (presentation, etc.), the MCP `_commit_target` patches the panel by calling `panel.set_deck(deck)` after every edit. If `set_deck` resets user UI state (selected slide index, scroll position, current zoom), every MCP mutation will yank the user's view back to a default — making collaborative HITL impossible. Preserve and clamp existing UI state inside set_deck, only resetting on FRESH load (which goes through a different path like `_on_panel_loaded`). Same caveat applies to any plugin: if your panel exposes set_X for the host, do not reset cursor/selection/scroll inside it.","component":"minerva-plugin-host-owned","key":"commit_path_preserves_ui_state"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019df8ff93ec7e0ba11bccfeb01d51b4","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Slide title lives in TWO places — set_slide_title alone won't change the visible header","created_at":"2026-05-05T16:36:32","updated_at":"2026-05-05T16:36:32","tags":["authoring","gotcha","mcp","presentation"],"value":"A presentation slide has two title sources: (1) `slide.title` — metadata used by list_slides, the tab thumbnail, and create_note alt-text; (2) the title text tile (typically tile[0]) that actually renders the visible header on the canvas. set_slide_title only updates (1). To rename the header the user sees, ALSO call modify_tile on tile[0] with the new content (preserving any BBCode wrapper like `[b][color=#1F2A2E]TITLE[/color][/b]`).","component":"minerva-presentation","key":"title_dual_source"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa66d558761ea167314f1b1ab07e","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Buffer-canonical document model for paired DSL+render plugins","description":"Replace today's \"disk-is-truth\" model with a buffer-canonical document model. Disk becomes a snapshot written only on explicit Save / Save All — including over MCP. Adds a generic platform substrate that lets any DSL plugin pair its render panel with a text editor, both attached to one shared buffer.\n\n## Architecture (locked)\n\nLayered:\n\n  PathResolver  -> DiskAccess  -> DocumentRegistry  -> MCP tools\n\n- `PathResolver` — single home for path normalization, validation, policy checks.\n- `DiskAccess` — single home for filesystem read/write/exists.\n- `DocumentRegistry` — singleton, path -> buffer; uses DiskAccess for load-on-first-access and save-on-flush. Owns dirty tracking, version counter, `text_changed` and `external_change_detected` signals.\n- MCP tools — thin wrappers (~5 lines each).\n\nTwo MCP namespaces, no overlap:\n\n- `minerva_doc_*` (read/write/edit/save/save_all) — go through DocumentRegistry. Buffer canonical. Writes never touch disk except via `save`.\n- `minerva_disk_*` (read/write/exists) — go straight to DiskAccess; never inspect the registry. For the rare case of \"I genuinely mean the bytes on disk, ignore any in-memory state.\"\n\n`minerva_disk_write` to a path with an existing buffer fires `external_change_detected` (symmetric with vim/git writing externally).\n\n## Plugin substrate\n\nPlugin manifest gains `render_mode: paired_dsl` plus a `layout_hint` (default `split_right`). When set, the file-open dispatcher opens both the plugin's render panel and a text editor against the same buffer from the registry. Layout follows Overleaf-style equal-split: render in the main pane, text editor docked right at ~1/3 width.\n\nPlugin contract: `BufferAwarePanel.attach_buffer(buffer)`. The panel:\n- Subscribes to `text_changed`.\n- Owns its own debounce timer (300ms idle default).\n- Owns its own cancellation: cancels in-flight render requests when a new `text_changed` arrives.\n\nCommunication uses the existing plugin broker IPC — no new co-mingled MCP tools added to Minerva for the substrate itself.\n\n## Conflict resolution\n\nExternal-change detection on watched files. When `external_change_detected` fires and the buffer is dirty, prompt the user with **Reload** or **Keep Buffer**. No diff view, no three-way merge.\n\n## Crash recovery\n\nNone. Save-or-lose-it. Dirty indicator on tabs and a \"save before close\" prompt are the only safeguards.\n\n## Migration strategy\n\nBig-bang on the semantic flip (the buffer-canonical rule must hold atomically — partial state = dual-truth races), transitional shims on the naming. Existing `minerva_file_*`, `minerva_get_editor_content`, `minerva_update_editor`, `minerva_save_editor`, `minerva_cad_get_document_source` become thin shims at the new tools, marked deprecated, scheduled for removal one release after this DCR ships.\n\n## Plugin-MCP-debt gap analysis\n\nNet reduction of co-mingled debt:\n\n- Platform additions (registry, doc/disk tools, buffer notifications) belong in Minerva by definition — they manage cross-plugin shared state.\n- Several existing Minerva-hosted tools become redundant under the new design (`minerva_get_editor_content`, `minerva_cad_get_document_source`, etc.) and are scheduled for deletion.\n- Plugin-specific render/parse logic stays in plugin processes. Substrate communication is over the existing broker IPC.\n- This DCR does not advance the separate broker-completion DCR for migrating plugin-MCP tools out of Minerva, but it does not add new debt to it either.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (DCR-level)\n\n1. Buffer is canonical: every editing surface (text editor, agent MCP tools, plugin panels) reads and writes via `DocumentRegistry`. Disk is only written by `minerva_doc_save` / `minerva_doc_save_all` and the `Save` UI affordance.\n2. `minerva_disk_*` is the only non-save path that writes the filesystem; doing so on a path with an existing buffer raises `external_change_detected`.\n3. The CAD plugin uses paired-DSL mode end-to-end: editing `t_beam.mcad` in the side text editor causes the CAD render panel to update on debounce; closing either pane prompts on dirty buffer.\n4. External edits (vim / git checkout) on a dirty buffer fire the Reload/Keep prompt.\n5. All redundant legacy tools (`minerva_file_*`, `minerva_get_editor_content`, `minerva_update_editor`, `minerva_save_editor`, `minerva_cad_get_document_source`) work as deprecation shims pointing at the new tools.\n\n## Not in scope\n\n- Three-way merge / diff UI on conflict (Reload/Keep only).\n- Crash recovery / autosave.\n- Migrating plugin-specific MCP tools (`minerva_cad_*`, `minerva_pcb_*`, etc.) out of Minerva — that is the separate broker-completion DCR and not advanced here.\n- OCCT watchdog / cancel for the worker process — adjacent and complementary, but a separate work item.\n- Annotation-lifecycle improvements (\"annotation marked done when work performed\") — separate RCA item.\n- Editor↔CAD live update beyond the buffer-subscription mechanism (no preview features, viewport sync, etc.).\n- New conflict semantics for non-DSL plugin file types (`.minpcb` etc.); those keep their single-UI model.\n\n## Non-goals (explicit anti-scope-creep)\n\nImplementers MUST NOT, while working tasks under this DCR:\n\n- Refactor unrelated subsystems \"while they're in there.\"\n- Add features not listed in a task's acceptance criteria.\n- Bundle bug fixes from other RCAs into this work — file separate items.\n- Combine multiple tasks into a single PR. One task = one PR.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:08:56","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:48:02","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","buffer","cad","document-model","mcp","plugins","substrate"],"resolution":"All 5 DCR-level acceptance criteria satisfied.\n\n(1) Buffer canonical — every editing surface (Editor.gd, plugin panels, MCP tools) reads/writes via DocumentRegistry. Disk only flushed via minerva_doc_save / minerva_doc_save_all / Save UI.\n(2) minerva_disk_* is the only non-save filesystem write path; on buffered paths it routes through registry.poke_path → file_changed dispatcher (post-T7 — same dispatcher handles external editor writes too).\n(3) CAD plugin paired_dsl mode end-to-end: T6 HITL'd live edits, debounced re-eval, supersession via cad.cancel_eval. Closing either pane prompts on dirty.\n(4) External edits on dirty buffer fire Reload/Keep prompt — T7 HITL'd via VSCode external write.\n(5) Legacy tools removed (T8) per scope revision (comment 330): minerva_file_* and minerva_cad_get_document_source deleted; editor_* trio kept as scratchpad API.\n\nFinal summary:\n* 9 tasks: T1 foundation, T2 doc/disk MCP tools, T3 atomic flip, T4 Editor↔registry, T5 plugin substrate, T6 CAD adoption, T7 external-change watcher + dialog, T7.5 host.fs.* broker channels (sibling, added during T7 pre-flight), T7.6 CAD host.fs.* adoption (closed \"no target\" — .mcad is closed-world), T8 legacy cleanup.\n* Tests: 14 suites, 613/0.\n* Tool count: 279 → 275 (T8).\n* Hints: godot-headless-tests/preload-autoload-identifier-hangs, minerva-ui/editor-pane-idempotency-by-file-only, mcp-tools/handle-is-coroutine-in-mcp-modules, godot-headless-tests/new-class-name-needs-import-pass, minerva-plugins/manifest-cache-stale-after-reload, document-registry/editor-buffer-attach-pattern, minerva-ui/two-unsaved-editor-dialogs, docket/work-item-state-chain.\n\nKnown follow-ups (out of DCR, filed):\n* host_capabilities DCR 019df8e2d093 #327 — host.viewports.capture sub-spec (CAD vision-loop).\n* host.fs.* PathResolver sandbox (T7.5 #329) — defer to PluginPolicy.\n* Chores 019dfe1b482a (MCPEditorTools headless testability) and 019dfea6418a (migration mirrors — partially obsolete post-T8)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plan: staged implementation of buffer-canonical document model","description":"Implementation broken into 8 ordered tasks. The buffer-canonical rule must hold atomically once the gating PR (step 3) lands. Steps 1-2 and 5/7 are additive and can be reviewed independently; steps 3, 4, 6 carry behavior change.\n\n## Order of execution\n\n1. Foundation classes — `PathResolver`, `DiskAccess`, `DocumentRegistry`. No callers yet. Pure addition, zero behavior change.\n2. New MCP tools — `minerva_doc_*` and `minerva_disk_*`. Additive; nothing migrated yet.\n3. **Gating PR — atomic flip.** Rewire `minerva_file_*`, `minerva_get_editor_content`, `minerva_update_editor`, `minerva_save_editor`, `minerva_cad_get_document_source` to delegate through DocumentRegistry. Mark deprecated. Buffer-canonical rule now holds.\n4. Wire `Editor.gd` (text editor) to attach to the registry's buffer instead of its own.\n5. Manifest schema — add `render_mode: paired_dsl` and `layout_hint`; define `BufferAwarePanel` IPC contract on the broker. Additive; no plugin uses it yet.\n6. Wire CADPanel to consume `text_changed` via attach_buffer; remove the direct dsl_text dispatch path.\n7. External-change watcher + Reload/Keep prompt when a dirty buffer's underlying file mutates.\n8. Cleanup — delete redundant legacy tools after one release of deprecation.\n\n## Sequencing constraints\n\n- Steps 1 and 2 can land in parallel (same PR or separate, reviewer's call).\n- Step 3 requires 1 and 2.\n- Steps 4 and 5 can land in parallel after 3.\n- Step 6 requires 5.\n- Step 7 requires 3.\n- Step 8 lands one release after the rest.\n\n## Acceptance for the plan as a whole\n\nAll 8 task children reach `done` (or equivalent terminal state). DCR acceptance criteria 1–5 are demonstrable end-to-end on the CAD plugin with a `.mcad` file.\n\n## Plan-level non-goals\n\nThis plan does NOT contain tasks for:\n- OCCT watchdog or worker cancellation hardening (separate item).\n- Annotation lifecycle improvements (separate RCA).\n- Plugin-MCP-out-of-Minerva broker work (separate DCR).\n- New DSL plugins beyond CAD (the substrate is generic but rolling it out to other plugins is post-DCR).","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:09:17","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:47:01","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","execution","plan"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa66d558761ea167314f1b1ab07e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa67972f71169c82e500b97bd993","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 1: Foundation — PathResolver, DiskAccess, DocumentRegistry","description":"Introduce three new classes that the rest of the DCR builds on. None of them are wired into existing code yet.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] `PathResolver` class exists with: `resolve(path) -> {ok, abs_path | error}`, applies path normalization and Minerva's existing path policy. Unit tests cover relative paths, absolute paths, denied paths.\n- [ ] `DiskAccess` class exists with: `read(path) -> {ok, text | error}`, `write(path, text) -> {ok | error}`, `exists(path) -> bool`. Uses `PathResolver` internally. Unit tests cover read/write/exists.\n- [ ] `DocumentRegistry` singleton exists with: `get_or_create_buffer(path) -> Buffer`, `has_buffer(path) -> bool`, `dispose_buffer(path)`. Buffer model exposes `text`, `version: int (monotonic)`, `dirty: bool`, signals `text_changed(text, version)`, `external_change_detected`, `saved`. Methods: `apply_edit(new_text)`, `save_to_disk()`, `reload_from_disk()`. Unit tests cover get/create idempotency, version monotonicity, signal emission, save/reload, dispose.\n- [ ] No existing code modified. CI green. No new MCP tools added (Task 2 covers those).\n\n## File scope\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/PathResolver.gd` (new)\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/DiskAccess.gd` (new)\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/DocumentRegistry.gd` (new)\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/DocumentBuffer.gd` (new)\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/tests/` (new test files)\n\nAnything outside this list is out of scope. Reviewer rejects PRs that touch other paths.\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Wiring any existing code to the new classes (Tasks 3–6).\n- Adding any MCP tool (Task 2).\n- File-watching for external changes (Task 7).\n- Touching `Editor.gd`, `CADPanel.gd`, or any plugin file.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:09:46","updated_at":"2026-05-05T23:23:45","priority":2,"tags":["additive","foundation","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa67bbe17648802195d32e775da2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 2: New MCP tools — minerva_doc_* and minerva_disk_*","description":"Register the new MCP tools. Each is a thin (~5-line) wrapper over the foundation classes from Task 1.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] `minerva_doc_read(path) -> {ok, text | error}` — reads via registry; returns `not_found` if no buffer and no file on disk.\n- [ ] `minerva_doc_write(path, text) -> {ok | error}` — calls `buffer.apply_edit(text)`. Buffer marked dirty. Disk untouched.\n- [ ] `minerva_doc_edit(path, old, new) -> {ok | error}` — string-replace edit on `buffer.text`.\n- [ ] `minerva_doc_save(path) -> {ok | error}` — flushes buffer to disk. Clears dirty.\n- [ ] `minerva_doc_save_all() -> {ok, saved_paths}` — flushes every dirty buffer in the registry.\n- [ ] `minerva_disk_read(path)`, `minerva_disk_write(path, text)`, `minerva_disk_exists(path)` — go straight to DiskAccess.\n- [ ] `minerva_disk_write` to a path with a buffer fires `external_change_detected` on that buffer.\n- [ ] No existing tool modified.\n- [ ] Integration tests: happy path for each tool; not_found path; disk_write triggering external_change_detected.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPDocTools.gd` (new) — minerva_doc_* tools\n- `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPDiskTools.gd` (new) — minerva_disk_* tools\n- Tool registration in MCP server bootstrap\n- Tests under MCP module test directory\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Modifying or deprecating `minerva_file_*`, `minerva_get_editor_content`, etc. (Task 3).\n- Wiring `Editor.gd` or any plugin (Tasks 4–6).\n- Conflict-resolution UI (Task 7).","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:09:55","updated_at":"2026-05-05T23:31:01","priority":2,"tags":["additive","mcp","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa67ee6d7747a9ca835e0e203796","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 3: Atomic flip — rewire legacy tools as registry-backed shims","description":"**This is the gating PR.** After it lands, the buffer-canonical invariant holds for every tool that takes a path. Partial state is forbidden — every named tool below must be flipped in this PR, no exceptions.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] `minerva_file_read` delegates to `minerva_doc_read` (or directly to registry).\n- [ ] `minerva_file_write` delegates to `minerva_doc_write` — does not touch disk.\n- [ ] `minerva_file_edit` delegates to `minerva_doc_edit`.\n- [ ] `minerva_get_editor_content` delegates to `minerva_doc_read` (resolving the editor's file_path).\n- [ ] `minerva_update_editor` delegates to `minerva_doc_write`.\n- [ ] `minerva_save_editor` delegates to `minerva_doc_save`.\n- [ ] `minerva_cad_get_document_source` delegates to `minerva_doc_read` for the CAD editor's file_path.\n- [ ] All shims emit a deprecation note in their tool description (not in the result payload — must not break callers).\n- [ ] Existing call sites work unchanged (verified by running existing test suite).\n- [ ] Buffer-canonical invariant test: writing via `minerva_file_write` does NOT change the file on disk; only `minerva_doc_save` does.\n- [ ] CI green; no behavioral regression in any existing test.\n\n## File scope\n\n- Existing MCP module files implementing the listed legacy tools\n- Tool description strings (for deprecation note)\n- Existing tests adjusted to assert buffer-not-disk behavior\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Removing the legacy tools (Task 8).\n- Wiring `Editor.gd` directly to registry (Task 4 — this task only changes the *tool* layer).\n- Wiring CAD plugin (Task 6).\n- Adding new functionality of any kind. This task is purely a layer reroute.\n\n## Risk note\n\nThis is the highest-risk PR in the DCR. Reviewer must confirm:\n- Every tool listed above is rewired (no partial flip).\n- No tool gains new behavior; only the implementation moves.\n- A regression test exists demonstrating buffer-canonical: write via tool → file on disk unchanged → save → file changes.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:10:08","updated_at":"2026-05-06T16:31:29","priority":1,"tags":["gating","high-risk","semantic-flip","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e","resolution":"Atomic flip shipped in two commits: 9884a2ba (initial delegation) + 2450dc6a (regression test unblock — runtime SingletonObject lookup in MCPCodeTools + reinstated test_legacy_buffer_canonical.gd, 16/0).\n\nAll 5 legacy shim families now delegate through DocumentRegistry: minerva_file_{read,write,edit}, minerva_get_editor_content, minerva_update_editor, minerva_save_editor, minerva_cad_get_document_source. Disk untouched until minerva_doc_save flushes. Buffer-canonical invariant verified end-to-end via in-app HITL on /home/imran/temp/ref1/butter-tart.txt.\n\nTest coverage: 248/0 across 7 Documents-related suites. Test scope is the minerva_file_* shims (MCPCodeTools); minerva_*_editor shims rely on PR-time review until chore 019dfe1b lands the body-level autoload-lookup refactor for MCPEditorTools.\n\nProduction refactor: MCPCodeTools._emit_tool_executed() static helper added; 9 SingletonObject.X emit sites + 1 SingletonObject.get_tree() lookup converted to runtime resolution via Engine.get_main_loop().root.get_node_or_null. Production behavior unchanged (autoload always present at runtime). The pattern is documented in hint godot-headless-tests/preload-autoload-identifier-hangs and is the recipe for chore 019dfe1b.\n\nHints saved during the work: godot-headless-tests/preload-autoload-identifier-hangs (toxic vs cascade autoload refs in --script tests), mcp-tools/handle-is-coroutine-in-mcp-modules (await-every-handle requirement). Cold-review opus agent added a third hint at 019dfdfac7d9 with a contradictory diagnosis — flagged to user for delete/supersede."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa681740744eaccd86208f7482eb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 4: Wire Editor.gd to attach to DocumentRegistry buffer","description":"Replace the text editor's internal buffer with an attachment to the registry's `DocumentBuffer`.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] On opening a file path, `Editor.gd` calls `DocumentRegistry.get_or_create_buffer(path)` and attaches.\n- [ ] User edits in the editor call `buffer.apply_edit(new_text)` (which fires `text_changed`).\n- [ ] External `text_changed` signals (from MCP edits or other panes) update the editor's visible text without re-emitting (no infinite loop).\n- [ ] Save (Cmd+S in editor) calls `buffer.save_to_disk()`.\n- [ ] Closing an editor tab on a dirty buffer prompts Save / Discard / Cancel.\n- [ ] Closing an editor tab on a clean buffer disposes it from the registry only if no other panel is attached (refcount or attachment-list).\n- [ ] Tests: edit-in-editor causes `text_changed`; edit-via-MCP updates editor visible text; save flushes correctly; close-on-dirty prompts.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Views/Editor.gd` (or whichever script holds the text editor's buffer logic)\n- `src/Scripts/UI/Views/Editor.tscn` if signal hookups change\n- Test file for editor↔buffer round-trip\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- CAD panel wiring (Task 6).\n- BufferAwarePanel substrate / manifest changes (Task 5).\n- External-change-detected prompt (Task 7).\n- Any change to the text editor's appearance, keybindings, syntax highlighting, etc. — buffer plumbing only.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:10:18","updated_at":"2026-05-06T19:01:00","priority":2,"tags":["editor","task","wiring"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa68477e72e8a0085875a748919f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 5: Plugin substrate — render_mode manifest field + BufferAwarePanel IPC","description":"Extend the plugin manifest schema and broker IPC so any DSL plugin can declare itself a paired DSL+render plugin and receive `text_changed` notifications.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] Plugin manifest schema accepts new fields: `render_mode: \"paired_dsl\"` (string enum) and `layout_hint: \"split_right\" | \"split_bottom\" | \"tab\"` (default `split_right`). Schema documented; existing plugins continue to validate.\n- [ ] When `render_mode: paired_dsl` is set, the file-open dispatcher opens both the plugin's render panel AND a text editor against the same registry buffer, in the layout hinted.\n- [ ] Broker IPC defines the `BufferAwarePanel` contract: messages `attach_buffer(path, version)`, `text_changed(text, version)`, `cancel_eval(request_id)`. Plugin receives these on its existing IPC channel.\n- [ ] A stub test plugin under `plugins/test_paired_dsl/` declares `render_mode: paired_dsl`, receives `attach_buffer` and `text_changed`, and acknowledges receipt. Used to verify substrate without depending on CAD.\n- [ ] Existing plugins (CAD included) keep working unchanged because they don't yet declare `render_mode`.\n\n## File scope\n\n- Plugin manifest schema/validator\n- File-open dispatcher in Minerva (the part that routes by extension/manifest)\n- Broker IPC message definitions\n- New test plugin under `plugins/test_paired_dsl/` (kept minimal)\n- Tests for substrate behavior\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Modifying CAD plugin or any other real plugin (Task 6).\n- Implementing debounce/cancel inside any specific plugin (the contract is defined; plugin-side adoption is per-plugin work in Task 6).\n- The Reload/Keep conflict prompt (Task 7).\n- Layout polish beyond getting the panes into the hinted positions.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:10:31","updated_at":"2026-05-06T19:42:17","priority":2,"tags":["broker","manifest","plugin-substrate","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa687bed74c8a8ce0c691a7cb4eb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 6: Wire CADPanel to attach_buffer + implement debounce + cancel","description":"Adopt the substrate from Task 5 in the CAD plugin. This is the validation that the substrate is fit for purpose.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] CAD plugin manifest declares `render_mode: paired_dsl` and `layout_hint: split_right`.\n- [ ] `CADPanel.gd` implements `attach_buffer(path)` — replaces the current dsl_text-on-open path. After attach, the panel evaluates once with the buffer's current text.\n- [ ] Panel subscribes to `text_changed`. On each event, restarts a 300ms debounce timer (one-shot).\n- [ ] When the timer fires, the panel sends `cancel_eval` for any previous in-flight request_id (if any), then dispatches a new evaluate.\n- [ ] Opening `t_beam.mcad` opens BOTH the CAD render panel and a text editor in a split-right layout, both attached to the same buffer.\n- [ ] Editing the text editor causes the CAD panel to re-render after debounce.\n- [ ] Editing the file via `minerva_doc_write` causes both panes to update.\n- [ ] Closing either pane prompts on dirty, per Task 4 semantics (or the symmetric panel-side semantics).\n- [ ] Manual smoke test on a real `.mcad` documented in PR description.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `plugins/cad/manifest.json`\n- `plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd`\n- `plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.tscn` (if signal hookups change)\n- Removal of the now-redundant direct dsl_text dispatch path from CADPanel\n- Plugin-side broker code if needed for cancel_eval\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Anything in the Python OCCT worker — the cancel is an IPC message; how the worker handles it is the separate OCCT-watchdog item (NOT this DCR).\n- Removing the now-redundant `minerva_cad_get_document_source` shim (Task 8).\n- Annotation-lifecycle work (separate RCA).\n- Layout polish beyond split-right at default proportions.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:10:44","updated_at":"2026-05-06T21:47:40","priority":2,"tags":["cad-plugin","consumer","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa68ad337ff683a9dd6cdf22adce","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 7: External-change watcher + Reload/Keep prompt","description":"Detect and reconcile external mutations to files with open buffers in the registry.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] DocumentRegistry watches the underlying file for any path with an active buffer (FileSystemWatcher / mtime polling — implementation choice, justify in PR description).\n- [ ] On detected change: if buffer is clean, silent reload (replaces buffer text from disk, fires `text_changed`).\n- [ ] On detected change: if buffer is dirty, fires `external_change_detected`. UI shows a prompt with two buttons only: **Reload** (replaces buffer with disk content, dirty cleared) and **Keep Buffer** (no disk read, dirty stays).\n- [ ] Prompt is non-blocking modal — other Minerva work continues until user picks.\n- [ ] If multiple external changes fire while the prompt is open, they coalesce — one prompt at a time per buffer.\n- [ ] `minerva_disk_write` to a path with an existing buffer triggers the same `external_change_detected` path (verified in Task 2 acceptance, but full prompt flow tested here).\n- [ ] Test: clean buffer + external write → silent reload. Dirty buffer + external write → prompt fires. Reload action → buffer text matches disk. Keep action → buffer text unchanged.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/DocumentRegistry.gd` (watcher integration)\n- New small UI component for the prompt (under `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/` or wherever such prompts live)\n- Tests under documents test directory\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Diff view / three-way merge — explicitly excluded by DCR.\n- Auto-reload preference toggle — not in this DCR.\n- Conflict semantics for non-DSL plugin types (.minpcb etc.) — single-UI per DCR.\n- Crash-recovery autosave.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:10:57","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:24:43","priority":2,"tags":["conflict-resolution","task","watcher"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfa68ca3175829454b60bb0024460","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 8: Cleanup — delete deprecated legacy tools","description":"Final cleanup pass. Lands one release after Tasks 1–7. Pure removal — no new functionality.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] `minerva_file_read`, `minerva_file_write`, `minerva_file_edit` removed.\n- [ ] `minerva_get_editor_content`, `minerva_update_editor`, `minerva_save_editor` removed.\n- [ ] `minerva_cad_get_document_source` removed.\n- [ ] No references remain in src/ or in any plugin.\n- [ ] CHANGELOG / release notes updated listing removed tools and their `minerva_doc_*` replacements.\n- [ ] CI green.\n\n## File scope\n\n- Removal of MCP tool registrations and their handler functions\n- CHANGELOG / release notes\n- Any docs referencing the old tool names\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Anything not on the removal list above.\n- Adding new tools.\n- Fixing bugs found during cleanup — file as separate items.\n\n## Sequencing note\n\nThis task must NOT land in the same release as Tasks 1–7. Hold for the release after to give external consumers (agents, scripts, CI integrations) time to migrate.","created_at":"2026-05-05T23:11:04","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:46:25","priority":3,"tags":["cleanup","removal","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfbb8c1af75da87e230946a29bb9d","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"SceneTree --script tests hang when preloaded script's BODY references an autoload identifier","created_at":"2026-05-06T05:18:02","updated_at":"2026-05-06T22:22:54","tags":["autoload","godot","gotcha","headless","preload","testing"],"value":"In `extends SceneTree` headless tests (`godot --headless --path src --script test/X.gd`), Godot does NOT resolve autoload identifiers (e.g. `SingletonObject`) at parse time. Body-level vs cascade matters:\n\n- **TOLERABLE**: a preloaded script whose own body is clean but whose dependency chain has unresolved autoload identifiers. Cascade compile error fires (visible as 'Failed to compile depended scripts'), but `.new()` on the preloaded class still works. Example: test_mcp_cad_tools.gd preloads MCPCadTools (body clean) which depends on MCPToolUtils (body has 11 SingletonObject refs). Test runs 108/0 despite the error log.\n\n- **TOXIC**: a preloaded script whose own BODY references an autoload identifier (e.g. `SingletonObject.mcp_tool_executed.emit(...)`). The `.reload()` parse fails at the unresolved identifier; `.new()` errors with 'Nonexistent function new in base GDScript'; if the test calls a method on the null/broken instance, GDScript halts execution mid-_init without quitting; SceneTree spins idle. Hang with NO stderr.\n\nDistinguishing in logs:\n- Tolerable: 'Compile Error: Failed to compile depended scripts.' (cascade)\n- Toxic: 'Compile Error: Identifier not found: <Autoload>' with the script's own path + specific line number\n\nFix when toxic:\n- Refactor the preloaded script's body to runtime-lookup the autoload: `var so = (Engine.get_main_loop() as SceneTree).root.get_node_or_null(\\\"SingletonObject\\\")`. Then `if so: so.signal.emit(...)`. The string node name doesn't trigger parser identifier resolution. Apply only at body-level call sites; leave MCPToolUtils etc. alone.\n- DON'T add `class_name SingletonObject` — Godot rejects it with 'hides an autoload singleton'.\n\nDiagnosed during Task 3 of DCR 019dfa66.","component":"godot-headless-tests","key":"preload-autoload-identifier-hangs","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfdfac7d97f49971385e466be3805","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"SceneTree script tests can preload modules with SingletonObject refs","created_at":"2026-05-06T15:49:24","updated_at":"2026-05-06T22:22:55","tags":["autoload","godot","headless","scenetree","testing"],"value":"SceneTree --script tests CAN reach modules that reference SingletonObject indirectly. test_mcp_cad_tools.gd preloads MCPCadTools which transitively loads MCPToolUtils (11 SingletonObject refs) and runs successfully. The hang in test_legacy_buffer_canonical.gd is more subtle than \"autoload identifier not found at parse time\" — Godot lazy-resolves bare autoload identifiers when the function is actually called, so a module fails only if a touched code path emits/reads SingletonObject. Diagnose by stripping the test to its minimum and bisecting which call path triggers the hang, NOT by assuming all SingletonObject refs are toxic.","component":"godot-headless-tests","key":"scenetree-script-autoload-resolution","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfe09bcc47fc985490c239486fb8e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"MCPToolModule.handle() is a coroutine — tests must await every call","created_at":"2026-05-06T16:05:44","updated_at":"2026-05-06T16:05:44","tags":["await","coroutine","gdscript","godot","gotcha","mcp","testing"],"value":"`MCPToolModule.handle(tool_name, args)` is declared as a regular function but its subclasses can use `await` internally (e.g. `MCPCodeTools._codetools_bash` awaits `term.execute_command(...)`). Once any `match` branch awaits, GDScript classifies the whole `handle()` as a coroutine. Headless tests that do `var r = tools.handle(\"minerva_file_read\", ...)` fail at parse time with: 'Function \"handle()\" is a coroutine, so it must be called with \"await\".' The script never loads.\n\nFix in tests: `var r = await tools.handle(...)` for every call, even for tools that don't actually await internally (e.g. minerva_file_read). The coroutine classification is per-method-signature, not per-branch.\n\nDiscovered Task 3 of DCR 019dfa66 while writing test_legacy_buffer_canonical.gd against MCPCodeTools.handle().","component":"mcp-tools","key":"handle-is-coroutine-in-mcp-modules","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfe1b482a78f2bdf6c8eda80e579d","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Make MCPEditorTools shims headless-testable (extend body-level autoload-lookup pattern)","description":"Follow-up surfaced during Task 3 of DCR `019dfa66` (buffer-canonical document model).\n\n## Problem\n\nThe Task 3 atomic flip rewires three editor-shim handlers to delegate through `DocumentRegistry`:\n\n- `MCPEditorTools._get_editor_content`\n- `MCPEditorTools._update_editor`\n- `MCPEditorTools._save_editor`\n\nThese shims have non-trivial dual paths (registry write + `editor.code_edit.text` mirror; `_save_editor` has a fallback to `editor.save()` when the registry can't allocate a buffer; `_update_editor` re-emits `text_changed` after setting `text =`). A regression here — e.g. someone removes the registry write and only mirrors to `code_edit.text` — would silently bypass buffer-canonical behavior. There is currently NO automated regression test for these three shims; we ship Task 3 relying on PR-time review only.\n\n## Why we can't test them headlessly today\n\n`MCPEditorTools.gd` body has six bare `SingletonObject.X` references (lines 334, 370, 521, 548, 604, 676 — `editor_pane` and `max_image_iterations`). In `extends SceneTree --script` headless tests, Godot's parser does not resolve autoload identifiers at parse time; body-level references make the script uninstantiable via preload (see hint `godot-headless-tests/preload-autoload-identifier-hangs`). Tests that try to preload `MCPEditorTools` and call `.new(null)` get a broken handle that hangs mid-`_init`.\n\nWe solved this for `MCPCodeTools` in Task 3 by introducing a `_emit_tool_executed()` static helper that runtime-looks-up the autoload via `Engine.get_main_loop().root.get_node_or_null(\\\"SingletonObject\\\")`. The same pattern applies here.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope:\n- Replace the six body-level `SingletonObject.X` references in `MCPEditorTools.gd` with a runtime lookup (helper or per-call). Production behavior unchanged: autoload always present at runtime; helper short-circuits when SceneTree/SingletonObject isn't available.\n- Add `src/test/test_legacy_editor_shims_buffer_canonical.gd` mirroring `test_legacy_buffer_canonical.gd` pattern: instantiate `MCPEditorTools.new(null)`, exercise `_get_editor_content` / `_update_editor` / `_save_editor` directly, assert disk-canonical behavior for each shim path including:\n  - editor with `file_path` set: read goes through registry; update goes through registry + mirrors `code_edit.text`; save flushes registry buffer.\n  - editor with no `file_path`: read falls back to `code_edit.text`; save returns error or invokes editor.save() per current behavior.\n  - the dual-path branch in `_save_editor` (registry success vs `editor.save()` fallback).\n\nOut of scope:\n- Refactoring `MCPToolUtils` (cascade-only refs are tolerable per the headless-test hint).\n- Refactoring `MCPCadTools._cad_get_document_source` for testability — its body is already clean; testing it requires a real `AnnotationHost` (integration territory).\n- Any change to the editor itself (that's Task 4 of the DCR).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `MCPEditorTools.gd` instantiates cleanly under `godot --headless --script test/X.gd`.\n- New test suite `test_legacy_editor_shims_buffer_canonical.gd` passes with cases for each of the three shims.\n- Existing tests still pass (especially anything that exercises MCPEditorTools through the running app).\n- Relevant docket hints referenced from the test file's docstring.\n\n## Why deferred from Task 3\n\nTask 3's DCR scope is the legacy `minerva_file_*` tools (MCPCodeTools). The editor shims are deprecated under the same DCR but their headless-testability is independent infrastructure work; bundling it would have inflated the gating PR. This chore makes the third shim group testable without a separate scene-tree harness.\n\n## References\n\n- DCR: `minerva:019dfa66d558761ea167314f1b1ab07e`\n- Task 3: `minerva:019dfa67ee6d7747a9ca835e0e203796`\n- Pattern reference: `MCPCodeTools.gd._emit_tool_executed()` (added Task 3) and `test_legacy_buffer_canonical.gd`.\n- Hint: `godot-headless-tests/preload-autoload-identifier-hangs`\n- Hint: `mcp-tools/handle-is-coroutine-in-mcp-modules`","created_at":"2026-05-06T16:24:54","updated_at":"2026-05-06T16:24:54","priority":3,"tags":["editor-shims","follow-up","headless","mcp","test-hygiene","testing"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfe2c13597e31abee96edcaa4168e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"work_item state chain has no shortcut from backlog to in_progress","created_at":"2026-05-06T16:43:14","updated_at":"2026-05-06T22:19:21","tags":["docket","gotcha","state-machine","workflow"],"value":"work_item state chain: `backlog → open → in_progress → blocked → done`. Backlog → in_progress is rejected with 'No valid path exists'. Pick up a backlog task with two transitions: first to `open`, then to `in_progress`. (Confirmed via state-machine reply 2026-05-06 picking up Task 4 of DCR 019dfa66.)","component":"docket","key":"work-item-state-chain","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfea5c3d678d18ecfe77f7c8b9585","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Two parallel \"unsaved\" dialogs in Minerva — pick the right one for the close path","created_at":"2026-05-06T18:56:09","updated_at":"2026-05-06T18:56:09","tags":["dialog","editor","minerva-ui","unsaved-editors","ux"],"value":"Minerva has TWO `ConfirmationDialog` instances for the unsaved-editors close UX. They are parallel paths and have always been — neither is a regression of the other.\n\n1. **`CloseDialog`** in `src/Scenes/Editor.tscn` — per-tab close (X button on a single editor tab). Triggered via `EditorPane._on_close_tab` → `Editor.prompt_close()` → `$CloseDialog.popup_centered`. Buttons: Save / Cancel / Close. Title/dialog_text were minimal until 2026-05-06 when fixed in Task 4 of DCR `019dfa66` to mirror the project-quit dialog wording.\n\n2. **`%ExitConfirmationDialog`** in `src/Scripts/ProjectMenuActions.tscn` — project-level save/quit. Triggered via `ProjectMenuActions.save_editor_panes()` when project state is dirty OR there are unsaved editors. Has a real layout: title `\"Are you sure?\"`, body `\"Do you want to save the project?\"`, ItemList of unsaved editor tabs, Save / Exit / Cancel buttons.\n\nIf you encounter a \"this dialog should look richer\" complaint:\n- Per-tab close → `Editor.tscn:CloseDialog`. Edit title / dialog_text / `add_button(...)` in `Editor.gd:534`.\n- Project-quit / save-all → `ProjectMenuActions.tscn:ExitConfirmationDialog`. Edit there.\n\nDon't confuse them; \"I remembered a better dialog\" usually means the user has been seeing the project-quit one, not the per-tab one.","component":"minerva-ui","key":"two-unsaved-editor-dialogs","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfea5fcee759f85ef8b63ecaba725","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"UI panel ↔ DocumentRegistry attach pattern (Editor.gd reference, Task 6 substrate)","created_at":"2026-05-06T18:56:24","updated_at":"2026-05-06T18:56:24","tags":["buffer-canonical","dcr-019dfa66","document-registry","task-6-prep","wiring-pattern"],"value":"When a UI panel mirrors a DocumentBuffer (Task 4 wired Editor.gd, Task 6 will wire CADPanel + future paired_dsl plugins), follow this contract:\n\n**Attach (on open):**\n```gdscript\nvar r := DocumentRegistry.get_instance().get_or_create_buffer(path)\nif r.ok:\n    _document_buffer = r.buffer\n    _document_buffer.attach()  # bumps refcount\n    _document_buffer.text_changed.connect(_on_buffer_text_changed)\n    code_edit.text = _document_buffer.text  # initial pull\n```\n\n**User edit → buffer (push):**\n```gdscript\nfunc _on_editor_changed():\n    if _document_buffer != null and not _applying_buffer_text:\n        _document_buffer.apply_edit(code_edit.text)\n```\n\n**Buffer change → editor (pull, with reentrancy guard):**\n```gdscript\nfunc _on_buffer_text_changed(new_text, _new_version):\n    if code_edit.text == new_text: return  # already in sync (e.g. our own push echoed)\n    _applying_buffer_text = true\n    code_edit.text = new_text\n    _applying_buffer_text = false\n```\n\n**Detach (on close, refcount-based dispose):**\n```gdscript\nfunc _exit_tree():\n    if _document_buffer == null: return\n    _document_buffer.text_changed.disconnect(_on_buffer_text_changed)\n    var remaining := _document_buffer.detach()\n    if remaining == 0:\n        DocumentRegistry.get_instance().dispose_buffer(_document_buffer.file_path)\n    _document_buffer = null\n```\n\n**Reentrancy guard rationale:** code_edit.text = X fires text_changed; without the guard, that fires _on_editor_changed which calls apply_edit, which (would) fire buffer.text_changed again — infinite ping-pong. apply_edit's no-op-on-equal check stops the loop but extra text_changed fires bump annotation revision unnecessarily. The flag tightly brackets the assignment.\n\n**Save As (path change):** when save_file_to_disc(new_path) and new_path != _document_buffer.file_path, detach from current buffer and re-attach to a buffer for new_path before save_to_disk().\n\nReference: `src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Editor.gd` `_attach_document_buffer` / `_on_buffer_text_changed` / `_detach_document_buffer` (committed in Task 4 of DCR 019dfa66).","component":"document-registry","key":"editor-buffer-attach-pattern","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfea6418a7e6e98546848a14f59be","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Remove migration mirror in MCPEditorTools after Task 4 wired Editor.gd to registry","description":"Follow-up to Task 4 of DCR `019dfa66` (buffer-canonical document model).\n\n## Background\n\nTask 3 added a temporary \"migration mirror\" in `MCPEditorTools.gd` to keep the visible editor pane in sync with MCP edits while `Editor.gd` was not yet attached to `DocumentRegistry`:\n\n- `_update_editor` writes through the registry buffer AND sets `editor.code_edit.text = content` directly (line ~462-464).\n- `_save_editor` mirrors `editor.code_edit.text` into the buffer before flushing via `buf.save_to_disk()` (line ~498-501).\n\nTask 4 (commit landing 2026-05-06) wired `Editor.gd` to the registry:\n- `_load_text_file` calls `_attach_document_buffer`.\n- `_on_editor_changed` pushes `code_edit.text` into the buffer.\n- `_on_buffer_text_changed` pulls buffer text back into `code_edit`.\n\nAfter Task 4, the migration mirror is **redundant**: writing to the buffer alone now propagates to the visible pane via the buffer's `text_changed` signal. HITL on 2026-05-06 confirmed the editor pane updates from `minerva_file_write` in real time, no `update_editor` call needed.\n\n## Scope\n\nRemove the redundant mirror lines:\n\n- `MCPEditorTools._update_editor`: delete the `editor.code_edit.text = content` assignment and the explicit `editor.code_edit.text_changed.emit()` afterward. The buffer's `text_changed` signal handles propagation.\n- `MCPEditorTools._save_editor`: delete the `(br.buffer as DocumentBuffer).apply_edit(editor.code_edit.text)` mirror; the buffer is already in sync because `Editor.gd` pushes every keystroke. Just call `buf.save_to_disk()` directly.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `MCPEditorTools._update_editor` no longer writes to `editor.code_edit.text`.\n- `MCPEditorTools._save_editor` no longer mirrors `code_edit.text` into the buffer.\n- HITL: same MCP edit flow as Task 4 HITL (write → editor pane updates; doc_save → disk updates) still passes.\n- Existing tests still green.\n\n## Why deferred\n\nTask 4 was kept narrow to the `Editor.gd` wiring + DocumentBuffer refcount. Mirror removal is a small follow-up cleanup, not part of Task 4's DCR DoD. Confirms the wiring is solid before pulling out the safety net.\n\n## References\n\n- DCR `minerva:019dfa66d558761ea167314f1b1ab07e`\n- Task 3 (migration mirror added) `minerva:019dfa67ee6d7747a9ca835e0e203796`\n- Task 4 (Editor.gd ↔ registry) `minerva:019dfa681740744eaccd86208f7482eb`\n- Hint `document-registry/editor-buffer-attach-pattern`","created_at":"2026-05-06T18:56:41","updated_at":"2026-05-06T19:01:19","priority":3,"tags":["cleanup","dcr-019dfa66","editor-shims","follow-up","mcp"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dfea6c6ea7f79b6c74e5a2f522667","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"work_item type rejects 'resolution' field — use comment or transition note","created_at":"2026-05-06T18:57:16","updated_at":"2026-05-06T22:19:19","tags":["docket","fields","gotcha","schema","work-item"],"value":"Calling `docket_update` with `resolution=...` on a `type=work_item` returns: `Field 'resolution' not valid for type 'work_item'`. The `resolution` field is valid for `dcr`, `bug`, `rca`, `discussion` but NOT for `work_item` (or `chore`).\n\nFor work_item / chore status closure notes, use one of:\n- `docket_comment` to attach a free-form comment\n- `docket_transition` with the `note` parameter (captured as transition event)\n- `docket_update` on `description` (replaces the body — preserve original DoD if doing this)\n\nDiscovered 2026-05-06 trying to set a resolution on Task 4 of DCR 019dfa66.","component":"docket","key":"work-item-no-resolution-field","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dff374bb37f9ea21340b1668a60a5","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin reload re-spawns binary but does NOT re-parse manifest.json","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:35:07","updated_at":"2026-05-06T21:35:07","tags":["cache","gotcha","manifest","plugins","reload"],"value":"Minerva caches the parsed manifest in `~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/plugins.json` at install time. `minerva_plugin_reload` (and the UI Reload button) only re-spawns the plugin binary — it does NOT re-read manifest.json. After editing manifest.json (adding render_mode, ipc_channels, etc.), you must REINSTALL the plugin via `minerva_plugin_install <abs path to manifest.json>` for the new fields to land in plugins.json. Symptom: dispatcher branches that read `panel_info.get(\"render_mode\", \"single\")` see the default `\"single\"` even though the manifest has `\"paired_dsl\"`. Verify with `python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/plugins.json')); ...\"`.","component":"minerva-plugins","key":"manifest-cache-stale-after-reload","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dff5d27e476afafd227218cb3891a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 7.5: host.fs.* broker channels — plugin-side file watcher access","description":"Expose the T7 `FileWatcherService` to plugins via platform-reserved broker channels, following the same shape as `attach_buffer` / `text_changed` / `detach_buffer` (T5 substrate) and the planned `host.viewports.capture` follow-up.\n\nPlugins are the major route of new functionality going forward; file-change notification is a generic capability they will need (e.g., a CAD plugin watching an external library include path, a notes plugin watching a sync directory). Building this once at the platform level avoids each plugin reinventing it.\n\n## Definition of done (binary)\n\n- [ ] `PluginScenePanelBroker` recognizes three new platform-reserved channels:\n  - `host.fs.watch {path}` — plugin → host. Subscribes the calling panel to changes on `path`.\n  - `host.fs.unwatch {path}` — plugin → host. Removes the subscription.\n  - `host.fs.changed {path, mtime, size}` — host → plugin. Pushed via `panel_root.receive()` (bypasses `ipc_channels` allowlist, same as attach_buffer/text_changed/detach_buffer).\n- [ ] Owner_id is `plugin_id + \":\" + panel_name` so plugin reload / panel teardown auto-unwatches via existing detach lifecycle.\n- [ ] When a panel detaches (existing detach hook), the broker auto-calls `FileWatcherService.unwatch_all(owner_id)` so plugins don't leak watches.\n- [ ] Watch authorization: paths must resolve through `PathResolver` (no `res://`, no `user://` escapes); failed resolution returns an error response instead of silently dropping.\n- [ ] Headless test: stub plugin under `src/plugins/test_paired_dsl/` (or new `test_fs_watch/`) registers via `host.fs.watch`, an external `DiskAccess.write` to the watched path triggers `host.fs.changed` on the panel within one polling tick.\n- [ ] Test: panel detach removes the watch; subsequent disk write does NOT push `host.fs.changed`.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginScenePanelBroker.gd` (new platform-reserved channel handling)\n- `src/Scripts/Services/Watcher/FileWatcherService.gd` (only if T7 didn't already expose `unwatch_all(owner_id)`)\n- Stub plugin updates for the test fixture\n- Test under plugin-broker test directory\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Watching directories (paths only).\n- Glob patterns (paths only).\n- Persisting subscriptions across plugin restart (panel re-subscribes on `_ready`).\n- Kernel-backed (inotify/FSEvents) watchers — polling per T7.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nLands in the same release as T7. T7 must complete first (this task depends on `FileWatcherService` existing).\n\n## Reference\n\nSee DCR `019df8e2d093` (host_capabilities) — same channel-shape pattern as the `host.viewports.capture` sub-spec filed there as comment 327. This task implements the second host_capability under that umbrella.","created_at":"2026-05-06T22:16:28","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:24:57","priority":2,"tags":["broker","host_capabilities","plugin","task","watcher"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dff63a03173babd9feadb0af31093","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Newly-added `class_name` files self-fail in --script mode until Godot re-imports","created_at":"2026-05-06T22:23:32","updated_at":"2026-05-06T22:23:32","tags":["class_name","godot","gotcha","headless","import-cache","preload"],"value":"When you add a NEW `.gd` file with `class_name X extends Y` and X is referenced inside that same file (e.g. `static var _instance: X = null` or `static func get_instance() -> X:`), `godot --headless --script ...` reports:\n\n```\nCompile Error: Identifier not found: X\n   at: GDScript::reload (res://path/to/X.gd:LINE)\n```\n\n…even though existing files (DocumentRegistry, DocumentBuffer) use the identical self-reference pattern successfully.\n\n**Root cause:** Godot only registers class_name → path mappings during the editor's import scan. The `.godot/global_script_class_cache.cfg` (or equivalent) is stale for newly-created files. Existing files work because they were imported on a prior editor launch.\n\n**Workarounds (any one):**\n1. Open the project in the Godot editor once to trigger the import (writes the class_name cache).\n2. Run `godot --headless --import` or `godot --headless --editor --quit` from project dir before running tests.\n3. Avoid self-reference: drop `-> X` and `: X` annotations on static funcs/vars inside the script that defines X. Tests + callers can still preload the path and use the methods at runtime; the script just can't type-annotate itself.\n\nDiscovered 2026-05-06 building FileWatcherService for buffer-canonical T7 (DCR 019dfa66).","component":"godot-headless-tests","key":"new-class-name-needs-import-pass","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dff96aa077cdda1911a95796da370","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Task 7.6: CAD plugin adopts host.fs.* for external-file watching","description":"First real-plugin adoption of the host.fs.* broker capability built in T7.5. The CAD plugin re-evaluates the model whenever an external file the .mcad DSL depends on (include / library / sidecar — TBD via pre-flight) changes on disk, the same way the buffer-canonical pipeline already handles the .mcad file itself.\n\nPre-flight (do BEFORE refining DoD): inspect `~/github/plugins/cad/` (Go source + ui/CADPanel.gd) to determine the actual external-file dependency vector. Candidates:\n  - `include \"...\"` directives in .mcad DSL (parsed Go-side at evaluate)\n  - Sidecar config (.mcad.cfg or similar)\n  - Material / texture libraries referenced by name\n  - None of the above (in which case file a clarifying note and revise scope)\n\n## Definition of done (binary, tentative — refine after pre-flight)\n\n- [ ] CAD plugin's UI panel (`CADPanel.gd`) calls `host.fs.watch` for each external-file dependency surfaced by the most-recent successful evaluate.\n- [ ] On `host.fs.changed` for any watched dep, the panel triggers a re-evaluate (same path as the existing 250ms-debounce text-changed flow).\n- [ ] When the dep set changes (text edit removes an include, etc.), the now-unused watches are released via `host.fs.unwatch`.\n- [ ] On `detach_buffer`, all CAD-owned watches are released. (Auto-unwatch from the broker should already cover this; verify by inspection.)\n- [ ] HITL: open `t_beam.mcad` (or any include-bearing .mcad) in Minerva. Externally edit a watched dep file. Within one poll tick the CAD render panel re-evaluates and reflects the change. Confirm via the CAD panel's existing render output, not a stub label.\n\n## File scope\n\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd` (host.fs.watch wiring)\n- Possibly `~/github/plugins/cad/main.go` (surface the dep set in evaluate response if not already exposed)\n- HITL fixture creation under `~/temp/` or `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/cad/examples/`\n\n## Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Any change to the broker / FileWatcherService (T7.5 substrate is fixed).\n- PathResolver sandbox (filed as known gap on T7.5 — see comment 329).\n- Cross-plugin file-watch coordination (e.g., two plugins watching same path).\n\n## Sequencing\n\nLands in the same release as T7 / T7.5, BEFORE the buffer-canonical DCR (`019dfa66`) closes. Required to demonstrate the platform-pattern goal: at least one real plugin uses host.fs.*. Cleanup task T8 still defers to the next release.\n\n## Reference\n\n- T7.5 broker substrate: `019dff5d27e4`\n- host_capabilities DCR umbrella: `019df8e2d093`","created_at":"2026-05-06T23:19:17","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:25:08","priority":2,"tags":["cad","host_capabilities","plugin-adoption","task","watcher"],"parent":"minerva:019dfa6727fc76a9af624d655be9983e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Plugin-shipped skills implementation","description":"## Scope\n\nImplement DCR `019df57b` per locked design decisions:\n\n- **D1** Storage: user.dct with `source=plugin:<plugin_id>` field.\n- **D2** Fork model: single record + `customised: bool` + `pristine_hash`.\n- **D3** Update semantics: hybrid — silent for pristine, prompt-with-diff for customised, mark `deprecated` for removed-upstream.\n- **D4** Cross-plugin `tool_deps`: install with `unsatisfied_deps` state; picker hides until resolved.\n- **D5** Uninstall: pristine auto-delete, customised auto-convert to `source=user` with single toast.\n- **D6** Skill ID prefix: `minerva_<plugin_id>_<skill_id>` (mirrors plugin tool prefix rule).\n\n## Task graph\n\nT1 (manifest schema) and T2 (record schema) are independent foundations.\nT3 (install) depends on T1 + T2.\nT4 (update) depends on T2 + T3.\nT5 (picker) depends on T2.\nT6 (uninstall) depends on T2 + T3.\nT7 (reactivity) depends on T2 + T3 + T6.\nT8 (tests + example plugin) depends on T1–T7.\nT9 (acceptance + ship) depends on T8.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Editing skills in-place from the picker UI (existing docket UI flow handles this).\n- Versioning/rollback of pristine_hash beyond the latest known upstream.\n- Per-user share/sync of forked skills.","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:06:39","updated_at":"2026-05-07T00:06:39","tags":["dcr-019df57b","plan","plugin-system","skills"],"parent":"minerva:019df57b0b1c7132ae400e7ac867abc3"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc2382d704ebe61a5663f1745c5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1: Manifest schema — accept skills[] array with prefix + dep validation","description":"## DoD\n\n- `PluginDefinition.gd` accepts optional `skills: []` array in manifest.\n- Per-skill validation: required fields (id, title, summary, system_prompt, outcome, preconditions, steps, tool_deps, target), optional `optimization`.\n- ID rule: every `skill.id` must match `^minerva_<plugin_id>_[a-z0-9_]+$` (mirrors tool prefix rule at PluginDefinition.gd:322-329).\n- Reject duplicate `skill.id` within the same manifest with `manifest_duplicate_skill_id`.\n- Reject `tool_deps` that aren't strings or are empty.\n- Unit tests covering: valid manifest, missing required field, bad prefix, duplicate id, malformed tool_deps.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- tool_deps resolution against installed registry (T3 owns that).\n- Default values for optimization (T3 owns at install time).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:06:51","updated_at":"2026-05-07T00:18:11","tags":["dcr-019df57b","manifest","task","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"Implemented in commit (forthcoming). PluginDefinition.gd: added `skills: Array[Dictionary]` field, `REQUIRED_SKILL_FIELDS` constant (9 fields), parse-time collection in `_from_dict_internal`, validation in `validate()` (required-field presence, prefix `^minerva_<id>_[a-z0-9_]+$`, duplicate detection, tool_deps shape), serialization in `to_dict()`. Test file `test_plugin_skills_manifest.gd`: 35 tests, 0 failures. Existing `test_plugin_definition.gd` regression check: 78/0. Hint `019dffcc6780` filed (godot-gdscript/typed-array-assignment-needs-coerce — silent no-op false-pass gotcha that bit me during test development).</resolution>\n</invoke>\n<invoke name=\"Bash\">\n<parameter name=\"command\">cd /home/imran/github/Minerva && git status -s && echo \"---\" && git diff --stat HEAD"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc25d0a7a1a9ad788e65c25bfd4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2: Skill record schema — add source / customised / pristine_hash / unsatisfied_deps / deprecated","description":"## DoD\n\n- Skill record gets new optional fields:\n  - `source: str` (default `\"user\"`; values: `\"user\"`, `\"master\"`, `\"plugin:<plugin_id>\"`).\n  - `customised: bool` (default `false`).\n  - `pristine_hash: str` (sha256 of canonical JSON of original manifest entry; empty for source=user).\n  - `pristine_content: dict` (original manifest entry, used for diffing on update).\n  - `unsatisfied_deps: [str]` (default `[]`).\n  - `deprecated: bool` (default `false`).\n- Hash helper `compute_skill_hash(entry: Dictionary) -> String` — canonical JSON (sorted keys), strips runtime-only fields.\n- Migration: existing skill records load with default `source=\"user\"`, `customised=false`. No data loss.\n- Unit tests: hash determinism (same input → same hash), hash changes when content changes.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Picker UI badges (T5).\n- Update reconciliation logic (T4).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:01","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:05:26","tags":["dcr-019df57b","docket","schema","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"Implemented `PluginSkillRecord.gd` (RefCounted helper class) at src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/. Static methods: `compute_hash(entry)` — whitelist-based canonical-JSON SHA-256 hash that's stable across manifest entries and full docket records (T4 reconciliation depends on this equivalence). Source classifiers: `is_plugin_seeded`, `get_plugin_id`, `normalize_source` (empty→\"user\" backward-compat). Predicates: `has_unsatisfied_deps`, `is_customised`, `is_deprecated`. Constants: SOURCE_USER / SOURCE_MASTER / SOURCE_PLUGIN_PREFIX, SKILL_CONTENT_FIELDS (whitelist).\n\nTest: src/test/test_plugin_skill_record.gd — 36/0 covering hash determinism, key-order independence, content-change detection, manifest-vs-record equivalence, runtime-field exclusion, nested-dict handling, array-order sensitivity for tool_deps, edge cases (empty/missing). Source classification + predicate variants.\n\nMigration coverage: existing pre-DCR records load with empty source; normalize_source maps that to \"user\" so consumers don't have to handle backward compat. The docket-side ALTER TABLE migration (chore docket:019e0020200e) provides the column defaults.\n\nOut of T2 scope per DoD: install/update/uninstall flow code (lives in T3/T4/T6), record-creation builder (deferred to T3 since it needs to integrate with plugin install dialog).</resolution>\n</invoke>\n<invoke name=\"Bash\">\n<parameter name=\"command\">git -C /home/imran/github/Minerva add src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginSkillRecord.gd src/test/test_plugin_skill_record.gd Docs/minerva.dct && git -C /home/imran/github/Minerva commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nT2 (DCR 019df57b): PluginSkillRecord helper — hash + source classifiers\n\nsrc/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginSkillRecord.gd\n- compute_hash(entry) — whitelist-based canonical-JSON SHA-256 over the 10\n  content fields (id/title/summary/system_prompt/outcome/preconditions/steps/\n  tool_deps/target/optimization).  Manifest entries and full docket records\n  hash to the same value when content matches; that equivalence is what T4\n  reconciliation depends on.\n- is_plugin_seeded(record), get_plugin_id(record), normalize_source(record) —\n  source-field classifiers.  Empty source maps to \"user\" so consumers don't\n  have to handle the pre-DCR backward-compat case at every read site.\n- has_unsatisfied_deps / is_customised / is_deprecated — predicate helpers\n  for T5 picker filters and T4/T6 branching.\n- Constants: SOURCE_USER / SOURCE_MASTER / SOURCE_PLUGIN_PREFIX,\n  SKILL_CONTENT_FIELDS (whitelist).\n\nsrc/test/test_plugin_skill_record.gd — 36/0\n- compute_hash: determinism, key-order independence, content-change detection,\n  manifest-vs-record equivalence, runtime-field exclusion, nested-dict\n  handling, array-order sensitivity, edge cases (empty/missing), 64-char hex\n  format.\n- Source classifiers and predicates.\n\nOut of T2 scope per DoD: install/update/uninstall flows (T3/T4/T6), record-\ncreation builder (T3 — couples to install dialog).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc2756978c8bf07b7d1ea44a908","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T3: Plugin install — seed skills with confirmation dialog","description":"## DoD\n\n- Plugin install pipeline reads `manifest.skills[]` after manifest validation (T1).\n- For each skill, resolve `tool_deps` against the active tool registry; record any missing into `unsatisfied_deps`.\n- Build install dialog showing: skill title, summary, tool list, count of unsatisfied deps. Single batch confirmation.\n- On confirm, materialize records via `docket_create type=skill` with:\n  - `source = \"plugin:<plugin_id>\"`\n  - `customised = false`\n  - `pristine_hash = compute_skill_hash(manifest_entry)`\n  - `pristine_content = manifest_entry`\n  - `unsatisfied_deps = [...]`\n- Idempotency: if a record with same `id` and `source=plugin:<plugin_id>` already exists with matching `pristine_hash`, skip (re-install no-op).\n- If existing pristine record has different hash → defer to T4 update path (do not silently overwrite here).\n- Tests: fresh install creates N records, re-install of same version is no-op, install with one missing dep flags correctly.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Update reconciliation when version bumps (T4).\n- Picker filtering on unsatisfied_deps (T5).\n- Tool_deps reactivity when other plugins install/uninstall later (T7).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:07","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:17:45","tags":["dcr-019df57b","install","plugin-system","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"Implementation done across 2 codebases. Minerva commit 94112096; ccsandbox commit 3d3d0f7. New files: PluginSkillSeeder.gd (resolve_deps / build_install_record / find_existing_record / materialize), PluginSkillSeedDialog.gd (ConfirmationDialog subclass with seed_decision signal), test_plugin_skill_seeder.gd (43/0). Modified: PluginManager.install_plugin is now async with auto_confirm_skills parameter; awaits the dialog when not auto-confirmed. PluginMCPTools and PluginManagerPanel call sites updated to await. Hint `019e0038d6e3` filed (docket/optional-fields-is-per-type-whitelist) — caught at test time when find_existing_record returned empty because `key` wasn't in skill type's optional_fields whitelist; fix was a 2-line schema change in both dockets. Regression: 93/0 docket integration, 78/0 plugin definition, 36/0 skill record, 35/0 skills manifest, 500/500 ccsandbox. Dialog UX has not been visually verified — that's the HITL gate. T3 acceptance criteria (DCR #3 install dialog, #5 idempotency) are satisfied by code + headless tests; visual confirmation belongs to T8 round-trip walkthrough."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc29bcd7184b3cd09c414cba8c0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T4: Update reconciliation — silent for pristine, prompt for customised, deprecate removed","description":"## DoD\n\n- Reconciliation runs after install pipeline detects an existing plugin install with an older version.\n- For each manifest skill entry, find existing record by `id`:\n  - **Not found** → call into T3 path (new skill seeded).\n  - **Found, customised=false** (pristine): if `pristine_hash` differs from new manifest hash, silent overwrite of all fields; update `pristine_hash` + `pristine_content`.\n  - **Found, customised=true**: if `pristine_hash` differs, show diff dialog (old pristine vs new manifest entry); on accept → overwrite + update hashes, on decline → leave record but update `pristine_content` so user can compare later.\n- For each existing `source=plugin:<id>` record NOT in new manifest: set `deprecated=true`. Do not delete.\n- Per-skill \"stop asking about updates\" toggle — `suppress_update_prompt: bool` on the record. When true, skip the prompt and leave the record untouched on hash mismatch.\n- Tests: pristine bumps silently, customised prompts and respects accept/decline, removed-upstream marks deprecated, suppress-toggle suppresses prompt.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The actual diff UI widget — start with text diff; richer UI is a follow-up.\n- Auto-undeprecate when an upstream-removed skill returns in a later version (defer; rare).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:17","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:51:56","tags":["dcr-019df57b","reconciliation","task","update"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"T4 update reconciliation. Commit cbdf9f7a (Minerva). PluginSkillSeeder.plan_reconcile + apply_reconcile split phase-1 classification from phase-2 commit so the dialog await happens cleanly between. Action kinds: SEED / SILENT_UPDATE / PROMPT_REQUIRED / NO_CHANGE plus deprecate-list for upstream-removed records. Decline path refreshes pristine_content (so user can diff later) but leaves edits intact. PluginManager.update_plugin wraps it with PluginSkillUpdateDialog hosting + auto_confirm_updates parameter. Headless-tested via 11 reconcile tests in test_plugin_skill_seeder.gd. Diff dialog UX is HITL-gated (Docs/HITL/dcr_019df57b.md §3). Suppress-update-prompt toggle deferred — would be a per-record bool field; not blocking."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc2bee6700288dff821cba1ba9c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T5: Skill picker — source badge, hide unsatisfied, copy-on-edit flag","description":"## DoD\n\n- Skill picker reads `source` field; for `plugin:<id>` records, render a badge with the plugin display name.\n- Picker filters out records with `len(unsatisfied_deps) > 0` (still browsable in docket UI; just hidden from active picker).\n- Deprecated records: shown with strikethrough or \"Deprecated\" tag; not picked by default.\n- On any edit through the docket UI / `minerva_skill_update` MCP tool to a `source=plugin:<id>` record:\n  - Set `customised = true`.\n  - Leave `pristine_hash` + `pristine_content` untouched (T4 reads them).\n  - Source field stays `plugin:<id>` (NOT user — uninstall logic at T6 distinguishes).\n- Tests: picker hides unsatisfied, edit flips customised, deprecated styled correctly.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Diff UI surface — that's invoked from T4's reconciliation path, not the picker.\n- Re-pristining (\"revert to plugin version\") action — backlog follow-up.","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:26","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:52:04","tags":["dcr-019df57b","picker","task","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"T5 picker integration. Commit cbdf9f7a (Minerva). FocusedChatPopup._add_skill_from_item captures source / deprecated / unsatisfied_deps. _render_available_list filters records with non-empty unsatisfied_deps from active picker; renders [from <plugin_id>] and [deprecated] badges; tooltips explain plugin lineage and deprecation reason. PluginSkillRecord.apply_user_edit auto-flips customised=true on user edits to plugin-seeded records (unless caller explicitly sets customised, so seeder.apply_reconcile silent path still works). docket_skill_get lean view extended with unsatisfied_deps for picker filter (the bloat concern was about pristine_hash/content; unsatisfied_deps is a small Array). Headless coverage: 4 apply_user_edit tests. Picker visual is HITL-gated (Docs/HITL/dcr_019df57b.md §2)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc2d9c6720cb79290f8ad401ffb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T6: Plugin uninstall — auto-delete pristine, auto-convert customised with toast","description":"## DoD\n\n- On `plugin_remove`, scan all skills with `source = \"plugin:<plugin_id>\"`:\n  - `customised = false` → delete the record.\n  - `customised = true` → set `source = \"user\"`; clear `pristine_hash` + `pristine_content` (no longer reconcileable). Keep `unsatisfied_deps` for T7 to recompute.\n- Single toast: \"Removed N pristine skill(s) from <plugin>. Kept M customised skill(s).\" Click → opens skill picker filtered to those orphans.\n- No modal interruptions during uninstall.\n- Tests: 3 pristine + 2 customised → 3 deleted, 2 source-flipped, toast text correct, customised records still readable post-uninstall.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Reverting customised orphans on plugin re-install (would need stored pristine_hash; currently cleared). Backlog.","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:33","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:52:08","tags":["dcr-019df57b","plugin-system","task","uninstall"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"T6 plugin uninstall. Commit cbdf9f7a (Minerva). PluginSkillSeeder.unseed scans skills with source=plugin:<id>; pristine records hard-delete via docket_delete; customised records auto-convert to source=user with pristine_hash + pristine_content cleared (no longer reconcilable) but user edits preserved. PluginManager.remove_plugin invokes unseed before _db.remove. PluginManagerPanel surfaces \"Removed N pristine, kept M customised\" in the status line. Headless coverage: 6 unseed tests. Toast wording is HITL-gated (Docs/HITL/dcr_019df57b.md §4)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc2fca1743da6b17067926b37d1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T7: Cross-plugin tool_deps reactivity — recompute unsatisfied_deps on install/uninstall","description":"## DoD\n\n- Post-install hook (after T3 + T4): iterate every skill record (any source) with `unsatisfied_deps != []`. For each, recompute deps against current registry. Clear satisfied entries; if list becomes empty, skill becomes pickable again (T5 picker filter re-evaluates).\n- Post-uninstall hook (after T6): identify all tool names removed by uninstalled plugin (`minerva_<plugin_id>_*`). For each remaining skill (any source), if `tool_deps` references a removed tool, append to that skill's `unsatisfied_deps`.\n- Hooks fire after the install/uninstall transaction commits. Failures here log but don't roll back the install/uninstall.\n- Tests: install A → install B that depends on A's tool → B shows OK. Uninstall A → B's unsatisfied_deps populated. Re-install A → B's unsatisfied_deps cleared.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Notification/toast on these state flips — picker recomputation is silent. Add later if user reports surprise.","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:41","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:52:13","tags":["dcr-019df57b","dependencies","reactivity","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"T7 cross-plugin reactivity. Commit cbdf9f7a (Minerva). PluginSkillSeeder.recompute_unsatisfied walks every skill record after a lifecycle event and recomputes unsatisfied_deps against the current available_tools (only writes when the set changed — avoids spurious updated_at bumps). PluginManager._build_available_tools combines MCP-registered tools with all installed plugins' manifest-declared tools[]. install_plugin and remove_plugin both invoke _recompute_skill_reactivity after their main work commits. Headless coverage: 5 recompute tests + reactivity smoke in T8 round-trip."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc3230d73e7b45ee31c8f57607b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T8: Tests + example plugin — round-trip integration","description":"## DoD\n\n- Pick an existing plugin (presentation or test_paired_dsl) and add one `skills[]` entry to its manifest. \"Make a slide deck\" if presentation; otherwise a trivial echo skill on test_paired_dsl.\n- Headless test (`src/test/test_plugin_skill_seeding.gd`):\n  1. Install plugin → record materialized in user.dct with `source=plugin:<id>`, correct `pristine_hash`.\n  2. Edit one field via docket update → `customised=true`, source unchanged.\n  3. Bump plugin version with same skill content → no prompt (hash unchanged).\n  4. Bump plugin version with skill content changed → prompt path (in test, auto-accept) → fields update, hashes update, customised stays true.\n  5. Bump plugin version removing the skill → record marked `deprecated=true`.\n  6. Uninstall → customised record converts to `source=user` (deprecated stays true).\n- HITL script in `Docs/HITL/dcr_019df57b.md`: human walkthrough of the same round-trip via UI + dialog confirmations.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Cold-Opus review (T9 owns).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:51","updated_at":"2026-05-07T02:52:19","tags":["dcr-019df57b","integration","task","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0","resolution":"T8 round-trip integration test. Commit cbdf9f7a (Minerva). test/test_plugin_skill_seeding.gd walks the 6-phase lifecycle from DCR acceptance criterion #8: install v1 → user edit → identical v1 re-install (no_change) → v3 with content shift (decline path then accept path) → v4 with skill removed (deprecate, not delete) → uninstall (customised converts to source=user). Plus reactivity smoke as bonus. 36/0 tests in this file alone; covers PluginDefinition + PluginSkillRecord + PluginSkillSeeder.materialize + plan_reconcile + apply_reconcile + unseed + recompute_unsatisfied end-to-end. Visual UI surfaces (install dialog, picker badges, update prompt, uninstall toast) are HITL-gated in Docs/HITL/dcr_019df57b.md."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dffc33e3c752ca44b0704b537d54a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T9: Acceptance walk + Cold-Opus review + DCR ship","description":"## DoD\n\n- Tick every acceptance criterion in DCR 019df57b's description (1 through 8) with a pointer to its test or commit.\n- Cold-Opus review on the cumulative T1–T8 changeset; address SHOULD-FIX before HITL.\n- HITL: run the human walkthrough from T8.\n- Final docket transitions: designing → implementing → reviewing → shipped, with resolution note linking to commits + test output (suite count, total tests, 0 failures).\n- Update `MEMORY.md` \"Active Work\" entry to reflect shipped status.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Follow-up DCRs surfaced by the review (file as separate items, link with `surfaced`).","created_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:58","updated_at":"2026-05-07T00:07:58","tags":["dcr-019df57b","review","ship","task"],"parent":"minerva:019dffc209e675908d79ae0d1720cca0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0435792c74b4913a5e74b901ffa0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Unified mcad_export — STL/3MF/STEP via single MCP tool","description":"## Scope\n\nOne MCP tool `minerva_cad_export {editor_name, format, path}` that produces:\n- Binary STL (`format: \"stl\"`) — build123d default\n- 3MF (`format: \"3mf\"`) — build123d Mesher\n- STEP AP214 (`format: \"step\"` or `\"stp\"`) — OCCT default schema\n\nASCII STL deferred behind explicit flag if asked; not in v1.\n\n## Plumbing layers\n\n1. **Worker** (`worker/mcad_worker/methods.py:333` — currently stubbed)\n   - Implement `export` method: re-parse + evaluate source, then call existing `mcad/translator.py:export_shape()`.\n   - Params: `{source: str, format: \"stl\"|\"step\"|\"stp\"|\"3mf\", path: str}`.\n   - Resolve path with same `_resolve_export_path` policy (absolute as-is, relative → home).\n   - Return: `{ok: true, path: <resolved path>, bytes_written: <int>}`.\n\n2. **Go plugin** (cad-plugin)\n   - New IPC channel `cad.export` (or extend cad.evaluate with an `export` block).\n   - Forwards `{source, format, path}` to worker; returns the worker reply.\n\n3. **Minerva MCP layer**\n   - New tool `minerva_cad_export {editor_name, format, path}`.\n   - Resolves editor via existing `_find_cad_editor()` pattern, fetches buffer text via DocumentRegistry, dispatches `cad.export` IPC.\n\n## Standards picked\n- STL: binary (build123d default — `ascii_format=False`).\n- STEP: AP214 (OCCT default — `STEPControl_AsIs`/`ManifoldSolidBrep`). AP242 deferred.\n- 3MF: build123d `Mesher`.\n\n## Out of scope (file separately)\n- ANSI/ISO machine-shop PDF — needs dimensioning UX design.\n- Multi-shape export from one document.\n- ASCII STL.\n\n## Done when\n- All three formats can be produced from a CAD editor via the MCP tool.\n- Worker unit test covers each format.\n- minerva_cad_modeling skill prompt mentions the export tool.","created_at":"2026-05-07T20:51:13","updated_at":"2026-05-07T20:51:13","tags":["cad","export","mcp","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e04382186738ca1357e6ab34d18b7","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"cad-plugin tool name vs IPC channel vs worker method","created_at":"2026-05-07T20:54:08","updated_at":"2026-05-07T20:54:08","tags":["cad","ipc","naming","plugin"],"value":"In ~/github/plugins/cad: ToolSpec.Name == manifest ui.ipc_channels entry (e.g. \"cad.evaluate\"), but the WORKER method name is the bare verb without the \"cad.\" prefix (e.g. \"evaluate\" — see worker/mcad_worker/methods.py). HandleX calls w.Call(ctx, \"verb\", params). New tool checklist: (1) add ToolSpec + HandleX in internal/tools/cad_X.go, (2) register in internal/tools registry, (3) add \"cad.X\" to manifest.json ui.panels[].ipc_channels and ui.ipc_messages, (4) add bare \"X\" handler to worker methods.py.","component":"cad-plugin/go","key":"tool_naming_convention","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e043a6ecb785f86454b943f548f1b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"How Minerva host code dispatches to a plugin's MCP tool","created_at":"2026-05-07T20:56:38","updated_at":"2026-05-07T20:56:38","tags":["dispatch","mcp","minerva","plugins"],"value":"From any host-side GDScript: `SingletonObject.plugin_manager.get_connection(plugin_id).call_tool(plugin_tool_name, args)` returns the plugin's tool result Dictionary (await — async). For policy-checked dispatch, prefer `SingletonObject.plugin_tool_registry.handle_tool_call(tool_name, args)` which auto-resolves plugin_id from `_plugin_by_tool`, verifies running, logs to PluginAuditLog. Both wired in singleton_object.gd:496+ during _ready; safe to assume non-null after boot. Use this from MCPCadTools etc. when a host-prefixed tool needs to delegate to a plugin-namespaced tool (e.g. minerva_cad_export → cad.export).","component":"minerva/plugins","key":"host_dispatch_to_plugin_mcp","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e04747ee57a37966b726156c3397c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CADPanel.set_viewport_for wiring — register SubViewports with annotation host","description":"## Resolution: already shipped\n\nInvestigation 2026-05-07 found both substrate and wiring already in place:\n- Cad_AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CadAnnotationHost.gd:217-274` (frame_post_draw + per-view cache).\n- CADPanel._register_host_viewports: `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd:697-746` registers SubViewports + cameras per pane in both wide and narrow layouts; called from _ready (line 211) and on every width-class transition (line 689).\n\nThe 9-day-old memory `project_render_overlay_plugin_compositing.md` claiming plugins don't implement render_content_to_image was stale. Updated.\n\nReported symptom (\"LLM can't see CAD model\") is now attributed to:\n- LLM hasn't discovered `minerva_annotations_render_overlay` as a snapshot path (it's framed as an annotation-compositing tool).\n- Or cold-start returns null on the very first call.\n\nBoth addressed by docket child 019e0474d3c17dec (minerva_cad_snapshot — agent-discoverable snapshot tool).\n\nClosing as no-op.","created_at":"2026-05-07T22:00:04","updated_at":"2026-05-07T22:03:16","tags":["annotations","cad","substrate","vision"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"Already shipped — substrate (Cad_AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image) and wiring (CADPanel._register_host_viewports) both pre-existed. Stale memory updated."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0474ac6d7f8da0256613cceb7ddb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CADPanel._on_panel_inject_toggle_changed — wire chat injection","description":"## Problem\nThe Editor's \"inject to chat\" CheckButton fires `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_inject_toggle(panel_root, enabled)` which calls `panel_root._on_panel_inject_toggle_changed(enabled)` — but CADPanel.gd doesn't define that method, so the toggle is a silent no-op. User's words: \"the 'inject to chat' toggle doesn't seem to do anything from the CAD plugin\".\n\n## Scope\n- Implement `_on_panel_inject_toggle_changed(enabled: bool) -> void` on CADPanel (~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd).\n- When enabled: register/maintain a snapshot + summary that gets attached to outgoing chat turns. Snapshot uses the render_content_to_image hook from task A.\n- DSL source already flows through the paired text editor — don't re-inject text. Inject the *visual* delta plus a one-line geometry summary (vertex/face count + bbox in mm).\n- When disabled: stop attaching.\n\n## Open question\n- What's the existing chat-injection plumbing for non-plugin editors? Some editor pushes content into the active chat history when toggled. Trace that path before designing the cad-specific call site (probably a SingletonObject signal or chat-history method).\n\n## Depends on\n- Task A (render_content_to_image) — without it, the snapshot is blank.\n\n## Done when\n- Toggle on a CAD editor → next chat turn arrives with the model image + summary attached.\n- Toggle off → chat turns stop carrying the snapshot.\n- Toggle is per-editor (different CAD panels can opt in independently).","created_at":"2026-05-07T22:00:15","updated_at":"2026-05-07T22:15:19","tags":["cad","chat","inject","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"CADPanel inject hooks shipped: _on_panel_inject_toggle_changed tracks state and logs ack; _on_panel_create_note_request returns {kind: \"image\", title: \"<editor> · <view> · V/F/E\", image: <render_view_to_image>} so chat note carries CAD-specific viewport instead of platform fallback. Defensive: gates on _inject_enabled, tolerates Array | PackedArray for mesh fields."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0474d3c17dec8ee209c8983f959f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"minerva_cad_snapshot MCP tool — agent-pulled CAD viewport image","description":"## Scope\n- New tool `minerva_cad_snapshot {editor_name, view?: \"iso\"|\"top\"|\"front\"|\"right\"|\"active\"}` in MCPCadTools.\n- Default view = \"active\" (whichever pane currently has focus / is the only visible one in narrow layout).\n- Returns image as a base64 PNG plus metadata (width, height, view, vertex_count, bbox).\n- Reuses Cad_AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image from task A; for non-active views, the panel needs to be able to expose each SubViewport's image (may require a CADPanel.get_view_image(view_name) helper).\n\n## Considerations\n- LLMs that have vision will see the image inline. Image-less LLMs still get the metadata payload.\n- If the panel is collapsed / not yet evaluated, return success=false with a clear \"no_geometry\" message.\n- Don't add a tool budget surprise — image bytes can balloon. Cap to a reasonable size (e.g. 1024px on the long edge) and document it.\n\n## Depends on\n- Task A (render_content_to_image substrate hook).\n\n## Done when\n- LLM can call minerva_cad_snapshot view=\"iso\" and receive an image showing the current model.\n- Each of the four named views (iso/top/front/right) actually produces a different image when the panel is in 2×2 wide layout.","created_at":"2026-05-07T22:00:25","updated_at":"2026-05-07T22:15:14","tags":["cad","mcp","snapshot","vision"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f","resolution":"minerva_cad_snapshot shipped: view selector (iso/top/front/right/active), max_edge clamp default 1024, optional return_base64, default output under user://cad_snapshots/. Cold-Opus review applied: tilde expansion is targeted (~/ only) with HOME-empty guard, res:// rejected, filename sanitized, ms suffix in default path. Substrate added render_view_to_image + get_active_viewport."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e047c63747b2bb38d32eb631ea139","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"PLUGIN_SCENE inject-toggle architecture: two hooks + screenshot fallback","created_at":"2026-05-07T22:08:41","updated_at":"2026-05-07T22:08:41","tags":["chat","inject","minerva","notes","plugins"],"value":"When user toggles \"inject to chat\" on a PLUGIN_SCENE editor, Editor.gd:_on_check_button_toggled (line ~2195) runs TWO independent paths: (1) `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_inject_toggle(panel_root, enabled)` calls panel's `_on_panel_inject_toggle_changed(enabled)` if defined — fire-and-forget notification only; (2) `_create_note` → `_create_plugin_scene_note` → `PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_create_note(panel_root, ctx)` calls panel's `_on_panel_create_note_request(ctx)` if defined — returns a payload Dictionary {kind: text|html|image|plugin_data, ...} that becomes a Note. If the create_note hook is missing OR returns invalid data, the automatic fallback `_create_plugin_scene_screenshot_note` runs (Editor.gd:2052) and captures the panel's region of the main viewport via `plugin_scene_root.get_viewport().get_texture().get_image()` cropped to plugin_scene_root.get_global_rect(). So even with NO hooks defined, every plugin gets a screenshot Note for free — the per-plugin hook is only worthwhile when you want a richer note (custom title, structured plugin_data, hand-rendered preview).","component":"minerva/plugins","key":"plugin_scene_inject_hooks","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e049d765c7cfdb0c510544178fea9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Godot parse errors cascade to \"Failed to load\" on UNRELATED files","created_at":"2026-05-07T22:44:48","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["autoload","debugging","godot","parse-error"],"value":"When the user sees `Failed to load script \"res://Scripts/Services/Providers/Core/Core.gd\"` (or any other autoload-adjacent file), DO NOT debug Core.gd first. The autoload/module chain reloads on any GDScript change, so a parse error in *any* loaded module — including MCP module files like MCPCadTools.gd — cascades to \"failed to load\" reports for unrelated files that depend on the broken module's class graph. The actual culprit is whichever file Godot reports `SCRIPT ERROR: Parse Error:` on. To find it: `cd src && timeout 15 godot --headless --check-only --script Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/<recently-edited>.gd 2>&1 | grep -iE \"Parse Error\"`. Standalone --check-only gives a benign `Identifier not found: SingletonObject` because autoloads aren't initialized in that mode — that's NOT the bug, look for \"Parse Error\" specifically.","component":"godot","key":"parse_error_cascade_misleading","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e056b02047fef9a515b177a5a919f","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"ANSI/ISO machine-shop PDF export — design discussion","created_at":"2026-05-08T02:29:19","updated_at":"2026-05-08T05:34:56","tags":["cad","design","drawing","export","pdf"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e08fdd00c72a19e1cf8e6fde6a0b0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Acceptance: CAD plugin must keep working through every broker rollout phase","description":"## Why this exists\n\nThe broker refactor (`019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`) and the move-out-of-core migration (`019df8ecca887c5a8a72ae0cefe0b670`) introduce dynamic tool registration, host-callback channels, and policy/manifest changes that every running plugin will be subject to. Today there are two real plugins shipped in `~/github/plugins/`:\n\n- `presentation` — the explicit migration target.\n- `cad` — currently in active use; not a migration target for this DCR but lives on the same runtime.\n\nThe migration DCR mentions CAD only as \"audit later.\" That is not strong enough: the broker must not regress CAD behavior at any phase of the rollout.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nFor every broker plan phase (dynamic discovery, host-callback channel, generic doc/panel state, policy/audit, presentation migration, core removal):\n\n- The CAD plugin loads, starts, and remains usable from the UI without manifest or backend changes.\n- All existing `minerva_cad_*` MCP tools continue to function with their current call signatures and return shapes.\n- CAD's panel state survives a host-led `host.documents.get_state` / `set_state` round-trip — even if CAD does not opt into those capabilities yet, they must not corrupt CAD's existing host-owned save path.\n- Re-entrancy from CAD tool calls into Minerva is either explicitly supported or explicitly rejected by policy with a clear error — never deadlocks.\n- The CAD plugin sees no policy denials it didn't see before, unless its manifest is updated to declare the new capability.\n\n## Verification — phased automated regression coverage\n\n### v1 — plugin lifecycle smoke test (gating, this round)\n\nA 100% automated GDScript test that runs in the Godot test harness via `godot --headless --path src --script test/test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd`. Lives under `src/test/`, never auto-loaded by normal Minerva startup. **Integration-boundary, NOT stub-based** (per existing CLAUDE.md feedback \"test platform code at the integration boundary\").\n\nGoal: catch broker regressions in subprocess spawn, IPC handshake, and runtime wiring — the high-blast-radius failures.\n\nTest outline:\n\n1. SceneTree-rooted entry point. Manually load `PluginDefinition` for CAD from `~/github/plugins/cad/manifest.json`.\n2. Instantiate the real `PluginManager` and the runtime services it requires (no stubs for the spawn/IPC path).\n3. Confirm the `cad-plugin` binary is present at the expected location; skip with a clear message if not built.\n4. Start the plugin. Verify the subprocess actually spawned (pid alive), the plugin reaches RUNNING state, and the MCP `tools/list` handshake completes cleanly.\n5. Stop the plugin. Verify the subprocess exits cleanly (no hung process, no orphan), and plugin state moves to STOPPED.\n6. Print PASS/FAIL counts and `quit(rc)` with non-zero on any failure.\n\nThis v1 test must run at the end of each broker plan phase and stay green as the regression baseline.\n\n### v2 — MCP dispatch surface (follow-up, separate task)\n\nOnce v1 is green and the broker work has begun, add dispatch-surface coverage:\n\n- Bootstrap enough of `SingletonObject` + `MinervaMCPServer` for in-process MCP routing.\n- Create a CAD editor via `minerva_create_plugin_editor`.\n- Call read-only `minerva_cad_*` tools (`get_mesh_info`, `list_edges_live`, `list_user_labels`, `get_selected_edge`) and assert clean response envelopes (success or clean error, never crash/malformed) on an empty editor.\n- Tools that require evaluated geometry (`annotate_edges`, `clear_edge_annotations`, `export`, `snapshot`) require the python build123d worker; gate behind a worker-available probe and skip cleanly when unavailable.\n\nFile this as a follow-up work_item under the broker DCR when v1 lands.\n\n## Out of scope (explicitly)\n\n- **Pixel-level visual verification.** The smoke tests do not assert that the CAD panel renders correctly, that edge picking works visually, or that snapshot pixels match a baseline. Filed as a separate follow-up if/when needed.\n- Migrating CAD's MCP tools out of Minerva core (CAD already owns its tools in the plugin backend conceptually; tool migration follows the broker DCR).\n- Adding new CAD features.\n- Verifying CEF-hosted panel input/rendering behavior.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The v1 test should detect \"binary not built\" and skip with a clear message rather than fail — keeps CI portable across machines that haven't built the Go binary.\n- `runtime-build/` (the embedded python worker tar.zst) is NOT required for v1 — only for v2's eval-dependent assertions.","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:08:32","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:31:33","priority":1,"tags":["acceptance","broker","cad","non-regression","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e08fdf72b7251bbc95d12c8d907b8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Acceptance: plugin skill self-containment preserved through MCP migration","description":"## Why this exists\n\nTwo pillars of the plugin platform the user wants protected through this refactor:\n\n1. Plugins self-contain their MCP code and footprint.\n2. Plugins self-contain their skills.\n\nThe current broker DCR (`019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`) and migration DCR (`019df8ecca887c5a8a72ae0cefe0b670`) explicitly defend pillar (1) — moving `MCPPresentationTools.gd` into the plugin backend. Pillar (2) — that a plugin's skills travel with the plugin install, not with Minerva core — is not currently asserted anywhere in this DCR tree.\n\nIf the broker work or migration accidentally couples skills to core (for example, by registering plugin-shipped skills through a path that requires Minerva to know about them at compile time, or by collapsing skill discovery into the same module that owns MCP tools), pillar (2) regresses silently.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A plugin can ship one or more skills inside its install dir (the same dir as its manifest/binary), and those skills become discoverable when the plugin starts and disappear when it stops — without core code changes.\n- After the broker DCR ships, removing a plugin removes its skills as well as its MCP tools. Neither leaks into the user's docket as orphans.\n- The presentation plugin migration ships at least one plugin-owned skill (or a clearly documented decision that it has none) and proves the round-trip on a fresh-install machine.\n- The CAD plugin, which already ships skills, sees no regression in its skill discovery during any broker phase.\n- Documentation (plugin_system_guide.md or successor) explicitly states the skill self-containment contract alongside the MCP self-containment contract.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Integration test: install a fixture plugin that declares one skill + one MCP tool, start it, list skills + tools through MCP, stop the plugin, list again, assert both vanish.\n- Manual: install presentation + CAD on a fresh-state machine, confirm their respective skills are present, uninstall, confirm they're gone.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Building a new skill packaging format.\n- Migrating the existing shipped baseline `master.dct` skill set.","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:08:42","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:08:42","priority":1,"tags":["acceptance","broker","plugins","self-containment","skills"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e08fe2615765e9733b3f651534d74","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"ScanSort: validate broker covers experiment→plugin transition needs","description":"## Why this exists\n\nScanSort currently lives at `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/scansort` as a stand-alone experiment. The intent is for it to become a Minerva plugin once the platform is ready. That makes ScanSort the second non-presentation real-world test of whether the new broker capabilities cover the needs of a plugin authored independently of Minerva core.\n\nIf the broker is designed only against presentation's needs, ScanSort's transition could surface late gaps (filesystem semantics for image scanning, panel state shape, image rendering capability, multi-step host calls, etc.) after the broker DCR has shipped. We want those gaps surfaced before the broker is locked.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A short capability gap analysis comparing what ScanSort needs from a Minerva host vs. what the broker DCR (`019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`) actually provides. Output: a list of missing host capabilities, if any.\n- Any missing capability is either added to the broker DCR's scope or filed as an explicit follow-up before the broker DCR is marked shipped.\n- A transition checklist for moving ScanSort from its current experiment layout (`~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/scansort`) to a plugin layout under `~/github/plugins/scansort/`, covering: manifest, MCP tool surface, skills (if any), panel/UI strategy, file/image capability requirements.\n- A no-code-change validation: with the broker DCR shipped, ScanSort can be ported as a plugin without requiring a new core feature.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Actually doing the ScanSort port. This task is only about ensuring the platform is ready for it.\n- ScanSort's own internal redesign.\n\n## Notes\n\nThis task is intentionally scoped as validation, not migration. The migration itself becomes a follow-up DCR after this analysis returns clean.","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:08:54","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:08:54","priority":2,"tags":["broker","migration","plugins","scansort","validation"],"parent":"minerva:019df8ecca887c5a8a72ae0cefe0b670"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0907647f746facd21e96c6a62bcd","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Existing plugin tests stub the runtime; they do NOT exercise real subprocess + MCP dispatch","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:18:59","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:18:59","tags":["broker","integration-boundary","plugins","stubs","tests"],"value":"test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd, test_plugin_scene_panel_host.gd, etc. all use duck-typed stubs (StubDB, StubPluginDef, StubBroker, StubSceneRoot) and never spawn the real plugin subprocess. They cover internal decision trees but would NOT catch broker/IPC/subprocess regressions. For broker-refactor non-regression coverage (CAD smoke test, ScanSort transition, etc.) write an integration-boundary test that spawns the real binary and routes through the real MCP dispatcher — see CLAUDE.md memory note 'Test platform code at the integration boundary'.","component":"minerva-plugin-tests","key":"existing-plugin-tests-are-stubbed","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e090eb9af7305a9190c33fbdfea74","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"MCPServerConnection.list_tools() is a coroutine, must be awaited","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:27:00","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:27:00","tags":["coroutine","gdscript","gotcha","mcp"],"value":"MCPServerConnection.list_tools() looks like a cache getter (returns `tools` array) but lazy-refreshes on empty cache — it `await refresh_tools()` internally. The Godot parser flags it as a coroutine and any caller must `var tools = await conn.list_tools()` even after refresh_tools has already populated the cache. File: src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MCPServerConnection.gd:152.","component":"minerva-mcp","key":"mcpserverconnection-list-tools-is-coroutine","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e091058887c5b84a94fd046cebd48","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"--script mode: top-of-file preload of autoload-coupled scripts fails","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:28:46","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["--script","autoload","godot","gotcha","scenetree","tests"],"value":"In `godot --headless --script test/foo.gd` mode, the test script and its preload chain are parsed BEFORE autoloads register. Any `preload(...)` of a script whose body references an autoload identifier (e.g. `SingletonObject`) errors with `Identifier not found: SingletonObject` and the whole compile chain collapses (depended scripts also fail). The autoloads DO still load eventually — just after the test's parse phase. Workaround: defer to runtime `load(path)` after `await process_frame`. Existing test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd is silently degraded by this issue (parse fails → TestablePluginManager.new() returns a script-less Object → methods report missing). Confirmed Godot 4.6.1 stable.","component":"godot","key":"script-mode-autoload-parse-order","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e09126b4878259a2ca38f8c13f177","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"MCPServerConnection.refresh_tools logs \"Found 0 tools\" when plugin returns non-empty tools/list","description":"## Symptom\n\nThe `cad-plugin` Go backend returns a non-empty `tools/list` response. Wire log shows it explicitly:\n\n```\n[MCP STDIO] Received: {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":\"2\",\"result\":{\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"mcad_validate\",\"description\":\"Validate .mcad source: parse + translate, no tessellation. Returns structured errors with line/col for the LLM inner...\"},...]}}\n[MCP] tools/list returned: { ... }\n[MCP] tools/list result keys: [\"jsonrpc\", \"id\", \"result\"]\n[MCP] Found 0 tools\n```\n\n`refresh_tools()` returns OK and `list_tools()` returns an empty Array even though the wire response carries tools. The result envelope has the expected JSON-RPC shape (`{\"result\": {\"tools\": [...]}}`).\n\n## Surfaced by\n\n`test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd` (acceptance item `019e08fdd00c72a19e1cf8e6fde6a0b0`). The smoke test asserts `tools/list returns an Array` (which it does) but logged \"cad-plugin reported 0 tool(s)\" — a sanity print, not a failed assertion. The wire log made the discrepancy obvious.\n\n## Likely cause\n\nThe MCP normalizer in `MCPServerConnection.refresh_tools()` (or the helpers it calls in `MinervaMCPServer`) is not unwrapping `result.tools` from the JSON-RPC envelope. Memory note `mcp_stdio_result_envelope` already documents that `tools/call` returns `{content:[{type:\"text\",text:\"<JSON>\"}]}` and is unwrapped via `_normalize_mcp_tool_result` — `tools/list` may need an analogous unwrap (or the same path took a wrong branch).\n\n## Why this matters now\n\nThe broker DCR (`019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb`) plan starts with phase 1 — *dynamic tool discovery from plugin backends*. That phase relies entirely on `tools/list` being correctly parsed. If the existing parser silently drops the array, the broker work would either fail to discover any plugin-owned tools, or work around it with a parallel parser — neither acceptable.\n\n## Reproducer\n\n1. Build cad-plugin: `cd ~/github/plugins/cad && go build -o cad-plugin .`\n2. Run: `godot --headless --path src --script test/test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd`\n3. Observe: wire response carries tools, `[MCP] Found 0 tools` is logged, `list_tools()` returns `[]`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Identify the unwrap site in `MCPServerConnection.refresh_tools` and fix it so `list_tools()` returns the array of tool dicts the backend declared.\n- Add a regression assertion to `test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd` that `tools.size() > 0` once cad-plugin's `tools/list` carries a non-empty array. Tighten the assertion at the same time we tighten the surface.\n- The fix must NOT regress core MCP server `tools/list` (used by external clients today).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Anything about the broker DCR's downstream tool registration. This bug is purely about parsing the wire response.","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:31:02","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:52:03","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["broker","cad","mcp","plugins","surfaced-by-smoke-test","tools-list"],"resolution":"Stdio tools/list now unwraps the JSON-RPC `result` envelope to match HTTP/_mcp_initialize. Verification: test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd 13/13 PASS, lists cad-plugin's 5 tools (mcad_validate, mcad_list_edges, cad.evaluate, cad.export, cad.cancel_eval) with populated name+description+input_schema. Schema validation also passed cold review (Opus reviewer caught 3 polish items, all addressed: behavior-named comment instead of line-number, diagnostic error string, schema completeness check in the test). Committed 4296d6d7.</resolution>\n<parameter name=\"note\">Awaiting HITL verification before transitioning resolved → verified → closed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0912b95d71a3afedc1de96f262fd","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd silently degraded — parse failure produces script-less Object","description":"## Symptom\n\n`godot --headless --path src --script test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd` does not produce a clean PASS/FAIL result. Instead it logs:\n\n```\nSCRIPT ERROR: Compile Error: Identifier not found: SingletonObject\n          at: GDScript::reload (res://Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginManager.gd:425)\nSCRIPT ERROR: Compile Error: Identifier not found: SingletonObject\n          at: GDScript::reload (res://Scripts/Services/MCP/MCPServerConnection.gd:86)\nSCRIPT ERROR: Compile Error: Failed to compile depended scripts.\n          at: GDScript::reload (res://test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd:0)\n```\n\n…then attempts to run anyway. `TestablePluginManager.new()` returns a script-less `Node` because the parse failed:\n\n```\nERROR: Parameter \"p_script->implicit_initializer\" is null.\n   at: _super_implicit_constructor (modules/gdscript/gdscript.cpp:143)\nSCRIPT ERROR: Invalid call. Nonexistent function 'register_live_panel' in base 'Node (TestablePluginManager)'.\n```\n\nEventually crashes with a C++ stack trace on a downstream call. `_pass_count` and `_fail_count` are both 0 — the test reports nothing useful.\n\n## Root cause\n\nSame as docket hint `019e091058887` — `--script` mode parses test dependencies before autoloads register. The test eagerly compiles `PluginManager.gd` (via `extends PluginManager`), which references `SingletonObject` at parse time, fails, and propagates compile errors up the chain. The class object is broken but the test runner still tries to invoke methods on it.\n\n## Surfaced by\n\n`test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd` (acceptance item `019e08fdd00c72a19e1cf8e6fde6a0b0`). The smoke test hit the same root cause but worked around it via deferred `load(...)` after `await process_frame`. Verifying the existing test still ran revealed that the existing test had already silently broken — likely some time ago when `SingletonObject` references were first introduced into `PluginManager.gd` / `MCPServerConnection.gd`.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThis test claims to cover the PluginManager hot-reload decision tree (632 lines of test code, 16 named test cases). All of it is currently dead. Anyone relying on it as regression coverage for plugin hot-reload behavior is getting nothing.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Either (a) refactor the test to use the deferred `load(...)` pattern (per hint `019e091058887`), or (b) refactor `PluginManager.gd` and `MCPServerConnection.gd` to look up `SingletonObject` defensively (`Engine.get_main_loop().root.get_node_or_null(\"SingletonObject\")`) instead of using the bareword identifier — option (b) makes BOTH the test and the production code more robust.\n- Confirm the test produces a clean `=== Results: N passed, M failed ===` line and `quit(rc)` correctly.\n- Verify the 16 named test cases actually exercise meaningful logic (not just call methods on a broken stub).\n\n## Notes\n\nWorth checking if any other tests in `src/test/` are similarly degraded — search for `extends PluginManager`, `extends MCPServerConnection`, or `preload` of any script transitively referencing `SingletonObject` at parse time.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The broader refactor of moving away from bareword autoload references throughout the codebase (it's a pervasive pattern).","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:31:22","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:31:22","priority":3,"severity":2,"tags":["godot","plugins","scenetree","surfaced-by-smoke-test","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e09259930743f84cfd4bb987b7981","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"docket_transition enforces step-by-step bug state walk; can't skip","created_at":"2026-05-08T19:51:59","updated_at":"2026-05-08T19:51:59","tags":["bug","docket","state-machine","transitions"],"value":"For `type=bug`, docket_transition will reject jumps that skip states. Path is strict: new → triaged → active → resolved → verified → closed. Even with a single small fix, you must call docket_transition four times (triaged, active, resolved). Same step-by-step enforcement likely applies to other types' state chains too.","component":"docket","key":"bug-state-machine-no-skips","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e093b2d0c72b795e1fc213575d704","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"SingletonObject.plugin_tool_registry is null until ~2s into _ready","created_at":"2026-05-08T20:15:33","updated_at":"2026-05-08T20:15:33","tags":["gotcha","initialization","plugins","race","singleton","tests"],"value":"SingletonObject._ready awaits `get_tree().create_timer(2.0).timeout` + `mcp_manager.initialize()` BEFORE calling `initialize_plugins()` (singleton_object.gd:455-460), which is the function that actually assigns `plugin_tool_registry = ToolRegClass.new(...)`. Until that runs, `SingletonObject.plugin_tool_registry` is null even though the property is declared. Implications: (a) any code using PluginManager._discover_backend_tools (or similar paths that look up the wired registry) called too early will silently skip with a `plugin_tool_registry not available` warning; (b) integration tests against the wired registry must poll for non-null before asserting. A `--script` SceneTree test calling `start_plugin` immediately after `await process_frame` will hit this — wait at least 3 seconds, or poll the property with a 0.1s timer until non-null with a 10s upper bound.","component":"minerva-singleton","key":"plugin-tool-registry-late-init","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e09660bfb7a7092bb7ca539e7effc","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"CapabilityBroker emits duplicate capability_denied audit when broker-level deny follows policy-grant","description":"## Surfaced by\n\nCold-Opus review of broker phase 2 (round 2 of broker DCR rollout). Verdict was `approve_with_notes`; this is the lone non-blocking nit.\n\n## Symptom\n\nWhen a capability is granted by `PluginPolicy.check_capability` (line passes) but then rejected at the broker level (e.g. unknown capability name handled by `_handle_mcp_proxy`-style fallback), the audit log gets TWO `capability_denied` entries for the same request:\n\n1. One from the named-capability handler returning `{success: false, error_code: ...}` at `CapabilityBroker.gd:103-106`.\n2. One from `_audit_dispatch` at `CapabilityBroker.gd:154`, which fires `EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED` whenever `result.success` is false — including after a broker-level rejection.\n\nThe exact path: `host.unknown_cap` test case at `test_host_capability_channel.gd:144-148`. Plugin manifest grants the capability namespace; broker doesn't recognize the specific name; one denial enters the log via the named-handler error, another via the dispatch tail.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n- Not a correctness bug — request resolves correctly, plugin gets a single response.\n- Audit log noise: the same denial appears twice with slightly different metadata, complicating downstream analysis.\n- Inconsistent with the policy-level deny path, which logs exactly once.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A capability denial logs exactly one `EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED` entry per failed request, regardless of whether the denial originates in `PluginPolicy.check_capability` or in `CapabilityBroker.dispatch` itself.\n- The fix preserves the metadata that's most useful for debugging (capability name, plugin id, error code).\n- A test asserts the single-entry property by counting denied audit entries before/after a known broker-level deny.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nEither skip `_audit_dispatch` after a named-capability deny return (the handler already logged), or have `_audit_dispatch` check for an audit-already-fired marker on the result dict. Whichever is cleaner.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Refactoring the audit log API.\n- Changing the `capability_denied` event payload shape (separate decision).","created_at":"2026-05-08T21:02:23","updated_at":"2026-05-09T05:41:18","priority":3,"tags":["audit-log","broker","capabilities","polish","surfaced-by-cold-review"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Folded into broker round 4 (commit aa25ded1 on user/imran/experiments/swarm).\n\nResolution:\n- Added EVENT_CAPABILITY_FAILED event constant in PluginAuditLog.gd, distinct from EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED.\n- Added _POLICY_DENY_CODES set in CapabilityBroker.gd. _audit_dispatch now routes denials by error_code:\n  - Policy-class denies (capability_not_granted, capability_not_in_manifest, etc.) → EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED.\n  - Broker-validation failures (editor_not_found, schema_validation_failed, not_buffer_canonical, version_conflict, mcp_tool_error, secrets_error, unknown_capability) → EVENT_CAPABILITY_FAILED.\n- Acceptance: a single failed request produces exactly one event of the appropriate class, no duplicate denied entries.\n- Round-4 HITL via MCP confirmed clean audit log shape on both happy and unhappy paths.\n\nNo follow-up needed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0973384d7785b9f03119bbe1e444","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Subagent worktrees can be checked out from a stale base, NOT the parent branch's HEAD","created_at":"2026-05-08T21:16:46","updated_at":"2026-05-08T21:16:46","tags":["agents","git","gotcha","harness","subagent","worktree"],"value":"When you launch an Agent with `isolation: \"worktree\"`, the worktree's HEAD is NOT guaranteed to match the parent's current branch HEAD. Witnessed: worktree spawned while parent was on `user/imran/experiments/swarm` at `9e427555`, but the worktree was checked out at `3f0e4adc Fixed the build workflow` (mainline May 2025). The agent saw `src/Scripts/Services/` containing only `Providers` (none of the post-2025 services existed in the stale base), spent ~10 tool calls discovering this, then ran `git reset --hard <parent-head>` to fix it. Mitigation: every implementer prompt for worktree-isolated agents should include a preamble like \"First action: `git log -1 --oneline`. If HEAD isn't `<expected-sha>`, run `git reset --hard <expected-sha>` before reading any files.\" Lost ~10 tool calls per agent without this preamble.","component":"claude-code-harness","key":"subagent-worktree-base-may-be-stale","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e09734fe87e759f3d244e08d5d951","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Implementer prompts should pre-cite file:line entry points, not just file names","created_at":"2026-05-08T21:16:52","updated_at":"2026-05-08T21:16:52","tags":["investigation-budget","pattern","prompt-design","subagent","work-cycle"],"value":"Phase 3 reads round failed because the implementer ran out of budget on legitimate but expensive investigation (reading CLAUDE.md + 6 named source files + Editor.gd which is 43K tokens). Lesson: when adding to existing patterns, the prompt should pre-cite the exact entry points so the agent mirrors immediately. Instead of \"see how host.echo is dispatched,\" write \"host.echo handler is at CapabilityBroker.gd:<exact-line>; mirror it.\" Instead of \"look up doc_write,\" write \"doc_write tool handler is at MCPDocTools.gd:<line>.\" Also: explicitly tell the agent which files NOT to read even if they seem related (e.g. \"Editor.gd is 43K tokens — you don't need to read it; DocumentBuffer.get_text() is the only path you need\"). Saves 10-20 tool calls of orientation per agent.","component":"work-cycle","key":"implementer-prompt-pre-cite-entry-points","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e09d61b587274a58d163e236443c6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Implement remaining host.documents.* capabilities (open + writes)","description":"## Scope\n\nThree host capabilities not covered by round 3 (which shipped reads only — `list_open` + `get_state` at commits 0273c1cf + bd02a019):\n\n- `host.documents.open` — open or focus a plugin document by `{plugin_id, panel_name, path}`. Navigation/focus, not destructive but UI-side-effecting.\n- `host.documents.set_state` — replace state on a plugin-owned panel/tab. Destructive: validation, dirty propagation, race with in-memory edits.\n- `host.documents.mark_dirty` — explicit dirty marker for editors that mutate canonical state outside the buffer path.\n\n## Schema findings to inherit from round 3\n\n- `editor_name` is the canonical lookup key (mirrors MCPDocTools).\n- Buffer resolution order in `CapabilityBroker._resolve_editor_buffer`: paired_dsl broker attachment → path-keyed `DocumentRegistry` → `editor.get_document_buffer()` fallback (aligned to `MCPDocTools._resolve_target` per cold-review note).\n- `kind` is a stable string mapped from `Editor.Type` ordinal (text_editor, plugin_scene, …) — do not surface raw enum integers to plugins.\n- For non-canonical editors (panel state lives in panel UI), `get_state` returns `buffer_canonical=false` with a structured `unsupported_reason: \"panel_state_via_host_owned_save\"`. **`set_state` MUST refuse on non-canonical buffers cleanly** — return `{success:false, error_code:\"not_buffer_canonical\", unsupported_reason:\"panel_state_via_host_owned_save\"}` rather than attempting a host_owned_save IPC roundtrip in this round.\n\n## Deferred from round 3 reads (cold-Opus review of 0273c1cf)\n\n- **Audit miscategorization** (chore 019e09660bfb) — `success=false` results route through `_audit_dispatch` as `EVENT_CAPABILITY_DENIED` even when the policy granted the call and only the broker-level lookup/validation failed. Round 4 compounds the issue: `set_state` will introduce more broker-level error_codes (`not_buffer_canonical`, `dirty_propagation_failed`, validation failures, etc.) all currently bucketed as denials. **Fix the chore as part of this round** rather than shipping more polluted audit data.\n- **Populated-list test gap** — round 3's headless test couldn't exercise the populated `list_open` happy path because `--script` mode doesn't boot the MainScene. Round 4 should boot a real `MainScene` (per `feedback_test_at_integration_boundary`) and round-trip at least one TEXT editor through list_open + get_state + set_state + mark_dirty.\n- **Strict args contract** — round 3 silently ignored extra args on `host.documents.get_state` (consistent with `host.echo`). Pin the contract before `set_state` ships: extra fields should either be rejected with `schema_validation_failed`, or ignored consistently. Make a deliberate call rather than inheriting `host.echo`'s leniency.\n\n## Proposed schema (subject to round-4 design pass)\n\n```\nhost.documents.open\n  args: {plugin_id, panel_name, path?}\n  result: {editor_name, kind, plugin_id, panel_name, path}\n  errors: not_found, plugin_not_found, panel_not_found\n\nhost.documents.set_state\n  args: {editor_name, buffer_text, expected_version?}\n  result: {editor_name, version, dirty}\n  errors: editor_not_found, not_buffer_canonical, version_conflict (if expected_version supplied),\n          schema_validation_failed\n\nhost.documents.mark_dirty\n  args: {editor_name}\n  result: {editor_name, dirty: true}\n  errors: editor_not_found, not_buffer_canonical\n```\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All three capabilities ship with manifest declaration support, policy enforcement, and audit coverage.\n- `set_state` round-trips through `DocumentBuffer.apply_edit` so the paired_dsl render panel reflects the change live (same path `minerva_doc_write` already exercises).\n- `mark_dirty` flips `DocumentBuffer.dirty` and emits `text_changed` if needed (decision: do we synthesize a no-op edit, or expose a `mark_dirty()` method on `DocumentBuffer`? Latter is cleaner).\n- Optional optimistic-concurrency support via `expected_version` on `set_state` — if the buffer's version differs, return `version_conflict` rather than overwriting.\n- Audit chore 019e09660bfb closed as part of the same commit set.\n- Tests: extend `test_host_capability_documents.gd` with set_state happy/conflict/non-canonical paths, mark_dirty happy path, plus MainScene-booted populated-list assertion. Regressions clean: `test_host_capability_channel.gd` 32/32 + `test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd` 37/37.\n\n## Rollout\n\n- Start with `set_state` + `mark_dirty` (the destructive pair). Defer `host.documents.open` to a polish slice if it expands scope materially — opening editors involves singleton wiring that crosses into the read-side `list_open` path.\n- Same work-cycle shape as round 2: Sonnet implementer, cold-Opus reviewer, integration-boundary test, WIP commit + 3b stop.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- `host_owned_save` IPC roundtrip wiring for non-canonical editors — explicit deferral; non-canonical editors return `not_buffer_canonical` cleanly.\n- New file/editor capabilities (`host.files.*`, `host.editors.*`) — those live under sibling task 019df8e3bd79.\n- Policy/manifest/audit hardening beyond the chore — that's task 019df8e3b763.","created_at":"2026-05-08T23:04:47","updated_at":"2026-05-09T03:02:46","priority":1,"tags":["broker","host-capabilities","plugins","writes"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"Shipped on user/imran/experiments/swarm. Two main commits + a follow-up + a latent-bug fix:\n\n  - aa25ded1 — initial implementation (set_state + mark_dirty + audit chore fold)\n  - 0e679f2e — review followup (set_state always-dirties contract via mark_dirty after apply_edit)\n  - d0a95e0d — sightline-surfaced GDScript warning cleanup (16 warnings cleared across 9 files)\n  - 57165134 — fix(plugins) preserve _backend_name in register_plugin_tools (round-1 latent bug; surfaced when external MCP clients tried to call prefixed plugin tools)\n\nCapabilities shipped this round:\n  - host.documents.set_state (with optimistic-concurrency expected_version)\n  - host.documents.mark_dirty\n  - Audit chore 019e09660bfb folded in: EVENT_CAPABILITY_FAILED splits broker-validation errors from policy denials.\n\nNOT shipped (deferred):\n  - host.documents.open — follow-up for a polish slice (no consumer yet).\n  - dirty_changed signal on DocumentBuffer for tab-UI auto-update — filed as polish chore 019e0aaf1902 (HITL confirmed: dirty flag flips in buffer but tab title `*` indicator does not).\n\nHITL evidence (real Minerva, real CAD plugin, real .mcad source on user/imran/experiments/swarm @ 57165134):\n  - probe_list returned 3 documents (Plugin Manager, t_beam.mcad source pane, t_beam.mcad (1) plugin_scene render pane sharing the same file path).\n  - probe_state baseline returned buffer_canonical=true with the actual t_beam.mcad source.\n  - probe_set_state mutated the buffer 5 times (versions 1→5) with varying length/width/height/thickness; user visually confirmed each render shape change in the CAD 3D panel.\n  - probe_set_state with stale expected_version=0 against current version=1 returned version_conflict with actual_version=1; no mutation occurred.\n  - probe_mark_dirty on a clean (saved) buffer flipped dirty=true (verified via subsequent probe_state) — the buffer-state path works; the tab-title `*` indicator is the gap captured in chore 019e0aaf1902.\n  - No new errors in the Debugger panel during HITL.\n\nTests: documents 52/52, channel 32/32, cad smoke 37/37 (no regressions across the four commits).\n\nRound 4 substantively done. Sibling tasks remain open under the parent broker DCR (T4 policy hardening, T5 fs/editors, T6 presentation migration, T7 integration tests).</resolution>\n</invoke>"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0aaf190277c9bb9eae701c2a53bc","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"DocumentBuffer.mark_dirty() needs a signal so tab UI auto-updates dirty indicator","description":"## Surfaced by\n\nCold-Opus review note #3 of broker phase 4 (commit `aa25ded1`) and CONFIRMED via real-Minerva HITL on 2026-05-08:\n> Calling `host.documents.mark_dirty` on a clean buffer flips `DocumentBuffer.dirty` to true, but the source-pane tab title's `*` indicator does NOT update.\n\n## Symptom\n\n`DocumentBuffer.mark_dirty()` (added in commit `aa25ded1`) does the minimal correct thing:\n\n```gdscript\nfunc mark_dirty() -> void:\n    dirty = true\n```\n\nIt updates the canonical state but emits no signal. Tab-title indicator code in EditorPane / Editor watches different signals (text_changed, save_requested, etc.) for dirty-flag derivation. Without a `dirty_changed` notification, the host-side \"explicitly dirty\" intent doesn't reach the UI.\n\nConfirmed during round-4 HITL: 5 sequential `host.documents.set_state` calls + 1 `host.documents.mark_dirty` call against `t_beam.mcad`. Each set_state DID update the render panel (DocumentBuffer.text_changed propagation worked) but neither tab (source `t_beam.mcad` or render `t_beam.mcad (1)`) showed the `*` indicator. The buffer state itself was correctly dirty (verified via subsequent `host.documents.get_state`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nFor LLM/agent workflows that mutate documents through host capabilities, the visible feedback to the user is missing. The user sees the render panel update (good) but no indication the source needs saving (bad — they may close the tab without realising).\n\nLess critical for set_state (text_changed already drives most UI), more critical for mark_dirty (no other signal fires).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `DocumentBuffer` emits a `dirty_changed(dirty: bool)` signal whenever `dirty` transitions (apply_edit setting it true, save_to_disk clearing, mark_dirty setting it true, reload_from_disk clearing).\n- Editor's tab-title-binding logic listens to `dirty_changed` and toggles the `*` indicator accordingly.\n- Idempotent: calling `mark_dirty()` on an already-dirty buffer should not fire the signal twice (squelch when state unchanged).\n- A test asserts the signal fires exactly once when transitioning false→true and not again on a no-op call.\n\n## Implementation sketch\n\nIn `src/Scripts/Services/Documents/DocumentBuffer.gd`:\n\n```gdscript\nsignal dirty_changed(is_dirty: bool)\n\nfunc mark_dirty() -> void:\n    if dirty:\n        return\n    dirty = true\n    dirty_changed.emit(true)\n\nfunc apply_edit(new_text: String) -> void:\n    if new_text == text:\n        return\n    text = new_text\n    version += 1\n    var was_dirty := dirty\n    dirty = true\n    text_changed.emit(text, version)\n    if not was_dirty:\n        dirty_changed.emit(true)\n\nfunc save_to_disk() -> Dictionary:\n    # ... existing logic ...\n    if was_dirty:  # before clearing\n        dirty_changed.emit(false)\n```\n\nSubscribe in Editor (or EditorPane.tab-title binding) and update the tab indicator.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Deeper UI work (e.g. icon overlays, status-bar messages).\n- Doing the same for non-buffer-canonical editors (those that store state in panel UI rather than DocumentBuffer) — those need `host_owned_save` IPC support first (already deferred per the broker DCR's 3e stop conditions).\n\n## Related\n\n- Round-4 implementation: commits `aa25ded1` + `0e679f2e` (always-dirties contract).\n- Round-3 follow-up task: `019e09d61b58` (this is one of its deferred polish notes).","created_at":"2026-05-09T03:01:47","updated_at":"2026-05-09T03:01:47","priority":3,"tags":["broker","document-buffer","host-capabilities","polish","surfaced-by-cold-review","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e0fb829c473ae81b2645233f03f63","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_plugin_manager_hot_reload SIGSEGV at line 291 (StubPluginDef.state assignment)","description":"## Symptom\n\nRunning `godot --headless --path src --script test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd` produces a deterministic native SIGSEGV (signal 11) with no `Results:` line. GDScript backtrace:\n\n```\n[0] _run_debounce_for_paths (test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd:291)\n[1] test_py_change_triggers_stop_start (test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd:307)\n[2] _init (test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd:39)\n```\n\nLine 291 is `def.state = PluginManager.S_RUNNING` where `def` is a `StubPluginDef extends RefCounted` returned by `mgr._db.get_by_id(plugin_id)`. The stub has `var state: int = 2  # S_RUNNING` and `PluginManager.S_RUNNING := 2`, so the assignment is type-correct.\n\n## Surfaced by\n\nT4 Layer-2 broader plugin smoke (2026-05-09). 14 of 15 plugin test suites green; this one crashes.\n\n## Why it's pre-existing (not T4)\n\n- `test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd` last touched 2026-04 (round-1 dynamic discovery work, well pre-T4)\n- `PluginManager.gd` not touched by T4\n- T4-touched files: CapabilityBroker.gd, PluginDB.gd (load_db tightening only), PluginDefinition.gd, PluginErrors.gd, plus 3 test files\n- Neither U1 follow-up (bcf861d9) nor U2-4 follow-up (0a20e0d5) baseline lists include this suite — it's not part of the canonical green set recently\n\n## Hypotheses\n\n- Native crash inside Godot's typed-property-assignment path on a RefCounted stub when the assigned value is a `const int` accessed via `<ClassName>.<CONST>` syntax. Possible engine bug (4.6.1 stable).\n- Resource cleanup ordering: prior tests in the file create `Control.new()` nodes; `mgr.free()` mid-suite plus background DocumentBuffer / SingletonObject lifecycle may corrupt heap.\n- The test file at line 39 `_init()` runs all tests in sequence — earlier tests may leave global state polluted.\n\n## Repro\n\n```bash\ncd src && godot --headless --path . --script test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd\n```\n\n## Workaround\n\nSuite is excluded from canonical Layer-1 baseline. T4 closure proceeds without gating on it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Identify the actual crash cause (engine bug vs. test fixture bug)\n- Either fix the test or file an upstream Godot issue and skip gracefully\n- Add to canonical Layer-1 baseline once green","created_at":"2026-05-10T02:29:48","updated_at":"2026-05-10T02:29:48","priority":3,"tags":["flake","headless","plugin-manager","pre-existing","test"],"environment":"Godot 4.6.1.stable.official, Linux 6.8.0-110-generic, headless","repro_steps":"cd src && godot --headless --path . --script test/test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e15965ef573458fd5ffd0dbe35182","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Broker DCR phase 5 — blob-split for host.documents.* (Bug D)","description":"Substrate fix to unblock T6 HITL: host.documents.get_state and set_state currently serialize the entire panel state (including embedded image blobs) into one wire payload. For typical presentation decks (text + color + images) the wire size is tens of megabytes. Migrated presentation plugin tools time out / overflow scanner buffers on real decks.\n\nApproved direction (Option 3): generic navigation + blob handle split + JSON Patch writes. Four new caps in host.documents.* family: get_node, patch_state, get_blob, put_blob. Plugin save-shape contract: blob values wrapped as {__blob__: true, content_type, bytes}; broker strips, assigns handle, replaces with {__blob_handle__, content_type} on outbound. Blob store at PluginScenePanelBroker, keyed by (editor_name, handle), reference-counted, GC at refcount=0, cleared on editor close.\n\nAudit log shape-only summary (op count, op kinds, paths, blob handle refs) — no user content. set_state stays for whole-doc replace (import, restore, factory reset); patch_state is the cheap default for incremental change.\n\nAcceptance: all four caps wired with audit + grants; blob store + refcount + GC in PluginScenePanelBroker; Go helper exposes Patch/GetNode/GetBlob/PutBlob; T6 tools reshape (total LoC down); 58MB alpha deck get_state response <1MB; 50-slide deck list_slides <100ms; cold Opus review passes; HITL pass on alpha deck.\n\nEstimate ~7 days substrate + Go helpers + tool rework + tests + review.\nDesign doc: Docs/design/host_documents_blob_split.md\nParent: broker DCR 019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","created_at":"2026-05-11T05:50:36","updated_at":"2026-05-11T08:27:17","tags":["blob-split","blocker","broker","dcr","host-documents","phase5"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e15a64d6175f99ad68ecd3d2450b7","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Plugin reload/restart does not re-parse manifest.json","description":"PluginManager.restart_plugin (src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginManager.gd:635) only stops and starts the plugin's subprocess. The in-memory PluginDefinition (loaded at install time) is reused. So manifest changes — capabilities added, tools updated, panels reshaped — are invisible until the plugin is removed and reinstalled.\n\nAggravating: the hot-reload path for .json file changes (PluginManager.gd:882) also routes to restart_plugin, so saving a manifest.json change doesn't pick it up either, despite the auto-reload code path explicitly handling .json.\n\nRepro:\n1. Install presentation plugin (manifest declares N caps)\n2. Edit manifest.json to add a new cap\n3. /plugin reload presentation OR wait for hot-reload\n4. Open Plugin Manager → caps list shows N, not N+1\n5. Remove + install → caps list now shows N+1\n\nFix shape: on restart, if manifest.json mtime has changed since the install-time parse OR if invoked by hot-reload of a .json file, re-parse manifest and rebuild PluginDefinition before respawning the subprocess. Validate the re-parsed definition the same way install does (validate_host_capabilities etc.); on validation failure, surface the error and leave the old definition in place rather than corrupting state.\n\nSurfaced during T6 R7 HITL prep, 2026-05-10. Filed as Bug A.","created_at":"2026-05-11T06:08:00","updated_at":"2026-05-11T06:08:00","severity":3,"tags":["broker-hitl-followup","hot-reload","plugin-platform","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e15a67f2e755a8ec4d5545dd6a8d4","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"No install-time or first-use prompt for plugin capability grants","description":"When a plugin declares host_capabilities in its manifest, the user is given no opportunity to grant them at install time, plugin start, or first-use. The only grant path is the Plugin Manager panel (PluginManagerPanel.gd:609-630), where the user must manually find the plugin and toggle each capability on.\n\nSymptom: a first-time user invokes a migrated tool (e.g. minerva_presentation_list_slides) and gets `{\"error_code\": \"capability_not_granted\"}`. The error envelope contains no breadcrumb pointing at Plugin Manager. There is no in-app affordance suggesting the toggles exist.\n\nThis is a structural UX gap, not just a missing button. Plugin authors can declare caps, but users can't discover what the plugin needs until tools start failing.\n\nFix shape — three options to evaluate during design:\n\n(a) Default-grant scoped caps (host.documents.*, host.editors.*) on install; require explicit consent only for sensitive families (host.files.*, secrets:*, network:*). Trust model: user already installed the binary, fine-grained gating is theatre for non-sensitive caps.\n\n(b) Install-time dialog listing all requested caps with grant-all / grant-individually / reject choices. Higher friction, higher transparency. Matches phone/browser-extension UX.\n\n(c) On capability_not_granted error: surface a toast or in-error breadcrumb pointing the user at Plugin Manager → plugin name → toggle. Lower-effort, doesn't fix the discovery problem but improves recovery.\n\nRecommendation deferred to design pass. (a) + (c) hybrid is leanest; (b) is most explicit.\n\nSurfaced during T6 R7 HITL prep, 2026-05-10. Filed as Bug B.","created_at":"2026-05-11T06:08:13","updated_at":"2026-05-11T06:08:13","severity":3,"tags":["broker-hitl-followup","capability-grants","plugin-platform","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e185d20ce7cb4abd942267a86e2b8","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"CAD worker returns empty edges array for some shapes (intermittent, geometry-correlated)","description":"## Symptom\n\nIn Minerva CAD plugin panel: edge clicks do nothing. No errors, no warnings, no apparent tool active. Geometry renders correctly (mesh visible in all 5 panes), but the edge-pick overlay produces no selection. Edge sidebar tree is empty.\n\n## Investigation (2026-05-11 HITL)\n\nOriginally suspected as a regression from the broker DCR phase 5 refactor (commits up to `09c1baf4`). Investigation **cleared the broker refactor** — bug is in the cad plugin worker.\n\n### Sightline-traced root cause\n\nWith diagnostic prints added to `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd` and `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/scripts/Cad_GeometryOverlay.gd` (gated by `_DEBUG_EDGE_PICK`), confirmed across multiple HITL sessions:\n\n**Bug repro session (T-beam with corner holes):**\n```\neval-response eval_result.keys=[\"shape_name\",\"mesh\",\"edges\"]\n              edges_var.type=28 (Array)  edges.size=0\n              mesh.keys=[\"vertices\",\"faces\"]\n_gui_input LMB at ... edges=0  candidates=0    (click reaches overlay, registry is empty)\n```\n\n**No-repro session (simpler T-beam):**\n```\neval-response edges.size=32  (32 edges)\n_gui_input edges=32  candidates=1  path=single emit=8\nCADPanel._on_edge_selected edge_id=8           (end-to-end pick works)\n```\n\nThe worker is returning `edges: []` alongside a populated `mesh` for some shape topologies — confirmed for \"T-beam with corner holes\"-class programs. The panel/IPC/wiring layers are not at fault: same code paths, same wiring, different worker output.\n\n### Ruled out\n\n- Plugin start order (panel created before cad plugin started running): unrelated; edge picking is pure Godot-side, no IPC to plugin process needed for click handling.\n- Signal wiring: `Cad_GeometryOverlay.edge_selected` connects on all 5 overlays, `has_signal=true`, `mouse_filter=PASS`.\n- Mouse input absorption (`AnnotationOverlay` MOUSE_FILTER_STOP theory): clicks DO reach `Cad_GeometryOverlay._gui_input`.\n- Resource cache / plugin reload: bug repros after full Minerva restart + plugin reinstall.\n\n### Root-cause location (next investigator's pickup point)\n\n`~/github/plugins/cad/worker/mcad/evaluator.py:evaluate_source` calls `translator.get_edge_registry(shape_name)` at line 59. The registry is empty when `_eval_assignment` (translator.py:256-258) saw `_pending_edge_registry == []` at the moment of the final `result = ...` assignment.\n\nUpstream candidates:\n- `translator.py:1097` — extrude with profile-vertex propagation path (`_build_extruded_edge_registry`)\n- `translator.py:1102` — extrude fallback path (`_enumerate_edges`)\n- `translator.py:1417` — `_enumerate_edges` returns `[]` if `not hasattr(shape, \"edges\")` OR if every edge classifies as `seam` and is filtered at line 1458.\n\nStrong hypothesis: boolean cut (corner holes) causes the final extrude's `_pending_edge_registry` to either (a) not be set, (b) be cleared by a later operation before assignment, or (c) be populated but all entries classified as seams.\n\n## Active diagnostic instrumentation (please remove or gate when bug is resolved)\n\nDiagnostic prints currently in place on local files (NOT committed to either repo as of filing):\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/CADPanel.gd` — `_DEBUG_EDGE_PICK` const + traces at `_push_mesh_to_geometry_overlays`, eval-response parse site, `_on_edge_selected`.\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/ui/scripts/Cad_GeometryOverlay.gd` — `_DEBUG_EDGE_PICK` const + traces in `_gui_input` and each `edge_selected.emit()` site.\n- `~/github/plugins/cad/worker/mcad/evaluator.py` — `_DEBUG_EDGE_PICK` const + `_dbg()` helper that writes to `/tmp/cad-worker-edges.log` (worker stderr does NOT surface in Godot output console; file is the workaround). Logs shape_type, raw_edge_count, registry keys, and full mcad source on every evaluate.\n\nPickup checklist:\n1. Restart Minerva so cad plugin worker re-spawns with the patched evaluator.\n2. Reproduce by asking the LLM for a T-beam with corner holes (or other boolean-cut shape).\n3. Read `/tmp/cad-worker-edges.log` — confirms which scenario triggers the empty registry:\n   - `shape_has_edges_attr=False` → wrong shape type returned by translator\n   - `raw_edges=0` → topology genuinely edge-less (degenerate)\n   - `raw_edges>0, registry[\"result\"].size=0` → assignment ran but pending was empty\n   - `raw_edges>0, \"result\" not in all_registry_keys` → assignment block never wrote for \"result\"\n4. Also captured in the log: full mcad source that triggered it — compare working vs failing programs to isolate the trigger construct.\n5. Add a regression test in `~/github/plugins/cad/worker/tests/test_dispatcher.py` (or a new `test_edge_registry.py`) that asserts edges > 0 for the failing program once isolated.\n6. Remove the `_DEBUG_EDGE_PICK` instrumentation when the worker fix lands.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nBroker DCR phase 5 HITL (2026-05-11). Investigation pivoted out of broker territory when traces showed the eval response itself carried `edges: []`. Broker refactor is cleared of responsibility.\n\n## Related\n\n- Nudge hint `cad-plugin/edge-click-bug-root-is-worker-returning-empty-edges` (saved 2026-05-11).\n- Nudge hint `minerva-plugin-platform/plugin-reload-doesnt-refresh-open-panel-gd-scripts` (saved 2026-05-11 during investigation).\n- Bug `019dd7162d52789fb055f62c1753e694` (edge_overlay.gd hover-highlight randomly stops) — different file (`edge_overlay.gd` vs `Cad_GeometryOverlay.gd`) and different symptom (hover vs click), but worth checking if both have the same upstream worker cause once this bug is rooted.\n\n## Workaround\n\nNone known beyond \"ask LLM for a different shape\" or \"remove the boolean-cut step\". The bug breaks the edge selection UX entirely for affected shapes.","created_at":"2026-05-11T18:46:56","updated_at":"2026-05-11T18:46:56","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["cad","edges","hitl-2026-05-11","intermittent","translator","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18691ff27574a550515ce534fd88","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Plugin Manager install-error label overflows pane (unreadable error message)","description":"## Symptom\n\nWhen the Plugin Manager surfaces an install-time error, the error label can overflow the pane and the user cannot read the message. Reported 2026-05-11 during phase 5 R7 HITL — the user could see *that* install had failed but not *why*.\n\nIn this specific repro, the error was a `validate_host_capabilities()` rejection: the plugin manifest declared a `host.documents.get_node` capability that wasn't in `PluginDefinition.gd:ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES` (separate bug — fixed in this session). The error message format is at `PluginDefinition.gd:1065-1068`:\n\n```\nunknown host_capability '<cap>' (allowed: <ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES>) or namespaces: <ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITY_NAMESPACES>)\n```\n\nThe full allowed-list enumeration is dozens of characters wide and the namespaces list adds more — this is the message that overflows. Any future expansion of the allow-list makes it worse.\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\nLong error messages should be readable in the Plugin Manager. Acceptable options (any one):\n\n- Wrap the label (`autowrap_mode = AUTOWRAP_WORD_SMART`) so multi-line errors fit the pane vertically.\n- Put the error inside a `ScrollContainer` with `clip_contents = true` so vertical and horizontal overflow is scrollable.\n- Surface long errors as a copyable tooltip / dialog (`AcceptDialog`) when the user clicks the truncated indicator.\n- Truncate to one line in the pane with an ellipsis + \"click to expand\" affordance.\n\nWhichever path is picked, errors should also be copyable (user couldn't `Ctrl+C` the truncated text either).\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Edit any plugin's `manifest.json` to add a non-existent host capability, e.g. `\"host.nonexistent.cap\"` under `permissions.host_capabilities`.\n2. Remove + reinstall the plugin via Plugin Manager.\n3. Observe: error appears in the Plugin Manager pane but the text runs off the right edge / off the bottom and the actual capability name is not visible.\n\n## Note for fixer\n\nWhen fixing this, also audit the other error surfaces the Plugin Manager renders (start failures, manifest-parse errors, capability denials) — they likely share the same label widget and will all benefit from the same fix.\n\n## Surfaced from\n\nPhase 5 R7 HITL, while validating presentation plugin's broker DCR phase 5 migration. The label overflow blocked debugging until we used `sightline` to read the actual error from the Godot console.\n\n## Related\n\n- Allow-list omission that triggered the unreadable error has been fixed in this session (PluginDefinition.gd:ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES now includes phase 5 caps).\n- Nudge hint `minerva-plugin-platform/new-host-capability-needs-allowlist-update` for the three-place lockstep rule.\n- Bug A (manifest re-parse on reload) `019e15a64d6175f99ad68ecd3d2450b7`, Bug B (no install-time grant prompt) `019e15a67f2e755a8ec4d5545dd6a8d4` — same Plugin Manager UX cluster.","created_at":"2026-05-11T19:00:03","updated_at":"2026-05-11T19:00:03","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["error-display","hitl-2026-05-11","plugin-manager","ui","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18946a1b78e7a874eefb817ae665","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Migrate presentation plugin save-shape to blob-contract (phase 5 R3 adoption)","description":"## Context\n\nBroker DCR phase 5 (`019df8e2d093`) shipped a blob-strip substrate in `PluginScenePanelBroker._strip_blobs_for_outbound`: any dict shaped `{__blob__: true, content_type, bytes}` in the panel state gets replaced with `{__blob_handle__: H, content_type}` on outbound (get_state / get_node), with bytes stored in a per-editor blob store reachable via `host.documents.get_blob`. The presentation plugin reshape (R6) updated 16 tool handlers to use the new caps. But the panel's own save-shape was never migrated — image bytes are still stored in the legacy raw-base64 form, so the broker has nothing to strip.\n\n## Symptom (R7 HITL 2026-05-11)\n\n`minerva_presentation_list_slides {tab_name: \"llms_overview_v1.mdeck\"}` (the 58MB image-heavy deck) fails with:\n\n```\nstdin read error: bufio.Scanner: token too long\n```\n\nSame error class phase 5 was designed to fix. The plugin's call to `host.documents.get_node /slides` returns the full slides array including embedded image base64, which exceeds the plugin process's stdin scanner cap (1MB) on the inbound capability-result.\n\n## Files / data shapes\n\n- `~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_model.gd:111-127` — `make_image_tile` produces `{kind:\"image\", src:\"<base64>\", ...}` — change to `{kind:\"image\", src:{__blob__:true, content_type:\"image/...\", bytes:\"<base64>\"}, ...}`.\n- `~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_model.gd:35,276` — `BG_IMAGE = \"image\"` background shape uses `value:\"<base64>\"`. Same wrapping needed.\n- `~/github/plugins/presentation/ui/slide_canvas.gd` (and image tile renderer) — load-side must accept both legacy raw-base64 AND new `__blob_handle__` form. For `__blob_handle__`, fetch via `host.documents.get_blob(editor_name, handle)`.\n- Any other image-byte field surfaces (spreadsheet chart image cells?) — audit before declaring done.\n\n## Backwards compatibility\n\nExisting .mdeck files on disk are in the legacy raw-base64 shape. Load path must handle both shapes silently. Save path should write the new shape, so existing decks slowly migrate as they're saved. Optional: on first load, write the converted shape immediately so subsequent gets are bounded.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. `minerva_presentation_list_slides tab_name=\"llms_overview_v1.mdeck\"` returns <1s with bounded payload.\n2. `minerva_presentation_get_slide slide_index=0` returns clean structure with image tile carrying `__blob_handle__` (not raw bytes).\n3. Image tiles render correctly in the panel after the round-trip (broker rehydrates handle → bytes on inbound `set_state` / `patch_state`).\n4. Image bytes still fetchable via `host.documents.get_blob {editor_name, blob_handle}`.\n5. Legacy raw-base64 .mdeck files still load without error (back-compat).\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent DCR: `019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb` (broker DCR phase 5 rollout)\n- Nudge hint: `minerva-presentation-plugin/blob-contract-adoption-incomplete-stdin-overflow`\n- R3 substrate commits: `c1838bcc` + `5ec76ddf` (Minerva)\n- R3 plugin contract: design doc `Docs/design/host_documents_blob_split.md` §\"Plugin-side contract\"","created_at":"2026-05-11T19:47:20","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:37:26","priority":2,"tags":["blob","follow-up","migration","phase-5","presentation","stdin-overflow"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb","resolution":"SHIPPED. Phase 5 R3 plugin-side adoption complete and HITL-verified on the 58MB image-heavy deck (~/temp/llms_overview_v1.migrated.mdeck).\n\nSix commits across both repos:\n- Minerva user/imran/experiments/swarm: d8f484ca (PluginDefinition allowlist), d0b015d8 (broker accepts base64-String bytes + migration script), 1245f1df (CapabilityBroker get_node buffer-canonical strip — second R3 coupling miss, caught by HITL).\n- ~/github/plugins main: 0d0cd37 (presentation manifest), 5b05182 (GDScript save-shape → blob envelope), d5362e5 (Go set_slide_background + validator hint).\n\nAcceptance:\n1. list_slides on migrated 58MB deck: 19 slides returned, ~2.5KB response, <1s, no bufio.Scanner overflow ✅\n2. get_slide(0): image tiles return as {__blob_handle__, content_type} placeholders ✅\n3. Visual: images render correctly in panel after round-trip (user-confirmed HITL) ✅\n4. Blob store populated; bytes fetchable via host.documents.get_blob (covered by 43/0 in test_host_capability_documents_get_node_blob) ✅\n\nTests: test_panel_state_blob_substitution 116/0, test_slide_model 195/0, test_blob_store 64/0, get_node_blob 43/0, patch_put 82/0, host_owned_save_ipc 24/0, channel 32/0, presentation Go suite clean.\n\nDurable hints saved: 019e18a8fca2 (JSON.stringify PackedByteArray quirk), 019e18b3a56c (Variant-local == bool runtime trap), 019e18d9aa92 (buffer-canonical bypass class).\n\nRound produced two R3-class coupling misses both fixed mid-round (Go-side set_slide_background caught by cold review, broker get_node buffer-canonical caught by HITL). Follow-ups filed as separate work items.</resolution>\n</invoke>\n<invoke name=\"mcp__docket__docket_create\">\n<parameter name=\"project\">minerva"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18e413617b96a627fd300d34a03e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Mirror buffer-canonical strip fix to host.documents.get_state/patch_state/set_state","description":"Phase 5 R7 fixed _handle_host_documents_get_node's buffer-canonical bypass of the strip walker (commit 1245f1df). The same code shape exists in:\n- _handle_host_documents_get_state (CapabilityBroker.gd:~463-488)\n- _handle_host_documents_patch_state (around L590)\n- _handle_host_documents_set_state (around L551)\n\nFor each: PLUGIN_SCENE branch routes on _resolve_editor_buffer. If buffer != null (incidental from path-keyed registry lookup), the buffer-canonical path is taken and the strip walker is NOT called. Same overflow class will fire the moment one of these handlers is exercised on a blob-carrying .mdeck.\n\nRequired: audit each handler, add the idempotent strip call after state is finalized (mirroring 1245f1df's pattern in get_node). Also rehydrate on the inbound side for set_state/patch_state if the caller sends back handles — confirm the broker's _rehydrate_blobs_for_inbound is wired through these paths.\n\nAcceptance:\n- New regression tests covering get_state, patch_state, set_state on a PLUGIN_SCENE editor with an incidental DocumentBuffer (see companion test work item).\n- All existing tests still green.\n- HITL: setting a slide background via MCP set_slide_background (which uses patch_state under the hood) on the 58MB deck must remain bounded.\n\nHint: 019e18d9aa92 captures the class. Reference fix: commit 1245f1df.","created_at":"2026-05-11T21:14:20","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:38:18","priority":2,"tags":["blob-strip","broker","follow-up","phase-5","r3-class"],"resolution":"SCOPE SPLIT (closed via 012a4be6's architectural fix + two refiled follow-ups). Original premise was that get_state/patch_state/set_state would have the same buffer-canonical strip bypass as get_node and need parallel per-handler patches. After the architectural fix at _resolve_editor_buffer landed (commit 012a4be6), the buffer-canonical BRANCH ENTRIES in those three handlers are unreachable for plugin-scene host_owned_save editors — they route through the panel-canonical request_panel_state path, which already strips. So the original mirror-strip work isn't needed there. Cold review found ONE remaining latent gap of the same shape: _describe_editor_state emits buffer.text raw for paired_dsl, which would bypass strip if a future paired_dsl plugin emitted JSON state. That latent gap is refiled as 019e18f86b9e (work_item) with companion test 019e18f89e89."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18e43acc75f0b8e5196877d66d4e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Architectural fix: _resolve_editor_buffer should not return incidental path-keyed buffers for PLUGIN_SCENE editors","description":"Root cause of the phase 5 R7 buffer-canonical bypass class. CapabilityBroker._resolve_editor_buffer (L1595) has fall-through logic:\n1. Try broker-attached buffer (paired_dsl flow). Return if non-null.\n2. Fall through to DocumentRegistry.get_or_create_buffer(ed_file). Return if registry returns ok.\n3. Try anonymous buffer via editor.get_document_buffer().\n4. Return null.\n\nFor PLUGIN_SCENE editors, step 1 is the correct path (paired_dsl). Step 2 incidentally creates a buffer from the file path for host_owned_save panels (e.g. .mdeck) — which then triggers the buffer-canonical branch in all host.documents.* handlers, bypassing panel-canonical (and thus the strip walker).\n\nProposed fix: inside `if ed_type == Editor.Type.PLUGIN_SCENE` (L1604), after checking for the broker-attached buffer, RETURN NULL instead of falling through. PLUGIN_SCENE editors should only be considered buffer-canonical if the broker explicitly attached a buffer (paired_dsl pattern). Path-keyed lookups remain available for text/graphics editors.\n\nThis closes the bypass class at the source — once shipped, the per-handler strip patches added by commits 1245f1df and follow-ups become defensive only. No new strip calls needed in get_state/patch_state/set_state because they'd take the panel-canonical branch in the first place.\n\nRisks:\n- Any code that relied on PLUGIN_SCENE editors being readable via path-keyed buffer lookups (e.g. doc_read on .mdeck) would break. Audit: MCPDocTools._resolve_target has its own logic and may not be affected. Direct calls via host.files.read should still work (different code path).\n- Tests for set_state/get_state buffer-canonical on PLUGIN_SCENE editors would need to switch to panel-canonical OR explicitly call attach_buffer_to_panel.\n\nAcceptance:\n- All existing tests still green.\n- Removing the per-handler strip patches (1245f1df + the get_state/patch_state/set_state follow-ups) still leaves blob-carrying .mdeck working correctly on HITL.\n- doc_read on .mdeck (if exercised today) still works OR is deliberately moved to a different MCP tool.\n\nHint: 019e18d9aa92. Companion work items: get_state/patch_state/set_state mirror fix, regression test.","created_at":"2026-05-11T21:14:30","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:37:55","priority":3,"tags":["architecture","blob-strip","broker","follow-up","phase-5","root-cause"],"resolution":"SHIPPED. _resolve_editor_buffer no longer falls through to path-keyed registry buffers for PLUGIN_SCENE editors — only broker-attached buffers (paired_dsl pattern) count. Per-handler defensive strip in get_node (1245f1df) removed as dead code. Commits: 012a4be6 (main fix + regression test + dead-code removal), be4d7f19 (cold-review fixes: invariant comment on _describe_editor_state + docstring dedupe). Test sweep: 411 broker/capability tests + 195 plugin model tests + 9 new regression tests, all green. Cold-Opus review verdict approve_with_notes; both notes addressed (invariant comment landed inline; missing paired_dsl test filed as 019e18f89e89; latent _describe_editor_state strip gap filed as 019e18f86b9e)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18e479157186880e2f85e2dcb784","type":"test","status":"passing","title":"Regression test: host.documents.get_node on PLUGIN_SCENE editor with incidental DocumentBuffer must strip envelopes","description":"Would have caught the second R3-class miss in phase 5 R7 (broker get_node buffer-canonical strip bypass, fixed in 1245f1df) on layer-1 instead of HITL.\n\nThe bug class: when a PLUGIN_SCENE editor's file path is in DocumentRegistry, _resolve_editor_buffer returns a non-null buffer. Capability handlers then take the buffer-canonical branch (parse buffer.text as JSON) which historically did NOT call the strip walker.\n\nSetup: PLUGIN_SCENE editor mock with editor.type=PLUGIN_SCENE, editor.file/plugin_id/panel_name set. Pre-register a DocumentBuffer in DocumentRegistry with JSON text containing blob envelopes. Do NOT call attach_buffer_to_panel (simulates host_owned_save, not paired_dsl).\n\nAssertions: (1) response success=true; (2) r.value contains tile.src as {__blob_handle__, content_type} placeholder, NOT envelope; (3) broker._blob_store has at least one stored entry; (4) bounded payload size.\n\nCompanion tests for get_state, patch_state, set_state recommended once those handlers are fixed.\n\nLinked: hint 019e18d9aa92, fix commit 1245f1df.","created_at":"2026-05-11T21:14:46","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:37:04","priority":2,"tags":["blob-strip","broker","buffer-canonical","phase-5","regression"],"test_setup":"SceneTree-based integration test extending src/test/test_host_capability_documents_get_node_blob.gd. Helper to register a DocumentBuffer in DocumentRegistry for the test editor's file path.","test_steps":"1. Helper _make_plugin_scene_editor_with_buffered_json(json_text) builds editor mock + stores text in DocumentRegistry.get_or_create_buffer(editor.file).text.\n2. Build test deck JSON with 2-3 image tiles in envelope shape (~100 chars base64 each).\n3. Invoke _handle_host_documents_get_node({editor_name, path:\"/slides\"}).\n4. Assert response shape + tile.src placeholder shape.\n5. Assert broker._blob_store_snapshot has expected blob count.\n6. Assert bounded payload size.","expected_result":"All assertions pass; total wall-clock <1s; test fails loudly if any future change to a buffer-canonical handler bypasses strip again."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18f86b9e726f93841b7861dc7976","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Latent: _describe_editor_state emits buffer.text raw; needs strip pass when paired_dsl plugin emits JSON","description":"Cold-review finding on commit 012a4be6. Currently dormant — no production code triggers it — but structurally same R3 coupling class as phase 5 R7's get_node bypass.\n\n**The latent gap.** CapabilityBroker._describe_editor_state (L1685-1711) sets state[\"buffer_text\"] = buffer.text directly with no strip pass. After the phase 5 R7 R7-follow-up architectural fix at _resolve_editor_buffer, only paired_dsl plugins (via PluginScenePanelBroker.attach_buffer_to_panel) produce buffer != null for plugin-scene editors. Today's only paired_dsl plugin (cad → .mcad) carries plain DSL text — no blob envelopes possible.\n\nIf a future paired_dsl plugin emits JSON state with {__blob__, content_type, bytes} envelopes (plausible: imagine a \"rich-text editor\" plugin paired with JSON text), get_state would return that JSON inline in buffer_text, overflowing plugin stdin or other consumers — same 58MB bug class.\n\n**Fix when needed.** In _describe_editor_state, when buffer != null:\n1. Try JSON.parse_string(buffer.text).\n2. If parse succeeds AND result is Dictionary/Array, run pbroker._strip_blobs_for_outbound(editor_name, parsed, plugin_id), then JSON.stringify and emit the stripped text.\n3. Else emit raw (current behavior — plain DSL or non-JSON).\n\nAlternatively, refactor get_state to NOT use _describe_editor_state for buffer-canonical responses; instead, branch on buffer != null at the handler level and run strip explicitly.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n- New test: a fake paired_dsl plugin emits JSON with __blob__ envelopes; get_state returns __blob_handle__ placeholders, not raw bytes.\n- Existing CAD plugin smoke + paired_dsl flows still work (plain DSL text passes through unchanged).\n- Companion test work item (see new docket item for missing paired_dsl get_state coverage).\n\n**Why filing rather than fixing now.** Currently dormant; the JSON-detect-then-strip-then-restringify logic has complexity that deserves a focused round with proper test scaffolding; the invariant comment landed in this commit gives future implementers visibility.\n\nHint: 019e18d9aa92 (the R3 bypass class). Parent DCR: 019df8e2d0937613a326389a4df133fb. Companion: missing paired_dsl get_state integration test (new docket item).","created_at":"2026-05-11T21:36:34","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:36:34","priority":3,"tags":["blob-strip","broker","follow-up","latent","phase-5","r3-class"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e18f89e897d37b14a0dde1e85ea9b","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Missing test: paired_dsl host.documents.get_state buffer-canonical path coverage","description":"Cold-review finding on commit 012a4be6. The commit message claimed \"37/37 paired_dsl still works\" citing test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd, but that smoke test does NOT exercise the buffer-canonical capability path through host.documents.*. It tests plugin lifecycle (start/stop), tools/list handshake, tool registry signals, restart cycle.\n\nFor phase 5 R7's architectural fix at _resolve_editor_buffer to actually be guarded against future regressions on the paired_dsl side, a dedicated integration test is needed.\n\n**Test scope.**\n1. Open a .mcad editor via _open_paired_dsl (singleton_object.gd:1465) so the broker explicitly attaches a buffer.\n2. Invoke _handle_host_documents_get_state on the resulting editor.\n3. Assert: buffer_canonical=true, buffer_text=<the DSL source>, version/dirty fields present.\n4. Same for _handle_host_documents_get_node on a path that resolves inside the parsed JSON — wait, .mcad text isn't JSON, so this would correctly return not_buffer_canonical. Assert that error code.\n5. Same for patch_state, set_state. set_state should accept buffer_text (paired_dsl write path). patch_state should be not_buffer_canonical (no JSON to patch into).\n\n**Why it matters.** Three R3 coupling misses this rollout. Each was caught by review or HITL, none by a layer-1 test. The pattern of \"broker substrate + one consumer wired, others bypass\" needs explicit per-handler coverage on the paired_dsl side too — not just host_owned_save (which test_resolve_editor_buffer now guards at the unit level).\n\n**Setup complexity.** _open_paired_dsl flow needs SingletonObject + EditorPane to be alive, which is non-trivial in --script mode. May need to adapt the test_cad_plugin_smoke fixture pattern (which does boot SingletonObject) or build a smaller scaffolding.\n\nReference: cold review of commit 012a4be6 (this round). Companion: the latent _describe_editor_state strip gap (new docket item).","created_at":"2026-05-11T21:36:47","updated_at":"2026-05-11T21:36:47","priority":3,"tags":["broker","buffer-canonical","paired-dsl","phase-5","regression","test-coverage"],"test_setup":"SceneTree test that boots enough of Minerva to run _open_paired_dsl (singleton_object.gd:1465). Likely needs SingletonObject autoload, EditorPane child, PluginScenePanelBroker. test_cad_plugin_smoke is the closest existing pattern.","test_steps":"1. Boot fixture; open a .mcad via _open_paired_dsl.\n2. Confirm broker attached the buffer (verify pbroker.get_attached_buffer returns non-null).\n3. Invoke _handle_host_documents_get_state on the editor name.\n4. Assert response has buffer_canonical=true, buffer_text=DSL source, no panel_state key.\n5. Invoke _handle_host_documents_get_node with various paths; assert all return not_buffer_canonical (since buffer.text isn't JSON).\n6. Invoke _handle_host_documents_set_state with new buffer_text; assert write propagates to broker-attached buffer and to disk on save.","expected_result":"All assertions pass; paired_dsl flow protected against future architectural drift; layer-1 test would catch any reintroduction of the R3 coupling class on this side."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1957d815783292d1ff477984a280","type":"test","status":"passing","title":"T7 follow-up: presentation plugin parity through plugin-owned MCP tools","description":"## Background\n\nT7 (parent) deliberately deferred case 6 of the broker-DCR integration-test plan to this follow-up because T6 (Migrate presentation MCP tools into plugin) is still in_progress as of 2026-05-11. Once the presentation plugin owns the MCP tools (create_deck, open_deck, add_slide, add_text_tile, add_image_tile, modify_tile, list_slides, get_slide, get_tile, z-order reorder), this test exercises the entire surface end-to-end through the plugin backend's stdio + host capabilities.\n\n## Test plan\n\nMirror test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd's existing in-core coverage, but drive every tool call through the running presentation plugin connection rather than the in-core MCPPresentationTools module. Verify:\n\n- Tool discovery: tools/list contains the migrated presentation tools (with minerva_presentation_* names).\n- Round trip: create_deck → add_slide → add_text_tile → modify_tile → get_tile returns mutated state.\n- host.documents.set_state path actually mutates the deck JSON via the plugin's panel broker (paired with the case-3 mutate test from T7).\n- Reorder semantics: z-order move_slide updates list_slides ordering.\n- Image tile creation + retrieval.\n\n## Done when\n\n- All assertions pass against a fresh Minerva binary with the presentation plugin installed and running.\n- Test fails if any presentation-specific MCP tool is still resident in Minerva core (regression guard for the migration direction).\n\n## Blocked by\n\nT6: minerva 019df8e3 sibling — confirm exact ID once T6 transitions to implementing/reviewing.","created_at":"2026-05-11T23:20:47","updated_at":"2026-05-12T05:27:07","priority":2,"tags":["broker-dcr","mcp","plugins","presentation","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e3c9d57472a7d721785f5f783c"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e19654be771c0b6f7b3d740180954","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"PluginPolicy.gd persists grants to disk, leaking state across test runs","description":"## Repro\n\n```\ngodot --headless --path src --script test/test_host_capability_documents.gd\n```\n\nFirst run after wiping `user://plugins/policy.json`: passes 72/0.\nEvery subsequent run: 70/2 — fails the unit-test deny-path assertions at lines 174-188 of `src/test/test_host_capability_documents.gd`.\n\nFailing assertions:\n- `ungranted capability has structured error_code` — gets `schema_validation_failed` instead of `capability_not_granted`.\n- `audit log records denied entry` — denied entries: [] (no policy denial fired).\n\n## Root cause\n\n`PluginPolicy._init()` calls `_load()` at `src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/PluginPolicy.gd:52`, deserializing `user://plugins/policy.json` into `_grants`. `grant_capability()` calls `_save()` at `:72`, persisting every grant back to disk.\n\nThe unit test's deny-path setup:\n1. Line 132-133: `policy.grant_capability(\"doc_probe_test\", \"host.documents.list_open\")` / `get_state`.\n2. Line 174-188: `broker.dispatch(\"doc_probe_test\", \"host.documents.set_state\", {\"editor_name\": \"x\"})` and assert `capability_not_granted`.\n3. Line 195-196: `policy.grant_capability(\"doc_probe_test\", \"host.documents.set_state\")` / `mark_dirty` (so the next blocks can exercise the granted set_state path).\n\nOn every run after the first, step 3's prior grants are loaded by step 0's `_init()` → step 2's dispatch sees set_state IS granted → bypasses the policy deny → enters the set_state handler → schema validation fires (no `buffer_text`) → returns `schema_validation_failed`.\n\nVerified by inspection:\n\n```\n$ cat ~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/policy.json\n{\n  \"grants\": {\n    \"doc_probe_test\": [\n      \"host.documents.list_open\",\n      \"host.documents.get_state\",\n      \"host.documents.set_state\",\n      \"host.documents.mark_dirty\"\n    ],\n    ... (other probe ids from sibling tests, all persistent)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Why it didn't get caught in CI\n\nCI presumably uses a clean Godot user data dir per job — the first-run case. Local devs hit it on the second+ run with no clear \"why did this just start failing\" signal.\n\n## Affected tests\n\n`test_host_capability_documents.gd` — the explicit failure.\nLikely also affects every other test that uses a probe plugin_id and asserts deny semantics. (`capability_probe`, `get_blob_test`, `get_node_test`, `patch_test`, `put_blob_test`, `probe_test` are all present in the live policy.json.)\n\n## Suggested fix shape\n\nThree viable options, in order of preference:\n\n1. **Constructor flag for no-persistence mode** — add `PluginPolicy.new(plugin_db, audit_log, persist := true)` and have all `extends SceneTree` tests construct with `persist = false`. `_load()` and `_save()` become no-ops when persistence is off. Smallest blast radius.\n\n2. **Test-side teardown** — every test that grants probe capabilities revokes them on exit. Brittle: easy to forget; partial-failure paths leave grants behind.\n\n3. **Override POLICY_PATH** — let the test instance point at a temp file. Requires injecting the path into the constructor, similar to (1) but more friction.\n\n(1) is the right one — it also fixes the parallel concern that running the full local test suite would interleave grants from one test with denies expected by another.\n\n## Tracking\n\nDiscovered while diagnosing pre-existing 2/70 failure pattern in `test_host_capability_documents.gd` during T7 cases-3+4 round (commit f31f224e). Hint saved as `minerva-testing/plugin-policy-persists-grants-across-runs`.","created_at":"2026-05-11T23:35:29","updated_at":"2026-05-11T23:40:12","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["broker-dcr","plugin-policy","stateful-fixture","testing"],"resolution":"Fixed on swarm at commit d093c85a. PluginPolicy gained a `p_persist: bool = true` ctor flag (production keeps default; --script tests pass false). _save short-circuits, _load skipped at construction. 7 affected suites verified green (311/0): documents 72/0, patch_put 82/0, get_node_blob 43/0, resolve_editor_buffer 9/0, channel 32/0, T7 mutate 36/0, cad smoke 37/0. Removed an obsolete workaround comment that previously documented the manual \"wipe policy.json before each run\" workaround.","environment":"godot 4.6.1 / linux x86_64 / branch user/imran/experiments/swarm"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1985ae3970c9845f0ee5a1735ee9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T6 tail: migrate the 8 remaining presentation MCP tools out of Minerva core","description":"## Discovered by\n\nSpike `src/test/test_presentation_plugin_spike.gd` on branch `user/imran/experiments/swarm` (2026-05-11) while unblocking T7 case 6.\n\n## Problem\n\nT6's resolution note (2026-05-11 21:44:27) claims: *\"Minerva no longer contains presentation-specific MCP tool implementation.\"* That claim is incorrect.\n\n`src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MinervaMCPServer.gd:116` still instantiates `MCPPresentationTools.new(self)`, which registers 8 `minerva_presentation_*` tools that the plugin does NOT own:\n\n```\nopen_deck\nadd_image_tile\nmodify_tile\nadd_annotation\nremove_annotation\nset_annotation_resolved\nlist_open_annotations\nget_state\n```\n\nThe plugin owns a disjoint 19 (`create_deck`, `add_slide`, `add_text_tile`, `add_spreadsheet_tile`, etc.).\n\n## Why this matters\n\n1. **T7 case 6** (sibling test task `019df8e3c9d57472a7d721785f5f783c` → follow-up `019e1957d815783292d1ff477984a280`) has done-when *\"Tests fail if presentation-specific MCP tools are required in Minerva core.\"* The regression guard will fail until these 8 are gone (or migrated to the plugin).\n2. **DCR `019df8e2d093` shipped-criterion** — the DCR's acceptance criteria includes *\"Presentation MCP tools can be removed from Minerva core and implemented in `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation` without losing live-tab behavior.\"* Currently only 19/27 tools are migrated.\n3. **User-facing inconsistency** — users see two sets of presentation tools in the LLM tool registry: core's 8 + plugin's 19, with no clear separation.\n\n## The 8 tools' significance\n\nThese aren't trivial leftovers. They include core LLM use cases:\n- `add_image_tile` — every \"add this picture\" request\n- `modify_tile` — every text/style/coord/rotation edit\n- `add_annotation` / `remove_annotation` / `set_annotation_resolved` / `list_open_annotations` — entire collaboration surface\n- `open_deck` — file-open\n- `get_state` — full-state inspection\n\n## Done when\n\n- `grep -rn \"minerva_presentation_\" src/Scripts/` matches zero lines.\n- `MCPPresentationTools.gd` deleted OR fully unwired from MinervaMCPServer.\n- Plugin `tools/list` advertises a 27-tool surface that includes the 8 currently-in-core names.\n- Plugin parity test (sibling case 6) regression guard passes.\n- HITL verification on a deck that exercises image tiles, modify_tile, and annotations.\n\n## Pointers\n\n- Core impl: `src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPresentationTools.gd` (lines 105-112 list the names; 154-290 register; 317-onwards handle).\n- Plugin: `/home/imran/github/plugins/presentation/` — `main.go` + tool definitions.\n- Migration pattern proven by Phase 5 R5/R6 for the 19 already moved.\n- Image tiles will need the host.documents.put_blob path (already used by the migrated 19).\n\n## Discovery hint\n\nSaved as nudge `minerva-plugin-platform/t6-presentation-migration-incomplete`.","created_at":"2026-05-12T00:10:51","updated_at":"2026-05-12T04:21:05","priority":2,"tags":["broker-dcr-tail","mcp","migration","plugins","presentation"],"parent":"minerva:019df8e3c3937dda9f7abeebf5f12eff","resolution":"T6 tail complete 2026-05-12. All 8 leftover presentation tools migrated across 6 rounds + 2 cleanup commits on swarm:\n\nR1 list_open_annotations (plugin 97cc584 / Minerva 44b6c2ff)\nR2 add+remove+set_resolved annotations (plugin a36c021 / Minerva 6a3b1f79)\nR3 modify_tile (plugin bc13280 / Minerva 4a4d4564)\nR4 add_image_tile + source_graphics_editor (plugin f448f06 / Minerva 75789937)\nR5 get_state via host_owned_save _ui_state piggyback (plugin 17859c1 / Minerva d9c44c08)\nCleanup: dead-handler removal from earlier T6 rounds (Minerva eb66d5f8)\nR6 open_deck + new host.editors.open capability + delete MCPPresentationTools.gd entirely (plugin 21fa6ac / Minerva 6914792a)\n\nNet Minerva diff: −1980 lines (MCPPresentationTools.gd + test_mcp_presentation_tools.gd both deleted). Plugin Go suite: 44 new tests across R1-R6. Manifest gained host.editors.export + host.editors.open.\n\nNew substrate: `host.editors.open` capability + `host_owned_save` _ui_state piggyback pattern (saved as nudge).\n\nCumulative regression guards through R6: channel 32/32, documents 72/72, mutate 36/36, cad smoke 37/37.\n\nDCR 019df8e2d093 acceptance criterion \"Minerva no longer contains presentation-specific MCP tool implementation\" is now actually met. T7 case 6 follow-up (019e1957d815) regression-guard \"no minerva_presentation_* in core\" is unblocked."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1a5ad6ec709784b205bba17a98d4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T6 tail R5: migrate get_state — needs panel UI-state IPC extension","description":"## Problem\n\nminerva_presentation_get_state returns selected_slide_index, slide_count, and the title of the selected slide. selected_slide_index is **panel transient UI state** (which slide the user is currently viewing) — NOT in the document buffer/deck JSON. Cannot be migrated to the plugin via the current host capability surface.\n\n## Current core impl\n\n`MCPPresentationTools._get_state` (lines 557+) calls `panel.get_selected_slide_index()` on the live `SlideEditorPanel` Godot Control. The panel exposes this method directly via the in-process panel-finding helper. The plugin (Go subprocess) has no equivalent path.\n\n## Substrate options\n\n**Option A — extend host_owned_save.get_response with ui_state**\n\nModify `SlideEditorPanel._handle_host_owned_save_get` to emit:\n```\n{success: true, state: _deck.duplicate(true), ui_state: {selected_slide_index: N}}\n```\n\nPlugin tool calls host.documents.get_state, reads ui_state from the response. Risk: the broker's R3 blob-strip walker may or may not preserve unknown sibling keys (need to verify); set_request must ignore ui_state when applying state back. Probably the cleanest path — small panel-script change + plugin-side extraction.\n\n**Option B — new host.panels.get_ui_state capability**\n\nAdd a generic capability that returns transient UI state independent of saved document state. Plugin manifest declares it; new broker handler + new panel IPC channel host_panels.get_ui_state_request. Cleanest separation of concerns but most code.\n\n**Option C — partial migration**\n\nPlugin returns slide_count + first_slide_title with selected_slide_index = null and a documented gap. Honest but breaks LLM workflows that rely on \"what slide is the user on?\".\n\n## Recommendation\n\nOption A is the right balance. ~30 lines plugin + ~10 lines GDScript panel update + Go extraction.\n\n## Tracking\n\nTail of T6 (~/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPresentationTools.gd:557). Once shipped, only open_deck (R6) remains in core, and core MCPPresentationTools.gd can be deleted entirely.\n\nVerified by inspection 2026-05-12 across both repos. The plugin's manifest covers host.documents.* + host.editors.export but not host.panels.* (doesn't exist yet).","created_at":"2026-05-12T04:03:41","updated_at":"2026-05-12T04:21:10","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","migration","plugins","presentation","substrate-extension"],"parent":"minerva:019e1985ae3970c9845f0ee5a1735ee9","resolution":"Shipped via T6 tail R5 (plugin 17859c1 / Minerva d9c44c08). Used Option A: extended SlideEditorPanel._handle_host_owned_save_get response with a _ui_state sibling inside state; set_request strips it before applying. Pattern saved as nudge minerva-plugin-platform/host-owned-save-ui-state-piggyback-pattern."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1a5af4337adbb525db2706326472","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T6 tail R6: migrate open_deck — needs host.editors.open capability","description":"## Problem\n\nminerva_presentation_open_deck opens an existing .mdeck file as a presentation editor tab — either focuses the existing tab if already open, or creates a new plugin-scene editor wired to the file. The \"create a new plugin-scene editor for this path\" operation has no host capability yet.\n\n## Current core impl\n\n`MCPPresentationTools._open_deck` (lines 196+) calls `_find_panel_for_path(path)` then falls through to `_open_deck_via_editor_pane` which constructs a plugin-scene editor via `Editor.create_plugin_scene` and attaches it to EditorPane. All in-process Godot work.\n\n## What the plugin needs\n\nA capability like:\n\n```\nhost.editors.open {\n  path: \"/abs/path.mdeck\",\n  plugin_id: \"presentation\",\n  panel_name: \"slide_editor_panel\"\n}\n→ {success: true, editor_name: \"deck.mdeck\", was_already_open: bool}\n```\n\nThe host (Minerva) does the lookup-or-create and returns the resulting tab_name. Plugin's `minerva_presentation_open_deck` becomes a thin wrapper.\n\n## Substrate work needed\n\n- Broker dispatch case + handler `_handle_host_editors_open` in CapabilityBroker.gd.\n- Editor creation via Editor.create_plugin_scene (already wraps the heavy lifting).\n- Allowlist host.editors.open in PluginDefinition's ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES (otherwise plugin install fails — see nudge minerva-plugin-platform/new-host-capability-needs-allowlist-update).\n- Plugin manifest declares the capability.\n- Plugin tool implementation.\n\n## Plugin side\n\nTrivial once the capability exists: `client.OpenEditor(path, \"presentation\", \"slide_editor_panel\")` returns the tab_name. The tool wraps it.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nAdd the capability as part of a broader \"editor lifecycle\" pass that might also cover host.editors.focus, host.editors.close, etc. Worth scoping as a single round rather than just open.\n\n## Blocks\n\nDCR `019df8e2d093` from transitioning `reviewing` → `shipped` (case 6 of T7 regression-guards \"no minerva_presentation_* tools in core\", which today is satisfied except for get_state + open_deck).\n\nVerified by inspection 2026-05-12.","created_at":"2026-05-12T04:03:48","updated_at":"2026-05-12T04:21:13","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","migration","plugins","presentation","substrate-extension"],"parent":"minerva:019e1985ae3970c9845f0ee5a1735ee9","resolution":"Shipped via T6 tail R6 (plugin 21fa6ac / Minerva 6914792a). Added host.editors.open capability — generic wrapper around SingletonObject.open_file_at_path. Args: {path}. Idempotent. Allowlisted in PluginDefinition.ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES. Plugin manifest declares it. Plugin's open_deck wraps OpenEditor + a follow-up get_state for slide_count."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Scansort plugin — functionally-equivalent redesign","created_at":"2026-05-12T15:43:12","updated_at":"2026-05-14T04:31:55","tags":["dcr","plugin","scansort","tax","vault"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1cdb9e197689b4163764d49f7ca2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1: host.providers.chat capability (keystone)","description":"# T1 — `host.providers.chat` capability design (v2)\n\n**Status:** design draft, HITL review round 2 (after first-pass corrections).\n**Owner DCR:** `019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1` (scansort plugin redesign).\n\n## How it fits — canonical, not provider-specific\n\nThe plugin sends a **canonical wire envelope** that mirrors `ChatHistoryItem` (`src/Scripts/Models/ChatHistoryItem.gd`). The broker reconstructs ChatHistoryItems, selects a provider via `ProviderModelManager`, then calls `provider.generate_content(prompt, additional_params)`. Each provider's existing `Format(chi)` translates to its native API shape (Anthropic blocks, Google parts, OpenAI messages, Core/Turnrock, Ollama, ChatGPT). **Plugin never knows the provider-specific shape.**\n\nFirst provider in scope: **Core (Turnrock) \"model-chat\"** — free, no budget pressure, default for scansort.\nProvider matrix the design must accommodate: GOOGLE, OPENAI, ANTHROPIC, LOCAL (Ollama), TURNROCK, OPENROUTER, CLAUDE_CODE, CHATGPT — all image-capable variants where supported (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI GPT-4/5 vision, Google Gemini, OpenRouter passthrough, ChatGPT, Ollama vision models).\n\n## The 8 design questions (regrounded)\n\n### Q1 — Envelope shape (canonical, not provider-specific)\n\n**Decision:** Mirror Minerva's internal `ChatHistoryItem` over the wire. One message per ChatHistoryItem.\n\nRequest:\n```json\n{\n  \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\",   // or \"default\"; resolved by ProviderModelManager\n  \"provider\": \"anthropic\",         // optional; if model is ambiguous across providers\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"system\",    \"text\": \"You classify tax documents.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\",      \"text\": \"What is this?\", \"images\": [\"<base64-png>\", \"...\"]},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"text\": \"It's a W-2.\"}\n  ],\n  \"max_tokens\": 4096,              // optional, additional_params\n  \"temperature\": 0.0               // optional, additional_params\n}\n```\n\nMapping to ChatHistoryItem:\n- `role` → `ChatHistoryItem.ChatRole` (USER, ASSISTANT, SYSTEM, MODEL, TOOL). Plugin uses lowercase strings; broker maps.\n- `text` → `Message` field (single string per message; matches current `Message: String` shape).\n- `images` → `Array[Image]` reconstructed via `Image.load_png_from_buffer(Marshalls.base64_to_raw(b64))` — same pattern as `ChatHistoryItem.Deserialize` lines 332–340.\n\nResponse:\n```json\n{\n  \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\",\n  \"provider\": \"anthropic\",\n  \"content\": \"...\",                  // BotResponse.FullText\n  \"stop_reason\": \"end_turn\",         // best-effort, from BotResponse\n  \"usage\": {\n    \"input_tokens\": 1234,\n    \"output_tokens\": 567,\n    \"cost_usd\": 0.0123,\n    \"free\": false                    // true for Core/Ollama\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRationale: scansort sends `system + user(+image)` for classification. Same shape works for any future plugin chat use case without re-design.\n\n### Q2 — Vision: base64 PNG, matching existing serialize convention\n\n**Decision:** Inline base64-PNG strings in `messages[].images[]`. Same wire format as `ChatHistoryItem.Serialize()` already produces.\n\n- Broker reconstructs `Array[Image]` exactly as `Deserialize` does.\n- Provider's `Format(chi)` already iterates `chi.Images` and emits provider-native image blocks (Anthropic base64 source, OpenAI image_url, Google inline_data parts, etc.).\n- **Proposed cap:** 10MB total request payload. Reject `payload_too_large` if exceeded.\n\nRationale: zero new code path for image handling — the plugin's wire format IS the serialize format the codebase already uses.\n\n### Q3 — Streaming: not in v1\n\n**Decision:** Complete-only. ✓ confirmed.\n\nRationale: most current providers already return full responses; broker dispatch is request/response. Streaming would need a second IPC channel back to the plugin. Defer.\n\n### Q4 — Model selection ✓\n\n**Decision:** Plugin requests by `model` (+ optional `provider` to disambiguate); host policy can override.\n\nResolution:\n1. `model == \"default\"` → user's default in AI Settings.\n2. Lookup in `ProviderModelManager`. Multiple providers for same model name (e.g. Sonnet via Anthropic vs Claude Code vs OpenRouter) → use `provider` field to disambiguate. Absent + ambiguous → `model_ambiguous` error listing candidates.\n3. Plugin policy (Q5/Q6) may rewrite model. Response always returns the actual model + provider used.\n\n### Q5 — Tool calls within plugin LLM: **NO in v1**, with explicit \"why\"\n\nUser asked: \"what tool calls would we need and why?\"\n\n**Answer:** For scansort: zero. Classification is pure text-vision-in → text-out. No tool calls.\n\nWhat \"tool calls\" would mean if enabled:\n- The plugin's LLM, given a prompt, could decide to call MCP tools (e.g. `minerva_doc_read`, `host.files.read`) from inside its response — agentic behavior.\n- **Confused-deputy risk:** the LLM's prompt comes from the plugin's input (potentially user-supplied). A crafted PDF could prompt-inject \"call host.files.write to /etc/passwd\". The LLM has the plugin's permissions; the plugin's prompt may not deserve those permissions for this call.\n- Solving correctly = re-authorize every tool call from inside an LLM turn, with provenance tracking. Real work, real surface, real testing burden.\n\n**Decision:** No tool calls in v1. If an agentic plugin (autocoder, scansort v2 with rule-suggestion) needs this, file `host.providers.chat.agentic` as a follow-up DCR with the auth model worked out. ✓\n\n### Q6 — Cost attribution + per-(provider, model) budgets with infinity\n\nUser's correction: \"Sonnet over anthropic is different billing than Sonnet over AWS. Per-model AND per-provider budgets. Infinity is valid.\"\n\n**Decision:** Extend `CostTracker` with **hierarchical budget keys**.\n\nBudget key grammar (used for both setting and checking):\n```\nplugin/<plugin_id>                              # plugin total (across all providers/models)\nplugin/<plugin_id>/<provider>                   # per-(plugin, provider)\nplugin/<plugin_id>/<provider>/<model>           # per-(plugin, provider, model)\n```\n\n**Resolution at call time:** Check most-specific first; if specific has no budget, fall back up. ANY budget in the chain that is exceeded → deny the call. A budget set to **infinity** (sentinel: `budget_usd = -1.0`) means \"explicitly unlimited\" — never denies at that level, but does NOT preempt parent-level limits.\n\n**Storage:** existing `_budgets: Dictionary = {key -> {budget_usd, warn_pct, period}}` already supports arbitrary string keys — no schema change needed in `_budgets`. Extension is in the **check** path: walk the key hierarchy from most-specific to least-specific.\n\n**Recording cost entries:** `record_chat_cost(bot_response, chat_id)` already records `{ts, provider, model, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost_usd, chat_id}` (CostTracker.gd:88). For plugin calls, we pass `chat_id = \"plugin/<plugin_id>\"` so the entry is attributed to the plugin's total bucket; **per-provider/per-model spend is derivable by filtering on `provider` + `model` columns** which the entry already carries. New helper: `get_spend_for_key(key, period)` walks entries and matches the key prefix.\n\n**Infinity semantics:**\n- `budget_usd = -1.0` → \"infinity\" — call always passes at this level.\n- `budget_usd = 0` or no entry → \"no budget set\" — falls through to parent level.\n- `budget_usd > 0` → real cap.\n\n**Free providers (Core/Turnrock model-chat, Ollama):** cost_usd entries are recorded as `0.0` (already happens — Core has `input_token_cost = 0.0`). Budgets still apply but are never hit. Response flags `usage.free = true` for clarity.\n\n**Defaults:** no built-in default budget for plugins. If the user wants to cap a plugin, they set one explicitly. (We could change this later.)\n\n### Q7 — Cancellation\n\n**Decision:** Best-effort. ✓\n\n- Plugin-initiated: plugin stdio request closes → broker's `await generate_content` continues but result is discarded. HTTP request leaks until provider's own timeout (≤120s).\n- Host-initiated: `SingletonObject.stop_all_requests(history_id)` works because we set `owner_history_id = \"plugin/<plugin_id>\"`.\n\n### Q8 — What should move from deferred to v1?\n\nUser asked me to check. **My recommendation: nothing moves in.** Audit:\n\n| Deferred item | Move to v1? | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Streaming | No | Scansort one-shot. Most providers return whole responses anyway. |\n| Tool calls in plugin LLM | No | Confused-deputy work to solve. No use case in scansort. |\n| Blob-ref images | No | Scansort already has bytes in memory; base64 is the same convention as core. |\n| Manifest model gating | No | Q6 budget layer handles cost control. Less manifest grammar churn. |\n| Multi-turn state held by host | No | Plugin sends full message array each call; statelessness is simpler. |\n| Embeddings (`host.providers.embed`) | No | Scansort uses classification, not embeddings. Separate DCR if any plugin needs it. |\n\nIf you want any of these in v1, say which.\n\n## Manifest declaration\n\nPlugin declares `host.providers.chat` in `permissions.host_capabilities`. Add to `PluginDefinition.gd:ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES` (line 161). **No sub-capability syntax** — model/provider restrictions go through Q6 budget layer, not the manifest.\n\n## New error codes (PluginErrors.gd)\n\n- `model_not_available` — Q4 lookup miss\n- `model_ambiguous` — Q4 multiple providers, no provider hint\n- `provider_disabled` — provider exists but user hasn't enabled it / no API key\n- `budget_exceeded` — Q6 budget hit (response includes `which_budget` field naming the key)\n- `payload_too_large` — Q2 image cap exceeded\n- `provider_error` — wrap provider HTTP failure (include status code in error_message)\n\n## What HITL needs to confirm (round 2)\n\n1. ✓ Wire shape mirrors ChatHistoryItem (role/text/images), broker reconstructs.\n2. ✓ Images = base64-PNG matching existing serialize convention.\n3. ✓ No streaming v1.\n4. ✓ Model resolution via ProviderModelManager + optional `provider` disambiguator.\n5. ✓ No tool calls v1 (confused-deputy risk explained).\n6. **NEW:** Hierarchical budget keys (`plugin/<id>`, `plugin/<id>/<provider>`, `plugin/<id>/<provider>/<model>`), infinity = `-1.0`, no defaults. **Confirm or specify a default policy.**\n7. ✓ Best-effort cancellation.\n8. ✓ Nothing moves from deferred to v1 (or specify what should).\n\n## Implementation deliverables (post-approval, WORK-CYCLE)\n\n- `host.providers.chat` handler in `CapabilityBroker.gd` (handle wire→ChatHistoryItem reconstruction, provider resolution, hierarchical budget check, audit, dispatch).\n- `CostTracker.get_spend_for_key(key, period)` + hierarchical `check_budget(plugin_id, provider, model)` helper. Infinity sentinel handling.\n- New MCP tools (optional this round, separate task): `minerva_plugin_set_budget(key, budget_usd, period)`, `minerva_plugin_get_budget(key)`, `minerva_plugin_budget_summary(plugin_id)`.\n- `PluginErrors.gd` new error codes.\n- `ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES` updated in `PluginDefinition.gd`.\n- Test fixture `provider_probe` (Python plugin under `src/test/fixtures/`) declaring `host.providers.chat`, with tools `chat_text` / `chat_vision`.\n- Integration test `test_host_capability_providers.gd`:\n  - happy path text-only via Core (free)\n  - happy path with image (base64 round-trip)\n  - model_not_available / model_ambiguous denial\n  - budget_exceeded at each hierarchy level\n  - infinity budget passes\n  - audit log records `{plugin_id, provider, model, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost_usd}`\n  - deny path: plugin without `host.providers.chat` declared.","created_at":"2026-05-12T15:43:35","updated_at":"2026-05-12T17:28:48","tags":["broker","capability","design-gate","plugin","providers","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1","resolution":"Shipped via commits 778f9007 (R1: broker handler + hierarchical budgets, 23/0 unit) and c9e3fa4a (R2: integration test, 45/0 + 23/0 + 32/32 regression). Cold Opus reviewed both rounds: approve_with_notes. 4 follow-ups filed under T1 for advisory notes (static-model cache, _redact_args nested arrays, cosmetic, tighten case 11). Branch user/imran/experiments/swarm pushed to origin.</resolution>\n</invoke>"}
