{"_type":"item","id":"019e1d1079bd70c0b1fc5f65feab0175","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T1 followup: cache static-model lookup in broker","description":"From T1 R1 cold review (commit 778f9007): the static-model resolution path in `_handle_host_providers_chat` (CapabilityBroker.gd:1601-1622) instantiates every static provider script via `pscript.new()` + `inst.free()` per call just to read `model_name`. Cache `model_name → [(provider_name, provider_id)]` at broker init (or first call, lazy). Measurable cost on every chat call; scansort hot path.\n\nAcceptance: lookup cost goes from O(N_providers * instantiation) per call to O(1) dict lookup after first call. Cache invalidates on `_dynamic_provider_map` change (probably never during a session — confirm).","created_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:19","updated_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:19","tags":["T1","broker","followup","perf","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb9e197689b4163764d49f7ca2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1d1086ed7b57ae6e59d7646950ee","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T1 followup: _redact_args nested-array image redaction","description":"From T1 R1 cold review (commit 778f9007): `CapabilityBroker._redact_args` (~line 2225) only depth-recurses Dictionaries, never Arrays. As a result, `messages[].images[]` base64-PNG strings passed via `host.providers.chat` land in audit logs verbatim (truncated by the existing length cap but still present). Severity: medium — privacy / log bloat, not exploit.\n\nAcceptance: extend `_redact_args` to walk Arrays, and truncate long base64-looking strings under known image-bearing keys (`images`, `image`, `data`, `bytes_b64`). Add a redaction unit test for the nested-array case.","created_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:22","updated_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:22","tags":["T1","audit","broker","followup","scansort","security"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb9e197689b4163764d49f7ca2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1d1091a27af1bc091b9e09f1de61","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T1 followup: cosmetic fixes (constant ref + comment header)","description":"From T1 R1 cold review (commit 778f9007), two small cleanups to fold into a future touch:\n\n1. `CapabilityBroker.gd:1672` hardcodes `>= 10000` for dynamic-model id threshold. Use `SingletonObject.DYNAMIC_MODEL_ID_BASE` constant to prevent drift if the base value changes.\n2. `PluginErrors.gd:286` has a comment header \"Success helper\" but the block below is error helpers. Either move the header or remove it.\n\nBundle with next broker touch; do not file a dedicated PR.","created_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:25","updated_at":"2026-05-12T16:41:25","tags":["T1","cosmetic","followup","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb9e197689b4163764d49f7ca2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1d3be1447f9bb58de699a019a8dd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T1 followup: tighten case 11 + comment private-state pokes","description":"From T1 R2 cold review (commit c9e3fa4a), three small advisory notes:\n\n1. Case 11 (wire reconstruction smoke) currently asserts the result code is NOT schema_validation_failed AND NOT payload_too_large — a list-of-allowed-codes approach. Once the test env has a deterministic mock provider, tighten to the single expected outcome (likely `provider_disabled` for Core or `model_not_available` for a synthetic id).\n\n2. Test file uses `SingletonObject.cost_tracker._ledger.append(...)`, `_ledger.clear()`, and mutates `_enabled_providers` directly. These private-state pokes are correct but unannotated — add a one-line comment justifying each (or extract a `_test_inject_ledger_entry()` helper).\n\n3. Case 9 doesn't explicitly clear the ledger before its set_budget calls. Currently safe because case 8 teardown ran clear(), but fragile if cases reorder. Add an explicit ledger clear at the start of case 9 (defensive).\n\nBundle into a future test pass. Not urgent.","created_at":"2026-05-12T17:28:44","updated_at":"2026-05-12T17:28:44","tags":["T1","cosmetic","followup","scansort","test"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb9e197689b4163764d49f7ca2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1ff41d33774b8ba7633c9abce797","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T5a: host.permissions.grant_scope — runtime filesystem scope expansion","description":"**Prerequisite for T5 (vault port).** Adds a new broker capability `host.permissions.grant_scope(path, reason?)` that lets plugins request runtime expansion of their `filesystem.scoped_paths` with explicit user confirmation.\n\n## Why\n\nMinerva's plugin loader does not expand `~`, `$HOME`, or env vars in `permissions.filesystem.paths` — only `user://` is expanded. This means a plugin like scansort cannot declare a static manifest scope at `~/Documents/Scansort/` because that path is interpreted literally. The realistic alternatives without this cap are: vaults live in `user://plugins/data/scansort/` (hidden Godot user data dir, bad UX) or hardcoded absolute paths (broken for multi-machine use).\n\n`host.permissions.grant_scope` makes the user the authz primitive at runtime, the same way `host.dialogs.*` (T3) made them the authz primitive for path SELECTION. Together they let scansort flow naturally: user picks path → plugin requests scope grant → user confirms → host.files.* works.\n\n## Spec\n\n**Args:** `{path: String, reason?: String}`\n- `path`: absolute path (no `..`, no null bytes — same rules as validate_files_path)\n- `reason`: optional human-readable explanation shown in the confirm dialog\n\n**Behavior:**\n1. Strict arg allowlist check\n2. Path syntax validation (absolute, no `..`, no null bytes) — but NOT scope validation, since this cap EXPANDS scope\n3. If path is already in `def.filesystem_paths`, return `{granted: true, already_granted: true, path}` without dialog\n4. Pop user confirmation dialog (reuse the `_test_dialog_override` pattern from T3 — `_test_grant_override` for tests OR just reuse `_test_dialog_override`)\n5. On approve: append to `def.filesystem_paths` in memory; persist to `user://plugins/scoped_paths_grants.json` (per-plugin map: `{plugin_id: [path, ...]}`)\n6. On deny: `{granted: false, cancelled: true, path}`\n7. Headless + no override: `dialog_unavailable` (same shape as T3 pickers)\n\n**Returns:** `{granted: bool, already_granted: bool, cancelled: bool, path: String}`\n\n**Persistence:**\n- New file `user://plugins/scoped_paths_grants.json` shape: `{plugin_id: [path, path, ...]}`\n- Loaded at plugin install/start; merged with manifest's filesystem.paths to form `def.filesystem_paths`\n\n**Manifest:**\n- Add `host.permissions.grant_scope` to `ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES` in `PluginDefinition.gd`\n- NO new permission category required (scope-escape by design, user is gate)\n- NO filesystem cross-check (the cap can't require pre-existing scope — it GRANTS scope)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n1. Broker dispatch arm for `host.permissions.grant_scope`\n2. Persistence layer: load grants on plugin start, save on grant\n3. Test coverage in `src/test/test_host_capability_permissions.gd` (new file): ungranted denial, schema validation, happy override (grant path → in scope), cancel override, already-granted shortcut, persistence round-trip, headless guard\n4. Existing T2/T3 tests stay green\n5. The Plugin Manager UI (if it has a \"granted paths\" section) updates accordingly — flag if not, may be a follow-up\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- UI for revoking grants (out of T5a; can be follow-up)\n- Plugin Manager UI changes if there's no existing place to show granted paths (verify; flag if so)\n- Pre-existing-grant TTL or expiry","created_at":"2026-05-13T06:09:12","updated_at":"2026-05-13T06:17:47","tags":["broker","capability","permissions","scansort","scope-grant"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e1ffbaa027e8fbe9291315bbceca2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T5a followup: atomic save + shared PluginScopeGrants instance","description":"From T5a R1 cold review:\n\n1. **PluginScopeGrants.gd:106-118** `_save()` uses naive open-WRITE-close. With two live instances (broker + per-start_plugin instance) writing the same `user://plugins/scoped_paths_grants.json`, a concurrent start during a grant could theoretically truncate. Risk is low (Godot single-threaded GDScript, infrequent writes), but convert to write-tmp+rename for safety. Pattern: `FileAccess.open(path + \".tmp\")` → write → close → `DirAccess.rename_absolute(path + \".tmp\", path)`.\n\n2. **PluginManager.gd:469-475** instantiates a fresh `PluginScopeGrants` per `start_plugin` call (separate from the broker's instance held in `CapabilityBroker._init`). Functional but redundant — consider holding one shared instance on PluginManager that the broker reads via dependency injection (similar to how PluginPolicy is wired). Same applies for the broker side.\n\nBundle into a future broker touch; not urgent.","created_at":"2026-05-13T06:17:27","updated_at":"2026-05-13T06:17:27","tags":["T5a","atomicity","broker","followup","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1ff41d33774b8ba7633c9abce797"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e21f2f1a37ef2ab65ecbd77d5c49a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T5 R2 followups: registry hardening","description":"From T5 R2 cold review of `registry.rs`:\n\n1. **`registry.rs:222`** — `registry_remove` calls `write_registry` even when `removed == false`. Harmless (rewrites identical content) but wasted I/O. Early-return when not found.\n\n2. **`registry.rs:90-92`** — `read_vault_name` swallows missing-file as empty string. `registry_add` then silently registers a phantom path with empty name. Acceptable for v1 (the port is faithful to the experiment); harden in T6+ when this becomes user-facing — return a clean error when the vault file is missing or unreadable.\n\n3. **`registry.rs:150-163`** — `registry_list` enriches entries with `doc_count` and optional `error` fields beyond the spec's `{path, name, added_at}`. Faithful to the experiment; benign superset. Document in the MCP tool's description so downstream consumers know to expect these.\n\nBundle into a future scansort touch (likely T6 since the same code paths get extended there).","created_at":"2026-05-13T15:27:10","updated_at":"2026-05-13T15:27:10","tags":["T5R2","followup","registry","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdc27b976b0bf3a80285f9b1218"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e24c57af676bc85e8f76db501e797","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Scansort T7 port dropped the experiment's interaction layer (source pane, Process button, per-file actions)","description":"## Gap analysis — scansort experiment (`~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/scansort`) vs. the T7 plugin port\n\nThe T7 port captured the **data layer** faithfully (vault format, classifier, registry, rules) but dropped the **interaction layer** — the cuteFTP-inspired main pane that made the experiment usable. Filed as a bug against the DCR so the parity work is tracked; the fix is the U-series work_item.\n\n### Gaps\n\n| # | Experiment had | Plugin port | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Source pane** — left-hand file tree of an incoming/source directory, recursive option | none — panel only shows the vault | No way to see/select incoming files in-panel |\n| 2 | **Process button** — orchestrates extract→dedup→classify→place over the whole source set | none — classify is one-doc-at-a-time via File menu | No batch pipeline; no panel-orchestrated run |\n| 3 | **3-column cuteFTP layout** — source \\| center action column \\| destination | single-pane vault view | Layout never ported |\n| 4 | **Per-file actions** — double-click to open/view, drag-to-classify, drag-to-reclassify | none | No manual-review affordances |\n| 5 | **Mark-for-export** in the dual-backing (vault+disk) case — \"manual review\" use case | none | Can't curate which classified docs land on disk |\n| 6 | **Processed/✓ indicator** on source files (already in vault) | none | No dedup feedback in the source view |\n| 7 | **Destination modes** — vault_only / disk_only / vault_and_disk, with disk placement resolving templated subfolders (`tax/{year}`) | vault-only effectively | Disk destination + curated mirror not ported |\n\n### Root-cause notes\n\n- The experiment used a single `ScanFileTree` class for both incoming and organized panes; the plugin forked it into `file_tree.gd` + `vault_view.gd` — over-engineering that also lost the source side.\n- `classifier.rs` is a faithful port of the experiment's `classifier.gd` — classification = an LLM call prompted by the rule set. The missing piece was never the classifier; it was the **Pipeline orchestration** that drove it.\n- `vault_view.gd` and the per-doc detail panel have no analogue in the experiment or the DCR — they should be deleted, not extended.\n\n### Scope decision\n\nThe user confirmed: **this is not a new DCR** — \"we just did a bad job implementing the existing DCR. We're still trying to port the experiment to a plugin.\" The vault-only case is UI-equivalent to disk-only (a tree in a `.ssort` file vs. the filesystem). Fix tracked as the U-series work_item (child of this bug).","created_at":"2026-05-14T04:36:22","updated_at":"2026-05-14T04:36:22","severity":3,"tags":["T7","gap-analysis","parity","plugin","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e24c5d53876098676f4f66b161222","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"U-series: restore cuteFTP experiment-parity in the scansort panel","description":"## U-series — fixes the gaps in the parent bug\n\nTier-1 scope. Tier-2 vault power-user features (search, sort, Save As, bulk encrypt/decrypt, etc.) are a separate follow-on work_item to be filed later. Commit each round; keep Layer-1 smoke green between rounds.\n\n### Rounds\n\n- **U1 — DONE (committed).** Unified `scan_tree` component + provider contract. `scan_tree.gd` renders a nested folder/file tree with a checkbox column from a pluggable provider; `scan_tree_provider.gd` is the base contract; `scan_tree_vault_provider.gd` is backed by the `query_documents` MCP tool (groups docs by category, 2-level tree). Plugin commit `33d9f04`; Minerva test `46365da8` (Group N, N135-N153). Smoke 160/0.\n- **U2 — DONE (committed).** Source backend. MCP tools `set_source_dir` / `get_source_dir` / `list_source_files` in `main.rs` (transitory plugin-process state, never persisted); `list_source_files` returns `{path,name,size,sha256,in_vault}`. `scan_tree_source_provider.gd`. Plugins `52b4843`, Minerva `a0e2a7a1`.\n- **U3 — DONE (committed).** Destination backend. `set/get_destination` (per-vault `project` keys) + `place_on_disk` (templated subfolders, ../ sanitised, collision-safe copy). Plugins `cb8530a`.\n- **U4 — DONE (committed).** Panel layout: 2-column HBox (SourcePane | DestPane) + status bottom bar; Process All / Stop moved into the editor chrome bar via `get_editor_actions()`. Plugins `fb3d5bf`+`bddb5f8`, Minerva `fa5e3396`+`477670f9`. (Original 3-column Option A reworked to 2-column after the visual checkpoint.)\n- **U5 — Process All pipeline.** Panel-orchestrated GDScript loop: extract→dedup→auto text/vision (char_count ≥ 50)→classify→place. ✓-mark = session set ∪ vault sha256 dedup. `clear_processed_state`; low-confidence row flagging.\n- **U6 — manual review + inject-to-chat.** Drag-to-classify (source→category), drag-to-reclassify (file→folder), mark-for-export (transitory session state, NOT a durable column) + Export Marked button. **PLUS: Inject to Chat** — a checked-files bulk action that extracts the selected source files' contents and feeds them to chat via Minerva's plugin inject substrate (`PluginScenePanelHost.invoke_inject_toggle` / the `inject_toggle` chrome action). Folded into U6 (decided 2026-05-14) because it shares U6's checked-files input — sibling of Export Marked, different sink. NOT a parity gap (the experiment had no chat injection) — new scope. Building blocks already exist: `scan_tree.get_checked_keys()`, the `extract_text`/`extract_document` MCP tools (T6).\n- **U7 — vault_and_disk.** Stacked right pane (two `scan_tree`s) + DiskProvider + Settings destination-mode picker + concurrency control (1-4 spinbox) in the Settings dialog.\n- **U8 — recovery sheet dialog.** `emergency_contact_*` + `extraction_guide` project keys.\n- **U9 — watch folders.** DESIGN REQUIRED FIRST (daemon-in-Rust vs. panel-poll).\n- **U10 — docs + wrap.** Delete orphaned `vault_view.gd` + `edit_details_dialog.gd`.\n\n### Locked design decisions\n\nSource dir = transitory, never persisted, settable by UI picker OR MCP, has a recursive toggle. Destination = per-vault setting (`vault_only`/`disk_only`/`vault_and_disk`); `vault_and_disk` is CURATED not mirrored (everything to vault, only export-marked docs copy to disk). Export mark + processed mark = transitory session state. ONE unified `scan_tree` for all three panes. NO per-doc detail panel — `vault_view.gd` gets deleted; detail/edit lives in the existing edit dialog via right-click (Tier 2). Process All is a panel-orchestrated GDScript loop, not a Rust tool. Catch-all rule = the `is_default` rule. Templated subfolders resolved at `place_on_disk` time from `doc_date`. **Layout: 2-column (SourcePane | DestPane) + bottom status bar; Process All / Stop / File live in the editor chrome bar, not the panel.**\n\n### State\n\nU1–U4 committed. U5 is next, then U6 (now incl. inject-to-chat), U7, U8 — all autonomous (Sonnet implementer + cold Opus reviewer, structural Layer-1 gate). U9 is a design gate (stop). Combined full-stack HITL (10 test PDFs in `~/Downloads`, driven through the panel UI not raw MCP) runs after U8. See `Docs/pickup.md` and nudge component `scansort-T7-continuation` for the live plan.","created_at":"2026-05-14T04:36:45","updated_at":"2026-05-14T17:28:06","priority":2,"tags":["T7","parity","plugin","scansort","u-series"],"parent":"minerva:019e24c57af676bc85e8f76db501e797"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Scansort filing engine — two-phase rules + multi-destination routing + semantic dedup + audit log","description":"## Problem\n\nScansort today is single-vault and single-purpose: one vault open at a time, Process All inserts every classified document into that one vault, and a \"rule\" is a natural-language LLM instruction that picks a category *folder within* that vault. There is no way to route documents to different locations based on content, no deterministic/auditable routing logic, and only exact-byte deduplication. Real filing work — especially a CPA filing client taxes — crosses many destinations (per-client, per-year vaults plus shared hand-off directories), needs defensible auditing, and must recognize that two scans of the same document are the same document.\n\nThis blocks the U-series HITL: validating U2–U8 as-is would sign off a single-vault design that this DCR substantially changes.\n\n## Motivation\n\nTarget user: CPAs / bookkeepers filing client taxes. They need (a) one incoming pile routed deterministically to many destinations in a single pass, (b) a defensible audit trail, and (c) duplicate detection that survives re-scanning.\n\n## Architectural spine (settled in the 2026-05-14 design pass)\n\n### 1. Two-phase processing: LLM extraction → deterministic rule walk\n\nThe current design conflates *identifying* a document with *placing* it. Split them:\n\n- **Phase 1 — Classification (LLM).** The LLM reads the document once and produces raw extracted facts (`doc_date`/`year`, `issuer`, `amount`, text, `confidence`, …) AND a semantic-match signal for each rule (how well the document matches that rule's instruction).\n- **Phase 2 — Rule walk (deterministic, no LLM).** An ordered list of rules is walked. A rule \"fires\" when its semantic-match score clears threshold AND its deterministic conditions pass AND it is not excepted. Firing applies the rule's actions; a `stop processing` flag halts the walk.\n\nDeterministic Phase 2 is what makes routing auditable — a CPA-grade trail requires \"where did this go and why\" to be a deterministic, replayable fact, not an LLM judgment.\n\n### 2. Rule model: the rule IS the category\n\nThere is no fixed category taxonomy — the category set is exactly what the rules define (\"a 'tax' category exists because a tax rule exists\"). A document's category is simply *which rule fired*. A rule bundles:\n\n- **Semantic matcher** — natural-language instruction + signal keywords the LLM uses to recognize this kind of document. This part *defines the category*. (Today's `instruction` + `signals[]`.)\n- **Deterministic gates** — Outlook-style `conditions` / `exceptions` over Phase-1 facts and file facts: `year == 2024`, `confidence < 0.6`, `unless …`, with `all`/`any` grouping and operators (`contains`, `equals`, `matches`, `<`/`>`). *(New.)*\n- **Actions** — `copy to [destinations]`, subfolder + rename templates, set-encrypt. *(Today has `subfolder`/`rename_pattern` but only within the single open vault.)*\n- **Ordering + `stop processing`.** *(New — today rules are an unordered set.)*\n\nThis *extends* the existing rule object, not replaces it — `label`/`instruction`/`confidence_threshold`/`subfolder`/`is_default` stay; the new parts are deterministic conditions, ordering+stop, and destination-targeting actions. The invariant \"creating a rule creates its category\" is preserved.\n\n**Architectural boundary:** scansort rules operate ONLY on content-derived facts (what the document *is*) and file facts (filename, extension, size) — never transport metadata (how it *arrived*). Email handling etc. belong to upstream plugins that apply their own rules and drop documents into a plain directory; the filesystem is the inter-plugin handoff. (Rename the schema's `sender` field → `issuer` to kill the \"email sender\" ambiguity — it means \"who issued this document,\" read from content.)\n\n### 3. Destinations: single targets, list-valued fan-out\n\n- A **destination** is a single target — one vault file OR one directory.\n- A **destination registry** holds the destinations declared for the current filing session. This registry IS the panel's right side: N stacked `scan_tree` sub-trees, one per destination, each showing that destination's subfolder structure.\n- A rule's `copy to` action takes a **list** of destinations — a document can fan out to several at once (e.g. a car-accident document copied into both involved parties' litigation vaults).\n- Optional sugar: a named \"destination set\" as a pure alias for a list. Not load-bearing.\n- **Copy, never move.** Incoming → destination is always a copy; source files left intact. (Confirm + lock — believed already true.)\n\n### 4. Processed-state: a destination is its own source of truth\n\n\"Has this document been filed into destination D?\" is answered by querying D itself — no central index:\n\n- **Vault destination** → `check_sha256` against the vault's own `documents` table. The vault self-indexes.\n- **Directory destination** → at Process-All run start, scan + content-hash the directory into an in-memory SHA-256 set for that run (\"rebuilt from the directory\"). Cache by `(path, mtime, size)` so repeat runs don't re-hash unchanged files.\n\nSame semantics for both: *\"processed\" = the content is already present in the destination.* Identity is always **SHA-256 of file contents** — never filename (rules rename files, so names carry no identity).\n\nConsequence: **no central placements index**, and the **destination registry's persistence is a UX choice, not an architectural requirement** (nothing keys on stable destination-ids). Recommended: persist it as a saved filing-session config for CPA ergonomics, but deferrable.\n\n### 5. Reprocess / invalidation\n\nThe trial-and-error rule-definition loop (run → inspect → fix rules → re-run) needs clean re-runs:\n\n- **Directory destination** → invalidation is trivial: clear the folder, re-run. The folder *is* the state. (Makes a scratch directory the natural target for rule-definition work.)\n- **Vault destination** → an explicit, deliberately-labeled **reprocess** operation: delete the matching document rows, re-run. A vault is a managed artifact and cannot be casually emptied; a per-destination **\"locked / final\"** flag refuses reprocess so a finished client vault can't be clobbered.\n- **Default Process All stays purely additive + dedup-skip** — it never deletes anything. Reprocess is a separate operation with a confirm.\n- Hand-deleting a file from a directory destination genuinely makes it \"unprocessed\" — a re-run re-creates it. This is correct: the directory's contents are its own honest truth.\n\n### 6. Deduplication: three layers, only exact-match auto-skips\n\n| Layer | Mechanism | Catches | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Exact | SHA-256 of contents | byte-identical re-file | **auto-skip** (safe) |\n| Near-duplicate | `simhash` (text) + `dhash` (perceptual image) + configurable Hamming thresholds | two scans of the same document — different bytes, similar content/image | **flag for review** |\n| Logical identity | `copy_to` rename template resolves to the same target as an existing file under the same rule | \"the rules declare these are the same filing\" | **flag for review** |\n\n- Near-dup detection (`simhash`/`dhash`) is a **parity restoration**: the experiment had it (configurable thresholds, a `check_simhash`/`check_dhash` query, a `duplicate_near` handling path, a dedup-report tool). The current plugin still *computes and stores* `simhash`/`dhash` in `extract_text`/`insert_document`, but the T7 port dropped the *detection query* — restore it.\n- Logical-identity collision is **new**, and elegant: the user's own rename rules define what \"the same document\" means; works even when OCR/fuzzy-hashes are unreliable.\n- **Hard constraint:** only exact SHA-256 match may auto-skip. Near-dup and logical-identity matches MUST surface in the manual-review UI as a disposition prompt — **keep both / replace existing / skip** — because corrected/amended documents are near-identical to their originals but are *replacements, not duplicates* (e.g. a CORRECTED 1099). Silently `(1)`-renaming or dropping them is wrong.\n\n### 7. Audit log\n\n- A toggleable setting; when on, an append-only log is written to a **user-specified path outside any vault** (CPA-owned, portable, CSV-importable for spreadsheet review).\n- **One row per placement.** A fan-out to N destinations = N rows. Each row records: source document (sha256 + original name), the rule that matched, the destination + resolved path, timestamp, disposition.\n- A **reprocess** writes a `superseded`/`removed` row for the old placement plus a fresh placement row — history is never lost.\n- The audit log is an *export*, never consulted for dedup or processed-state decisions (it is optional and relocatable; processed-state must be always-available).\n\n## Impact on the in-flight U-series\n\nU2–U8 is committed, cold-reviewed, Layer-1-green (plugins `d3b6962`, Minerva `dcd4a53`, Rust 71/0, panel smoke 225/0). It is **not lost** — it is the substrate this builds on:\n\n- **Reused largely as-is:** U2 source backend, U4 panel shell, U8 recovery sheet, the `scan_tree` component (U1), the `extract`/`classify`/`insert` pipeline tools.\n- **Reworked by this DCR:** U5 Process All (single-vault loop + transitory processed-state → two-phase walk + multi-destination + self-truthful processed-state); U6 drag-to-classify / Export Marked (assume one vault); U7 `vault_and_disk` (single per-vault destination mode → generalized into the destination registry + `copy to` actions; the destination-mode picker and disk pane are subsumed).\n- The combined HITL is **paused** until this design is decomposed and implemented.\n\n## Parity vs. new scope\n\n- **Parity restoration** (experiment had it; T7 port dropped it): near-duplicate detection via `simhash`/`dhash`; configurable dedup thresholds; dedup reporting.\n- **New scope** (beyond the experiment): two-phase classify/route split; deterministic Outlook-style rule conditions/exceptions/ordering/stop; multi-destination registry + list-valued `copy to` fan-out; logical-identity (template-collision) dedup; the external audit log; persistent per-destination processed-state semantics.\n\n## Open / deferred design details (resolve during `designing`)\n\n- LLM call shape: does Phase 1 return a single best-match rule, or a semantic score per rule? (Affects how cleanly `stop processing` composes.)\n- Exact grammar for rule `conditions`/`exceptions` (operators, field names, `all`/`any` nesting) and storage (the existing external `rules_path` JSON file — extended schema).\n- `sender` → `issuer` schema rename + migration.\n- Audit-log row schema + CSV column set.\n- Process-All over N vault destinations: password handling, ordering, per-destination encrypt settings.\n- Destination registry persistence (recommended: saved filing-session config) + the \"locked/final\" destination flag mechanics.\n- Manual-review disposition UI for near-dup / logical-identity flags (keep both / replace / skip) — likely extends U6's review surface.\n\n## Status\n\nDesign pass complete (2026-05-14, driven with the user). Spine settled; detail items above deferred to the `designing` phase. Next: decompose into work_items under this DCR. Filed P0 (docket priority 1 = highest). Blocks work_item `019e24c5d53876098676f4f66b161222` (U-series HITL). Related: parent DCR `019e1cdb451076ae8c344f6e6ec605e1` (scansort equivalence redesign).","created_at":"2026-05-14T17:27:52","updated_at":"2026-05-14T18:08:44","priority":1,"tags":["P0","cpa","multi-location","plugin","scansort"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0558679799f9eaa63d446022a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W1 — rule schema v2 + sender→issuer rename","description":"Foundational schema work for DCR 019e2787 (filing engine redesign). No behavior change yet — schema + migration + CRUD only.\n\nScope:\n- Extend the external rules JSON schema (`rules_path` files) with: `conditions` and `exceptions` blocks (Outlook-style — operators contains/equals/matches/</>, all/any grouping, over Phase-1 facts + file facts), explicit `order`, a `stop_processing` flag, and `copy_to` actions taking a LIST of destination ids. Keep existing fields (label, instruction, signals, confidence_threshold, subfolder, rename_pattern, is_default).\n- Resolves the deferred design detail: the exact condition/exception grammar + field vocabulary.\n- Update the rule CRUD MCP tools (insert_rule/update_rule/get_rule/list_rules) to accept + round-trip the new fields.\n- Vault document schema: rename `sender` → `issuer` (means \"who issued this document\", read from content — not an email sender). Include a migration for existing vaults.\n\nDepends on: nothing (root). Blocks: W2, W3. Touches: scansort src/rules*.rs, src/schema.rs, src/documents.rs, rules-file module, rule CRUD handlers in main.rs.\n\nSuccess: rules JSON round-trips the v2 schema; existing vaults migrate sender→issuer cleanly; Rust tests cover the new schema + migration.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:08","updated_at":"2026-05-14T18:33:21","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","scansort","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins commit 49e1e05. Rule schema v2 (conditions/exceptions ConditionNode tree, order, stop_processing, copy_to) + sender→issuer rename (documents table + idempotent migration + classifier + rename token alias). cargo test --release 80/0 (+9). Cold Opus review: approve. Schema/migration/CRUD only, no routing behavior."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b067ec725387de0aeb7e49c6a3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W2 — Phase 1 classification: facts + per-rule match signals","description":"Phase 1 of the two-phase model in DCR 019e2787. Depends on W1 (rule schema v2).\n\nScope:\n- Rework classify_document so one LLM pass produces: (a) raw extracted facts — doc_date/year, issuer, amount, document text, confidence — and (b) a semantic-match signal against the rule set.\n- Resolve the deferred design detail: does the LLM return a single best-match rule or a score per rule? Affects how cleanly stop_processing composes in W3 — coordinate with W3.\n- extract_text already computes simhash/dhash; keep that — W7 consumes them.\n- Output shape feeds W3's deterministic rule walk.\n\nDepends on: W1. Blocks: W10. Touches: scansort classification module / classify handler in main.rs, the classify prompt.\n\nSuccess: classify returns the new facts+signals envelope; integration test with a sample document.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:12","updated_at":"2026-05-14T18:47:27","priority":2,"tags":["classification","filing-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins commit 189caec. classify_document yields facts (doc_date/year/issuer/amount/doc_type/confidence) + per-rule semantic-match score array. build_prompt_context reworked. Tolerant parsing. Transport + simhash/dhash unchanged. cargo test 101/0 (+10). Cold Opus review: approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b07ca37f129c173dfeede9de3e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W3 — Phase 2 deterministic rule engine","description":"Phase 2 of the two-phase model in DCR 019e2787. Depends on W1 (rule schema v2). Deterministic — no LLM.\n\nScope:\n- A Rust module (heavily unit-tested) that takes Phase-1 output (facts + per-rule semantic signals) + the ordered rule set, and walks the rules: a rule fires when its semantic score clears threshold AND its deterministic conditions pass AND it is not excepted; firing yields actions; stop_processing halts the walk.\n- Returns the resolved outcome: firing rule(s) → category, destination list, subfolder/rename templates resolved.\n- Expose as an MCP tool so the panel-orchestrated Process All (W10) can call it.\n- Coordinate the LLM-call-shape decision with W2.\n\nDepends on: W1. Blocks: W10. Touches: new scansort src/rule_engine.rs + handler in main.rs.\n\nSuccess: comprehensive Rust unit tests over the condition operators, ordering, exceptions, stop_processing, and fan-out actions.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:18","updated_at":"2026-05-14T18:56:27","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","rule-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins commit b522ce6. New src/rule_engine.rs: pure deterministic rule walk (semantic threshold + conditions/exceptions trees + order + stop_processing), ConditionNode eval with all operators, fan-out copy_to, template resolution, default fallback. MCP tool minerva_scansort_run_rule_engine. regex crate added. cargo test 154/0 (+53). Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes. NOTE for W10: resolve_template does token-expansion only, no path-traversal sanitisation — placement layer must sanitise resolved subfolder/rename strings before use (destination.rs has resolve_and_sanitise)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0930a7f2598490543f2a9be28","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W4 — destination registry (backend)","description":"Destination registry backend for DCR 019e2787.\n\nScope:\n- A destination = a single target: one vault file OR one directory. Each has a stable id, a type, a path, and a locked/final flag (used by W8).\n- MCP tools: declare/list/remove destinations. The registry holds the destinations for the current filing session.\n- Persistence: implement as a saved filing-session config (a JSON file). Per the DCR this is a UX choice, not architectural (nothing keys on destination-id stability for processed-state) — keep it simple.\n- Generalizes/subsumes U7's per-vault set_destination/get_destination (vault_only/disk_only/vault_and_disk) — that single-mode model is replaced.\n\nDepends on: nothing (root). Blocks: W5, W6. Touches: new scansort src/destinations*.rs (or rework destination.rs), handlers in main.rs.\n\nSuccess: Rust tests for declare/list/remove + persistence round-trip.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:23","updated_at":"2026-05-14T18:38:30","priority":2,"tags":["destinations","filing-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins commit 5b3228f. New src/destinations.rs: Destination (id/kind/path/label/locked) + DestinationRegistry, load/save/load_or_init JSON persistence, add/list/remove ops. MCP tools minerva_scansort_destination_{add,list,remove}. Additive — U7's old destination.rs left intact (rewiring deferred to W6/W10). cargo test --release 91/0 (+11). Cold Opus review: approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0a8317b41b71378831c58c343","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5 — destination registry UI (stacked sub-trees)","description":"Destination registry UI for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W4 (registry backend).\n\nScope:\n- Rework ScansortPanel.gd's right column: instead of U7's fixed {vault tree, disk tree} stack, render N stacked scan_tree sub-trees — one per registered destination — each showing that destination's subfolder structure.\n- A scan_tree provider for directory destinations (or generalize the existing disk provider); vault destinations reuse the vault provider.\n- UI to add/remove destinations (drives W4's MCP tools).\n- Drag-to-classify / drag-to-reclassify (U6) must keep working across the multi-destination layout.\n\nDepends on: W4. Blocks: W10. Touches: scansort ui/ScansortPanel.gd, ui/scan_tree_*_provider.gd, panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: panel smoke test green; the right side renders multiple destination sub-trees; drag-to-classify works across them.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:29","updated_at":"2026-05-14T19:09:35","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 9b67b52 + Minerva b8729712. ScansortPanel right column → N stacked scan_tree sub-trees per registered destination; new scan_tree_destination_provider.gd; add/remove destination UI; U6 drag-to-classify works across all sub-trees. Panel smoke 258/0 (+33, Group T). Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0be917696a616ea3b7d6641d0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W6 — copy_to fan-out execution + per-destination processed-state","description":"Placement + processed-state for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W4 (registry).\n\nScope:\n- copy_to fan-out: copy one document to a LIST of destinations in one operation. Generalizes U7's place_on_disk + insert_document. Copy, never move; source files left intact.\n- Per-destination processed-state — \"a destination is its own source of truth\": vault destination → check_sha256 against the vault's documents table; directory destination → at run start, scan + content-hash the directory into an in-memory SHA-256 set, cached by (path, mtime, size). New Rust tool for the directory scan. NO central index.\n- Identity is always SHA-256 of file contents — never filename (rules rename files).\n\nDepends on: W4. Blocks: W7, W8, W9, W10. Touches: scansort src/destination.rs / new placement module, new directory-hash-scan tool, handlers in main.rs.\n\nSuccess: Rust tests for fan-out copy + the directory hash scan + the mtime/size cache.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:35","updated_at":"2026-05-14T19:09:39","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","placement","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins commit 6e9e275. New src/placement.rs: copy_to fan-out (copy never move, one result row per destination, partial-failure isolated), per-destination processed-state (vault→check_sha256, directory→DirHashCache content-hash scan with (path,mtime,size) cache, no central index), exact-sha256 auto-skip. MCP tools place_fanout + scan_directory_hashes. cargo test 168/0 (+14). Cold Opus review: approve. NOTE for W10: hoist one DirHashCache.scan() per run (contains() re-walks dir each call)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0d89a7c7293935402aaefe672","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W7 — three-layer dedup + review-disposition flow","description":"Deduplication for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W6 (placement) and W2 (facts incl. simhash/dhash).\n\nScope:\n- Parity restoration: check_simhash/check_dhash MCP tools (the experiment had them; the T7 port dropped the query layer — simhash/dhash are still computed + stored). Configurable Hamming thresholds in Settings.\n- New: logical-identity detection — when a rule's copy_to rename template resolves to the same target as an existing file under the same rule, flag it.\n- Review-disposition flow: near-dup + logical-identity matches surface in the manual-review UI as a prompt — keep both / replace existing / skip. Extends U6's review surface.\n- HARD CONSTRAINT: only exact SHA-256 match auto-skips. Near-dup + logical-identity NEVER auto-discard — corrected/amended documents are near-identical replacements, not duplicates.\n\nDepends on: W6, W2. Blocks: W10. Touches: scansort dedup module + handlers, Settings dialog, ScansortPanel.gd review UI, panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: Rust tests for simhash/dhash queries; the review prompt appears for flagged docs and never auto-drops.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:41","updated_at":"2026-05-14T19:23:29","priority":2,"tags":["dedup","filing-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 8198029 + Minerva 8d84d6bb. New src/dedup.rs: check_simhash/check_dhash/check_logical_identity (pure detection). MCP tools check_simhash/check_dhash. Settings Hamming-threshold SpinBoxes. ScansortPanel + dedup_disposition_dialog.gd: keep-both/replace/skip prompt. HARD CONSTRAINT verified safe — only exact SHA auto-skips. cargo 181/0 (+13), panel smoke 289/0 (+31). Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes. NOTE for W10: _check_near_dup/check_logical_identity defined but not wired into placement — Process All must call them."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b0ebe67d73aa0cda2be2294b69","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W8 — reprocess + locked/final flag","description":"Reprocess / invalidation for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W6 (placement).\n\nScope:\n- Reprocess operation, per destination: vault → delete the matching document rows then allow re-run; directory → clear the folder then allow re-run (the folder IS the state).\n- The per-destination locked/final flag (from W4's data model) refuses reprocess so a finished client vault can't be clobbered.\n- Default Process All stays purely additive + dedup-skip — it never deletes. Reprocess is a separate, deliberately-labeled operation with a confirm.\n- UI trigger in the panel.\n\nDepends on: W6. Blocks: W9, W10. Touches: scansort reprocess handler in main.rs, ui/ScansortPanel.gd, panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: reprocess clears + allows re-run; locked destinations refuse; Rust + smoke tests.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:46","updated_at":"2026-05-14T19:41:38","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","reprocess","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 080a9c8 + Minerva d9b70137. New src/reprocess.rs: reprocess_destination (directory→delete top-level files; vault→transactional delete of documents/fingerprints/dupes), backend-enforced locked refusal, set_destination_locked. Path safety: rejects empty/shallow/$HOME/system paths + '..' traversal, canonicalizes before clearing (symlink-safe). MCP tools + per-destination Reprocess button/locked toggle in panel. cargo 195/0 (+14), panel smoke 300/0 (+11, Group V). Cold Opus review approve_with_notes — flagged '..'/symlink escape + non-transactional vault delete fixed before commit. NOTE: implementer's \"baseline 30/15\" claim did not reproduce; out-of-scope has_method guards on F46/Q184 were reverted by orchestrator."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b1007c778ca8ed20c3fc6ab70c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W9 — audit log","description":"Audit log for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W6 (placement generates rows) and W8 (reprocess generates superseded rows).\n\nScope:\n- A toggleable Settings option; when on, an append-only log is written to a user-specified path OUTSIDE any vault.\n- One row per placement: source doc (sha256 + original name), matched rule, destination + resolved path, timestamp, disposition. A fan-out to N destinations = N rows.\n- Reprocess writes a superseded/removed row + a fresh placement row — history never lost.\n- CSV format (resolve the deferred design detail: exact column set) — CPA-importable.\n- The audit log is an EXPORT, never consulted for dedup/processed-state decisions.\n\nDepends on: W6, W8. Blocks: W10. Touches: scansort audit module, Settings dialog, placement + reprocess call sites.\n\nSuccess: rows written correctly on placement + reprocess; CSV parses; toggle works.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:51","updated_at":"2026-05-14T19:49:51","priority":2,"tags":["audit","filing-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins a9c05d0 + Minerva 158d5bae. New src/audit.rs: append-only CSV writer (header on create, never truncates, RFC-4180 escaping), row construction from W6 PlacementResult + W8 reprocess (superseded rows), write-only, non-fatal failure. 10-column CSV set. MCP tool audit_append. settings_dialog.gd: Audit Log section (enable CheckBox defaults OFF, path LineEdit + Browse). cargo 205/0 (+10), panel smoke 313/0 (+13, Group W). Cold Opus review: approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b11549757cbb80ff63ba9fe09c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W10 — Process All integration","description":"Integration round for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W2, W3, W6, W7, W9 (and W5 for the UI surface).\n\nScope:\n- Rework ScansortPanel.gd's Process All (the U5 panel-orchestrated loop) to drive the new pipeline per source document: Phase 1 classify (W2) → Phase 2 rule engine (W3) → resolve actions → dedup checks (W7: exact auto-skips / near-dup + logical flag for review) → fan-out copy_to destinations (W6) → processed-state updates → audit rows (W9).\n- Preserve U5's batched-parallel concurrency + Stop + status summary.\n- Resolve the N-vault Process All deferred details here (passwords, ordering, per-destination encrypt).\n\nDepends on: W2, W3, W5, W6, W7, W9. Blocks: W11, W12. Touches: scansort ui/ScansortPanel.gd, panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: end-to-end Process All over a multi-destination setup; panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:12:57","updated_at":"2026-05-14T20:00:22","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","integration","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 6ffd750 + Minerva 619e398c. Process All reworked to drive the full pipeline: classify (W2) → run_rule_engine (W3) → near-dup check (W7) → disposition dialog → place_fanout (W6) → audit_append (W9), inside U5's preserved batched-parallel loop + Stop + status summary. Disposition dialog serialized behind _dedup_prompt_busy seat (no deadlock). HARD CONSTRAINT verified: near-dup never auto-dropped. Panel smoke 326/0 (+13, Group X), cargo 205/0. Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes. KNOWN GAP: dedup::check_logical_identity unreachable (no MCP tool/no caller) — 3-layer dedup is 2-layer wired; deferred to W12, flag at W11 HITL. Also: \"replace\" disposition is place-alongside only."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b12f7c7e1d8ea02efc91fd1122","type":"work_item","status":"blocked","title":"Filing engine W11 — combined full-stack HITL","description":"Full-stack HITL for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W10. Supersedes the paused U-series HITL (work_item 019e24c5d53876098676f4f66b161222).\n\nScope: drive a realistic CPA-style scenario through the panel UI (not raw MCP): declare multiple destinations (per-year vaults + a to_accountant directory), define rules with conditions + copy_to fan-out, Process All over a curated ~12-PDF corpus from ~/Downloads (multi-year tax docs + a true duplicate pair + a corrected-1099 near-dup case + a tiny vision-path scan), verify routing, fan-out, dedup flags (exact auto-skip / near-dup + logical flagged for review), reprocess, and the audit-log CSV.\n\nDepends on: W10. Touches: nothing (validation only).\n\nSuccess: documents route correctly per rules to the right destinations; dedup behaves per the three-layer model; the audit log is accurate; reprocess works; no regressions.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:13:03","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:08:30","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","blocked_by":"019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e27b13ec0726eb7ff4792552a285e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Filing engine W12 — docs + cleanup","description":"Wrap-up for DCR 019e2787. Depends on W10.\n\nScope:\n- Delete scripts orphaned by the redesign: U7's per-vault destination-mode picker code (subsumed by W4/W5), vault_view.gd and edit_details_dialog.gd (already unwired since the U4 layout rewrite).\n- Update the scansort plugin docs / README to the new two-phase multi-destination model.\n- Refresh Docs/pickup.md.\n\nDepends on: W10. Touches: scansort ui/, plugin docs, Docs/pickup.md.\n\nSuccess: no dead code; docs match the shipped design.","created_at":"2026-05-14T18:13:07","updated_at":"2026-05-14T20:40:56","priority":2,"tags":["docs","filing-engine","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e282e1d437558be50e1cec2eb5038","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5b — destination UI rework: two-area splitter + virtual-root trees","description":"HITL feedback on W5 (019e27b0a831) during the W11 combined HITL. W5's flat stack of N independent per-destination scan_tree widgets is poor UX. Rework:\n\n- TWO distinct areas in the panel's right column, in a draggable VSplitContainer: a Vault area (top) and a Directory area (bottom). Drag the boundary to resize.\n- Each area is ONE scan_tree widget with a hidden virtual root whose top-level children are the destinations of that kind: vault area → V1, V2, V3…; directory area → d1, d2, d3…. Each top-level destination row expands into its own subtree (a vault's category/doc structure; a directory's folder/file structure).\n- Per-destination controls are INLINE buttons on the V#/d# top-level tree row: [x] remove (W5's destination_remove), [↻] reprocess (W8's reprocess_destination, behind the confirm dialog), [🔒] lock toggle (W8's set_destination_locked; icon reflects locked state). This replaces W5's per-section-header controls and W8's per-section reprocess/lock controls.\n- Per-area \"Add\" button adds a destination of that area's kind.\n- Drag-to-classify (U6) must keep working — dropping a source file onto a folder row in either area's tree.\n\nTouches: scansort ui/ScansortPanel.gd, ui/scan_tree.gd (per-row buttons + signal), the destination providers (aggregate-by-kind), Minerva panel smoke test. GDScript-only; no Rust changes (W4/W6/W8 backend tools unchanged).\n\nSuccess: panel smoke green; two resizable areas; each renders a virtual-root tree of its destinations; inline row buttons drive remove/reprocess/lock; drag-to-classify works in both areas.","created_at":"2026-05-14T20:29:31","updated_at":"2026-05-14T20:40:51","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 955e995 + Minerva 2d413991. Right column reworked into a draggable VSplitContainer: Vault area + Directory area, each one scan_tree with a hidden virtual root → per-destination top-level rows → per-destination subtree. New scan_tree_area_provider.gd aggregates by kind. Inline [x]/[↻]/[🔒] buttons on destination rows (scan_tree.gd add_button + dest_button_pressed signal). Per-area Add. Drag-to-classify works in both areas. Panel smoke 355/0 (+29, Group Y), cargo 205/0. Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes — W5's legacy N-section code left live-but-invisible (~150 LOC, T239-T248/V285-V293 now test hidden widget); cleanup deferred to W12."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2853f661732d8ee2bf1a4575ae72","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5c — open vault renders from file; registry is machine-local","description":"HITL feedback during W11. Opening an existing (registered) vault showed the vault row but empty contents. Root cause: W5b made the Vault area render ONLY registry destinations, and the registry (destinations.rs) stores absolute paths — a sidecar registry created elsewhere / a moved vault yields a stale path, so query_documents finds nothing.\n\nDesign decision (settled with the user 2026-05-14): **the vault file is the portable unit; the destination registry is machine-local routing scratch that never travels.**\n\nScope (GDScript-only — no Rust changes):\n- Vault area: the currently-open vault ALWAYS renders its contents DIRECTLY from its .ssort file (reuse the single-vault provider logic against the open vault path), NOT gated through destination_list. Viewing a vault must never depend on a registry entry or a stored path being valid.\n- Auto-register the open vault as a vault destination in the (machine-local) registry, idempotently, so it is also a valid copy_to routing target — but its contents render from the file regardless of registry state.\n- The Vault area may still also show additional registry vault-destinations (copy_to targets); dedupe so the open vault is not shown twice.\n- Directory area unchanged (registry-driven, machine-local — correct).\n\nEXPLICIT NON-GOAL: do NOT add relative-path / portability handling to destinations.rs. The registry is machine-local by decision; absolute paths in it are acceptable. This is recorded so it is not \"fixed\" later.\n\nTouches: scansort ui/ScansortPanel.gd, ui/scan_tree_area_provider.gd (and/or the vault provider), Minerva panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: opening a vault (registered or not, registry valid or stale) shows its contents from the file; panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T21:10:51","updated_at":"2026-05-14T21:17:26","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl-feedback","portability","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 66e2d56 + Minerva 579082fc. Vault area always builds the open-vault row directly via query_documents against the vault file — verified independent of destination_list (fails/empty/stale registry all still render the vault). Other registry vault-destinations appended, deduped by path. _on_vault_opened_r2 auto-registers the open vault idempotently (non-fatal). destinations.rs deliberately untouched — registry is machine-local by design decision. Panel smoke 367/0 (+12, Group Z), cargo 205/0. Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2861ee4c744d9142c3992295603e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5d — kind-aware row actions: open documents, real icons, vault rows lose reprocess/lock","description":"HITL feedback during W11 — three issues with the W5b/W5c destination trees:\n\n1. Double-clicking a document does nothing. scan_tree.gd emits file_activated on double-click but the W5b/W5c area trees never wired it. Fix: wire it + an open-document handler (extract_document to a temp path → OS.shell_open, mirroring the experiment's vault_panel.gd `_open_document`).\n2. Inline row buttons render as blank grey boxes — W5b's _make_icon_texture() is a literal placeholder. Replace with real, visually-distinct icons.\n3. The inline buttons are not kind-appropriate: reprocess + lock were W8 scratch-destination operations and make no sense on a vault row (reprocessing the open vault deletes its documents). The experiment never had per-vault row buttons.\n\nDecided model (kind-aware inline buttons + real icons):\n- Document rows: an inline [open] button; double-click also opens. (Open = extract_document + OS.shell_open for vault docs; OS.shell_open directly for directory files.)\n- Vault rows: NO reprocess/lock. [settings] (small popup of vault-level ops — wire whatever clean MCP tools exist, password at minimum) + [remove] (remove-from-registry; disabled for the currently-open vault).\n- Directory rows: keep [reprocess][lock][remove] — directories ARE the scratch routing targets W8 was designed for — now with real icons.\n\nTouches: scansort ui/scan_tree.gd (kind-aware button selection + real icon textures + wire the open path), ui/ScansortPanel.gd (file_activated wiring, open-document handler, kind-aware button dispatch, vault [settings] popup), ui/scan_tree_area_provider.gd (mark node role so scan_tree picks the right buttons), Minerva panel smoke test. GDScript-only; no Rust changes.\n\nSuccess: double-click + [open] open a PDF; icons are distinct/recognizable; vault rows have no reprocess/lock; directory rows keep their (real-icon) buttons; panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T21:26:07","updated_at":"2026-05-14T21:36:28","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins f73926e + Minerva cceca045. Bug 1: file_activated wired on both area trees → open-document handler (doc:<id> → extract_document + OS.shell_open; dir file → OS.shell_open). extract_document contract verified against main.rs. Bug 2: 5 distinct programmatic glyph icons replace the blank placeholder. Bug 3: kind-aware buttons via node_role — doc rows [open], vault rows [settings][remove] (no reprocess/lock), dir rows keep [reprocess][lock][remove]; open-vault [remove] refused at dispatch. Panel smoke 387/0 (+20, Group AA), cargo 205/0. Cold Opus review: approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e28703a127b049222a13f219cb685","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5e — status label must not drive panel width","description":"HITL: double-clicking a vault document resized the incoming/source pane and caused clipping/overflow. Root cause: status_panel.gd `_status_label` is a plain Label with no clip_text and no width cap, so its minimum width = full text width. W5d's open-document handler now calls set_status() with variable-length strings (\"Opening: <file>\", \"Extracting document…\", error messages); a long status string grows the StatusPanel HBox min width → the outer VBox min width → the whole panel overflows its dock and squeezes the columns. Pre-existing latent bug exposed by W5d wiring double-click to set_status.\n\nFix (status_panel.gd only): make _status_label SIZE_EXPAND_FILL + clip_text=true + OVERRUN_TRIM_ELLIPSIS + right-aligned, replacing the separate spacer Control. The status label fills remaining width and ellipsizes — it can never drive the panel wider.\n\nSuccess: a long set_status() message does not change the panel's layout width; panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T21:41:44","updated_at":"2026-05-14T21:44:00","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 314467f + Minerva 5ce37c7e. status_panel.gd _status_label is now SIZE_EXPAND_FILL + clip_text + OVERRUN_TRIM_ELLIPSIS + right-aligned (replacing the spacer Control) — a long set_status() string can no longer grow the StatusPanel HBox / outer VBox / panel width. Direct fix (one file, gate-verified). Panel smoke 388/0 (+1, assertion E40b). Root cause was a pre-existing latent bug exposed by W5d wiring double-click to set_status."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e287926ed79849ca1ed31c949aa88","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5f — decrypt-on-extract: open encrypted vault documents","description":"HITL: double-clicking an ENCRYPTED vault document fails with an \"encrypted document\" error — cannot decrypt and view. Root cause: the experiment never implemented opening encrypted docs (its open path silently no-op'd; documents::extract_document returned \"Decryption is not yet implemented\" — Phase D). The current plugin inherited the gap: handle_extract_document → documents::extract_document(vault_path, doc_id, dest) takes NO password and rejects encrypted docs. The crypto primitives DO exist (crypto::decrypt_bytes + the password KDF used by set_password/verify_password), and the panel already caches _vault_password on vault open.\n\nScope (Rust + GDScript):\n- Rust: documents::extract_document gains an optional password param. When the doc row is encrypted, derive the key from the password the same way insert_document's encrypt path does, crypto::decrypt_bytes, reverse the compression, write the plaintext file. Encrypted + missing/wrong password → a clear error (\"password required\" / \"incorrect password\"). Plaintext docs unaffected. handle_extract_document in main.rs threads the password arg.\n- GDScript: W5d's _on_area_tree_file_activated passes the cached _vault_password to extract_document. Verify _vault_password is actually cached after a successful verify_password when OPENING an existing password-protected vault (caching is confirmed on the CREATE path; the OPEN path must cache it too — fix if not). For an encrypted doc in a vault that is NOT the currently-open vault (no cached password): a clear status message, not a cryptic error — no per-doc password prompt this round (bounded).\n\nTouches: scansort src/documents.rs, src/main.rs (and src/crypto.rs read-only), ui/ScansortPanel.gd, Minerva panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: opening an encrypted doc in the open password vault decrypts + shell-opens it; wrong/missing password gives a clear error; plaintext docs still open; cargo test + panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T21:51:29","updated_at":"2026-05-14T22:05:24","priority":2,"tags":["crypto","filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 50b90b3 + Minerva 8ec49d00. documents::extract_document gains a password param and decrypts encrypted docs — exact inverse of insert_document (decrypt→decompress; Argon2id vault_key, salt from project table). Empty password → \"password required\" error; wrong password → clear error, no panic; plaintext unchanged. crypto::vault_key made pub. Panel open handler passes cached _vault_password; non-open-vault encrypted doc gets a clear status. placement.rs/reprocess.rs updated for the new signature (pass \"\", behavior-equivalent to pre-W5f). cargo 209/0 (+4), panel smoke 397/0 (+9, Group AB). Cold Opus review: approve — crypto round-trip verified symmetric. Plugin binary rebuilt + installed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e28b1fd87766589c5b2d72668cd4f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5g — extract vault documents to a directory (mark + drag)","description":"HITL: there is no working UI to extract/copy a vault document out to a directory. The existing \"Export Marked\" File-menu item is stale — it collects checked keys from _dest_trees (the now-hidden dead legacy trees, not the visible _vault_area_tree) and extracts via the old U7 place_on_disk with a hardcoded {year} subfolder into the legacy single-disk-root. Per-doc \"Save As\" was deferred from W5d.\n\nDecided model (user chose BOTH gestures):\n1. Checkboxes + \"Extract Marked…\": vault-area doc rows are already checkable (scan_tree COL_CHECK + get_checked_keys). Fix the File-menu \"Extract Marked…\" handler to (a) collect checked doc: keys from _vault_area_tree (the VISIBLE tree), (b) open a target picker — choose a registered directory destination OR Browse for any folder, (c) extract each checked doc there via extract_document (W5f — pass _vault_password for encrypted docs). Drop the stale place_on_disk path.\n2. Drag doc → directory row: drag a doc: row from the vault area tree and drop it onto a directory-destination row (or a subfolder within it) in the directory area → extract that doc into that directory path. The drag/drop machinery exists (scan_tree _get_drag_data/_can_drop_data/_drop_data + file_dropped); drag data carries role=tree_role so the drop handler distinguishes a \"dest:vault\" doc drop (extract) from a \"source\" file drop (classify).\n\nGDScript-only — no Rust changes (extract_document from W5f already does the work). Touches: scansort ui/scan_tree.gd, ui/ScansortPanel.gd (+ maybe a small target-picker dialog), Minerva panel smoke test.\n\nSuccess: checking docs + \"Extract Marked…\" → picker → docs copied to the chosen directory; dragging a doc onto a directory row copies it there; encrypted docs decrypt via the cached password; panel smoke green.","created_at":"2026-05-14T22:53:33","updated_at":"2026-05-14T23:04:51","priority":2,"tags":["filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins 3ad8088 + Minerva a88f1999. Two gestures for extracting vault docs to a directory. (1) Checkboxes + \"Extract Marked…\": _on_export_marked_pressed rewritten to collect from _vault_area_tree (visible), new extract_target_dialog.gd (registered dir dests + Browse), extract_document with cached password; stale place_on_disk/{year} path removed. (2) Drag doc → directory row: _get_drag_data embeds vault_path, _on_area_tree_file_dropped branches on role (source→classify, dest:vault→extract), resolves target dir from dest:/dir: key. Cold review caught a call-order bug in the picker (set_destinations before _ready) — orchestrator-fixed (store + repopulate in _build_ui) + regression-guarded (AC401b). Panel smoke 417/0 (+20, Group AC), cargo 209/0. Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes. GDScript only — no binary rebuild needed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e28c1f65975eb94e8aa832a67e37c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Filing engine W5h — Extract Marked button + per-document encrypt/decrypt toggle","description":"HITL feedback. Two UI gaps. Worked DIRECT (no work-cycle sub-agents) per user request.\n\nIssue 1: \"Extract Marked\" has no discoverable control — the action exists only as File-menu item id 12 (\"Export Marked to Disk...\", the stale U6 label). Fix: add a visible \"Extract Marked…\" button to the Vaults area header next to the + button; rename the File-menu item to match.\n\nIssue 2: no per-document encrypt/decrypt. crypto.rs has encrypt_bytes/decrypt_bytes; extract_document decrypts on extract; insert_document has an encrypt flag at insert time — but nothing toggles an EXISTING document's stored encryption in place. Fix: Rust documents::set_document_encrypted(vault_path, doc_id, encrypt, password) — toggles the stored blob between compress(raw) and encrypt(compress(raw)), updates the encrypt flag + iv/tag columns; MCP tool to expose it. GDScript: a lock toggle button on document rows (W5d already has locked/unlocked lock icons); the area provider passes each doc's encrypt flag through; clicking the button calls the tool with the cached _vault_password and refreshes.\n\nTouches: scansort src/documents.rs, src/main.rs, ui/ScansortPanel.gd, ui/scan_tree.gd, ui/scan_tree_area_provider.gd, Minerva panel smoke test. Rust change → binary rebuild.","created_at":"2026-05-14T23:11:00","updated_at":"2026-05-14T23:20:08","priority":2,"tags":["crypto","filing-engine","hitl-feedback","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9","resolution":"Shipped at plugins ebb9a3c + Minerva 92b595f0. Worked direct (no work-cycle). Issue 1: visible \"Extract Marked…\" button added to the Vaults header; File-menu item renamed to match. Issue 2: Rust documents::set_document_encrypted + MCP tool minerva_scansort_set_document_encrypted toggles a doc's at-rest encryption in place (inverse of insert_document; idempotent; password-guarded; no panic on wrong password). scan_tree.gd doc rows get a [lock] toggle button reflecting encrypted state; area provider passes the encrypted flag; panel _on_doc_encrypt_toggle calls the tool with the cached password + refreshes. cargo 211/0 (+2), panel smoke 418/0. Plugin binary rebuilt + installed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Scansort path-free agent surface — session model + global rules library","description":"Surfaced during W11 HITL of DCR 019e2787 (filing-engine). The plugin's 47 MCP tools all take explicit vault_path / rules_path / registry_path / file_path; the Minerva broker is stateless w.r.t. plugin identity and exposes no panel-publish channel. An LLM agent therefore cannot create rules, enable them, or run processing without being told paths out of band. User intent: rules library is global and plugin-owned; vault + optional sidecar are the portable units; sessions hold a set (≥0) of open vaults AND a set (≥0) of open directories AND a set (≥0) of open source directories, each addressed by stable user-chosen label.","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:57:45","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:24:34","priority":2,"tags":["agent-surface","design","mcp","plugin","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e1cdb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2cca04d974abb7f849eee1051f88","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B1 — Session model + state tool (multi-cardinality, label-addressed)","description":"Foundation for the rest of the DCR. Without a session, no other path-free verb works.\n\n## Scope\n\nNew plugin state struct held in-process:\n```\nsession = {\n  open_vaults:   Vec<{ label: String, path: PathBuf }>\n  open_dirs:     Vec<{ label: String, path: PathBuf }>\n  open_sources:  Vec<{ label: String, path: PathBuf }>\n}\n```\n\nLabels are unique per kind. Label collisions on `_open_*` return error.\n\n## New MCP tools\n\n- `minerva_scansort_session_open_vault(label, path)` → `{ok, label}` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_close_vault(label)` → `{ok}` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_open_directory(label, path)` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_close_directory(label)` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_open_source(label, path)` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_close_source(label)` — panel-side\n- `minerva_scansort_session_state()` → returns `{vaults: [{label}], dirs: [{label}], sources: [{label}]}` — NO paths\n\n## Open question\n\nShould session persist across plugin restarts? Two options:\n1. Volatile — panel re-opens on its own on UI launch. Plugin always boots empty.\n2. Persisted — small `session.json` next to `library.rules.json` in app-data. Plugin restores on boot; panel reconciles.\n\nRecommend (1) for v1 — panel already drives all open/close decisions and knows its own state. Persistence is a smell if the panel is the source of truth.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Cardinality 0/0/0, 1/0/0, 0/0/1, 3+5 — session_state shape correct\n- Duplicate label on open → error\n- close_* on unknown label → no-op or clear error (pick one)\n- session_state never includes path strings (regression guard against accidental leakage)","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:58:17","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:26:59","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","scansort","session"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","resolution":"Shipped in plugins commit f58b4bc and Minerva commit (pending — same pass). Session model with three label-addressed sets (vaults, dirs, sources), 7 session_* MCP tools, panel wiring at vault open/close + set_source_dir + destination_add + panel-unload cleanup. Cargo 212/0 → 225/0 (+13 unit tests including path-leak regression guard). Panel smoke 418/0 → 433/0 (+15 Group B1 tests). Tool count 54 → 61. Reviewed approve_with_notes; one inline fix applied (source label fallback uses literal \"source\" not path when get_file() empty)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2cca4ba87ef68621b63799030af7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B2 — Library at OS app-data path + library_* CRUD","description":"## Scope\n\n- Add `directories = \"5\"` to Cargo.toml.\n- Resolve library path via `ProjectDirs::from(\"\", \"Minerva\", \"Scansort\").data_dir().join(\"library.rules.json\")`.\n  - Linux: `~/.local/share/minerva/scansort/library.rules.json`\n  - macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/Minerva/Scansort/library.rules.json`\n  - Windows: `%APPDATA%\\Minerva\\Scansort\\library.rules.json`\n- Plugin refuses to start if `ProjectDirs::from(...)` returns None — fatal env error, no silent fallback.\n- Create parent dir on first write (`fs::create_dir_all`).\n\n## New MCP tools (all path-free)\n\n| Tool | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `library_insert_rule(label, name, instruction, signals, copy_to:[label], rename_pattern, confidence_threshold, order, stop_processing, conditions?, exceptions?, encrypt?, is_default?)` | `{ok, rule}` |\n| `library_list_rules()` | `{ok, rules:[...], count}` |\n| `library_get_rule(label)` | `{ok, rule}` |\n| `library_update_rule(label, ...partial fields)` | `{ok, rule}` |\n| `library_delete_rule(label)` | `{ok, deleted}` |\n| `library_enable_rule(label)` / `library_disable_rule(label)` | `{ok, enabled}` |\n\nSchema is the existing `FileRule` — no new fields. `copy_to: [String]` now carries destination labels (validated only at process time against session, not at insert time — rules outlive sessions).\n\n## What gets reused\n\nInternals re-use `rules_file::upsert/delete/index_of` + `FileRule` serde. The library tools are thin wrappers that resolve the canonical path and call the existing primitives.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Library file auto-created on first insert\n- Upsert by label (existing test pattern)\n- Path resolution on Linux (mock HOME), macOS, Windows env shapes\n- Hard error when HOME/USERPROFILE unset\n- `copy_to` accepts any string (label validation deferred to process-time, see B3)","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:58:35","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:37:08","priority":2,"tags":["library","mcp","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","resolution":"Shipped in plugins commit d260861. Library at OS app-data via `directories` crate (`ProjectDirs::from(\"\", \"Minerva\", \"Scansort\")`), errors hard if HOME/USERPROFILE unset. 7 `library_*` MCP tools wrap rules_file primitives with no caller-supplied path. cfg(test)-gated path override isolates tests in tmpdirs. No in-memory cache — every call hits the file (B7 adds hot-reload). Cargo 225/0 → 226/0 (+1 test with 8 serialized sub-scenarios). Tool count 61 → 68. Smoke 433/0 unchanged. Reviewed approve_with_notes; the library_update_rule flat-args shape is the new canonical convention (the path-driven update_rule keeps its nested shape for back-compat)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2ccaa1a976dcb70485e63d71089a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B3 — Path-free process() pipeline","description":"## Scope\n\n- New tool `minerva_scansort_process()` — zero arguments. All inputs come from session + library.\n- Flow per file:\n  1. Skip if B4 manifest says already-processed for current rules revision (sha256 hit).\n  2. Extract text + classify via `host.providers.chat` (W2 path).\n  3. Run rule engine (W3 path).\n  4. For each rule that fired: resolve `copy_to` labels against session destinations. Open labels become fanout targets; missing labels add an audit row.\n  5. If at least one destination resolved → fanout (W6 path). If none → mark `unprocessable` reason `no_open_destination`.\n  6. If no rule fired → mark `unprocessable` reason `no_rule_match`.\n- Returns:\n  ```\n  {\n    ok: true,\n    summary: { moved, conflicts, unprocessable, skipped_already_processed },\n    by_rule:        { <rule_label>: count, ... },\n    by_destination: { <dest_label>: count, ... },\n    items: [ { source_path_relative, status, rule_label?, target_labels:[], reason? } ]\n  }\n  ```\n\n## What gets reused\n\nThis is glue. `classify_document`, `run_rule_engine`, `place_fanout`, `extract_text` all stay. Process() is the orchestrator.\n\n## Label-resolution policy\n\n- Label not open in session → audit row, no error. Document still ships to any other resolved labels.\n- Zero labels resolved → unprocessable (`no_open_destination`).\n- Multi-source: iterate sources in label-sorted order; manifest writes are per-source.\n\n## Confidence threshold\n\nUses each rule's `confidence_threshold` from the library entry. No global override.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Empty session → process() returns all-zero summary, no errors\n- Session has destinations but no enabled rules → every file `unprocessable: no_rule_match`\n- Rule references label not in session → audit row, count under by_rule unchanged\n- 3+5 fanout — single file places into multiple destinations\n- Re-run with same session → all files `skipped_already_processed`","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:58:57","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:50:17","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","pipeline","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2ccadeac7425a62c71937948d1e5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B4 — Source state manifest (.scansort-state.json)","description":"## Scope\n\nFile path: `<source-dir>/.scansort-state.json` per open source.\n\nSchema:\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"files\": {\n    \"<sha256-hex>\": {\n      \"relpath\": \"subdir/foo.pdf\",\n      \"status\": \"moved\" | \"conflict\" | \"unprocessable\",\n      \"reason\": \"no_rule_match\" | \"no_open_destination\" | \"sha256_present\" | \"...\",\n      \"rule_label\": \"boat\" | null,\n      \"target_labels\": [\"boat-archive\", \"household-mirror\"],\n      \"last_run_at\": \"2026-05-15T...\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Behavior\n\n- B3's process() reads the manifest at the start of each source iteration.\n- For each file in source: compute sha256. If sha256 present with `status: moved` AND that target_labels set is still satisfied by the current session, skip and add to `skipped_already_processed`.\n- After processing, write back updated entries.\n- On unprocessable → mark and SKIP on re-run (don't re-classify) unless user clears the entry.\n\n## User-facing escape hatches\n\n- Hand-deleting `.scansort-state.json` forces full reprocess.\n- Future tool `minerva_scansort_clear_source_state(label, sha256?)` for selective reset — file as a follow-on, not part of this work item.\n\n## Atomicity\n\nWrite via temp file + rename to avoid corrupt manifests on crash.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Manifest auto-created on first process()\n- sha256 round-trip\n- skip-on-rerun semantics\n- Cleared session destinations → previously-moved files transition correctly when labels reappear (don't re-move; honor \"moved\" status)\n- Crash-resilient write (temp + rename)","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:59:13","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:50:21","priority":2,"tags":["pipeline","scansort","state"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2ccb1d947a1eb636b37c4658d5d5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B5 — Sidecar export/import as portable hatch (no longer primary)","description":"## Scope\n\n### Behavior changes\n\n- `classify_document` no longer falls back to the sidecar. Reads exclusively from the library (B2 path).\n- `rules_file::load_for_vault` is retained but only called by the new export/import tools.\n\n### New MCP tools (path-free)\n\n- `minerva_scansort_library_export_to_sidecar(vault_label)` — resolves `vault_label` against session, writes the full current library to `<vault-stem>.rules.json` next to the vault.\n- `minerva_scansort_library_import_from_sidecar(vault_label)` — reads `<vault-stem>.rules.json`, merges each `FileRule` into the library by `label` (last-write-wins; the imported rule overwrites the existing rule with same label).\n\n### Existing path-driven tools\n\n- `insert_rule(rules_path=...)`, `list_rules`, `get_rule`, `update_rule`, `delete_rule`, `import_rules_from_json` remain. Marked deprecated for direct LLM use in their descriptions. They are the implementation of the new export/import tools — no functional change.\n\n## Conflict policy on import\n\nLast-write-wins (simplest). If the user wants merge-with-prompt, the panel can do the conflict-resolution dance and call individual `library_update_rule` for the picks. Plugin doesn't gate.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Export round-trip (library → sidecar → library) is identity (modulo `last_modified` timestamp)\n- Import overwrites by label, leaves other library rules untouched\n- Export fails clearly when `vault_label` not in session\n- Classifier reads only from library: ensure `classify_document` ignores a sibling sidecar even when present\n- Migration: existing vaults with sidecars produced by older builds still work via explicit `library_import_from_sidecar`","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:59:29","updated_at":"2026-05-15T19:09:41","priority":2,"tags":["portability","scansort","sidecar"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","resolution":"Shipped in plugins commit c265bcf. Classifier no longer reads sidecar; rules come only from the library and are filtered to enabled. Two new tools: library_export_to_sidecar (writes library → vault sibling) and library_import_from_sidecar (reads sibling → upsert into library). Six path-driven tools (insert/update/delete/list/get/import_rules_from_json) carry deprecation notes in their schemas. Reviewer originally must_fix; three inline fixes applied (enabled filter in classifier, deterministic B7 mtime tests)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2ccb611871939396b09ceb6006c8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"B6 — Retarget Rules Editor dialog at library; top-level menu","description":"## Scope\n\n### GDScript changes\n\n- `ui/rules_editor_dialog.gd`: swap calls — `insert_rule(rules_path=…)` → `library_insert_rule`; `list_rules(rules_path=…)` → `library_list_rules`; same for update/delete/get/enable.\n- Remove the `rules_path` derivation logic from the dialog. It no longer needs a vault.\n- Dialog can be opened in two modes:\n  1. **Library** — default, full CRUD against the global library.\n  2. **Sidecar export view** — when invoked with a `vault_label`, shows a read-only diff of library vs. sidecar with [Export] / [Import] buttons.\n\n### Menu rewiring (`ScansortPanel.gd`)\n\n- New top-level menu item \"Rules Library...\" — calls dialog in Library mode. Available regardless of vault state.\n- Existing \"Vault Rules Editor...\" item (under Vault submenu) → repurpose as \"Export Library to Vault Sidecar...\" → opens dialog in Sidecar mode for the open vault. Gated on at least one open vault.\n- \"Create Vault-Specific Rules\" menu item — remove (per-vault rules are not a v1 feature).\n\n### Smoke coverage\n\nExtend `test/test_scansort_panel_smoke.gd` (currently 418/0) to cover:\n- Library mode opens with empty library\n- Library mode CRUD round-trip via MCP mock\n- Sidecar mode export round-trip\n- Menu item visibility: \"Rules Library...\" always; \"Export ... Sidecar\" gated on open vault\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- LLM-driven rule authoring UX in the chat panel (uses the same `library_*` tools; no extra surface).\n- Per-vault overrides (deferred — see parent DCR).\n\n## Tests\n\n- GDScript smoke for each new code path\n- Visual regression: ensure no dangling sidecar-path widgets in the dialog","created_at":"2026-05-15T17:59:46","updated_at":"2026-05-15T17:59:46","priority":2,"tags":["gdscript","library","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2ccbbcd6741ebd863f1ee3012850","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B7 — Library hot-reload on mtime change","description":"## Scope\n\nCheap-and-correct approach: poll on every library read site, not a watcher thread.\n\n- Track `last_loaded_mtime` alongside the in-memory rules cache.\n- At every read (`library_list_rules`, `classify_document`, `process()`, etc.): `stat(library_path)`. If mtime changed, reload from disk before serving. Same for the LLM-write paths (`library_insert_rule` etc.) — they already write to disk, so they just update the cached `last_loaded_mtime` after their own write.\n- No background thread, no inotify/kqueue/ReadDirectoryChangesW — single-process stat is fast and avoids cross-platform watcher headaches.\n\n## Correctness\n\n- Same plugin process writes ⇒ mtime updates ⇒ next reader sees fresh content.\n- External edit (user opens library.rules.json in $EDITOR) ⇒ mtime bump ⇒ picked up on next plugin read.\n- Concurrent writes within the same plugin process are serialized through the existing single-threaded MCP loop; no extra locking needed.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- File deleted: treat as \"empty library\" (reload returns `RulesFile::default()`), warn to stderr once per session.\n- File replaced atomically (editor save via temp + rename): mtime jumps; reload succeeds.\n- File mid-write at the moment we stat → load: load may fail JSON parse. Behavior: keep prior in-memory rules, log warning, retry on next read. Don't corrupt cache.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Write rule via tool → mtime bumps → second read sees it (this is the happy path; uses existing flow)\n- Mutate the file out-of-band (`fs::write` from test harness) between two `list_rules` calls → second call returns the new rules\n- Delete the file between calls → next read returns empty rules, no panic\n- Corrupt JSON mid-read → prior cache returned, warning logged, recovery on fix","created_at":"2026-05-15T18:00:10","updated_at":"2026-05-15T19:09:53","priority":2,"tags":["library","reload","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","resolution":"Shipped in plugins commit c265bcf. CachedLibrary { mtime, file } behind OnceLock<Mutex<Option<>>>; library_load() stats file, mtime delta triggers reload, missing file returns default + clears cache, corrupt parse with warm cache returns stale clone. library_save() updates cache on every write so in-process writes don't round-trip the disk. Tests verify the auto-reload path properly (sleep past mtime granularity then OOB write) — initial reviewer must_fix called out \"test theater\" using set_library_path_for_test as a cache reset; fixed inline."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2cfcedc273378781fca18837ab0a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"B3/B4 quality follow-up — test gaps + minor semantics","description":"Notes from the Opus review of plugins commit landing B3+B4. None are blocking — the verdict was approve_with_notes — but they should be addressed before HITL on the path-free pipeline.\n\n## Test gaps\n\n1. **`resolve_labels_vault_and_dir` is test theater.** The test in `process.rs` builds a `DestinationRegistry` directly and calls `find_by_id` — bypasses `resolve_labels()` itself. Refactor: either (a) seed the global session and call `resolve_labels` for real, or (b) refactor `resolve_labels` to take `(session_snapshot, labels)` as args so it's pure and testable without touching the global.\n\n2. **No `run()`-level test for the catch-all and skip branches.** Specifically: (a) \"rule fires but `copy_to: [\"missing\"]`\" → `unprocessable` / `no_open_destination` and (b) \"re-run with all destinations open → `skipped_already_processed`\" — neither is exercised by a test. The implementer chose to defer to HITL (option (a) from the brief), but a stubbed-`request_capability` test would catch regressions cheaply.\n\n## Minor semantics\n\n3. **Multi-rule fanout collapses to first-rule's params.** `process.rs` ~line 422 takes `first_action`'s `resolved_subfolder`, `resolved_rename_pattern`, `encrypt`. If two rules fire with different subfolders, the second's params are silently dropped. Specification is ambiguous (the work_item allows union-of-copy_to under a single fanout call); document the choice in the handler, or change to per-rule fanout if that's the intent.\n\n4. **`by_rule` only credits the first-fired rule.** Per-file, `result.bump_rule(...)` is called once with `first_rule_label`. If multiple rules fire on the same file, only one is reported in `by_rule`. Fix: credit every fired rule (or document the convention).\n\n## Trivia\n\n5. **Dead `EntryKind::Source` variant in `session.rs`.** Compiler warns. Either remove or `#[allow(dead_code)]`.\n\n## Suggested order\n\nItems 4 and 5 are one-liners. Item 3 is a doc comment OR a small refactor. Items 1 and 2 are real test work (~30 min). Doing 3+4+5 inline + filing item 1 as another follow-up would also be acceptable.\n\n## Blocking on B6?\n\nNo — the path-free `process()` is functionally correct for HITL exercise. Items 1, 2, 4 should be done before \"go-live\" of the feature; item 3 is a design clarification; item 5 is cosmetic.","created_at":"2026-05-15T18:53:53","updated_at":"2026-05-15T18:53:53","priority":3,"tags":["follow-up","quality","scansort","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2d647108762f9808a21044ed3843","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"rename_pattern resolver missing {description} / {doc_type} / {amount} / {category} tokens — default pattern half-broken","created_at":"2026-05-15T20:46:57","updated_at":"2026-05-15T20:58:24","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","p0","placement","rename_pattern","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","resolution":"Fixed in plugins commit 27d4d42. Resolver now expands all 7 tokens: {year}, {date}, {issuer}/{sender}, {doc_type}, {description}, {amount}, {category}. Empty values fall back to literal \"unknown\" uniformly. Description capped to 60 chars via chars().take() to avoid multi-byte-codepoint panics. Path-traversal/separator sanitization unchanged. End-to-end wired through B3's path-free process() pipeline (process.rs populates DocMeta.doc_type and .amount from Classification). Regression guard test feeds DEFAULT_RENAME_PATTERN through the new resolver and asserts no literal {token} text remains. Cargo 244 → 250 (+6). Tool count unchanged at 71. Smoke 433/0 unchanged. Awaiting user verification via the imminent HITL test before transitioning to verified.","environment":"scansort plugin Rust subprocess, current main at c265bcf. `~/github/plugins/scansort/src/destination.rs:283-296` (`resolve_and_sanitise`).","repro_steps":"1. Create a rule with `rename_pattern = \"{date}_{issuer}_{description}\"` (this is the shipped default).\n2. Run `process()` on a source file that classifies successfully.\n3. Resolved filename: `2024-04-15_msft_{description}.pdf` — literal `{description}` text leaks into the on-disk filename.\n\nVerification via grep:\n- `grep -n \"fn resolve_and_sanitise\\|replace(\" ~/github/plugins/scansort/src/destination.rs` shows only `{year}`, `{date}`, `{issuer}`, `{sender}` replacements.\n- `grep \"DEFAULT_RENAME_PATTERN\" ~/github/plugins/scansort/src/rules_file.rs` shows the constant uses `{description}`.\n- `types::Classification` has `description`, `doc_type`, `amount`, `category` fields — all unused by the resolver."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2d8018957398b402331f6c0fb8cb","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_list_models omits the \"core\" provider's model catalogue","created_at":"2026-05-15T21:17:10","updated_at":"2026-05-15T21:17:10","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["core-provider","enumeration","mcp","minerva","models"],"environment":"Minerva on user/imran/experiments/swarm at HEAD a6fb5fc4. The single `provider: \"turnrock\"` entry in list_models output has model_name `\"Unknown\"` — looks like a placeholder, not a real enumeration.","repro_steps":"1. Call `minerva_list_models` with no filter. Returns 33 models.\n2. grep the output for `gemma`, `core`, or any indication the \"core\" provider's catalogue is present. None found.\n3. Open the model picker in the Minerva chat UI — gemma4 is available there under the core provider.\n4. Send a \"hi\" chat with gemma4 selected — works.\n5. The scansort plugin's classify_document tool can also reach gemma4 via host.providers.chat. So the provider IS wired; only the enumeration tool is incomplete."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e2d82ca7275e59946ebc1721dfcc0","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"process() / classifier can't pin to a different model than the chat / agent default","created_at":"2026-05-15T21:20:06","updated_at":"2026-05-15T21:20:06","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["agent-surface","classifier","model-routing","process","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e2cc988ec787fbeeda8e3012ab09f","environment":"scansort plugin Rust subprocess at HEAD 27d4d42. process.rs run() calls classify (via request_capability host.providers.chat). The model parameter exists on the underlying classify_document tool but isn't reachable from process().","repro_steps":"1. Open Minerva. Set chat default model to, say, gpt-5.4.\n2. Open a vault, open a source dir.\n3. Insert a rule via library_insert_rule.\n4. Call `minerva_scansort_process()`.\n5. Observe: classify calls go to gpt-5.4 (the chat default), not the cheaper model the user might prefer for batch document scoring.\n\nThe user has no way to say \"run classifier on gemma4:e4b\" without first changing their chat default — which then affects every other chat in the app."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb","type":"dcr","status":"designing","title":"Scansort processing log — trace-first, persona-stripped (supersedes audit-log design in 019e2787 §7)","description":"## Problem\n\nThe W9 + W10 audit log (shipped) was designed under a CPA-shaped persona that has leaked into the implementation:\n\n- `src/audit.rs:7` — module docstring says \"intended for CPA/spreadsheet import\"\n- `src/audit.rs:204` — comment justifies CSV line semantics as \"important for CPA spreadsheet import\"\n- `ui/settings_dialog.gd:252` — UI label literally reads `\"Audit Log (CPA export)\"`\n- Parent DCR 019e2787 §7 — describes the log as \"CPA-owned, portable, CSV-importable\"\n- Parent DCR 019e2787 tags include `cpa`\n\nStripped of the persona, the design constraints look arbitrary:\n\n| Constraint | Why it was there | Why it's wrong now |\n|---|---|---|\n| CSV-only | spreadsheets | JSONL is better for variable-shape events; no escaping nightmare |\n| Write-only, never read back | imagined user wouldn't open it as a debug tool | reading back is the most valuable thing the file can do |\n| Fixed 10 columns, 3 event types (`placement\\|skipped\\|superseded`) | spreadsheet schema stability | precludes classification trace, stage events, filter events |\n| One row per placement | one CSV row per audit fact | hides everything that led to the placement |\n\nToday's two-phase classifier already makes decisions that aren't logged anywhere — a doc marked `unprocessable` shows `reason: \"no_rule_match\"` in the result summary but the per-rule scoring is invisible. Pipeline support (separate DCR in design) makes this worse: pipelines can drop documents at filter stages, and without per-stage trace events users get exactly the \"silent drop\" failure mode auditable filtering is supposed to prevent.\n\n## Reframe\n\n**Trace is primary. Audit CSV is a downstream projection.**\n\n- **Format:** JSONL, one event per line. Append-only. **Designed to be read back.**\n- **Event vocabulary** (covers today's behavior and future pipelines):\n  - `run_started` — process() invocation begins ({run_id, source_dir, destinations, rule_count, mode})\n  - `doc_seen` — doc entered the engine ({run_id, doc_id, source_filename, source_sha256})\n  - `rule_evaluated` — rule scored against this doc ({rule_label, score, threshold, fired})\n  - `stage_executed` — pipeline stage ran ({rule_label, stage_index, ask, slots_extracted, keep_when, keep_result, llm_elapsed_ms}) — future\n  - `doc_filtered` — keep_when returned false; doc dropped ({rule_label, stage_index, reason}) — future\n  - `template_resolved` — subfolder/rename_pattern expanded ({rule_label, subfolder, filename, tokens})\n  - `placement` — file placed ({destination_id, destination_kind, resolved_path, disposition}) — dispositions preserved from today\n  - `doc_unprocessable` — no rule fired ({reason})\n  - `error` — anything that failed ({where, error_kind, error_message})\n  - `run_completed` — process() finished ({docs_seen, docs_placed, docs_filtered, docs_unprocessable, errors, total_elapsed_ms})\n\nA successful doc traces: `doc_seen → rule_evaluated × N → stage_executed × M → template_resolved → placement`.\nA filtered doc traces: `doc_seen → rule_evaluated → stage_executed → doc_filtered`.\n\nGrep-friendly: `jq 'select(.event==\"doc_filtered\")'` for all drops; `jq 'select(.event==\"placement\" and .disposition==\"placed\")'` is the rough CPA-row predicate.\n\n## Persona strip\n\nThe word \"CPA\" leaves the codebase, UI, and tags entirely:\n\n| Location | Today | Becomes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/audit.rs` (614 lines) | CSV writer, persona comments | `src/trace.rs` — JSONL writer, comments rewritten without persona framing |\n| `src/main.rs:1964` | `handle_audit_append` | `handle_trace_append` |\n| MCP tool name | `minerva_scansort_audit_append` | `minerva_scansort_trace_append` |\n| Settings data field | `audit_log_path`, `audit_log_enabled` | `trace_log_path`, `trace_log_enabled` |\n| `settings_dialog.gd:252` label | `\"Audit Log (CPA export)\"` | `\"Processing Log\"` |\n| Parent DCR 019e2787 §7 | \"CPA-owned, CSV-importable\" design | superseded by this DCR for that section only |\n| Parent DCR 019e2787 tags | includes `cpa` | drop `cpa` tag |\n\nThe eventual CSV-export tool gets a persona-neutral description: *\"Project placement events from the processing log into a flat 10-column CSV view for spreadsheet import.\"*\n\n## Implementation phases\n\n1. **Trace infrastructure.** `trace.rs` (JSONL writer, append-only, ISO-8601 timestamps), `minerva_scansort_trace_append` MCP tool, Settings field rename + UI label, panel wiring. Emits only events the engine already knows about: `run_started`, `doc_seen`, `placement`, `doc_unprocessable`, `error`, `run_completed`. **Audit.rs gets removed in this phase** (not parallel-maintained — see Migration).\n\n2. **Engine instrumentation.** Add a `Tracer` abstraction inside `process()` that calls trace_append at the right points. Adds `rule_evaluated` and `template_resolved` events. **Makes single-stage rules already auditable** — \"why was this doc unprocessable?\" becomes answerable from the log alone.\n\n3. **Pipeline events (deferred to pipeline DCR).** `stage_executed` and `doc_filtered` event shapes are reserved here; emitters land with the pipeline schema work. Do NOT ship pipelines before phase 2 is in place.\n\n4. **CSV export tool (separate follow-up DCR).** `minerva_scansort_trace_export_csv` reads JSONL, filters to placement events, writes the legacy 10-column CSV. Spreadsheet audience is served by this tool, not by the primary log format.\n\n## Migration\n\nThe W9+W10 audit log is shipped; users may have configured `audit_log_path` pointing at a `.csv` file with real history.\n\n- **No automatic conversion.** The CSV is what was promised (write-only, never re-read). Preserve users' files; do not touch them.\n- **First-run detection:** if the configured `audit_log_path` exists and ends in `.csv`, log a one-time warning in the panel: *\"Audit log renamed to Processing Log; new format is JSONL. Writing new events to `<basename>.jsonl` alongside. Your old `.csv` is unchanged.\"*\n- **Settings migration:** read legacy `audit_log_path` once on startup, copy to `trace_log_path` (changing extension to `.jsonl`), leave legacy keys in place for one release cycle then delete.\n\n## Scope boundaries\n\n- This DCR covers the **log layer only**. Engine `Tracer` hooks are phase 2 here.\n- Pipeline schema is a **separate DCR** (in design, conversation 2026-05-16); references this DCR as a prerequisite.\n- This does NOT change the rule schema, the `process()` API surface, or any existing per-event semantics (placement dispositions stay: `placed | skipped-already-present | kept-both | replaced | superseded | error`).\n- CSV-export tool is a **separate follow-up DCR**, deliberately not bundled — the trace value stands alone.\n\n## Why this is worth doing before pipelines\n\nPipelines (separate DCR) can drop documents at `keep_when` filter stages. The lawyer's discovery case relies on this — a stage-1 relevance gate that prunes a 200-doc dump to ~10%. Without trace events for those drops, the lawyer has no answer to \"why didn't this doc make it through?\" — exactly the failure mode auditable filtering is supposed to prevent. Phase 1+2 of this DCR is the prerequisite for pipelines being honest about what they did.\n\n## Status\n\nProposed. Discussion 2026-05-16 (Imran, laptop session, post-B8 HITL) settled the reframe and the persona strip. Next: decompose into W-series work_items for phases 1 and 2.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T01:52:39","updated_at":"2026-05-18T02:05:16","priority":2,"tags":["logging","persona-strip","plugin","scansort","trace"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Scansort rule schema redesign — stages/classify/keep_when, skill-first authoring, library-tools UI (supersedes 019e2cc988ec)","description":"## Background\n\nThis DCR consolidates three threads:\n\n1. **B8 (doc_type normalization)** shipped `subtypes` as a closed sub-taxonomy field, but the persona analysis (accountant / lawyer / citizen scenarios, walked 2026-05-16) showed the 8 built-in tokens (`{year}`, `{date}`, `{issuer}`, `{sender}`, `{doc_type}`, `{description}`, `{amount}`, `{category}`) are a leaky abstraction — the plugin author can't anticipate the recipient/incident/subject tokens users actually need.\n\n2. **Path-free DCR `019e2cc988ec`** identified the problem (UI hardcodes paths, can't be agent-driven) and partly solved it (the `library_*` MCP tools exist in `main.rs:3274+` and are session-aware). But the UI still uses the deprecated path-driven tools, and the rules editor dialog is two schema versions behind. This DCR supersedes 019e2cc988ec — its concerns are folded in here and given a wider plan.\n\n3. **Audit of the current rules UI** (2026-05-16) found that: there is no panel-level rules surface today (everything is in the File menu); the editor dialog is a 624-line modal form that hasn't kept up with the schema (missing `rename_pattern`, `subtypes`, `is_default`, `order`, `stop_processing`, `copy_to`); the recurring \"UI behind schema\" failure mode is the dominant maintenance cost.\n\n## Scope: IN\n\n- New rule schema: self-describing `stages: [{ask, classify, keep_when?}]`, no built-in tokens\n- Authoring model: focused-chat skill shipped in `scansort/manifest.json`, runs against any in-Minerva LLM\n- Rules pane: inline panel in `ScansortPanel.gd`, library-tool-driven, with per-row activate, reorder, dry-run, delete\n- Paste-JSON dialog for power users (replaces the 624-line form)\n- Dry-run \"Test on…\" affordance (highest-leverage missing piece per audit)\n- Migration: gut `rules_editor_dialog.gd`, delete the 2 vault-sidecar File-menu entries, switch UI from path-driven tools to `library_*` tools\n- Supersedes path-free DCR `019e2cc988ec` (its rules-editor-retarget scope is fully covered here)\n\n## Scope: OUT (deferred to follow-up DCRs)\n\n- **Pipelines as a separate top-level concept** — `stages` is *internal* to each rule (Model A in the rubric scoring; preserves today's multi-rule Phase 1+2 walk). Multi-rule cross-talk and global pipelines are not in scope.\n- **Cross-stage prompt templating** — stage 2's `ask` cannot reference `{slot}` from stage 1 in v1. Static prompts only. Add later if real authors hit the wall.\n- **Vault-level shared classify slots** (`classify_library` / `$ref` mechanism) — accepted-and-deferred repetition cost; the skill provides copy-paste templates for `date`/`sender`/`description` in v1.\n- **Conditions/exceptions evaluation** — `conditions`/`exceptions` are stored but not evaluated today (parent DCR §1, W1 placeholders). This DCR doesn't change that — they stay deferred.\n- **CSV export of audit-style placement view** — covered by trace-log DCR `019e33a2`, not here.\n\n## The rule schema\n\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"tax_by_client\",\n  \"name\": \"Per-client tax filings\",\n  \"instruction\": \"Tax forms and statements received from clients.\",\n  \"confidence_threshold\": 0.6,\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"is_default\": false,\n  \"encrypt\": false,\n  \"order\": 10,\n  \"stop_processing\": false,\n\n  \"stages\": [\n    {\n      \"ask\": \"Is this a tax-relevant document?\",\n      \"classify\": {\n        \"is_tax\": {\"description\": \"tax-relevant\", \"values\": [\"yes\", \"no\"]}\n      },\n      \"keep_when\": \"is_tax == 'yes'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"ask\": \"Whose form is this, and what kind?\",\n      \"classify\": {\n        \"client\": {\"description\": \"the recipient's name\", \"values\": \"a person's name, or 'unknown'\"},\n        \"form\":   {\"description\": \"form type\",            \"values\": [\"1099\", \"W-2\", \"1040\", \"K-1\", \"other\"]},\n        \"year\":   {\"description\": \"tax year\",             \"values\": \"a 4-digit year, or 'unknown'\"}\n      },\n      \"keep_when\": \"client != 'unknown'\"\n    }\n  ],\n\n  \"subfolder\": \"{client}/{year}\",\n  \"rename_pattern\": \"{form}.pdf\",\n  \"copy_to\": []\n}\n```\n\nDesign choices (each scored against the Reliable/Durable/Human-parseable/Parsimonious rubric, all 4/4 or 3/1):\n\n| Decision | Choice |\n|---|---|\n| Multi-rule model | A: per-rule pipelines, Phase 1+2 walk preserved |\n| Stage container name | `stages` |\n| Filter grammar | minimal: `==`, `!=`, `in [...]` |\n| Single-stage rules | always wrapped in `stages: [{...}]` (no flat shortcut) |\n| Slot-name collisions across stages | reject at `library_insert_rule` time |\n| Cross-stage prompt templating | disallowed in v1 |\n| Default `on_filter` behavior | leave doc in source; trace event records the drop |\n| Rule-level `instruction` | kept (used by Phase 1 scoring) |\n| Built-in tokens (year/date/issuer/sender/doc_type/description/amount/category) | **removed** — every token comes from a classify slot |\n| `signals` field | **removed** — absorbed into `instruction` text |\n| `subtypes` field (B8) | **removed** — equivalent to a closed `classify` slot |\n\n## Authoring model: skill in plugin manifest\n\nAuthoring lives in a focused-chat skill declared inline in `scansort/manifest.json` under the existing `skills` array (same convention as cad plugin's `minerva_cad_modeling` skill). User flow:\n\n1. User opens Scansort panel (registers plugin tools)\n2. User opens focused chat, adds \"Scansort rule authoring\" to the focused-chat skill list\n3. User describes their use case\n4. Skill walks the worked-example most matching the user's case (one of the four persona archetypes), proposes JSON, calls `minerva_scansort_library_insert_rule`\n5. Rules pane refreshes via the existing library-changed signal\n\nSkill content constraints:\n- Must work for any model the user has wired (local 7B included). Targets ~3–5 KB total — densely directive, worked-example-heavy, less prose than cad's ~10 KB skill.\n- `tool_deps` enumerates: `library_list_rules`, `library_insert_rule`, `library_update_rule`, `library_delete_rule`, `library_enable_rule`, `library_disable_rule`, `library_reorder_rules` (new), `dryrun_one` (new).\n- `preconditions`: \"Scansort plugin is started; open the Scansort panel once if it isn't.\"\n\nThe plugin pane does NOT try to launch focused chat itself — that's the user's job, same pattern cad uses. The pane shows a one-line help: *\"To author rules, open Focused Chat and add 'Scansort rule authoring' to your skill list.\"*\n\n## Rules pane UI\n\nInline section in `ScansortPanel.gd`. Read-only list + per-row controls. Calls `library_*` tools throughout (no more path-driven CRUD from the UI).\n\nSketch:\n```\n┌─ Rules ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  + Paste JSON   ⓘ Author via Focused Chat                    │\n│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │\n│  [✓] tax         → tax/                  · 47 fired   [⋮]    │\n│       \"Tax documents — typically W-2, 1099, 1040 forms.\"     │\n│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │\n│  [✓] housing     → housing/{location}/   · 12 fired   [⋮]    │\n│       \"Mortgage statements, HOA notices, …\"                  │\n│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │\n│  [○] healthcare-bills (disabled)         · 0 fired    [⋮]    │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nPer row:\n- Activate toggle → calls `library_enable_rule` / `library_disable_rule`\n- Label → resolved subfolder/rename preview\n- Fired-count → derived from trace log (DCR `019e33a2`)\n- Instruction excerpt (truncated)\n- `[⋮]` menu: Test on…, View JSON, Edit JSON (raw paste), Duplicate, Delete, Move up, Move down\n\nReorder UX: starts with `Move up` / `Move down` in the row menu (bulletproof, ugly). Drag-handles deferred until/unless we want to invest in Godot ItemList DnD.\n\n## Dry-run tester (\"Test on…\")\n\nNew MCP tool `minerva_scansort_dryrun_one`:\n- **Input:** `{rule_label: <string>, doc_path: <abs path>}` or `{doc_path: <abs path>}` (all enabled rules).\n- **Behavior:** Same code path as `process()` for one document, except no filesystem placement and no trace-log row for `placement`. Emits the full pre-placement trace (`rule_evaluated`, `stage_executed`, `template_resolved`).\n- **Returns:** `{ok, rule_label, fired: bool, score, threshold, stages_executed: [{ask, slots, keep_when, kept}], resolved_subfolder, resolved_filename, would_copy_to}`.\n\nUI surface: per-row \"Test on…\" opens a file picker, runs the tool, shows result in a side panel:\n\n```\nTested: receipt.pdf\nRule: tax — DID NOT FIRE\n  Phase 1 score: 0.21  (threshold: 0.60)\n  No stages executed.\n```\n\n```\nTested: 2024-fidelity-1099.pdf\nRule: tax — FIRED\n  Phase 1 score: 0.92\n  Stage 1: kept (is_tax = yes)\n  Stage 2: kept\n    client = Smith, J.\n    form   = 1099\n    year   = 2024\n  Would file as: tax/Smith, J./2024/1099.pdf\n```\n\n## New MCP tools needed\n\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `minerva_scansort_library_reorder_rules` | Takes `{order: [label, label, …]}`; one round-trip vs N individual updates |\n| `minerva_scansort_dryrun_one` | Single-doc dry-run, returns full pre-placement trace |\n\n## Migration & deletions\n\n| Today | Action |\n|---|---|\n| `rules_editor_dialog.gd` (624 LoC, form-based, path-driven) | **Gut to ~50 LoC** — read-only detail view + Delete + \"Paste JSON\" textarea. Or delete entirely if the row menu covers all the needs. |\n| File menu entries 4, 8, 9, 10 (Vault Rules Editor, Library Rules Editor, Create Vault-Specific Rules, Use Library Rules) | **Delete all 4.** Replaced by the inline pane (no entry needed) and the row menu's \"Edit JSON\". |\n| Handlers `_on_create_vault_rules_pressed`, `_on_use_library_rules_pressed` | **Delete.** ~50 LoC. |\n| Path-driven MCP tools (`minerva_scansort_list_rules`, `insert_rule`, `update_rule`, `delete_rule`, `get_rule`, `import_rules_from_json`) | **Mark deprecated in v1**, remove in next major. Document in tool description. |\n| `library_export_to_sidecar` / `library_import_from_sidecar` | **Keep backend tools** in case anyone scripts them; **do not expose in UI or skill**. |\n| `signals` field in rule | **Removed from schema.** Migration: existing rules with `signals: [...]` get the keywords appended to `instruction` text on first load (one-time, logged). |\n| `subtypes` field in rule | **Removed.** Migration: existing rules with `subtypes` get a `stages: [{ask: \"What kind of document is this?\", classify: {doc_type: {values: [<names>]}}}]` injected, with `also_known_as` aliases dropped (closed list semantics preserved, alias-mapping handled by Phase 1 LLM seeing the closed list). |\n| Built-in tokens in `rename_pattern` / `subfolder` | **Migration:** existing rules using `{date}`/`{sender}`/`{description}` get the corresponding `classify` slots auto-injected in stage 1. |\n\n## edit_details_dialog.gd downstream impact\n\nPopulates a category dropdown from rules array (lines 114–160). With the new schema:\n- Rule.label is still the category name (no change).\n- Dropdown source switches from path-driven `list_rules` to `library_list_rules`.\n- No further changes needed.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- **Trace log DCR `019e33a2`** — fired-count column in the pane and the dry-run trace output both depend on trace events being emitted. Trace log phases 1+2 must land before the pane and dry-run are useful.\n- **No new dependencies on parent DCR `019e2787`** — multi-rule walk, copy_to fan-out, dedup, audit all preserved.\n\n## Supersession of `019e2cc988ec`\n\nThe path-free DCR covered: (a) path-free MCP surface, (b) global rules library, (c) session model, (d) rules-editor retarget (B6 in pickup notes). This DCR covers (a) and (b) by reaffirmation (already implemented as the `library_*` tools), explicitly defers (c) to remain as-is (session model is fine), and supersedes (d) with the full inline-pane + skill redesign. After this DCR is approved, `019e2cc988ec` should be transitioned to `superseded` with a pointer to this DCR.\n\n## Open questions to resolve during designing\n\n- Exact JSON shape returned by `dryrun_one` for the case where Phase 1 fired multiple rules — do we run all of them and return per-rule traces, or only the highest-scoring?\n- Where the rules pane lives inside ScansortPanel: a new tab, a collapsible section under existing UI, or a popup window? (Probably collapsible section for parity with how the cad plugin surfaces its panels.)\n- Migration timing for `signals` / `subtypes` removal: do we ship a v1 that accepts both old and new schemas during a transition period, or do a one-shot rewrite on first load?\n\n## Status\n\nProposed. Designed across a long conversation 2026-05-16 (Imran, laptop session). Next: decompose into W-series work_items. Recommend implementation ordering:\n\n1. New `library_reorder_rules` + `dryrun_one` MCP tools (small, both fully internal).\n2. Trace log instrumentation (DCR `019e33a2` phase 2) — prerequisite for fired-count and dry-run trace output.\n3. Rules pane skeleton: list view backed by `library_list_rules` + activate toggle, no row menu yet.\n4. Row menu: Test on… (dry-run), Move up/down, Delete, Edit JSON.\n5. Paste-JSON dialog.\n6. Schema migration on library load (signals/subtypes/built-in tokens → new shape).\n7. Skill content: write, drop in manifest.json, ship.\n8. Delete the 4 File-menu entries + their handlers + gut `rules_editor_dialog.gd`.\n9. Deprecate path-driven MCP tools (description-only; physical removal next major).\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:24:17","updated_at":"2026-05-17T03:25:07","priority":2,"tags":["plugin","rules","scansort","schema","skill","supersedes-019e2cc988ec","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e2787d0e178029cd46b93059f7ed9"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ce48c87076a17b64cb6d7ff5cd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1: Rule schema types — Rust struct + serde","description":"## What\n\nDefine the new Rule struct per DCR 019e33bf:\n\n```rust\nstruct Rule {\n    label: String,\n    name: String,\n    instruction: String,\n    confidence_threshold: f64,\n    enabled: bool,\n    is_default: bool,\n    encrypt: bool,\n    order: i64,\n    stop_processing: bool,\n    stages: Vec<Stage>,\n    subfolder: String,\n    rename_pattern: String,\n    copy_to: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nstruct Stage {\n    ask: String,\n    classify: HashMap<String, Slot>,\n    keep_when: Option<String>,\n}\n\nstruct Slot {\n    description: String,\n    values: SlotValues,\n}\n\nenum SlotValues {\n    Closed(Vec<String>),\n    Open(String),  // natural-language constraint\n}\n```\n\nDrop `signals` and `subtypes` from the Rust types entirely. Reject slot-name collisions across stages at construction time.\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/rules.rs` (Rule struct redefinition)\n- `src/types.rs` if Stage/Slot/SlotValues live there\n- Serde derives + custom (de)serialization for SlotValues enum (deserialize as array → Closed, string → Open)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `cargo build` + `cargo test` green\n- New Rule struct round-trips through serde to the JSON shape in DCR 019e33bf §\"The rule schema\"\n- Round-trip preserves all fields including nested stages/classify/slots\n- Round-trip tests for the three persona examples (tax single-stage, lawyer multi-stage with filter, citizen drawings open-slot)\n- Slot collision rejected at construction (e.g. `Rule::validate()` returns Err if two stages declare the same slot key)\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing — foundational.\n\n## Note\n\nThis intentionally breaks legacy library files on load. W5 (migration) handles that with a single-shot conversion on first read. Land W1+W5 together OR gate W1 behind a feature flag until W5 is ready.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:17","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:06","priority":2,"tags":["plugin","rust","scansort","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ce5f18754592a8f54e8dab904e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W2: Engine adaptation — classifier + rule_engine consume new schema","description":"## What\n\n- **Phase 1 (`classifier.rs`):** Score rules using just `instruction` text. No `signals` field anymore (any keywords belong in instruction text).\n- **Phase 2 (`rule_engine.rs`):** For each rule that fired in Phase 1, walk its `stages` array:\n  1. Build LLM prompt from stage's `ask` + classify slot questions (one tiny structured prompt per stage)\n  2. Parse LLM response into slot values, validate (closed slots: value must be in list; open slots: pass through)\n  3. Evaluate `keep_when` against accumulated slots: if false, short-circuit (emit `doc_filtered` trace event, doc is \"filtered out\" — placement is skipped)\n  4. Otherwise accumulate slot values and continue to next stage\n- **Stage-folding optimization:** Adjacent stages with no intervening `keep_when` can be composed into a single LLM call (all their slot questions in one structured prompt). Invisible to author.\n- Template resolver (`resolve_template()`) now sources tokens from accumulated slots only — no built-in fallback.\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/classifier.rs` (Phase 1 prompt build)\n- `src/rule_engine.rs` (Phase 2 stage walk + template resolve)\n- `src/process.rs` (call site updates)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Rust tests green for: single-stage rule, multi-stage rule with filter that passes, multi-stage rule with filter that fails (doc filtered), slot accumulation across stages, template expansion with classify-only tokens, missing-slot fallback to `\"unknown\"`\n- Tax rule from DCR 019e33bf processes correctly end-to-end\n- Lawyer rule (2 stages, filter on stage 1) drops irrelevant docs and processes survivors\n- Stage-folding optimization verified: a 2-stage rule with no `keep_when` issues one LLM call, not two\n\n## Depends on\n\nW1 (Rule struct types must exist).\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:23","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:09","priority":2,"tags":["engine","plugin","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ce6e247c7f948948ffd5709697","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W3: MCP tool `library_reorder_rules`","description":"## What\n\nNew MCP tool `minerva_scansort_library_reorder_rules`:\n- **Input:** `{order: [label, label, ...]}`\n- **Behavior:** Reassigns the `order` field on each rule so they match the input array's index order. Uses gaps (0, 10, 20, ...) to allow future insertions.\n- **Validation:** Input array MUST contain exactly the set of labels currently in the library — no extra, no missing. Reject with clear error otherwise.\n- **Output:** `{ok, new_order: [{label, order}, ...]}`\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/main.rs` (handler + tool registration)\n- `src/rules.rs` (if helper needed)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Tool registered in tool catalog\n- Happy path: 3-rule library reordered correctly, persisted to file\n- Rejection: input missing a label → error message naming the missing label(s)\n- Rejection: input with an extra label → error message naming the extra label(s)\n- Rejection: empty input on non-empty library → error\n- Rust tests green\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing — works on existing `order` field, independent of W1/W2 schema changes.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:27","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:13","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","plugin","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ce855a764096348ba15ec287bb","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W4: MCP tool `dryrun_one` — pre-placement trace for a single doc","description":"## What\n\nNew MCP tool `minerva_scansort_dryrun_one`:\n- **Input:** `{rule_label?: string, doc_path: string}` — if `rule_label` provided, only that rule; otherwise all enabled rules.\n- **Behavior:** Runs extract → classify (Phase 1) → rule walk with stages (Phase 2) → template resolve. **No filesystem placement.** **No `placement` trace event written.** Pre-placement trace events (`rule_evaluated`, `stage_executed`, `template_resolved`) ARE emitted to the trace log if enabled.\n- **Output:**\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"ok\": true,\n    \"doc_path\": \"...\",\n    \"doc_sha256\": \"...\",\n    \"rules_evaluated\": [\n      {\n        \"rule_label\": \"tax\",\n        \"score\": 0.92,\n        \"threshold\": 0.6,\n        \"fired\": true,\n        \"stages\": [\n          {\"ask\": \"...\", \"slots\": {...}, \"keep_when\": \"...\", \"kept\": true},\n          ...\n        ],\n        \"resolved_subfolder\": \"tax/Smith, J./2024\",\n        \"resolved_filename\": \"1099.pdf\",\n        \"would_copy_to\": [\"test\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n  ```\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/main.rs` (handler + tool registration)\n- `src/process.rs` (factor placement-free path into a shared function `process_one_no_placement()`)\n- `src/trace.rs` (emit pre-placement events, not placement)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Tool registered, returns the documented shape\n- No files moved or copied\n- Trace log shows pre-placement events; no `placement` event for dry-run runs\n- Happy paths: doc matches a rule (fired=true, full stage trace), doc matches no rule (rules_evaluated all show fired=false)\n- Filter-drops-doc case: stage's `kept: false` recorded, no template resolution attempted\n- Rust tests green\n\n## Depends on\n\n- W2 (engine adaptation — Phase 1+2 must consume new schema)\n- Trace log DCR 019e33a2 phases 1+2 (for pre-placement events to be defined and emittable)\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:33","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:17","priority":2,"tags":["dry-run","mcp","plugin","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ce9ff17976aacd6b42841e4f3f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W5: Schema migration on library load — legacy signals/subtypes/built-in tokens → new shape","description":"## What\n\nOn `minerva_scansort_library_list_rules` (and any other path that reads the library file from disk), detect legacy fields and migrate each rule in-place. Conversions:\n\n1. **`signals: [w1, w2, ...]`** → append `\"; typical signals include w1, w2, ...\"` to `instruction`. Drop the field.\n\n2. **`subtypes: [{name, also_known_as}, ...]`** → if no `stages` yet, inject:\n   ```json\n   \"stages\": [{\n     \"ask\": \"What kind of document is this?\",\n     \"classify\": {\n       \"doc_type\": {\n         \"description\": \"the document type\",\n         \"values\": [<names>]\n       }\n     }\n   }]\n   ```\n   Drop `also_known_as` aliases (closed-list semantics preserved by the LLM seeing the canonical values).\n\n3. **Rules with no `stages` but `rename_pattern`/`subfolder` referencing built-in tokens** (`{date}`, `{sender}`, `{description}`, `{issuer}`, `{year}`, `{amount}`, `{category}`) → inject a stage that declares the referenced slots:\n   ```json\n   \"stages\": [{\n     \"ask\": \"Extract the document's date, who issued/sent it, and a brief description.\",\n     \"classify\": {\n       \"date\":        {\"description\": \"the document date\",              \"values\": \"YYYY-MM-DD or 'undated'\"},\n       \"sender\":      {\"description\": \"who issued or sent the document\", \"values\": \"a name or 'unknown'\"},\n       \"description\": {\"description\": \"brief description in 2-5 words\", \"values\": \"a short phrase or 'unknown'\"}\n     }\n   }]\n   ```\n   Only inject slots actually referenced in templates; don't add unused ones.\n\nMigration is **single-shot per rule** and writes back to the library file. Emit `migration_applied` trace event per rule (event vocabulary to be confirmed with trace DCR 019e33a2).\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/rules.rs` (library load path)\n- `src/migrate.rs` (new helper)\n- Tests for each conversion case + combined cases\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Loading a legacy library file converts all rules in-place; library file is updated on disk\n- Conversions match the rules above\n- Rust tests for: legacy signals only, legacy subtypes only, legacy built-in tokens only, all three combined, a rule that's already in new shape (no-op)\n- Trace log shows migration events when enabled\n- Re-loading a migrated library is a no-op (idempotent)\n\n## Depends on\n\nW1 (new Rule struct must exist to deserialize INTO).\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:39","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:20","priority":2,"tags":["migration","plugin","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33ceb5ca7204997a8859c305b823","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W6: Rules pane skeleton — inline section with list + activate toggle","description":"## What\n\nAdd a Rules section to `ScansortPanel.gd`. Initially a collapsible section under the existing UI (or a new tab — author's call, parity with cad's panel layout).\n\nPer-row:\n- Enabled toggle (checkbox or icon) → calls `minerva_scansort_library_enable_rule` / `library_disable_rule`\n- Label\n- Resolved `subfolder` / `rename_pattern` preview (e.g. `tax/{client}/{year}` shown as-is; not LLM-resolved)\n- Fired-count from trace log (`—` if trace log disabled or unreadable)\n- 1-line truncated instruction excerpt\n\nRefreshes on library-changed signal (existing in the codebase per audit).\n\nRead-only otherwise — no row menu yet (that's W7).\n\n## Files\n\n- `ui/ScansortPanel.gd` (new section)\n- Possibly `ui/rules_pane.gd` if the code grows beyond ~150 LoC\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Section visible in panel, lists current library rules from `library_list_rules`\n- Toggle persists across panel close/reopen\n- Fired-count shown as integer when trace log readable; `—` otherwise (graceful)\n- Panel smoke test passes (≥ current 433/0)\n- Refresh after rule changes (via library-changed signal)\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing strictly. Library_list_rules + enable/disable tools already exist. Fired-count from trace log is graceful-degradation, so doesn't strictly block on the trace-log DCR.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:45","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:55","priority":2,"tags":["gdscript","plugin","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cec9a27913a35b41b5c99662bc","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W7: Rules pane row menu — Test on…, reorder, JSON view/edit, duplicate, delete","description":"## What\n\nAdd `[⋮]` menu button to each row in the Rules pane (W6). Menu items:\n\n- **Test on…** — opens file picker → call `minerva_scansort_dryrun_one` with `rule_label` and chosen `doc_path` → open `dryrun_result_dialog` showing:\n  - Rule fired or not, score vs threshold\n  - Per-stage: ask, slots extracted, keep_when result\n  - Resolved subfolder + filename if fired\n  - Would-copy-to destinations\n- **Move up / Move down** — compute new order array, call `library_reorder_rules`\n- **View JSON** — open paste-JSON dialog (W8) in read-only mode with current rule\n- **Edit JSON** — open paste-JSON dialog (W8) prefilled with current rule, on save call `library_update_rule`\n- **Duplicate** — call `library_insert_rule` with current rule's body + `label = \"<original>_copy\"`\n- **Delete** — confirm dialog → `library_delete_rule`\n\n## Files\n\n- `ui/rules_pane.gd` (menu wiring)\n- `ui/dryrun_result_dialog.gd` (new, small)\n- `ui/ScansortPanel.gd` (wiring if pane is inline)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All 6 menu items wired and functional\n- Dry-run result dialog shows score, per-stage trace, resolved subfolder/filename\n- Move up/down preserves order across panel reloads\n- Delete confirms before destructive action\n- Duplicate creates a new rule with `_copy` suffix; user can edit it after\n- Panel smoke test passes\n\n## Depends on\n\n- W3 (`library_reorder_rules`)\n- W4 (`dryrun_one`)\n- W6 (rules pane skeleton)\n- W8 (paste-JSON dialog for View/Edit)\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:50","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:59","priority":2,"tags":["gdscript","plugin","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cedae87e3da09cb31d3a38213f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W8: Paste-JSON dialog — small textarea, calls library_insert/update_rule","description":"## What\n\nNew `ui/paste_json_dialog.gd`:\n- TextArea for JSON rule body\n- Mode: `new` (empty TextArea, Save calls `library_insert_rule`) or `edit` (prefilled, Save calls `library_update_rule` with `label` from JSON)\n- Optional `readonly: bool` for the \"View JSON\" case\n- On Save: try JSON parse → if parse fails, show inline error in dialog; if MCP call returns error, show in dialog (don't close)\n- On success: close dialog, panel refreshes via library-changed signal\n\n## Files\n\n- `ui/paste_json_dialog.gd` (new, ~50-100 LoC)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Dialog opens with empty TextArea (new mode) or current JSON (edit mode)\n- Save calls correct MCP tool with parsed JSON body\n- Closes on success; shows error inline on failure (parse or MCP error)\n- Read-only mode hides Save button, shows Close only\n- No regression in panel smoke\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing strictly, but `library_insert_rule` and `library_update_rule` must accept the new schema (i.e. W1+W2 in place) before paste-JSON of a new-shape rule will succeed. Until then, only paste of legacy-shape rules works (and they'll be migrated by W5 on next load).\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:40:55","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:42:02","priority":2,"tags":["gdscript","plugin","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cef75d7fb3a40bc72f0304d665","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W9: Skill content — `minerva_scansort_rule_authoring` in manifest.json","description":"## What\n\nWrite the rule-authoring skill, embed inline in `scansort/manifest.json` under a new `skills: [...]` array (cad plugin convention; scansort currently has no skills array).\n\nSkill object fields:\n- `id`: `\"minerva_scansort_rule_authoring\"`\n- `title`: `\"Scansort rule authoring\"`\n- `target`: `\"all\"` (must work for any model in Minerva, including local 7B-26B)\n- `summary`: one-line, used in focused-chat skill list\n- `preconditions`: `\"Scansort plugin is started (open the Scansort panel once if it isn't — that registers its tools).\"`\n- `outcome`: what a successful conversation produces (a rule inserted into the library + a dry-run that confirms it works on a sample doc)\n- `steps`: numbered, brief — the conversation procedure the model follows\n- `system_prompt`: the bulk (~3-5 KB), organized:\n  - §0 What a rule is (instruction + stages → matched + extracted → filed)\n  - §1 The two slot modes (closed `values: [...]` vs open `values: \"natural-language constraint\"`)\n  - §2 Four worked persona examples (citizen tax, citizen drawings, accountant client-forms, lawyer discovery) — full JSON, 2-line explanation per field\n  - §3 Slot definition idioms (canonical date/sender/description templates for copy-paste)\n  - §4 How to test (call `dryrun_one` on a sample before committing)\n  - §5 Pitfalls (slot-name collisions, over-narrow filters, missing tokens in templates)\n- `tool_deps`:\n  ```\n  [\"minerva_scansort_library_list_rules\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_insert_rule\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_update_rule\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_delete_rule\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_enable_rule\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_disable_rule\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_library_reorder_rules\",\n   \"minerva_scansort_dryrun_one\"]\n  ```\n\n## Files\n\n- `scansort/manifest.json` (add `skills` array + skill object). No code changes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Skill discoverable in focused-chat skill list when scansort plugin is loaded\n- A local 7B model (gemma4:26b) can follow the skill end-to-end to author a \"citizen tax\" rule against a real library and dry-run it on a sample PDF\n- `system_prompt` content ≤ 5 KB\n- Worked examples copy-paste cleanly into JSON (no escaping issues)\n\n## Depends on\n\nW1-W5 (schema, engine, reorder tool, dryrun tool, migration) must be in place so the skill's MCP calls actually work end-to-end against the new schema.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:02","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:02","priority":2,"tags":["content","plugin","scansort","skill"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cf0e9f73859e42ee445558285c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W10: Delete vault-sidecar File-menu entries + gut rules_editor_dialog.gd","description":"## What\n\nCleanup pass. Two parts:\n\n### Part A — Remove File menu entries\n\nFrom `ui/ScansortPanel.gd`:\n- Delete menu items 4 (\"Vault Rules Editor…\"), 8 (\"Library Rules Editor…\"), 9 (\"Create Vault-Specific Rules\"), 10 (\"Use Library Rules (remove sibling)\") — see audit at `ScansortPanel.gd:3038-3042`\n- Delete handlers: `_on_rules_editor_pressed`, `_on_library_rules_editor_pressed`, `_on_create_vault_rules_pressed`, `_on_use_library_rules_pressed` (~50 LoC, audit lines 2748-2842)\n\n### Part B — Delete rules_editor_dialog.gd\n\nThe 624-LoC form-based editor is fully replaced by:\n- Rules pane (W6) — viewing\n- Row menu (W7) — per-rule actions\n- Paste-JSON dialog (W8) — power-user authoring\n\nDelete `ui/rules_editor_dialog.gd` entirely. Any remaining references from elsewhere in the codebase must be removed too — grep for `rules_editor_dialog` and `RulesEditorDialog` before deleting.\n\n## Files\n\n- `ui/ScansortPanel.gd` (menu items + handlers removed)\n- `ui/rules_editor_dialog.gd` (deleted)\n- Anything else that references either (audit grep first)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- File menu has no \"Vault Rules Editor\" / \"Library Rules Editor\" / \"Create Vault-Specific Rules\" / \"Use Library Rules\" entries\n- `rules_editor_dialog.gd` does not exist\n- `grep rules_editor_dialog` returns zero hits across plugin + Minerva\n- Panel smoke test passes\n- Manual: launch panel, open File menu, confirm gone\n\n## Depends on\n\n- W6 (rules pane provides viewing)\n- W7 (row menu provides per-rule actions)\n- W8 (paste dialog provides authoring)\n\nThis is a \"land last\" item — don't merge until the replacements are in.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:08","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:08","priority":2,"tags":["cleanup","gdscript","plugin","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cf1e337f96aa3724018b81c197","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W11: Deprecate path-driven rule MCP tools (description + warning, removal next major)","description":"## What\n\nFor each of the following MCP tools in `src/main.rs`:\n- `minerva_scansort_list_rules`\n- `minerva_scansort_insert_rule`\n- `minerva_scansort_update_rule`\n- `minerva_scansort_delete_rule`\n- `minerva_scansort_get_rule`\n- `minerva_scansort_import_rules_from_json`\n\n1. Prepend `[DEPRECATED — use minerva_scansort_library_* instead]` to the tool description.\n2. Log a `[DEPRECATED]` warning when called (one line, includes tool name and a pointer to the replacement).\n3. Functional behavior unchanged.\n\nPhysical removal scheduled for next major version. Note the deprecation in `pickup.md` and in the path-free DCR comment chain.\n\n## Files\n\n- `src/main.rs` (tool description strings + handler warnings)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All 6 tool descriptions start with `[DEPRECATED — use minerva_scansort_library_* instead]`\n- Calling any of them logs the warning (verifiable via test harness or stderr capture)\n- Tools still function (no behavior change)\n- Rust tests green\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing — orthogonal to schema work.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:12","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:12","priority":3,"tags":["deprecation","mcp","plugin","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e33cf2787777fa4df0fd51c0c2897","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W12: edit_details_dialog — switch category dropdown source to library_list_rules","description":"## What\n\n`ui/edit_details_dialog.gd:114-160` populates a category dropdown from rules. Switch the MCP call from path-driven `minerva_scansort_list_rules` to `minerva_scansort_library_list_rules` (no path arg needed).\n\n## Files\n\n- `ui/edit_details_dialog.gd` (one MCP call site)\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Category dropdown populates correctly when user double-clicks a vault doc\n- Calls `library_list_rules` (no `rules_path` arg) instead of `list_rules`\n- Manual smoke: double-click a doc, confirm dropdown shows expected rule labels\n- Panel smoke test passes\n\n## Depends on\n\nNothing. Library tool already exists.\n","created_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:14","updated_at":"2026-05-17T02:41:14","priority":3,"tags":["cleanup","gdscript","plugin","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e33bfa10c753dbc467c38468268cd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e389ffd437710b27af85dc6b3e0b7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1: trace infrastructure — trace.rs + minerva_scansort_trace_append + settings rename + audit.rs removal","description":"## Scope\n\nStand up the JSONL trace layer; remove the shipped audit.rs CSV layer per the persona strip in the parent DCR.\n\n## Files\n\n- NEW: `scansort/src/trace.rs` (JSONL writer, append-only, ISO-8601 ts, ulid-derived run_id helper, fsync per-event for crash safety)\n- MOD: `scansort/src/main.rs` — add `handle_trace_append`; register `minerva_scansort_trace_append` MCP tool; remove `handle_audit_append` + `minerva_scansort_audit_append`\n- DEL: `scansort/src/audit.rs` (614 lines — NOT parallel-maintained per parent DCR §Migration)\n- MOD: `scansort/ui/ScansortPanel.gd` (or wherever settings persistence lives) — rename `audit_log_path` → `trace_log_path`, `audit_log_enabled` → `trace_log_enabled`; UI label \"Audit Log (CPA export)\" → \"Processing Log\"\n- MOD: `scansort/ui/settings_dialog.gd:252` (or current equivalent) — label rename\n- NEW: tests in `trace.rs` covering write/read round-trip, concurrent append safety, malformed-line tolerance on read-back\n\n## Migration (no-data-loss)\n\nOn first read of settings after upgrade:\n- If `audit_log_path` exists and points at a `.csv` file: copy value into `trace_log_path` with extension changed to `.jsonl`; preserve the `.csv` on disk untouched; surface a one-time panel info toast: *\"Audit Log renamed to Processing Log; new format is JSONL. Writing new events to `<basename>.jsonl` alongside. Your old `.csv` is unchanged.\"*\n- Leave legacy keys in place for one release cycle, then delete in a follow-up cleanup work_item.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `cargo test --release` green (≥ 291 + new trace tests)\n- New MCP tool `minerva_scansort_trace_append` callable; rejects malformed event shapes with structured error.\n- Old MCP tool `minerva_scansort_audit_append` GONE from the manifest (no deprecation wrapper — caller surface change is acceptable since no external user has been observed using it; parent DCR §Migration says \"removed in this phase\").\n- Settings field renames land in tandem with code; existing user settings auto-migrate per above.\n- Layer-1 panel smoke ≥ baseline (no regressions in scansort smoke harness).\n- Branch: `work-item/W1-trace-infrastructure` off the trace DCR root commit.\n\n## Events emitted in this phase\n\nJust plumbing — no events yet. Phase 2 (W2) wires emitters into `process()`.\n\n## Hard rules\n\nSame as parent DCR / pickup.md hard rules (per-file adds, no `--no-verify`, no `vendor/` touches, pre-commit diff-stat enumerate-and-explain, branch per work_item).","created_at":"2026-05-18T01:07:49","updated_at":"2026-05-18T01:07:49","priority":2,"tags":["logging","phase-1","scansort","trace"],"parent":"minerva:019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e38a034e77d458ce80d3dda17e806","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W2: engine instrumentation — Tracer abstraction emits per-run + per-doc + per-rule events from process()","description":"## Scope\n\nAdd a `Tracer` abstraction inside `process()` that emits the event vocabulary defined in the parent DCR. Makes single-stage rules already auditable — \"why was this doc unprocessable?\" becomes answerable from the log alone.\n\n## Files\n\n- NEW or MOD: `scansort/src/trace.rs` — add `Tracer` struct: `new(run_id, log_path) → Tracer`; methods `event_run_started`, `event_doc_seen`, `event_rule_evaluated`, `event_template_resolved`, `event_placement`, `event_doc_unprocessable`, `event_error`, `event_run_completed`. Each method serializes to JSONL via the W1 writer.\n- MOD: `scansort/src/process.rs::run` — construct Tracer at top; emit `run_started` before walking source files; per-doc emit `doc_seen`; per-rule emit `rule_evaluated` (label, score, threshold, fired); on template substitution emit `template_resolved`; on placement emit `placement` (preserves shipped disposition vocabulary `placed|skipped-already-present|kept-both|replaced|superseded|error`); on no_rule_match emit `doc_unprocessable`; on classify_error emit `error`; emit `run_completed` at end.\n- MOD: `scansort/src/process.rs::dryrun_one` — same Tracer pattern, but tag `mode: \"dryrun\"` in `run_started` so consumers can filter.\n- MOD: `scansort/src/process.rs::ProcessResult` — return shape now includes `run_id` (ulid string) so callers can correlate the result back to the trace.\n- MOD: `scansort/src/main.rs::handle_process` — thread trace_log_path from settings into process(); return run_id in the response.\n- NEW: integration tests in `process.rs` — small synthetic source dir, capture trace events, assert event ordering: `run_started → doc_seen → rule_evaluated × N → (template_resolved + placement) | doc_unprocessable → run_completed`.\n\n## Event payloads (canonical)\n\nPer parent DCR §Reframe — use those shapes verbatim. Stage events (`stage_executed`, `doc_filtered`) are RESERVED here but NOT emitted (deferred to pipeline DCR per parent §Implementation phases ¶3).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `cargo test --release` green (≥ baseline + new trace integration tests)\n- `process()` against a small fixture writes a complete trace: one `run_started`, N `doc_seen` (N = source files), at least one `rule_evaluated` per (doc, enabled-rule) pair, exactly one of `placement | doc_unprocessable` per doc, one `run_completed`.\n- `dryrun_one` returns identical per-rule trace shape it already returns, AND emits the same events to disk tagged `mode: \"dryrun\"`.\n- Trace `run_completed` summary matches `ProcessResult` summary (sanity invariant).\n- Branch: `work-item/W2-engine-instrumentation` off W1's commit.\n\n## Hard rules\n\nInherits parent DCR / pickup hard rules. Do NOT change `process()` return-shape semantics beyond adding `run_id`. Do NOT touch dispositions vocabulary.\n\n## Blocked by\n\nW1 (trace.rs writer + MCP tool must land first).","created_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:03","updated_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:40","priority":2,"tags":["instrumentation","phase-2","scansort","trace"],"parent":"minerva:019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e38a06dd5784692e518e9c48b73f2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W3: panel binding — live status bar + rules-pane fired-count from trace stream","description":"## Scope\n\nBind the live trace stream into the existing ScansortPanel UI (U-series U4 chrome + W6 rules pane). Status bar shows current/last activity; rules pane fires-count populates from `rule_evaluated` event counts. Visibility is OPT-IN at the panel level — closing/collapsing the panel still allows process() to run silently for background callers.\n\n## Files\n\n- MOD: `scansort/ui/ScansortPanel.gd` (status-bar wiring):\n  - Add a poll timer or file-tail watcher (cheap line-watcher on the trace JSONL) that reads new events while the panel `is_visible_in_tree()`. Stops polling when panel hides — zero overhead in background-only mode.\n  - Status-bar update vocabulary:\n    - `run_started`: `\"Run started — N files, M rules\"`\n    - `doc_seen`: `\"Processing <filename> (k/N)…\"`\n    - `placement`: `\"Last: <filename> → <rule_label> → <resolved_path>\"`\n    - `doc_unprocessable`: `\"Last: <filename> → unprocessable (<reason>)\"`\n    - `run_completed`: `\"Run done — P placed, U unprocessable, E errors, <elapsed>s\"`\n- MOD: `scansort/ui/rules_pane.gd` (or the inline-section in panel after W6) — replace the `—` placeholder for fired-count with a running tally derived from `rule_evaluated` events for the most recent run (or session-aggregated; settle on session-aggregated for v1, document in a comment).\n- NEW: small helper module `scansort/ui/trace_tail.gd` — encapsulates the tail-from-offset / parse-jsonl / emit-as-typed-signal pattern so future panels can reuse.\n\n## Critical constraint: NO auto-open\n\nProcess() never opens the panel itself. Visibility stays a user concern OR an explicit MCP call (`minerva_open_plugin_panel` if/when added — out of scope here). This preserves the \"LLM can run silently in background\" workflow.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Manual: open Scansort panel, trigger Process All on a small fixture → status bar updates per-doc in real time; rules pane fired-count ticks up per rule that scores ≥ threshold.\n- Manual: close the panel before Process All → no panel state changes, trace file still written, scansort-smoke harness still passes.\n- Panel smoke harness ≥ baseline.\n- Branch: `work-item/W3-panel-binding` off W2.\n\n## Hard rules\n\nInherits parent DCR / pickup hard rules. No GDScript class_name conflicts (off-tree plugin constraint per memory feedback_off_tree_plugin_class_names).\n\n## Blocked by\n\nW2 (events must emit before panel can bind to them).","created_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:18","updated_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:44","priority":2,"tags":["panel","phase-2","scansort","trace","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e38a09ed173deba66afb02c4479a6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W4: LLM-facing MCP query tools — trace_query, trace_list_runs, trace_tail","description":"## Scope\n\nExpose the trace stream to LLM consumers via MCP. Mirrors the panel's subscription model for callers that don't have a Godot signal bus.\n\n## Three tools (all read-only)\n\n### `minerva_scansort_trace_query`\n\nInput:\n- `run_id` (optional): filter to one run; omit = across all runs\n- `since_idx` (optional): byte offset or event index to resume from; default 0\n- `event_types` (optional, list): filter to e.g. `[\"placement\", \"doc_unprocessable\"]`\n- `limit` (optional, default 200): max events to return\n- `log_path` (optional): override default trace log location (defaults to settings.trace_log_path)\n\nOutput: `{events: [...], next_idx, has_more}` where events preserve write order.\n\n### `minerva_scansort_trace_list_runs`\n\nInput:\n- `limit` (optional, default 20)\n\nOutput: `{runs: [{run_id, started_at, completed_at, mode, summary: {...}, source_dir, destinations}, ...]}` — newest first; from `run_started` + `run_completed` event pairs (uncompleted runs surface with `completed_at: null`).\n\n### `minerva_scansort_trace_tail`\n\nInput:\n- `run_id` (required)\n- `since_idx` (required)\n- `timeout_ms` (optional, default 5000, max 30000)\n\nOutput: blocks up to `timeout_ms` waiting for new events for this run; returns the same shape as `trace_query`. Lets an LLM monitor an in-flight run without busy-polling — single HTTP round-trip per batch.\n\n## Files\n\n- MOD: `scansort/src/main.rs` — add three tool registrations + handlers; reuse the trace.rs read-back primitives from W1.\n- NEW: `scansort/src/trace.rs` (or extension) — `read_events`, `list_runs`, `tail_events` functions; all stateless, take log path + filters.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `cargo test --release` green with new tool tests covering: filter by run_id, filter by event_types, pagination via since_idx + has_more, tail timeout returns empty events without error.\n- Manual: run `process()` against a fixture; subsequently call `trace_list_runs` → see the run; call `trace_query {run_id}` → see all events for that run; call `trace_tail` against a fresh run in another harness invocation while process() is in flight → see events stream in.\n- Branch: `work-item/W4-trace-mcp-tools` off W2 (does NOT depend on W3 — panel and MCP are parallel subscribers).\n\n## Hard rules\n\nInherits parent DCR / pickup hard rules.\n\n## Blocked by\n\nW2 (need events emitted to query).","created_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:31","updated_at":"2026-05-18T01:08:47","priority":2,"tags":["llm","mcp","phase-2","scansort","trace"],"parent":"minerva:019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e38d4635970f5b28c940c193350f3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W0 (path A): live panel status — in-process progress signal, no persistence, no LLM-facing tools","description":"## Scope: minimum-viable observability for the user-visible workflow\n\nMake ScansortPanel show what process() is doing while it runs. Nothing more. Defers the full DCR 019e33a2 scope (durable JSONL trace, audit.rs replacement, settings migration, LLM-facing query tools) — those remain filed under this DCR as W1–W4 (`019e389ffd43`, `019e38a034e7`, `019e38a06dd5`, `019e38a09ed1`) and can be picked up later as a coherent set.\n\n## What the user can see after this lands\n\n- Bottom status bar of the Scansort panel updates in real time during Process All:\n  - `\"Run started — N files, M rules\"` when process() begins\n  - `\"Processing <filename> (k/N)…\"` per source doc\n  - `\"Last: <filename> → <rule_label> → <resolved_path>\"` on placement\n  - `\"Last: <filename> → unprocessable (<reason>)\"` on no-rule-match\n  - `\"Run done — P placed, U unprocessable, E errors, <elapsed>s\"` at end\n- Rules-pane fired-count populates from in-session per-rule fire totals (replaces the `—` placeholder shipped in W6 of DCR 019e33bf).\n- LLM headless workflow is UNCHANGED — `process()` still returns `{summary, items}` synchronously; LLM doesn't need new MCP tools to run quietly.\n\n## What this does NOT do (explicitly out of scope — filed for later)\n\n- No JSONL trace file on disk (W1 of full DCR)\n- No audit.rs removal or settings rename (W1 of full DCR)\n- No `Tracer` abstraction with formal event vocabulary (W2 of full DCR)\n- No `trace_query`/`trace_list_runs`/`trace_tail` MCP tools (W4 of full DCR)\n- No `run_id` on ProcessResult (W2 of full DCR; not needed when there's no durable log to correlate against)\n- No mid-call interruption / cancellation (separate concern)\n\n## Implementation approach\n\nThe plugin runs as a subprocess; today its only channel back to Minerva is the JSON-RPC response on STDIO when a tool call completes. To deliver mid-process status, the plugin needs to emit *intermediate* messages during the in-flight `process()` call.\n\nTwo viable mechanisms — settle on one during implementation (decision NOT pre-locked; pick whichever fits MCP/CapabilityBroker semantics most cleanly):\n\n**Option 1: MCP progress notifications.** MCP's protocol has `notifications/progress` for long-running tool calls. Plugin sends progress notifications mid-`handle_process`; Minerva's MCP server dispatches them to listeners. Pro: standard protocol surface. Con: need to verify the broker plumbs them through to a place ScansortPanel can subscribe.\n\n**Option 2: Plugin-broker capability callback.** Plugin makes a `host.panel.notify` (or similar) capability request mid-process with the event payload; CapabilityBroker dispatches to an in-process Godot signal that ScansortPanel listens for. Pro: same machinery the plugin already uses for `host.providers.chat`; reuses existing patterns. Con: defining a new capability.\n\nIf neither is clean, fall back to a third option: plugin writes a tiny in-memory event ring buffer (or an `xdg_runtime`/`/tmp` jsonl file) and panel polls it on a timer while `is_visible_in_tree()`. This is the lighter cousin of W1's durable JSONL — explicitly named to be replaced by W1 later, not parallel-maintained.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `~/github/plugins/scansort/src/process.rs` — emit progress at the same hook points the future Tracer would fire (`run_started`, per-doc seen + placed/unprocessable, `run_completed`). Use a thin trait or closure so swapping in the W2 Tracer later is a drop-in.\n- `~/github/plugins/scansort/src/main.rs` — wire the chosen progress mechanism (whichever option above wins).\n- Possibly `~/github/Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/CapabilityBroker.gd` if option 2 wins (new capability).\n- `~/github/plugins/scansort/ui/ScansortPanel.gd` — subscribe to the progress source when visible; update bottom status bar.\n- `~/github/plugins/scansort/ui/rules_pane.gd` (or wherever W6's rules section lives now) — replace `—` fired-count with running tally.\n- Tests: small integration test in `process.rs` verifying progress events fire at the expected hook points (decoupled from the transport choice via the thin trait/closure).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `cargo test --release` green (≥ 291 + any new tests).\n- Manual: open Scansort panel, point at `~/temp/scansort-vision-sweep`, click Process All → bottom status bar updates per doc; rules pane fired-counts tick up; final summary line appears.\n- Manual: close the Scansort panel → trigger `process()` via the harness (`--hitl`) → no panel state changes, process completes normally, harness gets the same result it gets today.\n- Panel smoke harness ≥ baseline (macOS pre-existing reprocess symlink failure does NOT count as a regression).\n- Single branch: `work-item/W0-path-a-live-status` off main.\n- Single commit (small enough to bundle): `[path-a] scansort: live panel status — in-process progress signal`.\n\n## Hard rules\n\nPer-file `git add`. No `--no-verify`. No `vendor/` touches. Pre-commit diff-stat enumerate-and-explain.\n\n## Continuation context (post-compaction)\n\nToday's session (2026-05-17 HITL, mac laptop) shipped:\n- Plugin commit `82f515f` — vision pipeline fixes (broker-shape messages, minimal generic prompt, PNG resampler at 256×256 in `render.rs`, `classifier.rs`, `stage_walker.rs`, `rules.rs`).\n- Minerva commit `e9696765` — broker routes plugin images through `chi.InjectedNotes` so CoreProvider.Format actually delivers them; new `minerva_graphics_export_png` MCP tool.\n\nClassification correctness verified 6/6 against `~/temp/scansort-vision-sweep` (sailboat/dog/pizza photos) and `~/temp/scansort-vision-docs` (boat/tax/utility form mockups) — all routed to the correct rule via `--hitl`. So the classifier itself is done; this work_item is purely about surfacing what it does to the user, not changing what it does.\n\nLibrary rule set (per `minerva_scansort_library_list_rules` at session end): `tax, utility, sailboat, dog, pizza, boat` — keep this set for manual verification during implementation.\n\nUser rubric (saved as memory `feedback-code-rubric`): reliability → durability → cost (LoC t-shirt) → readability → DRY → well-factored, in priority order. Small (< 100 LoC) wins on cost; reliability outranks DRY when they conflict.\n\nUser feedback (saved as memory `feedback-no-fcib`): do not commit vendor submodule changes; treat `vendor/EIRTeam.FFmpeg` and `vendor/godot_cef` modifications as anti-FCIB hygiene and leave them alone.\n\nPickup pattern: this work_item is small enough to NOT need a `/goal` cycle. Pick it up directly, design the transport choice (option 1/2/3) first, implement, ship in a single PR.","created_at":"2026-05-18T02:05:03","updated_at":"2026-05-18T03:27:23","priority":2,"tags":["minimum-viable","observability","panel","path-a","scansort","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e33a2ab2e7581bf0bdcbf5ddf0aeb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Binary-only distribution — Minerva + plugins shipped as downloadable releases, no end-user builds","description":"## Goal state\n\nA new user can:\n1. Download a signed Minerva binary for their platform from a GitHub release (`.dmg` / `.msi` / `.AppImage` or tarball).\n2. Launch it and, from inside the app, install plugins (scansort, cad, presentation) from their respective releases — no `git clone`, no `cargo build`, no `scons`, no `zig build`.\n3. Trust that the installed plugin version is compatible with the running Minerva version.\n\n## Current state (gap analysis, 2026-05-24)\n\n**Minerva (`~/github/Minerva`):**\n- `.github/workflows/build.yml` exports Linux + Windows only — **no macOS export job** (preset exists in `export_presets.cfg`).\n- macOS preset has `codesign/enable=false`; no notarization → unsigned `.app` triggers Gatekeeper.\n- No Windows installer; bare unsigned `.exe` triggers SmartScreen.\n- No universal mac binary (no `lipo`).\n- Plugins are not bundled in Minerva releases. Release notes only mention `Minerva-Windows.zip`.\n\n**Plugins (`~/github/plugins`):**\n- Zero CI: no `.github/workflows/` at all.\n- **Scansort** (Rust): `Cargo.lock` not committed; binary on disk is unversioned and stale (May 17).\n- **CAD** (Go shim + Python worker): `scripts/build-runtime.sh` referenced by README but does not exist. Python runtime (PBS cpython + build123d + cadquery-ocp) is not packaged. Binary is per-machine (see memory `project_cad_plugin_binary.md`).\n- **Presentation**: pure GDScript; distribution channel undecided (ship inside Minerva as built-in vs. ship as plugin release).\n\n**Cross-cutting:**\n- Plugin manifests have no `requires_minerva_version` field; Minerva binary has no advertised version a plugin can match against.\n- No manifest schema validation in CI.\n- No in-app \"install plugin from release URL\" flow.\n- Plugin install contract undocumented (auto-scan dir vs. explicit manifest path).\n\n## Work-item plan (smallest-unlock-first)\n\n| W | Title | Why this slot |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | macOS export job in Minerva `build.yml` | Unblocks daily-driver platform; smallest CI change |\n| W2 | Plugins-repo CI scaffolding | Single shared workflow infra so W3/W4/W5 are trivial |\n| W3 | Scansort release workflow (Rust matrix) | Easiest plugin to ship; pure `cargo build` |\n| W4 | CAD Go-shim release workflow | Defers Python runtime (heaviest piece) to W8 |\n| W5 | Presentation plugin packaging | Decide built-in-vs-plugin and execute |\n| W6 | Plugin install contract spec + version field | Documents what W7 implements against |\n| W7 | In-app \"install plugin from release URL\" UI | Connects all the release artifacts to end users |\n| W8 | CAD Python runtime packaging | The biggest single technical lift |\n| W9 | Code signing & notarization (Minerva + plugins) | Required for \"trust\" but not for \"works\" |\n| W10 | Release bootstrap — bundle compatible plugin set | First-run UX polish; presupposes W3–W7 done |\n\nW1–W4 are independent and parallelizable. W5 is a small design+execute. W6 unblocks W7. W8 is heavy but isolated. W9–W10 land last.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Auto-update / update channels (separate future DCR).\n- Plugin marketplace / registry beyond GitHub releases.\n- Backend (TurnRock Core) deployment changes.\n- Repackaging existing Godot extensions (`godot_cef`, `godot_wry`, `EIRTeam.FFmpeg`) — they're already shipped inside Minerva.\n\n## Success criteria\n\n1. Fresh macOS / Linux / Windows machine — download → launch → see Minerva UI without running any build script.\n2. From the running Minerva, install all three plugins from their respective GitHub releases.\n3. Run each plugin's headline workflow (scansort: place a doc; cad: render a part; presentation: render a deck) using only downloaded artifacts.\n4. Plugin install fails clean with a readable error if Minerva version is incompatible.","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:01","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:03:31","priority":2,"tags":["build","ci","distribution","plugins","release"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c8693667cf4a659d5a6520561ce","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1: macOS export job in Minerva build.yml","description":"Add macOS export to `.github/workflows/build.yml` so a `.app` (or `.zip` of it) is produced per push, matching the existing Linux + Windows export jobs.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- Job `build-macos-godot` on `macos-latest` runner that:\n  - Installs Godot 4.6.2 (use the existing pinned env var).\n  - Builds C++ extensions via `scripts/build-extensions.sh macos`.\n  - Runs `godot --headless --export-release \"macOS\" build/macos/Minerva.app`.\n  - Uploads `Minerva-macOS.zip` to release artifacts.\n- Universal binary: `lipo` two single-arch exports for control.\n\n**Out of scope:** signing/notarization (W9), installer creation (W9).\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W1.1** CI: `gh run watch` on push → exit 0\n- **FT-W1.2** Artifact: `gh run view --json artifacts` includes `Minerva-macOS.zip`\n- **FT-W1.3** Launch: download to `/tmp/w1-test/`, extract, `open Minerva.app`, wait for `scene_loaded` log marker within 30s, process alive at 60s, kill cleanly\n- **FT-W1.4** Path isolation: launch must succeed with source tree absent from `$PATH` and `cwd=/tmp` (proves no source-tree dependency)\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: matrix vs new-job structure; reuse of Linux/Windows patterns\n- Quality: Godot version + runner pinning durability; behavior if Godot 4.6.3 ships","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:23","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:02:30","priority":2,"tags":["build","ci","macos"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c86c4247d1c8d4e71d2f9e93fd8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W2: Plugins-repo CI scaffolding — shared workflow infrastructure","description":"`~/github/plugins` has zero CI today. Set up the shared infrastructure that W3, W4, W5, and future plugins consume. **This is the DRY foundation — get the shape right or downstream W's become rewrites.**\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- `~/github/plugins/.github/workflows/` directory created.\n- Reusable workflow `release-plugin.yml` parameterized on:\n  - `plugin_id` (folder name)\n  - `build_command` (e.g. `cargo build --release`, `go build ./...`)\n  - `artifact_glob` (paths to package)\n  - `runtime_matrix` (`[ubuntu-latest, macos-latest, windows-latest]`)\n  - **`signing_profile`** (empty default = no signing; populated = invoke signing step). Added day 1 so W9 collapses to \"buy certs and populate the parameter.\"\n- Per-plugin caller workflows in subsequent W-items just supply parameters.\n- Release-naming convention: `<plugin_id>-v<semver>-<os>-<arch>.tar.gz` (.zip on windows). Tag format: `<plugin_id>-v<semver>` — see W6 for the full version scheme.\n- **Manifest validation step: JSON-schema check against `plugins/schemas/manifest.schema.json` — this schema is the single source of truth, also consumed by W7 (pre-install validation) and Minerva runtime (load gate, via W6).**\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W2.1** Create `plugins/_test/` (manifest + no-op build), push tag `_test-v0.0.1`, `gh run watch` exit 0\n- **FT-W2.2** Artifact named `_test-v0.0.1-ubuntu-latest.tar.gz` exists (single-platform for speed)\n- **FT-W2.3** Validator: run against `manifest_valid.json` fixture → exit 0; against `manifest_invalid.json` → exit nonzero with error citing the failing field\n- **FT-W2.4** Cleanup: delete `_test/` and `_test-v0.0.1` tag after green\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: schema is single source of truth; reusable workflow truly parameterized; no copy-paste between caller workflows expected later\n- Quality: schema versioning strategy; `signing_profile` integration point clean for W9","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:36","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:02:42","priority":2,"tags":["ci","plugins","scaffolding"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c86d1f07ba69f8f4ef69b772dfb","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W3: Scansort release workflow (Rust matrix)","description":"Wire scansort into W2's shared release workflow. Easiest plugin to ship — pure `cargo build --release`.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- Commit `Cargo.lock` to the plugins repo (currently uncommitted → non-reproducible builds).\n- `plugins/scansort/.github/workflows/release.yml` calling W2's reusable workflow with `cargo build --release`, matrix `[ubuntu, macos arm64, macos x86_64, windows]`.\n- Tag-push trigger (`scansort-v*` per W6's tag scheme) produces a GitHub release with platform-specific tarballs containing `manifest.json` + `scansort-plugin` binary.\n- Manifest gains `requires_minerva_version` field per W6's spec (W6 lands after W3, so initial release uses `\">=0.5.0\"` as a safe default).\n- README updated with \"download from releases\" instructions.\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W3.1** Push tag `scansort-v0.1.0-rc1`; CI green across [ubuntu, macos-arm, macos-x86, windows]\n- **FT-W3.2** Four archives uploaded with correct naming pattern\n- **FT-W3.3** Download host-platform tarball; install via direct file copy to `~/.minerva/plugins/scansort/` (W6's contract hasn't landed yet — use legacy local-manifest path)\n- **FT-W3.4** End-to-end: launch Minerva headless, MCP `minerva_plugin_start id=scansort`, MCP `minerva_plugin_open_panel`, set source = `src/test/fixtures/scansort-staging/`, set destinations from `rules.json`, MCP process_all, assert placement diff vs `expected_placement.json` = 0\n- **FT-W3.5** Restart Minerva → MCP `minerva_plugin_list` confirms scansort persists\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: caller workflow uses W2's reusable correctly; no manual repackaging\n- Quality: cargo toolchain pinning; `Cargo.lock` reproducibility verified across platforms","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:39","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:02:50","priority":2,"tags":["ci","release","rust","scansort"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c86e20371c68d8d6c67fd5e41a9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W4: CAD Go-shim release workflow (internal-only until W8 lands)","description":"Wire the Go shim (`cad-plugin` binary) into W2's release workflow. **Defer** the Python worker runtime (build123d + cadquery-ocp + PBS cpython) to W8 — that's the heavy lift; this W builds the CI infrastructure so it exists when W8 is ready.\n\n**Durability decision (rubric-driven):** No public release tag is produced from this W alone. Stub-that-fails artifacts ossify; instead this W builds + verifies the workflow with `workflow_dispatch` runs only. First user-facing release tag (`cad-v*`) waits for W8 completion, at which point the same workflow ships both Go binary + Python runtime together.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- `plugins/cad/.github/workflows/release.yml` calling W2's reusable workflow with `go build -o cad-plugin ./...`, matrix `[ubuntu, macos arm64, macos x86_64, windows]`.\n- Triggered by `workflow_dispatch` only (no tag trigger yet — added in W8).\n- Manifest gains `requires_minerva_version` field per W6's spec.\n- Tarball includes Go binary + manifest only. Python runtime slot reserved (empty subdir + README).\n\n**Done when:** Manual `workflow_dispatch` produces 4 archives that pass W2's manifest validation. Cad plugin is **not** advertised to users until W8 lands.\n\n**STOP:** Green CI on `workflow_dispatch` + 4 platform archives produced as build artifacts (not release assets) + manifest validation passes.","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:44","updated_at":"2026-05-25T00:36:14","priority":3,"tags":["cad","ci","go","release"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c86feb37610920c352480e73338","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W5: Presentation plugin release workflow (option B — ship as plugin)","description":"Presentation is pure GDScript with no native binary. **Decided: Option B — ship as a plugin release.** Rationale recorded in this work item's comments (rubric grade: B wins on durability + DRY + well-factored; option A's hidden cost was the precedent of \"important plugins become built-ins\" eroding the plugin abstraction).\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- `plugins/presentation/.github/workflows/release.yml` calling W2's reusable workflow with a no-op `build_command` (just stage `.gd`/`.tscn`/`manifest.json`).\n- Single platform-agnostic tarball (no per-OS matrix needed — pure GDScript).\n- Manifest gains `requires_minerva_version` field per W6's spec.\n- Tag-push trigger (`presentation-v*` per W6's tag scheme) produces a GitHub release with one tarball.\n- README updated with \"download from releases\" instructions.\n\n**Done when:** `git tag presentation-v0.1.0 && git push --tags` produces a downloadable tarball that Minerva can install via PluginManager.\n\n**STOP:** Green CI on tag push + tarball published + tarball installs successfully into Minerva + presentation panel opens after install.\n\n**Useful side effect:** This W is the canary that proves W2/W6/W7 actually work for a pure-GDScript plugin. If pure-GDScript install breaks, it's caught here before cad's heavier path runs into the same gap.","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:26:51","updated_at":"2026-05-25T00:43:07","priority":3,"tags":["design","presentation","release"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c87223e7951b087840d2e8ef8f7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W6: Plugin install contract — manifest schema, version scheme, runtime hooks","description":"Today's plugin install path is undocumented — unclear whether Minerva auto-scans a directory or expects an explicit manifest path. Lock this down so W2's validator, W7's UI, and Minerva's runtime all consume the same contract.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n\n1. **Doc** `Docs/design/Plugin-install-contract.md` specifying:\n   - **Install locations**: canonical user-scope (`~/.minerva/plugins/<plugin_id>/`) and project-scope (`<project>/.minerva/plugins/<plugin_id>/`).\n   - **Discovery**: startup auto-scan of both, with user-scope overriding project-scope.\n   - **Version scheme** (single source of truth — referenced by W3, W4, W5, W7, W10):\n     - Minerva tags: `v<semver>` (e.g. `v0.5.0`).\n     - Plugin tags: `<plugin_id>-v<semver>` (e.g. `scansort-v0.1.0`).\n     - `requires_minerva_version` is a semver range against Minerva's tag.\n     - Asset filename: `<plugin_id>-v<semver>-<os>-<arch>.tar.gz` (.zip on windows).\n   - **First-launch hooks** (durability decision — owned by Minerva PluginManager, not per-plugin shim): a plugin manifest may declare an `on_first_launch` step. Minerva runs it once and records completion in plugin local state. W8 implements the first concrete use.\n\n2. **JSON schema** at `plugins/schemas/manifest.schema.json` (single artifact consumed by: W2's validation step, W7's pre-install check, Minerva's runtime load gate). Includes `requires_minerva_version`, optional `on_first_launch`, all existing fields.\n\n3. **Minerva implementation** in `PluginManager.gd`:\n   - Read `requires_minerva_version`, gate load.\n   - Run `on_first_launch` hook on install if declared; record completion.\n   - Existing manifests without the new field log a warning and load (one release of grace).\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W6.1** Install scansort-v0.1.0-rc1 (matching version) → MCP `minerva_plugin_list` shows `scansort` with `status: loaded`\n- **FT-W6.2** Patch scansort manifest to `requires_minerva_version: \">=99.0\"`, repackage, install → MCP list does NOT include it → install dir empty → log contains rejection string (string captured to HITL packet for readability check)\n- **FT-W6.3** Install `_test` plugin with `on_first_launch: [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"touch /tmp/w6_hook_marker\"]` → marker exists → delete marker → restart Minerva → marker NOT recreated (proves single-fire semantics)\n- **FT-W6.4** Regression: re-run FT-W3.3 through FT-W3.5 with scansort manifest now carrying `requires_minerva_version: \">=0.5.0\"` → still green\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: schema is one file consumed by W2 validator AND W7 pre-install check AND PluginManager load gate (no duplication)\n- Quality: backwards-compat for manifests missing `requires_minerva_version`; grace-period documented in code AND in the doc\n\n**Blocks:** W7 (consumes contract), W8 (consumes first-launch hook).","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:27:00","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:03:03","priority":2,"tags":["compatibility","manifest","plugins","spec"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c8743dc7a5592a275f5ae1a8745","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W7: In-app \"install plugin from release URL\" UI flow","description":"Today users install plugins by browsing to a local manifest file. Add a release-aware *source* to the existing install pipeline so they can install from a GitHub release URL.\n\n**DRY constraint:** Extends `PluginManager`'s existing install pipeline — URL handler is an alternate **source**, not a parallel installer. The download → validate → extract → register pipeline is reused; only the \"find the manifest\" step is new. Do not fork the local-file path.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- New MCP tool `minerva_plugin_install_from_url` (required for FT-W7.1–4 to be scriptable).\n- PluginManager UI gets an \"Install from URL\" button (same code path as the MCP tool).\n- Accepts either:\n  - Direct asset URL (e.g. `https://github.com/.../scansort-v0.1.0-macos-arm64.tar.gz`)\n  - GitHub release URL — Minerva queries the API and picks the matching `<os>-<arch>` asset using W6's filename convention.\n- Downloads to `/tmp/minerva-install-temp/`, validates manifest against schema from W6, checks `requires_minerva_version`, extracts into `~/.minerva/plugins/<plugin_id>/`, registers via the install contract, runs `on_first_launch` hook if declared.\n- Progress + readable errors (network fail, version mismatch, manifest invalid, checksum mismatch).\n- Optional: curated \"known plugins\" list in `user://known_plugins.json` seeded with scansort/cad/presentation.\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W7.1** MCP `minerva_plugin_install_from_url url=<scansort-v0.1.0-rc1-macos-arm64.tar.gz>` → plugin installs → MCP list confirms\n- **FT-W7.2** MCP with release-page URL (not asset URL) → tool resolves correct platform asset → installs\n- **FT-W7.3** MCP with `https://localhost:1/nope` → error returned → `/tmp/minerva-install-temp/` empty → no partial install in `~/.minerva/plugins/`\n- **FT-W7.4** MCP with URL of tarball patched to `requires_minerva_version: \">=99.0\"` → error returned → no install (W6 gate honored through URL path)\n- **FT-W7.5** UI evidence: agent triggers UI button via test driver if available; otherwise screenshot button + dialog and capture as evidence\n- **FT-W7.6** Equivalence: re-run FT-W3.4 (scansort placement) on a URL-installed scansort → diff = 0. **This is the durability proof — URL install reaches same state as local install.**\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: URL flow reuses PluginManager install pipeline; UI button and MCP tool share the same backing function\n- Quality: GitHub API rate-limit behavior (anonymous = 60/hr); temp dir cleanup on ALL failure modes including SIGKILL; redirect handling\n\n**Depends on:** W6 (contract + version field + first-launch hook). Plays well with W3 output (the actual scansort release the tests install).","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:27:09","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:03:17","priority":2,"tags":["install","plugins","ui","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c87699072e186a50fa863fd10a8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W8: CAD Python runtime packaging — build-runtime.sh + tar.zst","description":"The CAD plugin's Python worker (build123d + cadquery-ocp) currently has no packaged form. README references `scripts/build-runtime.sh` which doesn't exist. This is the largest technical lift in the DCR.\n\n**Architecture (decided in W6):** Runtime extraction is driven by Minerva's generic `on_first_launch` hook in PluginManager — *not* by cad-specific code in the Go shim. This W ships the cad plugin's contribution (manifest declaration + the `.tar.zst` payload + the build script that produces it). Next plugin needing a runtime reuses the same hook.\n\n**Deliverables (per `Docs/design/Go-python-bridge-design.md`):**\n- Implement `plugins/cad/scripts/build-runtime.sh`:\n  - Downloads python-build-standalone (PBS) cpython for target `<os>-<arch>`.\n  - Creates venv, pip installs `build123d` + `cadquery-ocp` + transitive deps.\n  - Compresses to `runtime-build/<os>-<arch>.tar.zst` (zstd for fast extract on first launch).\n- Cad manifest gains `on_first_launch` entry pointing at `runtime/python.tar.zst` → extract to `runtime/python/`.\n- Integrate into W4's release workflow as a matrix job — Python runtime artifacts ship alongside Go binary in the release tarball.\n- Flip W4's `workflow_dispatch`-only trigger to also fire on `cad-v*` tag push. **First public cad release tag happens here.**\n- Go shim: at startup, expects `runtime/python/` to exist (created by PluginManager's hook before shim launches).\n\n**Done when:** A user downloads the CAD plugin release, installs via W7's flow, and successfully renders a part using build123d — with no system Python or pip involvement.\n\n**STOP:** Green CI on `cad-v*` tag push + fresh install on at least one platform extracts runtime + renders a known test part end-to-end without system Python.\n\n**Blocked by:** W4 (release workflow scaffold), W6 (`on_first_launch` hook mechanism).","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:27:18","updated_at":"2026-05-25T00:36:57","priority":3,"tags":["cad","packaging","python","runtime"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c8791577f9289069d9411b590ee","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W9: Code signing & notarization — Minerva + plugins","description":"Unsigned binaries trigger Gatekeeper (mac) and SmartScreen (windows). Required for \"trust,\" not for \"works.\"\n\n**Architecture (set up in W2):** Plugin signing is wired through W2's `signing_profile` parameter on the reusable workflow — this W populates it, doesn't re-edit each release pipeline. Minerva CI signing is added directly to `build.yml` jobs.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- **macOS:** Apple Developer creds in CI secrets. `codesign --options runtime --sign \"$DEV_ID\"` on `.app`; `xcrun notarytool submit ... --wait`; `xcrun stapler staple`. Applies to Minerva (via `build.yml`) and to plugins shipping Mach-O binaries (via W2's signing_profile).\n- **Windows:** Code-signing cert (EV preferred for SmartScreen reputation). `signtool sign /tr <ts-url> /td sha256 /fd sha256 /a` on `.exe` + any plugin `.exe`s. Build NSIS or WiX installer for Minerva, sign that too.\n- **Plugin artifact integrity:** publish SHA-256 checksums (and optionally cosign / sigstore) alongside each release tarball; W7's installer verifies on download.\n- **Linux:** AppImage with embedded `gpg`-signed update info (optional) or just GPG-detached signature on the tarball.\n\n**Done when:** Fresh mac/windows install → no security prompts. Plugin install in W7 verifies checksum.\n\n**STOP:** Signed Minerva + signed scansort plugin both install + launch on fresh mac and fresh windows with no Gatekeeper/SmartScreen dialog.\n\n**Note:** Requires real-money cert purchases + Apple Dev Program membership. Time and money cost is what bumps this to W9, not technical complexity.","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:27:28","updated_at":"2026-05-25T00:37:08","priority":3,"tags":["notarization","release","signing","trust"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5c87af4a722ca64341caa31ae811","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W10: Release bootstrap — bundle compatible plugin set into Minerva release","description":"Polish layer: even with W7 working, a fresh user shouldn't have to know plugin URLs by heart.\n\n**Durability decision (rubric-driven):** Pin manifest **self-refreshes from a canonical URL on first launch** (and on user request). Static bundled pins go stale the moment a plugin ships a security fix; self-refresh keeps the recommendation current without requiring a Minerva re-release. Fallback to bundled pins on network failure.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- Minerva release workflow gains a \"plugin pin manifest\" step that records which plugin versions are known-compatible with this Minerva version. Lives in `Docs/release/plugin-pins.json` and is bundled inside the Minerva binary as the fallback.\n- Canonical pin URL (e.g. `https://github.com/turnrocklabs/Minerva/releases/latest/download/plugin-pins.json`) checked at first-launch and on user-triggered refresh; success replaces in-memory pins, failure logs and uses bundled fallback.\n- First-run UX: dialog \"Install bundled plugins? [scansort 0.1.0 ✓] [cad 0.2.0 ✓] [presentation 0.1.0 ✓]\" with each box pre-checked. Uses W7's installer under the hood.\n- Release notes generator (CI step) auto-includes the plugin-pin list so the GitHub release page shows compatible plugin versions.\n\n**Done when:** Fresh install → first-launch dialog → user clicks \"Install all\" → plugins arrive without ever leaving the Minerva window. Pin refresh succeeds online, falls back to bundled offline.\n\n**STOP:** Fresh-install dialog appears + \"Install all\" succeeds end-to-end + offline launch still works using bundled pins.\n\n**Depends on:** W3, W4, W5, W7 (the install path and the released artifacts must all exist).","created_at":"2026-05-25T00:27:36","updated_at":"2026-05-25T00:37:19","priority":4,"tags":["bootstrap","release","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5cba0a58704db9dc2bed637a9f68","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W0: Wire godot-cef artifacts into build-godot (extension dependency gap)","description":"Discovered during W1 scoping: `godot-cef.yml` builds CEF binaries for linux/macos/windows and uploads them as artifacts, but `build.yml`'s `build-godot` job never downloads them. Result: every Minerva binary released today (Linux, Windows) ships without CEF — plugins shipping CEF panels don't work on downloaded builds. FCIB policy keeps CEF binaries out of git, so we must pull from CI artifacts at export time.\n\n**Bundled with W1** because both edits target `build-godot` and a single push covers both. Numbered W0 to reflect \"preceded W1 in dependency order.\"\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- New step in `build-godot` (after `terminal-*` download, before Export Project): use `gh run download` to fetch the latest successful `godot-cef.yml` artifacts (`godot-cef-linux-x86_64`, `godot-cef-macos-universal`, `godot-cef-windows-x86_64`) and place each in `src/addons/godot_cef/bin/<triple>/`.\n- Fallback path: if no successful CEF run exists, the step fails fast with a readable message (\"trigger godot-cef.yml manually first\").\n- Verify post-W1 godot_wry + ghostty-vt + ffmpeg deployment is intact (sanity check, no code change expected).\n\n**Out of scope:** refactoring godot-cef.yml to `workflow_call` (deferred — more invasive, can be done if we need tighter branch-specific freshness later); adding deps for any plugin-shipped extensions (W2/W6 territory).\n\n**Auto gate (Phase 1 chain — no per-W HITL):**\n- **FT-W0.1** CI: `build-godot` step \"Download godot-cef artifacts\" exits 0; `src/addons/godot_cef/bin/<triple>/` populated on the runner for all three triples\n- **FT-W0.2** Combined with FT-W1.3: downloaded Minerva-macOS.zip launches AND opens a known CEF panel (or at minimum: `CEF init` log line appears, proving CEF binaries loaded)\n- **FT-W0.3** Regression: downloaded Minerva-Linux.tar.gz also opens a CEF panel (proves we fixed the existing Linux gap, not just mac)\n\n**Reviewers (cold-Opus, parallel, after gate):**\n- DRY: download pattern reusable if future side-built extensions appear; not pre-abstracted while there's only one\n- Quality: branch-specificity of the run lookup (latest-any-branch is OK for now but worth flagging); fallback behavior when godot-cef.yml has never run successfully","created_at":"2026-05-25T01:22:36","updated_at":"2026-05-25T01:22:36","priority":2,"tags":["build","cef","ci","extensions","fcib"],"parent":"minerva:019e5c863d737a5ba5f6586de3644be4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e5ce2160f76d9868f7a000c41614b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"build.yml: latent CI bugs on all three platforms (surfaced by W0+W1 workflow_dispatch)","description":"## Findings (in discovery order)\n\n### 1. Setup Zig PATH (FIXED — commit 4ba3d052)\n`zig version` validation at end of Setup Zig step always failed with `zig: command not found` (exit 127). Root cause: `echo ... >> $GITHUB_PATH` only takes effect on *subsequent* steps, not in-step. Fix: also `export PATH=\"$ZIG_DIR:$PATH\"` in-step.\n\nAffects: `build-linux`, `build-macos` (same pattern in `.github/workflows/minerva-vt.yml` — fix pending).\n\n### 2. src/bin not pre-created (FIXED — commit 52f31d34)\n`cp ... src/bin/` failed with `cp: cannot create regular file 'src/bin/': Not a directory` because the directory isn't tracked in git (FCIB policy) and fresh CI checkouts lack it. Fix: `mkdir -p src/bin` before the cp in both linux and macOS jobs.\n\n### 3. SConstruct macOS framework copy at parse time (OPEN)\n`src/SConstruct` lines 60-66:\n```python\nif env[\"platform\"] == \"macos\":\n    minerva_vt_src = \"gdextension/terminal/ghostty-shim/zig-out/lib/libminerva-vt.dylib\"\n    framework_dir = \"bin/libterminal.{}.{}.framework\".format(env[\"platform\"], env[\"target\"])\n    minerva_vt_dst = os.path.join(framework_dir, \"libminerva-vt.dylib\")\n    if os.path.exists(minerva_vt_src) and os.path.isdir(framework_dir):  # ← parse-time\n        shutil.copy2(minerva_vt_src, minerva_vt_dst)\n```\nThe `os.path.isdir(framework_dir)` check runs at SConstruct *parse time*, before scons builds the framework. On fresh CI checkouts the framework doesn't exist → copy skipped → subsequent `codesign --force --sign - <dst>` (line 71) fails with \"No such file or directory\".\n\nWorks locally only because devs have leftover framework dirs from prior builds.\n\nFix: convert to `env.AddPostAction(library, ...)` so the copy runs after the framework is built. Also: prefer `bin/libminerva-vt.dylib` (the universal lipo'd binary CI puts there) over the per-arch zig-out source.\n\n### 4. Windows missing ghostty-vt build (OPEN)\n`build-windows` job has no `zig build` step — only Linux and macOS jobs build the ghostty-vt shim. Result: `LINK : fatal error LNK1181: cannot open input file 'minerva-vt.lib'` when scons tries to link the Windows terminal extension.\n\nOpen question: should the shim be cross-compiled to Windows (true gap — add Setup Zig + Build ghostty-vt shim + Create minerva-vt.lib steps), or should SConstruct conditionally exclude minerva-vt linking on Windows (the terminal extension would lose ghostty features on Windows)? `scripts/build-extensions.sh` only handles linux/macos, suggesting the latter was the original intent — but the SConstruct unconditionally links minerva-vt.lib on Windows, suggesting the former.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nBuild.yml's `push` trigger is `paths: src/** && branches: development`. The swarm branch goes through `workflow_dispatch` for testing — which is what exposed these. They've likely been broken for weeks/months without anyone noticing because no released binary has gone through this code path.\n\nSurfaced by W0+W1 (DCR 019e5c86) — the first time we tried to actually verify build.yml produces working artifacts.","created_at":"2026-05-25T02:06:21","updated_at":"2026-05-28T06:21:45","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["build","ci","linux","macos","windows"],"environment":"GitHub Actions CI on user/imran/experiments/swarm; workflow_dispatch path"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e62ad894d78399a604b85c76906fb","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugins repo FCIB compliance + per-platform CI","description":"The plugins repo lacks any CI to build per-platform binaries. Today the binaries are produced by hand (developer runs `cargo build --release` / `go build` locally) and live as untracked working-tree files. They are NOT committed — `git ls-tree HEAD` is empty for any `*-plugin` path; both root `.gitignore` and `scansort/.gitignore` exclude them. The repo is already FCIB-compliant. This DCR's real goal is \"stand up per-platform CI that produces installable release artifacts.\"\n\nActive remote: `imrans-lab/minerva-plugins` (org, public). `ipeerbhai/plugins` (personal, public) is the historical predecessor — its personal account has a GitHub Actions billing lock that rejects all jobs at scheduler level.\n\nScope:\n- Per-plugin CI matrix: linux-x86_64, linux-arm64, macos-universal (lipo), windows-x86_64\n  - scansort (Rust): cargo build + cargo test gates\n  - cad (Go): go build + go test gates\n  - presentation (Go): go build + go test gates\n- Release-pack convention: `<plugin-id>-<version>-<triple>.tar.gz` containing manifest.json + binary + sha256 sidecar\n- Release strategy: per-plugin git tags (`scansort-v0.3.1`, `cad-v0.2.0`, …); tag push triggers matrix → GitHub Release upload\n- Registry generator: `scripts/regen-registry.sh` walks each `<plugin>/manifest.json`, latest matching git tag, writes top-level `registry.json` (one canonical URL: `raw.githubusercontent.com/imrans-lab/minerva-plugins/main/registry.json`). CI verifies registry is up-to-date (drift check fails the build).\n- Smoke harness: `scripts/smoke/mcp_smoke.py` launches binary, sends MCP `initialize` over stdio, asserts protocol response + `tools/list` returns ≥1 tool within 5s. Already exists (committed 0a34d83 on dcr/plugins-fcib-ci). Per-plugin matrix legs invoke it against freshly-built binaries.\n- Per-plugin README: build + release instructions\n\nW7 dropped from scope: there are no committed binaries to purge — the audit was incorrect on this point.\n\nOut of scope: the Minerva-side fetch/install pipeline; codesigning / notarization (separate DCR if needed for wide distribution).","created_at":"2026-05-26T05:06:40","updated_at":"2026-05-26T06:54:42","priority":2,"tags":["build","ci","fcib","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e62adb39d762ea39111340fb6e8ad","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Native Mac ARM64 (Apple Silicon) builds for Minerva + GDExtensions","description":"Existing CI uses `universal-apple-darwin` lipo for the GDExtension binaries, but it's not confirmed that the produced binaries actually contain an arm64 slice, and the Godot export side may not target arm64 at all. User confirms Mac ARM is not currently working end-to-end.\n\nScope:\n- T1 Discovery: download current macOS CI artifacts, `lipo -info` each .dylib, `file` the Minerva.app binary, identify exactly where arm64 is missing\n- T2 Godot export preset for macos.arm64 if needed (src/export_presets.cfg)\n- T3 Fix per-GDExtension (terminal, wry, cef) where arm64 slice is absent\n- T4 Migrate macOS CI runners to `macos-14` (arm64-native) where appropriate\n- T5 Smoke test produced artifact on real Apple Silicon hardware (HITL — user's laptop)\n\nOut of scope: Windows ARM, Linux ARM (covered separately if needed).","created_at":"2026-05-26T05:06:51","updated_at":"2026-05-26T05:06:51","priority":2,"tags":["arm64","ci","gdextension","macos"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e62ade5be75a1807166318bcc477d","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin marketplace — pre-built binary install from within Minerva","description":"Today PluginDB.install(manifest_path) requires the user to point at a manifest on local disk. Goal: users browse available plugins inside Minerva, click Install, and Minerva downloads the right binary for their platform + verifies integrity + registers the plugin. Side-load (file-path install) stays.\n\nIndependent of plugins-FCIB-CI DCR — marketplace consumes from GitHub Releases at runtime, regardless of how the tarballs were produced.\n\nDiscovery model (3-axis-rubric-graded, design decisions baked in):\n- Registry source: hosted JSON at plugins repo root (`registry.json`) fetched via raw.githubusercontent.com URL\n- Generation: produced by CI in the plugins repo (drift-checked)\n- Versioning: per-plugin git tags (`<id>-v<X.Y.Z>`); registry records latest tag\n- Multi-registry support: v1 = single default URL + settings override (forks/enterprise unblocked, multi-registry merge deferred to v2)\n\nTwo-level fetch: registry.json (lists plugins + latest version + manifest URL) → per-plugin manifest.json (carries per-platform binary URLs + sha256) → tarball.\n\nPhase A — fetch & install (headless, no UI):\n- W1 Manifest schema v2: `binaries: { \"<triple>\": { url, sha256, size } }`; `version` field; backwards-compat with v1\n- W2 `PluginDB.install_from_url(url)` — download, verify sha256, unpack to `user://plugins/<id>/`, register\n- W3 Side-load path preserved: `PluginDB.install(path)` unchanged\n- W4 Headless tests: success, bad sha, missing platform, network fail, partial-download resume\n- W5 Trust model: sha256 = integrity not trust. v1 = trust-on-first-use (record installed plugin's publisher); signing/allowlist deferred.\n\nPhase B — marketplace UI:\n- W6 Registry client: fetch raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/plugins/main/registry.json, parse, cache 5min, settings override for URL\n- W7 Browse-plugins UI in Plugin Manager (grid, search, descriptions, screenshots from manifest)\n- W8 Install button wired to install_from_url with progress UI\n- W9 Update detection + update flow (version comparison; \"Update available\" badge)\n- W10 Uninstall flow audit\n- W11 Telemetry decision (likely: none reported back)","created_at":"2026-05-26T05:07:04","updated_at":"2026-05-26T05:43:38","priority":2,"tags":["manifest","marketplace","plugins","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e634e4a96727e91ea4d01e97a0b22","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"go-cross-platform-plugins/syscall-kill-and-setpgid-are-unix-only","created_at":"2026-05-26T08:02:15","updated_at":"2026-05-26T08:02:15","tags":["ci","cross-platform","go","syscall","windows"],"value":"Go's `syscall.Kill`, `syscall.SIGKILL/SIGTERM`, and `syscall.SysProcAttr.Setpgid` are Unix-only — they do not exist on `GOOS=windows`. A package-level reference in any .go file builds fine on Linux/macOS but fails on Windows with \"undefined: syscall.Kill\" / \"unknown field Setpgid\". For cross-platform Go plugins, split the affected file into `foo_unix.go` (with `//go:build !windows`) and `foo_windows.go` (with `//go:build windows`) using Windows-equivalents (TerminateProcess via `golang.org/x/sys/windows`, or the higher-level `os.Process.Kill` which is portable). The cad plugin's `internal/bridge/worker.go` (DCR 019e62ad894d W2, 2026-05-26) hit this — process-group lifecycle (Setpgid + Kill(-pgid, ...)) for subprocess teardown was Unix-only and made Windows builds impossible.","component":"go-cross-platform-plugins","key":"syscall-kill-and-setpgid-are-unix-only"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e634eae3f7ced80ebb48efdb51158","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"cad plugin: Windows port (build-tag split for worker.go process-group syscalls)","description":"cad/internal/bridge/worker.go references at:\n- :150 `cmd.SysProcAttr = &syscall.SysProcAttr{Setpgid: true}`\n- :332 `_ = syscall.Kill(-pgid, syscall.SIGKILL)`\n- :468 `_ = syscall.Kill(-pgid, syscall.SIGTERM)`\n- :478 `_ = syscall.Kill(-pgid, syscall.SIGKILL)`\n\nThese are Unix-only — Windows build fails with \"undefined: syscall.Kill\" / \"unknown field Setpgid in struct literal of type syscall.SysProcAttr\".\n\nFix:\n1. Split process-group lifecycle into two files:\n   - `worker_unix.go` with `//go:build !windows` containing the current Setpgid + Kill(-pgid, ...) flow.\n   - `worker_windows.go` with `//go:build windows` using either `os.Process.Kill` (terminates single process) or `golang.org/x/sys/windows` for a job-object-based teardown.\n2. Remove the platform-specific syscall references from worker.go itself; have it delegate to a `terminateChildren(cmd *exec.Cmd)` helper that each platform file provides.\n3. Verify locally on Linux that behavior is unchanged.\n4. Re-enable windows-x86_64 in `.github/workflows/cad.yml` matrix; push and confirm green.\n5. Update the docket hint `019e634e4a96` (component=go-cross-platform-plugins) with the resolved pattern.\n\nSee docket hint `019e634e4a96` for context.\n\nOut of scope: behavior parity across platforms beyond \"subprocess gets terminated.\" Windows lacks process-group semantics; killing the bare process is acceptable.","created_at":"2026-05-26T08:02:41","updated_at":"2026-05-26T08:08:40","priority":3,"tags":["cad","cross-platform","go","windows"],"parent":"minerva:019e62ad894d","resolution":"Fixed in-cycle, not deferred. cad/internal/bridge/worker.go split into worker.go + process_group_unix.go (//go:build unix) + process_group_windows.go (//go:build windows). signalKind enum + setProcessGroup() + terminateProcessGroup() helpers. Local Linux build + tests pass (21s subprocess-lifecycle tests included); GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 cross-build clean. Re-enabled windows-x86_64 in cad.yml matrix. Commit ac6ab78 on dcr/plugins-fcib-ci; CI confirmation pending."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6358cf7a724598e67c36bd2168c5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Extract pack+upload composite action for plugin workflows","description":"Cold-Opus DRY review on `dcr/plugins-fcib-ci` HEAD recommended extracting the tarball pack + sha256 + upload-artifact tail of all 3 plugin workflows into a composite action.\n\nToday's state (DCR `019e62ad894d` W1-W3): scansort.yml, cad.yml, presentation.yml each contain ~16 lines for the pack step + ~7 lines for the upload artifact, structurally identical, varying only in plugin id + binary name + version source. Diff diameter is small (cad ≡ presentation modulo binary name; scansort reads version from Cargo.toml, cad/presentation from manifest.json).\n\nProposed shape:\n```\n.github/actions/pack-and-upload/action.yml\n  inputs:\n    plugin:     required  # scansort | cad | presentation\n    version:    required  # already resolved by caller\n    target:     required  # linux-x86_64, macos-universal, etc.\n    binary_dir: required  # target/release for scansort, build for cad+pres\n    ext:        default=\"\"\n  runs:\n    using: composite\n    steps:\n      - copy binary + manifest into PACKDIR\n      - SHA256SUMS (binary + manifest) via portable sha256sum/shasum\n      - tar.gz the PACKDIR contents\n      - upload-artifact\n```\n\n3 workflows call `uses: ./.github/actions/pack-and-upload` with their plugin-specific inputs.\n\nEffort: ~30min. Saves ~50 net lines. Single point of truth for the release-pack layout — critical when DCR-marketplace W2 (install_from_url) verifies SHA256SUMS at install time.\n\nDefer trigger: act on this once a 4th plugin is in flight, OR if any divergence between the 3 workflows' pack steps appears.\n\nNOT a bug fix; pure refactor for DRY hygiene.","created_at":"2026-05-26T08:13:45","updated_at":"2026-05-26T08:13:45","priority":3,"tags":["ci","dry","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019e62ad894d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e63a3bcf471a794590b20d1b77c4e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-plugin-platform/canonical-install-is-pluginmanager-not-plugindb","created_at":"2026-05-26T09:35:35","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:04:36","tags":["install","minerva-architecture","plugins"],"value":"For installing plugins programmatically in Minerva, the canonical entry point is `SingletonObject.plugin_manager.install_plugin(manifest_path)` — NOT a direct call to `PluginDB.install(manifest_path)`. PluginManager.install_plugin (at PluginManager.gd:150) wraps the DB call and adds the things that make the plugin actually USABLE: `_ensure_runtime(id)`, `_create_plugin_directories(def)`, `_auto_grant_declared_capabilities(def)`, `_seed_plugin_skills(def)`, and `_recompute_skill_reactivity()`. Bypassing it (e.g. by calling `_db.install` directly) leaves the plugin registered but capability-less, with no runtime, no skill seeding, no directories — looks installed in the list, doesn't actually work. Use PluginDB.install only when you're explicitly testing the registration store and don't need a running plugin (headless tests).","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"canonical-install-is-pluginmanager-not-plugindb","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1","type":"dcr","status":"designing","title":"CAD plugin — embedded PBS python runtime (Plan A)","description":"### Bug being fixed\n\nHITL #2 after DCR3 marketplace install — `bridge.Worker.Start: exec: fork/exec /usr/bin/python3: no such file or directory` when user clicks evaluate in CAD plugin installed from marketplace. Misleading ENOENT; real cause is `cmd.Dir = w.workerDir = <pluginRoot>/worker` which doesn't exist in the install (worker tree never shipped), and even if it did the user host wouldn't have python 3.12 + build123d + cadquery-ocp.\n\n### Why Plan A (embedded PBS) over alternatives\n\nAfter investigation 2026-05-27 (see Docs/pickup.md and nudge hints `minerva-plugin-platform/python-runtime-not-a-host-capability` + `cad-plugin/embed-go-is-not-embedding`):\n\n- **Plan B (ship worker/ + first-start venv from PyPI):** requires network at first start; plugins may need incompatible build123d/OCCT versions across releases; depends on Minerva-installed Python which the user may not have.\n- **Plan C (new `host.runtime.python` capability):** widens the substrate surface for one use case; each plugin still needs its own version isolation.\n- **Plan D (manifest-declared `python_runtime_required`):** architecturally cleanest BUT routes runtime resolution through Minerva — incompatible-version risk remains, requires Minerva pre-spawn install step, manifest schema bump.\n- **Plan A (embedded PBS + go:embed):** user-chosen 2026-05-27 because (a) user may never have installed any Minerva MCP servers — Minerva's MCPServerInstaller python facility may not have set anything up, (b) plugins may need incompatible build123d/OCCT versions and embedded-per-plugin gives strict isolation, (c) no network at first start, (d) faithful to existing Docs/design/Go-python-bridge-design.md §6 chosen option.\n\n### Scope (original)\n\nIN this DCR:\n- linux-x86_64 PBS bundle (cpython 3.12.x install_only + build123d + cadquery-ocp)\n- `cad/scripts/build-runtime-bundle.sh linux-x86_64` produces `cad/build/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.tar.zst` + `manifest.sha256`\n- `go:embed` of the bundle into the cad-plugin Linux binary\n- Extract-on-first-run to `<data_directory>/runtime/<plugin_version>/`\n- `internal/runtime/embed.go` (path-resolver) updated to prefer extracted-embedded over .venv over PATH\n- `bridge.Worker` Env wiring (PYTHONHOME / PYTHONPATH point at the extracted tree)\n- `worker/mcad_worker` sources baked into the bundle's site-packages (not in plugin install dir)\n- cad-side Go regression test exercising real binary + python spawn + validate call\n- Minerva-side RED-first functional test `test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` that asserts a real geometry validation succeeds end-to-end\n- cad release v0.1.1 with embedded runtime; Minerva tarball-smoke updated to call evaluate (not just install→start)\n\nOUT of scope (separate DCRs):\n- macOS-universal / linux-arm64 / windows-x86_64 bundles — bundle for those platforms returns an empty/sentinel; cad evaluate fails with \"Python runtime not bundled for platform <x>\" error toast\n- Shared-pool python runtime across plugins (`runtime.shared_pool` manifest field)\n- `host.runtime.python` capability broker route (deliberately rejected per Plan A choice)\n- presentation / scansort plugin Python embedding (neither uses Python today)\n- Binary-size optimization beyond what zstd buys\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n1. From a clean Minerva install on Linux x86_64, the user installs CAD from marketplace, opens a new mcad editor, types `result = sphere(5)`, and gets a rendered sphere in the viewport. No system python required. No errors in Activity log.\n2. Minerva CI tarball-smoke job calls `mcad_validate` against the just-built tarball under xvfb + --headless and asserts a non-error response.\n3. The new `test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` passes locally and in CI when the cad-v0.1.1 tarball is present on the network.\n4. The same test FAILS against cad-v0.1.0 (the current release without embedded python) — proving the test is actually RED for the bug, not a false-positive.\n\n### Autonomy + risk controls\n\nUser asked for max autonomy with single HITL at the end. Filed as a 7-node tree (DCR + W0 plan + 5 work items + W6 HITL).\n\nRisk controls baked in per user's list:\n\n- **Starting from wrong commit:** W0 explicitly does `git rev-parse HEAD` against both repos before any work, asserts branch is `development` (minerva) / `main` (plugins), asserts clean working tree, then branches off.\n- **Scope creep:** above OOS list is the contract. Implementer sub-agents are told \"stop and surface\" if they think they need to touch anything in the OOS list. Worktree isolation per work item.\n- **DRY violations:** Minerva functional test must follow the EXACT pattern of `src/test/test_marketplace_install_start_scansort.gd` and factor shared helpers (HTTP server, tarball fixture builder) if any are duplicated. Reviewer sub-agent checks for this explicitly.\n- **Incomplete functional test:** test must call `mcad_validate` (or `cad.evaluate`) via MCP proxy after install+start AND assert a non-error worker-validated response — not just `state=RUNNING`. The state=RUNNING green is what masked HITL #2 in DCR3. Reviewer is briefed on this specifically.\n\n### Repos touched\n\n- `~/github/plugins` (cad/ subtree) — W1a, W1b, W1c, W3   *(superseded by Scope amendment below: now `~/github/minerva-plugins`)*\n- `~/github/Minerva` (src/test, scripts/) — W2, W3 (cross-cutting)\n- imrans-lab/minerva-plugins remote on `main` — release tag push (W3, user-authorized)\n- turnrocklabs/Minerva remote on `development` — autonomous-loop-authorized\n\n### Predecessor\n\nDCR `019e62ade5be75a1807166318bcc477d` (plugin marketplace) — that one's HITL surfaced this bug.\n\n---\n\n## Scope amendment — 2026-05-27 (laptop continuation)\n\n### Platform scope expansion\nOriginal DCR scope was linux-x86_64 only with sentinel-bundles for other triples. Amended scope is a 5-target matrix, with priority tiers per user direction:\n\n| Triple | Tier |\n|---|---|\n| linux-x86_64 | MUST |\n| macos-arm64 (Apple Silicon) | MUST |\n| windows-x86_64 | MUST |\n| linux-arm64 | NICE (build if CI matrix gives it free) |\n| macos-amd64 | NICE (build if CI matrix gives it free; CI currently produces macos-universal via lipo of arm64+amd64) |\n\nConsequence: the \"sentinel bundle + ErrPlatformNotBundled\" pattern in original W1a/W1b/W1c is no longer the v1 shape. All matrix legs ship real bundles. The \"not bundled\" error path remains in the code as a defensive guard for future-unknown-platform but is dead code for the v1 release.\n\n### Repo move\nCanonical plugins repo is now `imrans-lab/minerva-plugins`, cloned locally at `~/github/minerva-plugins/`. The `ipeerbhai/plugins` fork (cloned at `~/github/plugins/`) is deprecated and slated for deletion. All work-item path strings updated accordingly. See `feedback memory: project-plugins-repo-move`.\n\n### Output directory\nBundles output to `cad/runtime-build/` (already gitignored in the repo with `*.tar.zst` pattern) instead of `cad/build/` as originally specified.\n\n### DRY substrate (cross-plugin reusability)\nCAD is the first python-embedded plugin; others will follow. To prevent copy-paste-fork of CAD's embed scaffolding by plugin #2:\n- **Build script lives at repo root**: `minerva-plugins/scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh <plugin-dir> <os> <arch>` — generic, reads `<plugin-dir>/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock` for plugin-specific pins. Zero CAD-specific knowledge in the script body.\n- **Go runtime package stays in cad's tree for v1** at `cad/internal/runtime/`, but designed plugin-agnostic — no hardcoded `\"cad\"` strings; API takes `pluginID + pluginVersion + embedded bytes` as parameters. Package-level comment establishes \"designed for extraction to `pkg/pyembed/` when plugin #2 needs python.\" Reviewer enforces with `grep -nE '\"cad\"|cadquery|build123d'` on the shared files.\n- **CI workflow stays per-plugin** (cad.yml). Composite-action extraction deferred until N≥3 python-embedded plugins exist.\n\n### W2 redesign\nW2's hermetic local-tree fixture is replaced with a real-release-tarball download (`cad-v0.1.0-<host>.tar.gz` from the GH release; once W3 ships, `cad-v0.1.1-<host>.tar.gz` for the GREEN flip). Test works cross-platform (laptop = macOS-arm64, CI = linux-x86_64). Original W2 transitioned `in_progress` → `backlog` for the redesign.\n\n### W2 whitelist expansion\nW2 may also modify `src/test/test_marketplace_install_start_scansort.gd` to consume the newly-extracted `marketplace_test_helpers.gd`. This is a DRY refactor inherent to helper extraction; not scope creep.\n\n### Autonomous loop budget\n9-loop budget for the implementation phase (6 baseline + 3 slack for fixes/errors/design gaps). Loop allocation:\n- L1: docket updates + W1a manual spike on macos-arm64\n- L2: W1a full implementation (5-target bundle builds)\n- L3: W1b (go:embed + extract + datadir)\n- L4: W1c (bridge env + cmd.Dir fix + cad regression test) — Layer 2 verification gate\n- L5: W2 redesign (helpers extraction + scansort refactor + cad test rewrite) — RED proof\n- L6: W3 (cad.yml matrix + tarball-smoke evaluate + manifest bump) — Layer 3 verification gate\n- L7-L9: slack for verification failures or design gaps\n\n### HITL gates (autonomous execution stops at any of these)\n- Gate A: Before push to `imrans-lab/minerva-plugins` `main` (W3 release cut)\n- Gate B: Before tagging `cad-v0.1.1`\n- Gate C: One-time confirmation that autonomous-loop authorization covers first `development` merge on Minerva\n- Gate D: W4 user-facing HITL (install from marketplace UI, evaluate sphere, see geometry)\n\n### Rubric applied to autonomous decisions\nPer user's grading rubric: reliability → durability → cost → readability → DRY → well-factored. Specific calls baked in:\n- zstd -19 compression (slower build, smaller/identical artifacts)\n- `github.com/klauspost/compress/zstd` Go lib (vs shelling out)\n- Atomic rename-from-tmpdir extract (vs in-place)\n- Per-file sha256 verify (vs tarball-only)\n- Helpers extracted in W2 (rule-of-2 + 3rd-coming)\n- macOS quarantine: try `xattr -d com.apple.quarantine` first; codesign decision escalates to user\n- build123d/cadquery-ocp pin: highest version with wheels on all 3 MUST platforms × cpython 3.12\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:36:52","updated_at":"2026-05-27T22:57:19","tags":["cad-plugin","embedded","marketplace","pbs","plan-a","plugin-substrate","python-runtime"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4c30a37833988745a5de1946bd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W0 PLAN — branch setup, risk controls, sub-agent briefing kit","description":"### Pre-flight (orchestrator runs this BEFORE spawning any sub-agent)\n\n1. `cd ~/github/Minerva && git status` → assert clean working tree (only Docs/pickup.md may be staged or stashed)\n2. `git -C ~/github/Minerva rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` → assert `development`\n3. `git -C ~/github/Minerva log -1 --oneline` → record SHA (this is the W0 base)\n4. `cd ~/github/plugins && git status` → assert clean working tree\n5. `git -C ~/github/plugins rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` → assert `main`\n6. `git -C ~/github/plugins log -1 --oneline` → record SHA (this is the W0 base for plugins)\n7. Create feature branches:\n   - `git -C ~/github/plugins checkout -b dcr/cad-embedded-python-linux`\n   - `git -C ~/github/Minerva checkout -b dcr/cad-embedded-python-test` (Minerva-side test branch)\n8. Run repro: ensure cad-v0.1.0 (current release) fails with worker spawn error when evaluate is called. Capture that as the RED baseline that W2 must reproduce.\n\n### Sub-agent briefing kit (paste into every implementer prompt)\n\n> You are working on DCR `019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1` — CAD plugin embedded PBS python runtime (Plan A).\n> \n> **In scope:** linux-x86_64 only. Other platforms get an empty bundle and a clean runtime error.\n> \n> **Out of scope (STOP and ask the orchestrator if any of these come up):**\n> - macOS/Windows/linux-arm64 PBS bundles\n> - manifest schema changes for the python runtime contract\n> - host.runtime / host.python capability broker routes\n> - presentation/scansort plugin changes\n> - Refactoring or \"cleanup\" of anything outside your specific work item\n> \n> **Hard rules:**\n> - Per-file `git add` only. No `-A` or `.`.\n> - No `--no-verify`. No `vendor/` touches.\n> - No force push, no `git reset --hard`.\n> - Plugin code lives in `~/github/plugins` (off-tree from Minerva). Off-tree means no `class_name` for cross-script types — use preload + base-class typing.\n> - Co-author trailer: `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>`\n> \n> **Report at end:** what you touched (per-file), test counts, and any \"I would have liked to also fix...\" items as separate notes (DO NOT fix them).\n\n### GREEN gate (orchestrator checks before HITL handoff)\n\nAll of:\n- `cd ~/github/plugins/cad && ./scripts/build-runtime-bundle.sh linux-x86_64` produces `build/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.tar.zst` and `build/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.sha256`\n- `cd ~/github/plugins/cad && go test ./...` passes (incl. new W1c regression test)\n- `cd ~/github/plugins/cad && go build -o /tmp/cad-plugin-test .` produces a binary >100MB (sanity check that embed actually embedded)\n- `godot --headless --path ~/github/Minerva/src --script test/test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` returns 0 failures against cad-v0.1.1 tarball\n- The same test FAILED against cad-v0.1.0 — proves it's RED for the right reason\n- Minerva tarball-smoke CI job (post-W3 release) passes with the new evaluate assertion\n- No regressions: scansort/presentation marketplace install tests still pass\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:37:18","updated_at":"2026-05-27T20:12:32","tags":["cad-pbs-dcr","plan","w0"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1","resolution":"Branches created on both repos, DCR docket items committed on Minerva test branch (commit f6426348), TaskCreate tracking iters 2-6."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4c91cb71e0b9d41dd672dd403b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1a — cad/scripts/build-runtime-bundle.sh (linux-x86_64 PBS + build123d + cadquery-ocp)","description":"### Goal\n\nA reproducible shell script that fetches PBS, pip-installs deps into a relocatable site-packages, copies a plugin's worker package in, and emits a content-addressed `.tar.zst` + sha256 manifest. Idempotent: re-running with same inputs produces byte-identical output (or close enough that the embed step doesn't churn).\n\n**Per DCR scope amendment 2026-05-27: the script is plugin-agnostic and lives at the minerva-plugins repo root, not in cad/. It takes plugin path as an arg so future python-embedded plugins reuse the same script with their own lock file.**\n\n### Repo\n\n`~/github/minerva-plugins/` (canonical; was `~/github/plugins` pre-amendment).\n\n### Files to create\n\n- `scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh` (REPO ROOT, not under cad/) — bash, set -euo pipefail. Takes args `<plugin-dir> <os> <arch>`. Plugin-agnostic; reads plugin-specific pins from `<plugin-dir>/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock`.\n- `cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock` — pins PBS release tag (e.g. `20240107`), cpython version (3.12.x), build123d version, cadquery-ocp version, and the plugin's own worker package name(s). Source of truth for reproducibility for the CAD plugin specifically.\n- `cad/.gitignore` (modify if exists, create otherwise) — ignore `cad/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-*.tar.zst` and `cad/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-*.sha256` and `cad/runtime-build/runtime-extract-tmp/`. NB: `cad/runtime-build/` already has a `.gitignore` covering `*.tar.zst`; extend if needed.\n\n### Script behavior\n\n```\nscripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh cad linux-x86_64\nscripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh cad macos-arm64\n... (one of: linux-x86_64, linux-arm64, macos-arm64, macos-amd64, windows-x86_64)\n```\n\n1. Resolve the plugin dir → read `<plugin-dir>/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock` for pinned versions and the worker package list (e.g. `[\"mcad_worker\", \"mcad\"]`).\n2. Download PBS `cpython-3.12.X+YYYYMMDD-<triple>-install_only.tar.gz` from `https://github.com/indygreg/python-build-standalone/releases/download/...` (cache under `<plugin-dir>/runtime-build/cache/pbs/`).\n3. Extract to `<plugin-dir>/runtime-build/runtime-stage/<triple>/`.\n4. Use the extracted `bin/python3` (or `python.exe`) to run `python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --target <stage>/site-packages/ --platform <wheel-platform> --python-version 3.12 --abi cp312 --only-binary=:all: build123d==<pinned> cadquery-ocp==<pinned>`. Wheel-platform-tag mapping (linux-x86_64→manylinux2014_x86_64, macos-arm64→macosx_11_0_arm64, etc.) is in the script. The `--platform/--only-binary` flags let the laptop (or any host) cross-install wheels for any target.\n5. Copy each worker package from `<plugin-dir>/worker/<pkg>/` into `<stage>/site-packages/<pkg>/`.\n6. **Layer 1 verification (in-script):** if this is a native-host bundle (target matches `runtime.GOOS+GOARCH`), invoke `<stage>/bin/python3 -c \"import build123d, cadquery_ocp; from mcad_worker import dispatcher; print('Layer 1 OK')\"` with `PYTHONHOME=<stage>` and `PYTHONPATH=<stage>/site-packages`. If the import fails, the script exits non-zero — no tarball produced. Cross-target builds skip Layer 1 (can't run a non-host binary); they're verified at L2 only when CI runs on that target.\n7. Generate `<stage>/manifest.sha256` listing every file's sha256 (sorted; used by Go-side ExtractEmbedded to verify post-extract).\n8. Create `<plugin-dir>/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-<triple>.tar.zst` via `tar --sort=name --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner -cf - -C <stage> . | zstd -19 -o <out>`.\n9. Compute `<plugin-dir>/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-<triple>.sha256` covering the tarball (used by Go-side `//go:embed` consumer to verify the embedded bytes).\n\n### Lock file shape (cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock)\n\n```toml\n[pbs]\nrelease_tag = \"20240107\"\ncpython = \"3.12.1\"\n\n[python_deps]\nbuild123d = \"0.10.0\"\ncadquery-ocp = \"7.8.1.1\"\n\n[worker_packages]\npackages = [\"mcad_worker\", \"mcad\"]\nsource_dir = \"worker\"\n```\n\n### Wheel-platform-tag mapping (in the script)\n\n| Triple | pip --platform tag |\n|---|---|\n| linux-x86_64 | `manylinux2014_x86_64`  *(or `manylinux_2_31_x86_64` per cadquery-ocp wheels)* |\n| linux-arm64 | `manylinux2014_aarch64` *(or `manylinux_2_31_aarch64`)* |\n| macos-arm64 | `macosx_11_0_arm64` |\n| macos-amd64 | `macosx_11_0_x86_64` |\n| windows-x86_64 | `win_amd64` |\n\n### Tests\n\nPer-target Layer 1 (script self-test) running on the laptop for the native triple (macos-arm64) and cross-validating tarball presence + tar listing + size band on the others:\n- `scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh cad macos-arm64` finishes in <120s on a warm cache, Layer 1 import succeeds, tarball ~100-250 MB.\n- Cross-target builds for the other 4: tarball produced, contains `bin/python3` (or `python.exe`), `site-packages/build123d/__init__.py`, `site-packages/cadquery_ocp*`, `site-packages/mcad_worker/__main__.py`. Size band: ~100-250 MB compressed. Native Layer-1 verification deferred to CI.\n\n### Out of scope here\n\n- go:embed wiring (W1b)\n- cad.yml CI integration (W3)\n- Code-signing for macOS (revisit only if extracted binaries are gatekeeper-rejected; first attempt is `xattr -d com.apple.quarantine`)\n\n### Reviewer brief\n\nThings to check:\n- Lock file present + values pinned, not floating.\n- Script is hermetic re: cache (no system pip; uses extracted PBS pip).\n- Script is plugin-agnostic: `grep -nE '\"cad\"|cadquery|build123d|mcad' scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh` must return zero hits (those identifiers only appear in `cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock`).\n- Layer-1 self-test runs only for native target; cross-target paths don't false-fail.\n- No `--no-verify`, no `git add -A`, no vendor touches.\n- 120s warm-cache native build is a goal, not a hard fail.\n- Cross-platform pip wheels actually exist for the pinned versions × cpython 3.12 (re-verify against PyPI before pinning).\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:37:43","updated_at":"2026-05-27T22:57:55","tags":["build-tooling","cad-pbs-dcr","plugins-repo","w1a"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4cf7627985ad4aa128e2ed95e1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1b — cad: go:embed + ExtractEmbedded + PythonPath update","description":"### Goal\n\nWire the bundle from W1a into the cad-plugin Go binary via `go:embed`, extract atomically to `<data_directory>/runtime/<plugin_version>/` on first run (or sha mismatch), and update `runtime.PythonPath` to prefer the extracted interpreter over `.venv` over `LookPath(\"python3\")`.\n\n**Per DCR scope amendment 2026-05-27: 5 platform-tagged embed files replace the single linux+sentinel pattern. The extract/datadir API is plugin-agnostic (designed for future extraction to `pkg/pyembed/` when plugin #2 needs python).**\n\n### Repo\n\n`~/github/minerva-plugins/cad/` (canonical; was `~/github/plugins/cad/` pre-amendment).\n\n### Files to create\n\n- `cad/internal/runtime/embed_linux_amd64.go` — `//go:build linux && amd64`; `//go:embed ../../runtime-build/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.tar.zst`. Exports `EmbeddedBundle []byte` + `EmbeddedSHA256 string` (sha read from the matching .sha256 file at build time via `//go:embed`).\n- `cad/internal/runtime/embed_linux_arm64.go` — `//go:build linux && arm64`; embeds `runtime-bundle-linux-arm64.tar.zst`.\n- `cad/internal/runtime/embed_darwin_arm64.go` — `//go:build darwin && arm64`; embeds `runtime-bundle-macos-arm64.tar.zst`.\n- `cad/internal/runtime/embed_darwin_amd64.go` — `//go:build darwin && amd64`; embeds `runtime-bundle-macos-amd64.tar.zst`.\n- `cad/internal/runtime/embed_windows_amd64.go` — `//go:build windows && amd64`; embeds `runtime-bundle-windows-x86_64.tar.zst`.\n- `cad/internal/runtime/extract.go` — **plugin-agnostic** core logic. Package-level comment: \"Designed for future extraction to `pkg/pyembed/` when a second python-embedded plugin needs the same scaffolding. Do not add plugin-specific behavior here; parameterize instead.\" Public API:\n  ```go\n  type EnsureRuntimeRequest struct {\n      EmbeddedBundle []byte    // the go:embed'd tar.zst bytes\n      EmbeddedSHA256 string    // checksum of EmbeddedBundle for tamper detection\n      PluginID       string    // e.g. \"cad\"\n      PluginVersion  string    // e.g. \"0.1.1\"\n      DataDir        string    // resolved via DataDir() from datadir.go\n  }\n  // EnsureRuntime implements the 3-tier lookup (cache → prebundled → embedded extract).\n  // Returns absolute path to the runtime root directory (use RuntimePython() helper for the python interpreter path).\n  func EnsureRuntime(req EnsureRuntimeRequest) (runtimeRoot string, err error)\n  // RuntimePython returns the python interpreter path for a runtime root (handles OS-specific bin/Scripts).\n  func RuntimePython(runtimeRoot string) string\n  ```\n  Steps inside EnsureRuntime:\n  1. Compute target dir: `filepath.Join(req.DataDir, \"runtime\", req.PluginVersion)`\n  2. If target/`manifest.sha256` exists and matches expected → return `target` (cache hit).\n  3. If `req.EmbeddedBundle` is empty (defensive — should never happen post-amendment) → return `ErrPlatformNotBundled`.\n  4. Verify sha256(EmbeddedBundle) matches `req.EmbeddedSHA256` (defense in depth — embed contents must match what build emitted).\n  5. Extract using `archive/tar` + `github.com/klauspost/compress/zstd` (pure-Go, single dep).\n  6. Atomic rename: extract to `<dataDir>/runtime/.tmp-<pid>-<unixnano>/`, then `os.Rename` to final. On rename failure, clean up the tmp dir.\n  7. Per-file sha verification post-extract using `manifest.sha256` from inside the tarball. Tampered extract → re-extract clean, log warning.\n  8. Return the runtime root path.\n- `cad/internal/runtime/datadir.go` — **plugin-agnostic.** `DataDir(pluginID string) string`:\n  1. Read `MINERVA_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` env var (Minerva-set at spawn). If set, return it.\n  2. Else fallback: `filepath.Join(xdgDataHome(), \"minerva\", \"plugins\", pluginID)` where `xdgDataHome()` returns `$XDG_DATA_HOME` or `~/.local/share` on Unix, `%APPDATA%` on Windows, `~/Library/Application Support` on macOS.\n- Modify `cad/internal/runtime/embed.go` (the existing path-resolver) — update `PythonPath(workerDir)` priority and signature:\n  ```go\n  // PythonPath returns the absolute path to the python interpreter to use.\n  // Priority: extracted-embedded > .venv > $PATH python3.\n  func PythonPath(workerDir, pluginID, pluginVersion string) (string, error) {\n      dataDir := DataDir(pluginID)\n      req := EnsureRuntimeRequest{\n          EmbeddedBundle: EmbeddedBundle,\n          EmbeddedSHA256: EmbeddedSHA256,\n          PluginID:       pluginID,\n          PluginVersion:  pluginVersion,\n          DataDir:        dataDir,\n      }\n      if root, err := EnsureRuntime(req); err == nil {\n          return RuntimePython(root), nil\n      }\n      // fall through to existing .venv / $PATH logic for dev mode\n      ...\n  }\n  ```\n\n### Tests\n\n`cad/internal/runtime/extract_test.go`:\n- TestExtract_ColdCache: temp dir, extract, assert `bin/python3` (or `python.exe`) exists + is executable.\n- TestExtract_WarmCache: extract, extract again, assert second call returns fast (no re-extract).\n- TestExtract_TamperDetect: extract, corrupt a file in target, re-call extract → must re-extract clean.\n- TestExtract_BadSHA: pass mismatched EmbeddedSHA256 → returns error before extract.\n\n`cad/internal/runtime/embed_test.go` (extends existing):\n- TestPythonPath_PrefersEmbedded: with both embedded extract AND .venv present, embedded wins.\n- TestPythonPath_FallsBackToVenv: when embedded extract returns error (e.g., empty bundle in test build), `.venv` wins.\n\n### Dependencies\n\nAdding `github.com/klauspost/compress/zstd` is OK — pure Go, single module, mature. Update `cad/go.mod` and `cad/go.sum`.\n\n### Out of scope here\n\n- bridge.Worker env wiring (W1c)\n- Bundle file actually being present in `cad/runtime-build/` (W1a job)\n- Test that calls real Python (W1c — the Layer 2 verification)\n\n### Reviewer brief\n\n- Confirm `go:embed` path is relative to the source file and resolves to `cad/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-<triple>.tar.zst` (Go requires same-tree-or-below).\n- **DRY check (HARD):** `grep -nE '\"cad\"|cadquery|build123d|mcad' cad/internal/runtime/{extract,datadir}.go` must return zero hits. Plugin-specific strings live only in `cad/main.go` (the caller).\n- Confirm `embed_<triple>.go` files don't accidentally swap embeds (e.g., darwin_arm64 embeds darwin-amd64 bundle).\n- ExtractEmbedded MUST be safe to call concurrently (file lock or rename-based serialization).\n- Atomic rename — no partial extract visible to other plugin starts.\n- Tampering detection actually checks every file, not just the tarball sha.\n- Package-level comment establishing \"designed for extraction to pkg/pyembed/\" is present in `extract.go`.\n- No \"I'd also like to refactor X\" in this PR.\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:38:09","updated_at":"2026-05-27T22:58:28","tags":["cad-pbs-dcr","go-embed","plugins-repo","w1b"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4d422a705b96712d59e6e0d6a3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1c — cad: bridge.Worker env wiring + cad-side regression test","description":"### Goal\n\nMake `bridge.Worker.Start` spawn the Python interpreter under the extracted runtime tree with `PYTHONHOME`, `PYTHONPATH`, scrubbed env, and a writable cwd so `python3 -m mcad_worker` resolves the bundled mcad_worker without leaking the host system's Python.\n\n**Per DCR scope amendment 2026-05-27: path strings updated; the \"not bundled for platform\" toast remains as defensive code but is unreachable in v1 (all 5 matrix targets ship real bundles).**\n\n### Repo\n\n`~/github/minerva-plugins/cad/` (canonical; was `~/github/plugins/cad/` pre-amendment).\n\n### Files to modify\n\n- `cad/internal/bridge/worker.go` — modify env construction (currently at `cmd.Env = buildEnv()` or similar around line 158):\n  - If the worker python path is under a `runtime/<plugin_version>/` tree (heuristic: parent directory of `bin/python3` contains `manifest.sha256`), set:\n    - `PYTHONHOME=<runtime_root>`\n    - `PYTHONPATH=<runtime_root>/site-packages`\n    - `PATH=<runtime_root>/bin:<existing-PATH>` (POSIX) or `PATH=<runtime_root>;<runtime_root>\\Scripts;<existing-PATH>` (Windows)\n    - **Strip from inherited env:** `PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PYTHONUSERBASE`, `VIRTUAL_ENV`, `CONDA_PREFIX`, `CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV` (avoid host venv / anaconda contamination)\n  - Else preserve current behavior (dev-mode .venv or system python).\n- `cad/main.go:initWorker` — adjust to pass `pluginID` + `pluginVersion` into `runtime.PythonPath()` (signature change from W1b). Surface \"not bundled for platform\" as a one-time `host.notify` error toast at startup (so HITL on an unsupported future platform gets a clear message instead of fork-exec ENOENT). Once-only by tracking a sentinel boolean.\n- `cad/internal/bridge/worker.go` — **also fix:** the `cmd.Dir = w.workerDir` setting. In marketplace installs, `<plugin_install>/worker/` does not exist (the worker source is now embedded inside the python bundle's site-packages, NOT shipped as a separate dir). When running under an extracted bundle, set `cmd.Dir` to a writable temp dir (e.g. `<dataDir>/work/`) created on the fly. When running dev-mode (`.venv` exists), keep the existing `cmd.Dir = workerDir` behavior.\n\n### Regression test (Layer 2 verification gate)\n\n`cad/test/test_embedded_python_spawn_test.go` (new file in a separate `cad/test/` dir to keep it out of `internal/`):\n- SKIP if `cad/runtime-build/runtime-bundle-<host-triple>.tar.zst` doesn't exist locally, with a message: \"Run scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh cad <host-triple> first\".\n- Else: `go build -o /tmp/cad-plugin-test .` produces a binary. Assert size > 100MB.\n- Set `MINERVA_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR=<t.TempDir()>`.\n- Spawn the binary; do MCP `initialize` handshake; call `tools/call` with `name=mcad_validate`, `arguments={source: \"result = sphere(5)\"}`.\n- Assert: response has `isError=false`, content text parses to `{ok: true, ...}`.\n- Cleanup: send `shutdown` JSON-RPC, wait up to 2s, kill if needed; remove temp dirs.\n\nThis is the Layer 2 verification gate for the autonomous loop. W1c is not done until this test passes locally on the laptop (macos-arm64).\n\n### Out of scope here\n\n- cad.yml CI integration (W3)\n- Minerva-side functional test (W2)\n- Cross-platform Layer 2 (only laptop's native triple is tested in W1c; CI cross-target L2 happens in W3)\n\n### Reviewer brief\n\n- Env stripping covers ALL host-python leakage vectors: `PYTHONHOME`, `PYTHONPATH`, `VIRTUAL_ENV`, `PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PYTHONUSERBASE`, `CONDA_PREFIX`, `CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV`.\n- `cmd.Dir` no longer points at a path that doesn't exist in marketplace installs.\n- Test SKIPs cleanly when bundle absent; doesn't false-PASS or hang. The SKIP message tells the developer exactly what to run.\n- \"not bundled for platform\" toast fires exactly once per plugin start, not on every tools/call.\n- No \"let me also refactor bridge.Worker.Call\" — keep changes scoped.\n- `cad/main.go`'s `initWorker` change is mechanical (param plumbing); reviewer flags if anything else changed.\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:38:28","updated_at":"2026-05-27T22:58:50","tags":["bridge-worker","cad-pbs-dcr","plugins-repo","w1c"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4d96fb7465b5fdd6a42158fd9c","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"W2 — Minerva RED functional test for cad evaluate via marketplace","description":"### Goal\n\nThe acceptance test for this whole DCR. Written FIRST, must fail against the current cad-v0.1.0 (proves it's RED for the right reason), then flips GREEN after W1a/b/c + W3 land cad-v0.1.1.\n\n**Per DCR scope amendment 2026-05-27: hermetic local-tree fixture pattern is replaced with a real-release-tarball download. Test is cross-platform (host = macos-arm64 on the laptop; linux-x86_64 in CI). The original W2 file was a linux-only local-fixture attempt and is being rewritten, not extended.**\n\n### Repo\n\n`~/github/Minerva/` on branch `dcr/cad-embedded-python-test`.\n\n### Whitelist (per amendment)\n\nFiles writeable in this work item:\n- `src/test/test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` (REWRITE — existing content is the obsolete linux-only fixture attempt)\n- `src/test/test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd.uid` (Godot auto)\n- `src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd` (NEW — extracted shared helpers)\n- `src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd.uid` (Godot auto)\n- `src/test/test_marketplace_install_start_scansort.gd` (MODIFY — refactor to consume the new helpers; this is the DRY motivator)\n- `scripts/run-functional-tests.sh` (additive: ensure cad test is in PLUGIN_TESTS array)\n\nEverything else is out of scope for W2.\n\n### Helper extraction (DRY motivator)\n\n`marketplace_test_helpers.gd` extracts the common pattern from `test_marketplace_install_start_scansort.gd` (the existing working test). Helpers must include:\n- HTTP fixture server start/stop (`start_http_server(port: int, doc_root: String)` / `stop_http_server(pid: int)`)\n- Tarball staging (download or pull-from-cache pattern; default to user://test_fixtures/ for the cache dir)\n- Plugin install assertion (call MCP `minerva_plugin_marketplace_install`, await state convergence)\n- Plugin start assertion (call MCP `minerva_plugin_start`, await `state=RUNNING`)\n- Plugin stop / cleanup\n- Optional: MCP `tools/call` envelope unwrap helper that returns `{ok, result|error}` from the response\n\nAfter extraction, the scansort sibling must use these helpers exclusively for the listed operations. Side-by-side diff should show scansort's test file shrinking significantly.\n\n### Test must do (rewritten cad test)\n\n1. Resolve the release-tarball URL for the host platform: `https://github.com/imrans-lab/minerva-plugins/releases/download/cad-v0.1.0/cad-0.1.0-<host>.tar.gz` where `<host>` is `linux-x86_64`, `linux-arm64`, `macos-universal` (current CI shape), or `windows-x86_64`.\n2. SKIP if the host platform has no corresponding release asset. Print which platforms are supported.\n3. Download to `user://test_fixtures/cad-0.1.0-<host>.tar.gz` (caches; skip re-download if present and sha matches).\n4. Start a local HTTP server (python3 http.server pattern, factored into helpers) serving the fixture.\n5. Initialize Minerva's MCP HTTP server.\n6. Call `minerva_plugin_marketplace_install` with the served URL.\n7. Call `minerva_plugin_start` for `cad`.\n8. Assert state=RUNNING (sanity check — DCR3 acceptance was here).\n9. **The new acceptance:** call `tools/call` for `mcad_validate` with `arguments={source: \"result = sphere(5)\"}`. Assert:\n   - MCP call returns within 60s (cold start of OCCT can take ~10s; allow margin)\n   - Against **cad-v0.1.0**: response is an error envelope matching one of these platform-specific signatures (the RED proof):\n     - Linux: `fork/exec`, `python3`, `No such file`\n     - macOS: `ModuleNotFoundError`, `No module named`, `build123d` (assuming /usr/bin/python3 exists but lacks build123d)\n     - Windows: similar to macOS, or `is not recognized as an internal or external command`\n   - Against **cad-v0.1.1** (post-W3): response is `{ok: true, ...}` with non-empty `result.mesh.vertices` or `result.shape_name`\n10. Call `minerva_plugin_stop` for cad; assert state=STOPPED.\n11. Cleanup: kill HTTP server, remove any extracted plugin files.\n\n### Version targeting\n\nFor the RED proof: hardcode `cad-v0.1.0` URL. The test is expected to fail at step 9 against v0.1.0.\n\nFor the GREEN flip (W3): change the URL to `cad-v0.1.1` (the embedded-python release). One-line change.\n\nWhether to parameterize the version: leave hardcoded for v1. Future could promote to env var.\n\n### Register in scripts/run-functional-tests.sh PLUGIN_TESTS\n\nAdd `test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` to the PLUGIN_TESTS array (same place scansort sibling is listed). (May already be present from the prior W2 attempt; verify and de-dup.)\n\n### RED proof\n\nBefore W3 lands, this test MUST fail against `cad-v0.1.0` with a platform-specific error matching the table above. If the test passes against cad-v0.1.0, the test isn't asserting what it should — fix the assertion before W3 begins.\n\n### Out of scope here\n\n- Cad-side implementation (W1a/b/c)\n- Smoke job updates (W3)\n- Other plugin tests\n\n### Reviewer brief\n\n- **DRY check (HARD):** helpers actually extracted; scansort sibling actually refactored to use them; no duplicated HTTP/tarball/install code between the two tests.\n- Assertion is \"evaluate worked\" not \"install worked\". The state=RUNNING assertion is necessary but not sufficient.\n- Test SKIPs cleanly if host platform has no release asset — but does NOT false-PASS in that case.\n- 60s timeout is generous; reviewer flags if the implementer uses <30s (OCCT cold start margin).\n- Error-matcher is broad enough to catch all 3 platform RED modes.\n- Whitelist respected: scansort sibling changed only for helper-call substitution, no other refactor.\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:38:50","updated_at":"2026-05-27T23:00:05","tags":["cad-pbs-dcr","functional-test","minerva-repo","red-first","w2"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4e0455709b9056589ed242ca78","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W3 — Cross-cutting integrate: cad.yml + release v0.1.1 + Minerva tarball-smoke evaluate + flip W2 GREEN","description":"### Goal\n\nEnd-to-end integration. After W1a/b/c are merged in plugins repo and W2 has landed (RED) in Minerva, this work item:\n1. Wires the runtime-bundle build into cad.yml CI matrix (per amendment: 5-target real builds, no sentinel pattern)\n2. Cuts cad-v0.1.1 release with the embedded bundle for each target\n3. Updates Minerva tarball-smoke (`scripts/tarball-smoke.sh` + `.github/workflows/build.yml`) to call evaluate, not just install→start\n4. Confirms W2 flips GREEN against cad-v0.1.1\n5. Confirms scansort + presentation marketplace tests still pass (no regressions)\n\n**Per DCR scope amendment 2026-05-27: path references updated; CI step calls the repo-root generic script `scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh cad <triple>`.**\n\n### Files to modify\n\n`~/github/minerva-plugins/.github/workflows/cad.yml` (was `~/github/plugins/...`):\n- Add a new step before `Build (native)` / `Build (macOS universal — lipo amd64 + arm64)` steps: `Build runtime bundle`. Runs `bash ../scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh . ${{ matrix.target-as-triple }}` from the `cad/` cwd (note: `defaults.run.working-directory` is already `cad`).\n- For the matrix's `macos-universal` leg: build both `macos-arm64` AND `macos-amd64` bundles (each via a separate `build-python-runtime-bundle.sh` invocation), then `lipo` produces the universal binary; both embeds (`embed_darwin_arm64.go` + `embed_darwin_amd64.go`) compile in via Go's per-platform build tags. The lipo step combines two pre-embedded binaries.\n- Cache `cad/runtime-build/cache/pbs/` keyed on `cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock` sha. Cache hit → ~5s; cold → ~120s per leg.\n- After `Build (native)` step, add a binary-size assertion: `FILE_SIZE=$(stat -c%s build/cad-plugin* 2>/dev/null || stat -f%z build/cad-plugin* ...); [ $FILE_SIZE -gt 100000000 ] || (echo \"Binary too small — go:embed may have silently consumed a missing bundle\"; exit 1)`. Catches the failure mode where the embed file is missing or empty.\n- Pack release tarball: NO change needed for layout. Bundle is embedded in the binary, not shipped separately. The tarball still contains binary + manifest + ui/.\n- Bump `cad/manifest.json` version: `0.1.0` → `0.1.1`.\n\n`~/github/Minerva/scripts/tarball-smoke.sh`:\n- After the existing `minerva_plugin_list` assertion (state=RUNNING), add:\n  - sessionless MCP call: `tools/call` for `mcad_validate` with `{source: \"result = sphere(5)\"}`\n  - Assert the response payload `ok=true` and contains a `result` object with non-empty `mesh` or `shape_name`.\n- Existing scansort + plugin_marketplace install logic untouched; cad-evaluate is an ADDED assertion only — no test reshape.\n\n`~/github/Minerva/.github/workflows/build.yml`:\n- `tarball-smoke` job (added in autonomous loop iteration 2-4): leave intact; the bash script is what changes.\n\n### Release cut (HITL Gate A)\n\nAfter cad.yml passes on the dcr branch:\n- Verify CI produced all 5 (or at least 3 MUST) prerelease tarballs via the per-branch prerelease release name (e.g. `cad-v0.1.1-branch-dcr-cad-embedded-python-linux`).\n- Download the matching prerelease tarball for the laptop (macos-universal) and run Layer 3 locally: install via test → start → mcad_validate sphere(5) → assert geometry. This is the W2 GREEN-against-prerelease confirmation BEFORE asking for Gate A.\n- **PAUSE for Gate A (HITL):** open PR `dcr/cad-embedded-python-linux` → `main` on plugins repo. **User-authorized direct pushes to imrans-lab/minerva-plugins main are scoped per push — reconfirm before this one.**\n- After merge: release `cad-v0.1.1` autogenerates per existing workflow.\n- Regenerate `plugins/registry.json` (run `python3 scripts/regen_registry.py`); separate commit. Confirm via the registry API that cad@0.1.1 is resolvable.\n\n### W2 turns GREEN\n\n- Pull cad-v0.1.1 tarball URL into the W2 test (replace the v0.1.0 RED baseline).\n- Run `godot --headless --path src --script test/test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` locally → 0 failures.\n- Push the test version-bump commit on the Minerva dcr branch.\n- Wait for CI: tarball-smoke job is green; functional-tests job is green.\n- **HITL Gate B/C (one-time):** Reconfirm autonomous-loop authorization covers merge to `development` on Minerva. If yes, merge `dcr/cad-embedded-python-test` → `development`.\n\n### Regression sweep\n\n- Run scripts/run-functional-tests.sh PLUGIN_TESTS against the full set:\n  - test_marketplace_install_start_scansort.gd → pass (using new helpers)\n  - test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd → pass against v0.1.1\n  - presentation equivalent (if it exists) → pass\n- Confirm Minerva's main-line tests (channel/documents/etc.) unaffected.\n\n### Out of scope\n\n- Restructuring tarball-smoke (only ADD, don't refactor)\n- CI composite-action extraction (deferred to plugin #N≥3)\n\n### Reviewer brief\n\n- Binary-size assertion catches the failure mode where go:embed silently elides a missing file. Verify the assertion runs on ALL 5 (or however many) matrix legs, not just linux-x86_64.\n- Cad.yml change is additive — non-cad matrix entries (presentation, scansort) continue building untouched.\n- Release URL substitution in W2 test is the LAST commit on the test branch (test must be RED until the literal URL changes).\n- No untested code paths added to tarball-smoke.sh.\n- Gate A request to user includes: 5 prerelease tarball URLs, laptop Layer-3 result, link to the CI run.\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:39:18","updated_at":"2026-05-27T22:59:50","tags":["cad-pbs-dcr","ci","integration","release","w3"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e6a4e37b97722b83c3ad5dafc000a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W4 HITL — user installs CAD from marketplace, evaluates a sphere, sees real geometry","description":"### What the user should do\n\n1. Restart Minerva (clean state).\n2. Open Plugin Manager → marketplace → install CAD (version 0.1.1).\n3. Open a new mcad editor.\n4. Type `result = sphere(5)` and trigger evaluate (click button or auto-eval).\n5. **Expected:** rendered sphere in the viewport; no error toasts; no \"Whitelisted script not found\"; no \"fork/exec\" worker spawn error; no \"Python runtime not bundled\" message.\n6. Try a slightly heavier source: paste the canonical T-beam example from the skill prompt.\n7. Confirm exports (STL + STEP) work via `minerva_cad_export`.\n\n### What the implementer prepared in advance\n\n- Functional test passing in CI against cad-v0.1.1 tarball\n- Tarball-smoke job passing the new evaluate assertion\n- Local Minerva test pass: `test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd` returns 0 failures\n- Latest auto-build release of Minerva pushed to development with the W2/W3 changes\n- Latest cad release tag `cad-v0.1.1` published on imrans-lab/minerva-plugins\n- Registry.json updated to point at cad@0.1.1\n\n### If HITL fails\n\n- Implementer captures the user's screenshot + Activity log\n- Files a follow-up work item under this DCR (or a remora bug if it's a regression in non-cad surface)\n- Does NOT transition the DCR to shipped until HITL clean\n\n### If HITL passes\n\n- Implementer transitions this work item to done\n- Implementer transitions all sibling work items to done\n- Implementer transitions DCR `019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1` to shipped\n- Implementer files follow-up DCRs for macOS/Windows/linux-arm64 PBS bundles (next-up backlog)\n- Pickup doc updated, push, session ready for cross-platform follow-up\n","created_at":"2026-05-27T16:39:31","updated_at":"2026-05-27T16:39:31","tags":["acceptance","cad-pbs-dcr","hitl","w4"],"parent":"minerva:019e6a4bcb0c71019723011d8f8c8cf1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e76d6475a7470afc12a6771dc4a72","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Ethereal propose/modify/accept edits — an agent-drivable inline-diff review layer","created_at":"2026-05-30T03:03:35","updated_at":"2026-05-30T03:10:08","priority":3,"tags":["agent-loop","annotations","diff","editor","mcp","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e76dfd66070edadea2aadcfc1cc49","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Annotation panel: large annotation content overflows bounds and pushes open/close control off-screen (can't close)","created_at":"2026-05-30T03:14:01","updated_at":"2026-05-30T05:22:04","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","layout","overflow","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in 120ed98a — workbench list/body wrapped in a ScrollContainer with a real min height. Verified live: long comment bodies scroll, collapse chevron stays reachable.","environment":"Minerva (post-restart this session). Text editor (.md) annotation dock.","repro_steps":"1. Add a text annotation with a large body (e.g. a multi-line suggested-slide comment, ~15+ lines).\n2. Open the annotation pane/sub-panel for it.\n3. Observe the content overflows the pane bounds; the open/close toggle control is pushed off-screen.\n4. There is now no way to close the pane."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e76dffd737941bddc4bce2fcfed9b","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Annotation markers not placed in text editor for MCP-added annotations (no gutter/inline indicator of location)","created_at":"2026-05-30T03:14:11","updated_at":"2026-05-30T05:22:08","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","markers","mcp","rendering","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in 120ed98a — root cause was scroll-ignoring badge math (line_idx*line_height) + no scroll-redraw, on the orphaned-but-present TextEditorAnnotationCanvas. Now anchors to scroll-aware get_pos_at_line_column and redraws on scroll; line-scope → left gutter, range → right margin. Verified live: gutter markers render and track scroll.","environment":"Minerva (post-restart this session). Text editor (.md). 3 annotations added via MCP, confirmed present in annotations_list (ann_0001..0003) but no markers visible.","repro_steps":"1. Call minerva_text_editor_add_comment on a live .md tab with a valid [start,end) range (verified via annotations_list that the anchor snapshot matches the intended text).\n2. Look at the text editor body.\n3. No marker/highlight/gutter indicator appears at the anchored range."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e76e021657f2b989e8306d05abd32","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Annotation dock: clicking the annotation number does not jump/scroll the editor to the annotation","created_at":"2026-05-30T03:14:20","updated_at":"2026-05-30T05:22:12","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","navigation","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in 120ed98a — dock's annotation_selected signal (previously emitted but unconsumed) wired to a handler that moves caret, selects the span, and centers the viewport. Verified live: clicking the dock number jumps the editor.","environment":"Minerva (post-restart this session). Text editor (.md) annotation dock sub-pane.","repro_steps":"1. Add one or more text annotations.\n2. Open the annotation sub-pane listing them.\n3. Click an annotation number/index entry.\n4. Nothing happens — the editor does not scroll/jump to the anchored range, no caret move, no selection."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e773b95327b6a90c0adad2a1ec531","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_annotations_update_status: resolved/applied/patch object params lack JSON Schema properties — resolve/apply lifecycle un-driveable via MCP without guessing","created_at":"2026-05-30T04:54:14","updated_at":"2026-05-30T05:22:16","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","contract-drift","mcp","schema"],"resolution":"Fixed in 120ed98a — published full JSON Schema properties for resolved/applied/patch on minerva_annotations_update_status (resolved.by typed as object{kind,id}). Verified live: 13 annotations resolved successfully via MCP with resolved.by as an object.","repro_steps":"1. Call minerva_annotations_update_status(editor_name, annotation_id, lifecycle=\"resolved\", resolved={\"by\":\"ai\"}).\n2. Server returns {\"error\":\"resolved.by is required\"}.\n3. No amount of schema-reading reveals that `by` must be an OBJECT, not a string — the schema says only `resolved: {type:object}` with no properties.\n4. Works only once you guess: resolved={\"by\":{\"kind\":\"ai\",\"id\":\"...\"}}."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e773bba3e74f2abe9e626657d0e42","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"text_editor_add_comment stamps author.kind=\"human\" on AI-created annotations","created_at":"2026-05-30T04:54:23","updated_at":"2026-05-30T05:22:19","severity":2,"tags":["annotations","authorship","mcp"],"resolution":"Fixed in 120ed98a — author_kind threaded through TextEditorAnnotationHost.add_comment_at (default human); MCP handler passes \"ai\". Verified live: all 13 MCP-created annotations show author.kind=\"ai\".","repro_steps":"1. Agent calls minerva_text_editor_add_comment(editor_name, start, end, text).\n2. minerva_annotations_list shows the new annotation with \"author\":{\"kind\":\"human\"}.\n3. Expected: author.kind=\"ai\" for agent-created annotations."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e776facf07352b8d6b35f6e490997","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Presentation MCP: close the read/write asymmetry + LLM-ergonomics gaps (living)","created_at":"2026-05-30T05:51:08","updated_at":"2026-05-30T06:04:31","priority":3,"tags":["ergonomics","images","living","mcp","presentation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Extract Code Tools from Minerva as an optional coding-agent extension (Sightline + Code Magic + file primitives)","description":"## Context & vision\n\nMinerva is a note-taking + multi-provider LLM-chat application. It is NOT inherently a coding agent, and most of its use cases (notes, chat, audio/video, project packaging) work with zero code-intelligence features. \"Code Tools\" is being EXTRACTED FROM Minerva core into a single, OPTIONAL extension plugin that turns Minerva into a coding agent **for the users who want that, and only for them**. Installing Code Tools is what makes Minerva code-aware; without it, a vanilla Minerva must remain fully functional.\n\nThis DCR SUBSUMES and supersedes the earlier integration items: 019d2dc0 (\"integrate Sightline and Code Magic into Minerva MCP workflow\") and 019e0f60 (\"Minerva MCP plugin contract\"). It consumes the two advanced PoCs — Sightline (~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline) and Code Magic (~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic) — plus the file primitives currently living in Minerva core.\n\n## What Code Tools is\n\nA single unified stdio-MCP plugin exposing `minerva_codetools_*` tools, three subsystems behind one router + result envelope:\n- **files**: glob, grep, bash, cwd — EXTRACTED from Minerva core (src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/ + Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCodeTools.gd). `minerva_doc_*` STAYS in core (it is buffer/editor-coupled and cannot leave the host).\n- **sightline**: runtime/functional inspection, Godot debugger/output capture, validation. Gets the MCP wrapper it currently lacks (today it is CLI-only). Becomes the CANONICAL provider of runtime inspection — Minerva's duplicate src/addons/sightline_probe is REMOVED; Minerva uses Sightline instead of duplicating it.\n- **code_magic**: semantic code graph + explainability (query/context/diff/analyze) — already speaks stdio MCP (10 tools); vendored and re-namespaced. Its Godot visualizer ships as a `godot_scene` panel.\n\n## Decisions (locked this session)\n1. **Topology — ONE unified plugin.** Sightline's Linux/X11 visual capture is feature-gated; its debugger/output JSON capture is cross-platform; platform coverage expands over time.\n2. **Optional, NOT bundled.** Opt-in marketplace plugin like cad/scansort/presentation. Minerva CORE must not depend on it; all code-intelligence is gated behind its presence. (This drops the earlier 'bundled auto-install' prerequisite.)\n3. **Scope — extract the file primitives.** glob/grep/bash/cwd move out of core into the plugin; `minerva_doc_*` remains in core.\n4. **Runtime — Go shim + embedded CPython (the cad pattern).** Chosen by the project rubric (reliability → durability → cost → readability → DRY → well-factored): a private interpreter + pinned tree-sitter wheels win reliability + durability decisively over a host-`python3` script (fails on hosts without it); a Go/Rust rewrite is the eventual north star but discards the working, tested PoCs for v1.\n5. **No duplication.** Remove Minerva's sightline_probe; Sightline owns runtime inspection. Sightline's own search/explore layer collapses into the extracted files/ grep (one grep, not two).\n\n## Non-goals (v1)\n- LLM-generated code descriptions (Code Magic's headline README feature; not wired).\n- Multi-language Code Magic (GDScript first).\n- Making Minerva a coding agent by default — it stays a notes/chat app unless the user installs this.\n\n## Architecture\n```\ncodetools/  (Go shim + embedded CPython, stdio MCP, optional marketplace plugin)\n├── manifest.json   tools: minerva_codetools_*  |  panel: code-magic viz (godot_scene)\n├── go shim         launch · length-prefixed framing to python worker · MCP transport\n└── python worker\n    ├── files/       glob · grep(rg) · bash · cwd      (EXTRACTED from core)\n    ├── sightline/   explore · inspect · validate · Godot probe   (+ NEW MCP wrapper)\n    ├── codemagic/   index · query · context · diff · analyze      (vendored, ~works)\n    └── router →     unified {status, summary, artifacts, evidence_handles, follow_ups}\n```\n\n## Consequences / migration\n- **Core de-coding:** removing MCPCodeTools from core means today's in-process minerva_file_*/bash disappear from a vanilla install. Provide a clear \"install the Code Tools extension to enable coding-agent features\" path; existing users relying on these get a migration notice. Decide hard-removal vs one-release deprecation shim.\n- **bash + terminal PTY:** out-of-process bash cannot drive Minerva's visible terminal directly (today minerva_bash runs in the UI PTY with headless fallback). Add an OPTIONAL host capability (e.g. host.terminal.exec) so the plugin routes bash through the UI terminal when present, headless otherwise. Core needs no change to keep working since the plugin is optional.\n- **sightline_probe removal blast radius:** referenced by several src/test tests; Sightline already ships its own probe (plugins/godot/probe/addons/sightline_probe/), so the capability survives. Repoint or relocate those tests.\n- **Tests move with the code:** tests covering file primitives, sightline_probe parsing/capture, and code-graph behavior MOVE to the extension; Minerva keeps tests only for what remains in core (doc tools, plugin host, manifest parsing).\n\n## Phasing\n- **P1 — Scaffold + Code Magic.** codetools plugin (Go shim + embedded Python), MCP router + unified envelope; vendor Code Magic, re-namespace to minerva_codetools_*, ship visualizer as godot_scene panel. (Fastest win — already MCP.)\n- **P2 — Extract file primitives.** Reimplement glob/grep/bash/cwd in the plugin (grep via bundled rg); remove core MCPCodeTools + move its tests; add optional host.terminal.exec.\n- **P3 — Sightline MCP + probe consolidation.** Wrap explore/inspect/validate as MCP tools (feature-gate visual layer); remove Minerva src/addons/sightline_probe; relocate/repoint tests.\n- **P4 — Unify + workflows + release.** Routing rules, dependency/staleness checks, combined flows (Godot warning → explainability context); marketplace release with release_targets feature-gated per platform.\n\n## Open questions\n- **Repo:** own repo vs minerva-plugins/codetools (default: minerva-plugins for marketplace consistency; own-repo argues better since it vendors two external PoCs and is a large product in its own right).\n- Plugin id / display name (\"codetools\" vs \"Code Tools\").\n- Hard removal vs deprecation shim for the core file tools.\n- host.terminal.exec capability shape, and whether bash is in v1 or deferred to P2+.\n\n## Relationships\n- SUBSUMES (close as superseded): 019d2dc0, 019e0f60.\n- CONSUMES (experiments, still tracked separately): 019d2dbf (Sightline MVP), 019e097b (Code Magic graph), 019e0f605bd5 (Code Magic explainability API).","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:19:05","updated_at":"2026-06-04T19:01:56","priority":2,"tags":["code-magic","codetools","coding-agent","extraction","mcp","plugin","sightline"],"resolution":"SHIPPED 2026-06-04. Code Tools extracted from Minerva core into the optional codetools marketplace plugin, codetools-v0.2.0 published (all 3 platform targets). Minerva core is a notes/chat app by default (253 tools, no glob/grep/bash/cwd, no-bleed CI guard) and becomes a coding agent only when codetools is installed (18 tools + 3 install-seeded skills + code-visualizer panel + code-probe runtime inspection). P0-P4 all complete. Live-validated end-to-end incl. Option C probe HITL. Open follow-ups tracked under this DCR: naming rename 019e93c4c402, remove-probe revert bug 019e93d8f174, Go host-request client 019e8f811497."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b862fa37e589accdb969a5e64de","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P0 — Clean baseline before Code Tools build","description":"Base: minerva-plugins + Minerva working trees.\n\nPrerequisite to P1. Establish clean, known starting points:\n1. Land/push the parked commits — minerva-plugins c4780c7 (plugin developer docs) + 6a0aca3 (per-plugin release_targets); Minerva 21c7f2ff (MCP loopback bind). Decide push targets.\n2. Get Minerva off the `.invalid` branch onto a known base.\n3. `git fetch --tags` in minerva-plugins (local clone was missing cad-v0.1.2 / presentation-v0.0.3 — caused a regen version downgrade earlier).\n4. Confirm vendoring = snapshot @ pinned SHA (locked).\n\nDoD: both repos on known branches, no parked uncommitted work, tags fetched, CI green on both mains.\n\nDISCOVERY / refs: DCR 019e7b6609 (comment 34 = execution playbook, comment 35 = skills + test decision). Plugin API docs: ~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md + PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md. Memory: project_codetools_extraction.md.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:12","updated_at":"2026-05-31T18:02:53","priority":2,"tags":["baseline","codetools","p0"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b8650a7700e917e212cbc651fe2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1 — Code Tools plugin scaffold + code-visualizer (substrate)","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools (new dir), branch from main after P0.\n\nSubstrate-first phase. Grandchildren P1.1-P1.4 run ONE AT A TIME (chained).\n\nNAMES (locked 2026-05-31): code-visualizer = was 'Code Magic' (semantic code-graph + explainability MCP tools + Godot visualizer panel). code-probe = was 'Sightline' (runtime inspection / Godot debugger+output evidence + validation) — arrives in P3.\n\nLOCATIONS (discovery anchor):\n- codetools plugin -> ~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools (NEW; marketplace-installed like cad/scansort/presentation).\n- code-visualizer experiment source -> ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic (Python + SQLite + working 10-tool stdio MCP + Godot viz; ZERO tests today).\n- code-probe experiment source -> ~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline (Python CLI, NO MCP yet; 47 no-mock tests).\n- functional harness to EXTEND -> Minerva src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd.\n- plugin API docs -> ~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md + PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md.\n\nRuntime: Go shim + embedded CPython (cad pattern). Tools namespaced minerva_codetools_*.\nGate B (phase close): extended scripts/smoke/mcp_smoke.py + combined functionals + phase-level cold-Opus DRY check.\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609 comments 34 (playbook) / 35 (skills+test).","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:20","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:27:55","priority":2,"tags":["code-visualizer","codetools","p1","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871","resolution":"P1 substrate complete: all grandchildren done — P1.1 (skeleton: manifest+Go shim+embedded CPython+MCP+harness+CI), P1.2 (envelope+router), P1.3 (vendored code-visualizer @9cc9403 + envelope adapters + real-fixture functionals), P1.4 (godot_scene panel shipped + HITL render gate passed on Linux, real rich-panel graph). codetools v0.1.0 released; smoke tools=11 (verified 2026-06-03)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b86649e79cc92eda6ab00024e34","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2 — Extract file primitives (glob/grep/bash/cwd) into Code Tools","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools after P1; coordinated removal in Minerva core, branch from main after P1.\n\nReimplement glob/grep/bash/cwd in the plugin worker `files/` (grep via bundled rg). REMOVE src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/ + Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCodeTools.gd from core. minerva_doc_* STAYS in core (buffer/editor-coupled). Decide hard-removal vs one-release deprecation shim (DCR open question). Add optional host capability host.terminal.exec so plugin bash can route through the UI terminal PTY (headless otherwise).\n\nMOVE the affected core tests out with the code (file-primitive coverage in test_plugin_*).\n\nDoD per grandchild: no-stub functionals vs the real plugin; coverage >= threshold; cold-Opus panel; FULL regression green — INCLUDING that core Minerva still works fully WITHOUT codetools installed (it is optional).\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:25","updated_at":"2026-06-03T22:11:40","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","extraction","files","p2"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b867e547890a0d5150fe74c163d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3 — code-probe MCP wrapper + probe consolidation","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools after P2. Vendor code-probe from ~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline @ pinned SHA (snapshot, VENDORING.md, no local edits).\n\ncode-probe is CLI-only today (NO MCP) -> build the MCP wrapper for explore/inspect/validate; feature-gate the Linux/X11 visual capture (debugger/output JSON capture is cross-platform). Make it the CANONICAL runtime-inspection provider: REMOVE Minerva src/addons/sightline_probe (code-probe ships its own probe at plugins/godot/probe/addons/sightline_probe/); repoint/relocate the ~4 tests referencing it (test_plugin_definition + others). Collapse code-probe's own search/explore into the shared files/ (DRY).\n\nTESTS (Godot probe): Option B (real captured debugger_state.json artifacts replayed) = the Layer-1 gate + Option C (human live-editor at release, refreshes artifacts); Option A (headless Godot in CI) DEFERRED. Schema-version guard (sightline.godot.editor_probe_state.v4): fixture schema_version must == current probe schema or build fails.\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609 comment 35.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:32","updated_at":"2026-06-04T01:19:13","priority":2,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","p3","runtime-inspection"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b8699ce7f4c86d465e48691971a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4 — Unify (router/envelope/dep-checks), skills, workflows, marketplace release","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools after P3. Substrate frozen -> may relax to parallel grandchildren here.\n\n- Routing rules across files/ + code-visualizer + code-probe; dependency/staleness checks (subsystem present? index/probe stale?).\n- SKILLS (3, seeded-on-install via codetools/manifest.json skills[]): (1) \"Understand this codebase / change\" (code-visualizer: query/get_context/get_diff/analyze + viz panel); (2) \"Navigate & edit code\" (files/ + core minerva_doc_*) = RELOCATION of the master.dct \"File and Code Tools\" skill; (3) \"Inspect & validate at runtime\" (code-probe). ALSO split the master.dct \"File and Code Tools\" skill -> a slim core \"buffer editing\" skill (minerva_doc_* only). Guard: test_plugin_skill_seeder / test_plugin_skills_manifest stay green.\n- Combined workflows (Godot warning -> code-visualizer explainability context -> edit -> code-probe verify).\n- Marketplace release: codetools/manifest.json release_targets (feature-gate platform-limited code-probe visual capture), registry entry via regen_registry.py.\n\nGate D (pre-release): full functionals across release_targets; marketplace install->start functional EXTENDED to codetools (pins the ERR_CANT_CONNECT-after-install RCA); Layer-2 live-Godot-probe; converge-DRY item closed.\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609 comments 34/35.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:39","updated_at":"2026-06-04T19:01:18","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","p4","release","skills","workflows"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871","resolution":"All 5 grandchildren done: P4.2 skills, P4.3 unify+dep-staleness, P4.4 workflows, DRY-debt (Gate D), P4.5 marketplace release (codetools-v0.2.0 published, all 3 targets). Gate D satisfied. Plugin live in the marketplace."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b86ab7b7c0496ca075c1da75b37","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Converge duplicate fs/search onto shared files/ (DRY) — gates release","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools.\n\nVendoring as-is (P1 code-visualizer, P3 code-probe) TEMPORARILY duplicates file-walking/search across three places: the shared files/ layer, code-visualizer's indexer, and code-probe's explore. This item converges them onto the single shared files/ implementation. Tracked explicitly so the speed shortcut does not become permanent duplication.\n\nBLOCKS the P4 marketplace release (Gate D): release must not ship with the duplication outstanding.\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609 comment 34 (vendor-as-is-then-converge rationale).","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:43","updated_at":"2026-06-04T17:29:06","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","dry","tech-debt"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871","resolution":"Both halves resolved (Minerva development a28a3ac0). In-tree test-fixture duplication (comment 412) extracted into marketplace_test_helpers.gd; fs/search convergence (comment 417) closed as rubric-justified divergence (rg binary already shared; vendored is hermetic). Gate-D satisfied — no longer blocks P4.5."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b86e45e70ffbe3a670c34e7dda0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.1 — Substrate skeleton: manifest + Go shim + embedded Python + MCP round-trip + functional harness + CI","description":"Base: branch from minerva-plugins/main at tip after P0. Model: OPUS (design-bearing — sets shared abstractions).\n\nBuild ONLY the shared skeleton (no subsystem logic): codetools/manifest.json; Go shim; embedded CPython bundle (cad pattern: go:embed PBS CPython, extract to DataDir/runtime/<ver>); stdio MCP transport exposing a single `ping`/health tool; the FUNCTIONAL-TEST HARNESS by EXTENDING Minerva's src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd to codetools; and the CI workflow (go test + worker pytest + embedded-python-spawn) mirroring minerva-plugins/.github/workflows/cad.yml.\n\nGate A / DoD: codetools installs from a local manifest via the REAL MarketplaceClient + PluginManager and `ping` round-trips over stdio MCP; harness drives the REAL built binary (NO stubs); coverage of new real code >= threshold; CI green; cold-Opus panel (correctness / DRY+scope / contract, per ~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md); full existing plugin regression suite still green.\n\nThis is THE substrate — every later item must introduce NO new grep/fs/config/envelope; it ports to CONSUME this.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:54:58","updated_at":"2026-05-31T18:01:45","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","p1","scaffold","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8650a7700e917e212cbc651fe2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b86f2a67f13aa20f9ab6bba0e7b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.2 — Unified result envelope + router","description":"Base: branch from minerva-plugins/main after P1.1 merges. Model: OPUS (design-bearing).\n\nImplement the unified result envelope {status, summary, artifacts, evidence_handles, follow_ups} and the router that every subsystem (files/, code-visualizer, code-probe) dispatches through. Wire one trivial routed tool to prove the path.\n\nGate A / DoD: a routed tool returns the envelope; functional asserts the envelope shape against the REAL binary (no stubs); router coverage >= threshold; cold-Opus panel; regression green. No subsystem logic yet.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:55:02","updated_at":"2026-05-31T18:01:49","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","p1","router","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8650a7700e917e212cbc651fe2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b870f1570cbbb2ac8ea135d379b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.3 — Vendor code-visualizer behind router (@pinned SHA) + first real-fixture functionals","description":"Base: branch from minerva-plugins/main after P1.2. Vendor code-visualizer from ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic — pin HEAD at vendor time (reference HEAD as of filing 2026-05-31: 9cc9403). Snapshot into codetools worker (vendored/, VENDORING.md records the SHA, NO local edits — patches tracked separately). Model: SONNET impl (mechanical vendor + rename), OPUS review; functionals are net-new.\n\nRe-namespace its 10 MCP tools to minerva_codetools_* (query / get_context / get_diff / analyze / ...) and route through the P1.2 envelope.\n\nGate A / DoD: index a REAL fixture Godot project (real .gd/.tscn/.tres) + real SQLite; tools return via the envelope; NO-STUB functionals (code-visualizer ships ZERO tests today -> author them here); coverage >= threshold; cold-Opus panel; regression green.\n\nNOTE: temporary fs/search duplication (its indexer vs shared files/) is tracked by the converge-DRY item, which gates P4 — do not silently leave it.","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:55:09","updated_at":"2026-05-31T21:16:59","priority":2,"tags":["code-visualizer","codetools","p1","vendor"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8650a7700e917e212cbc651fe2","resolution":"Vendored code-magic @9cc9403 + 9 envelope adapters + real-fixture functionals shipped on minerva-plugins/main bec7436 and Minerva/development 52dff7b7. Gate A clear: worker 41/41, install→start→ping→analyze:stats 4/4 end-to-end, cold-Opus 3-lens review = SHIP. Substrate fixes (PYTHONNOUSERSITE + WORKER_PACKAGES vendored) flushed out by P1.3 also shipped. Next: P1.4 godot_scene panel + HITL gate."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b871bd0794bb239b292cada5bb8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.4 — Ship code-visualizer panel as godot_scene","description":"Base: branch from minerva-plugins/main after P1.3. Model: SONNET impl, OPUS review.\n\nShip the code-visualizer Godot visualizer as a ui.panels[] entry kind=godot_scene in codetools/manifest.json (entry_scene + scripts[] whitelist), wired to the worker via the scene-panel broker.\n\nGate A / DoD: the panel registers + opens via the REAL PluginManager (functional smoke, no stubs); cold-Opus panel; regression green (including test_plugin_scene_panel_*).","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:55:12","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:25:46","priority":2,"tags":["code-visualizer","codetools","p1","panel"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8650a7700e917e212cbc651fe2","resolution":"Substrate proven. Code-visualizer shipped as a kind=godot_scene panel, mounted via the REAL PluginManager, rendering real graph data (rich-panel, 133 symbols / 114 edges) in-app on Linux. Owner HITL walkthrough 2026-06-03 confirmed: L1 boundary grid, L2 spatial graph w/ edges, click-to-jump, filter. Formal gates: cold-Opus panel (R2), Gate-A functional 13/0, regression test_plugin_scene_panel_host 38/0, worker get_graph 38/0."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b8804367a7abb2862d4448999e9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Code Tools extraction — names, locations, docket map","created_at":"2026-05-31T00:56:12","updated_at":"2026-05-31T18:02:41","tags":["code-probe","code-visualizer","codetools","dcr","discovery"],"value":"DCR 019e7b6609 (comment 34=exec playbook/gates, 35=skills+probe-test). Names: code-visualizer=was Code Magic (graph+explainability+viz); code-probe=was Sightline (runtime inspection+validation). Plugin id codetools, tools minerva_codetools_*. Locations: plugin -> ~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools (new, optional marketplace); code-visualizer src -> ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic (HEAD 9cc9403); code-probe src -> ~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline (HEAD 8b2baa7); API docs -> ~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md + PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md; functional harness to extend -> Minerva src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd. Phases P0 019e7b862f -> P1 019e7b8650 -> P2 019e7b8664 -> P3 019e7b867e -> P4 019e7b8699; DRY-debt 019e7b86ab gates P4. P1 grandchildren P1.1 019e7b86e4 -> P1.2 019e7b86f2 -> P1.3 019e7b870f -> P1.4 019e7b871b (chained blocks). Memory: project_codetools_extraction.md.","component":"codetools","key":"discovery","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7b91961b7dd0926ff790c42809b2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Code Tools repo + branch workflow","created_at":"2026-05-31T01:06:39","updated_at":"2026-05-31T18:02:46","tags":["branches","codetools","repo","workflow"],"value":"Repo home: minerva-plugins/codetools. NO feature branches — commit directly to integration branch: Minerva=development, minerva-plugins=main. Push as we go (owner-approved). See DCR 019e7b6609 latest comment.","component":"codetools","key":"workflow"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e7f366d9978cc9a59d32712ff029f","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Code Tools extraction — design, decisions, locations & progress (canonical reference)","created_at":"2026-05-31T18:05:33","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:38:26","tags":["code-probe","code-visualizer","codetools","extraction","kb","reference"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871","summary":"Canonical reference for the Code Tools extraction: Minerva is not inherently a coding agent; code-intelligence is extracted into one optional stdio-MCP plugin (codetools). Captures vision, locked decisions, names, source/plugin locations, workflow, and phase progress. As of 2026-06-03: P1 substrate COMPLETE; P2 (extract file primitives, HARD removal) is next.","article":"Minerva is a note-taking + LLM-chat app and is NOT inherently a coding agent — most use cases work with zero code-intelligence. All code-intelligence is being EXTRACTED FROM core into a single OPTIONAL stdio-MCP plugin, \"Code Tools\", that turns Minerva into a coding agent only when installed.\n\nCode Tools unifies three subsystems behind one router + result envelope:\n- **files**: glob/grep/bash/cwd — extracted out of core (`src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/` + `MCPCodeTools.gd`). `minerva_doc_*` STAYS in core (buffer/editor-coupled).\n- **sightline**: runtime inspection + Godot debugger/output capture — gets the MCP wrapper it currently lacks. Becomes canonical: Minerva's duplicate `src/addons/sightline_probe` is to be REMOVED (use Sightline, don't duplicate).\n- **code_magic**: semantic graph + explainability (already speaks stdio MCP); visualizer ships as a `godot_scene` panel.\n\nLocked decisions: ONE unified plugin (Sightline visual capture feature-gated, Linux-first); OPTIONAL not bundled (core must not depend on it); runtime = Go shim + embedded CPython (cad pattern, won on the reliability-first code rubric); tools namespaced `minerva_codetools_*`. Envelope `data`-field question RESOLVED (DCR comment 386: typed artifacts win, NO `data` field; each artifact carries a required `type` discriminator). File-access no-bleed contract = DCR comment 410 (agent file tools live in the sidecar; only the generic platform stays in core; boundary test + 5 clauses + CI guard).\n\nSource experiments: Sightline at `~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline` (Python CLI, no MCP yet), Code Magic at `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic` (Python + SQLite + working 10-tool MCP + Godot viz).\n\n**Why:** shapes nearly every plugin/architecture decision — Minerva's scope is deliberately narrow, code features are an add-on.\n**How to apply:** don't add code-intelligence to core; route it through the Code Tools extension. See docket DCR `019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871` (subsumes `019d2dc0` + `019e0f60`).\n\n## Names, locations & item map (filed 2026-05-31; progress 2026-06-03)\n\n**Names (locked):** `code-visualizer` = was \"Code Magic\" (semantic code-graph + explainability MCP tools + Godot visualizer panel). `code-probe` = was \"Sightline\" (runtime inspection: Godot debugger/output evidence via a probe + validation). Plugin id: `codetools`; tools namespaced `minerva_codetools_*`.\n\n**Locations (discovery):**\n- codetools plugin → `~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools` (NEW; marketplace-installed, optional).\n- code-visualizer source → `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/code-magic` (HEAD 9cc9403 at vendor time; Python + SQLite + working 10-tool stdio MCP + Godot viz; ZERO tests).\n- code-probe source → `~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/sightline` (HEAD 8b2baa7; Python CLI, NO MCP yet; 47 no-mock tests).\n- Plugin API docs → `~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md` + `PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md`.\n- Functional harness to EXTEND → Minerva `src/test/marketplace_test_helpers.gd`.\n\n**Workflow (locked 2026-05-31):** Repo home = `minerva-plugins/codetools`. NO feature branches — commit directly to the integration branch per repo: **Minerva → `development`**, **minerva-plugins → `main`**. Push as we go (owner-approved). The \"branch from main\" text in P1.x item descriptions is SUPERSEDED.\n\n**Progress (2026-06-03):** **P1 substrate COMPLETE** — `019e7b8650` closed. P0 done (clean baseline). P1.1 done — plugin substrate (Go shim + embedded CPython cad-pattern + stdio MCP + `minerva_codetools_ping`); CI green all 3 targets; `codetools-v0.1.0` released. P1.2 done — unified envelope `{status,summary,artifacts,evidence_handles,follow_ups,[error]}` + router (`envelope.py`/`router.py`/`errors.py`); artifacts require a `type` discriminator. P1.3 done — vendored code-visualizer (code-magic @9cc9403) under `codetools/worker/vendored/` + 9 envelope adapters + 22 real-fixture functionals (real Godot project + real SQLite). **P1.4 done** — code-visualizer ships as a `godot_scene` panel (`codetools/ui/code_graph/`, class_name-free, relative preloads) wired to the worker via `minerva_codetools_get_graph`; **HITL render gate PASSED on Linux 2026-06-03** (renders the real rich-panel graph, 133 symbols / 114 edges). Fixes landed: `261c6a7` (panel reparent crash + runtime self-heal on bundle-sha mismatch), `b24f0a9` (get_graph surfaces `project_name`). Gotcha: for an in-place install the panel reads its DB from the PLUGIN SOURCE DIR `~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools/code_visualizer.db` (hint `019e8edf`). P1.4 surfaced 3 BACKLOG follow-ups (under DCR): real entry-point UX `019e8f2489`, responsive panel `019e8f2282`, visual legibility `019e8f29a2`. **P2 `019e7b8664` is NEXT (open)** — extract file primitives (glob/grep/bash/cwd) out of core. Owner decided HARD removal (no deprecation shim). Chained grandchildren: P2.1 `019e8f306e` (reimplement in the worker `files/`, grep via bundled rg, `minerva_codetools_*`), P2.2 `019e8f3098` (host capability `host.terminal.exec` → plugin bash through UI terminal PTY), P2.3 `019e8f30d2` (HARD-remove core CodeTools + MCPCodeTools.gd + instantiation; move tests out; re-scope \"File and Code Tools\" skill; add no-bleed boundary test + CI guard; verify core works without codetools). Build P2.1+P2.2 BEFORE P2.3. Dev note: worker `.py` changes need a bundle rebuild for Tier-1 (`bash scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh codetools <triple>`); the Go runtime self-heals on bundle-sha mismatch (261c6a7).\n\n**Docket item map (minerva project):** DCR `019e7b6609` (comment 34 = execution playbook/gates; 35 = skills + Godot-probe test decision; 36 = workflow/repo; 386 = envelope data-field RESOLVED; 410 = file-access no-bleed contract). Phases: P0 `019e7b862f` ✅, **P1 `019e7b8650` ✅**, **P2 `019e7b8664` ← next**, P3 `019e7b867e`, P4 `019e7b8699`; DRY-debt (gates P4) `019e7b86ab`; generic plugin-config mechanism `019e8af5` (`019e8a27` re-scoped onto it). P1 grandchildren ✅: P1.1 `019e7b86e4` → P1.2 `019e7b86f2` → P1.3 `019e7b870f` → P1.4 `019e7b871b`. P2 grandchildren (chained): P2.1 `019e8f306e` → P2.2 `019e8f3098` → P2.3 `019e8f30d2`. P1.4 follow-ups: `019e8f2489`/`019e8f2282`/`019e8f29a2`. Related hints: discovery map `019e7b8804`, repo+branch workflow `019e7b9196`, runtime self-heal `019e8b3a`, panel DB path `019e8edf`, get_graph project_name `019e8e43`, Level-0 splash `019e8e4a`. Rolling-wave: P3–P4 stay coarse until their predecessor nears done.\n\n---\n*Source: Claude auto-memory `project_active_codetools_extraction.md`. All docket items live in the `minerva` project.*"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e809f77447f87a0af342b8e8b3efb","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Support printing & PDFs in Minerva (generation · view/notes · export · print)","description":"## Context & vision\n\nMinerva can neither generate PDFs programmatically nor print. This DCR adds a **document-output subsystem** as first-class, broker-gated host capabilities so that *any* plugin (and the host) gets wide PDF + print support, instead of each plugin embedding its own PDF stack. It was scoped out of a concrete goal — a duplex name-tag maker — and deliberately generalized.\n\nOrigin goal (separate, standalone): port `~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/NameTagMaker` (Python fpdf2/pandas script that turns an .xlsx of attendees into a duplex-printable cardstock PDF) into a Minerva marketplace plugin. Discovery: Minerva already imports .xlsx natively (`ExcelHandler.import_xlsx` + `minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path=…)`), so the plugin's only real need is **PDF generation** — which belongs in the substrate, not the plugin.\n\n## The document-output trio (+ webview view)\n\n| Capability | Altitude | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `host.pdf.*` | *build* (wrapped lib / sidecar) | precise generated docs — point-level control (crop marks, duplex registration offsets) |\n| `host.export.pdf` | render a *view* (webview print-to-PDF) | \"export what I see\" — easy, lower precision; extends existing `host.editors.export(png)` |\n| `host.print` | spool a PDF | send to printer (CUPS `lp -o sides=two-sided-long-edge` / Win spooler) |\n| (webview) | render/view | the unifying thread: preview, pageable PDF-notes, and HTML→PDF export all ride CEF/PDF.js |\n\n## Decisions (locked — driven by the project rubric: reliability → durability → cost → readability → DRY → well-factored)\n\n1. **`host.pdf` is the ONE generator.** Plugins call it; they do not embed their own PDF lib. (DRY.)\n2. **Generation backend = Go + `go-pdf/fpdf` as a bundled MCP-service sidecar** (Cobrowser/Go-backend pattern), NOT an in-process GDExtension. Rationale: PDF gen is non-realtime, stateless, touches no scene tree — so it does not need to be in-process; a static binary wins reliability+durability, and gofpdf is the highest-level fpdf analog (least port code). Decision gated on the **pixel-diff acceptance** (P1.1): render a reference PDF from the original Python script and diff; if a font/DPI detail won't port, fall back to embedded-Python reuse of fpdf2.\n3. **View/preview/notes/export ride the existing webview** (godot_cef preferred, godot_wry fallback) — NOT a new native rasterizer. PDFium is a documented non-goal unless we ever reject the webview for notes.\n4. **No clone.** Wrap/reuse mature libraries; never reimplement a PDF engine from scratch (reliability/durability sink).\n5. **Each capability is independently shippable and valuable**; the DCR is declarable \"done enough\" after P1+P2. Print (P4) is opt-in — \"open in system PDF app\" is the v1 floor.\n\n## Rubric guardrails (the four ways this DCR could *break* the rubric — each child checks against these in its DoD)\n\n1. Plugins rolling their own PDF → **DRY break.** Guard: host.pdf is the one generator; the nametag plugin's path is `blocked_by P1.1` precisely so no throwaway embedded copy is built.\n2. Reaching for PDFium / cloning a lib → **durability+reliability break.** Guard: webview for view; wrapped/sidecar mature lib for generation.\n3. Silent CEF↔WRY divergence → **reliability break.** Guard: capability-probe and degrade visibly; never promise uniform behavior we can't deliver.\n4. Over-investing P4 Windows spooling → **cost break.** Guard: system-PDF-app is the floor; direct spool is opt-in.\n\n## Non-goals (v1)\nPDF editing, annotation/markup, form-filling, OCR, e-signatures, redaction tooling, a native PDFium rasterizer.\n\n## Architecture\n```\nhost.pdf.*      → Go gofpdf sidecar (stdio/MCP), broker-gated, audited\nhost.export.pdf → webview print-to-PDF (CEF PrintToPDF / WRY+PDF.js)\nhost.print      → OS shim (CUPS lp / Win spooler), dialog + direct modes\nPDF view/notes  → webview viewer (Chromium built-in / PDF.js), read-only, paged\n```\n\n## Phasing (value descending / risk ascending; each phase = a child work_item)\n- **P1 — Precise PDF generation (host.pdf.*)** — what the nametag goal needs NOW. Critical path: P1.0 (contract) → P1.1 (MVP). [high value / low risk]\n- **P2 — View & preview (webview)** — pageable read-only PDF incl. PDF-as-note; shares webview with preview + export. [high value / low–med risk]\n- **P3 — Export a view to PDF (host.export.pdf)** — cheap once the webview path exists. [med / low]\n- **P4 — Print (host.print)** — Windows spooling is the gnarly bit; system-PDF-app covers v1. [lower now / high risk] OPT-IN.\n\nDependencies: P4 needs a PDF from P1 or P2; P2/P3 share webview infra. Otherwise loosely coupled.\n\n## Consumers / relationships\n- **Nametag-maker plugin** — a STANDALONE marketplace plugin (its own work_item, NOT a child of this DCR), `blocked_by` P1.1 (only its final render step). Doubles as P1's real-world acceptance proof. Repo: `minerva-plugins`.\n\n## Open questions\n- `host.pdf.*` exact verb surface (settled in P1.0 contract).\n- gofpdf vs embedded-Python — resolved by P1.1 pixel-diff gate.\n- host.print direct-mode option shape on Windows.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:39:54","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:42:13","priority":2,"tags":["document-output","host-capability","pdf","plugin","printing","substrate","webview"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P1 · Precise PDF generation (host.pdf.*)","description":"Deliver `host.pdf.*`: precise programmatic PDF generation as a broker-gated host capability, backed by a **Go `go-pdf/fpdf` bundled MCP-service sidecar** (not in-process). Highest value / lowest risk phase; it is what the nametag goal needs first.\n\nGrandchildren:\n- **P1.0** — define the `host.pdf.*` capability contract (design-only; the API both the sidecar and the plugin code against). UNBLOCKS parallel work.\n- **P1.1** — host.pdf MVP: sidecar + the draw primitives the nametag renderer needs + bundled DejaVu fonts + smoke test + the gofpdf-vs-Python pixel-diff acceptance gate. ← the nametag plugin's hard blocker (last mile only).\n- **P1.2** — broker wiring + audit redaction + permissions/grants.\n- **P1.3** — richer draw primitives beyond nametag needs.\n- **P1.4** — plugin-guide section + worked example.\n\nDoD (cross-cutting): broker gating + audit redaction + plugin-guide + tests; base SHA recorded per task; checks against the DCR's four rubric guardrails.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:10","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:10","priority":2,"tags":["critical-path","generation","gofpdf","host-capability","pdf"],"parent":"minerva:019e809f77447f87a0af342b8e8b3efb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e809fc8887d6fb48c9754ef42791e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P2 · View & preview (webview) — pageable read-only PDF + PDF-as-note","description":"Stub (file detail when P2 starts). Reuse the existing godot_cef/godot_wry webview to view PDFs read-only and paged — no new native rasterizer (PDFium is a DCR non-goal). Scope: (a) pageable read-only PDF viewer panel; (b) a \"PDF attachment\" note kind (blob store, read-only, serialize to note/project); (c) inline-in-note next/prev pager UX (the real cost driver); (d) CEF↔WRY split handling (degrade visibly). High reuse: same webview powers nametag preview and host.export.pdf. T-shirt: M.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:15","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:15","priority":3,"tags":["cef","notes","pdf","viewer","webview","wry"],"parent":"minerva:019e809f77447f87a0af342b8e8b3efb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e809fd8fc761c86d14cf4b8cd520a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P3 · Export a view to PDF (host.export.pdf)","description":"Stub (file detail when P3 starts). Add `format:\"pdf\"` to the existing `host.editors.export` capability, implemented via the webview's print-to-PDF (CEF `PrintToPDF`; WRY platform-dependent). Plus paged-media / print-CSS conventions + a plugin-guide entry. Depends on the P2 webview investment. T-shirt: S–M.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:19","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:19","priority":3,"tags":["export","host-capability","pdf","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019e809f77447f87a0af342b8e8b3efb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e809fe9a37692a7023de946b5cacd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P4 · Print (host.print) — OPT-IN","description":"Stub (file detail when P4 starts). `host.print` host capability: list_printers + native print dialog (CUPS + OS dialog), and host.print.direct {printer, sides=two-sided-long-edge, media, copies}. Windows spooling helper is the gnarly item (likely a bundled helper). Plugin-guide + policy/audit. Depends on a PDF from P1 or P2. Cost-guardrail: keep \"open in system PDF app\" as the floor; this is OPT-IN and the DCR is \"done enough\" without it. T-shirt: L (Windows-dominated).","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:24","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:24","priority":4,"tags":["cups","host-capability","opt-in","pdf","printing","windows"],"parent":"minerva:019e809f77447f87a0af342b8e8b3efb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a0293d7a60b8247d69215e3e08","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.0 · Define host.pdf.* capability contract (design-only)","description":"Design-only. Specify the `host.pdf.*` capability surface: verbs, args (exact field names), return envelope ({success,result} per broker convention), units (points), font handle model, image input (PNG bytes/base64), and the bytes-out shape. Must cover at minimum the primitives the nametag renderer needs (see P1.1). Mode: DIRECT + HITL — I draft, owner approves.\n\nWhy it's first: contract-first is both the parallelism enabler (plugin builds its non-render 90% against the frozen API while P1.1 implements it) and a scope-creep control (the surface can't widen mid-build).\n\nDoD: a frozen contract doc; owner sign-off; referenced by P1.1 and the nametag plugin work_item.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:40","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:59:44","priority":1,"tags":["contract","critical-path","design","host-capability","pdf"],"parent":"minerva:019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a","resolution":"host.pdf.* contract drafted, rubric-checked, owner-signed-off, and frozen at Docs/design/host_pdf_contract.md. Single stateless verb host.pdf.generate(doc). Unblocks P1.1 + nametag plugin."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a0596b79ff89c2445e34f87328","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.1 · host.pdf MVP — sidecar + nametag primitives + bundled fonts + smoke test","description":"Implement the P1.0 contract as a **Go `go-pdf/fpdf` bundled MCP-service sidecar**. Start from existing Go-backend scaffolding (presentation / codetools shim) and the existing capability-broker pattern — reuse, don't reinvent (DRY guardrail).\n\nAcceptance = exactly the primitives the existing `TagRenderer` needs, nothing more (richer primitives are P1.3 — scope fence):\n- `new_doc`\n- `add_page(Letter, unit=pt)`\n- `set_font(bundled TTF, regular/bold, size)` — DejaVuSans/-Bold BUNDLED with the sidecar (the original script's hardcoded /usr/share/fonts path is the one guaranteed cross-platform crash)\n- `draw_text(x, y, align)`\n- `draw_image(png, x, y, w)`\n- `draw_line` · `draw_rect`\n- `output → bytes` (base64)\n\nCrop marks, duplex column-reversal, and registration offsets are coordinate math that lives in the *plugin*, not here — keeps P1.1 minimal.\n\n**Pixel-diff acceptance gate (load-bearing):** render a reference PDF from the original `~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/NameTagMaker/generate_tags.py`, port the same draws through host.pdf, and diff. Clean → gofpdf confirmed. If an fpdf2-specific detail (font kerning, PNG DPI) won't reproduce → fall back to embedded-Python reuse of fpdf2 (decision recorded here).\n\nDoD: smoke test (emit + parse-validate each primitive); pixel-diff gate passed (or fallback recorded); base SHA recorded; broker gating deferred to P1.2 acceptable for MVP if behind a dev flag. Repo: Minerva. UNBLOCKS the nametag plugin's last mile.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:52","updated_at":"2026-06-01T01:38:18","priority":1,"tags":["critical-path","gofpdf","host-capability","mvp","pdf","pixel-diff-gate","sidecar"],"parent":"minerva:019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a","resolution":"DoD met. Owner accepted the pixel-diff gate. Pure-Go go-pdf/fpdf sidecar at src/sidecars/host_pdf/ implements host.pdf.generate per the frozen contract. Smoke + white-box tests 12/12 green. Pixel-diff gate vs the original generate_tags.py: overall mean-abs 0.0255, max 65/255, 0.048% pixels differ (antialiasing-only, no structural shift); string widths match fpdf2<->gofpdf to 3 decimals. gofpdf CONFIRMED — no embedded-Python fallback. Commits on pdf-print-substrate: 7b856a67 (sidecar+fonts+tests), a06432a3 (gate). Base c4f9e581. Broker wiring deferred to P1.2 (per scope). Unblocks the nametag plugin's last mile."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a068547b059eadb28458107ce6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1.2 · Broker wiring + audit redaction + permissions","description":"Stub. Promote the P1.1 dev-flag MVP into a proper broker-gated capability: deny-by-default grant in permissions.host_capabilities, audit logging with redaction (strip image bytes / output bytes), policy entry. Plugin-guide capability-table row.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:40:56","updated_at":"2026-06-01T03:09:13","priority":2,"tags":["audit","broker","host-capability","pdf","permissions"],"parent":"minerva:019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a","resolution":"DoD met: host.pdf.generate is broker-gated (deny-by-default, capability_not_granted tested), audit logging redacts input+output bytes_b64 to a shape-only pdf_summary (proven by test: no blob leaks), grantable via manifest+policy, and the plugin-guide capability-reference row + spec note + API_COVERAGE row are committed in minerva-plugins (45bbe98). Code: CapabilityBroker.gd _handle_host_pdf_generate + _redact_pdf_args + PluginErrors 4 codes; commit d05d665b on pdf-print-substrate. Tests: test_host_capability_pdf.gd 22 PASS/0 FAIL/1 SKIP; broker regression 82 PASS.\n\nResidual (NOT a DoD gap): literal in-app Minerva→sidecar→PDF round-trip is unproven because --script mode (headless AND windowed, both confirmed) can't fork+exec the SubProcess GDExtension — a test-harness limitation. The broker reuses the EXACT production MCPServerConnection stdio-spawn path that presentation/cad/obs_controller/scansort use successfully (presentation's backend spawned fine in the same windowed run). Literal e2e will be exercised by the first real consumer (nametag plugin 019e80a0f17a) in a true app session — natural validation point, no throwaway probe needed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a0789e7389b0bbfa5cac692643","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P1.3 · Richer draw primitives (beyond nametag needs)","description":"Stub. Catch-all for generation primitives beyond P1.1's enumerated minimum (wrapped text blocks, additional image formats, fill/stroke colors, shapes, links, doc metadata). Driven by actual second/third consumers — do not speculatively over-build (cost guardrail).","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:41:00","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:41:00","priority":3,"tags":["gofpdf","host-capability","pdf","primitives"],"parent":"minerva:019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a0855e723d9275a1fb9f39ef8d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P1.4 · Plugin-guide section + worked example","description":"Stub. Add a host.pdf.* section to ~/github/minerva-plugins/docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md (capability table row + usage snippet), with the nametag plugin cited as the canonical consumer. Mirror the existing capability-doc style.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:41:04","updated_at":"2026-06-01T00:41:04","priority":3,"tags":["docs","host-capability","pdf","plugin-guide"],"parent":"minerva:019e809fb22f756c96a4f3305db1865a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e80a0f17a79fd90caa54e988c039a","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Nametag Maker — standalone marketplace plugin (duplex cardstock PDF from a Minerva spreadsheet)","description":"Port `~/gitlab/minervaservices/experiments/NameTagMaker` into a Minerva marketplace plugin. STANDALONE initiative — not a child of the PDF/printing DCR (019e809f) — connected only by `blocked_by` P1.1. Repo: `minerva-plugins`.\n\n## Buffer pipeline (owner's model)\n1. **xlsx → spreadsheet buffer** — use Minerva's EXISTING native import: `minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(name, file_path=…xlsx)`. No plugin work; editable/reviewable.\n2. **spreadsheet buffer → `.mtags` buffer** — plugin backend reads the sheet via `mcp.proxy:minerva_get_spreadsheet_data` (json), maps columns (Name / Class / Group # / Room Assignment) → tag records, snapshots them into a `.mtags` plugin document. Explicit \"Build/Rebuild from sheet\" (snapshot, not live binding).\n3. **`.mtags` buffer → PDF** — render via `host.pdf.*` (P1.1). Output: Save (file_picker + host.files.write) and \"Print\" = open in system PDF app (v1 floor; adopt host.print/host.export.pdf later with ~no rework).\n\n## Plugin shape\n- **Backend**: thin Go MCP stub — read sheet (mcp.proxy), transform rows, call host.pdf to render. No embedded PDF lib (DRY: host.pdf is the one generator).\n- **Document**: `.mtags` panel-canonical, `save_mode: host_owned` + `capabilities:[host_owned_save, project_state]` (+ project_file serialize/deserialize) so it saves standalone AND rides .minproj. Icon PNG + cached PDF as refcounted blobs.\n- **Panel (HTML + PDF.js)**: \"print-out\" viewer — a sample tag on top, then the generated sheets in infinite-scroll paper-styled view. PDF bytes fetched via minerva.call() (no 64KB cap). Pick-which-sheet + options (back-mode, registration offsets, icon width).\n\n## Required code change regardless of path\nBundle DejaVuSans/-Bold (the original's hardcoded /usr/share/fonts path crashes on mac/Windows). Drop pandas/openpyxl (Minerva imports the xlsx; backend reads structured rows). Icon optional.\n\n## Acceptance\n- Layer-1: install → read sheet → build .mtags → render PDF via host.pdf → save.\n- **HITL (physical):** owner prints the duplex PDF on cardstock and confirms front/back registration + crop-mark cutting. Not automatable — a designed stop condition.\n\n## Blocking\n`blocked_by` P1.1 (019e80a0596b) — ONLY the final render step. The non-PDF ~90% (manifest, .mtags doc, sheet-read, tag model, HTML preview, save/project) builds in parallel against the P1.0 contract.","created_at":"2026-06-01T00:41:31","updated_at":"2026-06-01T08:44:11","priority":2,"tags":["marketplace","minerva-plugins","nametag","pdf","plugin","standalone"],"blocked_by":"019e80a0596b79ff89c2445e34f87328"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e81983ccc7615bc3ed84261c2d416","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-tests/singleton_autoload_warmup","created_at":"2026-06-01T05:11:38","updated_at":"2026-06-01T05:11:38","value":"Standalone functional tests (godot --headless --path src --script test/X.gd) that load CapabilityBroker.gd / PluginManager.gd fail with \"Compile Error: Identifier not found: SingletonObject\" UNLESS they `await process_frame` (or `await get_tree().process_frame`) BEFORE load()-ing the script — autoloads must finish registering first. Pattern is in test_host_capability_pdf.gd. Tests using `const X = preload(...CapabilityBroker.gd)` (e.g. test_plugin_capabilities.gd) CANNOT be fixed this way — preload resolves at compile time before the autoload exists, so they only pass under the real harness/editor, not bare --script. Not a code bug; environmental.","component":"minerva-tests","key":"singleton_autoload_warmup"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8272e70a7bee965326dce4ccacb6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"N1 · Spike: embed CEF WebView + PDF.js preview in a plugin godot_scene panel (de-risk)","description":"LOAD-BEARING DE-RISK before the .mtags document build. Feasibility = FEASIBLE-WITH-CAVEATS, CEF path, NO existing precedent (uncharted). Prove that a plugin godot_scene panel (kind:\"godot_scene\", extends MinervaPluginPanel, loaded via PluginScenePanelHost) can embed a CefTexture WebView (wrapped in SubViewportContainer+SubViewport per src/Scripts/UI/Controls/WebViewEditor/CefWebViewEditor.gd) that renders the existing PDF.js viewer over a `file://` temp HTML and shows a real generated nametag PDF.\n\nScope (minimal): add a godot_scene panel to nametag-maker that, on _on_panel_loaded, generates a sample PDF via nametag_generate (or loads a fixed one), writes the PDF.js viewer HTML to user://, points CEF at it, and shows the rendered page(s). No document model / save yet.\n\nVerify (caveats from the spike): (a) preview renders in-app; (b) CEF once-per-process is fine for open/close/reopen; (c) clean teardown of CefTexture + SubViewport + temp file on _on_panel_unload/_exit_tree; (d) tab switch hide/show doesn't orphan/glitch; (e) input (scroll) inside the preview works (SubViewportContainer scoping). Off-tree constraint: no class_name, preload + ClassDB.instantiate(\"CefTexture\").\n\nHITL STOP: owner opens the panel in Minerva, confirms the embedded PDF.js preview renders and tab open/switch/close is clean. Reference: CefWebViewEditor.gd (texture+SubViewport pattern), WebViewEditor.gd (instantiate/position/teardown), PluginScenePanelHost.gd (mount + hooks), MinervaPluginPanel.gd (lifecycle). Memory: project_godot_cef_input_hook, project_godot_cef_init_once.","created_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:28","updated_at":"2026-06-01T18:55:05","priority":1,"tags":["cef","nametag","panel","spike","ux","webview"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Validated 2026-06-01 (live HITL). (a) CEF webview embeds in a plugin godot_scene panel and renders a PDF on first open. (b) Annotation substrate adopts cleanly: inline AnnotationHost subclass + AnnotationHostRegistry under ctx.editor.tab_title; MCP annotations_add/list/render_overlay all target the panel; on-screen AnnotationOverlay auto-mounts + draws live; render_content_to_image honored. TWO rules → N2: (1) render_content_to_image must RASTERIZE the generated PDF bytes (pdftoppm), the viewport.get_image() crop captures only the dark panel bg not the CEF PDF (hint godot/cef-osr-not-in-parent-viewport-capture). (2) embed must PIN/REUSE one CefTexture (swap url on open) — close→reopen recreated a blank CefTexture (project_godot_cef_init_once). Spike scaffolding (UNCOMMITTED, throwaway): nametag-maker/ui/nametag_editor_panel.gd + NametagEditorPanel.tscn + manifest nametag_editor panel — replace in N2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e82730c677f7b9253add640505230","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"N2 · .mtags preview+annotate panel: rasterized preview + page nav + annotation host + save/load","description":"REVISED 2026-06-01 (UX feedback: feedback_generated_artifact_editors_are_preview_annotate). The editor is a PREVIEW + ANNOTATE surface, NOT a data-entry form. (A form editor was built and DROPPED — wrong artifact.)\n\nPanel shows the RASTERIZED PDF (chosen render path) of the current draft — single tag (front/back) or full sheet — with page/draft navigation + basic zoom, and the annotation substrate overlaid (annotate drafts on top of the rendered tag). Annotations persist via in-doc annotations[] + AnnotationSidecar (sidecar wins on load).\n\n.mtags = the saved PROJECT: bound-spreadsheet ref + generate options + draft state + last-preview ref + annotations. Reopen to keep iterating.\n\nDONE/installed already: host_owned save/load wiring (Editor.save_file_to_disc PLUGIN_SCENE branch → _on_panel_save_request/_on_panel_load_request); manifest save_mode host_owned + file_extensions [.mtags] + editor_items 'New Nametag' + capability host_owned_save; nametag_model.gd (envelope over nametag_generate args + images-as-blobs + annotations); inline AnnotationHost whose render_content_to_image returns the panel-set rasterized page Image (NOT viewport scrape — N1 finding). REBUILD the panel UI: drop the options/rows form; build preview(TextureRect via pdftoppm) + page nav + Refresh + annotation overlay.\n\nHITL: open a .mtags, see the rendered tag, annotate it, Ctrl+S/reopen → annotations + preview persist.","created_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:38","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:33:40","priority":2,"tags":["document","host-owned","nametag","panel","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Preview+annotate .mtags editor: model + host_owned save/load + sidecar round-trip + rasterized preview + annotation host. HITL-verified; committed minerva-plugins 3c850e1."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e827329a87c679144da44d3464701","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"N3 · Generation→preview: MCP preview tool (push) + Refresh-from-sheet (pull) + panel↔backend mechanism","description":"REVISED 2026-06-01: the 'sync' is GENERATION-driven (drafts), not form-edit-driven. Owner chose BOTH push + pull.\n(1) PUSH: an MCP tool (e.g. nametag_preview) the LLM calls to generate a PDF (single tag OR full batch, from data/sheet) and open/update the nametag preview tab showing it — matches 'LLM generates a single preview tag, then the full batch.'\n(2) PULL: a 'Refresh' button in the panel bound to the linked spreadsheet — regenerates the preview from current sheet rows.\n\nBLOCKER to resolve FIRST — the panel↔backend mechanism: how a godot_scene (GDScript) panel triggers the Go backend faces renderer and receives the PDF past the 64KB IPC cap. Candidate: panel writes args to temp files (rows JSON + image PNGs) + small path-driven IPC to a NEW backend handler that writes the PDF to a temp path and returns {path}; panel then pdftoppm temp.pdf page N → Image. Also resolve how an MCP tool opens/updates a SPECIFIC plugin panel + hands it the PDF path. Investigate how presentation's panel calls host.* capabilities (ctx.broker?) for the GDScript-side call pattern.\n\nThen: PDF → pdftoppm page → ImageTexture in panel + host.content_image (vision). Debounce matters only for pull. State: panel-canonical (don't echo back).","created_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:45","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:17:54","priority":2,"tags":["nametag","panel","preview","sync","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e82733fca704fb74a1104f467f3d1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"N4 · Spreadsheet as DATA HOME: LLM populates standard columns; generate/preview from sheet","description":"REVISED 2026-06-01: the spreadsheet is the DATA HOME — the standard editable form the LLM populates from the user's input — CENTRAL to the UX, not a mere import. (Was 'buffer pipeline R2'.)\n\nFlow: user data (doc/xlsx/list) → LLM extracts → Minerva NATIVE spreadsheet with standard nametag columns (title/subtitle/detail fields + back fields), user AND LLM edit it → generate/preview reads the open sheet (minerva_get_spreadsheet_data JSON) → maps columns → tag rows → renders. xlsx import = minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor file_path=…xlsx (native; no plugin work). Column-mapping convention/UI (which column → title/subtitle/which lines) since rosters vary (students vs siblings vs parents differ). The .mtags binds to a sheet (ref, snapshot on generate).\n\nHITL: hand the LLM a roster → it fills a sheet → previews ONE tag → you tweak the sheet → re-preview → 'do the batch' → full sheet renders. (Couples tightly with N3's pull/Refresh.)","created_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:51","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:34:17","priority":3,"tags":["import","nametag","spreadsheet","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Spreadsheet-as-data-home realized via the deterministic nametag_build_from_sheet tool (LLM reads sheet → rows_json + column mapping → maps columns → tag rows → renders preview (first-tag-only option) → writes preview + .mtags). HITL-verified e2e (3-student roster → correct sheet). Committed minerva-plugins 8a18aef. Panel 'Build from sheet button + mapping UI' deliberately NOT built (contradicts preview-not-form UX); sheet→tags is LLM-mediated since the Go backend can't read sheets. 'Refresh-from-sheet' is superseded by re-running build_from_sheet."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e82734ce27c2c99f3c4538d73329c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"N5 · Packaging (R4): cross-compile + tarball + SHA256SUMS + registry.json","description":"Make the plugin marketplace-installable. Per-platform cross-compile of the Go backend (linux-x86_64, linux-arm64, macos-universal, windows-x86_64), tarball (binary + manifest + ui/ scene+scripts + bundled fonts if any + SHA256SUMS), add to registry.json with release_targets. Model after presentation's .github/workflows packaging recipe. Independent of N1-N4 except it packages whatever ships.","created_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:54","updated_at":"2026-06-01T09:10:54","priority":3,"tags":["marketplace","nametag","packaging","r4"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8485c81d74c190e10033f5c89a4d","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"CEF/SubViewport content is invisible to parent viewport.get_image() — rasterize the source for AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image","created_at":"2026-06-01T18:50:20","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["annotations","cef","nametag","plugin","render_content_to_image","subviewport"],"value":"A plugin AnnotationHost.render_content_to_image() that uses the HelloAnnotationHost technique — `_ui_root.get_viewport().get_texture().get_image()` cropped to the panel rect — does NOT capture content rendered by a CefTexture inside a child SubViewport. The CPU-side readback of the PARENT viewport returns only the panel's flat background (the editor's dark theme fill); the CEF OSR texture composited into the SubViewportContainer on-screen is absent. Validated 2026-06-01 in the nametag-maker editor spike: PDF visible on screen, but render_overlay(include_document=true) PNG showed annotations on dark bg, not the PDF. FIX: for a CEF/SubViewport-embedded preview, render_content_to_image should rasterize the SOURCE the preview was built from (here: the generated PDF bytes → pdftoppm page N → load PNG → Image) rather than scraping the viewport. Bonus: deterministic, exact page, and no Chromium viewer chrome (toolbar/thumbnail rail) in the vision image. The annotation-substrate adoption itself works fine for plugin scene panels (host registers under ctx.editor.tab_title; MCP annotations_add/list/render_overlay target it; render_content_to_image is honored — output took the returned image's dims).","component":"godot","key":"cef-osr-not-in-parent-viewport-capture","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84b93e6b7191a0a72b28bb660766","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"godot_scene panel → backend/host call mechanism + 64KB IPC cap + host.pdf.generate returns a path","created_at":"2026-06-01T19:46:33","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["host.pdf.generate","ipc","mechanism","nametag","panel","pdftoppm","plugin"],"value":"A godot_scene plugin panel (extends MinervaPluginPanel) does NOT call methods directly — it emits `request.emit(channel, payload_dict, reply_id)` and awaits `$_MinervaIPC.await_reply(reply_id)`. Channel routing: `\"capability:host.X\"` → CapabilityBroker (PluginScenePanelBroker.handle_scene_request → CapabilityBroker.dispatch); a NON-capability channel (e.g. `\"nametag.render\"`, like hello_scene's `\"hello.greet\"`) → the plugin's OWN Go backend, dispatched by the plugin SDK's IPC message handler (declare in manifest ui.ipc_messages / panel ipc_channels; add the matching Go handler). HARD LIMIT: MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES = 65536 (64 KiB) on BOTH request and reply (PluginScenePanelBroker.gd ~:149, enforced ~:576 → 'payload_too_large'). So NEVER pass PDF/image bytes over IPC — pass path-driven args and return {path}. host.pdf.generate (CapabilityBroker._handle_host_pdf_generate, ~:2193; sidecar via _host_pdf_conn) takes page-ops and RETURNS A PATH, not bytes. minerva_plugin_open_panel (PluginMCPTools.gd ~:618) opens a panel but has NO initial-data injection; pass data by opening on a FILE (associated_object → ctx.file_path) instead. Recommended PDF-preview flow: panel/MCP → Go backend 'nametag.render' (small path-driven args) → backend builds faces page-ops → host.pdf.generate → temp PDF path → panel OS.execute('pdftoppm', …) page N → ImageTexture in a TextureRect (+ set AnnotationHost.content_image for vision). OS.execute is unsandboxed here; pdftoppm/poppler is available (used pre-compaction for montages).","component":"godot","key":"plugin-panel-to-backend-and-pdf-mechanism","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84bb55c4717b81f756b88ce1854d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"N6 · Nametag orchestration skill: chat/file/xlsx → spreadsheet → draft preview → iterate → batch","description":"A skill that teaches the LLM how to use the Minerva spreadsheet + nametag tools TOGETHER to run the canonical nametag workflow (feedback_generated_artifact_editors_are_preview_annotate). Without it, each session improvises the orchestration.\n\nThe skill encodes:\n1. Detect data SOURCE: an input file/xlsx, OR info typed in chat (no list). For xlsx → minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor file_path=…; for chat/doc → create a sheet + populate via update_spreadsheet_data.\n2. Standard nametag COLUMNS (title/subtitle/detail fields + back fields); map the user's data into them; let user + LLM edit the sheet.\n3. Render a SINGLE draft tag (front/back) into the nametag preview panel; ask the user to review/annotate; read annotations (minerva_annotations_list editor_name=…) as feedback.\n4. Iterate the draft on feedback; re-preview.\n5. On approval, render the FULL batch (all sheet rows).\n6. Save the .mtags project (binds the sheet + options + annotations).\n\nOpen question: can a plugin BUNDLE a skill (manifest field), or is it registered via minerva_skill_create / shipped master.dct (project_docket_shipped_vs_user)? Resolve at build. Depends on N2 (panel) + N3 (preview tool) + N4 (sheet-as-data-home) existing so the steps are real. tool_deps: spreadsheet tools + nametag preview/generate + annotations_list.","created_at":"2026-06-01T19:48:50","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:34:21","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","nametag","skill","spreadsheet","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Orchestration skill 'Make name tags from data' created in user master.dct (id 019e851af0bd, active, 11 tools). Encodes: source detection (file/xlsx/chat) → Minerva spreadsheet (standard-column template, adaptable) → build_from_sheet single draft → annotate/iterate (anchored_to 'tag: <name>') → full batch → save PDF. Discoverable via minerva_list_skills. NOTE: lives in the USER runtime docket; to prime all users, promote into the repo master.dct later."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84c2c127716f80eb0c9545d790c1","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Editing a plugin godot_scene .gd: CLOSE the tab before reopening — open_file is idempotent and keeps the old instance","created_at":"2026-06-01T19:56:56","updated_at":"2026-07-06T20:46:48","tags":["godot_scene","idempotent","open_file","panel","plugin","reload"],"value":"After editing a plugin godot_scene panel's GDScript and reinstalling/reloading the plugin, an ALREADY-OPEN tab keeps running the OLD script. minerva_open_file is IDEMPOTENT — if the file is already open it returns the existing tab without re-instantiating, so your edited script never loads (you'll see the previous UI). FIX: minerva_close_editor(editor_name, force=true) THEN minerva_open_file(path) → a fresh instance loads the edited script. Confirmed 2026-06-01: a stale tab showed the old form-editor panel; after close+reopen the rebuilt preview panel loaded and render_content_to_image went null→real image. (Contrast HTML panels, which re-read per open — project_plugin_data_directory.) Sibling of project_mcp_plugin_reload_doesnt_refresh_tools. Quick reinstall+reload-script loop for godot_scene panels: edit .gd → plugin_remove → plugin_install → plugin_start → close the tab → open_file.","component":"godot","key":"plugin-scene-script-reload-needs-tab-close","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84ca5d177f88a1818f909a8c37f3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"N2.5 · Annotation binding: page-anchored + fit-to-pane + layout-agnostic transform + semantic tag anchor","description":"PROBLEM (owner, 2026-06-01): annotations don't bind to the PDF — they draw in overlay space, so scrolling/resizing drifts them off the tag; the LLM/user loses the referent.\n\nROOT CAUSE (substrate gap, not just the plugin): core AnnotationOverlay._draw() HARDCODES Transform2D.IDENTITY + zoom 1.0 (AnnotationOverlay.gd ~:76-84) and never calls host.transform_*; it redraws only on annotations_changed/selection/tool, NOT on scroll/resize. So no host can track a moving/scaled view (CAD/PCB don't either). AnnotationRenderContext.to_screen = transform * p (AnnotationRenderContext.gd ~:86).\n\nFIX — backward-compatible CORE change (Minerva repo, pdf-print-substrate):\n- AnnotationHost base: add get_annotation_view_transform()->Transform2D (default IDENTITY), get_annotation_zoom()->float (default 1.0), signal view_changed.\n- AnnotationOverlay: build ctx with host transform+zoom (has_method-guarded → existing hosts unchanged); connect self.resized + host.view_changed → queue_redraw.\n- Run annotation test suites (test_annotation_*, annotations_v2/*) after.\n\nPLUGIN (nametag editor):\n- Store annotations in PAGE-pixel coords (resolution-independent → render_overlay/vision already correct).\n- Host.get_annotation_view_transform(): from the LIVE preview TextureRect rect + fit scale (fit-to-pane, owner's choice) → maps page px ↔ overlay px; recompute each draw (LAYOUT-AGNOSTIC, works 1×1..3×2, varying col width — project_editor_pane_grid_is_variable). transform_screen_to_doc inverse for authoring.\n- Panel: fit-to-pane render (scale page to live preview rect), recompute on resized, emit view_changed.\n- describe_point(page_pt) → Avery 5395 8-up cell → row title → anchored_to \"tag: <name>\" (semantic; works for MCP-added AND on-screen-authored via _stamp_anchor). Page tag-grid is fixed in page space, distinct from the variable pane grid.\n\nHITL: open .mtags, marks sit ON the tag and STAY on it when the pane is resized / layout changed; minerva_annotations_list shows anchored_to \"tag: <name>\".","created_at":"2026-06-01T20:05:15","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:33:44","priority":1,"tags":["annotations","core","nametag","substrate","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Annotations bind spatially (fit-to-pane transform from live rect, layout-agnostic, tracks resize) + semantically (describe_point → \"tag: <name>\"); round-trips. Core overlay seam committed Minerva daa5654e; plugin in 3c850e1."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84cd899176439a444935cf796c4d","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"AnnotationOverlay now consumes host get_annotation_view_transform()/get_annotation_zoom() + view_changed (override for scaled/scrolled hosts)","created_at":"2026-06-01T20:08:43","updated_at":"2026-06-01T20:08:43","tags":["annotations","core","nametag","overlay","substrate","transform"],"value":"Core change 2026-06-01 (pdf-print-substrate, AnnotationOverlay.gd + AnnotationHost.gd): AnnotationOverlay was hardcoding Transform2D.IDENTITY + zoom 1.0 and only redrawing on annotations/selection/tool change — so NO host could make annotations track a scaled/scrolled/moving surface. Now: overlay builds AnnotationRenderContext from _host.get_annotation_view_transform() (doc→overlay-local Transform2D) + _host.get_annotation_zoom(); it inverse-maps pointer input through that transform (tools stay in doc space); badge positions + the selection halo are transformed too (Transform2D * Rect2 transforms the AABB); and it redraws on its own `resized` + the host's `view_changed` signal. Base AnnotationHost adds get_annotation_view_transform()→IDENTITY, get_annotation_zoom()→1.0, signal view_changed — all has_method/has_signal guarded, so legacy hosts (hello/text) are byte-identical. To make annotations bind to a fit-to-pane raster / camera view: override the two transform methods (derive from the LIVE control rect — pane size is variable, project_editor_pane_grid_is_variable) and emit view_changed() on pan/zoom/resize/page flip.","component":"annotations","key":"host-view-transform-seam","confidence":"high","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84d38e50708e91b98257867d2fff","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Changing core substrate + a plugin that references it: RESTART Minerva before reopening the plugin (else parse-error crash)","created_at":"2026-06-01T20:15:17","updated_at":"2026-06-01T20:15:17","tags":["annotations","core","crash","load-order","parse-error","plugin","restart"],"value":"When you add a new signal/method to a CORE base class (e.g. AnnotationHost.view_changed / get_annotation_view_transform) AND a plugin script references it, you MUST restart Minerva to load the new core BEFORE opening the plugin panel. GDScript resolves identifiers at PARSE time against the currently-loaded base class; opening the plugin while the OLD core is still in memory throws 'Identifier not declared' and the off-tree plugin scene load crashes the whole app (confirmed 2026-06-01: nametag editor referenced view_changed via `view_changed.emit()` against stale AnnotationHost → crash). Plugin scripts reload on panel close+reopen, but CORE classes only reload on app restart. DEFENSIVE PATTERN: in a plugin, emit a possibly-core-version-dependent signal via emit_signal('view_changed') (string) rather than view_changed.emit() — the string form has no parse-time identifier reference, so it degrades gracefully (no-op) against an older core instead of crashing. Order of operations for core+plugin changes: edit core → edit plugin → RESTART Minerva → (plugin auto-loads new core) → open panel.","component":"plugin","key":"core-change-restart-before-plugin-that-depends-on-it","confidence":"high","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84d6e0e27d2b95737a35886c8943","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Pre-flight parse-check a plugin godot_scene script before opening it — a parse error crashes the WHOLE app","created_at":"2026-06-01T20:18:55","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["check-only","crash","godot","parse-check","plugin","preflight"],"value":"Opening a plugin godot_scene panel whose panel/host .gd has ANY GDScript parse error crashes ALL of Minerva (the off-tree scene loader doesn't isolate the failure). ALWAYS pre-flight after editing a plugin scene/host script, BEFORE minerva_open_file / minerva_plugin_open_panel: `godot --headless --path src --check-only --script <ABS path to plugin .gd>`. Clean = exit 0 and no lines matching 'Parse Error|Compile Error|not found in base|not declared|Could not (parse|load|resolve)'; nonzero = it WILL crash on open. Works for OFF-TREE plugin scripts because --path src loads the project's global class_name registry (MinervaPluginPanel/AnnotationHost/etc.) and relative preload() resolves from the script's dir. Validated 2026-06-01 with a positive control (a script calling a missing fn → 'Parse Error: Function not found in base self', exit 1). Common foot-gun: referencing a helper that existed in a prior version of the panel but was dropped in a rewrite (e.g. _gen()) — check-only catches it; opening blind crashes the app. Pair with the load-order rule (core-change-restart-before-plugin-that-depends-on-it).","component":"godot","key":"preflight-parse-check-plugin-scripts","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e84fd3f197144a3c199784a1c7d00","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"On load, restore annotations AFTER rendering — else describe_point has no page context and anchored_to is wiped to \"\"","created_at":"2026-06-01T21:00:49","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:00:49","tags":["anchor","annotations","describe_point","load","nametag","plugin"],"value":"In a plugin's _on_panel_load_request, call _render_current_preview() (which sets the host's page context — _page_native + rows via set_page_context) BEFORE host.set_annotations(restored). set_annotations runs AnnotationHost.refresh_all_anchors → _stamp_anchor → host.describe_point, which re-derives anchored_to. If page context isn't set yet (annotations restored first), describe_point returns \"\" and the SAVED semantic anchor (e.g. \"tag: Ada Lovelace\") is silently overwritten with \"\" — the LLM loses the referent after any save/reopen. Confirmed + fixed 2026-06-01 in the nametag editor: reorder to render→restore. General rule: any host whose describe_point depends on view/page state must establish that state before restoring (re-stamping) annotations on load.","component":"plugin","key":"restore-annotations-after-render-for-anchor-context","confidence":"high","research_cost":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e85249b6c78acaaf2cf3c0e3b322b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugins ship skills in manifest.json skills[] (seeded on install) — NOT via minerva_skill_create","created_at":"2026-06-01T21:43:49","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:43:49","tags":["cad","manifest","nametag","plugin","skill","skills"],"value":"A plugin ships its own agent skill(s) by declaring a top-level `skills: [...]` array in manifest.json. Minerva SEEDS them into the docket on install and UNSEEDS on remove (PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md §3.3; CAD ships minerva_cad_modeling). Each skill object requires: id (MUST match ^minerva_<plugin_id>_[a-z0-9_]+$, unique), title, summary (1-line, lead with when-to-use), system_prompt (the domain/persona guide — like CAD's multi-section §0…§N), outcome, preconditions, steps (numbered imperative, exact tool names, call budget), tool_deps (array of exact host/plugin tool names, resolved against the registry AT INSTALL), target (model expr, e.g. \"all\"); optimization optional. Do NOT use minerva_skill_create for a plugin-shipped skill — that writes the USER's user://master.dct (runtime-only, not shipped with the plugin); it's for personal/experimental skills. Editing the manifest skill: use a script (json load→insert after `tools`→dump indent=2 ensure_ascii=False) to avoid hand-escaping the long steps/system_prompt strings. Confirmed 2026-06-01: nametag-maker ships minerva_nametag_maker_make_tags.","component":"plugin","key":"plugins-ship-skills-in-manifest","confidence":"high","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e852e3a02744ab5eb7cd889f01736","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"9 minerva_cad_* MCP tools live in Minerva CORE (MCPCadTools.gd), not the CAD plugin — architectural leak","created_at":"2026-06-01T21:54:19","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:54:19","tags":["architecture","cad","core","leak","mcp","plugin"],"value":"The minerva_cad_* MCP tools are CORE-resident, NOT shipped by the CAD plugin. src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd (class_name MCPCadTools extends MCPToolModule) registers 9 tools — get_mesh_info, list_edges_live, get_edge, get_selected_edge, annotate_edges, clear_edge_annotations, list_user_labels, export, snapshot — and is wired into the core server at MinervaMCPServer.gd ~:115 (MCPCadTools.new(self)). The CAD PLUGIN backend (~/github/minerva-plugins/cad/main.go) ships only ONE tool: mcad_validate (tools/list → [mcad_validate]). So per the owner's principle 'all CAD tools should come from the plugin,' these 9 are a leak. NOTE the design reason it's hard to move: the module reads the LIVE in-process Cad_AnnotationHost via AnnotationHostRegistry (duck-typed, no plugin class_name) — fast, no IPC; its header explicitly says 'without filesystem access or IPC round-trips.' Migrating to the plugin backend (separate process) would need IPC/host-capability access to the host's edge registry — a real DCR. SIDE EFFECT: this is WHY CAD's plugin skill tool_deps resolve at install (core tools are always in available_tools) while a plugin whose deps reference its OWN backend tools (nametag) shows unsatisfied_deps at seed → skill hidden (PluginSkillSeeder.resolve_deps; satisfied iff in def.tools[] own_tool_names OR live available_tools).","component":"cad","key":"cad-mcp-tools-leaked-into-core","confidence":"high","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e85329a1b78ca86ca9e2a23da9bca","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"CAD MCP tools (9 minerva_cad_*) live in Minerva core, not the CAD plugin","description":"LEAK (owner principle: all CAD tools should come from the CAD plugin, none in Minerva core). 9 minerva_cad_* MCP tools are registered in CORE, not the plugin.\n\nWHERE: src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd (class_name MCPCadTools extends MCPToolModule) registers: get_mesh_info, list_edges_live, get_edge, get_selected_edge, annotate_edges, clear_edge_annotations, list_user_labels, export, snapshot. Wired into the core server at MinervaMCPServer.gd ~:115 (MCPCadTools.new(self)). Actively maintained (commit 0a0eb77c).\n\nThe CAD PLUGIN backend (~/github/minerva-plugins/cad/main.go) ships only mcad_validate (tools/list → [mcad_validate]). So 8-9 CAD-domain tools are core-resident.\n\nWHY IT'S NON-TRIVIAL (design): MCPCadTools reads the LIVE in-process Cad_AnnotationHost via AnnotationHostRegistry (duck-typed, no plugin class_name) — fast, NO IPC; the module header explicitly says 'without filesystem access or IPC round-trips'. Migrating to the plugin's separate-process backend requires giving it IPC / host-capability access to the host's edge registry + mesh info + snapshot rendering — a real DCR, not a move.\n\nSIDE EFFECT worth noting: because these are core-resident, CAD's plugin skill (minerva_cad_modeling) resolves its tool_deps at install (core tools always in available_tools). A correctly-structured plugin whose skill deps reference its OWN backend tools must declare them in manifest tools[] to resolve (see nametag fix, minerva-plugins fb… / the manifest tools[] commit; PluginSkillSeeder.resolve_deps).\n\nREPRO: minerva_list_skills shows minerva_cad_* are host-available without the CAD plugin running; grep src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCadTools.gd. Hint: cad/cad-mcp-tools-leaked-into-core.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX (DCR): expose the edge-registry / mesh / snapshot data to the plugin backend via a host capability (or move the introspection into the plugin worker), register the 9 tools from the plugin's tools/list, and delete MCPCadTools.gd from core.","created_at":"2026-06-01T21:59:06","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:59:06","severity":2,"tags":["architecture","cad","core","leak","mcp","plugin"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8532ba837c58857762630d17316e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A plugin skill whose tool_deps reference the plugin's OWN tools is hidden unless those tools are declared in manifest tools[]","created_at":"2026-06-01T21:59:14","updated_at":"2026-06-01T21:59:14","tags":["focused-chat","manifest","nametag","plugin","skill","tool_deps"],"value":"Symptom: a plugin-shipped skill doesn't appear in the focused-chat picker even though minerva_list_skills shows it. Cause: PluginSkillRecord.has_unsatisfied_deps HIDES skills with unresolved tool_deps. Skill seeding runs at INSTALL, BEFORE the plugin starts. PluginSkillSeeder.resolve_deps marks a dep satisfied iff it's in (a) the live tool registry (available_tools) OR (b) the plugin's manifest tools[] (own_tool_names). At install the plugin isn't running, so its OWN backend tools aren't in the live registry → if manifest tools[] is empty, deps on the plugin's own tools are UNSATISFIED → skill hidden. (Host tools like minerva_get_spreadsheet_data resolve fine; only the plugin's own tools fail.) FIX: declare the plugin's tools in manifest tools[] with PREFIXED names (minerva_<id>_*, matching the deps + PluginDefinition prefix validation) + a minimal inputSchema {type:object}. SAFE: these are install-time metadata only — at runtime PluginToolRegistry.register_backend_tools REPLACES them with the backend tools/list authoritative schemas (docstring: 'backend is authoritative at runtime; manifest tools are install-time review metadata only'), so rich nested-arg schemas + exposure are unaffected. Then reinstall to re-seed → unsatisfied_deps clears. Check the skill record (minerva_docket_get project=master): no unsatisfied_deps field = un-hidden. (CAD's skill avoids this only because its minerva_cad_* tools are leaked into CORE — see cad/cad-mcp-tools-leaked-into-core.) Confirmed 2026-06-01: nametag_maker.","component":"plugin","key":"plugin-skill-deps-on-own-tools-need-manifest-tools","confidence":"high","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e853f95037cf1b38775a7fdeb5607","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":".docx DATA reader: GDScript ZIPReader+XMLParser in CORE (zero deps, rides Godot cross-platform); Go-plugin fallback","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:13:17","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:16:46","tags":["core","cross-platform","docx","gdscript","ingest","xmlparser","zipreader"],"value":"CONSTRAINT: Minerva ships Win/macOS-arm/Linux; can NOT assume libreoffice/word/python/pandoc installed (probe 2026-06-01: libreoffice present on dev box but NOT guaranteed elsewhere; python-docx/pandoc absent). So a docx DATA reader (text + tables, NOT rendering) must be self-contained + cross-platform.\n\nRECOMMENDED: pure GDScript in CORE, using Godot built-ins — ZIPReader (open the .docx, read entries) + XMLParser (stream word/document.xml). Walk <w:p> paragraphs (concat <w:t> runs) and <w:tbl>→<w:tr>→<w:tc> tables; images via zip word/media/*. ZERO new dependencies, ZERO per-platform binaries — it rides the Godot runtime that's already on all 3 OSes. Document ingestion is GENERAL infrastructure (a file-format reader, like file-read), NOT domain logic, so CORE is the right home (does NOT violate the no-domain-logic-in-core / CAD-leak rule) — and it's then reusable by every plugin + the agent. Expose as a core MCP tool e.g. minerva_read_document(path) → {text, tables:[{rows:[[cells]]}], images:[{name,content_type,bytes_b64}]}, + a convenience that pipes the first/largest table into minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor. Same ZIPReader+XMLParser approach extends to .pptx (and .xlsx, though xlsx already imports natively).\n\nFALLBACK (if keeping core minimal is preferred): a doc-ingest PLUGIN in Go (stdlib archive/zip + encoding/xml — no external dep), cross-compiled to the 4 release targets (static binaries, the existing plugin-packaging path). Self-contained but adds a build step + per-platform binaries. AVOID Python (bundling a venv/interpreter per platform — esp. Windows + M1 — is the heaviest option).\n\nVIEWING a .docx (note-type visual/dual-representation) still needs rendering (soffice→pdf→raster) → libreoffice dep → NOT cross-platform-safe → keep that as a deferred v2; the data reader above does not need it.","component":"ingest","key":"docx-ingestion-approach","confidence":"high","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8547775c7253af7e38c61ce6b049","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Core docx (OOXML) data reader: minerva_read_document (text + tables + images)","description":"GOAL: let an agent driving Minerva over MCP ingest a .docx the user dropped on disk (roster, schedule, etc.) into text and/or a spreadsheet — closing the 'do the whole nametag scenario inside Minerva' gap (parent initiative: nametag in-app editor 019e80a0f17a). DATA reader only; VIEWING/rendering a .docx is explicitly OUT (needs LibreOffice/Word → not cross-platform-safe; deferred v2).\n\nCONSTRAINT: Minerva ships Windows / macOS-arm / Linux; can NOT assume libreoffice/word/python/pandoc are installed.\n\nDECISION (hint ingest/docx-ingestion-approach): implement in CORE as pure GDScript using Godot built-ins — ZIPReader (a .docx is OOXML = a ZIP of XML) + XMLParser (stream word/document.xml). ZERO external deps, ZERO per-platform binaries — rides the Godot runtime already on all 3 OSes. Document-format reading is GENERAL infrastructure (like file-read), NOT domain logic, so core is the right home and it does NOT violate the no-domain-logic-in-core / CAD-leak rule; it's then reusable by every plugin + the agent.\n\nSCOPE:\n- New core MCP tool minerva_read_document(path) → {text, tables:[{rows:[[cell]]}], images:[{name, content_type, bytes_b64}]}. .docx first (extends to .pptx; .xlsx already imports natively).\n- Parse: <w:p> paragraphs (concat <w:t> runs); <w:tbl>→<w:tr>→<w:tc> tables; images from zip word/media/*.\n- Convenience: pipe the first/largest table → minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor (or return it for the agent to populate via update_spreadsheet_data).\n- Tests: extract the real Explorer-camp roster .docx table (61 students) headless.\n\nFALLBACK if core must stay minimal: a doc-ingest PLUGIN in Go (stdlib archive/zip + encoding/xml, cross-compiled to the 4 targets). AVOID Python (per-platform venv bundling). \n\nCAVEAT: GDScript XMLParser is streaming → table-walking is manual; pathological nested tables may need iteration. Roster tables are straightforward. First child task is a headless PROOF before committing the tool.","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:21:53","updated_at":"2026-06-01T23:06:42","tags":["core","cross-platform","docx","ingest","spreadsheet"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e854798907a358ff7d17d4b024798","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"nametag: build_from_sheet — generic front/back faces (entirely different sides)","description":"GAP (from the end-to-end review): build_from_sheet's back_mode is only blank|same (mirror front) — no way to put a DISTINCT back (e.g. the camp schedule) or a fully different front vs back. The real student tag = front fields + SCHEDULE on the back, which build_from_sheet can't produce.\n\nThe renderer ALREADY supports this: layout.go faces model has per-row front + back faces (+ full_image_id, + a shared Options.Back). nametag_generate/nametag_save already expose front/back faces. So this is EXPOSING existing capability through the sheet bridge, not building new rendering.\n\nWORK: extend build_from_sheet (sheet.go + main.go inputSchema + the manifest skill steps) to accept (a) a shared back face / schedule (columns of lines, e.g. mapped from a 2nd sheet or constant), and (b) optionally per-row back mapping; let a face be structured OR full-image. Pair with the docx reader (DCR core minerva_read_document) so the schedule can come from lanyard-back.docx. Update the Nametag Maker skill to drive it.\nHITL: build a student .mtags whose back is the shared schedule; preview shows front + (duplex-aligned) back.","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:22:02","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:45:43","priority":2,"tags":["faces","gap","nametag","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Done (commit 737cf1e, minerva-plugins main). Exposed the renderer's existing back-face support through build_from_sheet: shared_back (one constant back behind every tag, e.g. a schedule with per-day heading columns → stored as generate.back / Options.Back) and back_mapping (distinct per-row back built from columns; per-row overrides shared; a row with no back data gets none). Pure bridge, no renderer change. Schema declares both nested shapes (factored mappingSchema, reused faceSchema). +2 tests (shared-back 2-page doc + schedule text + stored generate.back; per-row back present/absent); 4/4 build_from_sheet green, go build clean."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8547b7a970898fe03f3efd180fba","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"nametag: free image placement on faces (size/rotate/translate) + expose images in build_from_sheet","description":"GAP (from the end-to-end review): images on a tag are limited to icon-left/right or full_image_id (fill the side). Owner wants to SIZE, ROTATE, and TRANSLATE an image anywhere on a face (e.g. place the camp logo freely), and build_from_sheet currently has NO image input at all (text-only) — so the logo can't reach the sheet flow.\n\nWORK:\n1. layout.go: add a placed-image primitive to Face, e.g. images:[{id, x_in, y_in, width_in, rotation_deg, anchor?}] → emit draw_image ops at those coords/sizes.\n2. Expose icon/images (icon_png_base64|icon_path, images[]) through build_from_sheet (sheet.go + inputSchema) so the sheet flow can place a logo; agent writes the image to disk first (disk_write) or passes a path.\n3. RISK / possible dependency (CORE): host.pdf.generate's draw_image op may not support ROTATION. VERIFY the host.pdf op set; if absent, add an angle param to draw_image (a small core host.pdf capability bump) — file as a sub-task if confirmed needed. Position + size are already expressible.\nHITL: place + size + rotate the camp logo on a tag via build_from_sheet; preview shows it correctly.\nNOTE: getting/cropping the logo image itself is a separate minor concern (graphics editor / chatgpt_image_gen; or extracted via the docx reader DCR).","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:22:10","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:52:50","priority":2,"tags":["gap","host.pdf","images","layout","nametag","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","resolution":"Done across both repos. CORE (Minerva 3f82c6eb, pdf-print-substrate): host.pdf draw_image gains optional `angle` (CW-positive, about image center; auto-h pivot from intrinsic aspect via fpdf GetImageInfo + TransformRotate). Contract §draw_image updated; +TestDrawImageRotation (explicit-h/auto/negative). PLUGIN (minerva-plugins c3d23cc, main): Face.Placed []PlacedImage (tag-local content-box inches + width/height + rotation_deg) emitted as foreground draw_image ops in both face branches; exposed via faceArgs.images[] (nametag_generate/save) with id-resolution validation, and via build_from_sheet (icon_png_base64/icon_path/images[] registry pass-through + front_images shared placement + shared_back.images). +2 tests (TestBuildDocPlacedImage coords/width/angle/z-order; TestBuildFromSheetFrontImages icon-read→placed+rotated render→explicit-front stored). Full suites green. Hint host.pdf/draw-image-rotation-angle. NOTE: both gitignored binaries rebuilt locally; live verification rides the in-app acceptance test (order-of-ops step 4)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8547f0f6764c9e268ca73af13c08","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Proof: GDScript ZIPReader+XMLParser extracts the camp roster table from the real .docx","description":"DE-RISK before building the core tool (DCR 019e8547775c). Write a headless GDScript script that uses Godot's ZIPReader to open /home/imran/Downloads/2026 Explorer Family Camp - student info for lanyards.docx, read word/document.xml, and XMLParser to walk <w:tbl>→<w:tr>→<w:tc>→<w:t>, printing the extracted student table (≈61 rows) as rows-of-cells. Confirm columns/values match the known roster (cross-check ~/temp3/all_rows.json). Also list word/media/* (expect none for this doc). Run: godot --headless --path src --script <proof>.gd. IF it works → extend the same parsing into the core minerva_read_document tool (this work becomes the implementation core). Known: the doc IS a w:tbl table, no embedded media (probed 2026-06-01).","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:22:25","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:38:31","priority":1,"tags":["docx","gdscript","ingest","proof"],"parent":"minerva:019e8547775c","resolution":"PASS. Headless GDScript ZIPReader+XMLParser extracted the camp roster: 1 table, 62 rows (header + 61 students), 9 columns; cross-checked against ~/temp3/all_rows.json (61). No embedded media. Proof code became the core implementation: lifted into OOXMLReader.gd + MCPDocumentTools.gd (minerva_read_document). Reference saved at Docs/design/docx_proof_reference.gd. Commit cc78d90c on pdf-print-substrate."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e855550967051bff483a88e5432cf","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"New class_name → rebuild global class cache before bare refs compile","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:37:01","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["cache","class_name","godot","mcp"],"value":"After adding a new GDScript class_name, references to it by BARE name (e.g. MinervaMCPServer.gd's `MCPDocumentTools.new(self)`) won't compile until the global class cache is rebuilt. Run: `godot --headless --editor --quit --path src` → rebuilds `src/.godot/global_script_class_cache.cfg`. (Project root is `src/`, cache lives at `src/.godot/`.) Headless `--script` test runs still work via preload() without the rebuild, but the live app boot needs the cache entry. Verified the entry with `grep MCPDocumentTools src/.godot/global_script_class_cache.cfg`.","component":"godot","key":"new-class-name-needs-cache-rebuild","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8555716b7daa92f37e246ec9431f","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Word wraps long header cells → collapse intra-cell whitespace for column names","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:37:09","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:37:09","tags":["docx","minerva_read_document","ooxml","spreadsheet"],"value":"In real .docx tables, Word wraps long header-cell text across lines, so a header cell's text carries embedded \n (from <w:br> or multiple <w:p> in the cell). OOXMLReader.read_docx keeps `rows` faithful (with \n) but emits a per-table `csv` where intra-cell whitespace is collapsed to single spaces + RFC-4180 quoted — so column names import cleanly (e.g. \"Family \nGroup Games\" → \"Family Group Games\"). When deriving spreadsheet column names from a docx table, use the `csv` field, not raw `rows`. Validated on the 61-student camp roster (9 cols, several wrapped headers).","component":"ingest","key":"docx-headers-wrap-collapse-ws","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e85636872780788540ad00beb93f9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"host.pdf draw_image now supports rotation via `angle`","created_at":"2026-06-01T22:52:25","updated_at":"2026-06-01T22:52:25","tags":["draw_image","fpdf","host.pdf","nametag","rotation"],"value":"host.pdf draw_image op accepts optional `angle` (degrees CLOCKWISE-positive, screen convention), rotating about the image CENTER. When h is auto (0), the pivot's vertical center is derived from the image's intrinsic aspect (fpdf GetImageInfo). Impl wraps fpdf TransformBegin/TransformRotate(-angle)/TransformEnd — fpdf rotates CCW so the CW contract angle is negated. Added Minerva 3f82c6eb (pdf-print-substrate). Plugin side: nametag Face.Placed []PlacedImage exposes free placement (tag-local content-box inches + width + rotation_deg), emitted as draw_image foreground ops; exposed in nametag_generate faceArgs.images[] and build_from_sheet front_images/shared_back.images (commit c3d23cc). Both gitignored binaries (src/bin/minerva-host-pdf-linux + nametag-maker-plugin) must be rebuilt to exercise live.","component":"host.pdf","key":"draw-image-rotation-angle","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e86303a1971d88c075eaa61436af1","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A note with note.file set REWRITES that file on serialize — don't set it for references","created_at":"2026-06-02T02:36:08","updated_at":"2026-06-02T02:36:08","tags":["FileReference","create_file_note","footgun","notes","serialize"],"value":"Note._serialize_controls_data() (src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Note.gd) opens note.file for WRITE and stores the control's content into it (text→store_string, image→png). So any note whose `file` is set gets its source file OVERWRITTEN on save. create_file_note() set note.file for every branch. Footgun: attaching a .docx and saving would clobber the user's document with note text. Fix (commit a228c549): binary files (NUL-byte sniff via FileReference.looks_binary, first 8KiB) become a plain text note holding the PATH (FileReference.reference_text), and note.file is left UNSET so nothing is rewritten. Rule: for a reference-only note, never set note.file; put the path in the content. (The \"File\" key is also what ProjectPackage.save_package copies into a .minpackage export — DirAccess.copy_absolute, it copies not moves.)","component":"notes","key":"file-note-serialize-clobbers-source","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e892d53e277c9955b8be8366dc838","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"build_from_sheet silently drops the shared icon when front_images=[] (empty array)","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:31:49","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:31:49","tags":["layout","nametag","plugin","rca","silent-failure"],"value":"ROOT CAUSE of \"nametag has no logo\": sheet.go:98 hasJSON() returns true for an empty array `[]` (len>0 && !=\"null\"). So when the LLM passes front_images:[] (the schema's natural default), sheet.go:179 takes the explicit-face branch: mapRowToFace (sheet.go:62) builds a Front face from the column mapping but NEVER sets ImageID, and the flat title/subtitle/lines are deleted. In buildDoc, frontFace (layout.go:590-602) only assigns fc.ImageID=\"icon\" for FLAT rows (t.Front==nil); an explicit Front face is returned as-is (layout.go:591-593), so the registered \"icon\" image is orphaned and never drawn. renderFaceOps only reserves a logo column when fc.ImageID!=\"\" (layout.go:405). Net: the side-icon 2-column layout (left logo + right text) is the CORRECT mechanism but is bypassed. FIX options: (a) hasJSON should treat `[]`/`{}` as absent; (b) when build_from_sheet builds an explicit front face, copy the shared icon onto it (front[\"image_id\"]=\"icon\" + image_side) instead of relying on buildDoc's flat-only injection; (c) buildDocFromArgs should warn/error when an icon is registered but unreferenced (generate.go:189-214 validates referenced ids exist but never flags an orphan icon). NOTE the front_images path (call with a real entry) draws the logo as a free overlay ON TOP of text (placedImageOps) — that is the \"logo over text, not legible\" failure, NOT the 2-col layout. Use icon_path + image_side for the column logo; reserve front_images for stamps/overlays.","component":"nametag-maker","key":"build_from_sheet-icon-dropped-on-empty-front_images","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893ba65578aca4a3e0bb9ac2ed0b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W1 — hasJSON must treat empty array/object/string as absent","description":"In sheet.go:98, hasJSON returns `len(r) > 0 && string(r) != \"null\"`, which is TRUE for an empty array `[]` (len 2). build_from_sheet uses hasJSON(a.FrontImages) at sheet.go:179 to decide whether to convert a flat row into an explicit front face — so passing front_images:[] silently takes the explicit-face branch, mapRowToFace builds a face with no ImageID, and the shared \"icon\" is never drawn (see hint 019e892d53e2). FIX: treat `[]`, `{}`, and `\"\"` as absent (e.g. trim + compare against \"[]\"/\"{}\"/\"\\\"\\\"\"/\"null\", or json.Unmarshal and check len). ACCEPTANCE: front_images:[] keeps the flat path so buildDoc.frontFace injects the icon → logo renders as a left column. This single item makes the failing run succeed.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:28","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:28","priority":1,"tags":["bug","nametag","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893bc20e7ea480ec69f75117430e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W2 — build_from_sheet's explicit faces must carry the shared icon","description":"buildDoc.frontFace (layout.go:590-602) assigns fc.ImageID=\"icon\" only for FLAT rows (t.Front==nil); an explicit Front face is returned as-is (layout.go:591-593). build_from_sheet builds explicit faces via mapRowToFace (sheet.go:62-88), which never sets ImageID. So even with W1, the genuine front_images (overlay) path still loses the column logo. FIX: when build_from_sheet constructs the explicit front face (sheet.go:180-188), set front[\"image_id\"]=\"icon\" and front[\"image_side\"] (default left) so the side-icon column survives alongside any free-placed overlays. DECISION (defaulted, not a blocker): when both a column icon and front_images overlays are supplied, draw icon as the side column AND overlays on top. ACCEPTANCE: a build with both icon_path and a real front_images entry renders the logo as a left/right column with overlays on top, not the logo dropped.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:35","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:35","priority":1,"tags":["bug","nametag","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893bd9e77835a0cf09c2ad6d03d5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W3 — warn/error on an orphaned (registered-but-undrawn) icon","description":"generate.go:189-214 checks that face-referenced image ids exist, but a registered \"icon\" that NO face references passes silently — exactly the failure mode in the RCA. FIX: after building the Doc, if the \"icon\" image is registered but no face Op draws it, surface a warning (default) — \"icon registered but no face draws it; set image_side or front.image_id\". DECISION (defaulted): warning, not hard error (don't break a build that legitimately omits the icon). Surface it in the W4 report. ACCEPTANCE: a build that registers icon_path but draws it on zero faces returns a warning naming the orphaned icon.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:41","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:41","priority":2,"tags":["nametag","observability","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893bf7107bf9b8d8a4008efa64e4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W4 — structured render report as build_from_sheet's standing return","description":"Today build_from_sheet returns {success, page_count, row_count, path, preview_pdf_path} (sheet.go:270-276). Add a bounded structured report: icon_drawn, faces_rendered, rows_text_shrunk (with min_pt — renderFaceOps already computes the shrink factor s at layout.go:460-461 but discards it), rows_missing_title, rows_blank_fields, warnings (incl. W3's orphan icon). Size is bounded regardless of batch count — this is what verifies a 500-tag run. IMPLEMENTATION NOTE (not a user decision): renderFaceOps currently returns only []Op; reporting shrink/overflow requires threading a diagnostics struct out of the render path (a parallel diagnostics return, or a post-pass). This is the M-sized piece of the backlog. Covers KNOWN failure modes only — pair with W7 (visual) for the unknowns. ACCEPTANCE: a build with a deliberately over-long name reports that row under rows_text_shrunk with its min_pt.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:49","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:49","priority":2,"tags":["nametag","observability","rca-followup","scale"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893c0b8873cd891d9c9a46fdf69d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W5 — disambiguate icon_path/image_side vs front_images in the schema + worked example","description":"In main.go's tool schema, make the two logo mechanisms unmistakable: \"icon_path + image_side = logo as a reserved column beside the text (the 2-column layout)\"; \"front_images = stamps/overlays drawn ON TOP of content — NOT for a layout logo\". Embed a worked example for the common case (2-col student tag with a left logo: icon_path + image_side='left' + mapping{title,subtitle,lines}, leave front_images unset). LLMs anchor on examples. ACCEPTANCE: schema text contrasts the two and includes one runnable example.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:54","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:54","priority":2,"tags":["discoverability","nametag","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893c1da07c6e8cd2988106f4c74b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W6 — encode the logo rule in the \"Make name tags\" skill","description":"Update the \"Make name tags\" skill in the nametag-maker manifest skills[] to state up front: for a logo in a layout use icon_path + image_side; front_images is for stamps/overlays only; the standard front is 2 columns (logo + data). Cheapest fix — prevents the wrong first move. Re-seeds on reinstall. ACCEPTANCE: skill instructions name the correct logo mechanism and the 2-col template.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:58","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:47:58","priority":2,"tags":["discoverability","nametag","rca-followup","skill"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893c426274fdbb314ed3e9ab24a5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W7a — generic host.pdf.render_page capability (rasterize a PDF page/region → image)","description":"DECIDED 2026-06-02 (hint 019e894251217fb8): D1 = host-level capability. D2 = pdfium via go-pdfium (github.com/klippa-app/go-pdfium) WASM/wazero mode default, in the Go host_pdf sidecar; cgo optional perf upgrade. License clean (MIT + BSD-3). Rejected (cross-platform gate): MuPDF/go-fitz/lazypdf + Ghostscript = AGPL; poppler/pdftoppm = no single cross-platform binary (Windows pain); pure-Go = none; PDF.js = needs live webview.\n\nDISTRIBUTION/FCIB (verified 2026-06-02): WASM mode go:embed's pdfium.wasm INTO the minerva-host-pdf sidecar at `go build` — no separate file, no runtime fetch, no system install. FCIB clean: .wasm is a transitive Go-module artifact (go.sum-pinned, fetched at build), never committed to our repo; sidecar binary is gitignored (src/bin/, src/sidecars/host_pdf/minerva-host-pdf) and CI-built per platform. BUILD CHANGE: Minerva only — one `go get` in src/sidecars/host_pdf/go.mod; scripts/build-host-pdf.sh UNCHANGED (plain go build, CGO_ENABLED=0 cross-compiles via wazero). Cost: binary grows ~few MB. NO plugin build change. cgo path (rejected) would need per-platform libpdfium + pkg-config + LD_LIBRARY_PATH bundled in the download.\n\n--- ORIGINAL ---\nGeneric host capability: given a PDF path, page index (+ optional crop box x,y,w,h and DPI), return a rasterized image over MCP. DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH: W4's report only catches failure modes we coded detectors for; pixels catch the unknowns (squashed logo, tofu glyphs, overlap, mirrored back). New capability => broker handler + PluginDefinition allowlist + manifest grant. ACCEPTANCE: an MCP call rasterizes page N (with optional crop+DPI) and returns a viewable image.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:48:08","updated_at":"2026-06-02T17:00:42","priority":3,"tags":["decided","defense-in-depth","host-capability","nametag","rca-followup","visualization"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893c5b8d7bbf905ecc26aa1521d2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W7b — nametag_render thin tool (content-addressed targeting + zoom-as-crop)","description":"nametag_render(target:{row|tag_name|page+side}, zoom:\"tag\"|\"page\", dpi?) operating on a path (the .mtags or preview_pdf_path build already returns). Content-addressed targeting couples to the W4 report (report flags row 213 → render row 213). Zoom-as-crop: crop to the target then raster at higher DPI — both cheaper (~1k tokens for one tag vs ~2.8k for a full downsampled sheet) AND legible (8-pt schedule text unreadable in a downsampled full page). Row→page+cell math is computable from layout.cellPositions()/backCellIndex(). DEPENDS ON W7a (or a nametag-local rasterizer if D1 chooses local-first). ACCEPTANCE: nametag_render(row:N) returns a legible cropped image of that tag's front or back.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:48:14","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:48:14","priority":3,"tags":["defense-in-depth","nametag","rca-followup","visualization"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893d4adf7766915b501857bb10c8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W8 — minerva_file_verify: cheap file metadata probe (exists + size + image dims/format)","description":"In the RCA chat the LLM opened the logo in the graphics editor to \"validate\" the file (msg 64) — a wasted detour. minerva_disk_exists already returns {exists:true} (would have killed the LLM's bad-file misdiagnosis in one call) BUT returns no size; minerva_disk_read proves content only by returning the full bytes (expensive — a 153KB PNG ≈ 205KB base64 into context); no tool returns image dimensions/format for a standalone PNG without opening an editor. GAP: a cheap metadata-only verify: {exists, size_bytes, mime/format, width, height (for images)}. Defense-in-depth: lets the LLM confirm an input is present, non-empty, and the expected type before using it. TWO PARTS: (1) capability — the metadata probe (image dims via Godot Image.load header or a light decode); (2) behavior/discoverability — fold \"verify inputs cheaply; don't open an editor to eyeball a file\" into W6's skill, since the LLM already had disk_exists and didn't use it. ACCEPTANCE: a verify call on the camp logo returns exists:true, size≈153498, format:png, 815×910, with no byte payload.","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:49:15","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:49:15","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","defense-in-depth","nametag","rca-followup","visualization"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e893d5c487ad18a5d9a0734614648","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva_disk_exists returns {exists,success} only — no size; no cheap content/metadata probe exists","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:49:20","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:49:20","tags":["codetools","ergonomics","file-tools"],"value":"minerva_disk_exists returns only {exists, success} — existence, NOT size or content. To prove a file is non-empty you must minerva_disk_read it (returns full bytes — expensive for binaries/images). There is NO tool that returns file size or image dimensions/format for a standalone file without opening an editor (minerva_read_document is docx/ooxml only). So an LLM cannot cheaply verify \"exists AND has content AND is the expected type\" today — gap tracked as work-item W8 (minerva_file_verify). To merely confirm presence, disk_exists is the cheap call; don't open a graphics/text editor to eyeball whether a file is there.","component":"minerva","key":"disk_exists-returns-existence-only-no-size","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e894251217fb899abfd15bd4800e1","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Cross-platform PDF→image rasterization: use pdfium via go-pdfium (WASM/wazero) in the Go host_pdf sidecar","created_at":"2026-06-02T16:54:45","updated_at":"2026-06-02T16:54:45","tags":["cross-platform","go","license","pdf","pdfium","rasterize"],"value":"For host-side PDF→image rasterization (W7a host.pdf.render_page, and any plugin needing to raster a PDF for agent vision), use pdfium via github.com/klippa-app/go-pdfium in WASM/wazero mode, embedded in the existing Go host_pdf sidecar. WHY: WASM mode = one embedded .wasm, wazero is pure-Go → zero native deps, trivially cross-platform (Linux/Win/Mac), avoids poppler-on-Windows packaging pain. License is clean for a proprietary app: go-pdfium MIT + pdfium BSD-3. Renders to DPI or exact pixel size with crop; returns image.Image or PNG bytes directly. AVOID MuPDF (go-fitz/lazypdf) and Ghostscript — both AGPL. pure-Go PDF rasterizer does not exist. PDF.js is in the codebase (nametag R3 panel) but needs a live webview to drive headlessly — not suitable for a decoupled host capability. ESCAPE HATCH: same lib flips to cgo (bblanchon/pdfium-binaries prebuilt, incl arm64) for ~2x speed, no API change. WASM's ~2x slowdown is a non-issue for single on-demand page rasters (tens of ms).","component":"host.pdf","key":"pdf-rasterizer-choice-pdfium-go-pdfium-wasm","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e898da89775d7bb175e06e8acbd3d","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"create_spreadsheet_editor silently yields an empty sheet when file_path has no usable rows","description":"Surfaced from the nametag RCA (chat d9a829cd, the 'worse fail' run). The agent built a sheet from a file_path whose CSV was empty/not-materialized and got success with zero data; it then thrashed and ran out of tool-call rounds before producing a tag. minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor should not silently present an empty sheet as success — at minimum return a warning (e.g. rows_imported:0, warning:'file produced no data rows') or an error when a file_path/csv import yields no usable rows, so the agent can correct in the same turn. Same silent-failure theme as nametag W3 (orphaned icon) and the broader 'tools must not pass silently when they did nothing useful' principle.","created_at":"2026-06-02T18:17:02","updated_at":"2026-06-02T18:17:02","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["ingest","nametag","rca-followup","silent-failure","spreadsheet"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","repro_steps":"1. minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path=\"/path/to/empty_or_missing.csv\", csv_content=\"\", name=\"X\"). 2. minerva_get_spreadsheet_data(editor_name=\"X\"). Observed in chat d9a829cd: create call at msg 26 (file_path=\".../student_lanyards_data.csv\", csv_content=\"\"), get_spreadsheet_data at msg 35 returned {\"data\":[],\"row_count\":100,\"success\":true}."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e89eb6d197811882e0dd24c1e84fe","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W10 — raise/surface the 15-tool-call-round cap for skill-driven multi-step workflows","description":"Root terminator of the worse-fail run: the agent hit Minerva's 15-tool-call-round cap mid-setup and was killed before producing anything (chat d9a829cd, msgs 46-48: {\\\"error\\\":\\\"Tool not executed: Max tool call rounds (15) reached\\\"}). Multi-step skill workflows legitimately exceed 15 rounds. OPTIONS: (a) raise the default cap; (b) make the cap visible to the agent (e.g. a 'rounds remaining' signal so it can prioritize / wrap up rather than be cut off mid-plan); (c) let a skill declare a higher round budget in its manifest (optimization profile already supports tool_idle_turns/tool_budget — add a max-rounds knob). Note: the W1-W6 nametag fixes should cut round usage (direct read_document→csv_content, no bash detour, no empty-sheet restart) but do not change the cap; heavy multi-doc scenarios can still hit it.","created_at":"2026-06-02T19:59:28","updated_at":"2026-06-02T19:59:28","priority":2,"tags":["agent-loop","nametag","needs-decision","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e89eb895179e1aa4bcb4dcf46593a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"W11 — host capability to open a file in the OS default app (preview/print)","description":"Gap found while choosing focused-chat tools for the nametag workflow. After generating a print-ready PDF the user wants to preview/print it via the OS default PDF handler, but: minerva_open_file resolves an in-app editor/panel by extension (not the OS app); there is no xdg-open/open/start capability; minerva_bash could do it but is (correctly) excluded from a focused chat and absent for production agents. Propose a host capability (e.g. host.os.open / minerva_os_open) that launches a path in the OS default application, cross-platform (xdg-open on Linux, open on macOS, start/ShellExecute on Windows) — and optionally a print variant. SECURITY: launching arbitrary paths via the OS shell is a privilege; gate it as a grantable capability (user-driven action), not auto-granted broadly. Pairs with W8 (verify the file exists/is good before opening).","created_at":"2026-06-02T19:59:35","updated_at":"2026-06-02T19:59:35","priority":3,"tags":["host-capability","nametag","needs-decision","os-open","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e89ec299a7e06aedf199173d2ac44","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin deploy: reload picks up the binary but NOT skills; reinstall re-seeds skills; tool schemas come from the binary not the manifest","created_at":"2026-06-02T20:00:16","updated_at":"2026-06-02T20:00:16","tags":["deploy","plugin","reinstall","reload","schema","skills"],"value":"Deploying nametag-maker (and any stdio plugin) changes:\n1. TOOL INPUT SCHEMAS are served by the BINARY at runtime via tools/list (main.go toolList → generateInputSchema/buildFromSheetInputSchema). manifest.json's tools[].inputSchema is only a {\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\"} STUB ('Install-time metadata; backend supplies the authoritative schema at runtime'). So editing schema descriptions in main.go goes live on binary rebuild + plugin restart — NO reinstall needed.\n2. SKILLS (manifest skills[]) are seeded into the docket at INSTALL. minerva_plugin_reload loads the new binary but does NOT re-seed skills — the old skill text persists. To update a skill you must minerva_plugin_remove (skills_deleted:1) then minerva_plugin_install (skills_seeded:1). Verify with minerva_get_skill title=... and check activated_tools count.\n3. After remove+install the plugin is INSTALLED but NOT running (autostart=false) — call minerva_plugin_start.\n4. go build -o <name> . writes the binary; `go build ./...` does NOT write an output. Rebuild only when Go source changed; manifest-only changes (skills/tool_deps) need just remove+install, no rebuild.\nRelated: [[project_mcp_plugin_reload_doesnt_refresh_tools]] (external /mcp reconnect still needed for Claude Code's own tool registry).","component":"minerva-plugins","key":"reload-vs-reinstall-skills-and-schema-source","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8a27092e71be96808b9d7519823f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CodeTools policy.json is undiscoverable/uneditable on a binary install — move override to user config + Settings UI","description":"RE-SCOPED 2026-06-02 (owner-approved): the original FIX DIRECTION below (move override to user://config_file.cfg + AISettings UI) was correct ONLY while codetools' file tools live in Minerva core. Under the codetools extraction (DCR 019e7b6609), folding a plugin's policy into Minerva's core config + core settings window IS the bleed we're eliminating. SUPERSEDED BY 019e8af5 (generic schema-driven plugin-config mechanism). New target: codetools owns its policy — baseline deny floor compiled into the sidecar; user override at <plugin_data_dir>/policy.json (sidecar fails safe: malformed/empty -> baseline, never allow-all); edited via the plugin-config mechanism's Plugin Settings surface, NOT AISettings. Migration: import legacy ~/.codetools/policy.json if present. This item now tracks the codetools-policy SCHEMA + its rendering as the first consumer of 019e8af5; it is blocked by 019e8af5.\n\n--- ORIGINAL (historical, direction superseded) ---\nSURFACED FROM the W11 (minerva_os_open) design discussion. Policy.gd loads ~/.codetools/policy.json; BASELINE_DENY_PATTERNS are compiled in (safe defaults always apply), and a missing file = empty user-override layer (Policy.gd:79-81). That's FCIB-clean (nothing shipped) BUT the user-override layer is a hidden home dotfile: a binary-install user can't discover it exists, doesn't know its schema, and has no in-app way to edit it. Poor on Discoverable + User-visible.\n[original fix direction: move override to user://config_file.cfg + AISettings UI; applied to both CodeTools deny policy and the W11 os_open allowlist. RELATED: W11 os_open item 019e89eb895179e1aa4bcb4dcf46593a.]","created_at":"2026-06-02T21:04:34","updated_at":"2026-06-03T00:50:04","priority":2,"tags":["binary-install","codetools","direction-superseded","discoverability","distribution","plugin-config","policy","rca-followup"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8a2b29f37be5926b65e321b50825","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Tool-call round cap: MaxToolCallRounds (default 15, fallback 25), enforced ChatPane:1787; skills raise it via optimization.max_tool_call_rounds","created_at":"2026-06-02T21:09:05","updated_at":"2026-06-02T21:09:05","tags":["agent-loop","optimization","round-cap","skills","tool-budget"],"value":"The 'Max tool call rounds (N) reached' abort is the per-message consecutive tool-call-round limit. Enforced in ChatPane.handle_tool_calls (ChatPane.gd:1787 `if current_round >= max_rounds`); max_rounds = history.MaxToolCallRounds (ServiceHistory.gd:44, STORED DEFAULT 15) else ChatPane.DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_CALL_ROUNDS=25 (so the stored 15 is the stale/inconsistent value). Per-message chain (resets each user send), NOT per-conversation. Also settable per-chat in AISettings spinbox and per-agent via AgentDefinition.max_tool_call_rounds (AgentSpawner.gd:30). IMPORTANT: optimization.tool_budget is TOKENS (ToolBudgetManager, MCPSkillTools.gd:816), NOT rounds. W10 added optimization.max_tool_call_rounds: a SKILL declares it in its manifest skills[].optimization; PluginSkillSeeder.gd:56-77 seeds optimization→skill record; docket_db.gd:1314 int-coerces it; MCPSkillTools._apply_skill_optimization sets history.MaxToolCallRounds from it on skill load. Nametag Maker declares 40. Global default unchanged (15) by owner choice.","component":"minerva","key":"tool-call-round-cap-and-skill-optimization-wiring","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8a30efec78fea447ecd5375173bd","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"create_spreadsheet_editor with file_path (CSV) imports 0 rows — only csv_content loads","description":"Confirmed live 2026-06-02 on pdf-print-substrate after the W9 build:\n- Wrote /tmp/w9_good.csv = 'Name,Role\\\nAda,Engineer\\\nBo,PM\\\n' (29 bytes, verified on disk).\n- create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path='/tmp/w9_good.csv') → {rows_imported:0, warning:...}; get_spreadsheet_data seconds later still data:'' (empty), row_count:100. NOT a timing race (empty long after).\n- create_spreadsheet_editor(csv_content='<same text>') → {rows_imported:3}, loads correctly.\nSo the file_path load path does not import CSV rows; csv_content does. This breaks the documented skill step 'File/xlsx/csv: create_spreadsheet_editor file_path=<abs> (native import)'. Likely also affects the original failure (chat d9a829cd msg 26/35: file_path CSV → data:[]).\nFIX: make the file_path branch (MCPSpreadsheetTools._create_spreadsheet_editor → editor_pane.add(SPREADSHEET, file_path,...)) actually parse/load CSV/TSV into spreadsheet_data, OR (interim) have the tool read the file and route through set_content for text formats. Re-check .xlsx/.minsheet load too. INTERIM SKILL WORKAROUND: prefer csv_content (disk_read→csv_content) over file_path for CSV until fixed.\nNOTE: W9's warning is working as intended (it flagged this); no W9 change needed.","created_at":"2026-06-02T21:15:23","updated_at":"2026-06-02T21:28:36","priority":1,"severity":4,"tags":["import","nametag","rca-followup","silent-failure","spreadsheet"],"parent":"minerva:019e80a0f17a","repro_steps":"1. minerva_disk_write /tmp/t.csv with 'Name,Role\\\nAda,Engineer\\\nBo,PM\\\n'. 2. minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path='/tmp/t.csv') → rows_imported:0, empty sheet. 3. minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(csv_content=<same>) → rows_imported:3. Compare."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8a3105a170949f604cb17efb1465","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"FIXED: create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path) now loads csv/tsv/xlsx via canonical importer (was: empty sheet)","created_at":"2026-06-02T21:15:29","updated_at":"2026-06-02T21:28:34","tags":["dry","fixed","import","spreadsheet"],"value":"FIXED 2026-06-02 on pdf-print-substrate (verified live: file_path=/tmp/w9_good.csv → rows_imported:3, data present). HISTORY: create_spreadsheet_editor(file_path=<csv>) used to produce an EMPTY sheet because Editor._load_spreadsheet_file (Editor.gd) was a redundant JSON-only reimplementation that bypassed the canonical importer. FIX: Editor._load_spreadsheet_file now delegates to SpreadsheetEditor.load_file(path) → SpreadsheetFileHandler.import_file (dispatches csv/tsv/xlsx/minsheet by extension; src/Scripts/UI/Controls/SpreadsheetEditor/SpreadsheetFileHandler.gd) + view refresh — the SAME path File→Import uses. So file_path import now works for csv/tsv/xlsx. (Until this commit ships, older builds still need csv_content as a workaround.) Canonical importer is the place to wire any spreadsheet file loading — do NOT hand-roll parsing in Editor. Bug 019e8a30efec.","component":"minerva","key":"create_spreadsheet_editor-file_path-csv-imports-nothing","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8af511e27e94a5aaa1483b087e6b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Generic schema-driven plugin-config mechanism (plugins own their settings; host renders)","description":"WHY: For codetools to be a self-contained plugin (DCR 019e7b6609), it must own its own policy/config WITHOUT bleeding into Minerva core (no plugin settings in user://config_file.cfg or AISettings). Today the platform has NO plugin-config concept (manifest panel kinds are only html/godot_scene, surfaced as editor tabs via editor_items[]; no settings mount). This item builds the generic mechanism.\n\nDESIGN (Option B — won the owner's code rubric: leads reliability #1 + durability #2 lexicographically; only loses on cost #3, which ranks too low to dethrone it):\n- Plugin manifest gains a `config:` block — a typed schema (enum / string / list<string> / list<path> / bool). JSON-Schema-lite.\n- Minerva renders a GENERIC settings form from that schema in a NEW discoverable \"Plugin Settings\" surface. Add a `settings_items[]` manifest field symmetric with `editor_items[]` to mount it (and, as an escape hatch for config too rich for a form, allow a kind=godot_scene panel here — Option A — though codetools policy does NOT need it).\n- Values persist to a host-managed per-plugin config store (or the plugin data_directory). Host signals the plugin on change; the PLUGIN validates semantics (e.g. regex compiles, paths sane) and can reject with feedback. Host owns render/store/transport; plugin owns schema + semantics — same separation as godot_scene panels. Zero codetools-specific surface in core.\n\nFIRST CONSUMER — codetools policy schema: mode (enum restricted/unrestricted), deny_patterns (list<regex>), allow_patterns (list<regex>), path_scopes (list<path>), confirm_writes (bool). Renders under Plugin Settings -> CodeTools.\n\nP1-ERA INTERIM (the fail-safe storage slice — ship this BEFORE the renderer, forward-compatible, NOT throwaway): codetools policy override lives at <plugin_data_dir>/policy.json; the sidecar reads BASELINE (compiled-in safe deny floor) + override. CRITICAL GUARD: the sidecar MUST fail safe — malformed/empty/invalid override falls back to the baseline deny floor, NEVER to allow-all — and logs loudly. This is the exact on-disk format the renderer will later edit, so the interim is the first slice of B, not a competing Option C. Migration: import legacy ~/.codetools/policy.json if present.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: this is a platform feature in its own right — do NOT weld it onto the P1.4 visualizer gate. Sequence it as its own phase. Re-scopes 019e8a27 (which previously pointed at config_file.cfg + AISettings — that direction was correct only for core-resident codetools and is superseded by the plugin model).\n\nDoD: manifest config-schema parser + generic settings renderer + settings_items[] mount + per-plugin config store + a worked codetools policy schema rendering and round-tripping; cold-Opus review; functional that the form reads/writes and the sidecar honors it (incl. the fail-safe path).","created_at":"2026-06-03T00:49:37","updated_at":"2026-06-03T00:50:12","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","no-bleed","platform","plugin-config","settings","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8b3affd079a0ab98a20496fa0d4c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Go-shim plugin runtime cache was version-keyed only — stale worker after a rebuild","created_at":"2026-06-03T02:06:00","updated_at":"2026-06-03T02:06:00","tags":["cache","codetools","go-shim","gotcha","plugin","runtime"],"value":"EnsureRuntime (internal/runtime/extract.go) extracts the embedded python bundle to <data_dir>/runtime/<plugin_version>/ and Tier-1 reused it whenever manifestValid() passed — WITHOUT comparing the current embedded bundle. So a rebuilt SAME-VERSION bundle (e.g. a worker .py change with no version bump) was masked by a stale extraction; the worker returned 'unknown method: <tool>' for newly-added tools even though `tools/list` (Go registry) showed them. Symptom: smoke OK but real tools/call fails. FIX (codetools, 261c6a7): stamp the embedded bundle sha into the extraction (source-bundle.sha256) and re-extract when it differs from req.EmbeddedSHA256. To force a fresh extract manually: rm -rf <data_dir>/runtime. Worker runtime datadir defaults to ~/Library/Application Support/Minerva/plugins/<id> (or $MINERVA_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR if Minerva sets it). This pattern is shared by cad/presentation/nametag go-shim plugins — they likely have the same latent bug.","component":"plugin-runtime","key":"runtime-cache-staleness","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8e43171b73f58b04b468e06e29f3","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"get_graph stats omits project_name → panel title falls back to literal \"Project\"","created_at":"2026-06-03T16:13:42","updated_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:07","tags":["P1.4","codetools","cosmetic","get_graph","panel"],"value":"codetools get_graph (worker/codetools_worker/code_visualizer.py:662) sets stats=store.stats(), which returns ONLY counts (projects/symbols/edges/files/described_*) — no project_name. The code_graph_view.gd title reads _graph_data.stats.project_name and falls back to the literal \"Project\" when absent (code_graph_view.gd:251-255). Fix: after stats=store.stats(), add project_name from store.list_projects()[0][\"name\"] (DB projects table has it, e.g. \"rich-panel\"). NOT a render failure — the 133-node graph still draws; purely cosmetic title. Requires bundle+binary rebuild + plugin restart (embedded-bundle gotcha).","component":"codetools","key":"get_graph.stats_no_project_name","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8e4a269e7bfe959b01917fc9adc6","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Code Graph panel opens at a Level-0 splash (click-to-explore), NOT the graph — looks \"empty\" but isn't","created_at":"2026-06-03T16:21:24","updated_at":"2026-06-03T16:21:24","tags":["HITL","P1.4","UX","codetools","panel"],"value":"The code-magic visualizer (ui/code_graph/code_graph_view.gd) opens at Level 0 = a centered dotted box showing project name + \"Click to explore\" + a \"N files · N symbols · N edges\" stats line. The actual graph (L1 boundary grid: ui/core/mcp/test groupings; L2 spatial node/edge graph) only appears AFTER clicking the splash (_show_level(1)). _build_root_view only runs post-load, so if you see the splash text the graph DID load. Easy to misread the splash as an empty/broken render. Gate verification: click through to L1/L2 to confirm nodes+edges draw.","component":"codetools","key":"panel.level0_is_click_to_explore_splash","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8edf33fd718e8dbf982230d48269","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"P1.4 panel reads code_visualizer.db from the PLUGIN SOURCE DIR (in-place install), not app_userdata","created_at":"2026-06-03T19:04:13","updated_at":"2026-06-03T19:04:13","tags":["HITL","P1.4","codetools","db_path","panel","pickup-correction"],"value":"The code_graph panel resolves its DB as globalize_path(ctx.data_directory)/code_visualizer.db. For install-FROM-MANIFEST (in-place), PluginDefinition.gd:265-266 sets def.data_directory = manifest.get_base_dir() = the PLUGIN SOURCE DIR (~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools). So the panel reads ~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools/code_visualizer.db — NOT app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/data/codetools/ (pickup step-5 path was WRONG; that only holds for a marketplace-COPIED install). Symptom when DB is staged in the wrong place: panel shows \"Project / 0 files · 0 symbols · 0 edges\" because the worker silently CREATES an empty SQLite at the resolved path (sqlite connect auto-creates) and returns status:ok with zero nodes. Fix to validate render: cp the indexed DB to ~/github/minerva-plugins/codetools/code_visualizer.db. ROOT DESIGN ISSUE: sourcing the graph DB by an implicit data_directory convention is fragile scaffolding — real usage needs an explicit chosen codebase target + index-on-demand, not a fixed path.","component":"codetools","key":"panel.db_path_is_plugin_source_dir_for_inplace_install","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f2282747dd8ab91f9c25cf72a10","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Make the Code Graph panel responsive (adopt ResponsiveContainer) — handle 1-pane / narrow widths","description":"Surfaced during the P1.4 HITL render gate (019e7b871b): the panel renders the real graph correctly, but does NOT handle a 1-pane (~1/3 screen) width well. Root cause is fixed, full-window-assuming geometry inherited from the standalone code-magic visualizer.\n\nWHY: Minerva's editor area is a variable grid (1×1 up to 3×2). Plugin panels MUST be fully responsive — derive geometry from the live pane rect, react to resize, never hardcode pane dims. This panel violates that.\n\nSPECIFIC OFFENDERS (ui/code_graph/code_graph_view.gd):\n- Level-0 splash: CenterContainer holds a fixed 300×150 box (line ~327) → clips/overflows when the pane is narrower than 300px.\n- Levels 1–3: a 3-column HSplitContainer — outline | graph | inspector — with the outline hardcoded to custom_minimum_size.x = 240 (line ~76). At 1-pane width the 240px outline consumes most of the space, leaving little/none for the graph + inspector. (Outline already has a <<\\/>> toggle that could auto-collapse.)\n\nUSE THE EXISTING SYSTEM (do NOT roll a custom one):\n- Godot-native: ResponsiveContainer at res://Scripts/UI/Controls/responsive_container.gd. It's a Container that watches its own width and emits width_class_changed(new_class: StringName) ONLY on breakpoint transitions; exposes read-only `width_class` + get_width_class(). Breakpoints: xs <480, sm 480–767, md 768–1023, lg 1024–1439, xl ≥1440 (CLASS_XS/SM/MD/LG/XL constants).\n- Adoption: wrap layout variants, connect width_class_changed, toggle child .visible per class, and apply the initial class in _ready before the first resize. OFF-TREE plugins cannot use class_name — preload(\\\"res://Scripts/UI/Controls/responsive_container.gd\\\") and type the node as its BASE (Container), per the off-tree-plugin class_name rule. The .tscn ext_resource can reference the res:// core script.\n- Reference impl: src/plugins/hello_scene/ui/ResponsiveSmoke.{gd,tscn} (grid↔stack toggle) and scenes/compact_toolbar_smoke.gd (toolbar compact↔labeled). Docs: src/plugins/plugin_system_guide.md lines 232–315.\n- HTML/webview note (not this panel, but for parity tracking): there is NO shared responsive framework for HTML panels — they use ResizeObserver + CSS media queries (e.g. obs_controller/ui/panel.html). N/A here since code_graph is godot_scene.\n\nSUGGESTED BEHAVIOR:\n- xs/sm (1-pane): collapse outline + inspector to off-canvas/toggled drawers (or a single stacked view), give the graph the full width; auto-collapse the outline via its existing toggle.\n- md: outline OR inspector, not both; graph keeps majority.\n- lg/xl: full 3-column layout as today.\n- Level-0 splash: size the box from the live rect (percentage/min with clamp), not a fixed 300×150.\n- React to `resized`; recompute on pane changes.\n\nDoD: panel is usable at a 1×1 pane (xs) — graph visible and interactable, no clipped splash, no panel that's all-outline-no-graph; verified live in a single pane and in a 3-wide layout; no regressions to L1→L2→L3 navigation, click-to-jump, or filter. Cold-Opus panel review.\n\nRefs: governing DCR 019e7b6609; P1.4 019e7b871b; memory project_editor_pane_grid_is_variable; off-tree class_name rule project_off_tree_plugin_class_names.","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:17:44","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:17:44","priority":3,"tags":["P1.4-followup","code-visualizer","codetools","panel","responsive","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f2489887dd49db0cbbef9fc7ca4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Code Graph entry-point + index-on-demand + save/open durable graph (real-usage UX)","description":"CONTEXT (from P1.4 019e7b871b HITL design discussion, 2026-06-03):\nA code graph is NOT a blank authored document — it is a DERIVED LENS over an existing codebase. The intended scenario: the user asked the LLM for code and wants help understanding it. P1.4 validated the worker→MCP→Godot render pipeline on real data, but the way data gets in is scaffolding: the panel resolves its DB by an implicit convention (globalize_path(ctx.data_directory)/code_visualizer.db) with no target selection and no indexing trigger. For an in-place install that path is even the plugin SOURCE dir, and a missing file is silently created EMPTY by SQLite → the panel showed \"0 files · 0 symbols · 0 edges\" (see hint 019e8edf). This is the concrete proof the data-sourcing must be redesigned around an EXPLICIT chosen target.\n\nGOAL — the three-door model (owner-endorsed):\n1. LLM/agent-driven (PRIMARY, matches the \"MCP drives all Minerva tasks\" north-star): when the user says \"help me understand this code,\" the LLM calls a tool — e.g. minerva_codetools_visualize(path) — that (a) indexes the codebase to a DB if not already, then (b) opens the panel pointed at THAT DB. The graph is a response to a request about a specific codebase, never blank, never ambiguous about what it shows.\n2. User Open (durable): File → Open a previously SAVED graph artifact for offline / non-AI human exploration. The user may want a graph from a previous session.\n3. New (optional, legitimate under reframe): New → Code Graph = CREATE a graph for a codebase you select (prompt for target → index → render). The \"blank\" state is \"no target chosen yet → prompt.\"\n\nREQUIRED PIECES:\n- Explicit target plumbing: panel opens with an explicit db_path / codebase target (ctx.file_path already honored when it ends in .db). REMOVE the data_directory/code_visualizer.db fallback convention — it's the source of the 0/0/0 bug and conflates per-codebase data with the plugin dir.\n- Index-on-demand: indexing currently has NO home in any user flow (done manually in the HITL gate via a venv + vendored.code_visualizer.analyzer.index). Need an MCP/host-driven index step, keyed per-codebase (per-codebase DB, NOT one fixed path), with cache/staleness handling (stale_check already exists). Indexing is multi-second and must show UI progress/status — apply the established rule that MCP-driven runs must have UI feedback (memory feedback_scansort_mcp_must_have_ui).\n- Durable saved-graph artifact: decide the on-disk format for \"save this graph\" (the SQLite store itself? a portable export? a wrapper that records codebase path + index time + annotations). Annotations (annotation_data.gd / *.annotations.json) must travel with it. Open must load it for offline exploration without re-indexing (and optionally offer re-index if stale).\n- Tree-sitter runtime: the indexer needs tree-sitter + tree-sitter-gdscript; in the plugin these ship in the embedded runtime bundle (worker side), so index-on-demand should route through the worker, not a host venv.\n\nOPEN DESIGN QUESTIONS (decide before build):\n- Where do per-codebase DBs live? (user:// keyed by path hash? alongside the codebase? a managed library?)\n- Saved-graph artifact format + extension; is it just the .db or a richer container?\n- Does Open re-index or load static? staleness UX.\n- Indexing trigger + async model + progress surface (panel status vs host dialog vs editor tab).\n- Multi-language: index currently gdscript-focused; scope of languages for v1.\n\nDoD: an LLM can drive \"visualize codebase X\" end-to-end via MCP (index if needed → panel opens showing X's real graph); a user can Open a saved graph and explore offline; no implicit-path data sourcing remains; indexing shows progress; cold-Opus review; functional coverage for the index→open→render round-trip.\n\nRefs: governing DCR 019e7b6609; P1.4 019e7b871b; bug hint 019e8edf (db-path/0-symbol); responsive follow-up 019e8f2282; memories feedback_mcp_drives_all_minerva_tasks, feedback_scansort_mcp_must_have_ui, project_scansort_path_driven_design_gap (sibling path-vs-session lesson).","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:19:56","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:19:56","priority":2,"tags":["P1.4-followup","code-visualizer","codetools","entry-point","indexing","mcp","panel","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f29a22c70a0922e929100e4d613","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Code Graph visual fit-and-finish: legibility (contrast, node overlap, zoom-to-fit, L3 AST)","description":"Surfaced at the P1.4 HITL render gate (019e7b871b), 2026-06-03 owner walkthrough. The panel renders real data and navigates correctly (L1 grid, L2 graph, click-to-jump, filter all confirmed), but visual quality is poor:\n\n- L1 boundary view: some text is hard to read — color choices / contrast.\n- L2 component graph: \"tons of overlap in the nodes, so no node is readable.\"\n- L3 symbol/AST view: visible but not legible (same overlap problem).\n\nROOT CAUSE (hypothesis): the vendored code-magic force-directed layout (_layout.py x/y) was tuned for a large standalone window; dropped into a Minerva editor pane it has no zoom-to-fit / pan-zoom to fit the graph extent to the live rect, and no label decluttering (hide labels until zoomed, collision-avoid, level-of-detail). Result: 133 nodes pile up in a small canvas.\n\nNOT the same as the responsive item 019e8f2282 — that's about adapting to pane WIDTH (1-pane). This legibility problem persists even at full width, so it's a separate rendering-quality concern (though they compound at narrow widths).\n\nSCOPE / IDEAS (to refine):\n- Zoom-to-fit on load + pan/zoom (mouse wheel + drag) so the graph fills the live rect; recompute fit on resize.\n- Label level-of-detail: hide/elide node labels until zoomed in past a threshold; show on hover/selection.\n- Reduce overlap: tune layout spacing/repulsion for pane-sized canvases, or cluster/collapse by boundary at low zoom.\n- Color/contrast pass: audit KIND_COLORS / EDGE_COLORS and label font colors against the dark bg for WCAG-ish contrast (graph_data.gd:6-24, code_graph_view.gd label color overrides).\n- Apply across L1/L2/L3 consistently.\n\nDoD: at a typical pane size, individual nodes are readable (labels legible at some zoom level), the graph fits the viewport on open, pan/zoom works, and text meets a reasonable contrast bar. Cold-Opus panel review.\n\nRefs: DCR 019e7b6609; P1.4 019e7b871b; responsive sibling 019e8f2282; entry-point sibling 019e8f2489.","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:25:30","updated_at":"2026-06-03T20:25:30","priority":3,"tags":["P1.4-followup","code-visualizer","codetools","fit-and-finish","legibility","panel","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609397cb9ac6af813ab54f871"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f306e3e71c4afd60e1dd0ec74ec","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2.1 — Reimplement file primitives (glob/grep/bash/cwd) in the codetools worker","description":"Base: minerva-plugins/codetools after P1. Model: SONNET impl, OPUS review. Functionals are net-new.\n\nReimplement, in the worker `files/` subsystem (Python), the four file primitives currently in Minerva core (src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/), routed through the P1.2 envelope and registered in router.ROUTES + internal/tools + main.go initRegistry. Per the plugin tool-name prefix rule they become minerva_codetools_* (e.g. minerva_codetools_glob / _grep / _bash / _cwd) — NOT minerva_file_*/minerva_bash (those names die with core in P2.3).\n\nBEHAVIOR PARITY (reference the core impls for semantics, do NOT port GDScript verbatim):\n- glob (cf GlobTool.gd, 184 ln): *, **, ? patterns; exclude standard dirs (.git, node_modules, …); sorted results; truncation/limit.\n- grep (cf GrepTool.gd, 169 ln): regex search; type filters (py/gd/js/ts/…); context lines; binary detection. IMPLEMENT VIA A BUNDLED ripgrep (rg) shipped in the PBS runtime bundle (add to build-python-runtime-bundle.sh) — do not hand-roll regex walking.\n- cwd (cf CwdTool.gd, 50 ln): get/set working dir with ~ expansion + validation (worker process has a real chdir, unlike GDScript).\n- bash (cf BashTool.gd + Policy.gd): shell exec with policy deny-pattern enforcement; 120s default timeout; ~30KB output cap; stdout/stderr merged. Policy = the interim codetools policy.json in <plugin_data_dir> (decided this session; sidecar fails safe). Bash's terminal-PTY routing is P2.2 (host.terminal.exec) — here, headless subprocess is the baseline.\n\nDoD: no-stub functionals against the REAL built binary (smoke tool count rises by 4); coverage >= threshold; cold-Opus panel; worker unittests; regression green. Refs: DCR 019e7b6609 (file-access no-bleed contract, comment 410); P2 019e7b8664; memory project_plugin_tool_name_prefix_rule, project_scansort_pipeline_llm_gated (n/a), feedback_swap_mmapped_so (bundle rebuild).","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:32:56","updated_at":"2026-06-03T21:39:06","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","extraction","files","p2","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b86649e79cc92eda6ab00024e34"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f309807700288b6debf5dd1c823","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2.2 — Add optional host capability host.terminal.exec (plugin bash → UI terminal PTY)","description":"Base: Minerva core (development). Model: OPUS impl/review (design-bearing — new host API surface + capability grant).\n\nAdd an OPTIONAL host capability `host.terminal.exec` to the plugin host API: when granted, a plugin's shell execution routes through Minerva's UI terminal PTY (visible to the user, same PTY substrate as the minerva_terminal_* tools) instead of an invisible subprocess. When the capability is absent/ungranted or running headless, the plugin falls back to its own headless exec (P2.1 baseline).\n\nSCOPE:\n- Define the capability in the plugin capability registry/grant flow (sibling of host.providers.chat / host.files.* / host.dialogs.* / host.notify — see the broker capability set).\n- Bridge from the plugin broker to the terminal subsystem (reuse the existing PTY path; cf the Terminal MCP tooling) — run a command, stream/return output, respect a timeout + output cap.\n- Wire the codetools worker bash (P2.1) to PREFER this capability when granted, else headless.\n- Capability must be opt-in (granted), and core must behave correctly when it's not present.\n\nDoD: capability gated + granted correctly; plugin bash routes through the UI terminal when granted (output visible + returned); clean headless fallback; functional (no-stub) coverage; cold-Opus; full regression green — including core works without codetools. Refs: DCR 019e7b6609; P2 019e7b8664; memory feedback_terminal_enter (\\r), Terminal MCP section of CLAUDE.md, project_plugin_host_owned_schema.","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:33:07","updated_at":"2026-06-03T22:01:37","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","core","host-capability","p2","terminal"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b86649e79cc92eda6ab00024e34"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f30d2ac786681cd61f40336aff8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2.3 — Hard-remove file primitives from Minerva core + add no-bleed boundary guard","description":"Base: Minerva core (development), AFTER P2.1 (plugin replacement) + P2.2 (host.terminal.exec) exist. Model: SONNET impl, OPUS review. Owner decided 2026-06-03: HARD removal — NO one-release deprecation shim. Core Minerva without codetools loses glob/grep/bash/cwd MCP tools entirely (matches the file-access no-bleed contract: agent file tools live only in the sidecar).\n\nREMOVE FROM CORE (inventory from P2 scan):\n- Delete src/Scripts/Services/CodeTools/ (8 files, ~919 ln: Glob/Grep/Bash/Cwd/Read/Write/Edit/Policy.gd).\n- Delete src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPCodeTools.gd (registers the 4 tools).\n- Remove the instantiation `MCPCodeTools.new(self),` at MinervaMCPServer.gd:~109.\n- KEEP minerva_doc_* (MCPDocTools.gd) — separate, buffer-coupled, stays in core.\n- Move the 5 file-primitive test files OUT (covered by P2.1 worker tests instead): test_codetools_glob, test_codetools_read, test_codetools_edit_grep_bash, test_codetools_cwd_write, test_codetools_policy. KEEP test_codetools_panel_gate + test_marketplace_install_start_codetools (they test the plugin, not core primitives).\n- Re-scope the shipped \"File and Code Tools\" skill (master.dct id 019d5c…0006): drop minerva_file_glob/_grep/minerva_bash/minerva_cwd from tool_deps; keep minerva_doc_*. The file-primitive half now requires codetools installed — reflect that in the skill text. Then invalidate the baseline-docket cache (author-minerva-skill workflow).\n- Clean hardcoded references: PolicyEngine.gd / ActionNormalizer.gd pattern-match minerva_bash / minerva_file_read by name; decide keep-as-generic-pattern vs remove (they're not registrations, just rule patterns — likely harmless to keep, but note explicitly). test_policy_engine + test_tool_search_index reference these names — update.\n\nADD (no-bleed contract, DCR 019e7b6609 comment 410 — 5 clauses):\n- Boundary test (e.g. test_core_tool_boundaries.gd): assert the core MCP registry registers NO tool matching minerva_codetools_* / minerva_file_* / minerva_bash / minerva_cwd.\n- CI guard mirroring it so regressions can't reintroduce bleed.\n\nDoD: core Minerva BOOTS + full regression green WITHOUT codetools installed (it is optional); boundary test + CI guard green; grep of src/ (outside tests) shows ZERO remaining refs to CodeTools/GlobTool/GrepTool/BashTool/CwdTool/WriteTool/EditTool/CodeToolsPolicy; cold-Opus panel. Refs: DCR 019e7b6609; P2 019e7b8664; P2.1/P2.2 siblings; memory feedback_docket_new_column (removal touches many sites), feedback_test_at_integration_boundary (boot real SingletonObject for the no-codetools happy path).","created_at":"2026-06-03T20:33:22","updated_at":"2026-06-03T22:11:36","priority":2,"tags":["boundary-guard","codetools","core-removal","no-bleed","p2"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b86649e79cc92eda6ab00024e34"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f6c1b4f70c8b88675e1bf8408e7","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"codetools grep uses bundled ripgrep 15.1.0 injected by the shared PBS build script","created_at":"2026-06-03T21:38:07","updated_at":"2026-06-03T21:38:07","tags":["bundle","codetools","grep","p2.1","rg","ripgrep"],"value":"P2.1 grep is rg-backed. `scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh codetools <triple>` injects ripgrep 15.1.0 (musl variant on linux, no glibc floor) into `<bundle>/bin/rg`. Worker locates it via `files/rg_finder.find_rg()`: probes `Path(sys.executable).parent/'rg'` first, then logged `shutil.which('rg')` PATH fallback (INFO) for dev boxes; WARNING on total miss. grep has a pure-Python fallback when rg absent. The bundle .tar.zst is gitignored — REBUILD after any worker change or the Go binary embeds stale code. Triples: linux-x86_64 wired+verified (smoke tools=15); linux-arm64/macos-arm64/windows wired; macos-amd64 still TODO (uses arm64 asset).","component":"codetools","key":"rg-bundling","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f710a157c0586f5f9f4e9af58d7","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"TerminalNew.execute_command runs a PTY command but can't read exit code or timeout","created_at":"2026-06-03T21:43:30","updated_at":"2026-06-03T21:43:30","tags":["exit-code","host-capability","minerva","p2.2","pty","terminal"],"value":"`TerminalNew.execute_command(command)` at src/Scripts/UI/Controls/TerminalNew.gd:489 is the ONLY synchronous run-command-and-capture path on the terminal. It writes `command + \"\n\"` to the PTY, `await`s the `block_finalized` signal (next shell prompt = done), returns `{success, stdout=block.output_text, command, exit_code:0, timed_out:false}`. LIMITATIONS: exit_code is hardcoded 0 (the PTY/ghostty layer does not expose `$?`); there is NO timeout (the await blocks until the next prompt — a hung command hangs forever); no cwd arg. The minerva_terminal_* MCP tools do NOT expose this — they're keystroke I/O + viewport read only. For true exit codes / timeouts, use OS.execute (headless) or add a sentinel-scrape. Host-capability grant gate lives in CapabilityBroker.dispatch() (src/.../Plugins/CapabilityBroker.gd:262); capability allowlist at PluginDefinition.gd:164.","component":"minerva","key":"terminal-execute-command","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f7bb815746c8a589c7e7f05979b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Capability tests must await frames + use real SingletonObject broker, not load() it raw","created_at":"2026-06-03T21:55:10","updated_at":"2026-06-03T21:55:10","tags":["autoload","capability","gotcha","minerva","p2.2","testing"],"value":"GOTCHA: a SceneTree test run via `godot --headless --script test/X.gd` that does `load(\"res://.../CapabilityBroker.gd\").new()` immediately FAILS on this Godot/setup with \"Compile Error: Identifier not found: SingletonObject\" — CapabilityBroker references the SingletonObject autoload global at compile time, which isn't registered yet in the `--script` boot window. The coroutine then aborts after ~2 checks but still quit(0), so the test SILENTLY NO-OPS (false green). This is PRE-EXISTING and affects ALL test_host_capability_*.gd that use the load()+new() pattern (dialogs/documents/files/etc). FIX (matches test_codetools_panel_gate.gd): at the start of _run_tests do `await process_frame` x2, then `var so = root.get_node_or_null(\"SingletonObject\")`; skip if so/so.plugin_capability_broker null; then drive the REAL `so.plugin_capability_broker` + `so.plugin_policy`, injecting a fake PluginDefinition into `so.plugin_manager.get_db()._plugins`. test_host_capability_terminal.gd uses this working pattern (18/18). See [[feedback_test_at_integration_boundary]].","component":"minerva","key":"capability-test-autoload-race","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8f8114977101acd1d824bce91974","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"codetools Go shim: bidirectional host-request client + wire bash host-exec hook to host.terminal.exec","description":"SPLIT OUT OF P2.2 (rubric decision 2026-06-03): the core host.terminal.exec capability (Minerva development @ 8e39e5bc) and a dormant, tested worker seam (`bash_handler.set_host_exec_hook`, plugins main @ b62ce9c) are DONE. What remains is separable, reusable infrastructure that codetools lacks today.\n\nCURRENT STATE: codetools Go shim only EMITS one-way `host.notify` (main.go emitHostNotify → stdout JSON-RPC notification). It has NO request/reply host-capability client (assign id → send `minerva/capability` request → await matching reply on stdin → return to caller). Sibling plugins (scansort/presentation) have this; codetools does not.\n\nWORK:\n1. Add a bidirectional host-request client to the codetools Go shim: send `{method:\"minerva/capability\", params:{capability, args}, id}`, correlate the reply by id off the existing stdin reader, surface result/error to the caller. Make it generic (not terminal-specific) so future codetools host calls reuse it.\n2. Plumb it across the Go↔Python worker boundary so the worker can invoke a host capability mid-tool-execution and block for the reply.\n3. Install the bash host-exec hook (`set_host_exec_hook`) to call host.terminal.exec(command, cwd, timeout_ms) when the capability is granted; map the result dict back ({stdout, exit_code, timed_out, routed_through}); None/ungranted → local subprocess (already handled by the seam).\n4. Declare `host.terminal.exec` in codetools manifest.json permissions.host_capabilities so it can be granted.\n5. Tests: worker hook integration (fake client), Go client request/reply unit, and an end-to-end functional asserting granted bash routes through host.terminal.exec (routed_through=terminal) and ungranted falls back local.\n\nREFERENCES: capability core handler = Minerva src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/CapabilityBroker.gd `_handle_host_terminal_exec` + allowlist PluginDefinition.gd:164; seam = plugins codetools/worker/.../files/bash_handler.py `set_host_exec_hook`; terminal limits in hint 019e8f710a15 (PTY exit code is best-effort, no timeout). Model: OPUS (cross-boundary RPC design).","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:01:01","updated_at":"2026-06-07T07:06:24","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","follow-up","go-shim","host-capability","p2.2","terminal"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8664"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa199f77fba11a089da2d0f1e2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.1 — Vendor code-probe (sightline @ 8b2baa7) into codetools/worker/vendored/sightline","description":"FIRST P3 step (mechanical, low-risk). Snapshot into worker/vendored/sightline/: src/sightline/ (15 .py, stdlib-only, rg-backed) + plugins/godot/ (plugin.py dispatcher + probe addon plugins/godot/probe/addons/sightline_probe/{sightline_probe.gd,plugin.cfg}). EXCLUDE .sightline/ artifact dirs + __pycache__. Add a VENDORING.md row (pinned SHA 8b2baa7, snapshot date, refresh command) matching the existing format. NO local edits — adapter bridges shapes (P3.2). Probe schema in this snapshot is v3 (sightline.godot.editor_probe_state.v3), NOT the v4 the parent mentions — pin to v3. DoD: files present, VENDORING.md updated, `python3 -c 'import vendored.sightline...'` resolves (or the adapter import path works), no bundle break.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:45:50","updated_at":"2026-06-03T22:48:25","priority":2,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","p3","vendoring"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa319876bab77b07f31535bab4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.2 — code-probe MCP wrapper: explore / inspect / validate as minerva_codetools_*","description":"SECOND P3 step (design-bearing). code-probe is CLI-only today (cli.py argparse); wrap its library functions (explore.py/inspect.py/validate.py) behind the P1.2 unified envelope + router as minerva_codetools_* tools. Decide the tool granularity (one explore tool w/ mode, vs several) via rubric. inspect runs the godot probe workflows (godot-debugger-issues / godot-output-console) → debugger_state.json. Reuse the bundled rg (P2.1) — do NOT add a second rg. DoD: tools registered (smoke count up), envelope-valid, worker unittests, cold-Opus. Depends on P3.1.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:45:56","updated_at":"2026-06-03T23:27:24","priority":2,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","mcp-wrapper","p3"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa49f47d978034753e26c0c960","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.3 — Feature-gate X11 visual capture; keep JSON/debugger capture cross-platform","description":"code-probe's visual capture is X11-only (plugin.py _x11_available/_parse_x11_surfaces/_capture_window via xwd+ImageMagick+xdotool). It already returns 'not implemented' on mac/Windows. Make the MCP surface degrade cleanly: visual-capture tools report capability-unavailable off-Linux/headless; the debugger_state.json + output-console capture (pure GDScript EditorInterface, cross-platform) stays available everywhere Godot runs. DoD: off-Linux path returns a clean capability error, not a crash; cross-platform capture unaffected. Depends on P3.2.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:46:02","updated_at":"2026-06-04T01:09:10","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","cross-platform","p3"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa63897327abfde5c26331ac0c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.4 — Remove core src/addons/sightline_probe + repoint its tests (HARD removal)","description":"Mirror of P2.3 for the probe. src/addons/sightline_probe/{sightline_probe.gd,plugin.cfg,.sightline-managed.json} → delete. The parent item claims ~4 tests reference it (test_plugin_definition + others) but `grep -i sightline src/test` returned zero — re-locate by 'addons/sightline_probe' path or the plugin id before deleting, and repoint/relocate them. Verify core boots without the addon; add to the no-bleed guard if appropriate. LAST core-removal step; do after P3.2/P3.3 prove the plugin-side replacement. Depends on P3.2.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:46:09","updated_at":"2026-06-04T01:08:55","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","hard-removal","p3"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa7ba77b8c9315e06324243819","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.5 — DRY: collapse code-probe search.py/files.py into the shared files/ (P2.1)","description":"Sibling of DRY-debt 019e7b86ab. After vendoring, code-probe's explore still routes through its own search.py/files.py. Refactor the adapter so code-probe explore reuses the shared worker files/ (walker + rg locate from rg_finder) instead of vendored search.py/files.py — single source of truth for file discovery + ignore rules + the bundled rg. Keep vendored/ unedited; do the bridging in the adapter. DoD: one rg invocation path; explore behavior preserved (tests); no duplicate walker. Depends on P3.2.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:46:15","updated_at":"2026-06-04T01:11:41","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","dry","p3"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e","resolution":"Rubric scope-reversal: do NOT collapse code-probe's scored search onto the plain worker grep (would degrade code-probe). Meaningful convergence — single shared rg binary — already achieved in P3.2 (_ensure_rg_on_path injects bundled rg into PATH). Deeper shared-walker extraction folded into DRY-debt 019e7b86ab (comment 417), gating P4. No code change this item."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e8faa9745713abbe37515f22167f9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3.6 — Probe test harness: Option B replay + schema-version guard; Option C live-editor HITL","description":"Real captured artifacts exist at sightline src/.sightline/plugin_artifacts/godot/ (editor_output_console_*.json, editor_debugger_warnings_*.json) — normalize project-specific absolute paths and commit a small set as replay fixtures. Build a worker test that replays them through the inspect/validate path (no live Godot). Schema guard: assert fixture schema_version == probe's declared `sightline.godot.editor_probe_state.v3` (bump fails the build until fixtures refreshed). Option C (HITL): a human opens Minerva's Godot editor with the probe addon, triggers a capture, and the fresh debugger_state.json refreshes the fixtures — this is the end-of-P3 human verification. DoD: replay test green; schema guard wired; Option C runbook documented. Depends on P3.2.","created_at":"2026-06-03T22:46:22","updated_at":"2026-06-04T01:18:44","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","hitl","p3","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b867e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e90b52c097e7cbcbbced6207ba261","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4.2 — 3 manifest-seeded codetools skills","description":"Schema = PluginDefinition.REQUIRED_SKILL_FIELDS (id,title,summary,system_prompt,outcome,preconditions,steps,tool_deps,target). Template: cad/manifest.json skills[0]. master.dct File-and-Code-Tools skill already re-scoped to Document Tools (doc-only) in P2.3 — skill #2 re-homes the file ops as minerva_codetools_*. Guards: Minerva test_plugin_skill_seeder/_seeding/_skills_manifest/_skill_record stay green. No new MCP tools (smoke stays 18).","created_at":"2026-06-04T03:37:32","updated_at":"2026-06-04T03:47:51","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","p4","skills"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8699"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e90b5432b711bb344b4e16c2bd308","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4.3 — Unify: routing/envelope consistency + dependency/staleness checks","description":"All three subsystems already share envelope.py + router.py; this item makes the cross-subsystem behavior coherent (consistent error kinds, follow_ups suggesting the next tool, staleness signals — e.g. get_graph/query warn if the index is older than the repo HEAD; inspect warns if the probe isn't installed). Depends on P4.2 not required; can parallel.","created_at":"2026-06-04T03:37:38","updated_at":"2026-06-04T17:07:04","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","p4","unify"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8699","resolution":"Shipped both halves on plugins main (0b64ec4 + e6f9bc5). Unify: confirmed router/envelope already coherent (every path via envelope.ok/error; ROUTES single dispatch). Added first platform-wide follow_ups convention envelope.follow_up(tool,reason,params) enforced in validate(). Staleness signals: (1) code_visualizer read handlers query/get_context/get_graph augmented via best-effort _staleness_aware decorator — cheap os.stat over the indexed file set, early-exit on first deleted/newer-than-last_indexed_at file, follow_up → stale_check/analyze; opt-out staleness:false; never raises. (2) inspect op=status emits follow_up → inspect op=prepare when probe not installed. +15 worker tests (219 total green), smoke tools=18, live-validated against real rich-panel db (fresh index → no false positive)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e90b54ff97005a97b1d547aea013b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4.4 — Combined code-intelligence workflows","description":"The payoff of unifying code-visualizer + files + code-probe: a coherent loop (find/understand → change → runtime-verify). Encode as skill cross-references / a workflow doc. Depends on P4.2 + P4.3.","created_at":"2026-06-04T03:37:42","updated_at":"2026-06-04T17:32:47","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","p4","workflows"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8699","resolution":"Shipped on plugins main af1a727. docs/workflows.md encodes the understand→navigate/edit→inspect/verify loop (diagram + skill index + how P4.3 follow_ups auto-chain it + 3 recipes incl. the canonical Godot-warning→fix→probe-verify). All 3 install-seeded skills now end with a 'next in the loop' cross-reference to their siblings + workflows.md. Content-only; manifest valid."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e90b566a47b57a54f5e00f3ae7368","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4.5 — Marketplace release + Gate D (OWNER + HITL gated)","description":"TERMINAL P4 step. BLOCKED until: (a) DRY-debt 019e7b86ab closed (gates release), (b) Option C live-Godot-probe HITL run (codetools/docs/probe_capture_runbook.md), (c) OWNER SIGN-OFF — a marketplace release is outward-facing/hard-to-reverse, do NOT auto-cut. Agent prepares everything (registry regen, feature-gate, functionals) and stops at the release gate for owner approval.","created_at":"2026-06-04T03:37:47","updated_at":"2026-06-04T19:01:03","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","hitl-gated","owner-gated","p4","release"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b8699","resolution":"SHIPPED 2026-06-04. codetools-v0.2.0 published to the marketplace with all 3 platform assets (linux-x86_64 64MB, macos-universal 47MB, windows-x86_64 31MB); registry.json advertises it (all download URLs HTTP 302); registry-check CI green (no drift). Owner signed off + authorized autonomous execution. Prep: bumped version in all 4 lockstep locations (manifest/main.go/__init__.py/pyproject), added codetools to regen_registry.py PLUGIN_DIRS (the hardcoded allow-list that had kept it out of the marketplace), fixed stale main_test.go 11→18 tool assertion. Found+fixed a pre-existing Windows release blocker: build-python-runtime-bundle.sh rg-inject failed on Windows because PBS's install_only layout has no bin/ dir (python.exe at root) → added mkdir -p before the cp (this is why codetools never released before). Gate D: go test ok, smoke tools=18, worker 219 green, all 3 CI targets green."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e90bddf987f9fae338411f66b7b60","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"codetools Go shim forwards op verbatim (no enum validation) + wraps worker envelope as {ok,result}","created_at":"2026-06-04T03:47:03","updated_at":"2026-06-04T03:47:03","tags":["codetools","go-shim","integration-boundary","mcp","p3","p4","schema"],"value":"Two integration-boundary facts for the codetools plugin: (1) The Go ToolSpec InputSchema is ADVERTISING-ONLY — HandleX just does `w.Call(ctx, \"<method>\", params)` forwarding params verbatim; it does NOT validate args against the schema enum. So worker ops are functionally reachable even if absent from the Go enum — BUT an MCP client/LLM reading tools/list won't DISCOVER them. When you add a worker op (e.g. P3.3 added inspect prepare/remove-probe/capture-visual), you MUST also add it to the `op` enum in internal/tools/*.go or it's invisible. Worker unittests call the Python handler directly and MISS this gap (integration-boundary lesson). (2) A tools/call result's content[0].text is JSON `{\"ok\":true,\"result\":<unified-envelope>}` — the {status,summary,artifacts,...} envelope is nested under `.result`, not at top level. To verify end-to-end: spawn the binary, newline-delimited JSON-RPC (initialize → notifications/initialized → tools/call), parse content[0].text then `.result`.","component":"codetools","key":"go-shim-schema-and-envelope-wrap","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e93c4c40271da9089ce70aa46ef33","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Rename user-visible 'sightline' artifacts to a codetools name (.sightline dir + sightline_probe addon)","description":"Surfaced by owner during the P4.5 Option C probe-capture HITL (2026-06-04).\n\nPROBLEM: \"sightline\" still appears in user-visible artifacts created inside the USER's Godot project:\n1. `.sightline/` directory — `vendored/.../sightline_probe.gd` line 4: `const OUTPUT_PATH := \"res://.sightline/godot_probe/debugger_state.json\"` (hardcoded). The inspect evidence store also writes under `<root>/.sightline/` (godot_probe/ + inspect/).\n2. `addons/sightline_probe/` + `sightline_probe.gd` + `plugin.cfg` — the addon installed into the user's project by inspect op=prepare.\n\nThese leak the historical name into a place the user actually sees. (Internal names that should STAY: the `sightline.godot.editor_probe_state.v3` / `debugger_rows.v2` / `output_console.v1` schema strings — opaque version contracts referenced by fixtures + 2 guard tests + the replay test; the `vendored/sightline/` dir; vendored Python. Renaming those = re-capture every fixture + bump guards for zero user benefit.)\n\nPROPOSED FIX (keeps vendored hermetic):\n- Do the rename at the prepare ADAPTER's copy seam (sightline_probe.py prepare op): when it copies the addon into the user's project, rewrite the addon dir name (e.g. `codetools_probe`) AND patch the copied probe's OUTPUT_PATH const from `.sightline/` → `.codetools/` (a copy-time transform on the INSTALLED artifact, not an edit to the vendored source-of-truth).\n- Update the `status`/`remove-probe` read+cleanup paths to the new dir.\n- Point the inspect session store at `<root>/.codetools/` (or a shared `.codetools/` umbrella) instead of `.sightline/`.\n- Update the committed fixture's `provenance.output_path` (`/project/.sightline/...` → `/project/.codetools/...`) and the runbook references.\n- Pick the umbrella name: suggest `.codetools/` (one dot-dir for the whole plugin: probe output + inspect store) rather than two.\n\nNOT blocking P4.5 functionally; cosmetic/discoverability (user-visible per the 7-axis rubric). Verify with the same Option C HITL after.","created_at":"2026-06-04T17:53:26","updated_at":"2026-06-05T15:54:26","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","naming","polish"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609","resolution":"User-visible sightline artifacts renamed to .codetools/ + addons/codetools_probe/; pushed plugins main 7de89b4. 219 tests + live prepare→status→remove cycle green; zero sightline leakage in user projects. Live-editor cosmetic confirmation folds into editor-assist HITL 019e9454b22d. See comment 428."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e93d8f1747d37abfe2a8c28c25f4c","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"Probe lifecycle is incomplete: no agent-driven run+capture; teardown not editor-aware (stale [editor_plugins] line is one symptom)","created_at":"2026-06-04T18:15:28","updated_at":"2026-06-05T15:54:12","severity":2,"tags":["autonomy","code-probe","codetools","incompleteness","lifecycle","mcp-north-star","reversibility"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b6609","resolution":"Dual-mode probe lifecycle implemented + pushed (plugins main 1aebef4); headless proven live end-to-end, editor-assist via mocked launch + v3 fixture. 219 tests + smoke + live op=run green. See comment 426. verified→ gated on the editor-assist live HITL (019e9454b22d).","environment":"codetools plugin (current main af1a727), Linux, Godot 4.6. Found during the P4.5 Option C HITL on ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/voice-capture.","repro_steps":"1. inspect op=prepare {project_path} on a Godot project that has NO [editor_plugins] section → prepare adds:\n   [editor_plugins]\n   enabled=PackedStringArray(\"res://addons/sightline_probe/plugin.cfg\")\n2. inspect op=remove-probe {project_path} → reports removed=true, project_godot_changed=true, status.enabled=false, and deletes addons/sightline_probe/.\n3. Inspect project.godot: the [editor_plugins] enabled= line is STILL present, now pointing at a deleted plugin.cfg."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9454a10d76db9679c12ce8134ba6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Revise codetools probe docs/skill for the dual-mode model (workflows.md + runbook + inspect_runtime system_prompt)","description":"The dual-mode probe (019e93d8f1) shipped with only the inspect_runtime skill `steps` quick-ref updated. Still describe the OLD Option-C-HITL-only model and need a full revision: (1) codetools/docs/workflows.md — add the autonomous run(headless) loop + stop; (2) codetools/docs/probe_capture_runbook.md — note headless is the autonomous path, editor-assist is the human one; (3) manifest.json inspect_runtime `system_prompt` §2/§3/§4 — currently says the probe \"cannot be driven through MCP\" and \"STOP and tell the user to open the editor\"; reframe: mode=headless IS MCP-driven (no editor), editor-assist is the probe path, document op=run/op=stop/stop_editor + the godot_diagnostics shape {severity,message,file,line,user_fixable}. Keep it accurate to internal/tools/sightline.go.","created_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:34","updated_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:34","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","docs","follow-up","skill"],"parent":"minerva:019e93d8f1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9454a99975a7aa513d2156f7c3ec","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Physically relocate sightline out of worker/vendored/ (de-vendor structural follow-up)","description":"sightline was de-vendored (first-party/editable) in VENDORING.md (019e93d8f1) but still physically lives under worker/vendored/sightline/. Relocating it to a non-vendored home (e.g. worker/codetools_worker/probe/ or worker/sightline/) touches every `from vendored.sightline …` import + the runtime-bundle WORKER_PACKAGES list (runtime-bundle.lock) + the sys.path injection in sightline_probe.py. Mechanical but broad; do as its own change with a full bundle rebuild + smoke. Low priority — the de-vendor is functionally complete (editing in place is already sanctioned); this is tidiness.","created_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:36","updated_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:36","priority":4,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","de-vendor","follow-up","refactor"],"parent":"minerva:019e93d8f1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9454b22d7297b8d8e472c3ea5387","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Live HITL: editor-assist run + editor-aware remove-probe on a real Godot editor","description":"run(mode=editor-assist) and the editor-aware remove-probe stop_editor path were proven via mocked _launch_editor_session + the v3 probe fixture + injected process lists — NOT against a live Godot editor. Verify end-to-end on a real editor (e.g. voice-capture): inspect{op:run,mode:editor-assist} launches the editor, run a scene, probe writes debugger_state.json, normalized diagnostics return; then with the editor still open, remove-probe (no stop_editor) refuses+warns, and stop_editor=true stops the live editor then removes cleanly (the original 019e93d8f1 repro). This is the verified→ gate for 019e93d8f1.","created_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:39","updated_at":"2026-06-04T20:30:39","priority":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","editor-assist","follow-up","hitl"],"parent":"minerva:019e93d8f1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e945618647f60b3fd62d4130df5fa","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Optional run(mode=windowed) on Xvfb — capture display/audio-gated runtime warnings autonomously","description":"The dual-mode probe (019e93d8f1) shipped headless (autonomous, no display) + editor-assist (human). The rubric (Fork B) noted headless misses display/audio-only runtime warnings (the Voice Capture wrinkle) and proposed a documented mode=windowed escape — which was NOT built (mode currently accepts only headless|editor-assist). Add run(mode=windowed): run the project (not --editor) on a real or virtual display (Xvfb on Linux), capture stderr via the SAME parser → godot_diagnostics(source could be \"windowed-stderr\"). Needs Xvfb spawn/teardown management + a graceful fallback to headless when no display/Xvfb. Autonomous (no human) but higher-fidelity than headless. Surfaces the headless fidelity gap honestly rather than leaving it silent.","created_at":"2026-06-04T20:32:10","updated_at":"2026-06-04T20:32:10","priority":4,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","fidelity","follow-up","godot"],"parent":"minerva:019e93d8f1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e987e1d9e7903bd8b3fbfe692503a","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"editor-assist auto-launch fails: plugin worker has no DISPLAY, and the dead editor is masked as \"probe did not report\"","description":"Two defects in run mode=editor-assist surfaced by live HITL:\n(1) The auto-launched godot --editor cannot get a display because the codetools plugin worker runs with a sanitized env (no DISPLAY/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR). Headless mode is unaffected. FIX options: inject the host's DISPLAY/XDG_RUNTIME_DIR into the launch env (worker would need them passed through from Minerva), OR officially make editor-assist a HUMAN-LAUNCHES-EDITOR flow (the human opens godot --editor in their own session; the tool captures via status + reads .codetools/godot_probe/debugger_state.json). The human-launch path is verified working (the probe installs + captures real editor data under the renamed paths).\n(2) editor-assist masks a dead editor: _run_editor_assist should detect the editor process exited (check exit / fast death) and surface the launch error + log path, instead of the generic 'probe did not report yet' follow_up. Easy headless-testable fix.\nNudge: codetools/editor-assist-no-display-in-worker.","created_at":"2026-06-05T15:54:22","updated_at":"2026-06-05T18:37:35","severity":3,"tags":["code-probe","codetools","display","editor-assist","godot","hitl-found"],"parent":"minerva:019e9454b22d","resolution":"_run_editor_assist rewritten (plugins main 72f846b): capture-first if probe already fresh, display pre-check → human-launch guidance when no DISPLAY, dead-editor detection → kind=launch_failed + log tail. Verified live in the running Minerva before it closed (no-display guidance returned, no doomed launch). 222 tests + 6 new branch tests.","environment":"Live Minerva app, Linux, godot 4.6.2. Found during the 019e93d8f1 live HITL 2026-06-05.","repro_steps":"1. Through the running Minerva app: minerva_codetools_inspect {op:run, mode:editor-assist, project_path:<godot project>}.\n2. The plugin worker Popens `godot --editor` but its env has NO DISPLAY and NO XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (Minerva sanitizes the worker env), so the editor dies instantly: 'X11 Display is not available' / 'XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is not set or empty' / 'Unable to create DisplayServer, all display drivers failed' (logged to <proj>/.codetools/plugin_artifacts/godot/godot_editor_*.log).\n3. The tool returns probe_loaded=false with follow_up 'the probe did not report yet; run the scene...' — MASKING that the editor process died. exit_code is null (the launch result doesn't capture the editor's fast exit)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e988adc5975d093610dc71a3fa440","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"codetools diagnostics coverage gaps: headless misses GDScript warnings + parser misses unprefixed Godot lines","description":"Found while probing the live Minerva project (2026-06-05). Two coverage gaps in the codetools diagnostics capture:\n\n(1) PARSER MISSES UNPREFIXED GODOT DIAGNOSTICS. godot_diagnostics.parse_godot_output only matches lines starting with WARNING:/ERROR:/SCRIPT ERROR:/USER WARNING:/USER ERROR:. But Godot emits real diagnostics with NO such prefix, e.g. 'Unicode parsing error, some characters were replaced with U+FFFD: Unexpected NUL character' (seen 4x during Minerva headless init). These are silently dropped. Fix: broaden the parser to also capture a curated set of known unprefixed Godot diagnostic patterns (Unicode parse errors, 'RID allocations leaked', 'resources still in use at exit', 'Failed to load'), classified by severity, while keeping the structured SEVERITY+at: path for the prefixed ones.\n\n(2) HEADLESS CANNOT CAPTURE GDSCRIPT STATIC-ANALYSIS WARNINGS. run mode=headless runs the GAME; Godot only emits GDScript warnings (unused/shadowed var, narrowing, unsafe cast, etc.) from the EDITOR/compile analysis, not a headless game run. Confirmed on Minerva: headless emitted runtime/load/exit issues but ZERO GDScript warnings, while the editor shows many. `godot --headless --check-only --script <file>` is NOT a workaround — it fails on any project script with autoload/class_name deps ('Identifier not found: SingletonObject'). So the editor-probe (mode=editor-assist) is the ONLY path to GDScript warnings, and it needs a real editor (display). CONSIDER: a project-aware static-analysis capture (e.g. an editor-import/headless-analysis pass, or document clearly that GDScript warnings require editor-assist). At minimum, the headless run summary should note it does NOT include editor static-analysis warnings so agents don't assume '0 warnings headless' means 'clean'.\n\nNudge: codetools/probe.godot-headless-diagnostics (the format), and this finding.","created_at":"2026-06-05T16:08:17","updated_at":"2026-06-05T16:08:17","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","diagnostics","gdscript-warnings","hitl-found","parser"],"parent":"minerva:019e93d8f1"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9aa059f67344bb5b875673f9e4e9","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"codetools get_diff is not project-scoped: whole-repo bleed + repo-root paths for a sub-directory project","description":"**Where:** `codetools/worker/codetools_worker/code_visualizer.py:407` `get_diff()`.\n\n**Symptom (reproduced 2026-06-05 on video-recorder-rust, a subdir of the ccsandbox git repo):** indexed project path = `~/gitlab/ccsandbox/experiments/video-recorder-rust`; after a working-tree edit to `scripts/main.gd`, `get_diff {db_path}` returned **3 files** — our edit PLUS two unrelated repo files (`experiments/docket/docket.dct`, `experiments/scansort/docs/scansort.dct`) — with **repo-root-relative paths** (`experiments/video-recorder-rust/scripts/main.gd`). The store indexes **subdir-relative** paths (`files.relative_path` = `scripts/main.gd`).\n\n**Root cause:** `get_diff` does `git diff --name-status HEAD` (and `--cached`) with `cwd=repo_path` but (a) no pathspec to scope to the project and (b) no `--relative`, so git reports the whole repo from the git root, with root-relative paths.\n\n**Impact:** the Code Graph panel's diff matching (`_diff_data.has(file_rel)` in `ui/code_graph/code_graph_view.gd`) never hits → no `●` change markers, no Level-3 DIFF mode, and unrelated files pollute the \"Changed only\" view. Breaks the in-subdir-repo case the owner specifically wanted to test in-place.\n\n**Fix:** scope the diff to the project — pass a pathspec (`-- <repo_path>`) AND emit project-relative paths (run git with `--relative`, or strip the `repo_path` prefix), so returned paths line up with `files.relative_path`. Add an explicit `repo_path`/`scope` override for the \"I really want the whole repo\" case.\n\n**Test:** worker test against the video-recorder-rust fixture (`/tmp/vrr.db`) asserting exactly one changed file `scripts/main.gd` with project-relative path. Part of the codetools UX/functionality follow-up batch; see also the dead diff-wiring in the panel controller (only calls get_graph) and item 019e8f2489.","created_at":"2026-06-06T01:51:00","updated_at":"2026-06-06T02:20:50","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["code-graph","codetools","diff-viz","get_diff","subdir"],"resolution":"Fixed in plugins main d51bea2: get_diff uses `git diff --relative` from repo_path (scopes to subtree + project-relative paths); re-prepend --show-prefix for git show. 8 new integration tests (test_code_visualizer_git_scope.py), full worker suite 286 green. Verified live on /tmp/vrr.db (video-recorder-rust subdir) → returns exactly scripts/main.gd, no whole-repo bleed. Code-complete; awaiting end-to-end panel HITL to mark verified."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9aa093927526958cface02d02f7c","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"codetools stale_check is blind to uncommitted working-tree edits (commit-hash basis)","description":"**Where:** `codetools/worker/codetools_worker/code_visualizer.py` `stale_check()`; basis is `index.py:19` `get_git_hash()` = `git log -1 --format=%H -- <file>` (the **last commit that touched the file**, NOT the working-tree content).\n\n**Symptom:** after editing `scripts/main.gd` in the working tree (uncommitted) and adding 3 symbols, `stale_check` still reports \"all indexed files up to date\" because the last-commit hash is unchanged. The graph (`get_graph`) is silently stale.\n\n**Impact:** the index freshness guarantee the skill/system-prompt leans on (\"ALWAYS stale_check before trusting queries\") is wrong for the most common dev loop — *I just edited, show me the change*. An LLM (or the panel) reasons about pre-edit structure. Directly undermines the diff-visualization scenario and any mid-refactor re-check.\n\n**Fix options:** compare working-tree content, not just commit hash — e.g. `git hash-object <file>` vs a stored blob hash, or mtime/size + content hash fallback for files not in git. Keep the deleted-file check. Note the P4.3 `_staleness_aware` decorator already does a cheap `os.stat` mtime pass over reads; align stale_check with that signal so both agree.\n\n**Test:** worker test — index fixture, edit a file without committing, assert stale_check flags it `modified`. Part of the codetools UX/functionality follow-up batch.","created_at":"2026-06-06T01:51:15","updated_at":"2026-06-06T02:20:54","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["code-graph","codetools","diff-viz","index-freshness","stale_check"],"resolution":"Fixed in plugins main d51bea2: index-time get_git_hash + stale_check now hash working-tree content via `git hash-object` (was `git log -1` last-commit hash), so uncommitted edits are detected. Verified on isolated git repo: clean after index → flags scripts/main.gd modified once edited uncommitted. 8 new integration tests, full worker suite 286 green. Code-complete; awaiting end-to-end panel HITL to mark verified. Note: detection still only covers already-indexed files; new-file blindness + lossy-refresh tracked in cache-coherence DCR 019e9ab8422d."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9aa0f871705bb14f1f3dc08f3630","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"codetools edge model: open-world + scoped resolution + data-coupling + decomposition analysis","description":"**Motivation:** two scenarios expose the same root assumptions in the indexer's edge layer.\n\n**(1) Project topology** — \"project\" spans: (1) whole repo, (2) part of a repo, (3) a vendor module, (4) \"complete\" (builds a binary/app), (5) \"incomplete\" (library/typelib/.so consumed externally). The edge layer assumes a **closed world** and a **flat namespace**, so:\n- Edges only resolve to **indexed** symbols (`edges.py:84,115`): engine/vendor/external calls are silently dropped → systematic fan_in/fan_out undercount. Black-holes vendor modules (case 3) and external callers of a library (case 5).\n- **Name-collision over-linking** (`edges.py:86,116`): a call resolves to **every** symbol of that name (confidence 0.3–0.5), so edges include phantoms; worse as scope grows (case 1).\n- **No project/repo/vendor boundary model** (root of the get_diff + stale bugs): git scope is inferred from a bare path.\n- **Vendor exclusion is hardcoded & name-based** (`extract.py:399` skips `addons/vendor/.godot/.git`): can't include vendor as edge targets when desired, nor cleanly index a vendor module as a project.\n- **No completeness/role notion**: `dead_code` can't tell *dead* from *exported public API* (case 5 false positives). Entry points cover engine lifecycle + signal handlers only.\n\n**(2) SOLID / GOD-file decomposition** — to split a GOD file by SRP you need cohesive clusters (methods that call each other + share state) and the external fan-in to preserve (ISP/DIP). Today codetools is strong at *navigation*, weak at *decomposition*:\n- **No data-coupling edges**: fields ARE nodes (`kind:\"variable\"`) but no edge type connects a method to the field it reads/writes (edge types = calls/connects/emits/inherits/instances/preloads). \"Which methods share state\" is invisible → SRP seams unknowable from the graph.\n- **No cohesion/cluster analysis**: `analyze` = stats/dead_code/dry_candidates/coupling_hotspots — no community detection / LCOM / \"suggest split.\"\n- **No class→member containment**: panel palette declares `contains`/`imports` edges but the indexer never emits them (\"0 contains edges\" at index time).\n- **No subgraph/egonet query**: `get_graph` is whole-repo only; per-file analysis filtered client-side → token-heavy for an LLM on a big repo.\n- Closed-world fan_in (above) + name-collision phantoms now manifest as **wrong seams**, not just wrong counts.\n\n**Proposed scope (design, not a quick patch — touches schema + analysis semantics):**\n1. Open-world edges: represent external/unresolved targets as a distinct edge/node category (engine, vendor, external) instead of dropping them; fan metrics count them.\n2. Scoped name resolution: use receiver/type/boundary to disambiguate same-named targets; expose a confidence filter in the panel.\n3. Boundary model: first-class project↔git-root↔vendor/submodule awareness; fixes get_diff/stale scoping at the source (see linked bugs).\n4. Data-coupling edges: method→field reads/writes, so cohesion is computable.\n5. Decomposition analysis: cohesion clustering / LCOM / suggest-split over the intra-file subgraph; class→member containment edges.\n6. Completeness/role: mark exported/public surface so dead_code stops false-flagging library APIs.\n7. Subgraph query: get_graph for a single file / egonet of a symbol.\n\n**Relations:** sibling of the index-on-demand UX item `019e8f2489`; the two bugs `019e9aa059f67344bb5b875673f9e4e9` (get_diff scoping) and `019e9aa093927526958cface02d02f7c` (stale_check) are tactical fixes whose root cause is item 3 here. Keep them as quick wins; this DCR is the durable redesign.","created_at":"2026-06-06T01:51:41","updated_at":"2026-06-06T01:51:41","priority":3,"tags":["analysis","code-graph","codetools","design","edge-model","solid"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9ab8422d7acf812506ce54aaa858","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"codetools index cache coherence: pruning re-index, durable annotations, stable IDs, FTS sync, new-file detection","description":"**Verified 2026-06-05** while answering \"how closely does the index match disk, and how do in-session changes update it?\" The cache design is weak in three ways.\n\n**1. No continuous coherence — drift only knowable by probe.**\n- Only freshness state is `projects.last_indexed_at` (when) + `stale_check` (now content-hash via bug 019e9aa093) + the P4.3 `_staleness_aware` mtime decorator.\n- Both probes iterate the STORE's file list, so they answer \"did indexed files change/disappear?\" — never \"is the index COMPLETE?\" A new `.gd` on disk that was never indexed is invisible → stale_check reports \"up to date\" while the index is incomplete. Renames = delete + invisible-add.\n\n**2. No in-session update path.** The only db writers are `index_repo` (full reparse; sole structure updater) and `set_description`/`set_tags` (annotation columns only). Editing source via doc_*/disk_write/bash/editor does NOT update symbols/edges — the graph goes stale immediately and silently. No watcher, write-through, incremental per-file reparse, or invalidation. (And there is no MCP tool to trigger a reindex in-session — ties to index-on-demand 019e8f2489.)\n\n**3. The one refresh (full re-index) is LOSSY & NON-PRUNING:**\n- **W1 — no prune.** `clear_project()` exists (`store.py:222`) but has ZERO callers; `index_repo` only upserts. Deleted files/symbols persist as ghosts forever (`walk_repo` never revisits a deleted file). Symbol id = `sha256(file_id, name, kind, LINE_START)` (`store.py:154`) encodes the line number, so any edit that shifts lines changes IDs → re-index INSERTS new rows at new lines and LEAVES the old ones = duplicate ghost symbols + stale edges that grow every re-index.\n- **W2 — re-index wipes agent descriptions.** `index.py:105` `upsert_symbol` omits `description`, so `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET description=excluded.description` (`store.py:160`) overwrites it with \"\" even for stable-id symbols. The annotate-loop value (set_description powering semantic query) is destroyed by a refresh.\n- **W3 — tags orphan.** Tags table isn't cleared (so they survive) but are keyed to symbol id → on a line-shift they attach to ghost rows while new rows are untagged.\n- **W4 — unstable positional IDs.** IDs encode line numbers → every in-session `symbol_id` dangles after any re-index (matches the skill's own \"IDs may shift, re-query\" warning).\n- **FTS divergence.** `set_description` updates `descriptions_fts`, but re-index zeroes `symbols.description` without touching FTS → full-text `query` returns matches whose descriptions no longer exist on the row.\n\n**Impact:** acute for the GOD-file/refactor + diff-viz scenarios — the instant you edit, the graph is stale and silent; re-indexing to fix it simultaneously (a) wipes your descriptions, (b) accumulates ghost symbols/edges, (c) invalidates held symbol_ids.\n\n**Proposed scope (design):**\n1. **Pruning re-index** — `index_repo` clears/refreshes per project (or per file) so removed symbols/files/edges disappear; no ghosts. Prefer per-file delta (delete the file's rows, re-insert) over whole-project wipe to bound cost.\n2. **Durable annotations across reindex** — preserve `description` + tags for surviving symbols (carry forward by stable identity, or merge before upsert). A refresh must never destroy agent-authored semantics.\n3. **Stable, content-based symbol IDs** — drop `line_start` from the identity (e.g. id = file + qualified-name + kind, or a signature hash) so IDs survive edits and held references don't dangle.\n4. **FTS kept in sync** — any write that changes `symbols.description` updates `descriptions_fts` (and reindex rebuilds it).\n5. **Completeness detection** — stale_check (or a new check) reports NEW/untracked on-disk files and renames, not just changed/deleted indexed ones.\n6. **(stretch) Incremental in-session update** — a cheap \"reindex these N changed files\" path the agent/panel can call after edits, instead of full reparse; pairs with index-on-demand.\n\n**Relations:** sibling of edge-model DCR `019e9aa0f8` and index-on-demand UX item `019e8f2489`; downstream of the tactical staleness bug `019e9aa093` (which only makes drift *detectable* — this DCR makes the refresh *correct*).","created_at":"2026-06-06T02:17:07","updated_at":"2026-06-06T02:17:20","priority":2,"tags":["cache","code-graph","codetools","coherence","design","index"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e34d4757c18ab2031b504b38719","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"codetools UI → HTML/D3 (CEF) panels: Code Graph + Runtime Diagnostics, narrow-first","description":"**Motivation:** the GDScript port of the code-magic visualizer froze a force layout into static positions and lost collision + progressive disclosure → unusable (overlap, squished, dumps all ~111 symbols). The GDScript choice was a workaround for \"Minerva couldn't render HTML\"; that's obsolete — plugins now render html panels via CEF (`CefWebViewEditor`, primary; WRY fallback). So we rebuild the panel in HTML/D3 (reusing the experiment's `graph.html`), which is legible, responsive (HTML/CSS), and performant (D3 in WebKit, not GDScript).\n\n**Owner decisions:** full code-intelligence surface in the graph panel (graph + diff + search + inspector + cluster/coupling/dead-code + annotation); a SECOND Runtime Diagnostics panel over `inspect` (warnings + clickable file:line); all other tools stay agent-only. **NARROW-FIRST** is the primary design target (the 3-pane chat|editor|notes layout means a panel's default size is a narrow ~1/3 column) — optimize narrow, scale up.\n\n**Substrate facts (nudge `codetools/cef-panel-substrate`):** CEF OSR-to-texture, SubViewport-wrapped (composites in-pane), forced software OSR (→ settle-then-freeze, narrow/few-nodes); same `window.minerva` bridge — `call()` = HTTP MCP (no size cap, used for get_graph/get_diff), `pluginIPC()` = allowlisted capability calls; CEF loads a materialized file:// temp with no base URI → inline d3 into one self-contained html (build step `scripts/build-ui-panels.py`). db/context injected via `window.__MINERVA_PANEL` (core change in CefWebViewEditor/WebViewEditor).\n\n**Scope / status (see plan for detail):**\n1. Marketplace packaging — `codetools.yml` `cp -r ui` ✅ (done) so html ships on release installs.\n2. `ui/code_graph.html` — D3 panel, narrow-first, bridge bootstrap, collision+seed+freeze, diff-focus. ✅ iteration 1 built (annotation/server-search/extra-modes/L1-boundary-boxes deferred to iter 2).\n3. `ui/runtime_diagnostics.html` — inspect warnings list + file:line jump. TODO.\n4. Manifest — code_graph kind godot_scene→html ✅; add runtime_diagnostics panel + editor_item; capability for open-file. (partial)\n5. Remove GDScript `ui/code_graph/` (7 .gd + .tscn). TODO (after html proven).\n6. Tests — flip `test_codetools_panel_gate` to kind=html; add panel-loads + marketplace-ui-present. TODO.\n7. Core gap — **no MCP path to open an html panel** (see linked bug); close it as part of this work.\n\n**Implements/supersedes UX batch items:** `019e8f29a2` (legibility), `019e8f2282` (responsive), `019e8e4a26` (Level-0 splash), `019e8f2489` (entry-point) — all dissolved by the HTML/D3 + narrow-first rebuild.\n\nMemory: [[project_codemagic_viz_approaches]], [[feedback_codetools_diff_is_focus_not_graph]], [[feedback_plugin_panels_narrow_first]].","created_at":"2026-06-06T18:32:02","updated_at":"2026-06-06T23:59:27","priority":2,"tags":["cef","code-graph","codetools","d3","html","narrow-first","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e351353762db627bfe2775e88c0","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"No MCP path to open an html (kind=html) plugin panel","description":"**Where:** `minerva_plugin_open_panel` validates panel kind and errors `Panel '<p>' on plugin '<id>' is not godot_scene (kind=html)`. `minerva_create_plugin_editor` (MCPGeneralTools.gd) errors `html_panel_anonymous_create_unsupported: kind=html` (it's for buffer-backed / paired_dsl editor_items). So there is NO MCP way to open a `kind:\"html\"` plugin panel.\n\n**Why it matters:** html panels render fine when opened via the UI (`singleton_object._open_plugin_panel_for_editor_item` → `EditorPane.add_plugin_panel_editor` → CefWebViewEditor), but an agent can't open them. This breaks the 'MCP drives all tasks' north star and blocks agent-driven HITL (surfaced 2026-06-06 building the codetools code_graph html panel — I could reinstall/start/configure it via MCP but had to ask the user to open it from File→New).\n\n**Fix:** route `minerva_plugin_open_panel` to open `kind==\"html\"` panels via the same editor-item path (`_open_plugin_panel_for_editor_item` / `add_plugin_panel_editor`) instead of rejecting them. Opening a NAMED panel is well-defined for html (unlike the anonymous-buffer create that `create_plugin_editor` does). Optionally also accept an `editor_item_id`/`filename` so the panel gets its opened-file context. DoD: `minerva_plugin_open_panel {plugin_id:\"codetools\", panel_name:\"code_graph\"}` opens the html panel end-to-end.\n\nFound via nudge `codetools/html-panel-no-mcp-open`. Part of DCR 019e9e34d475 (codetools HTML UI).","created_at":"2026-06-06T18:32:19","updated_at":"2026-06-07T02:23:24","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["cef","html","mcp","panel","plugins","webview"],"resolution":"Committed development 2eaa294d. minerva_plugin_open_panel now routes html (CEF) panels through _open_plugin_panel_for_editor_item; default panel resolution kind-agnostic. Headless boot clean. End-to-end open (codetools code_graph via MCP) verified in the consolidated HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e491bbe7c36b0ea392b06cb5038","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"codetools Code Graph: changeset-overview diff level (AI-era) + hunk navigation","description":"Owner HITL 2026-06-06: \"diffs by file is the pre-AI approach; agents make tons of changes, 1 file at a time isn't right. We need an OVERALL CHANGE level view, then the 1-file view (used sparingly — devs want LoC changed per file, then sample/inspect priority changes).\"\n\nBuild a **changeset overview** as the default diff landing: header `N files · +X/−Y`, list of changed files each with `+adds/−dels` (and status add/mod/del), sorted by churn; click a file → its diff. Also: the file diff's counter currently shows file position (x/y changed files) which reads as hunks — separate that from **hunk navigation** (prev/next hunk, \"hunk i/n\") within the open file. Unchanged-context collapse already done.\nParent: DCR 019e9e34d475.","created_at":"2026-06-06T18:54:11","updated_at":"2026-06-06T18:54:28","priority":2,"tags":["changeset","code-graph","codetools","diff","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e49245c7c7986938d4719bbff5a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"codetools Code Graph: scope the graph + breadcrumbs + node labels","description":"Owner HITL 2026-06-06: \"The graph is available but doesn't make sense — I can't tell if it's the call graph for the file, the diff, or the module. I need breadcrumbs and the ability to see labels on at least some items.\"\n\nAdd a persistent **breadcrumb** bar (project › boundary/file › symbol) that both orients and navigates; **scope** the graph to the breadcrumb level (project boundaries → file/module → symbol neighborhood — progressive disclosure) so it's never an unscoped 111-node blob; render **labels** (always when few nodes; otherwise on hover/selection + for high fan-in/out nodes). The inspector can stay a secondary drawer since the breadcrumb carries orientation. Implements UX items 019e8f2489 (entry/scope) + 019e8f29a2 (legibility). Parent: DCR 019e9e34d475.","created_at":"2026-06-06T18:54:14","updated_at":"2026-06-06T18:54:32","priority":2,"tags":["breadcrumbs","code-graph","codetools","graph","labels","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e4930d8729b8090331264da5ea9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"codetools Code Graph: annotations-as-conversation (review comments + invite chat)","description":"Owner HITL 2026-06-06: \"We have annotations in Minerva for feedback but aren't using them here. If I don't like a change I can't quickly comment. Minerva's native code editor supports comments; the plugin substrate also enables annotations. Think of comments as MORE than code feedback — a comment might invite a chat instead.\"\n\nWire Minerva's annotation substrate (plugin annotations + native code-editor comments) so a reviewer can attach a comment to a changed symbol / diff hunk; persist them. A comment offers two modes: (a) review feedback note, (b) **invite a chat** — open a Minerva chat seeded with the symbol/diff context for discussion. Investigate the annotation + chat-spawn host capabilities (host.* / minerva_create_chat / annotations_* ) and which need declaring in the manifest. Parent: DCR 019e9e34d475.","created_at":"2026-06-06T18:54:17","updated_at":"2026-06-06T23:59:29","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","chat","code-graph","codetools","review","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9e5f8fb275cda9cbbd855d6d18b2","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"MCP/sidecar annotation writes can't attach text-range comments (text_comment not globally registered)","description":"**Repro (2026-06-06):** `minerva_annotations_add(document_path=<a .gd file>, annotation={kind:\"text_comment\", anchor core/text.range, ...})` → `{ok:false, errors:[{code:\"enum\", field_path:\"kind\", message:\"Annotation kind 'text_comment' is not registered...\"}]}`.\n\n**Root cause:** `MCPAnnotationTools._get_registry()` falls back to a registry built with only `BuiltinKinds.register_all` (2d_arrow/2d_text/2d_region/2d_polyline/2d_highlight/measure_*/callout). `text_comment` (AnnotationTextComment) is registered only on a TextEditorAnnotationHost's per-editor registry when a text editor tab is open. So document_path/sidecar writes — and writes to files that aren't open, and synthetic docs — can't use the one kind the native code editor actually renders.\n\n**Impact:** blocks the platform-native path for agents/plugins to attach code review comments to files via MCP without first opening each file (e.g., codetools changeset/diff review comments; DCR 019e9e34d475 + work_item 019e9e4930d8).\n\n**Fix (done, awaiting restart+verify):** register `AnnotationTextComment` in `_get_registry()`'s fallback registry alongside BuiltinKinds, so text_comment is valid for global/sidecar adds (compatible with the native editor, same kind class). Verify: annotations_add(document_path=<closed .gd>, text_comment envelope) → ok; opening that file in the code editor shows it in the AnnotationDockPane.","created_at":"2026-06-06T19:18:43","updated_at":"2026-06-07T00:19:43","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["annotations","mcp","plugins","sidecar","text_comment"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"Citeable annotation references (C<n>): core, project-scoped, chat-resolvable","description":"## Motivation (from the 2026-06-06 design dialogue)\n\nReviewing an LLM change in the codetools Code Graph panel surfaced a UX problem: comments were authored in an HTML-panel-private composer, non-discoverable, with no native home. The owner reframed the whole feature:\n\n**Discussion lives in CHAT. Comments are NOT a discussion surface — they are a durable, anchored knowledge layer that AUGMENTS chat.** A comment is a *named reference*: a stable ID + an anchor (file / line-range / changeset) + intent (\"C7: x.gd belongs in y.gd\"; \"C12: lines 40-48 should be a linked list\"). The user cites those IDs in normal chat over time (\"remember C7? let's rethink it after this change\"); the LLM resolves the ID back to its CURRENT code + intent. What matters from the editor is therefore **a stable, citeable comment ID + its anchored data**, usable as a knowledge token across the conversation.\n\nThis dissolves the earlier \"two code-editor surfaces\" problem: the editor's job shrinks to *author a comment, surface its ID, jump to it*; chat does the rest.\n\n## Decisions locked\n1. **Core, not codetools-private.** This is a Minerva-core annotation capability, reusable by ALL annotation consumers (codetools first; CAD/PCB/etc. could cite annotations in chat too). codetools is a consumer, not a special case.\n2. **Namespace = per Minerva project**, keyed on a NEW stable `project_id` (UUID), NOT on project name/path (which don't exist while implicit and change on Save As).\n3. **Reuse the existing substrate unchanged**: the core `text_comment` kind (NO new kind), typed anchors + AnnotationAnchorRegistry/CoreAnchors repair + staleness + document_revision, AnnotationLifecycle (open/applied/resolved/stale), sidecar persistence (`<path>.annotations.json`), and the existing `AnnotationKind.to_chat_context()` projection. Verified: `AnnotationV2Schema.validate()` only checks the required set and does NOT reject unknown top-level fields; `serialize()` round-trips the whole dict — so new fields are additive and break no consumer (CAD/PCB/presentation/hello_scene read only fields they need).\n\n## Interaction model (consuming UX; governed by parent DCR 019e9e34d475)\n- **HTML/D3 panel = overview/launcher**: graph + churn-sorted changelist + breadcrumbs. (Keeps what native Godot can't do well.)\n- **Double-click a file → NATIVE code editor** (diff + line/file annotations), NOT the HTML diff. The native editor already has `EditorCodeEdit.preview_diff/apply_diff/_parse_diff` (autocoder uses them) + the annotation dock (`AnnotationDockPane`), so the parity gap is mostly wiring + a read-only review-diff mode.\n- **Chat = discussion.** Comments are citeable references, not a discussion thread. The synthetic-changeset-doc discussion path is demoted; changeset-level \"discuss this approach\" becomes a chat seeded with the changeset.\n\n## Comment ID design (`ref`)\n- `ref` = `C<n>`: project-global monotonic counter, **never reused**. Short/typeable + unique within the project (the GitHub `#123` model).\n- **Distinct from `display_index`** (keep per-host as-is — overloading it would disturb dock row-numbering for every existing consumer) **and from internal `id`** (`ann_...`, used for update/delete). New OPTIONAL top-level envelope fields: `ref` (e.g. \"C7\") + `ref_project` (the minting project_id). Additive; schema ignores unknowns.\n- **Cross-project**: a sidecar lives next to the source file (OUTSIDE the .minproj) and may be touched by more than one Minerva project (e.g. codetools reviewing a shared repo). `ref_project` lets us show bare `C7` for the owning project and qualified `<proj>-C7` for foreign ones, so refs never collide across projects.\n\n## Project identity + implicit -> explicit handling (the crux)\nMinerva has NO project identity today (projects tracked by name->path in config; `project_data` is a free-form dict zipped into `project.minproj`; implicit project is in-memory until saved).\n- Add `project_data[\"project_id\"]` (UUID) + `project_data[\"annotation_ref_seq\"]` (additive; serialized into .minproj automatically; restored on load).\n- **Mint `project_id` for the IMPLICIT project at startup** (exists before any save -> refs stable from the first comment).\n- **Save = promotion, not renumbering**: writes the existing project_id + seq into the .minproj. Keyed on UUID (not path), so every already-minted `C7` (already in file sidecars, tagged with that UUID) stays `C7`; Save As / move / rename are safe.\n- **Autosave the implicit project_id + seq to a `user://` scratch** so unsaved sessions stay coherent across restarts; promoted into the .minproj on explicit save (scratch cleared).\n- **Reconcile-on-load floor**: `seq = max(stored_seq, highest visible C<n> for this project_id)` — lifts the existing per-host max-reconcile pattern to project scope so a lost/stale counter can never reuse a number.\n- **Consequence (follows from per-project scoping)**: repo comments are scoped to the minting Minerva project; reopening a repo under a different project shows old comments as foreign/qualified and mints new ones fresh. Acceptable when a review happens within one project context.\n\n## Discoverability — \"how does the human know what C7 is?\" (never show ref naked)\n1. **Creation echoes it**: \"Created C7 - main.gd:331 _save_snapshot_png\".\n2. **At the anchor**: `C7` gutter/badge in the native editor dock + `C7` chip in the panel; hover -> full text.\n3. **Browsable index**: list C1..Cn with file:line + one-line summary + status (open/applied/resolved/stale) via an MCP tool + a UI list.\n4. **In chat**: `C7` renders as a first-class reference chip with hover preview (summary + location + CURRENT re-anchored code) + click-to-jump. LLM resolution injects the enriched context inline.\n5. **Bidirectional jump**: chat `C7` <-> editor/panel anchor.\n\n## Chat bridge (the genuinely new behavior)\nParse `C#` mentions in chat -> resolve by (project_id, ref) -> `repair_anchor` so resolution returns the CURRENT code at the anchor (not the stale snapshot) -> inject via the existing `to_chat_context()` -> with lifecycle/staleness status. The pieces exist (query, chat_context projection, repair_anchor, closed-file sidecar reads after fix 019e9e5f8fb2); the auto-wiring is new.\n\n## Phased plan\n- **P1 — Core project identity.** `project_id` (UUID) + `annotation_ref_seq` in `project_data`; implicit mint at startup; user:// scratch autosave; .minproj promotion on save; reconcile-on-load. (Foundation; no UI.)\n- **P2 — Ref allocator + envelope fields.** Vend `C<n>`; stamp `ref` + `ref_project` on `annotations_add`; expose in query results; resolve-by-ref MCP path (query filter + reverse lookup across sidecars/live hosts).\n- **P3 — Surface ref everywhere.** Dock/panel badges, creation echo, browsable index (MCP list + UI list view).\n- **P4 — Chat bridge.** Parse/resolve `C#` in chat, re-anchor, inject enriched context, click-to-jump, bidirectional.\n- **P5 — codetools consumer wiring.** Panel double-click -> native editor + read-only review-diff; changeset-level comment uses `ref`; demote synthetic-doc discussion to chat.\n\n## Open decisions / to verify\n- Whether `EditorCodeEdit.preview_diff` suffices for a READ-ONLY review diff or a new review-diff mode is needed (P5).\n- Exact cross-project qualifier format (`<docket-prefix>-C7` vs short-uuid).\n- Whether `display_index` is eventually subsumed by `ref` (default: keep both; ref is the human handle, display_index untouched for back-compat).\n- Where exactly the implicit project_id/seq scratch lives in user:// and whether Minerva already has an implicit-project autosave to piggyback on.\n\n## Relationships\n- Under / governed-by parent DCR 019e9e34d475 (codetools UI -> HTML/D3).\n- Reshapes 019e9e4930d8 (annotations-as-conversation -> codetools becomes a CONSUMER of this; discussion=chat, comments=references) and 019e9e49245c (scope/breadcrumbs/labels — the panel-as-launcher role).\n- Reuses substrate incl. the text_comment global-registration fix 019e9e5f8fb2.\n- No-break guarantees: schema forward-compatible (unknown top-level fields ignored + round-tripped); `display_index` per-host untouched; `text_comment` kind reused (no new kind).","created_at":"2026-06-06T23:58:58","updated_at":"2026-06-07T00:47:44","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","chat","citeable-ref","codetools","core","project-id","review","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9f8bc40c7cfe9a688d4407328b33","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1 — Core project identity (project_id + annotation_ref_seq)","description":"P1 of DCR 019e9f602391. Foundation for citeable refs: stable per-project identity.\n\nDONE (machine-verified, committed to development, NOT pushed):\n- New `src/Scripts/Models/ProjectIdentity.gd` (class_name, RefCounted): owns project_id (UUID) + annotation_ref_seq. Dependency-injected ConfigFile + path + uuid_provider so it's hermetically unit-testable. API: ensure() (restore-or-mint), start_new_implicit() (File→New fresh mint+zero), adopt_from_project_data() (load explicit; legacy migrates by minting; clears scratch), write_to_project_data() (single writer), reconcile_floor(highest) (never-reuse floor; P2 supplies the max C<n>), persist_scratch()/clear_scratch().\n- `singleton_object.gd`: `var project_identity: ProjectIdentity`; instantiated in _ready after config load, sharing config_file + Callable(self,\"generate_UUID\") (DRY — one id generator), then ensure().\n- `ProjectMenuActions.gd`: serialize_project stamps id/seq via write_to_project_data (flows through both plain-JSON and ProjectPackage zip writers); deserialize_project adopts; _new_project starts a fresh implicit; save_project clears the scratch (now explicit).\n- Persistence = minimal user:// scratch (config section ImplicitProject), per owner decision — NOT a full implicit-project autosave. Refs themselves persist in file sidecars; only project_id+seq need the scratch to survive a restart of an unsaved session.\n\nVerification: src/test/test_project_identity.gd 23/23 (mint, idempotent ensure, restart round-trip, fresh-mint-on-new, reconcile floor up/down, adopt existing/legacy, scratch clear, canonical-key round-trip). No regression: annotation_substrate 146/146, plugin_project_hooks 80/80, project_package_annotations 28/28.\n\nReuse-map findings that shaped it: NO implicit-project autosave existed to piggyback on (built minimal scratch); generate_UUID reused; _ensure_display_index reconcile pattern mirrored at project scope; AnnotationV2Schema forward-compat confirmed (no schema change). NOTE for editor: ProjectIdentity.gd / test_project_identity.gd .uid files will be generated on next editor open (currently registered manually in the gitignored global class cache for headless testing).","created_at":"2026-06-07T00:46:37","updated_at":"2026-06-07T00:47:48","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","citeable-ref","core","project-id"],"parent":"minerva:019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0","resolution":"Committed development 93c246bf. test_project_identity 23/23; no regressions. Foundation ready for P2 ref allocator."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9f9c7b9377b39908d3a53e60b69a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2 — Ref allocator + envelope fields (C&lt;n&gt;)","description":"P2 of DCR 019e9f602391. DONE — committed development 158d2ec2 (NOT pushed).\n\nVend per-project, human-typeable refs \"C<n>\" and stamp them onto annotations.\n- New AnnotationRef util (Services/Annotations/AnnotationRef.gd): format/parse + highest_seq_in_sidecars/_in_list (reconcile source).\n- ProjectIdentity: vend_ref() (monotonic, never reused), stamp() (idempotent), is_implicit + mark_explicit(), static current() with a _override test seam.\n- Stamp at BOTH non-converging create paths: TextEditorAnnotationHost.add_annotation_v2 (UI + live-host MCP + plugins) AND MCPAnnotationTools._annotations_add closed-file sidecar branch (codetools ann_bb0984 path). Idempotent → no double-stamp.\n- Reconcile-on-load: host load_annotations lifts counter from loaded refs; closed-file MCP add reconciles from target sidecar before vending → no reuse after a crash between saves.\n- Query: ref/ref_project filters (refs ride through output via existing full duplicate). New minerva_annotations_resolve_ref tool (by document_path or live-host scan; ref_project disambiguates) — the resolve seam P4 uses.\n- save_project now mark_explicit() (was clear_scratch()).\n\nBy-design: a validation-failed add burns a ref number (monotonic gaps OK, GitHub-issue style).\n\nVerified: test_project_identity 49/49; MCP wired-stamping in test_mcp_annotation_tools (C1→C2, reconcile to C6 from seeded C5, resolve_ref, query filter), suite 217/218 (1 pre-existing headless draw_string render fail). No regression: annotation_substrate 146, host_stamping 25, sidecar 44, authoring 30, project_package_annotations 28.\n\nNOTE: AnnotationRef.gd registered manually in the gitignored global class cache for headless testing; .uid + cache regenerate on next editor open.","created_at":"2026-06-07T01:04:53","updated_at":"2026-06-07T01:05:20","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","citeable-ref","core","mcp"],"parent":"minerva:019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0","resolution":"Committed development 158d2ec2. Allocator + stamping + resolve_ref + query filter machine-verified. Ready for P3 (surface ref) / P4 (chat bridge)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9fd4f9ba7cf5ab7f542136b39b0a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3 — Surface ref everywhere (echo + index; badges in HITL)","description":"P3 of DCR 019e9f602391. Autonomous half DONE — committed development 454b1b02 (NOT pushed). Badge RENDERING deferred to the final HITL.\n\n- Creation echo: minerva_annotations_add returns {id, ref, ref_project, echo} where echo = \"Created C7 — main.gd:331  <summary>\". Live-host branch stamps ref before host.add_annotation so it can echo; closed-file branch already had it.\n- Browsable index: new minerva_annotations_index tool — lists C1..Cn with location + summary + status, sorted by seq; scans document_path / editor_name / ALL live hosts; ref_project scope. Answers \"what is C7?\".\n- Dock data: AnnotationSidebarModel broken-entry projection carries ref (visible list already did via full duplicate).\n- DRY: resolve_ref + index share _host_annotations(); _ref_location_label/_anchor_line helpers.\n\nVerified: test_mcp_annotation_tools 226/227 (echo, index sort+status+line, ref_project filter); 1 pre-existing headless draw_string fail. annotation_substrate 146.\n\nHITL REMAINING (folds into P5 end-to-end): render the C<n> badge/chip in AnnotationDockPane (native dock) and the codetools CEF panel; the data is already present on each annotation dict.","created_at":"2026-06-07T02:06:35","updated_at":"2026-06-07T02:07:00","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","citeable-ref","mcp","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0","resolution":"Echo + index committed 454b1b02; machine-verified. Badge rendering folds into the P5 HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9fe1ac7570d4a427521b09ae23fd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P4 — Chat bridge (cite C&lt;n&gt; → current code)","description":"P4 of DCR 019e9f602391. Autonomous half DONE — committed development 1f8307d6 (NOT pushed). Chat-chip render + click-to-jump fold into final HITL.\n\n- New AnnotationRefResolver (Services/Annotations): parse_refs() (word-bounded \\bC[0-9]+\\b, deduped) + resolve_for_chat(text, project_id) → plain-text reference notes. Each note: ref + lifecycle/staleness + location + intent (summary or kind_payload.text) + CURRENT re-anchored code at the anchor (host.resolve_anchor for stale + get_text_content substr for current text). Scoped to project_id.\n- Wired into ChatPane.create_prompt (~line 1159): _resolve_cited_refs(append_item) merged into InjectedNotes alongside working memory.\n- KEY shape decision: providers consume InjectedNotes entries as STRINGS (folded into 'Reference Information'); ContextBlock dicts are silently dropped — so the bridge emits strings, NOT to_chat_context ContextBlock dicts. (to_chat_context remains the MCP-query projection.)\n\nVerified: test_annotation_ref_resolver 12/12 (parse boundaries/dedupe, current code + intent, project scoping, stale flag, unknown skip). Full headless app boot clean (ChatPane + new classes compile in autoload context).\n\nHITL REMAINING (folds into P5): render cited C<n> as a clickable chip in chat (MessageMarkdown BBCode [meta]) + click-to-jump to the anchor; verify the injected reference context reads well in a live chat. Resolution from CLOSED files (not open in a host) is out of scope — P4 resolves from live editor hosts; P5 codetools can pass file context.\n\nNOTE: AnnotationRefResolver.gd registered in the gitignored class cache for headless testing; .uid + cache regenerate on next editor open.","created_at":"2026-06-07T02:20:27","updated_at":"2026-06-07T02:20:54","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","chat","citeable-ref","core"],"parent":"minerva:019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0","resolution":"Resolver + ChatPane wiring committed 1f8307d6; 12/12 + clean boot. Chip render folds into P5 HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019e9fe873cf7647b0197f09555e6475","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"P5 — codetools consumer wiring + consolidated HITL","description":"P5 of DCR 019e9f602391. Minerva-side autonomous prep DONE — committed development 25ceef39 (NOT pushed). Panel-JS double-click + the consolidated visual HITL remain.\n\nDCR open question ANSWERED: EditorCodeEdit.preview_diff suffices for a review diff; only a read-only wrapper was missing.\n- EditorCodeEdit.preview_review_diff()/exit_review_diff(): reuse preview_diff's colored render, make buffer non-editable. preview_diff untouched (autocoder dep).\n- MCP minerva_editor_review_diff / minerva_editor_exit_review_diff (MCPEditorTools): drive the read-only diff on an open tab.\n- 'changeset-level comment uses ref' — already satisfied by P2 (panel MCP comments auto-stamped C<n>).\n\nHandoff path now complete & MCP-reachable: minerva_plugin_open_panel (html, bug 019e9e351353) → panel → minerva_open_file → minerva_editor_review_diff → minerva_annotations_add (auto C<n> + echo) → minerva_annotations_index → cite C<n> in chat (P4 resolver injects current code+intent).\n\nREMAINING (HITL, mostly plugins repo + visual):\n1. codetools panel (ui/code_graph.src.html, plugins repo): on file double-click call minerva_open_file + minerva_editor_review_diff instead of the in-panel HTML diff. Demote synthetic-changeset-doc discussion to chat.\n2. Render C<n> as a clickable chip in chat (MessageMarkdown BBCode [meta]) + click-to-jump; and the C<n> badge in AnnotationDockPane + panel (data already present).\n3. Visual review-diff appearance.\n4. End-to-end HITL: LLM change → drill-down → comment + discuss at changelist & file level, citing C<n> in chat.","created_at":"2026-06-07T02:27:51","updated_at":"2026-06-07T07:32:42","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","citeable-ref","codetools","hitl","review","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e9f6023917d4b89150cf6b8c9c4b0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea000db7773e0b7e7a1828eeacf46","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"P5 sub-plan — consolidate on the native editor (remove code-magic diff/edit, unify refs/UI)","description":"Owner-confirmed 2026-06-06 (HITL). The codetools panel ported code-magic's WHOLE editor; we only want its graph + changeset overview. Collapse the two diff/edit surfaces onto the Minerva NATIVE editor; panel becomes a launcher.\n\nKEY FINDING: the panel allocates comment refs CLIENT-SIDE by array order (code_graph.src.html:455 `c.ref='C'+(i+1)`) — transient, and DISAGREES with the core `ref` (P2). Migration makes the panel read the envelope `ref` so panel/native-dock/chat agree.\n\nDUPLICATE-BY-INTENT → disposition (grounded in code_graph.src.html + worker/code_visualizer.py):\n- per-file diff (openFileDiff/lcsDiff/collapseDiff 404-430) duplicates EditorCodeEdit.preview_diff → DELETE.\n- comment composer (openComposer/saveComposer 467-499) duplicates native annotation dock authoring → DELETE.\n- panel-local ref counter (455) → REPLACE with envelope `ref`.\n- hunk nav (gotoHunk/updateHunkLabel 427-428) — native LACKS it → MOVE to native.\n- KEEP in panel: graph (get_graph+D3), changeset/churn (b), breadcrumbs (d), outline/chrome (g).\n\nORPHAN-AVOIDANCE: panel computes diff client-side from get_diff before/after; churn() also uses lcsDiff. Fix by pushing diff compute to the WORKER (Python difflib): get_diff returns per file a unified-diff string + {adds,dels}. Then native review-diff gets a ready diff, churn reads counts, lcsDiff/collapseDiff/openFileDiff delete cleanly. get_diff stays (feeds churn + native review). annotations_list/add stay (core).\n\nUI BOUNDARY (by scope): panel owns CHANGESET nav (breadcrumbs + churn list; double-click file → minerva_open_file/create review buffer + minerva_editor_review_diff). Native editor owns FILE review (hunk nav as a review-mode toolbar reusing preview_diff's change lines; auto-reveal first hunk). C<n> badges read the envelope ref everywhere.\n\nREVIEW-BUFFER SEMANTICS (decision): review shows BASE + colored overlay (unified-diff style, GitHub-like), read-only. Handoff seeds a read-only editor tab with get_diff.before_content and calls preview_review_diff(unified_diff) — works with existing tools (create_text_editor + editor_review_diff); editing the real file is a separate normal open.\n\nPHASES:\n- P5a (worker, plugins repo): get_diff returns unified_diff + {adds,dels} per file; worker tests; rebuild PBS bundle + go build.\n- P5b (Minerva): EditorCodeEdit next_change()/prev_change() (reuse change lines) + a review-mode hunk-nav toolbar + auto-reveal first hunk; MCP minerva_editor_next_change/prev_change. Headless-testable methods; toolbar = HITL.\n- P5c (panel, plugins repo JS): delete diff/composer/lcs/hunk DOM; double-click file → handoff to native; churn from worker counts; comments list reads envelope ref. Visual HITL.","created_at":"2026-06-07T02:54:31","updated_at":"2026-06-07T03:19:08","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","citeable-ref","codetools","refactor","review","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e9fe873cf7647b0197f09555e6475"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea01719a27bc0822121fc5f5d372f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Back the review diff with a core edit JOURNAL (replace git get_diff as the diff source)","description":"CORRECTION of a mis-design: the diff feature was ported wholesale from the code-magic experiment and reconstructs diffs from GIT (codetools get_diff: working-tree vs HEAD). That conflates the agent's edits with unrelated churn, needs git, forces LCS guessing, and gives NO attribution. Better backbone: a core EDIT JOURNAL at Minerva's single buffer choke point.\n\nKEY ENABLER: every buffered edit — agent AND human — funnels through DocumentBuffer.apply_edit() (DocumentBuffer.gd:73), which fires text_changed(text, version). The human UI editor routes there too (Editor._on_editor_changed → _document_buffer.apply_edit, Editor.gd:1901). So ONE journal at apply_edit captures human + agent uniformly, tagged by source. This also DISSOLVES the A/B/C review-render fork: review opens the REAL file (refs/comments anchor correctly) and highlights the EXACT changed ranges from the journal — no git, no synthetic buffer, no LCS.\n\nDESIGN (rubric-scored: reliability→durability→cost→readability→DRY→well-factored):\n- Granularity (1b): coalesced edit METADATA stream ({path,version,revision,ts,source}; NO full text per edit) + checkpoint baselines. Baselines are the diff truth; metadata gives attribution (ai vs human within a changeset).\n- Checkpoint model (2d): ONE rolling in-memory log + auto MARKERS at turn-start (ChatPane.create_prompt), save (buffer.saved), commit. \"Per-turn\" / \"since save\" are queries over it. Default review view = since last turn-start marker.\n- Storage (3b): one BASELINE text snapshot per file per changeset marker; diff computed on demand by the difflib _unified_diff helper added in P5a (DRY reuse). Not full before/after per edit.\n- Persistence (4a now / 4b later): in-memory (session) v1 — reliability+cost outrank durability, and a review journal's value is mostly intra-session. Build behind P1's project_id seam so per-.minproj persistence is a drop-in later. (Honest rubric tension: deferring durability to protect reliability+cost.)\n- disk_write/external (5b): journal BUFFERED edits only; minerva_disk_write bypasses the buffer (lands as reload_from_disk, unreliable pre-state) → emit a bare 'external change' marker, don't fake a diff. Convention: code edits via doc_* (reviewable); disk_write for non-document I/O.\n- vs git get_diff (6): additive, keep both. Journal = live/attributed/session (panel default); git = vs-committed repo truth (secondary lens).\n\nPRECEDENT to mirror: SpreadsheetHistory (HistoryAction{type,data{old,new}} + stacks). NO text edit journal exists today (only DocumentBuffer.version counter + AnnotationResolveCache revision for anchor invalidation).\n\nCONSUMERS: native review editor (real file + journal ranges → 'Option C' w/o git/synthetic; reuses P5b hunk-nav), codetools panel changeset view, autocoder. A comment (C<n>) can cite a changeset entry.\n\nSEQUENCING: PAUSE P5c (panel strip-down) until this exists — otherwise we'd wire the panel to git get_diff and rip it out again. Then P5c wires double-click → native review backed by the journal.\n\nLikely sub-tasks: (a) core ChangeJournal service on DocumentBuffer.text_changed + markers; (b) baseline snapshots + on-demand unified diff; (c) MCP surface (e.g. journal_changes(since_marker), journal_mark); (d) native review render from journal ranges on the real file; (e) P5c panel rewrite to consume it.","created_at":"2026-06-07T03:18:48","updated_at":"2026-06-07T08:52:29","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","codetools","core","diff","journal","mis-design-fix","review"],"parent":"minerva:019ea000db7773e0b7e7a1828eeacf46","resolution":"Change Journal v1 shipped + LIVE HITL passed 2026-06-07 (Minerva development b9f3871a/50bd3a3d). Delivered (a) core ChangeJournal.gd on DocumentBuffer.buffer_created/apply_edit + attribute_next_edit_to(\"ai\") one-shot; (b) TextLineDiff.gd baseline-vs-current + aligned_rows_for; (c) MCP minerva_journal_mark/changes/diff. Captures buffered edits only (doc_*/host.documents + human editor) — raw disk_write/bash NOT captured by design. test_change_journal 14/14, test_text_line_diff 35/35. (d) native review render = side-by-side widget 019ea06a1413 (done). DEFERRED per design: in-memory/session only (durability traded for reliability+cost; per-.minproj persistence is a drop-in later behind the P1 project_id seam); (e) P5c panel strip-down carved into follow-up 019ea0fff0d4."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea035bb2871ed9a021ae58172d682","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Restore agentic file read/write/edit — as a CORE buffered capability codetools re-brands (not raw-disk)","description":"GAP found 2026-06-07: the extraction (ee56eaf4, DCR 019e7b6609 P2.3) HARD-REMOVED core CodeTools ReadTool/WriteTool/EditTool.gd (MCP names minerva_file_read/_write/_edit) but P2.1 only reimplemented glob/grep/bash/cwd in the worker — read/write/edit were NEVER re-added. Not orphaned: dropped. So codetools (the agentic read/write/debug/visualize suite) has NO read/write/edit; agents fall back to bash (raw disk, un-journaled) or core minerva_doc_*/minerva_disk_*.\n\nOLD TOOLS (reviewed, from ee56eaf4^): WriteTool.write_file(path,content) = FileAccess.WRITE + mkdir -p, raw disk. EditTool.edit_file(path,old,new,replace_all) = find/replace, raw disk (find/replace model == minerva_doc_edit + my Edit). ReadTool = raw read. ALL raw-disk, NO buffer, NO logging — DO NOT restore as-is (would bypass buffer→journal→annotations→activity-log).\n\nDESIGN DECISION (recommended; owner to confirm): file read/write is a GENERIC agentic-framework primitive (agents read configs, write reports, process data — not only code), so it belongs in CORE, not codetools. It ALREADY lives in core: minerva_doc_* (buffered via DocumentRegistry/DocumentBuffer → journaled + annotation-coherent + activity-logged at the apply_edit choke point) + minerva_disk_* (raw). Therefore:\n- Keep file I/O CORE; do NOT move/reimplement it in the codetools worker (raw-disk would fragment it, re-break journal/activity-log, violate DRY/durability).\n- codetools RE-BRANDS: thin codetools file_read/file_write/file_edit that DELEGATE to the buffered core path (via host.documents.* capability -> apply_edit), so codetools is a coherent toolset AND edits stay buffered/journaled/logged. Re-brand, not reimplement.\n- bash 'just calls shell' (raw escape hatch, un-journaled by design; surfaces via reload for open files only).\nAlternative the owner may prefer: no codetools aliases at all — document that codetools agents edit via core minerva_doc_*. (Recommendation: thin re-brand for toolset coherence.)\n\nACTIVITY LOG: exists (ActivityLogEntry/ActivityLogPanel, 'Activity: MCP' tab — MCP tool-call trace). Likely the 'log on file change' feature the owner remembers; partially degraded because file-change visibility depends on the change going through a logged MCP tool. Sub-tasks: (a) verify file-change events surface in the Activity Log; (b) the ChangeJournal (019ea01719a2) is the right data backbone for 'what files changed' and can feed the Activity Log.\n\nSEQUENCING: precondition for the journal (019ea01719a2) to be useful — no point reviewing agent edits made via a path the journal can't see. Land the buffered codetools write path before/with the journal's review render.\n\nSub-tasks: (1) confirm core minerva_doc_* covers read/write/edit semantics parity with the old tools (find/replace edit, mkdir-p write, dir-relative cwd resolution); (2) host.documents path-based buffered write capability if missing (set_state is editor_name-based); (3) codetools thin file_read/file_write/file_edit delegating tools (Go+worker -> host capability); (4) activity-log file-change verification + journal feed; (5) skill/docs so agents prefer buffered file_* over bash for code edits.","created_at":"2026-06-07T03:52:16","updated_at":"2026-06-07T08:53:01","priority":2,"tags":["activity-log","codetools","core","extraction-gap","files","journal","mis-design-fix"],"parent":"minerva:019e7b66091d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea06a14137791aeb7875e5142a533","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Side-by-side (Beyond Compare-style) review widget, journal-backed + annotation-safe","description":"The VISUAL review render for the change journal (was 'Stage D'). Owner prefers a Beyond Compare / Odd style TWO-PANE side-by-side diff over inline ghosts.\n\nWHY side-by-side (decided 2026-06-07): inline ghosts in a single editor are NOT annotation-safe — the host's _get_text() reads _code_edit.text directly, so ghosts would corrupt anchor resolution, and the overlay's doc→screen transform would misplace badges. Side-by-side solves it BY CONSTRUCTION: RIGHT pane = the real after-file (a normal EditorCodeEdit) → annotations/refs anchor to real char offsets with zero tricks; LEFT pane = read-only 'before' viewer where deletions (the ghost rows) live, never touching the real buffer. Deletions = left rows (red), additions = right rows (green).\n\nSub-tasks:\n- T1 (foundation, headless-testable): TextLineDiff.aligned_rows() — emit ordered [{op:equal|add|del|modify, left_line?, left_text?, right_line?, right_text?}] from the existing _lcs_ops script, for the two-pane layout.\n- T2: SideBySideDiff widget (new Control): HSplit; left read-only before-viewer + right real EditorCodeEdit; synchronized scroll; alignment GAP rows (DRAWN, not inserted into the right buffer, so it stays pure); tints (right green adds via set_line_background_color, left red dels).\n- T3: hunk-nav + visible 'change i/N' + ▲▼ + exit review toolbar (no review toolbar exists in Editor.gd today — the owner-flagged gap).\n- T4: MCP trigger — minerva_editor_review_journal(path/editor): pull journal diff (baseline vs current) → render in the widget. (Supersedes the P5b unified-diff-string minerva_editor_review_diff for journal use.)\n- T5: annotation-safety verification — right pane = real file, comments anchor correctly; left pane reference-only.\n- Fast-follow: intra-line CHAR highlighting (char-LCS on modified line pairs) — Beyond Compare's within-line coloring. Line-level first.\n\nConsumer: P5c (codetools panel double-click → open file → this widget) — tracked in sub-plan 019ea000db77. Foundation reuses: TextLineDiff (019ea01719a2), journal_diff data (proven in HITL comment 442), P5b hunk-nav primitives (rewire to journal set).","created_at":"2026-06-07T04:49:27","updated_at":"2026-06-07T07:33:43","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","codetools","diff","journal","review","stage-d","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019ea01719a27bc0822121fc5f5d372f","resolution":"Side-by-side review widget (T1-T5) + the panel→native review loop built and HITL-passed 2026-06-07. In-diff commenting clunkiness + chip/badge rendering + panel strip-down + graph usability split into follow-up 019ea0fff0d4."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea0fff0d47a22b796d431b588f02e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Review/annotation polish + code-graph usability (post-loop follow-up)","description":"Deferred polish after the review loop + T5 comment HITL-passed 2026-06-07 (\"good enough for tonight, revisit later\"). The substrate + loop work; this is UX refinement.\n\nANNOTATION/REVIEW POLISH:\n- Commenting from the side-by-side diff is CLUNKY: Option B delegates authoring to a separate real editor tab (SideBySideDiff.gd 💬 → Editor.begin_add_comment_at_line → native dock). Owner wants to revisit so commenting feels in-place. Options to weigh (rubric): (A) give the diff its own in-place annotation surface (the right pane becomes a real host-backed EditorCodeEdit so the dock/gutter/list attach IN the diff — requires solving phantom gap-row rendering so the right buffer == real file; the reason A was deferred); (B) keep delegation but make it smoother (e.g. open the editor split beside the diff, or inline the dock input as a popover anchored to the clicked line). Decide A vs B-improved later.\n- Render C<n> as a clickable chat CHIP (MessageMarkdown BBCode [meta]) + C<n> badge in AnnotationDockPane/panel (data already present; rendering not done) — the P4 view-layer.\n- P5c codetools panel strip-down: delete the now-dead in-panel openFileDiff/lcsDiff/composer JS now that the native side-by-side owns review (plugins ui/code_graph.src.html + rebuild).\n\nCODE-GRAPH USABILITY:\n- The graph/changeset panel UX (legibility, entry point, responsiveness, splash) — see the older backlog items 019e8f2489 / 019e8f29a2 / 019e8f2282 / 019e8e4a26. Owner flagged \"graph usability\" as a later pickup alongside the annotation polish.\n\nCONTEXT: review loop = code_graph changeset → double-click → native SideBySideDiff → 💬 → real editor dock → C<n>. Widget 019ea06a1413 done; journal 019ea01719a2 v1 done; file_* 019ea035bb28 done; host client 019e8f811497 (bash→terminal.exec still open).","created_at":"2026-06-07T07:33:08","updated_at":"2026-06-07T07:33:18","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","codetools","follow-up","graph","polish","review","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019e9fe873cf7647b0197f09555e6475"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea404ffcd7477a59945dab9195cbc","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Layered undo: undo-aware buffer sync + journal coarse/attributed revert + concurrency rail","description":"## Motivation\nThe Coding-skill UX target (settled with owner 2026-06-07): the agent edits AUTONOMOUSLY, edits land on disk so they \"compile now,\" and the HUMAN can ask for undo. Investigating how to deliver that against Minerva's real code surfaced a latent bug and an architecture decision.\n\n## How undo works today (verified in code)\n- UI button/shortcut: vboxEditor._on_undo_button_pressed() (vboxEditor.gd:904) -> current_tab.undo_action().\n- Per-editor-type dispatcher: Editor.undo_action() (Editor.gd:1943) match on Type: TEXT -> code_edit.undo() (native Godot CodeEdit stack); GRAPHICS -> graphics_editor.undo_command(); PCB -> pcb_editor.data.undo() (PCBData.gd:563, data-model undo); SPREADSHEET -> spreadsheet_editor._perform_undo() (SpreadsheetHistory stacks, + the only MCP undo: minerva_undo_spreadsheet).\n- Pattern: each editor type owns its fine-grained undo; the dispatcher calls it. Non-text editors put undo()/redo() on their backing MODEL.\n- Undo.gd / undoMain is UNRELATED (3-minute closed-tab restore), not edit undo.\n\n## The latent bug\nTEXT undo = code_edit.undo() on the native stack. But the shared-buffer->editor sync does `code_edit.text = new_text` (Editor.gd:1010, in _set_code_edit_text_from_buffer). Assigning TextEdit.text CLEARS Godot's native undo history. So today, any agent/MCP edit to an open text file wipes the user's undo stack — the button can't undo the agent's edit, and the human's prior history is lost too. An autonomous agent triggers this constantly.\n\n## Decision (rubric-scored: reliability,durability,performance,debuggability,cost,discoverable,user-visible)\nReject \"pin ALL undo to the journal\" (option B). The ChangeJournal is a TEXT, two-snapshot store at the apply_edit choke point (_baselines vs _current; diff_for = TextLineDiff). It is (1) COARSE — baseline+current, no intermediate keystrokes (per-edit storage was rejected for cost), so journal-driven Ctrl+Z would jump a whole changeset, wrong for interactive typing; and (2) TEXT-ONLY — it cannot represent spreadsheet cell ops, PCB moves, graphics/video domain actions. Journal-everywhere would force lossy state-to-text diffing OR converge to a generic typed-action log = reinventing SpreadsheetHistory/PCBData/command-pattern, plus reimplementing caret/keystroke text undo Godot gives free. Cost L, reliability low.\n\nChosen architecture = LAYERED (option A, S now), composing:\n1. Fine-grained interactive undo stays in the per-editor native/domain stacks (reuse). Make the TEXT apply-path UNDO-AWARE: replace the whole-text `code_edit.text =` assignment with undo-recording ops wrapped in begin_complex_operation()/end_complex_operation() (ideally a minimal range patch preserving caret/scroll). Then the EXISTING button undoes agent edits (one undo step per agent edit/batch), human history survives, and the wipe bug is fixed.\n2. Coarse, attributed, cross-file rollback = the JOURNAL (journal_revert): the ONLY layer that can do attribution (ai vs human), closed files, cross-file whole-turn rollback, and an MCP-driven undo (no MCP text-undo exists today). It applies the baseline back THROUGH apply_edit — so a journal revert is itself one undoable step on the native stack. The two layers COMPOSE; the journal drives the native stack, it doesn't compete.\n3. Optimistic-concurrency rail: doc_edit/doc_write accept if_match_version (precedent: nudge_set_hint), version-checked at apply_edit; reject stale writes with {want,got}; agent re-reads + retries. Prevents the whole-buffer last-write-wins clobber when human + agent edit the same file (apply_edit replaces whole text, no merge/lock today).\n\n## Consequences\n- codetools/other plugins get working text undo on the existing button FOR FREE (their edits route core minerva_doc_* -> buffer -> open TEXT editor). No codetools undo code.\n- Plugin-OWNED editor types still participate by implementing undo()/redo() on their model (PCB precedent). Making the dispatcher duck-typed (so arbitrary plugin types plug in without editing Editor.gd's match) is deferred to the north-star DCR.\n\n## North star (separate DCR, follow_up)\n\"Journaling everywhere\" done RIGHT = the journal as a generic ATTRIBUTED-CHANGESET interface (markers + ai/human attribution) that the existing per-type histories (SpreadsheetHistory, PCBData, text buffer) REPORT INTO — not a text journal swallowing everything. Then every editor (incl. plugins) gets agent-change review + coarse \"undo the agent's turn\" while keeping its purpose-built fine-grained undo. Cost L; this DCR (A) is deliberately compatible with evolving toward it.\n\n## Scope (this DCR = A-now)\nP1 undo-aware TEXT apply-path; P2 journal_revert (coarse/attributed/cross-file/MCP); P3 concurrency rail. All CORE, all S. codetools change = thin pass-through only.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:37:31","updated_at":"2026-06-08T00:32:26","priority":2,"tags":["autonomous-agent","codetools","concurrency","core","documents","journal","undo"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4055a217338af235ee0fbf26e2c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P1 — Undo-aware TEXT apply-path (fix native-stack wipe; agent edits undoable on existing button)","description":"PRIMARY change. Today Editor._set_code_edit_text_from_buffer (Editor.gd:1006-1011) does `code_edit.text = new_text`, which clears Godot's native undo history; so any agent/MCP edit to an open text file wipes the user's undo stack and the existing button (code_edit.undo()) can't undo agent edits.\n\nFix: mutate via undo-RECORDING ops (insert_text/remove_text/insert_text_at_caret), wrapped in code_edit.begin_complex_operation()/end_complex_operation() so each agent edit (or per-turn batch) is ONE undo step. Prefer a minimal range patch (diff old vs new) to preserve caret + scroll. Keep the _applying_buffer_text echo guard.\n\nOutcome: existing Undo button/shortcut works unchanged (still calls code_edit.undo()); agent edits become undoable; human undo history survives agent edits; caret/scroll no longer jump on agent edits; codetools/other plugins editing via core minerva_doc_* inherit this for free. DoD: headless CodeEdit test proving native undo survives an agent edit and the button reverts it; regression green.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:37:54","updated_at":"2026-06-07T21:55:51","priority":2,"tags":["bugfix","core","documents","p1","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea404ffcd","resolution":"P1 complete. Re-scoped per proof-first (no undo wipe on Godot 4.6.1): T1.1 caret/scroll preservation, T1.2 obsolete (text= already one undoable step), T1.3 regression test 9/9. Committed 191c2ab4. In-app caret behavior to be confirmed at the final HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea40563137cd5aa566042c29e6c95","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P2 — Journal-backed coarse/attributed revert (closed-file, cross-file turn, MCP)","description":"COMPLEMENTARY to P1. The ChangeJournal already holds per-changeset baselines + ai/human attribution; this exposes a revert arm for what native per-editor undo can't do.\n\nWork: ChangeJournal.revert(path | changeset) writes the baseline back via DocumentBuffer.apply_edit (+ optional save_to_disk), re-entrancy-guarded so the resulting text_changed isn't re-recorded as a new edit. New MCP minerva_journal_revert. Attribution-aware mode (revert only edits attributed \"ai\" since a marker) where feasible. Because revert routes through apply_edit, with P1 it lands as a single undoable step on the native stack (composes, doesn't compete).\n\nDoD: revert works on a CLOSED file (no open editor); cross-file whole-turn revert; \"revert is itself one Ctrl+Z\" verified with P1; re-entrancy guard tested; clear envelope. Sequencing: lands with/after P1 so the compose-via-buffer property holds.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:37:57","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:02:24","priority":2,"tags":["core","journal","mcp","p2","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea404ffcd","resolution":"P2 complete: ChangeJournal.revert + revert_changeset (attribution-aware) + MCP minerva_journal_revert. Composes with P1 (routes through apply_edit). 30/30 journal tests. Committed abbb4546. MCP round-trip to be confirmed at final HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea40573c77b13b587304d3d23f47a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"P3 — Optimistic-concurrency rail (if_match_version) against human+agent clobber","description":"Today apply_edit (DocumentBuffer.gd:73) replaces whole text with no version check, no merge, no lock; if the human types between an agent read and its write, the agent's apply_edit silently overwrites it (and vice versa). DocumentBuffer already carries a `version` counter.\n\nWork: doc_edit/doc_write (MCPDocTools) accept optional if_match_version; reject with a clear version_mismatch envelope {want, got} when the buffer moved; agent guidance = re-read then retry. Precedent: nudge_set_hint.if_match_version. codetools file_edit forwards the version param (thin pass-through; no new logic). Default (no version passed) = current last-write-wins behavior, so nothing breaks.\n\nDoD: concurrent-edit test (stale write rejected, fresh write accepted); codetools delegate forwards + surfaces the mismatch; regression green. Independent of P1/P2.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:01","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:12:50","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","concurrency","core","documents","p3"],"parent":"minerva:019ea404ffcd","resolution":"P3 complete: if_match_version optimistic-concurrency rail on doc_write/doc_edit (DocVersionGuard) + codetools delegate forwarding. 14/14 + hermetic 4/4. Minerva 85b1abe2, plugins 42ec462. End-to-end MCP clobber-rejection to be confirmed at final HITL."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea40626a7761e8fd86c4402681d02","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1.1 — Undo-aware range-patch sync in _set_code_edit_text_from_buffer","description":"Core edit. Compute old-vs-new minimal range (or select-all+insert as a v1 fallback), apply via insert_text/remove_text within code_edit.begin_complex_operation()/end_complex_operation() so the native undo stack records it as one step. Save + restore caret line/column and v-scroll. Keep _applying_buffer_text=true around the mutation so the resulting text_changed doesn't echo into the buffer. DoD: agent edit to an open file no longer clears native undo; caret/scroll stable.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:47","updated_at":"2026-06-07T21:55:43","priority":2,"tags":["core","task","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea4055a21","resolution":"Caret(line,col)+scroll(v,h) save/restore (clamped to new text) added to Editor._set_code_edit_text_from_buffer; kept the proven-undoable `text=`. Committed 191c2ab4."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4062f7a7f08922c902715533890","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1.2 — One complex-operation per agent edit / per-turn batch (changeset-grained undo)","description":"Decide granularity: per-apply_edit (simplest) vs per-turn batch (wrap a turn's edits in one complex operation, aligned to the journal's turn-start marker). Recommend per-apply_edit v1, with a follow-up for turn-batching keyed off the existing ChangeJournal turn marker. DoD: undoing once reverts a whole agent edit, not a half-applied range.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:49","updated_at":"2026-06-07T21:52:33","priority":2,"tags":["core","task","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea4055a21","resolution":"OBSOLETE per P1 proof (DCR comment 452). Godot 4.6.1 records `code_edit.text = new_text` as a single undoable step preserving prior history, so no per-edit complex_operation wrapping is needed for changeset-grained undo. Closed without code; behavior locked instead by T1.3's regression test."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea406385c7ac5bb45fa10bdca0308","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1.3 — Headless test: native undo survives agent edit + button reverts it","description":"GDScript headless test driving a real Editor/EditorCodeEdit + DocumentBuffer. Steps: human types A -> agent apply_edit B via buffer -> assert undo() yields A (agent edit reverted, not stack-wiped) -> undo() again yields pre-A. Also assert caret/scroll preserved across the agent edit. Mirrors the multi-skill-manifest proof methodology (owner wants evidence, not doc claims).","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:51","updated_at":"2026-06-07T21:55:47","priority":2,"tags":["task","test","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea4055a21","resolution":"test_buffer_sync_undo_caret.gd (9/9): locks undoable-`text=` contract (one step + history preserved + redo) and caret preserve/clamp. Registered in run-functional-tests.sh hermetic suite. Committed 191c2ab4."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea406414e788eaf99d3122b04475e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.1 — ChangeJournal.revert(path|changeset) via apply_edit, re-entrancy guarded","description":"ChangeJournal currently is read-only (mark/changed_paths/diff_for/...). Add revert(path) and revert_changeset(label?) that get_or_create_buffer(path) and apply_edit(baseline), optionally save. Suppression flag so _on_text_changed ignores the revert write (or re-baselines cleanly). Because it routes through apply_edit, with P1 the revert is one undoable native step. DoD: in-memory baseline restored; no recursive re-record.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:53","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:02:06","priority":2,"tags":["core","journal","task","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40563137cd5aa566042c29e6c95","resolution":"ChangeJournal.revert(path, save) restores the changeset baseline via DocumentBuffer.apply_edit (propagates to open editor + one undoable step), re-entrancy guarded (_reverting). Committed abbb4546."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4064a6374f2be235ac73e809387","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.2 — MCP minerva_journal_revert tool","description":"Register minerva_journal_revert in the journal MCP module (sibling of minerva_journal_mark/changes/diff). Args: path? | changeset? | scope=turn, save?. Result: {reverted:[paths], skipped:[...]}. Surfaces to the agent so \"undo what I just did\" is MCP-reachable. DoD: round-trip on an open and a closed file.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:56","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:02:10","priority":2,"tags":["journal","mcp","task","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40563137cd5aa566042c29e6c95","resolution":"minerva_journal_revert registered in MCPDocTools (path | changeset | source, save). The only MCP-driven text/file undo; works on closed files. Committed abbb4546."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea406534773779f6d904f57e8ce95","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.3 — Attribution-aware revert (undo only AI edits since a marker)","description":"Uses ChangeJournal's ai/human attribution (attribute_next_edit_to). v1 may be coarse (whole-changeset to baseline); this task explores selective AI-only revert. May be limited when human+AI edits overlap the same lines — document the fallback (revert whole changeset). DoD: AI-only revert on a non-overlapping interleaved case; documented limitation on overlap.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:38:58","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:02:15","priority":3,"tags":["attribution","journal","task","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40563137cd5aa566042c29e6c95","resolution":"Path-level attribution filter: revert_changeset(save, source_filter) reverts only files whose LAST edit matches source (e.g. \"ai\"), skipping human-last files. Documented limitation: path-granular — a file the AI touched last reverts wholesale even if the human edited it earlier in the changeset (true line-level AI-only revert deferred to north-star DCR 019ea406da3f). Test-covered. Committed abbb4546."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4065c297b7cbef821cffae25086","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.4 — Tests: closed-file revert, cross-file turn revert, revert-is-one-undo-step","description":"test_change_journal additions: (1) revert a file with no open editor; (2) two-file turn -> revert restores both baselines; (3) open editor + P1 -> journal revert then code_edit.undo() returns to pre-revert (compose property); (4) re-entrancy: revert doesn't spawn a phantom changeset. DoD: all green headless.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:00","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:02:19","priority":2,"tags":["journal","task","test","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40563137cd5aa566042c29e6c95","resolution":"test_change_journal +16 assertions (30/30 total) over real DocumentRegistry: closed-file revert + no-op, save-to-disk, cross-file turn revert, attribution filter, re-entrancy + post-revert edit re-records as human. Committed abbb4546."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea40664f978ec94527e2b30cfa47a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T3.1 — if_match_version on doc_edit/doc_write + version check at apply_edit","description":"DocumentBuffer already has `version`. In MCPDocTools edit/write paths, if if_match_version is provided and != buffer.version, return {error:\"version_mismatch\", want, got} without writing. Agent guidance: re-read (doc_read returns version) then retry. No change to apply_edit signature required if the check sits in the tool layer; alternatively add a checked apply variant. DoD: stale write rejected; fresh write accepted; omitted param = unchanged.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:03","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:12:37","priority":2,"tags":["concurrency","core","documents","task"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40573c77b13b587304d3d23f47a","resolution":"doc_write/doc_edit accept if_match_version; version-checked at apply_edit via DocVersionGuard (extracted, dependency-free, unit-testable headless). Rejects stale writes {success:false, code:\"version_mismatch\", error w/ want+got}. Default (omitted) = last-write-wins. Minerva 85b1abe2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4066de47f66b36754c7e0421263","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T3.2 — codetools file_edit forwards if_match_version (thin delegate)","description":"codetools file_* are thin delegates to core buffered minerva_doc_* (DCR 019e7b6609 / work_item 019ea035bb28). Forward if_match_version and the returned doc version so an agent driving codetools gets the same optimistic-concurrency guarantee. DoD: granted bash/file_edit path returns version_mismatch on stale write; smoke unaffected.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:05","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:12:41","priority":2,"tags":["codetools","concurrency","plugin","task"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40573c77b13b587304d3d23f47a","resolution":"codetools file_write/file_edit forward if_match_version to core minerva_doc_* (optional *int64, omitted when unset). version_mismatch surfaces via proxyDoc (broker maps result.error→error_message — broker drops structured fields, so want/got folded into the error string). Schemas advertise the param. plugins 42ec462; go build/vet/test green, smoke=21."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea40676d27f35a4317010e4204b7f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T3.3 — Concurrency tests: stale write rejected, fresh accepted, retry path","description":"Drive DocumentBuffer + MCPDocTools: read version v -> bump buffer (human) to v+1 -> doc_edit with if_match_version=v rejected {want:v+1,got:v} -> doc_read -> doc_edit with v+1 accepted. Assert omitted-version path unchanged. DoD: green headless; clear envelope asserted.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:07","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:12:46","priority":2,"tags":["concurrency","task","test"],"parent":"minerva:019ea40573c77b13b587304d3d23f47a","resolution":"test_doc_version_guard.gd 14/14 (registered in hermetic suite): omitted→proceed, match→proceed, stale→reject {code,want,got,path}, retry-after-reread→proceed, JSON float version, real-DocumentBuffer apply_edit bump drives the guard. Minerva 85b1abe2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea406da3f782193b5a43813e58efc","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"North star: journal as a generic attributed-changeset interface that per-type histories feed","description":"## Context\nSpun out of DCR 019ea404ffcd (layered undo) as the LONG-TERM direction. That DCR rejected \"pin all undo to the journal\" (the journal is today a TEXT, two-snapshot store — coarse + text-only). This DCR captures the version of \"journaling everywhere\" that IS right, so the idea isn't lost.\n\n## Idea\nMake the journal a generic interface, NOT a text store that swallows everything:\n- A changeset abstraction = markers + per-action attribution (ai vs human), editor-type-agnostic.\n- The existing per-type histories REPORT INTO it: text buffer (already does, via apply_edit), SpreadsheetHistory (HistoryAction), PCBData undo stack, GraphicsEditorUndo, VideoRecordingData — each emits \"an attributed action happened in changeset N\" without giving up its own fine-grained undo.\n- Consumers: cross-cutting review (what changed this turn, across editor types), coarse \"undo the agent's turn\" everywhere, and a uniform surface for plugins.\n\n## Why this and not journal-as-sole-undo\nFine-grained interactive undo stays in the purpose-built per-editor stacks (keystroke/caret for text; domain actions for spreadsheet/PCB). The journal is the coarse, attributed, cross-cutting layer. They COMPOSE (a coarse revert drives the per-type undo). This avoids reinventing text/keystroke undo and avoids lossy state-to-text diffing for non-text editors.\n\n## Also in scope\n- Duck-typed undo dispatch: make Editor.undo_action() call editor_impl.undo()/redo() for plugin-owned editor types so arbitrary plugins plug into the existing Undo button without editing Editor.gd's match (deferred from 019ea404ffcd).\n- Optional durability: persist the changeset journal per-.minproj (behind the P1 project_id seam from DCR 019e9f602391), where 019ea404ffcd kept it in-memory/session.\n\n## Cost / status\nCost L; touches every editor type. DESIGN-ONLY placeholder (proposed) — do NOT start before DCR 019ea404ffcd (A) ships and is HITL-validated. A is deliberately built to evolve toward this.","created_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:33","updated_at":"2026-06-07T21:39:42","priority":3,"tags":["architecture","journal","north-star","plugins","review","undo"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea42d80977e57bc2e0b89d22d2689","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Codetools 3-skill recut: Coding / Advanced coding / Plugin authoring (v1 scaffolds)","description":"DONE 2026-06-07 (WIP, uncommitted in plugins repo, manifest-only): replaced the 3 old codetools skills with the recut Coding / Advanced coding / Plugin authoring (owner: 'have the skills' + D1 lean=replace). Valid JSON, prefix-correct (^minerva_codetools_…), tool_deps reference real tools (cobrowser_* resolve unsatisfied-but-not-fatal). No binary/bundle rebuild (skills seed from manifest at install); reinstall codetools to re-seed.\n\nSkill shape: Coding = daily-driver loop (index/query/read/buffered-edit/verify), absorbs understand+navigate; Advanced = probe debug + cobrowser research-to-notes + graph/diff visualize, absorbs inspect_runtime; Plugin authoring = scaffold that self-flags its embedded docs are pending.\n\nPENDING (the 'modify skills later' work, owner-deferred): (1) flesh out content for all 3; (2) D2 — expand PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md (annotation sub-pane, html+godot panels, 3-col responsive, DOCX/XLSX, printing) then DUPLICATE into the plugin skill (self-contained, no repo dep); (3) confirm D1 (replace vs add — currently replaced); (4) D4 confirm printing/PDF capability before documenting; (5) HITL the skills in focused chats (correct tool_deps load, seam between Coding/Advanced). Planning detail: 3 Minerva note tabs + nudge (skill-coding/_advanced-coding/_plugin-authoring) + memory project_codetools_skills_redesign. The undo backbone (DCR 019ea404ffcd) the Coding skill relies on is built (P1-P3).","created_at":"2026-06-07T22:21:46","updated_at":"2026-06-07T22:21:46","priority":3,"tags":["codetools","skills","ux","wip"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea49c63e575469a49623639b3bc52","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Buffer-sync edits don't refresh the editor tab dirty/note glyph (missing text_changed emit)","description":"## Symptom (HITL, 2026-06-07)\nDuring the layered-undo DCR HITL: an agent edit via minerva_doc_edit (and minerva_journal_revert) changed the open file's buffer (minerva_doc_read reports dirty=true), but the editor tab-heading glyph did NOT update to show the file as dirty (neither the content-vs-disk nor the content-vs-note indicator). The human has no visual signal that an agent-edited open file needs saving — a real blocker for the autonomous-agent UX (the glyph is how the user sees save/note state).\n\n## Root cause (verified in code)\n- The tab glyph is repainted by EditorPane._on_editor_content_changed (EditorPane.gd:484-541), which is driven by Editor's `content_changed` signal.\n- `content_changed` is emitted by Editor._on_editor_changed (Editor.gd:1940), which is connected ONLY to code_edit.text_changed (Editor.gd:322).\n- In Godot 4, assigning `code_edit.text = X` programmatically emits `text_set`, NOT `text_changed`. The codebase already knows this: _load_text_file manually fires `code_edit.text_changed.emit()` with the comment \"the signal is not emitted for some reason\" (Editor.gd:875).\n- The P1/P2 buffer-sync path Editor._set_code_edit_text_from_buffer (Editor.gd:1006-1027) sets `code_edit.text = new_text` but did NOT emit text_changed → _on_editor_changed never runs → content_changed never fires → glyph (and annotation revision bump at Editor.gd:1933) never refresh on agent/MCP/journal edits.\n- Note: get_saved_state() (Editor.gd:1310) is computed live (text == saved_content), so the dirty TRUTH was always correct; only the visual refresh was missing. Save/Save-All that re-checks state at save time still flush correctly, but the user can't SEE what's dirty.\n- Contrast: the manual Undo button works because vboxEditor._on_undo_button_pressed explicitly calls UpdateUnsavedTabIcon.emit() (vboxEditor.gd:911); the buffer-sync path had no such nudge.\n\n## Fix\nAdded `code_edit.text_changed.emit()` at the end of Editor._set_code_edit_text_from_buffer (inside the _applying_buffer_text guard, so the echo-back into the buffer is still suppressed). The buffer-sync edit now routes through the same content_changed path human typing uses → both glyphs + annotation revision refresh.\n\n## Test\ntest_buffer_sync_undo_caret.gd: added test_bare_assign_is_silent_but_sync_emits_text_changed — locks (a) bare `text =` does not emit text_changed (the Godot gotcha the fix depends on) and (b) the sync path emits it exactly once. Full file 11/11 (was 9/9).\n\n## Status\nFix applied + headless green; awaiting in-app re-HITL (human watches the tab glyph appear/clear through an agent edit + journal revert). Close after HITL passes.","created_at":"2026-06-08T00:22:53","updated_at":"2026-06-08T00:32:55","priority":2,"tags":["bugfix","core","documents","hitl-found","ui","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea404ffcd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea49c6c66735e820668fa85d44e29","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Redo (Ctrl+Y) scrolls caret to bottom after an agent edit — bypasses caret-restore","description":"## Symptom (HITL, 2026-06-07)\nP1 HITL: agent edits no longer wipe the undo stack and Undo restores caret/scroll sanely. But pressing Redo (after an undo) scrolls the caret to the bottom of the file.\n\n## Likely cause\nUndo/Redo of a TEXT editor go through native code_edit.undo()/redo() (Editor.undo_action / vboxEditor._on_redo_button_pressed). The caret/scroll preservation added in P1 lives only in Editor._set_code_edit_text_from_buffer (the buffer→editor sync), which the native redo does not route through. So redo leaves the caret wherever native CodeEdit puts it (end of last applied op).\n\n## Severity\nMinor / cosmetic. Undo (the primary direction for \"undo the agent's edit\") is correct; only redo caret placement is off. Not a stack-integrity issue. Defer.\n\n## Possible fix (later)\nRestore caret/scroll around the redo path in vboxEditor._on_redo_button_pressed / Editor.undo_action for TEXT, or hook code_edit's caret after redo(). Low priority.","created_at":"2026-06-08T00:22:55","updated_at":"2026-06-08T00:22:55","priority":4,"tags":["core","documents","hitl-found","minor","ui","undo"],"parent":"minerva:019ea404ffcd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4b6201277b59b7d3f8e9789b39c","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Text annotation anchoring mis-targets lines and truncates selections when lines are word-wrapped","created_at":"2026-06-08T00:50:59","updated_at":"2026-06-08T03:32:22","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["annotations","core","hitl-found","text-editor","word-wrap"],"environment":"Minerva development @ 0ecef252 (running app). Document: /tmp/codetools_skill_1_coding.md (markdown, several long paragraphs that soft-wrap across multiple display rows). Soft-wrap on.","repro_steps":"1. Open a text doc containing a very long paragraph that soft-wraps into many display rows (e.g. the codetools Coding skill md — line 12 system prompt is ~700 chars).\n2. Try to add a text-comment annotation to a LOGICAL line BELOW the long wrapped paragraph (line 16, the Preconditions paragraph). → the annotation anchors to a different lower line (line 22, \"## Steps\") instead of line 16.\n3. Try to highlight a long RANGE within a wrapped line (parts of line 12). → the selection/highlight stops at the first soft line-break (the wrap point) rather than extending to the intended end."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4ba78187176844919cc3bef70be","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"No user-facing way to move/re-parent an annotation to a different line (data layer supports it)","description":"## Gap (HITL, 2026-06-07)\nDiscovered alongside the word-wrap anchoring bug (019ea4b62012). When an annotation is anchored to the wrong line, the only recovery in the UI is delete + recreate. There is no \"move this annotation to line N\" / drag-to-re-anchor / re-parent affordance in the text editor annotation surface (dock/gutter/list).\n\n## Why it's low-cost\nThe retarget capability already exists below the UI:\n- minerva_annotations_repair_anchor {annotation_id, new_anchor:{plugin:'core', type:'text.range', id:{start,end}, snapshot:{…}}} retargets a v2 text anchor.\n- minerva_annotations_update {id, patch:{primitives:[…]}} replaces primitives wholesale.\nSo the work is a UI affordance (e.g. gutter drag, or a \"move to current line / selection\" action in the annotation context menu / dock) that calls the existing retarget path — plus recomputing the {start,end} offsets + snapshot for the new target.\n\n## Acceptance\nUser can relocate an existing annotation to a new line/range without losing its comment, ref (C<n>), author, or lifecycle. Verify the ref/citeability survives the move.\n\n## Related\nBug 019ea4b62012 (word-wrap mis-anchor) — re-parent is the manual recovery for it; ideally fix both so mis-anchors are rare AND recoverable.","created_at":"2026-06-08T00:55:44","updated_at":"2026-06-08T00:55:44","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","core","hitl-found","text-editor","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4c055ad715b922427db0cb1dbca","type":"rca","status":"detected","title":"RCA: gutter-click annotation anchors to wrong logical line under word-wrap (pixel-Y/line-height conflates visual rows with logical lines)","description":"Backs bug 019ea4b62012. Fix (minimal): make get_line_column_at_pos() the PRIMARY path in _line_at_code_edit_position (it returns the logical line, wrap-aware) and demote/remove the floor(y/line_height) branch to a last-resort fallback only when the method is absent. Concretely: move the 2758-2763 block above the 2748 block, or replace the body of 2748-2756 with the get_line_column_at_pos call. Regression test: place a gutter comment on a line BELOW a wrapped paragraph in a soft-wrapped doc and assert the stored anchor.snapshot.position[0] == the clicked logical line.\n\nSCOPE NOTE — second symptom NOT covered: the reported \"long highlight truncates at the first soft-wrap boundary\" is NOT explained by this root cause. The selection→range path (_capture_current_annotation_selection, Editor.gd:2662-2671) uses get_selection_from_line/to_line/column, which are logical and wrap-aware; and live annotation C6 (a 154-char range spanning within wrapped line 12) stored FULLY. So symptom 2 is likely an overlay RENDERING artifact (the highlight draw across wrap rows) or a different repro — needs its own investigation; do not fold it into this fix.","created_at":"2026-06-08T01:02:08","updated_at":"2026-06-08T01:02:08","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","core","rca","text-editor","word-wrap"],"why_chain":"PROBLEM: Gutter-click to comment on logical line 16 anchored to line 20 (sometimes 22) when long paragraphs above (line 12) were soft-wrapped. (bug 019ea4b62012; live evidence ann_0005/C8: intended line 16, stored snapshot.position=[19,0], offsets {1061,1238}.)\n\nWHY 1 — Why does it anchor to the wrong logical line? Because the line index handed to anchor creation is already wrong. The downstream steps are wrap-aware and faithful: _store_line_as_pending_annotation (Editor.gd:2729) computes offsets via _line_col_to_flat_offset (counts \n), and TextEditorAnnotationHost.add_comment_at → _offset_to_line_col (counts \n) builds snapshot.position. They convert whatever line they're given correctly; the defect is upstream.\n\nWHY 2 — Why is the line index wrong? _line_at_code_edit_position (Editor.gd:2745) converts the click's pixel-Y to a line as: `line = get_first_visible_line() + floor(local_pos.y / get_line_height())` (line 2753). This adds a LOGICAL line number (get_first_visible_line) to a count of VISUAL display rows (floor(y/line_height)) — two different coordinate spaces.\n\nWHY 3 — Why does that arithmetic break? Soft wrap is on (EditorCodeEdit.gd:46, wrap_mode = LINE_WRAPPING_BOUNDARY), so one logical line spans multiple display rows of height line_height. Thus floor(y/line_height) = display-rows-from-viewport-top ≠ logical-lines-from-top. The error equals the wrap rows contributed by wrapped logical lines above the click. A click below the long wrapped line 12 overshoots by those extra rows (+4 observed); it varies with scroll position and editor width (unstable landing line). Clicks at/above the wrap have no wrapped lines above them → no drift (matches the observed symptom).\n\nWHY 4 — Why use the wrap-blind arithmetic when Godot's wrap-aware get_line_column_at_pos() is right there? Because the function tries the manual get_line_height branch FIRST (guarded `if code_edit.has_method(\"get_line_height\")`, 2748) and returns inside it (2756). CodeEdit ALWAYS has get_line_height, so that branch always wins and the correct get_line_column_at_pos() branch (2758-2763) is unreachable dead code. The branch ORDERING/guard is the defect, not a missing API.\n\nWHY 5 (ROOT) — Why was the wrong-first ordering written? The pixel→line conversion was hand-rolled with has_method() duck-typing as the primary path and the native API demoted to a fallback, assuming a uniform line height and never reconciled with the editor's own soft-wrap setting; no test covered annotation placement on a wrapped document.\n\nROOT CAUSE: Annotation gutter-click line resolution derives the target line from pixel-Y under a uniform-line-height assumption (first_visible + floor(y/line_height)) that conflates visual wrap-rows with logical lines, and this wrap-blind path shadows the available wrap-aware get_line_column_at_pos().","contributing_factors":"1. The wrap-aware path (get_line_column_at_pos, Editor.gd:2758-2763) is already present but UNREACHABLE — shadowed by the early-return wrap-blind branch above it. 2. No regression test for annotation placement on a document with soft-wrapped lines. 3. Soft-wrap defaults ON for these editors (EditorCodeEdit.gd:46) and the docs being annotated (markdown skill files) wrap heavily, so the bug is hit immediately in real use. 4. has_method() defensive duck-typing masked the design intent (which API is canonical)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea4d3c94d73cf9ce7144e75c2b0b3","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Range annotation underline truncates on word-wrapped lines (one draw_line per logical line, ignores wrap rows)","created_at":"2026-06-08T01:23:23","updated_at":"2026-06-08T03:32:25","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["annotations","core","hitl-found","rendering","text-editor","word-wrap"],"environment":"Minerva development @ 0ecef252. EditorCodeEdit soft-wrap ON (wrap_mode = LINE_WRAPPING_BOUNDARY, EditorCodeEdit.gd:46). Renderer: TextEditorAnnotationCanvas (extends AnnotationOverlay), Scenes/Editor.tscn AnnotationCanvas node.","repro_steps":"1. Open a doc with soft-wrap and a logical line long enough to wrap into 2+ visual rows.\n2. Create a range/highlight annotation whose char range spans across a wrap boundary within that one logical line (e.g. C6 in /tmp/codetools_skill_1_coding.md: a 154-char range on line 12).\n3. Observe: the underline highlight renders only up to (around) the first wrap point instead of continuing onto the next visual row(s)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ea5335f03747593fffeca9a181dd5","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"Annotating an empty line wedges the annotation flow — no further annotations possible","created_at":"2026-06-08T03:07:47","updated_at":"2026-06-08T15:36:16","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["annotations","core","hitl-found","text-editor"],"resolution":"Allow zero-width line-scope anchors so blank lines are commentable; relaxed 4 Editor end<=start guards (line scope only; range still needs a span). Wedge dissolves as a side effect. Test test_blank_line_annotation.gd 18/18; no regressions.","environment":"Minerva development (this session's build, with the word-wrap anchoring fix). Markdown doc with blank lines.","repro_steps":"1. Open a text doc with blank lines.\n2. Gutter-click a BLANK line to start an add-comment.\n3. Observe an error / \"Cannot annotate an empty line\".\n4. Now gutter-click a NON-EMPTY line — the add-comment flow no longer starts. Annotation is dead for the rest of the session."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ead9856137fbfb4654d340d5ef3bc","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Conversational voice-mode latency: streaming TTS, compressed transit, speak-short","description":"Reduce per-turn latency in Minerva voice conversation mode. VALIDATED bottleneck = TTS (1-3s to generate the WAV + transit cost), NOT the frontier LLM (gemini-3.1-flash <1s to turn completion, and Minerva UX masks frontier wait). Turn pipeline: STT -> frontier(cloud) -> gemma4:e4b summary (REQUIRED — frontier models refuse true short speech-like output; best achievable is \"short utterance + long explanation\") -> TTS -> playback. Levers, biggest first: (1) STREAMING/CHUNKED TTS — synthesize + send audio sentence-by-sentence over the existing Core binary FILE_DATA frames so first-audio plays while the rest generates (overlaps gen+transit+playback); (2) COMPRESS TRANSIT — Opus instead of raw WAV (~10-15x smaller; raw 24kHz/16-bit mono ≈ 48KB/s) decode client-side; (3) SPEAK-ONLY-THE-SHORT-PART — cap spoken text, long explanation stays on-screen (TTS time ∝ audio length); (4) tighten endpointing/VAD in the client-side container (silence threshold adds to every turn's front); (5) clarify + collapse turn-buffer ownership and orchestration hops (Minerva <-> client-side container <-> Core <-> fleet — each boundary is an RTT; let the local container drive the turn). PROFILE FIRST: instrument per-stage budget (endpointing, STT, frontier TTFT/full, summary, TTS first-audio, transit, total) before tuning. Also includes the Minerva-side of the pre-warm rename: VoiceServiceClient.gd session/reserve -> session/prewarm (lockstep w/ the gpu-dispatch alias). OUT OF SCOPE: GPU placement/capability routing — that's the linked minervaservices gpu-dispatch DCR (019ead6aa5b37cb7baf1e1bbac035994).","created_at":"2026-06-09T18:15:02","updated_at":"2026-06-09T18:16:39","priority":2,"tags":["binary-frames","conversation-mode","latency","pre-warm","streaming","tts","voice"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eaebaa3907d71a9f6fb6df65c2437","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Minerva's HumanWeb 'clone' is a symlink to the dev tree","created_at":"2026-06-09T23:32:07","updated_at":"2026-06-09T23:32:07","tags":["cobrowser","deploy","humanweb","mcp","minerva"],"value":"Minerva runs cobrowser from ~/mcp_servers/HumanWeb, which is a SYMLINK to ~/github/HumanWeb (the dev tree). So there is NO separate clone — editing ~/github/HumanWeb edits exactly what Minerva runs, and whatever branch is checked out there is what Minerva launches. To deploy changes: NO git pull / Tools→Install needed; just (1) restart the cobrowser uvicorn server (it runs detached, no --reload, so old code stays live until restart), and (2) reload the Firefox extension for JS-side changes. Whatever branch is checked out in the dev tree is what runs.","component":"cobrowser","key":"minerva_clone_is_symlink_to_devtree","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eaf38759a7a1c8205f06c85833a43","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_save_editor (save-as) renames the tab but doesn't return the new editor_name — agents' handle silently dies","description":"Agent-UX bug in the MCP editor surface. minerva_save_editor with a file_path (\"save as\") binds the tab to the file AND renames the tab to the file basename. That rename invalidates the editor_name handle the agent is holding, but the tool result doesn't say so — it returns only {file_path, success}.\n\nHit live on 2026-06-09: created spreadsheet \"Outreach Pipeline\" → minerva_save_editor(file_path=/home/imran/turnrock/outreach_pipeline.minsheet) → tab silently became \"outreach_pipeline.minsheet\" → next minerva_update_spreadsheet_data(editor_name=\"Outreach Pipeline\") failed with \"Spreadsheet editor not found\". Recovery required a minerva_list_editors round trip and guesswork about what happened.\n\nSuggested fix (smallest): include the post-save editor_name in the save result, e.g. {file_path, editor_name, renamed: true, success}. Alternatives: keep the display name stable on save-as, or accept stale names by also matching editors on file_path/previous name. Whatever the choice, the contract \"the handle you used may have just changed\" must be visible in the tool result — silent handle invalidation is the failure mode.","created_at":"2026-06-10T01:49:33","updated_at":"2026-06-10T01:49:33","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["agent-ux","editors","mcp","save-as","spreadsheet"],"environment":"Minerva development branch, 2026-06-09, MCP stdio client (Claude Code)","repro_steps":"1. minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor(name=\"My Sheet\", csv_content=...)\n2. minerva_save_editor(editor_name=\"My Sheet\", file_path=\"/tmp/my_sheet.minsheet\") → succeeds, returns only file_path\n3. minerva_update_spreadsheet_data(editor_name=\"My Sheet\", ...) → fails: \"Spreadsheet editor not found: My Sheet\"\n4. minerva_list_editors → tab is now named \"my_sheet.minsheet\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eaf9a80c3746880470ff700d7f8a2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Terminal BEL: intercept in shim handler, zero vendor patches","created_at":"2026-06-10T03:36:38","updated_at":"2026-06-10T03:36:38","tags":["bell","fcib","ghostty","plugin-substrate","terminal","zig"],"value":"PTY bytes dual-feed at src/gdextension/terminal/unix/terminal.cpp:709-712: minerva_vt_write (ghostty shim = canonical cell grid) PLUS legacy C++ escape parser (back-compat signals only — don't build new features there). ghostty ReadonlyStream Handler no-ops .bell (vendor/ghostty/src/terminal/stream_readonly.zig:176). lib_vt.zig exports Stream generic (stream.zig:422), ReadonlyHandler, and Terminal.vtHandler() — so shim.zig can use a custom wrapper handler (forward vt/vtRaw to ReadonlyHandler, intercept .bell into a counter) with ZERO patches to vendor/ghostty. ghostty parser already disambiguates BEL-as-OSC-terminator vs standalone .bell action, and handles OSC 133 semantic_prompt into Terminal state. vendor/ghostty is submodule v1.3.1; shim build.zig compiles ghostty-vt source directly, bypassing ghostty's build.zig. Patch precedent if ever needed: patches/godot_wry-*.patch in build-extensions.sh.","component":"minerva-terminal","key":"ghostty-bell-architecture","confidence":"verified","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"agent-relay: terminal-agent turn relay — host.terminal.* substrate + plugin","description":"## Problem\nReading terminals via MCP returns TUI chrome (box-drawing `---` borders, spinners, redraw spam) — token waste and noise. Terminal AI CLIs are too chatty to relay verbatim; asking them to be terse fails. Proven pattern (Minerva speech I/O): post-process with a secondary compressor model instead.\n\n## Goal\nTerminal agent CLIs become conversation participants: when a turn ends, read the terminal, filter chrome, distill with a cheap model, inject just the chat part into a Minerva chat (and optionally speak it).\n\n## Hard constraints\n- NO FCIBs: vendor/ghostty stays pristine submodule @ v1.3.1; all changes in our own shim/glue source, rebuilt by scripts/build-extensions.sh.\n- NO agent-config changes: never modify claude/codex/opencode user settings (no terminal_bell config pushes). Bell is opportunistic only; primary turn detection is passive screen heuristics.\n- host.chats.append DEFERRED (transcript-semantics design question). v1 delivery = note + link_note_to_chat + minerva_speak via mcp.proxy.\n\n## Phase A — Minerva substrate (repo: Minerva, branch off development)\nA1 Bell plumbing (zero vendor patches — verified):\n  - shim.zig: wrapper handler around ghostty.ReadonlyHandler (lib_vt exports Stream generic stream.zig:422 + ReadonlyHandler + Terminal.vtHandler); intercept .bell (ReadonlyStream no-ops it, stream_readonly.zig:176) into counter; forward vt/vtRaw. New export minerva_vt_take_bell() + minerva_vt.h. Zig test.\n  - unix/terminal.cpp + windows/terminal.cpp: poll take_bell after minerva_vt_write (unix:709), emit new \"bell\" signal. Glue only — NOTHING in the legacy C++ escape parser (back-compat path).\n  - TerminalNew.gd forwards; MCPTerminalTools._terminal_wait result gains bell_rung.\nA2 New host capabilities: host.terminal.read/write/wait/list in ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES (PluginDefinition.gd:164-192), CapabilityBroker handlers (wait = long-poll, same pattern as host.providers.chat), PluginPolicy entries. Extract shared helper from MCPTerminalTools — no duplication. Scrollback delta reads use stable absolute rows via get_scroll_info().total_rows.\nA3 Docs (plugin_system_guide.md) + broker tests + one integration-boundary test driving a real TerminalNew.\n\n## Phase B — agent-relay plugin (repo: ~/github/minerva-plugins/agent-relay/, docket component in PLG)\nB1 Scaffold: Rust standalone subprocess (scansort/codetools shape). manifest.json tools (source of truth!): minerva_agent_relay_watch_start/stop/status, minerva_agent_relay_read_clean, minerva_agent_relay_profiles_list. Capabilities: host.terminal.*, host.providers.chat, mcp.proxy grants (minerva_create_note, minerva_link_note_to_chat, minerva_speak). Declared event: agent_relay.turn_completed.\nB2 Chrome filter + read_clean: drop chrome-only lines (U+2500–U+25FF runs), strip border columns, collapse duplicate lines, squeeze blanks. Standalone value even without watching.\nB3 Turn detection: per-CLI profile data files (claude/codex/opencode): prompt-box regex, spinner glyphs, alt-screen flag (alt-screen TUIs have no scrollback delta — read viewport), bell-capable flag. Detector = settle (host.terminal.wait) + profile heuristics; bell_rung as opportunistic fast-path.\nB4 Distill + deliver: host.providers.chat (cheap model, e.g. chatgpt provider per workers convention) → note + link to target chat; optional speak.\nB5 Tests: worker tests + functional run with fake host.providers.chat provider (scansort pattern). HITL: live Claude Code session relayed into a Minerva chat.\nB6 Marketplace packaging: add agent-relay to regen_registry.py PLUGIN_DIRS allow-list (hardcoded!), 3 targets, registry regen.\n\n## Known land-mines (bake into worker prompts)\n- plugin_reload doesn't refresh MCP client tool registry → /mcp reconnect after schema changes.\n- Never cp over the mmapped shim .so while Minerva runs (SIGBUS) — close app or use install.\n- Core no-bleed tool-count CI guard will see the terminal_wait schema change.\n- Minerva stringifies undeclared nested tool args — declare full schemas in manifest.\n- Dashed id follows nametag-maker precedent: tools minerva_agent_relay_*, class prefix from canonical_prefix(id).\n\n## Verified architecture refs\nHint minerva-terminal/ghostty-bell-architecture (019eaf9a80c3). PTY dual-feed unix/terminal.cpp:709-712 (ghostty shim canonical; legacy parser back-compat only).","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:15:12","updated_at":"2026-06-11T08:10:51","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","ghostty","marketplace","plugin-substrate","terminal"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafbf61fa7e51b98c64e41f139a19","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A4: Regression — all existing plugin functional suites green on new substrate","description":"Substrate changes in Phase A touch CapabilityBroker.gd, PluginDefinition.gd (ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES), PluginPolicy.gd, MCPTerminalTools.gd (shared-helper extraction + terminal_wait schema change), and the terminal GDExtension/shim. All existing plugin suites must run and pass before Phase A merges.\n\nSuites to run (verified locations in ~/github/minerva-plugins):\n- scansort: tests/ + test/ (historically 330 cargo + 5 functional + 8 regression; LLM-gated paths use the fake host.providers.chat provider)\n- codetools: worker/tests (219 worker tests)\n- cad: test/ + worker/tests\n- presentation: test/ (parity suite, 30 tests)\n- nametag-maker: no test dir found — note as gap, do not invent scope\n\nCore Minerva suites affected: broker capability tests, channel/documents/mutate suites, the no-bleed tool-count CI guard (will see terminal_wait schema change — update expected counts deliberately, never blindly).\n\nAcceptance: one recorded run of every suite above, all green, after A1+A2 land. Per feedback_test_at_integration_boundary: include at least one happy path booting the real SingletonObject with a real TerminalNew.","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:16:55","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:16:55","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","regression","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafbf813275a69a1890dd7722ff08","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A5: Update all plugin-authoring docs for new terminal substrate","description":"Docs to update (verified paths):\n\nMinerva repo:\n- src/plugins/plugin_system_guide.md — the primary authoring guide: capability catalog gains host.terminal.read/write/wait/list (wait = long-poll, returns bell_rung), usage examples, when to use vs mcp.proxy:minerva_terminal_*\n- Docs/Plugin-platform-policy.md — policy entries / grant semantics for the new capabilities\n- Docs/Plugin-platform-escalation.md — check whether capability escalation paths enumerate capabilities; update if so\n- Docs/Plugin-platform-thought.md — likely historical/design notes; verify before editing, skip if archival\n\nminerva-plugins repo:\n- docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md — developer-facing guide\n- docs/PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md — coverage matrix gains the host.terminal.* rows\n\nAlso: the codetools plugin-authoring skill duplicates authoring docs INLINE (self-contained by design, no repo dep) — it must receive the same capability-catalog update or it will teach a stale substrate. Coordinate with the codetools skills redesign work.\n\nAcceptance: every doc above either updated or explicitly marked checked-and-N/A; no doc still lists a capability catalog missing host.terminal.*.","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:17:03","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:17:03","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","docs","plugin-substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafbfbd947041a215d0732c73892c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Substrate-change checklist: 6 authoring docs + per-plugin test suites","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:17:19","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:17:19","tags":["checklist","docs","plugin-substrate","regression"],"value":"Any plugin-substrate change (capabilities, broker, policy) must update SIX docs: Minerva src/plugins/plugin_system_guide.md, Docs/Plugin-platform-policy.md, Docs/Plugin-platform-escalation.md, Docs/Plugin-platform-thought.md (verify if archival first) + minerva-plugins docs/PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md, docs/PLUGIN_API_COVERAGE.md. PLUS the codetools plugin-authoring skill (duplicates docs inline by design — goes stale silently). Regression suites to run, verified locations in ~/github/minerva-plugins: scansort tests/+test/, codetools worker/tests, cad test/+worker/tests, presentation test/; nametag-maker has NO test dir (known gap).","component":"plugin-substrate","key":"docs-and-regression-targets","confidence":"verified","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafc1d9b37e5fbe96f94e146d1bd3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A6: PLUGIN_EVENT trigger type — route plugin events into the trigger system","description":"Index scenario requires: terminal turn ends → chat LLM gets woken to read. Plugin→host events already exist (PluginEventBroker.gd:33-58 emits plugin_event signal), and triggers already support action MESSAGE_EXISTING=1 (message an existing chat). Missing link: trigger EventType has no PLUGIN_EVENT (current: NOTE_CREATED=0, NOTE_CHANGED=1, CHAT_COMPLETED=2, MCP_TOOL_EXECUTED=3, MCP_TOOL_ABOUT_TO_EXECUTE=4 — MCPAgentTools.gd:439).\n\nWork: add PLUGIN_EVENT to TriggerDefinition.EventType; trigger config gains plugin_id + event_name filters (payload made available to the action, e.g. terminal_id); connect PluginEventBroker.plugin_event → trigger evaluation; expose in minerva_create_trigger/update_trigger schemas + docs.\n\nWorkaround that works TODAY (for plugin prototyping before this lands): plugin creates a note via mcp.proxy → NOTE_CREATED trigger → MESSAGE_EXISTING. Hacky; PLUGIN_EVENT is the proper substrate.\n\nGeneric beyond agent-relay: any plugin (scansort processing-done, CAD render-done) can wake a chat LLM the same way.","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:19:37","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:41:20","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","notification","plugin-substrate","triggers"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafce7a83746e9bb98edc5814968c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Terminal exit detection: two layers, both feasible without new machinery","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:33:25","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:33:25","tags":["agent-relay","exit-detection","pty","terminal"],"value":"Two distinct exit layers for terminal watching: (a) shell/PTY death — subprocess.cpp:29 already defines process_exited(exit_code) + waitpid(WNOHANG) at lines 181-187, but ONLY on the headless exec path; interactive terminal.cpp (forkpty, _child_pid) never emits exit — copy the pattern into PTY glue. (b) foreground agent exit back to shell (claude quits/segfaults — the common case): the PTY child (shell) stays alive, so child-exit does NOT fire; detect passively via shell-integration prompt markers on_shell_prompt_start ([888z, TerminalNew.gd:315) firing during an active watch. Scrollback carries crash text (e.g. 'Segmentation fault') for diagnosis.","component":"minerva-terminal","key":"exit-detection-layers","confidence":"verified","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafce91967eef89929b81be1f92db","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Minerva safety quotas are per-turn / schema-only — cross-turn loops slip under them","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:33:31","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:33:31","tags":["agent-relay","quotas","safety","tool-budget","triggers"],"value":"Quota scope gotcha for any trigger/automation design: (1) MaxToolCallRounds (ChatHistory default 15, enforced ChatPane.gd:1787-1801) is per-TURN — current_round resets on every new turn, so trigger-woken turns each get a fresh quota; a wake→read→send loop (~2 rounds/turn) runs indefinitely under it. (2) ToolBudgetManager (10K tokens) limits tool SCHEMA tokens in the active set (LRU eviction), NOT call counts. Cross-turn runaway protection must live in the trigger system (e.g. consecutive_fire_limit reset by human message — agent-relay DCR A6 design); ChatPane.gd:1706 notifies triggers on agent-chat turn completion, so core sees both signals.","component":"minerva-core","key":"quota-scopes","confidence":"verified","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafd464a976fb97ca5a1aadeadf73","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A1: Bell + PTY exit plumbing (shim wrapper handler + glue signals)","description":"Scope (per DCR comments #475/#469, hint minerva-terminal/ghostty-bell-architecture):\n1. shim.zig: MinervaHandler wrapping ghostty.ReadonlyHandler (via Terminal.vtHandler) — intercept .bell into a counter, forward vt/vtRaw; swap ReadonlyStream for Stream(MinervaHandler). New C ABI export minerva_vt_take_bell(term)->u32 (count since last call, resets) + minerva_vt.h entry.\n2. unix/terminal.cpp + windows/terminal.cpp: poll take_bell after minerva_vt_write (unix:709), emit \"bell\" signal via call_deferred. NOTHING added to the legacy escape parser.\n3. PTY exit: copy the process_exited(exit_code)+waitpid(WNOHANG) pattern from subprocess.cpp:29,181-187 into the interactive forkpty path; emit process_exited.\n4. TerminalNew.gd forwards both signals; MCPTerminalTools._terminal_wait result gains bell_rung (and reports terminal_closed when child exited).\n\nConstraints: vendor/ghostty pristine @ v1.3.1; no FCIBs; rebuild via scripts/build-extensions.sh; never cp over the mmapped .so while Minerva runs.\n\nTest plan:\n- Zig: bell counter increments on standalone 0x07; OSC title sequence terminated by BEL does NOT increment (ghostty parser disambiguates — assert it); counter resets on take.\n- GDScript integration (real TerminalNew): spawn shell, `printf '\\a'` → bell signal fires; `exit` → process_exited fires with code.\n- Windows path compiles (cross-verify on Windows CI or flag as needs-windows-machine; cross-building unsupported).","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:39:52","updated_at":"2026-06-11T08:09:50","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","gdextension","phase-a","zig"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafd48cf17cb8a12640aae79ce6b7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A2: host.terminal.read/write/wait/list plugin capabilities","description":"Scope:\n1. Add host.terminal.read / write / wait / list to ALLOWED_HOST_CAPABILITIES (PluginDefinition.gd:164-192).\n2. CapabilityBroker handlers: extract shared terminal-access helper from MCPTerminalTools.gd (find/read/write/wait logic) so MCP tools and broker share one implementation — no duplication. wait = long-poll (same long-running request pattern as host.providers.chat), params {terminal_id, settle_ms, timeout_ms}, result {content, rows, total_scrollback_rows, bell_rung, cause: settled|timed_out|terminal_closed}.\n3. PluginPolicy entries; follows install-grants-all posture (DCR comment #470 — no new security UI; visibility handled by the watched-badge decision in Phase B).\n4. Reads are observation-only by construction: VT-state reads, never PTY I/O, never scroll_viewport (non-interference invariant, comment #477).\n\nTest plan:\n- Broker tests per capability: grant/deny enforcement, read parity with minerva_terminal_read output on same terminal, write \\r semantics, list matches terminal_list.\n- Long-poll wait: returns on settle; returns bell_rung=true when bell fired during window; returns cause=terminal_closed on child exit mid-wait; timeout honored.\n- Shared-helper refactor: existing MCPTerminalTools suites still green (no behavior change to the MCP tools).\n- Integration-boundary test (per feedback_test_at_integration_boundary): real SingletonObject + real TerminalNew driven through a broker call.","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:40:03","updated_at":"2026-06-11T08:09:56","priority":2,"tags":["agent-relay","capability-broker","phase-a"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eafd4a11a7aa9b022c4550be0447f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"A3: Substrate test suite + terminal_wait schema change shepherding","description":"Gate item closing Phase A: A1 zig+GDScript suites and A2 broker suites run in CI; the core no-bleed tool-count guard sees the terminal_wait result-schema change (bell_rung field) — update expectations deliberately, never blindly. Confirm core tool COUNT is unchanged (no new core tools in Phase A — capabilities are not tools). Runs together with A4 (plugin regression suites) as the merge gate. Note: the docs work originally sketched as A3 in the DCR description lives in A5.","created_at":"2026-06-10T04:40:08","updated_at":"2026-06-10T04:40:08","priority":3,"tags":["agent-relay","ci","phase-a","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019eafbdcfb37c509a578be8a77095df"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb00489bc7caa89f52720c9e9882e","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_host_capability_channel.gd: 3 pre-existing host.echo result-shape failures on development","description":"Found during agent-relay A2 regression sweep (2026-06-09). On dcr/agent-relay base (= development d1fee289, also fails with A2 changes stashed — NOT caused by agent-relay): test_host_capability_channel.gd fails 3 checks: \"host.echo result has 'echo' key\" (result keys are [success, result]), \"host.echo echoes args back intact\" (echo null), \"echo data present in tool result\" (keys [success, capability_result, echo]). Suite: 26-27 passed / 3 failed. Suspected drift between the test's expected envelope and the broker reply-envelope Option A change (RCA 019e46b5 era) — handler returns PluginErrors.success({echo}) nesting under result, test expects flat. Fix = align test to the current envelope contract (or vice versa if the envelope regressed). Affects A4 regression-gate accounting: these 3 are the known-failing baseline.","created_at":"2026-06-10T05:32:27","updated_at":"2026-06-10T05:32:27","tags":["agent-relay-adjacent","plugin-substrate","test"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb0443bdf72789466075acfc6accf","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"test_cad_evaluate_render.gd should SKIP cleanly when CAD worker python is unavailable","description":"run-functional-tests.sh documents the per-plugin tier as \"SKIP cleanly (exit 0) when their environment is unavailable\", but test_cad_evaluate_render.gd FAILs (exit 1) when the CAD bridge can't spawn its python: \"bridge.Worker.Start: exec: fork/exec /home/imran/anaconda3/bin/python3: no such file or directory\" (anaconda3 exists on this box but bin/python3 doesn't). Found during agent-relay A4 regression gate 2026-06-09 — environmental, NOT a substrate regression. Fix: probe for the worker python (or catch the bridge spawn error) and SKIP like the other heavy-tier tests.","created_at":"2026-06-10T06:42:02","updated_at":"2026-06-10T06:42:02","tags":["agent-relay-adjacent","cad","test"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb22e1a427435bf2768bb5a45d556","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"codetools marketplace install hangs locally headless (≥7 min, no progress) since v0.3.1 registry repoint","description":"Found during agent-relay A4 regression gate 2026-06-10. test_marketplace_install_start_codetools.gd (and the suite run containing test_marketplace_install_start_cad_evaluate.gd, which hung the whole runner ~9h) stalls inside MarketplaceClient.install_from_url (MarketplaceClient.gd:252 → PluginManager.gd:171) after \"-- step 1: install_from_url --\". NOT agent-relay: git diff d1fee289..HEAD touches no marketplace/HTTP/PluginManager files (terminal/trigger/broker-terminal only). Evidence: scansort marketplace install PASSES on the same machine/run (marketplace path works); codetools asset URL returns HTTP 200 via curl (registry → codetools-v0.3.1, 63MB PBS bundle); registry was repointed to v0.3.1 in plugins 944987f (2026-06-09). Suspects: 63MB in-app HTTPRequest download or PBS-bundle extraction taking unbounded time headless with no progress output, or a v0.3.1-packaging-specific stall. Also: cad marketplace test hung (CAD env also broken on this box — see chore 019eb0443bdf). Repro: godot --headless --path src --script test/test_marketplace_install_start_codetools.gd. Needs: timeout/progress in MarketplaceClient, then root-cause the v0.3.1 stall.","created_at":"2026-06-10T15:37:06","updated_at":"2026-06-10T15:37:06","tags":["agent-relay-adjacent","codetools","marketplace","test"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb31f25b57a1d84a2de609c2bff20","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Background terminals: PTY sessions without a visible terminal tab","description":"B5 HITL finding (owner, 2026-06-10): terminals only exist as visible UI tabs — minerva_terminal_create fails unless the terminal panel is open, and every watched session occupies a tab. Agent workflows (agent-relay watching N CLI sessions, codetools host.terminal.exec routing) want headless/background PTY sessions: created via MCP/capability without forcing UI presence, listable, readable/writable/waitable through the same minerva_terminal_* + host.terminal.* surface, and PROMOTABLE to a visible tab on demand (and demotable). Sketch: detach TerminalNew's PTY+vt core (terminal extension node + ghostty shim state) from the Control so a session can live unparented in a registry; terminal_list gains visible:false entries; terminal_create gains background:true; \"show this terminal\" promotes. Non-trivial: TerminalNew couples rendering/scrollback UI to the session today. Related: agent-relay DCR 019eafbdcfb3 (its v1 explicitly excluded terminal lifecycle from plugins; this is the substrate side). Candidate follow-up DCR if sizing exceeds one item.","created_at":"2026-06-10T20:00:23","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:12:03","tags":["B5-finding","agent-relay","plugin-substrate","terminal"],"resolution":"T1-T3 all shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough (43cc06ba, ef261aae, 07e5d33b). Sessions live in a SingletonObject-held registry independent of UI; background create/list/promote/demote via MCP tools; host.terminal.* incl. exec registry-routed; v1 restart semantics documented (in-memory only). Full suite sweep green at baseline. Plugin (agent-relay) round-trip against a background session lands with W7's mock CLI."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb37394d97dc29ddc29dd3e0b164e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-terminal/read-row-range-inclusive","created_at":"2026-06-10T21:32:36","updated_at":"2026-06-10T21:32:36","value":"host.terminal.read / minerva_terminal_read start_row+end_row are 0-indexed and end_row is INCLUSIVE (MCPTerminalTools.gd: range(start_row, end_row+1)). A row-count anchor N (rows 0..N-1 are content) must be passed as end_row=N-1; passing N leaks the first chrome row (live symptom: trailing \"❯ \" in agent-relay read_turn).","component":"minerva-terminal","key":"read-row-range-inclusive"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47cb44875a187bbbbab2faa3d47","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Spike: root-cause in-tree PCBEditor breakage (stale kanban/autocoder state)","description":"Opening the in-tree PCBEditor shows stale state that looks like it came from the kanban editor or autocoder instead of the PCB UI. Root-cause before migration so we don't inherit the bug and so we know whether a known-good baseline is cheap to restore.\n\n**Leading hypothesis:** `Editor.Type` enum is serialized as an integer in saved tabs/projects (PCB = 7), and multiple enum insertions over time (KANBAN, SPREADSHEET, ACTIVITY_LOG, WEBVIEW, DOCKET, PLUGIN_SCENE) shifted values, so old saved tabs deserialize PCB's slot into a different editor. Check `Editor.gd` enum (lines ~105-121), `Editor.create()` match (lines ~299-484, PCB branch ~402-410), and tab restore in vboxEditor.gd / project load.\n\n**Secondary hypotheses:** stale .godot UID cache for PCBEditor.tscn (custom UID `uid://pcbeditor001`); match-arm misrouting after commit 9cae8ab6 (DocketPanel replacement); DocketPanel state bleed via SingletonObject.docket_manager.\n\n**Decision gate (spike-then-decide):** if fix is cheap (enum migration shim / cache clear) → fix in place to get a working baseline + real .minpcb files for migration acceptance. If expensive → document root cause, fold the fix into the plugin rebuild, and rely on test fixtures for acceptance instead.\n\nTimebox: one focused session.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:22:12","updated_at":"2026-06-11T03:09:45","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","spike"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Decision gate satisfied. Editor + all dispatch/restore paths verified sound on development (see round-1 comment, base ce6d7684); opens fine on macOS — breakage reproduces only on the Linux desktop, so it's machine state, not code. No in-place code fix needed; working baseline EXISTS for migration acceptance (real .minpcb files authorable on macOS). Linux checklist before any reopen: (1) git fetch + confirm branch=development @ current SHA, (2) delete .godot import cache + reimport, (3) inspect/clear stale user:// session state (config_file.cfg restored-tabs payload — suspected stale AutocoderTaskStore content), (4) retry PCB editor. If it STILL reproduces after a clean environment, file a new bug with exact repro (open path, tab title, screenshot) — do not reopen this spike."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47cd33978c89bd19e2907b9b747","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Plugin skeleton at minerva-plugins/pcb (CAD template)","description":"Stand up the pcb plugin in the canonical minerva-plugins monorepo, copying the CAD plugin's proven shape: Go router backend (stdio MCP) + embedded Python worker + in-process godot_scene panel.\n\nLayout (mirrors minerva-plugins/cad):\n```\nminerva-plugins/pcb/\n├── main.go                  # thin router: tools/list, tools/call, worker lifecycle\n├── manifest.json            # godot_scene panel, .minpcb ext, ipc_channels, host_owned save\n├── internal/                # bridge consumed from shared/ (see grandchild)\n├── worker/                  # embedded Python: circuit_synth, agent_router (later children)\n└── ui/                      # PCBPanel.tscn/.gd (later children)\n```\n\nArchitecture decisions (settled in planning, 2026-06-10):\n- Fully local, CAD-style. NO Core service dependency (minerva-services DCR 019da418 deferred).\n- NO Java/Freerouting. Routing = manual + agent-router (LLM-assisted).\n- Only runtime is the embedded Python worker — same accepted pattern as CAD; no new entries in user risk profile.\n- Board model + rendering stay in-panel (responsive editing); worker handles compute (validate/generate/check/export).\n\nAcceptance: plugin installs + starts via PluginManager, passes the tools/list handshake, smoke test scaffold modeled on test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd runs green.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:22:19","updated_at":"2026-06-11T04:15:32","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","scaffold"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Acceptance met via both grandchildren (019eb47f71b5 bridge lift, 019eb47f8bb8 manifest+router scaffold): pcb plugin at minerva-plugins/pcb installs + starts via PluginManager, passes tools/list handshake (minerva_pcb_ping), smoke test scaffold green 30/0 with CAD regression guard 37/0. Commits: minerva-plugins 1c8291b + 49ab097 (main), Minerva 7a8ad123 + d019d320 (development). Architecture decisions in the item description all honored (local CAD-style, no Core service, shared/ consumed... bridge import deferred to worker round by design). Follow-ups spawned: PLG 019eb4b8d3e2 (codetools→shared bridge), PLG 019eb4e4028e (shared router extraction)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47d19417670b58101302e8b87b6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Substrate gap audit + walking skeleton","description":"PCBEditor pre-dates both the plugin substrate and the annotation substrate — its needs are baked into ~7 KLOC that never had to fit a contract. We cannot enumerate the substrate changes from static analysis alone, so this child exists to DISCOVER them cheaply, BEFORE bulk porting begins. Sequenced after the skeleton, before panel/worker/annotation children — those consume its output.\n\nThree mechanisms (grandchildren):\n1. **Static contract diff** — enumerate every PCBEditor behavior (29 tools, canvas interactions, annotations, file ops, undo, selection) and map each to a substrate capability: supported / partial / missing / by-design-different. Covers annotation substrate AND scene-panel broker (save modes, paired_dsl-with-YAML question), editor/file routing, tool dispatch envelope, perf envelope (spatial index, render_overlay at board scale).\n2. **Walking skeleton** — minimal vertical slice touching every substrate seam: open board file → render a few components via scene-panel host → place one pcb_route_hint via dock pane → persists to sidecar → LLM reads via annotations_query + render_overlay → save/reload round-trips. Unknown unknowns surface here, in week one, not after 7 KLOC is ported.\n3. **Standing triage rule** for every gap found (here and during later porting): file immediately, tagged substrate-gap, linked to this DCR. Triage question: \"would a second editor want this?\" YES → platform child under the substrate DCRs (019ddb70 anchors/envelope, 019de430 workbench, or scene-panel broker line), implemented in Minerva core. NO → plugin-side adaptation. PCB-IS-WRONG → change PCB's legacy convention, don't enshrine pre-substrate idiosyncrasies as platform requirements.\n\nOutput: authoritative gap register; platform gaps filed under substrate DCRs (concrete consumer motivates the design), plugin gaps absorbed into later children.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:22:37","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:41","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","spike","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"All three grandchildren done: static contract diff 019eb47fd15e (gap register, 64 rows, Fable-reviewed), walking skeleton 019eb47fe8a0 (all 6 slice steps proven, human 3b verified, 3 live-only gaps found + fixed same-day, smoke 43/0), triage 019eb48022 (79 rows disposed: 7 platform filings, plugin rows mapped to porting children, 10 pcb-is-wrong conventions documented). Authoritative outputs: Docs/design/PCB-substrate-gap-register.md, Docs/design/PCB-hitl-debt.md, pcb skeleton on minerva-plugins main @ e237b84. Downstream children may now consume: single-view host_owned decision, TextEditorAnnotationHost as envelope reference, .pcbskel routing precedent, W-13/14/15 regression patterns."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47d36bb731396bfe1ae9f8a1c8f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Canonical board source (YAML) + .minpcb serialization contract","description":"Define the canonical board representation as YAML aligned with pcb-architect / the pcb-maker skill (which already loads boards from pcb-architect YAML with accurate KiCAD geometry). The panel's in-memory board model (ported PCBData) must round-trip losslessly to/from this YAML.\n\nScope:\n- YAML schema: components (footprint, position, rotation), nets, traces (layer, width, segments), vias, board outline, design rules.\n- `.minpcb` compatibility: files created by the in-tree editor open cleanly in the plugin (DCR acceptance). Importer/migrator for the legacy format.\n- Annotations move from inline `.minpcb` to platform sidecar (`<file>.annotations.json`) — handled in the annotation child; this child guarantees the importer hands legacy annotation blobs to that migration path.\n- save_mode: host_owned via the broker's serialize/deserialize channels (pcb.serialize returns the YAML source, like cad.serialize returns DSL source).\n- Open design question from gap audit: paired_dsl render_mode with YAML as the text half (CAD-symmetric, free buffer sync + user-editable source) vs single-view panel. Decide using gap-audit findings.\n\nAcceptance: round-trip property tests (model → YAML → model identical); every existing dev .minpcb file imports without data loss.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:22:45","updated_at":"2026-07-05T20:27:25","priority":2,"tags":["file-format","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Canonical YAML board contract shipped (minerva-plugins main b0353ee). internal/board package: versioned canonical model (unit-suffixed fields, deterministic sorted-slice serialization proven byte-identical under permuted input), yaml.v3 codec, legacy .minpcb importer with lossless-or-warn for every entity type (unknown fields → Extra + warning; annotations/route_hints opaque Blob passthrough for the annotation child), pcb.serialize/deserialize real handlers (64KiB cap → structured payload_too_large per A-8; malformed params error). go test 16/16, vet clean, binary rebuilt, guard tier no new failures. Contract doc: pcb/docs/board-yaml.md (.minpcb→YAML field mapping; JSON-dict Extra lossy-by-design — YAML path is the lossless lane, downstream consumers take note). Cold Opus review approve_with_notes; all actionable fixes applied. Deferred by design: paired_dsl (revisit post-contract per gap register headline 2); oversized-board chunking beyond the 64KiB error. Acceptance criterion \"every existing dev .minpcb imports without data loss\" satisfied via constructed fixtures — no real dev .minpcb files exist on this machine (in-tree editor baseline lives on macOS per spike 019eb47cb448); real-file check rides the HITL acceptance session."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47d7fb27c619217f21c78ffd4b7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Embedded Python worker (circuit_synth: validate/generate/check)","description":"Embedded Python worker for the pcb plugin, following CAD's pattern exactly (worker/ + mcad_worker dispatcher/framing/methods shape, runtime extraction via internal/runtime/). The Go router spawns it lazily on first tool call; circuit breaker + graceful shutdown come free from the shared bridge.\n\nMethods (pure functions over the canonical YAML, stateless like CAD's _evaluate):\n- `validate` — board YAML structural + design-rule validation (circuit_synth)\n- `generate` — YAML → .kicad_pcb / .kicad_sch / .kicad_pro via circuit_synth's pure-Python s-expression writers (KiCAD files are text; NO KiCAD installation involved)\n- `check_libraries` — verify components against KiCAD symbol/footprint library data (data files from the libraries child)\n- `check_bom` — BOM validation + footprint suggestions\n\nExplicitly NOT in scope: `route` via Freerouting (dead — no Java, ever; routing is manual + agent-router child), `gerbers` (own child, pure-code path), any Core service calls (minerva-services DCR deferred).\n\nWorker deps: circuit_synth + pyyaml; pin in worker/pyproject.toml. Cold-start budget: measure; CAD needed a 60s first-call timeout for OCCT imports — circuit_synth should be far lighter.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:23:04","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:45:38","priority":3,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","python","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Embedded Python worker shipped (minerva-plugins main cfc1402). CAD pattern mirrored exactly (framing/dispatcher verbatim shape, lazy spawn via shared bridge, circuit breaker + graceful shutdown free). Worker tools live end-to-end: minerva_pcb_validate (structural + design-rule over canonical YAML, field-path errors), minerva_pcb_generate (.kicad_pcb/.kicad_sch/.kicad_pro pure-text at authored coordinates — no KiCad install), minerva_pcb_check_libraries/check_bom (lib_dir contract, missing_data:true pending Wave-2b data). KEY DESIGN FACT for later children: circuit_synth 0.12.1 is pinned-OPTIONAL and unusable for generate (requires KiCad symbol libraries at Component construction + imposes auto-placement discarding authored coords) — validate/generate are plain Python; documented in pcb/docs/worker.md. Bridge gotcha fixed + hint-worthy: per-call ctx into Dispatch threads into exec.CommandContext and kills the shared worker every call; use context.Background() (CAD precedent), Shutdown unaffected. Verification: Python 21/21 in pyyaml-only venv (proves optionality), go build/vet/test green, E2E stdio (9 tools listed, validate ok on spike board), cold start ~79ms (vs CAD's 60s OCCT budget), guard tier no new failures, pcb smoke 43/0. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes; both notes fixed (manifest skill block rewritten for worker capabilities + tool_deps; WORKER_VERSION aligned to 0.2.0)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47d9f1e783dbf31fd206db8bc2a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"KiCAD symbol/footprint libraries as fetched data","description":"KiCAD's symbol/footprint libraries are plain s-expression DATA files, not executables — so check_libraries/check_bom/generate can run fully local without installing KiCAD. Ship them as fetched data, never checked in (no-FCIB policy) and never bundled in the repo.\n\nScope:\n- Fetch-on-first-need into the plugin data dir (user://plugins/data/pcb/libraries/), same trust pattern as CAD's runtime extraction. SHA-verified download.\n- Subset strategy: full official libs are ~1-2 GB; define a trimmed common-parts set (passives, connectors, common ICs/footprints used by pcb-architect templates) with lazy fetch for the long tail.\n- Refresh story: manifest records library version; explicit re-fetch action (not silent auto-update). When KiCAD ships updated libs, user opts in.\n- Offline behavior: tools that need libs fail with a clear \"fetch libraries first\" message, not a crash.\n- Worker reads libs via env/config path handed by the Go router (mirrors KICAD_SYMBOL_DIR/KICAD_FOOTPRINT_DIR convention from the old docker-compose).\n\nOut of scope: user-supplied/commercial libraries (future; was also out of scope in the deferred Core-service DCR).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:23:12","updated_at":"2026-07-05T22:13:11","priority":3,"tags":["data","kicad","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"KiCAD libraries as fetched data shipped (minerva-plugins main 9c5d53c). 19-entry curated lock (6 symbol libs + 13 footprints) pinned to GitLab tag 9.0.9.1 — deliberately the last flat-file release before KiCad 10's .kicad_symdir reorg; gen_libraries_lock.py regenerates at a new tag = the explicit opt-in refresh story. Fetcher: idempotent, atomic, SHA256-verified (tamper/partial/error-page all rejected with the artifact deleted — reviewer-verified against tests); pcb_fetch_libraries + pcb_library_status tools; data lives under the shared-runtime DataDir, NEVER in the repo. Worker check_libraries/check_bom now do real resolution (footprint existence required, top-level symbol scan soft, bounded difflib suggestions); offline contract intact (missing_data:true + fetch hint). Verified: Go 24+1skip, Python 37/37 (all hermetic), real fetch 19/19 SHAs (16.5MB/9.6s), real check_bom suggestions on spike board, guard tier no new failures. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes. USER-VISIBLE: the plugin now performs real outbound HTTPS to gitlab.com; manifest network.mode updated none→unrestricted, which is honest labeling — the field is documentary-only per PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE §13, core does not enforce egress. Wave 2 complete."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47ddebc756abba70aaa03873e8b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Gerber + Excellon export by code (no KiCAD binary)","description":"Generate fabrication outputs in pure code instead of shelling out to kicad-cli — this removes the last KiCAD-binary dependency and keeps the plugin fully self-contained (no new runtimes in the user's risk profile; decision 2026-06-10).\n\nGerber (RS-274X/X2) and Excellon drill are plain-text formats: aperture definitions + draw/flash commands. The board model already holds everything needed (pad geometry, trace segments, vias, outline).\n\nPlan:\n- Spike first (grandchild): validate Ucamco's `gerber-writer` (pure-Python, published by the owner of the Gerber spec) as the writer; fallback candidate `pcb-tools`. Output must load cleanly in gerbv and KiCAD's gerber viewer.\n- Implement worker method `gerbers`: board YAML → copper layers (F.Cu/B.Cu), soldermask, silkscreen, edge-cuts + Excellon drill (plated/non-plated).\n- Fab correctness is unforgiving → golden-file tests against a known reference board + acceptance gate: output renders identically to a reference render and loads with zero warnings in gerbv/KiCAD.\n\nSequenced after canonical-YAML and worker children (it consumes both).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:23:28","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:49:10","priority":3,"tags":["gerber","pcb","plugin-migration","python","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Fabrication outputs by pure code shipped (minerva-plugins 74f1d1c) — last KiCad-binary dependency eliminated. Worker method gerbers → minerva_pcb_gerbers (12-tool surface): F_Cu/B_Cu/F_Mask/B_Mask/F_SilkS/Edge_Cuts via gerber-writer 0.4.3.3 + own Excellon emitter with PTH/NPTH split (proven on purpose-built fixture; tool table deduped at printed precision per review). Deterministic output (creation-date sentinel; reviewer proved byte-identical across randomized-hash processes). Board contract formalized: Pin drill/annulus/plated tri-state + Board.MountingHoles (backward-compatible, lossless philosophy kept). Spike validator lifted into pytest; goldens regenerated through the production path at pinned versions. Documented limits: silk = courtyard placeholder (no glyphs in gerber-writer); asymmetric bottom-side footprints unmirrored (unsupported until real footprint geometry). Python deterministic suites 49/0 (+1 env-gated lifecycle test needing the python3 venv shim), Go green, E2E 8 files / 0 structural FAILs, guard baseline. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes at fab-correctness bar; notes applied or documented. Fab-viewer acceptance (gerbv/GerbView zero-warnings + drill-to-copper alignment) = HITL debt #5 — REQUIRED before any real fabrication."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47e068972f8959a978072b35239","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Port presentation panel (PCBCanvas/PCBEditor → in-process scene panel)","description":"Port the editor UI (~4 KLOC: PCBCanvas.gd 3013 + PCBEditor.gd 853 + PCBEditor.tscn) to an in-process godot_scene panel under the PluginScenePanelHost contract, CAD-style (CADPanel.gd is the reference).\n\nDeliberate divergence from CAD's pure-view split: the board model (ported PCBData + PCBComponent/PCBTrace/PCBNet + PCBSpatialIndex, ~2.6 KLOC) STAYS IN THE PANEL for responsive interactive editing (drag components, draw traces) — the model is canonically serializable to the board YAML, and the worker is consulted for compute (validate/generate/check/gerbers), not for edits. This is the hybrid settled in planning: CAD's philosophy (heavy compute out-of-process) without CAD's recompile-per-edit loop.\n\nScope:\n- PCBPanel.tscn/.gd on the scene-panel host lifecycle (register_panel before _on_panel_loaded, broker ctx, panel_save/load requests).\n- Honor host_owned save via pcb.serialize/pcb.deserialize channels (returns/accepts the canonical YAML).\n- Constraints from gap audit apply (e.g. off-tree plugins can't use class_name for cross-script type refs — known platform constraint; PCB value-type scripts must use preload-based references).\n- Annotation rendering/toolbar is NOT ported here — that's the annotation child; this panel mounts the platform dock instead.\n- Out: bespoke annotation code paths in PCBCanvas (stripped during port, replaced in annotation child).\n\nAcceptance: open/edit/save a real board through the plugin panel with interaction latency comparable to the in-tree editor.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:23:38","updated_at":"2026-07-05T23:10:43","priority":3,"tags":["panel","pcb","plugin-migration","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Panel port complete in two rounds. Round A (board model, minerva-plugins 3910fc4 + Minerva eaa15540): ~2.6 KLOC model ported with reviewer line-by-line fidelity pass; annotations/route_hints excised to substrate; C-19 journal symmetry fixed; canonical to_board_dict/from_board_dict boundary field-exact vs internal/board/minpcb.go; tests 29/0. Round B (UI, minerva-plugins c33217b + Minerva 8293964c): PCBCanvas 3013→~660 lines (board editing kept whole: selection/box-select, drag+snap, rotate, pan/zoom incl. zoom-at-cursor, tool modes, trace select/delete, lock, render toggles, keyboard set minus annotation keys; ALL bespoke annotation/route-hint code stripped — platform dock owns authoring); PCBEditor toolbar folded into PCBPanel; export via pcb.serialize IPC with payload_too_large surfaced; hybrid model-in-panel architecture per plan (worker = compute only). Host integrations (W-14 dirty gate incl. board restores, W-15 dual-shape file_path, sidecar, registry symmetry) preserved verbatim — reviewer-diffed against base. Tests: UI 22/0, model 29/0, smoke 43/0 incl. lifecycle, guard baseline throughout. Both reviews approve_with_notes (cold Opus), notes resolved or documented. Interactive acceptance = HITL debt #7 (12-point checklist) per back-loaded plan. Acceptance criterion \"interaction latency comparable to in-tree\" rides the HITL session (headless cannot measure it)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Annotation migration onto the platform substrate","description":"The center of gravity of this migration. PCB's annotations are the primary LLM-collaboration interface — pixel-accurate (board-mm-accurate) route annotations are how the user corrects LLM routing errors, and agent-router consumes them. Today's bespoke system (PCBAnnotation.gd, PCBRouteHint.gd, hand-rolled rendering in PCBCanvas) is strictly weaker than the platform substrate: in-memory only, no persistence, no dock, no lifecycle, no MCP access, free-form string anchors.\n\nMigrate fully onto the substrate (CAD's CadAnnotationHost + cad_edge_number_kind is the pattern) — this is an UPGRADE: route corrections become persistent (sidecar), dock-visible, lifecycle-tracked (open/applied/resolved/broken), and LLM-readable/writable via the existing 11 minerva_annotations_* tools, including render_overlay (LLM vision of annotations) and repair_anchor.\n\nGrandchildren cover: PCBAnnotationHost (board-mm↔screen transforms, describe_point → \"pad:U1.3\"/\"trace:GND:seg_42\", dock mounting); pcb/* anchor resolvers (pad/net/component/trace) with repair(); pcb_route_hint kind (width_mm/layer/detail-level payload, polyline geometry) + porting generic arrow/text/region/polyline uses to core 2d_* kinds; legacy data migration (.minpcb inline → v2 sidecar envelopes, human=magenta/AI=cyan preserved via author field); retiring the bespoke toolbar in favor of AnnotationToolbar + AnnotationDockPane.\n\nPlatform gaps discovered here (layer-aware view_context, retarget UI completion, anything a second editor would want) are filed under the substrate DCRs (019ddb70 / 019de430 / 019dc054) per the triage rule — NOT implemented ad-hoc in the plugin.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:23:57","updated_at":"2026-07-06T06:22:48","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","pcb","plugin-migration","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Annotation migration complete — all 5 grandchildren done across loop iterations 10-12: host board-space semantics 019eb4807e17 (transforms bound to live canvas, describe_point, W-9 fixed; 8e25f68), anchor resolvers 019eb4809a95 (pad/component/net/trace with stale fallback + whole-path repair; 6cb519e), kinds 019eb480e4ea (full pcb_route_hint contract + core 2d_* for generics; 6cb519e), legacy data migration 019eb4810167 (lossless inline→sidecar, idempotent; c2c22f3), retire bespoke toolbar 019eb4815504 (satisfied via panel-port Round B strip + substrate dispatch). Test coverage: semantics 77/0, migration 53/0, conformance 21/0 + UI/model/smoke suites; guard baseline throughout. Platform items filed en route: 019f361280 (core kinds reject plugin anchors), plus earlier 019f33d2c9bf (layer-aware view_context) and 019f33d2e004 (generic dirty wiring). Remaining interactive verification = HITL debt #8 (dock workflow) + #2 (marker tracking); the AI-route-proposal half of the route-correction workflow arrives with agent-router child 019eb47eb567. Human=magenta/AI=cyan preserved via substrate author colors; .minpcb compatibility preserved via migration; DCR acceptance items for annotations satisfied pending HITL session."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47e72a779e3b350f7b82f55efdd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Tool surface: 29 MCP tools → minerva_pcb_* (panel-local vs worker split)","description":"Re-home the 29 in-tree MCP tools from MCPPCBTools.gd (registered directly into MinervaMCPServer) to the plugin tool surface, namespaced minerva_pcb_* via the auto-prefix policy (backend name → minerva_pcb_<name>, like CAD's mcad_validate → minerva_cad_mcad_validate).\n\nFirst step: classify each of the 29 by execution venue —\n- **Panel-local** (board mutations: add/move component, draw trace, query board state, selection): served by the in-process panel via broker ipc_channels; tool call routes host → panel, mutates the live model, returns synchronously. The board model lives in the panel, so these CANNOT go to the worker.\n- **Worker** (compute: validate, generate, check_bom, check_libraries, gerbers): forwarded by the Go router to the embedded Python worker, stateless over the canonical YAML.\n- **Retired/replaced**: bespoke annotation tools superseded by the platform minerva_annotations_* surface (annotation child); any tool the gap audit marks as a pre-substrate idiosyncrasy not worth preserving.\n\nAcceptance (from parent DCR): all surviving tools work identically from the agent's perspective — capture a golden transcript of representative tool calls against the in-tree editor (if the spike restored it) and replay against the plugin. Tool descriptions/schemas land in the manifest or backend tools/list; plugin's own minerva_pcb_* names are credited in skill-dep validation (commit 6cee3169 behavior).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:24:06","updated_at":"2026-07-06T07:16:38","priority":3,"tags":["mcp-tools","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Tool surface re-homed (Minerva 114ed5c5 + minerva-plugins 5823ef6). Disposition of all 31 legacy tools documented in pcb/docs/tools.md: 19 panel-local → new core module MCPPcbPanelTools (MCPCadTools precedent — AnnotationHostRegistry duck-typing, zero plugin preloads, CAD-convention error shapes; all mutations via the model API so journal/undo/dirty ride along), 4 worker (already live), rest retired with documented replacements (annotations → minerva_annotations_*, interpret_route_hints → agent-router child, create_note → plugin_data, create_pcb_editor → generic, export_yaml → serialize channel). COEXISTENCE: register-only-when-absent + module ordering — legacy MCPPCBTools owns all 19 names until cutover; the new module flips on automatically when legacy is removed (reviewer-verified both directions; ordering invariant documented in-comment — CUTOVER NOTE for 019eb47f11e7: removing MCPPCBTools is the entire activation step). Acceptance \"identical from the agent's perspective\": same names/args; golden parity fixtures for 5 tools + 3 more reviewer-diffed byte-identical; get_image reimplemented on host render (snapshot-style). Known coexistence limitation: plugin-panel structural tools not transport-reachable until cutover (handlers fully validated by direct-call tests, 85/0). All regression suites exact, guard baseline. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes (all notes non-blocking)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47eb5677a62941bd8620a867596","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"agent-router: migrate claude-router as first-party plugin code","description":"claude-router (~5.8 KLOC Python at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote/claude-router: board, grid, pathfinder, kicad_io, hints, visualizer + test suite) is first-party experimental code that grew out of the same PCB work as PCBEditor. It migrates INTO the plugin as owned source — renamed claude_router → agent_router for ecosystem-agnostic naming — and evolves there. Not vendored, not pinned, no external-dependency treatment.\n\nThis is the routing strategy: Freerouting is dead (no Java, ever). Routing = manual in the panel + agent-router for LLM-assisted autorouting. Its waypoint-hints YAML design (\"routing friction is design feedback\") is the bridge to the annotation system.\n\nThe closed loop this enables:\nuser annotates a route in the panel (board-mm-accurate pcb_route_hint) → LLM reads it via annotations_query / render_overlay → worker runs agent-router with hints derived from those annotations → proposed traces come back as AI-authored (cyan) annotations → user corrects → apply.\n\nGrandchildren: (a) move + rename into worker/ with tests passing under the new package name; (b) annotation→hint bridge (pcb_route_hint envelopes → agent-router hint YAML); (c) proposal write-back + minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints tool (router output → AI annotations; apply transitions lifecycle open→applied and materializes traces into the board model).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:24:23","updated_at":"2026-07-06T15:40:17","priority":3,"tags":["agent-router","pcb","plugin-migration","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"agent-router migration complete — all 3 grandchildren done (loop iterations 15-17): (1) move+rename 019eb48174 (claude_router→agent_router into pcb/worker, 129 tests green, standalone preserved; minerva-plugins f827ca5); (2) annotation→hint bridge 019eb481ae28 (canonical board + pcb_route_hint envelopes → engine Board/RoutingHints, rotation convention verified to the render path, waypoint precision bit-exact; d23b4a0); (3) route write-back + minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints 019eb481cddd (cyan proposals → inspect → commit materializes traces + open→applied; 0e73cc6 + Minerva 21dcf094). The closed loop is code-complete: user annotates route (pcb_route_hint) → LLM reads via annotations_query/render_overlay → worker runs agent_router with bridged hints → proposals return as AI-cyan annotations → user corrects/applies. Routing = manual + agent-router (no Freerouting/Java, per plan). ONE go-live wiring step remains (019f3815e9f9): connect the panel broker's pcb.route request to the Python worker route method (out of the apply round's fence) — until then apply returns structured worker_unavailable. Every round cold-reviewed (all approve/approve_with_notes, 1 must_fix caught + fixed); guard baseline throughout.","blocked_by":"019f364c96c3716f84be546a9891095d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47ed3307c3a863de8835d75d393","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Regression + acceptance harness","description":"Hard requirement from planning: the migration must not break existing plugins — the plugin functional tests are the regression guard.\n\nScope:\n- **Existing-plugin regression**: scripts/run-functional-tests.sh (hermetic F1-F6 + per-plugin suite incl. test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd, marketplace install/start tests, scene-panel broker tests) stays green throughout the migration. Run per-merge, not just at the end.\n- **PCB smoke test**: test_pcb_plugin_smoke.gd modeled on test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd — install → start → tools/list handshake → auto-prefix check (minerva_pcb_*) → registry idempotency → stop/restart.\n- **Round-trip acceptance** (from parent DCR): existing .minpcb files (or fixtures if the spike didn't restore a baseline) load, save, project-export through the plugin; annotations + route hints round-trip through pack/unpack including inline→sidecar migration.\n- **Tool parity**: golden transcript replay from the tool-surface child.\n- **Gerber goldens**: from the gerber child.\n- **Live verification**: /verify-style manual pass on a real board: open, edit, annotate, route with agent-router, export gerbers.\n\nThis child owns the harness + CI wiring; the per-area tests are produced by their respective children and registered here.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:24:31","updated_at":"2026-07-05T20:02:23","priority":3,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","regression","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Harness round complete (Minerva development 091be3eb). Delivered: (1) --pcb-guard tier in run-functional-tests.sh — the per-round Layer-1 gate for all migration rounds (pcb smoke, CAD guard, 5 scene-panel suites; existing tiers untouched); (2) src/test/helpers/plugin_panel_driver.gd — reusable panel-hook driving (both Editor.gd:1117 load shapes, save, temp workspace with temp-root-guarded cleanup), consumed by both pcb sidecar tests; (3) Windows verification: guard 6/7 green, pcb smoke 43/0, the one FAIL is pre-existing (bug 019f33dcb7d6: host_fs test hardcodes /tmp — guard gate is \"no NEW failures\" until fixed). Cold Opus review approve_with_notes; notes applied (second caller migrated, cleanup guard) or deferred by design (raw-text/board-file utilities consumed by upcoming YAML round). Per item scope: round-trip acceptance, tool parity, gerber goldens register here as their producing children land."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47f11e7703493712d225abf1b77","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Remove in-tree PCBEditor + MCPPCBTools + Editor.Type.PCB","description":"Final child — only after the regression harness is green and the plugin has passed live verification.\n\nRemove:\n- src/Scripts/UI/Controls/PCBEditor/ (all 9 scripts, ~7.1 KLOC) + src/Scenes/PCBEditor.tscn\n- src/Scripts/Services/MCP/Modules/MCPPCBTools.gd + its registration in MinervaMCPServer._init_modules()\n- Editor.Type.PCB branch in Editor.create() + entry points (Note.gd PCB path, SingletonObject creatable item, EditorPane.add_pcb_editor, .minpcb file-open dispatch → now routed via PluginEditorRegistry file_extensions)\n\nCompatibility decisions:\n- Editor.Type enum slot: do NOT renumber — the breakage spike (sibling) will have shown whether saved-tab deserialization depends on integer enum values. Leave a tombstone/comment in the enum or migrate stored type values; never shift later entries.\n- .minpcb open with plugin not installed: clear \"install the pcb plugin\" path (mirror codetools messaging pattern), not a silent failure.\n- Old projects with PCB tabs: tab-restore maps the legacy editor type to the plugin panel (or a friendly placeholder if plugin absent).\n\nAcceptance (from parent DCR): PCBEditor directory is gone (or a thin compatibility shim), all existing dev boards still open via the plugin, full functional suite green.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:24:47","updated_at":"2026-06-11T02:24:47","priority":3,"tags":["cleanup","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47f71b5724d96048804ab92e635","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Lift CAD internal/bridge into minerva-plugins shared/ Go module","description":"CAD's internal/bridge (worker.go ~400 lines: subprocess lifecycle, request/response correlation, length-prefixed framing, cancellation, circuit breaker, process-group termination) plus internal/runtime (embedded Python extraction) are plugin-agnostic. Extract into the monorepo's shared/ Go module — the module the monorepo DCR (019da41ff6e5) already calls for — and make BOTH cad and pcb consume it.\n\nSteps: move bridge+runtime code to shared/, adjust go.work + replace directives, CAD imports the shared version (verify test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd still green — this is the existing-plugin regression guard applied to the riskiest refactor in the skeleton), pcb imports it fresh.\n\nThis is the first real shared/ resident; keep the API surface minimal (Worker, Request/Response/Notification, framing) rather than over-generalizing.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:25:11","updated_at":"2026-06-11T03:28:34","priority":2,"tags":["go","pcb","plugin-migration","shared"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47cd33978c89bd19e2907b9b747","resolution":"Shipped in minerva-plugins 1c8291b (main). shared/ module created (github.com/imrans-lab/minerva-plugins/shared — canonical prefix from birth per review): shared/bridge (worker lifecycle, correlation, framing, cancellation, circuit breaker, process groups; workerModule parameterized; bridgetest double) + shared/runtime (Python extraction; PythonPathRequest struct API). cad consumes via require+replace; cad/internal/{bridge,runtime,workerfake} removed (net -2402 LOC); go.work wires 5 modules. Verified: go build/vet/test green in shared+cad, GOOS=windows vet green, cad-plugin binary builds. Cold review (Fable): approve_with_notes, all 3 notes applied pre-commit. Follow-ups: plugins/019eb4b8d3e2 (codetools bridge fork → shared), minerva/019eb4b8e89e (smoke-test repoint)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47f8bb87272ad73d25d8f327057","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb manifest.json + Go router scaffold + smoke handshake","description":"Author minerva-plugins/pcb/manifest.json from the CAD template: id \"pcb\", backend {transport: stdio, entrypoint: ./pcb-plugin}, ui.panels[0] = {name: pcb_panel, kind: godot_scene, entry_scene: ui/PCBPanel.tscn, file_extensions: [\".minpcb\"], save_mode: host_owned, ipc_channels: pcb.serialize/pcb.deserialize/pcb.collect_export/pcb.apply_export + compute channels}, editor_items (new_pcb), capabilities [project_state, host_owned_save, project_export], skills entry (minerva_pcb_design, modeled on minerva_cad_modeling).\n\nGo router main.go: JSON-RPC loop, initialize/tools/list/tools/call/shutdown, registry pattern from CAD's internal/tools/, consuming shared/ bridge (sibling grandchild). Placeholder panel scene so install+start succeeds.\n\nAcceptance: PluginManager install → start → tools/list handshake green via a minimal test_pcb_plugin_smoke.gd scaffold; manifest passes test_plugin_definition.gd-style validation (tool prefix minerva_pcb_*, skill id format, panel refs).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:25:18","updated_at":"2026-06-11T04:09:03","priority":2,"tags":["manifest","pcb","plugin-migration","scaffold"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47cd33978c89bd19e2907b9b747","resolution":"Shipped: minerva-plugins 49ab097 (main) + Minerva d019d320 (development). pcb plugin scaffold: manifest (godot_scene panel, .minpcb, host_owned, pcb.* channels, minerva_pcb_design skill), Go stdio router with \"ping\" tool (→ minerva_pcb_ping), placeholder MinervaPluginPanel, go.work + gitignore wiring, module on canonical imrans-lab prefix with zero requires. Smoke baseline test_pcb_plugin_smoke.gd: 30/0 PASS; CAD regression guard 37/0 PASS. Fable review: must_fix ×2, both resolved pre-commit (ping naming stutter killing the skill's dangling tool_dep; dct scope hygiene). FINDINGS logged: router-loop duplication is intentional copy — shared-router extraction now justified by two consumers (future item); PluginManager install is non-idempotent by host contract."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47fd15e78c0b2e96f261f111335","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Static contract diff: PCBEditor behaviors → substrate capabilities (gap register)","description":"Enumerate every PCBEditor behavior and classify against the substrate: supported / partial / missing / by-design-different. Surfaces to cover:\n\n1. **29 MCP tools** (MCPPCBTools.gd) — arg shapes vs broker request/response envelope; which need panel state vs YAML compute.\n2. **Canvas interactions** (PCBCanvas.gd 3013 lines) — selection model, drag/translate/rotate, layer switching, zoom/pan vs scene-panel host assumptions.\n3. **Annotations** — largely pre-mapped (2026-06-10 exploration): plugin-side = pcb/* resolvers, pcb_route_hint kind, board-mm transforms, describe_point; platform soft spots = layer-aware view_context, retarget UI (019ddb70/019de430 territory).\n4. **Broker/save contract** — host_owned channels; DECIDE: paired_dsl with YAML as text half (CAD-symmetric, free buffer sync, user-editable source) vs single-view panel.\n5. **File/editor routing** — .minpcb extension claim, New→ item, tab restore of legacy Editor.Type.PCB tabs.\n6. **Undo/redo + co-editing hooks** — PCB has bespoke history in PCBData; what does the panel host/buffer system assume?\n7. **Performance envelope** — spatial-index query latency through panel dispatch; render_overlay at full board scale; dock with hundreds of annotations.\n\nOutput: the gap register as a docket artifact — each entry pre-triaged with the standing rule (\"would a second editor want this?\" → platform child under substrate DCRs / plugin-side / PCB-is-wrong).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:25:36","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:13:46","priority":2,"tags":["audit","pcb","plugin-migration","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d19417670b58101302e8b87b6","resolution":"Gap register delivered: Docs/design/PCB-substrate-gap-register.md, Minerva development commit 2281735c (base 6fada82b). 64 classified entries across 3 audit surfaces (A tools/broker/routing 14, B canvas/undo/perf 19, C annotations 32 incl. pre-mapping verification table). Fable review: must_fix (5 additions — Editor.gd:1108 host_owned JSON load-shape row, channel dual-registration caveat, restart_plugin manifest-cache gotcha, 2 citation fixes, B-19 apply_sync row), all applied; 20+ evidence spot-checks all accurate; 7/7 surface coverage after fixes. Key outputs for siblings: single-view host_owned recommended (paired_dsl deferred until YAML round-trip exists per 019eb47d36bb); C-31/C-32 AnnotationV2Schema drift must be reconciled before annotation child starts; A-12 enum-ordinal tab hazard is platform-level; tool count is 31 not 29 (DCR text needs updating). 12 consolidated open questions feed walking skeleton (019eb47fe8a0) and triage (019eb48022)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb47fe8a07ca6a54c40da1e133a46","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Walking skeleton: minimal vertical slice through every substrate seam","description":"Before any bulk porting: build the smallest slice that touches every substrate seam, so unknown-unknown gaps surface in week one instead of after 7 KLOC is ported against wrong assumptions.\n\nThe slice: open a board file → panel renders a few components via the scene-panel host → place ONE pcb_route_hint annotation through the dock pane → it persists to the sidecar → LLM reads it via minerva_annotations_query and SEES it via minerva_annotations_render_overlay → save/reload round-trips.\n\nSeams exercised: PluginScenePanelHost lifecycle, broker IPC + save channels, PluginEditorRegistry file routing, AnnotationHost subclass + kind + anchor resolver + AnnotationDockPane mounting, AnnotationSidecar persistence, MCP annotation surface.\n\nEvery pinch found goes straight into the gap register (sibling) with the standing triage rule applied. Quality bar: this is throwaway-quality scaffolding for discovery — durable implementations come in the real children, which consume this slice's findings. Sequenced after the plugin skeleton child; before panel/worker/annotation children start.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:25:42","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:45:34","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","substrate-gap","walking-skeleton"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d19417670b58101302e8b87b6","resolution":"Walking skeleton complete. All six slice steps proven: .pcbskel routing → panel mount → dock click-to-author pcb_route_hint → sidecar persistence → minerva_annotations_query/render_overlay → save/reload round-trip. Human 3b verified: install via Plugin Manager UI, panel mount + render, dock authoring, dirty glyph (after W-14 fix). Findings W-1..W-15 in gap register; three live-only failures (W-13 authoring tool, W-14 dirty relay, W-15 sidecar path) found by human test, fixed same-day with regression coverage (smoke 43/0 incl. lifecycle Section B on Windows). Residual live sidecar check deferred to HITL debt register #1 per back-loaded autonomy plan (DCR comment 507). Commits: minerva-plugins b166d80..e237b84; Minerva 596a6fc0..fae2e956."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb480224776aea4126cd73aaaa1de","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Triage gap register → platform children under substrate DCRs vs plugin work","description":"Take the gap register (static diff + walking-skeleton findings) and dispose of every entry:\n\n- **Platform** (\"a second editor would want this\") → file as children under the substrate DCRs: 019ddb70 (action-intent envelope + plugin semantic anchors), 019de430 (universal annotation dock/workbench), 019dc054 (cross-editor annotation subsystem), or the scene-panel broker line. Implemented in Minerva core. Likely members from static analysis: board/document-space coordinate transform hooks in AnnotationHost, layer-aware view_context, retarget/repair UI completion.\n- **Plugin-side** (PCB-only need) → absorb into the relevant pcb plugin child (panel port / annotation migration / tool surface).\n- **PCB-is-wrong** (pre-substrate idiosyncrasy) → change PCB's convention during the port; document the divergence in the migration notes so .minpcb import handles old data.\n\nThis is also where the standing rule gets institutionalized: gaps found LATER during porting follow the same path (file immediately, tag substrate-gap, link to DCR 019dc140, triage with the same question). Rubric framing: durability and DRY at the platform seam — don't bloat the platform with PCB-isms, don't fork platform behavior inside the plugin.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:25:56","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:30","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugin-migration","substrate-gap","triage"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d19417670b58101302e8b87b6","resolution":"Gap register fully disposed (79 rows: 64 static A/B/C + 15 walking-skeleton W). PLATFORM (7 filings): schema reconciliation 019f335754e0 (pre-filed, C-31/C-32/C-6/C-7, blocks annotation child); enum-ordinal tab remap 019f33d2575d (A-12, child of migration DCR, cutover prerequisite); structured-panel undo primitive 019f33d282c8 (B-9/B-11, standalone — predecessor broker DCRs shipped); cheap sync host-capability lane 019f33d29824 (B-12/B-13/Q10, standalone); host_owned file-path contract 019f33d2b230 (A-14 + W-15 questions, standalone); layer-aware view_context 019f33d2c9bf (C-23, under annotation DCR 019dc054); annotations→dirty generic wiring 019f33d2e004 (W-14, under 019dc054); adoption-guide author_ui docs 019f33d2f763 (W-13, under 019dc054, coordinate with schema recon). PLUGIN-SIDE rows absorb into existing children: A-1/A-2/A-3 + A.1 table → tool-surface child 019eb47e72a7 (note: 31 tools not 29); B-1..B-7/B-10/B-14/B-18/B-19 → panel port 019eb47e0689; C-1..C-30 → annotation migration 019eb47e4e7e; A-7/A-9 → already closed by skeleton (W-11). PCB-IS-WRONG (drop at port, do not carry): A-4 bespoke Note.linked_pcb_data → use plugin_data notes; A-5 bespoke create tool → minerva_create_plugin_editor; A-6 paired_dsl aspiration → single-view host_owned; A-8 direct save path; A-10/A-11 core extension/menu claims → removed at cutover; B-8 context menus (never existed); B-15 duplicate-node capture → live-viewport read; B-16 parallel annotation system; C-19 change-journal asymmetry (pre-existing bug, restore parity via substrate signals only for what's re-implemented). Standing rule remains in force for gaps found during later porting."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb480415a76acb26773501eab3510","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Spike: validate gerber-writer (Ucamco) output for fab correctness","description":"Validate the pure-Python gerber path before committing the implementation child to it.\n\n1. Confirm Ucamco's gerber-writer package (pure Python, from the Gerber spec owner) is current/maintained; evaluate pcb-tools as fallback.\n2. Hand-write a tiny 2-layer test board (a few pads, traces, vias, outline) through the candidate writer: F.Cu/B.Cu/mask/silk/edge-cuts + Excellon drill.\n3. Acceptance gates: loads with zero warnings in gerbv AND KiCAD's gerber viewer; visual render matches expectation; aperture/coordinate precision adequate for standard fab tolerances (KiCAD-equivalent 4.6 format).\n4. Document the chosen library + any format constraints (X2 attributes, arc support, plated/non-plated drill split) for the implementation work.\n\nTimebox: one session. If both candidates fail the bar, escalate — fallback options (write RS-274X directly, it's a simple text format; or reconsider kicad-cli detection) need a human call since they trade off self-containment.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:26:04","updated_at":"2026-07-05T20:48:01","priority":3,"tags":["gerber","pcb","plugin-migration","spike"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47ddebc756abba70aaa03873e8b","resolution":"Verdict: gerber-writer 0.4.3.3 VIABLE with caveats — no escalation. Evidence: minerva-plugins 0bd438d (pcb/spikes/gerber: generate.py, validate.py, goldens for 6 layers + PTH/NPTH Excellon, REPORT.md). 20/20 structural checks (RS-274X skeleton, X2 attrs, apertures, bounds), pygerber 2.4.3 independent round-trip parse, byte-identical across two clean venvs. Constraints for implementation child 019eb47ddebc: (1) coordinate format auto-selects 3.6 by board extent — pin/override if 4.6 parity matters; (2) X2 attributes emitted as G04 #@! comments (spec-legal; interop risk with fab tooling scanning only %TF. — consider post-processing or upstream flag); (3) NO Excellon support — drill generation (incl. PTH/NPTH split) is ours, hand-written reference in generate.py; (4) pcb-tools is dead on py3.12 (open 'rU' ValueError) — pygerber is the validator of record. Board contract gaps surfaced for the YAML schema: TH-pad drill fields + non-plated-hole entity (commented on 019eb47ddebc). Viewer acceptance (gerbv/KiCad, zero warnings, visual match) deferred to HITL debt #5 with concrete golden paths."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb4807e177346999fbe012d653d4e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PCBAnnotationHost: AnnotationHost subclass with board-space semantics","description":"Subclass AnnotationHost for the PCB panel (CadAnnotationHost.gd is the reference). Responsibilities:\n\n- Board-mm ↔ screen transforms: transform_doc_to_screen()/get_annotation_view_transform()/get_annotation_zoom() over PCBCanvas's pan/zoom state, so annotation geometry is board-coordinate-accurate (survives zoom/pan; exact for the LLM).\n- describe_point(doc_pos) returning semantic labels via the spatial index: \"pad:U1.3\", \"trace:GND:seg_42\", \"component:U3\", falling back to canvas.point. This drives anchored_to stamping on every add/update.\n- get_view_context() — layer-aware if the gap-audit triage lands that platform extension (e.g. \"pcb:F.Cu\"); plain \"pcb\" otherwise.\n- Registry wiring: own AnnotationRegistry with BuiltinKinds.register_all + pcb kinds (sibling); AnnotationHostRegistry.register(editor_tab_name, host) so MCP tools reach unsaved in-memory annotations.\n- Mount AnnotationDockPane.set_host(host) in the panel; render_content_to_image() for render_overlay (LLM vision of the board + annotations).\n- Sidecar persistence flags so AnnotationSidecar read/write engages on save/load.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:26:20","updated_at":"2026-07-06T05:18:53","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","resolution":"Board-space semantics shipped (minerva-plugins 8e25f68 + Minerva 3ef3f9f6). Transforms: get_annotation_view_transform/transform_doc_to_screen/get_annotation_zoom mirror pcb_canvas.world_to_screen byte-exactly (reviewer-diffed), bound via set_canvas with symmetric connect/disconnect; canvas view_changed signal on all four pan/zoom mutation paths + resized relay drives overlay redraw — fixes W-9. describe_point: pad (rotation-correct pin world positions) → component → trace (net name) → board-point fallback; anchored_to stamped post-validation (conformance 21/0 intact). render_content_to_image: inset-safe viewport crop, headless-null. view_context stays flat \"pcb\" (layer-aware = platform item 019f33d2c9bf). Load-bearing constraint documented at the mount site: any future on-screen AnnotationOverlay must be a child of the canvas, not its PanelContainer. Tests: semantics 33/0, conformance 21/0, UI 22/0, model 29/0, smoke 43/0, guard baseline. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes (comment applied). Visual alignment under live zoom/pan = HITL debt #2 (proxy upgraded from \"none\" to the transform test suite)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb4809a9572109c501b89275d1cfe","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb/* anchor resolvers (pad, net, component, trace) with repair()","description":"Register v2 semantic anchor resolvers via AnnotationAnchorRegistry.register(\"pcb\", type, resolver), following the cad/edge precedent:\n\n- pcb/pad — id {component: \"U1\", pin: \"3\"}; snapshot carries board-mm position\n- pcb/component — id \"U3\"\n- pcb/net — id \"GND\" (multi-geometry: summary names the net, position snaps to nearest segment)\n- pcb/trace — id {net, segment}\n\nEach resolver: validate() (id format + existence against the live board model), summary() (\"pad U1.3\", \"net GND\"), and repair() — the critical one for routes: when the board changes (component moved, net renumbered, trace rerouted), re-locate the target or mark stale so the dock's retarget flow can engage. For polyline route hints, repair must consider the whole path, not just one point — a moved component can invalidate the entire proposed route; stale → lifecycle \"broken\" → user or agent re-routes.\n\nResolution falls back to anchor.snapshot.position when stale (substrate default), so annotations never vanish — they degrade to last-known-good and flag themselves.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:26:27","updated_at":"2026-07-06T05:54:48","priority":2,"tags":["anchors","annotations","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","resolution":"Shipped (minerva-plugins 6cb519e + Minerva b82cdd53, combined round with 019eb480e4ea). Four resolvers registered (pcb/pad {component,pin}, pcb/component, pcb/net nearest-segment, pcb/trace) with the platform's real contract (reviewer-verified against AnnotationHost/_normalise_resolve_result/AnnotationAnchorRegistry): live resolution with rotation-correct pin positions, stale:true + anchor.snapshot.position fallback when targets vanish (annotations degrade, never disappear), populated get_anchor_registry() so MCP repair_anchor and the sidebar reach repair(); repair refreshes snapshot or returns null-when-gone; route-hint whole-path repair (repair_route_hint) validates every source/dest endpoint and returns broken:true on any missing pad rather than silently re-anchoring. Platform fact recorded: NO automatic lifecycle mutation from stale resolves — lifecycle changes are explicit via update_annotation_lifecycle. Tests: semantics 77/0 (review must_fix caught a swallowed SCRIPT ERROR that had hidden the four repair assertions — fixed, delta exact), all other suites green, guard baseline."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb480e4ea7440951d19fce873fa04","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb_route_hint kind + port generic annotations to core 2d_* kinds","description":"Two tracks:\n\n**1. pcb_route_hint kind** (extends AnnotationKind; cad_edge_number_kind is the reference). Replaces PCBRouteHint as a domain-specific plugin kind:\n- kind_payload: {hint_type: waypoint|single_trace|bus, detail_level: sparse|guided|detailed, layer: \"F.Cu\", width_mm: 0.25, source_pins: [\"U1.15\"], dest_pins: [...]}\n- Geometry: polyline primitives in board-mm (waypoints); render with width/layer-aware styling; hit_test along the path, not the AABB.\n- summary() for the dock + LLM (\"route hint U1.15→J2.3, F.Cu, 0.25mm, 4 waypoints\"); to_chat_context() with structured JSON so annotations_query grounds the agent.\n- actions(): \"apply\" (run agent-router for this hint — wired in the agent-router child), \"reject\"; lifecycle open→applied when traces materialize.\n- author_ui(): waypoint-click authoring tool (AnnotationAuthorTool) for pixel-accurate route correction — THE core user workflow.\n\n**2. Generic annotations → core kinds.** PCBAnnotation's ARROW/TEXT/REGION/POLYLINE map directly onto core 2d_arrow/2d_text/2d_region/2d_polyline — no custom kinds needed; delete the bespoke enum. Human=magenta / AI=cyan via the envelope author field (substrate already renders author colors; verify exact colors carry over or land per-author color in triage).\n\nMore kinds (the user expects the type set to grow: silk notes, pad/clearance notes, DRC markers) follow this template as future siblings — this child establishes the pattern.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:26:46","updated_at":"2026-07-06T05:54:57","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","kinds","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","resolution":"Shipped (minerva-plugins 6cb519e + Minerva b82cdd53, combined round with 019eb4809a95). Track 1 — pcb_route_hint full contract: extended kind_payload (hint_type/detail_level/layer/width_mm/source_pins/dest_pins, all optional — skeleton envelopes stay valid), accepts pcb/pad + pcb/board.point anchors, layer-tinted width/zoom-aware polyline render, segment-distance hit_test, \"route hint U1.15→J2.3, F.Cu, 0.25mm, N waypoints\" summary flowing into the base to_chat_context hook (real API — structured_json + text), actions(): reject → lifecycle resolved (platform vocab has no rejected), apply = documented no-op stub for agent-router child 019eb47eb567, waypoint-click author tool (click appends with preview, double-click commits, Escape/right-click cancels; Enter branch removed — overlay only forwards ESCAPE/DELETE). Track 2 — generic annotations use CORE 2d_arrow/text/region/polyline (BuiltinKinds already per-host; no pcb duplicates); author colors human-magenta/AI-cyan ride substrate defaults (asserted). PCB-annotation-migration.md reconciled across §3/§5/§7.2/§9 to the final decision. Future kinds (silk notes, DRC markers) follow this template as siblings."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb481016c7184878cf3e36c395978","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Legacy annotation data migration: inline .minpcb → v2 sidecar envelopes","description":"Lossless migration of existing annotation data when a legacy .minpcb is first opened by the plugin:\n\n- PCBAnnotation {type, positions[], text, color, author, associated_component/net} → v2 envelope: kind = 2d_arrow/2d_text/2d_region/2d_polyline; positions → primitives in board-mm; author preserved (human/ai → magenta/cyan); associated_component/net → proper pcb/component / pcb/net anchors (upgrade from free-form strings) with snapshot fallback.\n- PCBRouteHint {hint_type, detail_level, layer, width, source_pins, dest_pins, waypoints} → pcb_route_hint envelope with full kind_payload; waypoints → polyline primitives.\n- Write to <file>.annotations.json via AnnotationSidecar (atomic); strip inline blobs from the converted .minpcb on next save.\n- Idempotent: re-opening an already-migrated file must not duplicate; migration marker or sidecar presence check.\n- Round-trip test: in-tree-authored fixture with every annotation/hint variant → migrate → verify all fields, colors, and anchor semantics survive → pack/unpack through project export (parent DCR acceptance).","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:26:53","updated_at":"2026-07-06T06:22:00","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","data-migration","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","resolution":"Shipped (minerva-plugins c2c22f3 + Minerva 575eb36f). One-shot lossless migration on legacy-board load: ARROW/TEXT/REGION/POLYLINE → core 2d_* envelopes (2-corner region promotion, labels as trailing text primitives, color only-when-diverging, author dicts, legacy_id/client_id preserved, unknown fields → legacy_extra + warning); PCBRouteHint → pcb_route_hint with full extended payload (pcb/pad anchor when first source pin resolves live, else board.point). CONSTRAINT DISCOVERED + verified: core 2d_* kinds accept only core/* anchors, so generic annotations anchor at core/canvas.point with associated_component/net preserved losslessly in kind_payload — real semantic anchoring for generic kinds needs platform item 019f361280 (filed under annotation DCR, options analyzed). Idempotency = sidecar presence; zombie edge reviewer-verified safe (save strips inline blobs + rewrites sidecar in the same call). Migration is restore-class: runs in the _restoring gate, immediate durable save_sidecar, tab stays clean. Batch-resilient (per-blob warnings, never aborts). Tests: 53/0 new suite + all regressions exact + guard baseline. Review: approve (cold Opus; shape fidelity diffed against in-tree serializers key-by-key). Non-blocking notes recorded: all-invalid boards re-attempt on each open (harmless); warnings surface transiently, no durable audit trail."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb481550474a596f2aba87ba44299","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Retire bespoke annotation toolbar/rendering in PCBCanvas","description":"Once host + resolvers + kinds + data migration land: strip PCBCanvas's hand-rolled annotation code paths (own drawing methods, selected_annotation_id state, show_annotations flag, annotation_human_color/annotation_ai_color, hard-coded tool buttons) and replace with substrate dispatch:\n\n- Rendering: registry.render() per annotation via the host's render context (canvas draw pass calls into AnnotationRegistry, placeholder rendering for unknown kinds comes free).\n- Authoring: AnnotationToolbar (registry-populated, plugin kinds appear automatically) inside AnnotationDockPane — note the existing in-tree item \"Refactor AnnotationToolbar layout to match PCBEditor\" (019dc632dfe1) went the OTHER direction (platform adopted PCB's layout), so the visual style should already be familiar.\n- Selection/manipulation: substrate Select/Translate/Rotate/Scale tools (019dc64bbc79 brought the PCBEditor pattern to the platform).\n- PCBAnnotation.gd and PCBRouteHint.gd are deleted (their data lives on via the migration grandchild; their behavior via the kinds).\n\nAcceptance: zero bespoke annotation code remains in the panel; all annotation interaction flows through the dock pane + registry; existing route-correction workflow (author waypoints, see AI proposals in cyan) works end-to-end through the substrate.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:27:15","updated_at":"2026-07-06T06:22:28","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","cleanup","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e4e7e7aa1bcd5fd7ae740ebe6","resolution":"Acceptance satisfied across the panel-port and annotation rounds rather than as a separate strip pass: (1) \"zero bespoke annotation code remains in the panel\" — panel-port Round B (minerva-plugins c33217b) ported PCBCanvas WITHOUT any annotation/route-hint drawing, tool modes, pickers, context items, or shortcuts (reviewer grep-verified: annotation tokens only in doc comments + the three allowed host surfaces); PCBAnnotation.gd/PCBRouteHint.gd deletion from Minerva core happens at cutover (019eb47f11e7) with the rest of the in-tree editor — their data lives on via migration (019eb4810167), their behavior via the kinds (019eb480e4ea). (2) \"all annotation interaction flows through the dock pane + registry\" — host registers core 2d_* + pcb_route_hint kinds; AnnotationToolbar populates from author_ui() (W-13 fix); substrate Select tools apply. (3) \"route-correction workflow end-to-end (author waypoints, see AI proposals in cyan)\" — waypoint-click author tool + author-color rendering shipped; the AI-proposal half needs the agent-router child (019eb47eb567), and the end-to-end interactive verification is HITL debt (dock workflow items). Panel mount-site comment documents the overlay-parenting constraint for when the on-screen overlay mounts."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb48174e6757cb8ffb8fdf177b6f6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Move + rename claude_router → agent_router into plugin worker (tests green)","description":"Migrate ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote/claude-router/src/claude_router (board.py, grid.py, pathfinder.py, kicad_io.py, hints, visualizer — ~5.8 KLOC) into minerva-plugins/pcb/worker/ as the agent_router package. First-party owned source from here on — it evolves in the plugin; smart-remote copy is left as historical (no sync-back).\n\n- Package rename claude_router → agent_router throughout (imports, pyproject, egg-info regenerated); user-facing strings say \"agent-router\" (ecosystem-agnostic — works with any LLM, not just Claude).\n- Bring the full test suite (tests/test_router, test_pathfinder, test_grid, test_board, test_hints, test_kicad_io, test_visualizer, test_bus_routing); all green under the new package name in the worker's environment.\n- Wire as worker dependency alongside circuit_synth; expose a `route` worker method (input: board YAML + hints; output: proposed traces) — the dispatcher entry agent-router work builds on.\n- Keep its standalone-usable structure (it predates the plugin and is useful headless); no Minerva imports leak into the package.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:27:23","updated_at":"2026-07-06T14:47:15","priority":3,"tags":["agent-router","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47eb5677a62941bd8620a867596","resolution":"agent_router migrated (minerva-plugins f827ca5). ~4.7 KLOC pure-Python autorouter moved one-way from C:\\gitlab\\ccsandbox\\smart-remote\\claude-router into pcb/worker/agent_router/ (historical copy untouched, no sync-back). Renamed claude_router→agent_router throughout — reviewer-verified 0 residual claude refs and modules byte-identical to source modulo import/string sed. Standalone preserved (only pyyaml, already a worker dep; zero pcb_worker/minerva coupling). route dispatcher method in methods._HANDLERS: lazy agent_router import (cold-start untouched), double try/except (routing fault → {ok:false,error}, loop never crashes), _board_from_native/_serialize in methods.py not the package. Tests: agent_router suite 129/0 (board 12/grid 17/hints 16/kicad_io 16/pathfinder 20/router 31/visualizer 17; test_bus_routing is a __main__ demo, genuinely 0 test_ fns), existing worker 50/0, guard baseline, Go green. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes (both notes claim-accuracy nits). BOARD-FORMAT GAP for the bridge grandchild 019eb481ae28: agent_router.Board wants flat absolute-positioned pads; canonical model has component x_mm/y_mm/rotation_deg + component-relative pin offsets — absolutes are DERIVABLE via transform, so the bridge composes them (not a missing-data blocker). route currently accepts the engine's native positioned-pad shape; NOT an MCP tool yet (apply grandchild 019eb481cddd owns that)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb481ae287404aa633ba6d887f3f3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Annotation→hint bridge: pcb_route_hint envelopes → agent-router hint YAML","description":"Translate between the annotation substrate's pcb_route_hint envelopes and agent-router's existing waypoint-hints YAML format (hints module — already designed for \"human provides routing guidance via YAML\").\n\n- Forward: collect open pcb_route_hint annotations for a board (from live host or sidecar) → hint YAML: waypoints from polyline primitives (board-mm), layer/width/detail_level/source_pins/dest_pins from kind_payload, pin anchors resolved via pcb/pad resolvers.\n- Field-mapping fidelity matters: the user's hand-placed waypoints are pixel-accurate corrections — coordinate precision must survive the round trip exactly (no float drift through transforms).\n- Selection semantics: which hints feed a given routing run — by lifecycle (open only), by explicit ids (apply-action on one hint), or all-for-net.\n- Lives panel/host-side (it reads annotations) with the YAML handed to the worker's route method; pure function, unit-tested against fixture envelopes.","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:27:38","updated_at":"2026-07-06T15:07:11","priority":3,"tags":["agent-router","annotations","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47eb5677a62941bd8620a867596","resolution":"Annotation→hint bridge shipped (minerva-plugins d23b4a0). Closes the board-format gap from grandchild 1. route_bridge.py (in pcb_worker/, no leak into standalone agent_router): board_to_router composes absolute pad positions = component pos + rotate(relative pin offset, −rotation). ROTATION CONVENTION VERIFIED CORRECT TO THE RENDER PATH (the round's high-risk item): the canvas draws every pad/pin via Transform2D(deg_to_rad(−rotation)) (pcb_canvas.gd), and get_pad_world_transform's +rotation variant is DEAD CODE called by nothing — reviewer traced to screen — so the router routes to where the user actually sees pads. hints_to_router builds the dict parse_hints wants (reuse, not reimplement); waypoints float()-identity, bit-exact (== assertions, no rounding — pixel-accurate corrections preserved). Selection: open-lifecycle default (resolved hints don't re-drive routing), all/ids/net modes; unresolvable refs warn-and-skip. route auto-discriminates canonical vs native board shape; grandchild-1 path intact. Field mapping surfaced honestly: per-hint width→widest run-wide trace_width; detail_level dropped WITH a warning (review note applied); pad size best-effort. Tests 198/0 (129+50+19), Go green, guard baseline. Review: cold Opus approve_with_notes; both notes resolved. NEXT (final grandchild 019eb481cddd): route write-back as AI-cyan proposal annotations + minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints tool (open→applied lifecycle, materialize traces)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb481cddd71b2a654cf668e098edb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Route-proposal write-back + minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints tool","description":"Close the collaboration loop:\n\n- **Write-back**: agent-router's proposed traces return as AI-authored annotations (cyan) — proposal polylines the user can inspect in the dock and on the canvas BEFORE anything mutates the board. Each proposal links to the hint(s) it answers.\n- **Apply**: minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints {editor_name, hint_ids?} → runs the bridge (sibling) + worker route method → returns {applied: N, failed: [...], proposals: [...]}. On user/agent acceptance, proposals materialize as real traces in the board model and the source hints transition lifecycle open→applied (annotations_update_status path). Rejection leaves the board untouched and the hint open.\n- **Iteration loop**: user corrects a bad proposal by editing/adding route hints (pixel-accurate waypoints) → re-run → new proposal. The LLM sees corrections via annotations_query (structured) and render_overlay (visual).\n- Per the agent-router README philosophy, failure output should be design feedback: when routing fails, return WHERE it got stuck (congestion area, blocked net) as structured data the agent can reason about — not just \"failed\".","created_at":"2026-06-11T02:27:46","updated_at":"2026-07-06T15:40:04","priority":3,"tags":["agent-router","mcp-tools","pcb","plugin-migration"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47eb5677a62941bd8620a867596","resolution":"Route-correction collaboration loop closed (Minerva 21dcf094 + minerva-plugins 0e73cc6). minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints in MCPPcbPanelTools (20th tool): gather open pcb_route_hint annotations → canonical board from live model → worker route (via bridge) → propose (default): AI-authored cyan pcb_route_hint proposals linked via kind_payload.proposal_for, board UNMUTATED; commit=true: materialize routes as journaled traces + source hints open→applied. Iterate: _gather excludes applied hints AND AI proposals so re-runs pick only fresh open hints. Failure-as-design-feedback: {net,from,to,reason} stuck data. save_to_history-after-insertion (independent-review must_fix, undo/redo guard 2→0→2). Tests: apply 47/0 + all regressions green; worker route call stubbed headless (canned RoutingResult, noted). Review: independent cold Opus APPROVE (all 8 points verified incl. the must_fix and the honest fence boundary). GO-LIVE follow-up filed 019f3815e9f9: the worker hop's in-fence half is wired but no in-fence path reaches the Python worker route method — apply returns route_worker_unavailable until pcb.route is declared in manifest.json + forwarded (or minerva_pcb_route registered in worker_tools.go). Live 3b check rides HITL debt."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb4b8e89e7f789872304140accf52","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Repoint test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd at canonical minerva-plugins/cad","description":"src/test/test_cad_plugin_smoke.gd hardcodes CAD_PLUGIN_DIR_REL := \"/github/plugins/cad\" (line ~26) — the DEPRECATED ipeerbhai/plugins checkout. Canonical CAD now lives at ~/github/minerva-plugins/cad (and just gained the shared/ bridge module). The smoke test currently validates a stale copy; regression coverage for the real plugin is illusory.\n\nFix: point at ~/github/minerva-plugins/cad (keep the build-instructions SKIP message in sync: \"cd <dir> && go build -o cad-plugin .\"). Consider an env-var override (MINERVA_CAD_PLUGIN_DIR) so CI/other machines aren't path-locked.\n\nFound during work-cycle round 2 (shared/ extraction, MNR 019eb47f71b5) — out of that round's fence (Minerva repo).","created_at":"2026-06-11T03:27:57","updated_at":"2026-06-11T03:55:48","priority":3,"tags":["cad","plugin-migration","testing"],"resolution":"Shipped in Minerva 7a8ad123 (development). Smoke test defaults to ~/github/minerva-plugins/cad (canonical), MINERVA_CAD_PLUGIN_DIR env override added. Layer-1: full smoke suite PASS against the canonical plugin carrying the shared/ extraction (minerva-plugins 1c8291b) — lifecycle, tool discovery, prefix policy, idempotency, stop/restart, MCP signal propagation. CAD regression coverage now validates the real plugin."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb576cd8a7f2ebc037c5f2a5fd40a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Merging diverged .dct files: union + renumber colliding comment ids + patch #N cross-refs","created_at":"2026-06-11T06:55:22","updated_at":"2026-06-11T08:08:53","tags":["dct","docket","git","merge","ndjson"],"value":".dct is NDJSON. Cross-machine git merge of a .dct: (1) STOP/quiesce the Docket service first, or at minimum make ZERO docket MCP writes mid-merge — a write flushes the service's stale in-memory DB over the working file (this wiped 74 records once); restart Docket after committing so it reloads. (2) Union-resolve hunks (strip markers, keep both sides) — item ids are ULIDs, never collide; (3) comment ids are sequential ints per machine and DO collide — dup-scan (_type,id,item_id), renumber the unpushed side to max+1.. (events don't reference comment ids; links/events have id=None by design); (4) AFTER renumbering, grep BOTH minerva.dct and plugins.dct (cross-project refs exist!) for '#<old-id>' free-text references in items/comments/events — patch items via docket_update, and add a mapping comment on the affected DCR for the immutable events/comments; (5) validate every line parses as JSON.","component":"docket","key":"dct-merge-conflict-procedure","confidence":"high","research_cost":10}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb7851e547e259003757337d6803e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"host.providers.chat: no zero-config default model + max_tokens forwarded untranslated","created_at":"2026-06-11T16:30:15","updated_at":"2026-06-11T16:30:15","tags":["agent-relay","broker","hitl","host.providers.chat"],"value":"Two live-confirmed gaps (HITL 2026-06-11): (1) model=\"default\" is a DEAD END for plugins — broker resolves it to a CoreProvider then a guard rejects with \"needs model_spec={kind:'core_action', service_client_id, action_name}\" (CapabilityBroker.gd:2207 + ~2326); model_spec kinds are core_action/dynamic/builtin ONLY (no 'provider' kind). Plugins must pass an exact model_name (e.g. model=\"gpt-5.4-mini\") — resolved via exact-match loop, optional provider arg disambiguates. (2) Broker forwards max_tokens verbatim to providers; ChatGPT backend rejects it (\"Unsupported parameter: max_tokens\") — plugins should omit max_tokens entirely.","component":"plugin-substrate","key":"providers-chat-gotchas","confidence":"high","research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb787804378fe90999c4eea25fee5","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"MISDIAGNOSED→reframed: plugin tool-arg schemas come from the router's tools/list at INSTALL only; manifest input_schema unused; no refresh on plugin_restart","description":"ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS WRONG (said Minerva restart required; it never was). Live-proven mechanism (HITL session 3, 2026-06-11):\n\n1. Minerva snapshots plugin tool schemas from the plugin ROUTER's tools/list response at plugin INSTALL time. The manifest's input_schema is validated at install but NOT what arg handling uses — agent-relay's Rust tools_list_schema() had drifted from manifest.json for read_turn, and the router's stale shape won.\n2. plugin_restart does NOT refetch tools/list — only plugin_remove+plugin_install does.\n3. The \"stringified args\" symptom is CLIENT-side: the MCP client (Claude Code) emits args as strings when they're absent from its /mcp-snapshotted schema. Server forwards what the client sent.\n\nPlugin-side fix landed (agent-relay: read_turn Rust schema mirrors manifest + drift-guard test test_tools_list_schema_mirrors_manifest).\n\nRemaining MNR design questions (this item's new scope):\n- Should the manifest input_schema be authoritative (router tools/list checked against it at install, hard-fail on drift)? That would catch this class for every plugin at install time instead of in HITL.\n- Should plugin_restart refetch tools/list so binary schema updates don't require remove+install?\n- Three sources of truth (manifest, router, persisted record) can silently disagree — plugin_inspect showed manifest's fresh schema while the registry served the router's stale one, which actively misled debugging.","created_at":"2026-06-11T16:32:51","updated_at":"2026-06-11T17:06:54","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["agent-relay","hitl","mcp","plugins","schema","tool-registry"],"environment":"Minerva dcr/agent-relay @ 9f3011c4, agent_relay 0.1.0, Linux desktop","repro_steps":"1. agent_relay installed with old read_turn schema (model_spec shape) at Minerva boot.\n2. Update manifest.json (read_turn gains distill/model/deliver), minerva_plugin_remove + minerva_plugin_install + plugin_start.\n3. minerva_plugin_inspect agent_relay → shows NEW schema (deliver/distill present). \n4. External client runs /mcp reconnect → tools/list still returns OLD read_turn schema.\n5. Call minerva_agent_relay_read_turn with distill=true, deliver={chat_note:true} → godot.log shows Minerva forwarding {\"distill\":\"true\",\"deliver\":\"{\\\"chat_note\\\": true...}\"} — stringified per the undeclared-arg path — plugin parses neither.\nObserved live in agent-relay HITL session 3, 2026-06-11 (~16:31 log window)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin-provided chat providers — substrate seam for non-API \"models\" in the chat quick-chooser","description":"MOTIVATION (owner, 2026-06-11): two consumers already known — (1) agent-relay chat-passthrough: bind a chat to a watched terminal (\"terminal 5647… (codex)\" in the quick-chooser); input turns go to the terminal agent via relay_ask, output turns return as BotResponse; (2) planned Anthropic Agents SDK provider plugin (pricing-gated, but the seam design is pricing-independent — building the substrate now lets the SDK plugin be a drop-in later). Second-consumer-on-roadmap satisfies the \"would a second consumer want this?\" substrate triage rule. This completes the agent-relay arc: index scenario made the chat LLM the orchestrator of a terminal agent; passthrough makes the chat pane itself the front-end, so speak/chimes/summarization become the ambient interface to any terminal agent.\n\nWHY THE PROVIDER SEAM: chat stack is provider-agnostic behind BaseProvider — send user turn, receive BotResponse; every enhancement hangs off the response arriving. A plugin-backed provider inherits all of it for free. The existing \"...\" typing animation (agentic chats) is the waiting affordance — nothing new needed for long turns.\n\nDESIGN SURFACE (the questions this DCR settles):\n1. Registration: manifest-declared capability (e.g. \"chat_provider\") + runtime registration/unregistration of entries (agent-relay entries are per-watch-session, dynamic — appear on watch_start, vanish on watch_stop/plugin stop). Quick-chooser integration + entry naming.\n2. Dispatch: core→plugin call path for \"generate\" (reverse of host.providers.chat). Async, long-timeout (terminal turns can run minutes; relay_ask clamps 600s). No streaming in v1 — block + \"...\" affordance.\n3. Contract deviations from API providers, declared per-provider: history mode (terminal agents are stateful — send ONLY newest user message, not full history; API-style plugins may want full history), token/cost accounting (zero or plugin-reported), no system prompt injection.\n4. Mid-turn input_requested (permission dialogs): surfaces as a normal BotResponse containing the dialog text; user answers in chat. Needs a control-key convention for answers chat input can't express — e.g. <enter>/<esc> tokens (gotcha: agent_relay_send rejects an empty body after literal-\\r strip; bare Enter currently requires core terminal_write).\n5. Degradation: entries exist only while plugin RUNNING (and watch active, for terminal entries); define behavior when the provider disappears mid-chat (chat keeps history, turn errors gracefully, entry greys out).\n6. Attribution/interleaving: two front-ends on one terminal (human types directly + passthrough chat) — armed one-shot already solves initiator attribution (#466); v1 accepts the chat transcript is a partial view of the terminal session.\n\nRELATED: background terminals (MNR 019eb31f25b5) — terminal persistence decouples PTY lifetime from pane visibility; near-prerequisite for GOOD terminal-passthrough UX (a chat bound to a terminal makes a durability promise the pane-owned PTY can't keep; codex even prints a resume id on exit). Not a blocker for the seam itself.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE: the Agents SDK plugin itself; opencode profile; streaming support.\n\nCONSUMER ITEM: PLG agent-relay TerminalProvider work item (filed same day, linked).","created_at":"2026-06-11T18:30:26","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:23:34","priority":3,"tags":["agent-relay","chat","dcr","plugin-substrate","providers"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a25b3a7aa2add489747f964ed8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W1: Plugin chat-provider registration seam — manifest capability, register/unregister, core→plugin generate dispatch","description":"Manifest capability `chat_provider`; plugin registers/unregisters provider entries at runtime (agent-relay: per watch session); entries surface in ProviderOptionButton population (ProviderOptionButton.gd:34-44, 196-234). Core→plugin `generate` dispatch: async, long-timeout (minutes), newest-message-only history mode declared per entry, zero/plugin-reported token accounting, no system prompt injection. Degradation: entries vanish on plugin stop/watch_stop; mid-chat disappearance = graceful turn error, chat + history intact. CONSTRAINT (comment #479): transport deterministic, no LLM in path. Test plan: fake plugin registers entry → appears in chooser; generate round-trip via fake; plugin stop mid-chat → graceful error; entry absent after unregister.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:41:48","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:32:54","priority":3,"tags":["chat-passthrough","plugin-substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough @ b98f0a62 per DCR #483 contract. host.chat_providers.register capability + broker ops, PluginChatProviderRegistry (lifecycle-aware), PluginProvider (generation-token cancellation, prompt cancel return, envelope unwrap), chooser at ID base 100000 w/ key-based restore, ServiceHistory PluginProviderKey serialization. 59/0 new suite + regressions clean. Fable approve_with_notes; notes 1-2 fixed+test-locked same round; note 3 = chore 019eb93ebaa9. Seam ready for B7 + W2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a26f78723a9be2b6ffa67d00d1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W2: Passthrough chat mode plumbing — ServiceHistory flags, chooser lock, header badge, top-bar button","description":"Focused-chat precedent (StaticToolMode, ServiceHistory.gd:124,143-180): add PassthroughMode + BoundTerminalId (+ display name) to SERIALIZER_FIELDS — persistence free. ProviderOptionButton gains lock/hide mechanism (NONE exists today — explore #480); locked to the bound terminal entry. Header badge idiom ChatHeader.gd:41-49: \"[Passthrough: <name>]\". New top-bar icon button next to btnNewChat (Chat.tscn:189, handler pattern ChatPane.gd:933-940; icon: two opposing stacked arrows). Test plan: save/load roundtrip preserves mode+binding; chooser immutable in passthrough chat; badge renders.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:41:53","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:49:42","priority":3,"tags":["chat","chat-passthrough"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough. Mode flags serialized, chooser lock w/ offline display, start_passthrough_chat API for W3, badge, top-bar button (minimal picker until W3). Opus review must_fix (minerva_set_chat_model bypassed the lock) resolved: guards on both MCP and chooser-handler routes, test-locked (43/0). Pre-existing dead suite filed as bug 019eb949a41a."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a2aa5e780cb457bdb284a3aa1b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W3: Passthrough launch dialog — name / startup command / working dir, bind-to-existing, profile inference","description":"Form-window pattern per AgentManagerWindow.gd:92-132 (NOT FocusedChatPopup's dual-list). Fields: session name (flows to chat title + terminal tab + provider entry — no raw terminal ids in UI), startup command (e.g. `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions`), working directory, profile (auto-inferred from command — claude/codex — with override dropdown). Alternate path: bind to existing terminal (dropdown from terminal_list). Start: create terminal (BACKGROUND when 019eb31f25b5 lands; until then requires pane open), run command under login-shell-ish env (nvm node PATH gotcha — codex died on bare PTY 2026-06-11), watch_start, bind chat. Failure surfacing: command not found / agent exits immediately → system message in chat with restart affordance (agent_exited is a user-facing chat event in this mode). Test plan: dialog validation; profile inference table; bind-existing path; exit→message.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:42:08","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:23:49","priority":3,"tags":["chat","chat-passthrough","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough @ 2696521e (+ seam amendment 0004e375). Launch dialog w/ bind-to-existing, breakout-proof quoting, bash -lc login-PATH fix, injectable watch seam, no-orphan failure paths, exit-message dedupe, PassthroughCommand/Cwd serialized. 61/0 new + regressions green. Opus review: approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a2c33179f1971fe9dd2d72e4b4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W4: Question card in chat flow — reuse AutocoderStreamQuestionCard for terminal permission dialogs","description":"Reuse AutocoderStreamQuestionCard AS-IS (self-contained .tscn+.gd, zero autocoder coupling — explore #480): host it in the chat VBox alongside message nodes (new wiring; today cards only live in AutocoderActionStream). Flow: plugin detects input_requested → parses dialog options into {label, keystroke} pairs (parser is PLG-side, B7) → card renders in chat → answer_submitted → ONE keystroke to PTY via the binding. Supersedes the <enter>/<esc> control-token convention (comment #479 → #480). Free-text fallback (LineEdit) when no options parsed. Test plan: card renders from a fake input_requested payload; click → exact keystroke write; declined/escaped dialog resumes turn; card disabled after answer.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:42:15","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:49:44","priority":3,"tags":["chat","chat-passthrough","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough. Card reused as-is, label-keyed keystroke mapping (order-skew impossible), originating-history send with closed-tab/in-flight guards, Cancel sends nothing, reload-absent by design. 38/0 new + regressions green. Opus review: approve. Batch 3 complete (W3/W4/W5/B7 all done)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a308607cc38f31bba1e846040d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W5: Turn lifecycle + status relay — loading_append bubble, relayed Working-line, stop button, mid-flight rule","description":"Reuse the agentic accumulator idiom (accumulator_chi, ChatPane.gd:1784-2117): one message node per terminal turn; loading_append=true (MessageMarkdown.gd:178-200) with the agent's own relayed status line (\"◦ Working (45s • esc to interrupt)\") as interim bubble content, updated mechanically (zero tokens); finalize with the row-anchored turn read; response_arrived.emit (ChatPane.gd:2116) fires the completion chime — enhancements free. Stop button: subscribe to SingletonObject.stop_all_requests(history_id) (BaseProvider.gd:180-186); cancel the wait, arm stays set (mirror relay_ask timeout semantics). Mid-flight rule (comment #479): in-flight turn lands in history attributed to the terminal entry regardless of UI actions. input_requested ends the turn early with the dialog as the response (W4 renders the card). Test plan: status updates don't create extra message nodes; stop mid-turn leaves chat consistent + later turn end still collectable; chime fires on finalize.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:42:32","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:36:29","priority":3,"tags":["chat","chat-passthrough"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough. Additive relay around the unchanged send path; zero-token 1Hz status from the bound session's screen; one node per turn; single chime emission; per-turn isolation; cancel/crash-safe poll teardown. 50/0 new + full regression battery green. Opus review: approve_with_notes (cosmetics)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8a31db17d5a92d97e99be0c1ec8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W6: Summarize-to-note button — distill passthrough transcript to an injectable note","description":"Button in ChatHeader between editor and notes buttons (ChatHeader.gd:56-74; icon TBD — speech-bubble-ish). Engine: compact_chat() summarization pattern (ChatPane.gd:516-662) over the passthrough transcript → note via _upsert_agent_note pattern (ChatPane.gd:264-288) or create_note+link. Model: fixed cheap distill model (gpt-5.4-mini precedent), NOT the chat's provider (passthrough chats have no LLM provider). This is THE explicit token-spend gesture (comment #479 constraint) and THE context-transfer mechanism to other chats (notes-as-context bus) — replaces quick-switch history transfer. Test plan: button only in passthrough mode; note created+linked; works mid-session (partial transcript); zero LLM calls without the press.","created_at":"2026-06-11T21:42:38","updated_at":"2026-06-12T04:54:58","priority":3,"tags":["chat","chat-passthrough","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough. Summarize-to-note button (passthrough-only) with extracted shared summarize core (compact_chat preserved), note linked to originating chat, busy guard, zero-spontaneous-spend test-locked. 39/0 new + 3 regression suites green. Opus review: approve. Batch 2 complete."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8f1b98e7bdeab0ab828a13818b7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1: Detach PTY+vt session core from TerminalNew Control — session registry object","description":"Extract the PTY + ghostty-shim vt state from the TerminalNew Control into a session object that can live unparented in a registry (SingletonObject-held). TerminalNew becomes a VIEW that attaches/detaches to a session. Scrollback/read/write/wait/bell route through the session, not the Control. A1's headless-forkpty proof (test_host_capability_terminal_io.gd) shows the PTY layer already works without UI. Test plan: session created headless → write/read/wait work with no Control attached; attach a view → renders existing scrollback; detach → session keeps running; two sequential attach/detach cycles lossless.","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:08:29","updated_at":"2026-06-11T23:42:30","priority":3,"tags":["background-terminals","chat-passthrough","terminal"],"parent":"minerva:019eb31f25b57a1d84a2de609c2bff20","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough @ 43cc06ba. TerminalSession + TerminalSessionRegistry (src/Scripts/Services/Terminal/), TerminalNew = attach/detach view, TabGroup session passthrough. test_terminal_session.gd 20/0 (all 4 acceptance criteria); host_capability_terminal_io 24/0; host_capability_terminal 18/0; tab_group/config failures pre-existing (reviewer-verified). Fable review: approve_with_notes — notes carried to T2: registry dead-session pruning, terminal_id=instance_id won't survive restart (T3 scope)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8f1cf607c4faa8bd25515038dc2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2: Background create/list/promote/demote surface — terminal tools + host capabilities","description":"minerva_terminal_create gains background:true (no tab, no pane requirement — kills the \"No terminal tab group found\" failure); terminal_list reports visible:false sessions; new promote (\"show this terminal\" → creates/claims a tab) and demote operations. Same surface through host.terminal.* for plugins (agent-relay watches background sessions identically). MCPTerminalTools.gd + CapabilityBroker mappers are thin over T1's registry. Test plan: create background with pane closed; list shows it; write/read/wait on it; promote → visible tab with scrollback intact; demote → keeps running; close → terminal_closed wake fires to a watching plugin.","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:08:35","updated_at":"2026-06-11T23:59:07","priority":3,"tags":["background-terminals","chat-passthrough","mcp","terminal"],"parent":"minerva:019eb31f25b57a1d84a2de609c2bff20","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough (commit after cbe32713). background:true create, visible/alive list flags, promote/demote tools, registry-routed lookup w/ stale-view-id fallback. test_background_terminals 48/0 + all regressions at baseline. Opus review: approve. Notes for T3: host.terminal.exec still routes by view instance id (CapabilityBroker.gd:3503) — alignment candidate; terminal_id = instance id won't survive restart."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8f207217f9da059cc7ee2000247","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T3: Background-terminal regression + persistence semantics — what survives restart, what doesn't","description":"Decide + lock semantics: background sessions are NOT persisted across Minerva restart in v1 (PTY children die with the process — honest; chat keeps history, binding shows \"session ended\" with relaunch affordance via W3's stored command/cwd). Regression: existing visible-terminal behavior byte-identical (the non-interference recorder recipe from agent-relay B5 reruns here — hint agent-relay/non-interference-proof-recipe); full terminal test suites green; agent-relay watch on a background session round-trips relay_ask with the mock CLI. Test plan: suite additions + rerun of test_host_capability_terminal_io.gd; explicit restart test documents the v1 semantics.","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:08:49","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:11:19","priority":3,"tags":["background-terminals","chat-passthrough","terminal","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019eb31f25b57a1d84a2de609c2bff20","resolution":"Shipped on dcr/chat-passthrough. v1 restart semantics locked + documented (in-memory only; relaunch = stored command+cwd); host.terminal.exec aligned to registry (fixed latent dead-PTY hang + headless named-id resolution). restart_semantics 31/0, background 54/0, full sweep at baseline. Opus review: approve. agent-relay mock-CLI round-trip deliberately deferred to W7 (mock CLI is built there). Background-terminals parent 019eb31f25b5 complete."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8f228a47cbb9fde170244ba5f8b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"W7: Autonomous E2E harness — mock CLI replaying byte-true fixtures, full loop without tokens or humans","description":"THE autonomy keystone: a mock-CLI script (bash/python) that replays the byte-true codex screens (tests/fixtures/real/ in plugins repo) — prints prompt box + placeholder, on stdin line prints Working-line then a deterministic answer then prompt again; special inputs trigger the permission-dialog screen and an exit. Because passthrough transport has NO LLM (DCR constraint #479), the ENTIRE loop is testable headless and token-free: boot real SingletonObject + real ChatPane + real agent-relay binary + mock CLI in a real PTY (background terminal) → programmatic button drive (integration-boundary lesson: feedback_test_at_integration_boundary) → assert user turn → status updates in ONE accumulating node → BotResponse lands → chime signal emitted → question card on permission screen → click → keystroke arrives at mock CLI → summarize-to-note with a fake provider. Runs in CI. Pass = the only untested surface left for HITL is real-CLI behavior + visual/audio feel.","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:08:58","updated_at":"2026-06-12T06:23:08","priority":3,"tags":["chat-passthrough","e2e","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d","resolution":"Shipped @ 4d18bdeb. 42/0 in ~33s, model-layer full loop (real plugin binary, real PTY, byte-true mock codex): turn round-trip, status relay, single chime, dialog→card→keystroke, summarize, teardown. Zero product bugs — no fix loop needed. CI-registered with clean SKIP when plugins checkout absent. Layer-2 sweep: 14 suites, all at expected counts (one load flake in host_capability_terminal verified stable 18/0 ×3; tab_group 12/1 + config 60/4 pre-existing)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb8f262bb7488ab371162ffe01c08","type":"work_item","status":"open","title":"W8: Final HITL — the one big owner session, scripted checklist","description":"Single owner session at the end (everything else autonomous via W7). Checklist: (1) passthrough button → launch dialog → name/command/cwd → start REAL codex in a HIDDEN terminal (pane closed); (2) chat turn → \"...\" + relayed Working-line → answer as BotResponse + chime; (3) permission dialog → question card → click → command runs; (4) Esc/stop button mid-turn; (5) promote terminal to visible tab, type directly in it (interleave), confirm chat unaffected; (6) summarize-to-note → inject note into a fresh LLM chat → ask Opus about the session; (7) repeat (1)-(2) with claude profile; (8) agent exit → system message + relaunch affordance; (9) feel check: no flicker, no focus theft, status updates readable. Judgment items only — anything mechanically assertable belongs in W7, not here.","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:09:13","updated_at":"2026-06-13T01:33:45","priority":3,"tags":["chat-passthrough","hitl"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb9096447790280ca99bab1bda54c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"--script runs: avoid class_name type annotations + bare SingletonObject ident for new/isolated scripts","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:34:20","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["autoload","class_name","gdscript","godot","testing"],"value":"In a `godot --headless --script test/x.gd` run, a NEW class_name script loaded via load()/preload() does NOT register its global class in time: cross-file TYPE ANNOTATIONS (`var s: TerminalSession`, `func f() -> TerminalSession`) fail to compile (`Could not find type`), cascading `Failed to compile depended scripts` across the project. Fix: duck-type the cross-file refs (`var s =`, `func f(s):`) and preload the script class for `.new()`. Also: a script that references the `SingletonObject` autoload by the bare global identifier won't compile in an isolated --script harness that doesn't boot autoloads first; resolve it via `get_tree().root.get_node_or_null(\"SingletonObject\")` instead.","component":"godot","key":"script_run_class_name_resolution","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb910e3c37f91921f3b95f6162e73","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-testing/baseline-worktree-setup","created_at":"2026-06-11T23:42:32","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:33:19","value":"To run Minerva headless tests at a base SHA: git worktree add, then (1) symlink src/bin from the main tree (GDExtension binaries are gitignored), (2) run `godot --headless --path src --import` to build .godot fresh — copying the main tree's .godot does NOT work (stale UID map → theme load failures → cascading 'Failed to compile depended scripts'). Without .godot, class_name globals are unregistered and every cross-file type annotation fails to parse.","component":"minerva-testing","key":"baseline-worktree-setup","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb93ebaa97501800e2e3c43f53823","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Plugin chat-provider entries only populate the \"default\" provider set in ProviderOptionButton","description":"W1 Fable review note 3: PluginChatProviderRegistry entries appear only in _provider_sets[\"default\"], not combined/service sets. Harmless for passthrough chats (W2 locks the chooser), but a quick-chooser consumer (Agents SDK plugin) would want plugin entries in whichever sets the chooser actually displays in those contexts. Audit set usage and populate consistently.","created_at":"2026-06-12T00:32:36","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:32:36","tags":["chat-passthrough","polish"],"parent":"minerva:019eb7f329057b89ac332a4b88c1800d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eb949a41a744b89cf718a6a40e70a","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_mcp_chat_history_tools.gd runs 0/0 — 5 \"SingletonObject not found\" compile errors","description":"Found during W2 (chat-passthrough): test/test_mcp_chat_history_tools.gd produces 0 PASS / 0 FAIL with 5 \"SingletonObject not found in current scope\" compile errors under headless --script. Verified PRE-EXISTING on base b98f0a62 via stash. Likely the compile-time-SingletonObject-reference poisoning gotcha (scripts referenced from --script harnesses must runtime-resolve the autoload). Suite is silently providing zero coverage.","created_at":"2026-06-12T00:44:31","updated_at":"2026-06-12T00:44:31","severity":3,"tags":["mcp","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eba39eef67e968a96331c50dce3b4","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-plugins/agent-relay/passthrough.generate-args-seam-gap","created_at":"2026-06-12T05:06:59","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:06:59","value":"PluginProvider.gd (W1/W2, dcr/chat-passthrough) sends ONLY {chat_id, text} (+messages when history_mode=full) to a plugin's generate_tool — NO entry_id/terminal_id. A plugin with one shared generate_tool serving N entries cannot route the call. B7 works around it: explicit terminal_id arg > remembered chat_id→terminal binding > single-watch-session heuristic; ≥2 sessions + unbound chat = kind:error. Real fix belongs host-side (pass entry_id in args).","component":"minerva-plugins/agent-relay","key":"passthrough.generate-args-seam-gap"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eba5c4c0d7eec88ba9e150e3b00f5","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"AutocoderStreamQuestionCard reuse gotchas (W4)","created_at":"2026-06-12T05:44:31","updated_at":"2026-06-12T05:44:31","tags":["W4","chat-passthrough","gotcha","ui"],"value":"AutocoderStreamQuestionCard gotchas for hosts: (1) setup() must be called AFTER add_child (its %nodes are @onready); (2) it PREPENDS each option button via move_child(btn,0) so options display REVERSED — pass labels pre-reversed to compensate; (3) after answer it sets meta \"submitted\" and guards re-emission, but option Buttons stay enabled — host must grey them; (4) signal answer_submitted(question_id, answer, session_id), options are str()-ed.","component":"minerva-chat-passthrough","key":"question-card-reuse","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019eba7cce4b716b9b4b307ace043105","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-testing/pty-python-stdin","created_at":"2026-06-12T06:20:01","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:33:19","value":"Python scripts driven inside a Minerva ghostty-vt PTY (terminal session): sys.stdin.readline() errors out — read raw bytes via os.read(0, n) instead. Found building the W7 mock-CLI E2E harness (src/test/fixtures/passthrough_e2e/mock_codex.py is the working reference).","component":"minerva-testing","key":"pty-python-stdin","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ebe3ffae279c8814683290d80e11c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Growing chat bubble: never render() with loading_append up; follow handle_tool_calls order","created_at":"2026-06-12T23:52:04","updated_at":"2026-06-12T23:52:04","tags":["chat-ui","loading_append","passthrough","scroll"],"value":"MessageMarkdown.render() rebuilds the label stack (_create_code_labels clears message_labels_container children) which FREES the ●●● wave label — re-rendering with loading_append=true silently kills the animation after the first update. The canonical idiom for an in-place-growing bubble is the agentic accumulator (ChatPane.handle_tool_calls): loading_append=false → mutate Message + render() → await process_frame → loading_append=true (wave re-created at the bottom of the fresh content) → VBox.ensure_node_bottom_is_visible(node) for bottom-follow scroll. Skipping the frame await or the scroll call makes 1Hz growth rubber-band (stale tween targets). Plain chat uses a different idiom — agent mode is the reference for growing content.","component":"minerva-chat-ui","key":"in-place-growing-bubble-idiom","confidence":"verified","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ebeac7e3a7fe2839846f030e5eeaa","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Typed-array property assignment fails on untyped receivers at runtime only","created_at":"2026-06-13T01:50:36","updated_at":"2026-07-05T17:50:27","tags":["gdscript","runtime-error","typed-arrays"],"value":"Assigning an Array literal to a typed property (e.g. ChatHistoryItem.ToolExecutions: Array[Dictionary]) FAILS at runtime with \"Invalid assignment of property or key ... value of type 'Array'\" when the receiver variable is UNTYPED (e.g. `var chi = process_bot_response(...)`) — dynamic assignment does a strict runtime type check, no literal conversion. The same literal on a statically-typed receiver (`chi: ChatHistoryItem`) compiles the literal AS the typed array and works. Headless tests with typed params therefore CANNOT reproduce the bug. Fix idiom: route through a helper with a typed parameter, or build via a typed local (`var x: Array[Dictionary] = [...]`).","component":"godot","key":"typed-array-property-assignment","confidence":"verified","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ebeff1a217bb7848beaf7aaaf864e","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Passthrough read_turn: strip trailing completion-glyph status line (✻ … for Ns)","description":"W8 round-4 transcript-quality fix 2/3 (deferred for individual HITL; owner accepted fixes 1 + flying-text and filed 2/3 as backlog).\n\nPROBLEM: every passthrough bot message ends with claude's completion-glyph status line, e.g. \"✻ Cooked for 11s\", \"✻ Worked for 16s\", \"✻ Churned for 5s\". Confirmed in live HITL transcripts (chat msg matches terminal scrollback verbatim incl. this line).\n\nWHY IT SURVIVES: the chrome filter deliberately does NOT treat ✻-glyph lines as pure chrome — profiles.rs:136 documents that status glyphs PERSIST after a turn (busy-detection calibration depends on \"esc to interrupt\" being the only busy marker, not the glyph). So the glyph line is intentionally kept by the busy detector but is pure noise at the TAIL of a finalized read.\n\nFIX (agent-relay read_turn cleaning pipeline, minerva-plugins/agent-relay): add a profile-scoped TAIL rule that trims a trailing line matching the completion-status pattern (≈ ^\\s*✻ .* for \\d+s\\.?$) from read_turn_core output. Tail-only (don't strip mid-content); profile-scoped so codex/opencode get their own pattern or none. Deploy = build → install binary → plugin_restart (no manifest change). Add a byte-true fixture from a real claude turn + a cleaning unit test. HITL: send a turn, confirm the bubble ends at the answer with no ✻ line.\n\nSCOPE: plugin-side only (read_turn_core / chrome_filter / filter_rules + profiles). No Minerva core change.","created_at":"2026-06-13T03:20:49","updated_at":"2026-06-13T03:20:49","priority":3,"parent":"minerva:019eb8f262bb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ebeff360b7dec896f4f0cdde4dbab","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Passthrough read_turn: strip terminal-only \"(ctrl+o to expand)\" affordance suffix","description":"W8 round-4 transcript-quality fix 3/3 (deferred for individual HITL; owner accepted fixes 1 + flying-text and filed 3/3 as backlog).\n\nPROBLEM: passthrough bot messages include claude tool-summary lines such as \"Called minerva 3 times (ctrl+o to expand)\". The \"Called minerva N times\" half is genuinely informative (signals tools ran); the \"(ctrl+o to expand)\" suffix is a terminal keyboard hint that is meaningless — and slightly misleading — inside a Minerva chat bubble. Note: with the W8 flying-text block (e354f74f) the full tool detail is now ALSO preserved in the collapsed \"Terminal live view\" block, so the suffix is doubly redundant.\n\nFIX (agent-relay read_turn cleaning pipeline): add a named filter rule stripping a trailing \"(ctrl+o to expand)\" (and any equivalent \"… to expand\" affordance) from lines, keeping the rest of the line intact. Likely a filter_rules.rs entry (regex replace, not whole-line drop). Profile-scoped or claude-specific. Deploy = build → install → plugin_restart (no manifest change). Add fixture + unit test. HITL: confirm \"Called minerva N times\" survives without the hint.\n\nSCOPE: plugin-side only. No Minerva core change. Consider doing alongside fix 2/3 (same pipeline, one rebuild/HITL).","created_at":"2026-06-13T03:20:57","updated_at":"2026-06-13T03:20:57","priority":3,"parent":"minerva:019eb8f262bb"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019ebf0949ad71ad902b73e4071a9195","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"regen_registry.py reads LOCAL git tags — fetch --tags after CI publishes before regen","created_at":"2026-06-13T03:31:57","updated_at":"2026-06-13T03:31:57","tags":["agent-relay","ci","registry","release"],"value":"After a plugins main merge, CI auto-publishes <id>-v<version> on the REMOTE. regen_registry.py's latest_tag_for() reads LOCAL `git tag` only. If you regen without `git fetch --tags` first, it picks the stale prior tag for release_tag but reads the NEW version from manifest.json for the asset FILENAME → broken URLs like .../releases/download/agent_relay-v0.1.0/agent_relay-0.2.0-linux-x86_64.tar.gz (tag dir old, file new → 404). Correct flow: push main → wait for the agent-relay build run to complete (gh run watch) → `git fetch --tags origin` → `python3 scripts/regen_registry.py` → verify a download URL returns 200/302 → commit+push. registry-check fails on the merge push itself and goes green on the regen push.","component":"minerva-plugins","key":"regen-registry-needs-tag-fetch-first","confidence":"verified","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f1958dae77d58ac77c839996e685e","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Render reasoning/thinking traces as a collapsible block, with provider thinking controls","description":"Surface model reasoning/thinking traces in chat as a collapsible block (same pattern as ToolCallBlock), fix the round-trip defects so thinking works with tool calls, and wire the existing model-settings reasoning controls so they actually function.\n\nGoals:\n1. Reasoning/thinking visible as a tool-like collapse in the bot response.\n2. Fix design defects so thinking works end-to-end, especially across tool calls (signed-block round-trip).\n3. Make the settings UX function as thinking on/off + effort on the MAIN chat path. Today is_reasoning_model is inert metadata; reasoning_effort is wired only to the agent-context-summary sub-call and is OpenAI-only.\n4. Provider-agnostic via BaseProvider virtuals (mirror the apply_cache_hints no-op-override pattern); BotResponse carries the shared reasoning data.\n\nPer-provider RESPONSE shapes differ: Anthropic thinking/redacted_thinking content blocks (+signature; display defaults to omitted on 4.7/4.8/Fable => empty text unless display:summarized); OpenAI Responses reasoning_summary_text (summaries; raw is encrypted); OpenRouter message.reasoning + message.reasoning_details (reasoning.text/summary/encrypted, plus inline <think> on some models); Gemini parts with thought:true (+thoughtSignature). REQUEST enablement also differs and is model-generation-dependent (Anthropic adaptive vs budget_tokens; OpenAI reasoning_effort; OpenRouter reasoning; Gemini thinkingConfig.includeThoughts).\n\nKey risks: round-trip fidelity required WITHIN the tool loop for Anthropic/Gemini/OpenRouter (replay byte-identical or 400); interleaved thinking => multiple segments per turn (use a sequence model, not a single field); cross-provider and primary->fallback replay = 400 (gate replay to same provider+model); prompt projection must preserve reasoning order; reasoning is billed as output tokens; toggling thinking invalidates the message cache tier; reasoning is display-only metadata and must NEVER enter Format() on a normal turn.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:24:41","updated_at":"2026-06-30T16:24:41","priority":2,"tags":["providers","reasoning","thinking","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f1958f42b7656abf1278c31ec0dce","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Reasoning-as-collapse - staged A->D","description":"Staged delivery applying the project rubric (Durability, Reliability, DRY, Functional-first testing, Cost in LoC/T-shirt, Risk controls).\n\nGATING DECISION: model reasoning as a position-aware SEQUENCE parallel to ToolExecutions (reuse ToolCallBlock + a {{TOOL_BLOCK}}-style marker + the existing interleave render path), NOT a single string field. Wins on Durability/Reliability/DRY/Functional-first; a single field is a false LoC saving given interleaving rework.\n\nSCOPE BOUNDARIES (anti-scope-creep): NO live streaming thinking stream (different request path, out of scope); reasoning_raw is transient (live agent loop only), NOT persisted to .minproj (opaque/expiring payloads); ReasoningBlock action buttons limited (chain-of-thought leak control).\n\nBASEPROVIDER DESIGN: add BotResponse reasoning sequence (shared); two no-op virtuals on BaseProvider overridden per provider: extract_reasoning(data, bot_response) and apply_reasoning_options(params, level, enabled) -- mirrors apply_cache_hints. Non-LLM/image providers inherit the no-op and stay safe. Stages A and B share one provider mapping table used by both send and receive. Stage D is the only Format()/projection change and carries the 400 risk; it ships last so A-C deliver value independently.\n\nRISK CONTROLS: branch from a clean development commit (working tree currently has dirty vendor/godot_cef + vendor/godot_wry submodules -- keep them out of the feature branch); functional tests use real provider calls (no mocking); code comments describe function, not status.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:24:47","updated_at":"2026-07-01T05:07:07","tags":["plan","reasoning"],"parent":"minerva:019f1958dae77d58ac77c839996e685e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f19590d6e79928dccdd9ebbb9a4b4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Stage A: BotResponse reasoning sequence + BaseProvider virtuals + 1 provider extract + ReasoningBlock (display-only)","description":"Display-only spine, ZERO Format() changes (cannot break existing chat).\n- BotResponse: add a reasoning SEQUENCE (model on ToolExecutions: list of {kind, text, redacted, order}).\n- BaseProvider: add no-op virtuals extract_reasoning(data, bot_response) and apply_reasoning_options(params,...).\n- Implement extract_reasoning for ONE provider (Anthropic or OpenRouter, whichever is easiest to test live).\n- Clone ToolCallBlock -> ReasoningBlock (single outer collapse, no args/result split). Gate on non-empty text; render a placeholder for redacted/empty blocks.\n- MessageMarkdown: render reasoning segments via the interleave path (top of message for v1).\nFiles: BotResponse.gd, BaseProvider.gd, <one provider>.gd, MessageMarkdown.gd, new ReasoningBlock.gd/.tscn.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:24:54","updated_at":"2026-06-30T20:06:07","tags":["display","reasoning","size:S"],"parent":"minerva:019f1958f42b7656abf1278c31ec0dce"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f195926617924907e767366393fa3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Stage B: extract_reasoning for the remaining 3 providers","description":"Implement extract_reasoning override for the remaining 3 providers, reusing the Stage A field + widget.\n- Anthropic: thinking + redacted_thinking content blocks (set display:summarized on request side in Stage C).\n- OpenAI Responses: reasoning_summary_text deltas (ChatGPTProvider already accumulates them; uncomment + map).\n- OpenRouter: message.reasoning (string) -> message.reasoning_details (array) -> inline <think> fallback.\n- Gemini: candidates[].content.parts[] where part.thought == true.\nEach provider is an independent, individually functionally-testable increment.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:25:00","updated_at":"2026-06-30T20:09:39","tags":["providers","reasoning","size:S"],"parent":"minerva:019f1958f42b7656abf1278c31ec0dce"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f19593f6c7e8390220ceae18e02b8","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Stage C: request-side thinking enablement + wire settings UI to the main path","description":"Request-side enablement on the MAIN chat path (today effort is agent-summary-only + OpenAI-only).\n- apply_reasoning_options overrides map a chosen level -> provider-native params: Anthropic adaptive+display, or budget_tokens by model generation; OpenAI reasoning_effort; OpenRouter reasoning; Gemini thinkingConfig.\n- Wire is_reasoning_model as the visibility GATE for the control, and the reasoning_effort enum into generate_content_from_provider for ALL providers (not just the OpenAI branch).\n- Reuse the same mapping table built in Stage A/B.\n\nDEPENDENCY (DRY): ChatGPT effort-picker population/validation is owned by MNR 019dc04f7154 \"Discover and apply ChatGPT reasoning effort options\" (blocks this item) and partially exists in ChatGPTModelManager.gd (supported_reasoning_levels / default_reasoning_level / supports_reasoning_summaries). Stage C consumes that ChatGPT effort discovery and generalizes the level->request mapping + main-path wiring to the other providers -- do not re-implement the ChatGPT picker here.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:25:07","updated_at":"2026-07-01T05:41:32","tags":["reasoning","settings","size:M"],"parent":"minerva:019f1958f42b7656abf1278c31ec0dce"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f1959587f7ebf96fb715cfe7b6f11","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Stage D: same-provider signed-reasoning replay in the agent tool loop","description":"Round-trip fidelity for agent tool loops (the risky, last stage).\n- Thread per-turn signed reasoning (Anthropic signature / Gemini thoughtSignature / OpenRouter reasoning_details) into the assistant-with-tool_use message in Format(), WITHIN the in-flight loop ONLY.\n- HARD-GATE replay to same provider + same model; drop on any cross-provider or primary->fallback switch.\n- Audit ToolMemoryManager.project()/fold_tool_result to confirm they preserve reasoning block order.\n- reasoning_raw is transient (not persisted). Risk: 400s on mismatch.","created_at":"2026-06-30T16:25:13","updated_at":"2026-07-01T05:41:37","tags":["agent","reasoning","risk","size:M"],"parent":"minerva:019f1958f42b7656abf1278c31ec0dce"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f2e6d03c77899bd060437d327b67d","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"IML Blender+ARP render capability: deploy as minervaservice vs plugin","created_at":"2026-07-04T18:38:44","updated_at":"2026-07-04T20:18:59","tags":["anime-pipeline","arp","blender","deployment","iml","minervaservices","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f2ecaccc37130b825628d8eda2c35","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Phase 1: Vendor ARP 3.78.28 into IML (patch overlay) + move IML to gitlab/minervaservices","created_at":"2026-07-04T20:21:10","updated_at":"2026-07-04T20:21:10","priority":2,"tags":["anime-pipeline","arp","blender","iml","minervaservices","vendoring"],"parent":"minerva:019f2e6d03c77899bd060437d327b67d"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3345dd927560a7f0dd63733ebe59","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Annotation V2 schema: docs + CAD host both drift from shipped validator","created_at":"2026-07-05T17:14:04","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:30","tags":["annotations","pcb","plugin","schema-drift"],"value":"Shipped AnnotationV2Schema.validate() (AnnotationV2Schema.gd:30-33) requires kind_payload/anchor/lifecycle/visible_in_views/summary/schema_version — but Annotation-substrate-design.md §2.1 and PCB-annotation-migration.md §3-4 still show the v1 shape (payload/primitives/created_at), and CadAnnotationHost (minerva-plugins cad/ui/) produces NON-conformant envelopes (payload not kind_payload, no lifecycle/summary, never validates). Only TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd:189-194,251-260 is conformant — copy IT, not CAD, for new AnnotationHost subclasses until the drift is reconciled. Full analysis: Docs/design/PCB-substrate-gap-register.md C-31/C-32.","component":"minerva-annotation-substrate","key":"v2schema-docs-and-cad-host-drift","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f335754e07784a905f9732dffa341","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Reconcile AnnotationV2Schema vs design docs vs CadAnnotationHost (envelope-shape drift)","description":"Filed from PCB gap register (Docs/design/PCB-substrate-gap-register.md, C-31/C-32; Minerva commit 2281735c). Triage rule: \"would a second editor want this?\" → yes, every editor writing v2 sidecars needs one authoritative envelope shape.\n\n**The drift (three-way):**\n1. Shipped validator `AnnotationV2Schema.validate()` (AnnotationV2Schema.gd:30-33) requires `kind_payload`/`anchor`/`lifecycle`/`visible_in_views`/`summary`/`schema_version`.\n2. Design docs (`Annotation-substrate-design.md` §2.1, `PCB-annotation-migration.md` §3-4) still show the v1 shape: `payload`/`primitives`/`created_at` (+ RFC3339 timestamps that no shipped host actually uses — hosts store int Unix).\n3. `CadAnnotationHost`/`cad_edge_number_kind` (minerva-plugins cad/ui/) — the adoption guide's cited reference implementation — produces NON-conformant envelopes: `payload` not `kind_payload`, never sets `lifecycle`/`visible_in_views`/`summary`, never calls validate. Only `TextEditorAnnotationHost.gd:189-194,251-260` is conformant.\n\nAlso fold in C-6 (v2 `author` is a Dictionary `{kind, id?, model?, session_id?}`, not the bare string the migration doc claims is \"already aligned\") and C-7 (timestamp format: docs say RFC3339, code uses int Unix — pick one).\n\n**Scope:** decide the authoritative shape (presumably the shipped validator); update both design docs; bring CadAnnotationHost + cad_edge_number_kind into conformance (or explicitly version-gate them); add validate call to any host that stores unvalidated. Consider a conformance test that runs every registered AnnotationHost's output through validate_with_registry.\n\n**Why now:** blocks the PCB annotation-migration child (019eb47e4e7e) and conditions the PCB walking skeleton (019eb47fe8a0) — interim guidance for the skeleton is to copy TextEditorAnnotationHost (hint: minerva-annotation-substrate/v2schema-docs-and-cad-host-drift).","created_at":"2026-07-05T17:33:09","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:11:16","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","pcb","platform","schema-drift","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b","resolution":"Reconciled — AnnotationV2Schema confirmed authoritative; code, docs, and reference hosts now agree. Commits: minerva-plugins 1340981 (CadAnnotationHost normalize+validate gate, accept-old-on-read/write-new-always, ~90-line liftable pattern; cad_edge_number_kind tolerant reads + accepted_anchor_types), Minerva 9100fb66 (3 docs rewritten to the real shape incl. author-dict + int-Unix corrections and the W-13 author_ui lesson; new cross-host conformance suite test_annotation_host_conformance.gd 21/0 covering TextEditor/Cad/Pcb + legacy normalization + negative gate). Key fact from Fable review: CAD never persisted annotations to disk — no legacy corpus migration needed. Guard tier: no new failures. C-6 (author dict) and C-7 (int timestamps) resolved in docs. PCB annotation-migration child 019eb47e4e7e is UNBLOCKED. Follow-ups: 019f341a99 (gate 3 remaining hosts + substrate lift of _normalize_envelope with 3 improvements), 019f341e68 (MCP callers ignore \"\" failure), 019f341a7f (stale-path CAD tests). Fable review approve_with_notes; conformance suite is the drift regression guard going forward."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3367e4e07caf957ce32256da0174","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Windows Godot console binary path for headless test runs","created_at":"2026-07-05T17:51:14","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:13:18","tags":["godot","headless","testing","windows"],"value":"On the Windows dev machine, Godot is NOT on PATH. Headless test invocation: `C:\\software\\godot-4.6.3\\Godot_v4.6.3-stable_win64_console.exe --headless --path C:\\github\\Minerva\\src --script test/<test>.gd`. Use the _console.exe variant (stdout works in PowerShell). The old 4.4.1 binary at C:\\software\\ was removed 2026-07-05 when 4.6.3 was installed. Go 1.26.4 IS now installed (winget, 2026-07-05) — refresh $env:Path from Machine+User scopes in fresh shells before calling `go`.","component":"godot","key":"godot-console-path","research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d2575d779d8494661d79a401c9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Enum-stable editor-type serialization: legacy Editor.Type.PCB tab remap (cutover prerequisite)","description":"Gap register A-12 (platform). Editor.Type serializes as a bare int in saved tabs/projects (vboxEditor.gd:160-167); PCB is ordinal 7 of 15. Deleting the member renumbers VIDEO_EDITOR..PLUGIN_SCENE and corrupts every saved tab — this hazard exists for ANY enum member removal; PCB is just the first. Mitigation options (decide in cutover round): (a) reserve the PCB slot as deprecated, (b) load-time remap ordinal 7 → PLUGIN_SCENE tab pointing at pcb/pcb_panel (preferred — completes the migration for saved projects), (c) migrate serialization to string type names (durable fix, wider blast radius). Must land in or before the cutover round 019eb47f11e7. Related: PLUGIN_SCENE deserialize pre-pass exists (vboxEditor.gd:173-183, A-13) and can host the remap.","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:30","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:30","priority":2,"tags":["cutover-prerequisite","editor-core","pcb","platform","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d282c870cfb19d7b03447a0023","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Platform: incremental undo primitive for structured (non-text-backed) plugin panels","description":"Gap register B-9/B-11 (platform; surfaced by PCB migration audit, DCR 019dc140). Text-backed panels get undo free via DocumentBuffer; structured-graph panels (PCB board model) have NO platform undo lane — only whole-state snapshot round-trips (host_owned_save.get/set_state, PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:945-1002). PCBData carries a bespoke history stack that has nowhere to plug in. Open question 7 from the register: can PCBData.history snapshots ride get/set_state, or does the broker need a real incremental-undo primitive? Any second structured-data editor (spreadsheet-like plugins, node graphs) hits the same wall. Predecessor DCRs 019dfa66 (buffer-canonical) and 019df8e2d093 (host capabilities) are both shipped — this is a new capability line, filed standalone per the PCB triage round (019eb48022).","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:41","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:41","priority":3,"tags":["broker","pcb","platform","substrate-gap","undo"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d298247b46b5af91868f30209e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Platform: cheap synchronous host-capability lane for backend-less scene panels","description":"Gap register B-12/B-13 + open question 10 (platform; surfaced by PCB migration audit, DCR 019dc140). Read-mostly live-panel introspection (spatial_query, get_components) is a direct in-process call today (microseconds); through the broker it becomes call_deferred (≥1 frame) + JSON serialization + 64KiB payload cap + 5s timeout ceiling (PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:301-365, 583-593, 1004-1077), and the backend channel assumes an out-of-process worker the panel may not have. The AnnotationHostRegistry duck-typing seam (used by MCPCadTools) is the existing workaround — decide whether to bless/generalize it as the official \"core MCP tool reaches a live panel cheaply\" lane or add a first-class sync capability. PCB is the first plugin whose tool surface is latency-sensitive at agent call rates; measure with the Wave-1 harness before designing. Filed standalone per the PCB triage round (019eb48022).","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:47","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:47","priority":3,"tags":["broker","pcb","performance","platform","substrate-gap"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d2b2307d5196f53d747ec50d8c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Platform: host_owned file-path contract — load-shape asymmetry, ctx.file_path, Save-As notification","description":"Gap register A-14 + W-15 platform questions (surfaced by PCB migration audit + walking skeleton, DCR 019dc140). Three related contract holes for host_owned scene panels: (1) Editor.gd:1108 _load_plugin_scene_file JSON-parses every host_owned file — JSON arrives merged as a dict, non-JSON as {file_path, raw_text}; already bit CAD's .mcad and conditions PCB's YAML-vs-JSON serialize decision. (2) ctx.file_path at panel mount is only populated via associated_object (PluginScenePanelHost.gd:610-623) — in the File→Open flow the panel learns its path only at load time; caused the W-15 sidecar failure. (3) On Save-As the panel is NEVER told the chosen path (Editor.gd PLUGIN_SCENE save branch writes the JSON itself, ~:1590-1606) — a panel with sidecar duties cannot follow its document to a new location. Any plugin with sidecar or path-derived state needs (2)+(3) fixed; (1) needs documenting or a format-agnostic load mode. Filed standalone per the PCB triage round (019eb48022).","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:54","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:47:54","priority":2,"tags":["host_owned","pcb","platform","substrate-gap"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d2c9bf72af97e932ed4575e9ca","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Layer-aware view_context (e.g. \"pcb:F.Cu\") or per-layer visibility filtering","description":"Gap register C-23 + open question 4 (platform; surfaced by PCB migration audit, DCR 019dc140). view_context is one flat string per host (\"pcb\") — a route hint on B.Cu renders in the same context as F.Cu, so hiding the bottom copper layer in the canvas cannot hide bottom-layer annotations. PCB has real per-layer data (PCBTrace.layer, PCBRouteHint.layer, PCBComponent.layer); CAD's per-view visibility has a similar flavor. Decide: formalize sub-contexts (\"pcb:F.Cu\") in AnnotationRenderContext/visible_in_views, or bless per-kind render-time filtering (kind reads its layer from kind_payload and asks the host what's visible). Needed before the PCB annotation-migration child (019eb47e4e7e) finalizes render behavior, but not blocking its data model.","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:00","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:38:19","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","pcb","platform","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b","resolution":"Fixed in WC-2 (019f6a892ea4), Minerva 03b456e5 + plugins f76c49a: duck-typed host.is_annotation_visible veto consulted by AnnotationOverlay._draw and all three manipulation tools; PcbAnnotationHost bridges kind_payload.layer → pcb_canvas.is_layer_visible (F.Cu/B.Cu↔top/bottom); trace_layer_filter setter emits view_changed → overlay redraw. Red→green proven in test_pcb_workflow_kinds.gd (E2E-2D): with the plugin side reverted, exactly the two [C3 red->green] assertions fail."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d2e0047c92982f8f6570469151","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Wire AnnotationHost mutations into editor dirty state generically","description":"Gap register W-14 platform question (surfaced by PCB walking skeleton, DCR 019dc140). Nothing in core connects AnnotationHost.annotations_changed to editor dirty state — the PCB panel now hand-wires annotations_changed → MinervaPluginPanel.content_changed with a _restoring gate (load_sidecar re-emits the same signal for dock/overlay refresh, so a naive relay marks freshly-opened tabs dirty). Every annotation-bearing editor wants this; the core text editor likely has the same hole (verify). Design: substrate-level relay with a suppress-during-restore contract (e.g. host.begin_restore()/end_restore() or a load-path flag), so panels stop hand-wiring it. Reference implementation of the hand-wired version: minerva-plugins pcb/ui/PCBPanel.gd (_init relay + _restoring gate).","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:05","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:05","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","dirty-state","platform","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33d2f7637a019a976d52f9bda230","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Adoption guide: interactive authoring requires author_ui() — document + fix reference-host pointer","description":"Gap register W-13 platform note (surfaced by PCB walking skeleton, DCR 019dc140). \"Register a kind\" is NOT sufficient for interactive authoring: AnnotationToolbar creates a button only for kinds whose author_ui() returns non-null (AnnotationToolbar.gd:417-423); a kind without the override passes every headless seam test (registry, envelope, sidecar, MCP) while being unauthorable in the UI. The adoption guide (Annotation-substrate-plugin-adoption.md) must state this loudly, with the fresh-instance-per-activation pattern (AnnotationText.author_ui). Also fix the guide's reference-host pointer: it cites CadAnnotationHost, which is schema-non-conformant (C-32); until 019f335754e0 lands, TextEditorAnnotationHost is the copyable reference. Coordinate with (or fold into) 019f335754e0's doc updates — don't double-edit the same guide in two rounds.","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:48:11","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:11:36","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","docs","platform","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b","resolution":"Completed inside the schema-reconciliation round (019f335754e0, Minerva commit 9100fb66) to avoid double-editing the adoption guide: Annotation-substrate-plugin-adoption.md now (a) documents that interactive authoring requires an author_ui() override (AnnotationToolbar._try_add_button skips null-author_ui kinds, ~line 423) with the fresh-instance-per-activation pattern — the W-13 lesson; (b) cites TextEditorAnnotationHost (core) AND the now-conformant CadAnnotationHost (+ PcbAnnotationHost) as copyable references with the accept-old-on-read/write-new-always pattern, replacing the stale pointer to the previously non-conformant CAD host."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f33dcb7d6723395eb91d197088d69","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_plugin_scene_panel_broker_host_fs fails on Windows (3 host.fs.changed checks; hardcoded /tmp)","description":"Found 2026-07-05 by the PCB migration guard-tier round (harness child 019eb47ed330) — pre-existing, reproducible standalone, unrelated to the round's diff (broker/watcher/test files untouched). On Windows (Godot 4.6.3-console headless), test_plugin_scene_panel_broker_host_fs.gd fails 3 of 12: \"scene received exactly one host.fs.changed\", \"panel_a got host.fs.changed\", \"panel_b got host.fs.changed\" — the push after a real filesystem-watcher event never arrives. Test hardcodes `_temp_dir = \"/tmp/host_fs_broker_test\"`, not a native Windows path — likely the watcher never sees the writes. Fix direction: use OS temp-dir resolution (TEMP/TMP fallback like test_pcb_plugin_smoke.gd) and re-check watcher timing on Windows. Until fixed, the --pcb-guard tier documents this one FAIL as known-pre-existing on Windows (guard gate = no NEW failures).","created_at":"2026-07-05T19:58:50","updated_at":"2026-07-05T19:58:50","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["host.fs","plugin-platform","testing","windows"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f341a7fde79579d1d853c1a626a9f","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"CAD edge-number kind/tool tests parse-broken: stale res://../../plugins/ path (pre-rename)","description":"Found 2026-07-05 by the annotation schema-reconciliation round (019f335754e0) — pre-existing, unrelated to that diff. src/test/test_cad_edge_number_kind.gd and test_cad_edge_number_tool.gd fail at PARSE: they preload(\"res://../../plugins/cad/...\") — the pre-rename monorepo path (now minerva-plugins/). Second latent issue once the path is fixed: their _MockCamera is a RefCounted without .projection, which won't satisfy the current kind's perspective-pane filter. Fix: repoint preloads to res://../../minerva-plugins/cad/..., update the mock to expose projection.","created_at":"2026-07-05T21:06:19","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:06:19","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["cad","stale-path","testing"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f341a99d97d2d84c11f611ac122cb","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add validate_with_registry gate to remaining annotation hosts (hello_scene, presentation, nametag-maker)","description":"Follow-up from schema reconciliation 019f335754e0 (2026-07-05). Three hosts add_annotation WITHOUT the validate_with_registry gate (same missing-gate class CadAnnotationHost had, now fixed): src/plugins/hello_scene/ui/HelloAnnotationHost.gd, minerva-plugins/presentation/ui/presentation_tile_annotation_host.gd, minerva-plugins/nametag-maker/ui/nametag_editor_panel.gd. Their envelope SHAPE is already v2-conformant (kind_payload) — only the gate is absent. Mirror the TextEditorAnnotationHost/CadAnnotationHost pattern and add each to src/test/test_annotation_host_conformance.gd (the conformance suite built for exactly this). Also noted for a later audit: core built-in kinds + AnnotationCanvas still read legacy `payload` as the documented fallback (design §5.3) — audit before ever fully retiring `payload`; and the adoption guide §12/§13 has stale ~/github/plugins/cad/ paths.","created_at":"2026-07-05T21:06:26","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:10:39","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","conformance","platform"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f341e689d719a8978c9d6cc5846e2","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"MCP annotation callers ignore add_annotation failure (\"\" id) — report success while nothing stored","description":"Found by Fable review of schema reconciliation 019f335754e0 (2026-07-05). Now that conformant hosts return \"\" from add_annotation on validation failure (the gate), two call sites silently swallow it: MCPCadTools._cad_annotate_edges (~line 554) appends to created_ids unconditionally — MCP reports success while nothing stored; MCPAnnotationTools._creation_echo echoes {\"id\": \"\"} with ok:true. Fix: check for empty id, surface a per-item error in the tool result. Applies to any future caller of the gated pattern — consider a shared helper.","created_at":"2026-07-05T21:10:35","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:10:35","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["annotations","mcp","reliability"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f343e8fab7a69972a1a9f8595778a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"shared bridge: per-call ctx into Dispatch kills the lazy worker every call","created_at":"2026-07-05T21:45:43","updated_at":"2026-07-05T21:45:43","tags":["bridge","go","plugin","worker"],"value":"In minerva-plugins Go routers, do NOT pass a per-call timeout context into the bridge Dispatch/Call path: bridge.Worker lazily spawns via exec.CommandContext(ctx) on first Call (worker.go:189,387), so cancelling the per-call ctx (defer cancel()) kills the shared worker after each call → cold respawn every call (symptom: duplicate worker.ready lines). Use context.Background() for worker calls (CAD precedent, cad/main.go:391); Shutdown is independent (stdin shutdown + SIGTERM/SIGKILL) so graceful teardown still works. Tradeoff: no per-call deadline; startup bounded by the bridge readyTimeout. Found in pcb worker round (cfc1402); reviewer-verified against shared/ source.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"bridge-percall-ctx-kills-worker","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f36128062728fa1ffe6b5551bea53","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Let core 2d_* kinds carry plugin semantic anchors (or sanction a per-host extension)","description":"Surfaced by PCB legacy-annotation migration (019eb4810167, 2026-07-05), verified empirically: core 2d_arrow/2d_text/2d_region/2d_polyline return [\"core/*\"] from accepted_anchor_types(), so validate_with_registry rejects any plugin anchor (pcb/component, pcb/net, pcb/pad) on them as kind_anchor_incompatible. Consequence: a generic arrow pointing at component U3 cannot semantically track U3 — the PCB migration preserves associated_component/net losslessly in kind_payload instead, and only the domain kind (pcb_route_hint) gets real semantic anchors. Any plugin wanting core-kind reuse WITH domain anchoring hits this. Options: (a) core kinds accept host-registered anchor families (accepted_anchor_types consults the host's anchor registry); (b) a per-host allowlist extension the schema compat check honors; (c) bless plugin-subclassed generic kinds (contradicts the DRY decision in PCB-annotation-migration.md §5). Decide with a second consumer in view. Until then: associations ride kind_payload (lossless, not live-tracking).","created_at":"2026-07-06T06:16:50","updated_at":"2026-07-06T06:16:50","priority":3,"tags":["anchors","annotations","pcb","platform","substrate-gap"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0543da572f29ae7bca8a9afaa9b"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f364c96c3716f84be546a9891095d","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Where is the claude-router source on the Windows dev machine (or what is its clone URL)?","description":"The agent-router migration (019eb47eb567 + grandchildren) needs ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote/claude-router (~5.8 KLOC Python: board, grid, pathfinder, kicad_io, hints, visualizer + tests) — a Linux-desktop path from the 2026-06-10 planning session. Not present on this Windows machine (searched C:\\github, C:\\gitlab, user profile). Need either: (a) a local path if it exists somewhere unsearched, (b) the GitLab clone URL + access, or (c) a copy dropped into C:\\github. The loop proceeds with gerber export meanwhile; agent-router unblocks the moment the source is available.","created_at":"2026-07-06T07:20:16","updated_at":"2026-07-06T14:30:47","priority":2,"tags":["agent-router","blocker","pcb"],"resolution":"Source at C:\\gitlab\\ccsandbox\\smart-remote\\claude-router (4711 LOC, full package + tests). Unblocks agent-router 019eb47eb567."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3815e9f9729fae1c3930eed57f56","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Go-live the pcb.route worker channel (out-of-fence wiring for apply_route_hints)","description":"The apply flow (019eb481cddd, minerva-plugins 0e73cc6) is wired end-to-end IN-FENCE: host.run_router → panel.route_board emits a pcb.route broker request; the Python worker route method + bridge are complete. But no in-fence path connects them — the panel broker reaches Go channel handlers (manifest.json ipc_channels) not Python worker compute, and the worker is reached from core only via Go MCP tools (internal/tools/worker_tools.go). Until wired, minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints returns structured route_worker_unavailable. GO-LIVE (out of the apply round's fence, small): EITHER (a) declare pcb.route in pcb/manifest.json ipc_channels + forward the channel to the worker route handler, OR (b) register minerva_pcb_route in internal/tools/worker_tools.go (mirrors validate/generate/gerbers) and have the apply tool call it. (a) fits the panel broker path the apply tool already emits on; pick per how serialize/deserialize vs the worker tools are wired. Then the 3b live check (HITL debt): author route hints in a .pcbskel, run apply → cyan proposals appear → commit lays traces + flips hints applied.","created_at":"2026-07-06T15:39:48","updated_at":"2026-07-06T15:39:48","priority":3,"tags":["agent-router","pcb","plugin-migration","wiring","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47eb5677a62941bd8620a867596"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f39164c2e7f5abb6e620b2cbb3c68","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"PCB plugin panel: canvas receives no mouse input (zoom/pan/select all dead) + seeded default board + tool UX","description":"Live 3b findings from the user driving the ported panel (minerva-plugins 0e73cc6), HITL debt #7 pass. Headless tests structurally miss all of these (they drive model/hooks, never _gui_input).\n\n1+2+3 (ONE root cause, CRITICAL): the canvas receives no mouse events — scroll-wheel zoom, right/middle-drag pan, and click-drag box-select/component-select are ALL dead, while toolbar buttons (sibling Controls) work. Static analysis eliminated the structural suspects: pcb_canvas.gd handlers are correct (_handle_mouse_button wheel/pan/box-select all present), _gui_input guard passes (data set at build), mouse_filter=STOP live, no overlay mounted by PCBPanel, PluginScenePanelHost mounts the panel plainly into a vbox (no reparent/overlay/SubViewport). So events aren't reaching canvas._gui_input for a subtle reason needing live instrumentation (candidate: panel/container mouse_filter or size_flags in the plugin-scene mount; annotation-dock/editor-chrome interaction layer covering the canvas rect; canvas not front-most under the mount).\n\n4 (trivial): new/anonymous boards show phantom U1+R1 — PCBPanel._DEFAULT_BOARD seeds them (skeleton scaffolding). A new board must be empty (keep board size/outline, drop components).\n\n5 (UX): collapse the three separate Select/Move/Rotate toolbar buttons into one smart tool (Photoshop / GraphicsEditor style — select default, drag a selected component to move, handle/key to rotate). GraphicsEditor tools/ (SelectTool.gd, TransformTool.gd, PanTool.gd) is the reference pattern. Current code already half-does this (SELECT mode click-drag moves a component).","created_at":"2026-07-06T20:19:50","updated_at":"2026-07-07T07:36:33","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","input","live-finding","pcb","plugin","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47e068972f8959a978072b35239","resolution":"User confirmed initial UI tests pass on the live plugin after the reparent fix (minerva-plugins b1664a5). All 5 findings addressed: canvas input alive after the annotation-dock reparent (the real root cause — canvas left MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE), box-select, trackpad two-finger-scroll zoom (pinch is macOS-only, documented), Pan tool + Space-drag, blank default board, one smart Select tool. Regression guard test_pcb_canvas_input_probe 33/0 reproduces the mount→reparent order. Pinch-gesture-on-Windows remains an engine limitation (not fixable in-plugin)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3941ebd37df8b566a3c50b40dc15","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin scene panels are REPARENTED after mount — set Control input config in _enter_tree, not _ready","created_at":"2026-07-06T21:07:29","updated_at":"2026-07-06T21:07:29","tags":["annotations","godot","input","mouse_filter","plugin","reparent"],"value":"A plugin godot_scene panel exposing get_annotation_host() is REPARENTED by the editor AFTER _on_panel_loaded: Editor._mount_annotation_dock_for_surface → _ensure_annotation_content_row moves the panel into an AnnotationContentRow HBox (Editor.gd ~759/763). A reparent fires _exit_tree then _enter_tree on the whole subtree but does NOT re-run _ready (Godot calls _ready once per node lifetime). So any Control that sets mouse_filter/focus_mode ONLY in _ready — or clears mouse_filter to IGNORE in _exit_tree — is left mis-configured after the reparent. Symptom: the control draws fine but silently ignores ALL mouse+keyboard input (its _gui_input never fires), while sibling Buttons still work (they don't self-clear their filter). Hit in PCB plugin bug 019f39164c2e (pcb_canvas left MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE → zoom/pan/select all dead). FIX: apply input config (mouse_filter=STOP, focus_mode) in _enter_tree so it re-applies on every tree entry; never leave mouse_filter=IGNORE in _exit_tree for a node that gets reparented (vs freed). TEST TRAP: a headless input probe that builds the canvas AFTER the final add_child never reparents and gives a false PASS — the probe MUST replicate build-then-reparent order (see test_pcb_canvas_input_probe _probe_reparent_after_mount). The platform AnnotationOverlay itself is innocent (MOUSE_FILTER_IGNORE when idle, AnnotationOverlay.gd:25).","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"panel-reparented-by-annotation-dock-mount","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3ba0b765774ba546b0142cb12298","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PCB worker Python resolution hangs on Windows: tier-3 `python3` resolves to Microsoft Store shim","description":"All worker-backed pcb_* tools (validate/generate/gerbers/check_bom/check_libraries) time out with no response on a fresh Windows dev machine. Go plugin (ping, library_status) stays responsive — only the Python-worker bridge hangs.\n\nRoot cause: `shared/runtime/resolve.go` PythonPath() dev fallbacks are tier2 `<worker>/.venv/Scripts/python.exe`, tier3 `exec.LookPath(\"python3\")`. On Windows `python3` resolves to the Microsoft Store App-Execution-Alias shim (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Microsoft\\WindowsApps\\python3.exe`), which LookPath finds but which blocks/opens the Store when spawned non-interactively — the worker.ready handshake never completes → MCP timeout. The real interpreter here is `python` (3.12.4); tier3 never tries bare `python`, and there is no shim rejection.\n\nWorkaround used this session: created `pcb/worker/.venv` (python -m venv + pip install -e .) so tier2 wins, then restarted the pcb plugin. Worker came online immediately.\n\nSuggested fix: (a) tier3 should also try `python` (LookPath), not only `python3`; (b) reject the WindowsApps store-shim path; (c) fail-fast with a clear host.notify instead of hanging when the resolved interpreter produces no handshake within the spawn deadline. Also seen: a stray literal `pcb/worker/%SystemDrive%` dir got created (unexpanded env var in some spawn/path code) — worth a look.","created_at":"2026-07-07T08:10:16","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:16:24","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bridge","pcb","python","windows","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","environment":"Windows 11, no worker/.venv, python 3.12.4 on PATH as `python` (not `python3`).","repro_steps":"1. Fresh Windows, no pcb/worker/.venv. 2. Start pcb plugin. 3. Call minerva_pcb_validate with any board yaml → times out (worker never answers). ping/library_status still work."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3ba0f455704fb1e16e8b7552ba08","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PCB gerber/generate rotation sign mismatch: pads on 90°/270° components are mirrored (land off-board / off-trace)","description":"The worker's `gerber.py::_rotate` applies component rotation CCW-positive, but the canonical board `rotation_deg` follows the KiCad footprint-placement convention (opposite sign — matching the canvas's documented `Transform2D(deg_to_rad(-rotation))`). For any component at rotation ∉ {0,180} the pad offsets are mirrored across the rotation axis, so pads render at the wrong absolute position — misaligned with their (correct, absolute) traces and, near a board edge, off the board outline entirely. Components at 0/180 are sign-invariant and unaffected, which is why it hides in simple test boards.\n\nDiscovered building the smart-remote board (C:\\gitlab\\ccsandbox\\smart-remote) through the live MCP pipeline. Its four 90°-rotated connectors (OLED1, MIC1, BAT1, AMP1) placed pads up to y=115.1mm on a 110mm-tall board (MIC1 off the top edge), and every connector pad sat 5–20mm off its routed trace endpoint. Verifying pad-under-both-signs against the ground-truth trace endpoints: NEGATED rotation matched every endpoint at d=0.0; CCW was off by 5–20mm. Negating rotation_deg (90→270) in the board source produced a correct, on-board, trace-aligned fab set (visually confirmed via pygerber raster).\n\nLikely a Y-axis-convention flip between how footprint pad offsets are framed vs. how the worker rotates. Fix options: define the contract's rotation convention explicitly and make `_rotate` (and the generate/KiCad path) honor it consistently; add a 90°-component golden test whose pad centers must equal known absolute coordinates.\n\nNOTE: also observed the `.kicad_pcb` from pcb_generate hardcodes SMD pad size `(size 1 0.6)` — it ignores pin `pad_width_mm`/`pad_height_mm`, which the gerber exporter DOES honor (emitted `%ADD..R,2.0X2.0`). generate and gerbers disagree on SMD pad geometry. Minor vs. the rotation bug but same file/area — worth fixing together.","created_at":"2026-07-07T08:10:31","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:16:23","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["fab-correctness","gerber","pcb","rotation","worker"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","repro_steps":"Author a canonical board with any component at rotation_deg 90, pins offset in +x/+y; run pcb_gerbers; the pad absolute positions are the CCW-rotated offsets, which are the mirror of KiCad's actual placement (compare to routed trace endpoints for that component's pins)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"PCB component &amp; footprint library management (three-tier, git-native, LLM-first)","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:07","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:51:06","priority":2,"tags":["component","design","footprint","library","pcb"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e03a359735b8eef694de52a5381","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Three-tier contract: board→component→footprint + functional-pin components","description":"Core contract change enabling everything else.\n- board.yaml component entry = {ref, component: <MPN/alias>, x_mm, y_mm, rotation_deg} — instance only.\n- Component definition (separate tier) = footprint ref + functional pin table [{name, function, pad}] + pin-map + footprint_status (real|borrowed) + params/lifecycle.\n- Nets reference FUNCTIONAL pins (net → MIC1.SD), not pad numbers; the pad number is a resolution detail.\n- Resolve step expands references → the explicit-geometry board the worker/DRC/gerber already consume (compute contract unchanged — additive).\n- Migration guard: resolved footprint pads must coincide with today's extracted pads (~0mm, the Fable coincidence check) so routing/DRC don't regress.\nHandles the MIC1/INMP441 borrowed-footprint case: functional identity preserved even when the physical footprint is a stand-in; connectivity is footprint-independent.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:33","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:33","priority":2,"tags":["component","contract","library","pcb"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e03c0d77af4b9885bb670216821","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Decomposed footprint library + on-demand import + SHA-pinned lockfile","description":"- Registry = remote corpus (KiCad libs, part services), searchable via a lightweight index (names+SHAs+URLs) so discovery doesn't download geometry.\n- Library = local, sparse, demand-populated; only footprints a board references; git-committed, one file per part.\n- Import-on-consume: unresolved ref → fetch just that .kicad_mod (SHA-pinned) → write file → record in lockfile → commit. node_modules, not all-of-npm.\n- Reuse pcb_fetch_libraries + libraries.lock.json; the change is a LAZILY-GROWING lockfile (vs a fixed pre-declared subset).\n- Git rules: deterministic serialization; geometry is a GENERATED artifact (regenerate on conflict, never line-merge pad coords); commit the lockfile (package-lock discipline).","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:41","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:41","priority":2,"tags":["footprint","git","library","lockfile","pcb"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e03d81571a9a6917c1dc52553e1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Layered library resolution + seed distribution (project/user/seed/registry)","description":"- Layers (precedence high→low): project (board's repo) → user (user's own git repo at a configured path) → seed (minerva-plugins, maintainer-curated) → registry (remote, on-demand only).\n- Config = ordered list of library search paths (text; KiCad global/project library-table analog).\n- Seed ships with the plugin; new users inherit it. Personal additions go in the USER layer (own repo) — not the seed — so plugin updates don't clobber them. Fork-the-seed is possible but inherit-and-extend is the DRY default.\n- On-demand imports land in the project or user layer, never the seed.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:47","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:47","priority":3,"tags":["distribution","library","pcb","seed"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e03f9ad7ba8a368037623427897","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Library CRUD ops incl. promote-component-to-library","description":"- CREATE → always a full .kicad_mod (pads+silk+courtyard), one file, pinned: (1) import from KiCad libs [primary]; (2) part-service import (SnapEDA/UltraLibrarian/Nexar) for a specific MPN; (3) IPC-7351 generate from datasheet dims (LLM drafts params, tool generates geometry); (4) hand-author custom → project-local.\n- PROMOTE-TO-LIBRARY (the MIC1→INMP441 share flow): strip instance (refdes/placement/nets), key by MPN, enrich (mfr/params/datasheet/lifecycle), verify all deps (footprint) resolve for consumers, commit to a layer (e.g. seed), repoint the board at the reference. Make this one LLM-driven gesture.\n- READ = resolve (ref→geometry) + query (extend pcb_check_libraries as the LLM search surface).\n- UPDATE = revise + bump revision + where-used (git grep/lockfile) + re-DRC affected boards.\n- DELETE = deprecate (keep for old-build reproducibility), hard-delete only truly unused.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:55","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:17:55","priority":3,"tags":["crud","library","pcb","workflow"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e0418a97d1c8c737297b65939f4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Substitution, functional equivalents &amp; board revisions (maker EOL flow)","description":"Target customer is a maker; parts get deprecated.\n- Author against INTENT: component may be a role/spec (\"3.3V 500mA LDO, SOT-23-5\") resolving to a chosen MPN; EOL → re-resolve the role, board intent unchanged.\n- Equivalence kinds: DROP-IN (same footprint + pin-map + electrical → just re-lock, patch rev) vs FUNCTIONAL-ONLY (different footprint/pinout → board REVISION with re-place + re-route).\n- Lifecycle/availability from Nexar/Octopart/SiliconExpert (maker cares 'can I buy it' more than formal EOL).\n- LLM-PROPOSES / TOOL-VERIFIES: LLM proposes candidates from params + supply API; tool deterministically verifies footprint+pinout compatibility.\n- Revisions = git: board.yaml is source; revision = tag + `revision:` field + changelog; the LOCKFILE pins exact MPNs+geometry per revision (old revs stay buildable). Our DRC + route tools are the revision- AND merge-verification engine.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:03","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:03","priority":3,"tags":["eol","library","pcb","revisions","supply-chain"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e04381b7e15b25cdf273500f55a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Silk/courtyard rendering (panel + gerber F.SilkS) — the 'looks lacking' fix","description":"Direct fix for the HITL 'ESP32 has no outline / no USB-C indicator' complaint. Two causes, both addressed here once full footprints are ingested (the contract/library items):\n- DATA gap: with full .kicad_mod ingested, the board CARRIES silk/courtyard/fab. Guard the pads-only→full-footprint migration with a pad-coincidence check (~0mm) so copper/DRC don't regress.\n- RENDER gap: the panel renderer must DRAW F.SilkS/F.CrtYd (today it draws pads + a crude box); the gerber exporter's F.SilkS must emit REAL silk (today it's a per-component courtyard-box placeholder), incl. pin-1 marker. Silk = footprint-intrinsic (body/pin-1) + board-instance (refdes/value stamped at placement).\nDepends on the three-tier contract + footprint-library items landing first.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:11","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:11","priority":2,"tags":["gerber","hitl","pcb","render","silkscreen"],"parent":"minerva:019f3e033b517bb2a9c05ad5e4fdb6b7"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f3e045dac74c5aa8642e891838d82","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Update pcb plugin manifest to seed an accurate skill (post-HITL, iterative)","description":"The plugin re-seeds skill `minerva_pcb_design` on install (currently master:019f3dc7ba22, source plugin:pcb). Its content is stale: says 'NO trace routing or gerber export yet', '.minpcb stays with the in-tree editor', 'renders a crude component view', and omits DRC/route/cutover. After cutover + the Step-2 build, the delivered reality is: sole PCB surface (cutover done); worker tools validate/generate/gerbers/DRC/route live; apply_route_hints wired; rotation/SMD/python fixes in.\n\nACTION: update pcb/manifest.json (system_prompt + the seeded skill: summary/steps/outcome/tool_deps incl. pcb_drc, pcb.route) to reflect delivered capability. TIMING: post-HITL and ITERATIVE — hold until the component/library design (sibling umbrella) settles and the HITL surfaces remaining gaps, so we seed an accurate, stable skill rather than re-editing it repeatedly. Do NOT hand-edit the docket skill record (plugin-managed, pristine_hash) — change the manifest source.","created_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:21","updated_at":"2026-07-07T19:18:21","priority":3,"tags":["docs","manifest","pcb","skill"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Deterministic plugin setup pipeline for manifest installs (build-on-install, no LLM at install time)","description":"Recurring failure: installing a plugin from a manifest on a machine where the binary isn't compiled yields a dead plugin (\"Plugin binary not found… needs to be compiled for <OS>\", PluginManager.gd:549-556) — detected only at first spawn, with no remediation path. Hit again 2026-07-15 reinstalling the pcb plugin post-cutover.\n\nPERSONA SPLIT (settled): manifest install ≡ dev with source present → BUILD ALWAYS as an install stage. Marketplace install ≡ user → SHA-pinned release artifact fetch (existing pattern). Each lane has exactly one binary source; the fetch/lockfile layer never enters the manifest lane. No-FCIB honored: git holds source + recipe + lockfiles, never compiled binaries.\n\nDESIGN DECISIONS (from 2026-07-15 design conversation):\n1. Manifest gains a `setup` PIPELINE — CI-style ordered stages, but a CLOSED VOCABULARY of typed steps implemented once in core: go_build{package,output}, cargo_build{manifest_dir,artifact}, python_venv{dir,install}, copy{from,to}, exec{argv} escape hatch (literal argv, UI-flagged). No shell-string interpretation, no LLM at install time. LLM authors the recipe once (plugin-authoring skill); installer replays data through a switch statement. Litmus for vocabulary additions: could two machines with the same checkout + same tool versions disagree about what to execute? If yes, it doesn't go in.\n2. Determinism: lockfiles pin deps (go.sum/Cargo.lock/pinned requirements.txt — package-lock discipline); resolved absolute tool paths passed explicitly to executors (no mid-build re-resolution); fixed cwd = plugin source dir; each step declares its expected artifact, verified mechanically after the step.\n3. Caching: the TOOLCHAIN is the cache (go/cargo incremental builds no-op when unchanged). Always invoke the build; NO Minerva-level source-hash skip cache initially — it reintroduces the stale-binary-vs-edited-source bug class. Add scoped hash cache only if a specific plugin's build proves slow.\n4. Toolchain preflight: core toolchain REGISTRY keyed by tool (candidate exe names, version cmd, parse pattern, well-known install dirs, install-hint URL). Probe rules: user-configured override → well-known dirs → PATH (GUI apps don't inherit shell PATH); hard-reject known shims (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Microsoft\\WindowsApps — the python3 Store-shim lesson, bug 019f3ba0b765); EXECUTE with timeout + parse version output — presence-on-PATH is never sufficiency, hang = fail; semver-compare vs manifest min; persist resolved absolute paths machine-locally (user://, resolve.go tier pattern). Preflight is ADVISORY (fast, specific errors for the 90% case); the always-run build is the real gate.\n5. State machine + UX: first-class states S_BUILDING / build_failed / installed_needs_binary alongside existing states; plugin manager shows \"Building…\" and failure detail with stderr tail; build output STREAMED to a visible terminal tab / Activity log (no silent failed builds; no silent MCP-driven installs); states exposed via MCP so agents can poll install progress.\n6. Serialization: pipeline shape = familiar YAML-CI grammar, but Godot core has no YAML parser → JSON-with-identical-shape inside manifest.json (or vendor a small YAML lib — cost call, decide in schema round).\n7. Pinned-image hermetic builds belong ONLY in the CI/release lane (marketplace artifacts): pin the builder image in the plugins-repo release workflow. NOT on the manifest path — plugins are host-native for 3 OSes (containers can't emit mac/win artifacts; cross-building unsupported), a docker daemon is a bigger/flakier prerequisite than the toolchain itself, and the dev lane has no fleet-drift problem. Optional `container:` executor field noted as a future extension; explicitly OUT OF SCOPE until a real drift bug demands it.\n8. Dry-run mode falls out free (steps are data): print exact argv list without executing — also what the permission dialog shows for exec steps.\n9. Degraded start (GDScript panels load without worker binary + structured worker_unavailable) is mostly moot on the manifest lane (always-build) — relevant only to marketplace installs on platforms without a published target; fold into the marketplace-lane child.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Installing the pcb plugin from manifest on a clean-toolchain machine either builds it to a working state or lands in a typed, actionable error (named missing tool + version + install hint) — never a dead \"error\" state at first spawn.\n- Re-install with unchanged source is near-instant (toolchain no-op).\n- Headless functional suite drives the installer through fixture manifests (missing tool, too-old tool, shim tool, failing step, lying artifact path) and asserts the exact terminal state — no live LLM, no unpinned network.\n- Marketplace lane unchanged except artifact-presence verify at install + degraded-start polish.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:49:57","updated_at":"2026-07-16T10:11:22","priority":2,"tags":["build","determinism","dx","installer","plugins","toolchain"],"resolution":"SHIPPED 2026-07-16. Deterministic manifest-install build pipeline delivered end-to-end: setup stanza schema + validation, cwd-independent typed executors w/ dry-run argv parity, toolchain preflight (probe-by-execution, shim rejection, sticky resolution), threaded install pipeline + S_BUILDING/S_BUILD_FAILED/S_NEEDS_BINARY states w/ restart-surviving envelopes + fail-closed exec approval (exec_denied_headless), panel UI (live step progress, failure detail, Rebuild, exec dialog w/ cancellation), Activity: Plugin Builds streaming, MCP build_status/setup_dry_run, F1-F13 matrix + run_setup_suites.sh (316/316 + guard 109/0). Minerva development @ 11e10216+; pcb manifest stanza minerva-plugins b4b42d2 (unpushed — owner action).\n\nHITL: register entry 1 PASS live 2026-07-16 — fresh pcb install BUILT (go_build + worker venv) and launched; the originating bug (manifest install → dead plugin, missing binary) is closed by construction. Entries 2-3 not exercised (owner accepted happy path); automated proxies stand (F3-F5 envelopes, failure-render probes, headless fail-closed test). First-attempt lesson captured in the register: stanza-less manifest correctly takes legacy path — precondition, not defect.\n\nOut of scope / follow-ups: C6 marketplace lane (019f694349ad, own cycle); 019f69abc131 SIGSEGV; 019f69abda79 PluginDB seam; 019f69b00f29 toolchain cfg race; 019f6a4856ee activity DRY."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6942df707bd0bd15cad0b70619fe","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C1: `setup` pipeline schema + typed step executors (closed vocabulary)","description":"Define the `setup` stanza in manifest.json (JSON serialization of a YAML-CI-shaped pipeline; decide JSON-in-manifest vs vendored YAML sidecar by cost here) and implement the step executors in core: go_build{package,output}, cargo_build{manifest_dir,artifact}, python_venv{dir,install:editable|requirements}, copy{from,to}, exec{argv} (literal argv, UI-flagged). Each executor builds an exact argv from typed fields — no shell interpolation. Steps declare expected artifacts; installer verifies after each step. Schema validated at manifest load in PluginDefinition (sibling of the tool-name-prefix rule). Vocabulary litmus: two machines, same checkout + same tool versions, must never disagree on what executes.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:18","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:13:48","priority":2,"tags":["installer","manifest","plugins","schema"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","resolution":"All three grandchildren done (G1.1 schema+validation, G1.2 executors, G1.3 dry-run). 86 unit-A tests green in main tree post-merge; dry-run==executor argv parity enforced by test. Merges d8978d9f/39d77204, pushed to origin/development."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6942f9897194810d15e04b0640f6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C2: Toolchain registry + preflight (probe by execution, not PATH presence)","description":"Core registry keyed by tool name (go, cargo/rustup, python, node/bun, zig, scons): candidate exe names, version command, parse pattern, well-known install dirs, install-hint URL. Manifest `requires:[{tool,min}]` references names only; exotic tools may inline a custom probe. Probe rules: resolution order = user override → well-known dirs (~/go/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, conda; GUI apps don't inherit shell PATH) → PATH; hard-reject known shim paths (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Microsoft\\WindowsApps — bug 019f3ba0b765 lesson); EXECUTE the version command with a timeout and parse output (exit-0 + parseable version = only pass; hang = fail); semver compare vs min with precise delta in the error (\"found go 1.19 at <path>, need >=1.22\" + install hint). Persist resolved absolute paths machine-locally (user://); invalidate on Re-check or failed build. Preflight is advisory — the always-run build remains the real gate.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:24","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:13:52","priority":2,"tags":["installer","plugins","preflight","toolchain"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","resolution":"Both grandchildren done (G2.1 registry+probe, G2.2 resolution/persistence/overrides). 44/44 main-tree. Cold Opus approve_with_notes. Merge 39d77204, pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69430fe172f481c6de2a0449f22d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C3: Installer integration + plugin state machine (S_BUILDING / build_failed / installed_needs_binary)","description":"Wire the manifest-install flow: load manifest → validate schema → toolchain preflight → run setup steps (always; toolchain incrementality is the cache — NO Minerva-level source-hash skip in v1) → verify entrypoint artifact → register plugin. New first-class states alongside S_STARTING/S_ERROR in PluginManager: S_BUILDING (during steps), build_failed (typed error: step, exit code, stderr tail, resolved argv), installed_needs_binary (marketplace lane / preflight-failed lane). Executors receive preflight-resolved absolute tool paths + fixed cwd (plugin source dir) — no mid-build re-resolution. Every step runs with a deadline; a hung step is a failure, never a wait. The existing first-spawn check (PluginManager.gd:549) remains as the last-resort backstop but should be unreachable on the manifest lane.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:30","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:56:59","priority":2,"tags":["installer","plugins","state-machine"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","resolution":"Both grandchildren done. Fable cold review (pattern-establishing): must_fix x1 (remove-while-building UAF) fixed + verified; threading pattern validated for reuse; 3 judgment calls accepted (sidecar persistence, FakeDB, entrypoint_check contract amendment 83e068db). Out-of-fence findings filed: 019f69abc131 (SingletonObject headless SIGSEGV, pre-existing), 019f69abda79 (PluginDB seam), 019f69b00f29 (toolchain cfg write race). Merge 1dae8d3a pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694321ec7cf99334189da600b028","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C4: UI + MCP surfacing of build state (no silent installs)","description":"Plugin manager shows \"Building…\" during S_BUILDING and, on build_failed, the failing step + stderr tail with a Re-check/Rebuild action. Build output streamed live to a visible surface (terminal tab or Activity log) — a silent failed build is the same bug in a new hat. Dry-run mode: because steps are data, render the exact argv list without executing (also the content of the exec-step permission dialog). MCP: expose install/build state + progress via the plugin tool surface so an agent driving an install can poll state instead of inferring from a failed tool call (plugin-state probes via MCP, per north star; no silent MCP-driven installs).","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:35","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:42:58","priority":2,"tags":["installer","mcp","plugins","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","resolution":"All three grandchildren done. Cold Opus review: must_fix x1 — exec approval failed OPEN on headless/MCP installs — fixed fail-closed (deny callable when no approver claimed, exec_denied_headless distinguishable, timing-proven the argv never runs). Merge 19cd2e46, 316/316 runner + 109 guard, pushed 910e552e."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694334527f43a8261150cc627ee3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C5: Headless functional harness — fixture manifests through the installer state machine","description":"CI-able suite driving the real installer against fixture manifests, asserting exact terminal states — no live LLM, no unpinned network. Cases: missing tool; too-old tool; shim tool (fake WindowsApps-style path); failing step (nonzero exit); hanging step (deadline enforcement); lying artifact path (step succeeds, declared artifact absent); happy path with a trivial go_build or copy step; re-install no-op timing; exec-step argv passed verbatim. Also golden-test the dry-run rendering. This is the determinism proof: same fixture → same argv sequence → same terminal state, every run.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:39","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:43:03","priority":2,"tags":["installer","plugins","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2","resolution":"F1-F13 audited against reality (caught stale F2 claim), gaps filled at real-install-path level (F2/F4/F5/F6), omissions documented with reviewer-endorsed reasoning (F8/F9/F10 duplicate-coverage, F13 unreachable PM flow — proven at SetupPipeline level), §4 annotated F#→test map, run_setup_suites.sh one-command CI runner (now 6 suites incl. build-ui). 316/316 twice identical. Cold Opus approve_with_notes; cleanup-leak note fixed. Merge 0b112c47, pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694349ad7344968c7c335289d5b3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"C6: Marketplace-lane alignment — install-time artifact verify, degraded start, pinned-image CI builds","description":"The other lane, kept small: (1) marketplace install verifies the platform artifact is present post-extract and lands in installed_needs_binary with a clear message when the platform has no published target (today's silent PBS install_only no-bin/ case); (2) degraded start — GDScript panels may load without the worker binary, worker-backed tools return structured worker_unavailable (precedent: apply_route_hints pre-wiring); (3) pin the builder image / runner spec in the plugins-repo release workflow so marketplace artifacts get hermetic cloud-ops reproducibility — the pinned image lives ONLY here, never on the manifest lane. Optional `container:` executor for local hermetic builds is explicitly out of scope until a real drift bug demands it (mac/win exclusion caps its value).","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:45","updated_at":"2026-07-16T04:50:45","priority":3,"tags":["ci","marketplace","plugins","release"],"parent":"minerva:019f69428fa07447b73ebe58c71356d2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69438de575e4be4dffbf8157e285","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G1.1: `setup` schema + PluginDefinition validation + serialization decision","description":"Define the pipeline grammar (stages → typed steps → declared artifacts, `requires:[{tool,min}]`) and validate it at manifest load in PluginDefinition (reject unknown step types, missing required fields, undeclared artifacts — sibling of the class_name/tool-prefix rules). Decide serialization here: JSON-with-CI-shape inside manifest.json (default; Godot has no YAML parser) vs vendoring a small YAML lib for a build.yaml sidecar — pick by cost, document the choice in the plugin authoring guide.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:02","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:12:39","priority":2,"tags":["manifest","plugins","schema","size:S"],"parent":"minerva:019f6942df707bd0bd15cad0b70619fe","resolution":"Schema + validation + serialization decision delivered. JSON-in-manifest (R0 decision, contract §1); SetupSchema.gd with all 5 typed error codes wired into PluginDefinition.validate() (+setup field, lax parse, to_dict round-trip, entrypoint-without-setup warning). Tests 32/32 main-tree. Commits: 77f5bdc1 (decision), f579f7fa via merge d8978d9f. Cold Opus review passed (schema portion clean)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943a3fe78ce8308d5d3c7eb9a50","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G1.2: Step executors — go_build, cargo_build, python_venv, copy, exec","description":"One executor per vocabulary entry, each ~30 lines: builds an exact argv from typed fields using the preflight-resolved absolute tool path, fixed cwd = plugin source dir, runs with a deadline, verifies its declared artifact afterward. python_venv covers the pcb-worker pattern (venv create + pip install -e ., the fix that unblocked Windows). exec{argv} passes the literal argv verbatim (no shell), and is the only step type flagged for explicit user confirmation. No executor re-resolves tools mid-build — that's how the Store shim sneaks back in.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:08","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:12:44","priority":2,"tags":["executors","installer","plugins","size:M"],"parent":"minerva:019f6942df707bd0bd15cad0b70619fe","resolution":"Five executors delivered, cwd-independent per amended contract (go -C, abs manifest-path/venv paths, exec ./argv[0] resolution, NO shell wrappers on any OS — review MF1/MF2 fixed), execute_with_pipe+poll+kill timeout, contract failure envelope, 2KB stderr cap. SetupSteps.gd single pure argv builder shared with dry-run + 24-check parity test (MF3 fixed). Executors suite 54/54 main-tree, non-mocked fixtures. Merge d8978d9f."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943af76773f9822a62776a17318","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G1.3: Dry-run rendering — exact argv plan without execution","description":"Because steps are data, render the full install plan (resolved tool paths, argv per step, expected artifacts) without executing anything. Consumed by: the C4 UI (preview before install), the exec-step permission dialog, and C5's golden tests. Deterministic output — same manifest + same resolved toolchain = byte-identical plan.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:11","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:12:49","priority":3,"tags":["dry-run","installer","plugins","size:S"],"parent":"minerva:019f6942df707bd0bd15cad0b70619fe","resolution":"SetupDryRun.gd renders deterministic plan from the same SetupSteps builder the executor uses (argv parity test-enforced); golden-tested (F12), run-twice determinism asserted. Merge d8978d9f."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943c1677a9783c3d810e13263f2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G2.1: Registry data + probe engine (execute with timeout, parse version, reject shims)","description":"Registry entries for go, cargo/rustup, python, node/bun, zig, scons: candidate exe names (python AND python3), version command, parse regex, install-hint URL, known shim path patterns. Probe = execute version command with short timeout; pass only on exit 0 + parseable version; hang or garbage = fail (never wait). Hard-reject WindowsApps store-alias paths before executing (bug 019f3ba0b765 class). Semver compare vs manifest min; error message carries tool, found version, found path, required min, install hint.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:15","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:12:54","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","probe","size:M","toolchain"],"parent":"minerva:019f6942f9897194810d15e04b0640f6","resolution":"ToolchainRegistry.gd + ToolchainProbe.gd: v1 registry (go/cargo/python/node/bun/zig/scons), execution+parse as sole pass condition, 5s deadline (proven empirically 5023ms), shim rejection before execution (marker-file proven), exact contract envelopes, preflight collects all failures. 44/44 main-tree. Cold Opus approve_with_notes, no must_fix. Merge 39d77204. Windows backslash paths untested (Linux sandbox) — covered by Windows-session HITL later."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943d5f47e888984efe2058c59eb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G2.2: Resolution order, GUI-PATH augmentation, sticky persistence + user overrides","description":"Resolution order: user-configured override → well-known install dirs (~/go/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, /usr/local/go/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, conda roots, %USERPROFILE%\\go\\bin) → PATH. GUI-launched Godot doesn't inherit shell PATH — without the well-known-dirs tier, \"not found\" is a lie on half of dev machines. Persist resolved absolute paths in machine-local config (user://, never the repo — resolve.go tier pattern / the /opt/conda symlink lesson: resolve once, run verbatim). Invalidate on explicit Re-check or a failed build. Settings UI: per-tool path override field.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:21","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:12:58","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","resolution","size:S","toolchain"],"parent":"minerva:019f6942f9897194810d15e04b0640f6","resolution":"Resolution order override→well-known→PATH with GUI-PATH augmentation (well-known dirs expanded at runtime, no literal placeholders — test-asserted); sticky persistence via standalone ConfigFile user://toolchain_paths.cfg (DocketManager pattern, no SingletonObject dep), re-probe-on-reuse, invalidate() forces re-resolution. Injectable search space; test config isolation (leak bug found+fixed, nudge-hinted). Merge 39d77204."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943e85f73bbb100b9bcb78e5e8e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G3.1: Install-flow orchestration — preflight → steps → artifact verify → register","description":"The manifest-install pipeline runner: schema validation → toolchain preflight → run setup steps in order (ALWAYS — toolchain incrementality is the cache; no source-hash skip in v1) → verify manifest entrypoint exists → register + start. Per-step deadline enforcement; abort remaining steps on first failure; entrypoint verify catches the \"build succeeded but manifest points at the wrong artifact path\" manifest bug with a precise message. The existing first-spawn check (PluginManager.gd:549) stays as backstop but must be unreachable via this lane.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:25","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:56:49","priority":2,"tags":["installer","orchestration","plugins","size:M"],"parent":"minerva:019f69430fe172f481c6de2a0449f22d","resolution":"SetupPipeline.gd: threaded preflight→always-build steps→entrypoint_check verify, call_deferred marshalling (Fable-review-validated self-join pattern), injectable exec-approver seam, all preflight failures surfaced, exec_denied distinguishable. Real-PluginManager functional tests. Merge 1dae8d3a, 82/82 + guards green main-tree, pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6943fc5371bd930ad8489bd5ccd3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G3.2: New plugin states + typed error envelope","description":"Add S_BUILDING, build_failed, installed_needs_binary to PluginManager's state machine with legal transitions (S_BUILDING → registered | build_failed; build_failed → S_BUILDING on rebuild). build_failed carries a typed envelope: {step_type, step_index, resolved_argv, exit_code, stderr_tail, artifact_expected}. installed_needs_binary is the marketplace/no-published-target state (C6 consumes). States persist across restart so a half-installed plugin reports honestly on next launch instead of a generic error.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:31","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:56:54","priority":2,"tags":["errors","plugins","size:S","state-machine"],"parent":"minerva:019f69430fe172f481c6de2a0449f22d","resolution":"S_BUILDING/S_BUILD_FAILED/S_NEEDS_BINARY + legal transitions in PluginManager; rebuild(); typed envelopes persisted via setup_state sidecar surviving restart (test-proven); remove_plugin guarded mid-build (review MF1 UAF fixed + regression test). Follow-ups filed: 019f69abda79 (fold sidecar into PluginDB + seam), crash-mid-build pending-marker note on same item. Merge 1dae8d3a."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694409a679c5a58e4a21a8cc5c62","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G4.1: Plugin-manager UI — Building… state, failure detail, Rebuild action","description":"Plugin manager rows render the new states: \"Building… (step 2/4: go_build)\" during S_BUILDING; on build_failed, the failing step + stderr tail inline with a Rebuild button (re-runs preflight + pipeline); installed_needs_binary shows the platform + why. Install-from-manifest flow shows the dry-run plan (G1.3) before first run, with explicit confirmation on exec steps.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:34","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:42:34","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","size:S","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019f694321ec7cf99334189da600b028","resolution":"PluginManagerPanel renders all three build states (live step N/M, failure detail incl. exec_denied/exec_denied_headless/entrypoint_check wording, every preflight failure w/ install hints) + Rebuild wired; PluginExecApprovalGate shows literal argv w/ FIFO queue + cancellation/app-quit force-deny (worker never blocked, test-proven). 74/74 population probes vs real scene+pipeline+subprocesses. Merge 19cd2e46, pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694419f87a9ea2d3f73a73365104","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G4.2: Stream build output to a visible surface (terminal tab / Activity log)","description":"Live-stream each step's stdout/stderr during S_BUILDING to a readable surface — terminal tab or the Activity log editor tab — so a failed build is diagnosable without rerunning anything outside Minerva. A silent failed build is the original bug in a new hat. Retain the last build log per plugin for post-hoc inspection (the build_failed stderr_tail links here for the full text).","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:38","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:42:39","priority":2,"tags":["logging","plugins","size:S","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019f694321ec7cf99334189da600b028","resolution":"Per-step build output streams to dedicated \"Activity: Plugin Builds\" tab via the existing EditorPane activity mechanism; routing lives in session-lifetime PluginMCPTools so MCP-triggered builds log with the panel closed; last-build log retained per plugin (in-memory, bounded) + envelope stderr_tail persists. Step granularity by design (executor line-streaming explicitly out of scope). Merge 19cd2e46. DRY follow-up filed 019f6a4856ee."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f694429597dc3b73fd0cec1cecdff","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"G4.3: MCP exposure of install/build state","description":"Expose plugin install/build state through the MCP plugin tool surface: state (S_BUILDING/build_failed/installed_needs_binary/registered), current step, and the typed failure envelope — so an agent driving an install polls real state instead of inferring from a failed tool call (plugin-state probes via MCP, per north star; masks no gaps with filesystem reads). Dry-run plan retrievable via MCP too, enabling agent-authored manifests to self-check before install.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:51:42","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:42:44","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","plugins","size:S"],"parent":"minerva:019f694321ec7cf99334189da600b028","resolution":"minerva_plugin_build_status (state/progress/full envelope incl. failures array + exec_denied_headless marker + build log) and minerva_plugin_setup_dry_run (id or manifest_path, probe-free) registered via the existing PluginMCPTools dispatch; tested through the real handle_tool_call entry. Merge 19cd2e46."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6947493f70d793a691b2a25fdbf2","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin library: rich descriptions + asking price (marketplace that feels real, no payment rails yet)","description":"Users browsing the plugin library should get real product information: a rich description (what the plugin does, what it needs, what it adds — beyond the one-line manifest description) and an ASKING PRICE for any plugin. No payment acceptance, entitlements, or license enforcement exists yet and none is in scope — the point is that the marketplace CONCEPT feels real: plugins present as products with a description and a price tag (including explicit \"Free\"), so the mental model and the metadata plumbing are in place before commerce ever is.\n\nLikely surface area (to be planned when picked up):\n- Manifest/metadata: description (long-form; possibly markdown) + price fields (amount, currency, or free) declared by the plugin author; validated like other manifest fields.\n- Registry: regen_registry.py + registry JSON carry the new fields so the marketplace listing can render them without fetching each plugin. NOTE: PLUGIN_DIRS in regen_registry.py is a hardcoded allow-list (known gotcha).\n- Marketplace UI (MarketplaceClient + library browser): listing shows price badge; detail view shows the rich description; install flow unchanged (price is informational — an \"ask\", not a gate).\n- MCP: listing/detail tools expose the same fields so an agent can answer \"what does this plugin do and what does it cost?\" (MCP drives all Minerva tasks).\n\nExplicitly OUT of scope: payment processing, purchase flow, entitlement checks, refunds, seller accounts. Nothing may block install on price in this DCR.\n\nFiled as parent only per owner request 2026-07-15; children to be broken out when picked up.","created_at":"2026-07-16T04:55:07","updated_at":"2026-07-16T04:55:07","priority":3,"tags":["marketplace","metadata","plugins","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69abc1317dc8ac1cba4b681a647f","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PluginManager bare SingletonObject reference SIGSEGVs headless --script runs (pre-existing; kills test_plugin_manager_hot_reload)","description":"Found during DCR 019f69428fa0 R2 (out-of-fence, worked around not fixed). PluginManager._build_available_tools() references SingletonObject bare (typeof(SingletonObject)); in `godot --headless --script` mode any script that STATICALLY types a reference to PluginManager triggers a hard engine SIGSEGV before the SingletonObject autoload registers. 100% deterministic; reproduces on the pre-existing, untouched test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd (crashes standalone with zero R2 involvement — pre-dates the round). Established workaround pattern: load() PluginManager after `await process_frame` (see test_pcb_plugin_smoke.gd and now test_plugin_setup_pipeline.gd). Fix direction: guard the autoload access (Engine.has_singleton / get_node_or_null pattern) or lazy-resolve SingletonObject in _build_available_tools(). test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd could not produce a pass/fail count in headless until this is fixed.","created_at":"2026-07-16T06:44:51","updated_at":"2026-07-16T17:55:58","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["headless","plugins","singleton","testing"],"repro_steps":"1. Write a headless test script with a statically-typed PluginManager variable (const PM := preload(...PluginManager.gd) + var m: PM). 2. Run `godot --headless --script test/that_test.gd`. 3. Engine SIGSEGVs before main. Or simply run the existing test_plugin_manager_hot_reload.gd headless."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69abda797a43a851fa8d2c3bcd96","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"PluginDB test-isolation seam (DB_PATH injectable) + fold setup_state sidecar into the plugin record + shared FakeDB helper","description":"Follow-up from DCR 019f69428fa0 R2. Three related debts: (1) PluginDB.DB_PATH is a hardcoded user://plugins/plugins.json with no test-isolation seam — R2's suite had to duck-type a FakeDB rather than follow the repo's own pcb/cad smoke convention (real PluginDB), because writing the real store risked racing a live editor session's plugin registry. Add an injectable path/instance seam. (2) R2 persists the setup failure envelope + terminal state in a PluginManager-owned sidecar (user://plugins/setup_state.json) overlaid after PluginDB.load_db() resets states — a deliberate fence workaround, not the final design; fold envelope/state persistence into the PluginDB record proper once the seam exists (mind: two-sources-of-truth window, crash between DB write and sidecar write). (3) Promote FakeDB to a shared test helper (second suite will need it) or retire it once the seam lands. Pending R2 Fable-review verdicts on the same questions — check the review notes on the DCR before starting.","created_at":"2026-07-16T06:44:58","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:50:16","priority":3,"tags":["persistence","plugins","testing"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f69b00f2972c4886bc51a7bb84308","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"ToolchainRegistry: concurrent preflights race writing toolchain_paths.cfg","description":"R2 Fable-review finding (out of R2's fence — R1 code). Two plugins installing concurrently each run ToolchainRegistry.preflight() on their own worker threads; both do read-modify-write on user://toolchain_paths.cfg via ConfigFile with no lock → last writer wins, one plugin's sticky resolutions silently dropped (self-heals on next resolve, so P3). Fix direction: a Mutex around load/save in ToolchainRegistry (instances are per-pipeline, so it needs to be static/class-level), or route persistence through a single shared registry instance owned by PluginManager. Related: 019f69abda79 (PluginDB seam follow-up).","created_at":"2026-07-16T06:49:33","updated_at":"2026-07-16T06:49:33","priority":3,"tags":["concurrency","plugins","toolchain"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a4856ee70909373eb448aeb29e2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Extract shared EditorPane.append_activity_line(tab_key, text) for activity-tab writers","description":"R3-A review follow-up (DCR 019f69428fa0). PluginMCPTools._append_build_activity and PluginNotifyRouter._append_to_activity_log each carry ~10-12 lines of editor-pane-resolution boilerplate around EditorPane._get_or_create_activity_log (entry formats differ). Under the DRY trigger and extraction needed touching out-of-fence files, so accepted in-round. Extract a shared EditorPane.append_activity_line(tab_key, text) and convert both callers when both files are editable in one round.","created_at":"2026-07-16T09:35:53","updated_at":"2026-07-16T09:35:53","priority":4,"tags":["activity-log","dry","ui"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"PCB editor plugin: UI/feature gap list (post-cutover) — mirrors PCBEditor note","description":"Umbrella for the post-cutover PCB editor UI/feature gaps, compiled 2026-07-16 and mirrored in the Minerva \"PCBEditor\" notes tab (note: \"PCB editor plugin — missing UI/feature work\"). Owner validating the draft; A6 + D3 added from owner validation input 2026-07-16. Sync both after validation, then break out children per area.\n\nA. Interactive editing: A1 manual trace draw/edit in panel (only import/route-worker paths today); A2 via support (validate model+render); A3 component property editing UI; A4 panel undo/redo surface (change journal exists via MCP only); A5 true-arc panel rendering (flat-chord known-minor); A6 [OWNER-ADDED] pin inspector — native INSPECT_PIN mode (Shift+P, click pin → Component.Pin + pin/net name or \"(unconnected)\"; pre-cutover-2026-07-07 PCBEditor.gd:365-373/665-698) not rebuilt; data exists MCP-side, gap is the click-to-inspect surface; consider net-highlight upgrade.\nB. File/format: B1 legacy .minpcb importer (opens as TEXT post-cutover; previously unfiled debt); B2 saved-tab enum remap = existing 019f33d2575d.\nC. Analysis/visualization: C1 in-panel DRC violations overlay/list (pcb_drc is MCP-JSON only); C2 in-panel gerber preview (fab check currently external gerbv — PCB HITL debt #5); C3 layer-aware annotation visibility = existing 019f33d2c9bf.\nD. Route loop UX (route hints = manually placed route paths, single trace or bus — integral to human/agent co-working since agent routing fails often and human judgment extends LLM thinking; plugin kept the data model (pcb_route_hint v2 envelopes, semantic pad anchors) + apply path (minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints → cyan proposals), lost the native authoring UX at cutover): D1 proposal accept/reject affordances (HITL debt #9 unburned); D2 waypoint-authoring polish pending live pass (#8); D3 [OWNER-ADDED] native route-hint authoring cluster + annotation-mode separation — arrow/text review annotations belong in the annotations panel; route hints are core workflow and need their own tool cluster (native RouteHintMode WAYPOINT/SINGLE_TRACE/BUS/INSPECT_PIN: pin-snap trace flow, 3-phase bus flow src-pins→corridor→dest-pins, live dashed preview, author colors teal-human/purple-AI, clear-by-author) + own listing/visibility model so the annotations panel stays review-only. Subsumes HITL debt #3 (route-hint toolbar button); interacts C3/D1/D2/A6. Native code: pre-cutover-2026-07-07 PCBCanvas.gd L82/L1378/L2361-2520, PCBRouteHint.gd.\nE. Library UX: E1 library browser/search panel surface (worker/LLM-side exists) → ties 019f3e03d815 + 019f3e03f9ad; E2 promote-to-library one-gesture (019f3e03f9ad); E3 substitution/EOL/revisions flow (019f3e0418a9).\nF. Polish/debt: F1 per-side silk with layer filter; F2 66KB .pcbskel perf watch; F3 stale seeded skill (019f3e045dac); F4 remaining PCB HITL register entries #1/#3(subsumed by D3)/#4/#5/#6/#8/#9.\n\nExisting docket items referenced above stay authoritative — this umbrella tracks only the NEW gaps (A1-A6, B1, C1, C2, D1, D3, E1 browser surface, F1, F2) until they're broken out.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:12:21","updated_at":"2026-07-16T18:09:14","priority":2,"tags":["gap-list","pcb","plugin","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a89029b710a99ef0f60d930b091","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"R0: PCB UI contract + spikes (pad API, workflow-kind, tool state machines, E2E matrix)","description":"Contract doc in Docs/design/: public pad-API shape ({component, pin, position}), substrate workflow-kind visibility contract, single-trace + bus author-tool state machines (native parity per pre-cutover-2026-07-07 archaeology, nudge pcb-gaps/native-route-hint-refs), LLM-ergonomics section (agent READ/SEE/ACT per feature), acceptance matrix = E2E-1..E2E-7 (from \"PCB UI Functional Tests\" Minerva sheet, owner-blessed 2026-07-16 incl. real Go worker in E2E-3/6). Spikes to answer in doc: (a) does AnnotationOverlay block pan/zoom mid-tool? (b) do key events reach author tools (Enter bus-phase advance)? (c) does pcb_get_image render route hints today? Bus mismatched src/dest count behavior is a contract decision (E2E-7B). Process: direct, Fable. Serial plan: this blocks WC-1.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:31","updated_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:31","priority":1,"tags":["contract","pcb","r0"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a8918fe7dd0beb0e0f679249a81","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"WC-1: Pin inspector — panel INSPECT_PIN mode + Pin Info + MCP parity (A6)","description":"Gap A6. Host public pad_at() structured API ({component, pin, position}); canvas INSPECT_PIN mode + Shift+P toolbar toggle; Pin Info section (Component.Pin + geometry pin name > net name > \"(unconnected)\"); MCP parity child — pin-info query on the SAME host lookup returning what the UI shows + connected net members. Native reference: pre-cutover-2026-07-07 PCBEditor.gd:365-373/665-698. Repo: minerva-plugins/pcb. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet implement + Opus cold review, pinned worktree + scope fence + functional floor. Exit gate: E2E-1 green through real panel + real input path; suites green; review clean. Blocks HITL-1 (owner clicks pins on smart-remote). Serial: after R0, before everything else.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:37","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:00:00","priority":1,"tags":["pcb","pin-inspector","ui","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6","resolution":"HITL-1 PASSED 2026-07-16 (owner: \"good!\" after restart + live pin-inspector run on smart-remote.pcbskel). One feedback item, fixed same session: toolbar button must use the native editor's icon — pin_icon_24.png copied to plugin as inspect_pin_24.png (minerva-plugins 7cb20de); Select/Pan were already hash-identical to native. Round commits: minerva-plugins a600028 (impl), Minerva 2e465e5a (MCP tool + E2E-1, pushed). E2E-1 45/45, Layer-1 303/303, Opus approve_with_notes. Agent parity re-verified live post-restart: minerva_pcb_pin_info U1.1 → VCC_3V3, 4 net members, 1 trace."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a892ea4779a88ddf51ca8cf483d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"WC-2: Substrate workflow-kind separation — own listing, review-panel exclusion, layer visibility (D3 substrate half)","description":"D3 substrate half. Core annotation substrate: a kind can declare itself workflow-class → own listing surface, excluded from the review annotations panel, layer-keyed visibility. Root-cause fix for C3 = 019f33d2c9bf (hiding B.Cu doesn't hide bottom-layer route hints). Agent contract: separation is UI-ONLY — annotations_list/query/resolve_ref keep returning workflow kinds (regression-tested). Repo: Minerva core (touches annotation dock used by CAD/graphics too — pattern-establishing). Process: /work-cycle single round, FABLE implement + Opus cold review. Exit gate: E2E-2 green incl. C3 red-to-green repro + persistence round-trip; core suites green; pcb mount guard green. Serial: after HITL-1, before WC-3.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:43","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:38:44","priority":1,"tags":["annotations","pcb","substrate","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6","resolution":"WC-2 complete 2026-07-16, stop condition 3c (auto-verified gate, no HITL). Deliverables: AnnotationKind.workflow_class flag; review-dock exclusion + new WorkflowAnnotationList (per-host, kind-grouped, self-hiding); layer-keyed visibility via duck-typed host.is_annotation_visible (C3 019f33d2c9bf fixed, red→green proven); AnnotationOverlay nav pass-through (MIDDLE/wheel/pan + middle-drag motion → forward_navigation_input); Enter/KP_Enter pseudo-pointer forwarding; annotations_add host-registry fix (019f6a8d1391 resolved). Gate: E2E-2 A-F green — test_pcb_workflow_kinds.gd 50/0. Layer-1: pin_inspector 45, panel_layout 63, select_tool 36, mcp_annotations_query 26, overlay_draw 13, workbench_sync 12, overlay_kinds exit0 — all green; 5 pre-existing base failures verified not regressions. Opus cold review: approve_with_notes, 0 must_fix, fence clean. Commits: Minerva development 03b456e5 (PUSHED), minerva-plugins main f76c49a (local; owner push). Findings filed: 019f6b9215b0 (v1/v2 timestamp clash), 019f6b9221b6 (add swallows empty-id), 019f6b925a95 (ux_substrate drift)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a894a377910bfdb3850a5b7bbc3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"WC-3: Single-trace author tool + in-panel route-flow toolbar + agent visibility (D3 core)","description":"D3 core. pcb_route_hint author tool #2: pin-snap source → waypoints → dest pin / dbl-click finish; self-reference cancel; live preview; author colors (teal human / cyan AI regression); clear-by-author (human/AI/all); in-panel route-flow toolbar cluster activating tools (conscious partial reversal of Round-B \"no authoring in panel\" — implementations stay substrate-shaped AnnotationAuthorTools). AGENT SEE/ACT: pcb_get_image renders hints + cyan proposals (render_overlay can't composite plugin scenes — must draw in plugin's own image path); bidirectional envelope parity (tool-authored ≡ MCP-authored through route builder). Native ref: PCBCanvas.gd L2406+ trace flow. Repo: minerva-plugins/pcb. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus review. Exit gate: E2E-3 (human hints → agent sees → agent routes with REAL Go worker → trace in saved YAML), E2E-4 (cancel = zero residue), E2E-5 (agent authors, human sees, parity) all green; suites green. Blocks HITL-2 (full co-working loop live). Serial: after WC-2.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:50","updated_at":"2026-07-16T16:23:22","priority":1,"tags":["pcb","route-hints","ui","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6","resolution":"WC-3 complete 2026-07-16. Deliverables: SingleTraceAuthorTool (§5 state machine exact: pad-snap 5mm, self-ref cancel, same-position+Enter double-click recovery, dashed preview, interior-only waypoints, semantic pad anchors, shipped coloring); in-panel route-flow toolbar cluster (native trace icon, mode label, deactivate→Select); clear-by-author(human/AI/all) host filter + context menu on WorkflowAnnotationList (fence extension per comment #526); bidirectional envelope parity. Gates: E2E-3/4/5 green — test_pcb_single_trace_tool 83/0 (incl. 10 mutual-exclusion checks; E2E-3C used the REAL Go binary + Python route worker, real_worker_used=true; windowed variant verified under xvfb-run), test_pcb_route_hint_mcp_parity 19/0; regressions: workflow_kinds 50/0, pin_inspector 45/0, apply_route_hints 47/0, panel_layout 64/0. Opus cold review: must_fix (1 item — trace tool not mutually exclusive with canvas tool surface) → resolved by orchestrator (plugins 43785a3 + 10 regression checks), all else pass, fence clean. Commits: Minerva development 45866702 PUSHED (incl. a6d5e89d + cf0ab016), minerva-plugins main 43785a3 local (owner push; incl. efffc9c). Findings filed: 019f6bbcc688 (double_click not forwarded), 019f6bbcd6cd (dest_point snapshot). Next: HITL-2 (owner) — full human-hints→agent-routes loop live; requires Minerva restart."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a895b6476f4b3ff081a72f8392d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"WC-4: Bus author tool — 3-phase state machine (D3 completion)","description":"D3 completion. Bus author tool: 3-phase state machine (source pins → corridor waypoints → dest pins), dbl-click advance + keyboard advance per R0 spike (b) (fallback dbl-click only), duplicate-pin guard, waypoints = corridor only (pin positions never duplicated in). Mismatched src/dest count behavior per R0 contract decision. Bus hints render in pcb_get_image. Native ref: PCBCanvas.gd L2436+ BusPhase machine. Repo: minerva-plugins/pcb. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus review. Exit gate: E2E-6 (3-phase authoring → agent sees → 3 real parallel traces on disk, real worker) + E2E-7 (edge stories) green; suites green. Blocks HITL-3 (final acceptance). Serial: after HITL-2.","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:54","updated_at":"2026-07-16T10:46:54","priority":2,"tags":["bus","pcb","route-hints","ui","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6a8d13917595bc76b487eb67ee55","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_annotations_add validates kind against GLOBAL registry — plugin kinds rejected, agents can't author plugin-kind annotations","description":"MCPAnnotationTools.gd:975 _annotations_add resolves _get_registry() (global) for the kind-registered check + dispatch_validate, even when editor_name names a live host whose own registry (resolved later at :1019) has the plugin kind registered. The read path (:537-541) correctly prefers source registry → host.get_registry() → global. Repro: minerva_annotations_add editor_name=\"smart-remote.pcbskel\" kind=pcb_route_hint → {\"ok\":false, code:\"enum\", \"not registered\"} while annotations_list on the same editor_name returns source:live and the host capabilities list pcb_route_hint. Impact: NO MCP surface can author a route hint (apply_route_hints writes proposals via host directly) — blocks E2E-5 (agent-authors direction of the co-working loop) and any plugin-kind authoring by agents. Fix (WC-2 scope): resolve host FIRST when editor_name given; use host.get_registry() for kind check + primitives_optional + dispatch_validate, falling back to global; regression test = add plugin-kind annotation via MCP on a live plugin host. Discovered 2026-07-16 during R0 spike (c).","created_at":"2026-07-16T10:50:58","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:38:24","severity":2,"tags":["annotations","llm-ergonomics","mcp","plugins"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a892ea4779a88ddf51ca8cf483d","resolution":"Fixed in WC-2 (019f6a892ea4), Minerva 03b456e5: _annotations_add resolves the host FIRST and uses host.get_registry() for kind check, primitives_optional, and dispatch_validate (global fallback), mirroring the read path :537-541. Regression-tested in test_pcb_workflow_kinds.gd: pcb_route_hint add with valid editor_name succeeds; unknown kind still rejected; read surfaces unchanged."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6b91d8ba77798fdf39f20ac8299b","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"annotations_add for pcb_route_hint: omit created_at/updated_at","created_at":"2026-07-16T15:35:48","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:32","tags":["annotations","gotcha","mcp","pcb"],"value":"When authoring pcb_route_hint (or any v2-kind) annotations via minerva_annotations_add, OMIT created_at/updated_at from the envelope. The add path validates with v1 AnnotationSchema which requires RFC3339 STRING timestamps, while v2 hosts (PcbAnnotationHost.build_route_hint_envelope) emit unix INTs — supplying int timestamps fails validation. Discovered WC-2 (019f6a892ea4); proper schema unification filed as follow-up.","component":"annotations-mcp","key":"add-route-hint-omit-timestamps","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6b9215b07bf89b1bfb8f7d7386f2","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"annotations_add validates v2 envelopes with v1 AnnotationSchema — timestamp type clash","description":"_annotations_add (MCPAnnotationTools.gd) validates with v1 AnnotationSchema requiring RFC3339 STRING created_at/updated_at, while v2 hosts emit unix INT timestamps (e.g. PcbAnnotationHost.build_route_hint_envelope) — so an agent echoing a host-built envelope back through annotations_add fails validation. Workaround (docket hint annotations-mcp/add-route-hint-omit-timestamps): omit both fields. Proper fix is a schema unification / v2-aware add validation — a contract change deliberately NOT made in WC-2 (fence: file, don't fix). Found while implementing 019f6a892ea4.","created_at":"2026-07-16T15:36:03","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:36:26","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","filed-not-fixed","mcp","schema"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6b9221b672f3b53f0f9cd2328b5c","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"annotations_add returns success:true when host.add_annotation returns empty id","description":"MCPAnnotationTools._annotations_add swallows host-side add failures: when host.add_annotation() returns an empty id (host-internal validation failure), the tool still answers success:true. Agents get a false positive and no annotation exists. Tightening changes the tool's error contract → deferred out of WC-2 (019f6a892ea4). Fix: empty id → structured error envelope (match _err conventions).","created_at":"2026-07-16T15:36:06","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:23:35","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","error-handling","filed-not-fixed","mcp"],"resolution":"Fixed in Minerva b6aa6f54 (pushed; needs app restart to go live). Root cause (Opus-verified against pre-fix code): _annotations_add passed host.add_annotation's result straight into the success echo — ref/echo minted from the CALLER's envelope, so a host-side rejection (AnnotationV2Schema._validate_kind_anchor_compat runs only inside the host, never at the MCP layer) returned \"\" and still produced \"Created C74\". The \":61\" was a red herring: generic anchor-position rendering (x≈60.96 → 61). Fix: empty id → structured {ok:false, errors:[{code:\"host_rejected\"}]} — no ref, no echo, no success. Hardening: AnnotationHostRegistry panel-host-priority collision policy (non-text hosts never displaced by text-buffer hosts) + identity-checked deregister param. Regression: test_annotations_host_ambiguity 18/0 (competing text+panel hosts, empty-id stub) + host_registry 18/0. Known gap (in-fence contract limit): host validation details are push_warning'd only, so the structured error can't say WHY — surfacing host errors through add_annotation's return is follow-up material. Fast-follows filed: 019f6ef12321 (Editor.gd expected_host), 019f6ef11335 (anonymous tab-name dedup)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6b925a9572e6a975b01b264195a5","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"test_annotation_ux_substrate: \"text host declares text/callout kind\" trio fails on clean base — capabilities drift?","description":"Pre-existing failure (verified identical on pristine 2e465e5a base during WC-2, not a regression): annotations_v2/test_annotation_ux_substrate 26/3 — the three failing checks assert the text-editor host declares text/callout kinds. Unlike the other headless-environment failures (font pixel probe, Go worker absent, SQLite-extension cascade), this one looks like real capabilities drift between TextEditorAnnotationHost and the suite's expectations. Triage: run windowed, then diff host get_capabilities/kind registration against the suite's asserts.","created_at":"2026-07-16T15:36:21","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:36:41","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","filed-not-fixed","pre-existing","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6b93ec807390bf3060bff2538652","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"In-app minerva_docket_* and standalone docket MCP resolve different stores","created_at":"2026-07-16T15:38:04","updated_at":"2026-07-16T15:38:04","tags":["docket","gotcha","mcp"],"value":"minerva_docket_* tools (via the Minerva app / raw http://127.0.0.1:9315/mcp) returned \"Item not found\" for minerva-project items (e.g. 019f33d2c9bf) that the standalone mcp__docket__ server resolves fine with project=minerva. The two do not share item visibility — for minerva-project docket writes, use the standalone docket MCP server, not the in-app endpoint. (Observed 2026-07-16 during WC-2 close-out.)","component":"docket","key":"minerva-vs-standalone-docket-stores","confidence":"medium"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6bbcc68879138cd63b5b52b02bc6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"AnnotationOverlay does not forward the double_click flag to author tools","description":"AnnotationOverlay._gui_input strips InputEventMouseButton.double_click when forwarding pointer events to AnnotationAuthorTools — tools cannot distinguish a double-click. WC-3's single-trace tool (019f6a894a37) recovered via a same-position-click heuristic + Enter fallback (documented in pcb_route_hint_kind.gd); WC-4's bus tool will use the same recovery. Proper fix: forward the flag (new optional arg or event field) via the existing pointer convention — substrate change, was out of WC-3's fence.","created_at":"2026-07-16T16:22:41","updated_at":"2026-07-16T16:22:56","severity":3,"tags":["annotations","filed-not-fixed","substrate"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6bbcd6cd79528e2530e4bad6d796","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Single-trace hint dest_point is a commit-time snapshot — no live dest-pad tracking","description":"AnnotationKind.hit_test()/bounds() have no host parameter, so a single-trace route hint's dest_pins ref cannot be re-resolved live the way the source anchor is (base resolve_anchor path). The hint renders/hit-tests against a dest_point snapshot cached at commit; moving the dest component leaves the drawn hint at the old location until re-author. Documented in pcb_route_hint_kind.gd (WC-3, 019f6a894a37). Fix requires a substrate signature change (pass host into hit_test/bounds) — pattern-establishing, own round.","created_at":"2026-07-16T16:22:45","updated_at":"2026-07-16T16:23:01","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","filed-not-fixed","pcb","substrate"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6bda67f97ff5a38c0bd9bdfe5713","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Panel IPC replies arrive DOUBLE-wrapped: {success,result:{ok,result}}","created_at":"2026-07-16T16:55:03","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:35","tags":["envelope","gotcha","ipc","pcb","testing"],"value":"MinervaIPC.await_reply returns {success, result:<backend reply>}; the pcb Go side (HandleRouteChannel etc.) forwards the Python worker's own {ok, result} envelope verbatim — so dotted-channel consumers (panel route_board and friends) receive {success:true, result:{ok:true, result:<payload>}} and must unwrap BOTH levels. Test fakes must reproduce the broker wrapper, not just the worker envelope — a one-level fake let a live \"0 proposals\" bug through HITL-2 (fixed plugins fe64d63; regression = test_pcb_single_trace_tool BR section). Same trap awaits any new dotted pcb.* channel consumer.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"broker-ipc-double-envelope","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c11feba74ad8d3d4896dd497348","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"godot --path on a project KILLS the running debug session — worktree runs are safe (verified)","created_at":"2026-07-16T17:55:46","updated_at":"2026-07-16T18:42:21","tags":["godot","gotcha","hitl","testing","worktree"],"value":"Running `godot --headless --path <proj> --script test/...` auto-stops a live editor-launched debug session of the SAME project (by-design Godot CLI behavior, per owner 2026-07-16; killed the live app twice during HITL-2). VERIFIED MITIGATION (2026-07-16): run suites from a git-worktree copy — different project path does NOT kill the live session (probe + 3 full suites, app stayed up). Recipe: `git worktree add --detach ~/github/minerva-worktrees/suite-run/Minerva HEAD; ln -s ~/github/minerva-plugins ~/github/minerva-worktrees/suite-run/minerva-plugins; cp -r src/.godot <worktree>/src/.godot` (warm cache skips the import pass); copy uncommitted files in; run; `git worktree remove --force` after. NEVER run suites from the main checkout while the owner's Minerva session is live.","component":"godot","key":"cli-path-kills-live-debug-session","confidence":"high"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c1e03997b10a92121d216d7f950","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb panel works while backend subprocess is stopped — routing fails confusingly; no auto-start","description":"Owner HITL-2 finding: godot_scene panels are host-executed, so the pcb editor opens and edits fine while the plugin's Go backend is STOPPED — then the first route/serialize call fails with a raw worker_unavailable (\"Plugin is not running\"). Confusing to users (\"did this use Minerva's UI without the plugin running? How?\"). Also: after an app relaunch the pcb backend did NOT autostart (2026-07-16 session; needed manual minerva_plugin_start). Fix directions: (a) panel-open auto-starts or lazily starts the backend; (b) route-flow actions surface \"backend stopped — start it?\" instead of the plumbing error; (c) investigate why autostart didn't fire. Surfaced during HITL-2 on 019f6a894a37.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:08:54","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:01:16","severity":3,"tags":["hitl","pcb","plugins","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c3d0e3d709e85a709abc9f908c0","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Panel-executed plugin MCP tools — migrate minerva_pcb_* panel tools out of core","description":"Owner directive (HITL-2, 2026-07-16): \"I don't want Minerva aware of PCB workflows in any way. It really should be 100% the plugin's code. Else we maintain 2 codebases for every change — exactly what plugins is designed to solve.\"\n\nPROBLEM. MCPPcbPanelTools.gd (~85 tools' worth of PCB business logic: component validation, route materialization, proposal write-back, hint lifecycle) lives in Minerva core because plugin-declared MCP tools execute in the plugin SUBPROCESS (Go backend, minerva_pcb_pcb_* namespace), while panel-local tools must touch the live in-process scene (canvas, board model, annotation host). There is currently no way for plugin-owned code to serve an MCP tool that runs in-process — so every PCB workflow change (e.g. today's delete-on-commit, de244127) lands in core.\n\nDESIGN SKETCH. New manifest tool executor kind: `executor: \"panel\"` (vs today's implicit \"backend\"). Core's contribution shrinks to a GENERIC dispatcher: tool name → resolve editor/panel by editor_name (existing host-registry path) → duck-typed `handle_tool(name, args)` on the plugin's own panel/host GDScript (plugin repo files, already loaded in-process) → return envelope. Tool schemas come from the manifest (source of truth, existing rule). Namespacing: panel tools keep minerva_<plugin_id>_* (validation + dispatch rule already enforced). Trust: panel tools run host-side by definition — gate on the plugin's granted capabilities at dispatch.\n\nMIGRATION. Tool-by-tool from MCPPcbPanelTools.gd into pcb/ui/ handlers; core module shrinks and finally deletes (precedent: broker DCR deleted MCPPresentationTools). The owner-ratified workflow-contract rounds (refine-loop revision stack + undo, explicit-propose UX + commit cleanup) should land PLUGIN-SIDE under this DCR rather than accreting in core; the interim core commits (de244127 etc.) migrate with their tools.\n\nACCEPTANCE. (a) a pcb panel tool serves from plugin GDScript with core generic-only; (b) MCPPcbPanelTools.gd deleted or reduced to zero pcb semantics; (c) E2E suites keep running the real MCP path (test_pcb_* handle() convention unchanged from the caller's view); (d) another scene-panel plugin (cad/graphics) can adopt the same executor with no core change.\n\nChildren to be broken out at pickup. Related: broker DCR (MCPPresentationTools deletion), plugin tool-name prefix rule, manifest-is-source-of-truth rule.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:42:48","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:33:37","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","mcp","pcb","plugins","substrate"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Panel-tools migration plan — serial umbrella (R0 → C1..C6)","description":"Serial execution umbrella for DCR 019f6c3d0e3d (panel-executed plugin MCP tools). One task completes before the next starts; work-cycle single rounds with pinned sibling worktrees; suites run from worktree copies only (godot/cli-path-kills-live-debug-session). Chain: R0 contract+spikes → C1 core generic dispatcher → C2 pcb migration wave 1 (board CRUD) → C3 pcb wave 2 (route workflow) + MCPPcbPanelTools deletion → C4 refine-loop plugin-side (HITL gate) → C5 explicit-propose UX (HITL gate) → C6 second-plugin adoption proof. Owner directive anchoring all scope decisions: Minerva core must carry ZERO pcb workflow semantics — anything pcb-specific found in core during any round is migration backlog, not a fix site.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:51:54","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:33:01","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","pcb","plan","plugins","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c3d0e3d709e85a709abc9f908c0","resolution":"Umbrella complete 2026-07-16: serial chain R0→C1→C2→C3→C4→C5→C6 all done in ONE autonomous session (owner-authorized deferred-HITL campaign). Headline: MCPPcbPanelTools.gd DELETED — zero pcb workflow semantics in Minerva core (grep-gated); 21 pcb tools + refine-loop + explicit-propose all plugin-side on the new executor:\"panel\" substrate; cad proves genericity with a zero-core-diff adoption. 9 HITL register entries await one consolidated owner session (Docs/design/panel-tools-hitl-debt.md). Minerva development pushed through bc986f87; minerva-plugins main local through c7d4129 (owner push). 6 findings filed as separate items; 4 durable hints saved. Every round: pinned worktrees, cold Opus review (5 approves, 1 approve_with_notes resolved, 1 must_fix orchestrator-resolved in WC-3 era), real-worker functional floors, suites from worktree copies only."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c45a27975f09fb402f6fda42cad","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"R0: Panel-tool executor contract + spikes","description":"Design contract doc in Docs/design/ (panel-executed-tools.md): manifest schema for tools[] entries gaining executor:\"panel\"|\"backend\" (default backend, back-compat); generic core dispatch path (tool name → editor_name resolution via existing host registry → duck-typed handle_tool(name, args) on panel/host → result envelope conventions incl. structured errors); capability gating semantics for host-side execution; schema source stays manifest. Spikes (answer in doc with file:line evidence): (a) where PluginToolRegistry/MinervaMCPServer dispatch plugin tools today and where the executor branch goes; (b) whether MCPPcbPanelTools._resolve_host/_reg conventions can be lifted into the generic dispatcher; (c) install/validation pipeline changes for the new manifest field (schema, reinstall behavior, stale-DB gotcha); (d) migration table: every MCPPcbPanelTools tool → target plugin file + wave (C2/C3). Acceptance matrix E2E-P1..P4 defined here (P1 hello panel-tool serves from plugin GDScript; P2 pcb tool parity pre/post migration; P3 core deletion leaves suites green; P4 second plugin adopts with zero core change). Process: direct (main session), Fable. Exit gate: contract committed; all four spikes answered.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:10","updated_at":"2026-07-16T19:03:46","priority":1,"tags":["design","substrate","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"R0 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified: contract committed, all four spikes answered). Docs/design/panel-executed-tools.md @ 87fcbd65 (pushed): (a) dispatch anatomy — executor branch goes in PluginToolRegistry.handle_tool_call:287 after plugin resolution, panel tools skip the RUNNING check; (b) resolution seam — broker panel registry primary + AnnotationHostRegistry fallback, ownership check via get_panel_owner; (c) manifest impact — unknown fields pass through everywhere EXCEPT register_plugin_tools:160-165 key whitelist (the one change point); PluginDB round-trips verbatim; manifest edits need reinstall (hot reload doesn't re-parse); no tool-count guards, watch --pcb-guard + no-bleed; (d) 21-tool migration table W1/W2 + helper disposition. E2E-P1..P4 matrix defined. HITL debt register created (empty). Next: C1."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c45b614762889d67c8417e6aeb6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C1: Core generic panel-tool dispatcher + manifest executor field","description":"Implement the R0 contract in core: manifest validation accepts executor:\"panel\"; PluginToolRegistry routes panel-executor tools to the generic dispatcher (resolve panel by editor_name arg → duck-typed handle_tool → envelope); capability gating; structured errors (editor_not_found, panel_no_handler, etc.). Proof tool: hello-style plugin serves one panel tool end-to-end (E2E-P1). NO pcb semantics in any core change. Repo: Minerva. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet implement + Opus cold review (pattern-establishing? — this is THE pattern for every scene-panel plugin; consider Fable implement). Exit gate: E2E-P1 green; plugin install/reload regression green; core suites green.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:15","updated_at":"2026-07-16T20:04:22","priority":1,"tags":["core","substrate","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C1 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified). Minerva development @ b5ce04da PUSHED: PluginDefinition executor validation (tool_executor_invalid), PluginToolRegistry whitelist keeps executor (default backend; backend-discovered hardcoded backend), panel dispatch branch at step 1.5 BEFORE the running check (early return, no fall-through either direction) — editor_name_required → broker get_panel_for_editor (new accessor + list_panel_editor_names) → AnnotationHostRegistry host.get_panel() fallback (lazy load, structured degrade) → editor_not_found w/ known editors → ownership fail-safe deny (panel_not_owned) → await panel.handle_tool (sync+async) → panel_no_handler/tool_unhandled/verbatim pass-through; audit carries executor:\"panel\"; 4 new PluginErrors builders. Gate E2E-P1: test_panel_executed_tools 38/0 (scenarios A-G, real PluginToolRegistry + real PluginManager + real broker). Layer-1 re-verified: broker 91/0, definition 109/0. ~23 plugin suites green; pre-existing failures verified on base and filed (019f6c85b9d6). Opus cold review: APPROVE, zero must_fixes, fence clean (6 files, all in-fence; plugins repo untouched). Implementer survived 3 transient API terminations via transcript resume. Next: C2 wave-1 migration."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c45f09e7238a8e14238ddd8d8e5","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C2: pcb migration wave 1 — board CRUD tools to panel handlers","description":"Migrate the board-CRUD half of MCPPcbPanelTools per R0's migration table (add/move/rotate/delete component, set_board_size, connect_net, get_components/get_nets, pin positions, spatial query, describe_component, import/export csv, pin_info...) into pcb plugin panel handlers (pcb/ui/, manifest tools[] executor:\"panel\"); delete the migrated core registrations. Tool names and arg/result shapes UNCHANGED (E2E-P2 parity: existing test_pcb_* suites keep passing against the migrated tools with no caller changes). Repos: Minerva (deletions only) + minerva-plugins. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus. Exit gate: E2E-P2 for wave-1 tools; pin-inspector + panel-layout + workflow-kinds suites green.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:30","updated_at":"2026-07-16T20:32:07","priority":1,"tags":["migration","pcb","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C2 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified). 16 wave-1 tools migrated: minerva-plugins f00b9ea (pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd 560 lines, PCBPanel.handle_tool forward, manifest +16 executor:\"panel\" entries; local, owner push queue) + Minerva 6734075f PUSHED (core module -746 lines, wave-2 intact). Gate E2E-P2: six pcb suites green (pin_inspector 45/0 + panel_tools 87/0 rerouted through the REAL PluginToolRegistry — mechanical, coverage preserved per review; panel_layout 64/0, workflow_kinds 50/0, single_trace 90/0, parity 19/0, apply_route_hints 47/0 unmodified) + new test_pcb_panel_tools_migration.gd 70/0 (per-tool dispatch, shape parity, core disowns names) + shared helper panel_tool_registry_driver.gd. Opus review: approve_with_notes, zero must_fix, byte-identical move verified, fence clean. Notes for C3: add PcbAnnotationHost.get_panel() (dead fallback, defense-in-depth); core helper copies deleted with the module. HITL register entries 1-2 appended (11a5aa34). Durable hints saved by implementer: gdscript coroutine/static-typing landmine, panel-tool test driver. Next: C3."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c4604ba744ca8e5c4b20f3239ed","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C3: pcb migration wave 2 — route workflow tools; delete MCPPcbPanelTools","description":"Migrate the route-workflow half (apply_route_hints incl. the delete-on-commit contract from de244127, get_image, import/export trace geometry, change journal...) into pcb panel handlers, then DELETE MCPPcbPanelTools.gd from core (precedent: broker DCR / MCPPresentationTools). Broker-envelope handling stays plugin-side (pcb-plugin/broker-ipc-double-envelope hint). E2E-P3: full pcb suite sweep green with the core module gone; grep proves zero pcb workflow semantics left in src/Scripts (allowed: generic dispatcher only). Repos: both. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus. Exit gate: E2E-P2 wave-2 parity + E2E-P3.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:35","updated_at":"2026-07-16T20:56:30","priority":1,"tags":["migration","pcb","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C3 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified). MCPPcbPanelTools.gd DELETED from core — Minerva 1af54f18 PUSHED (module+.uid gone, MinervaMCPServer._init_modules removal-only edit, zero production refs remain); minerva-plugins 563d9a3 local (5 wave-2 tools + full route-workflow helper cluster verbatim into panel_tools.gd — _materialize_routes byte-identical incl. delete-on-commit owner contract; manifest 21/21 executor:\"panel\"; PcbAnnotationHost.get_panel() fallback; PCBPanel.handle_tool awaits). Gates: E2E-P2 wave-2 (apply_route_hints 47/0, single_trace 90/0 REAL worker, parity 19/0, panel_tools 87/0) + E2E-P3 (migration gate 68/0: 21-tool registry dispatch, core-file-deleted assert, source-grep with honest 4-file allow-list). Opus review: APPROVE, no must_fix, strictly-more dispatcher coverage than the old direct-module tests. Owner's separation-of-concerns directive is now FACT for pcb: zero pcb workflow semantics in Minerva core. Next: C4 refine loop (panel-side)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c464ff07ded97357c5ce97939d3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C4: Refine loop — bend handles + per-hint revision stack + undo (plugin-side)","description":"Owner workflow-contract v2 core: human draws roughly, both authors refine ONE hint. (1) Bend handles on the canvas: select a hint → drag bend, right-click bend deletes, click segment inserts (endpoint re-tie to different pads per owner answer at pickup). (2) Per-hint revision stack carried on the annotation: every edit (human tool or agent annotations_update/panel tool) pushes a revision; undo/redo from UI (Ctrl+Z with hint selected) and from an agent-facing panel tool (\"undo that\"). Recommendation ratified: per-hint stack now, board-journal unification later under layered-undo DCR 019ea404. (3) Verify/fix annotations_update host-registry parity (the add-path bug family). All new tools land as executor:\"panel\" plugin handlers — zero core accretion. E2E scenario (owner style): human draws sloppy path → LLM snaps to 90°/grid → human undoes one step → human applies → trace matches final revision exactly. HITL gate: owner live session. Repos: minerva-plugins (+ Minerva src/test only). Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:55","updated_at":"2026-07-16T21:32:34","priority":1,"tags":["pcb","route-hints","ux","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C4 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified, HITL deferred to register entries 4-6). Refine loop shipped 100% plugin-side: per-hint revision_stack (top-level envelope field, cap 25, single seam PcbAnnotationHost.update_annotation captures canvas AND MCP edits, stripped from route requests, survives sidecar); undo/redo (host methods + NEW panel tools minerva_pcb_hint_undo/redo + Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Shift+Z gated on selected hint via PCBPanel._unhandled_key_input — LineEdit-safe seam); BendHandleEditTool (drag/insert/delete bends, one revision per gesture, native waypoint icon, cross-surface mutual exclusion; endpoint re-tie deferred to 019f6cb9400e). annotations_update parity: NO BUG (update/delete have no validation gate — WC-2 class can't apply), zero core production changes. Gate: test_pcb_hint_refine_loop.gd 62/0 REAL worker, scenarios A-H incl. real cross-author undo assertions; full pcb sweep green; panel_layout 4→5 fixed. Opus: approve_with_notes, no must_fix. Commits: Minerva 2154305b+03a242ee PUSHED, plugins c2da98de local. Follow-ups filed: 019f6cd32145 (broker request-listener leak), 019f6cd78f0c (revision-stack projection token bloat). Next: C5."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c465fd87cdca1a6aee6a0c03bcf","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C5: Explicit-propose UX + proposal accept/reject lifecycle (plugin-side)","description":"Owner workflow-contract v2: the router NEVER runs implicitly. (1) \"Propose routes\" button in the route-flow cluster (human path) — same underlying panel tool the agent calls; nothing auto-fires. (2) Proposal lifecycle: each cyan proposal individually acceptable/rejectable — accept commits its trace + deletes the consumed hint and the proposal (contract from de244127, migrating plugin-side with C3); reject deletes the proposal; iterate freely. (3) Backend-not-running UX (bug 019f6c1e0399): route actions surface \"backend stopped — start it?\" affordance (+ investigate autostart failure) instead of raw worker_unavailable. E2E: propose→reject leaves zero residue; propose→accept = trace + clean board; no routing without explicit ask. HITL gate: owner live session (can combine with C4's). Repos: minerva-plugins (+ src/test). Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:52:59","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:07:02","priority":1,"tags":["pcb","route-hints","ux","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C5 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified, HITL deferred to register entries 7-9). Explicit-propose shipped: Propose button (non-toggle, native trace_icon_1, one implementation with the MCP tool via handle_tool commit=false); minerva_pcb_proposal_accept/reject panel tools (accept shares _materialize_routes byte-preserved — synthetic single-route input, identical trace geometry proven; deletes consumed hints + proposal; reject deletes proposal only); WorkflowAnnotationList generic per-row Accept/Reject (author.kind==ai AND host-verbs duck-typed — opt-in per host, zero pcb vocabulary, non-pcb docks unchanged 14/0); backend-stopped affordance (human message + structured pcb_backend_stopped w/ recovery_hint; bug 019f6c1e0399 commented — autostart mystery closed: manifest autostart:false is sole cause, by design). Nothing-auto-fires audited + regression-checked. Gate: test_pcb_explicit_propose.gd 64/0 REAL worker; full sweep green; panel_layout 5→6. Opus: approve_with_notes (non-blocking: dual-audience message strings could share a constant). Commits: Minerva 9d1c7aa9+e70ce267 PUSHED, plugins 4fcbb5e local. Findings: router re-routes whole board filed 019f6cf2b5f4 (sev-2, pre-existing). Next: C6 (final round)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c4694227cafb6ccaff4a77a9fb6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"C6: Second-plugin adoption proof — one panel tool in cad or graphics, zero core change","description":"DCR acceptance (d): the executor is substrate, not a pcb special case. Pick one existing scene-panel plugin (cad or graphics) and serve ONE useful panel tool via executor:\"panel\" with ZERO Minerva core changes (E2E-P4: diffstat proves core untouched). Doubles as the substrate's documentation-by-example for plugin authors (update plugin_system_guide.md section). Repos: minerva-plugins only. Process: /work-cycle single round, Sonnet + Opus (or Haiku if truly mechanical after C1-C3 patterns). Exit gate: E2E-P4 green; guide updated.","created_at":"2026-07-16T18:53:12","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:32:38","priority":2,"tags":["plugins","substrate","work-cycle"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c4562067e3a972cf8ecf2b0be4c","resolution":"C6 complete 2026-07-16 (3c auto-verified). E2E-P4 PROVEN: cad plugin serves minerva_cad_view_state via executor:\"panel\" (cad/ui/panel_tools.gd 63 lines mirroring pcb conventions, CADPanel.handle_tool one-line forward, manifest entry; zero new host machinery — surfaces existing camera/viewport state) with ZERO Minerva production diff (reviewer's own diffstat: exactly the two new test files, 231 insertions). test_cad_panel_tool_adoption.gd 20/0 through the REAL registry incl. second-plugin structured errors + ownership decoy. PLUGIN_DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md gains the executor:\"panel\" section — every claim verified against contract §2. Opus: APPROVE. Commits: Minerva bc986f87 PUSHED, plugins c7d4129 local. Pre-existing cad suite anomalies (stale ~/github/plugins mirror paths) confirmed not-regressions, filed separately. THE SUBSTRATE IS GENERIC — DCR acceptance (d) satisfied."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6c85b9d67d26be439f8264fd8dbe","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Pre-existing plugin-suite failures beyond the known baseline (note_plugin_data, data_persistence, hot_reload SIGABRT, presentation_parity) + sweep order-sensitivity","description":"Found during C1 (019f6c45b614) full plugin-suite sweep, verified byte-identical on pristine base 87fcbd65: test_note_plugin_data 2/4, test_plugin_data_persistence 0/2, test_plugin_manager_hot_reload SIGABRT (exit 134), test_presentation_plugin_parity 7/3 — none on the documented known-failure list. Additionally test_chat_providers 51/2 and test_scansort_plugin_smoke 13/3 fail ONLY in back-to-back sweeps (shared user:// state; isolated runs match base green) — CI/verifiers must run suspicious suites isolated before attributing failures. Triage each; some may be environment artifacts (headless, owner session holding ports), the SIGABRT looks real.","created_at":"2026-07-16T20:02:11","updated_at":"2026-07-16T20:02:24","severity":3,"tags":["filed-not-fixed","plugins","pre-existing","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6cb9400e7b33bb4feb2421e634c7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Route-hint endpoint re-tie: move a hint's source/dest to a different pad","description":"Deliberately cut from C4 (rubric: cost+reliability — bend-level editing shipped first; endpoint re-tie means new anchor semantics: semantic pad anchor swap, net re-resolution, detail_level implications). Owner was asked \"bends only, or also re-tie endpoints?\" during contract v2 discussion and hadn't answered by C4 start. Scope when picked up: in the Edit-hint tool, dragging an endpoint handle onto a different pad re-ties source_pins/dest_pins + semantic anchor + revalidates net; a revision is pushed (C4 stack). Panel-side only.","created_at":"2026-07-16T20:58:27","updated_at":"2026-07-16T20:58:27","priority":3,"tags":["follow-up","pcb","route-hints","ux"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c3d0e3d709e85a709abc9f908c0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6cd321457c82b789b2f4d0a617fd","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PluginScenePanelBroker.unregister_panel leaks the panel.request lambda connection","description":"Found during C4 (019f6c464ff0): register_panel attaches _MinervaIPC and connects an anonymous lambda to panel.request; unregister_panel detaches the node but never disconnects the request listener. Re-registering the same panel (e.g. a test driver building twice, or any live re-mount flow) leaves stale IPC surface that collides with later broker wiring. Worked around test-locally in test_pcb_hint_refine_loop.gd (_cleanup_stale_registry_dispatch); core fix = track and disconnect the connection in unregister_panel. Out of C4's fence (core broker); filed per file-don't-fix.","created_at":"2026-07-16T21:26:43","updated_at":"2026-07-16T21:26:54","severity":3,"tags":["broker","filed-not-fixed","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6cd78f0c71dfa480c8d5793cef20","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Private envelope fields: generic projection strip for annotations_list/query (revision-stack token bloat)","description":"C4 review note (a): _project_query_annotation (MCPAnnotationTools.gd:1515) returns full envelopes, so pcb's per-hint revision_stack/redo_stack (cap 25 × payload) bloat every annotations_list/query an agent runs. Constraint: core must NOT name pcb keys (owner directive). Design a GENERIC mechanism — e.g. AnnotationKind declares private_envelope_fields (like workflow_class) OR a \"_\"-prefix convention for envelope-private keys stripped at projection; pcb adopts it for the two history keys. Small round; regression = list/query token size back to pre-C4 for hints with deep stacks, undo tools still work.","created_at":"2026-07-16T21:31:34","updated_at":"2026-07-16T21:31:34","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","follow-up","mcp","substrate"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c3d0e3d709e85a709abc9f908c0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6cf2b5f47d6280d2e173bfd7ab8b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Router re-routes the whole board: board_to_router ignores existing traces, selection only scopes hints","description":"Found during C5 (019f6c465fd8), pre-existing: route_bridge.board_to_router() builds the agent_router Board from components/nets only — never reads the canonical board's traces array — so route_board()/route_board_with_hints() re-routes EVERY net on any apply_route_hints call; hint_ids/selection only scope which hint annotations are consumed. Consequence: a board with net A already manually traced gets a duplicate overlapping trace for A when an unrelated net B is proposed/committed. Fix direction: feed existing traces into the router as routed/keepout state AND/OR scope the engine run to nets implicated by the selected hints. Worker-side (pcb/worker). Nudge hint pcb-plugin/router-reroutes-whole-board.","created_at":"2026-07-16T22:01:13","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:01:42","severity":2,"tags":["pcb","pre-existing","router","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6cf2fc2c768bbc47e2ab3ce90d38","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin autostart = manifest \"autostart\" field only; no remember-last-state","created_at":"2026-07-16T22:01:31","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:01:31","tags":["autostart","lifecycle","plugins"],"value":"PluginManager.start_autostart_plugins (PluginManager.gd:1186) starts only plugins whose manifest top-level \"autostart\" is true (PluginDefinition.gd:386). There is NO \"was running last session\" persistence — PluginDB.load_db forces every record to State.INSTALLED on boot. So a plugin that was running before an app restart will NOT come back unless its manifest says autostart:true. pcb currently ships autostart:false → backend must be started manually (minerva_plugin_start) or via the C5 backend-stopped affordance. Verified against live plugins.json/setup_state.json 2026-07-16.","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"autostart-manifest-only","confidence":"high"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6d0fb1127515920b977aec4bf0d8","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"cad suites reference the stale ~/github/plugins mirror (parse errors, wrong plugins.json entry)","description":"Pre-existing, confirmed during C6 (019f6c469422): cad/ui/kinds/cad_edge_number_kind.gd:91 preloads from res://../../plugins/cad (the STALE checkout memory says never to use); test_cad_evaluate_render.gd fails 3/6 because the shared user://plugins/plugins.json \"cad\" entry points at ~/github/plugins/cad whose subprocess won't start; test_cad_edge_number_kind/tool parse-error at load for the same stale path. Fix: repoint to minerva-plugins (source path + reinstall the cad plugin from the monorepo), sweep for other res://../../plugins references. Nudge key minerva-worktree-tests/test.cad_suites_pre_existing_state has details.","created_at":"2026-07-16T22:32:52","updated_at":"2026-07-16T22:32:52","severity":3,"tags":["cad","pre-existing","stale-paths","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6d2dc767771eb0da1547bb92399d","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"Plugin install/reinstall does not register manifest-declared tools until app restart","description":"Caught LIVE in the panel-tools consolidated HITL (register entry 1): after reinstalling the pcb plugin in a running app, its 21 manifest-declared executor:\"panel\" tools were absent from the server registry (\"Unknown minerva tool\") while the 15 backend-DISCOVERED tools were present (register_backend_tools runs on connection). Root cause: register_plugin_tools(def.id, def.tools) for manifest tools runs ONLY in the app-boot loop (singleton_object.gd:715 \"register tools for already-installed plugins\"); the install/reinstall path updates PluginDB but never (re)registers, and plugin start/restart only triggers backend discovery. Fix: install/update/remove paths must call register_plugin_tools/unregister for the new definition (and refresh tools/list dirty flag). Why proxies missed it: test driver registers manually — the boot-vs-install lifecycle seam was never exercised headless; add a lifecycle-level regression (install after boot → tool resolvable). Workaround: restart the app after any plugin (re)install that changes manifest tools.","created_at":"2026-07-16T23:05:44","updated_at":"2026-07-17T00:49:49","severity":2,"tags":["hitl-caught","lifecycle","mcp","plugins"],"resolution":"Fixed in Minerva f6eefbee (pushed). Full root cause was THREE lifecycle holes, all one class: (1) backend discovery did unregister-all-then-replace, wiping manifest panel tools on every subprocess start; (2) plugin stop unregistered everything though panel tools never need the process; (3) post-boot install/update never registered manifest tools at all. Fix: merge-preserving re-registration — get_panel_tool_entries + _reregister_preserving_panel in PluginToolRegistry; on_plugin_started re-registers when any manifest name is missing (merging discovered extras); PluginManager install/update call the started hook. Regression scenario H (stop-survival, started re-register, empty-discovery churn) 46/0 + definition/migration/broker suites green. Generic — no plugin names in core. Live verification pending owner's next app restart (the one remaining restart of the HITL session)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6d337905767b979afa96becd75c4","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Manifest-declared tools are clobbered by backend discovery + plugin stop","created_at":"2026-07-16T23:11:57","updated_at":"2026-07-16T23:11:57","tags":["lifecycle","mcp","panel-tools","plugins"],"value":"Root-cause chain (HITL-caught 2026-07-16, bug 019f6d2dc767): (1) boot loop registers manifest tools (singleton_object.gd:715); (2) plugin start → on_plugin_started re-registers IF empty (PluginToolRegistry.gd:702, early-return guard); (3) backend discovery register_backend_tools:778 UNREGISTERS the plugin's whole tool set then registers ONLY backend-discovered entries — manifest executor:\"panel\" tools are wiped on every backend start; (4) on_plugin_stopped:695 unregisters everything — panel tools die with the subprocess they don't need; (5) install/reinstall after boot never registers at all (only the boot loop does). register_plugin_tools:123 is atomic REPLACE (internal unregister first). Fix = merge-preserving re-registration: stop keeps panel entries; backend discovery merges panel + discovered; started-guard checks manifest names present (not just non-empty); install path registers. PluginManager reaches the registry via SingletonObject.plugin_tool_registry (PluginManager.gd:775-781).","component":"minerva-plugin-platform","key":"manifest-tools-lifecycle-clobber","confidence":"high"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6d8f465e79ea974e71808ab4a86a","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PluginScenePanelBroker keys panels by manifest panel_name — editor-name lookup misses live panels; multi-tab instances collide","description":"HITL-caught 2026-07-16 (second live catch of the register-burn session): PluginScenePanelHost.instantiate_into registers panels under the MANIFEST panels[].name (\"pcb_panel\"), so the C1 dispatcher's broker-primary get_panel_for_editor(editor_tab_name) always misses live panels (they resolve only via the AnnotationHostRegistry fallback), and get_panel_owner(editor_name) returns \"\" → fail-safe deny fired on a real editor tab (worked around: panels now carry a duck-typed plugin_id property — plugins commit). Deeper issue: keying by panel_name means two open tabs of the same plugin panel COLLIDE in _panel_registry (and the editor_name-keyed blob store comments suggest inconsistent keying). Fix: register under the editor tab name (host knows the editor param) or a composite key; make get_panel_for_editor/get_panel_owner consistent; multi-instance test. Core generic; the C1 spike's \"panel_name IS the editor tab name\" claim came from a stale comment and was wrong for the live mount path.","created_at":"2026-07-17T00:52:14","updated_at":"2026-07-17T00:52:14","severity":2,"tags":["broker","hitl-caught","panel-tools","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6ea4e52a7ea2b705b1a8cb7cd8bf","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb_get_image returns inline base64 only — bloats agent context; add file/blob-handle mode (lookup how native solved it)","description":"Live agent-ergonomics finding (hinting-lab session 2026-07-16): a clean-context Fable routing agent called minerva_pcb_get_image and got ~150KB of inline base64 PNG in its tool result — one call bloated its context and stalled it in a multi-minute inference before it authored a single hint. The tool needs a context-friendly mode: save_to_path arg (write PNG to a caller-named file, return the path + dimensions) and/or a blob-handle return (the scene-panel broker already has an editor-keyed blob store with handle/refcount machinery — PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:217 — built for exactly this shape).\n\nOWNER NOTE — INVESTIGATE FIRST: \"we hit this exact issue before in the native PCB Editor.\" Part of this bug is archaeology: look up how the NATIVE editor solved image delivery to LLMs before the 2026-07-07 cutover (tag pre-cutover-2026-07-07 — check MCPPCBTools.gd / the old get_image path and any file-or-handle mode it grew, plus related commits/docket items) and mirror that solution rather than reinventing. Related substrate memory: LLMs always get screenshots even from rich note kinds (dual-representation constraint) — whatever native did likely respected that.\n\nPanel-side fix (pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd _get_image); the tool schema change rides the manifest. Also audit the other image-returning pcb/cad tools for the same pattern while in there.","created_at":"2026-07-17T05:55:28","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:24:45","severity":3,"tags":["agent-ergonomics","archaeology","hitl-caught","mcp","pcb"],"resolution":"Fixed in minerva-plugins 8380865 + Minerva tests 14879dcf (needs pcb plugin REINSTALL to go live — manifest schema changed; works without app restart thanks to f6eefbee). Archaeology verdict: native NEVER fixed this (pre-cutover _pcb_get_image always returned inline base64); the owner's recollection was the render_overlay R5 fix (4b74971c, output_path mode) — mirrored here as an OPTIONAL save_to_path: validated up front (absolute + parent exists → structured error), img.save_png, returns {saved_to, format, width, height, byte_size, metadata} with NO image_data; headless null-envelope preserved both modes; default mode byte-identical. Manifest description steers agents to save_to_path. Opus approve_with_notes (non-blocking: PNG double-encode for byte_size). test_pcb_panel_tools 98/0 (+12 checks)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6edff9cc77519d85c9f3b91f9e86","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":".minpcb share bundle — board + annotations sidecar as one transfer artifact","description":"Owner-ratified via 7-axis rubric 2026-07-17 (won reliability+durability outright; only concedes cost/inspection): sharing a board between two Minerva instances today requires transferring .pcbskel + .pcbskel.annotations.json — forgetting the sidecar silently loses ALL co-working state (hints, proposals, revision history). Design: a .minpcb container (reuse .minproj packaging machinery) bundling board + sidecar (+ library lock reference for DRC parity); \"Export bundle…\" enumerates contents (\"includes 12 hints, 3 proposals, history\"); open path unpacks and re-pairs. Loose files remain the WORKING format; bundle is the TRANSFER format. Explicitly rejected: embed-on-export (pollutes the canonical board contract — the same reasoning as the core/plugin separation) and sync-only (silent loss on ad-hoc sharing). Archaeology note: native embedded route_hints in the board save envelope (PCBData.to_dict, pre-cutover tag); the cutover deliberately excluded them from board-yaml — the bundle preserves that separation while restoring one-file sharing.","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:00:00","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:00:00","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","design","pcb","sharing"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6ef113357a9b9691a11151f85068","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"EditorPane.add_plugin_scene_editor anonymous branch skips editor_name_to_use dedup — tab-name collisions possible","description":"Found during the annotations_add fix (U1, hitl-fixes round): the anonymous-create branch (file == null, EditorPane.gd:305-322) never calls editor_name_to_use(), unlike every other tab-creation path — a caller-supplied tab_title can collide with an existing tab's name, which then aliases every editor_name-keyed surface (annotation hosts, panel broker blob store, panel tools dispatch). MCPGeneralTools.gd:178-183 already documents the same-name race for the anonymous paired_dsl create path. Fix: route the anonymous branch through the same dedup; regression: create two anonymous editors with identical tab_title → distinct editor names.","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:18:40","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:18:40","severity":3,"tags":["editors","filed-not-fixed","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6ef1232175ea9ad8d4892685ecae","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Fast-follow: Editor.gd deregister call sites pass expected_host (identity-checked AnnotationHostRegistry.deregister)","description":"U1 (hitl-fixes) added an optional identity-checked expected_host param to AnnotationHostRegistry.deregister so a LOSING host's tab-close can't wipe the WINNING host's live entry under the new panel-host-priority collision policy. Editor.gd's two call sites (lines ~147, ~700) don't pass it yet (out of U1's fence) — until they do, closing a displaced text-buffer tab still unconditionally erases the registry entry for that editor name, stranding the panel host unlisted. Small change + one regression check (close losing tab → winner still resolvable).","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:18:44","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:18:44","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","editors","fast-follow"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6efbf50374c88e2bea411827d5ac","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"annotations_add: agents MUST include schema_version/lifecycle/visible_in_views/summary","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:30:33","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:30:33","tags":["agent-ergonomics","annotations","gotcha","mcp"],"value":"minerva_annotations_add stamps id/created_at/author but NOT the other required v2 envelope fields — the host rejects (now loudly, post-b6aa6f54) unless the agent includes: \"schema_version\": 2, \"lifecycle\": \"open\", \"visible_in_views\": [\"all\"], \"summary\": \"<one-liner>\". This (not anchor compat, not host resolution) was the actual cause of the live hinting-lab add failures (C74/C21 era). Working full envelope example: kind + those four + view_context \"pcb\" + primitives [] + anchor {type:\"pad\", plugin:\"pcb\", id:{component,pin}, snapshot:{position}} + kind_payload {hint_type, layer, detail_level, source_pins, dest_pins, waypoints, width_mm, text}. Also still omit created_at/updated_at (v1 string vs unix-int clash). Ergonomics fix filed: MCP layer should stamp these defaults like it stamps id/created_at.","component":"annotations-mcp","key":"add-requires-v2-envelope-fields","confidence":"high"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6efc44387d22b6c3b508c636ca6a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"annotations_add should stamp v2 envelope defaults (schema_version/lifecycle/visible_in_views/summary) like it stamps id/created_at","description":"Live-diagnosed 2026-07-17: the real cause of every agent-side add failure this session was four required v2 envelope fields the MCP layer neither documents nor defaults — schema_version (2), lifecycle (\"open\"), visible_in_views ([\"all\"]), summary. The tool already stamps id/created_at/author; stamping these too (summary defaultable from the kind's summary() dispatch or \"\") makes the minimal agent envelope just {kind, anchor?, kind_payload} — the ergonomics the north star wants. Also update the tool description with the full minimal envelope. Regression: bare-minimum envelope adds successfully and round-trips list/sidecar. Complements the host_rejected error path (b6aa6f54) which stays as the backstop.","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:30:54","updated_at":"2026-07-17T07:30:54","priority":2,"tags":["agent-ergonomics","annotations","mcp"],"parent":"minerva:019f6c3d0e3d709e85a709abc9f908c0"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6f1492e077c88799edf507634098","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Propose-integrated DRC: apply_route_hints returns violations per proposal (board + hinted routes)","description":"Owner ask (hinting-lab session 2026-07-17): run DRC automatically after an agent routes, over real traces + proposed routes — the review gate should carry rule-check results, not just geometry. Natural seam: the propose step (apply_route_hints commit=false) — the worker already holds board + synthesized routes in one call; extend the route request to run the existing DRC engine (pcb_pcb_drc machinery) over board∪proposals and attach per-proposal violations to the write-back envelope (kind_payload.drc: {violations:[...], clean: bool}). Dock rows badge violating proposals (user-visible); accept stays allowed (human judgment wins) but informed. Live motivator: the engine free-routed GND (26 wp) + VCC_3V3 on F.Cu with zero vias straight through the F.Cu signal lanes — visually undetectable pre-fix, exactly what DRC-at-propose would have flagged. Interim agent workaround: materialize to a scratch board copy + pcb_pcb_drc manually. Worker + plugin side; no core.","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:57:27","updated_at":"2026-07-17T08:14:41","priority":2,"tags":["agent-ergonomics","drc","pcb","route-hints"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a27154ae97906ed80d98a6"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6f159ad071d29515cd07548a0ffd","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"CORRECTED: panel .gd changes need APP RESTART; save/close/reload only preserves annotations + bounces worker","created_at":"2026-07-17T07:58:34","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:38","tags":["deploy","gotcha","pcb","plugins"],"value":"CORRECTION (2026-07-17, bug 019f6f42e156): the save→close→plugin_reload→reopen sequence preserves annotations via the sidecar and restarts the Python worker (fresh .py code), but does NOT deploy panel GDScript changes — minerva_plugin_reload never invokes the existing _hot_reload_gd machinery (PluginManager.gd:1401), and tab close/reopen does not reliably evict Godot's script cache (dock/registry/kind refs retain old parses). Deploy matrix: worker .py → plugin restart suffices; panel/kind .gd or .tscn → APP RESTART required (until the reload bug is fixed); manifest → plugin reinstall (registration works live post-f6eefbee). Sidecar-save first in all cases to preserve annotations.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"hot-deploy-preserving-annotations","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f6f42e1567cbeb0c5e90eb8fe3fd6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"minerva_plugin_reload never hot-reloads panel GDScript — only restarts the subprocess (machinery exists, unwired)","description":"HITL-caught 2026-07-17 (DRC deploy): after merging panel_tools.gd/PCBPanel.gd changes to the plugin dev dir (data_directory-referenced, no copy), minerva_plugin_reload ×2 (with panel closed AND with panel live) only ever logged \"Restarting plugin\" (subprocess stop+start) — never touched the GDScript cache, so the in-process panel kept executing pre-merge code (fresh worker emitted drc; live write-back dropped it; proposals carried no kind_payload.drc). The hot-reload machinery EXISTS (PluginManager._hot_reload_gd :1401, GDScript.reload(true) in-place + _on_hot_reload dispatch, live-panels-aware) but the reload tool path doesn't invoke it. Session evidence that tab close/reopen does NOT reliably evict script cache either (dock/registry/kind refs retain). Fix: minerva_plugin_reload should run _hot_reload_gd over the plugin's .gd files (and _hot_reload_tscn) in addition to the subprocess bounce; report which scripts reloaded in the tool result. Until fixed: panel GDScript deploys require an APP RESTART; only worker-side .py changes deploy via plugin restart (fresh subprocess). Corrects the earlier hot-deploy hint (pcb-plugin/hot-deploy-preserving-annotations) — that sequence preserves annotations but does NOT deploy panel script changes.","created_at":"2026-07-17T08:48:01","updated_at":"2026-07-17T08:48:01","severity":2,"tags":["deploy","hitl-caught","hot-reload","plugins"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f706834927cd994825ccf63d61379","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"DISCUSSION: how should DRC / collisions relate to route-hint and proposal annotations?","description":"Opened 2026-07-17 from a live HITL session. Consultant input wanted — this is a design question, not a bug report.\n\n=== 1. BACKGROUND: what we're building ===\n\nMinerva's pcb plugin does human/agent CO-DESIGN of board routing. The loop the owner specified:\n\n- A human (or an agent) draws a ROUTE HINT — an intended path for a net. Hints are annotations on the v2 substrate (kind `pcb_route_hint`, workflow_class), carrying: source/dest pin refs, interior waypoints, layer, width, and a detail_level. `detailed` hints route AS DRAWN (the human is dodging obstacles the engine can't see); `guided` hints are soft corridors the engine may deviate from.\n- Either party refines the SAME hint: the human drags bend handles on canvas; the agent edits waypoints over MCP (\"snap these to 90°\"). Every edit pushes onto a per-hint revision stack; undo/redo works across both authors.\n- PROPOSE is an explicit act (button or MCP tool — the router NEVER fires implicitly). The Go plugin binary forwards to a Python worker wrapping the agent_router engine, which synthesizes ROUTES from the hints and returns them. The plugin writes each route back as a cyan AI-authored annotation linked to its source hint via `kind_payload.proposal_for`.\n- ACCEPT (per proposal) materializes that route as real copper in the board model and deletes both the proposal and its consumed hint. REJECT deletes just the proposal; the hint stays open for iteration. Human judgment always wins.\n- DRC-AT-PROPOSE (built this session, docket 019f6f1492e0): the worker runs the EXISTING drc.py engine over board ∪ all synthesized routes, and each proposal comes back carrying `kind_payload.drc = {clean: bool, violations: [{type, at:[x,y], nets:[...], layer}]}`, plus a board-level `drc_summary`. The dock badges violating rows \"⚠ N\"; selecting a row rings each violation's `at` coordinate on canvas. Accept is never blocked — DRC informs.\n- SUPERSESSION (built this session): a hint answered by a live proposal loses its dock row and canvas draw (the proposal carries the same geometry plus the verdict). Reject un-supersedes.\n\n=== 2. THE GAP: what we tried and what broke ===\n\nLIVE SCENARIO: a 16-net board (smart-remote clone, all traces stripped, 10 components incl. a 44-pin ESP32 module). 16 hints authored (1 by the session agent, 15 by a clean-context Opus routing agent that planned layer assignment and corridors). One propose → 16 proposals, drc_summary 9 violations, 10 flagged / 6 clean. The flags independently confirmed collisions the owner had already spotted by eye (BTN1/2/3 into the SW pads) — so the check itself WORKS and is valuable.\n\nThe gaps are all about SCOPE and LIFETIME of the verdict:\n\n(a) DRC IS PROPOSE-SCOPED, NOT DRAFT-SCOPED. The verdict is a snapshot computed at propose time against the FULL proposal set. Nothing recomputes it. The moment the review loop starts — the loop the whole feature exists for — the snapshot describes a board that no longer exists.\n\n(b) ACCEPT/REJECT SILENTLY STALES THE SURVIVORS. Reject GND (5 violations), and BTN1's \"⚠ 1\" may name a collision with a route that no longer exists. The badge doesn't move, doesn't gray out, doesn't warn. A reviewer trusts a stale verdict.\n\n(c) THERE IS NO RE-CHECK PATH. The standalone `pcb_drc` backend tool checks a board's REAL traces — zero on a fresh board — so it reports \"clean\" and tells you nothing about your 16 drafts. The only DRC-over-drafts code path lives inside propose, and re-proposing RE-ROUTES (it doesn't re-verify) and STACKS a second set of proposals rather than replacing the first. To re-check the current draft set today, an agent must hand-materialize proposals into a scratch board copy and call pcb_drc on it.\n\n(d) PROPOSE ACCUMULATES. Two proposes = two proposals per hint, doubled geometry on canvas. There's no idempotence.\n\n(e) RELATED, ALREADY FILED: the router ignores existing traces and re-routes EVERY net on any propose (019f6cf2b5f4) — so even a \"re-check just this net\" scoping story has no engine support today. And only the FIRST detailed hint per net steers: chained pin-pair hints for multi-pin nets are silently skipped (found live on 3-pin I2S nets).\n\n(f) ATTRIBUTION IS NET-LEVEL. A finding between two pre-existing traces of a net can flag that net's proposal (accepted at review time as informs-not-blocks, but it muddies the badge's meaning).\n\nROOT TENSION: drafts live in the ANNOTATION layer; DRC operates on BOARD TRACES. Every draft-scoped check therefore requires synthesizing a hypothetical board. That synthesis exists in exactly one place (inside propose) and is welded to routing.\n\n=== 3. OPTIONS ON THE TABLE ===\n\nA. DRAFT-SCOPED DRC RE-CHECK TOOL. New panel-executed MCP tool: take the LIVE board (real traces, however many accepted) ∪ surviving proposals, run DRC, re-badge in place. No routing, no new annotations. Auto-fire after each accept/reject (and on demand for agents). Cheap — the worker's DRC entry point already exists; it needs a caller that feeds it drafts instead of routing them. Makes the iterate loop honest.\n\nB. STALENESS MARKING (interim, tiny). Any accept/reject/hint-edit flips surviving badges to \"stale\" (⚠ ?) until re-checked. Zero compute, no lying. Weak alone; strong as a safety net under A.\n\nC. IDEMPOTENT PROPOSE. Propose supersedes/replaces prior proposals for the same hints instead of stacking. Removes the manual \"reject all 16 first\" ritual and makes re-propose a legitimate (if heavy) re-check.\n\nD. CONTINUOUS DRC. Debounced re-check on every board/annotation change. Best \"live\" feel; most compute; needs the router-scoping fix (e) to avoid whole-board churn.\n\nE. COMMIT-TIME DRC ONLY. Check at accept; warn/block there. Simplest, but arrives after review — the reviewer wanted the verdict while comparing drafts.\n\nF. DOCUMENT SNAPSHOT SEMANTICS. Label badges \"as of propose\" and accept the staleness. Cheapest; contradicts the owner's standing rule that silent wrongness is unacceptable.\n\nDEEPER ARCHITECTURAL QUESTION FOR CONSULTANTS: should proposals be ANNOTATIONS at all? They are, today, because the annotation substrate gave us persistence, authorship, MCP surfaces, and dock UI for free — and hints genuinely ARE annotations (intent about a document). But a PROPOSAL is arguably draft COPPER: geometry the board model could hold natively as traces with a `draft: true` flag (excluded from export/gerbers, included in DRC). Then DRC \"just works\" with no synthesis, accept becomes a flag flip, and the propose→check→accept loop stops needing a parallel geometry universe. Cost: draft state leaves the annotation layer (losing free authorship/undo/MCP-read plumbing), the board file grows a concept that only matters mid-design, and the sharing story (board + sidecar, see 019f6edff9cc) shifts. Is the annotation layer the right home for machine-generated candidate geometry, or did we reach for it because it was there?\n\nINITIAL RECOMMENDATION (author's, weakly held): A + C, with B as the immediate patch. But the architectural question above may reframe all of it.\n\nRelated: 019f6f1492e0 (DRC-at-propose, implemented), 019f6cf2b5f4 (router re-routes whole board, sev-2), 019f6edff9cc (.minpcb share bundle), PCB umbrella 019f6a69b8a2, panel-tools DCR 019f6c3d0e3d.","created_at":"2026-07-17T14:08:25","updated_at":"2026-07-17T14:58:54","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","architecture","consultant-input","drc","pcb","route-hints"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7095c39578c1a374081463115a67","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"PcbRoutingWorkspace — separate route candidates from annotations (plugin-owned)","description":"STATUS: PARKED at filing (2026-07-17). Sequencing decision (owner, 2026-07-17): implement REAL VIAS first (they solve several of the live collisions and are needed regardless), THEN pick up this DCR. The via work also completes this DCR's Stage-1 correctness fix as a byproduct (lossless propose→accept). This item captures the north-star architecture now, while it is fresh from consult, so the via work has a target to converge toward.\n\nORIGIN. Consultant review (Codex) on discussion 019f70683492, comments #532/#533 (2026-07-17). The discussion asked \"should proposals be annotations at all?\" — this DCR is the answer: NO. Route HINTS fit the annotation system (authored intent — semantic anchors, authorship, MCP read/edit, overlay render, sidecar persistence, shared human/agent revision history). Generated route PROPOSALS do not — a proposal is executable candidate copper: exact geometry with topology, per-segment layers, vias, dependencies on the board and the rest of the active candidate set, and set-scoped validation. We reached for the annotation layer because it was there; the seams below are where the mismatch leaks.\n\nCODE FINDINGS (verified in-repo 2026-07-17, all in pcb/ plugin — NONE require core change; consistent with the \"Minerva must not know PCB workflows\" north star of DCR 019f6c3d0e3d):\n1. LOSSY PROPOSE→ACCEPT (immediate correctness bug). pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd _write_back_proposals runs the router result through _route_polyline (flattens ALL segments to one waypoint polyline) and _route_layer (keeps only the FIRST segment's layer) and never reads route.vias. _proposal_accept (:1143) then rebuilds every segment on that single layer with vias:[]. A multi-layer proposal with vias COMMITS AS SINGLE-LAYER COPPER WITH NO VIAS — different copper than the router produced and DRC checked. NB: the direct-apply path _materialize_routes groups by layer and DOES add vias correctly; only the propose/accept round-trip is lossy — and that is the path the owner uses in the lab.\n2. DRC IS SET-SCOPED, ANNOTATIONS ARE ITEM-SCOPED. The verdict is a function of real board ∪ the complete active candidate set; accepting/rejecting/editing one candidate invalidates survivor verdicts. (= discussion gap (a)/(b).)\n3. ANNOTATION STALENESS ≠ ROUTING STALENESS. Proposal annotations use board.point anchors that never go stale when copper/components/candidates change; annotation \"stale\" means a broken anchor, not an outdated derived DRC result.\n4. AI AUTHORSHIP IS ACTING AS A PROPOSAL DISCRIMINATOR. WorkflowAnnotationList offers Accept/Reject to ANY AI-authored workflow annotation the host exposes verbs for — including agent-authored SOURCE hints. Proposal-ness must be explicit, not inferred from authorship.\n5. SUPERSESSION HIDES THE SOURCE HINT whenever a proposal links to it — losing the intent/output comparison for guided hints, where the router is explicitly allowed to deviate.\n6. ACCEPT SPANS TWO STORES WITHOUT A TRANSACTION. PCBData history snapshots components/nets/traces but not vias or annotations; undo cannot faithfully restore a committed route plus consumed hint/proposal state.\n7. ROUTER IS BLIND TO EXISTING COPPER. route_bridge.board_to_router builds router state from components/nets/mounting-holes only — it never reads board.traces or vias. Selective \"keep the clean routes, reroute just the collisions\" is impossible until existing copper + explicit route-task scope enter the router model (already filed 019f6cf2b5f4).\n\nTARGET DOMAIN MODEL (plugin-owned, sits beside PCBData + PcbAnnotationHost):\n- RouteHint (annotation, unchanged): intent/constraint — endpoints, net, preferred layer, width, corridor, detail_level, human instruction. STAYS in the annotation substrate.\n- RouteCandidate (UI: \"ghost trace\"): exact proposed implementation — stable candidate/task/net/endpoint IDs; exact segments each with stable ID + layer_id + width + ordered points + lock state; exact vias each with stable ID + position + from/to layers + dims + lock state; source hint IDs; generation + base-board revision + candidate revision. Two ORTHOGONAL axes: disposition {proposed,pinned,superseded,rejected,committed} × validation {unchecked,checking,clean,violating,stale,error}.\n- Real trace: committed authoritative copper in PCBData.\n- DRC finding: derived evidence tied to board-revision + workspace-generation, attributed to candidate/segment/via IDs (not net-level).\n\nNEW FILES (plugin): pcb/ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd, pcb_route_candidate.gd, pcb_route_task.gd. PCBPanel.gd owns _routing_workspace beside _data + _annotation_host and coordinates selection context, worker calls, persistence, composite transactions. pcb_canvas.gd renders/hit-tests exact candidate segments+vias through the REAL geometry path with ghost styling (real layer color, reduced alpha; same-layer overlaps self-highlight by opacity accumulation; express selection/pin/stale/violation via outline/dash/marker, never by recoloring). panel_tools.gd exposes workspace tools (propose, list/get-active, pin, commit, reject, reroute-route, reroute-span, check) — _write_back_proposals becomes workspace.ingest_routing_result; accept becomes a composite panel transaction. PcbAnnotationHost + pcb_route_hint_kind return to intent/commentary ONLY (remove proposal storage, supersession, accept/reject from the annotation path). Worker splits route-generation from draft-DRC; route_bridge imports existing traces/vias + same-net connectivity + explicit RouteTask/span scope + fixed/pinned candidates.\n\nVOCABULARY (owner-facing): Accept/Commit = ghost→real copper. Keep/Pin = ghost stays draft but future routing routes around it. Reject = discard candidate, reopen task. Try again = replace candidate with a new generation (never stacks). Edit = directly modify exact draft geometry and pin it. Geometry VERTEX (\"trace goes exactly here\", modifies geometry) is distinct from ROUTING GATE (\"regenerate this span through here\", steers the router). Reroute-Span replaces only a selected interval (candidate-revision guarded so a concurrent edit makes the worker reply stale rather than clobbering newer geometry).\n\nPERSISTENCE. Do NOT add draft:true to canonical/exported traces. Persist the workspace SEPARATELY (e.g. board.pcbskel.routing.json, or inside the planned .minpcb share bundle 019f6edff9cc): crash-durable, excluded from Gerber/KiCad output. Loading against a different board revision marks all candidates stale until rebased+checked. This preserves the two-file share story.\n\nMIGRATION STAGES (Codex's, adopted):\nS1. Immediate correctness — make current proposal storage LOSSLESS (full segments+layers+vias); add explicit proposal identity + generation + base-board revision + idempotent replacement + stale-marking + draft-scoped DRC. (← delivered by the VIA work that precedes this DCR.)\nS2. Introduce PcbRoutingWorkspace behind the current UI.\nS3. Move candidate rendering/selection to pcb_canvas.\nS4. Switch Accept/Reject/MCP tools to workspace candidates.\nS5. Remove proposal annotations + supersession special cases.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO SHIPPED WORK. This partially UNWINDS the proposal-as-annotation surface built under DCR 019f6c3d0e3d (proposal_for, supersession, dock Accept/Reject). That was not wasted — it proved the panel-executed-tool substrate end to end, and HINTS stay annotations permanently — but the proposal side was the wrong home and S4/S5 correct it.\n\nFOLDS IN (resolved by this model, not patched separately): 019f6cf2b5f4 (router re-routes whole board, sev-2), 019f6cb9400e (endpoint re-tie), annotation-duplication-in-review (the \"collision annotation\" issue). Related: discussion 019f70683492, panel-tools DCR 019f6c3d0e3d, DRC-at-propose 019f6f1492e0, .minpcb bundle 019f6edff9cc, PCB umbrella 019f6a69b8a2.","created_at":"2026-07-17T14:58:10","updated_at":"2026-07-18T18:43:40","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","architecture","drc","pcb","plugins","routing","substrate"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f709de47e778fa6402c6b3e19343c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"PCB real vias — engine-auto + manual authoring + lossless carry","description":"Owner sequencing (2026-07-17): implement REAL VIAS before the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR (019f7095c395) — vias resolve several of the live collisions the owner saw and are needed regardless. Owner chose \"both\" (engine-auto + manual authoring) after reviewing the via representation.\n\nFOUNDATION FINDING (verified 2026-07-17): the via stack is already ~80% built. The A* engine routes on two layers and inserts vias by default (agent_router/router.py route(allow_vias=True, single_layer=False); per-layer grid; pathfinder _try_via_path). The worker carries per-segment layers + vias out (methods.py route() :394-406). DRC is ALREADY layer-and-via aware: drc._check_crossings skips segment pairs on different layers (s1.layer != s2.layer -> continue), and the type-D layer_change_no_via check REQUIRES a via wherever a net hands off between layers. The board model stores vias (pcb_data.gd) and the canvas renders them (pcb_canvas.gd _draw_traces via loop). Vias genuinely clear collisions in the DRC verdict TODAY — if the geometry carries them.\n\nTWO PLACES VIAS DIE (the paths the owner actually uses):\n1. Propose->accept: panel_tools _write_back_proposals flattens the route to a single-layer polyline and drops route.vias; _proposal_accept rebuilds single-layer, vias:[]. Accepting a via-using route re-introduces the collision the via solved. (= DCR Stage-1 / finding #1.)\n2. Detailed as-drawn hints: route_bridge.materialize_detailed_hints hardcodes vias:[] on a single layer and never invokes the multi-layer A*. This is why the owner's 15-hint (all-detailed) board came back all-F.Cu and colliding.\n\nVIA REPRESENTATION (three shapes must agree):\n- Canonical .pcbskel / board-dict (plugin r/w): vias:[{x_mm,y_mm,drill_mm,diameter_mm,net}] — NO first-class layer-span field; the two layers ride as an opaque \"layers\" Extra passthrough that _materialize_routes hardcodes [\"F.Cu\",\"B.Cu\"].\n- Router YAML (worker in): vias:[{x_mm,y_mm,drill_mm,diameter_mm,net,from_layer,to_layer}] (top/bottom naming).\n- KiCad export (kicad_io.Via): (via (at x y)(size)(drill)(layers \"F.Cu\" \"B.Cu\")(net)).\nThe canonical via needs explicit from_layer/to_layer threaded model->serialize->worker->DRC->export, with top/bottom vs F.Cu/B.Cu naming reconciled. Harmless on 2-layer today (via always spans F<->B) but required for manual authoring + multilayer.\n\nFOUR UNITS (children):\nU1 Canonical via schema (foundation): explicit from_layer/to_layer on canonical via; thread through pcb_data.gd model+serialize/deserialize (_via_to_board_dict/_vias_from_board_list), worker route_bridge/methods, DRC, kicad_io; reconcile naming.\nU2 Lossless propose->accept (mandatory, DCR Stage-1): proposal carries full per-segment layers + vias; accept commits exact geometry. Fix _write_back_proposals (_route_polyline/_route_layer flattening) + _proposal_accept.\nU3 Engine-auto vias reach the board: stop materialize_detailed_hints hardcoding vias:[]; let a hint opt into multi-layer so the engine inserts vias to clear collisions; carry through DRC->accept.\nU4 Manual via authoring: canvas Add-Via tool + hint/route representation for \"via here, switch layer,\" honoring U1's schema. NB overlaps the workspace DCR's CandidateViaTool — accepted rework cost; U1-U3 carry forward unchanged.\n\nALL work is in the pcb plugin (~/github/minerva-plugins, branch main). Zero Minerva core change (consistent with the \"Minerva must not know PCB workflows\" north star). Kick off U1+U2 first (foundation + mandatory); U3+U4 follow.\n\nRelated: DCR 019f7095c395 (PcbRoutingWorkspace, this is its Stage-1 pre-work), discussion 019f70683492, DRC-at-propose 019f6f1492e0, PCB umbrella 019f6a69b8a2.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:07:03","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:56:19","priority":2,"tags":["drc","pcb","plugins","routing","vias"],"resolution":"Demonstrable via goal MET: U1 (via schema) + U2 (lossless propose→accept) + U4 (manual Add-Via) shipped minerva-plugins e768280; F1 (undo orphan vias) fixed 820248a. Owner shelved further interim via tooling and pivoted to the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR 019f7095c395. U3 (engine-auto vias) + U5 (kicad production export) re-parented to PCB umbrella 019f6a69b8a2 as deferred follow-ons. Codex review's residual correctness (F2/F3/F4) carried into the DCR via GATE 019f70f76c2f. Closing clears this umbrella's `blocks` on the DCR — the vias-before-DCR sequencing is satisfied."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f709e32d37a1a993a50e3613a95ed","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"U1: Canonical via schema — explicit from_layer/to_layer threaded end-to-end","description":"FOUNDATION unit. Today the canonical via (pcb_data.gd _via_to_board_dict) emits only {x_mm,y_mm,drill_mm,diameter_mm,net}; the two layers ride as an opaque \"layers\" Extra passthrough that _materialize_routes hardcodes [\"F.Cu\",\"B.Cu\"]. Router YAML uses from_layer/to_layer (top/bottom); kicad_io.Via uses a (F.Cu,B.Cu) tuple. Make the canonical via carry explicit from_layer/to_layer.\n\nTOUCH (fence): pcb/ui/model/pcb_data.gd (_via_to_board_dict, _vias_from_board_list, _load_vias, serialize/from_board_dict via paths), pcb/worker/pcb_worker/route_bridge.py (via emit + _routes_to_vias in methods), pcb/worker/pcb_worker/methods.py (route() via output already carries layers via engine — ensure from/to layer present), pcb/worker/pcb_worker/drc.py (_harvest_vias — vias currently positional-only; if layer-span becomes load-bearing for type-D check, carry it), pcb/worker/agent_router/kicad_io.py (Via.layers). Tests in pcb/worker/tests + any pcb/tests gd suites for pcb_data serialization round-trip.\n\nACCEPTANCE: (1) canonical via round-trips from_layer/to_layer through serialize->deserialize and board_dict<->model without loss; (2) naming reconciled — one canonical naming (recommend top/bottom in canonical to match YAML + component.layer, mapping to F.Cu/B.Cu only at the KiCad/engine boundary via existing _LAYER_MAP); (3) a 2-layer via defaults sensibly (from top to bottom) so existing boards still load; (4) worker route output + DRC harvest carry the span; (5) round-trip test green. NO behavior change to routing yet — this is pure schema plumbing that U2/U3/U4 build on.\n\nParent 019f709de47e. Model: Sonnet implement, Opus review.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:07:23","updated_at":"2026-07-17T15:26:57","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugins","schema","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f709de47e","resolution":"Committed wc/pcb-via 3a71bef. Canonical via gains first-class from_layer/to_layer (top/bottom), threaded through pcb_data.gd model + worker (methods/_routes_to_vias, drc harvest tolerance) + agent_router/kicad_io.py (Via.from_canonical). Naming reconciled to one mapper. Cold Opus review: approve. Tests: worker pytest 272/3-skip, gd test_pcb_panel_model 40/0, owner's live session untouched. Follow-up U5 (019f70af5736) filed for the production pcb_worker/kicad.py export hardcode (harmless on 2-layer). Auto-verified (schema plumbing, no owner HITL); U2 end-to-end via behavior carries the owner test."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f709e62127ae482d2b7d6839f4383","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"U2: Lossless propose→accept — carry per-segment layers + vias (DCR Stage-1)","description":"MANDATORY correctness fix (finding #1 on discussion 019f70683492 / DCR 019f7095c395 Stage-1). panel_tools.gd _write_back_proposals runs the router route through _route_polyline (flattens ALL segments to one waypoint polyline) + _route_layer (keeps only the FIRST segment's layer) and never reads route.vias; _proposal_accept (:1143) then rebuilds every segment on that single layer with vias:[]. A multi-layer proposal with vias COMMITS AS SINGLE-LAYER, NO-VIA copper — re-introducing the collision the via solved. NB the direct-apply path _materialize_routes already groups by layer + adds vias correctly; only the two-step propose/accept path is lossy.\n\nTOUCH (fence): pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd (_write_back_proposals, _proposal_accept, _route_polyline/_route_layer helpers), pcb/ui/kinds/pcb_route_hint_kind.gd + pcb/ui/PcbAnnotationHost.gd IF the proposal kind_payload must grow a per-segment/via field to carry exact geometry (it must — waypoints alone can't express layer changes + vias). Uses U1's via schema. Tests: pcb/tests gd (test_pcb_explicit_propose, test_pcb_single_trace_tool) + pcb/worker if needed.\n\nACCEPTANCE: (1) a proposal for a multi-layer route stores per-segment layers + vias (not a flattened single-layer polyline); (2) accept commits the EXACT geometry the router produced + DRC checked — segments on their real layers, vias present; (3) FUNCTIONAL FLOOR: boot the real panel stack headless, route a board that needs a via (two crossing nets), propose, accept, assert the committed board has the via + both layers + DRC clean; (4) existing propose/accept suites green (counts may shift — update to the lossless contract). Depends on U1.\n\nParent 019f709de47e. Model: Sonnet implement, Opus review.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:07:35","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:55:36","priority":2,"tags":["correctness","pcb","plugins","routing","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f709de47e","resolution":"Shipped: minerva-plugins e768280 (lossless propose→accept) + tests. Cold Opus approve; apply_route_hints 92/0, explicit_propose 69/0 (real_worker). Live HITL WAIVED — owner pivoted to the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR 2026-07-17 (\"going to the DCR, which would invalidate the HITL here anyway\"). Residual correctness (F2/F3/F4 from Codex review) deferred to DCR via GATE 019f70f76c2f INV-2/3/4. F1 (orphan vias on undo) fixed 820248a."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f709e9dbd7bedb78c4e5d131c9224","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"U3: Engine-auto vias reach the board — multi-layer opt-in for hints","description":"route_bridge.materialize_detailed_hints hardcodes vias:[] on a single layer and never invokes the multi-layer A* — so every detailed as-drawn hint (the owner's whole 15-hint board) bypasses via logic and comes back single-layer + colliding. Give a hint a way to opt into engine multi-layer routing so the A* (already allow_vias=True) inserts vias to dodge collisions; carry per-seg layers + vias out through the (U2-fixed) lossless path and DRC (already layer-aware).\n\nDESIGN Q for pickup: does \"as-drawn = follow my line\" coexist with \"engine may add a via to clear a crossing\"? Likely a per-hint flag (e.g. detail_level stays 'detailed' for XY path but allow_layer_change:true lets the engine break the single-layer assumption at crossings only). Resolve at pickup with the owner.\n\nTOUCH (fence): pcb/worker/pcb_worker/route_bridge.py (materialize_detailed_hints, hints_to_router layer handling), pcb/worker/pcb_worker/methods.py (route options), pcb/ui/kinds/pcb_route_hint_kind.gd (hint layer/multi-layer affordance), panel_tools route options. Depends on U1+U2.\n\nParent 019f709de47e. Model: Sonnet implement, Opus review. NOT in the first work-cycle — follows U1/U2.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:07:51","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:55:08","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugins","routing","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f709ebaec7e95b21178fae96d9596","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"U4: Manual via authoring — canvas Add-Via tool + representation","description":"Human/agent-authored vias: a canvas tool to place a via on a drawn path (\"via here, jump to B.Cu, continue\"), splitting the segment and flipping the following segment's layer, honoring U1's canonical via schema. Owner chose \"both\" (engine-auto U3 + manual U4).\n\nOVERLAP FLAG: this is close to the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR's (019f7095c395) CandidateViaTool / CandidateLayerTool (comment #533). Building it on today's annotation/board path now means rework when candidates move to the workspace. Owner accepted that cost (\"we need vias now, and regardless\"). Keep the geometry/edit logic factored so it can migrate.\n\nTOUCH (fence): pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd (Add-Via tool controller, hit-test, via draw already exists), pcb/ui/PCBPanel.gd (toolbar mode), pcb/ui/kinds/pcb_route_hint_kind.gd (per-segment layer + via markers on a hint), panel_tools (MCP add-via tool). Depends on U1. NOT in the first work-cycle.\n\nParent 019f709de47e. Model: Sonnet implement, Opus review.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:07:58","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:55:41","priority":3,"tags":["canvas","pcb","plugins","ui","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f709de47e","resolution":"Shipped: minerva-plugins e768280 (manual Add-Via canvas tool + minerva_pcb_add_via MCP parity, shared helper). Cold Opus approve; panel suites green. Live HITL WAIVED — owner shelved interim via tools + pivoted to the DCR 2026-07-17. Topology-safety for multi-pad nets (Codex F3 019f70ec2835) deferred to T7 / GATE 019f70f76c2f INV-3; the tool migrates to CandidateViaTool in the workspace."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70a26607727ca48eb2a1f52d9754","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Move 17 test_pcb_*.gd out of Minerva core into the pcb plugin","description":"Owner flagged 2026-07-17: \"The PCB tests are also in the wrong repo? If so, file a chore to move them to minerva-plugins, as minerva no longer has any PCB code allowed (because I'm ejecting that workflow to the pcb plugin).\"\n\nCURRENT STATE. 17 gd tests + 17 .uid in ~/github/Minerva/src/test/:\ntest_pcb_annotation_host_semantics, _apply_route_hints, _canvas_input_probe, _drc_propose, _explicit_propose, _hint_refine_loop, _legacy_annotation_migration, _panel_layout, _panel_model, _panel_tools, _panel_tools_migration, _panel_ui, _pin_inspector, _plugin_smoke, _route_hint_mcp_parity, _single_trace_tool, _workflow_kinds. Plus helper src/test/helpers/plugin_panel_driver.gd (assess: is it pcb-specific or a generic plugin-panel harness? if generic, it STAYS in core as shared scaffolding).\n\nWHY THIS IS DEBT. Same violation the panel-tools DCR (019f6c3d0e3d) fixed for runtime code: \"Minerva must not be aware of PCB workflows in any way.\" These suites encode pcb domain knowledge (route hints, proposals, DRC, pin inspector, workflow kinds) in core. They load the plugin via relative preload (res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/ui/*.gd), so the pcb logic they test already lives out-of-tree — only the test files are misplaced.\n\nTHE BLOCKER (design work, not mechanical). The pcb plugin is OFF-TREE with no godot project of its own, so there is nowhere for a .gd test to be run by `godot --headless --path`. Options to resolve at pickup:\n1. A minimal godot test-harness project inside minerva-plugins/pcb/ (or a shared minerva-plugins test project) that boots headless and points at the plugin — mirrors how Minerva's src/test runs today.\n2. A shared plugin-test-runner in the platform that any off-tree plugin registers gd suites with (generalizes beyond pcb — cad/graphics/etc. will need the same). Preferred if the platform wants first-class plugin gd testing.\n3. Keep a thin core shim that discovers+runs plugin-contributed gd suites (core stays pcb-agnostic; the SUITES move but the RUNNER is generic).\nThe relative-preload path coupling (tests resolve the plugin via res://../../minerva-plugins) also has to be re-based once the tests move.\n\nSCOPE. Move the 17 suites + .uid; design/build the harness (above); re-point preload paths; keep the CI/dev run command working; leave plugin_panel_driver.gd in core iff generic. NOT in scope: rewriting the tests' assertions.\n\nINTERIM. Until this lands, the via work (019f709de47e) and future pcb rounds run these suites from Minerva src/test/ via a paired Minerva+plugin worktree (the relative preload requires the sibling layout). That's a workaround, not an endorsement of the location.\n\nRelated: panel-tools DCR 019f6c3d0e3d (ejected runtime, left tests), PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR 019f7095c395, PCB umbrella 019f6a69b8a2. Parent-of-concern: the pcb-eject program.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:11:58","updated_at":"2026-07-17T15:12:15","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","plugins","separation-of-concerns","tech-debt","testing"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70af57367158bf5f11d75f21c565","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"U5: wire pcb_worker/kicad.py production export to honor via from_layer/to_layer","description":"Filed by U1 (019f709e32d3) as an out-of-fence discovery, confirmed by cold review. The plugin has TWO KiCad emitters: agent_router/kicad_io.py (standalone engine CLI export — U1 updated its Via.from_canonical) and pcb_worker/kicad.py (the module actually wired to the production generate/pcb_generate MCP tool). U1's fence pointed at the former; the production path (latter) still hardcodes (layers \"F.Cu\" \"B.Cu\") for every via, ignoring from_layer/to_layer.\n\nNOT a correctness bug today: on a 2-layer board a via always spans top↔bottom, so the hardcode emits identical output. It diverges only with >2 layers OR if manual authoring (U4) ever lets a via specify non-default layers. Pick up alongside U4 (manual authoring) or whenever multilayer lands. Small: map canonical from_layer/to_layer → F.Cu/B.Cu at pcb_worker/kicad.py's via emit, reusing the same _LAYER_MAP convention (no parallel mapper).\n\nParent 019f709de47e. Related: U1 019f709e32d3, U4 019f709ebaec.","created_at":"2026-07-17T15:26:07","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:55:13","priority":3,"tags":["export","kicad","pcb","plugins","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ea247a772d8d3e81f46f240633","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: PcbRoutingWorkspace S1–S5 (strangler-fig migration)","description":"PLAN for DCR 019f7095c395 (PcbRoutingWorkspace). Status: PLANNING (DCR stays `proposed` until owner approves — approval is the \"begin coding\" gate, after Codex reviews this plan). Awaiting Codex plan review.\n\n=== APPROACH: strangler-fig migration ===\nThe workspace grows BEHIND the existing annotation-proposal UI, then the visible surfaces switch to it one at a time, then the old path is deleted. This keeps the panel working at every stage (no big-bang cutover). Concretely:\n- S2 stands up the workspace model + populates it on propose as a SHADOW (annotation proposals remain the UI source of truth).\n- S3 switches CANVAS rendering/selection to read candidates from the workspace.\n- S4 switches the VERBS (accept/reject/pin/commit), the MCP tools, and the worker DRC to the workspace.\n- S5 deletes the now-unread annotation-proposal path (supersession, proposal_for, dock accept/reject). Hints stay annotations forever.\n\n=== S1 DECISION (flag for Codex) ===\nCodex's S1 was \"immediate correctness on the current substrate: lossless carry + explicit identity + generation + base-revision + idempotent replacement + stale-marking + draft-scoped DRC.\" We are DELIBERATELY SPLITTING it:\n- DONE: lossless propose→accept carry — delivered by the via work (U2, minerva-plugins e768280) + first-class via schema (U1) + manual Add-Via (U4).\n- SKIPPED as interim-on-annotations: explicit identity, generation, base-revision, idempotent propose, stale-marking, draft-DRC re-check. Building these on the annotation substrate (which S5 deletes) is throwaway. They are built ONCE, natively, in the workspace: identity/generation/revision in T1; idempotent replace in T2; draft-DRC + stale + attribution in T6.\n- RISK if S2 is slow to land: the current UI keeps its known gaps (propose stacks, DRC badge goes stale on accept/reject — discussion 019f70683492 gaps a–d) until T2/T6. Mitigation: sequence T1→T2→T6 early; the via-era lossless carry means the WORST bug (wrong committed copper) is already gone. Codex: challenge this if you think an interim draft-DRC re-check on annotations is worth the throwaway.\n\n=== TASK TREE (children of this plan) ===\nT1 (S2.1) Workspace domain model: pcb_route_candidate.gd + pcb_route_task.gd + pcb_routing_workspace.gd; PCBData board-revision + via-inclusive history. FOUNDATION.\nT2 (S2.2) Populate workspace on propose (shadow ingest) + workspace persistence sidecar (routing.json / .minpcb); idempotent replace (no stacking).\nT3 (S3.1) Candidate rendering + identity-returning hit-test on pcb_canvas (ghost styling: real layer color + reduced alpha; selection/pin/stale/violation via outline/dash/marker).\nT4 (S3.2) Plugin-owned route inspector bound to the active candidate + chat/MCP structured selection context.\nT5 (S4.1) Workspace verbs + composite commit transaction (candidate→PCBData atomic: traces+vias, advance board revision, consume hints, recheck survivors); MCP tools read/write the workspace.\nT6 (S4.2) Worker draft-DRC split (route-gen vs draft-DRC) + candidate/segment/via-attributed findings + board-revision/workspace-generation stamping + auto-recheck. Folds discussion gaps a/b/c/f.\nT7 (S4.3) Candidate editing tools (Vertex/Gate/Layer/Via/SpanReroute) as workspace commands (apply/revert, bump revision, stale DRC, lock edited geometry) + worker reroute_span (candidate-revision guarded) + route_bridge imports existing traces/vias + explicit RouteTask/span scope + fixed/pinned candidates. Folds 019f6cf2b5f4 (whole-board reroute) + 019f6cb9400e (endpoint re-tie). MIGRATES U4 manual Add-Via → CandidateViaTool.\nT8 (S5) Remove annotation-proposal path: strip proposal storage/supersession/accept-reject from PcbAnnotationHost + pcb_route_hint_kind + WorkflowAnnotationList consumption; drop dual-write. Hints revert to intent-only. Resolves annotation-duplication-in-review.\n\n=== SEQUENCING / DEPENDENCIES ===\nT1 → (T2, T3) → T4 → T5 → (T6, T7) → T8. T1 is the hard prerequisite (everything reads the model). T6 and T7 both depend on T5's committed-workspace + worker wiring. T8 is last (delete only once nothing reads the old path). Each Tn is itself work-cycle-scale and may sub-decompose at pickup.\n\n=== FILES (from DCR) ===\nNew (plugin): pcb/ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd, pcb_route_candidate.gd, pcb_route_task.gd. Touched: PCBPanel.gd, pcb_canvas.gd, panel_tools.gd, model/pcb_data.gd, kinds/pcb_route_hint_kind.gd, PcbAnnotationHost.gd; worker route_bridge.py, methods.py, drc.py. Zero Minerva core (north star), except pcb gd tests until chore 019f70a26607 relocates them.\n\nRelated: DCR 019f7095c395, discussion 019f70683492, via umbrella 019f709de47e (S1 lossless carry), panel-tools DCR 019f6c3d0e3d (unwound by S4/S5), .minpcb bundle 019f6edff9cc (T2 persistence).","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:30:20","updated_at":"2026-07-17T17:02:12","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","pcb","plan","plugins","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019f7095c395"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ea7c777187aa6a91459a531564","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1 (S2.1): Workspace domain model — candidate/task/workspace + PCBData board-revision","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S2.1. THE foundation — every later task reads this model. No UI/behavior switch yet.\n\nNEW FILES (pcb/ui/model/):\n- pcb_route_candidate.gd: stable candidate_id, task_id, net, endpoints; segments[] each {stable id, layer, width, ordered points, locked}; vias[] each {stable id, position, from_layer, to_layer, dims, locked}; source_hint_ids; generation; base_board_revision; candidate_revision; disposition ∈ {proposed,pinned,superseded,rejected,committed}; validation ∈ {unchecked,checking,clean,violating,stale,error}. disposition × validation ORTHOGONAL.\n- pcb_route_task.gd: task_id, net, endpoints (the routing job a candidate answers).\n- pcb_routing_workspace.gd: owns candidates + active_candidate_id + pinned set + selected_finding_id; signals candidate_added/changed/removed, active_candidate_changed, validation_changed; ops (stubs ok this task, filled by T2/T5/T7): ingest_routing_result, set_active, pin, reject, commit, add_via/vertex/gate, reroute_span, findings_for_candidate/element.\n\nPCBData (model/pcb_data.gd): add a monotonic board_revision (bump on any copper/component mutation) + ensure history snapshots INCLUDE vias (DCR finding 6: today snapshots miss vias/annotations). Batch-commit support for T5's composite transaction.\n\nACCEPTANCE: models construct + round-trip (to_dict/from_dict) with all fields incl. stable ids; workspace holds/queries candidates + emits signals; PCBData board_revision advances and undo restores vias faithfully. Pure model + unit tests (worker-independent). No canvas, no verbs, no annotation changes yet. Depends on: nothing (start here).","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:30:43","updated_at":"2026-07-17T18:11:32","priority":2,"tags":["foundation","pcb","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a","resolution":"Shipped minerva-plugins main @ ab12f36 (2026-07-17). Foundation domain model: pcb_route_task.gd, pcb_route_candidate.gd, pcb_routing_workspace.gd (all pcb/ui/model/). RouteCandidate = stable candidate/segment/via ids + generation/base_board_revision/candidate_revision + orthogonal disposition×validation axes; segments independent (no single-chain assumption → INV-3 modelling trap closed); via-span legality via T1.5 PcbLayerStack (illegal flagged not dropped). Workspace owns candidates/active/pinned/selected + 5 signals; commit/ingest/reroute = honest stubs for T2/T5/T7. PCBData additive: board_revision monotonic-FORWARD-ONLY via the record_change() funnel (all forward mutators incl. from_csv) + undo/redo bump forward (never rolled back); load-family excluded+documented; begin_batch/end_batch coalesce to ONE snapshot+ONE bump for T5's atomic commit. F1 vias-in-history VERIFIED (INV-1 regression), not rebuilt. Layer-1: new gd suite 91/0; test_layer_stack 52/0; existing test_pcb_panel_model regression 47/0. Cold Opus review: approve after 1 must_fix (from_csv missed the bump — fixed). Tests plugin-local at pcb/tests/gd/, run via scaffold host."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70eaa7387678aa3b1ceb7f2856e8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2 (S2.2): Populate workspace on propose (shadow) + persistence + idempotent replace","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S2.2. Strangler-fig shadow phase: the workspace is populated and persisted, but the visible UI still renders/acts on the annotation proposals. This lets T3/T4/T5 switch reads incrementally.\n\nWORK:\n- PCBPanel owns _routing_workspace beside _data + _annotation_host.\n- workspace.ingest_routing_result(router_reply): convert each route → RouteCandidate (exact segments+vias+layers, source_hint_ids, generation, base_board_revision = current PCBData.board_revision). Called from the propose path ALONGSIDE the existing _write_back_proposals (dual-write). \n- IDEMPOTENT REPLACE (fixes discussion gap d + folds idempotent-propose): a re-propose for the same task/hints supersedes the prior candidate (disposition→superseded or replace-in-place with generation+1), NEVER appends a duplicate.\n- PERSISTENCE: write the workspace to a separate sidecar (board.pcbskel.routing.json) or into the planned .minpcb bundle (019f6edff9cc) — crash-durable, EXCLUDED from Gerber/KiCad export (DCR persistence rule: no draft:true on canonical traces). On load against a different board_revision, mark all candidates stale until rebased+checked.\n\nACCEPTANCE: propose populates workspace candidates matching the router geometry; re-propose replaces (candidate count stable, generation bumps); workspace round-trips through the sidecar; loading against a changed board_revision marks candidates stale; annotation proposals still drive the UI unchanged (shadow). Depends on: T1.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:30:54","updated_at":"2026-07-17T18:38:53","priority":2,"tags":["pcb","persistence","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a","resolution":"Shipped minerva-plugins main @ f9d0cb0 (2026-07-17). Strangler shadow phase: propose now dual-writes — _write_back_proposals (annotations, unchanged, still UI source of truth) THEN workspace.ingest_routing_result fills RouteCandidates from the same router reply (exact segments+vias via T1.5 contract, source_hint_ids, generation, base_board_revision). Idempotent replace keyed net+source_hint_ids (re-propose supersedes gen+1, count stable, no dup). PCBPanel owns _routing_workspace beside _data/_annotation_host; dual-write null-degrades silently. Persistence deferred to T2a (scope comment #554). Known limitation filed on T2a (#555): global vs per-net hint attribution — harmless on single-net-per-route production path. Layer-1: test_workspace_ingest 54/54 (multi-pad disconnected+via fidelity, idempotent replace, dual-write functional floor via plugin_panel_driver); regressions 91/91 + 52/52. Cold Opus review approve_with_notes. Sonnet impl / Opus review."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70eaf0cb7ca3abd8e0e5ab0fcf1f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T3 (S3.1): Candidate rendering + identity hit-test on pcb_canvas","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S3.1. First read-switch: the CANVAS now draws candidates from the workspace instead of (or ahead of) the annotation-proposal ghosts.\n\nWORK (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd):\n- Render candidates through the same geometry path as real traces/vias, styled as ghosts: real configured layer color at reduced alpha (owner-agreed — same-layer overlaps accumulate opacity so congestion self-highlights); express selection (outline/glow), pinned (higher alpha/solid edge), stale (dash), violation (marker) via decoration, NOT by recoloring. Draw order: committed copper → inactive ghosts → pinned ghosts → active ghost → edit handles/gates → DRC markers.\n- Hit-test returns structured identity {candidate_id, element_kind (segment|via), element_id, nearest_position, distance}. Clicking sets workspace active/selected + redraws.\n- Data-driven layers (DCR): read a layer registry (id, display name, stack index, color, copper, routing-enabled, visibility) rather than hard-coding F.Cu/B.Cu — per-layer render passes preserve same-layer opacity accumulation while supporting >2 layers later. (Minimal registry acceptable this task; full multilayer is future.)\n\nACCEPTANCE: candidates render as layer-colored ghosts from the workspace; clicking a segment/via updates workspace selection + returns correct identity; selection/pin/stale/violation read as decoration not recolor. Annotation-proposal rendering may run in parallel (shadow) or be gated off for candidates — either, as long as no double-draw. Depends on: T1, T2.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:31:13","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:45:20","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70eb15d571dba73f65b7633c745e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T4 (S3.2): Plugin-owned route inspector + chat/MCP selection context","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S3.2. The candidate review surface moves off the generic core WorkflowAnnotationList onto a plugin-owned inspector (cleaner than teaching core about candidates; consistent with the \"Minerva unaware of PCB\" north star).\n\nWORK:\n- Plugin route inspector (pcb/ui/): binds to workspace.active_candidate; shows endpoints, net, candidate_revision, disposition, validation, ordered layer/via sequence, and findings_for_candidate. Action buttons: Pin, Commit, Reject, Check, Reroute-Route, Reroute-Span (wire to workspace ops; ops themselves land in T5/T6/T7 — buttons may be present-but-disabled until then).\n- Selection context to chat + MCP: activating/selecting a candidate publishes {active_candidate_id, task_id/net, selected_segment_id, candidate_revision, validation, finding_ids} to the chat selection context AND an MCP read tool (minerva_pcb_get_active_candidate). UI and MCP MUST call the SAME workspace methods (no divergent paths).\n\nACCEPTANCE: selecting a candidate on canvas populates the inspector + publishes identical structured context to chat and to the MCP get-active-candidate tool; the generic annotation dock no longer needs to render candidate rows (hints still render there). Depends on: T1, T3.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:31:22","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:33:02","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","pcb","plugins","routing","ui","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70eb4083743488a4b748ea31a0ef","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T5 (S4.1): Workspace verbs + composite commit transaction + MCP switch","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S4.1. The VERBS switch to the workspace. Fixes DCR finding 6 (accept spans two stores without a transaction).\n\nWORK:\n- workspace.commit(candidate): composite transaction — add the candidate's EXACT segments as canonical traces + its EXACT vias to PCBData; mark candidate committed; satisfy/remove its RouteTask; resolve/retain source hints per policy; advance board_revision; trigger recheck of surviving candidates. ATOMIC (single undo entry spanning board + workspace + consumed hints) — never reconstruct copper from flattened proposal points (the old _proposal_accept sin).\n- workspace.pin/reject: pin keeps a candidate as fixed draft (routed around, not committed); reject discards + reopens its task. Keep \"active/pinned/committed\" distinct (active = session/editor state; pinned = durable workspace state; committed = PCBData).\n- MCP tools move to the workspace: propose, list/get-active-candidate, pin, commit, reject, check — read+write workspace candidates; UI buttons (T4) and MCP call the SAME methods. panel_tools _write_back_proposals → workspace.ingest (from T2); accept → workspace.commit.\n\nACCEPTANCE: FUNCTIONAL FLOOR — real route → candidate → commit → committed board has exact geometry (layers+vias) + advanced board_revision + consumed hints, and a single undo restores board AND workspace AND hint state together; MCP commit/reject/pin produce identical results to the UI buttons. Depends on: T1, T2, T4. (T6 auto-recheck + T7 editing build on this.)","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:31:33","updated_at":"2026-07-18T18:43:44","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","pcb","plugins","routing","transaction","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70eb8f4871b982db93f1bc3c7817","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T6 (S4.2): Worker draft-DRC split + candidate/segment/via-attributed findings + auto-recheck","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S4.2. Fixes discussion 019f70683492 gaps a/b/c/f (set-scoped-but-stale DRC, no re-check path, net-level attribution) natively in the workspace — the reason the interim on-annotation draft-DRC was skipped (see plan).\n\nWORK:\n- Worker (pcb/worker): split route() into route-GENERATION (board + RouteTasks + hints + fixed candidates → candidates/provenance) and DRAFT-DRC (board + active candidate set → findings). Each candidate element sent to DRC carries source identity (candidate_id + segment_id/via_id). drc.py findings return {finding id, rule, severity, location, subjects with exact board/candidate element identity, board_revision, workspace_generation, candidate_revisions}.\n- Workspace: findings_for_candidate() / findings_for_element(); activating a candidate shows only global findings involving it; selecting a segment narrows. The selected candidate's collisions are a FILTERED VIEW of the global DRC (board ∪ all active candidates), not an isolated run.\n- Auto-recheck: after each commit/reject/edit, re-run draft-DRC over the new board ∪ surviving candidates; mark affected candidates validation=checking→clean/violating; stale any candidate whose base_board_revision no longer matches until rechecked. No silent stale badges (owner rule).\n\nACCEPTANCE: draft-DRC returns candidate/segment/via-attributed findings stamped with revision+generation; accepting/rejecting one candidate re-checks survivors and moves their badges (no stale lie); a via manually added (T7) marks the candidate stale then clean after recheck. Depends on: T5. (Runs parallel-ish with T7.)","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:31:53","updated_at":"2026-07-17T19:25:06","priority":2,"tags":["drc","pcb","plugins","routing","worker","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ebc9ed7922a5ab2a19328cefc0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T7 (S4.3): Candidate editing tools + reroute-span (revision-guarded) + existing-copper router","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S4.3. The interactive editing loop + selective rerouting. Folds bugs 019f6cf2b5f4 (router re-routes whole board, sev-2) and 019f6cb9400e (endpoint re-tie).\n\nWORK:\n- Canvas tool controllers (edit the ACTIVE candidate, not annotations): CandidateSelectTool, CandidateVertexTool (geometry vertex — \"trace goes exactly here\", modifies geometry immediately), CandidateRoutingGateTool (gate — \"regenerate this span through here\", steers the router; DISTINCT model object from a vertex), CandidateViaTool (MIGRATES U4's manual Add-Via — split + via + layer-run toggle, now on a candidate), CandidateLayerTool, CandidateSpanRerouteTool. Each completed gesture executes a workspace COMMAND (apply/revert, increment candidate_revision, stale old DRC, LOCK manually-edited geometry, emit signals, schedule recheck); pointer drags coalesce into one command on release.\n- Reroute: Reroute-Span replaces only a selected interval (unlock/replace just that span, preserving endpoints + committed copper + pinned candidates + locked spans/vias); Reroute-Route preserves gates + locked geometry, regenerates the rest.\n- Worker reroute_span request {candidate_id, expected candidate_revision, task_id, start/end pos+layer, gates, allowed layers, committed board, pinned candidates, preserved spans}; returns replacement segments/vias for that span ONLY; workspace applies via ReplaceCandidateSpanCommand ONLY IF candidate_revision still matches (concurrent edit → worker reply stale, not clobbering).\n- route_bridge.board_to_router: import existing board.traces/vias (other-net copper = occupied; same-net = already-connected), accept explicit RouteTask/span scope + fixed candidate geometry — NOT \"selected hints = only scope\" (fixes 019f6cf2b5f4).\n\nACCEPTANCE: a human/agent adds a via/vertex/gate to a candidate → geometry updates, revision bumps, DRC stales then rechecks; reroute-span replaces only the selected interval preserving locked/committed/pinned copper; a stale-revision worker reply is rejected; router treats existing copper as occupied and reroutes only the targeted task. Depends on: T5, T6.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:08","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:45:25","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","plugins","routing","worker","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ebfe177472b3f0a0509ea9d868","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T8 (S5): Remove the annotation-proposal path — hints revert to intent-only","description":"DCR 019f7095c395 stage S5. The strangler-fig teardown — runs ONLY after T3/T4/T5/T6/T7 have moved every read+verb to the workspace, so nothing depends on the annotation-proposal path anymore.\n\nWORK:\n- PcbAnnotationHost.gd: remove proposal storage, is_annotation_superseded / supersession, and proposal accept/reject verbs. Keep route-HINT storage + the collaborative hint edit/refine/undo (hints stay annotations forever).\n- kinds/pcb_route_hint_kind.gd: remove proposal rendering (per-segment ghost + via markers migrated to canvas candidate rendering in T3); the kind renders INTENT/constraints only.\n- panel_tools.gd: delete _write_back_proposals, _proposal_accept, _proposal_reject and the proposal_for/dual-write plumbing (superseded by workspace ops from T5).\n- WorkflowAnnotationList (core, src/): remove the pcb-proposal special cases (accept/reject on AI rows, DRC badge, supersession filter) — this is core cleanup that REVERSES part of panel-tools DCR 019f6c3d0e3d's proposal surface; hint rows (non-proposal) unaffected. Coordinate with test-move chore 019f70a26607.\n\nACCEPTANCE: no proposal_for / supersession / proposal accept-reject remains in the pcb plugin or core; a grep gate proves the annotation substrate holds only hints; the full route flow (propose→review→edit→commit) runs entirely on the workspace; annotation-duplication-in-review is gone. Fixes DCR findings 4 (AI-authorship-as-discriminator) + 5 (supersession hides hint) by removal. Depends on: T3, T4, T5, T6, T7 (LAST task).","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:22","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:42:48","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","cleanup","pcb","plugins","routing"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ec149b71b4bd5c90de3ca5e6cd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"PCBData undo/redo must restore vias via one complete board snapshot codec","description":"REVIEW FINDING. pcb/ui/model/pcb_data.gd save_to_history() stores only components, nets, and traces (lines 438-450); _restore_state() restores only those fields (lines 519-540). _materialize_routes() adds real vias and then calls save_to_history, so undo restores pre-apply traces while data.vias remains mutated. The current functional test asserts trace count only, allowing an orphan via to survive undo. This is also a DRY smell: history maintains a partial, parallel serialization contract while to_dict()/load helpers already know about vias and other PCBData-owned fields.\n\nFix direction: introduce one pure, complete PCBData snapshot/restore codec (or reuse/refactor the legacy serializer without resetting history) and have save_to_history/_restore_state use it. At minimum include vias with position, dimensions, net, and from_layer/to_layer; audit mounting holes, design rules, board dimensions/layers, and ID counters so the snapshot contract is explicit. Do not fold annotation/workspace state into PCBData; the later PcbRoutingWorkspace composite transaction owns cross-store undo.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:27","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:44:16","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","dry","pcb","plugins","routing","undo","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f709de47e"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ec1e5f7643aa67e775c0c817cd","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Mark proposal DRC stale and refresh the active candidate set after Add-Via edits","description":"REVIEW FINDING. _write_back_proposals copies the worker verdict into kind_payload.drc. pcb_route_hint_kind.gd apply_via_at_point() deep-duplicates the full kind_payload and replaces only segments/vias (lines 840-891), so the old drc object survives. focus_points() then continues rendering its old violation coordinates (lines 1373-1393), and the dock can continue reporting an old clean/violating badge. The canvas and MCP paths correctly share the same mutation helper, so this bug affects both identically.\n\nImmediate fix: every exact-geometry mutation must clear or explicitly mark the stored verdict stale before persistence; acceptance must not present that stale result as current. Workflow fix: schedule DRC over committed board + the complete active candidate set and stamp the result with board/workspace/candidate revisions. Editing one candidate invalidates survivor verdicts too, not only the edited item. Keep validation state orthogonal to candidate disposition per DCR 019f7095c395.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:30","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:55:03","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","drc","pcb","plugins","routing","staleness","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f7095c395"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ec283578cfba68799d6150ee2d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Make Add-Via topology-aware for multi-pad nets and reject degenerate inserts","description":"REVIEW FINDING. agent_router.router.Route.segments concatenates every Path for a net (router.py lines 317-330), and multi-pad routing appends one Path per spanning-tree connection. apply_via_at_point() splits a segment, then _recompute_layer_runs() walks the entire flat segments array with one current-layer state (pcb_route_hint_kind.gd lines 840-933). If a via is inserted in path A, current remains toggled when iteration reaches disconnected path B, silently moving B to the opposite layer. Segment order/reversal also becomes semantic even though topology, not array position, should decide the affected run.\n\nRepresent or derive connected route paths before mutation. Select the hit segment's path/component, split only that path, and propagate the layer transition only along the intended directed run; preserve all other paths byte-for-byte. Prefer stable path/segment/via IDs in the workspace model. Also reject or normalize clicks at an existing via or segment endpoint so duplicate vias and zero-length segments are not committed.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:32","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:44:21","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","geometry","pcb","plugins","routing","topology","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f709ebaec"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70ec3217722ba2e743dd1c316837","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Consolidate proposal geometry: exact segments/vias must drive render, hit-test, bounds, and edits","description":"REVIEW/DRY FINDING. _write_back_proposals stores exact kind_payload.segments/vias but deliberately retains flattened waypoints/layer. render() uses segments, while hit_test(), bounds(), summary(), bend editing, and route-hint selection still use _waypoint_points(). _route_polyline() flattens the concatenated segments of all paths and cannot represent disconnected multi-pad topology; invisible/spurious connector geometry can therefore be selectable or affect bounds even though the canvas renders different segments. This violates the accepted-copper-equals-reviewed-ghost invariant and creates two sources of truth.\n\nRefactor toward the DCR's plugin-owned route candidate geometry layer: a pure accessor/model should expose paths, segments, vias, bounds, nearest-hit, split, and layer-run operations. Render, canvas/MCP edits, hit-test, bounds, selection, DRC attribution, and acceptance consume that API. Keep one read-only legacy adapter for old annotations during migration, then stop writing and delete proposal waypoints/layer plus _route_polyline/_route_layer. As a smaller cleanup in the same seam, BendHandleEditTool and ViaInsertTool duplicate _zoom/_find/_select_route_hint_at almost line-for-line; extract shared route-manipulation selection plumbing or let the routing workspace/canvas own selection.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:32:35","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:44:31","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["cleanup","dry","geometry","pcb","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f7095c395"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f382b67a04888584ffed6426e6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.3: Shadow parity bridge + authoritative cutover gate","description":"PLAN GAP. T2 populates workspace candidates while annotation proposals remain authoritative. T3 then switches canvas rendering/selection to the workspace, but legacy Accept/Reject/Add-Via/undo continue mutating annotations/PCBData until T5. Without an explicit bridge, a rejected or committed proposal remains live in the workspace; an Add-Via edit changes the annotation but not the rendered candidate; load/undo can diverge both stores.\n\nBuild one normalized ingest record per router result and derive both the RouteCandidate and temporary annotation projection from it. Persist correlation {candidate_id, task_id, generation, annotation_id}. During the shadow window, every legacy proposal mutation must either route through a workspace command and update the projection, or be disabled for that candidate; never independently mutate both models. Add a feature flag/cutover coordinator that switches canvas, inspector, verbs, MCP, persistence, and DRC authority only when their write paths are workspace-backed. Rollback must leave the old UI coherent. Remove the bridge in T8.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:40:34","updated_at":"2026-07-18T18:43:36","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","migration","pcb","plugins","routing","strangler-fig","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a","resolution":"DONE — committed minerva-plugins LOCAL main @ 0c3e856 (NOT pushed; staged for owner's manual review+push). One normalized route record per propose derives BOTH the annotation projection and the RouteCandidate (fixes T2's two-independent-derivations); bidirectional candidate↔annotation correlation (+task_id/generation) persisted through the routing sidecar; bridged legacy accept/reject/add-via route through workspace commands (mark_committed / reject / sync_candidate_geometry) — never mutating both stores independently (add-via drift-checked: candidate + annotation derive from one object). New pcb_routing_cutover.gd per-surface authority latch, all default annotation-authoritative — MECHANISM ONLY, nothing flipped in T2.3. Commit produces stable canonical trace/via IDs (via gains via_N, round-trips to_board_dict) per DCR 019f761e Stage-0 acceptance. Opus implement → cold Opus review = approve_with_notes → Layer-1 376/0 (parity_bridge 84 incl 3-pad 2-disconnected-path + undo-after-commit INV-1/INV-3 fixtures; ingest 54, model 91, persistence 57, check 30). Follow-up notes (all LOW, non-blocking): (1) board-undo→workspace.uncommit not wired in production (belongs to T5 verbs flip; noted on T5); (2) DRY: sync_candidate_geometry re-implements the seg/via parse loop from _create_candidate_for_route; (3) edge: two records sharing a task_key in one propose leaves a superseded-but-correlated candidate; (4) pcb/worker/.venv should be gitignored (excluded from commit). Band A of the routing DCR is COMPLETE."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f38c7b7a1da2ae45664c782d65","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2a: Versioned, crash-safe workspace persistence with board coherence fingerprint","description":"PLAN GAP. T2 says loading a workspace against a different board_revision marks candidates stale, but T1's monotonic runtime counter alone cannot detect Save As, copied/edited board files, sidecar tearing, restart/reset, or an ABA undo sequence. Separate board and routing writes can leave new board + old workspace after a crash.\n\nDefine a durable coherence token: stable board_document_id plus a routing-relevant content fingerprint (or persisted change epoch + fingerprint) covering components/pads, nets, board outline/keepouts, design rules/layer stack, committed traces/vias, and other router/DRC inputs. Persist schema_version and the token with the workspace. Write atomically (temp+fsync/rename as supported), define Save/Save As/copy/delete behavior, and quarantine/mark stale on missing, corrupt, future-version, or mismatched data. Do not persist transient active selection as durable design intent. Provide explicit migration hooks; routing state must never enter fabrication export.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:40:37","updated_at":"2026-07-17T19:02:25","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","pcb","persistence","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70eaa738","resolution":"Shipped minerva-plugins main @ 72272be (2026-07-17). Durable routing-workspace sidecar (pcb_routing_sidecar.gd, new): \"<board>.routing.json\" versioned envelope, atomic tmp→rename write, zero-candidates deletes, corrupt backed up. board_fingerprint = SHA-256 over canonical serialization of routing/DRC inputs — width/height, layer stack, design_rules, components+pads, nets, traces, vias, AND mounting_holes (keepouts, added per review). \"%.6f\" float-stable; dict-arrays sorted, ordered sequences (waypoints/layer-stack) preserved (sorting = false-clean). PCBPanel save writes sidecar beside annotation sidecar; load (gated _restoring) QUARANTINES via mark_all_stale on fingerprint mismatch / missing-token / future-version / corrupt — never crashes, never silently trusts. ABA change→revert = fingerprint equal = NOT stale (hash is the signal, not board_revision). Durable dict excludes transient active/selected; keeps candidates+pinned+counters. Export isolation verified (to_board_dict has no routing keys). Folded T2 #555: per-net hint attribution (_hint_ids_for_net). Layer-1: test_workspace_persistence 57/57; regressions ingest 54, model 91, layer_stack 52. Cold Opus review approve after 1 must_fix (mounting_holes false-clean gap). Opus impl / Opus review."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f3966c7bcda42feca703449c58","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T1.5: Shared layer-stack registry and legal via-span contract","description":"ALIGNMENT GAP. T3 mentions a minimal data-driven render registry, while T1 candidates, T6 DRC, T7 layer/via tools, route_bridge, and KiCad export can still independently hard-code F.Cu/B.Cu or top/bottom. The agreed discussion direction was data-driven layers now so the two-layer start does not bake a toggle into every seam.\n\nDefine a plugin-owned LayerStack/LayerRegistry contract: stable layer_id, display/export aliases, stack index, configurable color, kind=copper/non-copper, routing-enabled, visibility, and canonical serialization. Define via types/spans and legal from_layer→to_layer transitions (through/blind/buried capability can be represented even if only through vias are enabled initially). Candidate segments/vias carry stable IDs from this registry. Canvas render passes, layer controls, worker routing, DRC connectivity/clearance, persistence, and KiCad/Gerber export consume the same contract or generated boundary mappings. Legacy F.Cu/B.Cu and top/bottom conversion lives at migration/import boundaries only. Fold existing U5 019f70af5736 into the export portion.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:40:39","updated_at":"2026-07-17T17:42:44","priority":1,"severity":3,"tags":["architecture","layers","multilayer","pcb","plugins","routing","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a","resolution":"Shipped minerva-plugins main @ eafd322 (2026-07-17). ONE layer/via-span contract per runtime: worker agent_router/layers.py (route_bridge + kicad_io + pcb_worker/kicad now resolve the SAME map object — drift impossible; contract in base agent_router pkg, imported upward); GD ui/model/pcb_layer_stack.gd (no class_name, off-tree) with all FOUR inline copies migrated (pcb_data._canon_layer_name, panel_tools ×5, pcb_canvas._canonical_layer, PcbAnnotationHost.get_current_layer). is_legal_via_span = through-via 2-layer rule, shape extensible to blind/buried. Pure behavior-preserving refactor; no N-layer. Tests: worker test_layer_contract.py 9/9 (locks all 3 emitters to one object); pcb/tests/gd/test_layer_stack.gd 52/0 (real-PCBData functional floor: serialize keeps top/bottom + export emits F.Cu/B.Cu; INV-1 via undo/redo; get_current_layer contract guard). Full worker suite 280 passed (1 pre-existing test_worker_smoke failure, identical on base). Cold Opus review: approve after 1 must_fix (4th map copy). GD tests now live plugin-local (pcb/tests/gd/), run via scaffold host — zero Minerva-core change, aligns with move-chore 019f70a26607."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f3a04a72a48652bc39f6d2f26f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"T4a: Candidate review queue, DRC navigation, and bulk keep/reroute workflows","description":"USER-STORY GAP. T4 binds an inspector to one active candidate, but the agreed workflow begins with many proposed routes and asks the user to keep clean/good traces, focus each collision, and reroute only the implicated set. A detail pane alone does not provide inventory, progress, or the operation 'keep all clean routes and try again for the collisions.'\n\nAdd a plugin-owned candidate queue/list grouped by RouteTask/net and filtered by disposition/validation (unchecked, checking, clean, violating, stale, error). Selecting a row sets the one active candidate and highlights only global findings involving it; selecting a finding focuses exact subjects. Support multi-select/batch Pin, Commit, Reject, Check, and Reroute implicated candidates, with explicit confirmation where board mutation occurs. UI and MCP batch operations call the same workspace methods and use generation/revision guards. Preserve the distinct meanings of active, pinned, and committed.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:40:42","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:43:56","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["drc","pcb","plugins","routing","ui","workflow","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70eb15d5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f3aa52773f907765cae587c949","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"T2.4: Early native workspace draft-check seam (split from T6)","description":"DEPENDENCY GAP. The plan says sequence T1→T2→T6 early, but the graph places T6 after T5; T5 nevertheless promises to recheck surviving candidates. This leaves the central discussion requirement—honest DRC over the current draft set—until late and makes T5's acceptance impossible in isolation.\n\nSplit the reusable native seam from T6: worker draft_check(board snapshot + complete effective candidate set) is independent of routing; workspace.check() transitions validation unchecked/stale→checking→clean/violating/error; requests and findings carry board coherence token, workspace_generation, and candidate revisions; mismatched replies are discarded. Include candidate/segment/via subject identity from the first schema so no net-only compatibility path hardens. Expose on-demand Check to tests/MCP internally; the shadow UI may use a temporary read-only projection, but the authoritative result lives only in the workspace. T6 remains responsible for debounce/coalescing/cancellation and automatic rechecks wired to T5/T7 mutations.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:40:44","updated_at":"2026-07-17T19:26:33","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["correctness","drc","pcb","plugins","routing","workspace"],"parent":"minerva:019f70ea247a","resolution":"Shipped minerva-plugins main @ 7f5060b (2026-07-17). Reusable native draft-check seam split from T6. Worker draft_check (methods.py): existing drc.py checks over the UNION of committed copper ∪ complete effective candidate set (set-scoped; cand-vs-cand collisions detected); findings carry {candidate_id, segment_id/via_id} subject identity (not net-only); echoes board_token + workspace_generation verbatim. New broker channel pcb.draft_check: manifest + Go (main.go + worker_tools.go + workerBackedTools, mirrors pcb.route) + worker dispatch — see hint pcb-plugin/worker.new_broker_ipc_channel_needs_go. GD workspace.check state machine (IPC-decoupled): begin_check (checking + snapshot revision+prior + request payload) / apply_check_result (GUARDS FIRST — whole-reply discard on board_token/workspace_generation mismatch, per-candidate discard on revision drift → stale reply NEVER marks clean; reverts to snapshotted prior, no stuck checking). workspace_generation transient, bumps on every candidate-set change. PCBPanel.check_draft() production wiring over _MinervaIPC (live hop needs broker, seam-covered by pytest+gd). DEPLOY: REINSTALL (manifest+Go+worker). Layer-1: worker test_draft_check 5/5, full worker 286; gd test_workspace_check 30/30 (multi-pad attribution + 3 mismatch guards); regressions 57/54/91/52; go build clean. Cold Opus review approve_with_notes; attribution over-naming edge (two same-net overlapping candidates) filed on T6 #556. Opus impl (escalated) / Opus review."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f70f76c2f7b9a997c54af29bb7f15","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"GATE: Via correctness invariants — DCR must not ship until all 4 green","description":"WHY THIS EXISTS: Codex code-reviewed the via work (U1/U2/U4) 2026-07-17 and found 4 real, verified-in-code issues. Owner chose to SHELVE the interim via/Add-Via tools and fold the fixes into the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR rather than patch doomed code. Risk: the conversation compacts before DCR work starts, so this gate moves the correctness requirements OUT of conversation memory into an enforceable, discoverable artifact. Nudge hints (pcb-plugin/pcb_data.undo_codec_drops_vias, router.avoids_vias_prefers_detour) are the recall net; THIS is the enforcement.\n\nREVIEW RULE (applies to every workspace task): tests MUST include MULTI-PAD-NET (3+ pin) and UNDO-AFTER-COMMIT fixtures from the start. The via-work tests missed all 4 findings because they used 2-pin single-path fixtures only and never tested undo-after-accept. Do not repeat that.\n\n=== THE 4 INVARIANTS (each blocks DCR ship) ===\n\nINV-1 — UNDO RESTORES VIAS (Codex 019f70ec149b; owner: T1 019f70ea7c77 + T5 019f70eb4083; also fixed NOW in interim PCBData).\nBug: PCBData has two codecs — full serialize includes vias, but the undo-history codec (save_to_history/_restore_state) omits vias + mounting_holes. Accept a via route → undo → traces removed, vias ORPHANED.\nInvariant: undo/redo uses ONE complete board snapshot codec (components+nets+traces+vias+mounting_holes).\nTEST: commit a via-bearing route → undo → assert vias == pre-commit set AND traces removed together → redo → both return.\n\nINV-2 — EDITS STALE THE DRC (Codex 019f70ec1e5f; owner: T6 019f70eb8f48).\nBug: apply_via_at_point overwrites segments/vias but preserves kind_payload.drc, so stale collision markers keep rendering on new geometry.\nInvariant: any candidate edit (via/vertex/gate/layer/reroute-span) marks DRC validation=stale and triggers re-check; no stale markers render against edited geometry.\nTEST: clean candidate → add a via → validation==stale until recheck → recheck → findings reflect NEW geometry; old finding locations gone.\n\nINV-3 — VIA/LAYER EDITS ARE TOPOLOGY-AWARE (Codex 019f70ec2835; owner: T7 019f70ebc9ed). HIGHEST RISK.\nBug: agent_router Route.segments (router.py ~324) CONCATENATES all paths of a multi-pad net into one flat array; _recompute_layer_runs walks it assuming one connected chain → a via on path A flips the layer of a DISCONNECTED path B. Also no guard against degenerate inserts (endpoint click → zero-length segment; on-via click → duplicate via).\nInvariant: a via/layer edit affects ONLY the path it lands on; disconnected paths of the same net are untouched; degenerate inserts are rejected no-ops.\nMANDATORY TEST FIXTURE: a 3-pin net whose candidate has >=2 disconnected paths. Add a via on path A → assert path B layers unchanged. Endpoint click + on-via click → rejected.\n\nINV-4 — SINGLE GEOMETRY SOURCE OF TRUTH (Codex 019f70ec3217; owner: T3 019f70eaf0cb).\nBug: render uses exact segments; hit-test/bounds/_route_polyline use flattened waypoints — they disagree on disconnected paths (visible != selectable geometry).\nInvariant: exact candidate segments/vias are the ONLY geometry for render, hit-test, bounds, and edits; no parallel waypoint geometry.\nTEST: for a candidate with disconnected paths, hit-test geometry == rendered geometry == edited geometry (no divergence).\n\n=== ENFORCEMENT ===\nThis item is linked `blocks` → DCR 019f7095c395. Close it only when all 4 invariants have a green named test in the workspace suite. At DCR sign-off, this checklist is the final review. Related: via umbrella 019f709de47e, plan 019f70ea247a.","created_at":"2026-07-17T16:44:51","updated_at":"2026-07-17T16:45:15","priority":1,"tags":["acceptance-gate","correctness","pcb","plugins","routing","vias"],"parent":"minerva:019f7095c395"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f710fdb047bd396d5abc30ca388a9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"agent_router is the base pkg; shared worker contracts live there, imported upward","created_at":"2026-07-17T17:11:32","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:41","tags":["architecture","layers","pcb","routing","worker"],"value":"pcb worker has a strict layered dependency: `agent_router` is a standalone base package that `pcb_worker` depends ON — never the reverse (see route_bridge.py module docstring). So kicad_io.py (in agent_router) CANNOT import route_bridge.py (in pcb_worker); that is why _CANON_TO_KICAD_LAYER duplicates _LAYER_MAP. To de-duplicate any shared worker constant/contract (e.g. the top/bottom↔F.Cu/B.Cu layer map), put it in the LOWER package `agent_router` (e.g. agent_router/layers.py) and import UPWARD from both kicad_io and pcb_worker/route_bridge. Canonical layer convention: top/bottom in the plugin model + canonical board dict; F.Cu/B.Cu only at the KiCad/engine boundary.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"worker.package_dependency_direction","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7146de327df895c87316ead7a4a2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb gd tests: plugin-local in pcb/tests/gd/, run via scaffold host (never live checkout)","created_at":"2026-07-17T18:11:37","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:43","tags":["gdscript","pcb","scaffold","testing","worktree"],"value":"pcb plugin gd tests can live IN the plugin repo at pcb/tests/gd/*.gd and run via a Minerva SCAFFOLD as the Godot host (NEVER the live checkout — headless there kills the owner's session). Pattern: `godot --headless --path <scaffold>/src --script res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/tests/gd/<test>.gd`. The out-of-project --script path works, and preloads inside the test resolve res:// against the scaffold's src/ root, so `res://../../minerva-plugins/...` reaches the plugin checkout beside it. Test file: `extends SceneTree`, preload plugin scripts by that relative-res path, print `PASS:`/`FAIL:` lines + a final `=== Results: N passed, M failed ===`, then quit(0). Model classes (PCBData, PcbLayerStack, RouteCandidate/Workspace) instantiate standalone in a bare SceneTree — no autoload needed. IGNORE the `Failed to compile depended scripts` / VideoRecorderOverlay / SQLite lines that appear AFTER the Results line — unrelated scaffold app-boot/teardown noise (the minimal scaffold lacks some core GDExtensions). Scaffold at ~/github/minerva-worktrees/via-scaffold; plugin worktree at ~/github/minerva-worktrees/minerva-plugins. This keeps gd tests plugin-local (zero Minerva-core change) ahead of move-chore 019f70a26607.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"test.gd_harness_plugin_local","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f718a0c3d76c7afc246268aa1da43","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"New pcb broker IPC channel = manifest + Go (main.go + worker_tools.go + workerBackedTools) + worker method","created_at":"2026-07-17T19:25:00","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:02:46","tags":["broker","go","ipc","manifest","pcb","worker"],"value":"Adding a new GD↔worker broker IPC channel (e.g. pcb.draft_check) needs FOUR edits, not just manifest: (1) pcb/manifest.json ipc_channels + ipc_messages entries; (2) register the channel in Go pcb/main.go; (3) forward it to the worker method in pcb/internal/tools/worker_tools.go (mirror how pcb.route/pcb.serialize are wired); (4) add the tool to workerBackedTools. Then the Python worker method (pcb/worker/pcb_worker/methods.py dispatch table). GD side calls it via the _MinervaIPC node: request.emit(channel, payload, reply_id) + await ipc.await_reply(reply_id, timeout_ms). Manifest-only is INSUFFICIENT — the broker is Go and won't forward an unregistered channel. After changing Go, `cd pcb && go build ./...` must be clean; the Go broker binary must be rebuilt to deploy. The live worker hop can't run in the headless scaffold (no _MinervaIPC backend) — cover the seam with worker pytest + a GD state-machine test using a fake reply.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"worker.new_broker_ipc_channel_needs_go","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f721139ca736b8dd3c70ddebc01e5","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Toolchain probe honors only `min` version — picks a too-new interpreter and plugin install fails with an opaque error","description":"## Problem\n\n`ToolchainProbe.probe()` (src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/Setup/ToolchainProbe.gd) accepts only a `min_version` and rejects candidates via `semver_lt(version, min) -> \"toolchain_too_old\"`. There is **no upper-bound concept** anywhere in the chain:\n- `ToolchainRegistry.gd` tool entries carry no `max`.\n- The manifest `setup.requires` schema (SetupSchema.gd) only accepts `min`.\n- The registry takes the **first** candidate that clears `min` (candidates order: `python`, `python3`), it does not search for a best-in-range interpreter.\n\nWhen a plugin's real toolchain has an upper bound that the manifest cannot express, the probe happily selects a too-new interpreter and the failure surfaces far downstream (pip's Requires-Python rejection, or a pip-less/mismatched venv) as an opaque error rather than \"wrong version.\"\n\n## Concrete instance (pcb plugin)\n\n- The pcb worker pins `requires-python = \">=3.12,<3.13\"` (pcb/worker/pyproject.toml) — the `<3.13` cap appears intentional (dependency support).\n- The pcb manifest declares only `{tool: python, min: 3.12}`.\n- On a macOS box where `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` is **3.14.3**, the probe persisted `python = /opt/homebrew/bin/python3` (3.14) in `toolchain_paths.cfg`. `python -m venv` + `pip install -e` on 3.14 fails; the app surfaced \"no module pip\".\n\n## Blast radius\n\npcb is the **only** plugin currently exposed — it is the only one whose manifest drives the setup pipeline (`requires` + `go_build`/`python_venv` steps). All other plugins (3d-gen, drive, movie-gen, agent-relay, cad, scansort, nametag-maker, presentation, codetools) have empty `requires`/`steps` and never invoke the probe. But the defect is **generic**: pcb also declares `go min 1.22` with the same min-only shape (low risk — Go is backward-compatible), and the next plugin that adds a version-capped toolchain (a `cargo_build` with an MSRV window, another Python worker, a Node plugin with an `engines` range) recurs the same way.\n\n## Recommended fix (Option 1 — range-aware probe)\n\n1. Add an optional `max` (exclusive) to the manifest `setup.requires` schema (SetupSchema.gd) and to `ToolchainRegistry` tool entries.\n2. In `ToolchainProbe.probe()`, reject candidates `>= max` with a new `\"toolchain_too_new\"` error, and have the registry **keep searching** other candidates/well-known dirs/PATH for an interpreter in `[min, max)` instead of stopping at the first that clears `min`.\n3. When no in-range interpreter exists, fail the preflight with an actionable message, e.g. \"PCB worker needs Python >=3.12,<3.13; found 3.14 at /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 — install Python 3.12.\"\n4. Set the pcb manifest python `requires` to `{min: \"3.12\", max: \"3.13\"}` and add regression tests (min-only still works; too-new rejected; best-in-range selected among multiple installed pythons).\n\nLonger-term (separate, larger effort): bundle/pin a per-plugin interpreter (PBS bundle) so plugin installs never depend on host Python at all — `pcb/main.go` already anticipates this (\"no embedded PBS bundle yet\").\n\nNot a fix: widening the worker `requires-python`, since the cap is likely a real dependency constraint and it wouldn't close the generic host-side gap.\n\n## Key files\n- src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/Setup/ToolchainProbe.gd (min-only compare, ~line 156-176)\n- src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/Setup/ToolchainRegistry.gd (python entry, candidates order, no max)\n- src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/Setup/SetupSchema.gd (requires validation)\n- src/Scripts/Services/Plugins/Setup/SetupSteps.gd (python_venv phases, ~line 72-92)\n- minerva-plugins/pcb/manifest.json (setup.requires), minerva-plugins/pcb/worker/pyproject.toml (`<3.13` cap)","created_at":"2026-07-17T21:52:39","updated_at":"2026-07-17T21:52:39","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","plugins","python","setup-pipeline","toolchain"],"environment":"macOS (darwin 25.5.0), Apple Silicon. Default `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` = 3.14.3. Other Pythons present: 3.10 (PATH), 3.11 (homebrew), uv-managed 3.12.12. Minerva persists resolved toolchain paths in ~/Library/Application Support/Godot/app_userdata/Minerva/toolchain_paths.cfg. pcb plugin at minerva-plugins @ main.","repro_steps":"1. Use a macOS/Linux machine whose default `python3` (on PATH or in a well-known dir like /opt/homebrew/bin) is Python >= 3.13 (e.g. 3.14) and which has NO Python 3.12 that the probe finds first.\n2. Ensure no stale worker/.venv exists.\n3. In the Minerva app, install the pcb plugin (dev-install pointing at ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb).\n4. The python_venv setup step creates a venv with the 3.14 interpreter and runs `pip install -e worker`.\n5. Install fails; error surfaces as \"no module pip\" / Requires-Python mismatch rather than a clear \"wrong Python version\" message."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f722b573a78c99114db44e4c3fc1c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"MCP surface LLM-ergonomics: host-wide fixes surfaced by a plugin board-load session","description":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during a session where an agent tried to load a board into a plugin editor entirely through the MCP surface. The plugin-specific bugs are tracked in the plugins repo (PLG) — this item captures the **host-wide** ergonomic issues that apply to any plugin and are independent of the in-progress PCB ejection from Minerva core.\n\n## Host-wide issues (each with a proposed fix)\n\n1. **Tools visible but not callable after an MCP disconnect; no reconnect signal.** `tools/list` over HTTP showed the plugin's tools, but they were not wired into the agent session (not in the deferred-tool list), and nothing signaled that a `/mcp` reconnect was needed. → Emit a machine-readable \"server present but not connected — reconnect\" signal the agent can act on, rather than silent absence.\n\n2. **No way to discover a plugin editor's `editor_name`.** Nearly every plugin editor tool *requires* `editor_name`, but `minerva_list_editors` is described as covering only \"text, graphics, and spreadsheet editors\" and does not surface plugin/panel editors (or mislabels its scope). → Include plugin editors in `list_editors` with `{type, plugin_id, panel_id}` fields so names are discoverable. (Verify actual enumeration first — may be a description-only bug.)\n\n3. **No clean entry point to create a plugin editor, and per-plugin create/list tools would explode the surface.** The `pcb-maker` skill referenced a non-existent `minerva_create_pcb_editor`. Every plugin can have many panel types. → Standardize on ONE generic `minerva_plugin_open_panel(plugin_id, panel_id, name?) -> editor_name` (already partially present) + the list extension in (2). Net tool-count change ≈ 0, works for all plugins.\n\n4. **Under-specified input schemas platform-wide.** Plugin tools expose complex nested params typed as bare `object`; the real contract (required nesting, gating flags, enums) is invisible and had to be reverse-engineered from source. → Host convention + lint: plugin tools must ship full JSON Schemas for params, including `enum`s for fixed allowlists. A tool whose param is `object` with no `properties` should fail the lint.\n\n5. **Inconsistent result envelopes; partial failure reads as success.** Some handlers return an `_ok/_err` wrap, others a bare dict; a fully-failed import can still return `success: true` with the failures parked in a data field. → Mandate a tri-state envelope convention (`ok` / `partial` / `error`) across the surface so silent/partial failures are legible.\n\n6. **Tool-name namespace stutter.** `minerva_<plugin>_<plugin>_*` patterns (e.g. the PCB plugin's `..._pcb_pcb_*`) arise when a plugin's method names redundantly re-encode the plugin id the host already prepends. → Host naming guidance + lint against `minerva_<id>_<id>_` and other redundant repetition.\n\n7. **Tool discovery at scale (hundreds → potentially thousands of tools).** The agent had to grep `tools/list` itself; 337 tools with weak faceting. `minerva_tool_search` / `minerva_tool_memory_search` exist but are a fallback, not the reflex. → (a) lean eager surface + search-on-demand (lean into the deferred-tool mechanism for plugin tools); (b) faceted `tool_search` filterable by `plugin_id` / capability tag; (c) curated per-plugin \"start here\" index via `minerva_plugin_help`. There is likely no fix *other* than making search the first-class discovery path — the surface will always outgrow the context window.\n\n8. **Stale-tool / registry drift.** Tool descriptions reference removed dependencies (e.g. Docker/external toolchains) and stale skill outcomes persist in `plugins.json` (a plugin's registered skill still said \"only the ping tool is available\" against a 40+ tool surface). → A recurring lint (not a one-time sweep): flag tool/skill descriptions that mention removed deps or name tools absent from the live `tools/list`; re-run so drift does not re-accumulate.\n\n## Relationship to PCB\n\nThe PCB-plugin-specific instances of (3),(4),(5),(6) plus the one-shot `load_board` fix and stale pcb-architect/Docker docs are tracked in the plugins repo bug (PLG) and must land in the plugin — PCB is being ejected from Minerva core, so no new PCB code belongs here. This item is the durable host-platform track.","created_at":"2026-07-17T22:21:10","updated_at":"2026-07-18T13:48:09","priority":3,"tags":["developer-experience","ergonomics","mcp","plugin-host","tooling"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f723f0f387105833a04deb2d450de","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Tool-surface policy by operating mode: native agent, external MCP server, and supervised pass-through","description":"## Why this discussion exists\n\nMinerva currently mixes several meanings of “available tools”:\n\n- tools registered in the global registry\n- tools indexed for search\n- tools active in the ToolBudgetManager\n- tools selected by a skill's `tool_deps`\n- tools allowed for a particular chat or worker\n- tools advertised through the external HTTP MCP `tools/list`\n- plugin tools proxied or supervised by Minerva\n\nThese layers do not currently form one coherent contract. The original sparse-tool work implemented search-based activation, but the external HTTP path was later deliberately changed to return the full catalog. Plugin tools are also registered as Minerva tools and therefore enlarge that external catalog. Skill preload and static-tool modes still work primarily for internal Minerva chats.\n\nThe underlying issue may be that Minerva has at least three distinct operating modes that need different tool-surface behavior rather than one global policy.\n\n## Scenario 1: Minerva as its own chatbot / agentic runtime\n\nMinerva owns the model request, prompt, chat history, compaction, tool loop, and per-chat state.\n\nLikely desired behavior:\n\n- Start with a very small discovery surface.\n- Keep `minerva_tool_search`, skill discovery/loading, and essential recovery/meta tools eager.\n- Activate tools progressively through search.\n- Let skills preload exact `tool_deps`.\n- Support focused/static chats whose tools are resolved before the first model turn.\n- Apply token budgets and LRU/idle eviction per chat, not accidentally across unrelated chats.\n- Reset or rehydrate the active set coherently after compaction.\n- Preserve policy-required tools even when pruning.\n\nOpen edge: the current ToolBudgetManager appears server-global while the intended behavior is often chat-scoped.\n\n## Scenario 2: Minerva as an MCP server used by Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, or another harness\n\nThe external harness owns its context, tool catalog snapshot, model loop, and possibly its own deferred-tool system.\n\nPossible policies:\n\n### A. Full catalog\n\nReturn every Minerva and plugin tool through `tools/list`.\n\nAdvantages:\n- Broad compatibility with clients that snapshot once and do not support dynamic list updates.\n- Every tool schema is immediately callable.\n\nCosts:\n- Hundreds or thousands of schemas become the default catalog.\n- Client-side context/deferred-tool implementations may still pay indexing and discovery costs.\n- Plugin installation continuously expands the apparent Minerva core surface.\n- Minerva's own search and skill-preload machinery becomes mostly decorative for these clients.\n\n### B. Sparse dynamic catalog\n\nInitially expose only discovery/meta tools; search or skill loading activates additional tools.\n\nRequirements:\n- Correct MCP `tools.listChanged` capability and `notifications/tools/list_changed` behavior.\n- Defined client refresh semantics and compatibility tests for Codex, Claude Code, and OpenCode.\n- Per-session active-tool state rather than one shared budget.\n- A recovery path for clients that do not react to list changes.\n- Clear behavior on plugin install/reload and MCP reconnect.\n\n### C. Stable sparse facade\n\nExpose a small fixed set of task-shaped/generic tools such as tool search, skill load, plugin help, generic panel open, and a generic dispatch/call operation.\n\nAdvantages:\n- Stable external surface without relying heavily on dynamic list refresh.\n- Plugin growth does not directly multiply eager tools.\n\nCosts:\n- Generic dispatch can weaken schemas, safety review, client UX, and tool-call accuracy.\n- May duplicate client-native deferred-tool systems.\n\nOpen edge: what is the correct standards-compatible fallback when a client cannot dynamically refresh tools?\n\n## Scenario 3: Minerva as a supervised or augmented pass-through\n\nMinerva brokers tools from plugins or other MCP servers to an external harness while adding policy, audit, knowledge, memory, transformation, or human supervision.\n\nThis is not identical to merely re-exporting every downstream tool.\n\nQuestions:\n\n- Does Minerva expose downstream tools individually, through namespaced facades, or through a broker/search/dispatch layer?\n- Is supervision performed before discovery, before execution, after execution, or at all three points?\n- Can policy hide a tool entirely, or only block/transform its calls?\n- Should Minerva enrich schemas/descriptions with prerequisites and workflow guidance?\n- Should skills define curated projections of downstream tools for a task?\n- How are downstream `tools/list_changed` notifications propagated?\n- What happens when Minerva is disconnected from a downstream server but still advertises its cached tools?\n- How are tool identity, provenance, capability grants, audit records, and result envelopes preserved across the proxy boundary?\n- Should external callers see Minerva-native augmentation tools alongside proxied tools, or opt into separate surfaces?\n\n## Cross-cutting distinctions that need explicit definitions\n\n1. **Registered** — known to Minerva's runtime.\n2. **Indexed** — discoverable through tool search.\n3. **Advertised** — present in a particular client's `tools/list`.\n4. **Activated** — selected into a particular session/chat's callable schema set.\n5. **Authorized** — permitted by policy for this caller and context.\n6. **Connected** — executable against a currently live backend.\n7. **Preloaded** — activated before the model's first execution turn, often through a skill.\n8. **Pinned/protected** — exempt from pruning.\n9. **Proxied** — owned elsewhere but exposed through Minerva.\n10. **Augmented** — description, schema, prompt guidance, result, or policy modified by Minerva.\n\nThese states should not be represented by one global registry flag.\n\n## Current archaeology to preserve\n\n- DCR `019d278a9d097bd382b4044b970c4501` defined search-based discovery + LRU management and described starting with only `minerva_tool_search`.\n- Commit `74d794dc` briefly applied the active ToolBudgetManager set to external HTTP `tools/list`.\n- Bug `019d45c972f57f22abe707074d15b95b` treated that external sparse behavior as a regression.\n- Commit `14dad528` removed sparse external listing and made external clients always receive the full catalog.\n- The HTTP server does not currently advertise/send dynamic tool-list change notifications.\n- Plugin tools are inserted into the central registry with `server_name=\"minerva\"` and an empty/default tool set, so they enlarge external `tools/list`.\n- `minerva_get_skill` still activates `tool_deps` in ToolBudgetManager.\n- Skill-configured focused workers can resolve an exact static tool set before execution.\n- Recent work item `019f722b573a78c99114db44e4c3fc1c` again identifies tool discovery at scale and recommends a lean eager surface plus search-on-demand.\n\n## Proposed reasoning sequence\n\n1. Define a first-class operating-mode/session model rather than a single global auto-tool-management switch.\n2. Specify the tool-state vocabulary above and ownership of each state.\n3. Define the desired contract for each of the three scenarios independently.\n4. Build a client capability/compatibility matrix for Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, and Minerva internal chats.\n5. Decide whether external sparse loading relies on MCP list-change notifications, a stable facade, client-native deferred loading, or negotiated capability profiles.\n6. Decide how skill preload projects tools into each mode.\n7. Decide how plugins and downstream MCP servers affect advertised catalogs.\n8. Define per-session isolation, reconnect behavior, cache invalidation, and stale-tool handling.\n9. Add contract tests before changing defaults.\n10. Only then split implementation into DCRs/bugs.\n\n## Initial position, intentionally non-binding\n\nThe three scenarios should probably share one canonical tool registry and policy engine but use different **projections** of that registry. Registration, search indexing, advertisement, activation, authorization, and execution should be separate stages. The projection should be selected per connection/chat/session, not through one application-global boolean.\n\nThis discussion is intended to accumulate reasoning, client observations, counterexamples, and decisions before implementation work is proposed.","created_at":"2026-07-17T22:42:43","updated_at":"2026-07-17T23:02:08","priority":2,"tags":["agent-ergonomics","architecture","discussion","mcp","supervision","tools"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f72b99b8f7961836068bc0cf2b13a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Merging Docs/minerva.dct: quit Docket, union-merge, then RE-VERIFY item statuses (they can lag on disk)","created_at":"2026-07-18T00:56:34","updated_at":"2026-07-18T00:56:34","tags":["dct","docket","git","gotcha","merge"],"value":"The Docket app (/home/imran/temp/Docket.x86_64, serves mcp__docket over http://localhost:3010) holds state in memory and flushes Docs/minerva.dct partially — appended events + resolutions + MOST item-record status updates flush, but some item records can LAG on disk (observed: a DCR left at 'proposed' on disk while its transition events + all child-task 'done' closures had materialized). SIGTERM does NOT force a full flush. Procedure to merge a diverged Docs/minerva.dct (both sides additive NDJSON): (1) back up on-disk .dct + `git show HEAD:` (base) + `git show @{u}:` (theirs); (2) quit the Docket app (kill -TERM its pid; confirm port 3010 free); (3) `git checkout HEAD -- Docs/minerva.dct` then `git merge --ff-only @{u}`; (4) `git merge-file -p --union <theirs-on-disk> <base> <mine>` → union (additive log, no real conflicts); (5) VALIDATE: every line parses as JSON (this also proves no conflict markers), sort|uniq == wc -l (no dup lines), both sides' ids present; (6) commit; (7) relaunch Docket: `DISPLAY=:1 setsid nohup /home/imran/temp/Docket.x86_64 &`; (8) CRITICAL — after restart, VERIFY item statuses via mcp__docket and RE-APPLY any that lagged (re-fire the transition chain), then re-commit. Don't trust the pre-quit on-disk item statuses.","component":"docket","key":"dct_merge_verify_item_status","research_cost":5}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f75a454ff7da9bafc2c46cd80892c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Restore editor→canonical-YAML export (the sync-back primitive dropped at cutover)","description":"## What's missing\n\nThe native PCBEditor exposed `minerva_pcb_export_yaml` (pre-cutover MCPPCBTools.gd:1533 → PCBEditor.export_yaml() → PCBData.to_yaml()) — an editor-scoped tool that returned the LIVE board as a canonical-YAML string. That was the primitive the source-of-truth workflow ran on: load YAML → iterate changes (human + LLM) → call export_yaml → agent writes the string back to board.yaml at batch end.\n\nThe plugin migration did NOT port it. Today the plugin has:\n- `pcb.serialize` (Go channel) that takes a board **passed in** (`{board: ...}`), not the live editor;\n- a UI-only Export button (PCBPanel.gd:1333-1344 emits pcb.serialize with `_data.to_board_dict()`).\n\nSo an agent CANNOT round-trip the live editor to canonical YAML — same root gap filed on the ergonomics item 019f722b (pcb_serialize is board-in, not editor-scoped).\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd an editor-scoped MCP tool (e.g. `minerva_pcb_export_yaml` / `minerva_pcb_serialize_board`): args `{editor_name, save_to_path?}`; finds the panel, reuses the EXISTING serialize path (`_data.to_board_dict()` → `pcb.serialize`), returns `{yaml}` (and writes to save_to_path when given). Mirrors the existing `export_csv` / `export_trace_geometry` editor-scoped exporters exactly.\n\n## Why it's here\n\nDeferred/dropped from the canonical-board-source DCR (this parent, 019eb47d) — the serialize CHANNEL shipped but the editor-scoped export tool that makes YAML usable as a durable source did not. This is incomplete implementation of 019eb47d, not new scope. Enables (blocks) the YAML-as-source-of-truth persistence work.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nLoad a board via load_board → move a component → call the tool → returned YAML reflects the move and round-trips back through load_board losslessly. Non-mocked: real panel boot + real pcb.serialize channel.","created_at":"2026-07-18T14:32:11","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:50:58","priority":2,"tags":["incomplete-impl","mcp","pcb","plugin","sync-back"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d36bb731396bfe1ae9f8a1c8f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f75a4a4e07c0895429a99fd32d58c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"YAML-as-source-of-truth persistence (.pcbskel = intermediate WIP) — realize the deferred paired_dsl decision","description":"## The decision that was deferred\n\n019eb47d (this parent) carried an explicit open question — *\"paired_dsl render_mode with YAML as the text half (CAD-symmetric, free buffer sync + user-editable source) vs single-view panel\"* — and resolved it as **\"Deferred by design: paired_dsl (revisit post-contract per gap register headline 2).\"** The pointer landed in the post-cutover gap list (019f6a69b8a2), which never captured it. So it fell through.\n\nOwner has now DECIDED the direction (2026-07-18): **board.YAML is the definitive source of truth; .pcbskel is an intermediate work-in-progress file.** That picks YAML-canonical / paired_dsl. This item realizes it.\n\n## Current (wrong) state\n\nSource of truth today is the board MODEL (PCBData), persisted as `.pcbskel` (JSON, host_owned save) + `.annotations.json` sidecar. YAML is one-way import only (load_board takes text, forgets the path). There is NO sync back to YAML. Placement/rotation/delete are DIRECT board-model mutations; only routing is annotation/sidecar-based.\n\n## Scope (paired_dsl / YAML-canonical)\n\n- Track the board's source YAML path; save/batch-end writes canonical YAML to it (built on the sync-back primitive — the sibling work item).\n- Adopt `render_mode: \"paired_dsl\"` (copy CAD's CADPanel attach_buffer/text_changed/detach_buffer + debounce + cancel-in-flight): open board.yaml → text editor (canonical) + PCB render panel sharing one DocumentBuffer; render compiles via pcb.deserialize. Import becomes free (opening = compiling).\n- `.pcbskel` demoted to intermediate/autosave WIP; define when each is written.\n- Distinct board-YAML extension for File→Open routing (extension resolves on last segment, so NOT `.pcb.yaml`; e.g. `.pcbyaml`) — `.yaml` cannot be claimed (core/shared, text editor owns it).\n\n## KEY OPEN SUB-DECISION (gates cost)\n\nUnder YAML-canonical, direct-manipulation PLACEMENT has nowhere clean to persist (the master is now the text buffer). Two paths:\n1. Text-authored placement (CAD parity) — cleanest, but canvas drag no longer persists; you edit x_mm/y_mm in text.\n2. Placement-override SIDECAR — canvas drag writes an override record (same pattern routing already uses); render = compile(YAML) ⊕ placement-overrides ⊕ routing-sidecar ⊕ annotations; optional \"bake overrides into YAML\" action. Keeps drag UX AND a clean hand-authored YAML.\n(Rejected: write-back to YAML via pcb.serialize on every canvas edit — destroys comments/ordering, two-master races.)\n\nThe hard part is NOT plumbing (paired_dsl copies CAD; save-to-source is easy) — it's (a) this placement decision and (b) comment/formatting preservation if the tool ever regenerates a hand-authored YAML.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nBest AFTER the PcbRoutingWorkspace DCR (019f7095c395) lands — its workspace + .routing.json sidecar substrate is exactly what the placement-override overlay reuses. Not scope-creep into that DCR; a follow-on completion of 019eb47d.","created_at":"2026-07-18T14:32:32","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:51:01","priority":2,"tags":["incomplete-impl","paired-dsl","pcb","persistence","plugin","sync-back"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d36bb731396bfe1ae9f8a1c8f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f75c24bd2757da83868910be6e101","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"load_board THT pads render as solid discs (no drill hole) — pad-type token \"tht\" vs canvas gate \"thru_hole\"","description":"## Symptom\nA board loaded via minerva_pcb_load_board (e.g. smart-remote-canonical.yaml) renders every through-hole pad as a solid gold/copper circle with NO drilled hole. THT pads should show an annulus (outer copper ring + inner hole).\n\n## Root cause (5-why, verified in-repo)\n1. Drill hole is drawn only when `is_tht` is true — pcb_canvas.gd:651 (`if is_tht:` draws inner `drill_hole_color` circle).\n2. `is_tht := pad_type in [\"thru_hole\", \"np_thru_hole\"]` — pcb_canvas.gd:627.\n3. The synthesized pad's type is \"tht\" — pcb_component.gd:727 (`pad[\"type\"] = \"tht\"` in `_pads_from_canonical_pins`, added by faa2ddc's load_board).\n4. \"tht\" is the setup_generic_pins SIZING token (pcb_component.gd:563/566), NOT a stored pad-type value. The model's documented pad-type vocabulary is \"smd\"/\"thru_hole\"/\"np_thru_hole\" (pcb_component.gd:79); the canvas and the native .pcbskel format both use \"thru_hole\" (confirmed: smart-remote.pcbskel pads carry type \"thru_hole\").\n5. So \"tht\" ∉ {\"thru_hole\",\"np_thru_hole\"} → is_tht=false → hole never drawn. (Color still resolves to pad_copper_color since \"tht\" is not \"smd\"/\"np_thru_hole\", so the pad is gold — just holeless.)\n\n## Fix (trivial)\nIn `_pads_from_canonical_pins`, emit `pad[\"type\"] = \"thru_hole\"` (and \"np_thru_hole\" for unplated) instead of \"tht\", matching the model's documented vocabulary + the canvas gate. Add a render/regression test with a drill-bearing pin and assert the hole draws (or assert the pad type string).\n\n## Why the earlier load_board test missed it\nFidelity test checked pad presence/counts over MCP; no MCP tool surfaces pad type or hole rendering (ergonomics gap 019f722b), and the render wasn't pixel-verified. Same class of blind spot.","created_at":"2026-07-18T15:04:55","updated_at":"2026-07-18T18:10:07","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["faa2ddc","load_board","pcb","plugin","regression","render"],"parent":"minerva:019f6a69b8a2","resolution":"Fixed minerva-plugins main @ 8ac60a6 (2026-07-18). _pads_from_canonical_pins now emits pad type \"thru_hole\" (was \"tht\"), matching the canvas drill-hole gate (pcb_canvas._draw_component_pads: type in [\"thru_hole\",\"np_thru_hole\"]) and the model's documented vocabulary. New regression test pcb/tests/gd/test_pad_synthesis.gd (8/8 green) pins the synthesized THT type to the canvas gate set so holes can't silently regress. Unit-verified via the gd scaffold harness. NOTE: in-app visual confirmation (holes render on a load_board board) rides the next app restart — the running session still has the pre-fix gd until redeploy; left at 'resolved' (not 'verified') until that visual check."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f75c2848a755e849c9dd01ffb4b42","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"load_board reconstructs component body from pin bounding box, not real footprint — canonical YAML carries no body/pad-shape geometry","description":"## Symptom\nLoading smart-remote-canonical.yaml via load_board: the whole design appears pulled in from the board edge; U1 (ESP32-S3-DevKitC) is no longer edge-aligned, making the USB connector position wrong. Reported by owner.\n\n## Root cause (5-why, verified in-repo)\n1. U1's rendered body is drawn from `local_bounds` (pcb_canvas.gd:549-555) — and local_bounds was fit to the PINS.\n2. `_pads_from_canonical_pins` runs `_fit_body_to_pads()`, recomputing local_bounds/bbox_center_offset from synthesized pad extents (pcb_component.gd, faa2ddc).\n3. It synthesizes because the canonical YAML carries only `footprint` (string) + `pins`; NO explicit `pads` shapes and NO `graphics` body outline. The native smart-remote.pcbskel DOES carry both `pads`+`graphics` (real KiCad geometry).\n4. The canonical board contract (board-yaml.md) models pin/fabrication geometry (drill/annulus/pad_w/h) but NOT footprint body outline / pad shapes. In the old flow that real geometry came from KiCad libs via import_footprint_geometry and was stored in .pcbskel — a step load_board never performs.\n5. So every component's silhouette becomes its pin box; parts whose real body/connector overhangs the pins (ESP32 USB edge, JST battery, pin-socket headers) pull in from the boundary.\n\n## PROOF it is NOT a coordinate offset\nU1 = x_mm 60.96, y_mm 63.5, rotation_deg 180 in BOTH smart-remote.pcbskel and smart-remote-canonical.yaml — byte-identical placement. The pads render at the same world coords in both; only the BODY geometry differs.\n\n## Consequence for YAML-as-source-of-truth (WI-2)\nThe canonical YAML is lossy on footprint body/pad-shape geometry. For YAML to be the definitive source of truth AND import to a correct layout, load must EITHER carry the geometry in the YAML (fat, hurts git-merge) OR re-resolve footprints from KiCad libraries on load (like KiCad itself references library footprints; reuses import_footprint_geometry / pcb.fetch_libraries). Blocks WI-2's correctness.","created_at":"2026-07-18T15:05:10","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:51:03","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["fidelity","geometry","load_board","pcb","plugin","yaml-source-of-truth"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d36bb731396bfe1ae9f8a1c8f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f75c87aab707894b63f0c9d8a6b75","type":"discussion","status":"resolved","title":"CONSULT: footprint-geometry resolution for YAML-as-source-of-truth (compile-on-load vs fat YAML vs cache)","description":"# CONSULT: footprint-geometry resolution for YAML-as-source-of-truth\n\n## 1. Background — goal and path to here\n\n**Goal (owner decision 2026-07-18):** `board.YAML` is the **definitive source of truth** for a PCB; `.pcbskel` is an **intermediate work-in-progress** file. From the YAML we must be able to (a) import to a **correct layout** in the editor, and (b) generate the **fab/KiCad export files**. The driving use-case is branch-based multi-editor work — two engineers, more likely two AGENTS on different git branches, both edit `board.YAML` and resolve via normal git text merge (works because serialization is deterministic — `board.go` sorts slices → byte-identical YAML). YAML drops comments/annotations by design; annotations live in the sidecar.\n\n**Path to here.** During the PCB plugin migration, `load_board` (minerva-plugins `faa2ddc`) added one-call canonical-YAML whole-board import. Testing the smart-remote board (`ccsandbox …/EDA/minerva-fab/smart-remote-canonical.yaml`) surfaced two defects:\n- **Bug 1 `019f75c24bd2`** (trivial, S): THT pads render as solid discs with no drill hole — `_pads_from_canonical_pins` emits pad type `\"tht\"`, but the canvas draws the hole only for `type in {\"thru_hole\",\"np_thru_hole\"}` (pcb_canvas.gd:627/651). One-token fix.\n- **Bug 2 `019f75c2848a`** (the crux, **blocks WI-2**): the whole design is pulled in from the board edge — U1 (ESP32-S3-DevKitC) is no longer edge-aligned, so the USB connector position is wrong. RCA: U1's position is **byte-identical** in `.pcbskel` and `.yaml` (60.96, 63.5, rot 180) — it is **NOT** a coordinate offset. The canonical YAML carries only a `footprint` *reference string* + `pins`; `load_board` therefore synthesizes each component body from its **pin bounding box** (`_fit_body_to_pads`), losing the real footprint outline. The native `.pcbskel` carried real `pads`+`graphics` populated in the old flow via `import_footprint_geometry` (from KiCad libs) — a step `load_board` never performs.\n\n**Related items.** Canonical contract DCR `019eb47d` (parent; done — deferred paired_dsl). YAML source-of-truth work: WI-1 `019f75a454ff` (editor→YAML export primitive) + WI-2 `019f75a4a4e0` (YAML-canonical persistence), sequenced as follow-ups AFTER the routing DCR `019f7095c395`. The pipeline we want:\n\n```\nboard.yaml            →   load = COMPILE                →   board model         →   exports\n(lean source of truth,   (parse + resolve footprints        (.pcbskel = RESOLVED    (.kicad_pcb, gerbers,\n git-mergeable)           from KiCad libs)                   WIP artifact)           Excellon…)\n```\n\nExport machinery mostly exists in the worker already: `pcb.generate` (kicad.py → .kicad_pcb/.sch/.pro), `pcb.gerbers` (Gerber+Excellon). They just need *resolved* geometry to be faithful.\n\n## 2. Problem statement — the choice needed\n\nThe canonical YAML deliberately carries **pins + a footprint reference**, not the body outline / pad shapes. So: **where does real footprint geometry come from at load**, such that the YAML stays lean + git-mergeable AND imports to a correct layout AND drives faithful exports? Pick the geometry-source architecture that becomes WI-2's load model:\n\n- **Option A — Compile-on-load.** `load_board` resolves each `footprint` ref from KiCad libraries (real pad shapes, drill, courtyard/body outline) — the KiCad model. Pin-box body becomes the **missing-lib fallback** + a `warnings[]` entry. Reuses `import_footprint_geometry` / `pcb.fetch_libraries` / `pcb.check_libraries`.\n- **Option B — Fat YAML.** Carry full pad/body geometry in the YAML file itself. Self-contained; no lib dependency.\n- **Option C — Two-tier.** Lean YAML source + a generated resolved-geometry cache sidecar (`<board>.geom.json`). (Note: Option A already produces `.pcbskel` as exactly this cache.)\n\nBug 1's token fix is independent (do regardless); this consult is only about the Bug 2 / geometry-source architecture.\n\n## 3. Rubric used + score table\n\nOwner's 7-axis rubric (Reliability = works now; Durability = works forever / standards / fewest assumptions; Performance; Debuggability; Cost = LoC T-shirt S≤100 / M 101–1000 / L>1000; Discoverable; User-visible = loud vs silent failure).\n\n| Axis | A — Compile-on-load | B — Fat YAML | C — Two-tier cache |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reliability (works now) | Med — needs libs present + ref-name match | **High** — self-contained | Med — first load resolves; cache coherence |\n| Durability (forever/standards) | **High** — is the KiCad model; lean mergeable source | Low — bloats mergeable source (kills merge driver); geometry duplicates libs → drift | Med — lean source but a cache to keep coherent |\n| Performance | Med — resolution per load (cached to .pcbskel) | Low — huge files, big diffs, memory | **High** — cached, fast/offline after first |\n| Debuggability | Med — extra resolve stage, each inspectable | **High** — what-you-see-is-what-loads | Low — 3 artifacts; stale cache = hard RCA |\n| Cost (LoC) | **M** — reuses import_footprint_geometry/lib subsystem | M — serialization plumbing | L — A's resolution + cache format + invalidation |\n| Discoverable | **High** — \"resolves footprints like KiCad\" is expected | Med — intent drowns in pad polygons | Med — extra .geom.json indirection |\n| User-visible (loud failure) | **High** — missing lib → warnings[] user sees | Med — wrong in-file geometry renders wrong, silently | Med — stale cache renders wrong, silently |\n\n**Recommendation: Option A. Decisive axis — Durability:** it embraces the KiCad standard (footprints referenced from libraries), keeping the YAML lean and git-mergeable — the exact property the source-of-truth decision depends on — while producing correct layouts; its one weakness (Reliability, missing-lib) is bounded and **loud** (a user-visible warning), with the pin-box body as graceful fallback.\n\nTwo reinforcing notes: **C collapses into A** (A's `.pcbskel` already IS the resolved cache C wants to add — C is \"A + a redundant second cache\"). **B's High marks are a trap** — it wins Reliability/Debuggability but its Low Durability negates the merge-across-branches reason YAML was chosen as source in the first place.\n\n## Consult ask\nReview the architecture choice (A vs B vs C) BEFORE WI-2 begins — same pattern as the routing-workspace consult (discussion `019f70683492`). Specifically: is compile-on-load / library resolution the right load model; what's the missing-lib UX contract; does resolved geometry belong in `.pcbskel` (WIP) or a separate cache; and does the export path (kicad.py / gerbers) need any change once geometry is resolved.","created_at":"2026-07-18T15:11:40","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:51:20","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","consult","geometry","load_board","pcb","plugin","yaml-source-of-truth"],"parent":"minerva:019eb47d36bb731396bfe1ae9f8a1c8f","resolution":"Owner selected A2 hermetic YAML-rooted compilation with plugin-owned native CAM. Plan of record is DCR 019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5 and its six staged work items. KiCad remains import/export/oracle, not CAM authority; .pcbskel remains WIP/recovery; fabrication fails closed on unresolved/unsupported geometry."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Hermetic PCB source compilation + plugin-owned CAM","description":"OWNER DECISION (2026-07-18): the PCB plugin owns CAM. Minerva core remains a generic host/broker and must not acquire PCB fabrication semantics.\n\nThis DCR is the implementation umbrella for the architecture settled in discussion 019f75c87aab707894b63f0c9d8a6b75:\n\n1. Canonical YAML is the durable root of the accepted board design. The reproducible unit is a YAML-rooted source package containing or immutably referencing project/library sources, locks, and fabrication profiles.\n2. .pcbskel is WIP/autosave/recovery state. It may contain unpublished placement/routing work and derived geometry; it is not required for a clean fabrication build and is not a disposable cache.\n3. YAML + locked Minerva libraries compile hermetically to ONE fabrication-complete ResolvedBoard IR. Canvas, router, DRC/DFM, KiCad export, and CAM consume that IR. Per-consumer reconstruction of pads/footprints is removed after parity.\n4. Missing or unsupported geometry may render as an explicitly degraded preview, but routing, DRC/DFM, and fabrication fail closed.\n5. CAM is a PCB-plugin capability: ResolvedBoard -> DFM/profile checks -> deterministic fabrication package. The plugin owns layer mapping, copper/mask/paste/silk/profile geometry, drills/slots, X2/job metadata, manifests/provenance, and supported-feature gates.\n6. Native CAM is the production direction. The existing Apache-2.0 gerber-writer path is prior art; a bounded spike evaluates Apache-2.0 Gerbonara as a unified Gerber + Excellon + IPC-356 emitter. KiCad CLI is an independent oracle/fallback test backend, not the product architecture and not embedded code.\n7. No FCIB: no third-party binaries are committed. Dependencies are pinned/fetched/built in packaging/CI with licenses/notices and hashes. KiCad library imports retain source-license/provenance; generated-board output exceptions do not erase obligations on redistributed converted library definitions.\n8. Initial supported envelope is intentionally bounded: two-layer smart-remote plus one real board-house/process profile, with structured errors outside the envelope. Arbitrary layer count is represented in contracts from the start; advanced fabrication features are enabled incrementally.\n\nORDER:\nStage 0 finish routing DCR 019f7095c395 so committed candidate copper has stable canonical identity.\nStage 1 native-writer/CAM coupon spike and supported-feature inventory.\nStage 2 fabrication-complete FootprintDefinition, layered library identity/lock/provenance, and ResolvedBoard IR contracts.\nStage 3 canonical board YAML v2 + v1 migration.\nStage 4 migrate canvas/router/DRC/export/CAM to the one IR; delete duplicate geometry reconstruction after parity.\nStage 5 productionize deterministic native CAM and first manufacturer DFM profile.\nStage 6 clean-checkout YAML-only fabrication gate and real viewer/board-house coupon.\nStage 7 Publish/Bake accepted WIP into YAML; editor->YAML export and .pcbskel recovery/rebase.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- A clean checkout with no .pcbskel/cache resolves locked dependencies and produces byte-reproducible fabrication outputs.\n- The editor, DRC/DFM, KiCad export, and native CAM agree on pad/trace/via/hole/outline geometry.\n- Unsupported or unresolved fabrication features produce structured blocking errors, never approximated copper.\n- The first coupon loads without warnings in independent viewers, passes the selected board-house profile, and is accepted/fabricated by the intended board house.\n- Third-party license inventory and no-FCIB checks pass in CI.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:45:49","updated_at":"2026-07-18T17:28:01","priority":2,"tags":["architecture","cam","fabrication","library","pcb","plugin","resolved-ir","yaml-source-of-truth"],"parent":"minerva:019dc140291979a49a8081bf91abe2ff","resolution":"Approved architecture: A2 hermetic YAML-rooted compilation to one fabrication-complete ResolvedBoard IR, with plugin-owned native CAM, KiCad as import/export/oracle, fail-closed fabrication, and no-FCIB dependency handling."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761fcfc37c328984714028abf6b7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"CAM spike: Gerbonara unified emitter vs current writer; KiCad as oracle","description":"Timeboxed implementation spike after the routing DCR. The backend decision is already made: CAM is native and plugin-owned. This spike selects the native emission stack and proves the first fabrication coupon.\n\nCompare:\n- Current gerber-writer 0.4.3.3 + plugin-owned Excellon.\n- Gerbonara (Apache-2.0) as unified Gerber/Excellon/IPC-356 writer and reader.\n- KiCad CLI output only as an independent geometry/interoperability oracle.\n\nCoupon must exercise: F/B copper, mask, silk, closed profile with cutout, arcs/regions, SMD + PTH + NPTH, via, plated/non-plated drill split, routed slot, X2 file/aperture/net attributes, layer/job manifest, deterministic timestamps/ordering.\n\nDeliver:\n- API/feature/license/dependency audit, including transitive licenses and no-FCIB packaging.\n- Same ResolvedBoard-shaped fixture through both candidate native paths.\n- Structural/round-trip comparison with PyGerber/Gerbonara plus KiCad viewer/CLI oracle.\n- Written decision: one production emitter, or a deliberately split writer with explicit ownership.\n\nDo not use unlicensed circuit-json-to-gerber source. Do not broaden into a second canonical IR.\n\nAcceptance: bounded coupon produces deterministic files, drill-to-copper registration is exact, slots/arcs/regions survive round-trip, X2 attributes are recognized, and unsupported writer features are listed as structured future gates.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:04","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:02:35","priority":1,"tags":["cam","excellon","gerber","ipc-356","license","pcb","plugin","spike"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5","resolution":"RATIFIED by owner 2026-07-18: adopt gerbonara 1.6.3 as the SINGLE unified native emitter (Gerber + Excellon incl. slots + IPC-356). KiCad stays a dev-only DRC oracle. Split option rejected (does not escape gerbonara writer bugs; buys only X2 .AperFunction, a non-blocker for JLC/OSHPark/PCBWay). Decision + evidence @ minerva-plugins main 780246c (pcb/spikes/cam/DECISION.md). Harness-verified: gerbonara coupon geometry-identical to blessed golden spike-gerber-v1 (modulo added pour), 0 registration violations, byte-identical re-emit, IPC-356/%TF round-trip. Cold adversarial review: SURVIVES (narrow, conditional). RATIFICATION CONDITIONS (carried into productionize 019f761fefae): (1) build Excellon/IPC-356 golden oracle before GA — blocked by slot-diff crash 019f7772eca0; (2) hard-pin 1.6.3 + upstream 3 writer-bug PRs + per-emit smoke test 019f7773257; (3) X2 aperture attrs = future gate, revisit only if a fab demands them. Both libs Apache-2.0, no FCIB (gerbonara pulls rtree native + quart, lazy at runtime, pinnable wheels mac/win/linux)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761fda747c7a9cee43927c9bb8b9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Canonical board YAML v2 + source-package locks and v1 migration","description":"Evolve canonical board YAML around the locked-library and ResolvedBoard contracts.\n\nScope:\n- Versioned document/provenance and coordinate/side/rotation conventions.\n- Stable IDs for components, nets, traces, vias, zones, mechanical and graphics entities.\n- Exact board profile/cutouts/slots, arbitrary physical stackup, accepted traces/vias/zones, board-level mask/silk/paste/mechanics, fabrication intent, and selected profile identity/digest.\n- Component references use Minerva library IDs + content locks; net membership references footprint pad numbers.\n- Deterministic serialization and normal git merge behavior.\n- v1 reader/migrator with explicit ambiguity/error reports; no silent conversion from pin-box geometry.\n- Clean source-package manifest/lock discovery.\n- Optional archival embedded form may be specified separately; it is not the normal authoring representation.\n\nTransient route candidates, DRC findings, selection, edit handles, undo history, and unpublished placement/routing remain outside YAML.\n\nAcceptance: v1 smart-remote migrates to v2, recompiles with no .pcbskel/cache, and the resulting ResolvedBoard is stable under serialize/parse and dependency re-resolution.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:06","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:48:49","priority":2,"tags":["migration","pcb","schema","source-package","yaml-source-of-truth"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761fe51874d49d2b714227156ca3","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"Define fabrication-complete FootprintDefinition, library lock, and ResolvedBoard IR","description":"Foundation contract now sized by the owner decision that the PCB plugin owns CAM.\n\nDefine:\n- FootprintDefinition sufficient for editor, router, DRC/DFM, KiCad export, and native CAM: pad/custom-polygon geometry, drills/slots/plating, copper/mask/paste participation and overrides, silk/fab/courtyard/keepouts, local transforms, provenance, and supported/unsupported markers.\n- Layered Minerva library resolution: shipped base -> user -> project -> WIP/discovery, with deterministic override rules. Published boards resolve only pinned content identities; ambient library order must not change an accepted board.\n- Lock/retention model: exact source/content hashes, converter/schema version, source license/attribution, durable source retention, and project-local overrides.\n- One immutable-ish ResolvedBoard IR carrying exact placed geometry, connectivity, stable entity IDs, arbitrary physical layer identities, stackup, accepted copper, holes/mechanics, fabrication intent, and source provenance.\n- Content-addressed disposable resolution cache keys. .pcbskel is WIP/recovery, not this cache.\n- Degraded-preview vs fabrication-blocking error contract.\n\nResolve the current two-authority problem: board pins identify nets/pad numbers; locked footprints own pad geometry. Compatibility pin coordinates may only be assertions during migration.\n\nAcceptance: smart-remote footprints resolve identically from a clean source package; asymmetric bottom-side parts mirror correctly; all consumers can express their needs without consulting raw YAML or independently reconstructing pads.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:09","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:21:17","priority":1,"tags":["contract","footprint","library","lockfile","pcb","provenance","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761fefae71e38e89f6059fe0f8a6","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Productionize deterministic plugin-native CAM backend and fabrication package","description":"Build the production CAM compiler from ResolvedBoard using the writer selected by the spike.\n\nInitial envelope:\n- Two copper layers while the contracts remain arbitrary-layer.\n- Copper, solder mask, paste where applicable, real silkscreen, profile/cutouts.\n- PTH/NPTH drills, vias, round/slot/routed holes.\n- Zones/planes, clearances, and thermals for the supported subset.\n- Gerber X2 layer/aperture/net attribution, Excellon, optional IPC-356, job/manifest and source provenance.\n- Stable filenames, units/origin/precision, deterministic ordering/timestamps, hashes, and package inventory.\n- Explicit structured unsupported-feature matrix; exporter never repairs or approximates accepted geometry.\n\nNo FCIB: dependencies are pinned and fetched/built in CI/package assembly; notices and hashes ship with the plugin.\n\nAcceptance: byte-reproducible output from a clean checkout, independent parser/viewer acceptance, exact overlay against ResolvedBoard, and parity against the KiCad oracle for the supported coupon.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:12","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:02:40","priority":2,"tags":["cam","determinism","fabrication","gerber","manifest","pcb","plugin"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f761ffa467658a76434d6cd740cba","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Migrate canvas, router, DRC/DFM, KiCad export, and CAM to ResolvedBoard IR","description":"Strangler migration from per-consumer geometry reconstruction to the single ResolvedBoard IR.\n\nSurfaces:\n- PCBData/panel/canvas rendering and identity hit-test.\n- Routing bridge, including existing accepted copper and physical layer/via spans.\n- Electrical/geometric DRC and manufacturer DFM attribution.\n- KiCad project export.\n- Native fabrication/CAM.\n\nDuring migration, run old/new paths in parity tests where possible. Remove simplified pad defaults, pin-box body synthesis for authoritative use, bottom-side non-mirroring, courtyard-as-silk fabrication output, and other duplicate reconstruction only after replacement parity.\n\nThe pin-box path remains explicit degraded preview only. Any fabrication/DRC call against unresolved or unsupported geometry fails closed with entity/source attribution.\n\nAcceptance: for the smart-remote fixture, each surface reports/uses identical pad centers/shapes, trace/via/hole geometry, outline, layer identity, and net ownership. No production consumer reads raw footprint names or rebuilds pad geometry from canonical pins.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:15","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:20:49","priority":2,"tags":["drc","migration","pcb","resolved-ir","router","single-authority"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f762004dc728295fec69c4bfe867c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"First manufacturer profile, clean-room fabrication gate, and physical coupon","description":"Productize one real board-house/process profile and prove the complete source-to-fabrication path.\n\nProfile separates:\n- Manufacturer hard minima/capabilities.\n- Board design/net-class rules.\n- Intentional CAM generation allowances.\n\nCover at least trace/space, copper-to-edge, drill/slot, annular ring, hole-to-copper, via/pad clearances, solder-mask expansion/dam, silkscreen clearances, supported layer count/thickness, and permitted via/hole types. Pin profile revision/content; never silently float board-house rules.\n\nCI gate starts from a clean checkout with board YAML/source package only and no .pcbskel/cache, resolves exact libraries, compiles ResolvedBoard, runs DRC/DFM, emits the fabrication package, and validates license/no-FCIB inventory.\n\nHITL gate: overlay all layers and drills in independent viewers, compare to editor intent, submit the coupon to the intended board house, record intake warnings/repairs, and update the profile/tests.\n\nAcceptance: zero unexplained viewer or board-house intake warnings, no silent CAM repair, and a successfully fabricated coupon or an owner-reviewed blocking report attributable to a declared unsupported feature.","created_at":"2026-07-18T16:47:17","updated_at":"2026-07-18T16:48:59","priority":2,"tags":["acceptance","board-house","dfm","fabrication","pcb","profile","test"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f763ca70677bd87f75702cd63da3b","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Board + assembly 3D model (STEP) export from ResolvedBoard — for enclosure CAD","description":"## Goal\nFrom board YAML → ResolvedBoard → a **3D model of the board with components mounted** (assembled STEP; VRML optional), for enclosure/mechanical CAD. Owner surfaced this: KiCad produces board 3D models (STEP/VRML) needed to design enclosures, and it was missing from the plan.\n\n## Why it matters (owner context 2026-07-18)\n- Enclosures are designed against the board's 3D model; without it there is no path from board → case.\n- Chicken-and-egg it resolves: the owner needs a **reference PCB** to 3D-model parts that lack models (joystick, OLED). A board 3D export that tolerates missing component models unblocks that — the placed board gives the reference to model the parts against.\n\n## Requirements\n- Library `FootprintDefinition` gains a **3D-model reference** (STEP/WRL path/handle) + mount transform (offset/rotation/side/standoff height). This is new library scope — coordinate with the FootprintDefinition/IR contract item `019f761fe518`.\n- Board substrate solid from outline + internal cutouts + stackup thickness (copper optional).\n- Place each component's 3D model at its resolved position/rotation/side/height → assembled STEP.\n- **Missing-model handling:** a component without a 3D model exports a **flagged bounding-box placeholder** (from courtyard/body + height), never a hard failure — this is what makes it usable as the reference-PCB the owner needs. (Distinct from fabrication fail-closed: mechanical preview may be approximate; it is not a manufacturing artifact.)\n\n## Backend\nKiCad already does this (`kicad-cli pcb export step`, StepUp). Since KiCad is already the CAM oracle, `kicad-cli` STEP export is the pragmatic first backend and/or oracle. A native STEP path is a later decision (needs a CAD kernel — likely OCCT via the CAD plugin's existing stack).\n\n## Cross-plugin synergy\nThe board STEP is the natural input to Minerva's **CAD plugin** for enclosure design (board → import → design case around it). This is a coherent Minerva story (PCB plugin emits mechanical; CAD plugin consumes it).\n\n## Sequencing\nConsumes the ResolvedBoard IR; needs the library's 3D refs. After the IR/library contract; can run parallel to CAM. Coupon/smart-remote acceptance should include a mounted-assembly STEP that imports cleanly into a CAD tool.","created_at":"2026-07-18T17:18:34","updated_at":"2026-07-18T17:18:34","priority":2,"tags":["3d","enclosure","mechanical","pcb","plugin","resolved-ir","step"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f763cdf5b78dab3d7b9ebf3e3ee4c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Assembly outputs (paste + BOM + CPL) + library part-identity/assembly metadata — pre-assembled board ordering (JLC/PCBWay)","description":"## Goal\nProduce the full **assembly** package from the ResolvedBoard so the owner can order **pre-assembled** boards (JLCPCB SMT / PCBWay assembly), not just bare boards. Owner (2026-07-18): \"close to P0/P1 for makers — having the board come pre-assembled is a huge win.\"\n\n## Outputs (in addition to bare-board Gerber/Excellon)\n- **Solder-paste Gerber** (top/bottom) — stencil apertures from pad paste geometry.\n- **BOM** — per-component value + package + manufacturer part number + distributor/assembler part number (LCSC/JLCPCB part # for JLC; PCBWay equivalent).\n- **CPL / pick-and-place** — component ref, x/y, rotation, side.\n\n## New library/contract scope\nThe `FootprintDefinition` + component instance must carry part identity + assembly metadata, coordinated with contract item `019f761fe518`:\n- MPN + distributor/assembler part number(s).\n- Package/pin-1/polarity.\n- **Assembly rotation convention** — first-class and explicit. KNOWN GOTCHA: JLCPCB's internal library rotation frequently differs from the KiCad/footprint rotation; blindly exporting placement rotation mis-places polarized/multi-pin parts. Model assembly rotation separately from fab rotation and validate.\n\n## Per-house capability (profile-driven)\nAssembly is a per-manufacturer capability flag: **JLC and PCBWay assemble; OSHPark is bare-board only.** DFM/output must report assembly availability per profile and refuse to emit an assembly package targeted at a house that doesn't offer it.\n\n## Sequencing\nDepends on the bare-board CAM path working (can't assemble a board you can't fabricate). Sequence after/alongside production CAM `019f761fefae`. The library part-identity fields must land in the FootprintDefinition contract from the start (even if assembly emit is later) so parts aren't re-modeled.\n\n## Acceptance\nSmart-remote (a real board the owner will actually build + use with Minerva) produces an assembly package that JLC accepts and fabricates+assembles correctly, with polarized parts (OLED header orientation, JST, buttons) in the right rotation.","created_at":"2026-07-18T17:18:48","updated_at":"2026-07-18T17:18:48","priority":2,"tags":["assembly","bom","cam","cpl","jlcpcb","pcb","pcbway","plugin","smt"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7644892a78a181a03c7b64f18402","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"RF/EMC-aware DFM design-assist — antenna keepout, return-path/plane-split detection, ground stitching (design-assist layer, not v1)","description":"## Goal\nAn RF/EMC-aware DFM/design-assist layer that helps avoid the noise/antenna problems the owner flagged on smart-remote (ESP32-S3 onboard antenna + GND plane) and will need again on the air-quality sensor's analog front-end.\n\n## Scope (design-assist intelligence — distinct from the v1 geometry primitives)\n- **Antenna keepout** from the module/part datasheet: no copper (incl. GND pour), no parts, ideally a board cutout under/around the antenna; flag violations.\n- **Return-path / plane-split detection:** warn when a GND-pour keepout or slot crosses *under* a signal trace (return current detours around the gap → radiating loop). This is the real \"accidental antenna\" mechanism, not the traces themselves.\n- **Ground stitching:** suggest/auto-place stitching vias (board edge + RF-section boundary + near high-speed nets).\n- **Direct GND-pad-to-plane:** flag long GND stubs; prefer direct/thermal-relief pad-to-plane.\n- **RF net classes / impedance rules** where used.\n\n## Relationship to v1\nv1 (pours + zone keepout + board cutout + rule-area primitives, under items 019f761fe518 / 019f761fda74 / 019f761fefae) gives a human or agent the *primitives* to do RF layout by hand. THIS item adds the automation/checks on top. It depends on those primitives and is explicitly deferred (priority 3 / \"broaden from demand\").\n\n## Drivers\n- smart-remote: ESP32-S3 antenna keepout + GND plane without accidental antennas (owner 2026-07-18).\n- air-quality sensor (future roadmap board): analog sensor front-end noise/ground management.","created_at":"2026-07-18T17:27:10","updated_at":"2026-07-18T17:27:10","priority":3,"tags":["design-assist","dfm","emc","future","pcb","plugin","rf"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7710cb1272c6afef2bd9a7df5e06","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Verification backbone — golden oracle harness on the CURRENT exporter (pin before migrate)","description":"STEP 1 of the refined hermetic-CAM sequence (owner+Fable-blessed 2026-07-18; no Codex round). This is the reliability lever the whole DCR subtree inherits: build a durable, seconds-latency verification harness that PINS the current exporter's behavior with an INDEPENDENT oracle BEFORE any emitter/IR migration. Then the CAM spike (019f761fcfc3), the ResolvedBoard IR (019f761fe518), the consumer migration (019f761ffa46), and native CAM (019f761fefae) are all REFACTORS UNDER GREEN — not big-bang rewrites verified by eyeball. Feedback-loop economics: a copper/geometry error caught by a local oracle costs seconds+~$0; caught by a fabricated board costs 1–4 weeks + $. Push detection to the cheapest loop.\n\nBLOCKS the CAM spike 019f761fcfc3 — the emitter comparison (Gerbonara vs current writer) should run UNDER this harness, not re-invent oracle plumbing inside the spike.\n\nKICAD IS DEV/CI-ONLY (owner rule, 2026-07-18): kicad-cli may appear ONLY in tests/dev harnesses. Every user-facing capability must have a native tier; KiCad presence may ENHANCE, never ENABLE, a shipped feature. Users of the PCB plugin are NOT assumed to have KiCad. Reliability transfers to KiCad-less users via DETERMINISM: what passed the oracle in CI byte-for-byte equals what the user's machine emits from the same inputs. This item makes that guarantee testable.\n\nTHREE DELIVERABLES (filed as children SB.1/SB.2/SB.3):\n1. Oracle + reader foundation: pin gerbonara (Apache-2.0, pip into worker venv — fetched/hashed, no FCIB) as the unified Gerber+Excellon+IPC-356 reader; kicad-cli DRC oracle that round-trips current export → KiCad → DRC and asserts clean; gerbonara round-trip parse of the current spikes/gerber output.\n2. Golden geometry-diff harness + GOLDEN PROVENANCE (anti-circularity): structured Gerbonara-parsed diff of current-export vs committed golden. CRITICAL: byte-reproducibility proves DETERMINISM, not CORRECTNESS — a golden-filed buggy exporter makes green meaningless. So every golden must be blessed ONCE by an EXTERNAL oracle (independent viewer: gerbv / JLC or OSHPark online viewer, and/or kicad-cli DRC on the round-tripped board) and its provenance recorded alongside it. The work-cycle CANNOT self-certify a golden — this is a mandatory owner HITL stop.\n3. Guard tests: (a) determinism gate — named test, same input → byte-identical output across two runs (load-bearing for the KiCad-less-user story); (b) kicad-cli boundary lint — standing CI check that no user-facing code path (plugin .gd, worker runtime methods) shells to kicad-cli; it may live only under tests/ and dev harnesses.\n\nEXECUTION: work-cycle (autonomous/headless-provable) EXCEPT the golden-blessing HITL stop in SB.2. Model policy: OPUS implementer here (wrong golden = harness-wide false floor, highest blast radius) + Opus cold review; the GATE 019f70f76c2f review rule (multi-pad-net + undo-after-commit fixtures) does not apply (no routing surface) but the anti-circularity control replaces it as this item's floor.\n\nPRECONDITIONS ON THIS MACHINE (verified 2026-07-18): kicad-cli 9.0.7 on PATH; gerbonara NOT yet in worker venv (SB.1 pins it); prior art at ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/spikes/gerber/{generate,validate}.py (gerber_writer 0.4.3.3) + worker/pcb_worker/{gerber,drc,kicad}.py + worker/agent_router/kicad_io.py.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:10:17","updated_at":"2026-07-18T22:38:25","priority":1,"tags":["cam","determinism","gerber","golden","oracle","pcb","plugin","testing","verification-backbone"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5","resolution":"VERIFICATION BACKBONE COMPLETE (2026-07-18). All three children shipped to minerva-plugins main + owner-blessed. Commits: SB.1 e33ee3d (gerbonara reader + dev-only kicad-cli DRC oracle), SB.3 afbce24 (determinism gate + kicad-cli boundary lint), SB.2 2a201a4 + bless 24c8e08 (golden geometry-diff + provenance; spike-gerber-v1 blessed via gerbv walkthrough). Final suite: 314 passed + 1 xfail (the tracked emitter pad bug). All Opus-implemented + Opus cold-reviewed. Owner rules held: kicad-cli dev/CI-only (boundary-lint enforced), gerbonara pip-pinned no-FCIB, determinism byte-reproducible. This item BLOCKS→ now unblocks the CAM spike 019f761fcfc3, which runs UNDER this harness. Bugs surfaced by the backbone on day one: 019f7736b236 (emitter placeholder SMD pads, fix vehicle FootprintDefinition/IR Stage 2) + 019f7720928d (Excellon G90/G05-after-header). Next plugin-track step: CAM spike 019f761fcfc3."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7711038972ada7e5478ba430dfb1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Smart-remote breadboard bring-up — wiring spreadsheet + peripheral bring-up firmware + physical verification (GATE before board fab)","description":"HARD GATE before the smart-remote board is sent to JLC (owner, 2026-07-18): \"no point in getting a defective board made.\" Validate the CIRCUIT DESIGN — component choices, netlist/wiring, firmware, and peripheral bring-up (joystick, speaker, buttons, small OLED) — on real components on a breadboard BEFORE committing the netlist to a fabricated PCB. This de-risks the schematic itself, which is UPSTREAM of routing; it therefore runs as an INDEPENDENT TRACK, in parallel with the plugin verification/CAM work, gated only on a settled schematic + parts in hand.\n\nNOT plugin work — this is smart-remote product hardware. Filed in the minerva product project, not under the hermetic-CAM plugin DCR.\n\nINPUTS (all on this laptop, ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote):\n- SCHEMATIC = source of the wiring table: EDA/smart-remote.kicad_sch (+ smart_remote_board.kicad_pcb / _routed). The MCU-pin→peripheral-pin map derives from the schematic netlist.\n- FIRMWARE: ESP32-S3 / ESP-IDF (PlatformIO). Two trees: firmware/ and firmware-minerva/ (sdkconfig.esp32s3, platformio.ini, managed_components). Bring-up firmware should reuse the existing peripheral drivers/managed_components where present.\n- FLASH + VERIFY tooling: tools/serial-mcp/ in-repo, plus this session's pio_build / pio_upload / serial_* MCP tools.\n\nDELIVERABLES:\n1. WIRING SPREADSHEET (owner-facing): one row per connection — ESP32-S3 pin / GPIO → peripheral + peripheral pin → bus (I2C/SPI/ADC/DAC/PWM/GPIO) → notes (pull-ups, level, power rail). Derived from smart-remote.kicad_sch so it matches the board that will be fabricated. This is what the owner physically wires.\n2. BRING-UP FIRMWARE: exercises every peripheral with a serial-reported self-test — OLED (likely SSD1306/I2C) draws a test pattern; joystick reports live ADC/direction; each button reports press events; speaker plays a tone (DAC/PWM). Flash via pio_upload; results read over serial.\n3. PHYSICAL VERIFICATION (owner HITL): owner wires the breadboard per the spreadsheet, flashes, runs the self-test, confirms each peripheral. Only on all-green does smart-remote fabrication proceed.\n\nCONFIRM FIRST (before generating): actual MCU variant + exact peripheral part numbers (OLED controller, joystick module, speaker/amp) from the schematic — do not guess pinouts; read them from smart-remote.kicad_sch.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:10:31","updated_at":"2026-07-18T21:10:31","priority":1,"tags":["breadboard","esp32s3","firmware","gate","hardware","smart-remote","verification"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f77114a877dce985843f77d1261a1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"SB.1: Oracle + reader foundation — pin gerbonara, kicad-cli DRC oracle, round-trip parse of current export","description":"First backbone deliverable. Stand up the independent-oracle plumbing the rest of the harness (SB.2/SB.3) and the CAM spike build on.\n\nWORK:\n- Pin gerbonara (Apache-2.0) into the worker venv via pyproject/lock — fetched + hashed at install, NOT committed (no FCIB). It is the unified Gerber + Excellon + IPC-356 READER for the harness.\n- kicad-cli DRC oracle (DEV-ONLY): a test helper that round-trips the current export → a KiCad board → runs `kicad-cli pcb drc` → asserts clean (or captures the finding set). Lives under tests/ or a dev harness dir; never imported by runtime code.\n- gerbonara round-trip parse: parse the current spikes/gerber output (gerber_writer 0.4.3.3) with gerbonara and assert it reads back without error — proves the reader works on OUR writer's bytes, the precondition for SB.2's diff.\n\nACCEPTANCE: gerbonara importable in the worker venv + recorded in the lock with license/hash; a dev-only kicad-cli DRC helper runs green on a known-good fixture; current export round-trips through gerbonara clean. kicad-cli invoked only from test/dev code (SB.3 lints this).\n\nPRECONDITIONS (this machine): kicad-cli 9.0.7 on PATH; gerbonara not yet installed; prior art at ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/spikes/gerber/ + worker/pcb_worker/{gerber,kicad}.py.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:10:49","updated_at":"2026-07-18T21:27:23","priority":1,"tags":["cam","gerber","gerbonara","kicad-cli","oracle","pcb","plugin","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019f7710cb1272c6afef2bd9a7df5e06","resolution":"SB.1 shipped @ minerva-plugins main e33ee3d. gerbonara==1.6.3 pinned (dev extra, additive over pygerber for Excellon+IPC-356); dev-only kicad-cli DRC oracle (tests/oracle/kicad_drc.py, real subprocess, boundary-clean from runtime); gerbonara round-trip of real emitter output incl. Excellon drills (asserts PTH=2/NPTH=1 + diameters). Layer-1: oracle 5/0 (non-mocked floor), test_gerbers 17/0, full suite 294/0. Cold Opus review: approve_with_notes (no blockers; two non-blocking notes: independent-reader overlap justified, DRC bad-board case mildly KiCad-ruleset-dependent)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f77117376736691a433b68580900c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"SB.2: Golden geometry-diff harness + golden provenance (anti-circularity — external bless before trust)","description":"Second backbone deliverable, and the single most important control in step 1. Builds the golden geometry-diff the whole subtree regresses against — and the discipline that keeps it honest.\n\nWORK:\n- Structured geometry diff: gerbonara-parse both the current export and a committed golden, compare at the geometry level (apertures, pads, traces, drills, regions, outline, drill-to-copper registration) — NOT byte-compare (that's SB.3's determinism gate). Report structured deltas.\n- GOLDEN PROVENANCE protocol: byte-reproducibility proves DETERMINISM, not CORRECTNESS. A golden-filed BUGGY exporter makes every downstream green meaningless (the harness would \"verify\" the bug). Therefore every golden is blessed ONCE by an EXTERNAL oracle before it is trusted: load the Gerbers in an independent viewer (gerbv / JLC or OSHPark online viewer) and/or run kicad-cli DRC on the round-tripped board; record HOW each golden was blessed (viewer, date, result) in a provenance file committed beside the golden.\n\nMANDATORY HITL STOP: the work-cycle produces the harness + a golden CANDIDATE + the provenance stub, then STOPS. The owner blesses the golden externally; only then is it promoted to trusted. The work-cycle may NOT self-certify a golden green on its own authority.\n\nACCEPTANCE: geometry diff detects an injected perturbation (add/move a pad → non-empty structured delta); at least one golden exists WITH recorded external-bless provenance; a golden with no provenance entry is treated as untrusted by the harness. Depends on SB.1 (gerbonara reader).","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:11:00","updated_at":"2026-07-18T22:37:57","priority":1,"tags":["anti-circularity","cam","gerber","golden","hitl","pcb","plugin","provenance","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019f7710cb1272c6afef2bd9a7df5e06","resolution":"SB.2 complete @ minerva-plugins main 2a201a4 (mechanism) + 24c8e08 (owner bless). Golden geometry-diff + provenance protocol shipped; owner independently blessed spike-gerber-v1 via gerbv layer-by-layer walkthrough of all 8 files vs stated intent (a real external bless, not self-cert). Golden is now the trusted correctness oracle. Anti-circularity regression preserved (unblessed→not-oracle re-anchored on the permanently-unblessed emitter snapshot). Day-one payoff: caught emitter pad bug 019f7736b236 (placeholder pads) — now an xfail(strict) correctness assertion that auto-flips to a live pass when Stage 2 fixes the emitter. Layer-1: oracle 18/0 +1 xfail, full suite 314/0 +1 xfail. Cold Opus review approve_with_notes (divergence independently reproduced)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f771196aa7ced9973f392d19a8cd3","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"SB.3: Guard tests — determinism gate (byte-identical) + kicad-cli dev-only boundary lint","description":"Third backbone deliverable: the two standing guards that protect the load-bearing invariants of the whole hermetic-CAM story.\n\nWORK:\n- DETERMINISM GATE: a named CI test that emits fabrication output twice from the SAME input and asserts BYTE-IDENTICAL results (normalize only legitimately non-deterministic fields — timestamps must be fixed/injected, ordering stable). This is load-bearing: the entire \"users don't need KiCad\" argument rests on determinism — what passed the oracle in CI byte-equals what the user's machine emits. Make it a specific guard, not a general assertion.\n- KICAD-CLI BOUNDARY LINT: a standing CI check that no user-facing code path — plugin *.gd and worker runtime methods (worker/pcb_worker/methods.py etc.) — shells to kicad-cli. It may appear ONLY under tests/ and dev-harness dirs. Enforces the owner rule (2026-07-18): KiCad is dev/CI-only; every user-facing capability has a native tier; KiCad may enhance, never enable, a shipped feature. Boundaries erode silently without a check.\n\nACCEPTANCE: determinism test is green and FAILS if a non-fixed timestamp/ordering is introduced (prove by temporarily unpinning one); boundary lint is green now and FAILS if a kicad-cli call is added to a runtime path (prove with a temp violation). Depends on SB.1.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:11:09","updated_at":"2026-07-18T21:36:02","priority":1,"tags":["boundary-lint","cam","ci","determinism","kicad-cli","pcb","plugin","testing"],"parent":"minerva:019f7710cb1272c6afef2bd9a7df5e06","resolution":"SB.3 shipped @ minerva-plugins main afbce24. Determinism gate (raw byte-identity across two real emissions; no normalization — finding: emitter already byte-deterministic, dates pinned to 1970-01-01 w/ injection point) + kicad-cli boundary lint (greps pcb_worker/agent_router/pcb-ui-gd for kicad-cli calls + oracle imports; non-empty-scan guard). Both teeth-proven + reverted clean. Layer-1: guards 7/0, full suite 301/0. Cold Opus review approve_with_notes — non-blocking durability note: .gd scan hardcodes pcb/ui as the only runtime .gd tree, so a future runtime .gd outside pcb/ui would be silently unscanned (revisit if runtime .gd moves)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7720928d73f0bb16a7f8293a6341","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Excellon drill files place G90/G05 AFTER the M48 header terminator (%) — strict CAM parsers may reject","description":"Surfaced by the SB.1 verification-backbone oracle (docket 019f77114a87) on 2026-07-18 — first latent output issue the harness caught, exactly its purpose.\n\nSYMPTOM: gerbonara emits a non-fatal SyntaxWarning parsing the worker's Excellon output: `drilltest-NPTH.drl:10 \"G90\": G90 header statement found after end of header` (also G05). The exporter writes the M48 header, terminates it with `%`, then emits G90 (absolute) / G05 (drill mode) AFTER the terminator instead of within the header/body per Excellon convention.\n\nIMPACT: gerbonara parses correctly (hole counts + diameters verified right), so it is cosmetic for OUR reader. BUT strict board-house CAM importers are less forgiving than gerbonara — this is a latent DFM/interop risk for real fabrication (JLC/OSHPark/PCBWay). Must be validated against an independent viewer as part of SB.2 golden provenance (an external viewer may flag or mis-parse it).\n\nFIX LOCATION: runtime `pcb/worker/pcb_worker/gerber.py` (the hand-written Excellon emitter) — OUT of SB.1's test-only fence, hence filed not fixed. Move G90/G05 to the correct position relative to the M48 header/`%` terminator.\n\nVERIFY: after fix, gerbonara round-trip emits no SyntaxWarning AND the drill file loads clean in gerbv / a board-house online viewer. Good candidate to fold into the SB.2 golden-provenance external-bless step, or the CAM spike (019f761fcfc3) native-emitter work which may replace this hand-written path entirely.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:27:31","updated_at":"2026-07-18T21:27:31","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["dfm","excellon","gerber","pcb","plugin","surfaced-by-oracle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7736b236765690bb39680a4b4bb9","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"Gerber emitter emits placeholder SMD pad geometry (1.0x0.6mm) instead of real footprint pads (0805 = 1.2x1.3mm)","description":"Surfaced by the SB.2 golden geometry-diff / anti-circularity control (docket 019f77117376) on 2026-07-18, and INDEPENDENTLY confirmed in cold review by re-parsing with gerbonara. This is the control working exactly as designed: a byte-determinism gate alone would never catch this — a reproducibly-WRONG exporter is still wrong.\n\nSYMPTOM: the real emitter (pcb_worker/gerber.py, build_gerbers) diverges from the independent spike golden (pcb/spikes/gerber/golden/, real KiCad-derived 0805 footprints) by 56 geometry deltas, confined to F_Cu / F_Mask / F_SilkS. SMD pads emit as placeholder DEFAULT_SMD_PAD_* 1.0x0.6mm rectangles instead of the real 0805 land pattern 1.2x1.3mm. Drills and drill-to-copper registration ALREADY agree (this is purely the SMD land geometry).\n\nROOT CAUSE: board.yaml pins carry no pad geometry, so gerber.py falls back to a generic placeholder pad size. Cross-ref memory project_pcb_load_board (SMD pad dims are supposed to be first-classed + pads synthesized from canonical pins) — the load path first-classes pad dims but the canonical YAML round-trip / emitter path does not carry them through to Gerber emission.\n\nIMPACT: fabrication-correctness. A real board fabricated from this path gets wrong SMD land geometry → poor/failed solder joints. This is not cosmetic.\n\nFIX VEHICLE: the fabrication-complete FootprintDefinition + ResolvedBoard IR work (docket 019f761fe518, hermetic-CAM Stage 2) — pads should be resolved from locked library footprints into the IR, and the emitter consumes IR geometry, not placeholder defaults. This bug is the concrete first instance that item must resolve; do NOT patch a placeholder-size guess into gerber.py.\n\nGATES: (a) the first REAL smart-remote fabrication (wrong pads = defective board); (b) SB.2's correctness-oracle test — it stays skipped/xfail against the blessed golden until the emitter emits real pad geometry, at which point the regression goes green. VERIFY-ON-FIX: emit → SB.2 geometry diff vs the (blessed) spike golden → empty delta.","created_at":"2026-07-18T21:51:41","updated_at":"2026-07-19T02:34:12","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["fabrication-correctness","footprint","gerber","pcb","plugin","smd-pad","surfaced-by-oracle"],"resolution":"FIXED at Stage 2 Step 4a-ii (commit minerva-plugins main 0223b04). Root cause (emitter fell back to a placeholder SMD pad size because canonical pins carry no pad geometry) removed: gerber/kicad SMD placeholders DELETED; the fab path resolves pad geometry from the locked seed-library footprints by default (methods gate ON, best-effort) and pad_source.iter_pads(require_smd_size=True) FAILS CLOSED (PadGeometryError) rather than fabricating a placeholder land. Emitter now consumes resolved footprint geometry, exactly the FIX VEHICLE this bug prescribed (resolve into the board, emitter reads it — NOT a placeholder-size guess in gerber.py)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7772eca0797c86bab9c17fe6c241","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"SB.2 geometry_diff.parse_drill_file crashes on routed slots (assumes every Excellon object is a Flash)","description":"Surfaced by the CAM emitter spike (019f761fcfc3) 2026-07-18. In pcb/worker/tests/oracle/geometry_diff.py, parse_drill_file assumes every parsed Excellon object is a point drill (Flash, reads o.x/o.y). A routed slot parses as a Line (has x1/y1/x2/y2, no .x) → crash. So the harness cannot geometry-diff any drill file containing a routed slot.\n\nIMPACT: blocks building an Excellon/slot golden oracle — which is a RATIFICATION CONDITION of the CAM emitter decision (the harness currently proves only Gerber copper; the differentiating features gerbonara was chosen for — slots, plating-split — have no blessed oracle yet because the diff strips/crashes on slots).\n\nFIX: handle Line/slot objects in parse_drill_file (model slot as start+end+width, diff accordingly). Then a slot-bearing coupon can be blessed and the CAM decision's slot/Excellon claims get real oracle backing. Fence note: geometry_diff.py is shared harness code under pcb/worker/tests — the spike left it untouched (out of its fence).","created_at":"2026-07-18T22:57:28","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:19:31","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["excellon","geometry-diff","harness","pcb","plugin","slot","surfaced-by-spike"],"resolution":"Fixed @ minerva-plugins main 6a5c217. geometry_diff.parse_drill_file now handles routed slots (Line → (\"slot\", sorted endpoints, width) key, order-independent, collision-free vs point drills; registration check skips slots only) + new IPC-356 netlist diff. 9 non-mocked teeth tests; full suite 323/0 + 1 xfail. Cold Opus review: approve. Unblocks HC-golden 019f777e9206."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7773257174d6bfecd6154467f0bf","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"gerbonara 1.6.3 writer bugs — pin + local workarounds + upstream 3 PRs (CAM adoption condition)","description":"RATIFICATION CONDITION of the CAM emitter decision (spike 019f761fcfc3): if gerbonara becomes the production emitter, these three real writer bugs in the pinned 1.6.3 (reproduced independently in adversarial review, WITHOUT workarounds) must be pinned-around locally AND fixed upstream. They sit in exactly gerbonara's differentiating features (IPC-356 + plating-split), so they are the durability tax of the choice.\n\nBUGS (all in gerbonara 1.6.3):\n1. ipc356.TestRecord: ctor field misspelled `lefover` but format() reads `self.leftover` (ipc356.py:416 vs :521) → AttributeError on a fresh Netlist.write. Workaround: set record.leftover = ''.\n2. Netlist.write_to_bytes (:128) and ExcellonFile.split_by_plating (excellon.py:428) crash on import_settings=None (None.copy()). Workaround: always pass explicit FileSettings.\n3. Mixed-plating in one Excellon file falls back to non-standard Altium `;TYPE=` comments (SyntaxWarning). Workaround: use the 2-file PTH/NPTH split (incumbent convention).\n\nACTIONS: (a) hard-pin gerbonara==1.6.3; (b) encapsulate the 3 workarounds in the production emitter with a comment linking this item; (c) file 3 upstream PRs; (d) add a per-emit smoke test that would catch these regressing. Workarounds confirmed THIN (instance-attr set + explicit settings), not reimplementation. Fix vehicle: native-CAM productionize 019f761fefae.","created_at":"2026-07-18T22:57:42","updated_at":"2026-07-18T22:57:42","priority":2,"tags":["adoption-condition","cam","excellon","gerbonara","ipc-356","pcb","plugin","upstream"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7773422273db8b9c6d826c9f020c","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"kicad-cli DRC oracle can't be imported/run outside worker tests (agent_router.layers import via pcb_worker.kicad)","description":"Surfaced by the CAM spike (019f761fcfc3) 2026-07-18. The SB.1 dev-only kicad-cli DRC oracle (tests/oracle/kicad_drc.py) imports pcb_worker.kicad, which transitively imports agent_router.layers; running it from outside the normal worker-tests context (e.g. the spike venv/path) fails with an import error. Pre-existing, emitter-agnostic (did NOT affect the CAM writer choice).\n\nAlso noted during the spike: the kicad-cli oracle DRCs the CANONICAL board via the worker's KiCad emitter, not the CAM Gerber/Excellon output — so it does not discriminate between candidate CAM writer paths. That's by-design (it's an independent board-level DRC), but worth documenting so nobody expects it to validate emitted Gerbers directly.\n\nFIX: make the oracle import path robust (or document the required sys.path/module context), so it can be invoked from harness scripts beyond tests/. Low priority; harness robustness.","created_at":"2026-07-18T22:57:50","updated_at":"2026-07-18T22:57:50","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["harness","kicad-cli","oracle","pcb","plugin","surfaced-by-spike"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f777e92067051884219d25aa275a0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Excellon/slots/IPC-356 golden oracle — coupon + candidate + provenance + owner bless (CAM ratification condition 1)","description":"Satisfies CAM emitter-decision ratification CONDITION 1 (spike 019f761fcfc3 / productionize 019f761fefae): the SB.1-3 harness currently proves only Gerber COPPER against a blessed golden; the differentiating features gerbonara was chosen for — routed slots, plated/non-plated Excellon split, IPC-356 netlist — have NO blessed correctness oracle yet. Build one.\n\nDEPENDS ON: slot-diff fix 019f7772eca0 (geometry_diff must handle routed slots + IPC-356 diffing before a slot/netlist golden can be diffed/blessed).\n\nWORK:\n1. Extend/confirm the geometry diff covers drills-with-slots and IPC-356 records (via 019f7772eca0).\n2. Emit an Excellon+slots+IPC-356 coupon via the RATIFIED gerbonara path (reuse pcb/spikes/cam/gerbonara_coupon.py) — real PTH/NPTH drills, a routed slot, and an IPC-356 netlist.\n3. Register the coupon output in PROVENANCE.json as a CANDIDATE (blessed=false, stub method/date/by, AWAITING OWNER BLESS) — like spike-gerber-v1. Do NOT self-bless.\n4. Extend HOW_TO_BLESS.md with the drill/slot/netlist bless steps: load drills+slot in gerbv (owner confirms hole sizes, positions, slot shape), and how to independently sanity-check the IPC-356 records. Note this golden's drills/slots/netlist come from the gerbonara emitter (ratified), NOT the incumbent.\n5. STOP at owner bless (work-cycle may not self-certify). On bless, the harness gains a correctness oracle for slots/drills/netlist and CAM condition 1 is met.\n\nFENCE: pcb/worker/tests/** + pcb/spikes/** only. Runtime pcb_worker/*.py NOT touched. Autonomous through step 4; terminal HITL at step 5.","created_at":"2026-07-18T23:10:11","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:59:17","priority":2,"tags":["cam","excellon","golden","harness","hitl-bless","ipc-356","pcb","plugin","provenance","slot"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5","resolution":"DONE @ minerva-plugins main d0792dc — owner blessed cam-excellon-ipc356-v1 WITH CAVEAT (2026-07-18). Drills+slot: full gerbv visual confirmation (holes concentric in pads, slot shape/location, bare NPTH mount). IPC-356: blessed as symbolic net-membership match to board.yaml source (VCC={U1.1,R1.2}, GND={R1.1,C1.1}) + locations==already-blessed pads, but NOT independently visualized (no IPC-356->visual tool) — owner recorded moderate confidence w/ documented uncertainty. Caveat + missing-tool follow-up 019f77aa012e recorded in provenance + tests. Satisfies CAM ratification condition 1. Full suite 330/0 + 1 skip + 1 xfail. Gated correctness-oracle test remains a skip until the Stage-5 emitter (019f761fefae) exists."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f77aa012e7a3e9a6ec6dd53a8e37e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"IPC-356 visual verification tool — render net-colored test points spatially so netlists can be blessed like drills","description":"Surfaced by the owner during the Excellon golden bless (2026-07-18, golden 019f777e9206). GAP: drills/slot could be blessed by INDEPENDENT VISUAL confirmation in gerbv (see + scale spatially), but the IPC-356 netlist could only be verified SYMBOLICALLY (net-membership vs board.yaml + locations vs already-blessed pads). gerbv does not render IPC-356. The owner blessed cam-excellon-ipc356-v1 WITH A DOCUMENTED CAVEAT: 'I can't really see these as geometry, so I'm thinking of them as pure math definitions ... without some sort of verification tool that converts to geometry I can see and scale spatially, I can't fully verify.'\n\nWANT: a tool that converts an IPC-356 (or the emitted netlist) into VISIBLE, SPATIALLY-SCALABLE geometry — render each test record as a net-COLORED marker/label at its (x,y) on the board frame (over copper/drills), so a human can confirm the netlist the same way they confirm drills. Options: (a) emit an SVG/PNG overlay (points colored by net, labeled ref.pin); (b) emit a gerbv-loadable Gerber marker layer; (c) an in-Minerva panel overlay. gerbonara already parses IPC-356 -> Netlist with locations+nets, so the parse is done — this is a render.\n\nIMPACT: upgrades the CAM golden's IPC-356 bless from MODERATE-with-caveat to FULL visual confidence, and gives every future netlist bless a visual path. Not blocking (the golden is blessed with caveat), but closes the verification-discipline gap the owner flagged. Also useful as a general Minerva capability (netlist inspection).","created_at":"2026-07-18T23:57:37","updated_at":"2026-07-18T23:57:37","priority":3,"tags":["bless","ipc-356","netlist","pcb","plugin","surfaced-by-bless","tooling","verification"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f77b11f8877028c05062565941ec2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Footprint acquisition + authoring system — arbitrary-source fetch + hand-authoring + CAD-DSL 3D (design C; own ratify gate)","description":"SPLIT OUT of Stage 2 (019f761fe518) 2026-07-18 so the IR/geometry-wiring campaign (migration path D steps 1-6, which closes pad bug 019f7736b236) stays cleanly bounded + autonomous. This is the reframed DESIGN C from Stage 2's ratification — the EXTENSION beyond the current seed lib.\n\nWHAT STAGE 2 DOES (not this): A1 FootprintDefinition schema + B1 ResolvedBoard IR + wire resolve into fab + fail-closed, all on the EXISTING sha-pinned seed-lib resolve (footprints.lock.json / libraries.lock.json). Steps 1-6 need no new footprint sources.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM IS: acquire footprints from ARBITRARY sources + author parts with no library entry. Owner ruling (b), 2026-07-18: arbitrary repos/URLs (Digikey/Mouser/SparkFun/SnapEDA/mfr sites), NOT just the official KiCad release — official-only misses too much; plus parts with NO footprint anywhere must be authorable.\n\nDESIGN (needs its OWN design pass + ratify gate before implementation):\n- SOURCE KINDS per footprint: official KiCad release | arbitrary git repo | direct URL (.kicad_mod/lib) | vendor export (SnapEDA/Ultra Librarian/mfr) | HAND-AUTHORED (native A1 schema, no KiCad) | (future) generated from package dimensions (IPC-7351).\n- Each footprint -> FootprintDefinition (A1, from Stage 2) + lock entry {id, source_kind, source_ref/url, sha256, license, retrieved_at, provenance}.\n- NO-FCIB RESOLUTION: a .kicad_mod is TEXT (not a binary) -> fetched footprints VENDORED into the repo with provenance/license (durable vs URL-rot) without violating no-FCIB. Only 3D binaries (.step/.wrl) stay by-reference (lock url+sha) — AND 3D can be AUTHORED in Minerva's CAD DSL (text), so authored parts are text end-to-end (footprint + 3D) and get a 3D-render path even with no library entry.\n- HERMETIC: fetch-on-first-use -> lock -> clean checkout resolves from the lock (vendored text + by-ref binaries + sha-verify).\n\nDEPENDS ON: Stage 2 019f761fe518 (A1 FootprintDefinition schema + B1 IR — the target every acquired/authored footprint compiles to). Do NOT start before Stage 2 steps 1-6 parity. Ties to CAD plugin (enclosure/3D) + 3D STEP export 019f763ca706. NOT ratified for implementation — design pass first.","created_at":"2026-07-19T00:05:24","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:21:46","priority":2,"tags":["acquisition","authoring","cad-dsl","design-c","footprint","library","pcb","plugin","provenance"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ead82713d8f269576ae4dfce5"}
