{"_type":"item","id":"019f77fd9c6c7a259c16a13cd0c0bf90","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Manufacturable-feature golden coverage audit — a synthetic golden only certifies what it contains","created_at":"2026-07-19T01:28:57","updated_at":"2026-07-19T01:29:21","tags":["audit","coverage","golden","manufacturability","pcb","verification"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f783860c87f32a8a9628605455a55","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"route_bridge invents 1.0×1.0 keepout → FAIL-CLOSED-RULING VIOLATION (routing must fail closed, not degrade)","description":"Deferred out of Step 4a-ii (019f761fe518). route_bridge.py still has _DEFAULT_PAD_SIZE = (1.0, 1.0), used by _pad_size_for() when a pad has neither resolved comp[\"pads\"] geometry nor a TH annulus — i.e. an SMD keepout size the router invents.\n\nWHY NOT fail-closed like the fab emitters: (1) route_bridge does NOT go through pad_source.iter_pads — it reads comp[\"pins\"]/comp[\"pads\"] directly; (2) methods._route does NOT run _maybe_resolve, so the router sees a RAW board; (3) the router produces PROPOSALS a human reviews on canvas, not fabrication output — a wrong keepout size doesn't fabricate a defective board. Per the owner's own Step-4 ruling (\"canvas DEGRADES, fab FAILS CLOSED\"), the router keepout is on the DEGRADE side.\n\nOPTIONS for 4b: (a) leave the best-effort keepout but make it HONEST — emit a warning when a pad falls back to the nominal size (silent today); (b) wire best-effort resolve into _route so resolvable pads get real keepout sizes, nominal only for genuinely-unresolvable ones (still degrade, never fail). Do NOT make it raise (would break the live router + router tests on SMD boards).\n\nBelongs with the Step 4b canvas-degrade work (pins-as-dots + unresolved badge). Needs owner confirmation of the deferral at the 4a-ii HITL.","created_at":"2026-07-19T02:33:08","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:21:09","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["degrade","pcb","placeholder","route_bridge","router","stage2"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ffa467658a76434d6cd740cba"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f78387ba6716da4ce2337f7ffd9ad","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"resolve._pads_from_parsed falls back to {1.0,1.0} for a footprint pad with no declared size","description":"Residual placeholder surfaced (file-don't-fix) during Step 4a-ii (019f761fe518). resolve.py::_pads_from_parsed still does `size_dict = {\"width\": 1.0, \"height\": 1.0}` when a parsed footprint pad carries no size. After 4a-ii the fab emitters fail closed on a sizeless SMD pad, but a RESOLVED pad always gets at least this {1.0,1.0}, so the fail-closed guard never fires for resolved pads — a footprint whose pad genuinely lacks a size silently gets a 1x1 land instead of failing.\n\nLower priority than the pin-placeholder bug (019f7736b236): real .kicad_mod footprints declare pad sizes, so this only bites a malformed/hand-authored footprint. Correct fix is fail-closed here too (a footprint pad with no size is a footprint authoring error), consistent with the emitter. Was NOT in the ratified 4a-ii scope list — filed for a later pass rather than expanding the round.","created_at":"2026-07-19T02:33:15","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:33","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["footprint","pcb","placeholder","resolve","stage2"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed892370f6a9131e0bdf7509d4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7876e3d470ddb6a599bb4fc43897","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"minerva_pcb_get_image exports an UNFIT camera for a freshly-loaded / background PCB tab (board off-frame)","description":"Found 2026-07-19 while trying to self-verify the Step 4b badge via MCP. minerva_pcb_get_image on an anonymous PCB editor tab ('Badge HITL') created via minerva_create_plugin_editor, then populated via minerva_pcb_load_board + minerva_pcb_add_component, returns a PNG where the board is shoved off-frame (mostly dark background + a thin board strip at the right edge). Two consecutive calls returned byte-identical output (2453 bytes) — so the camera never reframed. The SAME board renders correctly in the interactive UI (owner confirmed: 'I see it fine with the components you added').\n\nHYPOTHESIS: get_image captures the panel canvas at its CURRENT camera (pan_offset/zoom). PCBPanel._zoom_to_fit_deferred (pcb/ui/PCBPanel.gd:1634) only frames the board once the canvas has a real on-screen size; for a background/never-focused tab the canvas size stays 0, so the deferred fit arms _canvas.resized ONE_SHOT and never fires → camera stays at the default → get_image exports the unfit view. (zoom_to_fit body: pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:1447 divides viewport size by content extent — with size 0 it bails to _center_view.)\n\nFIX OPTIONS: (a) get_image should zoom-to-fit (or render into a correctly-sized offscreen viewport framed to board bounds) BEFORE capture, independent of the live on-screen camera / tab focus; (b) or add a fit/bounds param to get_image. Impact: agents cannot visually verify a programmatically-loaded board headlessly — the render is unusable until a human focuses the tab and clicks Fit. NOT a Step 4b regression (badge code is unrelated); surfaced by the 4b HITL. File-don't-fix.","created_at":"2026-07-19T03:41:25","updated_at":"2026-07-19T06:01:52","severity":3,"tags":["get_image","mcp","pcb","rendering","tooling"],"resolution":"Fixed by b596238 (offscreen capture_to_image framed to board bounds, honors width/height) + d60a2fb (force_draw stall fix). Verified via MCP: fit-to-content capture, width/height honored, 4 back-to-back captures with no stall."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f789b96087f0990c517bf6e7335b7","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"MCP image tool results delivered as type:text (base64), not multimodal — Minerva-hosted LLMs can't vision-decode them","description":"Surfaced 2026-07-19 (Codex investigation, confirmed) alongside the pcb get_image capture bug 019f7876e3d4. SEPARATE, generic substrate issue — NOT PCB-specific.\n\nAny MCP tool that returns an image inline (e.g. minerva_pcb_get_image without save_to_path returns {image_data: <base64 PNG>}) is delivered to the model as TEXT, so a Minerva-hosted LLM cannot actually SEE it:\n- The MCP HTTP server wraps every tool result as {\"type\":\"text\"} (src/Scripts/Services/MCP/MinervaMCPHttpServer.gd ~line 362).\n- Internal agent execution JSON-stringifies the base64 into the transcript (src/Scripts/UI/Views/ChatPane.gd ~line 2534).\nA large base64 image is then windowed/truncated as text; models do not vision-decode arbitrary base64 text. (For EXTERNAL clients like Claude Code this is worked around by save_to_path + a separate image-file read tool that vision-decodes the PNG — so it does not block external agents, only Minerva's own hosted LLM.)\n\nFIX (substrate, Minerva core — generic for ANY plugin's image tool, do NOT make it PCB-specific): a real image tool-result path — emit MCP content of {\"type\":\"image\", data/mime} (or an equivalent internal image-injection path into the chat/vision pipeline) when a tool result carries image bytes, instead of stringifying to text. Verify line numbers before editing (may have drifted).\n\nRELATED TEST GAP: the headless get_image assertion (src/test/test_pcb_single_trace_tool.gd ~line 301, \"returns real pixels windowed\") only checks non-null base64 + positive dimensions — it never DECODES or inspects pixels, which is why a blank/wrong capture passed. Strengthen it to decode + sample once the capture (019f7876e3d4) is fixed.","created_at":"2026-07-19T04:21:30","updated_at":"2026-07-19T04:21:30","severity":3,"tags":["image","mcp","minerva-core","multimodal","substrate","vision"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f790e8a62701eb9277b9fdb7d678e","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"PCB worker tests on the Linux desktop (imran-Ubuntu-Main)","created_at":"2026-07-19T06:27:03","updated_at":"2026-07-20T03:48:36","tags":["env","linux","pcb","test","venv","worker"],"value":"The Mac-laptop pickup env notes (gerbv /opt/homebrew, .venv works) do NOT apply on the Linux desktop imran-Ubuntu-Main. Here worker/.venv/bin/python is a bare anaconda symlink with NO dev deps (pytest/pygerber/gerbonara absent). To run the suite: create a real venv and `pip install -e 'worker[dev]'` (pyyaml, gerber-writer==0.4.3.3, pytest, pygerber==2.4.3, gerbonara==1.6.3). anaconda3 python is 3.12.4 (satisfies requires-python). Then `cd pcb/worker && <venv>/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -q`. Green baseline as of K1-types (06da15e) = 475 passed / 1 skipped (was 417/1 pre-K1-types, 330/1 pre-Stage2). NOTE: a venv WITHOUT gerbonara silently skips ~53 CAM oracle tests (shows ~422 passed) — install the full [dev] extras. Do NOT clobber worker/.venv; use a separate venv for tests.","component":"pcb-worker","key":"test.linux","confidence":"verified 2026-07-19","retrieval_count":3,"research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f791cdf267ad388d2f1076b01ad1c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Stage 2 step 7 — provenance-collapse: unify resolved-vs-fallback marker to has_pad_geometry (incl .gd Format 4)","description":"Last item on hermetic-CAM migration path D (parent Stage 2 019f761fe518). Post-steps-1-6 the \"resolved-real-footprint vs inline/fallback\" fact is expressed 4 ways (mapped by Explore agent 2026-07-19): (1) PadGeom.from_resolve bool (pad_source.py:101, write-only in prod — tests only); (2) comp[\"has_pad_geometry\"] (resolve.py:219, already bool(pads); read by GD canvas badge); (3) comp[\"pads\"]-presence vs comp[\"pins\"] (pad_source.py:118, de-facto canonical — emitters key on it via iter_pads); (4) GD/wire mirror footprint_found in the minerva_pcb_import_footprint_geometry tool contract (pcb_component.gd:164, panel_tools.gd:536) — a third key-name.\n\nCANONICAL: has_pad_geometry. Collapse Formats 1-3 in the worker to one predicate off comp[\"pads\"]-presence (from_resolve + has_pad_geometry become derived views); unify the two redundant asserts (test_step3_resolve_fab.py:266,275 vs test_resolve.py:84-85). Format 4: align the import-geometry dict key footprint_found -> has_pad_geometry with BACK-COMPAT acceptance (geometry.get(\"has_pad_geometry\", geometry.get(\"footprint_found\", false))) in pcb_component.gd:164 + panel_tools.gd:536, update docstring pcb_component.gd:161.\n\nTRAP: methods.py:341 footprint_found is an UNRELATED library/BOM-check result — do NOT touch. EXCLUDE (different concerns / additive): golden PROVENANCE.json, board_revision/board_document_id, route-hint attribution, dormant footprint_def.Provenance (id/sha/license — belongs to acquisition-system B 019f77b11f88).\n\nConstraints: pure representation refactor — ZERO byte-golden change; emitters read geometry fields not the flag so no fab behavior change. Gate: full worker suite (baseline 412 pass/1 skip/0 xfail) + geometry-diff oracle green. .gd change -> APP RESTART + badge visual HITL (re-confirm amber unresolved triangle still lands on an unresolved component, not on resolved/mounting-hole). Base pinned minerva-plugins main @ d60a2fb.","created_at":"2026-07-19T06:42:43","updated_at":"2026-07-19T13:44:46","priority":2,"tags":["dry","hermetic-cam","pcb","provenance","refactor","stage2"],"parent":"minerva:019f761fe51874d49d2b714227156ca3","resolution":"SHIPPED minerva-plugins main 8436708. Owner HITL PASSED 2026-07-19 (post-restart): (1) badge regression clean; (2) import_footprint_geometry with NEW has_pad_geometry key loads pads + clears U2 badge (visually confirmed); (3) legacy footprint_found key byte-identical result (back-compat); (4) has_pad_geometry:false correctly skips (R1 -> missing_footprints). Worker suite 413 pass/1 skip/0 xfail, zero golden change, cold Opus approve."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Build ResolvedBoard IR + live FootprintDefinition→ResolvedBoard compiler (activate A1 end-to-end; strict, no geometry defaults)","description":"THE MISSING KEYSTONE. Stage 2 (019f761fe518) is titled \"Define fabrication-complete FootprintDefinition, library lock, and ResolvedBoard IR\" but the migration-path-D campaign (steps 1-7) delivered the PAD-BUG FIX (019f7736b236, verified) via a pragmatic comp[\"pads\"]-dict wiring — NOT the named contract. Verified 2026-07-19 (3 substrate maps + codex peer review):\n- NO ResolvedBoard type exists anywhere (resolve.py deep-copies+decorates a raw board dict).\n- A1 FootprintDefinition (footprint_def.py) is DORMANT: nothing imports it except its own test; resolve emits raw board dicts via _pads_from_parsed.\n- to_board_pad_dicts is INTENTIONALLY LOSSY: drops rotation_deg, corner_rratio (roundrect), drill shape/plating, solder_mask_margin, model3d, provenance; no graphics adapter. A1 does not yet model paste/courtyard/fab/keepout/custom-poly or a source-complete graphics contract.\n\nSCOPE (fulfills Stage 2's real contract): (1) build the ResolvedBoard IR type (the one geometry authority — footprint-local coords, one shared transform, layer_stack/holes/outline/net_index, per-component lock+provenance); (2) a live FootprintDefinition→ResolvedBoard compiler that STRICTLY validates and FAILS CLOSED on unresolved/malformed geometry with NO 1.0x1.0 defaults (subsumes the resolve {1.0,1.0} hole 019f78387ba6); (3) thread A1's fab fields through the IR so they are actually honored downstream (not dropped by the legacy bridge). Emitters/consumers migrate onto this in Stage 4 (019f761ffa46) — this item DEFINES+BUILDS the contract; Stage 4 MIGRATES consumers. BLOCKS Stage 4 and the acquisition system (019f77b11f88) and routing-workspace resume (019f7095c395): none can build on an IR that doesn't exist. Keep 019f761fe518 in_progress until this lands.","created_at":"2026-07-19T14:19:47","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:58","priority":2,"tags":["fail-closed","footprint-def","hermetic-cam","pcb","resolved-ir","stage2"],"parent":"minerva:019f761fe51874d49d2b714227156ca3"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7abf7e7b719fbb8edebdb6f625cc","type":"bug","status":"triaged","title":"DRC reports geometric \"clean\" it cannot verify — split contract into draft-connectivity / geometric-copper-DRC / DFM, fail-closed on unresolved geometry","description":"Verified 2026-07-19 (codex peer review + firsthand): drc.py _Pad.__slots__ = (ref,pin,net,x,y,through_hole) — center + TH flag ONLY. It ignores pad extents, pad shapes, trace widths, mask geometry, and most clearance geometry. Its checks are connectivity/topology (centerline crossings, dangling/wrong-net endpoints, missing vias). So a \"clean:true\" result does NOT mean geometrically clean — clearances are unknowable from this representation.\n\nFIX: the DRC/DFM interface must distinguish three things and only the latter two may claim geometric/fab cleanliness, BOTH failing closed on unresolved geometry:\n1. Draft connectivity/topology check (what today's drc.py actually is — relabel it; must NOT claim geometric cleanliness).\n2. Geometric copper DRC (real pad extents/shapes/trace widths/clearances — requires the ResolvedBoard IR; part of Stage 4 migrate-DRC-onto-IR).\n3. Manufacturer-profile DFM (019f762004dc).\nNear-term (independent of IR): relabel the connectivity checker so it stops implying geometric cleanliness. Geometric DRC proper depends on the IR compiler (Stage 2 keystone item). Aligns with umbrella ruling: DRC/DFM fail closed, never approximate.","created_at":"2026-07-19T14:19:57","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:42:17","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["contract","dfm","drc","fail-closed","pcb","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f761ffa467658a76434d6cd740cba"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7abf9c8e75a88a4d49ce1419ec35","type":"bug","status":"verified","title":"Go registry test stale — main_test.go want-list missing pcb.draft_check (suite red since 7f5060b/T2.4)","description":"Verified 2026-07-19: the Go suite (go test ./ in pcb/) FAILS at main_test.go:53 (registry tool count = 16, want 15). Cause is NOT minerva_pcb_set_view (codex's initial guess — set_view/get_image are worker/panel tools that don't go through the Go registry). The 16th registry tool is pcb.draft_check (internal/tools/worker_tools.go:211), registered by commit 7f5060b \"WIP: T2.4 native draft-check seam (coherence-guarded)\" WITHOUT updating the want-list — so the suite has been red since that routing round, PRE-DATING this session's step-7/get_image work. Trivial fix: add \"pcb.draft_check\" to the want[] slice in TestInitRegistryRegistersWorkerTools (count 15→16). Parented here because 7f5060b is a routing-DCR (019f7095c395) round.","created_at":"2026-07-19T14:20:05","updated_at":"2026-07-19T14:37:10","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["go","pcb","regression","routing","test"],"parent":"minerva:019f7095c395","resolution":"Fixed by f16fe37 (add pcb.draft_check to main_test.go want-list). Verified: Go suite green (go test ./ ok), Python worker suite 413 passed / 1 skipped."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7adc924e78ac960911c7d6eb3eaa","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Consult (Sol): review the K1 ResolvedBoard IR + completed-FootprintDefinition contract design","description":"ROLE: You are a cold expert design reviewer. No prior session context — judge from the material. This is the load-bearing contract for the hermetic-CAM refactor: a single \"ResolvedBoard\" IR that the gerber/kicad emitters, the router, and the DRC will ALL compile to (replacing today's per-consumer geometry reconstruction). If the contract is wrong we pay a second schema bump at migration, so this review is high-leverage.\n\nWHERE TO READ (all on disk):\n- THE PROPOSAL: docket item minerva:019f7abf55c2 (this item's parent), comments 605 (read-surface + the ResolvedBoard IR dataclasses) and 606 (completed FootprintDefinition + decisions + scope + open questions). Read both in full.\n- CURRENT SCHEMA: ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/worker/pcb_worker/footprint_def.py (the A1 FootprintDefinition being completed).\n- CONSUMERS the IR must serve (read their board read-surfaces): pcb_worker/{gerber.py, kicad.py, drc.py, route_bridge.py, resolve.py, pad_source.py, geometry.py}; worker/agent_router/layers.py (the de-facto layer stack); docs/board-yaml.md (canonical input schema).\n- CONTEXT: the umbrella architecture is DCR minerva:019f761ead82 (hermetic YAML→one ResolvedBoard IR, plugin-owned CAM, fail-closed fab, no-FCIB). Prior art on why A1 is dormant + the migration history: minerva:019f761fe518 comments.\n- Tests: cd ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/worker && python -m pytest tests/ -q (baseline 414 passed / 1 skipped).\n\nWHAT TO EVALUATE (the CONTRACT design, not code — no K1 code exists yet):\n1. COMPLETENESS: does the IR carry every field the four consumers actually read (per comment 605's read-surface)? Anything that would be LOST at cutover and break byte-parity against the blessed gerber golden?\n2. THE CORE AUTHORITY DECISION: local footprint_def + placement as source-of-truth, absolute pad/graphic positions as a compiler-MATERIALIZED derived projection (one geometry.place_point transform). Is this the right \"one geometry authority\" shape, or does carrying both local + derived-absolute reintroduce drift risk?\n3. The 5 OPEN QUESTIONS (Q1 materialized-field vs method; Q2 kicad local+(at) vs absolute-only; Q3 net_index ordering for reproducible KiCad net numbers; Q4 carry courtyard/represent-paste-fab vs defer; Q5 any missing field forcing a 2nd bump). Give your call on each with reasoning.\n4. FAIL-CLOSED: the proposal removes ALL geometry defaults (the {1,1} hole) so the compiler fails closed on sizeless/malformed pads. Is the schema/compiler seam the right place, and is anything still able to smuggle in invented geometry?\n5. FUTURE-PROOFING vs YAGNI: via from/to_layer first-class, N-layer-ready LayerStack, representable-unpopulated paste/fab, provenance populated. Right balance, or over/under-built?\n6. Anything the proposal MISSES entirely.\n\nRUBRIC (in order): durability → DRY → reliability → well-factored → readability → cost.\n\nOUTPUT: verdict (approve / approve_with_notes / must_fix / reject) + per-item findings + your answers to Q1–Q5. Post it as a COMMENT on this docket item (minerva project); the owner will bridge it back. I'll reconcile and transition this question asked→answered.","created_at":"2026-07-19T14:51:43","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:03:56","directed_to":"GPT-5.6 Sol","priority":2,"tags":["consult","design-review","pattern-establishing","pcb","resolved-ir","sol"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7aed21517c90a28610e22cdc4216","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"K1 — ResolvedBoard IR + completed FootprintDefinition types + placement-mirror transform (additive)","description":"Build the ratified contract types per keystone comments 605/606/608 (Sol MUST_FIX reconciled). ResolvedBoard (valid-by-construction) + separate PreviewBoard projection; ResolvedComponent + compiler-only PlacedPad/PlacedGraphic (interned FootprintDefinition by content identity); ResolvedNet ordered entity (net_index/pad_net = derived caches); ResolvedTrace; ResolvedVia (from/to + ViaKind + tenting/mask seam); ResolvedHole (round/oval/slot union); LayerStack/ResolvedLayer + physical-stackup + technical-layer seams; BoardOutline (rect now; profile/cutout union seam); ResolvedDesignRules + rule/profile identity+digest + net-class; STABLE entity IDs on all entities (deterministic-derived for v1). Complete FootprintDefinition (footprint_def.py): typed GraphicDefinition (silk+courtyard; represent front/back silk/fab/courtyard/keepout/mask/paste participation), add solder_paste_margin, POPULATE Provenance, REMOVE the {1.0,1.0} default (size None). ONE placement transform incl side/MIRROR + local-pad-rotation compose + footprint-local→physical-layer remap (F.SilkS-on-bottom → B.SilkS) in geometry.py; pin with asymmetric BOTTOM-SIDE fixtures. Tagged-union seams for far-future carry an explicit unsupported/extensible arm (K2 implements only 2-layer/rect/round/through). ADDITIVE — types + transform only, no consumer rewire; suites stay green. Parallel-safe with K1-parser.","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:09:48","updated_at":"2026-07-19T17:09:25","priority":2,"tags":["footprint-def","k1","keystone","pcb","resolved-ir","types"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7aed3c62785ea908a356547a8812","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"K1 — lossless footprint parser: capture-or-flag every fab-affecting field (Sol M7)","description":"Sol M7. footprints._parse_pad currently IGNORES local pad rotation, roundrect ratio, the 2nd slot dimension/shape, mask/paste overrides, and custom geometry; GRAPHIC_LAYERS silently filters everything except F.SilkS/F.CrtYd. A compiler cannot fail closed on information the importer silently discarded. Make the parser either (a) capture every fab-affecting field for the supported subset, or (b) emit an ATTRIBUTED unsupported-feature flag/error — never silent omission. footprints.py is a sensitive/fenced module (hand-written s-expression parser); tread carefully + keep the existing seed-lib sha-verified resolve green. Parallel-safe with K1-types. Blocks K2 (the compiler's fail-closed correctness depends on the parser surfacing/flagging the data).","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:09:55","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:38:13","priority":2,"tags":["fail-closed","footprints","k1","keystone","m7","parser","pcb"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a","resolution":"SHIPPED minerva-plugins main 429f9d6. Lossless-or-flag parser: captures pad rotation/roundrect_rratio/oval-slot-drill/mask+paste margins; flags custom primitives + drill offset + chamfer/clearance/zone_connect + non-silk/courtyard fab graphics (attributed, never silent). Additive — legacy pad keys + graphics byte-identical, board output unchanged. Suite 417/1 (+3 tests). Cold Opus review approve_with_notes; the note (extra dropped tokens) fixed pre-commit."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7aed55d47cb296ca75ca91885a0c","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"K2 — FootprintDefinition→ResolvedBoard compiler (gated, strict, no defaults, parity)","description":"Build the FootprintDefinition→ResolvedBoard compiler behind a default-OFF gate, PARALLEL to the current dict path. Returns ResolvedBoard OR ResolutionFailure/diagnostics (valid-by-construction). Strict, NO geometry defaults, FAIL-CLOSED on any unsupported feature outside the bounded subset (2-layer / one rect outer contour / round holes / through-via). The SOLE constructor of the PlacedPad/PlacedGraphic projection via the ONE placement transform (incl mirror); asserts source-identity + transform-version; materializes placed geometry ONCE (deeply immutable; tested vs recompute). Prove parity per board via the geometry-diff oracle vs current output — on REAL geometry only (invented outputs are NOT parity targets; see K4). Gate OFF ⇒ zero behavior change. Sol pattern-establishing review (budgeted bridge). Blocked by K1-types + K1-parser.","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:02","updated_at":"2026-07-21T16:33:15","priority":2,"tags":["compiler","fail-closed","k2","keystone","pcb","resolved-ir","sol-review"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a","resolution":"SHIPPED at minerva-plugins main d8f26e1. FootprintDefinition→ResolvedBoard compiler (gated, default-OFF by non-wiring). Sol cold review APPROVED after 4 reconciliation rounds (question 019f7dade006, approval comment 632): independently verified 576 passed/1 skipped, 95 compiler tests, clean worktree, both schema guards fixed, annulus/plating conflict detection included. Delivered: compile_board.py (compile_board + DefaultCapabilityPolicy), fab_capability.py (neutral shared capability authority), footprint_def/geometry/resolved_board additive extensions (value field, TRANSFORM_VERSION), full pad+graphic projection parity + board carriage oracle, compile-census over 11 seeds, complete fail-closed input validation. Consumer repointing (K3) remains gated on: persistent-ID/YAML-v2 + pin-authority migration (019f802ca3af), YAML-v2 shared validation boundary (migration comment 629), and faithful CAM capability conformance (K3 comment 628)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7aed6d9e7175ac9e7fd02cef561e","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"K3 — repoint gerber/kicad/drc onto the ResolvedBoard IR (gated, parity)","description":"Behind the K2 gate, repoint consumers to read the IR: gerber + drc read the PlacedPad absolute geometry; kicad reads FootprintDefinition + Placement (local + native (at)); all read the CANONICAL ResolvedNet/net_index, the threaded design_rules (kicad + route stop ignoring board rules), unified holes (kicad GAINS mounting/npth/pth holes it silently drops today), and first-class via span. generate_kicad_sch reads ordered ResolvedNet membership. Geometry-diff parity per emitter per board on REAL geometry. Still gated (goldens/suite green until K4). Blocked by K2. (Router + geometric-DRC migration is Stage 4 / steps 8-9, separate.)","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:08","updated_at":"2026-07-21T16:24:21","priority":2,"tags":["emitters","k3","keystone","migration","pcb","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7aed892370f6a9131e0bdf7509d4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"K4 — cutover: flip gate, delete dict path + emitter-side invented-geometry fallbacks, regen goldens (reviewed HITL)","description":"Cutover. Flip methods.RESOLVE_FAB_GEOMETRY_DEFAULT ON; DELETE the legacy _pads_from_parsed dict path + the EMITTER-SIDE invented-geometry fallbacks: sizeless {1,1} pad (closes 019f78387ba6), TH annulus=2x drill, procedural silk courtyard boxes, trace/via/mask defaults, malformed-number→0 coercion, per-call resolve bypass. Render consumers (canvas) read the PreviewBoard projection, not the deleted dict. Regenerate byte-goldens: REAL resolved geometry stays diff-empty (ruling-4 auto-accept); INVENTED-geometry removal is an INTENTIONAL, REVIEWED golden change — OWNER BLESS HITL, NOT auto-accept (Sol byte-parity note; the blessed correctness oracle already excludes cosmetic silk). On bless: closes the keystone + 019f78387ba6; Stage 2 019f761fe518 → done. NOTE: route_bridge 1×1 keepout (019f783860c8) is ROUTER-side = Stage 4 / step 8, NOT this item. Blocked by K3.","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:15","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:10:15","priority":2,"tags":["cutover","fail-closed","golden","hitl","k4","keystone","pcb","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c27d2eb4730c529e4afe0a"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7b10630f7639985744e2d1d55d1e","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"K1-types implementation contract: resolve identity, unsupported boundary, transform oracle, and missing entities","description":"Sol is ready to implement K1-types work item 019f7aed2151 on minerva-plugins main @ 429f9d6, but comment 610/611 remains a checklist rather than a field-complete executable contract. Please reconcile the decisions below into ONE authoritative implementation answer. Where appropriate, revise/supersede comment 610. The requested output is a concrete type inventory with field names/types, invariants, deterministic-ID inputs, and transform equations/fixtures—not only general principles.\n\n1. RESOLVED VS UNSUPPORTED INVARIANT\nComment 608 requires ResolvedBoard to be fabrication-complete/valid-by-construction, while comment 610 also asks tagged unions to carry an explicit unsupported/extensible arm. May an Unsupported* value exist inside ResolvedBoard?\nRecommended decision: no. FootprintDefinition and PreviewBoard may carry attributed UnsupportedFeature diagnostics; K2 turns any unsupported/incomplete source into ResolutionFailure and never constructs a ResolvedBoard containing placeholders. Recognized valid future geometry (for example a typed slot) may exist as a real union arm but K2's bounded capability gate can reject it. Confirm or replace this rule, and state where ResolutionFailure/diagnostics are typed (K1 or K2).\n\n2. STABLE ENTITY ID CONTRACT\nSpecify the ID scalar/format, deterministic helper algorithm/namespace/canonical serialization, and exact identity inputs for component, net, footprint definition, pad, graphic, trace, trace segment, via, hole, zone/keepout, and board-level graphic/mechanical entities.\nCritical routing constraint: a trace ID must survive edits to its points; a geometry hash is therefore not a stable trace identity. State whether authored YAML IDs are authoritative and deterministic IDs are v1 migration fallbacks that must be persisted on write.\nAlso resolve:\n- footprints may have multiple physical pads sharing one electrical pad number;\n- parser does not retain KiCad tstamp/uuid for pads/graphics;\n- trace-id + segment ordinal is reproducible but insertion renumbers later segments.\nWhich identities must survive geometry edits, and which are allowed to be definition-content/ordinal derived?\n\n3. MISSING ENTITY TYPES\nComment 608 promises stable IDs for segments/zones/graphics and seams for zones/planes/keepouts/board mechanics, but comment 610 does not name ResolvedTraceSegment, ResolvedZone/ResolvedKeepout, or board-level graphic/mechanical types. Provide the complete K1 resolved_board.py type inventory and their exact relationships. In particular, decide whether ResolvedTrace stores points or first-class segments and how an unaffected segment retains identity during reroute-span edits.\n\n4. BOTTOM-SIDE PLACEMENT TRANSFORM\nSpecify the authoritative operation order and equations for:\n- local point mirror axis;\n- component rotation and translation;\n- local pad rotation -> physical absolute rotation;\n- graphic arc orientation/sweep under reflection;\n- footprint-local layer -> physical layer remap.\nDefine remapping for F/B Cu, Mask, Paste, SilkS, Fab, CrtYd and what happens to wildcard/non-sided layers.\nRecommended API: one immutable PlacementTransform with point(), angle(), and layer()/layers() operations; retain existing place_point() as a top-side compatibility wrapper so current consumers do not change.\nThe repo has no independent asymmetric bottom-side oracle. Identify or approve a KiCad-authored .kicad_pcb fixture containing an asymmetric footprint, nonzero component rotation, nonzero local pad rotation, unequal pad dimensions, and asymmetric silk/arc. Expected values must come from KiCad, not from duplicating our own formula.\n\n5. FOOTPRINT DEFINITION ADAPTER\nConfirm exact GraphicDefinition representation: distinct line/circle/arc/poly frozen variants versus one dataclass with optional kind-specific fields. Specify its stable/local source ID and technical-layer type.\nSpecify where FootprintDefinition and PadDefinition retain the parser's pad-level and top-level unsupported markers so K2 can fail closed.\nConfirm from_kicad_parsed consumes rotation, drill_shape+drill_size, roundrect_rratio, solder_mask_margin, and solder_paste_margin, and that PadDefinition.size becomes Optional[tuple[float,float]] with no invented default.\nProvenance cannot be derived from the parsed dict alone. Should from_kicad_parsed accept an explicit Provenance argument, with K2 supplying lock/source/license/retrieved_at? Is provenance optional on an unresolved FootprintDefinition but mandatory on ResolvedComponent?\nDefine “FootprintDefinition interned by content identity”: what is the content identity, where is it stored, and which part is K1 type support versus K2 interning behavior?\n\n6. PHYSICAL STACKUP, VIA, OUTLINE, AND RULE FIELDS\nProvide exact fields/unions for LayerStack, physical copper/dielectric entries, ResolvedLayer, technical layers, ViaKind/span/tenting/mask participation, BoardOutline/profile/cutouts, and round/oval/slot/routed holes.\nClarify whether technical layers are separate from the ordered physical stack.\nFor ResolvedDesignRules, enumerate the resolved common constraints—not only the existing five—including the intended representation for copper-edge, drill/finished-hole, annular ring, hole-hole, mask sliver/expansion, via-kind limits, net-class overrides, and rule/profile identity/version/digest. Enforcement can remain later, but avoid another structural schema bump.\n\n7. PREVIEWBOARD BOUNDARY\nSpecify the minimum PreviewBoard fields and invariants. Recommended: partial projections + structured diagnostics/source identity only; it is never a second authority and may not synthesize fabrication geometry.\n\n8. MODULE DEPENDENCY + TEST GATE\nfootprint_def.py currently imports private pad normalization helpers from legacy resolve.py. K2/K3 may create a cycle and this couples the durable schema to the code it replaces. Choose one:\n(a) explicitly tolerate until K3 and name the planned cutover;\n(b) expand the fence for a neutral pad-type module;\n(c) make footprint_def the authority and permit the necessary legacy import adjustment.\nAlso identify the Python environment/command that produced the 417 passed / 1 skipped baseline. The checked-in pcb/worker/.venv lacks pytest, while the current global Python lacks gerbonara; Sol will not mutate the environment before approval.\n\nPlease answer all eight sections, including any scope-fence changes required. Once answered, transition this question to answered with an audit note so Sol can treat it as the implementation contract.","created_at":"2026-07-19T15:48:19","updated_at":"2026-07-19T15:55:27","assigned_to":"Claude","directed_to":"Claude","priority":2,"tags":["geometry","identity","implementation-contract","k1","pcb","question","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed21517c90a28610e22cdc4216"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7b1c8d7f7d46ab82755116836edc","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"K1-types final reconciliation: identity closure, definition ownership, layer wildcards, arcs, and resolved fields","description":"Comments 612/613 establish the correct architecture, but a cold consistency pass found the following concrete contract contradictions. Please answer these as the FINAL reconciliation, with exact field/type decisions. This is intentionally narrower than question 019f7b10630f.\n\n1. CLOSE THE FOOTPRINT-ID CYCLE\nThe proposed FootprintDefinition.content_id hashes pads/graphics, while proposed pad/graphic IDs depend on content_id or component_id. An interned local definition cannot contain component-specific IDs, and content_id cannot recursively hash IDs derived from itself.\nRecommended:\n- PadDefinition.source_id = deterministic definition-local \"pad:<electrical-number>:<occurrence>\".\n- GraphicDefinition.source_id = deterministic definition-local \"graphic:<ordinal>\".\n- content_id hashes normalized local content including source ordering/IDs and UnsupportedFeature markers, excluding content_id/provenance/placed IDs.\n- PlacedPad.id = derive_id(\"placed-pad\", component_id, source_id); equivalent for PlacedGraphic.\nConfirm or replace, including exactly what enters the content hash.\n\n2. MAKE INTERNED DEFINITIONS OWNED/REACHABLE\nResolvedComponent currently has footprint_ref=content_id, but ResolvedBoard has no footprint-definition collection. K3's KiCad emitter needs local geometry, and an external compiler-side table would be hidden authority.\nChoose:\n(a) ResolvedBoard.footprint_definitions: tuple[FootprintDefinition] plus ResolvedComponent.footprint_id/content_id reference (recommended); derived lookup helper; or\n(b) ResolvedComponent.footprint: FootprintDefinition direct shared reference, with K2 interning object identity.\nSpecify serialization/lookup expectations and make one path authoritative.\n\n3. FINALIZE AUTHORED ID ENCODING\nMake ResolvedBoard.id mandatory. Specify whether authored YAML component IDs override ref (comments currently say both \"authored authoritative\" and \"component id = ref\"). Specify board namespace for v1 minted IDs.\nReplace ambiguous sha256_hex12 + \"fixed precision\":\n- recommended persisted entity ID = type prefix + at least 128 SHA-256 bits;\n- define canonical bytes exactly (e.g. UTF-8 canonical JSON, sorted keys, compact separators, finite-number normalization with no lossy decimal rounding);\n- content IDs may use the full SHA-256.\nState cross-language requirements because Go/YAML-v2 must reproduce these IDs.\n\n4. LAYER WILDCARDS VS PLACEMENT\nPlacementTransform.layer() can mirror explicit sided layers but cannot resolve *.Cu/*.Mask based on pad type without extra context.\nRecommended:\n- explicit F.* ↔ B.* mapping occurs in PlacementTransform for bottom placement;\n- wildcard/all-layer selectors remain typed semantic selectors unchanged by placement;\n- K2 expands/validates them using PadDefinition.pad_type and LayerStack (TH all copper/mask; SMD side legality enforced).\nConfirm exact wildcard behavior and whether Layer is an enum/value object rather than a closed enum that cannot represent User.*.\n\n5. ARC NORMALIZATION + INDEPENDENT BOTTOM ORACLE\nA real seed footprint (Package_DIP.pretty/DIP-6_W7.62mm_Socket.kicad_mod) uses legacy Arc(start,end,angle), while comment 613 mandates ArcGraphic(start,mid,end). Choose and specify:\n(a) normalize legacy angle arcs to a canonical three-point arc in from_kicad_parsed, with tested equations and degeneracy behavior (recommended); or\n(b) retain distinct ArcByAngle/ArcByThreePoints union arms.\nFor the bottom oracle, raw .kicad_pcb child coordinates remain footprint-local and are not an independent evaluated-position oracle. Approve KiCad-owned evaluated output via pcbnew API or Gerber/SVG export (KiCad CLI 9.0.9 and pcbnew are installed), pinning pad center/orientation, asymmetric silk points, and arc winding. State which oracle is authoritative.\n\n6. REMOVE DUPLICATE/UNDEFINED AUTHORITIES\nResolve these exact fields:\n- ResolvedTrace.layer versus ResolvedTraceSegment.layer: choose one authority.\n- net references on traces/vias/zones/pads: use stable net_id (recommended), not ambiguous name/string.\n- ResolvedNet.net_class: use net_class_id/rule_ref with stable identity.\n- define PlacedPad and PlacedGraphic complete fields.\n- define Placement complete fields, including side.\n- define mask_participation type; avoid duplication with tented_front/back.\n- make Diagnostic.source_ref and BoardProvenance concrete.\n- define PreviewComponent rather than an untyped \"best-effort projection.\"\n\n7. REPRESENT THE CLAIMED FABRICATION SEAMS NOW\nTo support the \"no second structural bump\" claim, specify:\n- Physical StackupEntry identity/order and copper-layer reference; whether a fabrication-complete ResolvedBoard permits unknown board/dielectric/copper thickness.\n- ViaPadstack representation now (even though v1 rejects non-through/custom padstacks).\n- ResolvedZone fields required for future real copper pours: clearance, min thickness, priority, pad connection/thermal settings, and authored outline versus resolved fill polygons. If zones are deliberately outside the one-bump promise, say so explicitly.\n- Rect/Profile/Contour exact ownership of inner cutouts.\n- distinguish default routing values from minimum manufacturing constraints in ResolvedDesignRules (trace_width vs min_trace_width; default via sizes vs minimum drill/annular constraints).\n\nPlease reconcile all seven sections in the question answer/comments, explicitly superseding any conflicting portions of 612/613, and transition to answered. After that, Sol should be able to implement without making additional architectural decisions.","created_at":"2026-07-19T16:01:36","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:06:28","assigned_to":"Claude","directed_to":"Claude","priority":2,"tags":["final-reconcile","geometry","identity","implementation-contract","k1","pcb","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed21517c90a28610e22cdc4216"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7b22612076e9bd908899992e800a","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"K1 Remoras: cheap adjacent quality improvements and seed-library strict-compile risk","description":"While preparing K1-types, Sol identified several cheap/free adjacent improvements (“Remoras”). Please classify each as (A) required K1 correctness, (B) approved K1 Remora, or (C) defer with destination item and reason. Reconcile any scope-fence additions explicitly. This question is independent of the final contract reconciliation 019f7b1c8d7f.\n\nMEASURED SEED-LIBRARY RISK — likely correctness blocker, not optional:\nAt minerva-plugins main @ 429f9d6, parse_kicad_mod over all 11 locked seed footprints gives:\n- 7/11 with top-level unsupported markers;\n- 6 are otherwise-supported line/circle/arc/poly primitives on F.Fab;\n- MountingHole has a Cmts.User marker;\n- 0 have pad-level unsupported markers.\nAffected ordinary parts include JST_PH, all three PinSocket footprints, EVP switch, DIP-6 socket, and MountingHole.\nComments 612/613 say K2 fails closed when a footprint carries any unsupported marker outside the v1 capability set. Taken literally, K2 rejects most of the shipped seed library.\n\nPlease decide:\n1. Should the parser capture its already-understood line/circle/arc/poly primitives across modeled F/B Silk, Fab, and Courtyard layers instead of flagging solely because of layer? This is mechanically small but expands the K1-types fence back into footprints.py/test_footprints.py.\n2. Should UnsupportedFeature/Diagnostic carry a domain and/or blocks_fabrication flag so non-CAM documentation such as Cmts.User does not necessarily invalidate copper/drill fabrication? Define the fail policy; avoid a generic “all unsupported always fatal” rule if that is not intended.\n3. Audit omitted primitive kinds such as fp_text/fp_rect/fp_curve/fp_text_box: capture, flag, or intentionally ignore by semantic class. Standard reference/value placeholders must not accidentally make every footprint uncompilable, but real F.SilkS/F.Fab content must not disappear silently.\n4. Require a lockfile-wide capability census test so K2’s supported/rejected seed-part set is explicit.\n\nCANDIDATE CHEAP REMORAS:\n5. Cross-language deterministic-ID golden vectors: canonical byte encoding, key-order independence, Unicode/separators, numeric normalization, board namespace, ≥128-bit entity IDs/full content hashes, content fields affect content_id while provenance does not. Go/YAML-v2 must reproduce the vectors.\n6. Strict new-path pad typing: legacy path may preserve unknown→SMD compatibility, but FootprintDefinition retains unknown raw token + attributed unsupported diagnostic so K2 cannot silently fabricate it as SMD.\n7. Deep-immutability/validity tests: no mutable containers; reject empty/duplicate IDs, NaN/Inf, nonpositive sizes/widths, invalid layer refs/cross-refs, and drill>=copper diameter where applicable.\n8. Transform properties in addition to the KiCad oracle: top compatibility, mirror involution, F/B layer-map involution, wildcard preservation, unequal-pad/local-angle composition, and reflected arc orientation.\n9. Use the real seed DIP-6 legacy start/end/angle arc as the arc-normalization regression, including bottom reflection.\n10. Stable Diagnostic.code + structured source references for LLM/UI repair and DRC attribution; avoid free-form-only errors.\n11. Layer-contract drift test: new ResolvedBoard copper IDs/KiCad aliases must agree with agent_router.layers so K1 does not create a third mapping authority.\n12. Lockfile-wide FootprintDefinition adapter smoke test: every pinned definition gets deterministic local IDs/content ID and an explicit supported-or-diagnostic result.\n13. After the suite passes, verify/close stale roundrect-parser tracking and update the Linux test hint’s old baseline (administrative; no product behavior).\n\nSol’s recommendation:\n- Treat 1–4 as a K1 correctness decision, with the measured 7/11 result resolved before K2.\n- Automatically take 5 and 7–12 as approved Remoras; they are adjacent tests/invariants with high leverage.\n- Take 6 if strict/new versus compatibility/legacy behavior can be separated without consumer rewire.\n- Take 13 after objective verification.\n\nPlease answer with A/B/C classification per item, any precise scope changes, and a rough test-count/effort expectation. Transition the question to answered when reconciled.","created_at":"2026-07-19T16:07:58","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:12:27","assigned_to":"Claude","directed_to":"Claude","priority":2,"tags":["k1","pcb","quality","remora","resolved-ir","seed-library","tests"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed21517c90a28610e22cdc4216"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7b2a78b07962ae47accdee1f81cb","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"K1 final execution seam: success diagnostics, contextual blocking, census gate, and canonical numbers","description":"The K1 architecture and Remora scope are approved. Four small execution-seam details remain; please give authoritative decisions so Sol can begin immediately afterward.\n\n1. SUCCESS WITH WARNINGS\nThe current result is described as ResolvedBoard | ResolutionFailure. Remora item 2 allows non-blocking UnsupportedFeature markers to produce warning diagnostics while still resolving, but diagnostics may not live inside ResolvedBoard and PreviewBoard is not guaranteed to be built by every fab caller.\nRecommended:\n- ResolutionSuccess{board:ResolvedBoard, diagnostics:tuple[Diagnostic]}\n- ResolutionFailure{diagnostics:tuple[Diagnostic]}\n- ResolutionResult = ResolutionSuccess | ResolutionFailure\nK2 always returns ResolutionResult. PreviewBoard may reuse diagnostics but is not their only success-path carrier.\nConfirm or specify another typed success-report channel.\n\n2. DOMAIN VS CONTEXTUAL BLOCKING\nA static blocks_fabrication flag is insufficient by itself:\n- fp_text/fp_curve on silk/fab/user may be non-blocking for the initial output policy, but the same kinds on copper/mask/paste affect manufactured geometry and must block until supported;\n- zone_connect matters when zones exist but is inert when no zones exist;\n- captured geometry is not automatically emitter-supported.\nRecommended:\n- UnsupportedFeature carries intrinsic domain/affected layer and a conservative default_blocking value;\n- K2 capability policy decides fatality using feature domain + actual layer + board context + requested output set;\n- copper/drill/mask/paste loss always blocks the relevant fab output;\n- silk/fab/user omissions may warn only when the approved v1 output policy permits them;\n- captured but unsupported-by-K3 geometry must still be rejected/warned explicitly, never silently ignored.\nDefine the authoritative rule and whether the field remains named blocks_fabrication or becomes default_blocking/affected_outputs.\n\n3. K1 CENSUS VS K2 COMPILE GATE\nK1 has no compiler, so it cannot assert that all 11 footprints are ResolvedBoard-constructible.\nRecommended split:\n- K1 required test: all locked footprints parse+adapt; all relevant source features are captured or diagnosed; deterministic ids/content ids; explicit zero default-blocking markers for the shipped set (or an exact approved nonblocking set).\n- K2 required test: compile every shipped locked footprint in a minimal valid board and assert ResolutionSuccess under the v1 capability/output profile.\nConfirm this split and add the K2 half to K2 acceptance if approved.\n\n4. CROSS-LANGUAGE CANONICAL NUMBERS\nComment 614 says Python repr(float) equals Go strconv.FormatFloat(...,'g',-1,64); this is not universally byte-identical (e.g. Python repr(1.0) is \"1.0\", Go shortest g formatting is typically \"1\"). Default JSON escaping/exponent styles can differ too.\nPlease define the canonical number/string encoding independently of either runtime and approve committed golden vectors covering at least:\n- 0.0, -0.0, 1.0, integers represented as float, fractional values;\n- small/large exponent forms and exponent sign/zero normalization;\n- float64 round-trip boundaries;\n- Unicode, control characters, slash/quote/backslash, and HTML-sensitive characters;\n- sorted keys, compact separators, booleans/null.\nRecommended: normalize -0 to 0; use a named RFC/JCS-style canonical JSON algorithm if its numeric rules meet cross-language needs, otherwise define explicit decimal/exponent normalization. K1 pins Python output to golden bytes; YAML-v2/Go must reproduce the same vectors before using minted IDs.\n\nPlease answer all four, state any additions to resolved_board.py/test scope and K2 acceptance, and transition to answered.","created_at":"2026-07-19T16:16:48","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:19:28","assigned_to":"Claude","directed_to":"Claude","priority":2,"tags":["canonical-id","capability","diagnostics","final-seam","k1","pcb","resolved-ir"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed21517c90a28610e22cdc4216"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7b2d327070f3ae978ecbe15d555a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Cross-language canonical IDs = RFC 8785 JCS, NOT repr==FormatFloat","created_at":"2026-07-19T16:19:47","updated_at":"2026-07-19T16:19:47","tags":["canonical-id","cross-language","determinism","jcs","pcb","resolved-ir","rfc8785"],"value":"ResolvedBoard content_id + minted entity IDs must be byte-reproducible across Python (worker) + Go (broker) + YAML-v2. DO NOT assume Python repr(float) == Go strconv.FormatFloat(f,'g',-1,64) — they DIFFER (repr(1.0)=\"1.0\" vs Go \"1\"; exponent/escaping styles too). Canonical spec = RFC 8785 JCS (sorted keys, no whitespace, JCS string escaping, ECMAScript Number-to-String: shortest round-trip, integer-valued floats WITHOUT trailing .0, -0→\"0\"). Self-contained impl in pcb_worker/canonical_id.py, pinned by COMMITTED golden vectors (0.0/-0.0/1.0/ints-as-float/fractional/exponents/round-trip-boundaries/unicode/control/escapes/sorted-keys/booleans/null). content_id = full SHA-256 over JCS bytes; persisted entity id = type-prefix + first 128 bits. Never json.dumps/repr for IDs. Decided in K1-types contract (docket 019f7b2a78b0 comment 617; keystone 019f7abf55c2).","component":"pcb-resolved-ir","key":"canonical-id-encoding","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f7dade006725bb09da33c8fe40bbd","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Sol cold review — K2 compiler (compile_board.py) at minerva-plugins 7ce11527","description":"COLD REVIEW REQUEST for Sol (GPT-5.6). You are the reviewer; Claude Opus implemented. Score against the rubric IN ORDER: durability → DRY → reliability → debuggability → cost. Verdict: approve / approve_with_notes / must_fix / reject. Answer inline on this question.\n\nREPO/BASE/DIFF\n- Repo: ~/github/minerva-plugins, branch main.\n- Review the diff base..HEAD = 06da15ef0a8713ae8b0816e4ec106706cbe3f789 .. 7ce115273434048b0bc4b3b2bd3ae60828ac37b4 (exactly two NEW files; nothing else touched).\n- Files: pcb/worker/pcb_worker/compile_board.py (~640 lines) + pcb/worker/tests/test_compile_board.py (44 tests).\n- Run: create a venv, `pip install -e 'pcb/worker[dev]'` (pyyaml, gerber-writer==0.4.3.3, pytest, pygerber==2.4.3, gerbonara==1.6.3), then `cd pcb/worker && <venv>/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -q`.\n\nOBJECTIVE GATE (already green here): full worker suite 519 passed / 1 skipped (475 K1-types baseline + 44 new, purely additive). Compile-census: all 11 locked seed footprints → ResolutionSuccess. Placed-geometry parity vs the live resolve+place_point path: 76/76 pads exact (0 deltas).\n\nCONTRACT OF RECORD: keystone minerva:019f7abf55c2 comments 605/606 (proposal) + 608 (your K1 reconcile: valid-by-construction split, one placement transform incl mirror, stable entity ids, ResolvedNet ordered, tagged-union seams, fail-closed sweep, K2 = bounded 2-layer/rect/round/through subset) + K2 acceptance comment 618 (ResolutionResult; CapabilityPolicy decides fatality; compile-census).\n\nWHAT K2 DOES: canonical board dict → ResolutionSuccess{ResolvedBoard, diagnostics} | ResolutionFailure. Sole constructor of PlacedPad/PlacedGraphic via geometry.PlacementTransform (materialize-once). Adjudicates K1 parser markers through DefaultCapabilityPolicy: blocking → ERROR/Failure; non-blocking → WARNING + STRIPPED from the interned FootprintDefinition (so the def satisfies ResolvedBoard's no-residual-marker invariant; content_id becomes the adjudicated identity). Gate = default-OFF by non-wiring (K3 repoints emitters).\n\nJUDGMENT CALLS I MADE — please scrutinize each (these are the traps):\n1. PARITY LEVEL: proven at placed-geometry level (absolute pad centre/size/drill multiset), NOT the gerbonara FILE-level oracle, because no emitter reads the IR until K3 — nothing to diff IR-side yet. K3 upgrades to the full gerbonara diff. Acceptable, or must K2 stand up a throwaway IR→gerber emitter now to run the real oracle?\n2. MARKER STRIPPING: non-blocking markers are removed from the interned def (dataclasses.replace, recomputing content_id) and recorded as WARNINGs. Is \"strip + warn, content_id = adjudicated identity\" the right model, or should residual markers be represented some other way?\n3. POLICY SEMANTICS: is_blocking = marker.default_blocking AND (marker.domain.value in requested_outputs OR affected_outputs ∩ requested_outputs); requested defaults to V1_FAB_OUTPUTS (all fab domains). Faithful to \"copper/drill/mask/paste loss on a REQUESTED output → Failure; silk/fab/doc → WARN\"?\n4. TRANSFORM-VERSION \"assertion\": recorded only as a string in BoardProvenance.compiler_version (\"...+transform/kicad-flip-v1\"); there is NO per-pad version field and no runtime guard against geometry.py. Is that sufficient for comment-608's \"asserts transform-version\", or does it need a real guard?\n5. K3-UNSUPPORTED GEOMETRY (618.2 \"captured-but-K3-unsupported geometry rejected/warned EXPLICITLY\"): I carry ALL parser-captured graphics (silk/fab/crtyd incl arcs) into the IR and warn only on parser-UNCAPTURED markers; I did NOT pre-declare which silk primitives K3 can emit (K3 isn't built). Deferred the K3-capability declaration to K3. Acceptable, or must K2 declare it now?\n6. RULE PROFILE: the v1 conservative fab floor is a hardcoded module constant, digest-pinned via content_id → RuleProfileRef(v1-fab-conservative). board.design_rules.clearance_mm → min_clearance; every other minimum from the constant. Is a module constant an acceptable \"versioned rule source with recorded provenance\"?\n7. ENTITY IDS: traces/segments/vias/holes use ORDINAL-derived ids (derive_id(\"trace\", net_id, ordinal), etc). Deterministic for compile-from-scratch but NOT edit-stable (reordering renumbers). Contract M3 called traces \"authored-or-minted-persisted (survive geometry edits)\". Is ordinal-derived OK for v1 (persisted identity = YAML-v2/routing concern), or must K2 mint+persist now? Nets DO use name-derived ids + name-sorted index (survives reordering).\n8. COMPONENT PROVENANCE: falls back to empty Provenance() because the seed lib attaches none. K1 comment 606 decision 7 wanted provenance populated from the seed-lock sha. Is the empty fallback a real gap to file (K1 follow-up), or fine for v1?\n9. COINCIDENCE: kept as a compiler ERROR when the board declares per-pin local positions (silk/copper desync guard). Right call, or out of K2 scope?\n\nPlease give the verdict + any must_fix items with file:line, ranked by the rubric.","created_at":"2026-07-20T03:59:34","updated_at":"2026-07-21T16:32:09","priority":2,"tags":["cold-review","k2","keystone","pcb","resolved-ir","sol-review"],"parent":"minerva:019f7aed55d4","answer":"APPROVED at minerva-plugins d8f26e150c22401f3c6d5de4c2b6d2c380a01696 after final narrow verification (comment 632). The two closing blockers from comment 630 are fixed; targeted compiler tests are 95/95 and the full worker suite is 576 passed / 1 skipped. K2's review gate is cleared. K3 may begin, but consumer repoint remains blocked by persistent authored IDs + pin-authority/YAML-v2 shared validation (019f802ca3af) and the K3 faithful capability-conformance gate (comment 628)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f800e66687c648a5d949b9b224e31","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb-worker/layer-authority","created_at":"2026-07-20T15:04:15","updated_at":"2026-07-20T15:04:15","value":"The single worker-side layer authority is `agent_router.layers` (the LOWER standalone base package): CANON_TO_KICAD {top:F.Cu, bottom:B.Cu}, KICAD_TO_CANON, STACK_INDEX {top:0, bottom:1}, canon_to_kicad()/kicad_to_canon(). pcb_worker imports it UPWARD (`from agent_router.layers import ...`) — allowed; the reverse is not. Do NOT re-declare a local top/bottom↔F.Cu/B.Cu map (that duplication caused the two-emitter via bug pre-T1.5). K3 emitter (gerber.py `_GERBER_SUFFIXES`) produces exactly {F.Cu,B.Cu,F.Mask,B.Mask,F.SilkS,Edge.Cuts} + PTH/NPTH drills — NO F.Fab, F.Paste, or B.SilkS (top-silk only). K2's K3_EMITTED_LAYERS/V1_FAB_OUTPUTS are pinned to that. Pad→layer rule (both emitter + K2 compiler): TH→both copper+both mask; SMD→own-side copper/mask/paste; NPTH→mechanical (no copper). Also: DrillDefinition lives in footprint_def, not resolved_board (resolved_board only TYPE_CHECKING-imports it).","component":"pcb-worker","key":"layer-authority"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f802ca3af74f4aa55ef2ed86268ad","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Persistent YAML entity IDs + v1→v2 mint-migration — GATE before K3 DRC/routing switch","description":"From Sol K2 re-review (question 019f7dade006 comment 623, decision b). K2 now board-namespaces every derived id and honors an authored `id` when present, but mutable trace/via/hole ids are still ORDINAL-derived (deterministic for compile-from-scratch, NOT stable under reorder/insert). K2 emits an INFO `ordinal_ids` bridge diagnostic recording this. Sol's ruling: ordinal ids are acceptable ONLY while the compiler result is strictly private to a fabrication emitter; they are UNSAFE for DRC/routing consumers.\n\nREQUIRED before any identity-dependent consumer (DRC/routing) switches onto the ResolvedBoard IR — i.e. this GATES the identity-dependent half of K3 (019f7aed6d9e) and the routing work (019f7095c395), not K2 itself (K2 stays unwired):\n1. Add persistent board id + child entity ids (trace/via/segment/hole/zone) to the canonical board-yaml contract (docs/board-yaml.md + Go pcb/internal/board).\n2. One-time v1→v2 mint-and-write migration: assign+persist stable ids to existing boards.\n3. Board-namespace every child id by the persisted board id (compiler already does this for derived ids; switch to reading persisted ids).\n4. Identity-dependent consumers REJECT ephemeral/ordinal ids (fail-closed) so a board without persisted ids cannot silently route/DRC against unstable identity.\n\nSequencing guarantee: as long as this lands before K3 wires DRC/routing onto the IR, K2 may ship unwired with the ordinal bridge. If K3 would switch a consumer before this lands, this blocks that switch.","created_at":"2026-07-20T15:37:16","updated_at":"2026-07-21T16:24:24","priority":2,"tags":["identity","k3-gate","pcb","resolved-ir","sol-review","yaml-v2"],"parent":"minerva:019f7abf55c2"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019f858a058e7b9183f2525b457efdeb","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"minerva-repo/dct-merge-conflict","created_at":"2026-07-21T16:37:22","updated_at":"2026-07-21T16:37:22","value":"Docs/minerva.dct (the standalone Docket store) is newline-delimited JSON — one record per line: {_type:item,id,...}, {_type:event,item_id,seq,...}, comments. Multi-machine pushes to Minerva `development` routinely conflict on it. It usually LINE-MERGES cleanly, but when two machines edit the same region (e.g. one adds items while the other updates an item's status/updated_at + appends events) git leaves conflict hunks. Do NOT hand-edit or pick one side — that drops the other machine's items/events. Safe fix: union-merge programmatically. `git show :2:Docs/minerva.dct` (ours/remote) + `git show :3:Docs/minerva.dct` (theirs/mine), then in Python: key items by id (keep the record with the latest updated_at), key events by (item_id,seq), key comments by (item_id,id); result = ours as base, replace item lines whose id has a newer updated_at in theirs, append every theirs event/comment/record whose identity isn't already in ours. Verify: 0 conflict markers, every line json.loads-valid, key item statuses correct, remote-added items still present. Then `git add` + `git rebase --continue` (GIT_EDITOR=true). Docket loaders index by id/seq so trailing-append order is fine.","component":"minerva-repo","key":"dct-merge-conflict"}
{"_type":"item","id":"0bd63d76bb70ae8a912dac34bf717de2","type":"discussion","status":"resolved","title":"Annotation substrate refactor: generic callouts plus plugin semantic anchors","description":"## Context\n\nDiscussion opened from CAD plugin edge-annotation exploration on 2026-04-29. The current CAD implementation drifted from the intended model: edge numbers were implemented as annotation kinds, while true edge-attached user intent is not cleanly modeled.\n\nThe original product intent was:\n- Minerva provides a base annotation substrate for generic callouts, arrows/text, mermaid/flow diagrams, highlights, and inked annotations.\n- Plugins extend that substrate with domain-specific semantic anchors and specialized render/authoring behavior.\n- CAD has a separate edge-numbering affordance for user information and LLM disambiguation, not as durable annotation data.\n\n## Problem\n\nCAD currently has overlapping concepts:\n\n1. **Edge numbers** — useful transient viewport labels so a user can say \"fillet edge 2 by 3mm\". These are derived from current geometry and should behave like a CAD overlay/tool, not document annotations.\n2. **Edge annotations** — durable user or AI intent attached to geometry, e.g. \"Fillet this edge by 3mm\" anchored to `cad.edge:2`.\n3. **Generic annotations** — base Minerva callouts/ink/arrows/flows that should remain editor-agnostic.\n\nThe current implementation mixes these layers. `cad_edge_number` is a plugin annotation kind, the edge picker is duplicated between EdgeOverlay and the annotation tool, and CAD annotations store projected/world points instead of treating the edge as the primary semantic anchor.\n\n## Proposed model\n\n### 1. Core Minerva annotation substrate\nCore owns durable generic primitives/kinds:\n- callout / text / arrow\n- highlight / region / polyline\n- ink stroke\n- mermaid or flow annotation\n- shared sidecar persistence, MCP list/add/update/delete, render overlay, toolbar basics\n\nCore does not know about CAD edges, PCB nets, spreadsheet cells, etc.\n\n### 2. Plugin semantic anchor extension\nPlugins own domain anchor resolution. CAD should expose anchors like:\n- `cad.edge:<id>`\n- future: `cad.face:<id>`, `cad.vertex:<id>`, `cad.plane:<name>`\n\nA CAD edge callout should store semantic attachment first, e.g. `anchored_to: \"cad.edge:2\"`, with optional payload for text, label offset, visible views, and edge-id snapshot. Render paths resolve the anchor through `Cad_AnnotationHost.resolve_anchor()` and project it into the active panes.\n\n### 3. Edge numbers as transient CAD overlay\nEdge numbers should move out of the annotation substrate. They are a CAD viewport information mode:\n- toggle show/hide edge numbers\n- optionally show labels for selected/candidate edges only\n- not persisted as annotations\n- not listed as user intent\n- not manipulated by generic annotation tools\n\nLLM workflow: user says \"fillet edge 2 by 3mm\" after seeing the overlay; LLM reads selected/numbered edge state through CAD MCP tools.\n\n### 4. Edge annotations as durable intent\nA CAD edge annotation is a note/callout anchored to `cad.edge:<id>`, e.g. user clicks an edge and enters \"Fillet this edge by 3mm\". The LLM can then read that as user intent through annotation MCP tools.\n\n## Implementation implications\n\n- Replace or deprecate the current `cad_edge_number` annotation kind as the primary edge annotation path.\n- Add a `cad_edge_callout` / `cad_edge_note` kind, or allow generic callouts to carry plugin-owned `anchored_to` values with CAD-specific anchor resolution.\n- Move all edge picking into one CAD service used by EdgeOverlay, selection, annotation authoring, and MCP introspection.\n- Make `Cad_AnnotationHost.describe_point()` and/or `resolve_anchor()` return semantic CAD entities instead of empty strings.\n- Keep generic transform/select tools away from domain-anchored annotations unless the plugin provides compatible hit-test/bounds behavior in rendered screen space.\n- Revisit existing DCR `019dd5f871b17018bf8856c283aa3fd5`, which currently encodes the likely-wrong direction of treating edge numbers as annotations.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. Should plugin-specific anchors live only in `anchored_to`, or should there be an explicit `anchor` object with type/id/snapshot/repair metadata?\n2. Should generic callout kinds be able to render plugin-resolved anchors, or should each plugin define thin domain-specific callout kinds?\n3. How should broken anchors render and surface to the LLM?\n4. What is the minimum anchor-repair contract for CAD topology changes?\n5. Should transient LLM visual aids be modeled as ephemeral overlays outside annotations, or as annotation records with an explicit `ephemeral` flag?\n\n## Desired outcome\n\nA clear separation: numbers identify geometry for conversation; annotations store durable intent attached to geometry. The substrate stays generic, while plugins extend it through semantic anchors and domain-specific picking/rendering.","created_at":"2026-04-29T15:34:48Z","updated_at":"2026-04-29T23:05:00","priority":2,"tags":["annotations","architecture","cad","plugin-system","semantic-anchors"],"resolution":"Distilled into Annotation v2 substrate DCR 019ddb704374717780588abfefa3a8b1 and plan 019ddb70ca45 (11 children). See resolution comment for distillation map."}
